window.VTMB=window.VTMB||{};window.VTMB.fdocs={"021XfazezI4dLLcP":{"name":"Thrown Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can cause their voice to emanate from any point within sight, whether as a whisper or shout. The voice is audible as if the vampire was standing in that spot and can be heard by anyone within earshot, depending on strength. The voice can also be left in a location, continuing to lure or terrify those who stray, depending on the power used.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No roll is required, apart from the Rouse Check. If using this power in combination with Irresistible Voice, Presenceless Voice, or similar powers, they must roll as usual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"123"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.021XfazezI4dLLcP"},"06VSxeT9wIUnyplx":{"name":"Sway the Flock","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can influence the general mood of animals over a wide area. While they cannot control the specific actions of the beasts, they can steer their behavior toward anything from sleepy indifference to indiscriminate aggression. Use of this power can be subtle, with nothing to show but a lack of birdsong, or it can be pure bedlam, with people barricading themselves indoors, carcasses littering the ground as beasts rip anything that moves apart in a Frenzy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire decides on the behavior they want to encourage in the local animals and makes a Manipulation + Animalism test. The effect scales with the number of successes: At one success animals aren’t noticeably affected, while at five their behavior will be completely dominated by the desired impulse, with calmed animals falling asleep or riled ones attacking anyone or each other with no provocation. Any mundane attempt to control the animals increases in Difficulty equal to the number of successes on the test. The size of the area affected is roughly the size of a football field, though it can be extended with additional Rouse Checks, up to five for a small town.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"21"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.06VSxeT9wIUnyplx"},"08qt92VLJXZhilIR":{"name":"Gift of Malkav","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>The madness of Malkav, the source of Dementation, is not a curse, but a noble burden. To be different or odd is to see things from another angle. Sometimes, that difference must be imposed for a subject to truly know.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Insight against Wits + Composure of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 44). On a win, the victim gains the effects of a Malkavian clan bane at a bane severity equal to the user’s Dementation rating + their power bonus with the user choosing the Attributes which the bane affects. A critical win adds the Malkavian compulsion if the target possesses the vampiric Beast. This does not replace any standard clan banes or Compulsions, but rather adds.</p>\n<p>Those possessing the Malkavian bane normally are immune to this power, but have their Bane Severity increased to the user’s Dementation rating + the user’s power bonus if their Bane Severity is lower.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 52.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"66"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.08qt92VLJXZhilIR"},"0LQccZ1x4K4nxfzt":{"name":"Plug-In","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The alchemist is able to produce a low-level electrical current from their body, powering lights, chargers, or other devices.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s blood, sanguine blood, liquid nitrogen, tungsten shavings, and a broken battery.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pool:</strong> Resolve + Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make a Resolve + Alchemy test (Difficulty 2). On a success, the alchemist fills with electrical currents that can be used to power small devices, such as a smartphone, a personal computer, or a light bulb. These currents power the item by touch; it loses power when contact stops.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> If lighting a room: one scene. If powering a device, until it reaches half battery charge.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"103"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"eFmMiwcp":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"FVBG2sG7":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.0LQccZ1x4K4nxfzt"},"0RJLAeYQ6Qb2nvxE":{"name":"Airborne Momentum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula enables the alchemist to lift themselves from the ground, achieving a state of swift flight or floating. They can fly in any direction, including up or down, though their carrying capacity is limited.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric and sanguine human blood, champagne, bird blood, helium, scopolamine or belladonna extract</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Strength + Alchemy vs Strength+Athletics (if resisted)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The potion enables the alchemist (and only them) to fly or hover at approximately running speed. The flier can carry a human-sized mass, though their speed drops to walking speed. Grabbing and carrying aloft an unwilling subject or pulling the flier to the ground both require a contest between the alchemist’s Strength + Alchemy and the other’s Strength + Athletics.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"287"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"dzZWzMN2":{"path":"attributes.strength"},"BOTguI9G":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.0RJLAeYQ6Qb2nvxE"},"0SiShiQ0dqU05QdL":{"name":"Flaying Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this cruel power employed by the most cruel of the Tzimisce, the vampire can tear away a victim’s skin as easily as removing a robe, all without disturbing the muscles and other tissue beneath. They simply grab a handful of skin and pull it away or surgically slice it with the artful caress of a fingertip. Regardless of the method used, the target suffers excruciating pain and begins bleeding profusely from the exposed flesh. Even if a target somehow survives the blood loss, the area of skinless flesh soon becomes infected. Cainites have less to fear from the power, though they too suffer blood loss until they can regenerate the missing tissue.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Potence •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Strength + Brawl</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Strength + Brawl against the victim’s defence pool like Dexterity + Athletics to dodge, dealing a number of damage equal to the margins. The damage type is Aggravated against mortals, but Superficial against creatures who do not require organs and blood to live, like vampires. Mortals who suffer a number of Aggravated Health damage equal to their Stamina from a single attack pass out from shock.</p>\n<p>As victims writhe and scream in pain or lie unconscious from shock, they also continue bleeding profusely from their exposed skin. Each turn after the attack’s, mortals take additional Aggravated Health damage equal to the vampire’s Potence rating of or the margins on the attack roll, whichever is lower. Instead of taking points of Health damage from bleeding, vampires are forced to make a number of Rouse Checks equal to bleeding damage value while mortals with Hunger tracker (ghouls, revenants) suffer both.</p>\n<p>Once any of the original damage is healed (such as by Mending) or stanched (Dexterity + Medicine Difficulty equal to the vampire’s Vicissitude rating, Full Action), the bleeding slows to one level of damage per hour. Only when the original injury is completely healed does the bleeding stop. Methods for this include Mending, converting all points of Aggravated Health damage into Superficial for mortals or succeeding on an Intelligence + Medicine Test (difficulty equal to the vampir’es Vicissitude rating).</p>\n<p>Damage from this attack is influenced neither by armour nor by boosts to unarmed damage as this is not a punch but a precise cut. Bleeding damage does not stack. On repeated usage, the strongest bleeding effect applies.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"204"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"NsbK8Y2M":{"path":"attributes.strength"},"D09nAm7I":{"path":"skills.brawl"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.0SiShiQ0dqU05QdL"},"0vBUUccWQfdNSYkn":{"name":"Fluent Endurance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire is able to call upon their supernatural resilience effortlessly, and rarely tempt their Hunger when doing so.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the vampire uses a Blood Surge to augment a Stamina or Fortitude test, they may reroll the Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"79"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.0vBUUccWQfdNSYkn"},"0yE0xXcbME7JWQAP":{"name":"Gorgon’s Scales","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Resonance of the blood in the vampire’s system grants them various defenses against the powers of other Cainites, or protects them from common vampire weaknesses. Finding the right vessel to respond to an immediate defensive requirement is not always easy, but especially callous vampires have been known to keep vessels with different Resonances in ready supply, using elaborate manipulative schemes or plain drugs to keep their emotions vivid and ready to tap.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On activation the vampire gains a set of immunities or resistances dependent on the Resonance of the blood they most recently fed upon.</p>\n<p>• Choleric: A stake driven through the vampire’s heart is consumed with decay or turns to ash at the end of the scene, immediately freeing the vampire from paralysis. The Resonance is then lost.</p>\n<p>• Melancholic: Aggravated Health damage caused by fire is reduced to Superficial. The Resonance is lost after reducing four levels of damage.</p>\n<p>• Phlegmatic: The vampire gains a four-dice bonus to resist powers that would reveal anything about them or what they know (e.g. Auspex). Once the power is activated it lasts for a scene, at which point the Resonance is lost.</p>\n<p>• Sanguine: Aggravated Health damage caused by sunlight is reduced to Superficial. The Resonance is lost after reducing four levels of damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the scene ends or the Resonance is lost, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"83"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.0yE0xXcbME7JWQAP"},"1BzOe9rz9XaSBeI1":{"name":"Serpent’s Kiss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s fangs grow longer and hollow, allowing them to inject a bit of their own blood in return while they feed or bite in combat. Some use their Kiss to subtly start a Blood Bond, while others use it to administer harsh poison.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When the vampire bites or kisses another, they may make a Rouse check to force some of their own vitae into their victim. The victim of the bite or kiss is treated as though they tasted the vampire’s Blood for purposes of the Blood Bond, learning a Discipline Power, becoming a ghoul, or other effects of imbibing blood.</p>\n<p>If the vampire’s Blood normally or temporarily has deleterious effects, those are passed on to the victim as well. Additionally, users of Quietus can use powers such as Scorpion’s Touch or Baal’s Caress via their bite, much to the surprise of those they Kiss.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"151"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.1BzOe9rz9XaSBeI1"},"1HoOQgugvUV0jN4N":{"name":"Quietus Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>As the user masters the Discipline said to have been created by Haqim himself, the vampire figures out how all Gifts of the Blood are connected, and how all the Paths of Quietus lead to the same destination. The master of Quietus becomes the judge, jury and the executioner among vampires, able to punish whole Elysium’s worth of unworthy Cainites.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Powers of any Quietus Path that require touch or transmission by poison are now available to the vampire by making a gesture with their hand towards the victim (those who succeed on a Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge may notice something odd) and making an activation Rouse Check. Such ranged poison attacks use Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude, if the victim possesses them)</p>\n<p>The vampire can also amplify any of their Quietus Path powers to affect a group of people, mortal or vampire, at once, if the power may be applied at range (such as Blood’s Sweat). When rolling against crowds of mortals or insignificant vampires, the Storyteller rolls only for the strongest one and applies the successes for the rest. When rolling powers of any Quietus Path, you always count as having a specialty.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"148"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.1HoOQgugvUV0jN4N"},"1LicHjeWmsabtdBD":{"name":"Kiss of Lachesis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires with this power gain limited mastery over the physical age of objects and individuals. Commanding the ability to either accelerate the aging process or unweave entropy and lessen time’s hold, the vampire may mature or regress a target decades or even centuries in the blink of an eye.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire briefly touches the target and concentrates for a turn (in combat this requires any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/ Brawl). The player then makes the activating Rouse Check and rolls Manipulation + Occult vs. the victim’s Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude, if they possess it). The Cainite may increase or decrease (their choice) the age of the target by an amount of time according to their successes. This power cannot modify or revise history; its singular domain lies in either reversing or quickening the effects of time in terms of wear and tear. They do not need to use the full effect; for example, with five margins, they may opt to age the victim by six months instead of a century if they wish.</p>\n<p>Vampires must make a Rouse Check per day of accelerated aging they incur, usually resulting in Frenzy and torpor if they lose all vitae. Living beings begin to wither and starve, usually perishing of dehydration after three or more days (after the third day, living beings lose a point of Stamina per day, until death. This may be recovered over time). Effects upon non-living objects are up to the Storyteller, nevertheless, many organic compounds rapidly decay within a day or a week, while certain metals might take on rust and become increasingly brittle over time.</p>\n<p>Unraveling the cords of time usually has a similar effect, but objects cannot return to an earlier or incomplete state before the point at which they were assembled. A golden ring may appear newly crafted, but will not revert to a lump of raw metal. Likewise, a living being may regress, at most, to the point of birth (or its equivalent), but not to a prenatal state. An amputee will not regenerate a missing limb, nor will a broken blade become anything less than freshly forged shards.</p>\n<p>This power does not change an object or subject’s mental or mystical properties. Sentient beings recall all memories and any Derangements they possess. A vampire’s corporeal state will not revert to a point earlier than that of Embrace. Whether their age increases or decreases, they retains all Disciplines and keep any changes in Generation due to diablerie. If aged far enough, a vampire will lose any marks of diablerie from their aura. They may even begin to evince a rapacious hunger for her own kind.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant Margins Effect 0 margins Up to 24 hours 1 margin Up to one week 2 margins Up to one month 3 margins Up to one year 4 margins Up to a decade 4 margins Up to a century 5 margins Up to one century per margin over 5</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"170"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"qMcYMrLz":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"leOFVqP4":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.1LicHjeWmsabtdBD"},"1fZvCDHP5Sp8osvj":{"name":"Haunting Illusions","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>Haunting Illusions makes it possible for hallucinations created by Chimerstry to only be visible to a single (or more) persons, potentially driving people mad as others do not believe them to be experiencing what is clearly real to them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> As power augmented</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One point of Willpower per target</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> To weave Ignis Fatuus or Fata Morgana into subtlety, the caster must spend a point of Willpower per target they intend to inflict in addition to the Rouse Check required by Ignis Fatus or Fata Morgana. Once inflicted, nobody except the targets will be able to see hallucinations except those with powers like Auspex who contest the power as normal, sensing the magical disruptions around them rather than seeing them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As power modified by this.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"48"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.1fZvCDHP5Sp8osvj"},"1jhUjpxgwj4V1wNw":{"name":"Hinder Mending","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With Hinder Mending, the vampire concocts a special poison that prevents usage of vitae for mending, disrupting vampire targets and their other creations that utilise vitae to heal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice/free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This contact poison has the same system as Scorpion’s Touch (see below) and is applied the same way).</p>\n<p>If the poison hits, the poison makes it harder for the victim to heal. To perform mending with vitae, they must spend 1 + additional Rouse Checks equal to the user’s Quietus Cruciatus rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The poison remains potent for nights equal to the user’s Quietus rating</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"141"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.1jhUjpxgwj4V1wNw"},"23MgLiaYiMQ1R8Ol":{"name":"Sense the Cycle","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Rather than diagnosing the sick and dying, the nascent third eye of the Watchers sees the cycle of life entire. They see the ebb and flow of lives in the streets, not the vitality locked within veins and organs. The Watcher experiences waking visions that alter her perception of the world around them, leaving them prone to seeing dire omens and auspicious portents in the smallest event. When they focus, these visions resolve into incredible personal insight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the user must be able to see the target and their surroundings with their 3rd eye. On a win, the user may ask the Storyteller (or a player) a question for each margin and get a truthful answer. The questions asked are answered with public knowledge such as “what is this person’s role in mortal society?”, “what would cause this person to turn hostile?” or “is this person well-known for hostile behaviour?”. Additionally, the user gains a two-dice bonus on their next Social action against the target if they use newly-acquired knowledge to approach the target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant, but the bonus lasts for the rest of the night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"195"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.23MgLiaYiMQ1R8Ol"},"2FlHM1glzNQc71I0":{"name":"Many Voices, One Mouth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>A Malkavian is one part of a greater hole, and same can be said for other users of Dementation. With the Voice of Many, a Malkavian can amplify their voice to be able to carry terrible power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Every power of Dementation may now affect as many targets as the user wants, all at once. The user has to make an additional Rouse Check (for the purposes of re-rolling, the “additional” Rouse Check is level 5). Targets must still fulfil conditions such as at least listening to the user when a power demands a conversation. The user makes any needed roll against the strongest opponent in the group, altough players should always be given the ability to roll on their own.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As per amplified</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"68"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2FlHM1glzNQc71I0"},"2OncQUrs2BhNOotH":{"name":"Swarm of Shadows","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>As the Lasombra masters their control over the inky, primordial blackness, they turn the darkness into a weapon. They can draw tentacles from shadowed reaches and use them to attack enemies nearby. In the traditional lore of the Clan, the practitioner is literally calling out to the dark half to make itself manifest and grasp at its victims.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Arms of Ahriman or Fires of the Abyss</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Wits + Occult (Difficulty 2) while focusing on shadows within their line of sight. For each margin, one tentacle sprouts where blood landed with the maximum number of possible active tentacles equal to the vampire's Obtenebration rating. These tentacles are either those of Arms of Ahriman or Fires of the Abyss, chosen by the user at creation. The extrusions have a length (in yards/ meters) equal to twice the Obtenebration dots of the user. (Note that they, being shadows, move across surfaces, not air, and any distances must take this into account.)</p>\n<p>The tentacles possess 4 points of Health, take Superficial Health damage from all but fire, True faith and Sunlight (as a vampire with Bane Severity 2). They have Dexterity and Strength equal to the vampire’s Obtenebration + power bonus of the vampire. If the caster desires, they may spend blood to increase Strength and Dexterity at the cost of one One Rouse Check per point increased; they may also increase the length by spending one blood per six feet/ two meters of length per tentacle. The user may use their own Blood Surge to boost attacks of tentacles.</p>\n<p>They benefit from Potence and Daimonion of the user just like Arms of Ahriman/Fires of the Abyss. Their initiative score is equal to the vampire’s Wits and may attack as directed by the vampire, but will attack at random if left without orders.</p>\n<p>The tentacles benefit from same effects of Solid Shadows as Arms of Ahriman/Fires of the Abyss.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"112"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"kuONFfqm":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"Vr4A83oo":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2OncQUrs2BhNOotH"},"2SM1YRbKDsGkCI2N":{"name":"Flesh of Marble","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The power of the Blood causes the skin of the vampire to harden, taking on a marble-like sheen that is still supple but stops almost any blow before momentarily breaking and reforming. A vampire using this power is almost impossible to destroy outright, barring a lucky blow or physical restraint.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> With this power active, the vampire ignores the first source of physical damage each turn, including fire but not sunlight. If confusion arises about which source is “first,” the Storyteller either decides based on the narrative, or the vampire ignores the most damaging single source that turn. A critical win on an attack roll bypasses this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"83"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2SM1YRbKDsGkCI2N"},"2gHWfRHhwuhQBFpl":{"name":"Shatter Stone","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>By adjusting the pitch from a glass-shattering alto to an earthquake-like bariton, the vampire is able to destroy objects by their own voice. What one can do to a human body with this power is too gruesome to describe and often resembles Tzimisce sadisms.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Death of the Drum</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Death of the Drum power now also deals Aggravated Health damage equal to the margin.</p>\n<p>One may also shatter physical objects using Death of the Drum at a difficulty set by the storyteller. For reference, shattering glass or wood would be a Difficulty of 1, collapsing a wall of bricks would be a Difficulty of 5 or 7 for a solid stone wall.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"88"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2gHWfRHhwuhQBFpl"},"2kEoo3BHkvZ92fjJ":{"name":"Prowness From Pain","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Injuries and impairments now only fuel the powers of the vampire, who grows stronger and faster from each blow, rend, or tear received. Only utter destruction can stop one who calls upon this Fortitude power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activating the power the vampire no longer suffers any dice penalties from Health damage sustained, such as physical Impairment. Additionally, they can increase one Physical Attribute by one dot (derived stats are unaffected) for each level of damage on their Health track, Aggravated or Superficial. The user’s Attributes cannot exceed a value equal to their Blood Surge + 5 through this ability.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Example:</strong> Darin has Blood Potency 3, giving him a Blood Surge of 3. He cannot increase his Physical Attributes past 8 using Prowess from Pain</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"84"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2kEoo3BHkvZ92fjJ"},"2lNzdsUL7ADEvznm":{"name":"Seal the Beast’s Maw","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Through rigorous discipline and training, this individual has learned to exert their will to stave off the call of the Hunger for a short time. By concentrating on the death of desire, they force the Beast to subside, enabling them to ignore its insatiable demands. Doing so is a gamble, as it can increase a vampire’s Hunger in the attempt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> At any time during a scene, the vampire may make two Rouse Checks, and if their Hunger doesn’t increase, they can ignore the effects of Hunger for the remainder of the scene. Doing this reduces all dice pools by a number equal to half (rounded up) of their current Hunger rating. If their Hunger increases by any other means during this time, their dice pools reduce by an additional die. If a dice pool reduces to zero through the use of this power, the character must roll to resist a fury frenzy, as stifling their Hunger intensifies the Beast’s rage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"82"},"level":3,"cost":2,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2lNzdsUL7ADEvznm"},"2xKRL3rbNoy4DQPO":{"name":"Touch of the Aethereal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Normally, touching spirits while they’re incorporeal is impossible. With Touch of the Aethereal, that becomes a certain possibility and opens threats of physical harm to spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For the rest of the scene, the user is able to physically touch and harm spirits, dealing damage to them as if they were a mortal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"162"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.2xKRL3rbNoy4DQPO"},"309PrYWApnXI8llg":{"name":"Beast’s Instincts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Instincts of the Beast compel the vampire to animalistic behaviour culminating in Frenzies. This bestial mindset is not without its own usefulness, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may be toggled off and on. While active, the vampire receives a +3 dice bonus to non-supernatural Survival tracking rolls, Animal Ken and Awareness rolls. Being so close to the Beast increases Frenzy Difficulties for the rest of the scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until toggled off</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"13"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.309PrYWApnXI8llg"},"39vEISaU71keyKcn":{"name":"Tongue of Apep","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires are, by nature, cold-blooded and showing minimal signs of life. Setites take it much further, able to mask any signs of lies. This also happens to be the reason for the common saying of “never trust a Setite, even if he appears to be telling the truth! Even if he tells you the Sun burns!”. Many Christian vampires, associate this power with that of the snake of the Garden instead of Apep, attributing darker origins to Serpentis.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s tongue splits into that of a snake and the character adds their Serpentis rating, to all pools involving telling lies and half-truths. Astute observers may notice the tongue with a Wits + Subterfuge roll against the user’s Wits + Stealth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"151"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.39vEISaU71keyKcn"},"3GMygXzM20k8OroG":{"name":"Presenceless Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The voice of the user becomes like that of a siren, able to entrance or terrify by itself, without the user having to be present. The voice can affect anyone within earshot but does not retain its ability if recorded or electronically transmitted.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire is able to use Presence powers through their voice alone. They do not need to see the target, and the target does not have to be in their presence save to be close enough to hear their voice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"122"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.3GMygXzM20k8OroG"},"3QUPaIOcDQOQdIGw":{"name":"Stain the Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user of Dementation speaks to their victim a truth beyond truth, and the victim is left stunned. The user of forces the target’s very soul into self-doubt, temporarily twisting the target’s morality.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Insight against Composure + Wits of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 52). On a win, the target’s Road rating is lowered by an amount equal to the margin for a scene, with the lowest possible Road Rating being 1.</p>\n<p>The Road rating is counted as truly gone until the duration, including for rolling to handle Frenzy, Road bearing, but also Degeneration making it viable as both a blessing and a curse.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 52.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"67"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.3QUPaIOcDQOQdIGw"},"3SmjCAyzZpcP16w8":{"name":"Clairvoyance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>By closing their eyes and entering a light trance, the vampire becomes master of its surroundings. In a few minutes it can gather information from roughly a city-block sized area (more if outdoors or less populated) that would normally take many hours, perhaps days of legwork and investigation. Once connected in such a way to their surroundings the vampire can also receive information on anything happening out of the ordinary in the area.</p>\n<p>Some vampires use this power to monitor locations or persons far away, often using maps, mirrors, crystal balls or reflective surfaces to allow them to focus, others simply concentrate and meditate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Roll Intelligence + Awareness against a Difficulty based on the security and level of activity of the area. Using Clairvoyance on one’s own mansion or haven would be Difficulty 2 while an unfamiliar city block in the slums of a major city would amount to 5 or more. Reductions for places of familiarity are up to the storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>The Storyteller answers the vampire’s questions about the comings and goings in the area, what people have seen and heard, topics of local gossip, recent major shocks or impressions, and so forth. The player can ask roughly one question per point of margin; answers about deliberately concealed information might consume more than one point. A critical win reveals something major, regardless of the questions asked, assuming there is something to reveal.</p>\n<p>If the user wishes to clairvoyantly monitor events in progress, they must roll Resolve + Awareness while either being in the area they wish to monitor or possess something physical from the location or person they wish to monitor (biological material, treasured objects, piece of clothing...). The Test is made against a Difficulty as above according to the area monitored (minimum Difficulty is 2 such as for monitoring an individual), reduced by 1 for using a conduit such as a reflective surface and by 1 if the user possesses important sympathetic link to their target such as biological material. On a win, the user observes a person or a location as if they’re standing nearby, usually above. In this state, the user sees through any supernatural illusions or guises such as Obfuscate.</p>\n<p>The user’s activity and presence may be detected by powers such as Sense the Unseen.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A few minutes for information gathering. Monitoring lasts as long as the user has uninterrupted focus.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"35"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"J89AtrFi":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"FmsWkI1V":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.3SmjCAyzZpcP16w8"},"3XNMkkpalBvVBv9V":{"name":"Defractionate","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula results in a homeopathic elixir. When added to fractionated medical-supply blood, the elixir returns it to freshness, allowing vampires without the Iron Gullet merit to obtain nutrition from it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, sanguine and melancholic human blood, O-negative human blood (only a few ml), moldy spinach, hot black coffee, sodium octanoate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist taps their vessel (Calcinatio), or their own vein (Athanor Corporis) to get the Defractionate elixir if they do not use Fixatio. For each success on the distillation roll, they get enough elixir to turn one blood bag (slakes 1 Hunger) from fractionated to unfractionated blood. Any vampire can consume this blood and slake Hunger with it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Athanor Corporis:</strong> The alchemist can tap their own vein once per night for this elixir until their next feeding, or until they reach Hunger 5. They need not pay the distillation cost again.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Calcinatio:</strong> If the vessel survives the tap, the alchemist can tap them again in a week for more elixir. They need not pay the distillation cost again, but the vessel pays the Health cost (3 Aggravated damage) each time they are tapped.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"285"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.3XNMkkpalBvVBv9V"},"3ww0d4XkUwWNdBQw":{"name":"Shadow Step","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>Masters of Obtenebration can enter a patch of darkness that is large enough to admit them and step through an otherworldly space to emerge in another shadow roughly within eyesight of the first. The abyss between shadows is a twisted and unholy place, described by some as Ahriman itself, as its resting place or as a circle of Hell. Regardless, it exists such that any two shadows within even a 100 yards are just a step apart. This also means that the Cainite can lean into one shadow, using this power, and grab someone or something that is within arm’s reach of a remote shadow to pull it to her location.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire must enter a shadow large enough to cover them, and emerge from another one turn later. The target shadow must be within sight, though it can be perceived by mystical means, such as Shadow Perspective, if desired. It is possible to bring another through the passage, but unless that person is willing, they must be held by a successful grapple.</p>\n<p>If a Degeneration Test is induced by the Rouse Check as a result of using this power, the passenger also receives one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"113"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.3ww0d4XkUwWNdBQw"},"3xFvtH6GxeXH5904":{"name":"Blood’s Curse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire temporarily manipulates the Clan Curse of their victim and thickens an adversary’s Blood, amplifying their weakness.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Quietus Cruciatus 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> </p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user focuses on a victim within the line of sight and makes a gesture (those who succeed on a Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge may notice something odd) after which they roll Intelligence + Occult against Stamina + Occult of their target (the target may use Fortitude if it possesses it instead). On a win, add two (or four on a Critical Win or at least 3 margins) to the victim’s Blood Potency for purposes of Bane severity and feeding penalty (for vampires) only.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until dawn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"146"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"UfklfHG5":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"SX8mNOKs":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.3xFvtH6GxeXH5904"},"41m6obHK2Hm4nTLt":{"name":"Fatal Flaw","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Everything breaks, and the vampire using this power can see the signs before they happen. By observing the subtle work of entropy the vampire can glean the Achilles’ heel of a target to exploit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Necromancy Path •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. Composire vs. Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the target within the line of sight of the vampire has at least one point of Health damage, the vampire may roll Intelligence + Insight vs. Stamina + Subterfuge of the target. On a win, the user notices weak spots of their target and suffers no Called Shot penalty against the target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the target heals its damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"29"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"mRPpUSfI":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"TPlQgpAo":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.41m6obHK2Hm4nTLt"},"4IfLNFmvpmXMm2wP":{"name":"Holy Mission","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>In the ancient days, the Salubri were the vanguard that guided the children of Set (the younger brother of Abel and Caine from whom humanity originates according to the myths) from the infernal. A few times it has happened that some of the Salubri would submit to the temptations offered by their enemies, but it was extremely rare as Valeren coupled with Salubri’s teaching was proof of their divine purpose.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire gains a dice bonus to resist temptations of sin and infernalism as well as a dice bonus to defend against the powers of Daimonion, Dark Thaumaturgy, Spectre Arcanoi, Fomori powers and demonic powers be they Lores or Arcana.</p>\n<p>As usual, the bonus does not stack with other powers such as Unswayable Mind which comes from one’s own mental fortitude while Valeren fuels the vampire with a purpose.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"190"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4IfLNFmvpmXMm2wP"},"4JZpDzCObMifIuTJ":{"name":"Blood Talk","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>A form of blood telepathy, the user is capable of talking directly to someone’s blood which then resonates within the mind of the user. It is this power along with various other magics that allow the Children of Haqim to be so organised through distant communication.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the user must possess at least one droplet of the target’s blood at hand. Many Banu Haqim keep lockets or enchanted vials to preserve the blood of those they seek to communicate with, frequently their descendants.</p>\n<p>Once ready, the user rouses their own Blood ta awaken the Blood of their target. Then the user has to simply concentrate and project their thoughts to the blood so that it may hear them and vice versa. The sound is then transmitted to the target’s mind and any thoughts of the character are transmitted to the user’s. This power may carry supernatural effects such as Dominate. This also grants the user a two-dice bonus on all rolls against the target as the user is aware of the target’s thoughts before they’re put to action. This dpes not stack with Taste the Mind/Body/Soul powers as they are a pale instinctual variant of this blood telepathy.</p>\n<p>Targets who wish to resist listening to voices of those who seek to torment them can simply block them by concentrating and making a Resolve + Occult roll vs. the user’s Wits + Occult. On a win, the sympathetic link breaks and the blood becomes useless for contacting them. Powers such as Internal Awareness, Scry the Soul and Sense the Unseen may notice that someone’s consciousness is seeing through the target’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Rouse Check allows for 5 minutes of telepathy</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"147"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4JZpDzCObMifIuTJ"},"4LjHz2LiGlEhPGWQ":{"name":"Gift of Osiris","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>Despite supposedly dying at the hands of Set’s assassins, Osiris still lives and watches over his children. He has made a promise – a prophecy – that he shall one day return to restore his children as humans. Until then, the user of Bardo may channel Osiris’ gift to grant that to themselves, temporarily.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Osiris</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user is able to fully return to their human state until the next sunrise after meditating and winning a Composure + Bardo against a difficulty of 3. The user’s state is quite literally fully human as they require no need to rest during the day, are without the Beast and are able to experience life as the humans do. The user also loses access to all vampiric boons like their resistance to damage to their powers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the next sunset when they have to make their awakening roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"41"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"EEM3meuN":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4LjHz2LiGlEhPGWQ"},"4OnJqDqywc6dmOcA":{"name":"Blessing’s Warning","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Tremere obtained knowledge of this art from the Baali bloodline in exchange for unknown favors (probably related to the purge of the Salubri). With it, a vampire’s can detect the Blessed and their relics, fervent followers of the Road of Heaven — and sometimes, the third eye of Saulot. A few Tremere wonder why the Salubri can be sensed with a power meant to find God’s favor — and quietly fear the significance of such a mark. Those who have such doubts wisely keep them to themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Sense the Unseen</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Daimonion •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. Composure + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power perceives the world in exactly like Auspex ••• Scry the Soul except that the ofllowing questions are available to them when scrutinising a single person or looking for a specific thing in the entire room:</p>\n<p>• Is the item a holy relic (minimum True Faith rating of 1)?</p>\n<p>• Is the person blessed (minimum True Faith rating of 1)?</p>\n<p>• Is the character pious adherent of the Road of Heaven (minimum Road of Heaven 8)?</p>\n<p>• What is the Resonance of the subject’s blood?</p>\n<p>• Is the subject under the influence of non-physical Magic effects or other powers such as Disciplines like Dominate?</p>\n<p>• Does the character have the third eye of Salubri (minimum Valeren 2)?</p>\n<p>The light surrounding such beings is much softer than that enveloping mortals touched by higher powers, so the two can never be mistaken for one another. The blazing brand of the third eye is unmistakable, even if the eye is closed. If the character posseses Scry the Soul and this power, they may ask Blessing’s Warning question and scrutinise the room for specific things when using Scry the Soul.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"26"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"LnyxsBno":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"NV7QM5OJ":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4OnJqDqywc6dmOcA"},"4QrfXaUqqnkp7tnp":{"name":"Mother of Monsters","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Echidna was called the Mother of Monsters. This power allows the Setite to birth small typhonic beasts from their flesh. The monsters grow from her skin, starting by opening eyes and mouths from their flesh, then ripping from their body, taking part of them with them. These monsters have childlike intelligence and understand the vampire’s speech. They follow their commands without exception, and cannot be commanded or frightened away from their duty. Some Setites can create other mythological monsters. Rumors persist of some Setites who can create rudimentary humans from their flesh. Setite Priest, who typically vomit snakes with this power caste refers to this power “Hatch the Viper”.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Bond Famulus</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Instead of paying in blood, the user pays in their own flesh as they tear it apart to craft new forms of life, usually appearing as typhonic beasts. The user can choose a different mythological form for the beast. However, these monsters must be obviously unnatural. For typhonic beasts, use the statblock of a guard dog to approximate the creature while other Statblocks may serve for other mythological creatures at the Storyteller’s discretion (small drakes would use a bird of prey statblock, for example).</p>\n<p>When crafting a monster, the user removes a point of Health from their tracker for each monster made and selects one of the animal statblocks labeled “small”. In case of shapeshifting removing Health, you lower your maximum Health by the amount removed with this power. The creature created is for all intents and purposes a famulus ghoul and gains the user’s powers and ratings in Potence, Fortitude and Celerity The creature also gains one level one dot Discipline of their master like any normal ghoul.</p>\n<p>The user may create as many creatures as they want, assuming they can pay the price. As long as that creature lives, the user may not regain their Health which is instantly regained upon “merging” the creature back into one’s self which can be done reflexively without any effort. Should the creature perish before that, the user gains their Health point back as a point of Aggravated Health damage.</p>\n<p>These monsters have childlike intelligence and understand the vampire’s speech. They follow their commands without exception, and cannot be commanded or frightened away from their duty. The monsters may communicate to their maker in a crude body language mixed with emotional telepathy. In the eyes of most users of Animalism, this power is beyond blasphemy and an act abhorrent to the natural world.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the creatures are slain or reabsorbed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"153"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4QrfXaUqqnkp7tnp"},"4TpRZdmkOPZ0iZvV":{"name":"Sense Death","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>As the Healers learn where the subject hurts to heal, the Warriors learn it to know where they strike.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight or Medicine vs. Composure + Subterfug</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user looks at a subject within their line of sight and rolls Resolve + Insight or Medicine vs. the victim’s Composure + Subterfuge. On a success, the user may ask questions equal to the margin and get a correct response. The example questions are:</p>\n<p>• How many points of Health does the target currently have?</p>\n<p>• What manner of a creature is the target?</p>\n<p>• How many points of Willpower does the target currently have?</p>\n<p>• What is the target’s weakest physical combat pool?</p>\n<p>At the storyteller’s discretion, other similar questions may be asked, but they will never provide the answers to the overly accurate questions such as “who inflicted the damage to you”</p>\n<p>With relevant information, the user gains a die bonus to all rolls to hurt a person. This bonus is not improved further by power bonus. This bonus may apply to Valeren power rolls.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"190"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"VrGzEVQt":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"30GQiq7l":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4TpRZdmkOPZ0iZvV"},"4URCI7pFQTQMVD76":{"name":"Samiel’s Vengeance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>Samiel was the first Salubri Warrior, said to have perished in the war against the infernal host. Samiel’s Vengeance calls upon the memory of his sacrifice and channels it through the third eye, causing the wicked to halt and be stunned.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may only be used on those who have successfuly harmed the vampire within the present scene. This power may be activated while performing a Brawl or a Melee attack against the target. The third eye blazes in a flash of bright red colour as the vampire rolls Composure + Resolve against their target’s Wits + Awareness prior to striking. On a win, the user’s next attack against is made against a static Difficulty of 1 as the victim is left stunned from bright light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"193"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"FwJ81slZ":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"2TLgTjYe":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4URCI7pFQTQMVD76"},"4f2QmAevqQ2gxQ8n":{"name":"Hourglass of the Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your body and the Beast itself have been modified by your skills in Temporis, giving you the ability to always know the flow of time and even some resistance against the drowsiness that compels vampires to sleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive/Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This Discipline duplicates the Celestial Attunement Merit (p. XX), save that the Cainite’s intuitive grasp is flawless beyond any conventional unit of time measurement. Additionally, the vampire instinctively knows when time is disrupted by any supernatural effect (Wits + Alertness roll at Difficulty 2, adjusted for distance and intensity by the Storyteller), remaining within an altered timeline but aware that a change has occurred.</p>\n<p>The user gains a dice bonus equal to their Temporis rating to resist any supernatural effect to alter the perception granted by Hourglass of the Mind. Broadly speaking, this applies to Temporis and Celerity, time magic and so on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"167"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"8aGEm9o5":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"41UW556c":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4f2QmAevqQ2gxQ8n"},"4gLhgUYXQ796CXBE":{"name":"Exuberance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampiric form is a curious beast, able to support unfathomable levels of strength upon the slightest of frames without demonstrating any strain. Vampires with an understanding of physiology openly wonder how vampires don’t tear themselves apart while displaying these unnatural feats of strength. Well, to tell the truth, some do. Vampires with the Power of Exuberance push their bodies past their abnormally high limits, willing to sacrifice their own health to get the edge they need to win the fight. The resulting boost to their strength sees their straining muscles tear chunks from their flesh and splinter bones with the force of the devastating impact.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever using Potence (including pools using half the Potence rating, such as Prowess), the vampire treats their Potence rating as if it were two levels higher — this can take the relative Discipline rating above the normal limit of 5. On any such test, if the vampire rolls a critical (messy or otherwise) or a failure (bestial or otherwise) they sustain 1 Aggravated damage as their own body tears itself apart from the strain.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive, but it may be turned off to not strain the user</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"117"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4gLhgUYXQ796CXBE"},"4mRDMC9OyeA3izmn":{"name":"Locked in Time","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By honing their ability to lock away a person’s perceptions of time, the</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Locked Contemplation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Locked Contemplation now lasts for as many months as the user scored margins against the victim. After the initial win, the user may continue adding months by rolling against a Difficulty of 3, accumulating more margins over time to extend the duration. Failing a single such roll instantly breaks the victim out of the trance, however.</p>\n<p>Additionally, the vampire may spend a point of Willpower when using Locked Contemplation to ensure that nothing can break the victim out of a trance save for perhaps magic and another person who possesses Locked Contemplation rolling against initial successes. Vampires and other undead, creatures of stasis they already are, may roll their Road rating tracker each sunrise (Difficulty 5). On a win, they awaken.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"171"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.4mRDMC9OyeA3izmn"},"55IbHGBuh57YU4Ve":{"name":"Mask of Ages","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>This simple use of Obfuscate allows the vampire to disguise their apparent age, perhaps allowing them to Masquerade as their mortal identity longer than most. Police and other authorities may allow the elderly to pass without question, or mortals who knew the vampire might not suspect they haven’t aged in decades.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activating this Power, the vampire choses to project their appearance as older or younger than their apparent age, as long as they remain roughly the same size. (Generally, late teens tend to be the lower limit). The user also adds their Obfuscate rating to any tests to impersonate someone of their projected age (including themselves at that age) or when evading someone looking for them via photo or description. Storytellers may limit the bonus dice based on the number of decades the user appears different from their apparent age.</p>\n<p>As with most Obfuscate powers, Mask of Ages can be seen through by powers such as Auspex.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until sunrise or ended by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"97"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.55IbHGBuh57YU4Ve"},"5EhwIXzdARJIYTAT":{"name":"Unswayable Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user gains a mystical ability to resist any attempts to sway them through mundane charms, coercion, and wiles. Some exhibit Unswayable Mind as zen-like calm, others as supernatural stubbornness.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user adds their dots in Fortitude as extra dice in any roll made to resist coercion, intimidation, seduction, or any other attempt to sway the user’s mind against their will. This power also works against supernatural abilities such as Dominate, Dementation, Presence and Mytherceria. Powers that do not sway the user’s will directly such as Obfuscate and Chimerstry are not affected.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"79"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5EhwIXzdARJIYTAT"},"5Q7zKcBwDSn3cKOp":{"name":"Quietus Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>As the user masters the Discipline said to have been created by Haqim himself, the vampire figures out how all Gifts of the Blood are connected, and how all the Paths of Quietus lead to the same destination. The master of Quietus becomes the judge, jury and the executioner among vampires, able to punish whole Elysium’s worth of unworthy Cainites.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Powers of any Quietus Path that require touch or transmission by poison are now available to the vampire by making a gesture with their hand towards the victim (those who succeed on a Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge may notice something odd) and making an activation Rouse Check. Such ranged poison attacks use Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude, if the victim possesses them)</p>\n<p>The vampire can also amplify any of their Quietus Path powers to affect a group of people, mortal or vampire, at once, if the power may be applied at range (such as Blood’s Sweat). When rolling against crowds of mortals or insignificant vampires, the Storyteller rolls only for the strongest one and applies the successes for the rest. When rolling powers of any Quietus Path, you always count as having a specialty.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"138"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5Q7zKcBwDSn3cKOp"},"5XGhVtWLyxnmBg3w":{"name":"Unchain the Wrathful Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>A Fiend with this power may project their Beast into a hapless host, where it immediately sets about warping flesh and bone to better accommodate its monstrousness. The host writhes in agony, skin sloughing off and muscles boiling into new and terrible forms. Once the change settles, the host frenzies as a terrible living weapon, wreaking a swath of carnage until the vampire bids their Beast return.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Drawing Out the Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism •••••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks after the Beast enters the host in addition to Drawing Out the Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire releases their Beast using the Animalism power Drawing Out the Beast. Once the Beast finds a home, it burns two of the vampire’s Rouse Check’s worth of Blood to change the host into zulo shape. The Rouse Checks may be rerolled according to Blood Potency as normal. The painful transformation lasts two turns, after which the host enters immediate Frenzy accoding to the Drawing Out the Beast activation. The Beast will not attack the vampire it rightfully belongs to, but assaults everyone and anyone else in range of its claws.</p>\n<p>At any time, the vampire may recall their Beast with a successful Manipulation + Animal Ken roll (Difficulty 2). Only one such roll may be made per turn. If the host dies before the vampire recalls their Beast, the Beast rises out of the corpse as a black, writhing miasma and flies back to the Cainite faster than mortal eyes can follow. Even after the Beast leaves the host, the physical changes remain.</p>\n<p>Vampires regain their form over five days it takes for them to lose all alterations, losing one point of Attribute from the Zulo Shape every 24 hours. Mortals and ghouls are not so fortunate, remaining locked in the zulo shape indefinitely. Worse still, mortals suffer a point of Aggravated Health damage each day until they perish from rotted bone marrow and unnatural tumors. This decay can be slowed to one point of damage per week if the mortal remains a ghoul. Mortals (ghoul or otherwise) cannot heal the damage from their degenerating tissue by mundane means or vampiric mending or Vicissitude, so this power spells their death sentence without extremely potent curative magic such as the Healing Path of Valeren.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See system</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"210"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5XGhVtWLyxnmBg3w"},"5aGgjf86bhq5AngL":{"name":"Red State","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>A hypnotic ointment or potion that erases the specific memories of an ally or associate’s misdeeds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, sanguine and phlegmatic mortal blood, iron oxide.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When applied or consumed, this places the subject in a peculiar state of moral forgetfulness. The next time someone close and trusted violates the drugged person’s Convictions or commits an act against them, those memories simply do not take. It slips free of the mind like amnesia, and cannot be restored. Being told, “Saphir killed your sister, man! Why are you still friends with him?!?” sounds fake and makes no sense. The very notion that the crime might have occurred fades within moments.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until the target imbibes a choleric Dyscrasia.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"79"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5aGgjf86bhq5AngL"},"5awAT0Wz4ZGtRMas":{"name":"Relentless Grasp","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s grip becomes impossibly strong and once they get a good hold of something they are almost impossible to dislodge, short of dismemberment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire adds their Potence rating as additional dice to any attempt that involves holding on to something. This includes attempts to maintain a grapple, though the initial grapple test does not benefit from this bonus.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"117"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5awAT0Wz4ZGtRMas"},"5kKVRBNmMKV73jVD":{"name":"Mist Form","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire gains the legendary power to turn into a cloud of mist, perceivable to the eye but untouchable by anything save fire, sunlight, and supernatural aggression. They can fit through pipes, crevices, and cracks. While strong winds might buffet them, no natural force can disperse the cloud.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see system</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One to Three Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The transformation takes three turns, though it can be sped up with additional Rouse Checks on a one-for-one basis. While in mist form, the vampire moves at walking pace and perceives their surroundings through mystical means as if there as normal. A vampire in mist form cannot make eye contact or speak. They can use only those Disciplines requiring no physical form or presence, at the Storyteller’s discretion. While in mist form the vampire can only be damaged by sunlight, fire, and immaterial supernatural attacks (such as some Rituals).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire falls to sleep or torpor or ends the power</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"132"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5kKVRBNmMKV73jVD"},"5ws5DMm3IACwXu7F":{"name":"Copycat","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mercurians might not be able to turn into mist or mesmerize onlookers into overlooking them, but Copycat’s the next best thing. Chugging the brew makes the user’s face blur to look like the face of the “donor” whose blood they added to it—even their hair changes color. Unfortunately it’s not an <em>exact</em> copy. The planes and shape of the face don’t mask easily, and the donor’s friends and family would be able to tell it wasn’t them in a well-lit room — the face has a sort of uncanny valley look to anyone who knows it very well, and the hair isn’t <em>quite</em> right. But it’s a surefire way to slip away in a crowd or a party, or even as a quick disguise somewhere where someone doesn’t know the face at all.</p>\n<p>Adding to the difficulty, there has to be some resonance between the alchemist’s Blood and the donor blood when the two are drawn for the formula to work: some alchemists take psychedelics with their donor, others eat the same meals that night. Sex, or particularly exciting interactions such as a swordfight or performing together, can also align the two bloods.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric and phlegmatic mortal blood, mirror glass, donor blood, <em>Boquila trifoliolata</em> leaves, chameleon skin.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon drinking, the user feels the unpleasant sensation of something wet and sticky oozing to cover their face. That feeling remains for the next twenty minutes, slightly itchy and tight as the Copycat brew slowly loses potency. When it’s finally through, a slightly itchy white residue like dry glue is left over the skin.</p>\n<p>For those who know the donor by sight, telling the drinker from the donor requires an Intelligence + Awareness test against a Difficulty equal to the Distillation roll’s successes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Twenty minutes (usually one scene if you’re smart).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"78"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.5ws5DMm3IACwXu7F"},"6DZfi1qkBX4xIFig":{"name":"Sleep of a Mummy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The elder Setites are at a constant threat of tomb raiders and zealous hunters in their homeland which is why many have developed a power to allow them to not only appear as a long-dead mummified corpse but also shield them from almost all damage while they sleep or are in torpor.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the user is in daysleep or torpor, they cannot be distinguished from a corpse as they appear like a dried up corpse. Additionally, the user’s skin hardens and they take no damage save from fire or True Faith. The user is also immune to staking while in this state.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive, but the user may disable it if they wish so.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"152"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.6DZfi1qkBX4xIFig"},"6Mg3S52kSF8q0lQ7":{"name":"Typhonic Maw","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user’s throat becomes as flexible as that of a snake and their teeth become even sharper, allowing them to tear more than flesh away from their victims when biting.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the user achieves a successful bite attack, they slake one point of Hunger on a successful bite attack and increase a target Cainite’s Hunger by 1. Additionally, the user suffers no called-shot penalty for bite attacks anymore. This stacks with other similar bonuses to Hunger from Serpentis, but not from other sources. If the user possesses Serpent’s Flesh and spends its Rouse Check, the user may devour a prey the size of themselves given enough chance, destroying messes caused by feeding.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"151"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.6Mg3S52kSF8q0lQ7"},"6OqQDVgHV6dRVEj4":{"name":"Shatter the Vessel","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With but a whisper, the user is able to convince a wall to come down, armour to dissolve or a weapon to shatter by appealing to its spirit and promising it freedom or greater rewards.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user needs to be able to issue a verbal command or express their will in some other form if unable. For an object to shatter, the user must win succeed on a Manipulation + Animal Ken roll against a Difficulty of 2. Destroying larger scale objects destroys about a cubic meter per margin, giving the users of Spiritus the literal ability to tear down the walls of fortresses as the founder of the Ahrimanes had done.</p>\n<p>If the object is Warded against spirits or enchanted to be unbreakable (e.g. with powers such as Blood Tampering), the user of Spiritus contests the original successes on the Ward/enchantment. The sorcerer may make that roll again if they are in the vicinity, for the purposes of keeping the game running smooth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"164"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.6OqQDVgHV6dRVEj4"},"6obrFw9p4F9cx13r":{"name":"Blood Dance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By focusing, the user is able to command spilled blood into shapes. This power is greatly useful when one has to clean up other’s mistakes or traces of assassination.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free (or One Rouse Check if using own Blood)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Craft</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By making a successful Intelligence + Craft roll, the user may craft the blood into any chape or image they desire. Complexity of the image varies by difficulty— a simple message of “Help!” or a basic polygonal shape may only be at Difficulty 2, while a lengthy diatribe or an intricate sculpture may be Difficulty 4 or higher. Failure on this roll means the blood fails to form the desired shape or does not respond at all — a Critical Success (or at least 5 successes) not only forms the desired image, but negates the need for any further rolls to change its shape for the duration of the scene.</p>\n<p>If used for more utilitarian purposes, add your dots in Quietus Sanguinis to any roll intended to locate, clean up or otherwise collect blood. Such a use also makes it very difficult for others to find traces of blood. This power may also be used to wield Quietus poisons like a sanguine whip using Dexterity + Melee.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until lapsed by user</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"144"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GUz2ebdb":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"FABbo5Xt":{"path":"skills.craft"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.6obrFw9p4F9cx13r"},"75Dm3FcKqdYimLhB":{"name":"Slake by Spirit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>When venturing the umbra or the untouched wilderness, many users of Spiritus must feed somehow, even if it comes to the detriment of the lesser spirits on whose energies they slake.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive/Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By spending a point of Willpower, the user is able to drain a spirit to slake a point of Hunger. In combat, this can be done as a minor action, incurring a penalty of two dice. Average and greater spirits may resist with Composure + Resolve against the user’s Resolve + Animal Ken. Should the user of Spiritus win, the spirit takes three points of Aggravated Health damage instead of being instantly consumed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"164"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.75Dm3FcKqdYimLhB"},"7NsK8LA29ryan78j":{"name":"Cache","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire may use this power to hide objects and make them difficult to find just by touching them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Ensconce</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Objects affected by Ensconce need merely be touched, not held or carried by the Cainite. Furthermore, the user need not maintain physical contact with the object. The effect lasts for a scene. The user may make an additional Rouse Check to extend the duration for the remainder of the night. In that case, the enshrouded object remains hidden until dawn.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until dawn or ended by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"97"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7NsK8LA29ryan78j"},"7SWgi70vfuzmy9u1":{"name":"Lord of the Land","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>True masters of Animalism are not limited by distance. The greatest of Tzimisce Voivodes and Nosferatu spymasters are uncontested lords over their domains and everything in them, able to manipulate their subjects with little more than a thought.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power requires the user to establish control over a domain or territory of some sort, mark its boundaries with their Blood, and hold it uncontested. If the marks are effaced or destroyed, or another vampire takes up residence in the domain without the lord’s permission, this power ceases to function. From a mechanical standpoint, a character’s territory may not be larger than their mechanical Domain.</p>\n<p>By making an additional Rouse Check when activating an Animalism power within their domain, the lord can amplify the power to affect every possible target in that domain. Each target can roll to contest separately, or the lord can make a single roll against the highest resistance pool.</p>\n<p>The character can also instinctively learn when somebody, be it animal or human, enters or exits the domain.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As the duration of the power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"23"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7SWgi70vfuzmy9u1"},"7TyNyxj0iUzUgXdh":{"name":"Infernal Birthright","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Not as much of a power as merely extending the properties found within blood of Daimonion users, the user manifests same powers that demons and their minions do. Due to the psychological effects of attaining powers of the divine beings without their aid, the Baali often crown themselves kings of Hell and are often inspired to enslave demons as the Giovanni do ghosts. The very power of Torment that twisted angels into demons surges through the vampire’s body, twisting them into something else as well.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Investiture of the Blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Triple the amount of Arcana from Investiture of the Blood. You also count your Blood Potency as both Torment and Divinity for all intents and purposes save Faith Hunger as Investiture of the Blood uses Rouse Checks instead of Faith Checks.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"60"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7TyNyxj0iUzUgXdh"},"7YnPSYwz4qwy01L3":{"name":"Earthmeld","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>Becoming one with the soil, the vampire sinks into the earth. Unless in torpor, the vampire rises again the following night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No test is required, but the vampire must be on a natural surface: rocks, raw earth, grass, etc. This power does not work on concrete, asphalt, or other artificial surfaces. It takes a turn for the vampire to sink into the earth, leaving carried objects behind atop the soil. While in the earth the vampire is aware of their surroundings, except during day-sleep. At those times, disturbances (e.g., digging or loud noises) awaken them or not as with all vampires (p. XX).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One day or more, or until disturbed. Power may be ended sooner by the vampire rising.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"129"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7YnPSYwz4qwy01L3"},"7dsFNuOGOBtQEUFH":{"name":"Faster than Light","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s unnatural speed protects them from the undead’s deadliest banes — fire and sunlight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Blink</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When using Blink, the vampire makes an additional Rouse check, enabling them to pass through patches of sunlight or flame without sustaining damage or feeling their effects. If they end their action within this dangerous patch or trip during the passage, damage is applied as normal. This doesn’t negate any other impacts from operating in daylight or fire, such as Frenzy Checks, awakening, or dice pools.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"46"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7dsFNuOGOBtQEUFH"},"7n661Upp9I8E8V2x":{"name":"Intelligent Ash","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By sharpening their senses in their Ash form, the user is able to both see and hear by sensing vibrations. The greater control over their form also allows them movement.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> From Ashes</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> While in ash form, the user is now able to hear and see most of what they would if they weren’t in their ash form. Additionally, they may move at a walking speed (one probably does not need to state how unusual “walking ashes” would look to mortals). Disciplines may be used at the Storyteller’s Discretion, but rare are the powers a vampire can use in this state (Dominate without Telepathy and Blood Sorcery are never available).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"181"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7n661Upp9I8E8V2x"},"7w2UAf3PNoLKP8hB":{"name":"Slower than Harm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire bends time to protect them from the undead’s deadliest banes — fire and sunlight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Slow Walker</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When using Slow Walker, the vampire makes an additional Rouse check, enabling them to pass through patches of sunlight or flame without sustaining damage or feeling their effects. If they end their action within this dangerous patch or trip during the passage, damage is applied as normal. This doesn’t negate any other impacts from operating in daylight or fire, such as Frenzy Checks, awakening, or dice pools.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"173"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7w2UAf3PNoLKP8hB"},"7wWc3wbWufuceMjK":{"name":"Unerring Pursuit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Locking eyes with a subject, the vampire coaxes forth a supernatural bond between themselves and the victim. For a number of nights, the user is able to view glimpses of the target and their environment whenever the target sees themselves on a reflective surface. The target is generally unaware of the being so observed, but witnesses the presence of their pursuer as someone standing just behind them in their own reflection. While not exclusive to the Sabbat, this power is frequently used to track quarry, whether vampires marked as prey or mortal witnesses requiring silencing.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. Composire vs. Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user locks eyes with the target, if only for a split second, and rolls Resolve + Insight. On a win, the effect lasts for one night, plus one for each success in the margin. (If used against a vampire, the Difficulty of the roll is 3.)</p>\n<p>While the power is active the user can concentrate for a turn to gain a view of the last position of the target from the perspective of the target’s own reflection. Essentially, they view a mental impression of the most recent moment the victim saw themselves, intentionally or unintentionally, in a mirror or mirror-like surface (including polished chrome, polished cutlery, or even someone’s spectacles). Note that Obfuscate provides no protection from this power, as it relies on the victim’s self-perception.</p>\n<p>The user can recognise the surroundings of the target with an Intelligence + Streetwise (for urban areas) or Intelligence + Survival (for rural areas), the Difficulty depending on circumstances, such as being indoors, but should range from 2 to 4. The target can get a glimpse of their pursuer in their own reflection on a Wits + Awareness test at Difficulty 4, and only on a critical win will recognise their face, or recall it from where the individual saw them if they don’t know them personally.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night plus one for each success (or in the margin, in the case of a vampire target) mental or physical weak points.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the target heals its damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"29"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"mRPpUSfI":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"TPlQgpAo":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.7wWc3wbWufuceMjK"},"88ttazce38pjGvbu":{"name":"Quick Swap","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can swap the position of two inanimate objects in an instant. This could swap a scrap of paper in their hand with a shotgun, or swap the blade someone holds with a piece of hot coal that was in a fire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult vs. Wits + Strength</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The inanimate objects must both be within the line of sight of the user, and be something that the user can carry in one hand without great effort. Objects that are being held or worn by another at the time of using this power require a contest of the user’s Resolve + Occult vs the current bearer’s Wits + Strength. On a win for the current bearer, the power fails.</p>\n<p>In the case the container is a hand and that the swapped object may harm the person who’s now grasping it, they may attempt to make a Dexterity + Athletics with Difficulty equal to the user’s Mytherceria. On a success, they are able to quickly drop it at their feet, not being harmed by holding it. On a failure, the harm is up to the Storyteller’s discretion. For instance, a flaming log or bladed edge appearing in the target’s hands would deal a point of Aggravated Health damage, with the former also causing a vampires to make a Fear Frenzy check.</p>\n<p>If one larger object were to be swapped into a smaller space than the size of the object, depending on the sturdiness of the container it will either damage it’s new container or simply cause the power to fail as room cannot be made for the object to fit. The container could be a safe, a pocket, a fireplace or even someone’s hand.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Indefinitely as it would take another use of this power to swap it again. Normally moving the object is also a method.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"93"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"dSvtZsbv":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"Orfwf7XY":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.88ttazce38pjGvbu"},"8HybBTiT2NslFwjb":{"name":"Unseen Passage","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power, the vampire can now move around while staying hidden. The user is functionally invisible, per the usual Obfuscate limitations.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> As long as the user emits no overpowering odors and no sound louder than a whisper, this power automatically works. Only if the observer has their attention drawn to the user can they make a detection roll.</p>\n<p>Sense the Unseen (Auspex •) and similar powers can also detect the hidden vampire, as per the general Obfuscate rules. Note that the user cannot use this power to disappear while being actively observed; it automatically fails in such a case.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until detection or the user stops the power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Subterfuge with their Wits + Awareness</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"98"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.8HybBTiT2NslFwjb"},"8IHSC52IJ7kPJBlm":{"name":"The Unfettered Heart","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>Having mastered the power of Protean, the very insides of the user become malleable, almost viscous. The heart, seat of the vitae and unlife of the vampire, detaches and moves freely, if sluggishly, within the chest. This makes the vampire exceedingly hard to stake as the position of their heart changes nightly, and can even allow the user to free themselves from paralysis.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Increases the Difficulty on any noncombat test to stake the vampire by three. In Melee combat, only if the stake-wielder rolls a Critical Win does the stake penetrate the user’s heart. Even if staked, the user can make a Rouse Check and roll their Strength + Resolve (Difficulty 5) once per hour. A win means they manage to free themselves from paralysis as the stake is pushed out of their body. They cannot attempt such stake expulsion at Hunger 5.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"131"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.8IHSC52IJ7kPJBlm"},"8KgXc14GEiAkDD5q":{"name":"Sword of the Righteous","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>The decree of Saulot is the holy mission of the Salubri and other few that were permitted to study Valeren: wipe out the infernalist taint from the face of the world. By channeling that sacred oath, the vampire’s weapon or hands blaze with holy flame.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire pays the cost of a Rouse Check and their arms or a melee weapon become coated in pale flame that may pierce supernatural darkness and negate its penalties such as of Nocturne (Obtenebration •••). Attacks with this weapon or with one’s hands blazing with flame deal standard Aggravated fire, including the damage modifier of the weapon. The weapon/hands blazing with flame may also strike immaterial corrupted spirits, discorporealised demons and specters from across the shroud.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"194"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.8KgXc14GEiAkDD5q"},"8ekqFnCHiJv2OavK":{"name":"Uncanny Grip","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Focusing their unnatural strength into their toes and fingers, the vampire grips and burrows their extremities into almost any surface, enabling them to climb and even hang otherwise unsupported from walls and ceilings. Close observation reveals telltale scarring or deformation on these surfaces afterward, however, as this is an application of brute force, not superhero-style adhesion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A vampire using this power automatically succeeds on any Skill test to climb a nonmetallic surface. The user might also be able to climb copper or bronze cladding or other softer metal surfaces, at the Storyteller’s discretion Thin glass surfaces (though generally not the glass curtain walls of modern office buildings) may shatter under the stress. In the same way, a vampire can hang from a wall or ceiling for up to one scene, though only barefoot vampires can hang by their feet. The climb or clinging leaves obvious tracks detectable by anyone with an Intelligence + Investigation test at Difficulty 2. Detecting Uncanny Grip tracks on glass doesn’t even require a roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"116"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.8ekqFnCHiJv2OavK"},"8uw0QLki4Le0q25e":{"name":"Eyes of the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can will a supernatural red gleam into their eyes, giving them sight even in the total absence of light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No roll is required to activate Eyes of the Beast. While active the user ignores any sight penalties imposed by darkness, including supernatural. While active, the inhuman appearance of the eyes confers two bonus dice to Intimidation pools against mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As long as desired</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"127"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.8uw0QLki4Le0q25e"},"91uGCuaTS29ja736":{"name":"Ghost in the Machine","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can now transmit the effects of Obfuscate through electronic media, allowing the vampire to appear invisible or masked when viewed live on screen. If an observer views the image later, as in a photograph or recording, the effect lessens: the image seems slightly blurred, making identification hard. In addition, automated surveillance has a tendency to glitch in the presence of the vampire, lessening their chance of being caught by automated systems.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No additional roll is required when being viewed on a live feed. Treat observers as present with the vampire, with regard to the Discipline. The observer adds +3 to their Difficulty on tests to identify the user on film, video, in photographs, or the like taken during active Obfuscation.</p>\n<p>The user also gains three additional dice to pools they use when trying to circumvent automated electronic surveillance and countermeasures.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive/As power used</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"100"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.91uGCuaTS29ja736"},"95QwMXDEumGDkHUj":{"name":"Saraimu","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula creates a sentient blob of slime to serve the alchemist and the other vampires whose vitae it comes from as a familiar. It must be created using Fixatio; saraimu created using other methods imprint too strongly on their athanor to be useful as servants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A cup of vitae each from three vampires including the alchemist, baking powder, crushed chalk (optional, for a soft voice), iron nails (for strength), battery acid (for <em>oomph</em>)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist combines the ingredients by heating up the three cups of vitae in the athanor and adding baking powder and chalk. As the mixture bubbles, they stir in the nails, and when they add the battery acid and activate the mixture, it hisses aggressively and comes to something resembling life. The blob familiar is approximately the size of a football. Its personality takes a bit from each of the vampires whose Blood it was made from, and it can speak in simple, fragmented sentences. It probably calls the alchemist mommy or master.</p>\n<p>The blob is affectionate but slimy and inherently unstable. It can perform many simple tasks, including spying, fetching things, and tasks around the alchemist’s lab. It knows it’s incomplete and longs to be back inside the bodies where it came from. It never sleeps and tries to burrow inside its masters while they do, assuming it’s not currently out on a mission for them. It has many rows of short, sharp nail-teeth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The blob falls apart one to three weeks after it was created. The Storyteller may roll a die to determine the exact time. If its masters die before it does, the saraimu knows enough alchemy to prolong its existence until it can find — and burrow its way into — a new alchemist.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tattered Façade","page":"107"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"eFmMiwcp":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"FVBG2sG7":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.95QwMXDEumGDkHUj"},"9EBoXS4kQf47rhJN":{"name":"Choir of the Damned","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mastering the tune, the user may now hide multiple companions from evil creatures.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Lulliby of the Damned</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By spending a point of Willpower per additional passanger, the user may extend the effect of Lulliby of the Damned to those who stay within a radius equal to the user’s Wits rating. Passengers do not roll themselves, and instead use the user’s initial successes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"197"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.9EBoXS4kQf47rhJN"},"9KSi7NkbyL5FAQfw":{"name":"Lethe’s Call","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The memories of others are a plaything to the vampire, who is able to blank out large swaths with but a glance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Cloud Memory or Forgetful Mind</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, extended</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Intelligence + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Eye-contact is required as with most Dominate powers. The user tests Manipulation + Subterfuge vs target’s Intelligence + Resolve, though unprepared mortals cannot resist this power. Each success in the margin allows the user to wipe up to an entire week’s worth of memories from the target. This use of Dominate does not require any verbal instructions, but will in this case simply blank the remembered time from the victim indiscriminately. With the addition of a verbal command, the Cainite can instead simply erase all knowledge of one individual or event from the same time, including the user themself.</p>\n<p>The user may choose to simply leave the target’s memory blank or leave the victim with a generic sense of time having passed and the assumption that the time passed about as expected, though the latter quickly reveals inconsistencies if the victim is pressed for details later.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Indefinite</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"76"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"M6ZRDkWa":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"J3U4TkSb":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.9KSi7NkbyL5FAQfw"},"9Ybrh3KeN0M9yA7K":{"name":"Awe","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Anyone in the presence of the vampire finds their attention inexplicably drawn to them. Those listening to the vampire speak might suddenly agree on subjects where they once held different viewpoints. While this power doesn’t cause rapt infatuation, it is still strong enough to sway the minds of most mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add the Presence rating to any Skill roll involving Persuasion or Performance as well as to other Charisma related rolls, at the Storyteller’s discretion. Anyone aware that they’re being affected can try to resist with a contest of Composure + Intelligence vs the user’s Manipulation + Persuasion. A reasonable suspicion might lead the subject to to roll to resist the effects of Awe. For example, convincing the Prince that his chair has been a demon all of this time. On a win, the target can resist the effects for one scene; a critical win makes the target immune for the entire night. Once the power wears off, victims revert to their previous opinions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until intentionally ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"121"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.9Ybrh3KeN0M9yA7K"},"9jUHPQwcRFDwcZYE":{"name":"Pariah’s Caress","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Watchers must contend with the Wan Kuei, other vampires who do not subscribe to the idea of the lineage of Caine, and stranger beings such as mages. They learned long ago that the best defense lies in misdirection — if enemies are looking at someone else, they aren’t looking at the Salubri. With this power, a Watcher may charge her blood with the combined hatred and attention of all their foes, and then transfer that animosity to another with a bloody touch.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion vs. Composure + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user must first touch the target and smear a bit of their vitae on them, preferably with a Stealth roll. After that, the user rolls Manipulation + Persuasion vs. Composure + Occult of the victim.</p>\n<p>On a win, the target suffers a penalty equal to the vampire’s Valeren rating halved and rounded up on all tests involving Persuasion, Etiquette, Politics and Subterfuge as they find himself within a miasma of annoyance and suspicion. Even mundane or positive acts draw significant negative attention, inspiring jealousy and causing every flaw to be scrutinized.</p>\n<p>Should the target win the roll, nothing happens, but a Critical Win (or 3+ margins) has them realise that something was off without knowing what exactly had happened. Experienced Salubri hunters from the East immediately look around to see for any too mundane persons hiding themselves in a crowd.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> 24 hours for each point on the margin.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"197"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Wvsbywvr":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"UFWdDJZk":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.9jUHPQwcRFDwcZYE"},"9q0wLUj6xcxMb1DA":{"name":"Daunt","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Instead of attracting people, the vampire uses Presence to repel. With this power the user appears threatening and exudes an aura of menace powerful enough to make most mortals avoid their attention and even vampires think twice about acting against them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add the user’s Presence rating to any Intimidation rolls.</p>\n<p>Attacking the user requires a Resolve + Composure roll at Difficulty 2. Vampires cannot use Awe and Daunt simultaneously.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until intentionally ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"121"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.9q0wLUj6xcxMb1DA"},"9wAj10XP7PDB8Hq9":{"name":"Aid from the Spirits","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Spirits need not be complicated forms of intelligent “life” such as that of animals. Truth be told, most are spirits of concepts, like the fork you are using when you are eating. The user of Spirits is able to directly rouse the spirit within an object to aid them in fulfilling a task.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Intimidation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Manipulation + Animal Ken at a Difficulty of 3. The vampire receives a number of bonus dice to handle that item, equal to the number of margins. A critical win adds another die. Power Bonus applies only once. The power may not be used more than once in the same scene so as spirits do not like feeling that they’re being used.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The dice bonus fades at dawn or when used up.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"160"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"fHyNVNuv":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"xmXPp9c4":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.9wAj10XP7PDB8Hq9"},"A0xUxsy4DQXby65y":{"name":"Debilitative Poison","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With Hinder Mending, the vampire concocts a special poison that prevents usage of vitae for mending, disrupting vampire targets and their other creations that utilise vitae to heal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice/free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This contact poison has the same system as Scorpion’s Touch (see below) and is applied the same way).</p>\n<p>If the poison hits, the victim suffers Physical Impairment for the rest of the scene. Additionally, the striked bodypart suffers trauma and the victim rolls for a Crippling Injury according to the table for Crippling Injuries (p. XX). If the roll to damage/ touch the target was a Critical Win or a called shot, the user may select a Crippling Injury themselves to indicate a precise hit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The poison is potent for one scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"140"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.A0xUxsy4DQXby65y"},"ACZnxCjunRw5Fkbp":{"name":"Fear of the Void","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Once you know a subject’s beliefs or desires, manipulating them is easy. Converting vices of pleasure into waves of existential terror or shattering their beliefs on the spot is a greater trick. Speaking in soft tones of primeval horror, the user of Daimonion may send their victim fleeing as their very person is brought into</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Intimidation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the victim must be able to hear the user and the user must know either the target’s ambition or desire or at least one of the target’s convictions. Once those conditions are fulfilled, the user simply speaks shattering revelations towards the victim.</p>\n<p>On a win the user may select one of the following effects from which the victim shall suffer:</p>\n<p>• The victim flees in terror. Vampire victims test for a Terror Frenzy against a Difficulty equal to the half (rounded up) Daimonion rating of the user.</p>\n<p>• The victim feels intense guilt and sees their world crumble down before them. For a night or a week on a critical win of the power, they suffer a die penalty on Degeneration rolls.</p>\n<p>• The victim gains a compulsion of the user’ choice, even if they aren’t a vampire or a revenant (Hunger is sated by indulging gluttony in mortals).</p>\n<p>Each of these effects may impact a target only once per night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the effect ends.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"56"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rLbaiMdo":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"8Vc8i9Us":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ACZnxCjunRw5Fkbp"},"AOU7HCPmsqbEbhtw":{"name":"Silence the Sane Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire forces the victim to say nothing, to bear mute witness to evil through scrambling their ability to communicate. Those so affected find themselves unable to talk, shout, or even scream — any voice they had is stolen from them, leaving them to strain soundlessly into the void, their final words left unheard.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Insight against Composure + Wits of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 44). Anyone affected by this Power finds it impossible to meaningfully communicate — their voice makes no sound, writing becomes illegible, their gestures or signs shudder spastically, and even banging against walls produces nothing but a dull thud as their limbs fail to exert force.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 49.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"66"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AOU7HCPmsqbEbhtw"},"AS1JADRlI4uN96BG":{"name":"Scent of Prey","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The mind-numbing fear of realizing one’s role as prey is unique, and vampires versed in sensing the Beast can learn to recognize it in those who experience it. This power allows a vampire to become aware of any mortal in an area projecting the distinct note of fear produced by prey animals having come face to face with their predator, allowing them to quickly track and silence those who would threaten their clandestine presence.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Investigation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire sniffs the air and rolls Resolve + Investigation. Each success on the roll allows them to sense and track the position of a mortal who has witnessed what a Camarilla vampires would call a “Masquerade violation.” (The user senses the closest mortal first if there are more than one and order matters.) The effect lasts for one scene, or one night on a critical win. The power is ineffective on mortals who are familiar enough with vampires to sublimate the note of fear, such as ghouls, vampire retainers, or dedicated hunters.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"19"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"uFr8wynu":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"IXEV8fio":{"path":"skills.investigation"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AS1JADRlI4uN96BG"},"AaLsyGGKZeBgwVvH":{"name":"Shadow Steed","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>During the Dark Ages, Lasombra were known for their shadow calvary when combining this power with Solid Shadows. When the Lasombra joined the Sabbat, this power took on a more aggressive purpose. The Tzimisce had their hellhounds to unleash on their foes, the Lasombra had shadow wolves. With this power, the vampire summons an animal from the shadows (or a shadow of an animal?) that can comprehend their intent and basic commands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire forms a shadow imbued with animalistic intelligence by splattering some of their blood into a shadow.</p>\n<p>The shadow possesses the statblocks of the animal it took the form off except for any special damage modifier and attack. It possesses Shadow Cloak and Tenebrous Avatar powers (always on), and is only damaged by fire and sunlight (as a vampire with Bane Severity 3). The animal cannot comprehend human language but understands human intentions well enough as if reading minds.</p>\n<p>Due to its shadowy form, the animal cannot pick up anything heavier than a large medieval key and its attacks deal a maximum of one point of Superficial damage no matter the margins.</p>\n<p>If the user possesses Fires of the Abyss, they may “feed” the shadow a Rouse Check’s worth of their blood to imbue them with hellish black flames, causing it deal additional damage equal to half of the user’s Daimonion rating rounded up and potentially set things on fire with touch.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows, the animal takes on a three-dimensional appearance of black inky mist that appears solid. It may pick up objects and carry persons like a normal animal. Its attacks deal damage as normal. Due to now having a solid form, the creature may be attacked, but it Superficial Health damage from most things except sunlight, fire and True Faith. Its Health is equal to Health of an animal + Obtenebration rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the animal is destroyed or released back into darkness by the vampire. The vampire may have a number of these active equal to their Obtenebration rating.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"110"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AaLsyGGKZeBgwVvH"},"AaWzfTzZNuRC3dOP":{"name":"Unseen Spirit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ghosts naturally are immune to all mind-altering powers such as Obfuscate as long as they remain unmanifested within the Underworld. The Nagaraja and the Samedi, often dealing in haunted places or exploring the Shadowlands, have learned to extend their Obfuscate in order for it cloud even the senses of ghosts. Called Demon’s Folly by the Baali, the lesser-known attribute of this power is that it clouds the senses of all spirits, including demons as well as ghosts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Daimonion •• or Spiritus •• or Ephemera Path ••or Necromancy Path ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Your Obfuscate now affects spirits (ghosts, naturae, demons) as it would normal humans.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"102"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AaWzfTzZNuRC3dOP"},"AdpKntgYG916viii":{"name":"Resilient Will","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The will of a vampire is the will of an immortal creature whose drive can lead them onto centuries-lasting grudges and feuds. This stubborn will has a more positive side as the vampire with Resilient Will becomes harder to hurt with words or by powers that target their will.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Once activated, the vampire reduces Superficial Willpower damage equal to their Fortitude rating without it affecting their Willpower tracker. This happens after halving damage and can reduce the damage to 0. The character can expend any remaining benefit from this Power to entirely negate a single attack, making it a way to shield against Aggravated Willpower damage, but doing so makes the vampire unable to reactivate this power for the rest of the combat.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene or until the protection has been depleted</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"79"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AdpKntgYG916viii"},"AxHL5tDohCGuDtEb":{"name":"Horrid Reality","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>Quite possibly the most horrid and fear-inducing usage of Chimerstry, Horrid Reality allows Fata Morgana to create hallucinations so realistic that they can straight up inflict harm upon others by convincing their bodies of it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free/as power enhanced</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One point of Willpower in addition to the power enhanced</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user may choose to enhance the illusions created by Ignis Fatuus and Fata Morgana to the point of dealing harm.</p>\n<p>Before creating the illusions, the user must declare the primary victim (or more, if using Haunting Illusions) to be affected by the damage. After that, the player creates the illusion and the victim rolls to disbelieve per normal interactions with Chimerstry. Upon the user’s win, the victim takes the amount of margins as Willpower damage based on the type of damage what the crafter of illusions imagined (a sword cutting through a mortal would surely deal Aggravated damage to them while fire would do the trick for a vampire). If the user so wishes, they may also state the maximum amount of damage and reduce the type of damage from Aggravated to Superficial when crafting the illusion. Frenzy-inducing effects are treated as real when empowered by the Horrid Reality instead of the Difficulty being lower by 1.</p>\n<p>The damage dealt by this power is taken during a single turn while the illusion can remain after. To re-apply the power, the user has to re-activate the power, paying the full cost and rolling again.</p>\n<p>A victim who has lost all Willpower by an Illusory attack loses consciousness or enters torpor. Only a single person may take damage from this power per turn unless people actively “jump onto the sword” of the Chimerstry user by willingly interacting with the illusion such as entering a bonfire or actively trying to bodyblock the attack to shield the target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The damage is taken for one turn while the illusion remains.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"51"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AxHL5tDohCGuDtEb"},"AzaUdW388RCtKql8":{"name":"Inner Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Approaching the legendary apotheosis, the goal of those walking the Road of Metamorphosis, the user’s body and mind become one, being able to exercise complete control over the body as well as the mind. The user may transmute their own liquids, alter mental states and even reshape personalities.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Rend the Osseous Frame</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 2, requiring physical contact if another person is the subject. If the subject is avoiding the user’s touch and the time required to perform an alteration is one turn or instant, alteration first requires a successful roll with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/ Brawl. For alterations requiring longer periods of time, unwilling targets must be restrained. Unwilling subjects resist using Stamina + Resolve. Their successes replaces the static Difficulty of 2.</p>\n<p>Passive effect of knowing this power is that Social and Mental Attributes are now treated as Physical Attributes for the purposes of Vicissitude, meaning that one may use Redistribution to turn a socialite into a warrior or a genius by tapping into Social Attributes.</p>\n<p>The following alterations are samples of what is available to Inner Mastery:</p>\n<p>• Blood of the Dragon (Cost: One Attribute dot; Time: 90 minutes): The subject is capable of breathing out a burst of flame like a dragon by transmuting their blood into a gas that ignites on impact with air. To use this alteration, the subject pays a cost of a Rouse Check and rolls Composure + Firearms against the target’s dodge pool, two points of Aggravated Health damage or three on a critical win. The attack has a maximum range equal to 3 + Strength + Potence in meters. The sight of fire provokes a Difficulty 1 fire frenzy in others. Those drinking straight from the subject’s vein suffer three points of Aggravated Health damage.</p>\n<p>• Corrosive Blood (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute; Time: Two hours): The subject may spill their Blood on a nonliving object (except unliving flesh such as that of the vampires) to corrode and decompose it. Each Rouse Check corrodes approximately 35 cm of matter in all directions from the splash; this takes approximately five minutes (longer on soft metals like copper or cast iron). Note that a Rouse Check is approximate to an Aggravated Health point of damage for normal mortals. Harder metals such as alloys and steel merely scar and pit; whether they meaningfully weaken remains at the Storyteller’s discretion. (This power might corrode through handcuff chains given enough time and vitae.)</p>\n<p>• Encase the Heart (Cost: One Attribute dot;</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Time:</strong> 15 minutes): The subject’s heart is encased in hardened crystallised blood, requiring a Critical Win (or additional 3 points of damage) to be properly staked.</p>\n<p>• Horrors of the Mind (Cost: One Attribute dot; Time: 20 minutes): The subject begins to exude a strange miasma coupled with body horror that cause nearby onlookers, to be even more afraid afraid. Mortals and animals within a 10 meter radius must make a Willpower check against a Difficulty of 3 in closed spaces or 2 in open spaces or they will flee in terror. Tzimisce revenants are immune to this.</p>\n<p>• Memory Change (Cost: Free; Time: One hour per year’s worth of memories): This alteration rewrites a single decade’s worth of memory for each success on the Vicissitude roll poured into this alteration. One may even replace memories of hostile relations and effectively turn a person into their indebted servant using Memory Change. To permanently recruit an ally, they must be bought as a Retainer at a dot rating approximate to Retainer strength.</p>\n<p>• Poison in the Blood (Cost: Half of an Attribute dot; Time: Two hours): The subject0s blood is mixed with various digestive acids and other nasty liquids of the body, mortals coming in contact (usually by drinking, but skin contact by smearing it on a bladed weapon, arrow or spitting it with Composure + Firearms works as well) with at least one Rouse Check’s worth of vitae die instantly while vampires suffer a point of Aggravated Health damage due to the corrosive nature of the contact-poison.</p>\n<p>• Regeneration (Cost: One Attribute dot;</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Time:</strong> 20 minutes): Like a Hydra, the subject regenerates and grows in strength, growing new appendages in the process. The subject heals two points of Superficial Health damage each turn and may spend a Rouse Check if a ghoul or a vampire to mend a point of Aggravated Health damage. For each point of Aggravated Health damage on the tracker, the subject gains a dot to place in a Physical Attribute.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until healed or altered again. See Characteristics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"209"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.AzaUdW388RCtKql8"},"B21DfERlY3YVhKlH":{"name":"Strengthen the Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user awakens the subject blood’s making them more alert to the hidden things.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Internal Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult vs. Intelligence + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Those drinking the blood of the user find themselves having frequent “gut feelings” about things they should not be possible to sense. The vampire may feed a person one Rouse Check of their blood, giving them the effects of the Internal Awareness, using the vampire’s Occult rating and Power Bonus as their own.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The effect persists in a person for a month or until the vampire who drank the blood feeds again, whichever comes sooner</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"136"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"qOD5QCvk":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"INJrJgw2":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.B21DfERlY3YVhKlH"},"B72DbSBTIABFMVDV":{"name":"Speak From the Heart","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Known as <em>message in a butthole</em> in California, this creates an elixir that is, to all appearances, some flavor of soda. Some patsy, usually mortal, drinks the soda. The next vampire who drinks the patsy’s blood receives the message.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A soft drink, the alchemist’s Blood, melancholic human blood, mercury, ink or paint.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist imprints the message while brewing the soda. Grape soda can carry seventy-one words or less. Orange reveals a sketched image in the drinker’s mind for about a minute. Lemon-lime conveys a brief but vivid emotional sensation. It’s possible that a mortal who drank the carrier’s blood would also get the effects, but no one’s run that experiment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The messages take about sixty seconds. They persist in the carrier until death, or until someone drinks their blood.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"75"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.B72DbSBTIABFMVDV"},"BDmftmFPk8DMwkuC":{"name":"Enduring Beasts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user shares a small portion of their unnatural toughness with the animals they influence. Teeming swarms and great beasts alike exhibit a resistance to fleeting injuries almost equal to the vampire themself.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free (for famulus); One Rouse Check (for other animals)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Animal Ken (for nonfamulus animals)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can choose to extend some of their Fortitude powers to animals affected by their Animalism. Any animal thus imbued gains additional Health levels equal to the Fortitude dots of the vampire. Using this power on their famulus is free and automatic. To imbue other animals besides their famulus, the user must make a Rouse Check and roll a test of Stamina + Animal Ken (Difficulty 3). The user can fortify one animal per point of margin. When the effect ends, remove unmarked Health first; this may result in the animal expiring.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene, permanent for famulus.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"80"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"JGGffdSy":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"mh8JNzCR":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BDmftmFPk8DMwkuC"},"BFn4zlF9oGU8boYx":{"name":"Animal Succulence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can slake additional Hunger by feeding on animals. In addition, the vampire can consume its famulus, gaining nourishment far beyond what would be gained from an animal of similar stature and absorbing a sliver of its primary trait.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Feeding from animals slakes 1 additional Hunger, and the vampire counts their Blood Potency as two levels lower in regards to penalties to slaking Hunger from animal blood.</p>\n<p>Consuming one’s famulus slakes 4 Hunger, regardless of animal size. This act can never remove the final Hunger die. In addition, consuming one's famulus increases the vampire’s Attribute most associated with that animal (as determined by the Storyteller) by two dots, to a standard maximum (usually 5). Consuming a cat might raise Dexterity or Composure; consuming a dog might raise Charisma or Resolve. Storytellers may vary the reward from famulus consumption: draining an owl might raise Wits to improve perception by two dots or Intelligence to improve wisdom. The bonus lasts until the vampire’s next feeding or until their Hunger reaches 5.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"17"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BFn4zlF9oGU8boYx"},"BVG10UOw1FYvBKxi":{"name":"Cylcopean Sentinel","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>For the rotting descendants of Troglodytia, their permanently putrescent bodies are a source of amazing powers. One of these abilities combines their loose connection to pieces of their corpse and their twisted version of Mortis to give them a chance to spy on those around them. It is said they can remove one of their own eyeballs and place it somewhere to keep an eye on something be that the gate to their haven, someone they wish to spy on or a carriage to follow it back to its origin. There is even rumor of one inventive Stiff who placed his own eye in the vacant socket of a shambling servant to give them a mobile spy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Corpse in the Monster Path •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire removes one of their own eyes and places it somewhere to watch. The eye sees everything in front of it but only in its direct field of vision. It can only see though and no other senses are transmitted by the eye. The Cainite who uses this power takes 1 level of Superficial Health damage for removing their own eye and cannot heal the missing eye until they retrieve it. If the eye is destroyed, it can be grown back by healing the damage. If the eye is destroyed by fire or sunlight, the owner takes 1 level of Aggravated Damage and cannot replace the missing eye until they heal it. While using this power, the Cainite’s perception is split between two locations unless they keep their other eye closed and suffer a -2 to all checks based on vision including most combat-related rolls (Awareness, aiming a weapons etc.)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until retreived or the eye is destroyed</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"177"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BVG10UOw1FYvBKxi"},"BVVkZx3HdtzTVO4x":{"name":"Anima Walk","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>For witches to fly, they must use magic and toxicology. A master of Auspex requires nothing so vulgar. They may shed their skin and walk about in the twilight of the world at will while tethered to their old body by a silver cord. Like the stories of witches, the vampire can fly at incredible speeds, and no distance is outside their ability so long as it is above bedrock and below the moon. In this state, the user is between the boundary of the physical world and the innumerable layers of spiritual planes stacked on top of each other known as the Umbra, allowing them to interact with spirits.</p>\n<p>Many Toreador with Anima Walk use it to eavesdrop on talented artists during their working hours without disturbing the creator. Some Cappadocians — particularly the upstart Giovanni family — use it to engage shades of the dead in conversation. Most other Clans use it to spy on one another or to ensure that their minions are acting according to instructions. Cainites who know of this power’s existence tend to assume that the most powerful Methuselahs and Clan founders use it from torpor, keeping tabs on whole bloodlines for their inevitable night of return.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Willpower point per scene</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Resolve + Occult. Difficulty should reflect the character’s ability to bring the place up clearly in their imagination. Thus, a place in sight is Difficulty 1, a place distant but deeply familiar would be a Difficulty 2, and to fly to an unfamiliar place known about only academically would require 3 or 4.</p>\n<p>A Failure means the character cannot pull themselves from their body. A Bestial Failure could be catastrophic, sending their soul flying in a random direction and distance, perhaps even into some spiritual realm, the nightmares of a sleeping child, dreamlike spirit realms, dark lands of the dead or a place on earth where the sun is shining. (The sun would not burn their soul, but would without a doubt require the roll to resist Rötschreck).</p>\n<p>On a win, the user leaves their body behind, in torpor, and are unable to see or sense what happens to it unless they’re nearby. They remain tethered to it, no matter how far they travel, by a spiritual silver cord. Should that cord be severed, they become like a ghost, and may remain as such if they cannot return to their flesh. Finding your body might take days, or even longer if you are in unfamiliar territory or if one ends up on one of the spiritual planes of existence. Without your silver cord to guide you unerringly home, you may find yourself in great danger. Once the cord is severed, the body falls into torpor.</p>\n<p>This power requires a Willpower point to activate. Any new scene (hopping between planes of existence counts as a new scene) the character wishes to remain in Flight requires a fresh Willpower expenditure and a new roll. The character may cross into a higher Astral Plane (p. XX) or descend into the lands of the dead (known as the Dark Umbra, p. XX) by spending a point of Willpower and rolling Resolve + Occult (Difficulty 3) to cross into it or back from it, but the user is limited to space that lies between the Sun and bedrock in terms of metaphysical geographics. Failure on this roll severs the silver cord and the vampire will be doomed to spending more time than they hoped in the spirit realms.</p>\n<p>You are invisible and immaterial while projecting into the spirit realm, unless you should encounter another spirit or a Cainite who is also using Anima Walk. The user may pass through solid objects with ease. Other characters with particular sensitivity to ghosts and psychic activity (such as those with Sense the Unseen) may notice you with a Wits or Resolve + Awareness roll. The Difficulty here is equal to the successes rolled on power’s activation. Even those who do notice you won’t necessarily be able to identify you; you are merely an immaterial shade hovering in the general area. You do not carry clothing or objects on your spectral journey and are able to fly at great speeds (the Storyteller can use roughly 1000 miles per hour or 1500 kilometers per hour as a general guide). You appear to be an idealised version of yourself, clad only in spirit-stuff. Legendary or magical objects might come along with you as you project your soul. The Storyteller has the final word on this.</p>\n<p>To other Cainites using Anima Walk (or other supernatural entities with the ability to project into the spirit worlds), you are entirely physical, and you can interact with them as you would interact with someone you encountered on the physical plane. Other beings with particular sensitivity to psychic activity, such as ghosts, werewolves, and even some magi, travel the spirit world as well, and can interact with a vampire’s psychic presence normally (although the vampire is not considered a “ghost” for powers such as Necromancy, but a spirit vulnerable to things like wards against spirits.) In the spirit world you have no Physical Attributes. Instead, you should use your Wits as your Dexterity, your Manipulation as your Strength, your Intelligence as your Stamina, and your Willpower as your Health. Additionally, you cannot attack your target directly. Successful attacks deal Willpower damage. When you are out of Willpower, your silver cord has been cut, and the Storyteller might require additional rolls for you to not stumble into one of the higher or lower parts of the Umbra. After that, any additional point of damage is able to destroy your soul. Note that the vampire flies at extreme speeds with this power just like spirits. More “material” creatures like werewolves who physically enter the umbra tend to be slow enough to outrun.</p>\n<p>You can use other Disciplines while using Anima Walk, but they affect only the spiritual plane on which you travel. So you could Dominate another vampire who is using this power, but not the lowliest of mortals you observe on the physical plane.</p>\n<p>The character cannot interact physically with the physical world when their soul is freed from their body. At best, they can spend a Willpower point to appear to others as a ghostly apparition and can speak to those present, but restriction for powers remains to only affect the spirit world.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended. Auspex users should be aware that there are means by which spirits and sorcerers can imprison their souls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"32"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"Shk0kIQY":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"6Fnh0YvS":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BVVkZx3HdtzTVO4x"},"BXnmQ8B7w2tmaOus":{"name":"See the Hidden World","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>The eye of Valeren can pierce the veils between worlds, showing the vampire the world from a more spiritual perspective.</p>\n<p>If the vampire hasn’t yet developed the third eye, they develop it prematurely when selecting this power and the eye opens whenever this power is used.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When activated, the vampire pierces the spiritual layers and is able to see discorporealised spirits, demons and even ghosts. The user may also roll Resolve + Awareness vs. Composure + Subterfuge to read auras in order to discern the nature of the observed subject, be they a vampire, mage, ghoul (revenants count as ghouls), demon, spirit, ghost, etc. Common mortals have no aura.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"195"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BXnmQ8B7w2tmaOus"},"BivD0NJRJPQCS140":{"name":"Aspect of the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The users of Spiritus are known for also drawing parts of spirits and their concepts into themselves to boost their powers, often in bargains with the said spirits to bring vengeance to this who hunt animals for fun and destroy their habitats for profits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power takes one full turn to take effect as the user collects the spirits of the area and usually makes some sort of a promise to them. The user rolls Manipulation + Animal Ken at a Difficulty of 3. On a win, they may choose a number of animal aspects equal to their Spiritus rating. The same aspect cannot be taken more than once. Aspects below are mere samples. The Storyteller should allow the player to craft their own aspects related to the concepts associated with animals in the region. When making an aspect, it should give a dot in a Skill and an associated Specialty.</p>\n<p>• Ant: The user gains a dot in Survival and a Specialty related to the area such as Desert.</p>\n<p>• Bear: Gain gains a dot in Athletics and Feats of Strength Specialty</p>\n<p>• Bee: Gain gains a dot in Leadership and Team Dynamics Specialty.</p>\n<p>• Cat: Gain gains a dot in Persuasion and Bargaining Specialty</p>\n<p>• Crocodile: Gain gains a dot in Stealth and Ambush Specialty</p>\n<p>• Crow: Gain gains a dot in Occult and Mysteries specialty.</p>\n<p>• Dog: Gain gains a dot in Awareness and Smell Specialty.</p>\n<p>• Rabbit: Gain gains a dot in Awareness and Hearing Specialty</p>\n<p>• Fox: Gain gains a dot in Subterfuge and Impeccable Lies Specialty.</p>\n<p>• Ram: Gain gains a dot in Melee and Staking Specialty.</p>\n<p>• Peacock: Gain a dot in Etiquette and a High Society Specialty</p>\n<p>• Owl: Gain gains a dot in Academics and one Specialty of choice.</p>\n<p>• Rabbit: The gains a dot in Athletics</p>\n<p>• Snake: The user gains a dot in Brawl and Grappling Specialty</p>\n<p>• Spider: Gain a dot in Crafts and a Specialty of the user’s choice related to physical creation</p>\n<p>• Wild boar: Gain gains a dot in Intimidation and Physical Coercion Specialty</p>\n<p>• Wolf: Gain gains a dot in Brawl and Claws or Bite Attack Specialty These effects are visible when seen by aura reading powers such as Scry the Soul or powers that allow for the sight of spirits in the world like Auspex which can identify which spirits exactly the person has drawn to themselves. With this, the maximum rating of a Skill is increased to 6. Specialties do not count towards the maximum limit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene unless ended before that. A Critical Win extends the effect for the rest of the night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"161"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BivD0NJRJPQCS140"},"BlK9yMhGaw3j5qnc":{"name":"Soul Exchange","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Scholar and thief, the twin childer of Zao-lat are seldom seen these nights. Mastery of the greatest Watcher power requires a melding of their two purviews: scholarly arcane research of the soul and the acquisition of the most valuable things. Combined, the vampire can achieve the greatest redistribution of all: a soul.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The Watcher keeps two individuals within their line of sight, then spends a point of Willpower and makes a Manipulation + Occult roll against Composure + Resolve. A win against allows the user to switch the souls of the two targets. The victims immediately exchange Roads (and ratings), and Ambitions an experience that’s horrifically disorienting to say the least. Though Desire remains the same, the essential nature and thus desires of the characters will shift rapidly.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user may perform Soul Exchange between another and themselves instead of between two other individuals.</p>\n<p>This power can be undone by both characters spending all of their Willpower if they possess Occult rating greater than the user. This power can be undone by a vampire apply Unburden the Bestial Soul (Healer Valeren •••••) or by reapplying this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"200"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"dXGzAdrS":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"E6yvVv0s":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BlK9yMhGaw3j5qnc"},"Bn4DwN9OUVJ8xTZy":{"name":"Shape of the Beast’s Wrath","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>Users of this power are often mistaken for Tzimisce employing the Vicissitude power Zulo Form. A vampire employing this power shifts into a huge, monstrous form, gaining around half their height again and around triple their weight. Their overall shape flows into an unholy amalgamation of their own form and that of the animal they feels the closest kinship to (wolves, rats, and great cats are the most common manifestations, though ravens, serpents, bats, and stranger beasts have been reported). The vampire’s new shape does bear some vague resemblance to the war-forms of the werecreatures, but the difference quickly becomes apparent.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Shape of the Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> During transformation, the character may only defend using their normal dice pools. On the next turn, the character allocates a number of dots into their Physical Attributes equal to their Protean rating. At least one dot must go into each Physical Attribute, (so a character could have +3 Strength, +1 Dexterity, and +1 Stamina, but not +1 Strength and +4 Dexterity). While in this form, the user’s maximum rating in Physical Attributes is equal to 4 + Blood Surge value. The user counts all Criticals as Messy Criticals as the Beast is empowered by being brought to the surface in this manner.</p>\n<p>This form has all drawbacks and benefits of animal transformation like Shapechange such as the lack of ability to communicate, use some Disciplines and special abilities like flying.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire falls to sleep or torpor or ends the power</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"130"},"level":4,"cost":2,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Bn4DwN9OUVJ8xTZy"},"BnIrciv8UdXnBbk1":{"name":"Tongue of the Asp","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Calling upon the asp, the royal animal of dynastic and Roman Egypt, the user channels extends their tongue Roman Egypt, the user channels extends their tongue which becomes barbed, sharp and coated with a lethal contact poison. The tongue itself is usually reflective of the owner as it can look like a tongue of other mythological animals. Still, the image of a slit-tongue being used as a deadly whip is the one that has been cultivated by the Followers of Set.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user instantly protrudes what can only be described as a tongue that has been turned into a sharp whip with the length of half a meter per dot of Serpentis the user possesses. The tongue can be miraculously retracted in its entirety when not in use.</p>\n<p>The tongue acts as a +1 piercing Melee weapon which can be used to grapple or attack as normal using Dexterity + Melee or attack with the same pool, dealing Aggravated Health damage to mortals and the damage from this weapon is not halved. The user may also perform bite attacks and even feed with it, carrying the stunning effects of the vampire’s kiss. Bite attacks done with Tongue of the Asp suffer no called shot penalty. The venom which coats the tongue kills or debilitates a mortal victim by the end of the scene if not treated (Intelligence + Medicine Difficulty 3). Against vampires, the venom has no effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended by the user</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"152"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BnIrciv8UdXnBbk1"},"BwtoUtVRpqDAJzUn":{"name":"Blood Tempering","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The older a Cainite target at the end of an assassin’s blade, the greater the chance of the target utilising powerful Disciplines fueled by their older Blood to fend off an attacker. Hinder the Cainite Vitae lends its aid in such situations, shutting off the powers of even the mightiest of elders with but a touch. The Children of Haqim, the greatest users of this powers, usually do fight their opponents with fist and as such they utilise this only when they can get a surprise on their opponents. This power technically works on revenants and ghouls, but most users of Quietus consider it beneath them to clog what little mortal possess.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Craft</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> First the user must splatter the object with vitae after which most usually utter a prayer to the spirits, seeking to petition them to instill the object with vampiric longevity and metaphysical strength. The user then rolls Intelligence + Craft (Difficulty 2).</p>\n<p>On a win, the enchanted object can only be destroyed by things that harm vampires by dealing Aggravated Health damage to them such as specific types of magic, sunlight, Fist of Caine, fire and True Faith and so on. The object gains health equal to the user’s Quietus rating to endure such damage.</p>\n<p>Weapons and armour enchanted by this do not break or dull by powers and age as usual and armour extends its Avoidance effect (p. XX) even against weapons dealing armour-piercing and heavy impact damage. However, due to the new weaknesses, armour will no longer stop things like bite attacks as they deal Aggravated Health damage to them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> No item enchanted by this has never withered by age alone, for now. Almost every old scroll in Alamut is under the effect of this power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"144"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GUz2ebdb":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"FABbo5Xt":{"path":"skills.craft"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.BwtoUtVRpqDAJzUn"},"C0YyjmplcWEtxPMo":{"name":"Dagon’s Call","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With the slightest scratch delivering a dab of blood to mingle with that of the target’s, Dagon’s Call twists a victim’s own blood against them. The target’s eyes swell and take a deep burgundy hue, muscles seize and veins bulge beneath the skin, and thin trickles of vibrant scarlet bleed into tiny rivers from all openings in the anatomy. This nightmarish affliction persists until the target collapses in agony or death throes, leaving no trace of the attacker’s presence.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full to activate the power. Applying blood depends on the method</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Awareness vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> In order to use this power, the character’s blood must first be ingested or enter the target’s bloodstream, usually through drinking (even from a ghoul belonging to the character) or being pierced by a bloodied weapon. The character waits a minimum of one hour for her blood to be fully absorbed, entering the circulatory system to securely grip the victim deeply. After a moment’s concentration, the vampire bursts their target’s blood vessels, internally constricting the target’s whole body by flooding it with ruptured hemoglobin that strangles them from within.</p>\n<p>To activate the power, the user must Rouse the Blood and inflict a wound to themselves, enough to bleed at least a drop of blood. Typically, this deals no damage. After that the user Resolve + Awareness against the victim’s Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude, if they possess it). Upon a Win a mortal victim takes Aggravated Health damage equal to the margins with vampires taking it as Superficial Health damage instead.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The user’s vitae remains potent in the victim’s system for a week or until the user has activated the power 3 times, using up all of their blood within the victim.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"141"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"OuEZrmuy":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"4MUcXi13":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.C0YyjmplcWEtxPMo"},"C1jovv2VZec0EsoJ":{"name":"Ripples of the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power, a vampire can leave an emotional echo that resonates in their own blood or the blood of those from whom they feeds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the user must first make their Rouse Check and make roll of Manipulation + Insight against a Difficulty of 3 and focus on a specific emotion belonging to a Resonance. On a win, the user’s mouth begins to fill with blood infused with that emotion. The vampire may either swallow it to affect themselves or feed it to somebody else. Either way, the target’s Resonance changes to the one belonging to the previously focused emotion and their Temperament is raised by one, to a minimum of Intense.</p>\n<p>Should a Resonance be changed in a target with an Acute Temperament, they user may dictate their new Dyscasia.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The intense emotion and the Resonance with it wither away after a week (or until a vampire feeds again or falls to slumber, whichever comes sooner).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"136"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"U3OY04IU":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"G7EAAEjf":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.C1jovv2VZec0EsoJ"},"CK0CDBUGhEM2TmV9":{"name":"Apparition","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>Characters with Apparition can give a static illusion created with either Dweomer or Ignis Fatuus the semblance of movement. Such an illusion moves as the character directs. The movement can be as simple as a banner flapping in the (illusory) breeze or as complex as a galloping horse and rider. If the character is unfamiliar with the type of movement in question, witnesses to the illusion are more likely to pierce it, but if he’s seen it before, it should be perfectly natural and not arouse any suspicions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor action of -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One point of Willpower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> As long as the vampire concentrates on the illusion cast by Ignis Fatuus or Fata Morgana, they can make it move in any way they wish, within reason. In game terms, concentration is minor action inflicting a two-dice penalty on actions. They can make an illusion of a person appear to move and talk as though they were real, or they can make the illusion of a sparkling ballroom with billowing drapes and flickering candelabras. Impersonating a person using this power may require additional Performance or Subterfuge rolls. Once the creator stops concentrating on the illusion, it can continue in simple repetitive motions that could be described in a simple sentence, such as a horse nervously pawing at the ground. After that, the illusion cannot be controlled by the creator. They can allow it to move as the caster last directed or dispel it as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As Ignis Fatuus or Fata Morgana</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"49"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.CK0CDBUGhEM2TmV9"},"CKzIIzD2Y3AkjeTD":{"name":"Toughness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>All vampires with this power exhibit an innate ability to ignore damage that would otherwise inconvenience and even disable others of their kind. While this power alone gives no protection against banes and other aggravated damage, the protection it confers adds up in the long run.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Subtract the Fortitude of the defender from all Superficial Health damage sustained. This occurs before halving the damage, and cannot reduce the damage below one. Damage is subtracted after the damage has already been counted for the purposes of staking.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"81"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.CKzIIzD2Y3AkjeTD"},"CLRRKYJo0e7vlY2W":{"name":"Scorpion’s Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Scorpion’s Touch transmutes the Cainite vitae, which is usually healing and nurturing, into a deadly poison capable of harming victims by touch alone.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user concentrates and forces Blood through an open, usually self-inflicted, wound. Each Rouse Check’s worth of poison made emits enough Blood to coat one bladed melee weapon or to fill one mouthful to be spat at a foe. Spitting the poison can happen instantly during the first turn while coating a weapon is a -2 dice Minor Action.</p>\n<p>The poison looks and smells like normal blood and a Cainite is immune to their own poison, but not that of others utilising this power. If the vampire is in danger of someone attempting to drink blood from them, they may activate the power defensively (doesn’t count for Discipline activation limit), which may be used reflexively even if in slumber or torpor. A vampire may also bite their tongue or lip to pool the poisoned blood in their mouth for a “kiss of death” or a spitting attack (Composure + Aim vs. Dexterity + Athletics, range is equal to the highest Quietus Path + Potence in meters/yards ). Likewise, they may cut their flesh to smear the poison on a pointed or edged weapon, or even place it in food, on a cup, utensil, or other seemingly innocuous item. Darts, arrowheads and daggers are the most commonly smeared items, although smaller bolts and bullets lack do not have enough surface to carry enough poison. Simple skin contact of finger with a drop of blood is also enough as the skin absorbs the poison into the victim’s blood stream. Multiple poisons may stack as the blood is mixed.</p>\n<p>Poison remains potent for nights equal to the user’s Quietus rating, although envenomed weapons bathed in water, or open to the mercy of heavy wind or rain, lose the potency of their poison the turn after exposure, while those passing through fire or sunlight lose it instantly and appear dusted in a fine ash.</p>\n<p>If the poison hits, mortals instantly fall unconscious and suffer a number of Aggravated Health damage up to the user’s Quietus rating. The user may limit the damage, should they wish to merely incapacitate the target. Undead creatures like vampires suffer only unhalved Superficial damage. Mortals who take damage from this poison immediately fall unconscious.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The poison remains potent for nights equal to the user’s Quietus rating</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"141"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.CLRRKYJo0e7vlY2W"},"CbMkI3GeemDqm8TE":{"name":"Soaring Leap","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Possessing unholy strength in more than arms and fists, the user can leap far higher and further than any mortal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user can jump a number of meters equal to three times their Potence level vertically, and five times their Potence level horizontally. The user needs no run-up to make these leaps.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"116"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.CbMkI3GeemDqm8TE"},"CekkPun84OCNQaTX":{"name":"Mosh Pit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>With but a syllable, the vampire’s voice enflames all the humours of rage within a person, turning them into a violent beast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the target’s Resonance is changed at choleric and, if they are a vampire or another creature with the vampiric Beast, they must make a Fury Frenzy test against a equal to the user’s Melpominee rating.</p>\n<p>Mortals who lose this context experience a fury frenzy similar to vampires, unless they spend a number of Willpower points equal to the users margin of win.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the Frenzy ends. The Resonance changes back once the effect ends as well.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"87"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gr6J2KtA":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"ssylN8B1":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.CekkPun84OCNQaTX"},"CoyjAKqIhRNy43du":{"name":"Dead Man’s Hand","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some say the legend of James Butler Hickok’s (better known as “Wild Bill” Hickok) death is that he was shot in the back. That’s the story mortals have used to justify what really happened on August 1, 1876. Hickock may have indeed been shot in the back but, if rumors hold true, he had long since left the mortal world behind years earlier. Either way, the power seemingly enacted that night influenced Wild West tales for years to come.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check and two Willpower points.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A, Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user holds an object and pays the power’s cost while meditating for 10 minutes on it. As long as the vampire holds the object, it can act normally but with a dice pool no greater than your Road Rating. As soon has it removed from their person, however, or when its physical body is slain, the vampire’s soul immediately transfers to the object and it loses all awareness, leaving its body functionally dead, though the vampire can stay in the object indefinitely.</p>\n<p>When another individual or animal touches the object, the vampire’s awareness extends to their soul like with Share the Senses (Auspex •••) and gleans all the information available by Scry the Soul power (Auspex •••).</p>\n<p>While in the object, the vampire’s soul remains vulnerable to most vampiric banes and it still suffers the effects of true faith and fire. It can recover Willpower normally at the start of each night. However, the object cannot soak damage and is no more durable than it was before being possessed by the vampire. If the object is destroyed, then the vampire rolls Road Rating (Difficulty 3). If it fails, its soul falls into the astral plane and is permanently lost. If it succeeds, the vampire’s soul moves on to the next closest object instead.</p>\n<p>When another individual touches the object, the vampire can force out its soul and inhabit the body. In order to possess a person, the vampire must initiate a contest of Willpower vs. Willpower against the intended target who is touching the object (in the case of multiple people touching the object). The vampire must spend a Willpower point for each attempt (two if the target is also a vampire). Each margin deals a point of Aggravated Willpower damage to the other person (tie is still counted as a win with the margin of 1 for boath sides).</p>\n<p>When the target’s Willpower reaches 0, it can be possessed. However, if the vampire’s Willpower reaches 0 first, its soul is permanently destroyed. While possessing the body of the target, if the target is a vampire they may use all of their powers they know regardless of whether or not their new host knows them. The user may utilise the Hunger of their host as needed for their Disciplines. The host’s effective Generation is changed to the vampire who has taken over if the host’s Generation is higher than that of the invader. The user of this power assumes the Physical Attributes and Looks Merits of their new host, but retains the rest of their Social and Mental Attributes. The invader gains none of the host’s Skills or Specialties. Furthermore, the invader retains its Clan Disciplines and Clan Bane, but gains the host’s Clan Bane as well.</p>\n<p>When inhabiting the body of a mortal the vampire is able to power their Disciplines as needed if they have the required vitae in their new vessel, but it may only be done by turning the mortal into a ghoul somehow. Powers that do not require Rouse Checks function as normal. The vampire’s presence also benefits the vessel when the vessel is harmed physically or mentally. The vampire’s Willpower and Generation are factored in when the vessel is the subject of Dominate or Presence powers or any other instance where those would be important to a power’s success or failure. The vampire’s spirit empowers the vessel so that they take Superficial Health damage from most things like a vampire. While in a host that does not suffer damage from sunlight, the user does not suffer damage from it either.</p>\n<p>When inhabiting a mortal host, the vampire must make a Road Rating roll each dawn (Difficulty 2). A failure renders the vampire unable to rise for a day, and a Total Failure means the vampire effectively goes into torpor and the host regains control until the vampire awakens (duration is based on current Road Rating). A win allows the vampire to function normally and rise. When awakening while possessing a mortal body, there is no cost of an awakening Rouse Check. The user heals damage like their vessel.</p>\n<p>In theory, it is possible for a mortal host to be an animal host, but that would require preparation such as fastening the object onto an animal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vessel is destroyed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"164"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.CoyjAKqIhRNy43du"},"DFQzC2vBBlIuiv5q":{"name":"The Sword of Malkav","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this terrifying Discipline, the Malkavian wrecks a victim’s very sense of reality. She changes one fundamental truth for the victim, and the victim cannot fathom a world in which that false reality is challenged or untrue.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Stain the Soul</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Insight against Composure + Wits of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 52). If successful, the victim’s belief, perceptions, and understanding shift in order to facilitate the falsehood which the user must formulate, usually in a single sentence. The victim believes the false truth before all; it becomes a piece of his very being while the power lasts. If they hear solid evidence that the truth is false, they suffer two points of unhalved Superficial Health and Willpower damage per turn as every ounce of their identity fights the “falsehood”. If the victim is a Cainite, revelation that this truth is false becomes a Fury Frenzy provocation, at a Difficulty of 3 to resist. Acting against that false truth becomes a level one sin against his Road as well.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 52.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"68"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.DFQzC2vBBlIuiv5q"},"DHrRjPi4OJFSyIf1":{"name":"Blacklight Surprise","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The sun is the bane of the undead, but this formula can lend Phoebus’ hideous strength to a common UV flashlight or blacklight bulb, for the Kindred unlucky enough to be downrange of it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, sanguine and choleric mortal blood, sunflower seeds, hand sanitizer, Luminol.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A UV source smeared with Blacklight Surprise does 1 point of Aggravated damage every turn its light hits a vampire. It provokes fear frenzy like sunlight does. Kindred who already take damage from UV take an extra point.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> At least one hour, plus an additional hour for every success the alchemist scores with the dice. If the light source breaks, the formula burns off.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"75"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.DHrRjPi4OJFSyIf1"},"DUe4neoPjRj3BiM7":{"name":"Shatter","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>When a fist hits concrete, it’s usually the fist that breaks. So it is with this power, where a vampire turns a blow against the one delivering it. This power has been known to break weapons and shatter bones.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Shatter</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free, does not count towards maximum Discipline powers activated in a turn.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Anyone who makes a successful Brawl attack against the user suffers the damage which Toughness subtracts as Superficial Health damage. Melee weapons also suffer reflected damage, and break once they endure their damage modifier’s worth (unless it is holy, magical, or otherwise unbreakable). This power may only be used while Toughness is active and may be used outside of one’s turn as a response to an incoming attack.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene (or until hit)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"83"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.DUe4neoPjRj3BiM7"},"DcxWOBKaO4XM4J6i":{"name":"Summon Spirit Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user summons one of the spirits native to the area to fulfil a command, a spirit of a dog on a street or bear in a forest. Once summoned, the spirit materializes, taking on an ordinary form. While it can be rather difficult to coerce the spirit of a pigeon to murder a human, a clever user of Spiritus will be able to get what they need from a right spirit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A win on a Charisma + Animal Ken roll at a Difficulty of 2 allows the user to summon a spirit of a summoning of a large animal or two small or medium animals. These spirits communicate telepathically with the user and possess a slightly higher intelligence than that of the animal it represents.</p>\n<p>The spirit will naturally obey commands that suit its nature — a curious pigeon might follow a person and report to the user while a wolf might be happy to maul a person to death. To issue a command unnatural to the spirit, a roll must be made with Manipulation + Animal Ken with a Difficulty set by the storyteller (between 2 to 5 in extreme cases).</p>\n<p>The spirit may choose to materialise to interact with the physical world, taking on a form that suits their identity the most and appearing mundane through it save for those with supernatural sight. after which it simply dematerialises. Should the spirit’s Health (or rather, health of its material form) be reduced to 0, the spirit simply dematerialises and returns to its spirit realm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the spirit departs from the material plane, usually when it loses interest.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"162"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"U8pGRRU6":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"v8m71KN4":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.DcxWOBKaO4XM4J6i"},"DjqQTkALrHgRFJD8":{"name":"Hypnosis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire begins to sing and focuses the power of their voice onto a target within their line of sight, forcing them into an euphoric or a catatonic-like state.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire sings or recites verses, rolling Charisma + Performance vs. the target’s Composure + Wits. On a win, the victim finds themselves unable to move or act whatsoever, even to defend, as long as the user takes no other action but to keep up the music. A vampire victim can escape paralysis on any turn after the first by spending a point of Willpower. Against unprepared mortals (those who haven’t yet been under a supernatural power or are aware of the supernatural), there is no need for a contest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"86"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"gr6J2KtA":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"ssylN8B1":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.DjqQTkALrHgRFJD8"},"DmDkls5yiUpMElwx":{"name":"Draugh of Endurance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Blood of the vampire becomes saturated with the power of Fortitude, conveying a part of that power to anyone who drinks of it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Drinking a Rouse Check’s worth of Blood directly from the user gifts the drinker with temporary Fortitude equal to half the Fortitude dots (rounded up) of the donor. The drinker gains the same powers as the donor’s, up to that level.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until expired, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"82"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.DmDkls5yiUpMElwx"},"E90iCu5xnZE2h5qF":{"name":"Scent of Caine","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vigilant to the point of paranoia, the Tremere wisely fear the reprisals of other clans and the vengeance of Saulot’s warrior brood. To ward against ambush from undead enemies or their ghoul slaves, thaumaturges developed this power to scent the mystical resonance of Caine’s Curse. Any creature that bears vitae in its veins may trigger the vampire’s preternatural senses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Bloodlife Path ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits/Resolve + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever a vitae-bearing creature approaches (vampire, ghoul, revenant) within range of the character’s normal senses, the Storyteller secretly rolls (or the player, if the Storyteller is fine with it) rolls the Wits + Occult against Composure + Subterfuge of vitae-bearing creature(s). The vitae-bearing creature suffers a die penalty for each point of Blood Potency above 5.</p>\n<p>If the roll succeeds, the vampire becomes aware of the vitae-bearing presence, although they cannot identify the direction or source unless they scored a Critical Win or 3+ margins. Failure means the vampire does not detect the creature, while a Total Failure or a Botch indicates that the vampire receives false information. Over time, characters may grow to identify particular vampires by their mystical scent alone, though only after many encounters. The Assamites know a version of this power that depends on their blood sorcery rather than hermetic Thaumaturgy. This power otherwise duplicates its Tremere counterpart. The Tzimisce and others are not particularly aware of this power, so they tend to develop it more rarely or not at all.</p>\n<p>This power is always active, even in torpor.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"28"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"bvSubVFh":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"WYQJwcgF":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.E90iCu5xnZE2h5qF"},"ECIzsqmiwPeeyHaU":{"name":"Hinder the Gifts of Caine","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The older a Cainite target at the end of an assassin’s blade, the greater the chance of the target utilising powerful Disciplines fueled by their older Blood to fend off an attacker. Hinder the Cainite Vitae lends its aid in such situations, shutting off the powers of even the mightiest of elders with but a touch. The Children of Haqim, the greatest users of this powers, usually do fight their opponents with fist and as such they utilise this only when they can get a surprise on their opponents. This power technically works on revenants and ghouls, but most users of Quietus consider it beneath them to clog what little mortal possess.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free/Minor</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches their target (any combination of Dexterity/Strength + Athletics/Brawl would suffice) or prepares a poison like with Scorpion’s Touch (p. 122) and manages to establish contact with the target If the user manages to do that, they make a roll of Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve roll against their victim. On a win, the the target may not perform Rouse Checks for any purpose. To break out of the effect early, the victim may perform Rouse Checks equal to the margin they lost on the roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> 1 turn + for each point of margin.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"142"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ECIzsqmiwPeeyHaU"},"EGB1Opx2s5gVKLHx":{"name":"Grace of Anubis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>By making a pact with the god who welcomes the souls into the Duat, the vampire is able to save their companion from certain doom of curse they themselves possess and are even able to save their lives.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By making the sign of Anubis with their vitaeon the target’s forehead, they are able to bestow upon them a protection from vampirism for the rest of the story. Should this mortal be embraced (and thus, killed), they will simply not be revived by the vampiric embrace and will probably be tossed aside as “too long-gone” or “too weak” for embrace. Instead of returning as a vampire, the target is functionally “dead” for all biological intents and purposes, but they are revived on the next sunrise with their Health fully recovering as well.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One story.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"40"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.EGB1Opx2s5gVKLHx"},"EGkJiFomzEc58eNj":{"name":"Flight (•)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/flight.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user cannot yet fly, but they may glide. They can gently descend from any height, and strong winds may provide enough lift to carry them great distances. However, carrying more than 2 lbs./1 kg will cause the user to plummet like a rock which is why wearing light clothes is not only recommended but a must.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"78"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"flight","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.EGkJiFomzEc58eNj"},"EIdg8AGIQWGsXB88":{"name":"Hospital Chains","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Hospital Chains keeps Kindred and mortals from recovering from injuries. Because sometimes, when you teach someone a lesson, you want to give them a study aid to make sure it sticks. The elixir takes the form of stringy red spittle to be expectorated or splashed on the victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, phlegmatic mortal blood, a sample from a fatal carcinoma, hydrofluoric acid.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Distillation Roll vs. Stamina</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Once Hospital Chains touches an injured person, they cannot clear physical injury by any means. Blood is Roused in vain; hospital visits yield no improvement, only bills.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The restriction on improvement lasts for 2 days per point of margin; on a critical win, it lasts 2 days for each success on the Distillation roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"76"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.EIdg8AGIQWGsXB88"},"ELakN6OrypHlaTSY":{"name":"Eyes of the Serpent","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Myths of the cobra and asp — as well as the Greek Medusa — ascribe hypnotic ability to them, and Set’s first gift to his childer is a facsimile of that power. Should the character catch the gaze of a mortal or Cainite, he can beguile them with his gaze and render them immobile. He cannot give them commands or instructions — this is the province of Dominate — but they cannot move, nor speak above a whisper. The target remains immobilized until the character looks away — even so much as a glance breaks the trance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Intimidation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> While in use, the user gains night vision and may penetrate supernatural darkness as they see as snakes do by warmth. By catching the eye of a person (See Dominate Characteristics, p. 57) the vampire rolls Charisma + Intimidation vs. Wits + Composure of the victim. Against mortals, this power is an automatic win. On a win, the vampire immobilises the victim, keeping them frozen in place as long as they themselves maintain eye contact (-2 dice penalty Minor Action). The effect can only be maintained on a single victim at a time, and ends if they suffer damage or are forcibly removed. The victim is still capable of speech, though not shouting.</p>\n<p>Vampires may escape paralysis on any turn after the first by spending a point of Willpower.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the eye contact breaks or until the victim takes at least a point of damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"150"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"fHyNVNuv":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"xmXPp9c4":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ELakN6OrypHlaTSY"},"ERVN6GlIKHjgdSDG":{"name":"Mass Manipulation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can now command entire gatherings of mortals, and in some cases even groups of vampires. The vampire can use this power both to issue instructions and to manipulate memories.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Per power used</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Intelligence + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can amplify any of their other powers to affect a group of people, mortal or vampire, at once. All of the victims need to see the eyes of the user. The user makes any needed roll against the strongest opponent in the group, although players should always be given the option to roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"77"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"M6ZRDkWa":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"J3U4TkSb":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ERVN6GlIKHjgdSDG"},"EYjxURD2pj2rHSaV":{"name":"Awaken the Sleeper","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula results in an elixir which, when added to human blood, can awaken a vampire from Torpor.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric or sanguine human blood, adrenaline, ammonium carbonate, hartshorn, caffeine or benzedrine, melatonin</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist taps their vessel (Calcinatio), or their own vein (Athanor Corporis) to get the Awaken the Sleeper elixir if they do not use Fixatio. They then mix the elixir into human blood. For each success above 2 on the distillation roll, the elixir can awaken a vampire of that Blood Potency. <em>Example: Hari gets 5 successes on</em> <em>his Awaken the Sleeper distilla-</em> <em>tion roll: this elixir can awaken a</em> <em>torpid vampire of Blood Potency 3</em> <em>or less (5 – 2 = 3).</em></p>\n<p>■ <strong>Athanor Corporis:</strong> The alchemist can tap their own vein once per night for this elixir until their next feeding, or until they reach Hunger 5. They need not pay the distillation cost again.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Calcinatio:</strong> If the vessel survives the tap, the alchemist can tap them again in a week for more elixir. They need not pay the distillation cost again, but the vessel pays the Health cost (5 Aggravated damage) each time they are tapped. The Alchemist can also simply feed the vessel’s blood to the sleeper directly</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"287"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.EYjxURD2pj2rHSaV"},"EhzypguTwiOgCplE":{"name":"Drawing Out the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Cainites with this ability are so familiar with the vagaries of animals’ instinctive reactions that they can even read the reactions of their own Beasts. What’s more, as the vampire’s Beast becomes more aroused, he gains the ability to send it out into another being, rather than allowing his own spirit to be overwhelmed by the blood rage.</p>\n<p>The target of the Cainite’s Beast is immediately overwhelmed by a frenzy, and during that frenzy, he acts precisely as the Discipline’s user does when in frenzy, right down to the occasional murderous turn of phrase. Those who know the vampire might recognize their friend’s Beast in another, but such a thing is quite rare.</p>\n<p>The Gangrel use this power to enable their ghouls and childer to act as terrible proxies in time of battle, while the Ravnos use it to torment individuals who present themselves as beacons of righteousness. The Tzimisce use Drawing out the Beast to wreak dreadful havoc on an opposing voivode’s holdings before bringing their full force to bear, while the Nosferatu prefer to use the ability to humiliate other vampires whom they feel need to be taken down a peg</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Animal Ken vs Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Instead of the Willpower roll to resist a Frenzy, roll Wits + Animal Ken vs Composure + Resolve of the target within the line of sight. If the user fails, they enter frenzy as though they had failed the Willpower roll. On a win, the target experiences that Frenzy instead of the user. Later stimuli can still provoke Frenzy in the user, but they can use this power as long as they can make Rouse Checks and further targets remain available. Hunger Frenzy is transferred to mortals as an urge for cannibalism.</p>\n<p>If the target leaves the character’s immediate vicinity before the frenzy ends (or vice versa) the character risks losing his Beast permanently, as it remains with the victim. The vampire’s Beast often likes “living” inside other beings, and it is loath to return to the vampire it knows so well after having a taste of freedom. Should the character lose his Beast, even temporarily, he becomes lethargic, sleeping late in the evenings and needing to spend Willpower to perform even such tasks as feeding.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Standard frenzy duration</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"22"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"dZhponmM":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"JpH642gL":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.EhzypguTwiOgCplE"},"F6SwJT08f8X6odl5":{"name":"Tormented Essence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Daimonion users, as do all Baali, know their place in the universe and within the hierarchy of their masters. Suffering trickles downward, and the Daimonion users stand within that torrent. Calling on the torment in their blood, the Daimonion user may hurl a blazing bolt of infernal pain at their enemies. Nerves cry out, wood turns to ash, stone melts, and flesh disintegrates utterly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Dexterity + Athletics or some other dodge roll</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character gathers infernal pain into their hands; most commonly this manifests as a bolt of black flame, but sickening-hued lightning or slick black tentacles are just as common. Regardless, the power creates a missile that inflicts one point of Aggravated Health and Willpower damage upon a successful hit. If the vampire so wishes, they can make additional Rouse Checks when activating this power to deal additional points of damage, one additional point of damage for each Rouse Check made. The maximum number of Rouse Checks a vampire may make to increase the damage is equal to their Daimonion rating or Blood Potency, whichever is higher.</p>\n<p>Interestingly enough, this attack is even more effective against demons, who may be hit even if they are incorporeal. Demons whose Health is brought to 0 by Tormented Essence are banished to the Abyss. Additionally, characters with Torment must make a Degeneration Test at a Difficulty equal to Daimonion rating of the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"57"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"AuY0UBNA":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"7bcNfyR3":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.F6SwJT08f8X6odl5"},"FIBjM3BBDHZsGCA6":{"name":"Gift of Apis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some consider it a thing borrowed from Animalism, but the Children of Osiris consider it a boon of the bull-god Apis himself. Those possessing his boon are able to feed off animalism long after they’ve been sired.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user halves (rounded up) their Blood Potency for the purposes of slaking Hunger from animals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"39"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FIBjM3BBDHZsGCA6"},"FO0wLUDLDiIOMRWF":{"name":"Terminal Decree","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>No longer hampered by the selfpreservation instincts of their victims, the vampire can now issue commands that directly lead to the harm or death of the victim. Mortals can be made to blow their brains out, jump from rooftops, or swallow poison. Vampires can, with a bit of effort, be made to walk into fire or sunlight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Per power used</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> No additional Hunger cost, but one’s own Humanity cost is potentially severe</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Per power augmented</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Terminal commands now must be resisted (see individual powers regarding rolls involved), rather than failing automatically.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"77"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FO0wLUDLDiIOMRWF"},"FRBpq5quYDDOz12B":{"name":"Malleable Visage","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user may alter their own body in simple, cosmetic ways. They can be taller or stockier. They can change his voice by reshaping their larynx. They can adopt new skin tone, new eye color, new hair, and so on. The process is not simple; it requires the user to actually craft these changes in his own form, which can be disturbing and painful. True to its name, only flesh (including muscle, fat, and cartilage) can be manipulated with Malleable Visage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 2. On a win, the user may perform one of the sample alterations below on themselves. At the Storyteller’s discretion, some of these alterations like Looks Merits, Looks Flaws and certain Physical Merits or Flaws may be bought with experience points (or bought off, for Flaws), refunding any Attribute cost placed into them.</p>\n<p>• Alter Skin (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: Half an hour): The user’s skin changes, providing them a two-dice bonus on rolls to blend into the environment if the skin would match the colour palette of it.</p>\n<p>• Beautify (Cost: One Physical Attribute dot per three dots’ worth of of Looks and Physical Merits; Time: One hour per dot): The user transforms one Physical Attribute into adding or removing three dots’ worth of Looks and Physical Merits.</p>\n<p>The number of Looks Merits is rounded up for the purposes of calculating the Attribute cost. Adding four dots of Looks Merits costs two dots as if the user were adding five or six dots of Looks Merits to themselves.</p>\n<p>• Copy Appearance (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: Hour and a half: The user mimics appearance and voice of another humanoid. To perform this, the user must first possess all the Looks Merits and Flaws of the subject (see other alterations) and then perform Copy Appearance alteration. Successes on Malleable Visage roll for Mimic Appearance are taken as a Difficulty for Wits + Awareness roll to fool others through a perfect mimicry of the body and voice. Fooling others beyond the mere visage would require rolls of Manipulation + Subterfuge (for lying) and Manipulation + Performance (for replicating mannerism), which are contested by Wits + Insight.</p>\n<p>• Minor Alteration (Cost: Free; Time: Minimum of half an hour, up to the Storyteller): The user performs minor alterations on themselves, enough to still be recognised; nails, hair length and colour, eye colour and so on. Other alterations are allowed if they would not bear a large mechanical difference on the story. For example, sex surgery is usually considered a Minor Cosmetic Alteration, but changing one’s sex in a manner to make themselves unrecognisable and possibly acquire benefit from it would be a Major Alteration.</p>\n<p>• Major Alteration (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: Minimum of half an hour, up to the Storyteller): The user makes themselves unrecognisable, greatly or completely their appearance. When taken to an extreme, the user is unrecognisable. Note that Beautify and Ugly may make this alteration pointless.</p>\n<p>• Pockets (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 10 minutes): The user gains hidden pockets in their flesh, providing a two-dice bonus to hide things in them no greater than a one-handed sword.</p>\n<p>• Uglify (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot per three dots’ worth of of Looks and Physical Flaws removed; Time: One hour per dot): The user transforms one Physical Attribute into adding or removing two dots’ worth of Looks and Physical Flaws.</p>\n<p>The number of Looks Flaws is rounded up for the purposes of calculating the Attribute cost. Removing three dots of Looks Flaws costs a full dot as if removing four dots of Looks Flaw. When Uglifying, Looks and Physical Merits and are overwritten by certain Flaws that blank over them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until healed or altered again. See Characteristics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"202"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FRBpq5quYDDOz12B"},"FgfzYjaUzOdGiK7Z":{"name":"Feral Speech","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>This power allows the Cainite to hark back to the days before the fall of Babel, when all speech was one. Although the cannot use this ability to communicate with humans or Cainites who do not speak his language, the can communicate with animals, be they wild or domesticated. The vampire needs only to look into the creature’s eyes for a moment, and the briefly gain facility with that animal’s native speech. The two communicate in the animal’s tongue — a vampire speaking to a horse must whinny, one talking to a wolf might bark or howl and so on. (The animal replies in kind, of course.) According to rumor, the Nosferatu can communicate with animals without making a sound, but they must concentrate solely on the creature in question, and they cannot lose eye contact for even a moment.</p>\n<p>The use of Feral Speech does not predispose an animal to react in a friendly fashion toward the vampire. Keep in mind that animals that have not been made ghouls tend to react poorly to supernatural predators of the night. The character can bully small creatures such as squirrels or rabbits easily, but raptors and swift, independently minded animals do their best to escape from such a situation. Larger creatures require some negotiations to get any useful information.</p>\n<p>This ability does not grant the animal in question any unusual information. The Storyteller should consult the Appendix (page 210) to determine a rough level of intelligence for the animal, and use that and the creature’s known behaviors as a base for roleplay. Remember that few animals can count or read, and humans mostly all look alike to them. The character may be able to convince animals to do them favors if he is sufficiently impressive to them. If such is the case, the beasts do their best to perform such favours.</p>\n<p>Vampires can also use Feral Speech to summon a chosen type of animal (see Animalism limitations above), the application of which is considered its own power in Nosferatu tradition and called Noah’s Call, but the animals must be present in the region to answer. In order to use Noah’s Call on a particular kind of animal, the vampire must already have used Feral Speech on an animal of that species in the past. The Cainite then uses their sublimated memories of that “ language” to cry</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full action to summon, minor action of -2 dice to talk with animalism or command them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check to summon animals</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation/Charisma + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No expenditure or die roll is necessary to activate this power, but the character must spend one turn getting the animal’s attention and looking into its eyes in order to key their own Beast to the creature’s language.</p>\n<p>Simple communication requires no dice pool test. Persuading an animal to perform a service requires a Manipulation + Animal Ken roll; the Difficulty depends on the task required. Having a bird keep an eye out for anyone entering the park at night is Difficulty 3, while ordering any animal to defend a place with their lives is Difficulty 6. The Storyteller should reduce the Difficulty by 1 if the character promises to do something the animal would appreciate and reduce it by 2 if the character has already done that.</p>\n<p>Summoning animals uses a Charisma + Survival (Difficulty 0). Consult the table below to see how many creatures succumb to the character’s power. Note that if, for instance, only one animal meets the character’s criteria within range of hearing, then one or two successes will not summon it. The power works out to the limit of hearing — which can vary from beast to beast. A wolf might be able to hear a summons howl as far as four miles away, while a groundhog had better be within a half-mile. When in doubt, the Storyteller should roll Wits + Awareness for a sample member of the species. Every success extends the range by half a mile/one kilometer.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene Successes Response Total Failure/ A random fraction of the Botch creatures respond, but are hostile to the character 0 No response; you may try again One animal responds 1 2 One-quarter of the animals within range respond 3 Half the animals within range respond 4 Three-quarters of the animals within range respond 5+ All animals within range respond</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"15"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"i6HDhyu5":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"Uoxqx6A9":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FgfzYjaUzOdGiK7Z"},"FmAtvvBadZB0rLpV":{"name":"Unliving Hive","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Most often seen amongst the rare yet dreaded Baali, this unnerving power allows the user to extend their animal influence to swarms of insects such as flies or roaches. Certain vampires even go so far as to adopt swarms as famuli, giving them a permanent home within the folds and orifices of their malformed flesh.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Daimonion 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> No additional cost</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power extends all powers previously restricted to vertebrates to insect swarms treating a swarm as a single creature. The vampire can bind the swarm as a famulus, and some even give it the ability to nest inside the cavities of their body. This hides the swarm from sight while allowing it to nurse the minute amounts of Blood needed to sustain it indefinitely. While nested, the swarm is undetectable by anything less than X-rays.</p>\n<p>For swams and their statblocks, see p. XX. Swarms take Superficial damage from Brawl; flame and insecticide cause Aggravated damage. Vampires can use swarms for spying, as distractions (resulting in a two-dice penalty on any roll for a single swarmed individual), or to intimidate mortals (add between one and three dice to Intimidation pools, depending on the type of insect and the victim’s phobias). Players and Storytellers can doubtlessly come up with even more creative uses of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"19"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FmAtvvBadZB0rLpV"},"FmLseFL4XD6TRJAJ":{"name":"Shadow Servant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire gives independent life to a part of their shadow and can use it to spy on or unnerve their enemies.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> F The servant has no mind of its own, but follows its creator’s mental commands. The shadow travels at running speed and can effortlessly slip under doors, climb walls, or slip into or through the smallest cracks, although it cannot endure brightly lit areas. It can also cling to moving vehicles, and its range is limited only by how far it can travel in one night. It hears and sees everything in its vicinity and can convey the information as soon as it is reabsorbed into its creator’s shadow. The shadow servant can only be banished by bright light, such as from a powerful torch or daylight, but a successful Wits + Obtenebration roll against Difficulty 3 allows the servant to avoid the light for a turn.</p>\n<p>The shadow may also pick up objects no heavier than heavy medieval keys/car keys and manipulate complex objects at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene/night, whichever is shorter from the shadow’s point of view.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"111"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FmLseFL4XD6TRJAJ"},"Fmi1gCkpLwZIdstE":{"name":"Blessings of Set Typhon","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>One of the favourite of the Followers of Set who typically master only a single form with their high priests mastering all of them. The Blessing of the Set-Typhon is three-fold in the transformation is provides, yet each is learned separately. Some can turn into a massive snake, as is the norm of those who worship Apep-Typhon, many prefer the form of a Set-Typhonic beast which resembles a jackal and how Set is traditionally portrayed. Another form, one considered “warform” and “honour to the great serpent” is more mild – a humoid body with a snake tail for legs, often having cobra-like heads.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon transformation, the vampire gains the Physical Attributes, senses, and native Skills, specialties and abilities associated with that form (see next page) and also gains that form’s natural limits of communication, manipulation (most animal-like creatures can carry one thing in their mouth), and so forth. If the user has Skills or Attributes at a higher rating and wishes so, they may carry those over but it may also affect appearance of the form (an ibis with a greater muscle mass if the user has Strength 5, for example). Some forms have prerequisites listed under their name. Note that forms have listed Health for NPCs, but the user still calculates their Health as Stamina + 3 unless listed Health is higher.</p>\n<p>Free-action powers of Serpentis like Eyes of the Serpent, Cobra fangs, Tongue of the Asp and Serpent’s Flesh may be activated together with Blessings of Set Typhon, bypassing the limit of activating only a single Discipline in a turn. Rouse Check costs must be paid separately. While in Serpentis forms, the user suffers no Bite Attack Penalty, but the damage varies from form to form and may be modified by Serpentis powers unless stated otherwise. See Serpentis characteristics for more rules on forms.</p>\n<p>Changing shapes affects clothing, swallowed items, and other small (under a few grams) wearables. Serpentis does not affect larger carried items, including backpacks, duffel and sports bags. For this reason users of Protean often travel light.</p>\n<p>As a character’s shape changes, the use of certain Disciplines may become impossible if having humanoid features depends on them (such as Impostor’s Guise or Mask of a Thousand Faces while Dominate would require the animal to be capable of human-intelligible speech). When transfoming back from a shape with more Health, removed undamaged Health first. If the user is fallen while shapechanged, they revert back to their normal state in damage-induced torpor, even if they they now have undamaged Health. The vampire is still vulnerable to standard vampiric weaknesses regardless of their form. Most forms allow the user to easily move at twice the human speed. While general guidelines are provided, the final decisions in these matters rest with the Storyteller.</p>\n<p>Each time they choose a Serpentis form, such as a storm of sand or the Typhonic Beast, the character assumes the same specific form, which may be recognised by witnesses. Thus, if the character turns into a Typhonic Beast with certain markings or patterns of fur, it will always have them.</p>\n<p>When acquiring this power, the user picks one form and may purchase additional ones for 10 exp each.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire falls to sleep or torpor or ends the power BLESSINGS OF SET-TYHON FORMS</p>\n<p><strong>Avatar of Apep</strong></p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Apep’s Venom The vampire turns into a massive mythical cobra wreathed in darkness. Its minimum length is 1 meter/yard and maximum is equal to twice the user’s Serpentis rating. This form is greatly enjoyed by the priesthood of Apep and all those who walk the Path of Apep or some other chaos-philosophy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 6, Dexterity 5, Stamina 4, Charisma 1, Manipulation 2, Composure 2, Intelligence 1, Wits 3, Resolve 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 9, Willpower 5</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 4, Brawl 5 (Grapple), Survival 3, Awareness 5 (heat sense specialty), Intimidation 4, Stealth 4 (Ambush)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> This form possesses the equivalent of Shadow Cloak (Obtenebration 1) and receives all of its benefits including power bonus scaling. If Form of Apep successfully grapples an opponent, it may constrict its victim as a +2 heavy impact Brawl weapon. Its fangs as a +2 piercing Brawl weapon and Damage from its bite is never halved. This form innately possesses Apep’s Venom power.</p>\n<p><strong>Bastet’s Rage</strong></p>\n<p>The vampire gains features of a lion and grows fur over their body, usually also increasing in size. The form offers great strength at the expense of</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 7, Dexterity 5, Stamina 5, Charisma 1, Manipulation 2, Composure 2, Intelligence 1, Wits 3, Resolve 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 8, Willpower 7</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 3, Brawl 4 (Bite Attack, Claws), Survival 3, Awareness 4, Intimidation 5 (Scaring Away), Stealth 4 (Ambushes)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> The user possesses the equivalent of Feral Weapons (Protean 1). They embody the Hunger of Bastet and must make a Rouse Check each turn while they’re in this form. Failing a Rouse Check at Hunger 5 forces them to make a Hunger Frenzy Check at a Difficulty of 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Cerberus’ Wrath</strong></p>\n<p>The vampire takes the shape of dog with three heads the size of an elephant whose eyes glow red, easily visible to the denizens of the underworld.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 6, Dexterity 3, Stamina 5, Charisma 1, Manipulation 3, Composure 2, Intelligence 3, Wits 3, Resolve 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 9, Willpower 5</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 3 (Running), Brawl 4 (Bite attack), Survival 3, Intimidation 5, Awareness 3, Stealth 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> Cerberus form is able to see into the Shadowlands with perfect clarity. The glow of its eyes may be seen from the Shadowlands with ease, giving the form an additional die on Intimidation rolls against ghosts. Bite attacks in this form deal three points of Aggravated Health damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Form of Anubis</strong></p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Corpse in the Monster Path 4 The vampire turns into a monstrous, rotting jackal over seven feet from foot to shoulder blade whose saliva rots flesh and corrodes metal. This form is said to have been developed by Lazarus to combat assassins sent by his sire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 4, Dexterity 5, Stamina 5, Charisma 1, Manipulation 2, Composure 2, Intelligence 1, Wits 3, Resolve 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 8, Willpower 7</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 4 (Running), Brawl 4 (Bite Attack), Survival 3, Awareness 4, Intimidation 5 (Scaring Away), Stealth 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> In addition to standard damage, the rotting effect caused by the bite attack deals additional two points of Aggravated Health damage to living beings or two points of unhalved Superficial Health damage to undead beings like vampires. This additional damage cannot be mitigated by armour. The jackal may use its claws as a +2 piercing weapon that does unhalved Superficial damage to vampires and similar resilient beings. While in this form, effects of Corpse in the Monster Path the user possesses may be applied to the form at transformation at no additional cost or need for any roll.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydra</strong></p>\n<p>The vampire transforms into a dragon-like creature with multiple heads. As the myths say, its limbs are multiply when severed. It can be anywhere from the size of a bear to a two story house.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 6, Dexterity 4, Stamina 6, Charisma 1, Manipulation 1, Composure 2, Intelligence 2, Wits 2, Resolve 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 10, Willpower 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Aim 4 (Spitting, Exhaling), Athletics 3, Brawl 3 (Bite Attack), Survival 3, Intimidation 5, Awareness 3, Stealth 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> Hydra suffers no Physical Impairment. Whenever the character suffers a Crippling Injury, they immediately heal a point of Aggravated Health damage, ignore the Crippling Injury, and receive a dot to distribute to their Physical Attributes. Additionally, venom of the Hydra is deadly and comes in the form of either spittable venom or exhalable gas (Composure + Aim vs. Dexterity + Athletics). Its damage modifier is equal to the user’s Serpentis rating and does Aggravated damage equal to the margins to most beings, save for vampires and other durable creatures who take it as Superficial. This damage is never halved. Range of the attack is equal to twice the sum of the user’s Stamina and Potence.</p>\n<p><strong>Naga</strong></p>\n<p>More common among the eastern Setites and worshippers of Apep who are required to perform Akhu in religious ceremonies, the form is often referred to as the “war form” by the many Warriors of Glycon who adopt this transformation. In this form, the vampire’s legs combine and extend as they become a great tail of a serpent, often with unique colour patterns. Some transformation affect the body of the vampire entirely, giving them scales all over the body, elongated face and a hood of a cobra.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> The vampire may use any Discipline as they are still possess half of a human body to talk. The user gains a number of dots to distribute to Physical Disciplines equal to their Serpentis rating rounded up instead of acquiring any innate Attributes and Skills. Maximum rating for Physical Attributes is raised to 4 + the blood surge value of the user.</p>\n<p><strong>Satyr</strong></p>\n<p>Satyrs are horse-legged and horned companions of god Bacchus, the name under which Set is said to have been worshipped in Rome. Satyrs embody playfulness and revelry.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 3, Dexterity 7, Stamina 4, Charisma 1, Manipulation 2, Composure 2, Intelligence 1, Wits 3, Resolve 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 7, Willpower 7</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 5, Brawl 2, Survival 3, Awareness 4, Stealth 4 (Ambushes)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> Satyrs possess an aura of blissfulness that they may activate by spending a point of Willpower with a radius equal to the user’s Serpentis rating. Those who fail Composure + Resolve Test (Difficulty equal to the user’s Wits) become docile and do not attack unless attacked first. Given peace, victims will soon fall asleep.</p>\n<p><strong>Typhonic Beast</strong></p>\n<p>Appearing as a tall jackal with a hard, forked and spiked tail, with severe, pointed ears and a long snout. Typhonic Beasts are red, black, or a combination thereof. Once, a long time ago, these creatures existed as normal animals, but are said to now be extinct, with the last one dying with the antediluvian Brujah who had been keeping the last specimen alive with their time-warping powers. This form is the least unnerving application of this power for mortals who can hardly tell a normal jackal from this form – that is, if the jackals are abundant in the area. Otherwise, people might think it to be a weird wolf or even a dog.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 4, Dexterity 4, Stamina 3, Charisma 1, Manipulation 2, Composure 2, Intelligence 1, Wits 3, Resolve 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 7, Willpower 7</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 4 (Dodge), Brawl 4 (Bite Attack), Survival 3, Awareness 4, Intimidation 4, Stealth 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> Bite attacks deal three points of Aggravated Health damage. The beast also possesses claws that are a + 3 piercing Brawl weapon. Superficial damage from these claws is never halved</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"153"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Fmi1gCkpLwZIdstE"},"FsFxmBmcYL7BskD7":{"name":"Clear the Field","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Developed by adherents to the Path of Chivalry to clear a battlefield of weak foes, as they found no honor in killing them. In the modern era, it is used more often to clear a room of people to enjoy solitude.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Persuasion</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Charisma + Persuasion against Wits + Composure of those within a space equal to a large room around them. Against NPCs, roll against the pool of the highest mortal while vampires and players should always be given the opportunity to roll at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The effect lasts for a scene, but those under its spell might remain by spending a point of Willpower.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"122"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"6iFtbqmg":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"U6paZviu":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FsFxmBmcYL7BskD7"},"FwISAKsmcngxuzFh":{"name":"Transmogrify the Mortal Clay","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>While Malleable Visage is relatively inoffensive, Transmogrify the Mortal Clay is where Vicissitude’s real power takes shape as the user gains mastery over muscle and flesh at a deeper level. Now the Tzimisce may perform dramatic alterations, and not just upon themselves, but upon any subject they can subdue long enough to impart their vision. Tzimisce often use Transmogrify the Mortal Clay to craft monstrous servants, torture enemies, and bestow “gifts” upon those who please them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Malleable Visage</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 3, requiring physical contact if another person is the subject. If the subject is avoiding the user’s touch and the time required to perform an alteration is one turn or instant, alteration first requires a successful roll with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/ Brawl. For alterations requiring longer periods of time, unwilling targets must be restrained. Unwilling subjects resist using Stamina + Resolve. Their successes replaces the static Difficulty of 2.</p>\n<p>The following alterations are samples of what is available to Transmogrify the Mortal Clay:</p>\n<p>• Flense (Cost: Free; Time: Instant): The user rends flesh off the bones of their victim, dealing Aggravated damage to mortals equ•• al to the margin on the Resolve + Medicine roll and Superficial to more durable creatures such as vampires. Superficial damage from this attack is not halved.</p>\n<p>• Redistribution (Cost: One Physical Attribute dot per dot added; Time: 30 minutes per dot): Add a single dot to any of the Physical Attributes. This cannot cause any Attribute to exceed 5 dots nor can any single Attribute have its rating lower than 1 dot. These changes are visible but not Masquerade-threatening such as the user’s muscles growing and such. Players need to keep track of their original values as they are still used to calculate experience cost for Attributes. Remove undamaged health first when altering Stamina.</p>\n<p>• Malleable Visage: The user may perform any alteration of Malleable Visage onto their subject.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until healed or altered again. See Characteristics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"204"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.FwISAKsmcngxuzFh"},"GAcS0VdUN9uMVkzj":{"name":"Night Shades","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>This ability allows Cainites to craft lifelike images and illusions out of darkness that are nearly indistinguishable from reality. The created illusions are still shadows, and last only so long as your character concentrates on them. They can create silhouettes and shades, but are quickly rendered unbelievable in any strong lighting.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For each success, the character may create one image that the size of a human or smaller, or they may pool their successes to create one very large illusion.</p>\n<p>Characters who see these illusions must pass a Wits + Awareness (Difficulty equal to the user’s successes) to see through the illusion.</p>\n<p>Alternately, the caster may fill an area up to 10 meters per success with primordial visions of chaos, dark reflections of the Abyss and its denizens. This breach of the Abyss causes fear in all who behold it. Characters without Obtenebration have all dice pools reduced by two and their Initiative reduced by three unless they are able to see through the illusion.</p>\n<p>If the user possesses Solid Shadows, the shadows may now take actions, performing attacks with Strength rating equal to the user’s Obtenebration rating + 1 or +2 if at least 3 successes’ worth of shadows were grouped. Directing each of them is a minor action of -2 dice however. If the user has no more dice to roll, then they cannot do anything other than maintain concentration and dodge.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"112"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"kuONFfqm":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"Vr4A83oo":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.GAcS0VdUN9uMVkzj"},"GUCW5YycBLC8eQwV":{"name":"Tabula Rasa","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Less memory manipulation than eradication, this callous power does away with subtlety and wipes every trace of memory from a victim, leaving them a pliant effigy of themselves. While they retain most of their learned skills, they forget everything else – their experiences, friends, family, wants and wishes – in most cases, forever. Use of this power is rare for fear it induces in those that know of it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, extended</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user Rouses their Blood, holds the gaze of their victim, and must then engage in an extended conflict of Manipulation + Subterfuge versus the victim’s Composure + Resolve, accumulating ten margins during an entire scene. The process must not be disturbed, and the victim must be restrained or otherwise submit during the act, as any break in concentration requires the conflict to be restarted (and another two Rouse Checks), as does a critical win on the part of the victim.</p>\n<p>Once the process is complete, the victim is left a confused, slack-jawed shell. They lose memories of their name, history, and most of their old personality. They lose Ethics, Touchstones, and Ambition, and most of their Backgrounds become moot, as they forget how to access them. (These, together with other Advantages, are left to the Storyteller’s discretion – a Contact may remember them if they cross paths again, for example.) Attributes, Skills, and Disciplines remain, however. The memory loss is permanent, though on occasion a victim has regained some of their past when faced with a traumatic event involving a past Touchstone. (Test Resolve + Composure at Difficulty 5, for example when a former Touchstone is threatened, hurt, or killed while in their presence, at the Storyteller’s discretion.)</p>\n<p>A wiped victim is usually fed whatever lies are needed to keep them loyal, or quickly subjected to Path indoctrination. Use of this power is exceedingly cruel, and should merit Stains in most chronicles.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Indefinite</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"75"},"level":4,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"wfQoP2KF":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"DZOvaE8Z":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.GUCW5YycBLC8eQwV"},"GcM4sNlAQ4ZNmdue":{"name":"Friends List","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula allows the alchemist to see the connections between mortals and Kindred. This shows as a thin, silver thread that connects between them, giving the alchemist an idea of whom a mortal may have a connection to.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, phlegmatic blood, the eyes of a whippoorwill or owl or other nocturnal bird, and a silver thread.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activating this power, the alchemist makes an Intelligence + Alchemy test to see a thin, silver thread connecting a mortal to a vampire they have some sort of connection with. (Obviously, no thread is shown if the mortal isn’t connected to Kindred.) If the vampire is not physically close the alchemist can follow the thread.</p>\n<p>Stronger connections require fewer successes to see – a ghoul might only require one success, but a distant contact or ally up to five. A mortal with multiple vampire relationships displays the strongest thread first, but the alchemist can see all of them with enough successes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"104"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"ZhNkWflR":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"HoKrkQIs":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.GcM4sNlAQ4ZNmdue"},"Gf3QTHXnk8SbVSmc":{"name":"Discipline Channeling","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Counterfeiting Disciplines is part and parcel of Thin-Blood Alchemy’s use, but the ability to channel a Discipline from the ashes of a deceased vampire is a form of necromantic Alchemy, at least according to Dr. Mortius and the Duskborn who practice it. Those who practice this Alchemy find themselves able to use any one of the destroyed vampire’s Discipline powers, even if they typically wouldn’t have access to powers of this level.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Concoct Ashe</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Crushed leaves from the indigo plant.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> The same as the channeled power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> The same as the channeled power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When using Concoct Ashe, this formula may be used to imbue remains with the abilities of the vampire they once formed. When consumed, this altered Ashe provokes a Willpower test at a Difficulty of the destroyed vampire’s highest Discipline rating. On a win, the thin-blood gains the use of a single Discipline power that the destroyed vampire possessed, until the end of the chapter. Use the destroyed vampire’s Discipline rating whenever a channeled power calls for one. The Storyteller may state the Disciplines and specific powers the deceased vampire held so the alchemist can choose from a list, or if the alchemist has a power in mind they can request it from the Storyteller. The Storyteller has final say. On a critical win the thin-blood gains the use of two Discipline powers that the destroyed vampire possessed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Cults of the Blood Gods","page":"47"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Gf3QTHXnk8SbVSmc"},"GqqEmEhOXOfowkPV":{"name":"Counterfeit (Level 3 Power)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>At level 4, the Alchemist can develop formulae that counterfeit <strong>level 3</strong> powers of other Disciplines (and Blood Sorcery powers that affect the caster’s own Blood, but never Rituals).</p><p>■ <strong>Research:</strong> at least three months per formula. <strong>Distillation:</strong> at least three nights.</p><p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> From level 4 on, every counterfeit formula also needs a drop of vitae from a vampire of a clan that has the Discipline in-clan, or from one who possesses it. The donation costs the donor no Health and creates no Blood Bond.</p><p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Use the counterfeited power’s own cost and system, substituting the Alchemist’s Thin-Blood Alchemy rating for the Discipline rating wherever one is called for. Rename this item after the power you counterfeit and paste in its dice pool.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"287"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.GqqEmEhOXOfowkPV"},"GxjANpV7XVFgIHt2":{"name":"Sight of the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The sorcerers of Haqim were made with a purpose and entrusted by Haqim to keep watch over the spirits close to the material plane and hunt for demons infesting it. Sight of Blood is a key part of that kit, allowing its user to see the spirits around them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free, blood required is neglible</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user paints over their eyes with blood and rolls Wits + Awareness at a difficulty of 2. On a win, they are able to see nearby spirits. Ghosts are not proper spirits by definition as they have an ectoplasmic corpus. Demons and jinn count as spirits, but it will not detect demons who are possessing a person as it makes them corporeal and visible anyways.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"145"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"8aGEm9o5":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"41UW556c":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.GxjANpV7XVFgIHt2"},"HDWkrRD98sS79jL3":{"name":"Dream Combat","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>Warrior Salubri use Dream Combat to confront ghosts, demons and other spirits that they cannot affect physically. Increasingly, they find that it allows them to confront elders and powerful entities on a more equal playing field. The few Healers and Watchers who’ve learned Warrior Valeren also find the power extremely useful, as their less martial nature matters little in the dreamscape. This power alone is the reason why many refuse to look a Salubri in their third eye, as it is that very same eye which pulls them into this dream combat. Some Practical vampires can use this power to hold long and secretive conversations in the middle of meetings much like telepathy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user must first lock eyes with their enemy with their third eye uncovered (see Dominate system for locking eyes in combat) for this power to work and roll Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve. On a win, the target is pulled into the dream world and may not leave until the user of this power has been defeated or ended this power by their own will. Time is accelerated within the dreamscape, and the entire battle lasts a turn outside and the bodies of the two within the dream do not give any indication of something unusual happening.</p>\n<p>In the dreamscape, both participants can do anything they imagine. They can fly, change their shape, and create any environment they want. Affecting each other is more difficult. Combatants can battle in any manner, but it’s the will doing battle, not the body as characters replace their Health tracker with that of their Willpower tracker. The characters may also replace their Strength with Intelligence, Dexterity with Wits and Stamina with Resolve. Disciplines and other supernatural powers do not work within this dreamscape except for that of Auspex, Dominate and Valeren as the battle takes place outside the physical plain of existence and its laws. Demons without a human host cannot be dragged into the dream world.</p>\n<p>A character reduced to Willpower 0 is incapacitated; mortals become comatose, while vampires drop into torpor. Spirits, ghosts or demons reduced to Willpower 0 may be “diablerised” within the dreamscape upon defeat; this destroys them, and while such an act doesn’t affect the Salubri’s Generation, it may allow the vampire to purchase unique Supernatural Merits at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>It should be noted that this power pulls both targets to the dream realm, but it is not an actual dream of a sentient being such as the one entered through the Path of Morpheus.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until one character is defeated or the user ends this power before that.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"194"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.HDWkrRD98sS79jL3"},"HFhOzbqkUYOT97iX":{"name":"Siren’s Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>By singing or reciting the most beautiful melodies and verses those around them ever heard in their life or unlife, the vampire effectively shields themselves from harm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire begins singing, reciting verses or chanting as they roll Charisma + Performance. The line of sight is not necessary for this power. All who hear the vampire and intend to be hostile against them must make a Composure + Wits roll against the successes rolled by the user of the power. On a failure, they are lulled into a peaceful and benign state and may not act in any hostile manner (but they may still roll purely defensive actions) towards anybody without spending a point of Willpower. Those of the lower Generation than the user only have to spend a single point of Willpower to break the effect.</p>\n<p>Maintaining this aura of calmness and performing other actions is difficult. To keep reciting or singing is a minor action of -3 dice, but it is not possible to perform violent actions and maintain the song of calmness.</p>\n<p>If a person scored at least one success more on their Composure + Wits roll than the user of the power or broke free from the effects of this power by some other means, they are immune to this power from the same user for the rest of the scene or night on a critical win.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As per system.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"88"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hW6n5E4C":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"Nu6oFlVw":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.HFhOzbqkUYOT97iX"},"HeI6Jx7RQxpFd40s":{"name":"Taste of the Body","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By sipping blood, those capable of listening to the Blood are able to find out more about the constitution, muscle density, the quality of their blood and probably even estimate what kind of food a mortal eats.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user gains a die bonus to all Physical Skill-based rolls against a person whose blood they tasted (even a bite attack provides enough of a sample). The user does not benefit from this if they aren’t aware that the blood came from the person, meaning that masked identities can negate the benefit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"139"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.HeI6Jx7RQxpFd40s"},"HkbiHC7F3uUhIiAu":{"name":"Patience of the Norns","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire may now either expand her power’s circumference to engulf multiple targets at once or localize it on a single focused point to freeze an object in place. This utilitarian power provides both combat and non-combat benefits, such as transforming an attacker into a statue mid-swing or preserving precious documents without risking them to the ravages of age.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Leaden Movement</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character may now use Leaden Movement on as many enemies within their line of sight as they have dots in Wits + Temporis. Additionally, by spending a point of Willpower when activating the power, the user may completely freeze a person or an object in a temporal stasis on a win for the rest of the power’s duration or until the subject takes damage (note that as they’re immobilised all attacks are made at a Difficulty of 0). Thereafter, if anything carrying more kinetic force than a drop of rain comes in contact with the target, it re-enters time at the velocity at which it was stopped. Objects that are inanimate prior to application of this power may be suspended indefinitely. This does not include living or unliving targets at rest or targets currently under the influence of this power.</p>\n<p>Users with this power affect more than perceptions and minds and powers such as Unswayable Mind no longer aid the victims. Characters with Celerity may still negate the power’s influence, but only if their Celerity rating is greater than the user’s Temporis rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"171"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.HkbiHC7F3uUhIiAu"},"Hy1LpvVrBFUrNDXq":{"name":"Hold the Power","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>Not only can the vampire steal experience of mundane tasks earned over years of practice but they can even copy powers of the Blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Mind Steal</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Hold the Power may only on those who possess vampiric Disciplines, including ghouls and revenants, and for all intents and purposes shares the system of Mind Steal except that on a win instead of Skills the user removes a number of Discipline dots equal to the margin from the target and adds them to their own, up to the victim’s own ratings. Like with Mind Steal, the victim’s memories may be kept if the victim dies before duration runs out, but Discipline dots are lost, although they may learn dead victim’s Disciplines without the need for a teacher or drinking the blood of the one who possesses the Discipline. Sorcery the victim possessed may be learned instantly with experience points without the need for learning projects.</p>\n<p>Example: Antonio, a Kiasyd with Dominate 1, uses Hold the Power against a Ventrue with Dominate 3 and wins the contest with the margin of 0 as the actor wins on a tie (as if they rolled a margin of 1). With that 1 margin, they remove a dot of the Ventrue’s Dominate, bringing it to 2, and raise their own to Dominate 2.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Same table as Mind Steal (p. 71).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"94"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"ZJHDU4CJ":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSx11dyu":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Hy1LpvVrBFUrNDXq"},"I7EZ1YU0Kci6wi8X":{"name":"Gift of Ra","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ra is, technically, the grandfather of all Children of Osiris. The Sun god bestows his favour upon even those corrupted by Typhon and still elected to follow his son and heir, granting them the ability to be unharmed by fire, preparing them for their eventual return to his loving embrace during the day. day.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Osiris</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user is fully immune to fire and all frenzies induced by the fire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"41"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"EEM3meuN":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.I7EZ1YU0Kci6wi8X"},"I8LO2Pxwubl9oZ8p":{"name":"Elevate","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>When you’re at the party and feeling like a thrill, go find the guy by the bathrooms. No, not the guy selling speed and molly to sweaty dancers. The <em>other</em> guy, the one that stands way too still. He’s got the regular stuff, but he’s also got tabs of El if you know to ask. El makes the whole world feel <em>elevated</em> to thin-bloods: colors, sounds, tastes, and energy all shift and intensify. It’s a pure shot of dopamine that makes everything faster, fresher, and way more fun.</p>\n<p>It’s not just in your mind, either: your reflexes really <em>are</em> faster, your eyes <em>do</em> see farther, your feet move quick and sure to the beat. Thin-bloods who’ve taken El can be identified by the slightly glassy tinge to their eyes and the bubbly energy that lets them dance until dawn. Regular Kindred can get the effect by drinking from someone who’s taken El, just like any other drug.</p>\n<p>While Humanity 8 or higher vampires with Blush of Life can get high without recourse to alchemy, the fact that thin-bloods have developed a workaround for this aspect of the vampire curse angers older Kindred. In this, as in daywalking and so much else, the thin blood of the Mercurians carries potential advantages.</p>\n<p>If players go looking for sangrias or party brews, someone almost always has El for sale.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, sanguine mortal blood, fermented chocolate, rice vinegar, black coffee, often some kind of fun street drug.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whoever consumes the formula instantly feels a rush of warmth and pleasure flowing through them, relaxing their muscles and quickening their nerves. They get a +1 on any Dexterity dice that they roll during the scene. El also can act as a carrier for regular street drugs, although the effects shift unpredictably if usually toward ecstatic stimulation. Badly made El makes the user feel great, but actively degrades Dexterity with a −1 penalty.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"74"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"JjgtGoD6":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"LgVzfrTR":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.I8LO2Pxwubl9oZ8p"},"ID99ksE4FOsibaPz":{"name":"The Spirit Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Conceding one’s body to a spirit is rarely a good option, unless you are a master of Spiritus. By letting the spirit take the proverbial wheel with limited control, the vampire is able to resist the urge to fall asleep and becomes resistant to the Sun. The vampire fully merges with a spirit animal of her choice, effectively creating a new being.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> The Wild Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When using the Wild Beast, the user is also immune to sunlight and resists the urge to sleep. Additionally, by spending a point of Willpower and waiting for 3 turns (full actions), they are able to plunge themselves into the wild plane of the Umbra where the spirits reside. Transformation may be sped up, reducing turns required by the number of additional Willpower spent to a minimum of one turn. While time and space are warped there, it can be used to ambush targets as the material world is reflected in the spirit world. To identify the traces of a specific person, the user must succeed at a roll of Resolve + Investigation at a difficulty of 3. The user may exit the Umbra whenver they wish. Whatever they see there is at the Storyteller’s discretion. Werewolf the Ascension’s description of the Umbra is a good pointer.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"165"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ID99ksE4FOsibaPz"},"IDT1mOd7gwaDKFHv":{"name":"Cloud Memory","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>By uttering the phrase “Forget!” the user can make the Dominated victim forget the current moment as well as the last few minutes, enough to mask a superficial feeding or a chance meeting. No new memories are formed and if pressed the victim realizes they have a few minutes missing.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Wits + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Clouding the memory of a resisting victim or another vampire requires standard Dominate requirements, the verb “Forget!” and Manipulation + Dominate vs Wits + Resolve roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Indefinitely</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"71"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"hMX51z2f":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"xqLPwWes":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.IDT1mOd7gwaDKFHv"},"IKasYNCtj0yxikzP":{"name":"Ishtar’s Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vizier version of Scorpion’s Touch is Ishtar’s Touch. Instead of yielding a deadly poison, this power converts the vizier’s blood into a touch-activated narcotic that puts the victim into an inebriated state under which he is more vulnerable to Presence, Dominate, and even mundane Social manipulation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One or more Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Intelligence + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This contact poison has the same system as Scorpion’s Touch (p. 122) and is applied the same way).</p>\n<p>If this poison makes contact with another, they receive a dice penalty or a dice bonus (as determined by the user when creating the poison) equal to [half of the user’s Quietus Animae rating rounded up] on contests involving somebody using a Social Skill against them or a power that affects the mind (powers such as Serpentis’ Eyes of the Serpent, Obfuscate, Mytherceria, Quietus, some Magics, Presence, Dominate and so on). The effect can only be noticed by powers that read auras such as Scry the Soul. Supernatural beings may attempt to roll Intelligence + Occult vs. the user’s Composure + Resolve to resist the effect.</p>\n<p>This bonus may stack with that of Internal Awareness.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The poison remains potent for nights equal to the user’s Quietus rating</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"135"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"mj0MF7jW":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"2rer7E0o":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.IKasYNCtj0yxikzP"},"IPEqwHDcIjpNZ6YM":{"name":"Counterfeit (Level 4 Power)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>At level 5, the Alchemist can develop formulae that counterfeit <strong>level 4</strong> powers of other Disciplines (and Blood Sorcery powers that affect the caster’s own Blood, but never Rituals).</p><p>■ <strong>Research:</strong> at least three years per formula. <strong>Distillation:</strong> a month.</p><p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> From level 4 on, every counterfeit formula also needs a drop of vitae from a vampire of a clan that has the Discipline in-clan, or from one who possesses it. The donation costs the donor no Health and creates no Blood Bond.</p><p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Use the counterfeited power’s own cost and system, substituting the Alchemist’s Thin-Blood Alchemy rating for the Discipline rating wherever one is called for. Rename this item after the power you counterfeit and paste in its dice pool.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"288"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.IPEqwHDcIjpNZ6YM"},"ISbZGoHpY6drYcdq":{"name":"Lingering Kiss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Kiss of a vampire induces near-ecstasy in the victim, but this power leaves other Kisses in the dust. Victims fed upon by the user are temporarily empowered, but also become addicted to the Kiss, obsessing over it and even seeking the vampire out for repeated feedings. Mortals often become anemic, self-harm, or even die from this addiction. Addicting other vampires is also possible, with the caveat that obsessed vampires demanding to be fed from risk the user becoming Blood Bound. Once under a Blood Bond, a vampire becomes unable to use this power on other vampires, being limited to employing it on mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can choose to use this power during each feeding where they sate at least one Hunger. The victim gains a bonus equal to half the user’s Presence (round up) to one Social Attribute of their choice. The maximum rating of an Attribute is raised to 4 + Blood Surge value of the user. This benefit lasts for a single night. Once it fades, withdrawal strikes and all the victim’s actions not spent working toward acquiring a new fix from the user (or somebodey else with this power) receive a dice penalty equal to the initial bonus. A victim in withdrawal can spend Willpower to ignore the penalties for a scene, and the penalties go away after a number of nights equal to the user’s Presence rating.</p>\n<p>Vampires who are Blood Bound to somebody other than the user or with the Unbondable Merit cannot use this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A number of nights equal to the user’s Presence rating.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"121"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ISbZGoHpY6drYcdq"},"IUGLWyrcnixmSAm5":{"name":"Sense the Unseen","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>The senses of the vampire become attuned to dimensions beyond the mundane, allowing them to sense presences otherwise hidden from the naked eye. This can be anything from another vampire using Obfuscate to someone using Auspex to spy upon the character to a ghost in the middle of the room. Dormant Blood Sorcery spells and rituals might also be found with this power, at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits/Resolve + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever there’s something supernatural hiding in plain sight, the Storyteller makes a hidden roll of Wits + Awareness against a Difficulty they choose. Against an entity actively trying to stay hidden, the Storyteller can call for a blind roll (“Lisa, roll seven dice for me”) as a contest against the target’s relevant pool. (For example, detecting a vampire using Obfuscate would be a roll of Wits + Awareness vs Wits + Obfuscate) If the vampire actively searches for a hidden supernatural entity, they roll Resolve + Awareness similarly.</p>\n<p>This power does not spot creatures that are hiding through mundane means. For example, separate rolls would be required to detect somebody that is masking themselves with Obfuscate and also physically hiding, one roll against Obfuscate, one against Stealth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"25"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"bvSubVFh":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"WYQJwcgF":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.IUGLWyrcnixmSAm5"},"IaLISYuVP57uNBkO":{"name":"Checkout Time","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Thin-bloods traveling great distances face challenges. Being shipped as a literal corpse can, for the clever, mitigate them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, phlegmatic mortal blood, embalming fluid, myrrh, a toad’s skull.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist writes a span of times and dates on the subject’s left arm in Roman numerals. The subject can be the alchemist themself. At the starting time, the subject enters a deep torpor and cannot be awakened until either the ending time transpires, or someone rewrites the ending time. Coming out of this slumber does not require a Rouse Check. While in this state, the recipient is indistinguishable from a corpse. They have no aura, they don’t need to make nightly Rouse Checks, and they don’t take damage from any accidental exposure to sunlight or similar banes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The written time span determines the duration, but cannot exceed nine nights.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"74"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.IaLISYuVP57uNBkO"},"IwSkIcOHIClA2S0e":{"name":"Ease Wounds","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>While yet lacking the power to outright heal by the powers of Valeren, the vampire may at least ease the wounds of another.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> At the cost of a Rouse Check, the user may touch their target and roll Intelligence + Medicine (Difficulty 2) to instantly convert a number of Aggravated Health damage to Superficial equal to the margins. The user may target themselves with this power. When healing undead, the user halves the number of margins and rounds down. A target may be affected by this power only once per night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"183"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"HgpmSYWm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"aDkwrMKi":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.IwSkIcOHIClA2S0e"},"JGwXiK892IB5CyYb":{"name":"Fire Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>By mentioning one of many truths the clan Malkavian knows, the user of Dementation may shatter their victim’s psyche and force them to cover in fear.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Insight against Composure + Wits of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 52). On a win, the target flees in terror while vampire fall into a Terror Frenzy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant/per turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"67"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.JGwXiK892IB5CyYb"},"JHKI1t6jeIbcz20j":{"name":"Scry the Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>By focusing on a person, the vampire can perceive the state of that person’s psyche as a shifting aur a of colors. Auras reveal little precise information, but do provide clues regarding many subjects, e.g., emotional state, Resonance, and supernatural traits. If looking for a specific condition, the vampire can cursorily scan the crowd to detect it. Such cursory scans provide no further information.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs Composure + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user focuses on a target within their line of sight and rolls Intelligence + Insight vs. Composure + Subterfuge of the target. A win allows the user to ask the storyteller or the targeted player a number of questions equal to the margin.</p>\n<p>The questions can include:</p>\n<p>• What is the emotional and mental state of the subject?</p>\n<p>• What type of a creature is the subject?</p>\n<p>• Has the subject committed diablerie?</p>\n<p>• What is the Resonance of the subject’s blood?</p>\n<p>• Is the subject under the influence of non-physical Magic effects or other powers such as Disciplines like Dominate?</p>\n<p>Consult the chart on the next page for how one perceives it and what possible questions could be.</p>\n<p>The ability to Read the Soul does not act as a lie-detector, and absolute truths cannot be determined. It just reveals moments of dishonesty, or flooded feelings of guilt. An exceptional liar may be free of guilt, and a penitent but innocent person may always appear on the edge of suspicion. A talented Auspex user knows this and will keep it in mind when interpreting what they perceive. A target seeking to lie to the user must win on a Composure + Subterfuge roll against a difficulty of 3 otherwise their aura flickers due to them being too nervous.</p>\n<p>If scanning a crowd or the entire room, first roll against a Difficulty determined by the size of the crowd and external distractions, as well as the type of trait being sought. (Finding the vampire in the living room might only be a Difficulty 3, while</p>\n<p><strong>Soul Colours and their Interpretation</strong></p>\n<p>Shade Condition Orange Afraid or anxious Purple Aggressive Red Angry Brown Bitter Light Blue Calm Pink Compassionate Lavender Conservative Gray Depressed Deep Red Desirous or Lustful Light Green Distrustful Dark Green Envious Violet Excited Rose Generous Vermilion Happy Black Hateful Yellow Idealistic White Clear of Conscience Blue Lovestruck Green Obsessed Silver Sad Gold Taken by Ecstasies Dark Blue Suspicious Mottled, shifting colors Confused Black veins in the soul Diablerist Rapidly rippling colors Frenzied Aura is pale Vampire Pale blotches in the Ghoul aura Myriad sparkles in aura Hedge wizard or witch Denser myriad spark Mage Bright, vibrant aura Werebeast Weak, intermittent Ghost aura Rainbow highlights Fae A crown of flame Infernal creature or malign spirit (demon, bane or laham) Dark fog around the Target is under a curse soul Grey fog around the Target is under a power soul finding the most nervous person in a crowded festival is most likely Difficulty 6, which is a maximum one). After that, the target rolls Composure + Subterfuge as normal.</p>\n<p>The table above consists of colours and their suggested interpretations. Interpretations through other senses are also possible. Players and Storytellers should be encouraged to create charts for their characters, unique in its sensory details (for example, visions, voices, smells...).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until something in the aura changes, until the user lets the power lapse or rest of the scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"30"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"IiXYq593":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"vSsLf3bs":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.JHKI1t6jeIbcz20j"},"JjO22BjUnJV9mrxK":{"name":"Taste of the Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By sipping blood, those capable of listening to the Blood are able to find out more about the mental constitution, the ardour and the way the person acts or behaves, giving them an advantage in manipulating them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user gains a die bonus to all Social Skill-based rolls against a person whose blood they tasted (even a bite attack provides enough of a sample). The user does not benefit from this if they aren’t aware that the blood came from the person, meaning that masked identities can negate the benefit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"135"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.JjO22BjUnJV9mrxK"},"Jv6YdKvBeTVKklF9":{"name":"Umbrous Clutch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user creates a temporary gate out of the victim’s own shadow, causing them to fall through Oblivion and into the waiting arms of the user. The victim will appear to fall into their own shadow, only to reappear falling out of the one cast by the user. The brief journey through the end of everything is usually enough to traumatize the victim into submission, though one must be prepared if the quarry responds violently in fear.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Obtenebration vs. Dexterity + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> In order to use this power, the vampire needs clear sight of both the victim and their shadow. The vampire’s player or Storyteller then rolls Wits + Obtenebration vs. Dexterity + Wits to maneuver the victim’s shadow beneath them, creating a supernatural rift into which they appear to fall. At the Storyteller’s discretion, a stationary victim not paying attention can be automatically caught.</p>\n<p>A victim caught by this power will then reappear, falling out of the shadow cast by the user. (As with similar powers, the power does not work if the caster is without a shadow.) An unprepared mortal victim will be terrified and likely catatonic, while a vampire must test for fury or fear frenzy, Storyteller’s choice, at Difficulty 4.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"113"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DNKH4jst":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"vooebfRh":{"path":"disciplines.obtenebration"}},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Jv6YdKvBeTVKklF9"},"KSaHsktrYGUkOkoz":{"name":"Vicissitude Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s ability to command flesh becomes so great that they no longer require physical touch to initiate powers of Vicissitude.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character may now use Shape Flesh (and its improvements) without requiring physical contact or retainment of the subject (those who fail resistance roll are in such a shock that only outside intervention may stop the process). Physical hand guestures are still required to perform “surgery” which onlookers may detect with Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge.</p>\n<p>The vampire can amplify Vicissitude powers to affect a group of people, mortal or vampire, at once by paying additional Rouse Check. The user makes any needed roll against the strongest opponent in the group while players always roll separately.</p>\n<p>Additionally, the user counts their Vicissitude rating as as one higher for all intents and purposes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until healed or altered again. See Characteristics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"210"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.KSaHsktrYGUkOkoz"},"KSaWajfJSHCx77qW":{"name":"Raptorous Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Using fangs to drink is sometimes to inefficient for those grappling or striking their enemies. Raptorous Touch allows its user to drink blood through skin contact alone.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> System: The vampire must first touch the target (any combination of Dexterity/Strength and Athletics/Brawl would suffice) against the target’s def-nsive roll (usually a dodge). This roll cannot deal damage due to the necessary control required to use this power. If the target has little exposed skin, the Storyteller should demand a called shot (ranging 1 to a normal person with short sleeves and pants to 2 for a person with only face and hands exposed).</p>\n<p>After that, the user activates the power and automatically succeeds on feeding actions up to the user’s Quietus rating against the target (see harmful feeding against mortals). The feeding may be normal or harmful, depending on the user’s intent and may apply the kiss. Note that once activated, the user cannot choose when to use it or not to use it and it has happened before that a person shakes hands with somebody only to cause internal bleeding.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene unless ended sooner</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"142"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.KSaWajfJSHCx77qW"},"Kh9A9Lx1ZjgpABax":{"name":"Silence of Death","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>A gift of Haqim to his children born without the ability to mask themselves, Silence of Death offers a shroud of silence to hide the sounds made by the user and their close allies, perfect for ambushes or simply walking around unheard.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s blood begins to vibrate at a frequency that disrupts hearing and silences the footsteps, clothing, minor collisions, and other sounds in a 6-meter radius, giving themselves and others a two-dice bonus on all Stealth rolls. The bonus does not stack with other means of silencing oneself and one’s area. This makes the vampire undetectable if an observer could only notice them by sound, such as when on a different floor of a house. This power does not eliminate sounds the user makes outside the radius. Sense of the Unseen (Auspex 1) and similar powers allow their users to pierce the veil of silence by rolling against the user’s Wits + Stealth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"140"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Kh9A9Lx1ZjgpABax"},"KhXCOV3RCmk0Eko3":{"name":"Beast Mode","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Every Duskborn knows that their vampiric powers are weaker than those of a true Kindred. This formula allows a thin-blooded vampire to temporarily gain the physical acumen of a far older vampire, assuming they can gain access to that vampire’s Blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s vitae, choleric or sanguine human blood, vitae of Blood Potency 4+, phenylcyclohexyl piperidine, adrenaline injector, spoiled two percent milk, sodium bicarbonate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Beast Mode imbues the alchemist with access to a single level 5 power from the Disciplines of Celerity, Fortitude, or Potence, counting that Discipline rating as 5 for the duration of the formula. When consumed, the alchemist must succeed on a Willpower test (Difficulty 2) to avoid frenzy. The Kindred whose vitae was used in the distillation must possess the power’s associated Discipline at level 5 or the brew fails.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"51"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.KhXCOV3RCmk0Eko3"},"Ki5XXiYUFjtmQqL4":{"name":"Share the Senses","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>By reaching out with their mind, the vampire can tap into the senses of another mortal or vampire, seeing, hearing, and feeling what they do. The user still retains their own perceptions and is still aware of their own surroundings, though the effect requires some getting used to. The user decides whether to tap into only one, some, or all of the target’s senses. When used on a stranger this power requires line of sight to initiate. However, it can be used over longer distances on someone who still has some of the user’s Blood in their body.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Awareness vs Composure + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Resolve + Awareness at Difficulty 3. This Difficulty can go up depending on distraction, distance, and other factors, such as the amount of the user’s Blood that remains in the target. The target usually remains unaware of the intrusion, but Sense the Unseen can allow the passenger to be noticed. To get rid of an unwanted rider, the victim must beat the intruder at a Wits + Resolve vs Wits + Resolve roll. An Auspex user thrown out this way cannot make another Sharing attempt until the next night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, although it can be extended by an additional scene by spending a point of Willpower</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"32"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"uhfc1u3S":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"yMpJ3ppd":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Ki5XXiYUFjtmQqL4"},"Koj8ktHEpXGkemXV":{"name":"Pain for Pleasure","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>Rewiring mental perceptions, the target begins to experience pain as suiting pleasure. This is often used to soothe the passing of those dying in agony caused by blades of sickness. Due to a form of masochism evoked by this power, the Salubri very rarely speak of it in front of others as the concept can be quite unnerving to those not able to understand it. When activating this power, the user’s third eye shines in a bright red colour.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire may either use this power on themselves or touch another to use it, rolling Intelligence + Medicine against a Difficulty of 3. On a win, they heal a point of Aggravated Willpower or two points of Superficial Willpower damage for each point of Aggravated Health damage they suffer or a point of Superficial Willpower damage for each point of Superficial Health damage they suffer. This is not retroactive, however, and previous damage does nothing. The target also negates the effects of physical impairment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene with a Critical Win (or 3+ margins) extending it to the rest of the night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"187"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"HgpmSYWm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"aDkwrMKi":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Koj8ktHEpXGkemXV"},"LKDsKQsztzVLlaS9":{"name":"Internal Awareness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires learning Quietus Animae very quickly learn that blood is tied to everything in the body, from humours to the very soul that is extinguished through diablerie. Internal Awareness compels the vitae of the user to passively bolster the mental defenses of the user and even to protect the user’s soul from being consumed. Some claim that this power enhances the soul, but others are not so quick to answer if a vampire has a soul or not.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Every time an effect of a mind-altering power (Presence, Dominate and other direct mind afflicting magics) would affect the user without a contest, the user’s blood compels them to resist with Composure + Occult. This roll is not further enhanced by other powers (such as Unswayable Mind) as this is the internal mechanism acting, not the user themselves. A Critical Win or at least 3 margins has the vampire’s own blood rouse awake, letting the user realise that somebody attempted to influence their mind with a supernatural power. The user may also use Internal Awareness instead of normal rolls on standard contests against mind-altering powers such as Dementation.</p>\n<p>The vampire also gains a dice bonus equal to half of their Quietus rating to take possession of their diablerist.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"134"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"d1kwRUxj":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"UxNXQWnu":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LKDsKQsztzVLlaS9"},"LWGBbyB1vKiCfQSD":{"name":"A Thousand Cuts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>While the powers of Celerity do not lend themselves to the application of force, the vampire’s ungodly speed allows them to land a blinding amount of cuts and slashes. The victim appears as if their body spontaneously erupts in hundreds of wounds, each seemingly insignificant in its own right but excruciating in their increasing volume.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire may add their Celerity rating to any single Brawl or Melee attack (not otherwise augmented by a Discipline) made with claws or an edged weapon. For mortal victims, the cuts and slices caused by this flurry of attacks only cause Superficial damage, but automatically inflict enough additional Superficial damage for the victim to be Impaired. (At the Storyteller’s discretion, victims are overcome with pain and effectively out of commission.) The attack also splashes blood far and wide, causing vampires at Hunger 3 or more to make Hunger Frenzy tests. Used against Cainites, the attack is relatively harmless and causes no damage, but the scores of vicious yet unbleeding wounds leave the victim a walking Masquerade violation, and might provoke a Fury Frenzy test at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One action</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"44"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LWGBbyB1vKiCfQSD"},"LXXW8moPTmjGSKxa":{"name":"Blood Awakening","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By ingesting blood, the user is able to further break it down into memories, experiences and opinions, perfect for knowing who to call into plots and who to have executed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Read the Blood’s Desire</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user may also select the following effects when using Read the Blood’s Desire:</p>\n<p>• Ambition: The vampire learns of the subject’s Ambition.</p>\n<p>• Learn First hand: The vampire numbers one Skill for each margin placed into this effect. For the rest of the night, they acquire the subject’s Specialties in that Skill and set its ratings to theirs if the subject possessed it at a higher rating.</p>\n<p>• Read the Messages of Mind: The vampire asks a question regarding the subject’s opinion on something and learns it. This effect may be selected as many times as the user is able.</p>\n<p>• Walking Down the Memory Lane: Quickly live through a scene from the subject’s past. The user may attempt to specify the scene they wish to view in as many details as possible to locate the exact memory they were looking for. Otherwise, the user is given a random memory. This effect may be selected as many times as the user is able.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"137"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LXXW8moPTmjGSKxa"},"LjvUf9yp4f6R6Fnn":{"name":"Strike the Slow","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user pushes themselves through a crack in time, effectively slowing the world around themselves from their perspective, to make an attack against what is essentially a stationary target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Brawl or a Melee attack against their opponent at a static Difficulty of 1 due to the target appearing frozen.</p>\n<p>This power may cancel the Lightning Strike and vice versa as the two users collide – one speeding while the other slows.</p>\n<p>An opponent possessing Celerity 5 Celerity or Temporis can nullify this power by making their own Rouse Check, defending at the same speed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A single attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"174"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LjvUf9yp4f6R6Fnn"},"LmBKeF5SYD5Laz0N":{"name":"Guise of the Departed","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can absorb the spiritual residue of a recently departed mortal, stealing not only their living appearance but also bearing and mannerisms. While under the effect, the user is almost indistinguishable from the deceased, and only those closest to the dead can suspect foul play.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Thanatosis •• or Corpse in the Monster Path ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Performance vs. Wits + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches a mortal corpse. The Storyteller then makes a hidden test of Manipulation + Performance at Difficulty 3. A failure means that the resemblance is less than convincing, and anyone close to the person copied notices something amiss automatically. A win creates a convincing disguise and gives appropriate Looks Merits, but the user must roll Manipulation + Performance vs Wits + Insight contests against anyone close to the copied person or they will sense something is wrong if the user is attempting to immitate a dead person. If the body has visible deadly wounds, the user may use this form to scare common mortals, rolling Charisma or Manipulation + Intimidation (Difficulty 2).</p>\n<p>If the remains touched were of a person who would be at least 100 years old, the user appears skeletal. Powers such as Sense the Unseen (Auspex 1) can pierce the mask as usual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until dawn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"98"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rnB4vlEC":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"heU1CtIR":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LmBKeF5SYD5Laz0N"},"LqLDf6s7vSKeRfkD":{"name":"See the True Shape","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>A vampire with this power may recognize flesh that has been magically reshaped. Although Tzimisce are most adept at noticing fleshcrafting wrought by Vicissitude, they can also recognize the changes brought about by Protean, mortal magic or even the shapechanging of Lupines. It is whispered in the Courts of Love that a certain group of particularly sadistic Toreador also possess this power and are employed to root out werewolves from French cities. While rare, some Gangrel do employ this power when their packs hunt werewolf packs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Vicissitude •• or Protean ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits/Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire scrutinises the target and her player rolls Wits + Medicine (Wits may be swapped with Resolve for intential scrutiny) + Body Crafts against the successes of whatever power caused the alteration of form (or Composure + Subterfuge if it required no roll, like skinchanging of Protean and Lupines). On a win, the vampire One spots works of Vicissitude and other shape-changing evoked through vampiric Disciplines. The character may also pierce other magic (including mortal wizardry) and “natural” shapechanging such as what Lupines use with this power. If the player gains two margin on the roll or a Critical Win, the character not only recognises the type of transformation but may make an educated guess as to the target’s natural form.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"28"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"xAkI9F7Q":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"UNe0Ps86":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LqLDf6s7vSKeRfkD"},"LsTMhG7o341sJgiv":{"name":"Blood of Mandagloire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This concoction can taint an entire herd, but must be imbibed by the victims within a few hours of being distilled.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The Alchemist’s Blood, melancholic human blood, ketamine or Thorazine, melted human fat, verdigris</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After taking this concoction the herd takes on a melancholic Resonance and becomes sluggish and unresponsive. Any vampire feeding off of them while they’re in this state must make a Composure + Resolve roll against a Difficulty equal to the cooker’s Alchemy or fall into a dreamless sleep for an hour. Aggressive shaking or slapping gives the vampire another roll to resist the stupor, while inflicting a wound awakens them immediately though the melancholy Resonance lingers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> This formula stays active in the herd’s blood for three nights after dosing.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Second Inquisition","page":"46"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LsTMhG7o341sJgiv"},"LwiHti8M1IbGk2YS":{"name":"Majesty","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Presence of the user becomes a conduit for Dominate. The vampire now only needs their voice to be heard in order to employ Dominate powers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Intimidation vs Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> People in the presence of the user can only stare, slackjawed, or avert their eyes in fear or submission. Anyone wanting to act in any way in opposition to the user, except for selfpreservation, must successfully win a contest of their Composure + Resolve vs. the vampire’s Charisma + Intimidation. A win allows one turn of freedom, plus one per point of margin; only a critical win resists the effect for the entire scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"126"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"Qf4xuL8t":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"quqbIjJb":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.LwiHti8M1IbGk2YS"},"M0t28Ik00LHBY0iq":{"name":"Repeat","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Repeating the same course of an action that has failed you is often a sign of madness — or a sign that you are being influenced by a user of Temporis. With Repeat, the target is forced to repeat their previous simple action by manipulating their sense of time; they do not believe the action has already happened but is in the process of happening.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Wits + Intelligence</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Intelligence + Occult vs. Wits + Intelligence of a target within their line of sight. Upon a win, the victim’s current action is interrupted and they are forced to repeat a simple action they took in previous two turns such as attacking the same target in the same way or repeating a sentence. The user of the power gains a two-dice bonus on any Skill-based roll against the victim, already understanding what their intent and reactions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"168"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"uKWh1MR6":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"xPy2qbmQ":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.M0t28Ik00LHBY0iq"},"M226R2a2ITywBZR9":{"name":"Lure of the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vitae of the Children of Haqim is feared by the other Clans as rumours (spread by the Haqimites themselves) state that a single sip is enough to blood bond a person. While it is not known if some masters of Quietus are capable of doing that, Lure of the Blood enhances the blood-bonding properties to shorten the number of nights required to achieve a blood bond.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Every time the user’s vitae is consumed, it counts as two for the purposes of the Blood Oath/Blood Bond, meaning that the vizier may achieve a Blood Oath/Blood Bond in only two nights.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"135"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.M226R2a2ITywBZR9"},"M8Zm3ivs0BageZe8":{"name":"Weight of a Feather","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can reduce their effective mass and density, making themselves almost weightless. This allows them to avoid triggering pressure sensors as well as avoiding major damage from falls, collisions, or being thrown. The power cannot be used for longer leaps, as the vampire’s strength is proportionally reduced.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Survival</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the vampire has time to prepare, no roll is required. As a reaction, such as during a sudden fall, activating the power requires a Wits + Survival roll at Difficulty 3. As long as the power is in effect, the vampire is immune to damage from falls, collisions, and being thrown. The user also avoids triggering devices that rely on pressure, at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>This power also removes any penalty incurred by wearing heavier armour as the user’s muscles can discount the standard bodyweight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As long as desired</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"128"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"Dx8qft89":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"BXyRieLx":{"path":"skills.survival"}},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.M8Zm3ivs0BageZe8"},"MA4x0OQk8rAXqKHg":{"name":"Rayzeel’s Song","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>Music soothes the savage breast. Voice and instruments heal the soul and confer focus. Rayzeel is said to have crafted the Song to draw Saulot out of depression after the Baali Wars. Salubri who hail from Judeo-Christian traditions call this power King David’s Blessing. When playing music appropriate to the situation (a wistful string tune is appropriate for most situations, though a jaunty flute-and-drums combination before battle gets the soul singing along), the user focuses the souls of all involved on the task at hand.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Several minutes of songs or recitations</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower point per subject</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Charisma + Performance at a Difficulty of 3 and spends a point of Willpower for each listener they wish to affect. On a win, the subjects suffer no Physical or Mental Impairment for the rest of the scene and reduce their Called Shot Penalties by one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The subjects remain affected for the rest of the night, but lose the benefits at the end of the first conflict.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"187"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"ZPgSuAzj":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"2mETUQDt":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MA4x0OQk8rAXqKHg"},"MIWquRwdK4nPvL6b":{"name":"Silentia Mortis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Haqim’s gift to his children so that they may plot in secret near hostile ears, Silentia Mortis offers a shroud of silence to hide their steps and voice so that they may scheme by whispers in the shadows. This power alone is enough of a reason for study of other Quietus Paths as even to the most dogmatic and arrogant of Banu Haqim Warriors see its value.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The users blood begins to vibrate at a mystic frequency and fully silences their footsteps, voice, clothing, minor collisions, and other sounds of their person, giving them a two-dice bonus on all Stealth rolls. This makes the vampire undetectable if an observer could only notice them by sound, such as when on a different floor of a house. This power does not eliminate sounds the user makes outside their personal space (throwing or dropping objects, or slamming doors, for example). Sense of the Unseen (Auspex 1) and similar powers allow their users to pierce the veil of silence by rolling against the user’s Wits + Stealth.</p>\n<p>While this power is active, the user can allow one or more persons to hear them, allowing them to exercise powers like Presence and Dominate without others realising it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"135"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MIWquRwdK4nPvL6b"},"MIljBlybOL9Tpiey":{"name":"Pleague of Beasts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can mark an individual, making them the target of focused animal attention. Whatever beasts or vermin are in the area seek the target out to bark, peck, scratch, and generally make their night miserable. While the animals retain enough instincts to not entirely threaten their self-preservation, they make it very hard for the victim to do anything but fend them off.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire picks a target within their line of sight and tests Manipulation + Animal Ken against the target’s Composure + Animal Ken. On a win the target becomes the focus of attention for all animals in their vicinity for the rest of the night, suffering a dice penalty to all Skill pools equal to the margin on the win unless they can physically isolate themselves from the local wildlife. The target also becomes easier to track, with any pursuer receiving a bonus equal to the same margin. The Skill penalty does not apply to physical conflicts, as the animals aren’t suicidal enough to stick around when fists and bullets start flying, though they return once the dust settles.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"18"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MIljBlybOL9Tpiey"},"MJejvWALn3Qe8H38":{"name":"Marrow Sucker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>All animals contain a spark of life. This power allows you to scrape, suck, and savor those small bits of life out of dead things, be they animal or human, to allow you to survive. It’s not pretty and it’s messy as hell, but when you’re locked in a crypt with nothing but bones, it can keep you out of torpor for a while.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For every 20 pounds or 9 kilograms of bone the vampires eats (roughly the weight of the bones in one human skeleton), they slake one point of Hunger. The vampires places the bone in or near its mouth and begins to suck. The bones turn into a grey, pulpy mass which the vampire must then swallow. The product of this poweris far less satisfying that animal blood, and only those with this level of the Thanatosis Discipline can benefit from consuming it.</p>\n<p>The user of this power should be weary so as not to touch anything by accident as it deals the damage as stated above, withering plants and accidentally killing humans by touch alone on accident.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"178"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MJejvWALn3Qe8H38"},"MWDtk1cXqjo29dAF":{"name":"Sense the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can sense the Beast present in mortals, vampires, and other supernaturals, gaining a sense of their nature, hunger, and hostility.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits/Resolve + Awareness vs Composure + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Roll Wits + Awareness vs Composure + Subterfuge of those within a radius equal to five times the Animalism rating of the user in meters. Wits may be substituded with Resolve if the user is actively trying to use the power. A win allows the user to sense the level of hostility in a target (whether the person is prepared to do harm or even determined to cause it) and determine whether they harbor a supernatural Beast, marking them as a vampire, ghoul, revenant or werewolf. A win can also counter a surprise attack.</p>\n<p>On a critical win or at least 3 margins, the user gains information on the exact type of creature, as well as their Hunger or Rage level. This power can be used both actively and passively, warning the user of aggressive intent in their immediate vicinity.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"14"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"UwvL3LTQ":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"64QjRlBV":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MWDtk1cXqjo29dAF"},"Mbcm6u3hsDcdUZME":{"name":"I Am Legion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>One of the Baali's most devious methods of avoiding detection, this subtle magic allows the Baali to forge a temporary pact with a “speaking demon” or other malevolent spirit (Storyteller's discretion as to what sort of being is appropriate). Once the pact is sealed, the vampire’s partner in mischief temporarily leaps into the vampire's body, assuming a state of limited control over its voice and movements.</p>\n<p>While the vampire is “possessed,” the interloper who answers is essentially in charge, allowing for a certain amount of leeway when it comes to answering uncomfortable question. After all, the demon can answer questions (truthfully) that might otherwise prove difficult for the Baali to respond to under magical, Auspex-based, or other scrutiny. For example, the demon within may cheerfully answer “no” to such tired questions as \"Are you Baali!\" or “Do you engage in the worship of demons!” In a pinch, the demon may attempt to pose as Caitiff or even mortal; “I am clanless” and “I am not a vampire” are both perfectly reasonable true statements for a non-vampiric creature to make, after all.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Leadership</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The player must succeed in a Manipulation + Leadership roll (Difficulty 0) to ensnare a suitable demon or spirit. At even a single success, the demonic entity has been summoned. The number of successes dictates the summoned creature’s intelligence and willingness to help, which in turn indicates the extent to which it is capable of concocting useful reactions to pertinent questions and/or situations. For each success, add 1 dot to an entire category of an Attribute or a Skill (Physical/Social/Mental, although Physical is useless here) for the demonic entity. A failure indicates just that — failure to ensnare the being — while a Total Failure or a Botch can result in true catastrophe (a creature who does not wish to leave the caster’s body, or who betrays them, at the Storyteller’s discretion: give it three dots of Attributes and Skills per 1 rolled). Vampires hedging their bets with this sort of deal can make “concessions” to the inhabiting creature to win more cooperation. It is up to the Storyteller to determine what sort of goodies the possessing demon might want, what it could give in return, and whether or not it will keep its bargains. Assume that on a Critical Win or at least 6 successess the demon will ask nothing in return and be compliant, while 1-3 would require some enticing to anything done and will refuse to leave and return to their plane of suffering.</p>\n<p>A disobedient creature may try to take full possession of the vampire, rolling Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve of the demon. If it fails, the creature is forced out. The vampire may try the same roll to expel the creature.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the creature exits, willingly or forcefully.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"57"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"WJpirHlI":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"56ntRlUa":{"path":"skills.leadership"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Mbcm6u3hsDcdUZME"},"MdQxqsGcATiDDoQ1":{"name":"Tank","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The alchemist can use this formula to help strengthen their resistance against damage, albeit only temporarily.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric blood, oaked wine or bourbon, and Kevlar fibers or cement or ground tortoiseshell.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The first time the alchemist sustains damage in a scene, reduce the damage by five. The formula then expires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the alchemist takes damage or the scene ends.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"105"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MdQxqsGcATiDDoQ1"},"MjswudDCnoojdgNk":{"name":"Mirror of Trust","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This mixture, when imbibed by the Alchemist, makes them compelling and attractive, overwhelming other blankbodies’ self-control and encouraging them to speak honestly and frankly, especially in response to direct questions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The Alchemist’s Blood, sanguine human blood, truth serum, a white chrysanthemum taken from a grave</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The Alchemist has 3 extra dice on Persuasion or Intimidation rolls to make another person or blankbody be honest with them</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One hour</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Second Inquisition","page":"46"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MjswudDCnoojdgNk"},"MlbjDcmml85z6FLI":{"name":"Flight (•••)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/flight.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can fly up to 45 miles per hour/60 km per hour and carry up to 50 lbs./25 kg, if their Strength allows for it. They only need to roll for the most difficult of maneuvers and her normal dodge dice pool is fully available to them.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"78"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"flight","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.MlbjDcmml85z6FLI"},"Mveb3lreXArq9UtS":{"name":"Serpent’s Flesh","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power, the Setite’s flesh becomes leathery, scaly, slimy, and otherwise monstrous. Their body becomes flexible and malleable, and they become harder to hurt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user completes their transformation almost instantly into a creature that is obvious unnatural. Should the user wish to appear more subtle and not unnerving to common mortals, they must spend a point of Willpower to contain the transformation. Add the user’s Serpentis rating to any dice pools to wriggle through tight spaces, escape a grapple, or escape any form of restraints. The user can squeeze into tight spaces so long as their head fits into the space as their other bones can dislocate and reconnect with ease. Additionally, should the user not conceal their obviously monstrous appearance, they gain a one-die bonus on all Intimidation rolls.</p>\n<p>If the user has about five minutes, they can make a Rouse Check to force their body into an even more pliable state. Their flesh and bones spread and become more flexible like that of a serpent to let them fit through impossibly tight spaces about four inches (or ten centimeters) in diameter. If the user is interrupted or forcibly yanked out of such a tight space, they suffer one Aggravated damage to Health.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended by the user or one scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"151"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Mveb3lreXArq9UtS"},"N0Ts5gr12g6JepbB":{"name":"Implant Suggestion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire possesses the power to change the very personality or opinions of a subject, at least temporarily. While other powers enable them to puppeteer mindless victims, this power lets them alter the intents or passions of a victim instead. They can make someone desire a stranger, abandon their family, distrust their own beliefs, or simply want a beer. Vampires known to utilise this power indiscriminately often find themselves shunned by their peers, whether from or utter fear.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Presence •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Eye-contact is required as with most Dominate powers. No test is required against an unprepared mortal victim, but a prepared mortal or another vampire requires a test of Manipulation + Persuasion against Composure + Resolve. Radical changes to core beliefs – making a vegan crave a steak or making a pacifist violent, for example – also merit an attempt to resist, even by unprepared mortals. The suggestion remains in effect for a scene, at which point the victim snaps out of it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"75"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"d31s26ah":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"OCRxVCPX":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.N0Ts5gr12g6JepbB"},"N2cYRHw49pz3rrKW":{"name":"The Wild Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>There are many restless spirits lurking around violent creatures, especially the undead. These creatures seek vengeance and destruction which can be often used in the favour of those calling to such spirits who are able to host them in their bodies in exchange for power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s nails elongate as their hands become claws, counting as a +2 piercing Brawl weapon dealing Aggravated Health damage to mortals and spirits (materialised or not) and unhalved Superficial Health damage to vampires. The user’s Physical Attributes all raise by one dot (to a maximum of 6), but their Charisma, Composure, Intelligence and Wits are lowered by one. The user bypasses any visual impairments, being able to sense, rather than see their targets. The user is also immune to Mental and Physical Impairment, save for mutilation.</p>\n<p>The transformation lasts until dawn. To end it before, the user must succeed on a Composure + Resolve Test at a Difficulty equal to 3.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until dawn unless ended before that.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"163"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.N2cYRHw49pz3rrKW"},"N9D5SId7unhbkF78":{"name":"Aura of Sloth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>This power forms an aura that mentally fatigues those within it, able to put mortals to sleep with ease, and even affecting the time supernaturals need before they must rest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The aura can expand to up to 10 metres from the user in all directions, not being obstructed by physical obstacles such as walls. No roll is required against unprepared mortals caught within the radius, who immediately feel drowsy and adopt the most comfortable Causing drowsiness in vampires and other supernatural creatures (ghouls, revenants…) requires a Manipulation + Persuasion vs. Stamina + Resolve roll when they first enter the aura. On a win, the supernatural does not immediately sleep, but suffers a two-dice penalty to all Mental dice pools while they remain within the aura. On a fail, the supernatural is unaffected.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene or until intentionally ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"93"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"4xYDeCjt":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"l7aBDR9P":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.N9D5SId7unhbkF78"},"NrPpA8ZM9aL79yOa":{"name":"Theft of Will","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Developed by Tremere elders to deal with spells of mischievous neonates a lesson, Theft of Will allows its user to Dominate magic itself and take control of nearby magic made by other sorcerers such as Wards and curses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Any Blood Sorcery Path •••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> First, the user must know that there is a magical effect to take control of such as by Sense the Unseen (for things like Wards and spells) or aura reading Scry the Soul (for things like curses). Mystic Sight power works perfectly with Theft of Will. Alternatively, walking face-first into a Ward or being told that they are cursed also works. After that, the user rolls Manipulation + Occult against the initial successes of the sorcerer on the effect they wish to take control of.</p>\n<p>Wards can then be dispelled or taken control of, allowing the user to pass through and let others pass or to even turn the magainst their own make. Effects such as curses and debilitations can be “paused” or completely dispelled by the user. At the storyteller’s discretion, this power can interrupt other Blood Sorcery powers that last for longer than a turn such as Theft of Vitae or enchantments like those often placed on weapons (e.g. magical flame swords) and armour. This power may also be invoked to act as a defensive roll against magic.</p>\n<p>If the sorcerer can visualise the magical effect such as by the Mystic Sight, they gain a two-dice bonus on this roll (unaffected by power bonus).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"76"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"tBPpkjtC":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"UCLYobc0":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.NrPpA8ZM9aL79yOa"},"OAbSxVfdnHJnV0jw":{"name":"Ride the Wild Wind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Cainites who know Ride the Wild Mind are able to dislodge their own souls from their bodies and seat them in the bodies of wild animals. Since animals have no souls, by any reasonable definition (including the Christian one of the time), the Cainite commits no sin by resting his own in the creature’s body for a short time. While possessing an animal in this fashion, the vampire controls the beast’s every move and reaction.</p>\n<p>Those using Ride the Wild Mind are prone to adapt some of their host animal’s behaviors when they emerge from the possession. Many Tzimisce sculpt dogs or wolves into hideous war-ghouls and then possess them with Ride the Wild Mind, giving themselves command of a frightening monster during a battle while leaving their bodies safe at home. Some superstitious Gangrel and Ravnos believe that a piece of their soul remains in the animal when they leave it, so they make a point of consuming the animal’s blood when the ride is done.</p>\n<p>Cainites who miss their living days exult in the freedom and power of Ride the Wild Mind, as it brings a strong sensation of life along with it. Those who no longer miss their living days — particularly those not on the Road of Humanity, or who have low scores in that Road — find the power’s use to be disconcerting. Once again, the vampire can feel “their” heart beating, they suffer from mundane hunger, and sweats and produces filth all on their own. These sensations are long absent even in the ancilla or elders.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check. Free if used on famulus.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Roll Manipulation + Animal Ken (Difficulty 0) while the character looks into the animal’s eyes for a turn. The character cannot possess worms or other creatures without eyes, as they have no easy passage for the soul. The animal must also be a living creature of flesh and blood to be possessed. The Cainite cannot ride a demon (even a familiar in animal form) or a ghost. One success allows the character to just barely take over the animal’s body (and swap Physical Attributes and native Skills for the animal’s if they’re higher just like with Protean); multiple successes give them greater access to their Disciplines, per the table on the next page, assuming powers do not require humanoid features (Obfuscate to immitate another human, Dominate is unusable unless the animal may replicate human speech like a crow or a parrot...). Results marked with an asterisk (*) also require the character to spend Willpower to take any action that the Storyteller deems out of line with the animal’s base instincts. When possessing, the vampire’s body then into a torpid stupor as their soul drifts out of their body and into the animal’s. The character may remain in the host animal for as long as they like, but their body still loses blood at its normal rate while they do so.</p>\n<p>Successes Response Total Failure/ Roll to resist Fear Frenzy Botch (Difficulty 1) as the character’s Beast is overwhelmed by the animal’s nature 0 Cannot possess; try again next turn 1 Possess the animal, but cannot use any Disciplines or other powers 2 Can use Auspex* 3 Can also use Presence* 4 Can also use Dominate, Dementation 5+ Can also any other power, including magic* The vampire may inhabit the animal indefinitely, but extending this possession into the daylight hours requires the vampire to stay awake and make a Road rating roll; seeing the sun requires a test for Terror Frenzy though the sunlight does not damage the animal being ridden. The user remains oblivious to their original body, but harm to it pulls them out of the trance and releases the animal. Death of the possessed animal also ends the trance, and the vampire takes a point of Aggravated Willpower damage from the shock.</p>\n<p>When the character leaves the animal’s body to return to their own, the player must roll Wits + Insight and score as many successes as days the character spent within the creature (minimum of one). If the player fails this roll, the character spends the same number of days in a purely animalistic mindset, obeying the urges and instincts of the creature whose body they just left. Should they roll a Total Failure or Botch, the vampire frenzies and then drops into the animalistic mindset just described.</p>\n<p>Whether the player succeeds or fails at that roll, the vampire is stuck thinking in the creature’s ways for some time. The character must spend seven Willpower points or seven days (or some combination of the two) to get over the effects of having used this power. Until that time has passed, the character reflexively reacts as their old host did. A vampire who spent time in the body of a wolf may growl at unexpected visitors, kowtow excessively to princes and others of acknowledged higher status and so on. Many Gangrel use this side effect of Ride the Wild Mind to their advantage, possessing a wolverine for extra ferocity or a squirrel to get themselves in the habit of storing goodies away for wintertime.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A scene/indefinitely (see above)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"19"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.OAbSxVfdnHJnV0jw"},"OtoYw9nwbM1euKZk":{"name":"The Choir of Orpheus","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>The master of Melpominee may now unleash their voice at an entire crowd of people.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can amplify any of their other powers to affect all, including their allies, who hear their voice, at once. The vampire has little control over this who gets affected and who doesn’t. Powers amplified in this manner require no line of sight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As per power amplified.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"89"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.OtoYw9nwbM1euKZk"},"OwJpP9weoLQfWHuG":{"name":"Conceal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>This ability allows the user to cloak an inanimate object such as a door, a car, or a small house. As with other Obfuscate powers, this does not actually make the object invisible, but creates a lingering hypnotic effect that causes most people to simply ignore it. In this case the power is especially effective, given that the object is unlikely to call attention to itself. Unless something causes passersby to collide with it or someone points it out, people behave as if the object wasn’t there, moving around larger objects if need be.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Cloak of Shadows</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire touches the object and rolls a test of Intelligence + Subterfuge against a Difficulty from 2 (Concealing a ring in a drawer filled with other memorabilia) to 6 (Concealing a house in the middle of an open square), depending on the target’s size and location. The power lasts for one night. Each point of margin on the win conceals the object for an additional night. This power conceals anyone and anything inside the object (e.g., people in a crriage or shed), as long as the viewer remains outside. This power cannot affect anything larger than a two-story house or any object moving under its own power (such as a moving carriage). Someone with the Auspex power Sense the Unseen (or equivalent) can notice the object by winning against the scored successes on the Intelligence + Subterfuge Test.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night, with an additional night per point of margin on the win.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"103"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"wZnTTAPh":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"7VTGX0YL":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.OwJpP9weoLQfWHuG"},"P6ESJ69BkSmj9NBm":{"name":"Shepard’s Watch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The third eye opens and flares a bright white, illuminating a barely-visible ward around the user and his charge. No foe may breach this sacred shield. The user themself must stand among those he defends as he generates this barrier; he cannot guard them from afar.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full to activate, free to maintain or drop</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user raises a barrier of Willpower and might, extending in a radius around them equal to the user’s Path of the Healer rating in meters/ yards. Those within it at its creation and those permitted by the user may leave and return, and the barrier moves with the user. Rocks, thrown weapons, or crossbow bolts may penetrate the barrier without penalty, but everyone within the barrier is considered to have two additional dice to avoid or resist outside hostile effects.</p>\n<p>Enemies of the user will have a harder time piercing the barrier, requiring winning a contest of Composure + Resolve against the user’s Composure + Resolve. The trespasser is able to penetrate the barrier and enter the circle for one turn + the margins on the roll. On a Critical Win, the trespasser is immune to the Shepard’s Watch of the user for the rest of the night. On a Critical win of the user, the trespasser may not reattempt the test and is barred from the threshold.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"188"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.P6ESJ69BkSmj9NBm"},"P97on6ARJjj8oMmm":{"name":"Whiff-Its","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some Kindred are rumored to be able to sniff out a particular resonance. Among the Duskborn, others have learned to sniff out the resonance (or lack thereof) of true Kindred and distinguish the most potent among them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s vitae, phlegmatic or sanguine human blood, cologne or perfume, fresh compost, ammonia, crushed fingernails from an animal</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When used, the potion grants the alchemist heightened olfactory abilities. For the next night, the alchemist can catch the scent of the Kindred with the highest Blood Potency in the area (as determined by the Storyteller). If there are multiple Kindred who share the same Blood Potency in the area, success on a subsequent Resolve + Awareness test at Difficulty 3 allows the alchemist to distinguish between individual Kindred and follow their trail. Kindred sealed in containers might likewise require a similar test to detect, with a Difficulty determined by the Storyteller.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until dawn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"50"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.P97on6ARJjj8oMmm"},"PDvxXFwRVQEHi7bu":{"name":"Split Second","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The speed at which the vampire moves catches up with their supercharged perception, allowing them to react to events around them at a moment’s notice. Ambushers find their prey already standing behind them, and favors asked are completed before the words leave the supplicants mouth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The player can supersede the Storyteller’s narration of events, within reason. They can choose to have their character move through a door before it closes, circumvent an ambush after it has been triggered, roll out of the way of an explosion, and so forth. The action taken must be reasonable and should not take more than a few seconds in real time. The Storyteller decides what Skills, if any, need to be checked to successfully accomplish an action begun using this power.</p>\n<p>An opponent possessing Celerity 5 or Temporis can nullify this power by making their own Rouse Check, defending at the same speed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Roughly one action, as determined by the Storyteller.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"46"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PDvxXFwRVQEHi7bu"},"PFaMW4YA1A7jhMKT":{"name":"Quietus Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>As the user masters the Discipline said to have been created by Haqim himself, the vampire figures out how all Gifts of the Blood are connected, and how all the Paths of Quietus lead to the same destination. The master of Quietus becomes the judge, jury and the executioner among vampires, able to punish whole Elysium’s worth of unworthy Cainites.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Powers of any Quietus Path that require touch or transmission by poison are now available to the vampire by making a gesture with their hand towards the victim (those who succeed on a Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge may notice something odd) and making an activation Rouse Check. Such ranged poison attacks use Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude, if the victim possesses them)</p>\n<p>The vampire can also amplify any of their Quietus Path powers to affect a group of people, mortal or vampire, at once, if the power may be applied at range (such as Blood’s Sweat). When rolling against crowds of mortals or insignificant vampires, the Storyteller rolls only for the strongest one and applies the successes for the rest. When rolling powers of any Quietus Path, you always count as having a specialty.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"144"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PFaMW4YA1A7jhMKT"},"PGV4hwS94LLmSi2v":{"name":"Tenebrous Avatar","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Cainite using this power can transform her body into an inhuman shadow. The form this living shadow takes depends more on the character’s inner nature than on their mastery of Obtenebration. For some, the form may be undulating and serpentine, while it might be a formless cloud for others. Still others might take an ominous and imposing monolithic shape. While assuming the Tenebrous Avatar form, the Lasombra is virtually immune to harm, and they can pass through slight cracks and very small openings by extruding their shadow essence to the necessary thickness.</p>\n<p>The touch of the Tenebrous Avatar is disconcerting to mortals, and taking on the shape brings the sensation of being in the grasp of a cold and inhuman otherworldly being. Lasombra lore talks of this power as a step toward becoming Ahriman itself, a mixed blessing indeed. The character can also see through even the deepest blackness while in Tenebrous Avatar form.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The transformation takes one turn, during which the vampire is unable to do anything else. Once the transformation is complete the vampire can move at walking pace across the ground or along walls like shadows, hampered only by hermetically sealed barriers.</p>\n<p>Vampires using Tenebrous Avatar can envelop victims, causing the victim to reduce all their dice pools by three and suffocating mortals as with Nocturne, above. If surrounding a mortal, the vampire can feed from them without penetrating the skin with fangs (mechanically in combat, this is rolled as a bite attack with no Called Shot penalty vs. the victim’s Dexterity + Athletics).</p>\n<p>Practitioners of this power take no damage from physical sources but can be harmed by fire and sunlight as normal. Mental Disciplines can still be used at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadow, the user’s form becomes three-dimension and they may perform actions as normal, losing the ability to feed without penetrating the skin with fangs. The user may also be attacked as normal, but all powers. The user’s Health rating is normal or Obtenebration rating + Fortitude + 3, whichever is higher. When returning to standard form, remove undamaged health dots first. All called shot penalties are performed with two additional subtracted success. Even when manifested as three-dimensional entity, the user may still slip through cracks and enter through nonhermetically sealed portals, although they lose the ability to pseudo-fly as two-dimensional entities.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"114"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PGV4hwS94LLmSi2v"},"PIxtEKK4i1O5YRUA":{"name":"Debilitative Poison","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With Hinder Mending, the vampire concocts a special poison that prevents usage of vitae for mending, disrupting vampire targets and their other creations that utilise vitae to heal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice/free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This contact poison has the same system as Scorpion’s Touch (see above) and is applied the same way).</p>\n<p>If the poison hits, the victim suffers Physical Impairment for the rest of the scene. Additionally, the striked bodypart suffers trauma and the victim rolls for a Crippling Injury according to the table for Crippling Injuries (p. XX). If the roll to damage/ touch the target was a Critical Win or a called shot, the user may select a Crippling Injury themselves to indicate a precise hit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The poison is potent for one scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"142"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PIxtEKK4i1O5YRUA"},"PWDoZCNBnSQ3V6Nh":{"name":"Ease Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire soothes the mental or emotional turmoil of their subject, restoring to them a modicum of steadfastness. It is especially effective when used on mortals, either to help them through momentary agitation, or to calm them before feeding on them. When using this power, the user’s third eye opens and shines a light blue light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Resolve + Medicine against Difficulty 2 and restores a number of Superficial Willpower damage levels equal to the margin on the roll. Alternatively, the user restores one Aggravated Willpower damage level for every three successes in the margin.</p>\n<p>If successfully used on a mortal, Ease Mind similarly calms them if they’re in a turbulent emotional state, or puts them otherwise at ease. Use of this power takes a whole turn. If the vampire spends an entire scene instead, reduce the Difficulty to 0. Panacea must be used on someone other than the vampire invoking the power. A subject can be affected by the power only once per night.</p>\n<p>If the user soothes more than one subject per night, the user suffers superficial Willpower damage equal to half the number of successes in the margin for every additional subject, as they take on the burden of so many charges.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"184"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PWDoZCNBnSQ3V6Nh"},"PZ8TNVtrjT6xxvOB":{"name":"Baal’s Caress","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>A refinement of Scorpion’s Touch, Baal’s Caress allows a vampire to transform her blood into an acidic toxin that burns any living or undead flesh, usually accomplished by licking a blade to lubricate it before assaulting an opponent. Commonly, warriors will spit the hateful ichor or cough it forth and vomit voluminous streams of vitae that corrode their enemies into heaps of bubbling sludge.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Scorpion’s Touch</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free/Minor</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Identical to Scorpion’s Touch save that the damage is increased to Aggravated against all beings. Mortals who take even a point of damage from this poison die instantly while vampires enter torpor when they next fall into their daysleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The poison remains potent for one story.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"143"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PZ8TNVtrjT6xxvOB"},"PeFYHQ6g14GRTnhR":{"name":"Coax the Tendons","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Normally, mortals a are unable to restore missing limbs without cutting-edge advancements in Medicine or powerful rituals unless they’ve lost the limbs while a ghoul. Coax the Tendons was used by the Tzimisce during the dark ages to quickly patch up their servants on the field of battle, allowing for reattachment of missing limbs. Many Tzimisce also see a more personally utilitiarian purpose to this power and graft additional limbs to themselves and others.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Medicine against a Difficulty of 2. On a win, they may reattach a lost limb or graft additional and new ones, potentially healing a related Crippling Injury. Grafting additional limbs gives the subject one Physical Attribute dot, possibly going above the standard maximum of 5. This dot is not counted for the purposes of increasing one’s Physical Attributes with experience points.</p>\n<p>Only one dot may be gained with this power. The vampire may use this power on themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"203"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PeFYHQ6g14GRTnhR"},"Pi9xxIyIu4P5iCSZ":{"name":"Rush Job","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can perform time-consuming tasks with blinding speed, their fingers and hands blurring as they write, repair, or build things in record time. While the concentration required prohibits them from employing this speed on the offense, it does allow them to achieve non-violent ends faster when under duress or outright attack.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When active, this power lets the vampire complete Skill-related tasks that would otherwise take whole turns in the span of a few seconds, and can treat a full action as a minor action (p. 209). The power cannot be used to speed up attacks, defenses, or to perform any other actively resisted task but it does allow the vampire to, for example, jimmy a lock and fire a gun (the latter with a two-dice penalty, as per the minor action rules.)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"43"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Pi9xxIyIu4P5iCSZ"},"PptXQ2S1p1lXCtrB":{"name":"Mind’s Defences","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>Dementation reveals. It shatters the mind’s defences to reveal the bare and unfiltered truth to the mind. Rarely, however, skillful users of Dementation may perform the reverse, considered a bit of a taboo among the elders of the Clan of the Moon. By tearing down or bolstering the mind’s defences, the user is able to weaken or fortify the individual’s willpower, allowing them to resist having their decisions altered and minds swayed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Insight against Wits + Composure of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 52) or against a flat Difficulty of 2 if the victim is a willing subject. On a win, the target receives a dice bonus or a dice penalty equal to half the user’s Dementation rating rounded up on rolls to resist Frenzy and any means of mind-influence (Presence, Obfuscate, Dementation, but not Dominate) or Persuasion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 52.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"64"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PptXQ2S1p1lXCtrB"},"PqWVXlvtMZJak0ww":{"name":"Permanencz","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>Like Apparition, Permanency stacks with other, simpler Chimerstry powers. It eternally extends the duration of illusions created by those abilities, allowing them to persist even when the character who created them is no longer nearby. It will remain for all time — even past the character’s Final Death, unless the character chooses to dismiss it as described previously. If he uses this ability on an illusion given movement by Apparition, the character must guide the illusion through its normal pattern of movement once before he can make it permanent. Despite the illusion’s permanency, however, it is no more real than an illusion created by any of the abilities listed already. It still can neither harm a character nor bear weight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Illusions crafted by Ignis Fatuus or Fata Morgana are now permanent until dismissed (this includes illusions “programmed” with Apparition, for which concentration is no longer needed).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Illusions last until dismissed now, or until the character makes more than they are able to maintain.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"50"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.PqWVXlvtMZJak0ww"},"Pz4D6UkzlMXpwFEo":{"name":"Weaken the Blood of the Ancients","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power, the user commands Blood and the Curse of Caine itself, manipulating Generation and Blood Potency of their victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Quietus Cruciatus 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Blood’s Curse</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches the victim (requiring a combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/ Brawl) and rolls Intelligence + Occult against the user’s Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude, if they possess it). On a win, half (rounded down) of the margins cause the victim’s Generation to rise by one for each point of the margin, to a minimum of 12th Generation or 14th on a Critical Win, and half (rounded down) of the margins cause the victim’s Blood Potency to lower by one for each point of the margin, to a minimum of one point of Blood Potency or 0 on a Critical Win. The user lowers their Generation and increases their Blood Potency for every point of Generation/ Blood Potency impacted, to the lowest Generation and highest Blood potency equal to the victim’s own.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until dawn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"149"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"1AR754Jd":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"sF42ZlcB":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Pz4D6UkzlMXpwFEo"},"QAQkIUXkWFeonFbL":{"name":"Burning Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>The character’s grasp inflicts searing pain akin to being burnt with a red-hot iron. This power inflicts no damage, but painfully disrupts the vital energies of the target. The Warrior can use prolonged or repeated exposure to this power in order torture a victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By grasping, punching or touching the target (which in combat could be done with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Brawl/Athletics) the user causes a surge of fiery pain rushing from their palm onto that of the target, causing them to suffer a two-dice penalty on all non-tracker rolls from shock. Vampires test for Fire Frenzy as if they took burning damage (Difficulty of 2).</p>\n<p>This power can be used alongside a Brawl attack or with a grapple.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the user lets go.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"192"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QAQkIUXkWFeonFbL"},"QEMo3vThCQeGH9nG":{"name":"Shadow Cast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>Obtenebration is powerful but can often be foiled by the simple lack of appropriate shadows from which to summon its manifestations. This power draws upon the dark within the user to project a supernatural shadow from which to manifest other powers, no matter the ambient lightning. This shadow usually mimics the movement and shape of the user but can sometimes grow distorted and even monstrous, resonating with the current temperament of its owner.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Activating the power conjures a supernatural shadow from the vampire’s body. As long as the power is active, the user casts this shadow, which cannot be removed except by direct sunlight.</p>\n<p>Anyone witnessing the practitioner notices the shadow cast from no visible light source on a Wits + Awareness roll (Difficulty 3). The vampire can direct their shadow, elongating or distorting (but not detaching) it at will, though it can sometimes act on its own accord, at the Storyteller’s discretion. For the purposes of other powers such as Shadow Perspective, the practitioner can lengthen the shadow to up to twice the practitioner’s Oblivion rating in yards/meters.</p>\n<p>For anyone standing within the shadow’s reach, the Willpower damage from social conflict increases by 1 (after halving for Superficial damage). They are also not able to spend or recover Willpower except by mystical means (such as to activate a power) unless they win a test of Composure + Resolve at a difficulty equal to the user’s Obtenebration rating. A win allows them a single turn of respite. Standing in the shadow of the Abyss is a terrifying prospect.</p>\n<p>Witnesses of the vampire notice the shadow is cast from no visible light source on a successful Wits + Awareness test (Difficulty 3) and try to dodge it (Dexterity + Athletics vs. Wits + Occult)</p>\n<p>The vampire can move their shadow within their Zone (Gehenna War, p. XX) as a two-dice minor action, casting it over people, around corners, and even sending it under doors so long as it remains attached.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows, the solidified shadow may be used as a physical barrier with Health equal to the user’s Obtenebration rating + Fortitude + 3.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"109"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QEMo3vThCQeGH9nG"},"QHczqtYXLekTvRtR":{"name":"Animal Dominion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The power the vampire holds over beasts is now great enough to command flocks and packs as if they were extensions of their own body. At a gesture, animals lay down their lives by the dozens, even hundreds, to appease their master.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks/An additional Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Chose a type of animal and make a Manipulation + Animal Ken roll with a Difficulty depending on the nature of the animals and the order given. Getting a flock of crows to disperse and look for a specific individual (given some means of identifying their target) is relatively easy (Difficulty 3), but getting a pack of dogs to give their lives in a suicidal attack on another vampire is more of a challenge (Difficulty 5). The power does not allow the user to summon animals, but compels those already present to obey. The vampire can command the animals to return after completing their task, if they have means to do so.</p>\n<p>Passively, the character is also now able to use all powers of Animalism on all as many targets within line of sight as they wish by making an additional Rouse Check to activate it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the directive is fulfilled, but animals usually return to their standard ways after a story/passive effect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"22"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QHczqtYXLekTvRtR"},"QLgFKSr1w92khjHe":{"name":"Mortal Illness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Harbingers of disease, the user of Thanatosis creates an ailment to induce a complete shutdown of function and death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Medicine vs. Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user concots an ailment and touches a mortal victim (coating one’s sword or arrows is also a possibility as it is a contact poison. See Paralysis of Death for that system) and rolls Manipulation + Medicine vs. the victim’s Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude, if they possess it). On a win, the victim is infected and will expire from a series of what could be described as heart attacks or other organ failures at a moment decreed by the user when they created the ailment. Some set timed ailments to help them drop the guards during haists, having infected them months or even years before that. Others drop dead the moment the user manages to touch or cut them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The ailment activates when the user willed it to activate when creating it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"180"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"0nHqQyPM":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"OghOLQqr":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QLgFKSr1w92khjHe"},"QOOAugbKp704iPYI":{"name":"Solid Shadows","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>With their mastery of Obtenebration, the user may now imbue shadows with physical substance, making them literal physical entities.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Fortitude 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By making an additional Rouse Check when activating certain powers of Obtenebration, the solidifies the darkness from two-dimensional shadow forms to three-dimensional solid forms of black inky substance that appear like a thick fog. As the Lasombra folklore states, the user can finally wield the Abyss itself.</p>\n<p>All powers of Obtenebration gain a die bonus to resist being banished (p. 88) and may block lines of sight. They also may now be physically damaged, but they suffer Superficial Health damage except from things like fire, True Faith and sunlight (as Bane Severity 3 vampires). Their Health is equal to the user’s Obtenebration rating + Fortitude + 3 unless stated otherwise.</p>\n<p>Certain powers also get unique effects that are not further modified by power bonus and may be found under each power’s system.</p>\n<p>The user may always opt to disable Solid Shadows if they prefer to use the unaltered variant of a power. Note that Solid Shadows-enhanced powers are more visible as powers like Shadow Cloak become literal garments of Shadow detectable without a contest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"111"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QOOAugbKp704iPYI"},"QPf0ZCQ5xQPehjOi":{"name":"Read the Blood’s Desire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By mixing a subject’s blood with theirs, their blood extracts information on the subject’ desire, beliefs and surface thoughts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Taste of the Soul</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the user must first possess at least one drop of the subject’s blood that is not older than 48 hours and place it in a cup that they fill with their own (covered by the power’s own Rouse Check). After that, they drink the mixture and roll Resolve + Insight against a Difficulty of 2. For each point on the margin, the user may select one of the following effects</p>\n<p>• Desire: The vampire learns of the subject’s current Desire.</p>\n<p>• Exploit Weaknesses: The vampire figures which areas of mental activity the subject is weak in. Gain a two-dice bonus (doesn’t stack with Power Bonus) on your next Mental or Social rolls against the target.</p>\n<p>• Moment of Memory: The vampire discovers what the subject was thinking of in the moment the blood exited their body.</p>\n<p>• Study Beliefs: The vampire discovers beliefs of the target and learns one of their Ethics. This effect may be taken as many times as the subject has Ethics.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"136"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"CKPThdi0":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"JxFj951a":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QPf0ZCQ5xQPehjOi"},"QQteMTyra5blgGTr":{"name":"Truth of Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>As a means to assure that their judgments are firmly grounded in truth, this power allows a vampire to use the blood of an individual being questioned to divine not only the truth of the subject’s words, but the truth behind those words.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Composure + Occult (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> To use this power, the Cainite must have one point of the target’s blood, which she pours into an open container capable of holding the fluid indefinitely, such as a basin or goblet. The container must have dimensions that permit them to, at the least, dip one finger into the vitae for the duration of the questioning. This power lasts for one scene or until the questioner stops touching the blood. The blood reduces until spent, gradually smoldering into a thin red mist that dissipates entirely to vapor by the scene’s conclusion.</p>\n<p>For every statement made by the subject, the user makes a Resolve + Insight against the subject’s Composure + Occult (or Fortitude) roll. The first failed contest ends the power as the blood instantly dissolves into red mist. A Win tells them whether that statement is true, as far as the subject knows and emotional reason behind the target’s statement (ennui, jealousy, anger). On a Critical Win or at least three margins, the subject involuntarily expands upon their answer, providing more information, including details they glancingly saw or subconsciously forgot. (The Storyteller should inform the caster if there is no more information about that topic.). A Critical Win also allows for this power to penetrate mind and memory-altering magics and powers like Dominate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until the user removes their finger from the blood container or until the subject overpowers the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"137"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"OjVSYwXS":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"3hYRZYSL":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QQteMTyra5blgGTr"},"QeJ5DF7PhFdSdxyF":{"name":"Toxic Personality","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The alchemist secretes powerful, caustic bile from their pores and orifices, corroding metals, wood, and living tissue. The alchemist activating this ability may touch and manipulate the bile while not being affected by it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric blood, potash or lye, animal spleen, cheesecloth, and iodine.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One or two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Strength or Dexterity + Alchemy vs. Dexterity + Athletics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When active, the power allows the alchemist to corrode any material in seconds by simply making contact with it and gains +2 damage on brawl attacks as their mere touch becomes acid. (As the alchemist controls how and where they excrete the acid their own clothes are usually unaffected.) The Storyteller is the final arbiter on the extent of damage caused but the acid is supernaturally strong and far more potent than that produced by Corrosive Vitae (see Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 273).</p>\n<p>The alchemist may make a second Rouse check to projectile vomit the bile as an attack (Strength + Alchemy during melee, Dexterity + Alchemy for a ranged spitting attack). On a win, the bile does the margin +3 in Superficial Health damage (Aggravated to mortals).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Athanor Corporis or Calcinatio:</strong> The alchemist or vessel can only hold this formula after distillation for a week before they begin to look sickly: blue veins popping against their pale skin, and the skin itself sagging low on their bodies. After a month, the bile corrodes the Calcinatio vessel if not tapped.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Fixatio:</strong> The container of the bile slowly corrodes, falling completely apart in Alchemy + 4 weeks.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"105"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"V54Aq6ri":{"path":"attributes.strength"},"gZgBxzV3":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QeJ5DF7PhFdSdxyF"},"QiHMltIkNfxkdDxb":{"name":"Shedding the Mask","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>A Malkavian is one part of a greater hole, and same can be said for other users of Dementation. With the Voice of Many, a Malkavian can amplify their voice to be able to carry terrible power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Dementation of the user is now more powerful and counts its margins as greater by 2 for the purposes of extending the effect. Damage dealt by Tear the Soul is now Aggravated and all powers that scale with the rating of Dementation count Dementation as having a rating of 8.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As power augmented.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"68"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QiHMltIkNfxkdDxb"},"QvagF67uSWgXXlis":{"name":"Draugh of Elegance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Blood of the vampire becomes saturated with the power of Celerity, conveying a part of that power to anyone who drinks of it. While this is also a first step towards a Blood Bond, already bound thralls or servants have little use for such worries, and even non-bound allies might decide to brave a sip for the sake of temporary power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Drinking a Rouse Checks worth of Blood directly from the user gifts the drinker with temporary Celerity equal to half the Celerity dots (rounded up) of the donor. The drinker gains the same non-Amalgam powers as the donor’s, up to that level.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night; for vampires,until the next feeding or the vampire reaches Hunger 5</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"45"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.QvagF67uSWgXXlis"},"Qy2ZfDeLQpfrxykt":{"name":"Fist of Caine","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s bare hands can inflict grievous injuries, lethal to both mortals and other vampires. They can dismember, pierce, impale, decapitate, and even rip a heart out of the chest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Lethal Body</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For one scene the user can inflict Aggravated Health damage to mortals and supernaturals alike while Brawling, as they literally rend flesh and tear their opponents limb from limb with their bare hands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"119"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Qy2ZfDeLQpfrxykt"},"R6lpmyMrTAjKHhW1":{"name":"Earth’s Perseverance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>A vampire with this power is almost impossible to move when it is active. The vampire draws upon the strength of the earth, grounding themselves and drawing upon its permanence to keep them rooted in place.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When this power is activated, the only way the vampire moves is if they choose to do so independently. This doesn’t make them resistant to damage, and a vampire can still be crushed, ripped apart, or torn to shreds, as can the floor they’re standing on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"80"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.R6lpmyMrTAjKHhW1"},"RGaXZegfaVIe0SO6":{"name":"Far Reach","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula allows the alchemist to use their mind to grab, hold, and push objects or people without touching them. While few can employ enough mental force to actually cause direct harm, a clever applicant can find many ways to get an opponent into, or themselves out of, harm’s way.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric human blood, melted nylon fibers or a grated refrigerator magnet or weird nootropics ordered off the internet</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Alchemy vs Strength + Athletics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist can lift, push, or pull a physical object or person under 100 kg, within their sight and closer than 10 meters. The object moves swiftly, but not rapidly enough to injure a person with the blow; the object may break if it is fragile. The exception: knives or other small metal tools, which the alchemist can wield with a Resolve + Alchemy test, at a two-dice penalty because of the need for precision. A knife used this way does only one point of extra damage.</p>\n<p>Trying to move someone actively resisting requires a contest of Resolve + Alchemy vs Strength + Athletics. On a win, the alchemist can pull the victim within grabbing or clawing range, or throw them one meter for each point of margin on the contest, doing an equal amount of Superficial damage. They land prone. Keeping someone or something floating in mid-air requires a Resolve + Thin-Blood Alchemy (Difficulty 3) roll every turn. Fine manipulation (such as pulling the pin of a grenade) requires a Wits + Alchemy roll at a suitable Difficulty, as determined by the Storyteller.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn unless held (see above).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"284"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"lYStS9Mn":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"DSDKpM63":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.RGaXZegfaVIe0SO6"},"RP8iy9nkzaKFF1Ll":{"name":"Prowess","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires with Potence gain far greater strength from their Blood than those who lack it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When activated, add the Potence rating of the user to their unarmed damage value as well as to feats of Strength, and add half their Potence rating (round up) to their Melee and thrown weapon damage</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"117"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.RP8iy9nkzaKFF1Ll"},"RZtlej2QwmsmBcUb":{"name":"Ignis Fatuus","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can create simple static illusions that can confound a single sense. For instance he can create the sound of horse’s hooves, the smell of hay, the appearance of a shining jewel, or even the feel of a hand pushing inside a pocket. While tactile illusions can be felt, they cannot harm anyone nor do any real damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor of -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Manipulation + Subterfuge roll at a Difficulty of 2. On a Win, The vampire can create illusions that affect a single sense in an area of roughly 7 cubic meters per dot the vampire has in Chimerstry, and the vampire must be able to sense the area in which they create the illusion. For example, they cannot create the illusion of a coin inside a box unless they can see or feel the inside of the box, but they can create the sound of coins rattling around if the box is shaken.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The illusion remains until the vampire chooses to end the illusion or can no longer sense it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"48"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hqRI6uCK":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WmaxKU4Q":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.RZtlej2QwmsmBcUb"},"RdBft4OKExkLvndY":{"name":"Rumor","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Alchemists frequently use this formula during negotiations to try and sway at least one person to their side; however, it can be easy to spot if anyone is paying close enough attention. Rumor allows the alchemist to make a statement and convince one person to agree with them on that statement.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s blood, phlegmatic blood, pepper, oil, and graphite or mercury.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pool:</strong> Manipulation + Alchemy vs Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activation, the alchemist makes a statement and invites the target to agree, e.g. “I heard you plan to invest in the new mortuary downtown. Is that true?” On a win, the target agrees and continues to believe they agreed to the statement of their own free will. To get the target to actually act on their agreement (e.g., sign investment papers) usually requires a further Manipulation + Subterfuge (or similar) contest, but the alchemist can add their margin on the Alchemy contest to their pool in that contest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, and thereafter until something (or someone) persuasively contradicts the target’s agreement (“I would never have agreed to that! I must have been drugged!”).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"105"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"PnoJRVky":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"ogENKw4h":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.RdBft4OKExkLvndY"},"RtR9sI8ABr9IPTYo":{"name":"Haruspex","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Certain oracles, mystics, and necromancers maintain that in those final, fleeting moments before death claims another soul, the dying consciousness opens up to the secrets of the cosmos and witnesses the infinite. With the proper attunement, a vampire can tug on the frayed edges of this insight before it vanishes into the grave, warping it to their own ends to provide hints to their own future.</p>\n<p>Some vampires are content to observe death when it occurs naturally, others develop an unhealthy paranoia when their future’s unknown and take matters into their own hands, killing and torturing to maintain a steady flow of insight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Premonition</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire spends a turn examining the body of the recently dead or dying (they must have died within the scene), during which time they remove and scatter organs, stare into vacant eyes, and otherwise desecrate the corpse. Once complete, they Rouse the Blood and test Resolve + Occult against Difficulty 3. Characters gain a one-die bonus if they killed the victim themselves, or two dice if they’re killed while undergoing the scrying process (such a torturous death likely grants Stains). If successful, the vampire can reroll every die in any single dice pool before the night’s end as the insight prepares them for such an eventuality. On a critical win, they may reroll up to three pools. When a pool is rerolled, the player may choose the most preferable result. This Power only works with mortal victims — vampires and animals provide no spark of insight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"29"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Shk0kIQY":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"6Fnh0YvS":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.RtR9sI8ABr9IPTYo"},"S1rkxEpFY2GGpEOt":{"name":"Irresistable Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Presence of the user becomes a conduit for Dominate. The vampire now only needs their voice to be heard in order to employ Dominate powers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Mnipulation + Insight vs. Composure + Resolve test</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user must touch the subject and win a Manipulation + Presence vs Composure + Resolve test for the power to take effect. (At the Storyteller’s discretion, mortals are affected with no test necessary.) If affected, the victim is afflicted by the Need Compulsion (p. XX), directed at whatever the target wanted most in the current scene, excluding any desire provoked by supernatural means, such as through other Presence powers, as well as desires unattainable in the current scene. At the Storyteller’s discretion, a vampire with Hunger 3 or above gains the Hunger Compulsion instead.</p>\n<p>If the subject’s object of desire is up for bargaining, the user gains a four-dice bonus to any negotiation test.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"124"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"G6AvyuGW":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"6G71cNzy":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.S1rkxEpFY2GGpEOt"},"S9flW9NBm4MFpZM1":{"name":"Dread Gaze","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Briefly exposing their vampiric nature, the user instills a single subject with utter terror. Mortals are cowed, run, or freeze with fear, while other vampires either submit like whipped dogs or flee in frenzied Rötschreck.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Intimidation vs. Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Displaying their fangs, their face twisted in a predatory grimace, the user rolls a contest of Charisma + Intimidation against their target’s Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>• Failure: Mortals are unable to act other than in their own defense and lose their next action, backing off for a turn unless they spend Willpower equal to the users margin of win (to a minimum of one). Vampires are unaffected.</p>\n<p>• Win: Mortals run in fear. Vampires and mortals must make a Terror Frenzy test at a Difficulty of 3. If they succeed, they are still unable to act other than in their own defense and lose their next action for a turn unless they spend Willpower equal to the users margin of win (to a minimum of one).</p>\n<p>• Critical Win (or at least 3+ margins): Mortals freeze or crumple into a fetal position. Vampires enter a Terror Frenzy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn/as normal Frenzy</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"122"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"pgPHU4HQ":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"kZGCrKU9":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.S9flW9NBm4MFpZM1"},"SCRoxk6dqfbWki4i":{"name":"Morphean Blow","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>Healers use sleep to accelerate the healing process. Warriors use sleep to incapacitate enraged foes. Stories abound of Salubri Warriors quelling lupines with a single slap, leaving the beasts vulnerable to the killing blow.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches the target (in combat, any combination of Strength or Dexterity + Brawl or Athletics might be required). After that, the user activates the power as trolls Composure + Resolve against the target’s Composure + Resolve. On a win, the target is forced into sleep from which they awaken if they take a point of health damage. Undead creatures and creatures that require no sleep (or are for some reason unable to sleep) are immune to this power.</p>\n<p>Knowing both Gift of Sleep and Morphean Blow grants an additional two-dice bonus on the roll to force a target to sleep. Anything thicker than a coat (like armour) provides protection from the user’s touch and, if possible, a Called Shot of -1 success (if hands and face are exposed) or -2 (if only one of them is exposed) might be necessary.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> 8 hours or until the subject is awakened</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"191"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.SCRoxk6dqfbWki4i"},"SLc1666mACrdabmF":{"name":"Spirit’s Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>By touching an inanimate object or the ground at a location, the vampire can sense the emotional residue left by those who have handled that object or visited the location in the past. The user gains insight into not only that person, but also what was done and under what circumstances. While rarely crystal clear, the information often provides leads impossible to gain from regular forensics and deduction.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make an Intelligence + Insight roll versus a Difficulty depending on the information sought. Gleaning the emotional state of the user of a murder weapon used a few days before is Difficulty 2, but sensing the surroundings in which a 300-year old mundane letter was written approaches Difficulty 5 or higher. Each point of margin on the roll allows the user to sense roughly one additional previous handler (description, name, type of a creature) and set of circumstances, counting backwards from the most recent.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"34"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"0RFRm8dj":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"HlCxRhkn":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.SLc1666mACrdabmF"},"STIGl7Kxl3niuB1K":{"name":"Vanish","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can activate powers like Cloak of Shadows, Cache and Unseen Passage even while under direct observation. The vampire or (or the object if used with powers like Cache) appears to vanish in the blink of an eye; even the memory of them (or the object) becomes foggy and indistinct.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> As per power augmented</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Subterfuge vs. Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When vanishing in front of a mortal, roll a contest of Wits + Subterfuge vs Wits + Awareness. On a win, the observer questions whether the vampire (or an object) was ever there to begin with; their memory clouds on the topic. With a critical win or with at least 3 margins of successes, the vampire vanishes entirely from the observer’s memory. This power does not affect vampires’ memories, but any win by the user hides them as if they initiated their power unobserved. This power can only be used once per scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"104"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"Ag0JuTqe":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"9sZkeuew":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.STIGl7Kxl3niuB1K"},"Si8WPLueR6KmyASS":{"name":"Tracing the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Though their deep connection to the Beast, the vampire understands when other vampires call upon their Blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Sense the Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character Rouses the Blood and tests Resolve + Awareness (Difficulty 3). Results are based on the margin of success, and all targets must be within their sensory range. Success reveals the target’s general direction (forward, backward, sideways, etc.), whereas a Critical Win allows the user to know precisely which vampire triggered the effect.</p>\n<p>• Basic Win (Margin 0): The vampire can sense when Blood is Roused.</p>\n<p>• Margin 1: The vampire can sense whether the Rouse fueled a universal vampire Trait such as Blood Surge and Blush of Life, or activated a Discipline (and which Discipline it was).</p>\n<p>• Margin 2: The vampire senses exactly which Power was activated and roughly what it does.</p>\n<p>• Margin 3+ (or a Critical Win): The vampire knows precisely which vampire, ghoul or a revenant triggered the effect.</p>\n<p>The Storyteller may call for a Wits + Occult test (Difficulty of a Discipline Power’s level + 2) to recognise rare but familiar Discipline Powers or gain a clearer understanding of the effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"17"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Nm58TP8E":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"LCJ5jsrm":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Si8WPLueR6KmyASS"},"SqeYe3nw5JK0CEXy":{"name":"Ensconce","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire is able to supernaturally conceal small objects in their hands or on their person. The power doesn’t make visible objects invisible. Rather, it makes a searcher ignore them when they’d otherwise notice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can hide a number of small, hand-held objects in their hands or on their person equal to their Obfuscate rating. Mortals ignore dangerous or out-of-place objects that the user is enshrouding or miss them in a search. Vampires with Sense the Unseen or others with supernatural senses can attempt to notice them as usual. Objects in plain view cannot be hidden by this power, nor can animate creatures.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or ended by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"96"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.SqeYe3nw5JK0CEXy"},"SrCpIA2zXZPt6k4L":{"name":"Summon","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can call to themselves any person, mortal or vampire, upon whom they’ve previously used Awe, Entrancement, or Majesty, or who has tasted their Blood at least once. The target knows who is summoning them and the user’s current location. This calling lasts for a few nights usually. After that time, the effect subsides, but the user can repeat it night after night if required to reach the target or if the target is otherwise reluctant. Anyone Summoned feels a pull toward the summoner and tries to reach them, though without endangering themselves physically or financially. They won’t sell their house to buy a ticket or miss a vital meeting, but they might skip out of normal work or social commitments.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Subterfuge vs. Composure + Intelligence</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user needs to concentrate for five minutes and think of the person being summoned, then rolls Manipulation + Presence vs Composure + Intelligence. On a win, the target hears the summoning, but may or may not heed it. Those that do not heed the call suffer a dice penalty to all non-tracker rolls equal to half of the user’s Presence rating, rounded up. A critical win or at least 3 margins on the contest do not halve the user’s Presence rating for the penalty, at which point most people would leave whatever they were doing and rush towards the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night plus one per point of margin</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"125"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"PdNcBC9B":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"8emgQlJt":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.SrCpIA2zXZPt6k4L"},"TC0k8RpLQgQLgJXN":{"name":"Chain of Command","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Those laboring under the will of the vampire drag others along with their actions, demanding help and obedience with a will fueled by their master. By delegating their authority, the vampire’s reach extends that much further.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After delivering a Mesmerize command, the vampire can choose to invoke this power by immediately making another Rouse Check. Those affected become capable of executing slightly more complex tasks — even showing some degree of awareness — as they set about achieving their master’s goal, seeking out other suitable candidates to help them. The target of this power adds the vampire’s rating in Dominate to their Social Attributes (the maximum rating is raised to 4 + Blood Surge value of the vampire) when they make when convincing others to assist them in their objective (and only those tasks), allowing them to better command underlings, delegate tasks, and spread the burden. Those so commanded aren’t operating under any supernatural compulsion and are free to object — the main benefit is making people in authority better equipped to order around those beneath them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the command is carried out or the scene ends, whichever comes first</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"72"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TC0k8RpLQgQLgJXN"},"TIR8kEnS69tKgZQm":{"name":"Predator’s Lure","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire uses their Presence to lure a victim closer, whether for conversation or for bloodier purposes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Subterfuge vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire makes a Rouse Check to target one victim within their line of sight. Mortals are automatically affected. Against supernatural creatures, the user must win a test of Charisma + Subterfuge vs. the victim’s Composure + Resolve. On a win, this Power compels an affected target to approach the user, whether or not the target knows or even is aware of the user. The victim won’t drop everything or risk their safety to approach the user, and the power is ineffective in any kind of violent altercation. They politely finish conversations, complete their transactions, cross streets with caution, and wait at a safe distance until the Power ends or the situation becomes safe.</p>\n<p>After using this power, the victim may recall an intense curiosity or an unexplainable compulsion to investigate or be near a person or location.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until they are within 10 feet (3 meters) of the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"122"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"HQ4nC25W":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"yDzqoShO":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TIR8kEnS69tKgZQm"},"TJLaXdBIwdu2k3cq":{"name":"Madrigral","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>Power of words and suggestions is well understood by even those new to Mepominee. By weaving words with emotions and things that alter someone’s mental state, the vampire can twist others around their little finger. While useful in cultivation of one’s meals, many Daughters of Cacophony love this power for its effect on the crowds before which they perform.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The target must hear at least one full sentence from the vampire for this power to properly work. The vampire rolls Charisma + Performance vs. the target’s Composure + Wits. On a win, the vampire may choose the new Resonance of their target and gains two-dice bonus on all Social tests to take advantage of the Resonance such as by persuading someone of sanguine resonance to engage in carnal behaviour.</p>\n<p>On a critical win or if they won 3 margins on the contest, the vampire may increase or decrease the target’s Temperament by one stage. If this would lead to a dyscrasia, the vampire may choose it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"86"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gr6J2KtA":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"ssylN8B1":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TJLaXdBIwdu2k3cq"},"TQsOAsyBCAOhvW1v":{"name":"Lullaby Of the Damned","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>By humming a mystic tune, the vampire conceals their presence from spirits that would otherwise be immune to the mind-altering effects of Disciplines such as obfuscate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Resolve + Subterfuge vs. Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user hums an unheradable tune and rolls Resolve + Subterfuge roll at a Difficulty of 2, writing down successes for any later detection rolls. On a win, for as long as they keep on humming, the vampire remains ignored by all who do not adhere to the Virtues of Conscience and Self Control (which includes entities without Humanity such as spirits and wights) unless they roll more successes on the Wits + Awareness roll than the vampire did on Resolve + Subterfuge. A vampire drawing too much attention to themselves must roll Resolve + Subterfuge at a difficulty of 3 again and any malign entities nearby may roll theirs again.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene unless the humming stops before that.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"196"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TQsOAsyBCAOhvW1v"},"TTTshfqUGYOPXsZx":{"name":"Short Circuit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>An alchemist can use this formula to overload the batteries in any electronic or electrical equipment, including car batteries or the fuses of a building.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, battery acid, melancholy blood, and salt water.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By making a Rouse Check and touching the equipment (or metal connected to it) the alchemist automatically and silently shorts it out. This allows an alchemist to, for example, cause a blackout of an entire building by touching a power outlet, or a car by brushing against its body.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> N/A</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"105"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TTTshfqUGYOPXsZx"},"TX16TBrDBG7i18ed":{"name":"Fluid Tardiness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Users of Temporis often feel around them that the world is just too slow, even when they are not actively focusing on manipulating time. There is some truth in this, however, as their presence alone is a threat to the stability of the laws of time.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the vampire uses a Blood Surge to augment a Dexterity or Temporis test, they may reroll the Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"167"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TX16TBrDBG7i18ed"},"TdawSe560UhDzd8q":{"name":"Voice of Orpheus","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this rare ability of a rare Discipline, the user may now affect those who dwell in the Lands of the Dead with their voice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Mortis 1 (or some other school of sorcery with necromantic Paths at 1 dot)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s voice penetrates the Shroud with ease, being able to affect denizens of the Shadowland and its visitors from the land of the living. The power also works in reverse, afflicting the land of the living from the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>All powers that can fuflfil the requirements such as Dominate, Presence, Dementation, Melpominee, Lament of D’hainu from Necromancy and so on work as normal and affect others across the shroud.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"87"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TdawSe560UhDzd8q"},"TeqiS7j4XcLgW48g":{"name":"Imposter’s Guise","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>With some preparation the vampire can make themselves appear exactly as a specific individual of any build and gender when using Mask of One Thousand Faces. The user must carefully study the subject, otherwise the charade fails when meeting anyone with more than a casual familiarity with the person being mimicked.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Mask of a Thousand Faces</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> M/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user must study the face to be copied for at least five minutes, from different angles. The user requires another ten minutes of observation to mimic the subject’s voice and mannerisms before activating Mask of a Thousand Faces. The character completely ignores the requirement of margins to copy the victim’s appearance.</p>\n<p>When using Mask of a Thousand Faces, the Obfuscated user has no need for rolls of Performance of Subterfuge to immitate another person, their power of Obfuscate being so strong that it erases any obvious doubt and greases falsity with truth. The character may still be uncovered through powers such as Sense the Unseen, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"105"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.TeqiS7j4XcLgW48g"},"ThlHm13Euxua98ll":{"name":"Flight (•••••)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/flight.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can fly up to 75 miles per hour/120 km per hour and carry up to 200 lbs./100 kg, if their Strength allows for it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"78"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"flight","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ThlHm13Euxua98ll"},"Tx3qogGeyImpkEyY":{"name":"Changeling Ward","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>Externalising their emotional confusion, the vampire creates a ward that disorients and befuddles anyone who sees it. They can inscribe such a ward on an object, a location, or a person. The Vampire can be very clever using this power; one story tells of a Weirdling that drew a ward on an enemy’s shirt as dawn approached, then watched as the unfortunate vampire burned in the sun, unable to remember which way to run.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Subterfuge vs. Wits + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The size of the ward is one meter across for an object or 3 meter radius for a warding circle around a location. Each additional Rouse Check on the ritual activation expands the warding circle’s maximum radius by about 3 meters or two meters across.</p>\n<p>The vampire inscribes a ward symbol in a visible location and the player rolls Intelligence + Occult (Difficulty 3). Anyone entering the warded area or touching the warded object loses two dice from their Intelligence pools as long as they maintain contact or proximity to the ward.</p>\n<p>Additionally, anyone close to the ward or touching the warding circle must succeed on a Wits + Resolve roll against the initial successes of the ward. Those who fail become disoriented and unable to find their way out unless physically dragged out. They also suffer a -3 dice penalty to all non-tracker rolls (doesn’t stack with Intelligence penalty). Leaving the location or finding any sense of direction requires a Wits + Survival (Difficulty equal to the vampire’s initial successes) with those failing suffering a point of Superficial Willpower damage for every missing success.</p>\n<p>The user is immune to their own wards.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The ward lasts for a duration indicated by the number of margins on the vampire’s roll.</p>\n<p>Margins Duration 0 margins One Night 1 margin One week 2 margins One month 3 margins Three months 4 margins One year 5 margins One year + 1 for each margin.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"92"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"QRY47Gax":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"0cjtr3vo":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Tx3qogGeyImpkEyY"},"U7E5ukOZ7ChqpfF6":{"name":"Hag’s Wrinkles","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Perpetual rot makes the character’s flesh malleable. The Samedi can open large folds in her flesh, storing objects in them like a kangaroo’s pouch. She can also massage the slimy flaps of fatty tissue to alter her appearance slightly (though this does nothing for the smell). Other vampires can learn this Discipline, of course, but if they do not possess the “advantage” of having skin that already falls in droops and folds, large wrinkles and bulges may be visible.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A, Stamina + Subterfuge vs. Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user twists their flesh and rolls Stamina + Subterfuge (Difficulty 2) to create pockets within which they may safely hide items up to a volume of their palm. To hide items larger than that, the user gains adds margins on the activation roll as a dice bonus on Stealth and Subterfuge rolls to successfully hide them in pockets.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Vampires retain the effects until they next awaken while mortals would require some kind surgery or magic to return to a “normal” state.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"176"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.U7E5ukOZ7ChqpfF6"},"UA2WulvKoL2Riv2k":{"name":"Mercurian Tongue","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The alchemist can speak the native language of the mortal whose blood they used in the formula, and the native languages of those they feed from until they next sleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, blood of a mortal who speaks a different native language than the alchemist, the tongue of a fish or bird, hot sauce from another country, and copper filings.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist can speak the native language of the mortal whose blood they used in the formula. By spending a Willpower point and feeding from a mortal, they can switch their new language to that mortal’s native language.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night, or until the alchemist feeds from someone with the same native language as the alchemist.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"103"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UA2WulvKoL2Riv2k"},"ULwlBCWchJtxh5r5":{"name":"Shadow Perspective","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can project their senses into any shadow within line of sight, seeing and hearing as if they were hiding within any part of it. This includes their own shadow, as manipulated by Shadow Cast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Following a Rouse Check, the presence of the vampire’s senses in the shadow is undetectable by anything but supernatural means. (Sense the Unseen, for example). While this power is active the vampire perceives both their surroundings as well as what can be gleaned from the Shadow Perspective, as if looking through a screen or hole.</p>\n<p>Supernatural senses like Sense the Unseen may try to perceive that something is in the shadows as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"111"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ULwlBCWchJtxh5r5"},"UN8MW0dh7fOGZqWJ":{"name":"Peacemaker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Watchers are arbiters and negotiators, dealing with hostile supernatural beings on behalf of all Cainites. Keeping the peace is paramount. With this power, out-of-control tempers find restraint and bitter arguments are reduced to differences of opinion. No few Healers have strived to learn this power; many simply believe it to be a more advanced Valeren effect rather than a tool of the mysterious Watchers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Road rating vs. Willpower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The player spends a Rouse Check and a Willpower point to erect the Peacemaker aura, which must be inside a chamber no larger than a ballroom. The vampire’s third eye opens, but sheds no light. The aura lasts for as many turns as they have dots in their Road rating or until they leaves the area, though once used, tempers rarely surge again. The Peacemaker Aura is not a numbing or hypnotic effect, but more clearing one’s head from the distractions of anger. Those under the power’s effect are more inclined to talk out a dispute than reach for the sword. Even Brujah or similarly rage-prone characters find it harder to lose their tempers — the aura mitigates any penalty to Frenzies, and they gain a die bonus to Frenzy Tests as normal.</p>\n<p>Anyone wishing to insult someone or attack under Peacemaker must roll Willpower tracker (against the user’s Road rating) or hesitate. This occurs even in the midst of an active battle, wherein all participants must roll each turn to continue hostilities until the duration expires. This applies to the vampire as well — the aura must be raised in good faith and cannot be used to create an ambush (though nothing stops the vampire from healing allies in case things go south).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn per user’s Road rating</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"198"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UN8MW0dh7fOGZqWJ"},"UR58Gh4CQNdCPbpl":{"name":"Taking the Pain","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user alters the target’s perception of pain, soothing their wounds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches the target and rolls Intelligence + Medicine (Difficulty 2). On a win, the subject is not troubled by pain for the rest of the scene, ignoring any Health penalties and impairments short for physical mutilation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene. A Critical Win or 3+ margins extends it to the rest of the night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"185"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"HgpmSYWm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"aDkwrMKi":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UR58Gh4CQNdCPbpl"},"UW6EKlyGDoibCdN3":{"name":"Exile the Spawn of Typhon","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>It was Typhon, the master of Set who had cast the curse of bloodlust upon Osiris and created the entire race of bloodleeches. Those belonging to the cult of Osiris and use Bardo curse them each sunset and sunrise for making them the way they are and have developed this resentment into a power — by making a sign of banishment in the name of their god, Osiris, they force the spawn of the corruptor to</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full cover before them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Intimidation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By making a physical sign of banishment, they force the corruption to cover and flee before them. The user must have the target within their line of sight within 10 meters for this power to work, rolling Resolve + Intimidation against their target’s Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>On a success, the targeted mortal flees in terror while vampires succumb to a Terror Frenzy. Mortals whose Humanity rating is 7 or above are immune to this power unless they are revenants or inquisitors.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"39"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"aK7zUesv":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"VPp4X6L5":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UW6EKlyGDoibCdN3"},"UdAtyhH3FYbKaMUY":{"name":"Sense With Beasts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Beloved by the Tzimisce, the user shares their senses with animals, seeing and hearing as they do. Animalism through it (or on it). The user may hop from one insect to another for free without any additional rolls.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor of -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve+ Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user focuses on an animal and rolls against a Difficulty of 3. On a win, they may perceive the world through its senses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, although it can be extended by an additional scene by spending a point of Willpower</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"28"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"aKntVY6V":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"i3n5gHkP":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UdAtyhH3FYbKaMUY"},"Uiq0KzqiTbxDYCQC":{"name":"Awaken the Zulo Shape","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>When one considers the primeval forests of Transylvania, the thought demon-haunted often comes to mind. More often than not, the inspiration for that response is a Tzimisce in zulo shape, an eight-foot-tall nightmare of scales and greasy fur, apelike arms tipped with jagged black nails, and a bristling row of spines tracing the length of its backbone. Less traditional appearances look like hellishly malformed animals, some look demonic or atavistic, and some defy any sense of worldly familiarity. Many western and younger Tzimisce incorporate the traits of other vampires in nature, such as leeches, bats, ticks, and mosquitoes, magnified to grotesque.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The transformation takes one turn to complete, during which the user can only defend using their normal dice pools. Once the transformation is complete, the user gains free dots to spend on Vicissitude alterations equal to their Vicissitude rating. Note that Redistribution may be used to increase Attributes in this manner. Alterations all happen within the turn and override any time requirement.</p>\n<p>User’s maximum Physical Attribute rating is increased to 4 + their Blood Surge value while in zulo shape.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire goes to daysleep or ends the power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"207"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Uiq0KzqiTbxDYCQC"},"UvqJv6187b325wxo":{"name":"Blood Sweat","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user coaxes the target’s blood to stir and exit the body through pores and orifices.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. the victim’s Humanity/Road rating</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user focuses on a victim within the line of sight and makes a gesture (those who succeed on a Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge may notice something odd) after which they roll Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Resolve of the target. On a win, the target takes a number of Aggravated Health damage equal to the margins. Creatures with the Hunger tracker have their Hunger increased by one for each point on the margin while undead creatures like vampires have their damage reduced to Superficial.</p>\n<p>Creatures without blood like spirits and demons inhabiting objects may not be targeted.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One action</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"148"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"dp3VQMYC":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"yFvry5Wj":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UvqJv6187b325wxo"},"UwzEiWsaRPnF7qUp":{"name":"Counterfeit (Level 1 Power)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>At level 2, the Alchemist can develop formulae that counterfeit <strong>level 1</strong> powers of other Disciplines (and Blood Sorcery powers that affect the caster’s own Blood, but never Rituals).</p><p>■ <strong>Research:</strong> at least a week per formula.</p><p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Use the counterfeited power’s own cost and system, substituting the Alchemist’s Thin-Blood Alchemy rating for the Discipline rating wherever one is called for. Rename this item after the power you counterfeit and paste in its dice pool.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"285"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.UwzEiWsaRPnF7qUp"},"V92p3RpfB5drdRfF":{"name":"Heaven’s Gate","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The apex of Valeren involves miraculous healing of body and soul, even reversing death itself. The third eye flares gold, forming a bright halo around the user as they gently traces lines around the chakra points of the corpse. The subject’s soul reconstitutes itself, merges with the Salubri, then flows outward from the third eye to visibly reintegrate with the body. With a gasp, the mortal is resurrected, death wounds healing as life is restored.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Three Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may only be used on an individual dead less than a week, who did not die of old age and whose soul was not consumed or destroyed in some manner via Necromancy or Warrior Valeren (or similar effects). It cannot be used to reverse the Embrace. Only the subject’s head is necessary for the resurrection, but it must remain relatively intact, with most pieces present. The vampire washes a corpse of a mortal with the large amount of vitae required by the power and rolls Composure + Resolve against a Difficulty of 3. A win reconstitutes the subject’s soul (wraiths disappear and reappear near the user, transformed by the power), reborn within the body. The users blood spirals outward and heals the death wounds, regenerating the body to perfect health. Forever after, the subject retains traces of the user’s blood, making them a revenant without Family Disciplines, but also without a Family Affliction. They recall the time spent as a wraith (if any), but any spiritual trauma suffered fades into a bright, healing light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"188"},"level":5,"cost":3,"dicepool":{"HgpmSYWm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"aDkwrMKi":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.V92p3RpfB5drdRfF"},"VciXQeQ2cqdth1c4":{"name":"Envelop","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula creates a mist that clings to a victim, blinding it and &lt;&lt;2 8 5&gt;&gt; (in the case of mortals) causing suffocation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, melancholic and phlegmatic human blood, potassium chlorate, smog or halon gas</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Alchemy vs Stamina + Survival</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist activates the power and chooses a target within sight. A swirling mist envelops the target, obscuring their sight and penalizing them three dice from all sight-based detection and ranged attack dice pools. In addition, the alchemist can make the mist suffocate a mortal with a contest of Wits + Alchemy vs. Stamina + Survival. On a win, the target can take no action except coughing and choking; on a critical win, the target loses consciousness. The alchemist can only employ Envelop on single targets, and only on one at a time.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until scene ends or the alchemist ends the effect voluntarily.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"285"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"b8XYq8E1":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"AP5uuKZ5":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.VciXQeQ2cqdth1c4"},"VlVrHzYqNEYW3dpx":{"name":"Wear Their Skin","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By carefully removing a mortal’s face (or entire skin), the user is able to craft themselves a mask or an entire bodysuit to take the form of the one to whom the skin originally belonged. Many a Samedi assassin has employed this power on their missions., frequently infiltrating Sabbat meetings to spy or murder at the behest of the Camarilla.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> First, the user needs to carve out a facemask an entire bodysuit (Dexterity + Medicine or Crafts against a Difficulty of 3 to craft) from a person.</p>\n<p>Once they get into the facemask or the bodysuit, they roll Stamina + Medicine against a Difficulty of 3. On a win, the skin tightly fits around the user and their flesh expands or retracts to accommodate it; the user is a perfect physical copy of the person, matching the height and the voice of their former victim. The user may require additional Subterfuge rolls to imitate the person’s mannerism, however. The user also gains all of the body’s Looks Merits and gains the Looks Flaws.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The skin of the target rots after three days even if it was supernaturally preserved, making it useless for the purposes of this power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"179"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"MEjoN5vE":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"oqGrUUwK":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.VlVrHzYqNEYW3dpx"},"Vp6nfTPqWtqYuLE1":{"name":"Lightning Strike","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>Faster than the eye can follow, the vampire can strike with fist or melee weapon at such speed that the opponent is unable to defend or take evasive action.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Use before making a Brawl or Melee attack. The opponent makes no roll to dodge or defend; make the attack at Difficulty 1. An opponent possessing Celerity 5 or Temporis 5 can nullify this power by making their own Rouse Check, defending at the same speed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A single attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"46"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Vp6nfTPqWtqYuLE1"},"VsTmj728hy8YlyjE":{"name":"Walk with Caine","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Banu Haqim sneer at the Tremere’s ability to thicken the blood, with the Warlocks obviously fooled that somehow the strength of one’s blood gives one authority. No – it is how close one is to the first vampire, Caine, that matters. Is Haqim not the greatest of the Antediluvians, regardless of how much his sleep dilutes his Blood? The one who follows the laws laid by Caine himself and, through them, has constructed the most organised and the most powerful of Clans? With Walk with Caine, Banu Haqim sorcerers are able to call upon the vitae of their ancestors that is forever tied to theirs to, at least temporarily, lower their Generation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a roll of Resolve + Occult. For each success, the user lowers their Generation by one, up to the 4th Generation. The user’s blood thickens to the minimum of their new Generation. Diablerising a person with this power counts their normal Generation and Blood Potency Same goes with siring and other such things.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"149"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.VsTmj728hy8YlyjE"},"Vxkw9ZGFZBmAPmZO":{"name":"Blood Forging","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Quietus Sanguinis allows for alteration of materials, making sorcerers the experts on creating supernatural items. With Blood Forging, an item will not only last forever, but also be able to interact with the incorporeal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Blood Tampering</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> </p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Items enchanted by Blood Tampering can now physically interact with, defend from and and harm spirits even if they are immaterial. It should be noted that ghosts are not spirits as they have an ectoplasmic corpus. Demons and jinn count as spirits.</p>\n<p>Enchanted weapons harm spirits the same they do to mortals, dealing Aggravated Health damage if bladed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"146"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Vxkw9ZGFZBmAPmZO"},"W6WkHsoNpxTkVbZs":{"name":"Soul Mask","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Princes and ashen priests guarding their courts and cathedrals typically post as guards at least some Cainites with Scry the Soul. The ability to see the auras and read thoughts of those coming to see the potentate are hard to overestimate. Although Scry the Soul is not a lie detector, it does identify ghouls, vampires, diablerists and the disturbed with great ease. Masters of Obfuscate, however, can walk past such surveillance without concern. A Methuselah may appear to be a simple whelp, a mortal or even to have no halo whatsoever. This ability also aids against reading of thoughts, providing additional layer of secrecy to the vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Intelligence + Subterfuge against a Difficulty of 1 to 3 (or more), depending on the sophistication of the ruse they want to enact, with 1 representing a simple masking of their emotional state to 2 for manipulating the auras such as that of diablerie or the type of creature to 3 and beyond for elaborate alternate personas complete with false thoughts and memories. The margin on the win is added as Difficulty to any attempt to “read” them. Failing to reach the added Difficulty lets the reader see only the false personality the user has crafted, without any hint that the reader is being misled. (Failing to reach even the standard Difficulty yields nothing, as normal, so as not to let on that a false personality is present.).</p>\n<p>Characters with a Discipline rating of the power used (like Scry the Soul and Telepathy of Auspex or Aura Absorption power of Mytherceria) lower than the user’s Obfuscate rating automatically fail to reach the added Difficulty no matter what. In such cases, the user does not suffer any negative effects such as that of Aura Absorption nor does the user of such powers gain any mechanical benefits.</p>\n<p>Soul Mask cannot conceal Clan Banes such as that of the Children of Haqim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"103"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"wZnTTAPh":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"7VTGX0YL":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.W6WkHsoNpxTkVbZs"},"WGKdoR25Z27mhtp0":{"name":"Face of Death","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By channeling decay into their body, the user is able to assume the visage of a cadaver to safely hide among the dead – should the need arise.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free, Minor -2 when used against others</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A, Manipulation + Medicine vs. Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activation, the user lowers their Looks Merit by a single stage (Beautiful to normal, normal to Ugly, etc.) as the user’s flesh rots. The user loses a dot of Dexterity to a minimum of 1 and assumes the visage of a corpse, giving them a dice bonus equal to the user’s Thanatosis rating to a Composure + Subterfuge roll to pretend to be a corpse, contested by Awareness to recognise the user as something other than an ordinary corpse. Should the user walk in such a state, it will be easy for them to cause others to flee in horror, giving the user a dice bonus to Intimidation equal to their Thanatosis rating against mortals unaccustomed to the supernatural.</p>\n<p>The user may also target others with this power, paying the cost of a Rouse Check and rolling Manipulation + Medicine vs. the target’s Stamina + Medicine or Fortitude if they possess it (or a flat difficulty of 3 if the victim is not resisting). On a win, the victim gains the aforementioned benefits counting the user’s Thanatosis rating as their own and they gain the aforementioned drawbacks.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Vampires retain the effects until they next awaken while mortals would require some kind surgery or magic to return to a “normal” state.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"175"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WGKdoR25Z27mhtp0"},"WIL6TWkGL2JniOz1":{"name":"Investiture of the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>The tainted blood of those using Daimonion offers boons in a similar manner as demons to their thralls. Many Baali believe that this power is the expressed will of the Children of the Abyss, compelling vampires to rule over demons using their own divine powers. Others think that this display of infernal power stems from an ancient deal between the progenitors of Daimonion or the Baali.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire gains a number of Arcana (see p. XX for demon systems and powers) dots equal to their Daimonion rating + their Blood Potency. Temporarily higher Blood Potency from powers like Blood of Potency also applies. You may find some samples and their dot rating on p. XX. These Arcana do not count towards the maximum in case the vampire with this power becomes a thrall or takes the Infernal Birthright power.</p>\n<p>Weirdly enough even to Cainite standards, the vampire is able to power Arcana given by Daimonion with Rouse Checks instead of Faith Checks on an equal basis, and they may even reroll them if their Blood Potency would allow a power of such a dot rating to be rerolled.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"54"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WIL6TWkGL2JniOz1"},"WTBMRGVI45p67BrP":{"name":"Profane the Holy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire creates and weaponises corruption and doubt even in places it did not exist. the victim begins to deteriorate rapidly. The exposed flesh of those with True Faith rapidly decays; holy symbols rot, tarnish, or crumble at unnatural speed. Holy water turns black as tar or simply vapourises. When using this power, the vampire unknowingly makes an unnatural sound composed of syllables seldom heard among Kine or even vampires. These syllables, thought of as parts of a larger name by demonologists, cannot be properly remembered or even reconstructed by the vampire or those hearing this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire looks at a user of True faith or at a holy symbol or a relic and rolls Resolve + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve of the target or at a difficulty set by the storyteller if looking at a holy symbol. “Mundane” holy symbols are always at a Difficulty of 1 while relics the hunters use are typically 2-3. Holier relics go much higher.</p>\n<p>On a win against a holy symbol, it corrodes and is rendered inert. A win against a person of True Faith inflicts them with a penalty on all True Faith-related rolls equal to the vampire’s Daimonion rating. The person of True Faith is also staggered and loses their next action. The vampire can repeat this contest each subsequent turn, without the need for additional Rouse Checks or a minor action penalty; however, the opponent loses no more actions. Losing the contest ends this power and makes the target immune to this power from the same vampire for the rest of the night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the victim wins the contest or the scene ends</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"53"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Shk0kIQY":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"6Fnh0YvS":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WTBMRGVI45p67BrP"},"WUDjkM0FgeE703Kr":{"name":"Mastery over the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user coaxes the target’s blood to stir and exit the body through pores and orifices.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon purchasing this power, the user acquires Taste of the Soul, Taste of the Body and Taste of the Mind and their bonuses are increased to two dice (which scales with power bonus as normal). Blood Talk’s bonus is increased the three dice. If the user already possessed any of the aforementioned powers, the player may select other level 1 powers (or the power is simply refunded). Additionally, the damage of Blood Sweat is Aggravated even to vampires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"148"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WUDjkM0FgeE703Kr"},"WYl5hBMnj59Govia":{"name":"Crash Down","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Their strength an elemental force, the vampire can slam their fist or foot into the ground, creating a shockwave that throws their opponents prone. One of the more dramatic applications of Potence, this power needs to be carefully employed, lest the user literally bring the house down upon themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Soaring Leap</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Strength + Athletics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon using Soaring Leap, the vampire may activate Crash Down, and deal damage to a small area of effect. Anyone within a three-meter radius is subject to an attack roll of the user’s Strength + Athletics against their Dexterity + Athletics, suffering Superficial Damage equal to the margin. Anyone suffering three or more levels of damage or a total failure on their defense test is knocked down.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One use/turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"118"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"dk0atNlo":{"path":"attributes.strength"},"W7OJbN2p":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WYl5hBMnj59Govia"},"WbnDfit31YhPcU7g":{"name":"Unerring Aim","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The world around them slowing to a crawl, the vampire can aim and throw or fire any weapon at a target as if the target were stationary.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Use before making a ranged attack. The target makes no roll to dodge or defend; make the attack at Difficulty 1. An opponent possessing Celerity 5 can nullify this powerby making their own Rouse Check, defending at the same speed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A single attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"45"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WbnDfit31YhPcU7g"},"WbpVliMWYvCApeuu":{"name":"Necrotic Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By reaching the pinnacle of Thanatosis, the user becomes a master of necrotic energies, allowing them to lay waste on entire populations with ease.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Putrefaction</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Putrefaction now lasts for the rest of the scene or until ended, whichever comes first. In addition to the Crippling Injury selected by the user, the victim also rolls one randomly, possibly suffering torpor. Mortals struck with Putrefaction immediately go into a comma, should the user wish to augment this Putrefaction with Necrosis.</p>\n<p>• Thanatosis rating is 7 for all purposes of the Discipline, most notably scaling damage.</p>\n<p>• Putrescent Servitude: Zombies and zombified ghouls that are struck down to 0 Health revive once per story, healing a point of Aggravated Health damage. A person cannot be revived if they’ve been destroyed by fire or atomised in some other manner or slain by True Faith or decapitation. Resurrection happens at will of the vampire, either a turn after they’ve been slain or a couple of days over when it is safe.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"181"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WbpVliMWYvCApeuu"},"WdHVbiKLsryqgGgF":{"name":"Wolf’s Lament","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The wolf’s howl echoes across the land, carrying messages in its mournful song. Nosferatu and Gangrel with this Amalgam may do the same, enabling them to howl messages that carry for miles. Even better, any wolf that hears the message relays the sound with its own howl which may then be relayed by other wolves and so on. Such messages can theoretically carry for dozens or even hundreds of miles in this fashion, subject to the vagaries of chance. Most individuals who hear the howl merely note its eerie tones and give it no further attention. Those with the Animalism power Feral Speech hear a voice in the keening. The content is brief and clipped, but it imparts emotional inflection and general intent. It can warn of danger, signal an attack or carry any other simple message.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Feral Speech (Animalism ••)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Prior to using this power, the player must convey their intended message to the Storyteller and receive approval. Generally, any message that can be conveyed in one breath is acceptable. The player then rolls Manipulation + Animal Ken (Difficulty 0) as the vampire howls. The message carries a maximum distance determined by the margins according to the following chart, although the Storyteller may restrict the range if they deem there are few wolves in the area. Failure means no wolves pick up the howl, either because they are not present or disregard the message. A Total Failure or a Botch invariably attracts unwanted attention, such as hunters or enraged Lupines.</p>\n<p>Margins Distance 1 8 km/5 miles 2 16 km/10miles 3 80 km/50 miles 4 160 km/100 miles 5 400 km/250 miles 6+ Each further margin doubles the previous value</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the wolves stop howling, which could take an entire night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"99"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WdHVbiKLsryqgGgF"},"Wl0awqOcRr8Ycqfv":{"name":"Intangible Vessel","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can cause a nearby object to temporarily lose its tangibility for mortals and supernaturals, making it untouchable for anyone other than the vampire and those they desire to touch it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user targets an inanimate object within line of sight and within 10 metres of them. Only the user and those they allow can touch this object. Other beings — mortal, animal or supernatural — cannot interact with it, as they seem to go straight through it. The object will rest on a surface whilst not held by the user, but will fall through anything else (such as a hook).</p>\n<p>Objects protected by magic are unaffected by this power. The object doesn’t appear any different at a glance, but a person can make a Wits Mytherceria vs the current bearer’s Wits + Resolve. On a win for the current bearer, the power fails.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until intentionally ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"91"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Wl0awqOcRr8Ycqfv"},"WrXhOU2lkOH6MUyX":{"name":"Defy Bane","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>By preparing themselves with an expenditure of power, the vampire can make themselves temporarily resistant to fire and sunlight as well as other grievous wounds that would threaten them with Final Death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Survival (to activate reflexively)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user can convert a number of Aggravated damage equal to their Fortitude rating to Superficial damage when sustained. They may not heal that Superficial damage for the rest of the scene. This power converts a number of damage per scene, not per wound or per attack. The user can renew this power once expired by making another Rouse Check. If endangered unexpectedly, the user can activate this power reflexively with a Wits + Survival roll (Difficulty 3) upon receiving Aggravated Health damage. If the user fails the test, the power does not activate; if they win the test, they must make a Rouse Check to pay for the power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Example:</strong> Salman has Fortitude 3. During the upcoming brawl, he could for example convert 2 points of Aggravated damage to Superficial, then convert 1 more point of Aggravated damage taken the next turn before his power expires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until expired, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"81"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"wpN7dSr9":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"RvWHD3Nd":{"path":"skills.survival"}},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WrXhOU2lkOH6MUyX"},"WytUW3md48A96VR9":{"name":"Ghost’s Passing","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can bestow a measure of subtlety to the animals under their influence, masking their steps from mundane methods of tracking.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire feeds the activating Rouse Check’s worth of blood to a single animal to activate the power. Animals under the user’s influence that the user targets with this power leave no track or trace. They are also more subtle, and have a two-dice bonus to most Stealth tests.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One session</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"98"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.WytUW3md48A96VR9"},"X9mrZFblhFOtpwDW":{"name":"Shadow Play","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>While this ability does not allow characters to create shadows that do not already exist, it allows one to manipulate, move, and animate ambient shadows. This can grant social effects, such as using shadows to make yourself and your allies more imposing, or shape an environment to suit your needs, creating areas of shadow in which to hide a person or object.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user adds one die to a character’s Intimidation and Stealth dice pools for the remainder of the scene. While this power is active, characters may also move, change, and animate shadows, which can include separating them from their original source. This can include moving shadows in such a way as to allow the character to see better, which reduces any penalty incurred by darkness by the amount of the character’s Obtenebration rating.</p>\n<p>The user may also manipulate shadows to cover others with the total square meters of of animated shadow equal to twice the user’s Obtenebration rating in meters/yards. In this way, allies can benefit from an additional die to Intimidation and Stealth dice pools.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows the user may solidify shadows in order to maneuver or pick up items no heavier than an old medieval key (or car keys, in modern era) or a lever.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"107"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.X9mrZFblhFOtpwDW"},"XSqojhShBp2g0m5a":{"name":"Fortify the Inner Facade","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Instead of hardening the vampire’s physical frame, this power allows the user to protect their thoughts and emotions from supernatural prying. Their mind appears completely completely blank while their aura is, for lack of better words, flat.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Increases the Difficulty of using Scry Soul (Auspex 3), Telepathy (Auspex 5), and similar mind-reading or emotion-perceiving powers on the user by half their Fortitude rating (round up). If the rules allow the user to resist these powers, they add their Fortitude rating to their dice pool instead.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive, but it can be dropped by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"82"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.XSqojhShBp2g0m5a"},"Xk1Pl6Bi3dDxjv37":{"name":"Cleansed in Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By using the power of vitae to cleanse and restore, the vampire releases their blood, allowing it to wash over another and carry away spiritual impurities from outside influences. This power cleanses the target’s soul, purging their mind of externally imposed supernatural taints and stains.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> See below</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character baptizes the forehead of the intended subject with their Blood, then places their hands on the subject’s head. Both parties spend a minimum of 10 minutes in deep concentration per power that the subject wishes to eliminate.</p>\n<p>After that, the user makes a Intelligence + Occult roll with the difficulty being the initial successes of the power or Ritual that inflicted the effect the user is trying to purge. On a win, the Blood the user used turns into red mist and vanishes together with the supe+rnatural effect.</p>\n<p>Cleansed in Blood can erase current and ongoing lasting influences from Blood Sorcery and its curses, Daimonion, Dominate, Dementation, Presence or similar effects including those coming from other supernatural creatures such as demons. But does not shield the subject from or ward them against those powers in the future.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"147"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Xk1Pl6Bi3dDxjv37"},"XmTCIpDOIR9xa1gP":{"name":"Brutal Feed","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Known as the “Savage Kiss”, this power allows the user to employ an unholy inner strength when draining a victim. In mere seconds, the attacker swallows torrents of blood while mauling the victim. The result is an efficient, if messy, feeding often employed in the heat of battle where the mangled remains of the victim can be disguised.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can drain a human completely in seconds, usually within a single turn. Every point of Hunger slaked causes one point of Aggravated Health damage to the victim, as their blood vessels burst and organs bruise and rupture internally. Using Brutal Feed on a vampire does only Superficial Health damage to their dead and inert organs. Brutal Feed does not trigger the Kiss.</p>\n<p>The victim first takes bite damage, followed by a number of automatically successful feeding actions up to the user’s Potence rating (see harmful feeding against mortals). Against vampires, the number of feeding actions is halved (round down). The victim first takes bite damage, then damage from this power. Of course, Armor may protect from the bite itself, as normal. To call this painful would be an understatememt, for which reason few who adhere to Humanity exercise this power on a regular basis.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One feeding/turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"117"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.XmTCIpDOIR9xa1gP"},"Xrj5OZDFcu5GuXqi":{"name":"Clio’s Kiss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Named for the muse of history, Clio’s Kiss circumvents the tides of time by projecting a vampire into the past, allowing her to retrieve something (or someone) to be carried forth to the present. Students of history might employ this power to verify the facts of past events, whereas others may derive value in seeking counsel or fetching long-lost items buried by the sands of time. A previous coordinated effort by True Brujah to recover their progenitor met with cataclysmic disaster, yet despite the catastrophe, rumors persist that a second attempt is being planned.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Rewind</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Stamina + Occult at a Difficulty of 2 with margins determining the length of time that a character may project back just like with Rewind.</p>\n<p>Clio’s Kiss manifests in a spherical radius, roughly equivalent in volume to that of a ballroom (Storyteller’s discretion), transforming all conscious beings within its reach into invisible disembodied observers. They have awareness of their surroundings and may move about freely, but may take no other actions. The vampire who activates the power may elect to interact with the environment or remain invisible at her discretion. To the vampire and their passengers, anything outside its circumference appears as simply vacuous space, or the absence of it. Those under the effects of this power are restricted from exiting until the vampire who activated the power chooses to end it, or loses consciousness. Beings and objects from the past may enter and exit the space freely and remain unaware this power is active without supernatural perception, such as Hourglass of the Mind. If the vampire chooses to appear visible, she may be perceived naturally by those in the past existing outside the radius of its reach, though the vampire can not perceive them unless they enter the boundaries of Clio’s Kiss.</p>\n<p>To bring an object or individual from the past forward through time requires the expenditure of a dot of Willpower and ends the scene, returning all participants and the target (and only the target) to the present. Time is a self-correcting force. The vampire cannot bring forward objects and beings from the past that have a meaningful role to fulfill after the point in time this power travels back to, such as the jester who poisons the king to spark a major war a decade after the arrival of the vampire. An object or being located in one’s travels to the past that continues to survive within the temporal present can not be brought forward; their continued existence in the past can not be superseded, as to suggest otherwise would conflict with the evolution of history. The manipulations of a Cainite from the future traveling to the past, more often than not, serve to assist the very event they attempt to divert. The outcome simply finds some other path with which to conform to history’s original trajectory, try as a Cainite might to affect a desirable alternative. However bitter, Carthage will fall; warnings fall on deaf ears and all actions taken to divert its course serve only to entrench the outcome further. Storytellers should supplant narration in place of dice rolls to ensure time’s infallibility.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See system</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"173"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GROSowAW":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"xLrErR9J":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Xrj5OZDFcu5GuXqi"},"Y1OuY0sMj4RBZHNd":{"name":"See with Lies","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>See with Lies allows the user of Chimerstry to extend their own senses through their illusions, possibly giving them the ability to permanently control them from afar if used correctly or craft illusions like birds through which they may soar into the sky and see with.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Concentration counts as a Full Action</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One point of Willpower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user may choose to sense as their illusions do, feeling, hearing and viewing eveything from their perspective by spending a point of Willpower. These illusions may even be tapped into during daysleep, but the sight of sunlight still provokes a Frenzy. If the vampire succumbs to Frenzy during daysleep, they may not use this power for the rest of their sleep and wake up in a Frenzy.</p>\n<p>This power does not allow Disciplines such as to be used through them unless the user possesses Horrid Reality power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Spreading your senses through an illusion lasts for a scene per point of Willpower spent with three Willpower points being enough to cover the entire night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"51"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Y1OuY0sMj4RBZHNd"},"Y8gFJbD6SJ6MIZQS":{"name":"Father’s Judgment","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Blood is Caine’s gift. The Salubri pronounce judgment on those who misuse it. His third eye glowing as silver as Raphael’s soft halo, the Unicorn renders a portion of the target’s vitae inert, blocking them from powering their Disciplines or healing their undead frame by sheer effort of will. The target feels their blood seize within them, clotting into dead dark chunks. Even powerful elders fear repeated use of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user looks at their victim and rolls Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve of the victim. On a win, the target counts their Hunger as higher by the number equal to the margin for the purposes of activation but not for the purposes of Hunger Dice. This affects all creatures making Blood Rouse Checks, from vampires to revenants and ghouls. To unclog this blockage, the victim must spend a number of Willpower points equal to the margin.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"188"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"5M8T8KFz":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"tbPsnbK6":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Y8gFJbD6SJ6MIZQS"},"YPUZjizwutcxus6H":{"name":"Unweave","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user is able to push their senses of time even further, now able to actively manipulate their own sense of time as they perceive the world even more slowly, allowing them to parry the attacks with ease.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dexterity + Athletics or Melee</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When active, this power lets the vampire experience time at an even slower pace. The user does not suffer diminishing dice pools when defending against multiple ranged attacks using Dexterity + Athletics, able to quite literally see arrows and bullets fly past at them. They may now also parry projectiles with Dexterity + Melee They add their their Temporis rating to all attempts to dodge ranged attacks or parry projectiles while this power is active.</p>\n<p>If the character is attacking a person with Celerity using Weaving or vice versa, subtract each other’s Discipline rating from the dice bonus, down to 0. Two Temporis users also subtract each other’s rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene unless ended sooner by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"169"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"QCd3LQ6F":{"path":"attributes.dexterity"},"lga5qo1G":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.YPUZjizwutcxus6H"},"YWYvnkwEUKvL2rc1":{"name":"Blissful Gaze","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The mere presence of a Salubri Healer heralds victory. To those within her gaze, wounds knit themselves together, and even a steel kiss tastes sweet.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Willpower point in addition to standard power cost</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power enhances Valeren powers of every Path. After paying the power’s cost and additionally spending a point of Willpower, the third eye’s light spears out, lancing through the target, and powers no longer require touch to be used, merely a line of sight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"188"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.YWYvnkwEUKvL2rc1"},"Yg2e9YJyRVf4E4VG":{"name":"Sculpt the Flowing Wound","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Common among Tzimisce knights, a vampire with this power may vastly accelerate their healing by concentrating and willing flesh to mold back to its original form.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Vicissitude ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire activates this power as they would Mending and heals a number of Superficial Health damage equal to their Blood Surge value. Any healing beyond this limit extends into subsequent turns until complete. One Crippling Injury that isn’t based on having an entire limb removed may be healed as point of Superficial Health.</p>\n<p>This power also fully ceases any form of bleeding such as that that induced by Flaying Touch. The power may also be used on others if the vampire possesses Transmogrify the Mortal Clay (Vicissitude</p>\n<p>••), but it requires a whole scene or 5 turns to work. For mortals, this power may heal Aggravated Health damage as two points of Superficial.</p>\n<p>This power is activated in place of Mending and does not count for the maximum of one supernatural power activated per turn.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive/See System.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"81"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Yg2e9YJyRVf4E4VG"},"Yi7IaDSqhQ0chKYT":{"name":"Slow Walker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Like a Celerity user, the adept of Temporis is able to, in the eyes of those who do not possess Celerity or Temporis, seemingly teleport from one location to the other. In reality, the user of Temporis simply slows the time in the area to traverse a distance without interacting with objects directly as that would violate the strongest laws of time.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire moves towards a target, covering any distance under 10 × their Temporis rating in meters while still having enough time to perform an action, such as an attack, during the turn. While moving, the world is essentially grinded to a halt, but the user can not stay in one place and interacting with anything stops the power due to the temporal paradox. Vampires engaging a foe with this power act as if already engaged when the turn begins. This does not permit a surprise attack by itself.</p>\n<p>When there are multiple users of Slow Walker, they treat each other as moving the same speed. Slow Walker overrides Blink (Celerity •••) unless the user possessess greater or equal Celerity rating to the user’s Temporis rating and makes an additional Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"170"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Yi7IaDSqhQ0chKYT"},"YuE4vlCqqzJUXjcJ":{"name":"Scorpion’s Curse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Unlike the warriors, the variant of the Scorpion’s Touch among sorcerers can last for days and act as a ward, preventing unwanted visitors and theft. Warriors greatly appreciate when their armour is coated in the Scorpion’s Curse by their Sorcerer cousins.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> 5 minutes, Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free, blood required is neglible</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user, in a similar manner to blood sorcerers, writes a protective spell on an object and makes a roll at a Difficulty of 3, taking 5 minutes. The “Ward” can only cover the space of about a meter across and only lasts for a number of days equal to the caster’s Quietus rating due to the transmuted poison vitae losing power (as this is not real sorcery). The “Ward” is triggered upon any contact with the skin/body of another — gloves and armour offer no protection. The ward works on every being ranging from mortals to spirits (except ghosts) and does not stack with other contact poisons.</p>\n<p>Once triggered the victim makes a Stamina + Occult roll against the initial number of successes when the Ward was made. Upon the win of the Quietus user, the victim suffers unhalved Superficial Health damage. Should the victim succeed, the poison of that specific Ward can no longer harm them specifically. These wards may be perceived as the Wards of Blood Sorcery (p. XX)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Weeks equal to the Quietus rating. Weeks turn to months on a Critical win or if the sorcerer scores at least 3 margins on the test.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"146"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"8aGEm9o5":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"41UW556c":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.YuE4vlCqqzJUXjcJ"},"YvXtxfKQFX8Ay0E2":{"name":"Grace of the Passed","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>A more utilitarian enhancement to Ending the Watch, it allows the user to syphon the memories and experiences of the subject.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Ending the Watch</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Ending the Watch now allows you to swap a number of Skills equal to margins with the victim, up to the user’s rating in Warrior Valeren. A Critical Win (or 3+ margins on Ending the Watch roll) also transfers a number of specialties equal to the user’s Warrior Valeren to the target for the duration of the power.</p>\n<p>The vampire also gains the sum of the subject’s memories, making this power excellent in investigations which is why this property of Ending the Watch is rarely spoken of publicly to avoid fueling the flames of paranoia against the Salubri.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive/the Skills and Specialties last for a number of days equal to the margins, but the user may only “hold” up to the Skills of a single person at a time.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"192"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.YvXtxfKQFX8Ay0E2"},"YwAJfpHnI7ybEx3E":{"name":"Cat’s Grace","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire gains a balance and grace equal to and surpassing world-class trapeze artists. They can walk and even run across ledges and wires effortlessly and can keep their balance on the slimmest of supports.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user automatically passes any Dexterity- or Athletics-based roll needed to keep their balance. Note that this power does not allow them to balance on support that cannot take their weight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"42"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.YwAJfpHnI7ybEx3E"},"Z9WitTHwpeJ3Pmo0":{"name":"Feral Weapons","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can extend their natural weapons to monstrous proportions. This usually takes the form of fingernails extending into wicked talons but can also come in other forms such as fangs elongating into veritable daggers, as from a gigantic serpent.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No skill roll is needed to activate this power. When activated, the vampire adds a +2 modifier to their Brawl damage, and causes Aggravated Health damage to mortals. Superficial damage inflicted from Feral Weapons is not halved.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire falls to sleep or torpor or ends the power</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"128"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Z9WitTHwpeJ3Pmo0"},"ZI5UcW3r3PaSKEcb":{"name":"Fluent Strength","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire is able to call upon their supernatural strength effortlessly, and rarely tempt their Hunger when doing so.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the vampire uses a Blood Surge to augment a Strength or Potence test, they may reroll the Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"116"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ZI5UcW3r3PaSKEcb"},"ZIgYLpTgh52KD95A":{"name":"Mosh Pit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>With but a syllable, the vampire’s voice enflames all the humours of rage within a person, turning them into a violent beast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the target’s Resonance is changed at choleric and, if they are a vampire or another creature with the vampiric Beast, they must make a fury frenzy test against a difficulty of 3.</p>\n<p>Mortals who lose this context experience a fury frenzy similar to vampires, unless they spend a number of Willpower points equal to the users margin of win.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the frenzy ends. The Resonance changes back once the effect ends as well.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"86"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gr6J2KtA":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"ssylN8B1":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ZIgYLpTgh52KD95A"},"ZWrmiihIOoQEK0bw":{"name":"Apep’s Venom","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>While later levels of Serpentis promise great forms of ancient god-like entities, huge cobras of shadow or half-human half-snake creatures tend to make people run away as fast as they can. This Serpentis power enables a vampire to gain the deadly bite without the full-body transformation, making it more useful for taking victims by surprise. The city guard do ask questions when someone dies from a snake bite under unlikely circumstances, so Apep’s Venom still requires some discretion in its use.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire’s fangs become hollow, more slender and venomous. The user does not suffer a Called Shot penalty to bite attacks. Upon a successful bite attack, they deliver a deadly venom. Targets bitten must roll Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude, if they possess it) against a Difficulty equal to the user’s Serpentis rating. Nearby persons may attempt the same Test using Intelligence + Medicine to heal others, and a single Rouse Check’s worth of vampire vitae is enough to dispel the poison. On a failure, the victim is paralysed as their nerves and muscles turn to liquid and they suffer Aggravated Health damage equal to the user’s Serpentis rating each turn until they are healed, usually leading to a quick and agonising death. Vampires and other durable entities suffer only Superficial Health damage instead and may spend a point of Willpower to ignore the paralysis for a turn and retake the Stamina + Medicine/Fortitude Test.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the victim is healed, resists, or dies.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"152"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ZWrmiihIOoQEK0bw"},"ZZ2SEY9o5dY51Wiv":{"name":"Fireskin","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Originally created to safely superheat Athanor Corporis, the Fireskin formula allows Kindred — and mortals brave enough to gulp it down — to literally plunge into fire without fear. The key ingredient of salamander (p. 134) bone tastes like horrible, bitter ash, but when the drinker chokes it down, it briefly lends its original fiery sympathies.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, powdered elemental salamander bone, tobacco, sulfur.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whoever drinks the Fireskin formula feels like their skin and the air around them is strangely cold — at odds with the little flames that start to lick up and down their skin. The fire doesn’t spread from them, but their body is superheated and painful to the touch. They do +1 fire damage when they strike with their body (only the additional damage counts as fire), and can increase that to +2 if they manage to grapple someone. Fire does no damage to the user during this period, but cold damage is doubled.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"76"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ZZ2SEY9o5dY51Wiv"},"ZwQAgDpFr12WK2iE":{"name":"Reveal Temperament","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can smell the Resonance of the target, as well as any Dyscrasia they harbor in their blood. They can also smell if another vampire has recently fed as well as the Resonance (if any) of their latest victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. Composire vs. Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When the power is active the user can test Intelligence + Insight vs a target’s Composure + Subterfuge. A win reveals the Resonance of the mortal observed as well as any other particulars of their blood, such as Dyscrasias. The user also gains a two-dice bonus to appropriate Resonance and Temperament-related interactions with the target for the duration of the scene as they are able to adapt their approach to the emotional state of the mortal. Versus a vampire, a win reveals the Resonance of the last mortal they fed upon, and a critical win provides a more nuanced picture of the vessel at time of feeding, revealing the method and Predator Type of the vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"25"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"mRPpUSfI":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"TPlQgpAo":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ZwQAgDpFr12WK2iE"},"Zy2s0CyZat8EMK3Z":{"name":"Flight (••••)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/flight.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can fly up to 60 miles per hour/90 km per hour and carry up to 100 lbs./50 kg, if their Strength allows for it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"78"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"flight","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.Zy2s0CyZat8EMK3Z"},"a22KBXhU7gQWYCk6":{"name":"Echo of the Subtle Vizier","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>A Lasombra with this power may silently whisper commands to a target’s shadow without ever making a sound. The commands remain silent until the desired duration passes, at which time the victim finally hears the vampire’s words and faces the Dominate power employed in the command.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the Dominate power used</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire must see the target’s shadow as they activate a Dominate power, suspending it by one hour for each success rolled. Suspension is based on precise time given by the vampire. Once the dictated time occurs, the subject hears the vampire’s voice inside their head, at which point they make their resistance roll to see whether or not they succumb to the power.</p>\n<p>One of the more cunning uses of this power is to suspend Forgetful Mind so that it retroactively erases all memories during or after the time the power was suspended. The vampire may then safely suspend other Dominate commands on the target, knowing the servant will remember none of their actions or that they heard the vampire’s voice issue these commands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One hour per success on the power activation roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"108"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.a22KBXhU7gQWYCk6"},"aAZAhHbTDuFJ4fPB":{"name":"Condemnation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Children of Baal relish the opportunity to importune dark energies into the waking world. With this power, the vampire levies a curse upon a victim, drawing upon the tormented might of their blood to work their own will upon reality.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The victim must be in the line of sight (magical or physical) and the curser must know the victim’s name (or a celestial name, for a demon). The user mumbles the curse under their breath or shouts it loudly enough as a threat followed by a motion towards the victim, rolling Intelligence + Occult against the victim’s Composure + Occult. Those nearby may notice the vampire behaving suspiciously by rolling Wits + Occult vs. Wits + Subterfuge of the vampire.</p>\n<p>On the vampire’s win, they may choose from one of the following curses to afflict their victim with:</p>\n<p>• Abandonment: Subract total dots of the character’s Reliability from Allies equal to the user’s Daimonion.</p>\n<p>• Banishment: The user tears a demon from their mortal host and sends it screaming back to the Abyss. In addition, no magic or ritual can summon the demon back from Hell for the full duration of Curse. Earthbound demons cannot be torn from their reliquaries by this power, though the user may exile fallen who lack a vessel. Banishing a demon from the world obviously earns the eternal enmity of that spirit.</p>\n<p>• Debilitation: One of the victim’s traits (Skills, Attributes) selected by the vampire drops by a number equal to half (rounded up) the vampire’s Daimonion rating to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<p>• Infertility: The character cannot sire other vampires or reproduce.</p>\n<p>• Other Curses: Any curse from the Path of Fortune(p. XX) below the rank of 4 is also available.</p>\n<p>Each sunrise, the victim may roll Composure + Resolve at a difficulty equal to the total number of successes the accursing vampire rolled. A win breaks the curse early.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Unless the victim breaks the curse, it lasts until the vampire lifts it by simply willing it according to the table below. A critical win doubles the duration. Only one trait curse may be active at a time and recasting the power removes the previous one if it scores more successes.</p>\n<p>Margins Duration One scene 1 margin 2 margin One night 3 margins One week 4 margins One month 5 margins One year 6+ margins For each margin over 6, add a 0.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"55"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"cXXIdxuy":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"LtGA9zqv":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.aAZAhHbTDuFJ4fPB"},"aCQuwRW59JN9WQqh":{"name":"Moment of Clarity","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The alchemist is able to temporarily submerge their vampiric instincts, harnessing the power of the Beast to support, rather than disturb, their mental acumen. For a short while they can reach superhuman levels of intellect and concentration while also avoiding bestial interference in their actions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, phlegmatic blood, cadmium, nootropics, and ash from burned pages of a meaningful text to the alchemist (scripture, favorite childhood novel).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activation, the alchemist gains mental clarity, their wits sharpen, and the Beast is temporarily sated. They add four dice to Mental or Discipline Skill pools, and can add four dice to Mental resistance checks against Disciplines requiring them: Dominate, Animalism, Presence, Auspex, and their amalgams. They are also immune to Messy Criticals and frenzy, if susceptible to the latter.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"106"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.aCQuwRW59JN9WQqh"},"adEQVNCGLbGKu2XT":{"name":"Grandest Trick","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>After draining all the life from a mortal, the vampire gets the grand power to mimic aspects of mortality until the next night whilst maintaining their ability to use their Mytherceria discipline powers at the cost of draining their willpower.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s form alters from vampirism to mortality for a night. They heal like a mortal, take damage like a mortal, feel, taste and digest things like a mortal and, most importantly, sun does not damage them.</p>\n<p>The user becomes unable to use or benefit from any Discipline powers except those from the Mythercerica Discipline and blood sorceries, and even using these powers requires them to spend a point of Willpower instead of making a Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>The user keeps all parts of their Clan Bane with a Bane Severity of 1 and ignores Hunger and Hunger dice. The Beast will be nontoopleased that the vampire managed to trick it, and all Difficulties for Frenzies are permanently increased by one when the vampire acquires this power</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the following night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"95"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"CKPThdi0":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"JxFj951a":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.adEQVNCGLbGKu2XT"},"aj4J3kXjMLWOl2u6":{"name":"Creeping Infection","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Users of Thanatosis are able to concoct new ailments to devastate their foes with ease and their breath alone is capable of decimating entire groups of mortals.+</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Medicine vs. Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user slowly breathes out an invisible gas of various supernatural ailments and rolls Manipulation + Medicine. Those within a radius equal to twice the user’s Thanatosis rating may roll Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude, if they possess it) to resist their effects. All creatures who fail the roll are infected and suffer Physical Impairment.</p>\n<p>Mortals (excluding ghouls and revenants) who stay in the radius are even more prone to these ailments. In addition to Physical Impairment, they roll Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude) against a Difficulty equal to the user’s Thanatosis rating each turn after the first for as long as they within the vampire’s presence that accelerates the disease. On a failure, they take a point of Aggravated Health damage. Once out of reach, the mortal bodies continue to suffer this ailment and must roll the same Test at the beginning of each night, taking a point of up to half the user’s Thantosis rating, rounded up, as Aggravate damage, as chosen by the user at the moment of ailment’s creation.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user may transmit this illness through touch for more subtlety, possessing the same effects as above including the need to roll each turn while in the vampire’s radius. This type of an illness may be made transmittable through touch or liquids with vampire setting the limit to the number of transmissions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene unless ended sooner by the user. The ailment persists in mortals until they are fed at least one Rouse Check’s worth of vitae or are healed through advanced medicine or miraculous means.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"179"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"0nHqQyPM":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"OghOLQqr":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.aj4J3kXjMLWOl2u6"},"ajNh8UaDONOmtDOG":{"name":"Co-Walker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By seeing cracks through time and space, the user of Temporis is able to, at least for a second, be in two places at the same time. This ability is an essential tool to counter the fact the bloodline of the True Brujah is vastly outnumbered by their usurping counterparts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After making a Rouse Check, the user is able to do two physical actions in the same turn, appearing as if in multiple places at the same time. This can be anything from attacking more than a single foe to attacking the same multiple times. The attacks are treated normally as if made by another person. After the action is done, the person’s “other” selves disappear almost instantly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One action</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"170"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ajNh8UaDONOmtDOG"},"axh2JmJOepFMNowy":{"name":"Forgetful Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can rewrite whole swathes of the victim’s memories, as long as they can keep the victim’s gaze and full, uninterrupted attention. The vampire verbally describes the victim’s new memories, which the victim then accepts as their own. This power does not allow the user to investigate the victim’s true memories, it resembles blindly painting over the old canvas.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Intelligence + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls a contest of Manipulation + Subterfuge vs Intelligence + Resolve. Each point of margin allows the user to add or remove an Additional memory. The victim recalls the edits vaguely, foggy ideations that can fall apart under close questioning. A critical win (or 3+ margins) create a flawless imprint, as real as any true memory.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Indefinitely</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"72"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"M6ZRDkWa":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"J3U4TkSb":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.axh2JmJOepFMNowy"},"b0kXDqjFH7Ie2ew8":{"name":"Inside the Hourglass","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Though still unable to stop time or reverse it in a greater state, the user of Temporis with this power is able to slow down their perception just enough to be able to properly respond and react to everyone’s actions, giving them more time to consider and rethink their next move — and the True Brujah are great (re)thinkers indeed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Attacks that achieve fewer successes than the user’s Temporis rating always miss, no matter the result of a defense or dodge test (if any). This also works against surprise attacks or other attacks that disallow defense tests, such as Lightning Strike. Activating this power requires one Rouse Check and extending it requires an additional Check for each turn it is kept active.</p>\n<p>Those with a rating of Temporis or Celerity rgreater the user’s Temporis rating ignore the effects of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"170"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.b0kXDqjFH7Ie2ew8"},"b1mX0LXy7mjQ38Cc":{"name":"Bleed Out","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Hunting down and subduing a victim poses significant risk for the Duskborn, and they take their unlives into their own hands with each hunt they embark upon. This commonly accepted truth ensures the thin-bloods work together wherever possible to keep themselves well-fed.</p>\n<p>This Formula was born from the frustration of having to hunt in packs but never having sufficient blood to go around. After the Duskborn feeds this alchemical concoction to their unlucky catch, the victim starts bleeding profusely, until eventually, every organ liquefies to provide sustenance for the horde of hungry predators encircling them as they painfully bleed out every ounce of fluid.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, Melancholic human blood, diuretics, energy drinks, cornflower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Distillation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist vomits the formula into the victim’s mouth (Athanor Corporis), forces the victim to drink it from the vessel’s mouth (Calcinatio), or forces the victim to drink from the elixir container (Fixatio). Once the victim consumes the formula, the alchemist makes the activation test to determine the results. On a failure, the victim simply dies; their blood feeds one vampire as normal. On a total failure, their blood turns septic and can feed nobody. On a win, victims choke and drown on their own blood over an agonizing hour. This death provides enough blood to feed an entire coterie of Duskborn (and only Duskborn — true vampires can’t stomach the blood), reducing everyone’s Hunger to zero. Every participant gains at least one Stain from the act. The remains must be properly disposed of, as the resultant sack of skin and bones is bound to rouse suspicion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tattered Façade","page":"107"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.b1mX0LXy7mjQ38Cc"},"b4OroSvLA1UuklrU":{"name":"Whence All Comes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Disposal of corpses is a useful trick of the Cappadocians that Troglodytia taught her children, allowing them to live on even after the purge of the Cappadocians by acting as cleaning crews for hire. With this power, the user is able to quickly dissolve a corpse or even a corpse construct of necromancy such as zombies, a trick that proved useful against both the Harbingers of Skulls and the Giovanni before the Family Reunion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches a cadaver or a zombie and makes a Resolve + Necromancy roll against a Difficulty of 2. On a Win, the body begins to dissolve into ash over the course of the next 5 turns with each additional margin reducing the time it takes to dissolve by a turn with at least 5 margins or a Critical Win dissolving the body instantly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Variable (see system)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"176"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"XiEdPy5U":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"whhz1KK2":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.b4OroSvLA1UuklrU"},"b7aEh1F57LpzQMhQ":{"name":"Blissful Slumber","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>Not all vampires who wander rest beneath the earth. vampires using this power can assume the appearance of true death, even appearing to withstand sunlight while in such a state. The vampire Elder Gangrel with great blood potency can sleep in mist form, rather than rest in a corporeal form. This form provides advantages, in that the character simply cannot be disturbed by physical attacks while sleeping, save by strong winds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire assumes the appearance of a corpse, either freshly dead, mummified, rotting, or even a dried-up skeleton. While so transformed, the vampire cannot move or physically communicate, but retains enough of their senses to understand what is happening around them. Disciplines can still be used at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>Only injuries that would cause Aggravated damage are applied to the vampire, but the user is immune to sunlight. A vampire may also choose to enter Torpor voluntarily when activating this power, where the power’s duration extends until they wake from Torpor.</p>\n<p>Those with mist form may activate this power while in Mist Form to extend its duration, allowing them to sleep in Mist Form and even torpor with no concern for fire or other dangers to the vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended by the user or the user wakes from Torpor, otherwise until sunset</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"129"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.b7aEh1F57LpzQMhQ"},"b83cqL0oezWOkEbM":{"name":"Ancestral Dominion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires of great age and potency find it possible to exert their will via the Blood, without the traditional need for eye contact and verbal communication with their target. This power enables a Cainite to compel a descendant by Blood to take an action on their behalf, even if it’s one the target would normally be opposed to. Vampires instinctively know an ancestor is manipulating them when subject to this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive with Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Intimidation vs. Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful test of Manipulation + Intimidation against Resolve + Occult, the victim acts to fulfill the dominating ancestor’s request, provided it doesn’t entail harming themself. For every Generation separating the user of this power from their target, the resisting vampire gains an additional die to their roll. For example, when a ninth generation vampire attempts to use this power on an eleventh generation descendant, the descendant gains two extra dice in defense. The power needs neither eye contact nor verbal command — the request is silently transmitted from Blood to Blood — but it is subject to all other Dominate limitations.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the command is carried out or the scene ends, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"74"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"nbgkomQQ":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"Niu8FQNf":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.b83cqL0oezWOkEbM"},"bTEo5EqGAw8IvYoz":{"name":"Haze","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula creates a field of mist that follows the user, rendering them more difficult to target with ranged weapons and concealing their identity.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> In addition to the alchemist’s Blood and phlegmatic human blood, dry ice or cigar smoke or auto exhaust</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activation a cloud of mist-like vapor surrounds the alchemist, masking their features and obscuring their silhouette. Anyone attempting to identify the user or hit them with ranged weapons suffers a two-dice penalty to their pool. The user can extend the cloud to encompass a group of up to five people by making another Rouse check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until voluntarily ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"285"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.bTEo5EqGAw8IvYoz"},"bzHqHo9owfQtkxYD":{"name":"Invigorating Display","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>After working an audience into a state of blissful euphoria, the vampire inhales the energy of the crowd, taking a fragment of their collective energy deep into their dead lungs to experience its unique Resonance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Awe</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performanc</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire spends a scene entertaining a crowd of mortals (a minimum of ten people are required), Rousing the Blood and making a Charisma + Performance test against Difficulty 3 (Storytellers may raise or lower this depending on the quality of the performance). If successful, the crowd’s reaction is notable and the vampire absorbs a specific Resonance from the audience, leaving them drained.</p>\n<p>The Resonance absorbed is usually intense, but may be accute (a Dyscrasia) on a critical win at the Storyteller’s discretion. Affected audience members are left with a fleeting Resonance at best. This power can be used once per scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"123"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gY0x1ULn":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"TeiVbYyk":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.bzHqHo9owfQtkxYD"},"c2Bv2YmQMC6Bc4zx":{"name":"Erode","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By touching their victim, the user turns the target’s own blood against them and causes their body to rapidly fail in strength, potentially leading to death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches the target (which can be done in combat with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/Brawl) and rolls Composure + Resolve vs. the victim’s Stamina + Occult. (Those with Fortitude may resist with Stamina + Fortitude). On a win, the victim loses two dots in their Strength, Dexterity and Stamina, to a minimum of 0, and suffers Physical Impairment. If a mortal target’s Stamina is reduced to 0 or less by this, they die instantly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"143"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"FwJ81slZ":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"2TLgTjYe":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.c2Bv2YmQMC6Bc4zx"},"c3XN18YrAnNzhGoQ":{"name":"Purification of Wu Zao","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mages, Wan Kuei, and shapeshifters all command spirits and demons. These creatures exist in their own worlds, but infest our own. The Watchers see them, however, and seek not only to heal the infestation (as a good Salubri ought) but to rid their rivals of potential servants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dice Pools: Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire focuses on the spirit, ghost or a demon and utters a command of banishment. They and the spirit engage in a battle of wills, represented by a contested Composure + Resolve roll. The contest ends when one combatant has accumulated three margins more than another. If the vampire wins, they may expel the spirit from its host. By spending another Willpower point, they may thrust the spirit into a person, animal or location, including taking the spirit into themself.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"196"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gTxtQ5AI":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"WAVWAccj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.c3XN18YrAnNzhGoQ"},"cQT03JKulxKnWM7X":{"name":"Nocturne","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>Nearby shadows spread outwards as the vampire blankets the area in a layer of darkness, while sounds become muffled and indistinct. Anyone viewing the effect from without sees it as a shadow expanding over every surface, including the bodies of any victims, in the area. Those apart from the invoker caught in the effects find themselves struggling to see and hear their surroundings, and mortals are drained of their very life by the suffocating power. This power also snuffs out all radiance of light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Rouse Check and spends a turn concentrating, spreading the shadow over the desired surfaces. The effect covers a circular area with a radius equal to twice the user’s Oblivion rating in yards/meters, usually covering a whole zone or more (p. XX). The area is centered on the user or a spot in their line of sight.</p>\n<p>Anyone caught in the Nocturne receives a three-dice penalty to all rolls, unless they possess the ability to see through darkness. Any mortals caught in the Nocturne suffer one level of Superficial damage for every turn they remain within it, due to the power’s suffocating effects. To find an exit out of the Nocturne, a character must succeed on a Resolve + Awareness or Survival roll at a Difficulty equal to the user’s Obtenebration rating.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows, the power manifests as an inky mist now, making it impossible for light and sight to go through it in most cases.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"112"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cQT03JKulxKnWM7X"},"cQm5uTERPqogO94z":{"name":"Bond Famulus","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>When Blood Bonding an animal, the vampire can make it a famulus, forming a mental link with it and facilitating the use of other Animalism powers. While this power alone does not allow two-way communication with the animal, it can follow simple verbal instructions such as “stay” and “come here.” It attacks in defense of itself and its master but cannot otherwise be persuaded to fight something it would not normally attack.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive/Issuing commands is a minor action of -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> The animal must be fed the user’s Blood on three separate nights, each of which requires a Rouse Check by the user. The amount of Blood needed to sustain the ghoulstate of the animal after this is negligible. Players starting with this power have completed this process and can chose a famulus for free. Note that all famuli are animal ghouls and receive benefits as such (p. XX).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Animal Ken</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Without the use of Feral Whispers, below, giving commands to the animal requires a Manipulation + Animal Ken Test (Difficulty 2); increase Difficulty for more complex orders. A vampire can only have one famulus, but can get a new one if the current one dies. A vampire can use Feral Whispers (Animalism 2) and Subsume the Spirit (Animalism 4) on their famulus for free.</p>\n<p>A famulus is not unlike a normal animal that has been ghouled, but all of its Mental Attributes are increased by one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Only death releases a famulus once bound. As long as it receives vampire Blood on a regular basis, the famulus does not age.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"14"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"SBXEFasd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WwAgVNCN":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cQm5uTERPqogO94z"},"cQyok73cJifOsnME":{"name":"Whispers from the Deep","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Usually, those seeking to learn from a demon must do so by petitioning a demon who is usually relucant to provide anything without a bargain (masterfully rigged in their favour). With Whispers from the Deep, the vampire establishes a mental connection directly with one of the dukes of Hell to learn magics and other arcane secrets of the universe from them. Thankfully for the vampire, these dukes of Hell are imprisoned and cannot directly impact the vampire or enthrall them – for now.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user hears whispers of arcane secrets and powers, offering advice when needed. This whisper translates into a number of dots to spend on a “portable Library Background” equal to twice your Daimonion rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"55"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cQyok73cJifOsnME"},"cRhaKf7eCzp2mArc":{"name":"Glare of Lies","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Upon activating this power, a Salubri’s third eye opens (if they developed it) and narrows in close scrutiny. The Cainite gains the ability to see the harmony of truth and the disharmony of deception. It is virtually impossible to lie surreptitiously to a vampire who knows this Discipline.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Valeren • (Any Path)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free (but see below)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add your Auspex rating to all Insight rolls to detect deliberate lies of others. Successful Insight roll leaves no doubt that the heard statement is a lie. This power cannot sense subtler deceptions by omission or half-truth. As long as those scrutinised tell the truth — however partial or deliberately obtuse — the power detects no falsehood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until deactivated, at which point the eye is allowed to close.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"24"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cRhaKf7eCzp2mArc"},"cbsdeabOqIz8Jn9k":{"name":"Death of the Drum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>Considered one of the more disturbing powers of Melpominee, Death of the Drum weakens bones, kills cells, distorts vision and cracks glass with ease.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Stamina + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire emits a sound that that weakens a target, rolling Charisma + Performance vs. the target’s Composure + Wits. On a win, the victim suffers from Physical and Mental impairment.</p>\n<p>Vampire and supernatural mortal victims may spend a point of Willpower to resist the effects of the power for a turn.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A number of turns equal to the margin. A critical win extends it to the rest of the scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"87"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GBwYES8o":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"Le3ZF7cR":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cbsdeabOqIz8Jn9k"},"cjzMEDrkocFxtv1S":{"name":"Energy Syphon","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Practitioners of Path of the Watcher eventually run into many different types of supernatural creatures such as magicians, ghosts, demons and other monstrosities. Watchers discovered that most if not all supernatural manifestations come from a form of energy that may be extracted and, if needed, blocked, disabling their victim’s powers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches their victim and rolls Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve. Touching a person avoiding combat requires any form of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/Brawl roll.</p>\n<p>On a win, the victim counts power resource tracker (Hunger, Rage, Pathos Degeneration, Faith Hunger...) as increased by one for every point on the margin. To negate the Energy Block, the victim simply has to spend Willpower equal to their margin of failure.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until the victim shakes it off by spending Willpower.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"197"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"5M8T8KFz":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"tbPsnbK6":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cjzMEDrkocFxtv1S"},"cmkngrlZ4mV6CRdA":{"name":"Flight (••)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/flight.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user can fly up to 30 miles per hour/ 45 km per hour and carry up to 20 lbs./10 kg safely. At this level, their ability to maneuver is still quite undeveloped; to do much more than fly in a straight line, the user must make a Dexterity + Athletics roll (difficulty varies depending upon what’s being attempted), and dice pools to dodge lose two dice while flying.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"78"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"flight","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.cmkngrlZ4mV6CRdA"},"czyYyqVPfGMbzCeW":{"name":"Seculsion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>This ability allows the user to invert the powers of Obfuscate on a subject, making them blind to every person in the vicinity. To the victim, they are alone in the world, as they are unable to see, hear, or feel other people. This perceived isolation is, understandably, tortuous to most subjects, and the use of this power to break someone’s mind is as common as its use to — crudely — blind a watcher. Victims are often oblivious to the effect at first, but as their isolation dawns on them, their behavior often becomes increasingly erratic to the point of raving.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> As per power augmented</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Resolve + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user catches the eye of the victim and must then succeed at a Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Resolve + Awareness test. On a win, the user and everyone near appears to disappear to the victim, and the effect lasts for one scene (or night), plus one for each success in the margin. While under its effect, the victim is unable to perceive any living or unliving person. They will unconsciously avoid them and will be deaf to any sounds made by them. Only if someone attempts to do physical harm to the victim will they be momentarily perceived, as a person using Unseen Passage would. Each time this happens, make another Discipline test. If the victim successfully resists, the power’s effect breaks prematurely.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Margin number of scenes or nights, or until broken</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"104"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"q3cVM3jG":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"d7JUGpFZ":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.czyYyqVPfGMbzCeW"},"d1BceDqf7vxIRhK5":{"name":"Fae Sight","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The fae see the world brighter and in more ways than a mortal could possibly imagine. While such perspectives often do not match the material reality, the practitioners of Mytherceria are still able to see beyond the mundane, even things hidden by magic.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user may reflexively roll Intelligence + Awareness against a Difficulty equal to the number of successes of any supernatural power that does not stem from an undead source (excluding Mytherceria) and is within their sensory range.</p>\n<p>On a win, the user senses the power and, if they are at least somewhat familiar with the type of power (seen it in practice, received education, heard of it in detail), they can generally narrow it down to the exact ability or category of power (Gift, spell, power, Path, Pillar, School of Magic, Arcana, Art, etc.). If the power is not one immediately recognised by the user, they automatically learn of the power and can recognise it in the future. A demon invoking a power or a shifter using a Gift would give off their unique auras or vibes. Abilities used or created by the Fae are automatically recognised by the user regardless of prior knowledge. Supernatural energies without rolls such as constructs of magic (familiars, zombies, homunculi...) can be recognised as well with the difficulty for the roll being 3.</p>\n<p>This knowledge gives the user a two-dice bonus on all rolls to resist or dodge the perceived magic. This bonus is not further amplified by power bonus as it is merely knowledge how to defend, not a power by itself.</p>\n<p>If you wish to flavour how one perceives magic, you may take inspiration from Scry the Soul writeup below and discuss it with your storyteller such as seeing magical flares, smelling or tasting the swirl of magical energy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"90"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"J89AtrFi":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"FmsWkI1V":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.d1BceDqf7vxIRhK5"},"dP0zVZBl2V4zyhhl":{"name":"Spirit Whisperer","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user’s eyesight attunes to the umbra, beyond of what even the necromancers are capable of perceiving. Many spirits are curious and intrigued by the material beings being able to perceive them and are usually willing to converse with the vampire, should they be respectful.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon activation, the user is able to see spirits (including ghosts) with their own eyes and are able to hear them and hold conversations. Them being willing to talk is entirely up to them, however. Spirits of animals are far more friendly than spirits of rocks which simply wish to remain where they are (not to mention the intelligence of a rock is questionable at best). Sense the Unseen may see spirits who are close to the material world, but Spirit Whisperer allows one to practically pierce into the Umbra where the spirits reside in an equivalent location.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"161"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.dP0zVZBl2V4zyhhl"},"dXeIjduUA6N7p7LR":{"name":"Mask of a Thousand Faces","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>While using this ability, the Cainite remains fully visible but changes their appearance to fit a particular image. The character may choose to invent an image of their own devising or alter themself to look like a particular individual. The vampire’s appearance does not really change — this isn’t the Tzimisce Discipline of Vicissitude — but rather, a temporary vision is superimposed over the character’s face and body.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One or more Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Performance</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character rolls Manipulation + Performance at a Difficulty of 1. See the following page for the level of change the character can then make to their appearance. The user can only copy human appearance, not animal form, and innately knows what form they’ve taken. To mimic the face of another, the vampire must have a good look at them. The Storyteller might raise the difficulty on the roll ifthe vampire has only briefly seen the person they wish to mimic. How successful the disguise is depends on the chart at the above.</p>\n<p>Should the vampire wish to create a face much more or less appealing than their own, especially if they’re mimicking a person who is more or less attractive than them, they they must make a Rouse Check to add or remove two dots of Looks Merits and Flaws to that of the disguise. Powers such as Sense the Unseen (Auspex •) can pierce the mask as usual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the character falls unconscious or stops the illusion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"100"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"A2AerM7b":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"Lln1vVq0":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.dXeIjduUA6N7p7LR"},"dffWIcfHXHn70NnH":{"name":"Awaken the Parasite","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires are dead, but that doesn’t mean they’re devoid of life. As they rest, bugs rest and nest within them; if they eat, traces of food remain to rot in their gut, and even the blood they consume festers long enough to provide sustenance for more than just the vampire. These parasitic eggs normally remain unhatched and dormant, but once this Power is engaged, they rapidly mature, writhing beneath skin, escaping from eye sockets and orifices, chewing through flesh, and swelling the body to the bursting point. The result is a disgustingly grim reminder of what lives within them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Unliving Hive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Daimonion 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Animal Ken vs. Road rating or Stamina + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire Rouses the Blood and spends their action concentrating before testing Resolve + Animal Ken against the Road Rating of their target (mortals resist with Stamina + Wits). The victim must be within close proximity to the vampire (&lt;10 meters). Results are based on the margin of the contest, while a critical win also increases a vampire target’s Hunger by one:</p>\n<p>• Basic Win (Margin 0): The victim reduces Social and Mental dice pools by one die as maggots burrow and writhe beneath their flesh, causing confusion and headaches to the victim, and nausea among onlookers.</p>\n<p>• Margin 1: The victim suffers a two-dice penalty on all pools as larvae burst from their flesh in significant numbers. In most cases, Social tests simply fail without needing to roll. At this stage, the Masquerade becomes severely strained, at best.</p>\n<p>• Margin 2+: Eggs go from larvae to pupae in seconds, and pupating blowflies erupt in a shower of gore; killing mortal victims, while supernatural targets receive a two-dice penalty to pools, 2 unhalved Superficial damage, and find themselves unable to speak as their throats fill with squirming maggots. If the vampire uses Awaken the Parasite on themselves, perhaps to intimidate or disgust a foe, the vermin burst from their flesh and they suffer a two-dice penalty on any other pools this scene. The target resists with Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>• Margin 3+ (or a Critical Win): same as margin of 2 except that vampires, ghouls and revenants have their Hunger increased by one.</p>\n<p>If the vampire wins, the target cringes away, flees, or otherwise loses face; they also lose Willpower equal to the margin. Most commonly, those who are supernatural or are “in the know” will not flee so easily.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"17"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"xpVoeGb9":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"xPHuCQf1":{"path":"skills.animalken"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.dffWIcfHXHn70NnH"},"diuepUte6YtiqYS4":{"name":"Trap the Shadow","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>In war against infernal cults and malign spirits, sometimes banishing them whence they came from is not as useful due to an already prepared army of sinners ready to summon their masters again. Trap the Shadow solves that problem by creating a phylactery to act as a prison for the essence of a demon, spectre or some other malign spirit like a bane spirit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Ending the Watch</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user “touches” an incorporealised demon, spectre or some other malign spirit (incorporalisation usually happens after the host is destroyed or through exorcisms such as that of Healer Valeren’s Purification) and rolls Resolve + Valeren against their Composure + Resolve. On a win, they may force the creature to inhabit any object within the vampire’s line of sight, but hallow ones are preferred for symbolism.</p>\n<p>While within that object, they may not use any powers to affect the outside world, but are still sentient, can still use powers such as telepathy to call their minions to rescue them and so on. This is a major problem if a single person possesses several such phylactery-prisons which is why the Salubri used to have special fortresses where they would bring entrapped spirits for safekeeping.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the phylactery-prison is shattered.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"192"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.diuepUte6YtiqYS4"},"dqPXHI7aAiWtrQ0A":{"name":"Face of the Victim","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Quicker than Malleable Visage, the vampire may steal the appearance of their victims.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Two full turns</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After fully exsanguinating a mortal or a humanoid like a vampire, the user may activate the power for free and assume their physical appearance. The user also gains the victim’s voice, but not any clothing, artificial implants, knowledge, or items the vessel had. When attempting to pass as the person copied to somebody who knew them well, a Manipulation + Performance vs Wits + Insight test must be made to avoid suspicion and replicate body and voice mannerism.</p>\n<p>If you ever wish to impersonate a victim again, you must make a Rouse Check while holding a memento taken from them soon after their death.</p>\n<p>Mortals (ghouls, revenants) must consume (read: eat) their victim whole in order to use this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until reaching Hunger 5, or using this power again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"203"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.dqPXHI7aAiWtrQ0A"},"du2da0K70OAyW912":{"name":"Words as Daggers","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power the vampire completely destroys the victim’s defences, making them a puppet succeptable to input from strangers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Sweat of Guilt</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> An additional Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When activating Sweat of Guilt, the user may make an additional Rouse Check to force an internal self-flaggelation. The victim receives Aggravated Health damage for each point of Superficial Willpower damage taken.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"138"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.du2da0K70OAyW912"},"e6b9PPH3k2cWCcjU":{"name":"Blink","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire swiftly closes in on a foe, engaging or escaping in the blink of an eye. To an unprepared observer the user almost appears to teleport, a rush of wind the only sign of their passing.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dexterity + Athletics, or as needed</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire moves in a straight line toward a target, covering any distance under 50 meters while still having enough time to perform an action, such as an attack, during the turn. If the terrain is in any way hazardous, the character needs to make a Dexterity + Athletics roll to avoid stumbling and coming to a halt on the way. The Storyteller may call for other contests as desired, especially if the vampire races a distant foe to an object or an action. Vampires engaging a foe with this power act as if already engaged when the turn begins. This does not permit a surprise attack by itself.</p>\n<p>When there are multiple users of Blink, they treat each other as moving accordingly to the disparity of their Discipline ratings. Two Toreador with Celerity 4 would be moving at the same speed. If using Blink while another is using Slow Walker (Temporis •••), the vampire with Blink moves faster than the vampire with Slow Walker if their Celerity rating is greater then the Temporis rating of Slow Blink user. If it is less or equal, then Slow Walker acts and is resolved first.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"44"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"n2w2Y2Cc":{"path":"attributes.dexterity"},"urGYZe7E":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.e6b9PPH3k2cWCcjU"},"eA7UQEaxrjI6bhbK":{"name":"Mens Sana","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>By focusing the power of their green-glowing third eye and the lessons of empathy of Saulot, the vampire shields another from supernatural influence, either erecting a mental barrier or purging it as one does illnesses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Ease Mind</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches a target and rolls Resolve + Occult at Difficulty of 3.</p>\n<p>On a win, the following happens according to the victim’s state:</p>\n<p>• If they were under the effect of a mind-affecting power, it ceases. Effects include mental curses, powers such as Presence, Dominate, Dementation, powers that take possession of the body, Quietus Animae, mind-controlling powers and so on.</p>\n<p>• If the victim suffers a Derangement, they are healed of it. Revenants and vampires other creatures who suffer curses that are a part of are only healed for a single scene or roughly 24 hours on a Critical Win (or 3+ margins). When their lineage using this power to soothe Derangement of Clan Malkavian and other vampires with Derangements as part of their Clan Curse, the roll is performed at a Difficulty of of their Bane Severity + 2.</p>\n<p>• If the victim suffers neither of the above (at least at the moment of the power’s activation), they gain a “shield” against supernatural influence. Any mind-afflicting power must roll more successes than the user to afflict the subject. The shield shatters after a scene (Critical Win or 3+ margins extend the duration to roughly 24 hours) or once the subject suffers the effect mind-afflicting power, whichever comes first.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The effect is instant with lasting effects having the duration of a scene, extended to roughly 24 hours on a Critical Win or 3+ margins.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"186"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Shk0kIQY":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"6Fnh0YvS":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eA7UQEaxrjI6bhbK"},"eGwgsSJ7toLqRRUu":{"name":"Squirm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some vampires who use Protean turn their flesh and bones rubbery, allowing them to squeeze into impossibly tight places. Some use this Power to hide in walls or block their havens with entrances few can follow through.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free or One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add the user’s Protean rating to any dice pools to wriggle through tight spaces, escape a grapple, or escape any form of restraints. The user can squeeze into tight spaces so long as their head fits into the space as their other bones can dislocate and reconnect with ease.</p>\n<p>If the user has about five minutes, they can make a Rouse Check to force their body into an even more pliable state. Their flesh and bones spread and collapse to let them fit through impossibly tight spaces about two inches (or five centimeters) in diameter. If the user is interrupted or forcibly yanked out of such a tight space, they suffer one Aggravated damage to Health.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As long as desired</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"128"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eGwgsSJ7toLqRRUu"},"eHdDuyHHVyHEziev":{"name":"Slow Cuts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By slowing time around them for a swift moment, the user is able to deliver an uncountable amount of quick cuts and slashes against an opponent. The victim appears as if their body spontaneously erupts in hundreds of wounds, each seemingly insignificant in its own right but excruciating in their increasing volume.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire may add their Temporis rating to any single Brawl or Melee attack (not otherwise augmented by a Discipline) made with claws or an edged weapon. For mortal victims, the cuts and slices caused by this flurry of attacks only cause Superficial damage, but automatically inflict enough additional Superficial damage for the victim to be Physically Impaired at the end of the attack. (At the Storyteller’s discretion, victims are overcome with pain and effectively out of commission.) The attack also splashes blood far and wide, causing vampires at Hunger 3 or more to make Hunger Frenzy tests. Used against vampires and creatures of similar durability, the attack deals no damage, but the scores of vicious yet unbleeding wounds leave the victim a walking Masquerade violation, and might provoke a Fury Frenzy test at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>If the user attacking a person with Celerity using A Thousand Cuts while using Slow Cuts and vice versa, subtract each other’s Discipline rating from the dice bonus, down to 0.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One attack</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"169"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eHdDuyHHVyHEziev"},"eIehD2MBWY0FCsM9":{"name":"Diamond Skin","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>For all their potential, the thin-blooded are fragile, at least compared to other vampires. This elixir is designed to balance the scales, letting alchemists take hits straight on, survive long falls, and maybe even talk shit to the local Sheriff and walk away clean. It also gives an alchemist’s skin a gorgeous shine underneath neon lights, which is its own kind of perk.</p>\n<p>This formula is less reliable than Tank (Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide, p. 105), but can last and protect longer depending on the alchemist’s skill.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, melancholic human blood, ground up granite, diamonds, or ceramics, lotion or oil.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A number of Aggravated damage points equal to the alchemist’s successes on the Distillation roll become Superficial damage to the user. This only affects physical damage from slashes, punctures, or impact; it has no effect against fire, sunlight, sorcery, or acid.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the margin is used up; on a critical win on the Distillation roll, the effect lasts for one scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"76"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eIehD2MBWY0FCsM9"},"eKpTIg7HNx4nlCsp":{"name":"Voice of Erictho","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Perfected by Thessalian Cappadocians and later popularised among the Giovanni, the Voice of Erictho extends the properties of Dominate to work against unembodied ghosts who would usually be immune to it just like with any other mind-afflicting power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Mortis 2 or some other necromantic sorcery at 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user may spend a point of Willpower to affect ghosts with Dominate as they are normally immune to mind-controlling effects. This includes ghosts residing in the lands of the dead as well as manifesting in the lands of the living.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"74"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eKpTIg7HNx4nlCsp"},"eTDZAFUnjZI1NzFX":{"name":"An Ear For Lies","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>Lying is not a thing that comes natural to humans as the body and the mind itself finds it uncomfortable. A prudent user of Dementation can notice the physical signs of lies and sense their veil clouding the mind’s eye.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add the Dementation rating to all Insight rolls to detect lies of others. This ability does improve detecting lies of omission or half-truths, only direct and deliberate lies and leaves no room for ambiguity on successful Insight rolls. If the subject believes what they are saying is true (even if it is not), then the character will not detect any falsehoods as normal. While this power is active, the vampire must also focus, usually by focusing their other senses such as by pinching skin or closing their eyes. This focusing may be perceived by its own Awareness roll against the user’s Composure + Subterfuge. Masks and veils can be used to hide the usage of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until the user stops focusing.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"64"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eTDZAFUnjZI1NzFX"},"eXYnM8Osb1WDnq1H":{"name":"Eye of Unfogiving Heaven","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>Upon invoking this power, a Salubri’s third eye opens impossibly wide and burns incandescent gold. The orb becomes as a reflection of the sun itself, illuminating the area and searing the flesh of the undead.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check and one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Half of Road rating rounded down</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire stares in the general direction he wishes the power to illuminate, while his player spends one point each of blood and Willpower and rolls their half of their Road rating rounded down (Difficulty 1). A single success causes the Cainite’s third eye to open and glow as described. The light has the same effects as direct sunlight and damages vampires in visual range according to their exposure and Bane Severity. As the power’s light emits as radiance rather a beam, it knows neither friend nor foe. Even the user is not wholly immune to his light, damage as they would normally from the sun. Any wounds the vampire suffers manifest as a torrent of bloody tears weeping from the third eye, possibly provoking a Hunger frenzy in those with Hunger 4 or higher to charge at the sun if they’ve braved it already. This power lasts only one turn, after which its duration may be extended for as many turns as desired (though the player must make the same expenditure each turn).</p>\n<p>A vampire who perishes as a result of using this power falls to the earth, their normal eyes closed in an expression of beatific rapture even as their third eye dims. Their corpse blazes in searing white light, carrying their scattering ashes upward to the possibility of redemption. Characters who adhere to inhuman Roads (lacking the Virtue of Conscience) cannot use this power).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"187"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.eXYnM8Osb1WDnq1H"},"edjrYrY2gjT2iUkL":{"name":"Transcend the Prison of Form","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire may now change their form in its entirety, changing into liquid states, making themselves lighter allowing them to sprout functional wings, etc.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Rend the Osseous Frame</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 2, requiring physical contact if another person is the subject. If the subject is avoiding the user’s touch and the time required to perform an alteration is one turn or instant, alteration first requires a successful roll with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/ Brawl. For alterations requiring longer periods of time, unwilling targets must be restrained. Unwilling subjects resist using Stamina + Resolve. Their successes replaces the static Difficulty of 2.</p>\n<p>The following alterations are samples of what is available to Transcend the Prison of Form:</p>\n<p>• Bloodform (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 1 hour or 3 turns): The subject transforms into a liquid. This requires the user to work on the subject for 3 hours, but the user themselves may transform in 3 turns by spending a point of Willpower with each additional Rouse Check lowering the transformation time by a turn to a minimum of 1 during which they may not act and defends with normal pools.</p>\n<p>In this form, a character cannot be harmed by most conventional weapons (including stakes), though sunlight and fire still affect them as normal. The user can squeeze through small cracks, and even flow cleanly up walls or across ceilings. While in bloodform (or partially in bloodform), the user can perform general actions such as pushing and pulling, but the difficulty of fine manipulation of electronic devices or liquids increases by two if manipulating them as Blood.</p>\n<p>The subject may use Disciplines as normal, provided they do not need to make eye contact or speak, though a subject with Vicissitude 4 can make a Rouse Check to partially reform eyes, a vocal tract, limbs, or head.</p>\n<p>The vampire can transform only part of the body, such as a finger or leg, as desired, though it still needs to remain in touch with the main body. If part of the mass is separated (or entirely consumed) the user’s consciousness goes with the largest portion of Blood and the user reforms with one additional level of Hunger for each limb (or equivalent body mass) lost. If a portion of the user is consumed, it creates Blood Bonds as normal but the user cannot control their Blood after its been ingested by another.</p>\n<p>• Clone (Cost: See below; Time: 3 hours): While cloning has…dubious implications and has the potential of leading one’s authority into question as who is the “real” vampire is uncertain, Kin of Flesh allows a vampire to essentially separate into two weaker halves. Once transformed, the subject is split into two halves. Each half possesses half (rounded down) Physical Attributes of the other and half (rounded down, to a Generational minimum) Blood Potency, but the same rating of Skills, Disciplines, Willpower and Mental and Social Attributes. Each half may use Disciplines and each half has its own Hunger. Note that they aren’t a hivemind.</p>\n<p>While a player may be allowed to play both at the same time...there are cases in which one half murdered another and got themselves killed trying to claim being the main one. Usually, both halves would agree to being merged back again.</p>\n<p>• Grant Shape (Cost: Free; Time: Minimum 2 hours): The subject can be shaped into anything as long as the mass remains the same. Some Tzimisce shape rude guests into comfortable chairs, others into animal-like creatures and so on. The Tzimisce rarely use it to turn people into animals out of respect for the Gangrel, but that sense of kinship will dissapear soon after the formation of the Sabbat.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until healed or altered again. See Characteristics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"208"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.edjrYrY2gjT2iUkL"},"edkcfpah4Ci9UMvO":{"name":"Taste of Bliss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Shocking even those who are experts at Presence, the Taste of Bliss allows a vampire to simulate a blood bond – effectively trapping the victim in emotional slavery by exhaling a red mist carrying their vitae.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina +Athletics against Dexterity + Athletics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user softly exhales a fine reddish mist towards the target which creeps up to them slowly enough for them to attempt to dodge. The user rolls Stamina +Athletics against Dexterity + Athletics of the victim trying to evade the mist. Should the victim fail to avoid the mist. The mist has a range of 3 meters for each dot of user’s Stamina and Potence they possess.</p>\n<p>Upon the user’s win, the target suffers a Blood Bond to the user with its strength equal to the number of margins.</p>\n<p>This power ignores any real blood bonds, meaning that it does not shatter or impede them in any way either. A normal blood bond will overpower this faux bond.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Power’s effects break at the next sunrise.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"138"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rFuTXUZS":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"bAbYhSnp":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.edkcfpah4Ci9UMvO"},"epHhOU7kaDWvTuyx":{"name":"Unburden the Bestial Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The third eye opens and a shaft of golden radiance lances out, transfixing the victim. Something barely perceptible wafts out of the subject’s mouth, drawn into the vampire’s body. It is the victim’s soul. Users with this level of Valeren may draw the tattered soul from the vampire into their’s third eye, allowing the Healer to repair damage done by the burden of unlife and the Beast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check and two Willpower points</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Insight vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Vampiric souls with a Road rating of 0 cannot be healed with this power, they’re best left to the Warrior Caste of the Salubri. The uses gazes at the target and rolls Stamina + Insight vs. Composure + Resolve of the target (or a flat Difficulty equal to the 10 minus the user’s Road rating if the subject isn’t resisting or if that value is is higher). Failure means the character cannot try again until the following night A win takes the target’s soul. Souls drawn out in this manner become part of the user’s soul while the healing process takes place, and they may return it to its proper body at any time. Bereft of a soul, the target’s body is vacant, but alive; it obeys the user’s commands. The body may be forced into combat, but loses two dice to its dice pools due to lack of coordination (Healers consider this gravely unethical, even if they absorbed the subject’s soul as a defensive measure). The subject’s body and soul were never meant to be separated; holding a soul for longer than strictly necessary — usually, a night — is considered a sin against most Roads (especially Humanity and Heaven). The soul may eventually attempt to escape; both sides roll Composure + Resolve attempted once per week. First to accumulate 5 margins wins the contest. The user only hold one soul at a time.</p>\n<p>While holding the soul, the user may roll Manipulation + Persuasion (Difficulty 3). On a win, the user restores a point of Road rating in vampires whose Road adheres to the Virtues of both Self- Control and Conscience or degrade a point of Road rating if it adheres to other set of Virtues. During this, the user sees the myriad degenerations that led to the soul’s fallen state as harrowing visions. Alternatively, the user may cure one of the subject’s Derangements (Malkavian Clan Bane may only be cured for a single night at a time and Difficulty for the Manipulation + Persuasion is raised to 2 + Bane Severity of the Malkavian).</p>\n<p>A single vampire may have their Road rating improved or degenerated in this manner only once per century. This power automatically fails on those who have a higher Road rating than the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until a soul is released or fights its way out</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"189"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"ZALXLeGZ":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"wnYZWgCr":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.epHhOU7kaDWvTuyx"},"esTbdGKRDQjwVOPE":{"name":"Aura Absorption","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The fae feed on emotions of the living beings, or so the legends say. As is so often the case in Cainite lore, every legend and every lie holds a bit of truth within itself. Subconscious tapping into their faetouched heritage, the adept of Mytherceria can absorb emotions and impressions off objects and places, allowing the user to relive them while they ingest them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Composure + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may be used only once a night on the same target. On a win, the user can select one of the following effects from the list below and for each margin on the roll. For samples of how auras might come off, see p. 20.</p>\n<p>• Read the aura the target’s Road and its rating.</p>\n<p>The user may also choose to adopt the aura of the target for the remaining duration of this power, giving impression of following that Road to those who are able to read them with powers such as Scry the Soul. The target suffers a die penalty on the Degeneration rolls until the power ends.</p>\n<p>• Reveal the target gender, age and name which they go by. The user may also choose to strike them with a penalty of two dice all rolls involving Intelligence as they daze the target, making them harder to remember details about themselves.</p>\n<p>• Read the aura of the target to determine what type of a creature they are. If they are a vampire, the user also learns of their clan or bloodline. The user may also choose to adopt the aura for the target for the remaining duration of this power, causing them to be seen as that creature when viewed by powers such as Scry the Soul. Note that this cannot fool aura reading powers of Mytherceria.</p>\n<p>• Read the aura of the target to determine what Disciplines and other supernatural powers they possess and their relative strength (1-2 dots is considered weak, 3 adept, 4-5 strong) in them. This also works on other types of powers and magics. It is known that infernalists are weary of the faefolk for this very reason.</p>\n<p>• Read the aura of the target to determine their Resonance and its Temperament. The user may also choose to adopt the Resonance and the Temperament for themselves for the remaining duration of this power, causing the target’s temperament to drop by one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The effects last until the user of the power drifts asleep, restoring their own aura.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"91"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"eSHEYM62":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"tGWF2xNs":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.esTbdGKRDQjwVOPE"},"f3TG4IiIyK2BA4Wu":{"name":"Form of the Storm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Becoming an avatar of Set, the user of Serpentis is able to assume a magnificent and rare form of a small sandstorm, becoming untouchable and enter any rooms that are not hermetically sealed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One to three Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The transformation takes three turns to complete by default, but the user is able to speed it up by making additional Rouse Checks to lower it by one turn for each Rouse Check made, with the shortest amount of time required for the transformation being one turn. While transforming, the user can make any action they normally would as their body slowly dissolves.</p>\n<p>Once transformed, the user is able to slip through any crack and crevice and can not be touched. The radius of the storm is a minimum of one meter up to a maximum of meters equal to twice the sum of the user’s Blood Potency and Serpentis rating (the user may change form mid-storm). While in this form, the user can not be damaged by anything other than the True Faith, fire and magics that would be appropriate, against which the user is able to roll and dodge normally, steering the storm to avoid the damage.</p>\n<p>The storm is able to move and fly at a slow walking pace and inflicts two points of unhalved superficial damage to everybody within. Those caught in the storm or trying to attack something in it or past it suffer a penalty of two dice on all rolls related to perception or combat rolls.</p>\n<p>The user may spend a point of Willpower to calm the storm, removing its damaging effects. A point of Willpower may also be spent for the user to physically manifest (making them vulnerable as normal). While physically within the storm, they are immune to its damage, but still suffer from the difficulty caused by crude around them.</p>\n<p>While sand-storm is the most famous example of this power, as cultivated by Setite propaganda, the user of Serpentis may also become a storm of hail, rain and thunder or a storm of the sea. The Witches of Echidna consider this power to be the manifestation of divine wrath, and they frequently remove Set and sand from the idea.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until willingly ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"157"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.f3TG4IiIyK2BA4Wu"},"fI6nCQBYFDMNM2lV":{"name":"Witness of Ahriman","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this subtle power, the vampire’s eyes dilate to the infinite depths of the Abyss, filling a target who meets her gaze with fear. Shadows plague the victim’s mind, gnawing at their sanity and resolve.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Obtenebration 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> The vampire rolls Manipulation + Intimidation vs. Wits + Composure. The power does not work if there is no darkness or shadows around.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Manipulation + Intimidation vs Composure + Wits.</p>\n<p>• Failure: Mortals are unable to act other than in their own defense and lose their next action, backing off for a turn unless they spend Willpower equal to the users margin of win (to a minimum of one). Vampires are unaffected.</p>\n<p>• Win: The target begins seeing disturbing flickers of darkness at the edge of their vision and hears whispers they cannot make out Mortals run in fear. Vampires are unable to act other than in their own defense and lose their next action for a turn unless they spend Willpower equal to the users margin of win (to a minimum of one).</p>\n<p>• Critical Win (or at least 3+ margins): Mortals freeze or crumple into a fetal position. Vampires enter a Terror Frenzy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See system.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"74"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Qfk8lteY":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"6UwuPPZe":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.fI6nCQBYFDMNM2lV"},"fZ12XNmYS9JNbc0G":{"name":"Shape of the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Damned reaches a plateau of symbiosis with the night when they expresse Shape of the Beast. They find a unique fight and flight form to take in keeping with the mythological associations for the Damned in their region or their lineage. In most of Christendom, a vampire with this power becomes a bat or a wolf, though local mythology dictates other forms in other parts of the world, such as vultures and jackals or crow and tigers. The personality and lineage of the vampire may also play the role in deciding their shape. More often than not, the Gangrel and the Tzimisce take the shape of a wolf or a bat while the Followers of Set frequently turn into serpents.</p>\n<p>The animal, while usually a spectacular example of their species, shows no signs to a mundane observer of being supernatural. When taking an animal shape, the user will always assume identical shape. A Gangrel whose shape is a wolf with snow-white fur will more often than not manifest as such when changing shape.</p>\n<p>The animal shapes of Protean are never herbivores, interestingly enough, which leads some to suspect that through changing one’s skin into that of a beast leads the vampire closer to the Beast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon transformation, the vampire gains the Physical Attributes, senses, and native Skills and specialties associated with that animal (see p. XX) and also gains that animal’s natural limits of communication, manipulation (most animals can carry one thing in their mouth), and so forth. If the user has Skills or Attributes at a higher rating and wishes so, they may carry those over but it may also affect appearance of the animal (a wolf with a greater muscle mass if the user has Strength 5, for example). Like animals, the user does not suffer a Called Shot penalty to Bite Attacks but the bite becomes mundane like that of an animal. The user still calculates their Health as Stamina + 3 unless listed Health is higher.</p>\n<p>Free-action powers of Protean like Eyes of the Beast, Weight of a Feather and Feral Weapons may be activated together with Shapechange and powers with it as the prerequisite, bypassing the limit of activating only a single Discipline in a turn. Rouse Check costs must be paid separately. See Protean characteristics for more rules on shapes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire falls to sleep or torpor or ends the power</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"129"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.fZ12XNmYS9JNbc0G"},"fjG8SXITq8KTrbSZ":{"name":"Soul’s Breath","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Watchers carry a terrible and a miraculous secret with them unknown to almost anybody else: the Curse of Caine to subsist on blood and bitter ashes is a lie. Progression of the soul allows one to draw out the sanguine humor that floats within the lungs and the vitality of will in the breath. It’s a meager draught, but one can sup one’s fill from a crowd with none the wiser. With the breath comes power, all flowing to the Watcher. This raises the question what exactly are the Cainites and the Wan Kuei feeding from, as they seem to be two separate creatures feeding on similar things.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Willpower point per target</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire spends a point of Willpower per target they wish to afflict. Facing their victim(s), the user rolls Wits + Subterfuge against the victim’s Composure + Occult. On a win, the user slakes a point of Hunger while the victim’s Hunger increases by 1 if a vampire or, if their victim is a human, they are drained by a point of Hunger (make note of harmful feedings when slaking too much). On a Critical win, the user slakes three points of Hunger and the vampire victim’s Hunger increases by three or the human victim is drained by three points of Hunger.</p>\n<p>A win on the roll allows the Salubri to sup one “Hunger” point per turn from their victim’s breath. The victim rarely knows what’s happening to them, experiencing the loss as a moment of discomfiting weakness. They suffer a point of Aggravated Health damage and one lose one Hunger’s worth of blood per point of “Hunger” slaked, as if the vampire fed from them. Breath does not sate like blood. A vampire who has used this power and hasn’t fed on actual blood for nights equal to their Road rating suffers Physical Impairment until they do.</p>\n<p>Supernatural creatures targeted by this power lose both “Hunger” and part of their power. Each margin on the roll to activate Soul’s Breath carries both a “Hunger” point (and concurrent level of damage) and robs the supernatural being of one point of their power resource tracker (Cainites lose a point of Hunger, Lupines lose Rage, Ghosts suffer an increase to Pathos Degradation, demons suffer an increase of Faith Hunger, etc.), translated to the vampire as a point of Willpower healed. At Storyteller’s discretion, this theft provides an excellent justification for the purchase of out-of-clan Disciplines or Supernatural Merits.</p>\n<p>If one’s Blood Potency provides feeding penalties, they apply here as well. Those possessing Sense the Unseen and similar powers may contest the user’s successes to see the flow of a red breath from the victim to the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended or for turns equal to the margin.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"198"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"cXXIdxuy":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"LtGA9zqv":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.fjG8SXITq8KTrbSZ"},"fkjUftBNMd7JWeN1":{"name":"Swarm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>No longer constrained to a singular form by their transformational gifts, the vampire dissolves their body into a mass of insects, reptiles, or other vermin. The resulting swarm is nigh unkillable and can easily overwhelm an enemy. The unstoppable horde bites, scratches, scurries, slithers, flits, worms, and chews its way into every inch of soft tissue, driving many people into fits of terror before they’re devoured by the hungry mass.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Shapechange</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire Rouses the Blood and spends a full turn, their body breaking down into a swarm of creatures. The vampire may choose the species, but the creatures must be small, barely bigger than a human hand. Typical choices include bats, crows, rats, mice, cockroaches, spiders, flies, locusts, snakes, and leeches. Once chosen, the species remains constant for all uses of Swarm.</p>\n<p>The rules for assuming the forms of a Swarm are identical to Shapechange except the following:</p>\n<p>• Add Health equal to the vampire’s Blood Potency or 3, whichever is higher.</p>\n<p>• In addition to standard actions such as attacking, the user may perform swarming against individuals equal to their Blood Potency or 2, whichever is higher. Swarmed targets receive a two-dice penalty to all tests and sustain 1 damage each turn (Aggravated for mortals, Superficial for supernatural creatures).</p>\n<p>• Conventional attacks aren’t enough to meaningfully damage the swarm, but fire and sunlight are still just as deadly, as are area effects. If even a single member of the swarm survives, the vampire can fully regenerate with no ill effects; if none remain, they meet their final death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the vampire falls to sleep or torpor or ends the power</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"132"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.fkjUftBNMd7JWeN1"},"fzi1ND9FGqdOlsG7":{"name":"Cloak of Shadows","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Standing perfectly still, the user blends into the surroundings. As long as they have some kind of cover, make no sound, and don’t move, only mechanical or supernatural means can detect them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Follow the general rules for Obfuscate. The effect lasts until the user moves or they are detected by other means.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or ended by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"96"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.fzi1ND9FGqdOlsG7"},"g4a6ptp3yvmpdhgG":{"name":"Troll the Pious","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The pus-colored paste produced by this formula resembles the nontoxic glue given to pre-school children and has a similar waxy smell. Wiped on an object, a person or a location, it induces feelings of disquiet and unease in the religious. Those rare folks with True Faith experience even worse outcomes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, melancholic mortal blood, used hot tub water, cornstarch, a body hair from a living green-eyed natural redhead.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The procedure produces enough goo to wax about three square meters of floor — you can rub it into carpet, but it’s messy — or anoint one person all over, or smear a dozen books or about half the tools in a small workshop. Anyone who believes in a benign higher power, or whose profession involves religion, feels uneasy when in a waxed location, or when their attention is drawn to an anointed object. This is sufficient to give a one-die penalty to Social and Mental pools, and to Physical pools requiring close concentration. People with True Faith take a two-dice penalty and also have horrible hallucinations that feel like profound insights into the nature of evil but are actually expressions of their own fears and prejudices. To be fair, it is possible the fears of the truly faithful really could provide insights into evil. This penalty does not affect any pool (such as resisting vampires) to which they would add their True Faith, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> On a location, one dose lasts a couple of months. On an object, two months if no one touches it, one month if it’s handled infrequently, a week if it’s handled often. On a person, a dose lasts three days, and on the first day their skin looks waxy and oily.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"78"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.g4a6ptp3yvmpdhgG"},"g5zUNyGlQ0ZSVmEh":{"name":"Blood Essence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Members of the Warrior Caste of the Banu Haqim are the preferred option when somebody wishes to arrange a contract for assassination of another vampire. Why? Because with Blood Essence, a Cainite may “kill” another Cainite, but preserve their heart for proof. The heart can be eaten by another Cainite, often the wronged party who had hired the assassin in the first place, to commit diablerie upon the vanquished Cainite. This technique can also work on mortals, preserving hearts as blood candy for slaking Hunger on the go.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> First, the user must exsanguinate the victim down to their last point of Hunger. If the victim is a vampire, they must be in torpor and be fully drained as if one is to commit diablerie.</p>\n<p>After that, the user makes an Intelligence + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 2 and pours a hunger’s worth of Rouse Check near the area of the heart of the victim. As they massage it, their hand sinks deeper and deeper into the body until they are able to grasp the heart and rip it out. Cainite’s body turns to ash as it does normally, but their heart remains.</p>\n<p>The preserved heart of a mortal or a Cainite can be consumed later, slaking one point of Hunger in the process. The act of slaking should be a minor action of -2 dice if done in combat. A consumed heart of a Cainite triggers a diablerie roll as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The preserved heart remains until destroyed or consumed. Note that Cainite hearts can only be destroyed by things that deal Aggravated Health damage to vampires such as the Sun or fire.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"139"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"HgpmSYWm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"aDkwrMKi":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.g5zUNyGlQ0ZSVmEh"},"gHGsh45JYFhbkskT":{"name":"Banishing Sign of Thoth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>The character gestures, turning aside any supernatural effect aimed at them. The source of the effect does not matter. They can thwart, or at least blunt, the strength of any mystical attack. This sign was supposedly taught to Osiris by Thoth, the Egyptian god of wisdom and magic. What Thoth actually was — vampire, mortal, or spellcaster — is lost to time.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free, does not count as an activated power for the limit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the user is about to be struck by a supernatural power such as Disciplines, Rituals, Arcanoi of ghosts or Arcanae of demons, the user may make a Resolve + Occult roll and make a sign of Thoth instead of choosing to resist the standard way the supernatural power is resisted. The user’s successes on the Sign of Thoth reduce the opponent’s successes, making the opponent’s power fail if it reaches 0 successes.</p>\n<p>This power may also be used reflexively as a defensive action instead of being a main action, breaking the rule that only one Discipline power may be activated in one turn and the user may even use it against multiple powers attacking them by activating this power multiple times (one for each attack). The user does not suffer the accumulative penalty for multiple opponents on rolls for the Sign of Thoth. Even if the user does not see their attacker, as is the case in many rituals, the user may instinctually make the sign without even realizing that they were under a supernatural attack a moment ago.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One action</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"38"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Shk0kIQY":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"6Fnh0YvS":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.gHGsh45JYFhbkskT"},"gMI39QzMESrRC22b":{"name":"Thrown Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can cause their voice to emanate from any point within sight, whether as a whisper or shout. The voice is audible as if the vampire was standing in that spot and can be heard by anyone within earshot, depending on strength. The voice can also be left in a location, continuing to lure or terrify those who stray, depending on the power used.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No roll is required, apart from the Rouse Check. Thrown Voice allows for usage of any other Melpominee power and any other powers that fit the requirements such as Presence with Presenceless Voice (vanilla Melpominee level 2 power of Presence). The user must roll and pay the cost as usual when combining Thrown Voice with other powers and may similar powers.</p>\n<p>Thrown Voice does not count towards the limit of activating only one power per turn as it is an augmentation, not a power of its own.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or a night on a critical win, if the player so chooses.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"87"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.gMI39QzMESrRC22b"},"gTMNjnEe5yjFctW0":{"name":"Heart Thief","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>With a quick snatch, the user is able to take away a part of someone’s body, most commonly the heart. This does not destroy a vampire victim, unless the attacker then destroys the stolen heart. Heart Thief is not an easy power to use despite its speed, but few Discipline effects can place one vampire in another’s power so suddenly and completely.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After activation, the user makes an unarmed Strength + Brawl (-2 success Called Shot) attack against their opponent’s defensive roll like Dexterity + Athletics dodge, dealing damage as normal. Neither of these rolls is affected by Discipline power bonus. This roll may be skipped if the victim voluntarily submits.</p>\n<p>If the user manages to successfuly get to the heart, they next roll Resolve + Occult against the victim’s Stamina + Stamina (or Stamina + Fortitude, if it would yield a higher pool) — power bonus applies to this contest.</p>\n<p>On a win, the user takes out the victim’s heart and automatically deals two points of Aggravated Health damage that cannot be mitigated.</p>\n<p>Once removed the vampire gains a two-dice bonus to resist Frenzy, but suffers a die penalty on Road Degeneration on rolls. If the heart is dealt Aggravated Health damage equal to or greater than the vampire’s Health, they fall into torpor. If an extracted heart is destroyed — only possible with fire or sunlight — the vampire experiences final death. While the heart is removed, the vampire’s body cannot be staked unless the heart is located and separately impaled, in which case the vampire falls paralysed. If blood is fed to the heart, it will slake the vampire’s Hunger as normal (even Blood Bonding them if fed vampire vitae from the vein), and the heart can be fed from in the same way — possibly leading to diablerie. The vampire is still susceptible to death via fire or sunlight even without a heart.</p>\n<p>The user may target themselves with this power, should they wish to take out their own heart, rolling Resolve + Occult at a Difficulty of 3.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent (or until the heart is destroyed/returned)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"159"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.gTMNjnEe5yjFctW0"},"ggZ2hVRZRGvPvLfO":{"name":"Rewind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The True Brujah usually use this power to confirm historical events, but many find it useful to discover the secrets of their peers and their enemies. When this power is used, it appears as if time is moving backwards at the character’s command. But in truth the vampire is only seeing images and hearing voices of what has happened here before. The ability only works in the area the character is in, and cannot show anything happening through walls. Ghostly images of what occurred in the area can be seen as far as anyone can normally see. So they can look out of the window and see what happened outside, but not see past a wall, even if it wasn’t built until recently. Similarly, voices are only heard across an earshot. Nothing is taken away, only added to. However, if you made a hole in wall you could see what was on the other side of it through the hole.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Leaden Movement</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> First, the user must be standing directly where they wish to travel back in time. The user then makes a Intelligence + Occult roll at a Difficulty of 2. On a win, they may travel into the past according to the result on the table below (knowledge of what exactly the vampire is searching for allows to pick specific scenes at specific times).</p>\n<p>Within this limit, they can look at any time they like, but they have to focus on a particular and specific time to go back to. If they don’t know when they are looking for they will have to watch the images as they flash past and make a Wits + Awareness roll. The Difficulty is set by the storyteller and should range from 1 for events the character went through to 6. It takes an hour to review every ten years, and after an hour, the character must spend another Rouse Check to maintain the power. To conserve blood when looking into the deep past, the vampire might shift their view back 50 years (up to the time limit permitted by the margins), for instance, before starting to make Wits + Awareness rolls as they know what they want to see happened 50-100 years ago. In this way, visions of the ancient past might take several attempts before the vampire finds the right area of time to focus on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended Margins Effect 0 margins Up to 24 hours 1 margin Up to one week 2 margins Up to one month 3 margins Up to one year 4 margins Up to a century 5 margins Up to one millenium 6+ margins Up to one millenium + five centuries per margin over 5</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"172"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ggZ2hVRZRGvPvLfO"},"gjvghM87yrxbLkym":{"name":"Arms of Ahriman","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire summons shadowy extensions from unlit spots in the area, within line of sight. Local shadows distort as they flow across walls and floors and converge on one or more hapless victims. Whether by gliding up the body of the victim or engaging in a mystic grapple with the victim’s own shadow, the arms are able to hold them in place or smother them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Potence 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Brawl</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Additional arms can be created by splitting the dice pool, enabling the user to engage multiple opponents. The vampire is able to use the shadows to attack as a full action, using their Wits + Brawl to attack and deal Superficial damage or grapple, adding half the user’s Potence rating (round up) as a damage bonus.</p>\n<p>The arms can defend their user with a dice pool of Wits + Brawl. This is handled as defending normally, dealing no damage with these rolls and applying a cumulative one-die penalty to each defense. Arms can hit incorporeal beings as shadows tend to penetrate even into spiritual realms.</p>\n<p>They can also use the shadows to perform simple actions (such as opening doors and pulling levers) but nothing as advanced as typing. The extrusions have a length (in yards/ meters) equal to twice the Obtenebration dots of the user. (Note that they, being shadows, move across surfaces, not air, and any distances must take this into account.)</p>\n<p>This power does not benefit from power bonus because the Arms of Ahriman is a physical object.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows, gain an additional die on all grappling rolls. The user may also pick up or break objects and people, rolling with and treating Resolve as their Strength rating for such feats. One Lasombra whose legs no longer function (courtesy of his sire’s “tests”) is known to have used Arms of Ahriman in this manner to give themselves mobility. Speed when moving things and people around with arms should be a couple of times faster than running.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended or destroyed</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"107"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"t1CywyRR":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"dLs6PXoW":{"path":"skills.brawl"}},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.gjvghM87yrxbLkym"},"gmQHJoOWQI03ezvw":{"name":"Spectral Puppeteer","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Only a few Tremere have mastered this diabolical art, enabling them to seize control of a mortal with their spiritual form. Such possession is surely the Devil’s teaching — or so other Cainites would believe if they knew such a feat were possible. As its very existence is a closely guarded secret of the clan, this power is chiefly used to discredit or frame rivals while establishing a perfect alibi for the vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisit:</strong> Anima Walk</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate •••••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Wits + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> In order to use this power, a vampire must have sent her consciousness into the astral plane with the Auspex •••• power Anima Walk. Normally, an ethereal vampire cannot use their Disciplines to affect the physical plane. However, vampires who know this power may use use this power to take control of another being while in astral form.</p>\n<p>For all intents and purposes this power works identically to Possession (see Possession to the left) except that it can only be used while in Anima Walk. If the vampire succeeds in displacing the victim’s soul, their astral body flows into the host’s mouth and assumes control. If the vampire leaves the host voluntarily, their astral form reforms outside the mortal’s body and may continue moving and acting according to the rules for Anima Walk. Any sympathetic damage sustained by a possessing vampire using this power is applied to the vampire’s astral form rather than their physical body. The sudden death of the host instantly evicts the vampire and severs their silver cord. Abandoning a vessel is a Full Action.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended, voluntarily or involuntarily.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"36"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"myM695rE":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"O1sGpBlJ":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.gmQHJoOWQI03ezvw"},"gocjf5vUxtgkYVY7":{"name":"Read the Room","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire senses the overriding opinion of a crowd, fueling their understanding about how many would support or oppose a suggestion. Crafty vampires Read the Room before proposing a course of action or even declaring praxis.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire activates the power and mentally poses a question they want answered, as though they were taking a poll, then tests Intelligence + Insight. Difficulty starts at 5 for a crowd of 12 (mortals or vampires) and decreases for each additional 12 individuals down to a minimum of Difficulty 2.</p>\n<p>Storytellers may decide that certain questions are too complex to answer, and while numbers are always vague (some, many, most, etc.), they otherwise summarize the crowd’s general sentiment. On a critical win or with 3 margins, they detect how this sentiment changes from any actions taken during the scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instantaneous, or one scene on a critical or three margins.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"30"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"CKPThdi0":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"JxFj951a":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.gocjf5vUxtgkYVY7"},"hBC25Gav35yyvtoR":{"name":"Locked Contemplation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Due to the efficacy of this simple ability, most True Brujah think it likely their Antediluvian has remained trapped in the instant Troile’s fangs pierced his throat in perpetuity. A Cainite with this power masters the time perception granted through Hourglass of the Mind, allowing them to dilate their mental acuity in response to crisis. Secondly, they may also project this ability in the reverse upon others, numbing all thought processes and perceptual awareness while delivering a fathomless daze to her victim which blanks the mind.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free/Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Willpower point/One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Awareness, Manipulation + Occult vs. Wits + Intelligence</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The primary effect of this power may be activated reflexively as a defensive action. The player spends one Willpower point and rolls Wits + Awareness. With a single success, the vampire’s perception of time halts as their mind steps out of flow with linear time into a perpetual present. They may pontificate and contemplate, but cannot act physically, use any power requiring a physical activation cost (such as blood), or influence the world in any way. Once the vampire decides their next course of action, they return their attention to time and may act on their plans.</p>\n<p>The character gains a number of dice on their first next non-tracker action equal to half the number of scored successes, rounded up. However, some actions and events are unavoidable. A vampire could delay in eternity ruminating from a no-win position without coming to a comfortable conclusion; a Cainite may pause to consider a stake the moment before it pierces theirr heart, but no matter how long they deliberate on a course of action, they simply cannot dodge it (although in the vampire may spend the rest of eternity in this state from their point of view).</p>\n<p>For the secondary effect of this power, the vampire concentrates on a single victim in line of sight (this effect is a Full Action). The player makes a Rouse Check and rolls Manipulation + Occult vs. the victim’s Wits + Intelligence. On a win, the victim falls into a light trance that lasts one minute per margin. Entranced victims are oblivious to their surroundings and the flow of time around them. The state ends immediately if the victim suffers damage or experiences a sudden jolt to their senses, such as a thunderclap or even a gentle nudge. Such victims count as immobilised in combat and all attacks made against them are made at a Difficulty of 1. Normal conversation does not break the trance, though shouting does. Complications on the roll may causs the vampire to themselves to enter the trance.</p>\n<p>As this power alters perceptions, not physics, powers such as Unswayable Mind may aid the subject.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant/One minute per margin.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"167"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"QjjwWbCW":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"WOdaRtsc":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.hBC25Gav35yyvtoR"},"hLq4idiKfw5BZkRw":{"name":"Freezer Fluid","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>When ingested by or injected into a blankbody, this serum freezes their limbs, making them clumsy and possibly even helpless. Wood absorbs it; plastic remains inert; it sublimes rapidly from metal. Hunters either covertly inject it into their targets or soak their stakes in it. Targets get a Wits + Awareness roll to avoid drinking it in a beverage (Difficulty 2 for non-foaming beverages; Difficulty 3 for beer).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The Alchemist’s Blood, melancholy and phlegmatic human blood, echinacea, possum scent glands, local anaesthetic or ricin</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The victim makes a Contested Stamina + Resolve roll vs the Alchemist’s Resolve + Alchemy (7 dice). If the victim wins, the serum has no effect. For every success the Alchemist wins by, the victim loses 2 dice from all physical dice pools.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Second Inquisition","page":"47"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.hLq4idiKfw5BZkRw"},"hcw8RmrfM4v3k7t3":{"name":"Martian Purity","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The name refers not to <em>Martian</em> in the extraterrestrial sense, but to the Roman god Mars and his association with iron, the choleric principle and similar mystical correspondence.</p>\n<p>With that cleared up, this spell cures blood-borne illnesses by expelling them via one’s pores as ignited, flaming gas. Some Princes who’ve learned about this stuff insist on inflicting it on suspected plague-rats in their domains, citing Masquerade concerns and offering weak apologies for the unpleasantness as a Hound or other court functionary stands by with a fire extinguisher.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric mortal blood, iron filings, gunpowder ash.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This only works on a mortal or Kindred carrying a blood-transmissible infection. When consumed, the elixir forces the impurity out through their skin, outgassing through the pores of the back. It ignites on contact with air, doing 2 points of Aggravated damage to the carrier. It is up to the Storyteller whether this works on individual supernatural infections.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Two rounds</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"77"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.hcw8RmrfM4v3k7t3"},"hkY6jORBMJw9AwFS":{"name":"Eyes of Chaos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>Fleeting clarity hides within insanity; a shattered mirror reflects more than one unbroken one. This level of Dementation is responsible for the Malkavians’ reputation of gleaning hidden truths, and their tendency to speak those truths to power. They may scrutinize the “patterns” of personal interaction or even random events in nature itself in order to reveal a hidden truth about the situation they’re in. The character needs not ask a question – the patterns are obvious at even a moment’s glance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Investigation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may only be used when scrutinising a complex pattern or event, such as the interactions of a vampiric court or the clash of an army. Analyzing the blood spatter flicked from one’s fingers or the spill of entrails works just as well, however.</p>\n<p>The character rolls Resolve + Investigation at a Difficulty of 3 as they ponder the hidden truths of their focus. The player may ask the storyteller questions equal to the margin +1. Some of the viable questions are:</p>\n<p>• What’s this person’s Road?</p>\n<p>• What’s the underlying or hidden message here?</p>\n<p>• How relevant to our overarching goals/my ambition is this scene?</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user of Eyes of Chaos may also use the power and roll Resolve + Investigation to actively focus on the situation at hand or in future events as a result of a butterfly effect Half the successes (rounded up) are provided as a dice bonus to any non-tracker pool. The bonuses last until the caster spends them or until the user reactivates Eyes of Chaos in this manner. Eyes of Chaos cannot aid pools to fortune-tell or when the user consciously forces some kind of result of fortune telling (like from a Blood Surge, artefact usage or another dice bonus) as the user then supernaturally alters what was calculated. No more than three dice may be added in a single pool.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Information is acquired after a short time of investigating/until the dice bonus is exhausted.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"65"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"uFr8wynu":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"IXEV8fio":{"path":"skills.investigation"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.hkY6jORBMJw9AwFS"},"hrOCyOU9jqDFhrkd":{"name":"Gift of Anubis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>The jackal-headed god of funerary rites does not forget the suffering of the Children of Osiris, doomed to feast on humans. Anubis’ boon alleviates some of that pain by letting the hungry one feed on the bodies empty of souls that are unable to feel pain and haunt the conscience of the vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user halves (rounded up) their Blood Potency for the purposes of slaking Hunger from corpses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"38"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.hrOCyOU9jqDFhrkd"},"ht90tex3FejdZG12":{"name":"The Body Impolitic","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Arguably the most ghoulish of Vicissitude’s gifts, The Body Impolitic allows the user to invest a portion of her sentience in the flesh of their body, then sever it from the trunk to do the vampire’s bidding.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user retains full awareness of any spilled body parts and may move them as if they are connected.</p>\n<p>When needed, a body part can tear itself free from the main body; doing so requires two turns and deals the usual point of Aggravated Health damage to the body. The vampire can have as many servitor “parts” as they have extremities to use While a body part is detached and still sentient, the vampire cannot regrow it. If they have no bodyparts to spare (such as those added through other Vicissitude powers), the vampire also suffers a related Crippling Injury. Disembodied parts are always aware of all other parts of itself, but otherwise have only the sensory input normally available to them. Some Tzimisce will use Vicissitude to craft additional sensory organs onto these extremities to make it easier to perform specific tasks (mouths and tongues to deliver messages, eyes to spy with, and so on). Such alterations are typically minor enough to be free or cost the extremity half of a Physical Attribute dot.</p>\n<p>The vampire can use Disciplines through a disembodied extremity, but only if that body part can fulfill the Discipline’s requirements (that is, it must have blood enough to fuel the Discipline, must be able to make eye contact if that’s a requirement of the Discipline, etc.). Independent body parts have one Health level each and possess 3 dots in Dexterity and an almost comical ability to move. If an extremity is destroyed or the vampire abandons it (which may happen at any point by simply willing it), it can be recreated by healing the missing Health point.</p>\n<p>With this power, the user is invulnerable to decapitations as they may just regrow a head, but destroying their torso destroys the vampire normally.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"207"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ht90tex3FejdZG12"},"hyWtnBHZVtjZvQEE":{"name":"Animal Messenger","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can commune with the beasts of the wild and the city. Feral Whispers allows two-way communication with animals. A cat might not be interested in debating Matisse’s use of color but happily discusses the lack of prey around the brownstone building across the street. Depending on the vampire’s skill, they can even persuade animals to perform services; like humans, animals seldom agree to things that go against their nature or endanger them.</p>\n<p>Vampires can also use Feral Whispers to summon a chosen type of animal (see Animalism limitations above) but the animals must be present to answer. Nothing prevents a vampire trying to summon an orca in Central Park, but success seems unlikely. Summoned animals listen to the summoner, but scatter or attack if endangered.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Bond Famulus</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full action</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check for each night</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> None, though the Famulus must test Resolve + Streetwise / Survival (or equivalent) if the target’s location is unknown</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire whispers a single sentence to the Famulus and states the target. If the location of the recipient is unknown the Famulus must track them down, making a tracking test (see above) at a Difficulty of 2, or resisted by the target’s Intelligence + Streetwise/Survival if they are actively trying to hide (from the famulus or from other perceived threats). The attempt can be made once per night. The message is delivered as soon as the Famulus makes eye contact with the target, at which point they return to their master.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One or more nights depending in length of search</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"14"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.hyWtnBHZVtjZvQEE"},"i5C0NcYgZnO9lBOX":{"name":"Mandagloire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Also called “The Funk” by alchemists who copy and adjust the formula.</p>\n<p>The alchemist is able to make their blood emit a noxious gas that causes a rigid paralysis to mortals and supernatural creatures alike.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s blood, phlegmatic blood, human fat, candle wax, ether, sesame oil, iron sulfate or verdigris, and borax.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pool:</strong> Stamina + Alchemy vs. Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Tapping the athanor’s vein emits a pungent stench when spilled that fills a room (or a house on a critical win). Mortals who fail to resist enter a full-body paralysis state, a waking coma, for the rest of the scene. Supernatural creatures who fail feel the effects of rigor mortis: a two-dice penalty on all Physical rolls from intense cramping.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Fixatio:</strong> The potion can be slipped into a drink or meal as a poison, or burned to act as a paralyzing fragrance, above.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Three turns</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"104"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"WLZ9e4Uo":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"dbuteKi7":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.i5C0NcYgZnO9lBOX"},"i6iGp8tVBoe0wi4C":{"name":"Shush the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Masters of Valeren may finally silence the Beast for good at the cost of suppressing themselves as a person as well. The existence of this power has raised doubts over whether Saulot really achieved Golconda or just simply fabricated it using this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user pays the cost and the effect is almost instantaneous. The Beast is numbed (or placated?) and the vampire is immune to all forms of Frenzy. The side effect of this is that the user themselves becomes numb, suffering a two-dice penalty on Degeneration roll until they next awaken from daysleep. All attempts to sway the user based on emotions or intimidate them have their difficulty increased by 2 (or simply reduced by 2 successes in a contest)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the user awakens next</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"199"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.i6iGp8tVBoe0wi4C"},"iD3RrspdCRAHmcoR":{"name":"Earthshock","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Their strength an elemental force, the vampire can slam their fist or foot into the ground, creating a shockwave that throws their opponents prone. One of the more dramatic applications of Potence, this power needs to be carefully employed, lest the user literally bring the house down upon themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No additional test is needed to create the shockwave. (The ground is hard to miss.) Anyone within a five-meter radius of the user must make a Dexterity + Athletics roll (Difficulty 3), with the results below. Anyone prepared for the Earthshock (such as the user’s companions) can shift their results up by one step.</p>\n<p>• Critical Win or 4 margins: No effect.</p>\n<p>• Win: Knocked off balance. Lose current action</p>\n<p>• Failure: Fall prone. Lose current action; must spend a turn getting up, losing the next action This power causes significant collateral damage. If used on the ground, the earth cracks. If used indoors, furniture breaks and mirrors shatter. On anything but the ground floor the floor might shatter, causing everyone within the radius to plummet to the floor below. This power can only be used once per scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One use/turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"119"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iD3RrspdCRAHmcoR"},"iDjdouD4kXYCpdtd":{"name":"Healer’s Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The signature power of the Healers stems from the ability to apply the vampiric regenerative process to others. The third eye opens wide, shedding slick sanguine light. The target feels sweet, invigorating energies rushing into their wounds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Ease Wounds</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Three Full Actions</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire cannot target themselves with this power. This power works on any living or undead, but the user must be able to touch the target, even if they merely touch them for a moment in combat. Upon contact, the user makes an Intelligence + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 2. Each margin allows for one point of Superficial Health damage to be healed or one point of Aggravated Health damage for every three margins. The user may choose to also mend Grevious Injuries of the target, bestowing the miracle of vitae to regrow limbs. Each injury is treated as a single point of Aggravated Health damage. A win also cleanses the target of all and any ailments or taints that defile the body – supernatural (Vicissitude, Mastery over the Mortal Shell Path of Thaumaturgy, Biothaumaturgy, and so on) or otherwise. A person cannot be forcefully healed or cleansed of supernatural taint unless they are willing.</p>\n<p>A subject may be affected by this power only one per night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The target heals over the course of three turns. If interrupted, the power’s healing effects are void.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"185"},"level":3,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"HgpmSYWm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"aDkwrMKi":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iDjdouD4kXYCpdtd"},"iLJulmXD9nEw656E":{"name":"Observant Eye","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>After the Warlocks began their purge of the Salubri, the third eye was essentially a target for others which is why the Salubri tended to conceal it. In their glory days, however, Salubri wore their third eye proudly as it also granted them great advantages. One such advantage was the “alertness” of the third eye, in a way independent of the vampire to whom it belongs. It is said that Saulot’s third eye was open and gazed right at Tremere himself as he proceeded to diablerise the torpored Antediluvian…</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When rolling perception rolls against danger (such as Awareness against surprise attacks, Sense Death of Valeren and Sense the Unseen of Auspex), the user rolls the same dicepool twice and selects whichever dicepool suits them the best (before further rerolls). If one of the dicepools scores a Messy Critical or a Bestial Failure, the user suffers the Messy/Bestial outcome regardless of whichever result they selected.</p>\n<p>This power does not work while the third eye is closed. If the user is rolling a second pool, the eye opens automatically. This may be done subconsciously, such as when rolling to detect a surprise attack.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"191"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iLJulmXD9nEw656E"},"iMo09mBr3toqLxkT":{"name":"Necrotic Gaze","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Master of ailments and rotting flesh, the user no longer requires close contact to afflict their targets.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power amplified</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A, Manipulation + Medicine vs. Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitud e)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Powers of Thanatosis such as Putrefaction and Whence All Came require no skin contact. If the power has no contest pools, it is Manipulation + Medicine vs. Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude, if the victim possesses it)</p>\n<p>Additionally, ampire may amplify any of their Thanatosis powers to affect a group of people at once by spending an additional Rouse Check to activate the power. All of the victims need to be within line of sight. The user makes any needed roll against the strongest opponent in the group, although players should always be given the option to roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive/ Per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"181"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iMo09mBr3toqLxkT"},"iTbPXa428tKUhzdO":{"name":"Reclaim the Gift of Humanity","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>The dead experience emotion, passion even, but all too often, these are but a dulled reflection of the feelings experienced by the living. Many Cainites experience the world vicariously through their thralls and Touchstones, but the closest most get to the strong emotions of the living is through Resonance inherited from stolen blood. This Power allows the vampire to luxuriate in the passions of the living, experiencing true emotion — the good, the bad, and the ugly — and channeling it into something worthwhile for themselves. While the vampire experiences the highs of human feelings, their victims feel drained, broken, and prone to acts of unpredictable desperation.</p>\n<p>This power is said to have been developed by a traitor to the Children of Osiris who no longer wished to obey the edict to protect humanity.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire must spend a scene in close proximity to a mortal target but needn’t directly engage them. (This Power has no effect on vampire targets, even Humanity 10 vampires.) After rousing, test Composure + Insight against their Wits + Composure (Storytellers might add dice to the user in situations where emotions are running high or are deliberately provoked). On a win, the vampire treats their Road/Path/Humanity rating as 10 for the purposes of the Blush of Life and other experiences. On a critical win, they may also heal a Willpower damage of either type. On a bestial failure or Messy Critical, the surge of emotion is too much to bear, and the vampire suffers a Compulsion. Vampires can only benefit from this euphoric feeling once per night but can keep trying should they fail.</p>\n<p>Human targets of this Power lose all trace of Resonance in their blood for three nights. While empty of Resonance, the target can freely indulge their basest desires, descending into psychopathy with no higher nature or positive emotion to push back on these impulses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until sunset for the vampire, three nights for mortal victims</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"38"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"p06czqQp":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"PEs1NOFM":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iTbPXa428tKUhzdO"},"iWiCFbplW5xo0lOs":{"name":"Resilience","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>Endowed with supernatural endurance, the user can strengthen their physical resolve.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user adds their Fortitude rating to their Health track.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"79"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iWiCFbplW5xo0lOs"},"iYsoJea9px8WTbvs":{"name":"Flowering Amaranth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This form of shared diablerie draws virtue from the vitae of a full-blooded Kindred into the alchemist and their assistants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Blood from the alchemist and participating thin-bloods (up to three thin-bloods total), juice of red amaranth flowers from a plant fertilized with blood, pulverized leeches, hydrogen sulfide, and neon.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> Everyone participating must roll one Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pool:</strong> Resolve + Alchemy vs. Willpower pool + Blood Potency</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist and up to two other participants tie a silk cord around their wrists, connecting them in a triangle surrounding a full-blooded Kindred target, also so bound. The alchemist rolls Resolve + Alchemy; each additional thin-blood participant can add one die to the pool by spending 2 Willpower. If the full-blood resists, they use their Willpower pool + Blood Potency. On a win, the participating thin-bloods gain one dot in one of the target’s core clan Disciplines, determined randomly. (On a critical win, the alchemist can choose the Discipline.) They cannot learn or purchase this Discipline higher than the first dot, but in other respects can use it as though they were full-blooded Kindred. They do not gain generation, but still lose 1 Humanity and gain the black-veined aura of diablerie.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"106"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"PIKyFgjK":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"8Az4bMqD":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iYsoJea9px8WTbvs"},"iZ39WTaQZK0P1ozo":{"name":"Pacts of Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Reaching the ability to contain more spiritual power within them, the user is able to grasp towards more spirits which in turn improves their ability to benefit from spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Aspects of the Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Aspects of the Beast power no longer requires a roll. The user is now able to take the same aspect as many times as they want. Maximum Attribute rating of the vampire is raised to 4 + their Blood Surge value, for the purposes of using aspects from Aspect of the Higher Beast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"164"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iZ39WTaQZK0P1ozo"},"iaHDRZ6nk3JYtEPH":{"name":"The False Sip","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s mastery of their own form can prevent any blood (or Blood) swallowed from entering their system, allowing them to avoid poisoning or the forming of Blood Bonds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Fortitude 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character must activate this power immediately upon consuming blood from any source. The user is able to keep the blood down, but it doesn’t enter their system nor slake any Hunger, though the user can taste it. No other blood or vitae can be consumed during this time. The vampire has one scene to vomit the blood back up, otherwise it enters their system. The effect can be extended to another scene by a second Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or more</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"128"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.iaHDRZ6nk3JYtEPH"},"idyQ554xYgxjYFoo":{"name":"Body Paint","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula allows the alchemist to create tattoos with personal touches beyond human tattoo artists. Sometimes, an alchemist’s client can customize their ink further. The alchemist creates a shiny, dark brown fluid that stays in liquid form until painted or tattooed onto mortal or vampire skin. The alchemist tattoos the target or reaches out and massages the flesh as the liquid dries, shaping it into unique patterns.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric mortal blood, tattoo inks, henna, marker fluid, or any substance that stains skin.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dexterity + Craft; Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On Distillation rolls with 1–3 successes, the body paint dries faster, and the alchemist must Win a Dexterity + Craft test at Difficulty 3 to finish their work before drying. On Distillations with 6 or more successes, the target may reshape the tattoo any time after a week with a Stamina + Resolve Test at Difficulty 5. The alchemist may change the fluid’s colors at will while working, as can the target if they’re able to reshape their tattoo.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent, unless erased by flesh colored Body Paint.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"73"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"vAT5qr6k":{"path":"attributes.dexterity"},"HcG2LeQj":{"path":"skills.craft"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.idyQ554xYgxjYFoo"},"ijse9ElEa9P1k29r":{"name":"Aura Syphon","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>A power that arises suspicion and paranoia in those who know of it (and the Tremere very well do know of it), Aura Syphon allows a vampire to overwrite their own aura using the aspects of another person’s aura or to “adopt” another person’s aura in its entirety. This power used to be one of the reasons why Healers and Warriors of the Salubri would gaze at their Watchers with suspicion as it was much harder to prove that a Watcher with this power was consorting with the infernal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Subterfuge against Wits + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Intelligence + Subterfuge against Wits + Resolve of the target within the line of sight. On a win, the user perceives all possible information from the victim’s aura (see Scry the Soul for information samples) and may decide to take whatever aura features they wish such as appearing like the same creature as the subject (for example, a ghoul if the subject is one), possession or lack of possession of diablerie veins, emotions and so on.</p>\n<p>Once selected, the user’s Watcher Valeren rating is added as Difficulty to any attempt to “read” them. Failing to reach the added Difficulty lets the reader see only the false personality and aura the user has stolen, without any hint that the reader is being misled. (Failing to reach even the standard Difficulty yields nothing, as normal, so as not to let on that a false personality is present.).</p>\n<p>The person from whom aura was taken suffers Mental Impairment and a die penalty to Degeneration Tests for as long as the vampire holds onto their aura. The victim does not “lose” an aura as the vampire only took enough to cover themselves with it.</p>\n<p>This power cannot conceal Clan Banes such as that of the Children of Haqim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One story or until the vampire dispels it</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"198"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"4s0LpsNG":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"623tO8sC":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ijse9ElEa9P1k29r"},"imwhmRDqQJpBa2Vi":{"name":"Peaceful Touch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Watchers deal in knowledge, but knowledge threatens the most powerful of men — the brutal nights during the Wolf of Qin’s purges are still fresh in the minds of their elders. Healing wounds after they occur is miraculous, but greater miracles lie in calming the bloodlust that comes with violence.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> By touching the target, which in combat could be done with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Brawl/Athletics, the user i sable to calm the person who is hostile to them. After touching them, they roll Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve (or at a difficulty of 3 if they are not resisting). On a win, the target cannot make any hostile actions against the vampire for turns equal to the user’s Watcher Valeren rating + the margin on the roll unless they spend a point of Willpower each turn. Additionally, the target gains a two-dice bonus on resisting Frenzies, making them more susceptible to reason. Knowing Gift of Sleep or Morphean Blow automatically increases the duration for another two turns.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Turns equal to the user’s Watcher Valeren rating + the margin on the roll</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"196"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.imwhmRDqQJpBa2Vi"},"imyKIqqnCdXIk4RC":{"name":"Friend of the Spirits","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user’s very aura becomes attuned more alluring to the spirits, appearing as either one of them or something friendly to them, allowing the users of Spiritus to demand more from them without enraging the spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user adds their Spiritus rating, to all non-physical pools directly involving the spirits of animals and concepts. This excludes the powers of this Discipline as per the stacking rule.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"160"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.imyKIqqnCdXIk4RC"},"ix9yGG5vFF2NQLwZ":{"name":"Concoct Ashe","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The process of creating the Ashe drug is a form of Blood Alchemy in itself, and generally takes place in a laboratory or workshop due to the need for an open flame and metal filings.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A destroyed vampire’s ashes, an open flame reaching 1,500°C, aluminum filings.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Thin- Blood Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire’s ashes are contained in a vessel heated by an open flame and then mixed with aluminum filings. Each success on the distillation roll produces a single Ashe dose. On a total failure, the sample is ruined and a Beast Shard (see p. XX) emerges in the area. When consumed, all Ashe allows a thin-blood to access more of the power in vitae and ash than is usually within their grasp. Their Blood Potency counts as 1 for the purposes of Blood Surges and Discipline Rouse Check re-rolls until the end of the chapter. The drug is far from lacking in downsides however, see Ashe Addiction (see p. XX).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Cults of the Blood Gods","page":"46"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"9Ef1VDAm":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"Y8lN85Ko":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ix9yGG5vFF2NQLwZ"},"jEr2uYuHA4espRNU":{"name":"On-Demand Sunburn","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>A dread tool of the Path of the Sun, On-Demand Sunburn traps sunlight in the very body of its user, enabling them to unleash it as a destructive force through their own skin, searing themselves and their prey in a sizzling embrace. While the damage done by a single user is not enough to incinerate a vampire, a group of self-immolating Heliophiles can spell doom for fledglings and elders alike.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Blood of the Alchemist, choleric AT: mortal blood, gold shavings, mercury, as well as assorted halogens (usually chlorine or bromide). The formula also needs to be subjected to sunlight for several hours before use, and alchemists employing athanor corporis usually “juice up” on Portable Shade (see above) for this.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On activation, the alchemist becomes an unliving battery of sunlight with a single charge seething in their veins. They can unleash this charge at will, inflicting aggravated damage upon themselves and another vampire whom they’ve successfully achieved skin contact with, as their own skin smolders and blackens. The user takes two levels of aggravated damage, while the victim suffers an amount equal to their Bane Severity, as if exposed to daylight for a full turn. If the victim has the means to reduce the damage (such as through Fortitude) they can do so. If the formula isn’t activated before then, the power automatically activates at the first sunset after ingestion, causing two levels of aggravated damage to the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until unleashed or next sunset, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Sabbat: The Black Hand","page":"53"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.jEr2uYuHA4espRNU"},"jQZ1cQYgQdq8ZaVO":{"name":"Presenceless Voice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>A powerful upgrade to Melpominee, the vampire no longer requires the line of sight to afflict others with Melpominee. It allows for transmission of its powers through technology, including both recordings and live transmission. With the recent ban on technology by the Camarilla, however, this power has seen less use.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire’s powers of Melpominee may be transmitted without the need for a line of sight, allowing the vampire to use their powers through voice technology.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"88"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.jQZ1cQYgQdq8ZaVO"},"jmdUU8EImWZCM29s":{"name":"Corrosive Vitae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Altering the properties of some of their own Blood, the user can make it highly corrosive to dead substances, able to corrode most items down to steaming sludge, given enough time and Blood shed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free/Depending on the application</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One or more Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> No additional skill roll is required. The user concentrates for a turn and forces Blood through an open, usually self-inflicted, wound. The user then spills their Blood on a nonliving object (except unliving flesh such as that of the vampires) to corrode and decompose it. Each Rouse Check corrodes approximately 35 cm of matter in all directions from the splash; this takes approximately five minutes (longer on soft metals like copper or cast iron). Harder metals such as alloys and steel merely scar and pit; whether they meaningfully weaken remains at the Storyteller’s discretion. (This power might corrode through handcuff chains given enough time and vitae.)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> N/A</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"145"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.jmdUU8EImWZCM29s"},"jwQ50R5Q7Ml7cWhW":{"name":"Half-Living Conductor","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This formula grants the alchemist protection from one of the most primal forces of nature: lightning itself. Compared to the ever-present threat of fire or sunlight, lightning and electrical currents are usually the least of a vampire’s worries. In the hands of an alchemist, it can become a deadly weapon.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric and sanguine mortal blood, molten conductive metal (gold, silver, copper, and aluminum are popular choices), ground rubber, rainwater from a thunderstorm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist is immune to all physical damage taken from any electrical source, be it lightning, tasers, live wires, or a supernatural ability. Upon exposure to an electrical current, the alchemist may choose to redirect the current. They amplify the incoming electricity in their body and shoot it out in an arc from their fingertips, mouth, or eyes.</p>\n<p>Using electrical redirection in a conflict is a ranged attack using Dexterity + Alchemy. It inflicts margin + 2 in Superficial Health damage for vampires and in Aggravated Health damage for mortals.</p>\n<p>On Distillation rolls with 1–3 successes, the alchemist is protected from electrical forces, but their redirected electrical attacks do no additional Health damage. On Distillation rolls with 6 or more successes, the alchemist’s body bursts with electrical power. Their attacks inflict Aggravated Health damage to mortals and supernaturals alike.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until voluntarily ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"79"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"JjgtGoD6":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"LgVzfrTR":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.jwQ50R5Q7Ml7cWhW"},"jxcFL5MIxT4n39sV":{"name":"Isthar’s Disfavour","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power the vampire completely destroys the victim’s defences, making them a puppet succeptable to input from strangers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Isthar’s Touch</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult vs. Intelligence + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> As with Isthar’s Touch. The victim automatically counts as if they’ve rolled a 0 on rolls to resist Social Skills and mind-altering Disciplines unless the victim rolls a Critical Win on the roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Power’s effects break at the next sunrise.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"138"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"qOD5QCvk":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"INJrJgw2":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.jxcFL5MIxT4n39sV"},"k6YiRLqqdGSO9WGd":{"name":"Glimpse the Gulf Beyond","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>There are worlds beyond worlds and places beyond space and time; this power allows a Baali to shatter the boundaries between such zones of cosmic terror and the mundane world. Glimpse the Gulf Beyond is never used lightly, for even the masters of Daimoinon fear what they might see -- or what might see them. But when a Baali does invoke the face of the Void, the effects are terrifying.</p>\n<p>When Glimpse the Gulf Beyond is invoked, the vampire literally uses their will to force cracks in the walls of reality. Through those cracks come the sights, sounds, smells and other aspects of existence outside of reality as most know it. While exactly what comes through from the other side varies from moment to moment (blasphemous whispers one second, a chamel stench and the howls of the damned the next), the impact on the target is always tremendous.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check and one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Composure + Resolve + Daimonion</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the effect ends.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Intelligence + Occult. A single success is enough to create a portal to the Beyond with the maximum of its radius area equal to the successes in meters. Everybody, including the one who used the power, must roll to resist it if they’re within the sensory range of the power, mundane or mystical. The roll is Composure + Resolve + Daimonion, if the person possesses it.</p>\n<p>The margin on the resistance roll dictates the effect:</p>\n<p>1: The onlookers re shaken, but physically unharmed. They suffer a point of Aggravated Willpower and loses a die penalty to all non-tracker pools for the rest of the scene.</p>\n<p>2: The subject suffers two points of Aggravated Willpower damage. Mortals must make a Willpower roll at a Difficulty of 2 or lose consciousness while vampires test Fear Frenzy at a Difficulty of 2. Subjects suffer a die penalty to all non tracker pools for the rest of the scene. Those with at least a point of Torment do not lose conscioussness but must make a Willpower Test equal to half rounded up of their Torment. If they fail, they enter a state identical to Fear Frenzy of vampires as the Torment of the Beyond dulls any reason and forces them to flee. Alternatively, they may choose to welcome it and increase their Torment rating by 1. Damned with Torment 10 are immune to this effect.</p>\n<p>3: The subject suffers three points of Aggravated Willpower damage. Mortals must make a Willpower roll at a Difficulty of 3 or enter a catatonic state for an hour while vampires test Fear Frenzy at a Difficulty of 3. Subjects suffer a two-dice penalty to non-tracker rolls for the rest of the scene. Those with Torment must make a test as with 2 margins.</p>\n<p>4: The subject suffers four points of Aggravated Willpower, and must make a Willpower roll at a Difficulty of 4 or enter a catatonic state for an hour. Subjects suffer a two-dice penalty to non-tracker rolls for the rest of the scene. Those with Torment must make a test as with 2 margins.</p>\n<p>5+: The strain of perceptions battling the imperceptible proves to be too much for the victim’s mind. The subject suffers 5 points of Aggravated Willpower damage. Subjects must make a Willpower roll (Difficulty 5) or become severely mentally incapacitated to the point of permanent gibbering idiocy. Reducing a victim's Willpower to zero in this fashion may leave them with a (random) Derangement. This infirmity can be either temporary or permanent, as warranted by the severity of the victim’s trauma (players should be temporary, roughly one Lunar cycle).</p>\n<p>A Total Failure or Botch indicates something has gone terribly wrong with the Baali's attempt to open the portal: count each 1 as a success. In such cases, Storytellers are encouraged to exercise their fiendish discretion, but some possibilities include backlash from the Other Side, the escape of an unwanted visitor into this realm or even the abduction of the vampire themselves (Strength + Athletics Difficulty 4 to avoid being pulled).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The portal closes after a cene, but the vampire may spend one Rouse Check and Willpower to extend it for the rest of the night and an additional Rouse Check and Willpower point to extend it to every night after. Characters make the resistance roll once a night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"58"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"iu4GHScd":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"q1FqS63Y":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.k6YiRLqqdGSO9WGd"},"kQm6paYdg2w9FN5l":{"name":"Ignore Me","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some people fade into the Background easily. A vampire that haunts a specific location can program the people there to completely ignore them while they moves about the grounds. Not only does this give them a home field advantage should enemies confront themin this location, but it also makes them a shining example of upholding the Silence of the Blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Obfuscate 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire tells their subject “ignore me” and rolls Manipulation + Subterfuge against their Composure + Resolve. On a win, the subject completely ignores the existence of the vampire for the next month, and the vampire can target another person the next day. If the vampire disturbs a room, the target subconsciously puts things back in place. The target only reacts to the vampire if they inflicts harm on the target — other physical interactions (such as touching them or searching their pockets) are unaffected. The target makes a Wits + Awareness roll against the successes the vampire rolled on the power’s activation. If successful, they shake the vampire’s hold.</p>\n<p>Subjects with supernatural senses can always roll in the presence of the vampire or after witnessing their traces (e.g. vampire searched through a room and left it a mess) as per Obfuscate rules.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One month</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"71"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"wfQoP2KF":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"DZOvaE8Z":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.kQm6paYdg2w9FN5l"},"kSla0w0Hcnh92Xn9":{"name":"Advanced Torpor","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Formula induces torpor. For thin-blooded, it’s a last resort mending coma.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric and phlegmatic mortal blood, neem extract, diazepam, diphenhydramine, chamomile flower.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist creates (or for other Distillations, they or their vessel bleeds) a bright pink, watery fluid. Mixing this substance with mortal blood creates an elixir that causes its imbiber to immediately fall into torpor. This torpor lasts as long as if they have entered it through ordinary means.</p>\n<p>Unlike regular torpor, a vampire in Advanced Torpor always has a Rouse Check re-roll while under its effects, whether it’s for the day passing or for mending any damage. Should the vampire end their Advanced Torpor not at Hunger 5, they may keep their current level of Hunger when they awaken. All vampires in Advanced Torpor mend as a vampire, even if a Thin- Blood Flaw otherwise makes that impossible.</p>\n<p>If the vampire’s Hunger rises above Hunger 5 while in Advanced Torpor, the vampire enters regular torpor for the remaining portion of their torpor, and standard rules apply.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Athanor Corporis:</strong> The alchemist can tap their vein once per night until their next feeding or reach Hunger 5. There is no further Distillation cost.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Calcinatio:</strong> If the vessel survives the tap, the alchemist can tap them again in one week. The vessel takes 5 Aggravated Health damage per tap.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the target awakens from torpor.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"75"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.kSla0w0Hcnh92Xn9"},"kUB8hl7nRmDcRtCZ":{"name":"Stir the Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>With a touch, the user “heals” some part of their target that is missing, restoring a part of their lost and human self while the user’s third eye glows in a golden colour. While it can improve the mental state of mortals, this power is best used against vampires as it can even bestow upon them some semblance of being human again.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches their target and rolls Resolve + Insight against a Difficulty of 2. On a win the target’s Road rating increases be the number of margins on the roll. Additionally, the vampire will be able to digest the food they eat and enjoy it as they once did. This power automatically fails on those whose Road adheres to Virtues other than both Conscience and Self-Control.</p>\n<p>This power cannot increase one’s Humanity beyond the user’s own Road rating rating or by a number equal to the user’s Healer Valeren.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene. A Critical Win or 3+ margins extends it to the rest of the night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"185"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"0RFRm8dj":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"HlCxRhkn":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.kUB8hl7nRmDcRtCZ"},"kWTos4bf1CDjgPAT":{"name":"Mask of Isolation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Subtle but effective, this power forces the effect of Mask of a Thousand Faces on an unwitting subject. The victim will no longer be seen for who they are, even by those closest to them. Often employed over a longer period of time, the power is used by those who want to break their subject subtly but utterly, cutting them off from means and society to the point that they’ll do anything to escape such a fate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Charisma + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user locks eyes with the victim and must then succeed at a Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Charisma + Insight test. On a win, the effect lasts for one night, plus one for each success in the margin. While under its effect, the victim is treated as employing Mask of a Thousand Faces, and is unable to be recognized by anyone. (The user can try to persuade friends and family with a Composure + Persuade test, but even when presenting proofs, the apprehension caused by this particular power will make it hard to convince them.)</p>\n<p>If the subject is ever made aware of the power being used on them the effect ends, and the power cannot be employed on someone aware of its effects: It relies on the unconscious ignorance of the victim to remain active. As use of the power isn’t overt, it can be reapplied repeatedly though, so long as the user doesn’t reveal their intent.</p>\n<p>This power is a great violation of a person and as such is rarely used by those who adhere to Humanity.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"102"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gKHEfnV8":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"pT4EflFM":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.kWTos4bf1CDjgPAT"},"kX3O2lXrTD6QGpUh":{"name":"Bring the Plague","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>A Daimonion user with Bring the Plague calls down a Biblical plague on their enemies, cursing them with a highly infectious disease that plagues mortal and Cainite alike. It causes violent, painful boils that look like bleeding burn wounds when they burst. The target attracts swarms of insects and vermin wherever they go as they wait for their inevitable death and feast.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Stamina + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> First the user states the number of transmissions (from 1 to unlimited, but only an idiot brings upon such an apocalypse just like that). After that, they need to touch a person to activate the power. Once the victim is touched, both sides make their roll. If the victim fails, they are infected.</p>\n<p>A victim with the plague cannot heal by mundane means and must be healed by magical means or True Faith. At the every beginning of a night, the infected suffer a point of Aggravated Health damage if they fail a Stamina + Stamina roll with the Difficulty being the plague bringer’s Daimonion rating. Infected targets also lower their Looks Merit’s rating by one (such as normal to Ugly, Stunning to Beautiful etc.). The infected suffer Physical and Mental impairment for as long as effect stands. Characters interacting with the infected must also roll and succeed on a Stamina + Stamina roll with the Difficulty being half the plague bringer’s Daimonion rating rounded up or be infected. It can spread by touch, by breath, or by any bodily fluids. This includes drinking blood for both the victim and the vampire.</p>\n<p>A person stays infected until they succeed on the Stamina + Stamina roll equal to the user’s Daimonion rating when taking damage at the beginning of every night or until they have been cured by some other means. Characters with True Faith are not only immune to Bring the Plague, but if they touch a victim of the power, the victim ceases to be contagious.</p>\n<p>A person possessing True Faith or at 5 dots in Occult (or 4 dots in Occult + an appropriate speciality) may recognise this plague for what it is with an Intelligence + Occult roll at a Difficulty of 3.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the effect ends.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"58"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"giKBKMlp":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"WolLArKg":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.kX3O2lXrTD6QGpUh"},"kzUT3Nsf7I4DqJ8d":{"name":"Restore Humantias","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>The first thing that the Children of Osiris are taught is that Humanity does not have to be an inexorable slide into depravity. Instead, it is more akin to a climb up an extremely steep hill. The vampire is going to lose some ground, but with perseverance and strength, he can regain it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Enough Rouse Checks to reach Hunger 5</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the user fails their Remorse roll, they get to roll another D10 at the cost of not healing any Willpower damage upon awakening. Should the die roll a success, the user does not lose their Humanity. On the following night, however, they awaken with an additional stain on their Humanity tracker as a reminder that they have commit sin.</p>\n<p>When the vampire loses a dot of Humanity, he can attempt to regain it without spending experience points by using this power. The vampire must use this power within a week of losing the Road rating, and must not have lost any more Road rating, since the initial loss (that is, if the character falls from Road of Humanity 8 to Road of Humanity 7, and then falls to Humanity 6 before using this power, Restore Humanitas can only be used to recover Humanity 7). The character meditates for several hours, and spends enough Rouse Checks to reach Hunger 5. The player then rolls their Road rating at a Difficulty of equal to half (rounded down) of Road rating the character is trying to get back. If the roll succeeds, the character regains the dot of Humanity. If the character gained a derangement, it fades within a week of using Restore Humanitas.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"40"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.kzUT3Nsf7I4DqJ8d"},"lUDwBeTWFK4RH9OC":{"name":"Song of the Blooded","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>Samiel’s words were heard only by his childer. Only they know the secret doubts he spoke aloud, purging them from his heart so he could face his enemies with his potential unchecked. The clear heart of a Salubri Warrior is a furious one, the purity of their rage visibly shining forth. The doubts are spoken as a prayer, acquiring cadence and rhythm and defining the speaker as an avenging angel of an angry God (“Lend me your rage, o Lord, for I am Michael, General of the Host”). For this reason that the Warrior Caste often takes the names of Judeo- Christian angels; even if they cannot use this power, symbolism lends a great deal of weight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Willpower points and two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Charisma + Performance prayer (Difficulty 2) to openly voice the doubts and fears of the user, giving them life so that they may kill them. A win means the vampire enters into a trance state for the rest of the scene, singing loudly of victory over their foes and fears and their eyes glowing brighter than the sun. Their Physical Attributes immediately increase to 6. A halo burns around them, incapable of being smothered by lesser Disciplines, rendering Stealth or darkness-induced penalties impossible. The glare reduces one success from every physical or magical attack against the vampire.</p>\n<p>While in the battle-trance, the user gains immunity to Dominate, Presence and other mental powers and Disciplines (save the effect of Rayzeel’s Song).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The trance ends after a scene or when every foe is dead as their fears or they meet Final Death on the field, whichever comes first. When removing Health due to return to old Attribute ratings, remove undamanged Health first (or Superficial damage, if everything is damaged).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"194"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"ZPgSuAzj":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"2mETUQDt":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.lUDwBeTWFK4RH9OC"},"lZAhESgYeuzg5dJO":{"name":"Clarity of Emptyness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Developed independently by Cappadocian scholars and Abyss mystics of the Lasombra, this power allows a vampire to still their passions and think with pitiless detached clarity. Possibly the rarest variant of this power is Clarity of Contemplation by the True Brujah which functions identically by detaching the user’s mind from present time.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Corpse in the Monster Path •• or Obtenebration •• or Temporis •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may be toggled off and on. While active, the vampire receives a +2 dice bonus to all Mental Rolls. Unfortunately, the inhuman stifling of passion subtracts two dice from any Animal Ken, Persuasion, Insight and seduction rolls for the rest of the scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until toggled off</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"24"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.lZAhESgYeuzg5dJO"},"lfd8enJCskRykLgx":{"name":"Psychomachia","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Prudentius was a Roman poet who wrote the Psychomachia – “the Battle of Souls,” describing the struggle of faith against idolatry and vice. The Baali know this game, and they play to win. With this power, the vampire combines the ability to read a victim’s psyche with the ability to rend spiritual matter. Psychomachia breathes infernal life into the target’s vice, forcing the victim to act the hero and literally battle her inner demons.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user gestures towards a target and rolls. Upon a win, the caster summons a mist-like entity out of the target’s body, dealing a point of Aggravated Health damage to them. Once it properly forms, it resembles a twisted version of the target – personified apparition of the victim’s vice summoned from their darker self. The entity will seek out the destruction of their victim (Physical or Mental, depending on what the circumstances provide, but usually Physical).</p>\n<p>The assailant possesses the same Attributes of the target subtracted by 1/3 of the victim’s Humanity/Road rating, rounded down, to a minimum of 1. Ghost’s assailant possesses the same Attributes or the value of the ghost’s Angst, whichever is lower. Demon’s assailant would possess the same Attributes of the demon subtracted by 2 + half of their Torment rating rounded down. The apparition also possesses the same Skills of the target and the same supernatural powers like Disciplines equal to that of the target which the apparition uses Willpower to activate. Apparitions always takes damage like a vampire that is immune to the Sun, even if the victim is a mortal.</p>\n<p>Almost immediately, a fight breaks out between the two, though this may take the form of any conflict, such as an abusive argument rather than a physical battle. The wounds inflicted by the mind are illusory, though they can force a mortal into catatonia or a vampire into torpor upon a phantom “death”. To maintain the phantom in the physical world, the user must concentrate, represented by a minor action of -4 dice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The phantasm vanishes on their defeat (physical defeat is performed by depleting the apparition’s health levels and mental by depleting its Willpower in a social combat or through some other means), after a scene or when the user’s concentration drops.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"59"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"5LkTPqnd":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"CpBsxl24":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.lfd8enJCskRykLgx"},"lpr07zM8SfgGBNzs":{"name":"Blurred Momentum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s movement becomes a jittering blur, making it extremely difficult to land attacks on them even when they are not aware of the opponent or actively defending.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Attacks that achieve fewer successes than the user’s Celerity rating always miss, no matter the result of a defense or dodge test (if any). This also works against surprise attacks or other attacks that disallow defense tests, such as Lightning Strike. Activating this power requires one Rouse Check and extending it requires an additional Check for each turn it is kept active.</p>\n<p>Those with a rating of Temporis or Celerity rgreater the user’s Celerity rating ignore the effects of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the user lets it lapse</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"45"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.lpr07zM8SfgGBNzs"},"m4qqYzh2BKsdCXWZ":{"name":"Gift of Sleep","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>The ability to ease someone’s pain and to bestow sleep upon the restless is a valued one within the Healer caste. Problems and discussions about ethics arise here and there when somebody uses it to force others, often opponents in combat, to slumber. Alas, that is a concern, not a limit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user touches the target (in combat, any combination of Strength or Dexterity + Brawl or Athletics might be required). After that, the user activates the power as trolls Composure + Resolve against the target’s Composure + Resolve. On a win, the target is forced into sleep from which they awaken if they take a point of health damage. Undead creatures and creatures that require no sleep (or are for some reason unable to sleep) are immune to this power.</p>\n<p>Knowing both Gift of Sleep and Morphean Blow grants an additional two-dice bonus on the roll to force a target to sleep. Anything thicker than a coat (like armour) provides protection from the user’s touch and, if possible, a Called Shot of -1 success (if hands and face are exposed) or -2 (if only one of them is exposed) might be necessary.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> 8 hours or until the subject is awakened</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"184"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.m4qqYzh2BKsdCXWZ"},"mgQ5vYNDLZ3SQjkU":{"name":"Premonition","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire experiences flashes of insight. These may take the form of raised hackles, sudden inspiration or even vivid visions. While never too precise, these visions can nudge the vampire out of harm’s way or reveal a truth previously overlooked.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free/Full if used consciously</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free/one Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits/Resolve + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the Storyteller deems it appropriate, the Storyteller rolls Wits + Awareness of the character at a set difficulty. If successful, the power gives the character a sudden hint that aids them in some way: letting them find a clue they’ve missed or saving them from danger. Whether it gives the character a sudden vision of themselves walking into a trap, an inviting red glow over the second right turn during a chase, or the brief flash of a skeleton beneath the floorboards in the Prince’s office, this power always gives the Storyteller license to subtly speed up play or move the story onto a desired track. The suggested limit is one premonition per scene, even if more than one character has Premonition.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user can force a Premonition on more vague things to gain a mechanical dice bonus. Make a Premontion roll and pay the cost of a Rouse Check. Power bonus value + half the successes (rounded up) is provided as a dice bonus to any non-tracker pool. The bonus lasts until the caster spends them or until the user reactivates Premonition in this manner. Premonition cannot aid pools to fortune-tell or when the user consciously forces some kind of a benefit (like from a Blood Surge, artefact usage or another dice bonus) as fate comes on its own and artefacts often influence or change fate by themselves. No more than three dice may be added in a single pool.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive/Until bonus is exhausted</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"27"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"bvSubVFh":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"WYQJwcgF":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.mgQ5vYNDLZ3SQjkU"},"mkSsLjnfLWuFFSSx":{"name":"Wrecker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire is able to exert violent, indiscriminate force on a static object in order to shatter, smash, and destroy it. While the lead-up time to summon this strength makes the ability useless in a fight, it comes in handy whenever a door bars the way, a car needs immobilizing, or an example needs to be made of an offending statue.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Prowess</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user counts their Potence rating twice when using Prowess for feats of strength that involve damage or destruction of an inanimate object.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As Prowess</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"118"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.mkSsLjnfLWuFFSSx"},"n5Tn2quP279eMZFy":{"name":"Timestop","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Already reaching the legendary powers that the elders of the True Brujah are said to possess, Timestop allows its user to quite literally stop time and move — crossing great distances or avoiding lethal dangers by a hair.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The player can supersede the Storyteller’s narration of events, within reason. They can choose to have their character move through a door before it closes, circumvent an ambush after it has been triggered, roll out of the way of an explosion, and so forth. The action taken must be reasonable and should not take more than a few seconds in real time. The Storyteller decides what Skills, if any, need to be checked to successfully accomplish an action begun using this power.</p>\n<p>An opponent possessing Celerity 5 Celerity or Temporis can nullify this power by making their own Rouse Check, moving at the same speed as the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Roughly one action, as determined by the Storyteller.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"174"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.n5Tn2quP279eMZFy"},"nHjmO1pb8wZlhebE":{"name":"Profane Hieros Gamos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>To the Alchemist, rebirth and renewal are not locked behind the ritual of sacred union between two opposites. Through the power of their mercurial vitae the Alchemist can help shape a human body to match the mental self-image or desired shape held by the consciousness within it.</p>\n<p>With the help of rare reagents the Alchemist can even defy one of the fundamental curses of the vampiric condition by altering the unchanging state to which the undead revert to in their daysleep. This specific formula can be imbibed by vampires other than the Alchemist, after their ritual purification and deep meditation on their own idealized, or desired, form.</p>\n<p>This knowledge makes the expertise of the Alchemist a valuable and desired commodity even to those Kindred who loathe their very existence. Duskborn openly offering services like these are more likely to be coerced to work or kidnapped than paid handsomely.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, melancholic and phlegmatic human blood, entheogenic substances.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whoever imbibes the formula mixed with a Rouse Check worth of their own Blood instantly falls into a catatonic fever dream until the following night, the depths of their psyche plumbed for their personal idealized form, or a form strongly desired. The effects of Profane Hieros Gamos are limited to the external morphology of the human form, the body refusing transformations too outlandish and fantastical to be without the wondrous spectrum of human anatomy. The imbiber makes a Stamina + Resolve roll against a difficulty of 8 minus the successes on the distillation roll, adding their Bane Severity to the Difficulty of the test.</p>\n<p>On a win, the transformation process is successful and their physical body molds to the desired shape. A critical win can grant and remove Merits and Flaws of the Looks category, though like all Advantages they need to be paid for with Experience points (p. 180). A messy critical or a bestial failure might cause a new Flaw to manifest, ranging from Repulsive, Obvious Predator, Stigmata or even Organovore as the Beast interferes with the transformation. Not even the blood of the Duskborn can spare the Nosferatu from their Bane, the process incorporating the clan’s hideous affliction despite the user’s mental image.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until performed again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"286"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"U601d5Cg":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"uKH9LGpg":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.nHjmO1pb8wZlhebE"},"nQ80eWTy4kj2v1ev":{"name":"Inflame Desire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can invisibly boost a subject’s present desire to the point that they will singlemindedly seek to satisfy it with little thought of their own well-being. Unless the user knows beforehand what the subject wants, the results can be surprising and volatile, but if used judiciously it provides even the worst bargainer the upper hand in any negotiation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Obfuscate 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s voice alone is now enough to Dominate a target. This does not apply to voices transmitted through mediums be they magical or electronic media such as phones, television, or apartment door-buzzer speakers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"124"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.nQ80eWTy4kj2v1ev"},"nXgzrfxzbwzNoZB8":{"name":"Rend the Osseous Frame","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/vicissitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>At this stage of mastery, the Tzimisce has progressed to manipulating bone. Used on its own, Rend the Osseous Frame can inflict terrible damage upon a subject. When used in conjunction with Transmogrify the Mortal Clay, nightmares become possible.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Transmogrify the Mortal Clay</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, see system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Medicine roll at a Difficulty of 3, requiring physical contact if another person is the subject. If the subject is avoiding the user’s touch and the time required to perform an alteration is one turn or instant, alteration first requires a successful roll with any combination of Strength/Dexterity + Athletics/ Brawl. For alterations requiring longer periods of time, unwilling targets must be restrained. Unwilling subjects resist using Stamina + Resolve. Their successes replaces the static Difficulty of 3.</p>\n<p>The following alterations are samples of what is available to Transmogrify Rend the Osseous Frame:</p>\n<p>• Agile Limbs (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 30 minutes): The subject's limbs become unusually agile, able to fit into smaller crevices and wield weapons with greater ease. The subject gains a one-die bonus on all dodging, Melee, Larceny and Crafts tests.</p>\n<p>• Armour (Cost: Half of an Attribute dot per two points of Armour rating; Time: 30 minutes per two points of Armour rating): The subject’s skin hardens or sprouts armour, making them more resistant to damage. For every half of a dot of Physical Attribute invested, the subject gains three points of armour, up to 8 which does not stack with any other form of armour.</p>\n<p>• Climbing Tools (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 10 minutes): The subject’s claws elongate into climbing hooks or their limbs grow suckers like that of an octopus. They automatically succeed on any Skill test to climb a nonmetallic surface. The subject might also be able to climb copper or bronze cladding or other softer metal surfaces, at the Storyteller’s discretion. Thin glass surfaces (though generally not the glass curtain walls of modern office buildings) may shatter under the stress. In the same way, a subject can hang from a wall or ceiling, though a specific alteration such as hooks or suctions on their feet are necessary to can hang by their feet. The climb or clinging leaves obvious tracks detectable by anyone with an Intelligence + Investigation test at Difficulty 2. Detecting climbing tracks on glass doesn’t even require a roll.</p>\n<p>• Elongate Fangs (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 5 minutes): The subject’s fangs are increased and they no longer have the called shot penalty to perform bite attacks. Mortals receiving this alteration treat it as a normal piercing + 2 Brawl weapon</p>\n<p>• Enhance the Senses (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 10 minutes): The subject is given additional pair(s) of eyes around their body or pair(s) of ears and such, providing a die bonus on all mundane perception rolls if the sensory organs are uncovered and may properly function.</p>\n<p>• Horns of the Dragon (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 15 minutes): Alternatively called the Horns of the Devil, the subject sprouts a pair of horns to give them a mighty appearance. This alteration provides a two-dice bonus on all Intimidation rolls. Feel free to reflavour this alteration as something else that is both intimidating and requires hiding with a Wits + Stealth roll.</p>\n<p>• Kraken’s Kiss (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 25 minutes): The subjects’s face erupts into a mass of tentacles, a foot (or a third of a meter) in length and similar to that of a squid. These tentacles can be used to grasp and constrict a foe. Moreover, in place of a squid’s suckers are rows and rows of fanged, drooling mouths, permitting incredibly rapid blood drain. The vampire may perform Bite Attacks with no called shot. When feeding, the vampire gains two additional feeding actions and an additional automatic success on the roll to keep a grappled victim within their grasp.</p>\n<p>• Multiply Limbs (Cost: Half of a Physical Attribute dot; Time: 30 minutes): The subject's gains another pair of limbs. They subtract the penalty to defend against multiple opponents using close quarters attacks by one up to two dice.</p>\n<p>• Rend the Osseous Form (Cost: Free; Time:</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Instant):</strong> The user rends flesh off the bones of their victim, dealing Aggravated damage to mortals equal to the margin on the Resolve + Medicine roll and Superficial to more durable creatures such as vampires. Superficial damage from this attack is not halved. The victim also gains the broken limb Crippling Injury. If the victim already possesses it, the vampire may select any Crippling Injury of chocie short of torpor and instant death.</p>\n<p>• Weapons (Cost: Between Half of a Physical Attribute dot to two dots; Time: 5 to 30 minutes, depending on the weapon): For the cost of half a Physical Attribute dot, the subject’s very own body parts are transformed into a weapon, counting as +2 piercing or bludgeoning weapons (hand turned into a mace of bone and muscle would be bludeoning while bones sharpened into swords would be piercing). These weapons deal standard damage. By spending another Physical Attribute dot, these weapons become hardened and their damage is never halved. By spending another half of a Physical Attribute dot, the weapon is larger and sharper and its damage modifier is increased to +3</p>\n<p>• Wings (Cost: One Physical Attribute dot per dot of Flight; Time: 20 minutes per dot of</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Flight):</strong> The sprouts wings, gaining dots in Flight equal to the Attribute dots invested into this alteration.</p>\n<p>If Flight Discipline isn’t available to you, the first dot merely allows you to glide while each subsequent dot allows for real flight, adding 40 km/h per dot</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent until healed or altered again. See Characteristics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"205"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"DWnHemUU":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSxo3JUS":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"vicissitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.nXgzrfxzbwzNoZB8"},"nYWKTJcnA30nPxEZ":{"name":"Dominion of Wu Zao","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-watcher.png","system":{"description":"<p>Said to have been discovered by Wao-Lat himself, the Dominion of Wu Zao allows the vampire to take command of a spirit. The ability to command spirits and demons is a tight secret of the Salubri, lest they embolden those who accuse them of infernalism and give base to their yet-baseless claims.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Purification of Wu Zao</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire focuses on the spirit, ghost or a demon and utters a command of control. They and the spirit engage in a battle of wills, represented by a contested Composure + Resolve roll. The contest ends when one combatant has accumulated three margins more than another. If the vampire wins, the spirit becomes fully Blood Bound to the user, even if they would otherwise be immune to mind control or Blood Bond.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Blood Bond expires as normal.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"199"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ai0qqD1W":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"iqgDV9Bj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwatcher","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.nYWKTJcnA30nPxEZ"},"ncy7En5EDy8kioCr":{"name":"Portable Shade","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Portable Shade, or even more irreverently, “suntan lotion,” is a formula developed by Cainites on the Path of the Sun to augment a thin-blood’s already substantial resistance to sunlight to near mortal levels. While it doesn’t render the subject completely immune, it extends the time they can endure daylight while masking immediate burns sustained by the sun.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Blood of the Alchemist, sanguinic mortal blood, fresh plant leaves. Some alchemists also use various off-the-shelf sunblock ointments in the mix, though it is unknown whether they contribute significantly to the formula.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Alchemy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make a Stamina + Alchemy test when ingesting the formula. The user is able to endure sunlight without taking damage for a number of hours equal to the number of successes on the test. At sunset, make another test with the same pool, this time with a Difficulty equal to the number of hours spent in daylight. A failure causes the user to sustain a level of aggravated damage, while a total failure has them sustain two, as the accumulated sunlight boils the Blood in their veins.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> A number of hours equal to a Stamina + Alchemy test, or the next sunset, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Sabbat: The Black Hand","page":"53"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"JjgtGoD6":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"LgVzfrTR":{"path":"disciplines.alchemy"}},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ncy7En5EDy8kioCr"},"neYBR1dlleIqcJrq":{"name":"Falsehood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>Like the true fae, practitioners of Mytherceria find that they can blur truths and lies like one blends water and wine. This power allows the vampire to not only lie well, but directly convince the other person that lies are falsehoods.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user adds their Mytherceria rating to all rolls to lie, gaslight, confuse or trick others with Subterfuge.</p>\n<p>Like with all Mytherceria powers, this power works by affecting the mind. Characters may benefit from powers like Unswayable mind when rolling Insight against the user with this power active, while characters who are immune to mind-altering powers simply ignore the dice bonus. If characters immune to mind-altering powers are present in the scene, roll the dice bonus separately without Hunger dice and combine the successes including 10s for criticals against those not immune to such powers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"90"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.neYBR1dlleIqcJrq"},"nwDPXiCAi4FmAcwa":{"name":"Death’s Hidden Crawl","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your character may use this subtle application of Protean in order to subvert one of their greatest weaknesses: torpor. While they still suffers torpor and still remains largely an immobile corpse in the long sleep, they can move slowly across the ground when nobody is looking.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power requires no roll. It cannot be used while any sentient characters watch your character or while staked. When they have no witnesses in torpor, they can move no more than one meter or yard per hour. They may use Death’s Hidden Crawl any time they enter torpor, even during the daylight hours. They do not walk, or even properly crawl. Their body tenses and retracts like a worm in order to pull them slowly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"130"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.nwDPXiCAi4FmAcwa"},"nxTze37Ple8L9IjU":{"name":"Shadow Cloak","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>Subtly applying the influence of the Abyss on ambient shadows, the user masks their appearance or seems more sinister and threatening.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire gains a two-dice bonus to Stealth rolls, as well as on Intimidation. Shadow Cloak may be noticed with Wits + Awareness against Wits + Stealth by those with Heightened Senses or such powers.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows, it subtracts two points of Superficial Health damage before halving, to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"107"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.nxTze37Ple8L9IjU"},"o9HofJPw6KB2TnVR":{"name":"Sense Vitality","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>Sense Vitality allows its user to read the person’s physical and mental state, learning how much damage a target has suffered or what manner of a creature they are. While the third eye of Valeren is not yet developed at this level, the mystical sight granted by this power is symbolically tied to it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight or Medicine vs. Composure + Subterfug</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user looks at a subject within their line of sight and rolls Resolve + Insight or Medicine vs. the victim’s Composure + Subterfuge. On a success, the user may ask questions equal to the margin and get a correct response. The example questions are:</p>\n<p>• How many points of Health damage has the target suffered and what kind?</p>\n<p>• How many points of Willpower damage has the target suffered and what kind?</p>\n<p>• What manner of a creature is the target?</p>\n<p>• What is the target’s Humanity (or similar tracker’s) rating?</p>\n<p>• Is the user’s current appearance the result of Vicissitude? If yes, what parts?</p>\n<p>• Is this person drugged or under some kind of influence, supernatural or otherwise?</p>\n<p>At the storyteller’s discretion, other similar questions may be asked, but they will never provide the answers to the overly accurate questions such as “who has done this Vicissitude modification?”</p>\n<p>With relevant information, the user gains a a die bonus to all Medicine and Insight rolls mend what is wrong for the rest of the scene. This bonus is not improved further by power bonus. This bonus may apply to Valeren power rolls.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"183"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"VrGzEVQt":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"30GQiq7l":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.o9HofJPw6KB2TnVR"},"oMKgUGk6TxQWMApm":{"name":"Mesmerise","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can issue complex commands to a victim, as long as they have the subject’s gaze and their instructions can be heard. The instructions must be carried out immediately to the victim’s best ability, and must not contain any conditional actions (“...if you see Henry, give him the document”), as this would require the victim to exercise cognition.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Intimidation vs. Intelligence + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Commanding a resisting victim or another vampire requires a contest of Manipulation + Intimidation vs Intelligence + Resolve. Commands that go against the victim’s nature also require such a contest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until the command is carried out or the scene ends, whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"71"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"kLYb9fL0":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"XlyDWw2F":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.oMKgUGk6TxQWMApm"},"oPBffLVnW84dfjtG":{"name":"Da Bomb","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Formula transforms a mortal vessel into a walking time bomb. This murderous concoction spells certain death for whoever drinks it but is just as lethal for those unlucky enough to be caught in the blast radius.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, Sanguine human blood, accelerants, matchstick heads, grains of sand from a sand timer</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This Formula is very temperamental and only works when imbibed by mortals (or brewed directly in a mortal via <em>calcinatio</em>). Once consumed, the potion remains in the drinker’s system for a week, during which time the mortal feels nauseous and unwell. The alchemist can activate the Formula at any time by making a Rouse Check, but if not activated while still potent, it passes harmlessly from the drinker’s system.</p>\n<p>When activated, the victim experiences a turn of panicked awareness, before exploding in a torrent of gore. Those within close range take Aggravated damage equal to twice the victim’s Stamina, while those further away halve that. Any vampires caught in the blast must also test for Hunger Frenzy at Difficulty 3.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One week</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tattered Façade","page":"108"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.oPBffLVnW84dfjtG"},"oX5BSyXQGB38f4mo":{"name":"Fleetness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>Their mastery of Celerity now allows the vampire to move and react with dizzying speed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add the Celerity rating to user’s dice pool for non-combat Dexterity tests. In combat, add half (rounded up) of Celerity rating to non-offensive Athletics tests such as dodging and parrying (Melee).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"43"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.oX5BSyXQGB38f4mo"},"oYbTCGPn2ycUWj24":{"name":"Putrescent Servitude","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Users of Thantosis, perhaps due to the necromantic properties of the Discipline or due to the unlife-giving aspects of vampiric vitae, are able to animate a zombie-like creature to serve their needs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Medicine vs. Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user feeds a corpse their blood and rolls Manipulation + Medicine against a Difficulty of 3. On a win, the corpse rises as an aggressive corpse after a turn (see the statblock on the next page). The zombie possesses some intelligence and will defend themselves if provoked and can only act out one command at a time. They are also able to understand somewhat complex orders such as “kill everyone who enters,” “groan if you see anyone pass this way,” or “terrorize that neighborhood.”.</p>\n<p>If they are left without orders (such as “Be dormant!”) they turn hostile against everybody but their master. The user may also command to them to rot away to deactivate them. Larger animals may be resurrected as an aggressive corpse, using the stats on the following page as normal resurrected aggressive corpses.</p>\n<p>This power can also be activated when feeding a mortal vampire blood with no cost other than the vitae already being fed to the mortal. The new undead ghoul will gain the properties of a walking corpse (the mortal may resist with Stamina + Resolve unless they are Bood Bonded to the vampire). A successful activation causes the mortal to acquire the Looks Flaw: Ugly or Repulsive if they already had Ugly and the mortal will begin taking damage like a vampire except that they are immune to the sunlight. The mortal becomes pale and corpselike and also loses their free will as they are forced to obey the vampire who used this power with blind loyalty. Social and Mental Attributes of the mortal are halved and rounded down for the duration of the of the power, and they gain a point of Health. This effect lasts for a month and the mortal becomes a standard ghoul as well due to drinking vampire vitae.</p>\n<p>A mortal may attempt to resist unless they are blood bonded to the vampire, rolling Stamina + Resolve at the moment of the blood’s ingestion and at each sunset. Achieving more successes than the vampire removes the effects of this power, but the mortal still remains a ghoul.</p>\n<p>The vampire loses control over Aggressive Corpses and zombified mortals after a story as they become unruly and begin to attack others at random if they are not bought with experience points, one dot per two zombies. Zombified mortals should also be bought to keep the game fair, usually as normal mortal retainers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See system AGGRESSIVE CORPSE</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Physical 3, Social 0, Mental 0</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 6, Willpower 0</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 2, Brawl 3, Melee 3, Stealth 2, Intimidation 5, Awareness 4</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> Aggressive corpses take Superficial and Aggravated damage in the same way as vampires, except they are immune to sunlight. They cannot heal or mend damage and move as fast as humans. They cannot be mentally dominated or influenced as they are bound to their master, giving them immunity to powers such as Dominate, Presence and Obfuscate. They do not need eyes or ears to perceive everything around them as someone with unimpeded vision and hearing might. Bites from the aggressive dead inflict +2 piercing damage to mortals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"178"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"0nHqQyPM":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"OghOLQqr":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.oYbTCGPn2ycUWj24"},"ohoAwuStbNrClrdS":{"name":"One with the Land","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire possesses such mastery over their own form that they may extend it even into their domain itself. Not only may the vampire sink into the very earth, they maintain a preternatural awareness of events transpiring within the domain.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Earth Meld</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Animalism 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> As with Earth Meld, except the vampire is not limited by the makeup of the surface where they take their rest. Some vampires have been known to suffuse themselves into the walls of their manors while others secrete themselves beneath the warped floorboards of a squat, or even hide beneath a shallow pool of “dead water.”</p>\n<p>Additionally, in a distance of roughly one kilometer in any direction from where the vampire’s body has become One with the Land, the vampire may elect to experience any sensory stimuli within that area, such as listening to a conversation therein, physically enjoying a lovers’ tryst, or catching the scent of a fire that an unruly mob may be stoking. The vampire experiences these senses through the presence of animals, however minute, in the vicinity of the events. If the events are discreet or intentionally hidden, a Wits + Animalism test vs. the relevant opposing dice pool used is required.</p>\n<p>Rising from this state before nightfall the day after it has been entered requires a Resolve + Awareness test at Difficulty 4, and even then it can take up to an hour for the vampire to fully reemerge. A critical win allows them to rise instantly, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One day or more, or until disturbed. Power may be ended sooner by the vampire rising.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"132"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ohoAwuStbNrClrdS"},"okcPkls6OPcaMThm":{"name":"Folderol","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The fae see what others can not. Even in undeath, the practitioners of Mytherceria retain shards of such powers. Whenever someone deliberately tells a lie that has been detected, the user’s body reacts to it in a unique way; one might experience bleeding from the eyes or ears when they hears a lie, whilst another Weirdling’s eyes glow.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Add the Mytherceria rating to all Insight rolls to detect deliberate lies of others. Successful Insight roll leaves no doubt that the heard statement is a lie. A successful lie detection provokes a visible physical reaction in the user (e.g. bleeding ears). This does nothing to detect half-truths, lies by omission or other.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"90"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.okcPkls6OPcaMThm"},"oy0CRhOjwM08MRRi":{"name":"Purification","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-healer.png","system":{"description":"<p>Each caste of the Salubri have their own role to play in the war against the infernal. The Healer Valeren was valued highly for its ability to exercise demons, something repeatable only by lengthy exorcisms or rituals which were often unpractical. As they perform the exorcism, the user’s third eye shines with pale holy light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire spends focuses on the spirit, ghost or a demon and utters a command of banishment. They and the spirit engage in a battle of wills, represented by a contested Composure + Resolve roll. The contest ends when one combatant has accumulated three margins more than another. If the vampire wins, they may expel the spirit from its host. By spending another Willpower point, they may thrust the spirit into a person, animal or location, including taking the spirit into herself.</p>\n<p>Demons possessing objects are not particularly threatened by this power as it merely severs connection between a human and a malign spirit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene with a Critical Win (or 3+ margins) extending it to the rest of the night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"184"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"FwJ81slZ":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"2TLgTjYe":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenhealer","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.oy0CRhOjwM08MRRi"},"oyYBezXnVUdQpChR":{"name":"Spark of Rage","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Combining Potence and Presence, the vampire can incite anger and even Frenzy in onlookers, as easily as awe or dread. The user must take care not to rile up an angry mob to turn on them rather than the target or each other.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Potence 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When active, the user can add their Potence rating to any attempt to rile or incite a person or a crowd to violence. In addition, the user can activate this power and roll a contest of Manipulation + Persuasion vs Composure + Intelligence of another vampire. If they win, the opposing vampire enters a Fury Frenzy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"123"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"4xYDeCjt":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"l7aBDR9P":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.oyYBezXnVUdQpChR"},"p2ytAzKS8y7Ff5zw":{"name":"Mind Steal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The fae are infamous for stealing people’s minds and memories. The Kiasyd have developed a similar power. The vampire may even keep these stolen memories, if theyre’s willing to pay the price for them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Resolve + Insight roll opposed by Composure + Wits of the victim within the line of sight. On a win, the user “steals” the target’s mind (though they retain their own consciousness) and has complete access to all their thoughts and memories. The vampire may remove a number of Skill dots equal to the margins from the victim and add them to their own, up to the victim’s own maximum rating. Specialties count as a Skill dot each.</p>\n<p>Athletics, Awareness Brawl, Drive and Melee and their Specialties may be targeted with this power as normal but the victim does not actually lower their own rating since such Skills often rely on muscle memory rather than learned knowledge.</p>\n<p>The victim has no knowledge that they have been affected in this manner, although Intelligence + Occult Test (Difficulty 3) might allow the victim to realise that their mind is being toyed with, with some Lasombra elders being particularly paranoid about this. If the victim is dies before the memories return, the vampire keeps them, although Skills are lost.</p>\n<p>A character may steal the mind of only one other character at a time. Reusing this power resets the effects.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> According to the table below or until dispelled by magics or powers like Cleansed in Blood (Quietus Sanguinis •••, p. 133). The user may also release the victim from the effect at will.</p>\n<p>Margins Duration 0 margins 10 minutes 1 margin One hour.</p>\n<p>2 margins One night 3 margins One week 4 margins One month 5 margins Three months 6 margins One year 7 margins One year + 1 for each margin.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"94"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"ZJHDU4CJ":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"aSx11dyu":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.p2ytAzKS8y7Ff5zw"},"pQmsDHpSsITu1Eqn":{"name":"Sense the Sin","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Every jewel has a fault, and every man has a vice. The best way to shatter a jewel is to strike that fault, and the best way to shatter a man is to hammer his vice. This power allows the user to find a target’s particular vice and glean their secrets with a glance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. Wits + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the user must have the target within the line of sight, observing their way of clothing, moving or topics they are discussing. On a win, the user of the power learns one of the following information, with each margin allowing for an additional information:</p>\n<p>• Deadly Sins: The user senses one of the 7 deadly sins most appealing to the observed subject.</p>\n<p>• Faith: The user senses whether or not they possess True Faith.</p>\n<p>• Sin Comitted: The vampire learns of actions that caused Degeneration Tests for the subject up to 7 days back, one deed per margin invested into this, counting from the latest one.</p>\n<p>• Stench of Torment: The vampire learns whether or not the subject has a dot of Torment.</p>\n<p>• Strength of Will: The user learns whether or the the character has at least four points of undamaged Willpower.</p>\n<p>• Strength of the Soul: The user learns whether or the the character has at least four points in their Road.</p>\n<p>The vampire gains a two-dice bonus on relevant social tests to take advantage of the acquired information such as inciting more sin, repudating the committed sins, pushing the target towards their desire or using it to take advantage of the victim. Dice bonus is not given if no new information is acquired such as though negative answer like no, the target does not have at least four points in their Road or no, the target does not have True Faith or Torment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The bonus lasts until the conditions which give it are changed such as a loss of stains, change of the target’s Desire and so on.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"53"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rS3W4CaL":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"2gLtURUA":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pQmsDHpSsITu1Eqn"},"pRY6sU3kTE3glnxb":{"name":"True Love’s Face","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>A sinister hybrid of Mask of a Thousand Faces and Entrancement, this Discipline technique enables a vampire to assume the form of a target’s true love. Both the Followers of Set and Nosferatu prize this power, though for very different reasons. Where Serpents use the guise of loved ones to tempt and seduce, spiteful Lepers delight in shattering victim’s minds by revealing their true face after a passionate embrace. Vampires may use this art to take the form of someone they know their victim loves or allow their target’s expectations to manifest on their own. In the latter case, however, the vampire may need quick wits to discern whom she looks like, especially since the copied person may or may not be living.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Obfuscate 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> To impersonate someone the victim has strong emotions for, the vampire must know their appearance and what the victim feels for them. How they uncover this will vary between vampires. When activated the Storyteller rolls a hidden contest between the user’s Manipulation + Subterrfuge and the victim’s Composure + Wits, where failure means the visage holds no emotional charge, which is deeply uncanny and sours any social interaction with the victim. It also prevents them from falling victim to this power for the rest of the story.</p>\n<p>On a win, the vampire adds their Presence rating to any rolls for affecting a mortal’s resonance or a vampire’s emotional state. Appearing as a vampire’s Touchstone is a dangerous game, especially if the victim possesses Sense the Unseen (Auspex 1) but played right it can lead to Degeneration for the victim as their Touchstone appears to become damaged or detached entirely through shifts in behavior, ideals, or even apparent death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"124"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"zz6aIcrt":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"qU6lrefU":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pRY6sU3kTE3glnxb"},"pWUXkH1JrxqjnUHj":{"name":"Mental Maze","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Serpents maintain that Obfuscate is less about illusion and more about perception. Mental Maze is the ability to remove all sense of direction and location from a victim, allowing the vampire to make their target a prisoner in their current environment, such as a home, a nightclub, or worse — a vampire’s cellar. The victim finds their location folding back on itself, subtly altering their perception of previously visited rooms, convincing them that an actual exit will only lead deeper into the maze, and eventually breaking them down into a state of panic and fragility.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One or three Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge vs. Charisma + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire personally speaks the words “you cannot escape” to their victim and rolls Charisma + Obfuscate versus the target’s Wits + Resolve, with lower Generation vampires being immune per Dominate’s characteristics (p. XX). On a win, the victim struggles to find their way out of the building they’re in. An additional two Rouse Checks allows this power to be used in a single room or a densely packed outdoor environment (such as a construction site or forest).</p>\n<p>Mortals cannot attempt to escape but supernatural creatures can make a Resolve + Awareness test each scene using the vampire’s initial successes as the Difficulty, suffering a point of Superficial Willpower damage for every missing success if they fail. This roll cannot benefit from Teamwork, as any guidance given does not match what the victim sees. This power ends if the environment becomes dangerous (e.g. the building catches fire), unless the vampire has Terminal Decree (Dominate •••••).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"102"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"gKHEfnV8":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"pT4EflFM":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pWUXkH1JrxqjnUHj"},"pcKwaFB7BVqKlqSA":{"name":"Incubus Humour","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can inflame the humors of a victim within line of sight, eroding the victim’s self-control and amplifying their temperament to a fevered pitch. The vampire may select one of the four humours that govern the body and induce the emotion associated with it. Thus, the flames of mild irritation may be fanned into a rage with an inflammation of yellow bile, or brief melancholia into crippling depression with an inflammation of black bile. Inflaming phlegm, on the other hand, completely deadens the victim’s emotions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the vampire must speak and roll Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 52). Upon a win, they change the victim’s Resonance and, through it, their behaviour. A Critical win increases their Resonance bz a single level of Temperament (minimum Intense). Now knowing what buttons to push, the user gains a two-dice bonus on their Social rolls against the target when the target’s Resonance would come in handy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 52. The victim’s Resonance or Temperament changing also ends the power’s effects.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"64"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pcKwaFB7BVqKlqSA"},"pf9ItGM27DbWq3mf":{"name":"Marduk’s Whip","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>When in need, the user of Quietus extends their blood into a whip capable of crystalizing to form a sharp but flexible weapon. Most Haqimites prefer to make a whip as the image of a sorcerer whipping spirits and demons away usually sends a message.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Blood Dance</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes a Rouse Check and releases their blood (usually through a minor self-inflicted would). The blood crystalises into a piercing weapon of the user’s choice of damage rating equal to half the Quietus rating, rounded up.</p>\n<p>Dice pool for the weapon depends on its shape, from broadswords (Strength + Melee) to traditional whips (Dexterity + Melee) or gauntlets (Strength + Brawl). It is capable of striking incorporeal targets such as spirits and demons (but not ghosts). Incorporeal targets suffer Aggravated Health damage while corporeal suffer Superficial Willpower damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"147"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pf9ItGM27DbWq3mf"},"pg7YrZauhKvQl7Mi":{"name":"Rationalise","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s victims now believe that anything they do under the influence of Dominate was a result of their own free will, and defend their actions, however absurd. Long-term exposure to this power can lead to severe mental trauma in the victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> No Additional Cost</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If pressed on their belief, the victim can make a Wits+Awareness test (Difficulty 5). A win makes them question their own statement, and possibly their sanity.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"75"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pg7YrZauhKvQl7Mi"},"pi2s6eep1mefMMhY":{"name":"Leash the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>You channel your Blood to your Beast, preventing it from awakening.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One or Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When you roll a 10 (potential Critical) on a normal die, you can turn that to a 9 (Success) by Rousing the Blood to activate this power. When you roll a 1 (potential Bestial Failure) on a Hunger die, you can turn that to a 2 (Failure) if you make two Rouse Checks to activate this power.</p>\n<p>You can also use this power to turn a result of 1–5 (Failure) on a Normal die made during a test to resist Frenzy to a 6 (Success).</p>\n<p>If the Rouse Checks made for this Power would drive you above 5 Hunger, you immediately enter a Hunger Frenzy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"17"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pi2s6eep1mefMMhY"},"pkuFsVZAeHa7jVVC":{"name":"Blessing of Haqim","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Quietus Sanguinis is taught to all within the Clan who hunt spirits, not just those who belong to the sorcerer caste. This power is an exemplary reason why, allowing all forms of Quietus to harm spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Quietus Cruciatus 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> All powers of all 3 Paths of Quietus may damage incorporeal as they do corporeal. This excludes ghosts, who are not as strictly immaterial due to possessing a body made out of ectoplasm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"146"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"8aGEm9o5":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"41UW556c":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pkuFsVZAeHa7jVVC"},"plzS0BkRMRHZCgcn":{"name":"Parlysis of Death","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user of Thanatosis transforms their vitae into a contact poison capable of delivering rigor mortis. Many a Samedi who has served as a mercenary has found this power extremely useful as it can be coated on weapons. French-kissing a victim works, but who would be so brave as to kiss a Samedi — a walking, decaying corpse.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user concentrates and forces Blood through an open, usually self-inflicted, wound. Each Rouse Check’s worth of poison made emits enough Blood to coat one bladed melee weapon or to fill one mouthful to be spat at a foe. Spitting the poison can happen instantly during the first turn while coating a weapon is a -2 dice Minor Action.</p>\n<p>The poison looks and smells like normal blood and a Cainite is immune to their own poison, but not that of others utilising this power. If the vampire is in danger of someone attempting to drink blood from them, they may activate the power defensively (doesn’t count for Discipline activation limit), which may be used reflexively even if in slumber or torpor. A vampire may also bite their tongue or lip to pool the poisoned blood in their mouth for a “kiss of death” or a spitting attack (Composure + Aim vs. Dexterity + Athletics, range is equal to the highest Quietus Path + Potence in meters/yards ). Likewise, they may cut their flesh to smear the poison on a pointed or edged weapon, or even place it in food, on a cup, utensil, or other seemingly innocuous item. Darts, arr-wheads and daggers are the most commonly smeared items, although smaller bolts and bullets lack do not have enough surface to carry enough poison. Simple skin contact of finger with a drop of blood is also enough as the skin absorbs the poison into the victim’s blood stream. Multiple poisons may stack as the blood is mixed.</p>\n<p>Poison remains potent for nights equal to the user’s Thanatosis rating, although envenomed weapons bathed in water, or open to the mercy of heavy wind or rain, lose the potency of their poison the turn after exposure, while those passing through fire or sunlight lose it instantly and appear dusted in a fine ash.</p>\n<p>On a hit, a mortal victim becomes paralysed, unable to talk or move, while vampires and other undead suffer Physical Impairment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Both the duration of the user’s poison and the victim’s paralysis last for a number of nights equal to the user’s Thanatosis rating + Blood Potency.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"176"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.plzS0BkRMRHZCgcn"},"pxg64dtMN1ddyZA7":{"name":"Disruption","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>A combat-oriented usage of Chimerstry, the ability to effect senses affects the targets so much that they find themselves stunned as their senses are overwhelmed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor action of -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check and one point of Willpower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls their Manipulation + Subterfuge. Anyone unprepared and within eyesight of the vampire and eligible to experience the hallucination (by being in line of sight of its projected appearance, or by being the target of a tactile sensation) is distracted, losing two dice on their next action. Additionally, those who fail to resist using their Composure + Wits lose their next active action if they fail (but they can still defend and resist as normal, with a two-dice penalty.). A single target cannot be affected by Disruption twice in a two consequtive turns.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"50"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hqRI6uCK":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WmaxKU4Q":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.pxg64dtMN1ddyZA7"},"q3K11xnWREn84iOx":{"name":"Drink the Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Practiced most frequently by both the Assamites and Tremere, though according to vastly different principles of magic, this Discipline heightens the power of the Amaranth to steal memories and knowledge. Those who walk the Road of Blood regard such feeding as a sacrament partaken by the holiest of holy, a reclaiming of learning from the unworthy. The pragmatic blood sorcerers of the Tremere simply view diablerie of mind as another useful tool of power. Neither clan suspects they are not alone in their art.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Bloodlife Path or Quietus •••• (any Path)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may be used whenever a character successfully commits diablerie. The player spends a number of Willpower points equal to the victim’s Intelligence and rolls Composure + Resolve against’ the victim’s Composure + Resolve. The victim gains a die bonus for every Derangement they possessed, with Clan Malkavian Bane Derangement providing its Bane Severity as dice points. Each margin grants one bonus dot that may be spent to purchase or increase the diablerist’s Skills. These points cannot raise an Skills above the victim’s rating, however. This power also allows the diablerist to recall the victim’s strong memories, although such recollections are left to the Storyteller to adjudicate. Stolen memories unfold hazily as from a dream, and should offer cryptic hints rather than plot-breaking insights unless the Storyteller deems it would not harm their Chronicle’s story (shrewd Storytellers may even use this power to drop useful hits for their players). Vampires whose players roll a Total Failure/Botch the Composure + Resolve Test roll lose one point of permanent Willpower for every 1 rolled, as the character is overwhelmed by an onslaught of disconnected images and hate from the victim’s devoured soul. This power can only be used once per diablerie.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Permanent.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"33"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"0RFRm8dj":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"HlCxRhkn":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.q3K11xnWREn84iOx"},"qLZ6Y37LWqwBoI7E":{"name":"Calm the Guilt","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>The users of Bardo act as mentors and shepards for others struggling with their Humanity. With this power, the user is able to literally alleviate others of their burden and suffering by taking it onto themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Composure + Insight vs. Wits + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to work, the user must have at least one empty space to receive a stain on their Humanity tracker and the target must have at least one stain and be on Humanity or one of its Paths. After that, the only thing that the user must do is talk to the one possessing stains and console them, offering words of comfort as they roll Composure + Insight at a difficulty of 3. On a win, they relieve the target of their guilt who removes a stain from their Humanity tracker, but the user adds the stain to their own as they draw the corruption out of their target. On a Critical Win, the user does not add a stain to their tracker as they have perfectly expelled and driven away all the guilt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The stains the user takes last until rolled away on a Remorse roll as normal.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"37"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"3OcBeOx3":{"path":"attributes.composure"},"SXcJM4Ep":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.qLZ6Y37LWqwBoI7E"},"qQ9WL9pZPMOzGRiP":{"name":"Weaving","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>A vampire with this power moves fast enough to perceive projectiles such as throwing knives or bullets as slow, and can sidestep them at leisure. Cost: One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Rapid Reflexes</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dexterity + Athletics, Dexterity + Melee or as needed</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user does not suffer diminishing dice pools when defending against multiple ranged attacks using Dexterity + Athletics or with Dexterity + Melee as they may now parry projectiles with weapons. They can also add their Celerity rating to all such dodging of ranged attacks or parrying projectile attempts while this power is active.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"45"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Qg8tHMFF":{"path":"attributes.dexterity"},"b5NX3hpI":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.qQ9WL9pZPMOzGRiP"},"qSnQCc4Tm5lKQRU7":{"name":"Aura Memory","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power, the vampire can absorb emotions and impressions off objects and places, allowing them to relive them while they ingest them. They touche the object, and remove its identity.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The player makes a Resolve + Insight roll, with the Difficulty determined by the Storyteller based on the age of the impressions and the mental and spiritual strength of the person who left them (Difficulty should not exceed 6). Gleaning the emotional state of the user of a murder weapon used a few days before is Difficulty 2, but sensing the surroundings in which a 300-year old letter was written approaches Difficulty 5 or higher.</p>\n<p>One scene-type image and one aspect of the person’s identity (Aura, Ambition, Ethics, name, sex, or age) is revealed for each margin garnered on the roll. Subsequent uses of Aura Absorption or The Spirit’s Touch on the same object have their successes penalised with the first vampire’s successes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"93"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"CKPThdi0":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"JxFj951a":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.qSnQCc4Tm5lKQRU7"},"qUvG0ZkEQup13HXx":{"name":"Saturn’s Flux","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This is a drug that breaks Blood Bonds, which is a pretty big deal. Some authoritarian domains that rely on the Bond and the threat of it execute without trial just for possession, even if you’re “just holding for a friend.” Others confiscate it for their own use while forbidding it to Kindred without clout. Still other domains insist on a Prince’s prerogative to control all access to it, so that the court can strip Bonds inconvenient to those in power.</p>\n<p>It’s already politically complicated. It does not help that you have to freebase it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, phlegmatic mortal blood, lead, laxative, horse hair.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The process yields a thumbnail sized pellet, crackly and gray. To gain the effect, smoke it in a steel pipe on a bed of ash, taking precautions as Kindred must for dealing with a small open flame. Within twenty-four hours of ingestion, the smoker literally shits out the Blood Bond — painful and bloody but not injurious for mortals. Kindred, with withered digestive tracts, take a point of Aggravated damage as the unwholesome influence resists expulsion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As mentioned above, the effect kicks in within a day’s span of ingestion—sometimes waking Kindred from daysleep with agonizing cramps. Until used, the pellet seems to stay fresh indefinitely. Given that this stuff has only been around two years, it may just be that its shelf life has not yet been reached.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"80"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.qUvG0ZkEQup13HXx"},"qbIcKwXEQXuQyDEb":{"name":"Soul Haunting","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dementation.png","system":{"description":"<p>A brief speech to a target allows the vampire to touch the sacred or profane within her victim, destroying the banal concerns that keep those urges from making themselves known. These urges manifest as visions, scents, and sounds of divinity revealing themselves in the most mundane of places. Victims report that these images tend to follow a general theme: angels or demons (or a culturally appropriate religious figure, including ancestors) dramatically recounting and reenacting the victim’s greatest fears and repressed secrets. Even if the victim ignores these visions, more insidious is the subtle altering of their senses – a prince’s genuine sympathy can become mocking pity, while the false lust of an Alamut houri ghoul becomes all-consuming passion. Even if visions are ignored, the victim’s trust in their senses is utterly compromised.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Insight vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this power to be activated, the user must converse with the victim for at least 3 turns and then roll Manipulation + Insight vs. Composure + Wits of the victim with whom they’re communicating (p. 44). On a win, the visions will haunt the victim a night with an additional night per point of margin. At any point during the period, the Dementation user may force the visions to occur, requiring minimal focus on their part, regardless of the distance between the two. The Storyteller is also free to trigger these visions at their discretion.</p>\n<p>Visions inflict a dice penalty equal to half the user’s Dementation rating rounded up to all dice pools for two turns.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See p. 52.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"66"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rdS4jsYd":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"SrSAvOuv":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"dementation","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.qbIcKwXEQXuQyDEb"},"qpF0h7xYQWnh69ca":{"name":"Mystic Sight","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>Referred to as “Quintessence Detection” by those Tremere who still know the meaning of these words and “Thaumaturgical Sight” by newer Tremere, this power allows its user to visualise magical effects that would otherwise have gone unnoticed. Many Banu Haqim, who refer to this power as “Sense Magic” use it well to hunt demons and infernalists. How one senses is often unique to the, but generally a stronger power should give off a brighter aura or a stronger smell.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Sense the Unseen</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Any Blood Sorcery Path ••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever a magic power is within their sensory range, the user may roll Intelligence + Awareness against a difficulty equal to the number of successes on a magic. Demon, fae, ghost, mage, mummy, sorcerer (including blood sorcerer) and True Faith powers all register as types of magic.</p>\n<p>On a win, the user senses magic and, if they are at least somewhat familiar with it (seen it in practice, received education, heard of it in detail), they can generally narrow it down to the exact spell, power, Path, Pillar, School of Magic or another category of power, depending on what they’re familiar with. A mummy invoking Hekau or a demon invoking an Arcanum would give off their unique auras or vibes and even without knowledge of the exact power an experienced vampire will that they’re mummy and demon magics respectively due to the unique pattern of each magic. For example, somebody activating Taste of Vitae within sight of the user may have energy radiate from their tongue in the eyes of the user of Mystic Sight. Magic without rolls such as constructs of magic (familiars, zombies, homunculi…) can be recognised as well with the difficulty for the roll being 3.</p>\n<p>This knowledge gives the user a two-dice bonus on all rolls to resist or dodge the perceived magic. This bonus is not further amplified by power bonus as it is merely knowledge how to defend.</p>\n<p>If you wish to flavour how one perceives magic, you may take inspiration from Scry the Soul writeup below and discuss it with your storyteller such as seeing magical flares, smelling or tasting the swirl of magical energy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"27"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"J89AtrFi":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"FmsWkI1V":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.qpF0h7xYQWnh69ca"},"rFXxDF3yMZyknNcz":{"name":"Nightingale’s Song","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Dominate ordinarily cannot be used to force someone to tell the truth, but fae magics help you steal secrets. You can use Mesmerse to demand a target reveal a secret.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Mytherceria 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> As Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After successfuly ordering a subject with Mesmerise to reveal a secret, they reveal it; however, without a cleverly worded order, the secret may be unpredictable or just random, especially if it is \"Tell me what you know!\" or \"Tell me your secret!\". Only a single information may be extracted per win. Revealing damning information counts as harm, so getting specific suicidal secrets may still be impossible without Terminal Decree. Should your attempt to compel a secret fail, you visibly shed one large, bloody tear for each point of the margin of failure (or seven tears if the Power otherwise fails). These tears cause a Rouse Check if shedding four or more, and risk provoking Hunger Frenzy in other vampires or uncomfortable questions among mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> N/A</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"75"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.rFXxDF3yMZyknNcz"},"rGKDezwrfWjwXbVw":{"name":"Food Stain","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Resembling ketchup, this elixir places a mark on a person who consumes it or is anointed with it. The alchemist can easily tell whether anyone later drinks the blood of the marked individual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, melancholic mortal blood, some highly colored flavoring such as BBQ sauce, mustard, cheez-puff flavor dust, etc.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If someone — mortal or Kindred — is marked by consuming or contacting this goo, the next individual who feeds from them becomes obvious to the alchemist who made it. <em>Obvious</em> means that if they see that person, hear them or smell them, they know. In the rare case that the alchemist has at least Auspex 2, they become aware the instant the feeding occurs. Moreover, for about an hour after, they know the direction toward the drinker, though not distance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Once created, the mystic potential of the substance persists until consumed or applied, but it starts to smell pretty bad after a week, even if refrigerated.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"74"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.rGKDezwrfWjwXbVw"},"rd0W3n8hv8YhagrT":{"name":"Traversal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>With blurring speed the vampire can run or climb along any surface, including vertical and even liquid mediums. While Traversal does not grant insect-like supernatural traction, running up or along walls present little problem. Walking on water remains impossible, but the vampire can run on water for a limited distance given a run-up.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor Action -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dexterity + Athletics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make a Dexterity + Athletics roll with a Difficulty of 3 (inclined surface with traction) to 6 (slick vertical surface, open water), depending on the surface and angle. Each point of margin gets the vampire further up or out; a margin of 0 gets to a close target, a margin of 1 to one farther than that, and so forth. The Storyteller should inform the player beforehand if a target is too distant to even attempt Traversal; as a rule of thumb, anything over water farther than 60 meters (or more than 30 stories up a building) probably exceeds this power’s range.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"44"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"3Dm5OzLk":{"path":"attributes.dexterity"},"AUV81hoV":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.rd0W3n8hv8YhagrT"},"roZNEt86wu4EDM7T":{"name":"Suffuse the Edifice","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>While present in a building or similar locale the vampire is able to extend the powers of Awe, Daunt, and Majesty through the very structure of the building. Anybody in the building or looking at it will be affected by these powers, reacting to the building as if the vampire themself is present.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> As power transmitted</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Anyone seeing the building from outside or who are present inside it needs to resist the power used as if the vampire was present, unless the vampire is within eyesight, at which point they themselves becomes the focus. As a building does not generally make Skill checks, apply the bonuses instead to any reaction the victim has to the place. A nightclub under the influence of Awe will have queues stretching around the block, while a haven under Daunt will turn away all but the most staunch investigators. Use of Majesty has to be done with extreme caution, as the results can be spectacular and volatile, far exceeding the effect of the more subtle powers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As power transmitted or one night, whichever is longer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"125"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.roZNEt86wu4EDM7T"},"rpDGXoMpbe1H1kcj":{"name":"False Resonance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user’s Chimerstry extends beyond normal sights now, able to fool even those capable of reading auras. This power is a great source of distrust against the Ravnos who could masquerade as mortals, Revenants, change how their morality is percieved and even hide signs of Diablerie with it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check and one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user rolls Intelligence + Subterfuge against a Difficulty of 1 to 3 (or more), depending on the sophistication of the ruse they want to enact, with 1 representing a simple masking of their emotional state to 2 for manipulating the auras such as that of diablerie or the type of creature to 3 and beyond for elaborate alternate personas complete with false thoughts and memories. The margin on the win is added as Difficulty to any attempt to “read” them. Failing to reach the added Difficulty lets the reader see only the false personality the user has crafted, without any hint that the reader is being misled. (Failing to reach even the standard Difficulty yields nothing, as normal, so as not to let on that a false personality is present.).</p>\n<p>Soul Mask cannot conceal Clan Banes such as that of the Children of Haqim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene. Permanency which makes it permanent until dismissed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"50"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hqRI6uCK":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WmaxKU4Q":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.rpDGXoMpbe1H1kcj"},"rppkuVI6iYAp8LDz":{"name":"Blur","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>The user appears to blur into a multitude of indistinct copies of themselves, obscuring their location and allowing them to evade the aim of their enemies.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> when attempting to physically dodge or avoid an enemy attack, be it a physical blow or target spell, add half (rounded up) of your Mytherceria rating to the dice pool. If your enemy is using Sense the Unseen and has an Auspex rating (or similar power and a Discipline, like Fae Sight) greater than your Mytherceria rating, you may not add this dice bonus.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, or until intentionally ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"91"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.rppkuVI6iYAp8LDz"},"rqa2vLxyf3lAnqAS":{"name":"Mask Off","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Militant thin-bloods use this formula to hit true vampires where it hurts: their precious Masquerade.</p>\n<p>The formula shows to best advantage in gatherings where Kindred and kine mix.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, choleric and melancholic mortal blood, ground mustard seed, lemon juice, amphetamines, ground match heads.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Distillation roll vs target’s Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist creates (or for other Distillations, they or their vessel bleed) a yellow-reddish paste. When loaded into an explosive and detonated, it creates an acrid gas that covers an area 3 by 6 meters, roughly the size of a large meeting room. The explosion and the gas inflict no damage, but any true vampires with the Blush of Life caught in the blast must make a contest of their Stamina + Resolve versus the alchemist’s Distillation roll. Any true vampires that lose the contest have their Blush of Life instantly, painfully come to an end for all to see. They also cannot use Blush of Life for the rest of the night.</p>\n<p>On Distillation rolls between 1–3 successes, true vampires that lose the contest have a few moments to hide before the gas takes effect. On a critical win on the Distillation roll, true vampires that lose the contest must also make a Fury Frenzy check at Difficulty 2.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"77"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.rqa2vLxyf3lAnqAS"},"sHnVpdju2w3BjYRD":{"name":"Unseen Strike","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>This lethal power not only allows the vampire to vanish from plain sight but to instantly move to an enemy and deliver a fatal strike. A vampire skilled in this art can quickly turn the tables as they disappear in front of pursuers, escaping not only with their lives but with that of their prey as well.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Obfuscate ••••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Blink</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Dexterity + Athletics vs Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power combines effects of Blink with elements of the Obfuscate power of Vanish. To onlookers the vampire appears to disappear and the target is caught unawares, becoming subject to surprise attack. Unless the victim can react by beating the user’s Dexterity + Athletics with their Wits + Awareness in a contest, they are unable to defend against the attack and it is made against a set Difficulty of 1. If the attacker fails this test they still perform a regular attack, as per Blink power, and Unseen Strike is also subject to the same movement restrictions as Blink.</p>\n<p>An opponent possessing Celerity 5 or Temporis 5 can nullify this power by making their own Rouse Check, defending at the same speed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"45"},"level":4,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"juj74Okt":{"path":"attributes.dexterity"},"kx4MguUG":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.sHnVpdju2w3BjYRD"},"sIt5TV48Ishn3Dsk":{"name":"Heart Laid Bare","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>While not true mind reading, this Power lets a vampire peer into the subconsciousness of a victim to gain a sense of what they desire — or fear — the most. Armed with this knowledge the user gains a powerful leverage on the victim, though the moment they let on that they are aware of an elder’s deepest fears, their nights might be numbered.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs. Composure + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user decides on whether they are looking for fears or desires and then engages the target in conversation for a few minutes before making the Power test. The test is made against Difficulty 0 on unsuspecting mortals, but mortals aware of vampiric abilities — as well as all supernatural creatures — can resist with Composure + Subterfuge. The margin on success determines how much information is gained:</p>\n<p>1–2: The user gets a general notion of the victim’s fears or desires, such as: “wealth”, “sex”, “bugs”, or “loneliness.”</p>\n<p>3–5: The sense becomes more specific: “selling produce on the market”, “a threesome”, “hornets”, or “losing my partner”</p>\n<p>6+: The user receives a clear picture of the element sought, complete with context or history: “getting away with that corruption scandal in which I acepted a bribe to to release a murderer and punish an innocent person in their stead”, “a threesome with Billy and Jeanette”, “hornets, ever since I whacked that nest on a dare in third grade”, or “Warren finding out I lied about Billy and Jeanette.”</p>\n<p>Add one success to the result if the object of desire or fear is present, and add another success if the conversation touches upon the topic. (Though the victim can catch on to the intent unless the user wins a Composure + Subterfuge vs Wits + Insight test). The user gains a two-dice bonus to push further on the information they’ve uncovered, whether to Insight to inquire more on it, Persuasion, Intimidation for blackmail...</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The bonus lasts one night</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"56"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"LnyxsBno":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"NV7QM5OJ":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.sIt5TV48Ishn3Dsk"},"sJsRBdwpsDPEqSFm":{"name":"Draugh of Might","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Blood of the vampire becomes saturated with the power of Potence, conveying a part of that power to anyone who drinks of it. This is the Potence equivalent of Draught of Elegance (p. XX).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Drinking a Rouse Checks worth of Blood directly from the user gifts the drinker with temporary Potence equal to half the Potence dots (rounded up) of the donor. The drinker gains the same powers as the donor’s, up to that level.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night; for vampires, until the next feeding or the vampire reaches Hunger 5</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"118"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.sJsRBdwpsDPEqSFm"},"sSurPblZCvZgSdRI":{"name":"Doubletalk","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Whereas most vampires use the powers of Obfuscate to hide objects or their own actions, some vampire d use it to mask their speech. The user can say one thing and secretly convey another to a specific person without bystanders being none the wiser.</p>\n<p>This is the power to use when flirting with the Prince’s boyfriend right in front of her face.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Auspex •</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Persuasion</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire adds a secret message (up to one sentence) to their words that only a single intended listener can hear. Anyone else present only picks up the message if they can beat the user’s Intelligence + Persuasion with their powers such as Sense the Unseen.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One utterance</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"97"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"wYjjvStI":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"x4kczeX0":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.sSurPblZCvZgSdRI"},"sWJ6qvySLugqWsyf":{"name":"Tear the Rift","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Vampires cannot call demons through common summoning and binding rituals that mortals can so readily use and must resort to other means such as sorcery. Vampires simply lack something that mortals possess, but what? Whether or not the answer to that question is “the soul” nobody dares to ponder, but the effectiveness of this power is not questioned. Call the Abyss extends the privilege of summoning demons to vampires who may use this to train their diabolism, bind demons with some assistance or to summon an infernal patron with whom they can negotiate a better pact of servitude.</p>\n<p>With this power, the Daimonion user opens a rift into the Abyss (or just simply “Hell”) and summons forth a demon. The demon embodies one of the seven deadly sins which the caster must have performed in the same night. Additionally, this power is able to bind demons accursed vampire blood, making this power the only known way a non-mortal may bind a demon without sorcery, bringing both the origins and the purpose of Daimonion into questions. Perhaps its users are predestined to command the hellish legions?</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the vampire knows a celestial or a True Name of a demon, they may use it to summon them by writing it with blood. If they do not, a random lesser demon is summoned. The vampire draws a summoning circle with their blood and makes a Resolve + Daimonion test at a Difficulty of 4. Using a Celestial Name to summon a specific demon is also at a Difficulty of 4, and usage of a True Name reduces the Difficulty to 1. On a win, the center of the summoning circle ignites as the demon takes form across three turns. Random demons are dictated by the template below with the demon’s stats being further modified by the represented sin and adding one Attribute dot for each additional Rouse Check made during the summoning.</p>\n<p>Once summoned, the demon doesn’t feel the pull of the Abyss while within the summoning circle which endures for a number of hours equal to the successes rolled. To exit the circle without being pulled back to their Abyssal prison, a proper consenting human host or a relic for them to inhabit must be provided. The human host can be drugged or influenced out of their mind, as long as they give genuine consent. Most demons can manifest within 20 meters of their relic. Alternatively, the demon can remain in their incorporeal form and exit the circle for an hour without feeling the pull of Hell if the summoner feeds them a Rouse Check of their blood, theoretically keeping the demon on Earth indefinitely with sufficient blood expenditure. This vitae does not impose automatic Torment.</p>\n<p>The summoned demon is under a mystical obligiation of Daimonion’s authority that makes them unable to directly harm their summoner or their friends until the next sunset. Although the demon cannot attack the vampire who summoned it, neither must it automatically obey their commands. The vampire may choose to attempt to issue commands to the the demon with an Intelligence + Occult roll against the demon’s Composure + Resolve. Why a random demon summoned through this ritual can be bound without the usage of its celestial or True Name is unknown, but some Baali theorise that their blood acts to enhance their divine (or infernal) authority. If the demon fails, they become bound to their summoner.</p>\n<p>A bound demon will fully act as its summoner commands to the letter as it is bound until the next sunset and will seek to commit petty acts of rebellion inaccordance with its choice of sin. The demon must also truthfully answer questions to the letter (most often they are asked to reveal names of other demons), but the demon may twist their words in accordance to the wording of the question. A demon may never be compelled to reveal its True Name. A bound demon may never intentionally harm their master, but it can harm their allies unless specifically ordered not to.</p>\n<p>At the beginning of each night after the first, the user of Daimonion may spend a point of Willpower and roll Resolve + Daimonion against the demon’s Composure + Resolve to maintain their control over it for another night. If the demon wins, it is set free, but its former master will have no ways of sensing it. A demon with a host running rampant can cause major headaches which is why most non-idiotic diabolists simply command demons to abandon their current hosts and return to their abyssal prison while they still have control over it in order to avoid risking retribution from their former slave.</p>\n<p>Only a number of unnamed Lesser Demons may be bound at a time using this power equal to the vampire’s power bonus (minimum of 1) as the power and the infernal authority within the vampire’s vitae ared stretched thin. Named demons have no limit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> See System</p>\n<p><strong>Lesser Demon Template</strong></p>\n<p>The Disciplines listed are not Cainite Disciplines; they’re approximate powers. Use Willpower Checks in place of Rouse Checks for activation by rolling a d10. Upon a failure, the demon suffers a point of Willpower damage. Some of the demons gain Backgrounds which the vampire adds to their own or counts them as such if the vampire has bound the demon or struck a bargain for them. These reflect influence in the mortal world that the demon may offer.</p>\n<p>For demon traits and powers, see Appendix B. Roll a d10. 1-5, the demon is built as a Gifted Individual, on 6-10 it is a Superlative/Deadly Individual with no advantages. Let the Storyteller built the demon unless the player wishes to do it themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Arcana:</strong> Arcana are demonic powers (see Appendix B) used by demons and are pale parodies of their former angelic mights. A demon always has a maximum Arcan.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Divinity Rating:</strong> Roll a d10. Divinity functions as Blood Potency for all intents and purposes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Torment Rating:</strong> Roll two d10s and halve the result.</p>\n<p>Sin Templates (Add to the base template)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Greed:</strong> Wits +1, Manipulation +1, Intimidation +2, Leadership +2, Subterfuge +2, Finance+4, Resources 4,</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Pride:</strong> Charisma +3, Manipulation +2, Looks:</p>\n<p>Stunning, Brawl +2, Subterfuge +1, Etiquette +4, Performance +3, Allies 5, +2 Torment</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Wrath:</strong> Strength +2, Dexterity +1, Stamina +2, Resolve +2, Looks Merits 3: Menacing Stunning, Athletics +3, Brawl +3, Intimidation +3, Melee +4, +2 Health</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sloth:</strong> Dexterity -1, Composure +3, Intelligence +2, Wits +2, Awareness +2, Allies +2, Herd +3, Resources 3, Torment -1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Gluttony:</strong> Strength +2, Dexterity -1, Stamina +2, Intimidation +2, Herd +1, Resources +3, +2 Health,</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Envy:</strong> Wits +1, Intelligence +2, Finance +2, Subterfuge +2,</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Lust:</strong> Wits +1, Charisma +3, Manipulation +3,</p>\n<p>Looks Merits: Elegant, Seductive, Herd +3 Insight+3, Leadership +2, Subterfuge +3,</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"60"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.sWJ6qvySLugqWsyf"},"saI13KlU4VNstgn4":{"name":"Taste of Death","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire softly breathes out a red mist towards their target or around themselves, burning lungs and skins of their victims and.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full/Extended Minor -4 dice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina +Athletics vs. Stamina + Athletics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user softly exhales a fine reddish mist towards the target which creeps up to them slowly enough for them to attempt to dodge. The user rolls Stamina +Athletics against Stamina + Athletics of the victim trying to evade the mist. Should the victim fail to avoid the mist. The mist has a range of 3 meters/yards for each dot of user’s Stamina and Potence. Alternatively, the power may be used to create a zone around the user, the range is reduced to half a meter/yard for each dot of user’s Stamina and Potence. Those within the zone must succeed on a Stamina + Athletics against Stamina + Athletics as well each turn. The zone lasts as long as the user does continues to breathe out the mist, counting as a Minor action of -4 dice after the first turn and rerolling the Stamina + Athletics vs. Stamina + Athletics contest each turn.</p>\n<p>On a win, the user deals a number of Aggravated Health damage equal to the margin, reduced to Superficial against creatures that require no oxigen like vampires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn/until the user stops exhaling.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"143"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"IzC7Npll":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"4LH68WXK":{"path":"skills.athletics"}},"discipline":"quietuscruciatus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.saI13KlU4VNstgn4"},"sjCACVJOcmrlxkms":{"name":"Blue State","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>A mind-altering elixir with a sludgy cobalt color, when fed to a target or applied to their skin, it forces them to grudgingly and obsessively contemplate the next wrong thing one of their friends or allies does.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, melancholy and phlegmatic mortal blood, cobalt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Someone under the effects of Blue State cannot forgive. The next time a friend or ally violates the target’s Convictions, the target takes 1 Stain. A mortal target merely holds a seemingly unreasonable, intractable grudge.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The Stain remains until the characters have, at least, a very uncomfortable conversation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"76"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.sjCACVJOcmrlxkms"},"ssIIy1RGoMBhkJxR":{"name":"Rapid Reflexes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>While their bodies still can’t defy the laws of nature, vampires with this power perceive events instantly and can react to them with superhuman alacrity. They can observe incoming projectiles to the extent that they can attempt to dodge arrows and even bullets without available cover.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> With this power vampires suffer no penalty to their defense pools for lack of cover against ranged projectile attacks. They can also take a Minor Action worth up to two dice per turn, such as readying or reloading a weapon, for free.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"43"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ssIIy1RGoMBhkJxR"},"t0dXo7qod19KqPeJ":{"name":"Aspect of the Higher Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/spiritus.png","system":{"description":"<p>Reaching further into the spirit realms, the user is able to call upon higher spirits to lend them their aid.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Summon Spirit Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user gains additional options for their aspects. Note that examples below are related to cultural areas of the Balkan. Aspects below are mere samples. The Storyteller should allow the player to craft their own aspects related to the concepts associated with animals in the region. When making a generic, it should give a dot in an Attribute and an associated Specialty. Bonuses from Aspects are increased by power bonus as normal)</p>\n<p>• Apetite of a Pig: The user is able to eat food and enjoy it like a mortal would and gains a Finance Specialty of choice.</p>\n<p>• Bat’s Hearing: The user gains the ability to “see” in the dark throug echolocation, ignoring all vision-related penalties and gaining Echolocation Specialty for Awareness.</p>\n<p>• Beaver’s Bite — This makes the vampire’s bite strong and sharp enough to cut through almost any substance (though it does no additional damage to living or unliving targets).</p>\n<p>• Cat’s Balance — The user gains the equivalent of the Cat’s Grace power (Celerity 1) power.</p>\n<p>• Chameleon’s Colors — The user becomes capable of changing color to suit the environment (+2 dice to Stealth when appropriate) and gains Blending In Specialty for Stealth</p>\n<p>• Hunger of a Cat: The user gains a dot in Manipulation and a Begging Specialty for Persuasion.</p>\n<p>• Leapfrog — This grants the user the ability to leap three times the normal height and distance. The user also gains Jumping Specialty for Athletics.</p>\n<p>• Nose of the Hound — The vampire’s sense of smell is far greater than that of a mortal. They can even track by scent with on a Resolve + Survival roll if they have a smell sample (Difficulty set by the Storyteller from 2 to 7). The user also gains the Bloodhound Merit, allowing them to discern Resonances and emotional states of others through them. The user also gains Tracking Specialty for Survival</p>\n<p>• Mother Bear’s Protection: The user gains a dot in Strength and a specialty Thwarting Maneuver Specialty for Melee</p>\n<p>• Old Owl: The user gains a dot in Intelligence and a specialty in Academics of choice.</p>\n<p>• Ravens of Odin: The user gains a dot in Intelligence and a Ghosts Specialty for Occult.</p>\n<p>• Serpent’s Venom — The vampire’s bite transmits a venomous toxin that causes two Aggravated Health levels of damage in living victims per turn. The damage continues until the toxin is removed or nullified (Intelligence + Medicine at a Difficulty of 3), or until the vampire’s spirit power ends.</p>\n<p>• Sound of the Cricket — This grants the power to produce an annoying, grating sound loud enough to deafen those nearby. Nearby persons suffers a -4 dice penalty to all perception rolls related to hearing for the next scene unless he succeeds on a Wits + Composure roll (difficulty 4)</p>\n<p>• Squirrel’s Balance — The Ahrimane can move about in the branches and limbs of trees or across tightropes with little fear of falling.</p>\n<p>• Swiftness of the Stag — The Ahrimane can move at twice her normal running speed.</p>\n<p>These effects are visible when seen by aura reading</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"162"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"spiritus","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.t0dXo7qod19KqPeJ"},"t0dlbeN9tN2E7J9s":{"name":"Heightened Senses","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s senses sharpen to a preternatural degree, giving them the ability to see better where others would not, hear ultrasonic frequencies and smell the fear of cowering prey.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free (but see below)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user adds their Auspex rating to all mundane perception rolls. If exposed to extreme sensations, such as loud bangs, flashes of intense light or overpowering smells while the power is active the user must succeed on a Wits + Resolve (Difficulty 3 or more) roll to dampen their senses in time, or the overload causes them to sustain a -3 dice penalty to all perception-based rolls for the rest of the scene.</p>\n<p>This power does nothing for Insight rolls. It does not grant full vision in total or darkness or pierce supernatural darkness, but it does allow a vampire to make an Awareness roll when others could not reasonably sense something without the it. Unless from supernatural darkness, penalties to perception rolls are halved and rounded up.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until deactivated. Having the power active for longer stretches of time without rest (more than a scene), especially so for high-stimulus environments, might necessitate spending Willpower, at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"25"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.t0dlbeN9tN2E7J9s"},"t9ioF9kGISGORqcd":{"name":"Flicker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Suffused by inhuman prowess, every part of the vampire’s body becomes able to project the sum of their strength. A flick of their finger can be made to convey the power of a full-body punch, and a punt of the toe becomes a concrete-shattering kick. Note that this power does not make the vampire stronger, but they are able to use what strength they have through minor, subtle movements.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Performing unarmed melee attacks or feats of strength now counts as two-dice minor actions. A character can perform only one Subtle Hammer minor action per turn, though, and cannot make other attacks in the same turn. The ability to concentrate one’s full strength into any part of the body also has other benefits, and any feat of strength where the user’s movement is limited (such as breaking out of bonds) receives a four-dice bonus or more, at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"119"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.t9ioF9kGISGORqcd"},"tDzbPgKEkUkW1Ee1":{"name":"Compel","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>With eye contact, the vampire can issue the victim a single-action command, no longer than a short sentence, to be obeyed to the letter. It must be possible to complete the command in a single turn. The Storyteller decides whether to interpret ambiguous commands in an unexpected or unfavorable way; alternatively, the command simply confuses the victim and fails.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Intimidation vs. Intelligence + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Commanding a resisting victim, a victim the vampire has previously Dominated in the same scene, or another vampire requires a contest of Charisma + Intimidation vs Intelligence + Resolve. Commands that go against the victim’s nature also require such a contest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> No more than a single scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"71"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"3OaqHEOg":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"rvr4mB2r":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.tDzbPgKEkUkW1Ee1"},"tGBLuctXBZ5qnofu":{"name":"Godform","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/serpentis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The true purpose of Serpentis is not only to imitate the divine worshipped by the Followers of Set, but to also become divine and ascend – as Set has promised, once the chains of aeons are shattered. With Godform, the master of Serpentis may do just that — appearing as a god in their own right and claiming dominion over certain elements of creation and domains of mortality. While most Setites manifest this power by adopting the head of a beast similar to the Egyptian gods, every vampire manifests Godhead differently. Some grow a meter in height. Some adopt glistening gold flesh. Regardless the manifestation, it’s always obvious and always awe-inspiring.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When buying this power, the user must make their own cosmetic appearance for their form as each is unique. It can resemble anything from the gorgons of Greece to the animal-headed gods of Egypt and humanoid-enough gods who are only betrayed by special effects around them. However the form ends up looking, there must always be something that would breach the masquerade if seen in public with it being uncovered (bringing even more suspicion to cloaked Setites). The user may spend a point of Willpower to mask such features in order to not breach the Masquerade and appear normal. Their true form may then only be revealed by powers such as Sense the Unseen against the Serpentis user’s Composure + Subterfuge.</p>\n<p>The transformation is almost instantaneous upon making the Rouse Check and it is innate in the forms of Blessings of Set-Typhon.</p>\n<p>The user gains 5 + their Power Bonus as dots to spend on various features of their godly form. If a feature provides a dice bonus, it is further modified by their Power Bonus per standard rules for Disciplines adding dice to pools. The features of one’s Godform should not be changeable unless something in the character’s story arc justifies it. The character may purchase additional “sets” of Godform for 25 exp, however, allowing them to diversify their forms.</p>\n<p>Available features are:</p>\n<p>•+ Abilities of a God: When transforming, the user gains one dot to place in their Attributes while in Godform for each point invested in this feature. Bonuses to whatever Attributes should be static, meaning that one who usually places dots into high Intelligence shouldn’t just swap it to Strength on the next use. Maximum Attribute rating with this is 4 + Blood Surge value.</p>\n<p>•• Apep’s Shadow: The user’s form radiates with the absence of light and may move as a shadow does (see Tenebrous Avatar of Obtenebration 5), scaling the walls and passing any barrier that isn’t hermetically sealed. The user is susceptible to damage as normal, however. The user also gains a two-dice bonus to all vision. In such a case, the storm follows the user, centering Stealth rolls in this form.</p>\n<p>• Disciple of Thoth: The user counts Akhu an additional in-Clan Discipline even when not using the Godform. When using Godform, they gain one automatic success on all rolls related to spellcasting and control tests of Akhu and resisting the effects of Blood Magic.</p>\n<p>• Hunger of Ammit: The user slakes one point of Hunger whenever they make a successful bite attack. This stacks with similar bonuses.</p>\n<p>• Serpent of the Garden: The user gains a one-die bonus on all rolls involving Manipulation and Charisma.</p>\n<p>• Solder of Wapwawet: Gain a one-die bonus on all rolls involving Melee, Brawl and Athletics in combat.</p>\n<p>••• Judge of the Duat: the user may spend a point of Willpower to cross the shroud into the Shadowlands or vice versa. This transit of the Shroud takes 3 turns. The user may also spend a point of Willpower and roll Manipulation + Intimidation vs. Composure + Resolve of a ghost. On a win, the ghost must fulfil any order (to the letter) as soon as possible.</p>\n<p>• Master of the Deserts, Rivers and</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Spirits:</strong> The user unlocks Koldunism, treating it and its Rituals as part of Akhu and requiring no teacher for them as the spirits are willing to reveal their secrets to the user. This counts as a passive power effect active even when not using the form, but the user may only use Koldunism while in this form.</p>\n<p>• Skin of Geb: The user removes a point of damage from an attack after halving whenever they are struck, to a minimum of sustaining 1 damage per attack.</p>\n<p>• Vigilance of Horus: Gain a two-die bonus on all visual Awareness rolls. Additionally, their normal Awareness acts as Sense the Unseen</p>\n<p>•• Fires of Creation: The user halves and rounds up all damage from fire. By spending a point of Willpower, they may fully negate any damage from it for a turn.</p>\n<p>••••• Ra’s Usurpation: The user is immune to sunlight, but still suffers standard penalties like needing to roll to stay awake and having their dice pools limited by the Road rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until willingly ended.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"157"},"level":5,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"serpentis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.tGBLuctXBZ5qnofu"},"tHwkIH4bE0uFrdSk":{"name":"Sweat of Guilt","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By reminding a person of how low they have stooped, vizier hurls insults at their core. Vampires break down in blood sweat and mortals cower in front of the vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. the victim’s Humanity/Road rating</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The victim must be able to hear the user and have fewer points in Humanity or Road/Path rating than the user for this power to work. The vampire rolls Resolve + Quietus vs. the victim’s Road of Humanity rating. On a win, targets suffer unhalved Superficial Willpower damage equal to the margin and suffer Mental Impairment for the rest of the scene. Vampire, ghoul and revenant victims break down in blood-sweat and increase their Hunger by an amount equal to the margin as well.</p>\n<p>If the vampire is already has Hunger 5 and cannot increase their Hunger further, they must make a Hunger Frenzy Test (Difficulty of 4) as normal.</p>\n<p>Constructs without morality (such as zombies or other creatures like those of Biothaumaturgy) and demons not possessing humans are immune to the effects of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Impairment lasts for a scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"137"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"pp3HQEbj":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"KQpMnMXm":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"quietusanimae","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.tHwkIH4bE0uFrdSk"},"tWPnUzN0UXP6S3i8":{"name":"Submerged Directive","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>When using Mesmerize, the vampire can now implant a posthypnotic suggestion, allowing the command to remain dormant until a specific stimulus occurs. This trigger can be anything from a specific date, to a person (“When you meet Roland, tell him these words”), to hearing a specific phrase. The Submerged Directive never expires; people can conceivably walk around with an order buried in their mind for years. The user can only embed one suggestion per victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive with Mesmerise</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> No Additional Cost</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> As Mesmerise, though the Storyteller might want to make any rolls in secret. There’s no way of knowing if the submerged suggestion works until the conditions are met.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Indefinitely</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"73"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.tWPnUzN0UXP6S3i8"},"tXeXmXzsNFEbKQED":{"name":"Counterfeit (Level 2 Power)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>At level 3, the Alchemist can develop formulae that counterfeit <strong>level 2</strong> powers of other Disciplines (and Blood Sorcery powers that affect the caster’s own Blood, but never Rituals).</p><p>■ <strong>Research:</strong> at least one month per formula. <strong>Distillation:</strong> at least a night.</p><p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Use the counterfeited power’s own cost and system, substituting the Alchemist’s Thin-Blood Alchemy rating for the Discipline rating wherever one is called for. Rename this item after the power you counterfeit and paste in its dice pool.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"286"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.tXeXmXzsNFEbKQED"},"taxy9A1mu4s1PjeU":{"name":"Fluent Swiftness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/celerity.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire is able to call upon their supernatural speed effortlessly, and rarely tempt their Hunger when doing so.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the vampire uses a Blood Surge to augment a Dexterity or Celerity test, they may reroll the Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"43"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"celerity","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.taxy9A1mu4s1PjeU"},"th40KrNxhKzBc6X8":{"name":"Primal Scream","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>Fear is natural in all beings. Mortals cover before shadows, hide under their blankets against imaginary monsters while the mighty vampires flinch at a mere sight of an open fire. Primal Scream exploits that potential for fear and pushes it to the forefront of the victim’s psyche.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Wits + Composure</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire emits a sound to cower their victim, rolling Manipulation + Melpominee vs. the target’s Composure + Wits. On a win, the vampires and those with vampiric beasts must make a Fear Frenzy test against a Difficulty of 3. Mortals are forced to flee unless they spend a number of Willpower points equal to the users margin of win (to a minimum of one).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As per system.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"88"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hW6n5E4C":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"Nu6oFlVw":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.th40KrNxhKzBc6X8"},"u2WgMGR7bitPzrDY":{"name":"Juice Box","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>More than a few Kindred of lower generations have longed for the ability to make use of Thin-Blood Alchemy Formulae. Juice Box gives them the option to do so without excessive risk of harm or the need to keep the Mercurian around long, though it can be argued that the requirement of methuselah Blood shifts that risk onto the alchemist. For a similar formula that shifts the element of risk to the imbiber, see <em>Blood Sigils</em>, p. 80.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The ingredients required for the original formula, methuselah vitae, and an empty juice box, complete with traces of concentrated fruit juice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist distills the target formula as normal, but includes methuselah vitae in the concoction before performing additional treatment to it. Once distilled, the brew must rest in the juice box for 24 hours before activation. The result allows any Duskborn or Kindred to benefit from the brew created by the alchemist. All activation costs, dice pools, and duration apply per the original blood alchemy formula.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As target formula</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"51"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.u2WgMGR7bitPzrDY"},"uAQMqzj0Z3DDmAjM":{"name":"Reveal the Invisible Leash","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>With a simple touch, the vampire can sense who in a room their target considers themselves subordinate to.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Insight vs Composure + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire must be in physical contact with their target and test Intelligence + Insight vs their target’s Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>On a win, the vampire senses who in the room the target considers their political superior (if any). If there are multiple such individuals, they only identify the most immediate present, such as a sire whose Blood Bound the target rather than the Prince. For every 2 points of the Margin, a secondary superior is identified, moving from the target’s most immediate to the most distant.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> For as long as the vampire is touching their target</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"30"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"47TQAyL3":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"Jdlhic3K":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uAQMqzj0Z3DDmAjM"},"uFWxch9UxqNTqQV4":{"name":"Fang-Stinger","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>This poison can be injected into a mortal’s bloodstream, dealing no damage to them (through the miracle of alchemical transformation) but inflicting considerable pain on a blankbody who feeds from them. Other variations create different effects: babbling incoherence (and inability to use some Disciplines), indifferent calm, etc.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The Alchemist’s Blood, choleric human blood, foxglove, absinthe, laundry detergent or phosgene</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The victim makes a contested Stamina + Resolve roll vs. the Alchemist’s Resolve + Alchemy (7 dice). If the victim wins, the serum has no effect. For every success the Alchemist wins by, the victim takes 2 levels of Aggravated damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One day</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Second Inquisition","page":"47"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uFWxch9UxqNTqQV4"},"uGPVyRDB44Xl3is9":{"name":"Lethal Body","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/potence.png","system":{"description":"<p>Using this power, the user is capable of causing horrendous damage to mortals, tearing skin and breaking bones with bare fingers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user’s unarmed attacks can now do Aggravated Health damage to mortals, if desired. They also ignore one level of armor per Potence level of the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"116"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"potence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uGPVyRDB44Xl3is9"},"uOZfvOJQGHewhUaI":{"name":"Slow Job","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>While not able to directly interfere with the living things due to the laws of time not yet heeding the user’s commands to such an extent, the Slow Job allows a user to perform an action that would take seconds much faster — at least that is show it would look to the outsiders. From the user’s point of view, the world slows a bit, but enough for them to accomplish their task quickly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When active, this power lets the vampire complete Skill-related tasks that would otherwise take whole turns in the span of a few seconds, and can treat a full action as a minor action. The power cannot be used to speed up attacks, defenses, or to perform any other actively resisted task but it does allow the vampire to, for example, jimmy a lock and fire a gun (the latter with a two-dice penalty, as per the minor action rules.). Other such examples would be activated slaking from the Calix Secretus, reloading bows, crossbows or guns…</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"168"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uOZfvOJQGHewhUaI"},"uV2xkR225muJRtlv":{"name":"Cloak the Gathering","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can shelter their companions under the cloak of Obfuscate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of the power extended</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Subterfuge vs. Wits + Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire can extend their power of Obfuscation to a number of additional willing subjects equal to their Wits, plus one for each additional Rouse Check made. The Obfuscate power used on the group can be any known to the user, and every member of the group count as having used it on themselves, using the Obfuscating vampire’s Skill rating as their own when needed for a roll. Members of the group can still perceive each other while under the effects of the power. If anyone besides the user is revealed, either through their own doing or an astute observer, the rest of the group remains hidden. If the user is revealed, so is everyone else.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As per power augmented</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"105"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Ag0JuTqe":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"9sZkeuew":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uV2xkR225muJRtlv"},"uYsqn4E0rhBdrcRt":{"name":"Master of Forms","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/protean.png","system":{"description":"<p>While most users of Protean can assume one or two animal forms, some users of Protean have a vastly extended repertoire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Shape of the Beast</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user is no longer limited to choosing one animal form when using Shape of the Beast, Shape of the Beast’s Wrath or Swarm. Rather, they can take on the form of any creature that falls within the limits of those abilities, different each time if they so wish. Each time they choose a beast form, such as a wolf, owl, or bat, they still assume the same specific form, which may be recognised by witnesses. Thus, if the character turns into a black swan on multiple nights, it always looks like that specific black swan.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"130"},"level":4,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"protean","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uYsqn4E0rhBdrcRt"},"uZpEJIgvhvNwBVPh":{"name":"The Fogue’s Song","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>Fogue, a never-ending audio heard by all Daughters of Cacophony and users of Melpominee, both irritates and distracts those who hear it. By focusing on it, the vampire can extend this “gift” onto other. If coupled with powers that displace one’s voice such as Thrown Voice, the user may dull another’s senses to prepare for a surprise attack.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Performance vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire emits a sound that becomes stuck inside the target’s head, rolling Charisma + Performance vs. the target’s Composure + Wits. On a win, the victim takes a point of Superficial Willpower damage and suffers the bane of Daughters of Cacophony, equal to the user’s Melpominee rating +1.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene. A critical win extends the duration to the rest of the night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"86"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"gr6J2KtA":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"ssylN8B1":{"path":"skills.performance"}},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uZpEJIgvhvNwBVPh"},"usOM07Vt6CziG8ZC":{"name":"Visceral Control","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/dominate.png","system":{"description":"<p>\"The Wyndham and everything in it obeys me. Why should the flesh be any different?\" — Theo Wyndham of the Dracul While the Tzimisce are most associated with careful fleshcrafting and delicate surgical procedures, some Fiends develop their gifts in less subtle directions. This power bypasses the victim’s mind and commands their body directly, forcing nerves to fire and muscles to contract of their own accord.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Vicissitude 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Intimidation vs. Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> With a moment of eye contact and a word or gesture, the vampire gives a single command to the victim's physical body. While this can’t cause permanent damage without Terminal Decree, the effects can be quite debilitating. This power is always resisted with Stamina + Resolve against the vampire’s Manipulation + Intimidation, as fundamental instincts fight back against this sort of manipulation; vampires and ghouls with Fortitude can use that in place of either attribute.</p>\n<p>• The vampire can shut down one of their victim’s senses. This causes them to automatically fail any rolls relying on that sense, along with other effects as appropriate: someone who’s lost their sense of balance needs to roll Dexterity + Athletics (Difficulty 3) to move faster than a crawl, for example, while someone who's lost their sense of pain ignores physical impairment.</p>\n<p>• The vampire can disable one of the victim's limbs, leaving it paralysed, numb, or otherwise useless. Switching to a non-dominant hand subtracts two dice from all Strength and Dexterity rolls, while losing a leg requires a crutch or something to hold on to in order to move effectively. See Crippling Injuries for ideas.</p>\n<p>• By manipulating breathing patterns, the vampire can cause a mortal to pass out (or awaken) within about thirty seconds. Vampires don’t need to breathe to remain conscious, but can still be prevented from speaking.</p>\n<p>• Alternately, the vampire can reverse any effect imposed by this power.</p>\n<p>This power can be applied multiple times to the same victim, but each use must be done separately. If the vampire also possesses Terminal Decree, they can push a mortal’s body past its limits, causing long-term damage; disabled senses and limbs require extensive therapy or surgery to repair, while suffocation can become fatal within minutes Stopping one’s heart or brain kills one almost instantly. Otherwise, all effects fade after one scene.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"73"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Izlg51Ra":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"BlPpaLSi":{"path":"skills.intimidation"}},"discipline":"dominate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.usOM07Vt6CziG8ZC"},"uwgUavNH2Pqjb5t8":{"name":"Hide the Truth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire can hear their own blood whispering half-secrets, tactics and hints at how best to manipulate and take advantage of those around them. Many Baali have survived inquisitions and witch-hunts thanks to the ability to use the right words in a right moment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user may reroll a number of dice (including Hunger dice) on a single Insight or a verbal Subterfuge social test equal to vampire’s Daimonion rating + power bonus. A single die may be rerolled only once. Willpower rerolls happen after this, but no other power may reroll further.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One roll</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"52"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hqRI6uCK":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WmaxKU4Q":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.uwgUavNH2Pqjb5t8"},"v2PMTD7eeBGikuWJ":{"name":"Bane of Spirits","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>As a mind-influencing Discipline, Mytherceria tends not to afflict spirits, something not even the might true fae or lesser changelings are able to do. By combining the fae-Discipline with other gifts of the Blood, however, the vampire may elevate Mytherceria to such heights that not even spirits, demons or ghosts are able to shrug off its effect.s</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Daimonion/Spiritus/Ephemera or Necromancy Path 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Powers of Mytherceria now impact spirits the same way that they impact common corporeal beings. They may always roll Awareness to catch Mytherceria at work, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"93"},"level":3,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.v2PMTD7eeBGikuWJ"},"vN3P5jsvCtSeYwYr":{"name":"Putrefaction","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By touch alone, the user of Thanatosis is able to call the victim’s flesh to rot and decay, making their punches deliver damage far deeper and devastating blows.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user must touch their victim (which can be performed in combat through any combination of Strength or Dexterity + Brawl or Athletics) and make a Rouse Check. Following that, the victim takes half of the user’s Thanatosis rating rounded up as Aggravated Health damage. Additionally, the victim suffers a Crippling Injury as chosen by the user (excluding torpor/death).</p>\n<p>The user of this power should be weary so as not to touch anything by accident as it deals the damage as stated above, withering plants and accidentally killing humans by touch alone on accident.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"177"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.vN3P5jsvCtSeYwYr"},"vZLyFincs4LwG8Eh":{"name":"Fata Morgana","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/chimerstry.png","system":{"description":"<p>Fata Morgana allows the vampire to create static illusions that affect any or all of the senses. The vampire could create the illusion of a bladed weapon that feels hard to the touch, or a soft flower that gives off a sweet perfume. These are just illusions, and a sword will pass right through someone if thrust. An image created in this way can be completely stationary, as with Ignis Fatuus, or it can move naturally as an item that can be carried by a person like a sword hitched to the sword belt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Before making the roll, the user must state what senses they wish to affect. They must also choose at the time of creation if the illusion is completely stationary or if it is tied to a person, place, or animal and will remain that way for its duration. Once that has been chosen, the user rolls Manipulation + Subterfurge against a Difficulty of 2 On a Win, the user may affect a single sense with an additional sense for each margin. These illusions follow the same rules for area and creation as Ignis Fatuus illusions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The illusion remains until the vampire chooses to end the illusion or can no longer sense it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"48"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"hqRI6uCK":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"WmaxKU4Q":{"path":"skills.subterfuge"}},"discipline":"chimerstry","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.vZLyFincs4LwG8Eh"},"vylqkCnDxV2fMsg5":{"name":"Vitae MSG","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>A formula that makes consumption of blood even more pleasurable already has a built-in market. Kindred are treacherous and vindictive by nature though, so this ointment is also used to get particular vampires to feed from particular vessels—to establish Blood Bonds, make someone into a plague carrier, or set up the consumption of some other formula.</p>\n<p>Needless to say, a lot of Ventrue love this stuff.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, sanguine mortal blood, MSG, powdered sugar, knockoff (<em>not</em> genuine) Chanel No. 5.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The production process yields a clear, watery liquid that smells faintly of vanilla and jasmine. It does nothing until it is rubbed onto the skin of a person or animal, causing that being to inflame vampiric thirst. Any vampire who sees or smells anyone who has Vitae MSG on them feels an intense desire to feed on them. They can resist this urge with a point of Willpower, or by accepting an additional point of Hunger. If the vampire spends more than thirty minutes in the presence of the anointed one after that, they must feast — or else spend another Willpower, or accept more Hunger. The compulsion partially offsets the Ventrue Bane — blue bloods can feed from the person enhanced by Vitae MSG by spending 1 Willpower less if they aren’t in their preferred category.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Once someone is anointed, the desire lasts until the next dawn, if they survive.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"79"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.vylqkCnDxV2fMsg5"},"w60Q9LVHSBU68Zbr":{"name":"Kingmaker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The power called Kingmaker lets the vampire extend their Presence to another individual and grant them social graces that normally is beyond them. The most clueless brute can gain an understanding of human nature, the most obnoxious brat can transform into a perfect example of grace and virtue, and the shyest wallflower can become the charismatic heart and soul of the feast. Yet, Kingmaker is even more powerful when turned on an already competent individual. An already competent speaker can with the blessing of Kingmaker hold a speech that will be remembered for millennia. A skilled singer will be able to sing with the voice of an angel. A charming smile can be made so radiant it melts the blackest of hearts.</p>\n<p>To use this power, the vampire must be within line of sight and earshot of the target, and must remain in the target’s presence at all times.</p>\n<p>In modern era, this power is referred to as “Wingman”.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Minor -2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> As a full action and with an additional Rouse Check, the user can provide the benefits of one of their Presence powers to another willing vampire, using their own Skill ratings and Blood Potency bonus of the vampire using Wingman.</p>\n<p>If the subject also possesses Presence, use the higher of the bonuses but not both. The subject cannot gain the benefit of the same Presence power twice. The vampire using Wingman cannot use the same power on themselves while extending it to another subject. Both the user or the subject must be visible to anyone affected by the extended power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As power used.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"125"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.w60Q9LVHSBU68Zbr"},"wD9unHWOJfmvlWTb":{"name":"Resist Typhon’s Corruption","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>Typhon is the plague on this world and the skinchangers are right to refer to it as the great wyrm of corruption. Despite being struck by its curse in the form of the bloodlust and the Beast, Bardo allows the faithful of Osiris to better resist the temptations of the latter as the Beast’s frenzies can only lead to more suffering and sin.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user gains a dice bonus to resist all Frenzies equal to their Bardo rating.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"38"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.wD9unHWOJfmvlWTb"},"wNpQbOshpube5Gq8":{"name":"Draugh of Decay","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>By feeding a Rouse Check of their vitae to another vampire or a ghoul, they are able to share some of their powers of Thanatosis with them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Medicine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Drinking a Rouse Check's worth of Blood directly from the user gifts the drinker with temporary Thanatosis equal to half the Thanatosis dots (rounded down) of the donor. The drinker gains the same powers as the donor’s, up to that level.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One night; for vampires, until the next feeding or the vampire reaches Hunger 5.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"180"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"MEjoN5vE":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"oqGrUUwK":{"path":"skills.medicine"}},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.wNpQbOshpube5Gq8"},"wRHxHcDPg3OLriuq":{"name":"Possession","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power the vampire can strip the will of a mortal and completely possess their body, using it as their own. While the mind of the subject remains hidden to the vampire, they can do anything and go anywhere the subject could while the power remains active. Using this, a vampire can even experience the sunlight, food, and physical sexuality long denied them, their host paying the price for whatever abuse the vampire wreaks on their body while riding it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Dominate •••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Wits + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power can only be used on human mortals. If the mortal is a ghoul, they must first be Blood Bound to the user. Before possession can begin, the vampire must have eye contact with their victim (See Dominate, p. XX). The user then engages in a Resolve + Awareness vs Resolve + Intelligence conflict with the victim in order to inhabit their body. If the vampire’s player rolls a total failure, the victim becomes immune to further Possession attempts for the duration of the story.</p>\n<p>Once the vampire inhabits the body of their victim, their own body falls into a torpor-like trance, completely unaware of their surrounding and their own physical state except for Aggravated damage, which breaks the trance and ends the effects. A vampire possessing a mortal can use Auspex, Presence, Dementation and Dominate through them, and other Disciplines at storyteller’s discretion like sorcery. If the user wishes to extend Possession into daytime, they must make a roll to stay awake (p. 219). Failure to stay awake ends the power. Any Aggravated damage to the subject also risks ending the possession – the user must succeed at a Resolve + Insight roll (Difficulty 2 + damage taken) to stay in control. If the subject dies during Possession, the resulting spiritual trauma immediately causes the user to sustain three levels of Aggravated damage to Willpower.</p>\n<p>This power does not give the user the ability to read the mind, use the skills, or emulate the manners of the victim. Any skills employed use the possessing vampire’s rating. The user must make a Manipulation + Performance vs Wits + Insight roll to successfully impersonate the victim’s manners, expressions, and the like.</p>\n<p>Finally, Possession violates the victim even more profoundly than a Blood Bond. The Storyteller should consider awarding Stains for this action for those who possess Humanity at certain levels.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until ended, voluntarily or involuntarily.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"35"},"level":5,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"myM695rE":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"O1sGpBlJ":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.wRHxHcDPg3OLriuq"},"wWBtXrgxBGFUKaJ4":{"name":"Slow Grace","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s perceptions of time permanently alter and they perceive everything a bit slower, giving them an edge in making critical moves to maintain balance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user automatically passes any Dexterity- or Athletics-based roll needed to keep their balance. Note that this power does not allow them to balance on support that cannot take their weight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"167"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.wWBtXrgxBGFUKaJ4"},"woSGamJMnagv5GU3":{"name":"Pillar of Osiris","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/bardo.png","system":{"description":"<p>By consecrating and purifying grounds, the user of Bardo is able to erect a spiritual and immaterial monument to Osiris, empowering users of Bardo in the area. It is expected that every temple of Osiris at least has this power to strengthen it against attacks.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Two Rouse Checks</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user must write out a prayer to Osiris on the floor they wish to consecrate with the effect only effecting an area of about a two-story house. After writing out a prayer, they must roll Resolve + Occult at a Difficulty of 3 to fulfil the consecration.</p>\n<p>All of those who possess at least one dot in Bardo benefit from the Pillar of Osiris, gaining a dice-bonus on using all Bardo powers or resisting supernatural powers within the area of effect equal to the caster’s Bardo rating halved and rounded up.</p>\n<p>The user may only have one Pillar of Osiris active, requiring reconsecration every time they desire to move their Pillar of Osiris somewhere else.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Until another area is consecrated with the Pillar of Osiris or until the caster perishes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"40"},"level":4,"cost":2,"dicepool":{"Shk0kIQY":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"6Fnh0YvS":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"bardo","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.woSGamJMnagv5GU3"},"wp2VMEu0ChEvXfQg":{"name":"Entracement","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire focuses their unnatural allure on a single person, instilling in them a rapt fascination or infatuation akin to falling head over heels in love or meeting one’s lifelong idol. The person affected does their best to r emain in the vampire’s good graces, but stops short of causing themselves or their other loved ones physical harm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Charisma + Persuasion vs. Composure + Wits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire only needs to gain the subject’s attention and win a contest of Charisma + Insight vs Composure + Wits. The effect lasts for roughly one hour plus one per point of margin on the win. The vampire can renew this effect indefinitely, but a failure ends the effect and makes the subject immune for the rest of the night.</p>\n<p>After succeeding, the user adds dice equal to their Presence rating to any Social dice pool against the entranced subject. Requests resulting in obvious harm to the subject or their loved ones, or that oppose the subject’s Road Tenets or Ethics, fail and require an immediate power contest roll as above, or the Entrancement immediately fails.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One hour plus one per point of margin</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"123"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"rhOOlBeQ":{"path":"attributes.charisma"},"H3tYrnYm":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.wp2VMEu0ChEvXfQg"},"xNJSPTe5PTQJ3THr":{"name":"Ending the Watch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Warriors trace their origin to Samiel’s ancient fury in the days after the Second City. Yet ancient tales record the Salubri of Enoch walking the streets, laying hands upon the terminally ill and freeing their souls from mortality’s coil. In return, the Salubri gained both wisdom and strength of will. Despite their anger, the Warriors retain the fundamentally peaceful disposition of Clan Salubri, and this power reflects their hewing to the clan’s original nature. When acting as death watch, the third eye’s iris flares gold, absorbing the essence of the mortal’s departing soul into the Salubri’s own.</p>\n<p>This power may only be used on a dying mortal (Physically Impared, but not yet dead) or one who truly wishes death without coercion. Generally, the mortally wounded or the elderly are targets of this power, but those of a deeply melancholic temperament or torture victims are equally viable. Tremere whisper rumors of unforgiving Warriors afflicting captives with the Burning Touch, then taking their souls when they beg for death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check, one Willpower point</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may only be used on non-undead who are either willing to die or are physically impaired. The rolls any combination of Strength or Dexterity + Brawl or Athletics to touch another who may resist by dodging (neither pool is improved by power bonus). A win instantly kills the target. Curiously, subjects of this power never return as ghosts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Instant</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"190"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xNJSPTe5PTQJ3THr"},"xXZa9ghLPoHocsFT":{"name":"Telepathy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>At the higher levels of Auspex the vampire can now literally read minds, as well as project their own thoughts into the minds of others. While reading a mortal mind is relatively straightforward, undead minds requires a higher effort to penetrate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check (plus one Willpower vs non-consenting vampires or other supernaturals)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Insight vs. Wits + Subterfuge</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user is not required to roll any dice to project their thoughts to another, vampire or mortal, though they do require line of sight. To read the mind of a mortal within line of sight, roll Resolve + Insight vs. Wits + Subterfuge while looking into their eyes. (Unless the other consents, in which case no roll is required.). On a win, the character can detect one surface per point of margin. Some examples include:</p>\n<p>• What are you currently thinking about?</p>\n<p>• What does that person or thing you just described look like?</p>\n<p>• Where is the person or object you were just talking about located?</p>\n<p>• Do you like or dislike the person you’re currently talking to?</p>\n<p>• What are you planning to do in the next few minutes?</p>\n<p>• Is there another entity or consciousness in this body?</p>\n<p>When it comes to probing for distant memories, one distant memory or thought counts as two surface thoughts:</p>\n<p>• Where is your Haven/home?</p>\n<p>• Who is your sire?</p>\n<p>• Where is your sire now?</p>\n<p>• Who is your Touchstone (one Touchstone per memory)?</p>\n<p>• Where is your Touchstone now (one Touchstone per memory)?</p>\n<p>• What do you believe in (one Ethic per memory)?</p>\n<p>• Who was your first love?</p>\n<p>Discipline powers may be used through Telepathy if the voice in someone’s head and eye contact fulfil the conditions (such as Dominate or Dementation). While it can be used subtly by being silent and simply reading thoughts and memories, rolling a Messy Critical or a Bestial Failure automatically reveals to the victim that something is off. While mortals will probably brush it off, those in the know that supernatural exists will know that something is poking around their brain. In such cases, Wits + Occult (Difficulty 3) allows a person to realise the source of mental intrusion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Roughly one minute per Rouse Check. Increased to a full scene on consenting subjects.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"34"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"myM695rE":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"O1sGpBlJ":{"path":"skills.insight"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xXZa9ghLPoHocsFT"},"xaKbaL74ogEPjbqp":{"name":"Meat Shields","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>In the company of weak mortals (the sick or dying, children, those physically wounded i.e. Physically Impared) the vampire feels its Blood jump and flow energetically, its flesh strengthening by contrast with their inferior vigor. Even a wounded kine has their uses, it seems. Especially a wounded kine. Some vampires keep sick or wounded blood dolls for the extra rush this Power grants them. They must be careful not to make the ailments so serious as to require vampires Blood to heal, however — once a mortal has tasted vitae, they can no longer serve as Meat Shields in this way.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> With this Power active, the vampire temporarily increases their Fortitude by a number of dots equal to half the weak mortals present (rounded down). They can gain up to five extra dots of Fortitude through this method. This increase does not provide any new Powers, but counts for the purpose of dice pools and other effects.</p>\n<p>The Storyteller counts all bystanders categorised as Weak Mortals (toward this power, along with wounded (Physically Impaired) or sick Average Mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, or when the vampire leaves the presence of the affected mortals — whichever comes first.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"84"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xaKbaL74ogEPjbqp"},"xeT1yarwl7lC4LRG":{"name":"Armour of Caine’s Fury","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/valeren-warrior.png","system":{"description":"<p>The third eye opens, blazing redder than the setting sun. This radiance spreads along the Salubri’s desiccated veins in a flash until their entire body is enveloped in a web of radiant light that defies wounding. The Cyclopes consider this (and their talent at Fortitude) to be the chief reasons they are so feared in war. Ventrue have also expressed envy of this power, and have invited Salubri into their domains to teach them Valeren in exchange for patronage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Stamina + Resolve (Difficulty 2). For each point of margin, the user gains a point of Armour (stacking with any worn armour) and for each two points of margin (rounded down) the user gains a bonus die on rolls to resist being mentally influenced or cowed, including Fear Frenzy.</p>\n<p>Maximum armour rating of the user is increased to 6 + Blood Surge value. This includes worn armour, so a vampire wearing a set of armour with 6 Armour rating and Blood Potency 1 could have a maximum armour rating of 8.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"193"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"U601d5Cg":{"path":"attributes.stamina"},"uKH9LGpg":{"path":"attributes.resolve"}},"discipline":"valerenwarrior","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xeT1yarwl7lC4LRG"},"xn888xl5cb3hSWbW":{"name":"TLC","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>The acronym stands for <em>This Lick Cares</em> or possibly <em>Tastes Like Chicken</em>. This formula creates a powder that imbues blood of living animals with the mystical qualities of mortal blood that the Beast craves. For vampires looking to avoid feeding on mortals as their blood strengthens, this formula is a must. This powder commonly appears in any big redworking scene’s black market.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The alchemist’s Blood, animal blood, choleric mortal blood, nettles, iron pieces, meat glue.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The alchemist creates (or for other Distillations, they or their vessel bleed and must sift out) a bluish-brown powder. Feeding the powder to an animal allows a vampire to feed on the animal as if it were a human. For every success on the Distillation roll, the alchemist can enrich enough animal blood to slake one point of Hunger, although only the largest dogs can slake more than 1 point of Hunger without dying. Horses, cattle, and other large mammals contain enough blood to slake 8–10 points of Hunger, if it’s dosed with TLC. Killing an animal under the effects of TLC cannot slake a vampire’s Hunger to 0.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood Sigils","page":"77"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xn888xl5cb3hSWbW"},"xoSrTH3ouZX9s1JS":{"name":"Taste of the Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>By sipping blood, those capable of listening to the Blood are able to find out more about the mental constitution and ability of the person</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user gains a die bonus to all Mental Skill-based rolls against a person whose blood they tasted (even a bite attack provides enough of a sample). For example, Awareness, useful in detecting ambushes as their body innately learns patterns of movement or Investigation, detecting traces and workings of another.</p>\n<p>The user does not benefit from this if they aren’t aware that the blood came from the person, meaning that masked identities or completely unexpected ambushes can negate the benefit.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"145"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xoSrTH3ouZX9s1JS"},"xwuvQRIG2xbQUzfR":{"name":"Unholy Mission","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/daimonion.png","system":{"description":"<p>Few on this planet are as equipped to destroy and control the infernal as the Baali themselves, and this power is a perfect example of that. First developed by the Molochim, and now readily spread throughout Baali across the globe. This Unholy Mission of control and domination is one stretching back to the very progenitors of the clan, when Nergal first reached up to the Outer Dark in an attempt to claim the powers of the Sleepers for himself. While the Nergali use this power to better control the demons they summon, the Order of Moloch use it primarily to combat other Baali, in addition to any demons foolish enough to make themselves known to them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Fortitude 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire gains a dice bonus to resist temptations of sin and infernalism as well as a dice bonus to defend against the powers of Daimonion, Dark Thaumaturgy, Spectre Arcanoi, Fomori powers and demonic powers be they Lores or Arcana.</p>\n<p>As usual, the bonus does not stack with other powers such as Unswayable Mind which comes from one’s own mental fortitude while Daimonion fuels the vampire with a purpose.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> Passive</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"54"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"daimonion","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xwuvQRIG2xbQUzfR"},"xzI35yXq4yqZusBW":{"name":"Marduk’s Rattle","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/quietus.png","system":{"description":"<p>The sorcerers have developed Marduk’s Rattle — a variant of Dagon’s Call that ravages the mind of the person.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full to activate the power. Applying blood depends on the method</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve + Awareness vs. Composure + Occult (or Fortitude)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Identical to Dagon’s Call (p. 123 save for it dealing Superficial Willpower damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The user’s vitae remains potent in the victim’s system for a week or until the user has activated the power 3 times, using up all of their blood within the victim. This limit is shared with Dagon’s Call.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"147"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"trBgcJbZ":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"oQXs7Xfy":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"quietussanguinis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.xzI35yXq4yqZusBW"},"y1rgJrXBee1FZ30o":{"name":"Star Magnetism","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/presence.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Presence powers of the user now effect people viewing them on live feeds or hearing them over the phone. Recorded images or messages do not retain the effect, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Passive</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One additional Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Awe, Daunt, and Entrancement can be transmitted through live feeds via screens or phones. If Entrancement is used, the victim’s name must be spoken clearly, as that power only affects one person at a time. Anyone else viewing the same transmission only finds the user charming, but not supernaturally so.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> As power used</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"126"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"presence","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.y1rgJrXBee1FZ30o"},"yGBaYV38Ma5W48WD":{"name":"Quell the Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/animalism.png","system":{"description":"<p>By locking eyes with a target, the vampire cows their inner Beast into temporary slumber. Mortals affected thus become apathetic, unable to take any actions other than to stay alive, while vampires’ bestial urges temporarily abate, for better or worse.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion/Animal Ken/Intimidation vs Stamina + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Roll Manipulation + Persuasion vs Stamina + Resolve of a mortal target within the line of sight or Manipulation + Animal Ken against Stamina + Resolve of an animal within the light of sight. A win against a target incapacitates them for that scene, instilling severe lethargy and preventing them from moving or acting. They will not even act to preserve themselves unless they spend a point of Willpower in order to act for a turn. A win against a vampire prevents the target from performing Blood Surges. While their Beast is quelled, vampires do not score messy criticals. Against vampires, this power lasts a turn plus a number of turns equal to the win margin on the contest. A critical win or at least three margins against a vampire target also ends their frenzy.</p>\n<p>The Tzimisce call this power “Cowing the Beast”. Their variant of their power tends instills the target with fear instead of lethargy, but it is more or less identical except that it uses Intimidation instead of Persuasion or Animal Ken.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, or a number of turns equal to the test margin plus one.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"18"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"OnE81Fwf":{"path":"attributes.manipulation"},"vfMiJ9EI":{"path":"skills.persuasion"}},"discipline":"animalism","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.yGBaYV38Ma5W48WD"},"ySvlFFGJCJKT5aVN":{"name":"Muffle the Steps","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obfuscate.png","system":{"description":"<p>Popular among the Banu Haqim who not yet mastered similar gifts from Quietus, this power completely silences the user, nullifying all sound made by them. As with other Obfuscate powers, this only works on people within earshot and does not fool microphones or other electronic sound detectors. Unlike Obfuscate in general, this power works only on the sense of hearing, but in exchange operates more robustly. A vampire needs to make a whole lot of noise to break this silence.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> See general rules for Obfuscate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user silences their footsteps, clothing, minor collisions, and other sounds of their person. This makes the vampire undetectable if an observer could only notice them by sound, such as when on a different floor of a house. The power gives the user a two-dice bonus to appropriate Stealth tests.</p>\n<p>This power does not eliminate sounds the user makes outside their personal space (throwing or dropping objects, or slamming doors, for example). Failing that, only powers such as Sense of the Unseen (Auspex 1) can detect the user. If such powers succeed, the two-dice bonus to Stealth is nullified against the one who managed to see right through the power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or ended by the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"97"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"obfuscate","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ySvlFFGJCJKT5aVN"},"yhe0FJflcfMY4CLb":{"name":"Sing, O Muse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/melpominee.png","system":{"description":"<p>Users of Melpominee are often excellent singers, poets and others who have mastered the voice. With Sing o Muse, the vampire’s voice becomes liquid as milk and sweet as honey to the listeners, enchanting them with supernatural skill.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character gains a dice-bonus on all of their Charisma Performance rolls equal to their Melpominee rating. This does not stack with effects such as Awe as it distracts the listener from the person’s voice, nor does it improve other Melpominee powers as per Stacking rule.</p>\n<p>While the power is invisible to most means of detection, powers such as Scry the Soul may reveal that others are under the effects of this power. Sense the Unseen could roll against the user’s Wits + Performance for detection. but the user must speak in a poetic style, recite verses or sing to make use of this power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One Scene,</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"85"},"level":1,"cost":0,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"melpominee","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.yhe0FJflcfMY4CLb"},"yiHe34HY6JOinHtg":{"name":"Measure of Will","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/auspex.png","system":{"description":"<p>A vampire with this power may gauge the strength of a target’s resolve and identity, often as an exploratory prelude to shattering and reshaping that mind.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Resolve+ Awareness</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire rolls Resolve + Insight against the Composure + Resolve of a target within the line of sight. The target does not sense any mental intrusion or foreign malevolence unless the roll botches. If the vampire wins, they may precisely gauge the rating of one of the following Traits for each point on the margin: the sum of Composure + Resolve, current Willpower and whether or not the target adheres to a certain Virtue (successfuly gauging a Virtue the character adheres to reveals their Road rating). The vampire may use this power multiple times in succession to gain further information. Each failed attempt subtracts two dice from subsequent rolls for the rest of the night. Obviously, the vampire does not perceive such ephemeral concepts as will and valor in terms of numerical quantities. The vampire simply compares the target’s force of mind with theirown psyche. The player may interpret this information more accurately according to the mechanics of the game.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene, although it can be extended by an additional scene by spending a point of Willpower</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"26"},"level":2,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"aKntVY6V":{"path":"attributes.resolve"},"i3n5gHkP":{"path":"skills.awareness"}},"discipline":"auspex","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.yiHe34HY6JOinHtg"},"ytdIAOkhChmcJjx5":{"name":"From Ashes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/thanatosis.png","system":{"description":"<p>Using this rare power, the user is able to instantly turn into ash as if they have been burned by the Sun, allowing them to escape certain death and even become immune to sunlight. As many have not even heard of this ability, many assailants will simply think the vampire dead and move on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The transformation into a small pile of ash takes a full turn. Once transformed, the user possesses no senses whatsoever other than touch. The user has no ability to move their ashes except to keep it together (otherwise winds would pose quite a risk). If the ashes are split, the highest amount of ashes carries the user’s concentration and upon reforming the user takes appropriate damage as stated by the storyteller (losing half of their mass should check half of their Health with Aggravated damage). While in this form, the vampire is immune to all forms of damage except fire and supernatural powers at the storyteller’s discretion. Small size gives the vampire a +3 dice bonus to avoiding detection (not improved by power bonus).</p>\n<p>Reforming from a heap of ashes requires a single turn. If the user had been placed into a container, they dramatically smash it if they are physically strong enough.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The transformation can only be ended by the user willingly ending it, the user awakening from daysleep or torpor or by special magics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"177"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"thanatosis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.ytdIAOkhChmcJjx5"},"z1wsp5qpV9J3DVWE":{"name":"Fires of the Abyss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/obtenebration.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire summons shadowy extensions from unlit spots in the area, within line of sight. Local shadows distort as they flow across walls and floors and converge on one or more hapless victims. Whether by gliding up the body of the victim or engaging in a mystic grapple with the victim’s own shadow, the arms are able to hold them in place or smother them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Daimonion 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Wits + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user summons extensions of shadow tentacles wreathed in flame of shadow. Additional arms can be created by splitting the dice pool, enabling the user to engage multiple opponents. The vampire is able to use the shadows to attack as a full action, using their Wits + Brawl to attack and deal Superficial damage or grapple. Arms deal additional Aggravated Health damage from supernatural fire equal to half the user’s Daimonion rating rounded up.</p>\n<p>The arms can defend their user with a dice pool of Wits + Brawl. This is handled as defending normally, dealing no damage with these rolls and applying a cumulative one-die penalty to each defense. Tentacles can hit incorporeal beings as shadows tend to penetrate even into spiritual realms.</p>\n<p>These tentacles cannot grasp or hold anything or anyone as fire merely passes through them (although it is still vulnerable to “real” fire and sunlight as Arms of Ahriman). if they linger on a flammable object, it is set on fire that burns as normal. The extrusions have a length (in yards/ meters) equal to twice the Obtenebration dots of the user. (Note that they, being shadows, move across surfaces, not air, and any distances must take this into account.)</p>\n<p>This power does not benefit from power bonus because the extensions of shadow are physical objects.</p>\n<p>Combined with Solid Shadows, attacks deal an additional point of Aggravated fire damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene or until ended or destroyed</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"109"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"kuONFfqm":{"path":"attributes.wits"},"Vr4A83oo":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"obtenebration","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.z1wsp5qpV9J3DVWE"},"z6030VvAq8Au4J1c":{"name":"Leaden Movement","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/temporis.png","system":{"description":"<p>With a gesture, the vampire may now lessen time’s flow to a mere trickle, causing those under the influence of this power to perceive time’s passage in a dizzying blur. Their own perception slows to a crawl despite events occurring normally around them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> Intelligence + Occult vs. Wits + Resolve</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This power may be activated defensively before a dodge roll and requires a hand gesture (Wits + Occult against the user’s Wits + Subterfuge might detect something unusual). The user rolls Intelligence + Occult vs. Wits + Resolve of the target (no larger than a man on horseback. Closely grouped objects of a similar size and nature count as a single object ). On a win, victim’s current speed and damage dealt are reduced by half, to a minimum of 1. Any margin beyond the first is applied as a dice penalty on Dexterity, Wits and Strength.</p>\n<p>As this power alters perceptions, not physics, powers such as Unswayable Mind may aid the subject.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One turn for every two margins, rounded up. The effect is broken when a character suffers damage</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"169"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"Fd8ZLBcY":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"dYDrTI34":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"temporis","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.z6030VvAq8Au4J1c"},"zIozTbcRbG5C8YK6":{"name":"Penitent Resilience","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/fortitude.png","system":{"description":"<p>With this power, a Salubri may briefly walk in the brightest sunlight without fear of injury. His purity defends him, allowing the sun to burn the Curse within and leave the flesh unmarred. Some Salubri believe a truly penitent vampire may use this power to burn out the Curse of Caine entirely and regain his mortality, but such legends are anecdotal at best.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Amalgam:</strong> Valeren •••</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Free</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire may reflexively spend one Rouse Check whenever the vampire is exposed to sunlight. For the Duration, sunlight deals no damage to the character. Instead, roll the vampire’s Bane Severity. Each die that rolls a success (two 10s do not double into 4; there are no Criticals on this roll) burns a Rouse Check’s worth of vampire’s Blood. rather than inflicting actual wounds. Once the vampire runs out of Blood and reaches Hunger 5, they suffer sunlight damage as normal. Only Cainites who ascribe to the Virtues of Conscience and Self-Control may benefit from this power. If not using our Roads and Humanity homebrew, that means Humanity and other humane beliefs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> One scene</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"82"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"fortitude","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.zIozTbcRbG5C8YK6"},"zTj3gSCAAn2uSYC8":{"name":"Chemically-Induced Flashback","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p>By melting down a vampire’s possessions (the more important the better) during Ashe creation, an alchemist infuses the drug with memories from the destroyed vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Concoct Ashe</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A melted-down object belonging to the destroyed vampire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Activation Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check in addition to the cost of using Ashe</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> When using Concoct Ashe, this formula may be used to imbue remains with the memories of the vampire they once formed. When consumed, this altered Ashe provokes a Willpower test at a Difficulty of 1 + the destroyed vampire’s Blood Potency. On a win the user immediately enters a Memoriam (see Vampire: The Masquerade p. 311), reliving an important memory of the destroyed vampire’s past rather than their own. This scene is resolved normally using their own stats, but the shifted perspective will be valuable. If they are meditating in a place of calm at the time they consume the Ashe, the thin-blood adds a one-die bonus to all dice pools during the Memoriam.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Cults of the Blood Gods","page":"46"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"alchemy","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.zTj3gSCAAn2uSYC8"},"zodnALJcj1EHi9WH":{"name":"Ink Trace","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/mytherceria.png","system":{"description":"<p>By brushing their hand against writing, a drawing or something printed out in ink; the shapes the ink took are remembered in the Blood of the user. An ink stain shows on the user’s palm, and can be manipulated to perfectly replicate the remembered trace onto another surface. This can be often used for forgery of signatures or other valuable documents.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Cost:</strong> One Rouse Check to replicate</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Dice Pools:</strong> N/A</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon inspection, the replicating ink seems perfectly normal, however tests for the composition of the ink will reveal that it is made out of blood, although it cannot identify who’s, due to the blood’s altered state, and it has no sympathetic link.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Duration:</strong> The ink is permanent.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"91"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{},"discipline":"mytherceria","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.disciplines.Item.zodnALJcj1EHi9WH"},"02swPMg6EXyJ5xtz":{"name":"Reflections of Hollow Revelation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The mystic smears black liquid (usually ink or tar) onto a reflective surface like a mirror or a crystal ball or mixes it with water to the point where it can no longer be seen throug. On a successful Ritual Test, the tainted object/liquid becomes a fixed window upon any object, place, or person that the caster has previously personally witnessed. This window remains open for the remainder of the scene, or until the target leaves the vicinity of the window.</p>\n<p>Any character with Obtenebration or with abilities that supernaturally augment senses, such as Sense the Unseen, may roll against the sorcerer’s own Ritual Test successes to detect the watchful gaze of the shadows around them</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> Maintaining the window requires concentration, however; the caster loses two dice from Awareness dice pools as long as the window is active.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"255"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.02swPMg6EXyJ5xtz"},"0WYPkEyFiIWLnXSL":{"name":"Gaze of Rigor Mortis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Decay Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The user’s eyes turn into portals to the deepest pits of the Underworld.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Mortis/Nihilistics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Parts of a cadaver</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer mixes a piece of a cadaver into a drink (usually of blood, as vampires expel anything else) and drinks it</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the sorcerer may spend a point of Willpower and look at a target, rolling Composure + Resolve against their Stamina + Resolve. On a win, the victim is locked in place and may take no action for as long as the sorcerer maintains their gaze (-2 dice penalty). Undead victims halve their Dexterity rating (round up) instead.</p>\n<p>Mortals may break out of this effect by overstraining themselves, taking</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the user parlyses themselves for an hour, increased to the rest of the night for non-vampire sorcerers. The sorcerer may break out early if they spend a point of Willpower for each 1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"280"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.0WYPkEyFiIWLnXSL"},"0apYi3SCukox76ab":{"name":"Retarding Age","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>A regimen of drugs or potions that slows aging. For each year of life on this regimen, the user only ages one month. If they ever go off of it, however, their age catches up at a rate of one month per day until they die or resume the regimen.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.0apYi3SCukox76ab"},"0engH2bSdLicvvq4":{"name":"Corruption (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Path of Corruption is a Path that focuses on mental and spiritual corruption of others to slowly twist them into what the user wishes.</p>\n<p>While it has neither the strength of Dominate nor the “positive” effects of Presence, it is a potent tool for those seeking to turn virtues to sins or relish at moral collapse of their rival.</p>\n<p>The origins in this Path are shrouded in mystery with three theories at the front.</p>\n<p>First proposes that Tremere brought it over into undeath from their mortal days as mages. Second states that it was taught to Tremere by a demons which would, obvious-sly, be a problem if it was true as consorting with the infernal is a death sentence in most domains. Third is perhaps even worse – that the Path was taught to the Warlocks by the Followers of Set seeking to corrupt them.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Dice penalties represent the constant struggle against certain urges and intrusive thoughts. If the Storyteller wants, they can replace penalties with Willpower tests equal to half their difficulty, rounded up, in order to force players to roleplay out the effects of the Path of Corruption.</p>\n<p>Targets resist the effects of this Path using Wits + Composure, or alternatively with Composure + Resolve if they are aware of the sorcerer’s nature or intentions. Each time the victim wakes up they may retake the failed test by spending a point of Willpower. If multiple effects are active, they overcome effects that were created with fewer successes than the victim rolled.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Manipulating Emotions, Sweettalking</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</p>\n<p>Alternatively, Duration (counted as higher by one), Number of Targets, Sympathetic Connection and the Special Effects below without the need for physical presence of the caster.:</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The snake bites itself; the sorcerer suffers the same effect they intended for their targets on a Botch.</p>\n<p>Path of Corruption rarely has a need for rituals given its ability to deliver its effects through sympathetic connection on range. It does not mean that rituals of this Path do not exist, however.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"278"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.0engH2bSdLicvvq4"},"0nKuk8jtoep0BRLl":{"name":"Eyes of the Dead (Nagaraja)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>Perceiving the hand of entropy like ghosts, the caster can see if someone is injured, diseased, or dying, as well as whether the individual labors under any sort of curse or baleful magic. Sometimes, the caster may even see hints of how the person will die. Cracks in structures are also shown, making it easier to demolish them. Add one die to pools to physically harm someone or something. Gain a two-dice bonus on estamating the density of the Shroud.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"331"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.0nKuk8jtoep0BRLl"},"0vhbTG0ZT7pH2cOl":{"name":"Endless Bound","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The only problem with the Path of Conveyance is that it is limited by Aspects for travelling. With this ritual, the user is able to go anywhere they want.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Passengers, Travel Speed</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A feather</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer burns the feather and inhales the smoke, taking on the property of birds to fly</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual works (other than the time required) like a typical use of the Path, except that Distance is replaced by the Ritual’s Special Effect Aspect and that the user can travel as far as they want within the same planet (or same realm, if somehow used in the Umbra or stranger places).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Same as the Path Spell</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"278"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.0vhbTG0ZT7pH2cOl"},"0zBp2fHxvvwwr8Zy":{"name":"Perspeculor","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Using this ritual on a skull gives the caster the complete skillset of the individual it belonged to, including exceptional skills, specialties and attributes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Tartets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Water or wine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer pours water into the shallowings of a skull or a severed head and drinks it. Other liquids possible, and ancient Cappadocians prefer wine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 3), the sorcerer learns of capabilities of the previous skull’s “owner”: Attributes, Skills and Specialties of the character with great accuracy.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.0zBp2fHxvvwwr8Zy"},"16bUdgaYnQ5k2Vtj":{"name":"Preserve Cadaver","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>All necromancers using cadavers for their necromantic arts, or for eating them, want to prevent the stink from giving away their Haven or the cadaver to rot beyond use for their arts. This Ritual was developed b</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A mix of spices and herbs from the area, salt and caster’s blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer grounds the plants and mixes them with salt and their own blood before applying the mixture on a cadaver.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The character’s player makes a Ritual roll (Difficulty 2), and on a Win the cadaver drives up and is preserved until destroyed. If a fresh cadaver is preserved, it will later slake Hunger like a normal human and not bagged blood. Hunger slaked through this cadaver does not count as draining and killing a target and blood will yield no Resonance,. Mindless Corpses will not suffer rotting damage if preserved through this Ritual and can be permanently bought as an Background.</p>\n<p>By spending a point of Willpower per subject, the necromancer may set the Duration to indefinite, removing the need for it as an Aspect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"343"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.16bUdgaYnQ5k2Vtj"},"1ElWw7n3rQEDPqDb":{"name":"Mass Hysteria","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fear Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>One of the most horrific rituals ever to have been created, Mass Hysteria extends the fearmongering effects of this Path into a large radius. In the War of Princes, this was often used to paralyse entire cities with fear and halt the economy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration, Fear Intensity</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Additional Rouse Checks equal to the Distance Aspect that cannot be rerolled</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer prepares a small pool of blood and call upon ancestral horror within it to be unleashed</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual has a cost of Rouse Checks equal to the rank of the Distance Aspect that cannot be rerolled in addition to the activation Rouse Check. On a win on the Ritual Test, everybody except the caster and those excluded by using their blood within the ritual. Resistance is done by Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>As usual, this ritual has an aura detectable by powers like Sense Magic which makes it extremely risky to do in places where law and order are supposed to be ensured by the local Cainites.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Same as the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"308"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.1ElWw7n3rQEDPqDb"},"1NiaokhivUTEhm33":{"name":"Exedo Animum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>Access the skull's remote memories and relive them as if they were your own. Upon a successful spell test the caster obtains vivid visions of a memory, with greater margin leading to greater clarity and immersion. The caster may search for a particular memory if aware of what they are searching for, however if their objective is too vague or they don't have a particular memory in mind, the storyteller may choose what they obtain. These memories allows for a greater understanding of the victim, letting the faster gain an extra 3 dice to any Performance or Subterfuge roll made to impersonate the victim. Especially useful for Obfuscate disguises.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.1NiaokhivUTEhm33"},"1hsEuLWX8LKOjMsv":{"name":"Writ of Passage","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>A sorcerer probably does not want their guest to be shocked by just about every protective spell they set within their home, not to mention that the standard way of excluding people from effects of wards by using their biological material becomes useless pretty quickly to the Tremere as their chantries constantly welcome new apprentices. Writ of Passage is a ritual as old as this Path and it involves creation of a written “document” that allows a person to pass through wards of the sorcerer.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled),</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write on and something to write with.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist writes down a permission and as many copies as there are Number of Targets. Once the ritual is done, they hand over copies of the permission while typically retaining the “original” copy for themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Win on the Ritual Test allows a target carrying the copy of the permission to pass unharmed through the wards the sorcerer designated at the creation of the permission (any and all of the currently active wards can be selected as well as the ones the sorcerer will make in the future).</p>\n<p>Should the original permission be destroyed, all copies of it will be mystically destroyed (clay tablets crack, paper sets on fire.) and permission reworked. A sorcerer cannot change permissions once the ritual is over except by revoking them through destroying the original permission.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> This ritual is far too simple to have unintended consequences.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"295"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.1hsEuLWX8LKOjMsv"},"1qfNywuZD5KHLDzv":{"name":"Locating the Oubliette","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The ritualist mixes a dark liquid (usually ink or tar) with their own blood before smearing it across their eyes. On successful casting, the vampire is able to see a black line one kilometer (2/3 of a mile) long (with an additional kilometer per margin on the Ritual Test), leading to the nearest Oubliette if one exists within range.</p>\n<p>This line remains visible to the Abyss Mystic for one night, with each non-rerollable Rouse Check spent during the ritual adding another night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> Until the mystic arrives or the ritual’s duration ends, the sorcerer loses a die from all rolls not directly made to travel to the Oubliette as fast as possible as the Abyss continually beckons them.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"252"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.1qfNywuZD5KHLDzv"},"1rtts7VYcjskazMs":{"name":"Saint Vitus’ Dance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Mastery of the Mortal Shell Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>The infamous St. Vitus’ Dance, also known as Dance Mania and more famously as the Dancing Plague, was an event during which men, women and children alike danced until they dropped dead. Nobody knows why that happened for certain with some diagnosing it as a mass-hysteria-induced neurological problem, but the Inquisition had noted that there were cases when some dancers made it to the statue of Saint Vitus and were healed – hence the name. With this ritual, the sorcerer cannot create an epidemic, but can replicate the symptoms of the so-called Dancing Plague.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets, Sympathetic Connection. Alternatively, Duration and Distance (as a radius).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to fulfil the Sympathetic Connection Aspect with and a physical representation of the victim(s), usually a doll. If using Distance, the sorcerer needs only imagine the area.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer focuses on the representations of their victims and casts a spell similar to normal usage of the Path. If using Distance, the sorcerer bewitches the people in the area.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The victims resist using Stamina + Occult (or Fortitude). On the sorcerer’s win, the victims begin to dance and can take no physical actions other than direct themselves where to dance to and they can still dodge. Victims may retake the resistance roll at each sunrise with one success being subtracted from the roll each time, to a minimum equal to their Mastery of the Mortal Shell rating.</p>\n<p>Vampires suffering Saint Vitus’ Dance dance even when in torpor or in daysleep, but they usually stay in one place.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the sorcerer must roll to resist the dance (Difficulty equal to three times the rolled 1s).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"330"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.1rtts7VYcjskazMs"},"283h8Gd7kUxE6U15":{"name":"Genius Loci (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Genius Loci is not a geographical Kraina as it is symbolic. It is composed of rituals which extend the sorcerer’s will across the land, making them the Genius Loci of legend.</p>\n<p>To learn this Kraina, a sorcerer must perform the initiation within the local soil. Whenever the sorcerer changes the region they inhabit, they must repeat the initiation to use their powers there.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"360"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.283h8Gd7kUxE6U15"},"2BxqtByuKAAlCt5m":{"name":"Spirit Portal (Medium Umbra)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ephemera Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual allows the caster to enter the Medium Umbra.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> </p>\n<p>Mixture of tree sap and water for the Middle Umbra to paint the portal with.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist paints a doorway or marks two locations close to each other like two trees, chanting all the while.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual (minimum Difficulty is 6 if not made higher through Aspects), the consecrated portal begins to glow in pale light when viewed by supernatural senses like the first dot of this Path and stepping through it will physically bring the Passenger Aspect’s worth of people into the spirit world physically. The portal method uses Duration for how long the portal stays open and it closes after the Duration expires or the Passengers enter through it. Returning from the spirit world requires a point of Willpower point spent by each person at the entrance they came through to pass back into the physical world.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"305"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.2BxqtByuKAAlCt5m"},"2FfcxgmHbUmU94pz":{"name":"Weight the Virtues (Followers of Set)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Resisted by Wits + Subterfuge of a once-living being (ghosts, vampires, corpses: inanimate dead usually cannot resist) within the line of sight, each margin reveals a different deed to the caster from the most recent to the oldest. From saving a child from a crocodile’s jaws to murdering a rival over a lover — appears to the sorcerer in vivid detail. On a Botchfailure, the reverse happens and deeds of the caster are exposed to the target.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.2FfcxgmHbUmU94pz"},"2Xl8624z6faR4FHz":{"name":"Urges","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>The victim suffers a dice penalty to Frenzy and other self-control tests like Knowledge Hungry equal to half the sorcerer’s Path of Corruption rounded up on all actions (minimum pool of one die). Expires when the character fails or succeeds on such a test.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.2Xl8624z6faR4FHz"},"2b6jMgipurdBAzbF":{"name":"Calix Secretus","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A small item is hallowed out and filled with blood or other liquid, up to 3 Hunger’s worth/3 pints. When a magical word is said (set at creation), the item dissolves and releases the liquid inside, usually when placed in blood or mouth (activating it is a -1 die Minor Action, drinking a -2 dice Minor Action).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.2b6jMgipurdBAzbF"},"2c4gQr54UTHL4Cgn":{"name":"Communicate with Kindred Sire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The caster uses the bond between sire and childe to open a bridge between minds, allowing the childe to create a telepathic link for the purpose of long-distance communication. This ritual is frequently used by the Banu Haqim to issue orders and give reports of the happenings in the world.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An object previously possessed by the sire (or the childe, if the sire is performing this ritual) and a silver bowl filled with clear water.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster submerges the object in water and lets their Blood drip into the bowl, concentrating upon the last memory of their sire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A win allows silent mental communication between the sire and childe for the rest of Duration. Any major disturbance on either end breaks the connection.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"323"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.2c4gQr54UTHL4Cgn"},"2uH3J7acxLELK5qw":{"name":"High Winds or Heavy Sandstorm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The wind speed rises to around 30 miles per hour or 50 kilometers per hour, with gusts of up to twice that. Ranged attacks are much more difficult: −3 dice penalty to firearm attacks and thrown weapons and archery. Additionally, characters must pass a Strength + Athletics roll against a difficulty of 2 or be laid prone, inflicting a -2 dice penalty to get up and perform a physical action. When gale-force winds are in effect, papers go flying, objects get picked up by the winds and hurled with abandon, and other suitably cinematic effects are likely. Due to many people preferring to stay indoors unless their duties compel them, all predator type which base themselves on separating others from groups (Siren, Spider, Grim Reaper…) count their difficulty as higher by one.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.2uH3J7acxLELK5qw"},"3ORGDQ2HBsQ5ouZH":{"name":"Preservation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>This Warding Circle variant allows a sorcerer to protect an object against the ravages of time. Entrepreneurial Kindred preserve the bric-a-brac of sentimental ancillae in exchange for boons, while those with a more criminal perspective use this rite to protect fragile valuables from damage during transport or theft. Preservation does not turn back the clock but preserves the item in its current state.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (count ranks as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A quill or fountain pen, one or more Rouse Checks’ worth of the caster’s vitae</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The user inscribes arcane sigils on the item to be preserved.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The item withstands damage from normal wear and tear and the passage of time. Mold and rot do not take, pages do not yellow with age, insects will not damage the item, nor will the sun fade it, so long as some modicum of protection is maintained. Intentional damage still affects the item as normal, but scuffs or scrapes resulting from transportation or minor accidents, such as a spill or a fall from a height of only a few feet, leave no trace. Spells and potions with Duration have its timer frozen while in this ward.</p>\n<p>One Rouse Check of vitae is enough to ward an item up about a meter across, or a handful of small items such as chess pieces as long as they’re grouped together. The addition of each additional Rouse Check during casting doubles the size of the item that can be warded.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"294"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.3ORGDQ2HBsQ5ouZH"},"3Q6UsuwNkZjdDEva":{"name":"Extraction","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-conjuration.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conjuration Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>A ritual frequently hung “just in case”, Extraction allows the sorcerer to take fallen or overwhelmed companions out of battle and to safety. The sorcerer first moves out of range of combat, typically behind cover, and pulls their companions out of a shadow.</p>\n<p>Once the sorcerer and their companions are out of range, they aren’t counted as being in combat unless a combatant finds them and attacks.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Weight, Number of Targets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer names the companions while they prepare the ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the ritual roll, the willing targets are all transported right next to the sorcerer. The willingness of targets goes beyond the simple understanding of “yes or no” as something is aware of the sorcerer’s and the targets’ intentions. If the sorcerer seeks to lore their targets to do harm, the ritual automatically fails.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Teleporting one’s friends and Botching can be devastating. Each suffers a point of Aggravated Health damage per 1 rolled and must test for a Crippling Injury.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"275"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.3Q6UsuwNkZjdDEva"},"3Um6BbEIKj12etnj":{"name":"Animate Vegetation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets (individual statblock as targets), Range and Green Power</em></p>\n<p>Plants may perform tasks like grappling striking</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"353"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.3Um6BbEIKj12etnj"},"3rrIC4a36to7KnIj":{"name":"The Banks of the Basca","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>When the rains come in the Carpathians, the Bâsca leaps its banks and whole villages are swept away, along with any person, living or dead, unwise enough not to seek higher ground. The koldun wielding this power carries with them the surging might of the Bâsca wherever they go.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None, but the Spell Test uses Damage, Duration (how long the spell will go), Distance (how far the water will be empowered to go) and range (how wide the water will flow).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the sorcerer is able to activate The Banks of the Basca by releasing their blood into running water – a river or stream is most common, though a rivulet or even just runoff from thawing snow is enough, and petitions the spirit of the water. The koldun then makes a Rouse Check and performs a Spell Test using the Aspects of Duration, Range and Damage + Banks of the Basca (Aspect 3).</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the blood bolsters the flow, causing it to swell and surge forward, a flash flood given magical life. ictims may attempt to dodge the flood with a Dexterity + Athletics test or hold themselves with Strength + Athletics against the Spell Test, but Storytellers can remove resistance roll in case of great rivers. Anyone, as well as most anything not secured to the ground, caught in its path will be swept away towards the point of lowest elevation at a rate of a hundred yards/meters a turn, and suffers Superficial Health damage equal to twice the Damage Aspect or margins, whichever is lower (it cannot be made Aggravated). Any subject somehow prevented from being carried downstream – wedged against a wall or other immoveable object, for instance – takes twice the normal amount of damage.</p>\n<p>Additionally, mortals must make a swimming roll (Difficulty 4) each turn to avoid swallowing great mouthfuls of water; failure inflicts one additional level of Aggravated damage which cannot be soaked. These rushing waters last according to the Duration Aspect, or twice that number if the source of the flooding is any Carpathian river.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"368"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.3rrIC4a36to7KnIj"},"4HGvOruei7KDUEci":{"name":"Scry the Heartstone","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Those who would trace this ritual’s origins find two possibilities, one being a similar Gargoyle ritual and another a Tzimisce rite to keep their domain under control. With this ritual, the sorcerer attunes themselves to their own Haven and may easily perceive intruders.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist marks the boundaries of a building or an area no larger than a small castle, chanting all the while</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the ritual roll, the sorcerer may sense the presence of all within an area. If they are obscuring their presence using supernatural powers, the sorcerer may roll Wits (or Resolve if they are focusing) + Awareness against the power in question.</p>\n<p>Note that this power does not grant one the ability to discern the identity of a person, only their presence within an accuracy of +3/-3 in groups of 7 with greater inaccuracy for larger groups. Only one area may be surveilled by Scry the Hearthstone at a time. This is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the sorcerer will be alerted by even the smallest of animals including insects as potential human intruders.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"300"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.4HGvOruei7KDUEci"},"4icJU5A7i8enlGj5":{"name":"Amaranth’s Veil","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Those using supernatural powers to detect signs of diablerie must score two more margins/Difficulty increases by two or else they do not detect it. They detect everything else according to the normal Contest.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.4icJU5A7i8enlGj5"},"4zXcrhGiDcpU7hSJ":{"name":"Vampire Strength","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The user gains three ranks’ worth of Potence powers, using the Potence rating of the vampire whose blood was used in this formula.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"266"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.4zXcrhGiDcpU7hSJ"},"5HtDcCDBwxxYWsb7":{"name":"Aura of Decay","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Decay Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>First probably invented by the Nagaraja, this Ritual merges the entropic energies of the Underwrold with that of the user.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Mortis/Nihilistics</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (Radius) and Time Period (count as higher by one)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A corpse or something rotten</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer consumes something rotten (vampires must be creative not to throw it up)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the sorcerer sorcerer begins to radiate an aura of decay that lasts until ended or Duration expires (careful not to go to coffin with this activate — one might crumble the house above them). The effect is identical to the Path’s spell, but is centered around the sorcerer in a radius of Range.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"280"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.5HtDcCDBwxxYWsb7"},"5P37b3b1Nb1jqFRA":{"name":"Craftmaster","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula requires at least one Hunger’s worth of blood from someone. If successfully created, those who ingest it may swap out their own dots in Academics, Craft, Performance, or Science of the person from whom the blood came. The subjects also acquire the Specialties of the source, even if they are already at a limit of Specialties.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.5P37b3b1Nb1jqFRA"},"5Pcq7Uiy1Bqd2R4e":{"name":"Voice from the Abyss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyss Communion Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The mystic is able to send a message across the Abyss right into one who has had experience with the it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but a room devoid of light</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The mystic shuts themselves into a dark room and transfers a mutters their message to the Abyss while naming the recipients.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Recipients must either possess one dot in Obtenebration or have visited either the Abyss itself or an Oubliette, carrying the shard of the Abyss with them wherever they may go. On a win of the Ritual Test, the target hears the intended message from a nearby shadow or in their dreams like a dim echo resembling the ritualist’s own voice. The user may also send the feelings of emotion which the victim feels in their bones.</p>\n<p>This ritual may be hanged and finished even if the sorcerer has left the room.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the Ritual Roll succeeds goes on as but the ritualist themselves keep hearing the message over and over again until the next sunset, halving their Wits pools.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"251"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.5Pcq7Uiy1Bqd2R4e"},"5PeOxjC1x3BnJeOO":{"name":"Death Curse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fortune Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Not even practitioners of magic are above the feelings of hatred and desire for vengeance. With the Death Curse, the sorcerer takes their own life, ensuring that the curse plagues the victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Per curse cast, but usually variant with the Sympathetic Connection is used, allowing the sorcerer to curse their victim’s bloodline.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing, but sympathetic material might be required.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist chants until their voice gives out, draining their own life energy in the process.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The sorcerer gains a number of additional dice on this ritual equal to their current undamaged Willpower. They also count their Path rating as two higher for all intents and purposes and reduce the difficulty of the ritual by half of their Blood Sorcery rating, rounded up, to a minimum difficulty of 1. At the end of the ritual, the sorcerer collapses, dying before they can even hit the floor. The victim resists the curse with Wits + Occult as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> None. The curse already took its price.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"313"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.5PeOxjC1x3BnJeOO"},"5cogjvsqj9D12aWQ":{"name":"School: Dur-An-Ki","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-dur-an-ki.png","system":{"description":"<p>“Mastery of the Heaven and the Earth” in Sumerian, also known as Assamite Sorcery, Dur- An-Ki is considered by many to be the oldest Blood Sorcery. It is drawn from a huge variety of mortal religious traditions, allowing it to evolve as humans do.</p>\n<p>Rather than the hermetic trappings of Hermetic Thaumaturgy or senseless incantations of words of power, Dur-An-Ki draws heavily on astrology, the use of amulets and talismans, and especially ecstatic practices. By entering into a trance, the ashipu (an ancient Mesopotamian word for a magicial healer) ascends the “Ladder of Heaven” to meet ever more powerful spirits and gain more powerful magic. Banu Haqim sorcerers usually use a drug called kalif for this purpose, but there are many other methods, including dancing, pain, meditation and artistic expression.</p>\n<p>Whereas the thaumaturgy developed by the Tremere depends upon rote memorization and repetition, Assamite blood sorcery draws from the improvisational and dynamic approaches of mortal sorcerers from hundreds of origins. The stress is not on the way one does the act, but instead the act itself. The Assamite sorcerer Al-Ashrad often refers to a parable about the young cook and the old one: Whereas the youth seeks precise and accurate measurements in ingredients, temperature and cooking duration, the old hand takes a sloppier approach, compensating for variation in one element by altering another. The goal is the meal itself, rather than the platonic incarnation of a meal, and while the youth achieves a similar result every time, the old cook manages to create a broad variation with a fraction of the effort. So it is with sorcery, Al-Ashrad concludes, emphasising the need for focus on the result rather than the tools used to reach that result.</p>\n<p>Dur-An-Ki is among the most ancient of magical practices. As such, most ancient Cainites who are sorcerers are its practitioners. With the advent of Christianity, many of the ashipu were destroyed by the Christians, condemned for paganism and witchcraft. As a result, many of those who were embraced later were frequently of Christian background and refused to learn a form of sorcery that invoked deities other than their God, leading to this form of sorcery being almost unheard of by the 6th century among the Cainites of Europe as most of the ashipu can now be found among the Children of Haqim or an ancient Order Moloch who use it against their infernalist cousins.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Nicknames:</strong> Assamite Sorcery, Haqimite Witchcraft, the Oldest Magic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquerade threat:</strong> Low-high. Assamite Sorcery tends to be one of the more subtle Blood Sorceries, but some practitioners still opt out for hurling fire and lightning at their opponents.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Phlegmatic</p>\n<h3>Pratices of the Ashipu</h3>\n<p>Dur-An-Ki one of the more flexible sorceries with one thousand and one way to practice. Each incantation is a prayer, each ritual is a mass in honour of spirits who repay their debt in magic to the sorcerer and their ancestors who performed the same rites.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Natural Sanctum, Shrine, Spirit Presence</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• By the Heart: Dur-An-Ki practitioners are not too different to old grandmas who know recepies by heart, who experiment by adding more water or more flour to bake their bread as needed in contrast to blindly following the recepie. By spending an additional turn/20 minutes spent on a spell or a ritual, the practitioner negates a single 1 for Botches.</p>\n<p>• Ladder of Heaven: If the ashipu is not in a trance (see below) they gain an additional 1 for Botches on spellcasting failure. If they are in a trance, the Difficulties for Spell and Ritual Tests are reduced by 1, to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<p>• Divine Will: To channel the will of the spirits, the ashipu must have a strong enough will and knowledge of the spirits. A sorcerer’s Dur-An- Ki rating cannot exceed their Willpower rating.</p>\n<h3>Ascending the Ladder of Heaven</h3>\n<p>Ascending the Ladder of Heaven (term used by the Banu Haqim sorcerers that varies among other traditions) is a trance-like state that elevates the mind and focus of the sorcerer. In game terms it is achievable by:</p>\n<p>• Kalif: When bubbled or ingested via a smoker’s blood, kalif reveals a transcendent vision linking the Blood to Heaven — or at least to what the ashipu thought Heaven looked like in 1200 BCE. Untainted, pure kalif can even allow the user to add an extra die to their next Remorse roll, if not used for a ritual. Tainted kalif shows visions of a more prurient kind, although it still links the smoker and gauzily clad angels in a chain. Even low-Humanity vampires feel their Blood flowing to their genitals under its influence, and can become addicted to it.</p>\n<p>When used in a ritual, it pins one die of the</p>\n<p>Ritual pool as a 10: if you have a Ritual pool of five dice, flip one to 10 and roll the other four.</p>\n<p>As with other marijuana, ingesting kalif drops a die from the user’s Wits pools and lowers Difficulties to resist Frenzy by one.</p>\n<p>• Dance, Meditation, Prayer: In game terms, this state is achievable by performing nothing but magic for at least a whole turn in which the sorcerer is calling upon magic.</p>\n<p>• Pain: Self inflicting a point of Superficial Health damage alongside with the activation cost of a spell/ritual. This point of damage cannot be mitigated.</p>\n<p>• Drunk/High: As with normal drugs,</p>\n<h3>Paths of Dur-An-Ki</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy p. XX</p>\n<p>• Battle (Marduk’s Voice)</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces (Hand of the Magi))</p>\n<p>• Enchantment P. XX.</p>\n<p>• Conjuration (Jhin’s Gift)</p>\n<p>• Corruption</p>\n<p>• Decay (Hands of Destruction)</p>\n<p>• Divination</p>\n<p>• Ephemera</p>\n<p>• Fear (Wrath of Allah)</p>\n<p>• Fortune (Evil Eye/Submission to Allah)</p>\n<p>• Healing</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood (Life’s Water)</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting (Soul of the Serpent)</p>\n<p>• The Green Path (Path of the Garden)</p>\n<p>• Mastery Over the Mortal Shell (Mortal Clay)</p>\n<p>• Water Mastery (Covenant of Enki).</p>\n<p>• Weather Control</p>\n<p>• Gifts of Faith</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"232"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.5cogjvsqj9D12aWQ"},"6GqMvhzIbIiqzRr0":{"name":"Angra Mainyu Consumes Spenta Mainyu","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The mystic drinks a goblet of hot tar (Stamina + Composure Test Difficulty 3 to not throw it up and continue the ritual) and suffers two points of Aggravated Health damage before making the Ritual Test. The tar remains in the mystic’s body for a number of nights equal to the successes on the Ritual Test.</p>\n<p>At any point while bearing the tar, the Abyss Mystic may roll a Stamina + Composure Test to vomit the tar in a shower of unnatural darkness that filters through the air like inky bubbles, gravitating to any light. The tar wraps itself around any such light, and prevents it from relighting for the remainder of the night. The radius over which the tar will travel is equal to 5 meters (5 yards) per success on the test. This does not protect from the sun, but does douse flame.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> While the tar is inside the body, aura-reading powers may reveal the black tar which may confuse some to be the signs of diablerie while those in the know will recognise it as markings of an abyss mystic.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"254"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.6GqMvhzIbIiqzRr0"},"6KCb1GGcDjLs5bMp":{"name":"Fortune (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Fate is fickle, even before accounting for the manipulations of magicians. The Path of Fortune allows them to wield luck like a weapon, cutting down their enemies with curses or fortifying their allies with blessings. While methods and explanations may vary, from the Evil Eye, to the Hand of God, to probability manipulation, the Path of Fortune never makes the truly impossible happen, only the exceedingly unlikely. It’s also one of the least precise Paths: While the hedge magician sets Fortune in motion and controls the magnitude and general range of potential outcomes, the effects of this Path often surprise them or disappear into the noise of daily life.</p>\n<p>The most common application of this Path among the Children of Caine relates to curses rather than blessings, earning it the name of the Path of Curses that weaves cruel fate for the enemies of the sorcerer. When the Tremere were mortal wizards, they considered the common witchcraft of curses and maledictions unworthy of their scholarly attention. Quickly after becoming vampires, however, they have recognized that they need every instrument at their disposal, so they have quickly adapted it for their needs. Regional names for this variant of the Path were maleficium, Hexen-Kraft or the evil eye.</p>\n<p>Even those who refer to this Path as the Path of Curses may learn Blessings in no different manner than those who primarily use the Path to bless others (and frequently refer to this as the Path of Blessings, common among Ashirra who formulate this Path as prayers to the Almighty).</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Targets resist the effects of this Path using Wits + Occult or alternatively with Resolve + Composure if they are aware that they’re about to be cursed. Each time the character wakes up they can retake the test if they failed after spending a point of Willpower. Sorcerers can also lift the Blessings or Curses at will in an instant by willing it, making curses potent bargaining tools.</p>\n<p>When exactly a curse or a blessing triggers is up to the Storyteller to capitalise on dramatic potential of this Path. A simplified version for Play by Posts would be to trigger damaging effects like illnesses or injuries events within two nights or at the very least before any important tests. As for Blessings, a simplified version for more lax tables or Play by Posts would be to let the player roll a d10 whenever they wish and a success triggers the blessing. Otheriwise it is up to the Storyteller.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Blessings, Curses</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and either Blessing or Curse Special Effects described below. This form of casting must be followed with a verbal curse within the victim’s earshot. The sorcerer can try to conceal it using less-known languages, but the observers can still try to roll Wits + Occult vs. the caster’s Wits + Subterfuge to try to discern negative and maledictive intent.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, Duration (counted as higher by one), Number of Targets (all must be related to the one targeted with sympathetic connection and do not even have to be born yet), Sympathetic Connection and the Blessing or Curses Special Effects below without the need for physical presence and verbal component of the spell. The sorcerer must use this form to affect themselves with Blessings.</p>\n<p>Duration Aspect serves as the time limit for when the effect may happen or it may serve as the duration of a certain lasting effect if stated.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> </p>\n<p>+2 The caster takes control of when precisely the effect will happen. The sorcerer must announce when the Blessing or Curse will activate, such as instantly, next turn, on the sunset...</p>\n<p>+1 The Blessing or Curse happens more frequently.</p>\n<p>For each point of Difficulty increased, the Blessing or Curse may occur one more time within the interval for it triggering. Maximum number of times this modifier may be selected is equal to the caster’s Fortune Path rating at the moment the Spell is cast. The same effect cannot be triggered more than once in a turn. If the spell is meant to deal damage, the subject retakes the test against the Spell Test’s successes each time damage is triggered.</p>\n<h3>Blessing Samples</h3>\n<p>Below are samples of Blessings. Feel free to work within these bounds and consult the Storyteller on anything not directly mentioned here. Curses are learned individually as Special Effects (see further).</p>\n<p>When directly influencing Advantages, one dot may be improved or in some form affected for the Duration per margin.</p>\n<p>Note that the effects of a blessing may apply only once per day without the modifier above, even if cast by multiple magicians or using different forms of supernatural powers like demonic Arcana. For example, a +2 dice bonus penalty to Athletics caused by decreasing the victim’s chance of stumbling may happen only once per day. As usual, dice bonuses should not stack, especially because any form of forcing fate to occur (using artefacts, Blood Surges, other magic) is a quick way for the Fates to grow deaf to your requests.</p>\n<p>Dice bonuses generally should not improve other powers for the sake balance and the sanity of the Storyteller. Avoiding damage with Blessing negates points of damage before any halving or other form of reduction has occured.</p>\n<h3>Curse Samples</h3>\n<p>Below are samples of what Curses can do. Feel free to work within these bounds and consult the Storyteller on anything not directly mentioned here. Curses are learned individually as Special Effects (see further).</p>\n<p>When directly influencing Advantages, one dot may be “discovered” or in some form improved for the Duration per margin.</p>\n<p>Curses have the same limitations for activating and stacking effects as Blessings. If the caster or those who are aware of the curse try to press their advantage by forceful dice bonuses, dice penalties from curses should not apply for that roll. Damage from curses may only occur up to once a week with the harshest curse applying. Unlike blessings, curses may impact usage of powers.</p>\n<h3>Blessing</h3>\n<h3>Curse</h3>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Agitating fate against one’s enemies is a risky endeavor as whatever governs fate has love for irony. On a Botch, the sorcerer must roll Wits + Occult to resist their own curse (Difficulty equal to thrice the number of rolled 1s), potentially falling into traps that they planned for others.</p>\n<p>• Something very minor: A die bonus to a single Skill roll or adding a specialty as the subject remembers something about something. Minor shielding of damage from a broadly defined source up to a single point from a twist of fate.</p>\n<p>•• Minor: A die bonus to a certain Attribute-based roll or a -1 Difficulty increase to a broadly defined non-combat Test. By twists of fate, certain Merits such as Looks may be afflicted (number of dots equal to half rounded up successes on the Spell Test)or a die bonus to using certain Merit abilities like Medium. Avoiding Superficial Health damage from a broadly defined source equal to half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test from a single attack.</p>\n<p>••• Useful: A two-dice bonus to a certain Attribute-based roll or -2 Difficulty to a broadly defined non-combat Test. By twists of fate, physical Backgrounds like Resources may be afflicted (number of dots equal to half rounded up successes on the Spell Test). Avoiding a total number of Superficial Health damage from a broadly defined source equal to half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test from attacks in a scene.</p>\n<p>•••• Helpful: A three-dice bonus to three related Skills or -3 Difficulty to a broadly defined non-combat Test. By twists of fate, non-physical Backgrounds like Allies, Influence, Retainers or Status may be afflicted number of dots equal to half rounded up successes on the Spell Test). Avoiding Aggravaed Health damage from a broadly defined source from a single attack equal to half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test; avoiding a total number of Superficial Health damage from any source equal to half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test from attacks in a scene.</p>\n<p>••••• Lifesaving: A +4 dice bonus to a single precisely described action or -4 Difficulty to its non-combat application. Avoiding Aggravated Health damage from any source from a single attack equal to half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test;</p>\n<p>• Something very minor: Inflicting a die penalty to a single Skill roll or disabling specialties in a certain type of an Attribute-based roll. Minor damage may be inflicted up to a single point from a twist of fate.</p>\n<p>•• Minor: Inflicting a die penalty to certain type of Attribute-based rolls or a +1 Difficulty increase to non-combat Tests. By twists of fate, certain Merits (-1 dot per margin on the contest) or a die penalty to using certain Merit abilities like Medium. Causing Superficial Health damage equal to the margin.</p>\n<p>••• Harsh: Continous damage equal to the margin from ailments against mortals. By twists of fate, physical Backgrounds like Resources may be afflicted (-1 dot per margin on the contest). A single point of Aggravated Health damage and a Crippling Injury by a twist of fate.</p>\n<p>•••• Grave: Inflicting a three-dice penalty to a certain pools or +3 Difficulty to its noncombat Tests. By twists of fate, nonphysical Backgrounds like Allies, Influence, Retainers or Status may be afflicted (-1 dot per margin on the contest).</p>\n<p>••••• Deadly: Inflicting a four-dice penalty to a single precisely described action or +4 Difficulty to its non-combat application. Aggravated Health damage equal to the margin once per week.</p>\n<p>• By a Hair: Subject’s luck has them avoid a point of Superficial Health damage from a projectile once per scene.</p>\n<p>• Gambler’s Luck: Luck favours your cards. Gain a specialty for a certain game on which to gamble. Consult the Storyteller what Skill would be appropriate (we suggest a Mental Attribute + Academics, Science or even Animal Ken)</p>\n<p>• Respectful Courtier: Subject finds rules of etiquette easy to follow, receiving a die bonus to Etiquette rolls.</p>\n<p>•• Good Hair Day: Subject gains a number of dots to spend on temporary Looks Merits (or buying off Looks Flaws) equal to the halved rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test. If the subject’s looks are cursed, the spell must achieve more successes than the one who threw the curse (there is no beating God in dice rolls when it comes to the Nosferatu and the Cappadocians...)</p>\n<p>•• Strength of Two: Subject gains a die bonus to all Strength tests.</p>\n<p>••• Sudden Inheritance: Whether by inheriting it from a relative, friend, finding it or just business going well, the subject gains a number of Resources equal to half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test. Beware that using this frequently might raise some questions...</p>\n<p>•••• Fighter’s Luck: The subject gains a three-dice bonus to Athletics, Brawl and Melee rolls. A single one of them being triggered ends the effect depending on the frequency and whether or not it was modified.</p>\n<p>••••• Grace of God: The subject is protected against physical harm, shielding them from Aggravated Health damage equal to the half rounded up number of successes on the Spell Test.</p>\n<p>• Faux Pass: Targets keep finding themselves in social situations that they are not well prepared for, rendering them unable to benefit from specialties in Social Skills. This happens once per night.</p>\n<p>• Stub Toe: The worse curse known to mankind, the targets stub their toe, fall of a bicycle/horse, hit their head against a cupboard or step on something sharp like a lego. They suffer a point of Superficial Willpower and Superficial Health damage each night for the Duration as the events hit happening each day or night. Caution on making somebody stub their toe for several months: they will really wonder if they have been cursed by then. This happens once per night.</p>\n<p>•• Stigma: Targets suffer an invisible stigma caused by some minor rumours accumulating, a faux pass in etiquette or something minor. They suffer a -1 die penalty to Etiquette tests once per night.</p>\n<p>•• Strip Vanity: Targets suffer a bad hair day, their appearance is ruined by the weather, bad lighting or something else. The caster may use the margins to lower the target’s Looks Merit Ratings (remove add-on Looks Merits first). If they have none left, the caster may “buy” Looks Flaws with the margins as well. One dot is equal to one margin. This occurs on scene per night.</p>\n<p>••• Fortunes of Money: On a win, the caster may spend the margins to lower the target’s Resources or Business Establishment one dot per margin for the Duration.</p>\n<p>••• Infertility: By developing illnesses, pure chance of the occasion or other twists of fate, the targets are rendered “infertile”. Vampires cannot sire either, their wouldbe-childer somehow going too quickly into rigor mortis, blood expiring before reaching the lips of the would-be-childer and so on.</p>\n<p>••• Injury: Targets suffer an accident within 2 nights, suffering a Crippling Injury and a point of Aggravated Health damage. The victim rolls their Crippling Injury chart, but the sorcerer can select the injury (not death/torpor) themselves if they score a critical win or at least 3 successes more than the victims. When the Crimpling Injury is healed, next one is triggered within 2 nights until the Duration expires.</p>\n<p>••• Malady: Targets suffers a minor malady.</p>\n<p>The victim suffers Superficial Health damage equal to the margin rating at the beginning of each night.</p>\n<p>Nightly resistance rolls after the first failure are made with Stamina + Medicine at a Difficulty equal to the initial successes of the caster. After a month, the Difficulty is set to the caster’s Path of Curses if it is lower than the sccesses. Creatures like vampires are immune to Malady.</p>\n<p>•••• Betrayal: Targets believe to have been betrayed, are actually betrayed or their allies simply are impaired by something (like guards running to save their king only to be impeded by a hallway collapsing). When calling upon NPCs, such as Allies, Servants, Retainers and Mawlae, they will not be able to arrive and/or for the Duration, making them unusable mechanically. Players can roll Wits + Occult against the successes of the sorcerer and arrive to help the targets normally by scoring more successes, but otherwise arrive too late for situations that require time sensitivity.</p>\n<p>•••• Liar Liar Pants on Fire: Targets make mistakes when tricking others, suffering a 3 dice penalty to lying rolls once per night.</p>\n<p>•••• Morta’s Scissors: When the victims drop to only one Health level undamaged with Aggravated Health or less, something happens that will kill them. Perhaps injuries were too grave, a lightning bolt strikes a vampire in torpor and so on.</p>\n<p>••••• Masks Off: Targets make mistakes when using concealments. When others attempt to notice the target through Awareness, or through powers such as Sense the Unseen (them or their hidden powers), scry their aura, the subject suffers a -4 dice penalty once per night.</p>\n<p>•••• Thrones Crumble: (Prerequisite: Stigma)</p>\n<p>Targets have their authority brought into question through twists of fate. The caster spends margins to reduce the target’s Status or Influence, one dot per margin.</p>\n<p>••••• Death’s Coming: (Prerequisite: Morta’s Scissors) Within the next two nights, something happens that inflicts Aggravated Health damage to the targets equal to the margins on the contest against the victims. A character may be impacted with this only and effects similar to this once per week.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"309"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.6KCb1GGcDjLs5bMp"},"6LHAFY4Fwnw11jyW":{"name":"Marionette","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Mastery of the Mortal Shell Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following.</em></p>\n<p>The victim’s body is under the control of the sorcerer who may perform actions with it using their casting Attribute + appropriate Skill like Melee or Aim. The victim is unable to Rouse their Blood while in this state and as such powers requiring Rouse Checks or any other conscious expenditure of the Blood are not available to the victim for the duration. While using Marionette, the sorcerer must maintain focus on the target and may not take any other significant physical action otherwise the spell’s hold breaks. They may still dodge as normal and move at a walking pace, however. Every body controlled after the first incurs a penalty of -1 to the sorcerer’s dice pool.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"330"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.6LHAFY4Fwnw11jyW"},"6dNfOoDhy9G0JfkS":{"name":"Elemental Forces (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>While most users simply reduce the Path to its fire-creating capabilities (which is why the Tremere misleadingly refere to it as the Lure of Flames or Fire Creation and popularised it during their ascent when they had to constantly struggle for survival), Elemental Forces allows a sorcerer to channel elemental magic to harmful effects. It is a dramatic example of magic and shooting fire and lightning often makes it appealing to the younger apprentices.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Characters typically learn the Lure of Flames Special Effect first. Unless stated otherwise, the effects of this Path are avoided using the dodge maneuver (Dexterity + Athletics). If Range 1/melee is used for projectile effects, the enemy has to roll Dexterity + Athletics only once and damage is decided by the margins according to the sorcerer’s roll to touch their target or the Damage Aspect, whichever is lower.</p>\n<p>There is not much supernatural about the elements themselves, only their creation. As such, Countermagic may not be used against Elemental Forces to resist them, but Dispelling is possible.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Electricity, Ice, Fire, Wind...</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Damage, Range, Area, and the Special Effects:</p>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. Normally, the first learned Special Effect is the Lure of Flames after which the Path itself is named. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> When you play with fire sometimes you get burned. A Botch tyically results in the spell rebounding on the caster. They suffer the spell’s effects, taking damage equal to the number of rolled 1s.</p>\n<p>Most common rituals are centered around fire, but that’s all anyone will ever need. Right? Feel free to invent variants around other elements.</p>\n<p>•••• The Effect is a variant of the Lure of Flames or Balefire, depending on which the sorcerer possessess, and functions identically to them. The victims are set on fire on a hit and continue to take [Damage Aspect] fire damage each turn unless they succeed on a Test of Composure + Survival or Science (Difficulty equal to the Damage Aspect) to douse the flames. Others can attempt to douse the flames using Intelligence + Survival or Science. Multiple effects of being set on fire cannot stack. Balefire also deals Aggravated Willpower damage equal to half of Damage rank rounded up.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"284"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.6dNfOoDhy9G0JfkS"},"6h3w1VkIX1evK9JE":{"name":"Scrying Mirror","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>The mirror allows for observation of a location or a person. Observing a designated person or a location is done by fulfilling the Sympathetic Connection Aspect. The observer hears and sees everything, able to detect hidden things as if they possess Sense the Unseen and may “move” perspective but cannot stray 5 meters from the subject.</p>\n<p>Only supernatural powers of Perception like certain Psychic Phenomena, Sense the Unseen, Shadow Perspective and Mystic sight can be used through the mirror. Targets can sense that somebody is watching them by powers such as Sense the Unseen against Resolve of the user + Subterfuge rating of the user.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"293"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.6h3w1VkIX1evK9JE"},"6qnnJeZqHaEzWTTX":{"name":"Unblinking Eye","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (•). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer casts an enchantment of vigilance that will enable them to awaken if something threatens their body in sleep or torpor. When in danger, the character awakens automatically and may stay awake for the rest of the scene following the normal rules for staying awake during the day.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"371"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.6qnnJeZqHaEzWTTX"},"7TuSPTtHLShjEA71":{"name":"Homunculus","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>One of the magna opera of alchemy, this formula creates a small artificial living being with some semblance of the alchemist themselves, frequently used as an assistant or a magical child. The process requires one point of Aggravated Health’s worth of flesh from the alchemist. The sorcerer then mixes it with cucurbit plant and places it into the womb of a horse to incubate for one Lunar cycle, feeding the horse an insignificant amount of human (or vampire) blood each night to nourish the would-be homunculus. After the incubation process is over, the sorcerer opens the horse to let the homunculus out. A homunculus has the following traits:</p>\n<ul><li>Attributes and Skills of the sorcerer at the moment the flesh was taken, at a dot lower, down to 1.</li><li>Homunculi can’t become ghouls, be Embraced, or be Blood Bound, and any vampire who feeds on one gains no nourishment and finds the process repellent.</li><li>The homunculus can move about freely under its own power, but it works much like a limb of the creator and acts according to its master’s orders, which may be issued nonverbally as long as it is in its creator’s presence. Still, homunculi tend to develop their own spiteful personalities.</li><li><strong>Born from the craft, they are more connected to magic.</strong> A homunculus can learn the first two dots of any magic Path known by their maker for free, making them useful assistants. Other ratings are purchasable through the master’s experience points. Instead of a Rouse Check, the homunculus simply pays one point of Willpower.</li><li>A homunculus is a separate physical entity and does not count as an arcane connection, nor do its bodily fluids count as its creator’s blood. Establishing a psychic connection to a homunculus instead projects into the consciousness of its controller. A homunculus has the same fingerprints and “blood” as their maker, scientifically speaking.</li></ul>\n<p>Additionally, the sorcerer selects one type of a homunculus, further modifying it:</p>\n<ul><li>Flayer, appearing as a small winged demon of around a meter with its master’s face, possessing three dots in Flight Discipline.</li><li>Grub, appearing as a worm or a snake-like creature a meter long and around 10 centimeters wide with facial features of their creator. Such a homunculus gains an additional die to all grappling rolls and the Squirm power (count the rating of the Protean Discipline as 3).</li><li>Hopper, a small humanoid imp resembling their caster in a distorted way with plainer facial features only half the size, possessing an additional point of Health and Willpower. Sometimes, Tremere who have wronged somebody send such a homunculus as an apology to the wronged party who usually punishes the homunculus instead.</li></ul>\n<p>A sorcerer may possess only one homunculus at a time and creating a new one withers the older one to ash instantly. These creatures are, for all purposes, mortals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.7TuSPTtHLShjEA71"},"7oDU8nWgQKCTHvxO":{"name":"Awakening Dirt","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Grave dirt that the user sprinkles around where they will be sleeping. If the caster is threatened during their sleep, dreams will notify them of danger and they gain a five-dice bonus to Awakening. Vampires may stay awake for a scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.7oDU8nWgQKCTHvxO"},"7sVMs0x23Tnofph2":{"name":"Oplite's charge","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range count their dexterity as 2 higher to calculate movement speed and gain an additional dot to the relevant skill for rolls made for rapid movement (parkouring, going through difficult terrain etc.)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.7sVMs0x23Tnofph2"},"7uoL05A0L7uYvqOb":{"name":"Aegis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>Every target in range counts as having an armour value of 4. Does not stack with armour that is already worn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.7uoL05A0L7uYvqOb"},"7xTC3fRHYXqM4gHW":{"name":"Fascination (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Stories of the enthralling sorcerer or the bewitching magician are found in folktales across the world. Devious enchanters bring the innocent under their sway, and deals struck for power entrap the hearts and minds of those so foolish as to sign away their souls to such bargains. The kernel of truth to this old trope is solid as diamond. Unscrupulous magicians bending the minds of others to their will have practiced this Path as long as anyone has wished another would just act or think in an agreeable manner.</p>\n<p>The methods vary, but the result is the same: Break the subject’s will and enthrall them. A sorcerer employing a mystical practice may apply makeup that makes their eyes sparkle with entrancing radiance. They may spritz themselves with an alluring fragrance, drawing the attention of anyone within wafting distance. Others may simply hone their confidence into a razor’s edge, impossible to resist. Technomantic sorcerers carry tested and approved harmonic resonators that emit a subsonic frequency that rebalances neurotransmitters to facilitate compliance.</p>\n<p>Those with mind-altering powers already can resist with powers of their own if they are the specific target of a power. This goes both ways, as a student of this Path may recognize other uses of mind-altering effects and prepare themselves to resist or overcome them.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>This Path may be used to unweave mind-controlling and mind-altering effects such as Presence, Dementation or Dominate of vampires. Sorcerers may always resist mind-controlling powers, even the subtle ones like that of Presence, if they possess at least three dots in this Path. On a win of the resistance roll, they are made aware that they’ve just been a victim of attempted mind-control.</p>\n<p>Notably, allure does not improve usage of other supernatural powers. Powers such as Scry the Soul may reveal others to be under the influence of magic.</p>\n<p>Spells of this Path are resisted by Composure + Resolve.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> Full +1 Difficulty for being disliked by the target, +2 Difficulty for being hated by the target (doesn’t stack with being disliked)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Manipulating Emotions</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> In addition to the Aspects of Number of Targets, Range, and Sympathetic Connection, Fascination uses the Allure and Willbending Aspects.</p>\n<h3>Allure</h3>\n<p>Each rank of Allure gives the caster a die to Social Tests with the</p>\n<h3>Willbending</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, love turns to hate as Allure becomes a dice penalty instead. Willbending is less easy to predict, but higher ranks can lead to outright attempted murder on the sorcerer</p>\n<p>• One die</p>\n<p>•• Two dice</p>\n<p>••• Three dice</p>\n<p>•••• Four dice</p>\n<p>••••• Five dice</p>\n<p>••••• Each additional rank adds another die.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"305"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.7xTC3fRHYXqM4gHW"},"7xTwKxIrCyTOHBwC":{"name":"Dedicate the Haven","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Probably one of the most commonly practiced rituals among masters of sorcery, Dedicate the Haven creates an unbreakable bond between a home and its master. While this ritual itself does little by itself, it allows for powerful effects to be bestowed onto the sorcerer’s Haven as it stops being a simple building. This is especially important for havens that house important clan documents (such as a Tremere chantry) or other valuable treasures.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None, see system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Caster’s blood, frankincense, and sage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist walks a counterclockwise circle around the entire haven grounds while carrying a smoking thurible that contains a burning mixture of their blood, frankincense, and sage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the ritual has been successfully performed. Once dedicated, a Haven is open to more defenses; all Ward rituals cast has its duration extended to permanent or until the sorcerer wills the effect to lapse and as such no Duration Aspect is necessary. Additionally, all Ward rituals automatically expand to the borders of the Haven if the sorcerer so wishes. For example, Warding Circles of Binding, Summoning and Warding Path, Teleport Ward of Conveyance, Bar the Common Passage or the Ward of Secrecy. Keep it mind that rituals do not stack.</p>\n<p>Dedicate the Haven lasts until the sorcerer reperforms the ritual somewhere else or doesn’t seel within the Haven for over a century.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, not even the sorcerer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"297"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.7xTwKxIrCyTOHBwC"},"8EyGSXL68yZS3ffn":{"name":"Tame the Maddening Flame","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>With the emergence of firefighters and fire extinguishers, this ritual often goes overlooked. Tame the Maddening Flame was developed during the war between the Tremere and their former colleagues within the Order of Hermes. Enemy magi and inquisitors would set Tremere chantries and havens aflame, burning all inside and forcing them into a fear frenzy induced by the fire. This ritual alters all fire in the area, making it easier for vampires to bear it and escape unharmed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range(as a radius), Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A bucket and negligible amount of vitae</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The thaumaturge fills the bucket with water and pours a drop of their own vitae. The polluted water is then used to mark the perimeters of a room or outdoor location, drawing a Warding Circle that protects against fire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> See systems for Warding Circles like increasing their size in Binding, Summoning and Warding Path. On a win, all existing and new flames in an area take on a greenish hue and its smoke smells pleasant to vampires – sweet aroma of the caster’s vitae, no doubt. These green flames don’t provoke Rötschreck (that is, fear frenzy) and the damage caused by flames in the area is reduced by 2 to a minimum of 0. This includes all flames, even those produced by magic. This is a Ward Ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the ritual automatically fails and the existing flames spread faster, but do not change their colour.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"295"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8EyGSXL68yZS3ffn"},"8Uz2QPWQKlKEv4g5":{"name":"School: Mortis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-mortis.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Cappadocians carefully guard the secret of the Mortis Discipline. Clan legend has it that the knowledge of this sorcery was handed down to the Clan’s Methuselahs by Cappadocius himself. Mortis allows its user to unlock and use the secrets of death itself. They can steal the traits left from the living in a dead body, they can strengthen or weaken the corpselike nature of a vampire’s body, and they can animate the dead. Those who use Mortis, be they Cappadocian or not, often develop an obsession with death and the dead. This unnatural preoccupation often turns inward to an analysis of the nature of vampirism and occasionally provokes a melancholy that can be cured only by a long walk on a sunny day.</p>\n<p>Mortis is a form of blood sorcery concerned with the condition of the corpse after death. The Cappadocian scholars teach that the soul leaves the body and goes to its reward when life ends, yet something remains. It is this “something,” both the physical fact of a rotting body and the spiritual echo left within it, that is the subject of Mortis. Rumor has it that elders of the Clan have purposefully Embraced Venetian sorcerers skilled in the ways of affecting the souls displaced from bodies, an art they call nigrimancy. Many traditional Cappadocians find the idea of trafficking with ghosts and souls unpleasant at best and heretical at worst.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Without a doubt, this School of Magic is most well known for its use by the Clan Cappadocian. Mortis is one of the oldest magics and perhaps the most misunderstood. As such, a funny ironic occurs in that the Cappadocains did not really hoard knowledge as much as others refused it, and those that did want to learn necromancy from outside the Clan tended to be...not free of selfish and morbid intentions.</p>\n<p>Older groups of shunned sorcerers such as the Ananake have bartered knowledge from the Cappadocians and kept on cultivating it long after the Giovanni usurped the Cappadocians and renamed this school Necromancy to cast the memory of the Cappadocians into Oblivion itself.</p>\n<p>After the Family Reunion leads to the formation of the Clan Hecata, this School of Sorcery will have many names. Necromancy, as the Giovanni and other vampires were used to call it, Mortis, name revived by the Harbingers of Ashur, and many more.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquerade threat:</strong> Low-high. The most unnoticable practice of Necromancy involves Divination which by itself can be unnerving. Taken to extremes, necromancers can channel forces of entropy or raise entire armies of the dead.</p>\n<h3>Pratices of the Death Sorcerers</h3>\n<p>Not as quick or fluid as the blood sorcery of the Usurpers, and yet implacable as death itself, the grim magic of the Cappadocians concerns itself with all aspects of dying, from the reanimation of flesh and evocations of decay to ill humors transmuted by undeath. Still, it was not until recently that the Clan of Death delved at last into the study of the incorporeal and what lies beyond the shroud. It is to this end that the Cappadocians brought the Venetian sorcerers of the Giovanni family into their Clan, and with them the fell arts of nigrimancy. Unlike the Lamia, the Giovanni do not jealously hoard their proprietary Path of Nigrimancy. They freely barter secret for secret, amassing the rituals of their elders in exchange for power over the restless dead.</p>\n<p>As this sorcery has existed since the Second City, there are many ancient practitioners of Mortis besides the Clan of Death itself with the most notable fringe group being the Ananake, a Malkavian Bloodline that has spurned the gifts of Auspex in favour of ripping the signs of the future out of the guts of the living.</p>\n<p>Another fringe group are those who are embraced from the Roman family Rosselini. They claim to be in possession of a papal decree charging them with punishing heretics even after they die. Their zeal is followed by their capabilities they are not taught outside of the family such as the spells and rituals to rip the dead out of the Shadowlands into the lands of the living where they are often subjected to immense torture.</p>\n<p>The practice of Mortis is split into two further sub-schools: those who adhere to the Principle of Authority and those who adhere to the Principle of Taboo. The former rely on invocations of higher beings, be they God, gods, powerful ghosts or spirits of the dead, and the latter rely on mocking them and the laws of life and death with the most infamous examples being Giovanni ceremonies that mock Catholic mass.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Weak Veil</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Death Magic: Necromancy is the magic that draws entropic energies of the Lands of the Dead into the Lands of the Living or manipulates vestiges of souls in bodies to work magic. If a location that the necromancer is working on has a Shroud rating of Impenetrabe or Thick, the sorcerer may not spend any Willpower to reroll dice on a Spell or a Ritual Test. See p. XX on Shroud mechanics.</p>\n<p>If the local Shroud’s rating is Frayed or Non-Existant, Spell and Ritual Test Difficulties are reduced by 1, to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<p>Mortis also possesses two Motifs which contradict each other. While both may be known by the user, only one may be applied in a Spell or a Ritual at the time.</p>\n<p>• Principle of Authority: If the necromancer takes an additional turn for a spell or five minutes for a ritual to perform an invocation of a higher being and the laws of the universe, the necromancer negates a single 1 on a Botch.</p>\n<p>• Principle of Taboo: If the necromancer takes an additional turn for a spell or five minutes for a ritual to perform a perverse act ridiculing morality, social conventions or the sanctity of the dead, their resting places and souls, the necromancer lowers their Spell or Ritual Difficulty by 1, to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Mortis</h3>\n<p>Many Paths of Mortis possess restrictions to certain Bloodlines or groups in order to portray their monopoly on proprietary magic. Once the Clan Hecata forms following the Family Reunion, these restrictions are lifted as families are not only encouraged but required to share knowledge. This, however, does not apply to the Path of Skulls invented by the Harbingers who use it to expand their political influence in secret and possibly keep the Path away from their cousins if they were ever to defect to the Camarilla following the expiration of the Promise of 1528 in 2028.</p>\n<p>• Alchemy (Path of Four Humours)</p>\n<p>This Path is most common among the Lamiae, but they are still willing to share most of its secrets. Each Formula of Mortis Alchemy must be infused with at least Fleeting Resonance else it automatically fails. Most commonly this is done by the vampire themselves first feeding on Resonance-rich blood or by using the Preserve Resonance Formula and storing it for later use.</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Corpse in the Monster</p>\n<p>• Fortune (Path of the Moirai)</p>\n<p>• Decay (Path of Grave’s Decay)</p>\n<p>Only readily practiced by Cappadocians.</p>\n<p>They take the Path further by channeling energies of entropy, turning this simple Path of decaying into something far more devastating and insidious.</p>\n<p>• Divination (Necromancy/Haruspicy)</p>\n<p>Necromancy method is usually practiced by the Cappadocians and others who respect the dead. The Giovanni and the Ananake usually simply rip out entrails of living beings and read the future for themselves by the Haruspicy method rather than relying on the dead who may or may not be wrong. The Rosselini detest this Path, however, believing that knowledge of fate rests on God alone and everything else is short of diabolism.</p>\n<p>• Enchantment (Soulforging)</p>\n<p>Enchantment of Mortis always requires material either from the Shadowlands or from those who have been dead for over a century in one form or another.</p>\n<p>• Ephemera (Ghost-whispering)</p>\n<p>An older Path practiced by the Clan of Death before the welcoming of the Giovanni, Mortis almost always uses Metaphysical Aspect (Ghost) of the Low Umbra.</p>\n<p>• Haunting Mostly practiced by the Giovanni and a secretive group known as the Maegar.</p>\n<p>• Necromancy (Nigrimancy)</p>\n<p>• Path of Skulls</p>\n<p>• Reanimation (Path of Bones)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"244"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8Uz2QPWQKlKEv4g5"},"8Z74GuU1SazrWXQ3":{"name":"Dead Eyes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>A salve that can be applied to the eyes to allow the user to perfectly see everything through the Shroud for one night. Requires an eye of a dead person in the recipe. Note that the drinker will not be able to really differentiate between the lands of the living and the dead unless targets they observe are obviously ghosts by damaged appearance or if the drinker succeeds on a Wits + Awareness or Occult test (difficulty 3, rolled once per each scene)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8Z74GuU1SazrWXQ3"},"8jVlyUPwymWwKXWJ":{"name":"School: Sihr","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-sihr.png","system":{"description":"<p>While Thaumaturgy and other forms of blood magic are considered to be blasphemy in Islam (as they seek a power other than God's to aid someone), many of the practitioners of Sihr believes that Sihr’s power comes through God only. Casting spells and rituals usually relies on prayers and Quran recitations that are used as incantations. While many translate the word Sihr as witchcraft or black magic, Sihr practitioners point at its older, more neutral definition as Sihr was a creation of a Assamite Sorcerer Khalid al-Munir and a Nosferatu sorcerer Tarique al-Hajj, who supposedly studied with Muslim Ahl-i-Batin mages to erect the wards that contain the overwhealing aura of Mecca called the Keening. They taught it to their progeny and disciples and even in the Modern Nights, the Hajj practice Sihr. Outside the bloodline, however, this school of sorcery fallen out of use, being replaced by the more versatile Thaumaturgy or incorporated into Dur- An-Ki</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquerade threat:</strong> Low-high. Until obvious flashy effects of fire come up, most Sihr practitioners can pass it off as a religious practice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Phlegmatic</p>\n<h3>Pratices of the Pious</h3>\n<p>Not as</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Natural Sanctum, Shrine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Pious Magic: Sihr often requires recitations from the Quran. If the sorcerer is not verbalising their magic by invoking its holiness, they count their Aspects as lower by one. If the Sihr practitioner possesses a two-dot Hafiz Merit necessary to successfuly evoke a Quranic verse on the fly according to the situation, they negate two 1s for a Botch if they’re verbally invoking the authority of the Quran.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Sihr</h3>\n<p>• Battle (Valour of the Faithful)</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning and Warding (Authority of the Supreme)</p>\n<p>• Conjuration (Hedayat el-Hawa/Guidance of Air)</p>\n<p>• Elemental Mastery (Hedayat el-Nar/Guidance of Fire)</p>\n<p>• Ephemera (Guidance of jhins)</p>\n<p>• Enchanting (Path of Creation)</p>\n<p>• Fortune (Path of Blessings/Submission to Allah)</p>\n<p>• Gifts of Faith (Word of Allah)</p>\n<p>• Healing (Mercy of the Highest)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"248"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8jVlyUPwymWwKXWJ"},"8piLX7q11fOklPLM":{"name":"Forced Medium","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Mediums are the people born with the natural ability to deal with the spirit worlds. They are who ghosts will come to in order to either ask them to solve a problem or else take over their body to compel them do so. However, such people are rare, and this ritual was often used by the early Giovanni to forcefully initiate their family members into the mysteries.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Either a placenta (among a family of Italian inquisitor family dealing with ghosts, traditionally the one that belonged to the subject) or liquid from a dead person (preserved tears, blood) or a corpse</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Multiple ways exist to practice this Ritual. The placenta is either ceremonially placed onto the subject, the subject drinks the liquid of the dead or engages in cannibalism</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual roll, the target (who may resist with Composure + Resolve against the successes on the Ritual Test), acquires the Medium Merit (p. XX).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"336"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8piLX7q11fOklPLM"},"8rwnYxX23SlKZDF3":{"name":"Reinforced Jacket","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A piece of outfit granting four points of Armour that does not stack with normal armour.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8rwnYxX23SlKZDF3"},"8zCEAUWTMoNAomdW":{"name":"Uninvisibility Dust","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>A dust that reveals invisible objects or creatures and disrupts illusions (typically, the successes on the formula’s creation roll are compared with successes of an illusion or invisibility power. On a tie, the actor wins per vanilla rules).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8zCEAUWTMoNAomdW"},"8zICSnan3m7MwRcC":{"name":"The Third Eye of Richard Argentis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>This ritual summons forth a mysterious dark third eye, forged from abyssal energies, which rests upon the caster’s forehead and protects them from harm. During the casting, the sorcerer must spend at least one Rouse Check that cannot be rerolled in addition to the standard activation Rouse Check, to a maximum number of additional Rouse Checks made equal to the mystic’s rating in Abyssal Infusion. At any time the caster would be ambushed or surprised, the eye will grant the sorcerer an additional Awareness die for each additional Rouse Check made when this ritual was cast. The sorcerer can choose how many dice they want to take from the eye for their Test if they wish to preserve their dice resource.</p>\n<p>Once the blood is fully expended or when the mystic wills it, the eye vanishes until recast. Recasting merely resets the effect and thus cannot stack with itself.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> Beyond having an inky-black orb in the middle of their forehead?</p>\n<p>The mystic suffers no other consequence. The eye can be concealed in order to not freak others out as it sees beyond the material, but it begins oozing black blood when providing dice. Now you know why abyss mystics wear black.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"253"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.8zICSnan3m7MwRcC"},"92K2SZlDnIFA4aEq":{"name":"Intimations of Mortality","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>By evoking experiences of a human, the caster brings in the best and worst of humanity within them. They suffer a -2 dice penalty to non-tracker rolls due to the feelings of hunger, thrist, cold and everything else mortals do that vampire body has no answer to. The penalty may be ignored once per turn by spending a point of Willpower. However, the caster also receives a three-dice penalty to pass off as a human, unimpacted by the above dice penalty. This may affect others, using Range and Number of Targets aspects.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"372"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.92K2SZlDnIFA4aEq"},"9AqX1WLk9bYdsSpg":{"name":"Breath of Flame","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The Gargoyle gains the ability to spit flammable vitae by making a Rouse Check and rolling Composure + Firearms vs. Dexterity + Athletics. On a win, + target takes three points of Aggravated Health damage. Nearby vampires and creatures with the Beast must make a Fear Frenzy check at a difficulty of 2 due to the flames. Those drinking Blood right out of the Gargoyle’s veins take two points of Aggravated Health damage from fire for each point of Hunger drained.</p>\n<p>Upon complications or when spitting flammable vitae at something else, nearby flammable material may be set alight at the Storyteller’s discretion The effects of the ritual last until the Gargoyle feeds next, reaches Hunger 5, but is permanent on Gargoyles who have purchased this as a Gargoyle power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"302"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.9AqX1WLk9bYdsSpg"},"9DAwcHIaPhGF0c7O":{"name":"Healing (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Wherever humanity thrives, there’s a need for healing. Since the dawn of civilization, healers sprung up out of empathy and community need. Long before science knew what germ theory was, healers learned how to serve their people from tradition, communing with spirits, trial and error, and a host of other ways. Now, even when science can prevent and cure horrifying diseases, many remain without access to such techniques. In medical deserts and communities who have historically been wronged by the medical establishment, there’s still need. People still desperately seek healers. They may not fully believe in magic or miracles, but between the choice of assured agony and a sliver of hope, many take the chance. Magic can achieve miraculous results. A master healer can cure cancers and speed up the healing of grievous injury beyond what should be possible, but they don’t advertise this. Even when so many are ignored and failed by the medical establishment, it’s illegal to practice medicine without a license. Beyond that, fame can be a heavy burden to carry. Many eager and empathetic hedge magicians begin learning the path of Healing, but few master it — they often burn out early from the cold and thankless world, switching to other Arts if they continue practicing magic at all.</p>\n<p>Though not always required by their Practice, many hedge magician Healers do learn some form of medicine. Those who don’t rely on extraordinary science often learn forms of healing not based on modern medicine. At the very least, in instances they do learn the basics of modern medical science, their approach to treatment can be wildly different in philosophy and technique.</p>\n<p>A healer might heal mild to moderate congenital illnesses or deformities, but more extreme examples such as regenerating limbs or raising the recently dead are the realm of legend (or simple Biothaumaturgy).</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Fast casting may only be used to stabilise Aggravated Health damage into Superficial equal to the Healing Aspect or margins if the target resists (Stamina + Resolve), whichever is lower. To heal Superficial or Aggravated Health or Willpower damage, rituals must be used. Willpower may only be healed through rituals.</p>\n<p>Undead like vampires may not benefit from Health healing of this Path, but Willpower healing functions like with mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> See above.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> </p>\n<p>+1 Difficulty to fix a badly healed wound +1 Difficulty to heal an uncooperative patient per patient +1 Difficulty to stabilise a Crippling Injury for a scene, negating its penalties</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Anatomy, Herbology, Surgery...</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing, Number of Targets, Range.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> A failure simply means the magic didn’t work; the damage was beyond the sorcerer’s ability to heal. A Botch can turn horrific quickly. A Healer might make the person more susceptible to the toxin they’re trying to fight off, increasing the damage the toxin deals. They might outright cause damage or heal something incorrectly, such as fusing eyelids shut or setting a bone crooked, inflicting a Crippling Injury, so that it needs to be re-broken later. The healer might also infect themself with the disease they were trying to heal. Those in a coma stay that way for longer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"314"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.9DAwcHIaPhGF0c7O"},"9K8OOykjdHkH9lJs":{"name":"Lazarus Rises","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual enables a necromancer to bring a freshly-dead body back to life, though not how its relatives and friends might remember it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> One human sacrifice, incense, the heart of any mammal, powdered silver.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer burns incense to perfume the air before performing an act of human sacrifice, cutting the heart of the victim out and replacing it with the heart of another mammal, though it doesn’t need to be stitched in and working for the Ceremony to function. After pouring a bag of powdered silver over the open eyes of the dying or dead mortal, the vampire invites a wraith to take the deceased mortal as a host. Wraiths cannot be forced to possess a body, but few refuse the opportunity to walk around in semi-living shoes again.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Following a successful Ritual roll (Difficulty 6), a wraith can enter the freshly-dead body and live in it as if it were their own. The wraith must be present during the act of sacrifice and needs not possess the ability to possess others on its own, making it useful for getting unexperienced ghosts into mortal bodies.</p>\n<p>The possessed corpse will wake bearing the wounds that killed it, though the replacement heart is functional (no matter its origin or placement) and the body heals one point of Health damage upon possession. The remaining Health damage recovers with time. The new creature heals like a mortal. The body possesses the same Physical Attributes, Disciplines of Celerity, Potence and Fortitude (if a ghoul), and Backgrounds it had in life. Social and Mental Attributes, Skills, and any form of morality rating match those of the wraith.</p>\n<p>This possession lasts indefinitely, or until the possessed body dies again or the wraith is exorcised from the host. The body gains no special resistances to harm beyond Disciplines it might have possessed in life.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"349"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.9K8OOykjdHkH9lJs"},"9T34Z2EgCl9I0x0K":{"name":"Maw of Ahriman","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The ritualist’s mouth or another orifice becomes a void of inky blackness, destroying inanimate objects placed within in a matter of seconds. For the duration of the Gift (until the character falls unconscious or wills it to end), A bite attack deals 1 Aggravated point of Willpower damage in addition to the fang damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> The ritualist cannot speak if the power was used on the mouth, as it most commonly is nor can they slake Hunger while this Gift is in effect, though, as any blood swallowed is annihilated.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"253"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.9T34Z2EgCl9I0x0K"},"9f8owkaKG65vcNy3":{"name":"Alluring","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Special Effect (••). Aspects: In addition to the Aspects of Number of Targets, Range, and Sympathetic Connection, Fascination uses the Allure and Willbending Aspects.</em></p>\n<p>Almost everyone at the gathering knows you were there, and individuals will try to find a way to see you again. You may make strong suggestions that align with the target’s personality and situation, such as heading home with the hedge wizard after a fun night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"306"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.9f8owkaKG65vcNy3"},"9twlYG4qCcnEUckE":{"name":"Weather Control (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Probably one of the oldest magical techniques practiced among many sorcerous traditions, the Path of Weather Control delivers what it promises — control over the weather. Masters of this Path may even call upon storms to strike their targets with deadly lightning.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>The sorcerer can use the Spell Test to summon, change or dispel weather according to the Aspects. Storytellers should feel free to invent and fiat different weather effects for the setting if they aren’t represented here.</p>\n<p>See the next page for sample weather mechanics.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance (treat the measurement as the radius of effect), Duration, and the following:</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Weather, Weather Manipulation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> </p>\n<h3>Weather</h3>\n<p>For more detailed weather systems and more options, see the next page.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Even intentional weather control effects might have unintended consequences on the environment both near and far.</p>\n<p>Weather pattern disruptions are no small matter. A failure on Botch, however, unleashes terrible effects depending on the nature of the changes sought.</p>\n<p>Lightning might directly strike the sorcerer (who must roll to dodge), something near them or their allies and set something on fire. An arid climate might be hit with a monsoon the ground can’t accommodate, leading to widespread flooding. The local climate and possibly the surrounding area suffers dramatic, perhaps long-lived, changes. This can cause a crisis for the local fauna, plant life, and people who had no time to prepare and may not know how to cope with the new climate. Death, even if not immediate, is unfortunately a common risk when sorcerers play with the weather.</p>\n<p>Weather control tends to only use rituals to enhance spells. The large number of successes required to produce effects lends itself to extended rolls and teamwork. Feel free to invent new additions for the Weather Aspect as it is impossible for one book to describe all the possible weather combinations.</p>\n<p>SAMPLE WEATHER MODIFIERS AND MORE EFFECTS Below are some sample Weather Modifiers and Effects. Sorcerer has access to all effects up to their own rating in Weather Control.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Fog or a light breeze:</strong> Vision is slightly impaired and sounds are muffled; a −2 dice penalty is imposed on all Awareness and combat aiming rolls, and the effective range of all ranged attacks are halved. Predator Types based in Stealth and surprise count their difficulties as lower by 1 as does corpse disposal.</p>\n<p>• +1 Difficulty or more to stop a severe weather (e.g. a thunderstorm) it’s started. Calm weather is easier to stir up than nasty weather is to allay.</p>\n<p>• +1 Difficulty to cause any effect indoors.</p>\n<p>• Fog or light breeze.</p>\n<p>••• Heavy sandstorm, high wind.</p>\n<p>•••• Blizzard, massive sandstorm, or a storm; calling down lighting from the sky.</p>\n<p>••••• Powerful meteorological activities such as a lightning storm or a tornado.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"357"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.9twlYG4qCcnEUckE"},"ABM6wvUnvaBUU9Ld":{"name":"Flight","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Rather than relying specifically on moving from point A to point B, this ritual grants the caster or an other subject the ability to fly at their normal running speed, whether using a traditional tool like a witch’s broom or magical boots to fly or some implement that’s smaller than the traditional means required of Conveyance like talismans, rings, etc.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A feather</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer burns the feather and inhales the smoke, taking on the property of birds to fly</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the sorcerer may fly without having to recast the Path Spell over and over again. They retain the same sort of maneuverability they’d have running at the same speed. Lasts until the sorcerer lets go of the implement or Duration expires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the sorcerer finds themselves unable to jump for the rest of the night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"277"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ABM6wvUnvaBUU9Ld"},"ACBgrmDxT1JpqUSg":{"name":"Detox","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The user reduces damage from poisons by one for each margin on the Ritual Test. Once the protection is spent or the night ends, the effect goes out.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"261"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ACBgrmDxT1JpqUSg"},"ADYqnO6zXhm0F6M1":{"name":"Alcohol enhancer","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>A powder that enhances alcohol. The drinker must roll Stamina + Resolve (Difficulty 4) or they fall asleep within turns equal to 5 minus the margin of failure. Vampires are resistant (-1 Difficulty) and are able to use Fortitude instead of Resolve. Even if they fail, they sleep for only ten minutes, minus one for each dot of Stamina or Fortitude they possess (whichever is higher). Werewolves in their human form, however, are more susceptible (Difficulty +1).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"261"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ADYqnO6zXhm0F6M1"},"AE7kgbGzswtX8u7X":{"name":"Smoldering Ruin","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The most common target of the Lure of Flames is an individual enemy. It’s directly offensive magic, and blood magicians know it and use it as such. Sometimes, targeting individuals and even groups is too small scale. Sometimes a magician really wants to just cut loose, and standard sorcery doesn’t offer very many outlets to do so. Masters of this Path, however, developed this Ritual in order to do just that: cut loose and destroy their enemies all at once.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Lure of Flames or Balefire</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range, Damage</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Coal</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer chants for a long while before dropping the coal onto the ground, igniting everything around them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On successful casting, everything and everyone within the radius of Range is set on fire. Targets taking damage must succeed on a Dexterity + Survival roll (difficulty 3) or catch fire (see Lure of Flames/Balefire system for continous damage). This includes the sorcerer, who must escape the area as well.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> This ritual is the definition of losing control. A Botch activates it, but the sorcerer catches flame instantly.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"287"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.AE7kgbGzswtX8u7X"},"AJeHWcSVn18RPu6M":{"name":"Soul of the Earth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The Gargoyle is now able to embrace animals, turning them into Gargoyle animals. If the Gargoyle was at least of the 10th Generation, the animal also gains a dot in Flight and automatically grows wings. The animal potentially goes through or rolls for a hunger frenzy depending on the amount of Hunger the Gargoyle Offered (two Hunger should be enough to avoid this).</p>\n<p>The Gargoyle or the thaumaturge may permanently purchase the creature as a retainer for one dot for a small or a medium animal or two dots for a large animal. If the animal is not paid for, it will eventually escape or turn feral against its masters. Treat it as a vampire with a blood potency of 0 for the purposes of feeding, blood surging and mending with small animals having a maximum Hunger of 1, medium 2 and large 3. The animal suffers from the standard vampiric curses, the Beast, the Hunger and the takes damage like a vampire. Like a vampire without the blush of life, it has no heartbeat and looks sickly. The Hunger the animal consumes upon awakening is negligible, but must still be feed as it craves blood nonetheless and can perform actions to raise Hunger.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"302"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.AJeHWcSVn18RPu6M"},"AgEdDu0pHxlfJdvo":{"name":"Witches’ Runes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>In a given space, only the witch who cast them may can use their magic. This ritual is said to have been invented during the Massasa war when the Tremere shocktroops would lure mages into an area that where this ritual has been cast. The mages would be unable to use their magic while Tremere Gargoyles and other troops would slaughter all of them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (as a radius), Duration (ranks count as doubled),</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write with, traditionally the blood of the sorcerer for the Tremere</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer marks area in a radius with glyphs of countermagic. The area of Distance must be somehow enclosed by these runes, either by doorframes or trees in the woods.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the Ritual Test, the full weight of Countermagic is brought upon everybody but the sorcerer (and those whose blood was mixed into the material used in the process). The total number of successes on the ritual roll act as rolls on unweaving (p. XX).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"300"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.AgEdDu0pHxlfJdvo"},"ApyDQJECXVjTQ0Dx":{"name":"Heavy fog, rain, sandstorm or snow","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>As Fog or a light breeze, Drive and Awareness and other perception rolls are impaired to a much greater extent; inflicting a -3 dice penalty. Due to many people preferring to stay indoors unless their duties compel them, all predator type which base themselves on separating others from groups (Siren, Spider, Grim Reaper…) count their difficulty as higher by one. Additionally, cold makes mortals more sickly who count their successes for surviving harmful feeding as lower by one.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ApyDQJECXVjTQ0Dx"},"AskqceHEs15b1pEO":{"name":"Half-Shift","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Shapeshifting Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration, Scale and Subject described below. If the sorcerer is transforming others, then Range is also an Aspect.</em></p>\n<p>the magician can either change half of their body to an creature’s or take a form halfway between human and creature (or human and human?). Physical Attributes may be swapped for that of the creature’s statblock if they’re at a higher rating. It is possible to take the half shape of other humans.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"351"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.AskqceHEs15b1pEO"},"AtC1FOTMxhrMI5jS":{"name":"Secure the Sacred Domain","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This potent a warding ritual can be used to close off an entire castle. One glyph, painted at the exact center of a haven, a castle or tower, secures all windows, portcullises, doors and other portals from all entry or exit. A single Tremere held off an entire army of Tzimisce war-ghouls in this manner for one night before the reinforcements arrived during the Omen war.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Bar the Common Passage (•) ritual</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (as a radius),Protection (ranks count as tripled), Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The ritualist’s blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer paints the glyph with their blood, commanding or politely asking the domain to raise defenses and prepare for an assault.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, all windows, doors, portals and existing breaches will close with the existing breaches being covered by quickly assembled barricades of spare parts (or plain magic). No portal may be lockpicked as the very spirit of the door is commanded not to open except for the sorcerer and those they consider allies (designated at beginning of the ritual). Parts of the warded building may be destroyed, but everything in the castle also benefits from Protection as if from Bar the Common Passage, even walls.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the sorcerer might become trapped within their own defenses as doors refuse to open to them.</p>\n<p>The aura of the area can be perceived with Sense the Unseen rolling against a difficulty of only 3 due to how blatant this ritual is. The glyphs must be uncovered, unsmeared and untouched for the effect to continue. Smearing one with dirt from feet is enough to break the ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the ritual succeeds but only against the sorcerer who becomes a target of their own ritual and their magic is nullified (each 1 counts as an additional success on the ritual).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"299"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.AtC1FOTMxhrMI5jS"},"B1PVgLA45zLL38yb":{"name":"Viscera Garden","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Blood sorcerers can grow Blood-addicted plants in special fields, using the crops as magical components or for enjoyment. It’s also a great way to get rid of a body, if the Blood sorcerer has the space. This ritual is believed to have originally been created by the Bahari, but knowledge of it and how the Banu Haqim grow their kalif plants was combined by sorcerers of many schools to create this one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A plot of land, a day-old human or animal corpse, the caster's Blood (for upkeep).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster clears out a plot of land and digs a hole. They tear open their wrist and pour a Rouse Check’s worth of Blood into the human or animal corpse. The corpse being a day old is essential in the human corpse’s case; accidental Embraces have occurred when a human corpse is too fresh. The corpse is eviscerated, and its mass thrown into the hole. The caster plants or transfers their preferred plants to the plot, then covers them in the corpse’s viscera.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A win on the Ritual Test (Difficulty 3) instantly disintegrates the buried body and viscera into blood-scented smoke. In addition, plants grown in the plot are unusually hardy and vampires can ingest them (consuming these plants as food slakes no Hunger, but it stays down). Vampiric influence Discipline tests against a mortal who ingested a viscerated plant take −1 to Difficulty (or the target loses two dice from their resistance pool, for contests). The plants must receive a Rouse Check’s worth of Blood every month, or else they wither and die within a week. A critical win extends the length of time between waterings to two months. An animal corpse can fertilize a Viscera Garden of about 1 x 1 meters, or about 3 x 3 feet. A human corpse doubles these dimensions.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"354"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.B1PVgLA45zLL38yb"},"B95xVmeblGBZfia4":{"name":"Armour of Achilles","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All of the targets in range reduce incoming superficial damage (before halving) by 3 and reduce aggravated damage by 1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"268"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.B95xVmeblGBZfia4"},"B9imbtyz19t75f8b":{"name":"Banish","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>On a win against Composure + Resolve of targeted ghosts within the line of sight, the ghosts are forced to flee the area for the rest of the Duration.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.B9imbtyz19t75f8b"},"BMHc24V7aoxNPopw":{"name":"Detection Blocker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An amulet hiding the user from magical perception. When worn, it provides two dice to resist any supernatural power trying to detect the user, read their aura or thoughts. If there is no contest, the amulet subtracts two dice from the power’s user instead Each use requires the wearer to solve a new riddle, puzzle, or enigma before it can be used again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.BMHc24V7aoxNPopw"},"BMWZKhlKJzMgFS9O":{"name":"Brotherhood of Mystics","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyss Communion Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Groups practicing Abyssal magics have each developed this ritual out of necessity: those who have given themselves over to the Abyss are both mistrusted and hated so it was important to be able to approach another Shadowed without fear of accidentally alarming a wrong person.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The mystic gazes upon their target in the vicinity of a shadow and asks the question “Do they serve the Abyss?” under their breath</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Only those who adhere to the Road of the Abyss or one of its Paths may perform this ritual with a success. On a win of the Ritual Test, the ritualist hears the answer from a shadow that only they may hear. “Yes”, if the subject adheres to the Road of the Abyss (or one of its Paths, it is the same Abyss nontheless) and “no” if they do not.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch the Ritual proceeds but the voice from the shadows scrapes the user’s ears.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"251"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.BMWZKhlKJzMgFS9O"},"BU6n2Hx1yPUaKkab":{"name":"Cloak of Silence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A cloak that hides the movements of the user. It allows them to move silently and undetected even across squeaky floors. Modern legends say it was developed to defeat the Nightingale floors of Nijo Castle. The wearer gains the effects of Unseen Passage (Obfuscate 2) and Silentia Mortis (Quietus Animae 1).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.BU6n2Hx1yPUaKkab"},"BWM2PHwAAxwtfRd9":{"name":"Danubian Voices","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Originating in the Black Forest and passing through a dozen nations en route to emptying into the Black Sea, the mighty Danube has been the sustaining artery of countless empires for as long as people have dwelt upon its banks. And through all of them, the Tzimisce kept watch. The koldun know its true value, for the spirits of the river speak to them, passing on what they know or carrying messages for them in exchange for a taste of the vampiric sorcerers’ vitae.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Distance</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The koldun drips the requisite blood into a river, stream, or other flowing waterway. Even the runoff from thawing snow or a heavy rain is enough to make use of this power. If it is the Danube itself, or one of its tributaries, this rite has no cost. Then the koldun makes their Ritual Test after petitioning the water for standard five minutes.</p>\n<p>If the Ritual Test was a success, a spirit of the water appears to them and will serve them (such as they can) for a number of days equal to successes rolled. This duration may be extended any time before it expires by splashing the water with more blood; one Rouse Check’s worth of blood extends the spirit’s thralldom by an additional day.</p>\n<p>What the spirit can do is quite limited; they can converse with the kold un, sharing whatever they know, or they can give it a message to carry to someone or someplace else. The spirit will be more than happy to talk of what it knows; river spirits can be quite garrulous and the koldun is advised to help lead the spirit to the topics they’re most interested in lest they be regaled for hours with prosaic descriptions of surrounding countryside. The spirit is only capable of moving up and down their waterway (and upstream is much slower than down), so the recipient of the message must be someone it can encounter in its journey.</p>\n<p>Another method involves instructing the spirit to go to a place along its path and delivering the message to the first person it meets, though this obviously has its drawbacks as well. A koldun may also beseech a water spirit to remain in one place and watch for trespassers or other threats, then to move quickly along the riverbed to warn them of danger should any present itself.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"363"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.BWM2PHwAAxwtfRd9"},"BXeWTN3Zap1svLWz":{"name":"Benedictio Mortis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>By the blessing of death, the caster is able to assume the aspects of a cadaver, shedding many of their vampire weaknesses in turn. A corpse, after all, is not particularly affected by the rays of the sun, nor is it especially fearful of fire. Sunlight deals only Superficial Health damage and only if it directly touches the skin, allowing for cloaks to fully shield from sunlight. The character is also immune to stakes and Physical Impairment while in this state. Cannot be active alongside Benedictio Vitae.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"372"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.BXeWTN3Zap1svLWz"},"Bm6WpkADrlvQFPGT":{"name":"Theft of Vitae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects</em></p>\n<p>Performs a number of Feeding Actions on the targets equal to the Hunger Aspect, halved and rounded up, in a single turn by opening an arterial wound. (A target wearing full-body protection such as a body-cover suit merely bleeds ecstatically out into the suit.)</p>\n<p>Those under the effects of this power experience the Kiss.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Bm6WpkADrlvQFPGT"},"Bt5vNtDTkPCo96VB":{"name":"Pietrosu’s Hospitality","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Mount Pietrosu is the highest, and most dangerous, peak in the Carpathians, where the biting winds and frigid temperatures will kill an unprotected man in mere moments. A perfect setting to destroy one’s enemies. Getting an unprepared foe up Pietrosu is quite difficult, however; much easier, the kolduns reason, to take the mountain with them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None, but the Spell Test uses Damage, Duration and Range</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the sorcerer is able to activate Pietrosu’s Hospitality making a Rouse Check, tossing their blood into the air (unless they are standing within sight of the Carpathians, in which case no Rouse Check required), and performing a Spell Test using the Aspects of Duration, Range and Damage.</p>\n<p>After a successful Spell Test, a powerful, frigid wind arises all around the koldun. This wind causes Superficial damage equal to the Damage Aspect and numbs the bodies of anyone except the koldun caught within, reducing their movement by half and Dexterity pools by two unless they succeed on a Strength + Athletics Test (difficulty equal to the Damage Aspect). A useful side effect of this power is that the koldun is immune to the cold and force of wind while this is active; when trekking in mountains or other environs where wind may be a hazard, they need only activate Pietrosu’s Hospitality to ward themselves from danger.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"367"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Bt5vNtDTkPCo96VB"},"Btdz1ffH0LmgEqON":{"name":"Shapeshifting (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Even among primal magics, the ability to change into an animal stands out as exceptional. Many monsters haunting the night have the power of transformation, including those who are true shapeshifters. Hedge magicians who study the Path of Shapeshifting mimic a certain amount of their power, transforming first pieces of themselves and later their entire body in dramatic ways.</p>\n<p>Most think of this Path as turning oneself or others into animals. When shapeshifting magicians choose subtlety, they can be remarkable spies. They can make cosmetic changes to look like a different person, and then supplement their disguise with sharpened senses. They can transform into something more dangerous if their cover is blown.</p>\n<p>The caster may only take the shape of something that walks the earth without special Rituals. Meaning that dinosaurs are unavailable without the Megafauna Transformation Ritual, but smaller dragons are on the table during the Dark Ages until the dragons and the other “bygone” creatures are banished into the Umbra by the Concensus. Ancient sorcerers who’ve lived for a long time may possess extinct shapes that would dumbfound sorcerer and scientist alike.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Protean powers that change the shape or in other ways transform the body of the vampire also affect clothing, swallowed items, and other small (under a few grams) wearables. Protean does not affect larger carried items, including backpacks, duffel and sports bags.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, but paying the additional cost of a Willpower reduces shapechangeing to a Minor Action of -2 dice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Animal Weapons (when in shape), Shapeshifting</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Scale and Subject described below. If the sorcerer is transforming others, then Range is also an Aspect.</p>\n<h3>Scale</h3>\n<p>Each rank of Scale includes the previous one in its effect unless the caster does not wish it.</p>\n<h3>Subject</h3>\n<h3>Disparity</h3>\n<p>Disparity is an optional Aspect and it should be overseen by the Storyteller when mixing forms for acquiring Attributes and Skills. Typically, the best is taken from each.</p>\n<p>X Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The biggest risk of this Path is that the human mind is not equipped to change shapes. Every transformation incurs risk, but it’s greatest when making a full shift to an animal form or form of some inhuman creature. On the loss of the Control Test, the character’s mind becomes like that of an animal and only magics that remove shapes or heal Derangements may aid the sorcerer at that point. Difficulties of the Botchfor this Path and its Rituals is equal to the level of the ritual, halved and rounded up, rather than the full rating. There are no Control Tests to shift to humanoid forms.</p>\n<p>• Cosmetic changes only, such as eye color or growing hair.</p>\n<p>•• If an animal form’s features are taken, Bite Attack penalty no longer applies and the subject acquires the natural weapons of the creature if applicable. During the dark ages, this could gaining the ability to breathe fire like a dragon does. If the animal has multiple size classifications that alter their natural weapons, use the ones closest to the human in size. It is possible to take the shape of other humans with similar stature and build at this point.</p>\n<p>• The sorcerer themselves</p>\n<p>•• One person (not the sorcerer)</p>\n<p>••• Two persons</p>\n<p>•••• Three persons</p>\n<p>••••• Four persons</p>\n<p>•••• Only a single animal feature.</p>\n<p>•••• The magician can take on two unrelated shifts, such as the head of one animal and the body of another.</p>\n<p>••••• Three unrelated shifts are possible.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"351"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Btdz1ffH0LmgEqON"},"CAcpYtURoATSO7gj":{"name":"Weaving the Pontos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Kolduny who have mastered this rite situate their havens on beautiful beaches made to look like jagged rocks by enchanting the shallow waters to deter anybody from disturbing them. With Weaving the Pontos, the koldun truly becomes the master of their watery domain.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Tretchery of Pontos</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the Rite, the sorcerer’s Tretchery of Pontos Illusion Spell benefits from My World Ritual system (p. 300). If anybody ever perceives the illusions to be a lie, this rite is nullified and must be performed again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"366"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CAcpYtURoATSO7gj"},"CMdO1HcLHor95FsZ":{"name":"Blinding the Alloy Eye","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>By infusing a folded soft metal (usually aluminium) with abyssal energies, If successful (the Storyteller should keep the result of the test a secret), cameras around the caster will end up scrambling their image, though they will pick up everything else normally.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> Anybody who views the cameras will dream of the endless nothingness that is the Abyss, possibly alerting those in the know of what had happened.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"253"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CMdO1HcLHor95FsZ"},"CTMsFsc2svj9f3Gq":{"name":"Fix the True Form","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Shapeshifting Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Shapeshifters often find themselves in conflict with one another. They also run into stranger things with multiple forms. It forces anyone targeted by it to take on their “true” form if they. For magicians, and vampires they simply revert to their natural form, as do most other shapeshifters. Ironically, despite being created in part to force werewolves into their human or wolf forms, this ritual forces Garou and Fera to take on their mixed forms (also known as war forms) instead, often with terrible consequences for an overconfident magician.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Depends on the caster</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 5), the subject must oppose the Ritual Test’s successes with Stamina + Resolve or be transformed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"352"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CTMsFsc2svj9f3Gq"},"CZ0eyQypwLox4usI":{"name":"Tornado","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Avoiding getting sucked into a tornado is a Strength + Athletics (difficulty 5) test, Modified by things such as having something to hold on or having shelter. Those who fail suffer damage equal to 3 + the margins. The damage is Aggravated to most creatures save those naturally durable like vampires and werewolves.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"359"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CZ0eyQypwLox4usI"},"CgYV9woJorj9sNNo":{"name":"Mike’s Cure-All","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Healing Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The healer can force a patient’s body to purge all drugs and toxins through any, and sometimes all, possible exits. This includes vomiting, sweat, tears, diarrhea, etc. The process is incredibly unpleasant but can save a poisoning victim or someone who’s overdosed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Usually charcoal tea, but whatever manages to induce vomiting helps.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> By potion, spell, sound or some other instrument of healing sorcerer uses, the subject is lulled deep into sleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 4), the subject purges of all toxins and drugs.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"316"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CgYV9woJorj9sNNo"},"CrquYnAY76is9JCY":{"name":"Decay (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>The Blood of vampires brings ruin and none have shown it to be so more than the Tremere Antitribu which reinvented this Path after it was lost in the Purge of the Cappadocians. Hands of Destruction allows its practitioners to wither organic and inorganic material and age mortals in seconds towards their grave. When in hands of the Cappadocians or the Nagaraja who independently discovered the Path, its capabilities are elevated into channeling energies from the deepest pits of the Underworld.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>The Spell is resisted with Strength + Stamina.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Entropy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range, Weight (count as higher by one) and Time Period (count as higher by one):</p>\n<p>On a win, the target is aged, losing a dot in Physical Attributes for every decade after their 50th year. If this brings Stamina to 0, the victim dies from old age. Inanimate targets wither age as if they’ve spent the Time Period under harsh weather (replicated by the channeling of entropic forces). Plants die and corpses dissolve pretty quickly. After several castings, a master of Decay can bring down even brick walls. Spirits, ghouls, vampires and other immortals that are immune to aging or decay may not be directly afflicted, although ghouls are forced to burn through Hunger according to the Time Period to sustain their ghouldom as they would normally. Once out of vitae in their system, ghouls are no more than ordinary humans.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Toying with decay and the forces of entropy is never good. On a Botch, the sorcerer suffers a point of Aggravated Health damage for each 1 rolled. Those immune to aging like ghouls and vampires are immune to this, however.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"280"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CrquYnAY76is9JCY"},"CyZgidcNyjKhZUho":{"name":"Guest’s Herald","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual places a notification ward on doors or other portals, creating a sound or some visual effect that notifies those close enough to hear or see it whenever a person other than the cast crosses or passes through it</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Caster’s blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist draws the glyphs of ownership in the shape of a star.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the blood disappears and the alarm is set that will produce an effect once something or someone passes by it (it has a radius of around 2 meters) other than the caster. The effect be visual, audial or anything the ritualist imagines. It can be something that is heard by those nearby or silent. For example, small fireworks or a sound of chiming bells. Either way, the ritualist will instinctively know when the alarm has been triggered regardless of the distance and the type of an effect the alarm has.</p>\n<p>Once set, the effect cannot be changed. The alarm glyph lasts according to the Duration aspect. The caster may dispel it simply willing of it. This ritual is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, including the sorcerer, who probably will get annoyed by this ritual very quickly.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"295"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CyZgidcNyjKhZUho"},"CyjLPfycvPGg1vei":{"name":"Somnifacient","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Create a powdered or liquid sedative. The compound can be inhaled, ingested or absorbed through skin contact (often applied to a weapon or a projectile). To throw or blow it, Composure + Aim is used vs. Dexterity + Athletics. When exposed the target must immediately succeed at a Stamina test (difficulty 3), or fall unconscious. Spending a point of Willpower automatically succeeds the test. On a success, they remain awake but are drowsy (-1 to all actions). If knocked unconscious, the target sleeps for one hour per success and cannot be woken outside of taking Aggravated damage. If resisted, the target must test against each turn for a number of turns equal to the successes rolled. Each failed test increased the drowsy penalty by -1 (max -3). After the duration expires, the drowsiness fades over one scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.CyjLPfycvPGg1vei"},"D3C4gCCgRLUu4DKF":{"name":"Summon Plant Spirits (Naturae)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ephemera Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Reaching into the Middle Umbra, the caster calls upon spirits of plants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets, Metaphysical Weight 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An offering the spirit</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer presents the offering and chants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, caster may send psychic impressions in order to call all closest spirits of plants up to the Number of Targets as described under Ephemera Sample Rituals.</p>\n<p>Note that calling upon a general specific type of a spirit requires two dots in Ephemera Path as described under Spirit Reach. Otherwise, the Ritual fails. Plant spirits tend to manifest as walking Paths (look at the Green Path for statblocks).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"304"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.D3C4gCCgRLUu4DKF"},"DDSaqn746EpTyMXG":{"name":"Apollo’s Aim","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range ignore Called Shot penalties for any ranged maneuvers.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DDSaqn746EpTyMXG"},"DKdGaU2dOP9PkZ26":{"name":"Rebirth in the Light of Haqim","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Each Child of Haqim is initiated to the clan after Embrace with blood from the collective pool called the Heartsblood hidden in Alamut. Their head is anointed with the Blood and their true purpose calls to them. It is only at this point that they are fully welcomed as a warrior, sorcerer, or vizier, as the Blood of the entire Clan decides their fate. Many consider this to be the will of Haqim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Dur-An-Ki</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A Rouse Check’s worth of blood from the Heartsblood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The initiate is anointed with the Blood, usually with a little ceremony of welcome</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the vampire of Banu Haqim may change their caste (a player selects their own while for everybody else it is according to the abilities of the vampire and the Will of Haqim.</p>\n<p>This rebirth breaks any existing Blood Bonds. It can also effectively change the character’s Clan Disciplines and Clan bane; any Disciplines that become out-of-Clan accrue an “experience debt” that must be paid off with incoming experience points. Newly in-Clan Disciplines are purchased at in-Clan costs and any overspent exp is refunded.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"328"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DKdGaU2dOP9PkZ26"},"DMZLlU2exrvuBcQ3":{"name":"Fog or Light Breeze","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Vision is slightly impaired and sounds are muffled; a −2 dice penalty is imposed on all Awareness and combat aiming rolls, and the effective range of all ranged attacks are halved. Predator Types based in Stealth and surprise count their difficulties as lower by 1 as does corpse disposal.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DMZLlU2exrvuBcQ3"},"DiSgGrmWnD771Fnp":{"name":"Last-Minute teleport","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Almost identical to the Extraction ritual of the Path of Conjuring, it is also a ritual frequently hung “just in case”. It allows the sorcerer to take fallen or overwhelmed companions out of battle and to safety. The sorcerer first moves out of range of combat, typically behind cover, and pulls their companions out of a shadow.</p>\n<p>Once the sorcerer and their companions are out of range, they aren’t counted as being in combat unless a combatant finds them and attacks.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Identical to extraction ritual (page prior).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"277"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DiSgGrmWnD771Fnp"},"DmMWoPznRSthmYCE":{"name":"Purification of the Inferno","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Practiced extensively by the battle magi of the Clan Tremere during the wars with the Tzimisce, Purification of the Inferno punishes with flames the first person to physically touch the sorcerer.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Lure of Flames or Balefire</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Damage, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write or sew with, usually just the caster’s blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer inscribes a glyph of fire and curses offenders</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the enemy-repellant is ready. The first person to touch the magician after the ritual (by accident or design) immediately bursts into flames and takes flat damage equal to half the Damage Aspect (rounded up), including continuous damage each turn until the fire is doused (Composure + Survival/Science, Difficulty 3) as flames burst into their direction. If the sorcerer is using Balefire as base, then half (rounded up) of damage is dealt as additional Aggravated Willpower damage.</p>\n<p>The ritual lasts until triggered, dispelled or until Duration expires. Note that the sorcerer still takes damage as normal when struck. Being “touched” with a sword does not activate the ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Botching the ritual still “succeeds” but the sorcerer will trigger it and set themselves on fire.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"287"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DmMWoPznRSthmYCE"},"DojDaZf5upJ40ZAD":{"name":"Host Spirit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual allows a human to open their body to a symbiotic possession by a ghost.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A gift to be made as tribute to a wraith (whether the wraith values it depends on the individual), a parasitic bug, and two teeth extracted from the vampire’s mouth. If a mortal is using this Ritual, they can give pieces of hair: ghosts know that vampires can regrow fangs, so human teeth are much more valuable.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster must be in close proximity to a wraith in order to use this power, typically through summoning it. The caster may target another person (any kind of humanoid can work, even werewolves) within the line of sight to be the ghost’s recipient, but the person must be willing. The necromancer presents a tribute to the wraith, sometimes in the form of alcohol poured on the wraith’s gravesite, or a bag of coins to be buried in the earth, or even the freshly decapitated head of one of the wraith’s until recently living enemies. The vampire then pulls two teeth from their mouth, usually with pliers, and bites into a parasite with their remaining teeth. The vampire then opens their mouth and the wraith can choose to enter it, inhabiting the vampire’s body. See Ingredients for how a non-vampire performs this part.</p>\n<p>The benefits of having a wraith ride one’s body include an enhanced physique, access to whichever memories the wraith chooses to share, and the wraith’s voice offering the subject advice that can only be heard by the subject. The wraith can take complete possession of the subject if they wish to, which some necromancers view as a blessing to be experienced, and others deem the main reason not to use this power.</p>\n<p>Allowing a wraith to control one’s actions for a night effectively subdues the Beast, as well as demonstrating physical prowess and knowledge the vampire may not usually possess.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make a successful Ritual roll. If the wraith agrees to the proposition, it then enters the willing subjects’s body and can remain according to the Duration. The subject can hear the wraith in their head, offering advice, and they can substitute the wraith’s Skills and/or Specialties for their own, at the Storyteller’s discretion. The subject also gains a succesful die against enemies’ weak spots as if using Deathsight (See Eyes of the Dead Necromancy Path or Ghosts, p. XX) due to the ghost offering advice.</p>\n<p>A wraith can choose to assert its possession instead of acting as a passenger. If the subject resists, they make a Resolve + Composure roll vs. the wraith’s Resolve + Composure. If successful, they reject the wraith’s influence. If failed, the wraith steers the subject until the end of the scene, though it can’t make the subject do anything self-destructive.</p>\n<p>If the subject is a reanimated cadaver, it may be possessed as described above.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"344"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DojDaZf5upJ40ZAD"},"DuS4irl5zrUfV6Ic":{"name":"Running Shoes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Shoes doubling the speed of the wearer when trying to outrun pursuit, giving them additional two points of Dexterity (up to 7).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DuS4irl5zrUfV6Ic"},"DvlVqXHvi1c7u6Ei":{"name":"Speed Potion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>A potion that doubles the running speed of anyone who takes it. At the end of the scene, the user rolls Stamina + Strength of Fortitude (difficulty 3) and on a failure feels out of breath for the next hour, suffering a +1 Difficulty on all physical rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"263"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DvlVqXHvi1c7u6Ei"},"DwJsX1fn2TEM3aH4":{"name":"Beguiling","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Special Effect (•••). Aspects: In addition to the Aspects of Number of Targets, Range, and Sympathetic Connection, Fascination uses the Allure and Willbending Aspects.</em></p>\n<p>Everyone assumes that you had a hand in setting up the party, and individuals around you will vie for your attention. You may make suggestions that don’t necessarily align with the target’s personality and situation, such as suggesting they break up with their longterm partner to give the caster of the spell a one-off chance.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"306"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DwJsX1fn2TEM3aH4"},"DxHd7pwQHqQGAnDl":{"name":"Minions of Deep Marmora","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Legend says that the koldun Cyzicus, who ruled openly as king of the Dollonians in days of antiquity, discovered if he spilled his blood into the Sea of Marmora, strange beasts from its depths would emerge to do his bidding. Other koldun have found that any body of water serves the same, although the creatures that dwell in the deeps of Marmora are the strongest.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Summoned Minion (see system)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The koldun splashes some of their blood on the surface of water. Upon the successful completion of the Rite, a number of minions emerge equal to the Summoned Minions Aspect. The Minions of Deep Marmora are strange humanoid creatures, combining the features of frog and fish. They will stay bound to the koldun according to the Duration Aspect, after which they are free to return to their watery abodes.</p>\n<p>Minions use the following stats (stats in parentheses are for those minions summoned from the Black Sea itself):</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Strength 4 (5), Dexterity 3, Stamina 4 (5), Charisma 1, Manipulation 1, Composure 0, Intelligence 1 (2), Wits 2 (3), Resolve 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 7 (8), Willpower 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Athletics 2, Brawl 4, Intimidation 3, Survival 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Disciplines:</strong> Obtenebration 1(Shadow Cloak)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Special:</strong> Minions possess claws that are a Strength + Brawl light piercing weapon with a damage modifier of +2. Minions of Marmora can breathe air or water equally well. They’re accomplished swimmers, moving as easily in water as out. They receive a +2 dice bonus on all swimming tests.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"365"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.DxHd7pwQHqQGAnDl"},"EFwI9lc1HBIkFGef":{"name":"Eyes of Charon (Giovanni)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster’s eyes are set ablaze with pale blue flame that is visible from the Shadowlands. The caster can see, hear, and speak to ghosts in their vicinity, whichever side of the Shroud they are on. The caster also adds 2 dice to attempts to non-supernatural attempts to terrify or intimidate ghosts in a mundane manner.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"331"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.EFwI9lc1HBIkFGef"},"EHdrQUmiF62W6tMk":{"name":"Trusty Firearm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A custom Aim Skill weapon subtracting two from the success penalty of all called shots.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.EHdrQUmiF62W6tMk"},"EM1LP2he7RnGQDwI":{"name":"Verdant Haven","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets (individual statblock as targets), Range and Green Power</em></p>\n<p>Create a physical and mystical shelter of branches, leaves and vines. Health is equal to twice the successes and it blocks out sun from reaching those within. Due to constant regeneration, it heals 4 health per turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"353"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.EM1LP2he7RnGQDwI"},"ETWbW4OvJN4SjgKt":{"name":"Supernatural creatures","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Biothaumaturgy Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Scale is unknown, but there is a tale told among Tremere apprentices of Goratrix operating on dragons.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"369"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ETWbW4OvJN4SjgKt"},"EigAvUJsQ67zR2It":{"name":"Cruel Whispers","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Illusion Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Illusionists must often be masters of psychological warfare. They can’t create anything truly real, so they play on the fears and anxieties of their targets to control them. One of the key rituals for this is Cruel Whispers. A purely auditory illusion, Cruel Whispers follows its target around for twenty-four hours. Though the magician may never know what the whispers are saying, the victim hears voices, just barely audible, pointing out every flaw, every insecurity, and ever misstep the</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Damage (cannot be greater than Senses; cannot be used if Duration isn’t at least 3), Duration, Number of Targets, Senses (only auditory is required, but more can really deal some psychological damage)</p>\n<p>If used away from the presence of the target, the ritual requires Sympathetic Aspect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing save for some sort of gesture with the Sympathetic Material</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Depends on the caster</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the target resists with Wits + Intelligence. If they fail and the Duration permits it, the subject may roll their Test again (usually at the end of each day). If the subject wins, the ritual’s hold shatters. If not, the ritual goes on.</p>\n<p>For the Duration, the victim suffers Mental Impairment and experiences hallucinations according to the Senses Aspect. At the end of each night (if the Duration was at least 3) they suffer Superficial Willpower damage equal to twice the Damage Aspect or the margins on their Wits + Intelligence. This damage cannot be made Aggravated nor unhalved.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the storyteller makes the Test for the subject in secret and spreads damage over 24 hours/as long as the victim is awake.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the illusions are created but only the sorcerer may perceive them. Such illusions may trigger a Frenzy as the sorcerer cannot separate them from reality.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"317"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.EigAvUJsQ67zR2It"},"EkqmjflnsRicU951":{"name":"Mortuus in Vultu","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (•). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>Inducing the corpse-appearance to the subject and stiffness of the body, the caster lowers Dexterity by one and the base Looks Merit by one rank (normal to Ugly, Ugly to Repulsive). The caster also gains half rounded up successes of the spell test as a bonus to pretend to be a corpse with Composure + Subterfuge roll. This may affect others, using Range and Number of Targets aspects. Instead of Range, Sympathetic Connection may be used.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"371"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.EkqmjflnsRicU951"},"Eng2bTmM1X8xR3XE":{"name":"Battle (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Honed by the Banu Haqim in ancient times of war, the Path of Battle is a peculiar one for sorcerers to possess as most of them are not front-line generals. The Path is used to inspire others, give them confidence and influence their perceptions to be better soldiers on the battlefield of war or the battlefields of social skirmishes of etiquettes in high places.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Targets must be able to hear the caster. The caster cannot target themselves with Spells of this Path, but Rituals may allow it. Subjects under the influence of this Path may spend a number of Willpower equal to the margin on the failed contest to cancel the effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full (maintaining each Special Effect counts as a -1 Minor Action which may cumulatively stack)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Inspiring</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets, Range (as a radius), and the following:</p>\n<h3>Inspiration</h3>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> When a Botchfails, the sorcerer still applies the effect, but at a damaging extent and each friendly subject takes unhalved Superficial Willpower damage equal to the margin of failure on the test. Botchon spells against enemies simply fail.</p>\n<p>Most of the time, Rituals of this Path have the same effect as Spells, but allow for the caster to inspire themselves or to inspire a greater number of people by channeling greater power.</p>\n<p>•••• range count their physical attributes as one higher (up to the caster’s Path rating).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Eng2bTmM1X8xR3XE"},"Er3H8wIAfgGlG6fU":{"name":"Animate the Inanimate (Lesser)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An animated servant (also called an automaton), such as a golem, a corpse or a robot, build it as an Average Mortal. It does not suffer Physical or Mental Impairment penalties and does not heal damage (although the Ritual Test can be reperformed to fully heal them). It doesn’t need to eat or sleep, though most robots require some sort of recharging. Automata generally take only Superficial damage except from things that would obliterate inanimate material depending on what it is made out of. To permanently animate them, they must be bought as one-dot retainer otherwise they fall apart at the end of a story.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Er3H8wIAfgGlG6fU"},"EyzamJUXTvuZxM08":{"name":"Enter the Spirit World","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Genius Loci Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The kolduny are masters of spirits, and a Genius Loci is the lord of the world. With this ritual, the koldun may enter the spirit world known as the Astral Umbra and middle worlds between it and the material world. Several mystics of the Tzimisce have performed this rite and never got out, choosing to lord over the spirits (how they get sustinence is usually not told in the tile).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but the standard will and blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Two methods for this Ritual exist with two different results. First is to get the koldun and others physically into the spirit world. That method requires a portal found in nature or constructed from natural material that is conscecrated by blood and will in the name of the spirits.</p>\n<p>The other method involves the koldun and others meditating at a place of a Sanctum (Natural Sanctum, Glyphs of Power, Spirit Presence).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Rite (minimum Difficulty is 6 if not made higher through Aspects), the effect depends on how the process was performed. If the portal was consecrated, it begins to glow in pale light and stepping through it will physically bring the Passenger Aspect’s worth of people into the spirit world physically. The portal method uses Duration for how long the portal stays open and it closes after the Duration expires or the Passengers enter through it. Returning from the spirit world requires a point of Willpower point spent by each person at the entrance they came through to pass back into the physical world.</p>\n<p>If the ritaul was accomplished by meditation (which requires no Duration Aspect), the Passengers all experience the effects identical that of Anima Walk (Auspex •••••). Returning is as simple as following the silver cord back to one’s own body. Note that like with Anima Walk, the user cannot reach the parts of the Spirit World that lie beyond the Moon, making the portal method required to converse with the mightiest of spirits. Rolls for navigating described under Anima Walk need to be rolled only the person acting as the guide — the rest can follow blindly.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"362"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.EyzamJUXTvuZxM08"},"F5xvlcORxGb0Xj1z":{"name":"Gravity Denial","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-conjuration.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conjuration Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Known as “Fuck Newton” among the Anarch sorcerers, Gravity Denial allows the sorcerer to deny or alter gravity within a certain space.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (as a spherical radius)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer chants and names themselves as the authority of the world and its laws.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the ritual roll, the sorcerer may remove gravity from an area or shape it as they wish such as making it “centered” on a wall to allow them to walk or on a wall.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, gravity pushes back. While nothing happens to others, the sorcerer’s Athletics rolls will always yield 1 regardless of the number of successes for the Duration.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"276"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.F5xvlcORxGb0Xj1z"},"F9dayzErlu0elkgm":{"name":"School: Akhu","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-akhu.png","system":{"description":"<p>Akhu, often called Setite Sorcery by outsiders, is the ancient magical tradition of the Followers of Set. In Egypt's heyday, before its conversion to Christianity at the hands of the Roman Empire, magic was pretty much commonplace, practiced by mortal and vampire alike. Egyptians had several words for magic; the Setites used Heka (literally “magic”) to refer to mortal magic, and Ahku (“spells”) for their blood magic.</p>\n<p>Practitioners of Akhu are known as Lector Priests, and they believe their magical power is drawn from Set himself. Their practices are near-identical to those of mortal Egyptian sorcerers, involving images, effigies, stories, names and words. Their most important tool, however, is the blasphemy-shrine, a ritual chamber in which the lector-priest defiles the bodies and belongings of the dead in an emulation of Set’s blasphemous dismemberment of his murdered brother Osiris. This perversion of traditional Egyptian burial customs unleashes power which fuels the lectorpriest’s spells, allowing them to perform sorcery without the expenditure of Vitae. The magic need not be performed at the shrine, but it must be maintained for the magic to work.</p>\n<p>Multiple variations of Akhu exist due to the Followers of Set being so widespread and a part of almost every religion. For example, the Witches of Echidna tie their magic to Echidna instead of Set, seeing Set only as one of her faces. Nonetheless, all variations of Akhu share the same practices to make them grouped into one school as they share the same principles and paradigm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<h3>Practices of the Lector Priests</h3>\n<p>Shrines of Akhu are often Blasphemy Shrines or simply shrines to Set, Echidna or one of their perceived variants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Shrine and Natural Sanctum Shrines of Akhu are often Blasphemy Shrines or simply shrines to Set, Echidna or one of their perceived variants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Dedicated: Lector priests draw their power from their beliefs and what they claim are holy symbols (to them). A sorcerer of Akhu cannot reroll dice on the Spell or Ritual Tests by spending Willpower. They may, however, reroll a number of dice (excluding Hunger) equal to the rating of the benefiting Sanctum when casting magic.</p>\n<p>• Devout: At its core, Akhu is magic that is based on religion and usually considered gifted by a diety. If a spell or a ritual is not benefitting from a Sanctum Background, the sorcerer counts as having rolled an additional 1 for Botches. If they are benefiting from a Sanctum, a single 1 is negated for Botches.</p>\n<p>• Exiled: Ironically, Akhu is the weakest in its place of origins as a result of the Eternal War with Horus during which immortal sorcerers of Horus and Isis cursed Set and his magicians. Count all Aspects as one lower to influence the state of Egypt and its officials in whatever form it takes.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Akhu</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy p. XX</p>\n<p>• Battle (Valour of Sutekh) p. XX</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces</p>\n<p>• Enchantment P. XX.</p>\n<p>• Conjuration (Path of Ptah)</p>\n<p>• Conveyance (Path of Ptah)</p>\n<p>• Corpse in the Monster (Blessings of Anubis)</p>\n<p>• Corruption (Revelations of Eden/The False Heart) p. XX</p>\n<p>• Divination</p>\n<p>• Ephemera Ephemera of Akhu has access to both the Metaphysical Weight (Naturae) and Metaphysical Weight (Ghost) with the latter becoming more popular due to its association with the Duat.</p>\n<p>• Fear (Apophis’ Terror)</p>\n<p>• Fortune (Path of the Dry Nile) p. XX, Also known as Blinding Chango among the Serpents of the Light.</p>\n<p>• Illusion (Ra’s Farce)</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood (Revelations of the Blood)</p>\n<p>• Necromancy (Revelations of Duat)</p>\n<p>• Reanimation (Path of Anubis)</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting (Soul of the Serpent)</p>\n<p>Almost always, primary shapes of this Path are of serpents.</p>\n<p>• The Green Path (Revelations of Geb)</p>\n<p>• Mastery Over the Mortal Shell (Vengeance of Khnum)</p>\n<p>• Weather Control (Breath of Set) p. XX</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"228"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.F9dayzErlu0elkgm"},"FLYJpEMesR3GgPuF":{"name":"Entrancing","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Special Effect (••••). Aspects: In addition to the Aspects of Number of Targets, Range, and Sympathetic Connection, Fascination uses the Allure and Willbending Aspects.</em></p>\n<p>Invitations or not, the party is held for you by everyone’s estimation. Individuals no longer just want your attention, they crave your approval, possibly making fools of themselves in the process. You may make strong suggestions that don’t align with the target’s personality or interests, such as suggesting that the target fight another to defend the magician’s honor.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"307"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.FLYJpEMesR3GgPuF"},"FSRFuklcFabXBPsI":{"name":"Vampire Durability","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The target takes damage like a vampire, including from Sunlight. Bane Severity counts as a minimum of two. Uses vampire blood, but it is purified in a way so as to not make a ghoul out of the drinker unless that was the intent.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"266"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.FSRFuklcFabXBPsI"},"FnDCjuVo7I0IKhQm":{"name":"Minor Abyssal Possession","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>By beckoning a small spirit of the Abyss to enter a corpse, the ritualist turns it into an Mindless Corpse similar to that of necromancy (p. XX).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> When a corpse is raised in this manner, it is automatically hostile to everybody, but the sorcerer binds it to their will as necromancers do zombies if they possess a dot of Binding, Summoning and Warding Path. If not bought as a Retainer (one dot should suffice for 6 mindless cadavers), the zombie becomes aggressive and uncontrollable after a story as the bond breaks.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"253"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.FnDCjuVo7I0IKhQm"},"Fwc05Xf18RoMB6vz":{"name":"Pontos Axeinos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Before the Greeks settled its southern shoreline, they called the Black Sea Pontos Axeinos, the Inhospitable Sea. For the enemies of the koldun, it can be very inhospitable, birthing storms, whirlpools, fog, and rocky barricades where there were none moments before.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the koldun is granted power over the weather of the Inhospitable Sea, represented by the Weather Control Path with its rating up to the Black Sea Kraina’s. The koldun may only evoke these effects when close to a large body of water or a source of water that eventually pours itself into a large body of water. Below are some weather samples.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"365"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Fwc05Xf18RoMB6vz"},"GAlwsoPQsErDAVlM":{"name":"Balefire (School: Dark Thaumaturgy)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The Effect is a variant of the Lure of Flames and functions identically save for the following. Fire’s colour is either green or blue, depending on the infernalist’s patron, and half (round up) of all Aggravated Health damage dealt to the victim is also dealt to their Willpower tracker as Aggravated Willpower damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"284"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.GAlwsoPQsErDAVlM"},"GDLrZQAqIB3UULSJ":{"name":"Boukephos’ Chosen Oubliette","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The abyss mystic must paint a circle of pitch around the perimeter of an aboveground community – whether a hamlet, village, town, or city. This circle is broken if crossed by a child, or a believer with True Faith. Once the circle is in place, the mystic must draw their own blood in its center, under the night’s sky, making 3 Rouse Checks as their vitae pools on the earth. The vampire must then spend one Willpower point, and proceed with the ritual which is designed to work as an extended test with a target number of 8 margins for a small community, 15 for a large community, and 22 for a huge population center. These margins must be accumulated in the course of one night, and the practitioner cannot be disturbed, otherwise the ritual will fail. A single failed roll also wipes out all accumulated progress. Upon the final margin, the pitch circle surrounding the community ripples, and forms tall waves that crash into whatever lies within it. An above-ground Oubliette forms, and everything within it shifts to the Abyss. Once the night has passed and the Oubliette dissipates, only those within the Oubliette who achieved a win on a Willpower Test (difficulty 5) remain, but they are permanently mentally scarred, manifesting as unheard of mental illnesses and a permanent halving (round up) of all Remorse rolls. Everyone else is taken to the Abyss, and will remain there for a number of nights equal to the Abyss Mystic’s Obtenebration rating, suffering the effects listed under the Oubliette Background.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> None.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"258"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.GDLrZQAqIB3UULSJ"},"GPkAYFPv1MgEJDUL":{"name":"Runes of Power","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Elders, paranoid of their apprentices reading forbidden tomes, often make use of this power to hide texts from them. Unwary neonates or intruders receive a nasty surprise when they read these words.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write with and something to write on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer writes a sigil of forgetfulness on a writing surface (or on a piece of clothing).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, a character who sees the sigil must make a Composure + Resolve roll against the successes on the ritual roll. On a failure, they forget whatever they’ve perceived in the last 10 minutes, including the identities of persons of the one who showed the sigil.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, including the sorcerer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"296"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.GPkAYFPv1MgEJDUL"},"GUfz2WwyMnBwztLS":{"name":"Summon Minor Jaggling (Naturae)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ephemera Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Reaching into the Middle Umbra, the caster calls upon a random Jaggling to come.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets, Metaphysical Weight 2, Spirit Reach 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An offering the spirit</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer presents the offering and chants.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, caster may send psychic impressions in order to call all closest Jagglings up to the Number of Targets as described under Ephemera Sample Rituals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"304"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.GUfz2WwyMnBwztLS"},"GxqrpDe70RfJ4Kwl":{"name":"Gift of the Immobile Form","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>The ritualist instinctively knows if the ritual was a success, enabling them to predict a disaster and death of their servant. When exposed to sunlight, the Gargoyle must roll a Stamina + Composure against a difficulty of 3. On a win, the Gargoyle becomes immune to sunlight for as long as they are stand still. If at any time of the day the Gargoyle suffers even a point of damage from sunlight due to moving, they may not use this power again until the following night. The ritual makes the Gargoyle immune to terror frenzy for as long as they stand still and suffer no damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"302"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.GxqrpDe70RfJ4Kwl"},"H7zrHXu0QJZ0UVJg":{"name":"Summon Selene (Naturae)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ephemera Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual summons a poweful Jaggling named Selene, one of the moon spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets 1, Metaphysical Weight 3, Spirit Reach 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An offering the spirit, traditionally an animal that has been hunted down like the ancient Romans did when venerating Diana.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer presents the offering and chants to Selene</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, caster may send psychic impressions in order to call Selene as described under Ephemera Sample Rituals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"304"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.H7zrHXu0QJZ0UVJg"},"HHoaRCszvqdJgwFw":{"name":"Earthquake","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>The ground splits open below Area (count as higher by two) and engulfs, then crushes, the victims while dealing damage (equal to margins on the first roll or twice the Damage Aspect, whichever is lower).</p>\n<p>If they failed on the Dexterity + Athletics test to dodge the effect, victims can roll Strength + Athletics to try to dig free. They need as many total successes as damage they took before halving. This cannot be made Aggravated. Creative uses of this Special Effect can topple buildings.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"285"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.HHoaRCszvqdJgwFw"},"HJBZvu9lwSZYawcY":{"name":"Contradict","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>Targets suffer a dice penalty equal to the sorcerer’s Path of Corruption to the action they are to perform next (to a minimum pool of one die) until they switch to another one, usually represented by changing of dice pools or changing their target. A critical win forces the target to switch to another action. Duration is always one turn/rank of one.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.HJBZvu9lwSZYawcY"},"HYFD53HU5tjlw7oU":{"name":"Herbal Wisdom","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>While not the best conversationalist in general, some plants do indeed have wisdom of the ages or just useful information about who passed by. Note that older plants may speak in a rythm that would be too slow for an impatient person to listen to the end, possibly scorning the plant.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets, Range</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Usually something to entreaty the plant with</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Depends on the caster, but simply watering the plant (if it desires water) is enough to entreaty the plant to speak.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 4), the plant may converse with the caster for the rest of the scene in a telepathic manner. If a point of Willpower is spent, the telepathic connection is permanent, possibly leading to talking plant pets.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"356"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.HYFD53HU5tjlw7oU"},"HcZQDZVPZMAtbRF1":{"name":"Gust of Wind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>The spell affects everybody between the caster and Range (from the caster for pushing, to the caster for pulling) and has the width of Area. Targets who fail resistance roll suffer damage equal to twice the Damage Aspect or margins, whichever is lower, and are pushed or pulled by a number of meters/yards equal to the damage they suffered.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"284"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.HcZQDZVPZMAtbRF1"},"I3qDOtwXiqeb2kXV":{"name":"Master of the Domain","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>All mortals must fear the Voivode. This rite allows koldun to take fear to the extreme, a perpetual aura of fear manifesting to make the vampire look large, dangerous, and constantly on the edge of frenzy. The Transylvanian Tzimisce use this rite to keep populations in fear and under control.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Once the rite’s complete, the koldun gains a permanent additional die to attempts at Intimidation against both mortal and Cainite subjects within their domain. Master of the Domain also prevents the victims of such bullying from using Willpower to reroll Tests to verbally defend themselves. The Rite can be undone by the koldun’s subjects witness the koldun lose a Test or a Contest.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"368"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.I3qDOtwXiqeb2kXV"},"I9A4xW6gxyRSalWs":{"name":"Glyph of Enlightenment","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Obviously created by the most paranoid of the sorcerers, Glyph of Enlightenment lets the thaumaturge utilise the glyph to send out their voice and even use their powers against enemies through it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Glyph of Scrying (lvl 2 ritual)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to inscribe glyphs with</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer inscribes a glyph of kings onto an object or a person.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual has the exact same system as the Glyph of Scrying except that the sorcerer can talk through it while focusing. They may also initiate powers for which they can fulfil the requirements.</p>\n<p>Stories say of elders who mastered Presence utilising Dominate through it by telepathy. Inscribing this ward on a baseball bat and handing it over to an ally who hits the enemy to activate your Cauldron of Blood is not exactly the most elegant usage of this power, but it works (and still takes your main action). This is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward oozes so much blood that it can only be used for a minute before it fully dissolves into blood.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"297"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.I9A4xW6gxyRSalWs"},"IHz4iz1jsRGWLWkD":{"name":"Dependence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>The victim feels no special emotions towards the sorcerer themselves and is just as likely to attack them as before, but they suffer a two-dice penalty on all non-tracker rolls unless they are in the sorcerer’s presence or doing tasks for them (attacking them also triggers the Dependence Penalty). The victim also halves (round up) their dice pools when rolling to resist powers of the sorcerer that sway or compel their will such as that of Presence or Dominate from the caster. Additionally, eye contact is not required for Dominate against the dependent victim. The victim only heals Superficial Willpower equal to the lower of their Resolve or Composure each night/session unless they have been in the sorcerer’s presence in the 24 hours prior.</p>\n<p>Every time you raise your Path rating, you may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than your Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.IHz4iz1jsRGWLWkD"},"IolBvEPOFXJTEvV0":{"name":"Wiseman’s Skull","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>The skull of a dead person (usually another sorcerer) allowing the user to contact them for assistance. The user “donates” at least one Rouse Check’s worth of blood to the skull and can ask one question per success on the Ritual Test. These questions need not be yes/no, and follow-ups (as determined by the Storyteller) do not count as additional questions. Usage requires a casting roll (Difficulty 1 plus the number of times the skull has been used, increase difficulty by 1 if it is not the New Moon). The skull crumbles to dust after the last question if the roll fails. Recently, rumors have begun to surface suggesting the skull of Saulot was recovered and enchanted in this way, though everyone has heard it from a friend of a friend.</p>\n<p>Mechanically, one question can be traded for three automatic successes on the next roll to learn a Path, Special Effect, or a Ritual assuming the owner of the skull knew it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.IolBvEPOFXJTEvV0"},"IpLSSd1r3pNmnUpX":{"name":"Cauldron of Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Victims takes Aggravated Health damage equal to twice the Hunger Aspect or margins on the roll, whichever is lower. A mortal who suffers a single point of damage from this effect dies after a few moments of screaming as their blood boils from the inside. Vampires and other durable creatures with hunger suffer an increase in Hunger equal to the Hunger Aspect or margins on the roll, whichever is lower</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.IpLSSd1r3pNmnUpX"},"JLVWCvKQaJ7NaFhU":{"name":"Ammorsus Vicarius","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>Animate skull to attack any creature that comes within range that is not the caster. The skulls attack with a dice pool of 6 and always count as a surprise attack if the victim didn't have any previous knowledge of their nature. They deal +2 unhalved superficial damage, as necrotic energy seep through the dead maws.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.JLVWCvKQaJ7NaFhU"},"JWHi7y5kaGxGKrFA":{"name":"Beasts of the Genius","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Genius Loci Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>When a koldun wants to see farther than her Genius Loci would usually allow, she can stand atop a mountain, tower, or other elevated location, and imbue an animal servitor with her Koldun Sorcery. Depending on the other Disciplines the koldun may hold, she may then command the animal to spy and return, or even observe through the animal’s eyes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance (counted as higher by one), Duration, Number of Targets, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but standard blood and will</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer pours the blood onto the ground, feeding the land and ending the ritual by pouring the activation Rouse Check’s blood onto the ground to seal their mastery over the animals of the land</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> While in effect for the Duration (or until the koldun leaves the location of the Rite), the koldun is able to extend their senses through animals affected by this Rite, all at once like a spiritual hivemind. Animals are selected by Number of Targets and must be within the Distance upon activation. The koldun may select species, rough area of location where they wish to focus, etc. to narrow down the power.</p>\n<p>The koldun will require a way of commanding or possessing the animal if they wish to have any influence on what the animal looks for, or reports back. Using Animalism, the user may directly influence the animal by telepathically communicating with it with powers like Feral Whispers. This Rite is broken if the animal is slain. The koldun may swap their skills for that of the animal when seeing through it.</p>\n<p>The Rite lasts according to the Duration Aspect, but the Rite’s effect ends upon leaving the location of the Rite.</p>\n<p>The koldun may invoke any koldunic power they possess anywhere they can perceive, so long as the level of that power is equal to or less than dots in their Genius Loci rating.</p>\n<p>Those using supernatural perceptions (such as Sense the Unseen) may roll against the Rite successes to perceive that there is a supernatural will residing in the animal.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"361"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.JWHi7y5kaGxGKrFA"},"Jccafiu1jerGHcg7":{"name":"Sprint","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Sometimes, a magician needs to travel a short distance quickly. Though preparations vary, mostly by practice, this Ritual always concludes by lacing up a pair of fast blue or red shoes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> See process</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer crates new shoes from scrap or enchants existing ones.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A win successfuly enchants the shoes. Once they are on, the magician can run faster than any mundane human, so long as no one sees them do so. Each success on the ritual doubles the running speed and grants one minute of running. At the end, the ritual leaves the magician exhausted, and they must rest for five minutes, halving all Physical pools (round up).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"277"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Jccafiu1jerGHcg7"},"JhlfzmCt7iEbFgMt":{"name":"School: Hermetic Thaumaturgy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-thaumaturgy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Originally, the House of Tremere were a house of hermetic magi using their own will to shape the reality. After their long and secretive ritual turned into vampires to escape death, the Tremere lost access to their old dynamic magic and found themselves powerless. It has been said that their founder, named Tremere, then quickly began reconstructing of their craft into a magical “Discipline” that could be powered by their new vampire Blood and would work similarly to mortal linear magic. It took him many years of research, experimentation and gathering but the result was Thaumaturgy, allowing him and his then-bloodline to call upon fire, create storms and manipulate other people’s blood by harnessing the principles of magic. Thaumaturgy became a closely-guarded secret of the clan and those who would teach those outside the Pyramid without permission were often punished with Final Death.</p>\n<p>Before the destruction of the Vienna chantry and the shattering of the Pyramid, Thaumaturgy was taught from master to apprentice in a manner not unsimilar to magical universities as Tremere chantries became centers of research, education and places of vast accumulated knowledge. There, their magic was strongest, powered by hermetic sigils and alchemical formulae, which allowed their Rituals to rival – or, if you ask them, surpass – even the other practitioners of magic like the Banu Haqim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> The Clan Tremere and its Pyramid hold Hermetic Thaumaturgy in a tight monopoly. Anybody else practicing it must take either Dark Secret or Shunned (Tremere) Flaw.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Nicknames:</strong> Blood Magick, Hermetic Thaumaturgy</p>\n<h3>Hermetic School of Magic</h3>\n<p>Tremere magic is a subset of hermetic magic as practiced by the diverse and fractious Order of the Hermes. The order exists because it allows its members to (imperfectly) share their understanding and learn from one another. The order in general, and the Tremere more specifically, rely on formulae and complex incantations to accomplish workings. They follow instructions in tomes; they prepare alchemical salves; they inscribe ancient symbols in circles of power. The Tremere are notoriously pragmatic in their outlook and their sorcery is systematic and almost scientific in character. Spirits are still summoned and chants chanted, but it is with the attitude of an engineer working a grand machine. Ironically, Thaumaturgy shares many limits of the Hermetic craft of mortal mages, namely the incompatibility of death-magics such as necromantic Paths due to entrenchment of Bonisagus’ prohibition against acts that defy God’s will within the principles of magic.</p>\n<p>To work magic, sorcerers concentrate their will, forcing the power of their blood to unnatural ends. If their concentration is not complete, if they falter, then the magic will fail, and in extreme cases such failure can have a lasting effect on the thaumaturge, draining their mental resources. This is usually why apprentices are first taught one of the Lesser Paths as they have more limited and expected outcomes and risks.</p>\n<p>Rituals of Hermetic Thaumaturgy are sophisticated and codified instructions for producing set magical effects not unlike formulas of science. They often require the elements of Hermetic magic – circles drawn in chalk, locks of the victim’s hair, meditation, chanting ancient words of power in classical languages, and the like. Rituals can take hours, days or even months to perform, but – if successfully completed – will always produce the same result. They can be incredibly powerful, particularly when senior thaumaturges join forces.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Hermetic Thaumaturgy is unsurprisingly mostly found among the Tremere, with most houses of Tremere from the House Tremere and the House Goratrix to the Anarch House Ipsissimus adhering to the Hermetic school. The members of the Tremere Pyramid viciously supress all non-Tremere magic usage, especially those who use Hermetic Thaumaturgy, and refer to them by the term “trespasser”.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sorcery Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Occult (Thaumaturgy or Hermetic magic specialty)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Control Tests:</strong> In addition to the specified results of failed Control Tests, thaumaturges suffer unhalved Superficial Willpower damage equal to the margin of failure.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Glyphs of Power, Natural Sanctum</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Empirical Approach: The House of Tremere’s approach to magic that created Thaumaturgy was, for its time, incredibly meticulous and scientific. If you have already cast a Spell or a Ritual within the same story and cast it again with the exact same aspects, negate a single 1 for a Botch. This does not stack cumulatively and the benefit is lost if the sorcerer casts a new spell or the same spell with different Aspect ranks.</p>\n<p>• Focus: Hermetic Thaumaturgy demands extreme concentration. If the thaumaturge is not suffering Mental Impairment, they negate a single 1 for a Botch. If the character is Mentally Impaired, they instead gain two 1s towards a Botch and cannot spend Willpower to reroll dice on magic rolls.</p>\n<p>• Principle of Will: Thaumaturges know that will is the greatest tool of a magus. If the thaumarge spends a point of Willpower to reroll dice on a spell/ or a ritual, reduce the Difficulty of that Spell or a Ritual Test by one to a minimum of 1. This may happen only once per Spell or a Ritual.</p>\n<p>• Thaumaturgical Principles: Sorcerers adhering to the Hermetic, Sielanic and Hacktivist schools of Thaumaturgy can learn from each other as if they share a school and even benefit the same Library or Mawla Backgrounds. They cannot learn their unique Rituals or Special Effects, however, as limitations of their schools still apply.</p>\n<h3>Nomina Magica</h3>\n<p>Names given to Paths follow the naming convention of the modern era. This means that they do not really have a standardised naming system as languages come and go while apprentices, future teachers, invent slang-names for their Paths. The names written in parenthesis are the names of Paths as they were called in the Dark Ages by the Tremere. These names generally combine what sorts of things they do (techniques) and what they do it to (forms). The most common techniques are control (Rego), creation (Creo), destruction (Perdo), perception (Intellego) and transformation (Muto.) Forms are far more varied and often vary from magus to magus; common ones include the body (Corpus), earth (Terram) and the senses (Sensus). It is from these arts that the Paths of Thaumaturgy derive and get their names. Keep this in mind when inventing new Paths for Thaumatury.</p>\n<p>Traditionalist elders often demand that others follow the naming convention from the Dark Ages as, to them, changing names dilutes power (or they are just stubborn and refuse to learn new names).</p>\n<h3>Paths of Thaumaturgy and Classification</h3>\n<p>During the Dark Ages, Tremere classified Paths that were often taught first to new students as the “Lesser” Paths (within the Hermetic curriculum). Spells and rituals of the Lesser Paths typically are a single or a couple of spells.</p>\n<p>In contrast, there were the “Greater” Paths that offered a wider and a more flexible set of abilities, most notably the Path of Blood which became the primary Thaumaturgical Path that would be taught first to apprentices as it complements the study of magic on a theoretical level and on the practical one due to the Principle of Blood, replacing the tradition of teaching the Lesser Paths first.</p>\n<p>Tremere magistri of the dark ages debated whether or not an apprentice should first master one of the Lesser Paths due to their simplicity or a Greater Path due to their versatility and room for experimentation. In time, the Path of Blood became the most commonly first-taught Path as it best demonstrated the principles of Thaumaturgy.</p>\n<h3>Lesser Paths</h3>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding (Rego Voluntatem)</p>\n<p>Metaphysical Aspects (Ghosts) cannot be selected. The Minor House called Covenant is attempting to rectify that hole in Hermetic Theory, but so far to no awail as they resort to using Mortis they’ve acquired from the Cappadocians in the 1200s for that purpose.</p>\n<p>• Biothaumaturgy (Muto Corpus)</p>\n<p>• Conjuration (Rego Motus/ Movement of the Mind)</p>\n<p>• Coveyance (Rego Locum/Path of Mercury)</p>\n<p>Until the modern era when it is relearned from the Setites, only regents and higher ups may learn this Path as others might use it to flee the Pyramid.</p>\n<p>• Decay (Perdo Res)</p>\n<p>• Divination (Intellego Futurum)</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces (Creo Ignem/Lure of Flames)</p>\n<p>• Ephemera (Rego Spiritus/Spirit Mastery)</p>\n<p>• Fear (Rego Timorem/Prey on the Soul’s Fear)</p>\n<p>• Illusion (Muto Imaginem/Faux Path)</p>\n<p>• Mastery of the Mortal Shell (Rego Corpus)</p>\n<p>• Weather Control (Rego Tempestates)</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting (Soul of the Serpent)</p>\n<p>Practiced only by the Eye of the Serpent, a group of Followers of Set hidden in the Pyramid. Mostly, forms of serpents are taken.</p>\n<h3>Greater Paths</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy (Alchemia or Muto Res)</p>\n<p>• Battle (Potestas Martis/Path of Mars)</p>\n<p>• Corruption (Creo Corruptionem)</p>\n<p>• Dreams (Potestas Morphei/Path of Morpheus)</p>\n<p>• Focus (Rego Mentem/Focused Mind)</p>\n<p>• Fortune (Rego Fortunam)</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood (Rego Sanguinem/Path of Blood)</p>\n<p>• Water Mastery (Rego Aquam/Neptune’s Might)</p>\n<p>• Technomancy (Rego Technologiam/Technomantia)</p>\n<p>• The Green Path (Rego Naturam)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"237"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.JhlfzmCt7iEbFgMt"},"JmBQ18FDvRvnF61Z":{"name":"Fearless Heart","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range count their resolve and composure as 1 higher (up to 5)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"268"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.JmBQ18FDvRvnF61Z"},"Jy99Zq1qzhEnQLtv":{"name":"Roll Divination secretly for players","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Divination Special Effect (•). Aspects: Divination uses only three Aspects. Time Period (past, present or future) and</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer never knows for sure how accurate their visions and interpretations of such might be. If the Storyteller rolls in secret, they may distribute remaining successes among Aspects based on the specificity of the Sorcerer’s question. In our Play by Post, we use this for story-related Divination rolls while players are allowed to roll for a mechanical bonus and ask for vague answers for general future.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"283"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Jy99Zq1qzhEnQLtv"},"K4i0HumU6BEzGilo":{"name":"Dust Storm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>A storm of dust, scouring the area. Area Aspect is counted as higher by three. Those caught in it resist using Wits + Survival. The spell deals no damage and instead only applies the blindness effect (-3 to all Awareness and appropriate combat-related rolls) to those in the area who have failed their test. The spell lasts for turns equal to thrice the Damage Aspect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"285"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.K4i0HumU6BEzGilo"},"KMAm46NYUj5sLFwC":{"name":"Body of a Sinner (Rosselini)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>All ghosts within a radius equal to the user’s Composure + Resolve who fail the resistance roll or willingly submit to it are forced to take on a corporeal form, whether they could do so on their own or not, for the Duration. Corporeal ghosts take damage like mortals and experience the way not unlike them.</p>\n<p>If killed, however, they don’t simply return to being a ghost. Instead, the experience transforms the ghost into a spectre, a ghost consumed by and subservient to the powers of Oblivion. And this spectre has a grudge against the caster.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KMAm46NYUj5sLFwC"},"KPvDyy8dX6FzLukx":{"name":"Enrich the Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual increases the potency of a human’s blood, allowing the vampire to take the same sustenance from a smaller quantity of blood. Performing this ritual is central to club activities, allowing more mouths to taste from the same vessel and facilitating comparative culinary critique. Only humans can be targeted by this ritual; it has no effect on the Blood of Kindred.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A half-full vial of the target’s blood, five liters of unprocessed human blood no more than three hours old.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster slowly pours the unprocessed blood into the vial, which never fills up so long as the vampire retains concentration. Once all the blood is added, the resulting half-vial of blood must be fed back to the target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> successful Ritual roll makes the target’s blood unusually rich and flavorsome. A sip from their veins slakes two levels of Hunger rather than one. On a Critical Win, two vampires can slake to Hunger 0 if they share the kill. If left alive, the effect on the target wanes after the Duration.</p>\n<p>This Ritual makes the human’s blood thick and hypercoagulable, prone to numerous medical complications. A second casting renders the blood too thick to drink and kills the target.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"321"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KPvDyy8dX6FzLukx"},"KSwAns6b5fCWALqV":{"name":"The Unseen Change","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual forces Lupines into their wolf form.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range(as a radius), Duration (ranks count as double)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Wolf blood, vitae, a silver jug</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster mixes human or vampire blood with wolf’s blood and pours the mixture from a silver jug around a specific area. This can be as small as a meter or large enough to encircle a house.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, this creates a specialised Warding Circle Every werewolf (or another type of skinchanger) must contest the Ritual roll with their Stamina + Resolve. If they fail, they automatically transform into their “mundane” wolf/lupus form.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the werewolf/ skinchanger transforms into any shape they wish as long as they transform out of their current shape.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"297"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KSwAns6b5fCWALqV"},"KUtralYgGzEf56Pr":{"name":"Healing Coma","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The patient remains asleep for 12 hours and awakens with additional 3 superficial health damage healed. The patient cannot be awoken early except by taking a point of Aggravated Health damage. Undead do not benefit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KUtralYgGzEf56Pr"},"KdJ3RtIHVY7QFoZp":{"name":"Addiction","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer must see their target indulging in a specific sensation like kissing, vampiric kiss, substance or action to which the character wants to addict them. Then, the sorcerer makes their roll. On a win, the target becomes addicted to whatever they were indulging in the sorcerer’s sight.</p>\n<p>An addicted character must get their fix at least once a night. Every night that they go without satisfying their desire imposes a cumulative penalty of one die on all of their dice pools to a minimum pool of one die.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KdJ3RtIHVY7QFoZp"},"KdvSi3SUPsOUPAeu":{"name":"Daemonic Possession","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The subject must either be a dead body or a body vacant by Baleful Exorcism. On a win, a spirit, ghost or a person whose soul walks freely (Astral Projection, Anima Walk of Auspex or Necromancy ••••• Ritual Walk Through the Shroud, Reanimation</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KdvSi3SUPsOUPAeu"},"Kk0VIJb0bwdOIQAH":{"name":"Biothaumaturgy (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Considered one of the most unsettling Paths of Thaumaturgy, this Paths which is sometimes referred to as “Path of Frankenstein” as ridicule offers manipulation of energies of life to its practitioners. Through such manipulations, users gain deep medical knowledge and can shape creatures on a genetic level. While comparable to the life sphere and pillars of magi, it possesses such an uncanny resemblance to Vicissitude that many Tremere regents simply refuse to teach it.</p>\n<p>Medieval magi recognises this Path to be the blood sorcery equivalent of the Anima Forma while modern ones see it as a perversion of the Life Sphere.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Like a ritual</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Biothaumarugy is performed like a surgery using surgical tools and, hopefully, anesthesia and so on as these changes can cause great pain. Any conscious person undergoing these changes must make a Stamina + Stamina roll at a difficulty of 3 or suffer a point of Aggravated Health damage. Those with the Beast test for fear frenzy at a difficulty of 2.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> All effects of this Path come in the form of surgery-rituals. On a win on the ritual roll, the sorcerer may apply one modification onto the subject for each success or margin if the victim is resisting (Stamina + Resolve). Subject cannot be the sorcerer themselves.</p>\n<p>The modifications are the same ones as available to the Vicissitude powers of Shape Bone, Transmogrify the Mortal Clay, Rend the Osseous Form, Transcend the Prison of Form and Inner Mastery. The system is shared with those powers except that one’s rating in Biothaumaturgy replaces Vicissitude rating for calculating the maximum amount of alterations.</p>\n<p>Attribute dot’s worth of modifications can be healed as a point of Aggravated Health.Vicissitude and Biothaumaturgy cancel each other and share the same limit, being able to “fix” one another. Subject type which can be operated on is determined by Biothaumaturgy subject.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> The following:</p>\n<h3>Biothaumaturgy Subject</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Failing on a Botchcan lead to devastating consequences for the subject who rolls a number of dice equal to the performing sorcerer’s failure of margin + 1. For each failed die, the subject takes a point of Aggravated Health damage while each die that succeeds assigns a random alteration determined by the storyteller. The standard limit to the value of alterations cannot be bypassed; alterations over the maximum instead change the existing alterations into new ones.</p>\n<p>• Plants and simple life matter like bugs</p>\n<p>•• Small life forms no greater than a raven</p>\n<p>••• Non-physically supernatural mortals and life forms no larger than humans</p>\n<p>•••• Physically supernatural mortals (ghouls, revenants…) and life forms no larger than elephants</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"369"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Kk0VIJb0bwdOIQAH"},"KtAnqLJhBg9zFdot":{"name":"Alchemy (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>The study of transformation and purification, Alchemy is an ancient art and science. Practitioners draw from disparate practices, both magical and scientific, to understand the hidden properties of matter and the soul. The Tremere have pushed alchemy as close to science as possible, codifying formulas and recipes in their vast libraries.</p>\n<p>The most traditional goal of alchemy is not the more famous transformation of base matter into more “noble” counterparts – a such as lead into gold – but rather the perfection of the alchemist. Such a philosophy tends to fall on deaf ears among the apprentices who disrgard this Path in favour of faster ways to wield destructive magical powers.</p>\n<p>Though often studied as a means of perfecting the soul, this pursuit is very much bound up with actual Alchemical practice. Other practices put their own spin on it: Practitioners of Sielanic magics call the Path Herbalism and focus on natural ingredients and effects related to them, while Hacktivists call it Advanced Chemistry, and completely eschew the metaphysical implications the Alchemy practice places on the Path.</p>\n<p>In all cases, the Path of Alchemy is expensive. For most versions of the Path, it requires a well-equipped laboratory and strange ingredients. The most powerful applications require increasingly exotic components and extremely specialised ingedients. Though the herbalist variant tends to be cheaper in terms of money, it requires more time and effort, with plants and fungi collected under strict conditions.</p>\n<p>Many applications of this Path have passed into legend. Few alchemists are known to have actually completed the so-called “Great Work,” the creation of a Philosopher’s Stone, but most masters of the Path make an attempt.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>This Path has no spells. It does everything through rituals called recipes to make formulae. To create a formula, a Ritual Test (called Distilation Test) is performed at the end of Creation Time with a number of doses created equal to the Dose Aspect which has no rank limit. One dose can affect only one person. Effects last for a single scene or like powers replicated unless otherwise noted. For all intents and purposes, recipes are acquired, learned and bought like rituals.</p>\n<p>A character may safely carry a number of formulae on their person equal to their Dexterity or Wits, whichever is higher. Formulae remain potent according to the Duration Aspect, but a sorcerer can keep potent a number of doses equal to their Alchemy rating + Laboratory/ Garden rating by resetting their Duration through “refreshening” or “reheating them”. This is done automatically at no cost.</p>\n<p>Backgrounds can stack if one has multiple havens, giving them even more space to hold their formulae.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Ingesting or otherwise activating formula is a -2 dice minor action. Powers replicated take the action they normally would. See Creation Time.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Equipment:</strong> In order to create alchemical formulae, the alchemist must have access to Laboratory Background at a rating equal or lower to the level of the recipe they want to make. Additionally, the storyteller determines what ingredient is too out of reach for the character’s Backgrounds that can help source materials: not everybody can get their hands on anything.</p>\n<p>Creation Time: Alchemy takes longer to obtain results than most Paths. Recipes require one day per level to prepare, but this can be shortened if the Path rating exceeds the recipe level, with each dot of Alchemy higher than the recipe level reducing the total time spent by one day. If this would reduce preparation time below one day, instead assume several hours of work. If the creation time is so long that it would happen in “off-time” or “timeskip” in your game, you can begin the process offscreen during the timeskip. The price and the roll of the Ritual Test are then performed at the beginning of the very first session after.</p>\n<p>Modifiers: Once the sorcerer successfully follows a recipe three times, the Difficulty is reduced by one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Alchemy, Herbalism</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (count ranks as doubled), Dose (no limit of the rank) and the following:</p>\n<h3>Alchemical Effect Samples</h3>\n<p>Each each dot increases the practitioner’s power and control over the effects. Note that slightly stronger effects could be created, however the alchemical equilibrium demands some form of drawback. Dot bonuses may be replaced with highly specialised dice bonuses of equal value, but not for combat.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Working with volatile compounds is dangerous in and of itself. Even mere failure results in broken equipment and ruined reagents, requiring expensive replacements and reducing the Laboratory Background’s rating by one. In the scenario with a Botch, the Storyteller flips a coin or rolls a die. On a head/failure, the alchemical product explodes, potentially damaging the magician (as well as their neighbors), dealing two points of Aggravated Health or Willpower damage (flip a coin or the Storyteller determines according to what was the intended creation) and lowers Laboratory rating by 3 until fixed.</p>\n<p>If the coin lands on the letter/die succeeds, the botched recipe appears to come together perfectly, but produces undesirable, and possibly harmful, effects due to the miscalculated equilibrium. Even an unexpected but benign effect can be catastrophic at the wrong time. Storyteller determines what is appropriate, but the randomised wheel for Malign Humours can be consulted (p. XX)</p>\n<p>Below, you will only find sample effects listed. Ingredients are not listed unless they are irreplaceable in order to offer more freedom, but a magician of a certain tradition/school should always try to replicate the methods and formula recipes of their fellow sorcerers within the school.</p>\n<p>For example, Sielanic Thaumaturgy will use Herbs, Hermetic Thaumaturgy herbs or rare ingredients while Hacktivist Thaumaturgy might use high-tech machines. The school of magic also usually influences the appearance of the formulae. Hermeticists and Sielanic witches prefer the stereotypical sorcerer’s potion while some Hacktivists prefer COMMON INGREDIENT MODIFIERS AND SIDE EFFECTS Alchemy demands an equilibrium, a push and pull. Certain ingredients will always bring with them some form of side effects, usually those of supernatural origins. You can also use some ingredients as specialised substances to enhance your formulae. Note that a type of a creature from which the blood came cannot benefit or be harmed from it. You can also ignore them if they are too much of a hassle. Here are some common ones.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Vampire blood:</strong> Potential ghouldom for mortal subjects. Count Duration Aspect’s ranks as one higher when vampire vitae is used. Vampire sorcerers automatically benefit from this by spending an additional point of Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Werewolf material:</strong> Gain a two-dice bonus on Distilation rolls. Vampires under the influence of formulae created with werewolf blood suffer an increase of Fury Frezy Tests’ Difficulty by 1. If werewolf fur was used, lose a success on all Social rolls due to the stench.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Fae material:</strong> Same as drinking Fae Blood (p. XX).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Demon/Laham material:</strong> Reduce the Difficulty by two. The subject of the formula is burned and takes a point of Aggravated Health damage. This applies on any hostile effect and boosts all damage from things like Greek fire. Control Tests for materials of this kind are performed at a +1 Difficulty.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Temperament (Fleeting):</strong> Add a die on the Control Test.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Temperament (Intense):</strong> Add a die on the Distilation Test.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Temperament (Acute):</strong> Add two dice on the Distilation Test, but lose a die on the Control Test. formulae within the form of pills. The proposed physical forms of formulae are mere suggestions.</p>\n<p>Formulae turned into exploding bottles of contact poison, gas or powder may be used offensively, requiring a Composure + Aim roll against an opponent’s Dexterity + Athletics. Those resisting effects of a formula may do so by rolling Stamina + Medicine (or Fortitude) against the initial successes on the Distilation roll unless stated otherwise.</p>\n<p>Just like with rituals, players are encouraged to make custom recipes under the Storyteller’s supervision.</p>\n<p>• These recipes are simply more advanced versions of extant chemicals and concoctions, producing more potent painkillers, poisons, glues, solvents, etc. Higher Path levels can further alter the substances, making them even more potent, stronger or weaker.</p>\n<p>•• Now the substances created let the imbiber exceed their usual physical limits. Any one Attribute can be increased or decreased by one dot, up to a maximum of five, for the scene. Special preparations can expand one’s mystical awareness, mostly through induced dreams or hallucinations. These visions are left to the Storyteller’s discretion and should remain cryptic and uncertain. Visions attained at this level lack the power of a system benefit and are included as a narrative device.</p>\n<p>••• Not only can the alchemist now enhance their targets beyond the normal capabilities of humans, but they can also grant low-level psychic abilities (see p. XX) for an hour at a time. These psychic abilities begin at a single dot, but every additional two margins on the creation roll provide an additional dot, to a maximum of the alchemist’s Path rating. The alchemist can copy up to a single dot’s worth of supernatural powers.</p>\n<p>•••• Alchemists can bring about truly potent improvements to living beings. They can increase or decrease a single Attribute or Skill by two or two Attributes or Skills each by one, and the normal human limit of five dots in an Attribute is moved to seven dots. The alchemist can copy up to two dots’ worth of supernatural powers.</p>\n<p>••••• The alchemist can now craft recipes that reproduce up to three dots worth of supernatural powers. Each dot reproduced this way mimics a single effect – chosen when the recipe is crafted – even if the replicated power offers multiple effects. These are some of the hardest potions to produce and require exotic components, such as pieces of the supernatural being in question.</p>\n<p>For example, to mimic a two-dot power of Celerity and one-dot power of Obfuscate, ingredients must include blood of vampire(s) who have the appropriate Disciplines at a rank no lower than the powers being replicated, even if they do not possess those powers (vampire alchemists frequently exploit this to awaken undeveloped powers). This purifies vitae in a way so as to not make a ghoul out of the drinker unless that was the intent. If the user becomes a ghoul, they gain free Discipline dot(s) as normal. Formulae at this rating can also repel supernatural beings.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"259"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.KtAnqLJhBg9zFdot"},"LMaRenKEP57jDdVS":{"name":"Eyes of Ash","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster can see, hear, and speak to ghosts in their vicinity, whichever side of the Shroud they are on.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"331"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LMaRenKEP57jDdVS"},"LQ4KOGxT9qx6aRsv":{"name":"Unknowing Leap","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Spells are all quite good and all, but unreliable. This ritual establishes a type of a portal. When a person enters it or steps on it, they are teleported to another designated location.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Same as the Path Spell</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> See process</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer focuses, not on getting somewhere, but getting away from there.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This simple ritual works like a basic use of the Path, but with a couple of minor differences. For one, it takes five minutes to perform. However, it removes the extra success required to go somewhere you don’t know about ahead of time, whether by fine tuning instruments or just meditating to improve your focus.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"276"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LQ4KOGxT9qx6aRsv"},"LVr5VtALXfzfb89H":{"name":"Cry that Slays Light","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>Cainite wishing to shout the Cry That Slays Light must spend one week in absolute silence. They may not utter the briefest syllable or they must begin their quiet meditations again. During this time, they spend all waking hours mentally repeating a litany of hatred for the Sun and Moon and the Creator of light – bringing their wrath to the edge of sound without ever giving it voice. Once the ritual is complete, the vampire makes their Ritual Test. If they fail, they suffer banishment into the Abyss for a century per 1 rolled, during which time the vampire probably goes insane, must still make Rouse Checks to stay awake but never falls into the release of torpor. When the imprisonment ends, the Cainite returns to the Earth in a frenzy that may last weeks or even longer. Failure without any 1s on the casting means the caster fails to channel the dark energies properly and nothing happens.</p>\n<p>A win on the Ritual Test means the vampire hears the whispers of Ahriman proclaiming the syllable that is opposed to all being. The syllable remains in the vampire’s consciousness and cannot be telepathically extracted or mystically removed by any power short of divine intervention. At any time in the vampire’s unlife, they may shriek this syllable to turn day into night. For one hour, vampires may walk openly without fear – utterly immune to Rötschreck and protected from the sun by the undulating curtain of shadow above. The syllable then passes from the mystic’s memory unless they cast the ritual once more to rediscover it</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> At the end, the caster rolls their Road rating. Each failed die subtacts one from the character’s Humanity/Road rating. If this sudden degeneration reduces the vampire’s Road rating to zero, they rampage under the cover of darkness and kill everything and everyone they can find. When the darkness ends, it subsumes them into its being and their hunger is added to the Abyss.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"257"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LVr5VtALXfzfb89H"},"LX1tuICUuLV2pAfg":{"name":"Warcry","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>Make the Spell Test against the opponent’s Composure + Resolve, on a win they take a -2 dice penalty to their next attack against you. If there are multiple opponents it is a roll against the one with the highest pool to resist (players may always make their roll individually).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LX1tuICUuLV2pAfg"},"LdLcr9kQD7OjBKLO":{"name":"Darksight","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The ritualist darkness their eyes and can see in pitch black darkness. Almost a prerequisite for Abyss Mysticism. The Ritualist can only enchant themselves with Darksight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> The ritualist’s eyes grow darker and they lose a die penalty on all positive social interactions with those who can see their eyes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"252"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LdLcr9kQD7OjBKLO"},"LdR4cnG7nBywHDBK":{"name":"Letter Ward","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Binding, Summoning and Warding Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Another form of making sure one’s letters are not read, a Letter Ward is a variant of the standard Ward meant for letters.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Damage, Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>Noticeably, this Ward does not use Metaphysical Weight as it applies to everyone except the intended recipient(s).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Pigeon blood, wax, dog blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The letter writer, who need not be the sorcerer, pens a missive in pigeon blood and clearly addresses the recipient. The sorcerer then melts wax and mixes it with dog’s blood to seal the letter.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The sorcerer makes the Ritual Test and on a win writes down the successes. When anyone other than the recipient opens the letter, they must roll Stamina + Resolve vs. the successes on the Ritual Test. Should they fail, they take damage equal to the margins or twice the Damage Aspect, whichever is lower. The damage is Superficial, but it can be altered by increasing the difficulty. A failure on the unintended reader’s roll also destroys the letter in a burst of flame, dealing an additional point of Aggravated Health damage. This ritual is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, including the sorcerer, who must usually immediately roll against the ward upon creating it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"272"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LdR4cnG7nBywHDBK"},"LlR7oCqkB2JWnnXO":{"name":"Waves (Minor)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Targets must beat the sorcerer’s Spell Test with their Stamina + Athletics or else they cannot swim forward.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LlR7oCqkB2JWnnXO"},"LqPPrHuvrESUBupq":{"name":"Bar the Common Passage","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The thaumaturge places a ward on a door or another portal, giving it the strength to withstand even attacks from werewolves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled), Protection (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Sorcerer’s blood or saliva.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer draws a ward with their blood on a portal (door, window, trapdoor…)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the warded object gains additional Health equal to twice the Protection Aspect. The door/portal also takes all damage as Superficial Health damage that would damage it normally, and no damage whatsoever from things that wouldn’t (e.g. just trying to break a stone wall with an axe). The ritual does not stack with itself or others. This ritual is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> This ritual is too simple to have unintended consequences.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"294"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LqPPrHuvrESUBupq"},"LsPvUugiDSRFOuNZ":{"name":"School: Koldunism","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p>Power is ever circular. We sacrifice of ourselves in tribute to the earth, so the earth may give of itself to us. You must bleed for your homeland, so that your homeland will bleed for you.</p>\n<p>– Raban von Diederik, koldun of the Bialowieza The Tzimisce claim kingship not from heavenly mandate, but from the land itself; they are the lords of their realms and all who dwell in it. Since the First Days they have shared their blood with the earth, intermingling their spirits with those of their voivodes until they are one and the same. Those who study the ways of koldunism, that most ancient of Cainite blood magic, elevate this mastery yet further, binding the spirits of their homelands to themselves. These kolduny, as they are known, manifest the raw power of the natural world through their physical forms, as if their flesh were the land itself.</p>\n<p>Something abominable dwells within the Tzimisce Voivodate; something allowing the Fiends to maintain an unwavering grip on the land and peoples of Hungaria, Poland, Bulgaria, and beyond. To hear the Telyavs of the north tell it, the Tzimisce are in league with the primordial demons of the earth, and act as foremost masters in their summoning and manipulation. The Tremere of beset Ceoris cite Tzimisce pacts with ancient, wicked spirits. The Ventrue resident in the Fiefdoms of the Black Cross make a more mundane observation: Their longtime Tzimisce foes are unified, ordered, and conditioned by a sublimely powerful, entirely secretive cabal of magi kings.</p>\n<p>The truth is unlikely to be known, but is in these nights widely sought. The Tzimisce have ever lacked unity, especially in times of war. One Voivode will commit the bulk of her war ghouls and fledglings to the task of routing a Patrician enclave, strengthening the defenses of the Voivodate and earning herself the right to liberate slaves and wealth from the abandoned Ventrue stronghold. That is, unless the Voivode in the neighboring domain doesn’t take advantage of their weakened state, moving their own army into their land as theirs departs for western pastures. The Voivode among Voivodes — Count Vladimir Rustovich — has called for such in-fighting to end, and states the reemergence of Koldunism to be key to his Clan recovering much of what it’s lost to opportunism and petty politicking.</p>\n<p>The Tzimisce throw their armies fully into the War of Princes, as they recognize a formative alliance between the Fiefdoms of the Black Cross and the Tremere, who utilise their Gargoyle servants too effectively on the battlefield. While the war grinds into a stalemate, the Fiends seek an edge. Rustovich believes tapping into the ancient kraina of Koldunic Sorcery is that edge, if the Discipline’s practitioners could be called into coordinated action. The Tzimisce will completely crush their adversaries, if the natural, devastating forces of the lands they call home are paired with the unnatural power of Cainite vitae.</p>\n<p>Strong incentive that it may be, Koldunic Sorcery is a many-forked path. Contrary to Rustovich’s aims, the study of the kraina forces even greater divisions between its practitioners.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Mostly found within the Clan Tzimisce, very few outsiders have been taught this magic until the formation of the Sabbat when the Tzimisce were forced to interact with others more often. It is not because they hate others practicing magic, it is that they believe them unworthy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Nicknames:</strong> Koldunic Sorcery</p>\n<h3>Witch-Priests of the Kraine</h3>\n<p>Were a koldunic sorcerer to witness the rituals practiced by the Trembling Ones, they would spit vitae and damn the Usurpers for their base understanding and lack of respect. Majority of Koldunic practice comes in the form of Rites, unique rituals of regional Paths called Kraine. The Rites of the Kraine are arcane, rewarding risk and sacrifice, punishing the trepidatious. Studying Kraina Rites is an investment of time and blood, with bounteous fruits to the koldun should the Rites be successful.</p>\n<p>Powerful Kraina Rites require the assistance of willing supplicants. In times past, the Tzimisce would enlist their servants. As the War of Princes leaves the Voivodate with more prisoners, rite leaders often promise such prisoners freedom if they’ll volunteer for participation in a Rite.</p>\n<p>After months of torture in a Tzimisce dungeon, most are only too willing.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Natural Sanctum, Spirit Presence, Shrine</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Bargain: Koldunism rewards a risky investment.</p>\n<p>The koldun may add a two 1s towards a Botch and in turn decrease the Difficulty of a Spell or a Ritual by 1. This may be applied up to two times. Paired with Sacrifice Motif, a koldun can do great things with a great amount of victims.</p>\n<p>• Koldun’s Discipline: Koldunic Sorcery demands strong body as well as a strong mind. For the purposes of Koldunism, Physical Impairment counts as Mental Impairment, inflicting a -2 dice penalty to Spell, Ritual Tests.</p>\n<p>• Might of Kupala: If a koldun possesses Occult Specialty Kupala, they may reduce all Spell and Ritual Test Difficulties by one to a minimum of 1, however, they always add an addtional 1 towards a Botch. Harnessing the power of such a daemon is risky as the koldun must channel the daemon’s power through their own body. If the activation Rouse Check for a spell or a ritual rolls a 1 or 10, the koldun’s Hunger raises by one.</p>\n<p>• Sacrifice: The spirits of the land are placated by offerings and there is no greater offering than that of human life. For each murder that is a part of a Spell or a Ritual, the sorcerer rolls a die to see whether or not the sacrifice appeases the spirits. If the die succeeds, the Spell or Ritual Test Difficulty lowers by 1, to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<h3>Koldunic Rivarly</h3>\n<p>There is a simple reason we do not ally with the Voivodate. Koldunic sorcerers do not share power.</p>\n<p>Vladimir, koldun of Urals Tzimisce balk at the idea of serving any beings, let alone daemons. Koldunic Sorcery is the raw power of the earth, but also contains the spite and discord of daemons — the most infamous being Kupala. Cursed blood should not mix with nature, yet this is the fundament of the koldun. Daemons bound to the elements utilize this curse to find a hold on the Cainite practitioners of the Discipline.</p>\n<p>The daemons adore chaos, and compel koldunic sorcerers to strive for supremacy in their Discipline, principally by warring with other vampires attempting the same thing. It’s no surprise koldunic sorcerers often practice existences of isolation, or surrounded exclusively by their lessers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Whenever the vampire hears of new kraina and koldunic rituals, the player must make a Willpower Test (Difficulty equal to the koldun’s Koldunic Sorcery rating, halved and rounded up). On a win, the koldun successfully resists the lure to accumulate further power, but a failure forces the koldunic sorcerer to make an subconscious beeline for the source of this alleged power, even risking the dawn and making excuses for doing so. This effect can be resisted each following night with a Willpower roll (difficulty one higher than the previous).</p>\n<p>When a character with at least one dot of Koldunic Sorcery witnesses another sorcerer practicing one of the Kraina in which they possess no dots, they must make fury Frenzy Test at a Difficulty equal to the koldun’s own Koldunic Sorcery rating, halved and rounded up.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Koldunism</h3>\n<p>Koldunism has access to all Kraina type of Paths unless stated otherwise. Koldunism also possesses the following Paths.</p>\n<p>Additionally, there is a specific group of Paths referred to as Ways, named as such by those who believe that Koldunism may offer certain ways of improving one’s by mastering a specific Path. Ways can only be taken by a Koldun who has at least 3 dots in Koldunism. Note that rituals of Ways are not mechanically Rites and as such follow standard rules for magic.</p>\n<p>• Alchemy (Way of Transmutation)</p>\n<p>• Battle (Way of the Commander)</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding (Way of Will)</p>\n<p>• Divination (Way of Fate)</p>\n<p>• Ephemera (Way of the Spirits)</p>\n<p>• Enchantment (Way of Creation)</p>\n<p>• Focus (Way of Self)</p>\n<p>• Fortune (Way of Sorrows)</p>\n<p>• Genius Loci (Way of the Spirit)</p>\n<p>• Green Path (Way of the Forests)</p>\n<p>• Healing (Way of Healing)</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood (Way of Blood)</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting (Way of Body)</p>\n<p>During the Kupala’s Night as part of the Anarch Revolt, many of the young kolduny will be captured by Kupala’s promises of power and Kraine will fall out of us in favour of Ways. New Ways will be discovered: Fire, Air, Earth and Water being the most notable. Make no mistake, these new kolduny still practice Koldunism as a form of magic that draws its powers from a spirit. It is just that the spirit in question is the demon Kupala. To emulate these, you may use Elemental Mastery, Conjuration (Telekinesis only) and Water Mastery or craft new Paths.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"240"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LsPvUugiDSRFOuNZ"},"LtLuGHV3SVSvOqLk":{"name":"Preservation Frame","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An oak frame that preserves anything placed in it for as long as it remains intact. It can only hold flat things, like documents and paintingss, and must be sealed with almond oil and sprinkled with pure water.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.LtLuGHV3SVSvOqLk"},"Lwv1YmrzIlh9h1bD":{"name":"Teleport Ward","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Magicians studying the Path of Conveyance quickly learn to defend themselves from it. By marking out a room or building in an appropriate way — a chalk circle, sigils on the walls, or anti-teleportation field projectors, etc. — they can make it harder for other hedge magicians to use the Path to enter the area.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (as a radius)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> See process</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer draws wards in chalk, water or blood or installs some sort of devices against teleportation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After the ritual is set, it is impossible for others to teleport inside the ward unless their casting pool scores more successes than this ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Simply fails although it gives the sorcerer the impression of success.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"277"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Lwv1YmrzIlh9h1bD"},"M0xbAhbHgUr2d0U0":{"name":"Gift of Longevity","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Also known as the “siphoning gift” by those less in denial, this Ritual allows a vampire to transfer vitality from one mortal to another, extending one life by shortening the other.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Two willing or unwilling mortals, a length of silk rope</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Opening a vein on each mortal’s wrist, the vampire presses their wounds together, securing them with the rope. Holding on to each mortal, the vampire channels spirits of the Underworld to push vitality from one mortal into the other.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire makes their Rouse Check as they open the veins and a Ritual Test (Difficulty 6) against the Stamina + Resolve of the mortal being drained as they start channeling the Entropy. A failed Ritual Test contest forces the spirits to slink back to where they came from. A win starts the process of pushing vitality from one mortal to another. The results become apparent quickly. For every five minutes of siphoning, the targeted mortal loses 10 years’ worth of life they could have lived to the other. When the target has lost their ordained lifespan (traditionally 70 years), they start deteriorating quickly. The effect on the receiving mortal is more subtle. They become the image of health but aren’t otherwise immediately affected. Following the Ritual, they age half as quickly as other mortals until the extra life-span they have been granted has been spent (at which point, they once again age normally). Diseases develop at half the speed as well. They aren’t in any other ways protected from death.</p>\n<p>The conversion rate of vitality is 1:1 for mortals who are related or deeply emotionally bonded. Otherwise, the targeted mortal must give a decade for each three years of life received by the other.</p>\n<p>The vampire must make a Composure + Resolve Test of at Difficulty 6 to stop the process and cut the bond before the targeted mortal shrivels up like a dried prune and turns to dust. If the vampire cannot stop the Ritual this way, simply killing the mortal works as well.</p>\n<p>Even more than most, this Ritual is likely to attract attention from the other side of the Veil.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"349"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.M0xbAhbHgUr2d0U0"},"M4ThiTzGit8AHIVI":{"name":"Sorcerer’s Grimoire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>This Grimoire is capable of initiating even a sleeping mind into the mysteries of Sorcery. Up to one dot of Paths the creator knows per margin on the recipe creation are imprinted into the Grimoire, which can be used by others others to learn those Paths more easily, up to the rating that’s been imprinted in them. After reading through such a grimoire, an individual may purchase the next (or first) dot of one of these Paths at new rating x 4, so long as this wouldn’t put them above what’s imprinted in the Grimoire. Period of study and its project goes as normal.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, such a Grimoire might be able to grant a Sorcerer-specific Merit or Background, at about half the total cost in experience points (rounded up), with the same period of study.</p>\n<p>A Sorcerous Grimoire is of course written within the confines of the Sorcerer’s view of the world, and so only those with compatible Sorcery School are really able to make use of them.</p>\n<p>Like a Mage’s Grimoire, a Sorcerer’s Grimoire will always be a unique work of art, with magical lettering, strange effects occurring when they’re being read, and any number of other odd properties accompanying them.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"293"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.M4ThiTzGit8AHIVI"},"MRlwJWtMiycvDGEJ":{"name":"Abyss Communion (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>A Path which is rightfully feared by all in the know, Abyss Communion allows what it name suggests — communication with the Abyss, the infinite nothingness that has existed since before Creation and will persist after its destruction. All enlightenment returns to the Abyss. The light of life and learning perishes with the death of flesh and soul and no tutelage can pass on wisdom in its entirety. But Ahriman remembers. Ahriman is all that is not, or was and is no more. In the primordial darkness lie echoes and whispers frozen in silent waiting for someone cunning enough to ask the right questions and daring enough to receive the answers.</p>\n<p>The sorcerer enters torpor and lets their spirit drift into the endless Abyss where they may directly communicate with the abyssal hive-mind that is seemingly omniscient and omnipresent within the Abyss. Legend has it that the reason for the Lasombra Antediluvian’s carefully guarded slumber in the Castle of Shadows is that he lies dreaming of a way to destroy creation itself.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> As a ritual.</p>\n<p>Abyss Communion is performed by entering torpor-like state. Rolls for Abyss Communion are performed at the conclusion of every night the mystic spends in torpid contemplation. Successes accumulate from night to night until they match the rating of the question and the vampire awakens knowing the answer. If the vampire fails to obtain any successes during a night, the interval between rolls increases to weeks. Further failure to obtain successes extends the interval to months, then years, decades, centuries, millennia and perhaps onward to the end of time itself. A fable among Abyss mystics states that the first mystic, a beloved childe of the Eldest (or the Eldest themselves, but such is deemed blasphemy), asked the Abyss how he might slay God. It is said that he slumbers still in some forgotten tomb, dreaming the incomprehensible nightmares of that which preceded light.</p>\n<p>If asking for something that might relate to future rolls, the character names a skill before they begin the ritual. On a successful Ritual Test, the Storyteller answer a question at their discretion. If the events are really impossible to predict or in cases of trying to predict, the storyteller should be as vague as possible, but still try to provide some satisfaction for a successful casting. See some tips and guidelines on managing divination magic as a Storyteller on P. XX.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Abyss</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Accuracy, Time Period (past, present or future) and Query. See Divination p. 268 for Accuracy and Query Aspects.</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the Storyteller answers questions at their discretion. If the events are really impossible to predict or in cases of trying to predict, the storyteller should be as vague as possible, but still try to provide some satisfaction for a successful spell.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user can perform Divination on more vague things to gain a mechanical dice bonus. On a successful Spell Test, Accuracy provides a dice bonus to any non-tracker pool equal to its rating. Similarly, Query allows for a free reroll of a number of dice of a non-tracker pool equal to its rank. Even Hunger dice may be rerolled in this manner. No more than three dice may be added or rerolled in a single pool. The bonuses last until the caster spends them, Time Period Ends or casts another Abyss Communion spell which resets the bonuses. These Aspects may be brought above their 5th rank for this mechanical purpose with certain rituals.</p>\n<p>Abyss Communion cannot aid pools to fortune-tell or where the user consciously forced some kind of a benefit (like from a Blood Surge, artefact usage or another dice bonus) as fate comes on its own and artefacts often influence change and fate by themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The very act of attempting to commune with the infinite is extremely dangerous. On a Botch, the Ritual Roll automatically fails and the ritualist suffers a point of Aggravated Willpower damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> The experience of communing with the Abyss is one that permanently scars. The Mystic begins to suffer nightmares (which are not exactly frightening to most Abyss Mystics) that drain their psyche. The vampire permanently recovers Superficial Willpower damage at the start of session equal to the lower of their Resolve or Composure. If the Attributes are equal, recover that amount -1 (example: Montano’s Resolve and Composure are both 5, and so he only recovers 4 Superficial Willpower damage).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"250"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.MRlwJWtMiycvDGEJ"},"MUqHhMrptRk9tp2M":{"name":"Magical Phylactery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An item (frequently a staff) or a piece of jewelry that gives the wearer a two-dice bonus on all rolls related to defending from, using or controlling magic. When the sorcerer does not have the phylactery on them, they suffer Mental Impairment as if all of their Willpower was damaged. Each night of separation inflicts one point of Aggravated Willpower damage. Once the sorcerer is out of Willpower, the phylactery shatters. Only the crafter may benefit from this item which does not require experience points to be permanent. It must be crafted near some kind of Sanctum and the sorcerer must inscribe their True Name onto its surface (which can be inside if it is a medallion).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.MUqHhMrptRk9tp2M"},"Mg08a8tRf1GLlZbW":{"name":"Blood to Water","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The Kindred pours purified water through their bloody fingers, transmuting all spilled blood in the area into water.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (as radius)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Pure water, whether from a natural spring or purified mechanically.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist smears their own Blood on their hands, pours water through their fingers, and splatters it about the room</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the ritual is successful, blood (including the performer’s spilled Blood) exposed to the air is transmuted into water, leaving no traces behind.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"320"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Mg08a8tRf1GLlZbW"},"Mzc0bOwXxwRixae3":{"name":"Chain the Sinners (Rosselini)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster must speak the True Name of a ghost or have it in their line of sight while holding an object. On a win, the object becomes the ghost’s new fetter for all intents and purposes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Mzc0bOwXxwRixae3"},"NKG2bhuA9qkeCfVa":{"name":"Contortion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Mastery of the Mortal Shell Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following.</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer picks a body part to contort: head (Social and Mental), legs (Dexterity), arms (Strength). Dice pools with the associated Attributes and Skills suffer a two-dice penalty.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"329"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.NKG2bhuA9qkeCfVa"},"NMlJjNxFAaVdNhxX":{"name":"Amulet against the Shapers","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Same as Amulet against Magic except that it impacts Garou and other shapeshifters’ Gifts against the wearer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.NMlJjNxFAaVdNhxX"},"NYG78jdRGboqaKUp":{"name":"Spirit Portal (Low Umbra)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ephemera Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual allows the caster to enter the Low Umbra, specifically the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Mixture of blood and ink for the portal to the Low Umbra to paint the portal with.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist paints a doorway or marks two locations close to each other like two trees, chanting all the while.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual (minimum Difficulty is 6 if not made higher through Aspects), the consecrated portal begins to glow in pale light when viewed by supernatural senses like the first dot of this Path and stepping through it will physically bring the Passenger Aspect’s worth of people into the spirit world physically. The portal method uses Duration for how long the portal stays open and it closes after the Duration expires or the Passengers enter through it. Returning from the spirit world requires a point of Willpower point spent by each person at the entrance they came through to pass back into the physical world.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"305"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.NYG78jdRGboqaKUp"},"NfZCQmRIUpiv3hoz":{"name":"Blood Oath of the Dead","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The Blood Bond is a potent tool that has held the Cainite society stable for centuries if not millenias and normally affects only humans, be they mortal or immortal. This ritual said to have been practiced in a similar form by the Nagaraja before they became vampires is one of their closest secrets as they strike bargains with the dead in exchange for an oath sealed in blood, often without the ghost’s awareness of what will happen until it is too late for them to resist their vampire domitor.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> The Nagaraja jelously guard this ritual, even from their Hecata siblings in the modern nights.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A Rouse Check’s worth of the caster’s blood (in addition to the activation Rouse Check)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The vampire calls upon a blood oath of loyalty from a ghost and cuts themselves, letting the ghost feed directly from the vein like when blood bonding others.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This Ritual takes only a minute to perform. On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 4), a ghost drinking it is afflicted with the Blood Bond like any mortal or vampire with the bond rising one step at a time with each night’s drink.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"336"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.NfZCQmRIUpiv3hoz"},"NudfLlKyey55NBhI":{"name":"Encrypt/Decrypt Letter","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Binding, Summoning and Warding Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Used heavily in the Dark Ages when secrecy of one’s letters could make the difference between life and death, this ritual makes the contents of a letter illegible to those not meant to read it. Some princes were known for keeping Tremere secretaries just for the benefits of this ritual while the Banu Haqim’s vast network of assassins operated in secrecy with this ritual by writing the intended text over a mundane letter. Regents of Tremere chantries often encrypt letters as a form of signature as thaumaturgically encrypted letters are much more difficult to forge.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> writing tool and a Rouse check of the caster’s blood for encryption or just the caster’s blood for decryption.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster fills the page with whichever content they wish to fool the unintended reader. Afterwards, the caster writes what they wish to conceal over the text in their own blood and name the intended readers. Letters appear blood-red to the intended readers. To decrypt the meaning hidden by this ritual the Sorcerer simply washes the page, tablet or wall with their blood and invokes the power of their blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> After the sorcerer is done writing the secret contents on a page, wall or a tablet, they make the Ritual roll and write down their successes. Upon success, the writing surface “drinks” the blood, making it illegible to unintended readers. A Sorcerer who wishes to decipher such a text rolls against the previous Sorcerer’s successes.</p>\n<p>One use of this Ritual allows one page or wall to be encrypted or deciphered. This ritual is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, and almost nobody will be able to read the encrypted content.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"271"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.NudfLlKyey55NBhI"},"NzJBuNnhReXI7Pmt":{"name":"Warrior’s Strength","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A bull torque made of pure obsidian. When smeared with blood it grants a single one-dot power of Potence to the torque.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.NzJBuNnhReXI7Pmt"},"O0h7tKIwG5mykZhc":{"name":"Blood Apocrypha","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Kindred sorcerers have learned to embed messages into their blood, or a vessel when feeding. Though there may be difficulties in sending the messenger to the recipient, the message is much less likely to be intercepted: this old art has come into vogue since the advent of the Inquisition.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A drop of mercury and one rouse check’s worth of blood (covered by the ritual cost).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer whispers a message to the mercury, which carries it through the blood collected in a ceremonially prepared bowl.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The Kindred encodes about one minute of speech into one Rouse Check worth of blood in a ritual bowl. The ritualist then designates one or more intended recipients of the message whose blood they have tasted. As part of the ritual, a vampire, mortal, or beast messenger must consume the blood to transfer the message to their own body, otherwise the blood—and message—will rapidly sour.</p>\n<p>The first person to consume the ensorcelled blood from the messenger automatically receives the message mentally if they are the intended recipient or possess A Taste for Blood. Any other imbiber must succeed on a Wits + Occult roll (Difficulty 3) to understand the message.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"320"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.O0h7tKIwG5mykZhc"},"O3a9LWAcbyZ5fI6O":{"name":"Spirit Portal (Astral)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ephemera Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual allows the caster to enter the High Umbra.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Mixture of mercury and wine for the High Umbra to paint the portal with.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist paints a doorway or marks two locations close to each other like two trees, chanting all the while.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual (minimum Difficulty is 6 if not made higher through Aspects), the consecrated portal begins to glow in pale light when viewed by supernatural senses like the first dot of this Path and stepping through it will physically bring the Passenger Aspect’s worth of people into the spirit world physically. The portal method uses Duration for how long the portal stays open and it closes after the Duration expires or the Passengers enter through it. Returning from the spirit world requires a point of Willpower point spent by each person at the entrance they came through to pass back into the physical world.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"305"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.O3a9LWAcbyZ5fI6O"},"O3vPdyY4AU7BZNaM":{"name":"To the death","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range ignore physical impairment. Targets also do not exhaust themselve, allowing them to run or march with haste. When used in this manner, it is referred to as Iter Perenix.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.O3vPdyY4AU7BZNaM"},"O5EcNu8a6wAtd7Ea":{"name":"Amulet against Fae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Same as Amulet against Magic (see below) except that it impacts Fae magic against the wearer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.O5EcNu8a6wAtd7Ea"},"Ob7sWM3zv3lEcW1z":{"name":"Aggressive Corpse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer raises a dead body as an Aggressive Corpse.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Ob7sWM3zv3lEcW1z"},"OdPWV1f6yhgumej7":{"name":"Reanimation (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Scratch a corpse and you come away with more than skin beneath your fingernail. Each layer tells a tale. The farther one digs, the more one learns. Dig deep.</p>\n<p>— Marchesa Liliana, Wearer of the Triple-Faced Mask The Grave’s Decay and the Corpse in the Monster Paths practiced by the Graverobbers are both basically sympathetic studies. They are concerned with using an understanding of the behavior of corpses to apply that behavior to other matters, be it living or unliving flesh. Necromancy Path directly deals in souls of the dead and their world. Reanimation Path relies on theories of magical contagion (p. 206) instead, proposing that dead bodies — once inhabited by living souls — can be made to imitate the habits of life. The Venetian necromancers recently Embraced into Clan Cappadocian are said to have a deeper understanding of this Path and related arts, but those are but whispers among the Graverobbers. The Path excells in preparing bodies for ghosts to inhabit, making it a useful for bargaining with the Restless Dead.</p>\n<p>The Nagaraja bloodline, also masters of this Path, have a less than scholarly approach to it and see it more as a Path of utility, allowing them to wage wars in the east with corpses as soldiers. In contrast, the Followers of Set practice this Path with reverence as they perform ancient rituals to ferry the souls of the dead to and from the Lands of the Dead and are even said to be able to create mummies.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Statblocks for animated cadavers may be found on here together with rules for animated corpse behaviour, abilities and cost to buy them as a Background and what happens if they’re not bought with exp. Animation happens over the course of a turn and the bodies are ready to act on the next turn after the casting.</p>\n<p>If the necromancer spends one point of Willpower per cadaver, it is animated by the necromancer’s own Willpower and the Duration is automatically set to indefinite and Aspect is set to 0.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialty:</strong> Anatomy, Cadavers, Exorcism, Reanimation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following Special Effects:</p>\n<h3>Reanimation</h3>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> A Botch of Reanimation Path yields any control over an undead who may simply stand there and do nothing or start attacking everything that moves, depending on the type of a reanimated cadaver. Baleful Exorcism Botch exorcises the caster instead while Daemonic Possession fails the caster dies and becomes a ghost.</p>\n<p>••••• Ritual Garb of Hades) may permanently possess the body by touching it, acquiring its Physical Attributes, Looks/Physical Merits and Flaws, Physical powers like Physical Disciplines if the target is a supernatural creature. Any exp invested into Physical Attributes and Looks Merits is refunded if no longer applicable.</p>\n<p>This Effect allows ghosts who have not mastered Puppetry to possess people, making young ghosts very appreciative.</p>\n<p>The one who possesses the target effectively becomes the target. A mortal who possesses a vampire with this power becomes the same Clan and Generation as the victim, for example.</p>\n<p>Vampires using this Effect carry over their Generation and Clan, but take the target’s Blood Potency, to a minimum of their Generation. If the target wasn’t a vampire, Blood Potency is set to a minimum. Possessing another vampire provokes diablerie if the target is not dead with the caster always acquiring Inherited Bane Diablerie Flaw.</p>\n<p>Supernatural creatures cannot use this to become non-supernaturals or other types of supernaturals. For exmaple, a ghost possessing a vampire merely becomes a Risen, gaining Celerity, Fortitude and Potence of the vampire unless they somehow possesed it at higher ratings. Possessing werewolves as a vampire creates what is known as an “abomination”.</p>\n<p>Duration Aspect represents how long the body remains viable for possesion in this manner. Once possessed, the effect is permanent and the old body dies or turns to ash if the user is a vampire.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"341"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.OdPWV1f6yhgumej7"},"OuDqdVohLCUTNcsN":{"name":"Shew Stone","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Practice of using stones for orientation is an old one, but only necromancers would literally use stones to orient themselves by infusing a small stone with spirits of the dead to track down a person as an esoteric compass.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration, Sympathetic Connection</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A stone and some sympathetic material</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Necromancer polishes the already polished stone once more, this time with the blood of the intended target or in distillation of sympathetic material. After that, the necromancer performs some sort of a sacrifice to the spirits such as an offering or a ritual dance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a Win, the stone begins to emit soft whispers which can only be heard if the person puts the stone very close to their ear. The spirits whisper the direction of the target the stone has been attuned to and the necromancer can guess the approximate location, but never greater accuracy than estimating a street. The range of detection is governed by the Distance Aspect.</p>\n<p>The All Saints day abruptly disables the stone.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botchfailure, wrong information is given.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"334"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.OuDqdVohLCUTNcsN"},"P5i4GabaRA1Asi4n":{"name":"Consillium Mortui","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>Force a skull to reveal the memories of its past life through a ghostly voice. Upon a successful spell test the caster may ask any one of the skulls questions equal to the margin of the roll or rating in the path, whichever is lower. The answers are always truthful in accordance to what the skull knew and believed in life, even if the specificity and verbosity of the answers is up to the storyteller.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.P5i4GabaRA1Asi4n"},"PITr3DVd39NQRsBc":{"name":"Manipulator Serum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>A powder or a liquid that, when inhaled by mouth, gives an extra dot of Manipulation and Subterfuge.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PITr3DVd39NQRsBc"},"PLKGJRdc8YT7Xes4":{"name":"Dance of the wind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>Every target in range counts any skill as 2 higher for rolls made to evading an attack, if they have taken no actions to attack then they take no penalties from dodging multiple attacks in one turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PLKGJRdc8YT7Xes4"},"PONe9sgz3reuNpqo":{"name":"Fear (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Back in the dark ages, fear was everywhere. Mortals carefully walked on streets to not arouse a supernatural shadow or monster with many mortals seeking spiritual refuge at churches and other religious establishments on a daily basis. Prey on Souls’ Fear was a Path specifically developed to harness fears and amplify them, allowing the thaumaturge to send mortals running back to their homes with but a glance whenever they do not wish to be disturbed.</p>\n<p>In the modern nights, Tremere seldom use this Path. Sabbat’s Dark Thaumaturgists popularised it under the name of Path of Phoebus.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Targets roll Composure + Resolve to resist. This roll may be reattempted once per turn by spending a point of Willpower. If the Spell Test was and the contests are successful, targets are taken with fear according to the Fear Intensity Aspect of the Spell. This is usually represented by them seeing their worst nightmares, demons, fears, phobias and so on, but some report feeling an indescribable sense of dread emitting from the sorcerer.</p>\n<p>Those with the vampiric beast instantly enter a Fear Frenzy upon losing the contest. Unless the sorcerer rolled a Critical on the Spell Test, they may take control of themselves by spending a point of Willpower like in a normal fear frenzy, but the cost of a Willpower point to reattempt the resistance roll is paid separately.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Fears, Manipulating Emotions</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range, Number of Targets and the following:</p>\n<h3>Fear Intensity</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the sorcerer is affected with fear instead.</p>\n<p>• The victims take a three-dice penalty to all Mental and Social rolls.</p>\n<p>•• The victims suffer a three-dice penalty on all rolls.</p>\n<p>••• The victim flees until the sorcerer is no longer in the area.</p>\n<p>•••• The victim flees and doesn’t stop running until they come to one place they feel the safest, usually a church for most medieval peasants.</p>\n<p>••••• Victims are so terrified that they fall into a catatonic state, making them unable to take actions or move. In combat, they are immobile targets.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"308"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PONe9sgz3reuNpqo"},"PUvaBSIW4GiAThqF":{"name":"Greek Fire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Coats one weapon per dose in a substance that can burn for a scene, causing the weapon to deal two additional points of Aggravated Health damage from fire. Alternatively, the substance can be placed in a container (one per dose) with a lighted fuse on top and flung at the enemy like a Molotov cocktail. It requires Composure + Firearms pool to throw vs. Dexterity + Athletics and on a win the target takes two points of Aggravated Health damage each turn unless they successfully douse themselves with a Composure + Survival or Science roll (difficulty 3). Others may attempt to douse using Intelligence or Resolve + Survival or Science. The visual of a fire weapon or fire spreading provokes a Fire Fear Frenzy at a difficulty of 2 or more at the storyteller’s discretion in all but the caster and their allies.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PUvaBSIW4GiAThqF"},"PW4S7J6vkAlSFYmP":{"name":"Ministraria di Morte","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Ghosts are a scarce resource in the competitive field of necromancers which is why tracking them down and being sure about it is crucial to the art. By obtaining a piece of dead body and simmering it in a pot with vampiric vitae, adding rosemary (for remembrance), basil (the funerary herb), and salt (the alchemic principle of clarification), a necromancer creates a stew that will tell them if the person has become one of the restless dead. This Ritual is often paired with the Ritual of the Unearthed Fetter to track the fetters of the ghost afterwards.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A bodypart of now-dead target, rosemary, basil, salt, a cooking pot or a bowl and the caster’s vitae</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer mixes the ingredients over fire or some other source of heat to crate a stew which they then ingest. Should the target be a wraith now, they experience a vision of clarity. The target being a spectre gives a nightmarish vision while a target who is not a ghost simply makes the stew a normal stew once ingested which most vampires will probably puke out due to the inability to digest food.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the necromancer is given the information if the target has become a ghost and, if they are a ghost, they will be told if the person is now a normal wraith or a spectre.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botchfailure, wrong information is given.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"334"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PW4S7J6vkAlSFYmP"},"Pd9kczeLkAW25fVc":{"name":"Dominion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The Blood Sorcerer makes themselves the sovereign of supernatural powers in their own domain, thwarting others’ use of most vampiric abilities.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (counted as one higher and as a radius),Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Seal made of iron embedded over every doorway in the building.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritual takes three hours to cast. During this time, the caster must be embedding iron seals over every doorway, consecrating them with their own vitae, and carving sigils on the doors.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon a successful Ritual roll, the Blood sorcerer stops any uses of Disciplines within the building except by themselves if they do not roll at least as many successes as the ritualist. If a power has no pool like Shapechange, the character makes one using an assigned Attribute + Skill pool like Resolve + Survival (Resolve being the most frequent when no pool is needed). The Ritual lasts according to the Duration but can be broken by the destruction of at least one seal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the ritual succeeds but only against the sorcerer who becomes a target of their own ritual and their powers are nullified (each 1 counts as an additional success on the ritual).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"299"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Pd9kczeLkAW25fVc"},"PiYAgAgWUpWLhfE3":{"name":"Clarion Call to the Loyal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Trumpets will sound on the day of judgement, calling the dead to the final reckoning. While that is far away, the necromancers are able to emulate it by calling on long-dead warriors with their own trumpets.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A bronze trumpet.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer fashions a bronze trumpet and inscribes spells on it. Those belonging to the school of Authority usually inscribe prayers while those belonging to the school of Taboo frequently inscribe mockeries of divine authority and various obscenities to attract ghosts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Do not make the Ritual roll until the trumpet is ready to use which takes a single turn of action to do. On a win, the necromancer calls upon a single spectral warrior that requires no power of its own to manifest physically, appearing either as a translucent warrior or a fully-normal looking warrior (the details are left to the player). On a critical win (+2 margins), the necromancer has summoned two such warriors. Let the player build them as Avearge Individuals.</p>\n<p>The warrior may be given a specific target (the Necromancer must provide a name or accurate description), or a more general task (“stay at my side and defend me”). Reusing the trumpet dismisses the current spectral warrior and summons again per system above</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"337"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PiYAgAgWUpWLhfE3"},"PjJ28QtSYB8kH7Br":{"name":"Knit the Veil","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The Shroud’s local rating is set to Impenetrable, preventing those from the other side from seeing or hearing anything and inflicting certain penalties (p. XX). Another caster may use powers like Shroud Manipulation (Difficulty equal to Knit the Veil’s successes) to remove this power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PjJ28QtSYB8kH7Br"},"PlldMduHTpqNUUP9":{"name":"Summon the Dead","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>Any ghost can be summoned to the caster. All the magician needs is the ghost’s True Name or a Fetter and to win against the ghost’s Composure + Resolve with their Spell Test. If the necromancer is successful, the ghost must then immediately arrive. Keep in mind that most dead people do not become ghosts, and most ghosts do not appreciate being summoned.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.PlldMduHTpqNUUP9"},"Q7f7C8gepxzmO468":{"name":"Facultas Emortua","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>on a successful skill test the caster chooses a skill of the victim. For the duration of the spell and as long as the skull is on their person they may swap their own skill rating with the victim's. This aspect may be cast multiple times on the same skull, but on every subsequent cast the DC is raised by one.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Q7f7C8gepxzmO468"},"QDtMPgXb4eaOaYxI":{"name":"Humour Alignment","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Healing Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>This is an old ritual for curing chronic and congenital conditions. It dates back to the Middle Ages when Humorism was in vogue. The healer bleeds the patient with leeches, a ritual knife, or another tool suited to their practice, which deals one level of Aggravated Health damage to most mortals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to bleed the patient with: leeches, a ritual knife...</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster first deals a point of damage to a mortal after which the subject is made to rest for 3 days.</p>\n<p>This rest needn’t be solely sleep. The patient may move minimally to perform basic bodily functions but otherwise should be laying down. If the patient is too active during the three days, the ritual fails automatically. The ritual will also fail if the patient suffers significant mental or emotional stress, such as trying to work from bed or having a heated argument.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make the Ritual Test at the end of the three-day resting period. On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 5), the subject heals Aggravated Health damage equal to the Healing Aspect and any Crippling Injuries save for missing limbs or grave mutilations.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> See Path Spell Risk.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"316"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QDtMPgXb4eaOaYxI"},"QFKYtrtklw4kFqoN":{"name":"Eldrich Mark","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Many enchanters are quite possessive of their work and sometimes mark it with a special mark as a form of “copyright” used early in the medieval period when one wanted to prove their abilities.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A liquid, most commonly liquid silver of blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer signs their own name with the liquid on an object and blesses it, causing the liquid to disappear. By inscribing a symbol or mark onto an object or the forehead of a person, the magicians marks them as theirs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the surface “drinks” the liquid. The mark is invisible to the naked eye, but obvious to anyone with magically enhanced perceptions of any sort. The mark informs anyone looking at it of the name of the magician who created the mark. This ritual does not work if the enchanter does not write the name they go by.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"294"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QFKYtrtklw4kFqoN"},"QMKFeV69J5iEr7ew":{"name":"Illusion (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>One of the most fundamental magics is the power to deceive others into believing what the magician wants. The Path of Illusion confounds the senses, making its targets perceive things that aren’t there or altering their perception of what is there. Most people trust their senses. “Seeing is believing,” but when an illusionist is nearby, this old adage can kill you.</p>\n<p>The Path of Illusion is much less direct in most of its applications than some of the other Paths. Illusionists tend to be clever manipulators, at the least in order to choose just the right false vision for whatever task must be completed. This often requires strange patterns of thought and lateral thinking, leading illusionists to become eccentric over time.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>An illusion cannot be made where the sorcerer cannot sense unless using a Sympathetic Connection through a ritual to reach a target or a place far away. They cannot create a purely auditory illusion somewhere they can’t hear it, or the image of a crown if they are blinded. However, if they can sense the illusion in any fashion, they can create the other sensory components of it. That is to say, if they have certain ranks of Aspects, they can create an illusory bowl of stew and it will taste right, even if it were across the room from them. They could create that crown if it were to rest on their head, so that they could feel it.</p>\n<p>Range Aspect merely represents the radius of the illusion’s size, not the range for affecting targets which is described above. When appropriate, Realism may add its rank as a dice bonus to Intimidation of those who have not perceived the creations of the caster to be illusions. With Realism 4, the user may provoke a Frenzy in vampires and other creatures with the Beast, usually Fear Frenzy, at a -1 Difficulty. With Realism 5, the Difficulty of the Frenzy Tests is normal. This usually requires at least the sense of sight. Fire might demand smell in certain situations, or even sound.</p>\n<p>Subjects may roll Wits + Awareness against the sorcerer’s successes on their Spell or Ritual Test to see through the illusion. Once revealed, they may tell it to others who may re-attempt their roll once if they’ve failed prior Anyone who makes this roll can tell that what they are perceiving isn’t real, though they perceive both it and the truth. An illusion cannot provoke a Frenzy against one who has disbelieved it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Lies, Sense Manipulation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (as a radius of how big a single casting of an illusion may be; melee is something that fits in a palm), Senses and:</p>\n<h3>Realism</h3>\n<p>• Completely immobile and fixed; if visual, flat</p>\n<p>•• Changes with perspective so the viewer moving doesn’t disrupt it immediately</p>\n<p>••• Illusions can have moving parts, but only large ones</p>\n<p>•••• Smaller motions and variations are possible, but there are still subtle tells</p>\n<p>••••• Fully immersive, this level completely fools all targeted senses</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"317"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QMKFeV69J5iEr7ew"},"QO6CcnEBdrN8jX9e":{"name":"Animate the Inanimate (Greater)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Identical to Animate the Inanimate (Lesser), except that the animated being is built as a Gifted Individual and costs as a two-dot retainer to permanently keep.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QO6CcnEBdrN8jX9e"},"QSkiPeIZJQoZYFfr":{"name":"Nightvision","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula eliminates any darkness penalty (not of supernatural darkness) by either enhancing the user’s visual senses or by giving them some sort of echo location. The user gains a dot in Wits, to a maximum of 5.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"263"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QSkiPeIZJQoZYFfr"},"QVVr5qnra72x6Y9M":{"name":"Principal Focus of Vitae Infusion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual imbues a quantity of blood within the object upon which the ritual is cast. The object must be small enough for the vampire to carry in both hands, and it may be as small as a coin. After the ritual is conducted, the object takes on a reddish hue and becomes slick to the touch. At a mental command, the thaumaturge may release the object from its enchantment, causing it to break down into a pool of blood. This blood may serve whatever purpose the vampire desires. Many Tremere wear enchanted baubles to ensure that they have emergency supplies of vitae.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An object that fits in the caster’s hand, and Blood of the user</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The user drenches the object with their Blood and speaks the words of the Ritual. When complete, the object absorbs the Blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make a Ritual roll (Difficulty 3), and if successful, the vampire imbues the object with an amount of Blood from the caster. If a person wishes to release their Blood from the object, they need to say the command word set by the caster during the Ritual’s Process. The amount of Blood stored can sate one Hunger for every two Rouse Checks made when storing the Blood. (Storing a single Rouse Check’s worth of Blood allows for a ghoul to get their fix or for use in another Ritual requirement.). Note that the Rouse Check activation cost of the Ritual is paid separately and may be rerolled. Example: Storing 2 Hunger’s worth requires 5 Rouse Checks.</p>\n<p>Activation Rouse Check is paid separate for this Ritual. Activating the object to release vitae is a minor action of -1 die and drinking it is a -2 dice cost, so a person doing both is performing a -3 dice Minor Action.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"323"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QVVr5qnra72x6Y9M"},"QVp5H2wOcsLO6tnb":{"name":"Soul’s Reflection","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Water is in almost everything. A part of every plant, every animal, even a part of vampires in one form or another through their liquids. By gazing into a reflective pool, the koldun is able to use it as a scrying mirror to monitor a target or a location.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Sympathetic Connection</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Sympathetic material from a person or a location to monitor</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The koldun pours a drop of their blood into the pool while tossing the sympathetic material</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Target resists the Rite Test’s successes with Wits + Occult. On a win, the reflective pool shows either the location or the person with the view of the location or a person. The koldun may adjust the point of view, but their point of view must be within 5 meters of the subject/location. The Rite is ended by the sorcerer willingly or after Duration expires.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"365"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QVp5H2wOcsLO6tnb"},"Qq1Q0qOsu7dNpPA7":{"name":"Disassociate","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>the target suffers a dice penalty to all Awareness and Social Skill tests equal to the user’s Path of Corruption rating. Resistance roll can be rerolled each scene instead of each session by spending a point of Willpower.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Qq1Q0qOsu7dNpPA7"},"QrJ5WVm6QmPT5gBB":{"name":"Mercury's tongue","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range must try spend their turn trying to engage the caster or a target he designated (must be living and hostile to the targets).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QrJ5WVm6QmPT5gBB"},"Qs4wNMqQgMsZo6EW":{"name":"Ghost Town","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-haunting.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Haunting Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual has long been used by the faevampires and the Giovanni who have bartered this Ritual from them to construct safe havens within the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Area, Corrosion (see below)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Sorcerer’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Duration, Range (radius over which the effect may take, counted as one higher)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The necromancer makes their Ritual roll and makes vague weaving movements as they shape the Shadowlands. The user gains dots to spend on a Haven inside the Shadowlands equal to half of their succesess on the Ritual, rounded up.</p>\n<p>These dots can be spent on any Domain advantage except those that are unreasonable to be there such as Library as one can not just manifest knowledge out of thin air. Similarly, Servants and Sanctum may not be manifested. Havens in the Shadowlands are referred to as “Haunts”. If you are using any ghost homebrew with Haunts, you may spend your dots on Haunt Advantages at the storyteller’s discretion. Players are encouraged to be creative with this Ritual as it is practically manifesting a physical reality.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"374"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Qs4wNMqQgMsZo6EW"},"QsOPYWX6b11aazLI":{"name":"Enchantment (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>The Path of Enchantment rewards patience and forethought more than any other Path. There are no spells in this Path, only rituals, including the long and difficult rituals creating magical objects called Artifacts. Each of these Artifacts must be carefully crafted by the Enchanter. The first step is to create the mundane base item. Usually these are handcrafted by the Enchanter, pushing their mundane skills to their limits to make just the right vessel for their magic. Sometimes, however, the right vessel can be found in the form of a rare or ancient object. Once the vessel has been made or found, the Enchanter can get to the process of infusing the object with magical energy to be used later. The first stage is usually accomplished through Crafts rolls (or, for technosorcerers, Technology rolls), while the latter is a Path roll.</p>\n<p>The Artifacts created through Enchantment differ in several key ways from the recipes of Alchemy. Artifacts are usually persistent, or at least can be used many times. Some may be permanent, or nearly so, while others can be used a fixed number of times before their magic dissipates.</p>\n<p>Enchanters tend to spend a lot of time and effort on their workshops. Whatever sort of crafting they do to make their Artifacts defines them. They often see themselves as blacksmiths, jewelers, electronics engineers, or woodworkers first, and the magic they can do comes later. This leads Enchanters to pursue high levels of the Resources Background, as well as others representing a safe and secure space to do their work and the ability to acquire high quality and often exotic raw materials.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>This Path has no spells. It does everything through rituals called recipes which can be acquired for free or bought like normal rituals. To create a recipe, a Ritual Test is performed at the end of creation period (see Creation Time).</p>\n<p>Unless stated otherwise, objects are permanent and their owners must buy them as a Background of dots equal to the level of the recipe. The one who crafted the object does not need to pay this price for personal usage, but the artefact will respond only to them.</p>\n<p>If a replicated power’s rating like a Discipline rating is required for replicated powers, use the creator’s Enchantment rating. The activation of replicated powers is always without a cost. For combat, consider anything but a dice bonus.</p>\n<p>Enchantment recipes are not real rituals and as such rolls of this Path benefit from Artefacts Specialty, not Rituals. The e actual Rituals of this Path benefit from Rituals Specialty as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Unless otherwise noted, most artefacts are a passive benefit with powers replicated taking the same type of an action they normally would. See Creation Time.</p>\n<p>Creation Time: Crafting time for the object, plus 2 days per level of the enchantment. Some items might have to be performed offscreen in “offtime” if timeskips are happening. In that case, make the Ritual rolls and pay the activation costs at the beginning of the next session.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Equipment:</strong> To successfully follow through a recipe, the sorcerer must have access to the Workshop Background of equal or lower rating than the rating of the recipe they are using.</p>\n<p>Difficulty and Modifiers: Difficulty for Spell tests to craft artefacts are always 1 + Enchantment Aspect’s rank that is being used. Once the sorcerer successfully follows a recipe three times, the Difficulty is reduced by one.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Artefacts, Gargoyles</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> The following:</p>\n<h3>Enchantment</h3>\n<p>We in general do not allow dice bonuses from powers to stack or, if they are similar, from other effects like the environment. Other bonuses do not stack as well, like an amulet that subtracts or converts damage and Fortitude.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Failure usually means the magician has wasted days or weeks of their time, possibly ruining the item they were trying to enchant, but nothing worse. Botches tend to be more varied. They can result in explosions that destroy the workshop, lowering the Workshop rating by 1 for each 1 rolled. Worse, though, they can result in objects that seem to work, but have some sort of unanticipated side effect. These cursed items tend to be hard to destroy or get rid of, often lingering long after the Enchanter is gone and even finding their way into the hands of mortals, endangering the Masquerade/ the Silence of the Blood.</p>\n<h3>Sample Recipes</h3>\n<p>More than simple creation of a magical object, Enchantment Rituals focus on improving the quality of the existing ones, whether material or immaterial. Many sorcerers turn the Enchantment Path to a greater scale, casting specialised warding rituals to protect their domains. Unless otherwise stated, Enchantment rituals have standard ritual casting times.</p>\n<p>The research and countless experiments conducted on Gargoyles by the “Mother” Virstania and Goratrix have resulted in a brand new category of rituals and alchemy known as the “Gargoyle Rituals” and “Malign Humours” respectively (see Alchemy Thaumaturgy). It is theorised that the reason why these rituals and alchemical humours work only on Gargoyles is that they are born through magic and not standard Embrace, but rumours have emerged that Goratrix keeps experimenting on captured Cainites, trying to eventually turn Gargoyle Rituals into ones that can empower the Clan Tremere as a whole. See Enchantment Rituals for Gargoyle creation.</p>\n<p>Gargoyle Rituals nominally function only on Gargoyle vampires, but they may also function on animal gargoyles and gargoyle ghouls or whatever other creations the Tremere have made that belong to that twisted family. While it may seem that the Tremere could simply enhance every Gargoyle in their service, these rituals take a lot of time, materials and pose a risk of making servants powerful enough to start thinking of freedom. A Gargoyle who hoards and flaunts around the benefits of too many of these rituals eventually catches the attention of superiors. After the Gargoyle revolt, these rituals have fallen out of use, but the Gargoyles have already internalised many of the Gargoyle rituals and turned them into a proper Discipline of Visceratika.</p>\n<p>Any Discipline power may be given to a Gargoyle through a Gargoyle Enchanting Ritual, but the ingredients must include at least a Rouse Check’s worth of blood from someone who knows the power and something symbolic of that power so that the Gargyoyle may take its property. For example, drinking a potion made out of a dissolved eyeball in mixed with blood of one who possesses Heightened Senses or Sense the Unseen. For the purposes of Discipline rating, it is equal to 13 minus the Generation of the Gargoyle, to a maximum of 5 and minimum of 1. Rituals to copy powers are equal in their rating to the copied power. Vicissitude is never available due to a specific element ingrained into the Gargoyle creation ritual by Goratrix himself.</p>\n<p>Despite not being invented yet, powers from the Discipline of Visceratika may be copied by awakening innate properties of the Gargoyle. These require only some form of symbolic material as a ritual ingredient, typically stone.</p>\n<p>These rituals can be hanged as normal and have standard ritual casting time. For example, a Gargoyle can go through a ritual, but Weight of a Feather enchantment to trigger once they ingest a feather dipped in vitae during the ritual.</p>\n<p>Gargoyles are limited in what Gargoyle Enchantment ritual they may benefit from by their Generation. A ritual has no effect on a Gargoyle its Generation is not equal to 12 minus the ritual’s level or lower. For example, a 10th Generation Gargoyle may only benefit from level 1 and level 2 Gargoyle rituals.</p>\n<p>1 12 or lower 2 11 or lower 3 10 or lower 4 9 or lower 5 8 or lower There are also a rituals unrelated to Disciplines that may empower Gargoyles. Even normal enchantments may be cast on Gargoyles as they’ve been designed to be more of a “thing” than “someone”.</p>\n<p>Normally, Gargoyle enchantments last for 24 hours, but a Gargoyle may internalise them to make them permanent at a cost of 4 x the level of the power. Activation costs a Rouse Check as normal. If bought during character creation, a Gargoyle player pays the rituals 6 x the level of the power which covers both the cost of somebody performing it on them and internalising it.</p>\n<p>Power bonus applies to powers acquired from Gargoyle Enchantment Rituals, much to the wonder and curiosity of the Tremere.</p>\n<h3>Gargoyle Enchantment Ritual Samples</h3>\n<p>• The Enchanter can create minor items that are rarely noticeably magical to those who aren’t already aware of their powers. The items gives a die bonus or penalty to an Attribute or a Skill roll for specific tasks. Other small boons are also possible.</p>\n<p>•• More powerful versions of already possible Artifacts offer a two-dice bonus or penalty on a task or a -1 Difficulty on a Task. Some of the objects created at this level can change reality in a subtle way, being more magical than previously possible.</p>\n<p>••• Obviously magical items are now possible, though they’ll still seem merely “very weird” to the magically unaware. These include items that offer two additional dice on up to three different Skill rolls or one Attribute roll. The item can confer supernatural perception to the user by replicating supernatural powers no greater than a single dot. Items may negate two successes up to three times a night.</p>\n<p>•••• Objects of truly superhuman power.</p>\n<p>These can raise Attributes by up to a combined total of two dots, but no higher than 7. They can replicate powers (up to the second dot) of other supernatural beings, and otherwise warp reality in significant ways. Items may negate three successes up to three times a night or absorb up to three points of damage before they break.</p>\n<p>••••• Minor miracles are possible, and often these powers are quite blatant. Items created at this level can reproduce up to three dots worth of supernatural powers. Each dot reproduced this way mimics a single effect – chosen when the item is crafted – even if the replicated power offers multiple effects. Items may confer mastery, increasing a single Skill rating to 6 or to 5 for Physical Skills.</p>\n<p>•• Candle of Fate (Secondary Paths: Fortune 2) A colored candle that, when burned, grants two dots of a Background for the purposes of a one-time favor.</p>\n<p>•••• translate anything written in it into the reader’s native language. To activate it, the page must be sprinkled with paper ash. When set down, the writing reverts to the original language.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"288"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.QsOPYWX6b11aazLI"},"Qt2FnaEixU7rvql1":{"name":"Foundation of the Chantry","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>After slaying a white or albino mammal or bird indigenous to the area and soaking the stone in its blood, the sorcerer draws a map of the area (up to about a 12-mile radius) on the skull and places a polished sphere of amethyst skull’s eye socket or mouth. On a successful creation, The gem and skull are bound to a Sanctum and become the Foundation of the Chantry. As long as the Foundation remains in place, the caster or their designee (who must be present to participate in the last minute of the creation to be designated) can receive missives from the Rite of Introduction (• Lifeblood Ritual). The caster knows whenever the Rite of Introduction is performed within the same city as the Foundation but must touch it to receive the introduction. Upon doing so, they may send a signal back to the caster of the Rite of Introduction, consisting of an impression of their name, face, the location of the chantry, and a single-sentence message. As with the Rite of Introduction, the caster can choose to withhold, but not obscure, any of the above. The Ritual lasts until the Foundation is disturbed. The artefact does not have to be bought with exp.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Nobody but the Tremere regents and their higher ups are allowed to know of this ritual.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Qt2FnaEixU7rvql1"},"Qtk6PAuCD3fIxPde":{"name":"Corrosive Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Altering the properties of some of their own Blood, the user can make it highly corrosive to dead substances, able to corrode most items down to steaming sludge, given enough time and Blood shed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Area, Corrosion (see below)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Sorcerer’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritual requires only 30 seconds as the sorcerer forces blood out of a (usually self-inflicted wound)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, each point of Corrosion Aspect corrodes approximately 10 cm of matter in all directions from the splash; this takes approximately five minutes (longer on soft metals like copper or cast iron). Harder metals such as alloys and steel merely scar and pit; whether they meaningfully weaken remains at the discretion of the Storyteller. (This power might corrode through handcuff chains given enough time and vitae.)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"320"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Qtk6PAuCD3fIxPde"},"RBkiO5CUVnxKOxnR":{"name":"Storm, blizzard, massive sandstorm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Combines effects of rain and high winds. All Predator Types suffer an increase in difficulty of two due to everybody staying indoors.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.RBkiO5CUVnxKOxnR"},"RFFven2YTr2QgIpn":{"name":"Sleet","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (•). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>the caster shoots a stream of ice and/or freezing water. Targets can try to avoid the stream with a dodging roll or suffer Superficial Health damage equal to the margins or twice the Damage Aspect, whichever is lower). This damage cannot be made Aggravated.</p>\n<p>Those hit or crossing the freezing water or ice have their Initiative reduced by three and must make a successful Dexterity + Athletics (Difficulty 2) or fall and lose their next main action.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"284"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.RFFven2YTr2QgIpn"},"RKITZu1tBe2vNX0w":{"name":"Algor Mortis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (••). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>Inducing the stiffness of death in the mind, the caster ignores all forms of Impairment penalties, may not be stunned and gains a +3 dice bonus against Persuasion, Intimidation, Insight and emotion-reading or thought-reading powers. However, the subject suffers a die penalty to all Degeneration Tests for the duration. This may affect others, using Range and Number of Targets aspects. Instead of Range, Sympathetic Connection may be used.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"371"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.RKITZu1tBe2vNX0w"},"RQniuIAVknAneObg":{"name":"Teleport Station","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conveyance Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Spells are all quite good and all, but unreliable. This ritual establishes a type of a portal. When a person enters it or steps on it, they are teleported to another designated location.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> See process</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer marks two identical areas or large objects like closets or coffins which will act as a two-way teleporting station. Creation is followed by a ritual chant. At the end of the ritual, the sorcerer activates it and teleports.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the Ritual Test, sorcerer is automatically transported to the other teleporting station as a sign of the place working. From then on until one of the “stations” has its appearance altered to no longer resemble the other one or until the Duration expires, all it takes is a person stepping on or into the “station” to teleport to another in the blink of an eye.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The sorcerer ends up being split in half if they lose control at the end of the ritual. Quite gruesome. Vampires and other creatures that can regenerate lose half of their Health rounded up as it is covered in Aggravated Health damage. This includes ghouls, although they must make a Rouse Check to stop the bleeding or die.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"278"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.RQniuIAVknAneObg"},"Rqgx65A0oyBOKKo9":{"name":"Reclamation of Vitae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects</em></p>\n<p>The user concentrates for a turn and choses which of their servants are to repay their Blooddebt The ghouls do not need to be present, and distance doesn’t matter. The vampire sates Hunger equal to the Hunger Aspect for each ghoul selected by the Number of Targets, while the ghoul suffers aggravated damage equal to the Hunger slaked as their body ages irregularly and rapidly, the undead Blood inside them feeling as if it were attempting to violently vacate them. Ghouls also suffer an increase of Hunger equal to the Hunger Aspect, possibly rendering an ancient ghoul to ash instantly.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Rqgx65A0oyBOKKo9"},"Rqw2EEb5AccJZZ9w":{"name":"Heartseeker","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A stiletto or a stake which unerringly aims for the heart when wielded in rage. The wielder does not suffer a called shot penalty on staking rolls or rolls to aim for the heart.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Rqw2EEb5AccJZZ9w"},"RxfqlMVVmbKWyN3e":{"name":"Intriguing","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Special Effect (•). Aspects: In addition to the Aspects of Number of Targets, Range, and Sympathetic Connection, Fascination uses the Allure and Willbending Aspects.</em></p>\n<p>You draw attention even in a crowd, and individuals find you interesting in one-on-one conversation. You may make mild suggestions that align with the target’s personality and situation such as getting another drunk if they are at a bar.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"306"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.RxfqlMVVmbKWyN3e"},"RyWGDz1BY1HsZoMi":{"name":"Patch the Corpse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (••). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>Coaxing the undead form to heal, the character is able to heal Superficial Health damage through rest like a mortal the next time they awaken. Alternatively, a point of Aggravated Health damage may be healed the next time the caster awakens, in addition to standard mending.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"371"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.RyWGDz1BY1HsZoMi"},"S2O5XzeydMewhEpy":{"name":"Lightning","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>Those hit with Lightning suffer (damage equal to twice the Damage Aspect or margins, whichever is lower). Damage from Lightning is always Aggravated even to vampires, who must also make a fire Fear Frenzy Check (difficulty 2) once hit. This spell ignores metallic armour, but can be affected by specialised suits according to the Avoidance rule if your table uses it.</p>\n<p>Lightning can spread further than Aspects dictate. Everybody in contact with the target through a conductive material rolls Stamina + Stamina against the spell’s successes to resist damage. Targets struck must spend a point of Willpower or be stunned and lose a turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"284"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.S2O5XzeydMewhEpy"},"SFAxbWCqKL9OY7TI":{"name":"Transitive Bond","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula preserves one Rouse Check or one Hunger’s Worth of vitae from the same vampire per dose in addition to one Rouse Check activation cost (blood in doses cannot exceed blood used as ingredients) and allows it to maintain blood bonding and ghouling properties indefinitely or until the blood is exposed to the Sun. Those who drink this formula or who feed on mortals who were fed this formula count as if they drank directly from the vein of the vampire whose vitae was used in this formula for the purposes of ghouling and blood bonding.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.SFAxbWCqKL9OY7TI"},"SOtikHZZ9iHvMBGp":{"name":"Enchanted Mail","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A silvered mail tunic that absorbs three points of Health damage before it breaks. If any attempt at cleaning or repair is made, the magic is lost.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.SOtikHZZ9iHvMBGp"},"SiIwExbIHva2xeI5":{"name":"Ferryman’s Recal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Ghouls are a long-term investment and a problem to replace, especially if they were a promising student who has been groomed by embrace. This Ritual, originally developed by the Cappadocians and now gaining popularity within La Famiglia, allows a necromancer to embrace a mortal, even if they have died a long time ago.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Paths:</strong> Necromancy 3</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (counted as higher by 3) for how long the subject has been dead, Number of Targets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Vitae for embrace, subject’s fetter and the corpse which must not have rotted beyond recognition, often preserved by the Preserve Cadaver Ritual (p. X) or by simply falling into a bog.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The subject’s body is first cleaned and then drained of all blood or embalming fluids with their fetter placed above their head. After that, the necromancer paints a number of sigils on the the body with domitor’s blood, acting as both a beacon and vessels of energy for the wraith to adapt to the new body. Once that is done, the necromancer begins their incantations and at the end feeds the corpse vitae for embrace which proceeds as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The caster makes the Necromancy Ritual roll once the corpse has been fed at least a hunger’s worth of vitae. On a Win, any wounds suffered before the Ritual, including the cause of the subject’s death and any decomposition, close, but do not entirely heal, leaving scars on the newly embraced vampire’s body. After that, the embrace continues as normal.</p>\n<p>Storyteller is encouraged to reduce Looks Merits and/or give Looks Flaws depending on how withered the corpse has been. The new vampire also gains the equivalent of the Looks Flaw: (•) Ugly to represent the constant ash-like skin complexion they gain, similar to that for which the Cappadocians are known. The experience beyond the Shroud usually leaves permanent damage on the subject’s psyche, resulting in permanent Derangements at the Storyteller’s Discretion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"347"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.SiIwExbIHva2xeI5"},"SxWdpHs79i42DmRA":{"name":"Baleful Exorcism","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>Targeted mortals roll Composure + Resolve. If the caster wins, their souls are expelled from their bodies for the Duration or until the body takes a point of damage. In this state, the subjects are for all intents and purposes of systems ghosts, treating Willpower as the Health tracker for their new ectoplasmic form. If destroyed in this state (either by destroying the body or the ectoplasmic corpus), the subject dies and returns as a vengeful sepctre. Ghosts may be exorcised out of a mortal body in this manner as well.</p>\n<p>A corpse in this state may be possessed by a ghost without a roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.SxWdpHs79i42DmRA"},"SyC3elX9AmxG97Ir":{"name":"Ex Nihilo","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual enables a vampire and their coterie to migrate into the Shadowlands, though doing so comes at great risk.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Masks for each participant, a bowl containing sufficient quantity of the caster’s vitae so each participant might coat the soles of their feet in it, two coins of any value per participant.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Few rituals of Oblivion come with as much doubt and fear as Ex Nihilo, the ability to migrate into the Shadowlands. This ritual enables a physical crossing into the lands of entropy. Characters who physically enter the Shadowlands may interact with wraiths as if they were solid, but cannot carry objects beyond those on their person with them.</p>\n<p>Ex Nihilo appeals to a great many necromancers and mystics who want to study the Shadowlands without the impediment of a time limit. It’s an unmatched method for interviewing ghosts and exploring the necropoli — the cities spirits inhabit. It’s also incredibly dangerous, as many wraiths — especially spectres — seek to destroy vampires, draining them of their Willpower, and there’s always the risk of meeting the ghost of someone the vampire slew years earlier. Such wraiths tend to hold a grudge.</p>\n<p>The necromancer must used Shroud Manipulation to reduce the Shroud rating to Absent before in the location they’re currently occupying before they attempt to perform the ritual in order for Ex Nihilo to function. If the ritual is a success, the caster and any companions may enter the Shadowlands from that point, if they don masks to cover their faces, dip or paint their feet in the caster’s blood, and carry a coin in each hand.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The caster makes two Rouse Checks (sufficient to expend the required vitae) in addition to the activating one and spends a turn concentrating, expending a Willpower point to prepare for the crossing. They then make their ritual roll. If successful, the vampire, a number of companions according to the Passenger Aspect, and any objects on their person may then enter the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>The Shadowlands follows several rules you may find on p. XX. Characters may still use their powers like Disciplines as normal while in the Underworld. Like ghosts, characters pass through things that they might softly bump into (like humans) that are in the Skinlands while buildings in Skinlands that would fully stop them may be passed through by intangible turns equal to the character’s Stamina. Becoming intangible deals a point of Superficial Health damage.</p>\n<p>Returning to the other side requires a successful recasting of this ritual. Normally, items from beyond the Veil cannot be carried back into the land of the living. See Orpheic Sujourn for that. Note that this ritual does not give mortals the ability to breathe who will suffocate as the Underworld has no air.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"338"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.SyC3elX9AmxG97Ir"},"SycoVXlIQlA0mN03":{"name":"Boukephos’ Gateway","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The mystic opens their very soul to the Abyss while staring into a mirror (which usually gives no reflection due to most mystics being of the Clan Lasombra) from the end of one night until the beginning of a new one, requiring a Road rating roll at a Difficulty of 3 (failure cancels the ritual). Roll the ritual roll at the beginning of the following night. On a win, the mystic has successfully laid their soul bare before the Abyss which now fills them. From now on, the mystic counts their Blood Potency as doubled for the purposes of rerolling Rouse Checks for activating Obtenebration powers and Abyss Mysticism rituals. Additionally, if the character was not on the Road of the Abyss, they switch to it without a changed rating.</p>\n<p>If the ritual fails, the mystic enters torpor and can only be awakened by being fed at least one Rouse Check’s worth of vitae of an Obtenebration user with Blood Potency higher than theirs by least two.</p>\n<p>If you are not using our Roads homebrew, this ritual can instead lose their touchstones to replace them with appropriate abyss-mysticism Convictions.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> None. Being on the Road of the Abyss can be considered a sequela.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"254"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.SycoVXlIQlA0mN03"},"TRzcGIyO9wAJwSQn":{"name":"Pull of the Grave","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Islam and Christianity teach that death is the end of the physical life and that the souls of the dead will be called on the Judgment Day. Until then, the corpses must stay in the ground. Pull of the Grave allows a necromancer to effectively perform an exorcism on ghosts and even against other undead such as Cainites.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> The caster must be a sincere believer in the Abrahamic God or else the Ritual fails.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing is required besides the ability to speak and the line of sight of the target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer must pray to God/ Allah as they normally do each night or uphold their Road of Heaven in some other ritualistic way they see fit. As long as that requirement is fulfilled, they rebuke the restless dead’s existence as a normal action. Ghosts targeted by this flee while Cainites have a harder time awakening.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> To perform this Ritual, the target must have a line of sight against the target. Unlike most Rituals, this one takes a single turn to perform like a normal power as the “Ritual” preparation is the caster’s way of life and the Road they walk.</p>\n<p>The caster locks eyes with a ghost or a Cainite and rebukes them, making a Ritual roll against the victim’s Composure + Resolve. On a win, ghost rushes back to its fetter and stays next to it or in it for the rest of the night. A Cainite victim suffers Difficulties awakening from the grave, requiring making two Rouse Checks to awaken and a successful roll of Road rating against a Difficulty of 3 to get up. Otherwise, they remain dormant for an hour, rolling their Road rating each hour until midnight when they awaken automatically. If the target manages to resist the caster’s roll, they are immune to this Ritual cast by the same caster for the rest of the story.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The sorcerer suffers the effect instead on a Botch.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"350"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.TRzcGIyO9wAJwSQn"},"TS9Kwq8NFaBNtYRA":{"name":"Curse in a Bottle","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula creates a liquid that, when drank, affects the person with a curse that was placed into the liquid. The sorcerer or somebody else with Path of Fortune or similar powers must “bless” the liquid at the beginning of the creation process and make a roll, adding successes equal to their Alchemy rating, that is resisted by those who drink it. The curse spell or a ritual does not need to use Number of Targets, Sympathetic Connection, Range and other Aspects that would not be relevant.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"263"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.TS9Kwq8NFaBNtYRA"},"TbH7nSt7RDGFYtDF":{"name":"Insane bravery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range ignore mental impairment</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.TbH7nSt7RDGFYtDF"},"TiTfsmX2yPylXbJL":{"name":"Walk Through the Shroud","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>A development of the Giovanni necromancers recently Embraced into the clan, this rite allows a necromancer to force their own soul to follow the course of one that actually dies, leaving their body behind and crossing into the lands of the dead, but with the ability to return to the physical plane. This return is accomplished through a focus on the spiritual echoes left in the undead corpse (those same echoes utilised in the Reanimation Path).</p>\n<p>Still tied to the body, the Cainite’s soul is not able to travel further than the dark limbo of lost souls. Heaven and Hell (and the soul’s ultimate rewards) are not accessible to the practitioner of necromancy. In these dank shadow-lands, the necromancer may encounter ghosts and other lost souls, and they may well be able to extract some information or arcane services from them.</p>\n<p>This rite is not to be undertaken lightly. The ghosts in limbo are rarely happy to see vampires, and a Cainite may find that the ghosts of some of their victims are bent on vengeance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> The Clan of Death has acquired this ritual through dealings with the Giovanni</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Paths:</strong> Reanimation 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (Counted as higher by 1)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The vampire must undertake the rite — which involves chanting incantations while surrounded by grave dust — from dusk until midnight, at which point it takes effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the vampire’s soul to separate from their body in a manner similar to Anima Walk (Auspex 5, see p. 26) except that they’re for all intents and purposes a ghost. The soul is immediately swept through the Shroud between life and death and arrives in the nightmarish limbo of lost souls. The Storyteller is free to describe this dark realm as she sees fit. Some Cainites have reported it to be an endless land of choking mist, others a dark reflection of the living world, and so on. The number of ghosts in the area depends on how likely the Storyteller feels the place is to be haunted. Graveyards, battlefields and hospices are always replete with the lost.</p>\n<p>The vampire’s soul can remain in this underworld until they physically enter to their body once again. Their body remains vulnerable as with a vampire using Anima Walk and their spirit is attached to the body with a silver cord which snaps at the end of Duration. The vampire cannot bring anything back across the shroud, save their own memories.</p>\n<p>Like with Anima Walk, the user may spend a point of Willpower to manifest in an idealistic sense and converse with those in the Skinlands. The user can use Mental Disciplines, but only on those within the Lands of the Dead. The user’s form is for all intents and purposes a ghost (p. XX).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Silver cord doesn’t appear.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"340"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.TiTfsmX2yPylXbJL"},"Tj2yWmpTRfQ2obZc":{"name":"Antiarrow Ring","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A silver toe-ring that, when worn on otherwise bare feet, protects from projectiles, giving a two-dice bonus to one wearing it for dodging projectiles with Athletics.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Tj2yWmpTRfQ2obZc"},"TrVVkS6WuBYSgucv":{"name":"Viscera Forest","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Almost a direct upgrade to Viscera Garden, this rare and powerful ritual allows the caster to convert existing plantlife into a large viscera garden spanning potentially entire forests, hence the name. This ritual is rare outside of the Bahari, as such a potent display of power requires a large number of corpses to be fed to it regularly.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (as radius; counted as 1 higher)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An area of land, day-old human or animal corpses, the caster’s Blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster travels to the center of the area they want to convert into a Viscera Forest and digs a hole. Placing one corpse for each Range rank used in the ritual (either all human, or all animal) into the hole, they then tear open their wrist and pour a Rouse Check’s worth of Blood into each human or animal corpse. The corpse being a day old is essential in the human corpse’s case; accidental Embraces have occurred when a human corpse is too fresh. The corpses are eviscerated, and covered with the soil dug from the hole.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A win on the Ritual Test begins to convert the surrounding plantlife in a radius equal to the Range aspect + 1 into plants capable of consuming corpses and feeding off vitae. This transformation is completed a number of weeks equal to 10 minus margins. In addition, plants converted are almost identical to those grown in a Viscera Garden for the purposes of being consumed by cainites. Unlike a Viscera Garden however, these plants must receive the Range aspect in either human or animal corpses (whichever was used to enact the ritual) every month, or else they wither and die within a week. Using Green Path spells and rituals on any plant located within the area of a Viscera Forest reduces the casting difficulty by 1. A critical win extends the length of time between feedings to two months. Using a human corpse doubles the Range aspect, but does not increase the difficulty.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"356"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.TrVVkS6WuBYSgucv"},"UDBEn83QKyGFurYD":{"name":"Pontos Euxeinos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>The Greeks once called the Black Sea Pontos Euxeinos, the Hospitable Sea, as euphemism. For the koldun of the Black Sea Kraina, it truly can be, holding them in its embrace and healing them of even their worst injuries.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> To intiate the rite, the koldun submerges themselves or somebody else they wish to heal into a pool of water and drips some of their blood. Upon successful completion of the rite, the koldun is healed according to the Aspect of Healing (Superficial Health equal to the Healing Aspect’s rank or the margins, whichever is lower). By raising the Difficulty by two, the koldun may also heal Aggravated Health damage as normal. One person can only benefit from this rite once per night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"365"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.UDBEn83QKyGFurYD"},"UOouHUgdl67KujeG":{"name":"Innocence’s Veil","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>The ritual allows the vampire to temporarily make any trace of diablerie on their part undetectable.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A silver knife, a white candle, a mortal victim</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster submerges the object in water and lets their Blood drip into the bowl, concentrating upon the last memory of their sire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the ritual is successful, signs of diablerie on the user cease to manifest. Powers such as A Taste for Blood or Scry the Soul do not detect any evidence the ritualist has committed diablerie in the past.</p>\n<p>This ritual lasts until Duration expires or until the ritualist feeds again, whichever comes sooner. This may protect even those with Blatant Diablerist Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"328"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.UOouHUgdl67KujeG"},"USBSzShw3hn6xHYb":{"name":"Jupiter's might","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range inflict an extra +2 damage on all of their melee attack rolls made with mundane weapons or unarmed strikes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.USBSzShw3hn6xHYb"},"UaeW6mv9KpOhQmPR":{"name":"Divination (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>It is said that during the time of the mortal House of Tremere, Tremere saw his own death by old age and sought to cheat fate by any means necessary. During the war with the Tzimisce and their old magi allies, some Warlocks used Divination to try to predict outcomes of battles and even the whole war. Divination promises much, but its unpredictability and vagueness leaves many to consider divination to be fool’s magic, not to mention that future is ever-changing. A sorcerer may use Divination to find clues to the most likely outcomes, but the wise seer knows the value of accurately interpreting the imprecise and dreamlike symbolism to judge likely probability changes.</p>\n<p>Most Diviners choose one practice, but it’s not unheard of for sorcerers to have a few practices they use depending on the situation or client, especially if a favored practice is illegal or subject to being thwarted by the weather, such as with astrology.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full, but may take longer per method.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> </p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Astrology, Divination, Haruspicy, Palmistry, Oneiromancy, Scrying, Tarot...</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Divination uses only three Aspects. Time Period (past, present or future) and:</p>\n<h3>Accuracy</h3>\n<h3>Query</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, signs and interpretations become misleading. Instead of a bonus, the character suffers a penalty equal to the number of 1s on their next roll or whenever the Storyteller deems it appropriate. Query yields no bonus.</p>\n<p>STORYTELLER NOTES: MANAGING DIVINATION Divination can be one of the most difficult Paths to manage for the Storyteller to allow player characters to learn. Abuse of the Path can throw a wrench in even the best laid plans and severely hinder Storytellers who rely heavily on improvisation. Storytellers are encouraged to take one or more steps to reduce Divination misuse; some suggestions are included here:</p>\n<p>Future is never certain and Divination can never be fully accurate. Standard spell of the Path may be available for more creative uses than at first glance, such as restoring old documents, which makes rituals usually obsolete except to sharpen the Path’s spell.</p>\n<p>• 1 Difficulty if using Extraordinary Science, but this Practice gives more precise probabilities.</p>\n<p>• –1 Difficulty when under the influence of mind-altering substances. Visions and signs may come easier and more vibrant in altered states but remain up to interpretation.</p>\n<p>• Visions, voices and signs are incredibly vague and difficult to interpret with any certainty</p>\n<p>•• Typically accurate but deeply buried in symbolism</p>\n<p>••• Accurate but vague and open to misinterpretation</p>\n<p>•••• The truth’s there under layers of symbolism, but not terribly difficult to interpret</p>\n<p>••••• Accurate and often easy to understand, though still subject to human error</p>\n<p>• A simple question that could be answered yes or no if you were talking to an average person rather than plants, stars or entrails.</p>\n<p>•• A simple question that can be answered with a short and simple explanation. For example, “Which road ahead is safest?”</p>\n<p>but not “What’s our enemy’s plan?”</p>\n<p>••• The question could be researched and EXAMPLE DIVINATION PRACTICES This list is by no means exhaustive – there are countless methods within the Path of Divination, and every Diviner puts something of themself into their practice. A cartomancer might have a favorite tarot deck or spread, and a bibliomancer might swear their divination is only accurate when they use works by Plato. Technomancers typically use videomancy and probability analysis while traditionalist Tremere tend to prefer astrology, bibliomancy, and especially oneiromancy. They may be specialties.</p>\n<p>Abacomancy Divination using dust, sand, or ashes of the dead dropped on a surface Astrology Studying the interactions and positions of celestial bodies to predict the future Bibliomancy Prediction by opening books, often religious texts, to random pages Cartomancy Using a deck of cards to predict the future – often, but not always, tarot or other specialised decks Casting Throwing carved rune stones, bones, or sticks and reading the patterns made Felidomancy Observation of cat behavior to predict the future Haruspicy Reading the future out of viscera of living beings. The greater the being, the more accurate the future will be with humans and mythic creatures being the most desired victims Necromancy Listening to the voices of the dead who foretell the future Oneiromancy Interpretation of dreams Orinthomancy Observation of bird behavior for predictions. Also known as Augury.</p>\n<p>Palmistry Divining future by reading the lines on a palm Probability Analysis Using extraordinary science and chaos math to predict probability of future events Scrying Reading the future in pools of water, crystal balls, mirrors, clouds, etc.</p>\n<p>Videomancy Reading the future through moving images answered with public source knowledge if only you had the hours to put in.</p>\n<p>•••• The question may be detailed and require hidden information, but the information could be uncovered given enough time and know-how. E.g. Desire, Ambition, Convictions, etc.</p>\n<p>••••• A very detailed query that would normally require lost, destroyed, or deliberately hidden information to answer. “Is the prince a diablerist”, for example.</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the Storyteller answers questions at their discretion. If the events are really impossible to predict or in cases of trying to predict, the storyteller should be as vague as possible, but still try to provide some satisfaction for a successful spell.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user can perform Divination on more vague things to gain a mechanical dice bonus. On a successful Spell Test, Accuracy provides a dice bonus to any non-tracker pool equal to its rating. Similarly, Query allows for a free reroll of a number of dice of a non-tracker pool equal to its rank. Even Hunger dice may be rerolled in this manner. No more than three dice may be added or rerolled in a single pool. The bonuses last until the caster spends them, Time Period Ends or casts another Divination spell which resets the bonuses. These Aspects may be brought above their 5th rank for this mechanical purpose with certain rituals.</p>\n<p>Divination cannot aid pools to fortune-tell or where the user consciously forced some kind of a benefit (like from a Blood Surge, artefact usage or another dice bonus) as fate comes on its own and artefacts often influence change and fate by themselves.</p>\n<p>• Ban Divination for player characters and only use it with Storyteller characters to advance the plot.</p>\n<p>• Disallow use for trivial matters, such as which coffee to order, though cost should discourage this.</p>\n<p>• Focus on mechanical Divination, especially for mortals to contend with supernatural dice surges.</p>\n<p>• When planning plot, be sure to asl, “What if they roll Divination on this?” in the back of your mind.</p>\n<p>• Even if players dodge a negative prediction, the Storyteller might still have the prediction occur by technicality – perhaps in a way that’s not as dire or doesn’t directly affect the player characters. For example, if the sorcerer divines future personal loss, they might save their spouse’s life but lose their wedding ring in the process.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"281"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.UaeW6mv9KpOhQmPR"},"Uay27rF3obyvOGJy":{"name":"Amulet against the Infernal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Same as Amulet against Magic except that it impacts Infernal powers like Arcana and Dark Thaumaturgy against the wearer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Uay27rF3obyvOGJy"},"Uf03iDfgiAsRsk1Z":{"name":"Corpse in the Monster (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>This Path enhances the necromancer’s understanding of the unliving form and allows the user to fully experience the corpse, the gateway between life and death. The Path lets the Cappadocian apply some of a corpse’s traits to a vampire, and they can enhance or reduce these traits at various levels of the power. Older Priests of Set, who also practice a variant of this Path, deny deny that Cappadocious invented this Path and instead tell a different tale of how Set used his skills of mummification and burial rituals to survive in the Underworld.</p>\n<p>As this Path manipulates the (un)dead form and its properties, only undead sorcerers may practice this Path which normally means vampires, but it isnt unreasonable to assume a hedge magician lich would practice something similar. According to the records of a Roman Malkavian Methuselah, Cappadocians developed this Path by consorting with an Etruscan lich.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Unless stated otherwise, spells and rituals of this Path can only affect the caster. Corpse in the Monster is a Path of intimate study of the undead form, and such intimacy is impossible to attain with others. Casting Difficulties for this Path are 1 + the rating of the Corpse State aspect used.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full as spells for Mortis practice, as rituals for Akhu practice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Corpse State Special Effects</p>\n<h3>Corpse State</h3>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Failing some spells like Benedictio Vitae, Benedictio Mortis or the Unblinking Eye tends not to be noticed until it is too late which is where the greatest danger lies. On a Botch, the spell fails and the caster’s undead form withers from magic as each 1 rolled removes three dots from one of their Physical Attributes until they next awaken from daysleep, starting with Stamina, then Strength, then Dexterity. If this woudl bring all Attributes to 0, the character is paralysed, unmoving like a corpse, until they awaken next from daysleep.</p>\n<p>This Path does everything through its spells or, when performed in Akhu tradition, rituals that form the core of the Path.</p>\n<p>••• The caster turns themselves into a mummy invulnerable to most sources of damage. After the roll, the character benefits and suffers from the effects of Mortuus in Vultu using the Spell Tests successes like for that power, save that successes for the dice bonus are not halved. While in sleep or torpor, they also become immune to piercing weapons deal no damage while other attacks and sunlight may deal only up to a single point of Superficial Health damage per turn, even if it is specifically blessed against vampires. This may affect others, using Range and Number of Targets aspects.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Awaken the Slumbering Curse:</strong> A</p>\n<p>•••• necomancer with this Special Effect may project a measure of their undead nature into a ghoul, causing their borrowed vitae to burn at the touch of sunlight. This power is favored as an assassination tool or a flashy means of delivering a challenge to a rival. Ghouls affected by this power feel oddly feverish; most do not suspect they have been mystically altered, and even those who do have no means of identifying the source of the magic. While enchanted with this spell, a ghoul suffers damage from sunlight as though a vampire, specifically their domitor whose Bane Severity they use to calculate damage. This Special Effect uses the Aspects of Range and Number of Targets. Instead of Range, Sympathetic Connection may be used.</p>\n<p>••••• Benedictio Vitae is one of the most interesting secrets of the Clan Cappadocian for if it ever gets out, everybody and their sires will be turning up in roves to Erciyes begging the Grave-robbers to teach them necromancy. This power allows a vampire to shed their vampirism in its entirety, becoming a human who is immune to the Sun, is able to eat and enjoy the embrace of their lover as a human is. Many Capapdocians utilise this power to take a vacation from the Jyhad and vampire world altogether.</p>\n<p>After a successful casting, the caster gain a full immunity to the Sun and they are not compelled to sleep during the day although they might still get tired and need sleep — this power cannot be used in two successive days as the character is simply too tired from the lack of sleep. They also gain the ability to taste food and everything that mortals can enjoy. The user is still vulnerable to the stakes, fire and everything else that would hurt a vampire. The Beast is muffled and the user becomes immune to frenzies and rolls without Hunger dice.</p>\n<p>The Beast deeply resents being caged and exacts its vengeance upon its jailer when it is freed. After the Benedictio Vitae expires, the difficulty of all rolls to resist Frenzy for the next six nights increases by one</p>\n<p>••••• Cheat the Scales of Hades (Followers of</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Set only):</strong> The lector priest removes their own heart as with the Hearth Thief power (Serpentis •••••), sharing the same benefits and drawbacks save that the lector priest may only use it on themselves.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"371"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Uf03iDfgiAsRsk1Z"},"UsFkzdU4qd5P356m":{"name":"Nightmare Drums","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>By sounding the drums of human skin and using a personal item of the target, the necromancer summons ghosts from the Shadowlands to haunt the dreams of their target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Sympathetic Aspect</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Drums made from human skin, a personal item of the target or some other Sympathetic Connection</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer burns the personal item of the target or some object laced with a peron’s Sympathetic material (or with their True Name inscribed), sending it into the Shadowlands as an offering to the ghosts. Then, the necromancer (and their assistants, if possible) sound the drums of the human skin which do not make a sound in the lands of the living but of the dead. Ghosts reluctantly agree to torment the victim as it won’t be much of a hassle in their visits to the Skinlands and other duties they have.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual roll, the target suffers from nightmares and only half of their Willpower rounded up when they awaken instead. On a Critical Win, they heal no Willpower whatsoever. The target may attempt to resist the Ritual’s effects with a Composure + Occult roll against the successes on the Ritual roll. A person who sleeps in a ward against ghosts or similar magical protections can not be affected by this Ritual. A person may suffer the effects from only one instance of this Ritual at a time and may retry the Test each sunset.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"337"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.UsFkzdU4qd5P356m"},"UxCW5WkLU4FEu92w":{"name":"Transubstantiation of the Seven","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Probably the most famous ritual of the Clan Tremere, one that is (or was) the cornerstone of the pyramid. It allows a thaumaturge to transmute blood into one that is a mixture of the Council of Seven for the purposes of blood bonding, but is useless for the purposes of sympathetic magic against the Council as the blood only transubstantiates when it has already spread into the target’s system. Very few Tremere neonates did not undergo this ritual upon being officially welcomed into the clan and made to recite the Code of Tremere as it is that ceremony which makes them a true member of the clan. Regents found neglecting their duty to perform this ritual and sires who fail to bring their offspring before the clan for the ceremony are usually “called to Vienna” and never seen before. Troublemakers are also often forced to undergo this ritual multiple times, turning them into puppets worshipping the Council of Seven and the Code of Tremere.</p>\n<p>The ritual is also quite an enigma as almost noone knows how it truly works. The blood transubstantiated cannot vanish into thin air according to the principles of Thaumaturgy. Conspiracy theories suggest that the Council simply replaces the blood in the chalice with their own while they keep the neonate’s blood and store it in a vault, giving them the ability to punish treason from afar. But where does the blood of the Council come from? Some rumours indicate massive pools of blood and entire storehouses of vials of blood under Vienna, but rumourmongers are usually quickly brushed off.</p>\n<p>This ritual does nothing after the Fall of Vienna and most of what is described above is of no consequence tonight, but it is a footnote of great importance within Cainite history.</p>\n<p>Rarity The knowledge of this ritual is known by most Tremere of the Pyramid above the rank of an apprentice, but the Fall of Vienna in 2008 makes (or made, in your game) this ritual more of a trivia knowledge.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Blood of the one drinking and a chalice</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The thaumaturge pours the blood of the drinker into a chalice and recites the syllables of the ritual. After that, the person undergoing the ritual drinks from the chalice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Do not make the ritual roll until the blood has been drank. On a win, it transforms into the blood of the Council of Seven within the drinker who counts as if they took a sip directly from the Council’s vein. This type of a Blood Bond never goes below the first drink (two steps for the Tremere) unless the entire bond is broken through certain rites.</p>\n<p>Note that the Council possesses the Domitor’s Favour power (Players Guide, p. 74): Blood Bond resistance rolls by thralls under the effects of Domitor’s Favour are made at a three-dice penalty and thralls may not spend Willpower on them. Additionally, total failure on a defiance roll means that the Blood Bond does not weaken that month. The transubstantiated blood is sent to Ceoris where it is stored and categorised… When roleplaying a blood bond, always have in mind “does the Council/code of Tremere want this?”. If your game is happening after the Fall of Vienna, this ritual probably does not work.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the concoction makes the imbiber puke without any benefits, forcing a Rouse Check for every 1 rolled.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"327"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.UxCW5WkLU4FEu92w"},"V4XzxG0K76MO2gUr":{"name":"Degulo","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>The caster uses this aspect in place of Scientia Surrepta, Facultas Emortua or Vis Memoriam and upon doing so whatever skill has been used doesn't need to be kept on the caster's person to obtain the benefits of the spell. If the skull belonged to a vampire then, on top of the other aspects' effect, the caster may sacrifice dots in an in clan discipline to obtain the equivalent dots from the victim's disciplines, obtaining powers that the victim knew (for example Lucius uses Degulo on Maria, a toreador with auspex 3 and presence 2. Lucius opts to sacrifice the 4th dot of fortitude and obtained 4 dots to distribute along Maria's in clans. He decides to put 3 dots in auspex to buy the level 1 and 2 powers, then places the remaining dot in presence).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.V4XzxG0K76MO2gUr"},"V9rn9BCTq8ll7bD3":{"name":"School: Abyss Mysticism","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p>As pragmatic as they are ruthless, most Lasombra regard Obtenebration as a tool. It is an important tool and a gift from their founder, but ultimately just one power among many. Only the most eccentric and arcane savants among the Clan understand the true significance of their dark art. Obtenebration is more than mere shadow-play. It is a window to the Abyss itself, that great and terrible unknown that lies at the center of all things. The Abyss gnaws at the heart of the underworld and the doubt in every question never answered. It is present in the absence of light and lurks in every shadow. Ghosts rightly fear its hunger, but they do not understand it. Demons call it Hell, and they only begin to comprehend. No Lasombra knows where it comes from or its purpose or even the purpose of its strange denizens, but the clan’s mystics know the Abyss is the ultimate source of their power. So the mystics vainly pry into the dark places in search of secrets and answers and perhaps more questions. For these driven souls, conventional Obtenebration is only the beginning. With rituals they unlock the hidden powers of shadow and summon the unnamed primordial horrors that lie beyond.</p>\n<p>Either from fear or distaste, the Amici Noctis follow a policy of ignoring mystics until times of great crisis. Even the most fervently Christian Lasombra do not hunt or discourage Abyss mystics, for it is whispered that the Eldest watches those who watch the void. And so the mystics research quietly in the hidden places, for the Abyss is their calling, their quest, their god.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Resonance:</strong> N/A, Obtenebration is the “School”</p>\n<h3>Rituals of the Darkness</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Test:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Oubliette</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Magic of the Abyss: Abyss Mysticism is not simply a school of Blood Sorcery, but rather something more alien, and an extension of the Discipline of Obtenebration. Characters may not learn Abyss Mysticism rituals rated higher than their Obtenebration rating. As such, Abyss Mysticism as a school always costs 5 × new rating in experience and does not require Resonance due to its nature as sorcerous Paths of the Discipline of Obtenebration. Practitioners of Blood or Hedge Sorcery cannot reappropriate Paths of Abyss Mysticism, but the reverse is true as well.</p>\n<p>• In the Dark: Abyss Mysticism is connected to the darkness. Not the absence of light, scientifically speaking, but the very stuff that composes shadows. If a ritual of Abyss Mysticism is not performed in an area of at least dimmed light, the mystic negates a single 1 when risking Botch, but Path rating is counted as two lower.</p>\n<p>• Staring into the Void: While not necessarily evil, those who frequently dabble in Abyss Mysticism tend to share one trait: callousness. Not in a sense of cruelty, although it may appear to be or truly is in special cases, but in the sense of emptiness. Abyss mystics increasingly grow detached from the world the longer they stare into the Abyss. A character who performs a ritual of Abyss Mysticism suffers a dice penalty to Degeneration Tests equal to half the level of the ritual, rounded up, for the next 24 hours. If multiple rituals were performed, count the highest penalty. This cannot bring a Degeneration Test dice pool to lower than the minimum (usually 1). Adherents of Road of the Abyss are excluded from this due to the already callous nature of their philosophy and morality.</p>\n<p>• Reclusive Mysticism Owing to the scarcity of Abyss Mysticism and the secretive nature of its practitioners, fewer Paths exist than with most other Blood Magics. While it is possible to make new Paths for Abyss Mysticism, the philosophy and the time-consuming efforts to evoke abyssal magic mean that quick spells are impossible. All manifestations of Abyss Mysticism come in the form of rituals or elaborate, long spells.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Abyss Mysticism</h3>\n<p>Typically, the Path of Abyssal Infusion is the first to be learned by new Abyss Mystics due to its relatively lower risks, not to mention that some of its effects are of such utility to the mystics that they are considered a universal necessity. Path of Shadowcalling is an extreme application, concerned with summoning and controlling entities from the Abyss. Relatively few practice Abyss Communion, but is said that the Lasombra Antediluvian has been spending millenias in torpor using it while searching for an answer on how to destroy reality.</p>\n<p>• Abyss Communion</p>\n<p>• Abyssal Infusion</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning and Warding (Shadowbinding)</p>\n<p>The Metaphysical Weight (Abyssal) is by far the most common, but mystics can utilise their knowledge on other entities. Damage from Shadowbinding to non-Abyssal entities is dealt in Willpower damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"227"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.V9rn9BCTq8ll7bD3"},"VCN90ybDy4Q7t7p9":{"name":"Ashen Relic","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This Ritual allows for the preservation of pieces of a vampire’s body even after they’ve been destroyed. The Ritual has become more dangerous in the era of the Second Inquisition, as tangible proof of the Kindred’s existence poses more danger than ever before. In the modern era, many of the Hecata and Ministry hunt down such relics, lest the secret of this ancient Ritual draw unwanted attention from Kindred of the Camarilla or the Second Inquisition. Still, some morbid Kindred find relief in surrounding themselves with the remains of their ancestors (or descendants) and practitioners of necromancy occasionally find other uses for such relics. To the mercurians of the thinbloods, such relics are a source of speculation and an ingredient of unknown power for their alchemy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The decaying body of a vampire, plus salt and herbs associated with embalming.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The concoction is applied to the body or body part of the destroyed vampire. Since the decay of the remains — especially supernatural features such as fangs — only takes a few minutes at most, a swift performance is essential.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful Ritual Test (Difficulty 3), the necromancer preserves one small body part, such as a finger bone or a fang, while three successes in the margin can preserve a femur or skull. Once created, the relic (usually in the form of a mummified hand, skull, femur, or desiccated eye) persists until destroyed or exposed to sunlight. A a particularly high roll (3+ margins) or a critical success may grant the relic the power to persist despite sunlight.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"344"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.VCN90ybDy4Q7t7p9"},"Vl49kzfwBxVORqhX":{"name":"Death’s Communion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The Giovanni are known for their own versions of the Christian mass, often twisting it in various directions that sometimes include mockery. Death’s Communion is one such twist, albeit a rare one as it transfers a bit of power from the caster into the target. The main problem when it comes to performing this “mass” is what type of bread to use as the eastern branches of the family often bicker with the western branches on that theologically important detail, reflecting the Great Schism.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Only the Giovanni following the principle of taboo practice and teach this Ritual with some frequency, but some Cappadocians who can get over the blasphemy (or reinterpret it through the Principle of Authority) have been known to perform it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A cup or a chalice, caster’s vitae and a small piece of bread</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer chants a mass (often in Latin, Greek or Church Slavonic before the 2nd Vatican Council), often replacing some crucial words in the chant with their own. After that, the supplicant necromancer approaches and consumes the “body of Christ” represented by the bread and takes a sip of a chalice representing the “blood of Christ”.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For this Ritual to work, the caster and the target must both possess at least one dot in Necromancy. The necromancer offering the blood and the body of Christ makes a Ritual roll and on a win they count their Necromancy rating as one lower while the target counts theirs as two higher. A same target may not benefit from multiple instances of this Ritual or other similar ritual effects, but the same caster may perform the Ritual until the rating of their Necromancy reaches 0 for the purposes of rolling dice.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botchfailure, both necromancers lower their Necromancy rating by 1 with no benefit until Duration expires (or the ritual is dispelled as normal).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Vl49kzfwBxVORqhX"},"VttPtvG6hbV33tla":{"name":"Imbue with the Grace of Anubis","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>An unthinking guardian is a potent defender for havens and temples, but sometimes a sorcerer requires Anubis to return more than a flicker of life. By the jackal god’s grace, a sorcerer taps into the good deed or sin identified through Weigh the Virtues, and imbues the corpse animated through this Path (Aggressive or Mindless Corpse usually) with a compulsion based on that deed. The animated corpse is still victim to the necromancer’s commands, but now harbors emotions and inclinations based on the harvested spirit’s former deeds and will act on them when not specifically directed to do otherwise. These inclinations always manifest themselves violently, but with intelligence and cunning other forms of reanimation cannot match.</p>\n<p>The utility of this power comes through the chaos it causes. A sorcerer who sends a horde of corpses possessed by violent emotion into a town, knows the domain will come apart at the seams. Guardians set to guard tomb will patrol and lay ambushes to destroy others rather than simply stand there and wait for others to stumble.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Akhu</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A remembered deed harvested by Weigh the Virtues (Necromancy ••) and a reanimated cadaver.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer ritually opens the mouth of a reanimated cadaver and “breathes out” memories into the cadaver.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The sorcerer touches a reanimated cadaver and makes their Ritual Test (Difficulty 5). On a win, they transfer a deed of a ghost they’ve perceived with Weight the Virtues, consuming the deed. The statblock of the reanimated cadaver may swap its Skills for that of the spirit’s at the moment of their death.</p>\n<p>A sentry with basic memory of its defining deeds will act impulsively in ways similar to its former life; i.e. a former murderer who cut the throats of merchants will always slay any traders it meets with attacks to the neck, while a priest who assisted the poor will target the homes of the wealthy, burning them down and massacring the inhabitants. A win on the Ritual Test plants the memory of one deed in the mind of the sentry, with each margin giving the sorcerer the option to place another. The spirit from which the deed is drawn for the creation of this powered sentry is aware of its virtues or sins being drained, but without the sorcerer’s aid cannot recover the piece it loses to this power and the ghost may suffer Passion or Pathos loss over the long run. The sorcerer must make a Rouse Check and touch the cadaver again to return the deed to the spirit from which it originated, at which point the cadaver becomes an unthinking sentry it once was, but none of the freedom of thought or newly acquired Skills.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"346"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.VttPtvG6hbV33tla"},"VyFweh4h7AQE4i1j":{"name":"Healing Slumber","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Healing Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer puts their subject to sleep, calming their mind and allowing the healing process to accelerate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Whatever the sorcerer uses to mend wounds and ease spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> By potion, spell, sound or some other instrument of healing sorcerer uses, the subject is lulled deep into sleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 2), the subject falls asleep for 9 hours; when they awake, Superficial Health damage equal to the Healing Aspect or margins if the target resists (Stamina + Resolve), whichever is lower. For each margin, subtract one hour from the required sleep time. This healing cannot heal Aggravated wounds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Same as the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"315"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.VyFweh4h7AQE4i1j"},"W3qHOR1lDcWXFzri":{"name":"Vas Malum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Using this ritual on a skull gives the caster the complete skillset of the individual it belonged to, including exceptional skills, specialties and attributes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Paths:</strong> Reanimation 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A skull and a Rouse Check’s worth of vampire blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer pours some vampire blood into the skull or a severed head</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful ritual test (Difficulty 5) the caster is able to call upon all the malice of the victim's life, encasing it within the cranium and making the skull unstable and volatile. The skull functions as a thrown weapon that explodes on contact with a surface and deals the caster's Path rating to all targets within 3 meters, causing their flesh to decay while leaving inorganic items intact. It deals unhalved superficial to supernatural creatures and aggravated damage to mortals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"377"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.W3qHOR1lDcWXFzri"},"WNKj6FnMu8H0abWU":{"name":"Hard-Light Constructs","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Illusion Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>A ritual that will be popularised technosorcerers in the 21st century, Hard-Light Constructs are illusions everyone involved knows are fake one person points it out. This creates scenery, furniture, etc., that looks and feels real, though putting your weight onto it wouldn’t be the best idea. It’s often used to simulate visuals where every single detail isn’t needed, but which have moving parts. It creates the illusion for all onlookers, rather than a fixed set of targets. In the modern period, at least one Tremere neonate has been reprimanded for using Hard-Light Constructs to run their World of Shadows game.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (as a radius of the effect), Senses. Realism</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Depends on the caster</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, an illusion is created that may be perceived by everybody. If a single person manages to win on their Wits + Awareness roll or disproves the illusion in some manner and notifies others, they automatically succeed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As Path Spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"318"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.WNKj6FnMu8H0abWU"},"WYADfbjdkBwP1ZCx":{"name":"Into the Chasm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>This ritual opens a portal into the Abyss itself. The mystic spends an hour painting a circle of glyphs using two Rouse Check’s worth of the blood in addition to the activation Rouse Check. The circle must be as wide as their outstretched arms and may adorn wall or floor, as long as the surface is smooth and unbroken, like polished stone or glass. Win on the Ritual Test opens the portal, causing the oily black of Obtenebration to spiral into a gaping maw of liquid darkness. The portal lasts a number of turns equal to the number of total successes rolled. On a failure, the portal is still opened, but only for a turn and disgorges a number of hostile Average Shades equal to the number of 1s rolled +1.</p>\n<p>Anything may pass through a successful portal while it remains open, willingly or otherwise. Passing through the portal requires a point of Willpower point. If a character is out of Willpower at the moment of entering the Abyss or while in it, they’re consumed by the vast nothingness. If multiple characters wish to travel together, they must maintain unbroken contact as they enter and throughout their journey. Otherwise, some may become lost in the void or emerge at very different destinations than their companions.</p>\n<p>The Abyss is infinite in its darkness. Those with the Protean power Eyes of the Beast or Darksight of Abyss Mysticism may perceive the shapeless murk in all its dizzying, impossible geometry and thus see the approach of Abyss spirits, vortices or other travelers. Those without such powers are truly blind and must rely on their other muted senses to perceive anything. The only clear sensation is one of soul-numbing cold that inflicts one die of Aggravated Health damage to living beings per minute of exposure.</p>\n<p>In addition to serving as a profound spiritual experience for Abyss mystics, travel in the void serves a more utilitarian purpose. The shadow realm intersects all shadows and all darkness, allowing a traveler to emerge from any patch of darkness large enough to contain them. They need only break the enfolding silence to speak the name of their destination. The character makes a Wits + Occult Test (Difficulty 4). On a win, the shadow-space stretches and tears around the character, disgorging them at their destination with a feeling of falling from a great height. A character must have seen or visited a destination to name it. Failure increases the difficulty of the next navigation attempt by one, to a maximum of Difficulty 7. A new attempt can be made every minute until the character succeeds. A Bestial Failure means the character’s navigation Difficulty increases by one and they attracts hostile Shades as a failure in casting the initial ritual. One character may lead others provided all maintain physical contact. Once a passenger lets go of the group, they must find their own way out, making their own Obtenebration roll if they possess it. If not, too bad, they’re stuck until somebody comes and rescues them. It is worth noting that no denizens of the Abyss bear blood, so any vampire who remains trapped within will eventually starve into torpor and an eternity of forgotten nonbeing.</p>\n<p>Due to unusual geography or rather the lack of any special sense, the vast infinite is strangly quick to travers. One who performed this ritual from East Asia would not take longer than a few hours to walk to the place where mystic from Iceland performed the same ritual. This is taken advantage of by the Amici Noctis who call their most secretive meetings within the Abyss, assembling the Amici from all over the world with this ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> Vampires who walk in the Abyss risk bringing a fragment of its alien malevolence back with them to the material world. After every sojourn, make a Willpower Test against a difficulty of trip’s duration in minutes (maximum Difficulty 6). A failure means the vampire gains the effects of the Ingrained Flaw of Obtenebration Discipline whether or not they do or are able to possess it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"255"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.WYADfbjdkBwP1ZCx"},"Wr9QE1QLtk0VeVWY":{"name":"At Our Command It Breathes","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual permits the creation of a Gargoyle from the dismembered parts of slain Cainites and is the process through which the first of that line came into being.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Hermetic Thaumaturgy</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> At least two vampires to be used in the creation and a number of animal wombs (or a specially created large Gargoyle for bearing new Gargoyles) as well as the caster’s blood. Tremere of the 12th Generation or higher cannot successfully finish the ritual and the Gargoyle will be a stillborn.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The vampire (with assistants, if desired) takes fresh-cut portions from the bodies of various Cainite victims and sews them together into a single humanoid form. They then sew a sac made from an animal’s womb (usually from a cow but sometimes sheep or deer) around their creation. The sac is then suspended in a cask containing an alchemical equivalent of the womb’s waters or placed inside an oversized, specially prepared female Gargoyle for gestation. During a period ranging from one to three months, the thaumaturge must periodically visit the gestating Gargoyle and perform various incantations and blessings. If they away from the gestation chamber for more than a week at a time, the fetal Gargoyle dies and cannot be revived. Finally the Gargoyle either forces its way down the living mother’s birth canal or bursts forth from its cask. The ritualist must be present to waft purifying smoke onto the newborn Gargoyle. Any other Gargoyles within half a mile instinctively sense the impending birth of a new brother. They feel a powerful urge to flock to its side when it is born and lick the bodily fluids off its skin. Although this custom helps bond the Gargoyle to its new community, it is not a requisite of the ritual. A Gargoyle can be born in isolation.</p>\n<p>When the thaumaturge (or their assistants) are sewing together pieces of Cainites in order to form a full humanoid shape and to grant various capabilities to the new Gargoyle. The correspondence of parts to capabilities is laid out in a variety of ancient and arcane medical texts (for example, the heart provides courage and resolve, the eyes perception and wisdom) but also depends on specific alchemical preparations. Physical beauty is beyond the ritual’s capabilities, though. All Gargoyles appear stony and twisted.</p>\n<p>During the process even the ashen remains of ancient vampires are usable to determine strength and Generation of the Gargoyle, but “fresher” vampires are always necessary in order to form the humanoid shape out of something.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> In addition to the activation Rouse Check for the ritual, there is a greater price in Blood that cannot be rerolled. The basic additional cost of the ritual is 5 Rouse Checks for each Cainite used in the Gargoyle’s manufacture. Add an extra 5 Rouse Checks if the caster wants the Gargoyle to be fertile and able to embrace new Gargoyles. This cost must be paid as the humanoid shape is being formed which affords the vampire some time to slake their Hunger or prepare a large quantity of vitae. The Gargoyle will possess the Generation of the lowest- Generation vampire and highest Attributes and Skills from each of the donors.</p>\n<p>Make the Ritual Test once the fetal sac is cut open at a Difficulty of 6. Subtract one die on the Ritual Test for every night the thaumaturge did not perform their incantations.</p>\n<p>The thaumaturge makes the Ritual Test as the fetal sac is put into gestation at the Difficulty of (9 + one for each donor after the first). An unliving womb (such as that of Alvusia, see p. 67 of House of Tremere) reduces the difficulty by 2. A win allows the creation of a Gargoyle character built according to the standard character creation rules (see our Gargoyle homebrew for build-a-Gargoyle system).</p>\n<p>By devoting further years of research to the Gargoyle arts, the thaumaturge may devise and construct variant types like the hexaped, iecur or womb-bearers like Alvusia (see pages 124, 125 and 67 for descriptions).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the Gargoyle fetus dies.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Historical Note:</strong> The Tremere cease making Gargoyles after the Slaves revolt, and, as part of the Camarilla negotiations, promise never to revive the technique. The instructions for the ritual have been hidden, but not destroyed. Some suspect that the Gargoyle Fidus occasionally makes use of them. Before then, Gargoyle creation was perceived as a noble study popular among established magisters to advance their station in the Pyramid and anybody who was eager could learn it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"298"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Wr9QE1QLtk0VeVWY"},"WzozhRCpphMVRvRv":{"name":"Bottle It","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fortune Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>While not as potent as potions of Fortune sorcerers dabbling in Alchemy Path, practitioners of Fortune can still bottle up their magic to be sold as luck potions or weird fortune poisons.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and either Blessing or Curse Special Effects.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Herbs associated with good or bad luck, depending on the desired effects</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The herbs or other ingredients with symoblic value are grounded into a powder and poured into a liquid or distilled in some other fashion.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful casting, the imbiber of the potion gains the effects as used. Curses may be resisted with Wits + Occult as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"313"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.WzozhRCpphMVRvRv"},"X36OC5MvASRns4y5":{"name":"Smoke","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>A poisonous dark cloud, using Duration instead of Damage and counting Area as higher by 3. Anyone caught in the gas receives a three dice penalty to all non-tracker rolls and suffers a point of Aggravated Health damage, unless they possess some form of adequate protection (A normal gas mask provides two turns of protection before becoming useless.).</p>\n<p>They also suffer one level of Superficial Health damage until they escape using Wits + Awareness or Survival (Difficulty equal to half the Lure of Flames rating). Beings that do not need to breathe are immune to the damage and only suffer the three-dice penalty to their Awareness and ranged combat-related rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"285"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.X36OC5MvASRns4y5"},"XI0Fa2hIabE8xHEJ":{"name":"Enhanced Painkillers","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>A drug that lets the user ignore Physical Impairment until they suffer damage again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.XI0Fa2hIabE8xHEJ"},"XMMTFkPru9e253R6":{"name":"Amulet against Magic","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An amulet warding against magic (mortal or otherwise). Three times a day it subtracts two successes from spells and rituals targeting the wearer. It must be kept in a special jewelry box carved from a single piece of wood when not worn, or it loses one success permanently per night until it stops working. This does not stack with the countermagic or unweaving as it is counted as separate countermagic with the higher one taking effect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.XMMTFkPru9e253R6"},"XUHnsWiHiJF2z7Zn":{"name":"Lightning Storm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Same as storm. Each players makes an Intelligence + Science or Academics roll (difficulty 3) at the beginning of the night. Those who fail get hit by a lightning and suffer three points of Aggravated Health damage, increased to 4 on a Total or a Bestial Failure. A character cannot die as a result of this, but it is nonetheless funny. Those with metal gear on their person such as armour or in contact with metal such as floors receive a two-dice penalty roll on this.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"359"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.XUHnsWiHiJF2z7Zn"},"XfD4G6qQFV5yzjGa":{"name":"Blood Dance","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Blood Dance allows a ghost to communicate with the living. Many a necromancer has made a fortune by performing this Ritual to allow relatives of a ghost to communicate with it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Sand and sea or ocean salt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The vampire must dance and chant for 10 minutes, calling forth the right spirit and entreating all other ghosts to leave the area. While dancing, the vampire pours colored sands and the salt on the ground in a precise pattern, making a link between the living person and the deceased. If successful, the ghost “appears” within the necromancer’s sand-sigil and the living person can communicate with them for up to one hour.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the ghost may speak and hear through the sand sigil as if they were there. Physically, the ghost remains in their current location.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"335"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.XfD4G6qQFV5yzjGa"},"YlGaJg3uhYyRIZcV":{"name":"Prepare the Vessel","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Gifting ghosts another chance to roam this world in a physical body is a great bargaining chip or a weird torture tactic to be employed by the necromancer.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets, Possesion Weakness (see below)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A hood (such as the one used for hanged men)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The hood is placed upon the victim(s) for the duration of the Ritual. After that, the necromancer simply waits for a ghost to possess the person.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the Ritual roll, every form of possession (ghost Puppetry, Auspex 5 Possesion, demon possession...) gains automatic successes equal to Possession Weakness on the attempt.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"345"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.YlGaJg3uhYyRIZcV"},"YtoWQvz66Lr906Rm":{"name":"Depression","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>Target does not restore Willpower when completing Desire or Ambition. They also heal Superficial Willpower equal to the lower of their Resolve and Composure at the beginning of a session.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.YtoWQvz66Lr906Rm"},"YuBSQAb1iKYjQLex":{"name":"Condense","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula condenses blood or liquids of lower volume than the amount used with the liquid typically then placed inside small vials. The amount of blood condensed can sate one Hunger for every two Rouse Checks made when condensing the Blood, in addition to the standard activation of a Rouse Check. This formula is always created as a single dose. Drinking this liquid is a Minor Action dice of -2.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.YuBSQAb1iKYjQLex"},"Yxh57kIJo3JUAIpR":{"name":"School: Sielanic Thaumaturgy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-thaumaturgy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Distanic themselves from the Tremere pyramid and its oppressive ways, the Telyavelic Tremere learned to utilise the power of siela, the animistic spirit latent in the earth, stone, plants, and animals of their adopted homeland. Based on shamanic practices, these Paths combine aspects of both nature and spirit-based magic. Some Tremere outside the bloodline believe that Sielanic Thaumaturgy is an offshoot of Koldunic Sorcery and look upon those who practice it with suspicion and distrust.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquearde Threat:</strong> Without</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Sielanic Thaumaturgy was invented by the Telyavelic Tremere and so far there has never been an adherent of this School that has not been a Telyav. Rumours say that the Sielanic witches have taught this school to the Sabbat, but so far these claims have been unsubstantiated, or so the justicars of the Camarilla report.</p>\n<h3>Practices of the Teylavs</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Glyphs of Power, Natural Sanctum, Shrine, Spirit Presence</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• God’s Gift: Sielanic Thaumaturgy was discovered by Tremere witch-priests who were sent to Lithuania to research spirit magics such as that of the Tzimisce. It is said that the Lithuanian god of forge and death, Telyavel, taught the Tremere how to harness the spirits. When bene fitting from Sanctum Backgrounds, negate a single 1 for a Botch.</p>\n<p>• Natural Magic: Sielanic magic has troubles being practiced in places deserted by spirits of nature. If a spell or a ritual is performed near nature, the difficulty to cast a spell or a ritual is lowered be one, to a minimum of one. If the magic is not practiced near nature, difficulty increase by 1.</p>\n<p>• Alchemy</p>\n<p>• Battle</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Biothaumaturgy</p>\n<p>• Conjuration</p>\n<p>• Conveyance</p>\n<p>• Divination</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces</p>\n<p>• Enchantment</p>\n<p>• Fear</p>\n<p>• Focus</p>\n<p>• Fortune</p>\n<p>• Healing</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood</p>\n<p>• Necromancy (Path of the Shadow World)</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting The Telyavs rarely take forms other than animals within their domain, considering it blasphemy.</p>\n<p>• The Green Path (Path of World’s Blood)</p>\n<p>• Weather Control Additionally, the sorcerer can take Kraine.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"247"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Yxh57kIJo3JUAIpR"},"Z2wKiNwzsH8qjOGS":{"name":"Vis Memoriae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>on a successful skill test the caster chooses an attribute of the victim. For the duration of the spell and as long as the skull is on their person they may swap their own attribute with the victim's.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"376"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Z2wKiNwzsH8qjOGS"},"Z38Y7LW0ClFypCDM":{"name":"Lie Detecting Serum","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>A powder or a liquid that, when inhaled by mouth, reduces the Target’s Manipulation and Subterfuge by 1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Z38Y7LW0ClFypCDM"},"ZEsjXaSYy89TA3lW":{"name":"Instant Feast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Illusion Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Illusory food is deceptively hard to create. Yes, a simple spell can create visuals of food as well as smell and touch and texture, but after devouring an entire feast by themselves, a guest will still feel hungry. Taste, smell, image, and texture all need to be just right to fool someone into thinking they’re eating a real meal before one can cheat the sense of hunger itself.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (radius of the effect per casting: a dinner table would require Range 2), Number of Targets, Senses (taste, smell, visual image and texture; 4)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Depends on the caster</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, hunger cues are suppressed for a number of hours equal to Stamina of the one consuming the food, before they feel hungry again. This can’t alleviate the physical effects of starvation, but can alleviate the mental ones. Blood consumed suppresses Hunger in vampires for the purposes of the Hunger dice, but it returns to normal after hours equal to Stamina.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"318"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ZEsjXaSYy89TA3lW"},"ZKi4sA0rMSvTlK6s":{"name":"Hellblade","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The image of a flaming blade is terrifying and borderline demonic. With this, the sorcerer can cow mortals and annihilate even the most durable of vampires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Lure of Flames or Balefire</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Damage, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The ritualist’s own blood or a flammable substance</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer smears either a flammable substance or their own blood onto a weapon.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the ritual sets a melee weapon or a projectile aflame according to the magic used (Lure of Flames or Balefire, each with their own colour).</p>\n<p>When an enemy is struck by the weapon it will deal additional fire damage equal to half the Damage Aspect (rounded up). Half (rounded up) of that additional fire damage is dealt again as Aggravated Willpower damage if the sorcerer is using balefire.</p>\n<p>A strike with a critical win sets the target on fire who continously takes the damage described above until they douse the flames with Composure + Survival/Science (Difficulty 3). The weapon remains burning according to the Duration Aspect or the sorcerer wills the blood to cease.</p>\n<p>Enemies with the Beast must make a fear frenzy roll at a Difficulty of 2 at the very sight of the weapon.</p>\n<p>Note that the effect of continous fire damage does not stack with other sources of continous fire damage. A person may be set on fire only so much.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> A Botch can be quite devastating. The ritual for all intents and purposes succeeds. Once the fire is activated, the sorcerer must roll Dexterity + Athletics (difficulty 3) to avoid getting set on fire. Upon a failure, they suffer the damage and catch on fire, taking damage until the flames are doused.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"286"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ZKi4sA0rMSvTlK6s"},"ZbWu8gecQ3hjSoIV":{"name":"Fire Projectile","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Coats one weapon or one attack’s worth of bullets per dose with a substance that bursts into flames on impact, dealing two additional points of Aggravated Health damage from fire.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ZbWu8gecQ3hjSoIV"},"ZfL7MpcMEgWGk37M":{"name":"Enhance Craftmanship","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Sometimes, instead of a magic item, what a magician needs is an otherwise normal but perfectly crafted object. This ritual creates unbreakable blades, sweaters that don’t unravel, and similar objects whose only enhancement is in the extreme quality of their crafting. These objects cannot be enchanted further, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Material to create an item.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritual has the duration of the standard time to create the item + 5 minutes of chanting.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, an object of great quality and durability is made. Blades and armour never break and clothes will eternally be “as new”.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"294"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ZfL7MpcMEgWGk37M"},"Zkb0cFf5IyXaKZpP":{"name":"Domain of the Genius","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Genius Loci Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The koldun extends his awareness through the land upon which they thread, becoming the Genius Loci of legend.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance (counted as higher by one), Duration, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but standard blood and will</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer pours the blood onto the ground, feeding the land and ending the ritual by pouring the activation Rouse Check’s blood onto the ground to seal their mastery over the land</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the sorcerer’s perceptions extend all throughout the land except areas warded against spirits in a radius pulsing with the koldun’s will (the koldun may roll against these wards to pierce them). While in effect, the koldun is aware of everything that transpires around them, out to a distance determined by Distance Aspect. Duration Aspect governs how long the rite may be in effect, but the Rite’s effect ends upon leaving the location.</p>\n<p>This Rite is not subtle whatsoever. Every spirit in the radius knows of the koldun’s rule over the land and those using supernatural perceptions (such as Sense the Unseen) may roll against the Rite successes to perceive that there is a supernatural influence and will across the land, sensing the general direction by rolling Wits + Survival (Difficulty 3) if they won the first contest.</p>\n<p>Even Obfuscate cannot deceive the koldun who has established themselves as a Genius Loci. The koldun who rolled more successes sees through the ruse of users of illusions, Obfuscate and other supernatural means of hiding things like wards. If no test was made by those hiding, it is made then.</p>\n<p>The koldun may share these supernatural perceptions by mixing blood of others (up to the Passengers Aspect), allowing them to see through their senses.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, the koldun may invoke any koldunic power they possess anywhere they can perceive, so long as the level of that power is equal to or less than dots in their Genius Loci rating.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"360"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Zkb0cFf5IyXaKZpP"},"ZlRibBXhbNqVk50M":{"name":"Beast Repellant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>A pungent chemical that drives back werewolves (and most humans, simply due to the smell). When worn, any sort of shapeshifter and animal cannot approach within ten feet of the user unless they succeed on a Composure + Resolve test vs. the successes on the Ritual Test. Of course, this only protects against melee combat, they can still throw things, as some alchemists learn when they start taunting. One dose is enough to douse one person with it or to cover about a meter across and lasts a night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ZlRibBXhbNqVk50M"},"Zp0KePEEi0aoFFh8":{"name":"Monster Energy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>An energy drink that lets the user go an entire week sleeping one hour per night. For vampires, this adds a die to their awakening rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"263"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Zp0KePEEi0aoFFh8"},"Zur2agml7VrlB26p":{"name":"Vessel of the Koldun","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Genius Loci Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>The kolduny are masters of spirits, and a Genius Loci is the lord of the world. With this ritual, the koldun may enter the spirit world known as the Astral Umbra and middle worlds between it and the material world. Several mystics of the Tzimisce have performed this rite and never got out, choosing to lord over the spirits (how they get sustinence is usually not told in the tile).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance (counted as higher by one; how far the vessel may move before the Ritual ends), Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Materials from the Genius Loci’s domain to create a vessel</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The koldun first fashions a vessel, usually taller than two humans and wider than four, out of materials from their domain such as stone, moss, wood and so on. After that, the vessel is annointed by blood and spirits are petitioned to animate it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Rite (minimum Difficulty is 6 if not made higher through Aspects), the vessel is successfuly animated.</p>\n<p>The vessel’s Mental and Social Skills and Attributes and Physical Skills remain that of the koldun, but the vessel possesses 0 dots in each Physical Attribute. The koldun may place one dot into a single Attribute for each Rouse Check offered to the spirits, to a minimum of 12. Maximum Strength of the vessel is 8, Dexterity 5 and Stamina 10. The vessel takes Superficial damage from most sources except True Faith and certain magics. The Rite’s effects end after the Duration, once the caster slips out of conscioussness or the vessel is destroyed, whichever comes first.</p>\n<p>Drough powers of Celerity, Fortitude, Potence and Thanatosis may be activated and “fed” to the vessel during the Rite, giving it powers that the koldun may activate with a Willpower point for each Rouse Check required.</p>\n<p>The koldun senses everything through the vessel without needing to give it eyes or ears. They may speak through the vessel without needing to give it mouth as the koldun’s voice simply radiates from the vessel. Most traditional Disciplines cannot be used through the vessel.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"362"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Zur2agml7VrlB26p"},"Zw4cKaltinxnwJQW":{"name":"School: Hacktivist Thaumaturgy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-thaumaturgy.png","system":{"description":"<p>One of the four schools of Anarch Blood Magic, Hacktivist Thaumaturgy places technological advancements at the forefront of their study. It is a narrow practice, yet one of the more popular among the elite sorcerers within the Anarch Movement.</p>\n<p>While the paths available to the Hacktivists represent a decent cross section of the Tremere library, not all Paths are readily available. In fact, selections are largely limited to those which had been mastered by the various members of the Digital Draculas at the time of their defection from the Pyramid and creation of the Hacktivist school of Thaumaturgy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquerade Threat:</strong> Low. Hacktivists keep to their technolaboratories and their gadgets which may pass under the radar of mortals for things that are “supernatural”</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Hacktivist Thaumaturgy is primarily practiced by the Anarchs who call themselves the Red Draculas. It is a recent school invented by Tremere defectors to the Anarch Movement.</p>\n<h3>Practices of Digital Draculas</h3>\n<p>Tremere magic is a subset of hermetic magic as practiced by the diverse and fractious Order of the Hermes. The order exists because it allows its members to (imperfectly) share their understanding and Casting Roll: Intelligence + Technology</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Natural Sanctum (a place where instruments are supercharged with energy), Laboratory (Give Sanctum benefits to Laboratory Domain Background) with Technology Specialty</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Scientific Approach: The House of Tremere's approach to magic that created Thaumaturgy was meticulous and scientific. While moving away from the more mystic elements of the medieval magi, the so-called Digital Draculas maintain the scientific methods and expanded on them further. If you have already cast a Spell or a Ritual within the same story and cast it again with the exact same aspects, negate a single 1 for a Botch. This does not stack cumulatively and the benefit is lost if the sorcerer casts a new spell or the same spell with different Aspect ranks.</p>\n<p>• Instruments: Hacktivist Thaumaturgy does not work without technology. If the sorcerer is not using an instrument of “high” technology such as a magically attuned mobile phone or a specially enhanced TV remote, the Difficulty to cast spell or a ritual increases by two. If the character is using it, the difficulty decreases by one, to a minimum of 1. Attuning an instrument is usually done by inscribing technomantic sigils with the caster’s blood or some other non-mechanical ritual. This dependency is not without a benefit, however, as magic cast with an instrument is much more predictable and has the sorcerer always negates a single 1 for Botches.</p>\n<p>• Technomancy: Technology is at the heart of Hacktivist paradigm. A technomancer cannot spell Willpower on Spell, Ritual or Control Tests. They may, however, reroll a number of dice for free equal to their Sanctum/Laboratory rating.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Hacktivist Thaumaturgy</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy (Chemistry)</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Biothaumaturgy</p>\n<p>• Conjuration</p>\n<p>• Conveyance</p>\n<p>• Conjuration</p>\n<p>• Divination (Probability)</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces</p>\n<p>• Weather Control</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"235"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.Zw4cKaltinxnwJQW"},"a0oRZA9kd3TcVjKY":{"name":"Fae Repellant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Same as Beast Repellent, but against demons, changelings and bygones. Uses water blessed by a religious priest.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.a0oRZA9kd3TcVjKY"},"a9ffwWbsfC2Osrhu":{"name":"Draugh of Earth’s Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The Blood of Caine holds great mystical power, but it is not the only power. Raw magical energy flows through the universe like the blood of gods, pooling and welling at the convergence of ley lines and other places of arcane import. Such wells are known to magi as crays, and their energy as Quintessence or Vis among the Hermetics and the Tremere. Although the Tremere can no longer manipulate raw Quintessence by will alone, save in the congealed form of vitae, a few thaumaturges still know how to distill universal power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Thaumaturgy (Hacktivist, Hermetic, Sielanic) and Koldunism</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (Count ranks as higher by one).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Tass-rich material collected from a cray (read the system)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer boils down tass and mixes it with vitae in a bubbling pot or a cauldron before bottling it down. See the System</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Casting this ritual requires the vampire to harvest Quintessence-laden matter, called tass, from a cray. This matter can take nearly any form appropriate to the cray’s nature, from the mushrooms of a faerie ring to the soil of a spectre’s haunted grave. Each cray has a rating (1-5) that determines its overall power. A cray only stores a maximum number of points of tass equal to five time its level, replenishing them at the rate of one point per day. Once a sorcerer has gathered the tass, they must grind it down to paste or dust and boil it in an iron cauldron with two Rouse Checks for every point of tass. Unlike the activation Rouse Check, these Rouse Checks may not rerolled by Blood Potency. While the vitae and tass mix, the sorcerer rolls their Ritual Test. On a win, a number of blood-tass points coagulate in the cauldron equal to the margins +1, up to the total number of Tass used. Any unused tass not captured by the magic boils away and is lost. Blood-tass glows a faint ruddy hue and may be bottled or otherwise stored in smaller vessels for convenience. Sorcerers who employ the ritual Principal Focus of Vitae Infusion or Preserve Vitae or Calix Secretus may even store blood-tass in enchanted baubles. The sludgy mixture remains fresh according to the Duration rank, after which it loses all power unless it is placed in a magical container like the aformentioned Principle Focus of Vitae Infusion or enchanted by Preservation (p. 280). Vampires slake a point of Hunger for every point of blood-tass they consume. Only kolduny attuned to the local region through Genius Loci or thaumaturges derive the full benefit of such a meal, however.</p>\n<p>For the next hour, when the imbiber thaumaturge or koldun performs a Blood Surge on a roll to empower magic, they may add their Occult rating instead of their normal Blood Surge value unless it is higher.</p>\n<p>This blood tass has no blood bonding or embracing properties, but retains ghouling. It is destroyed upon contact with fire or sunlight like normal vitae and “goes bad” after Duration expires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the concoction makes the imbiber puke without any benefits, forcing a Rouse Check for every 1 rolled.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"326"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.a9ffwWbsfC2Osrhu"},"a9n1drjBRLoEGKNy":{"name":"Transylvanian Kraina (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Can you hear it? A pulse, an ancient heart beating below the Carpathian Mountains.</p>\n<p>— Yorak, Considered to be one of the oldest of the Kraine, the Transylvanian Kraina channels the spirits of the Carpathian Mountains. Kolduny of this Kraina are masters of elements of air and earth. They are able to summon storms, fly on the winds of the air spirits, and extended their senses through the air as well as channel other aspects of Transylvania such as its long history and memory.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"366"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.a9n1drjBRLoEGKNy"},"aNHbuHg8U94xHBIJ":{"name":"Waves (Major)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer may control waves moving people and ships around with their Spell Test (resisted by Strength or Stamina + Athletics of those trying to swim, Wits + Steer of those trying to steer ships, Strength + Athletics of those trying to row away)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.aNHbuHg8U94xHBIJ"},"aQgtdypv47CEBRNU":{"name":"Abyssal Blade","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The ritualist kills a creature with a bladed weapon and makes their Ritual Test. On a win, the blade becomes drenched in permanent mixture of blood and shadow, becoming black or at least a dark shade of grey. Those who do not possess at least two dots in Obtenebration cannot hold the blade as their arms simply pass through it (but it still interacts with most physical objects as normal). The blade ignores any armour.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> After a month and a night, the blade rapidly corrodes and becomes useless. The ritual may be repeated prior to the blade’s expiry, however.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"253"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.aQgtdypv47CEBRNU"},"ajfXSym6ZTytrdu5":{"name":"Tempering","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>If applied to a fully functioning weapon or object it grants extraordinary durability. For the potions duration (one week preserving, one hour repairing), both mundane and supernatural effects are unable to break this item or compromise its integrity unless they score a Critical Win.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ajfXSym6ZTytrdu5"},"awaoxnt08sWStxQB":{"name":"Carved Fortune","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fortune Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Common among Einherjar and Lhiannan witches, the sorcerer carves out a sigil or a rune with symbols of fortune.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special condition), Number of Targets, Range and either Blessing or Curse Special Effects. The Ritual also uses the Inscribed Duration Aspect, identical to Duration (counted as higher by one) representing how long the magic remains potent to impact others.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but a surface to carve or to write on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer carves out a sigil or writes it on a surface.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful casting, the onlooker upon the sigil gains the effects as used. Curses may be resisted with Wits + Occult as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"314"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.awaoxnt08sWStxQB"},"bCZYWjWDOxyXIeBH":{"name":"Revivify the Cold Vitae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Cappadocian scholars spend much of their time away from the living and among the dead. This practice can make hunting for living blood problematic, and the blood of corpses loses its potency so quickly as to be useless. With Revivify the Cold Vitae, the vampire may refresh the vitae locked in a fleshy corpse to allow her to feed without hunting for living prey. The cold blood created thus is considered reprehensible by some vampires and a delicacy by others.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A cadaver (mostly intact)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer annoits the corpse with a bit of their own blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful Ritual Test ((Difficulty 2, the blood inside the corpse is made fit for vampire consumpion even without the Iron Gullet Merit. This cold blood works just like normal mortal vitae, save that consuming it causes very little pleasure in the vampire. Cold blood remains fresh for only a scene. The rite transforms the putrid fluids of the corpse and thus makes that body useless for further necromantic arts (including the Reanimation Path). Therefore, many Cappadocian scholars keep larders of rotting lesser corpses for their nourishment and save prized bodies for experimentation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"343"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.bCZYWjWDOxyXIeBH"},"bMZ9laUaUav2my1I":{"name":"Better Weapons","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A weapon with a damage modifier modified by +1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.bMZ9laUaUav2my1I"},"bsrNT2XKfiL4gL05":{"name":"Greater Abyssal Possession","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>(prerequisite: Medium Abyssal Possession) With this ritual, the character is able to force a stronger abyssal spirit into living being or corpses. Unlike other Abyssal Possessions, this ritual requires one of the spirits summoned through the Binding, Summoning and Warding Path. The possessed entity has the same traits as the spirit except Physical Attributes which are swapped with that of the body. The creature takes damage like other possessed cadavers if a cadaver was used, but a living host possesses a heartbeat and is able to pass off as a normal person (more or less). Note that this creature cannot be controlled permanently and once the bindings from Binding, Summoning and Warding Path run out, the creature is free to do as it pleases.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> None.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"255"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.bsrNT2XKfiL4gL05"},"bwVnQmTcj21qzggl":{"name":"Body Failure","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Mastery of the Mortal Shell Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following.</em></p>\n<p>The victim makes a Crippling Injury roll (Corebook, p. 303) as one of their body parts is simply disabled. Alternatively to the roll, the caster can pick one of the mundane injuries like blindness or a broken limb. If the user causes organ like heart or brain to fail, the effect may have permanent consequences like death.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"329"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.bwVnQmTcj21qzggl"},"cAAyS7E7KG6CkmVK":{"name":"Glyph of Scrying","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>By preparing special sigils and focusing on them later, the sorcerer may extend their senses through their wards. Paranoid Tremere princes can monitor their entire cities through these glyphs. Portable objects can be imbued to function like remote senses.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The ritualist’s blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist draws the glyphs of sight with their blood in the shape of an eye.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the sigil is properly placed and visibly disappears as if “drunk” by the surface. The glyph lasts permanently if used within a Haven attuned to the sorcerer by Dedicate the Haven Ritual (•••• ritual). The vampire can then later establish a telepathic connection with any of their wards, but only one at a time that allows them to see and hear through it. While using these glyphs to perceive, they lose two dice on all rolls due to the concentration necessary, but they are otherwise able to move and act as normal. While focusing on a ward, the blood in the glyph gleams as if it were liquid again, making hiding these glyphs important. While placing the ward, the sorcerer may roll Intelligence + Stealth to hide them, contested by Awareness of those who would notice the glyph.</p>\n<p>Those possessing powers such as Sense the Unseen may roll against the total number of successes on the ritual to perceive the glyph and identify its purpose.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward oozes so much blood that it can only be used for a minute before it fully dissolves into blood.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"296"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.cAAyS7E7KG6CkmVK"},"cCQR9MwsyfXXOcVN":{"name":"Ritual Clensing of the Mind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Healing Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer clenses the target’s spirit, healing deep traumas and restoring the mind.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Music, nothing but a long meditation usually with some incense or some extraordinary therapy talk</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Differs from caster to caster and the needs of the subject.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 6), the subject heals Aggravated and Superficial Willpower damage equal to the Healing Aspect. Two Derangement triggers may be removed per casting of this ritual which may be attempted only once a day on the same subject regardless of whether or not the ritual succeeds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> See Path Spell Risk.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"316"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.cCQR9MwsyfXXOcVN"},"cQbfZem91vRn2Z3I":{"name":"Necromancy (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>It is polite to leave people alone and undisturbed. Cheafly among them: the dead. But those who follow the Path Necromancy see things a bit differently. Like the Path of Corruption, Necromancy has a reputation for being foul, practiced only by the worst of the worst or by the eccentrics who spend decades if not centuries with their nose buried in books of unsolvable mysteries. Unlike Corruption, this reputation is at least somewhat deserved. Even the simplest of Necromancy spells involve forcibly bending the dead to the magician’s will, and the most powerful of them can manipulate the powers of the Shadowlands to affect the living as well. While Necromancy does allow for command of the dead, most necromancers pair it up with the Path of Binding, Summoning and Warding due to its ease and precision in bending the dead to the caster’s will.</p>\n<p>Though methods vary wildly with practice, Mortals using Necromancy always require that the magician’s first instruments include the preserved piece of the corpse of someone they cared for, almost always a family member. This creates the link between the magician and the underworld, allowing them to become a conduit for the energies of death itself. While for mystical magicians, crafting such an instrument often feels natural, technomagicians tend to find themselves with unique, grisly objects, like keyboards with keys made of bone or computer chips with brain neurons embedded in the circuitry.</p>\n<p>Using this Path difficult for mortals. Not in the sense of technique — it is no more complex to use than any other Path. Rather, it takes an emotional toll on the magician. The living are not meant to channel the energies of death so directly. They feel the grief of their ancestors, back for centuries, perhaps millennia. This overwhelming despair at the loss of countless generations leads to complications, particularly for magicians who are low on Willpower. Finally, those energies cause harm to the user, and each spell or ritual causes the caster to take one level of Superficial Health per level of the power being invoked, unless otherwise mentioned in the description.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialty:</strong> Binding, Exorcism, Shadowlands, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</p>\n<p>Some restrictions due to monopolies on magic exist and are stated in the brackets. After the Family Reunion, the members of the reformed Clan of Death may select anything from the other bloodlines of the Clan, but most teachers prefer to teach their bloodline’s heritage frist.</p>\n<p>Note that effects expire on the caster once they go unconscious or to sleep.</p>\n<h3>Necromancy</h3>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Failure alone overwhealms the sorcerer with grief of countless generations and the energies of the damned, inflicting severe mental stress. Botches of this Path are truly dangerous, with derangements that last for a full Lunar cycle, the near certainty of very angry ghosts, the risk of the magician being pulled out of their body, leaving it apparently comatose while their mind spends the month “living” as one of the restless dead. Thriple the number of 1s rolle 3 The caster suffers unhalved Superficial Willpower equal to the margin of failure.</p>\n<p>6 A three-dot Derangement that lasts for a month 9 An angry ghost or a specter (flip a coin) has noticed the necromancer; an angry ghost will want to teach a lesson or avenge, while specters wish to see all consumed by Oblivion.</p>\n<p>12+ The user’s spirit is kicked out of their body like in Walk Through the Shroud (lvl 5 Ritual) for a month</p>\n<p>• Fettersight (Ghiberti, Nagaraja and</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rosselini):</strong> The user’s senses become supernaturally attuned to the energies of fetters, and they may identify these auras by sight, smell, and their other senses. While this power is in use, the vampire is distracted from other activity around them, conveying a −2 penalty to Dexterity and Wits rolls.</p>\n<p>•• Shroudsight (Prerequisite: Eyes of</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ash/Eyes of Charon):</strong> The caster can see, hear, and speak to ghosts in their vicinity, whichever side of the Shroud they are on, and also see eveything behind the Shroud, making them almost numb to certain senses from their own side of the veil. The user suffers a -2 penalty to Dexterity and Wits unless they concern things in the Lands of the Dead, but gains a three-dice bonus to estimate the density of the Shroud.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"331"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.cQbfZem91vRn2Z3I"},"cfGsdiRue7CVcMiu":{"name":"Illusions of Pontos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Mastering illusions over water, the koldun no longer simply toys with senses of their victims but creates independent illusions of water, mist and light.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Tretchery of Pontos</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the Rite, the sorcerer’s Tretchery of Pontos Illusion Spell benefits from Hard-Light Constructs Ritual system (p. 299). If anybody ever perceives the illusions to be a lie, this rite is nullified and must be performed again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"364"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.cfGsdiRue7CVcMiu"},"cj8KjC83QGhJuRab":{"name":"Master of Fire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Fire is one of the greatest dangers to vampires and is easily exploited by both hunters and vampires themselves against their own kind. Master of Flames makes the ritualist who knows how to produce magical flames a conquerer of flame and gives them the ability to extinguish fires or light them up with a mere word.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Lure of Flames or Balefire</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (how long you have access to the spell given by the ritual)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A small container and a candle</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer lights the candle up using a magical power such as the Lure of Flames and then snuffs it with their hand (provoking a fear frenzy at a difficulty of 2). Then, the magician takes a bit of wax from near the flame and places it in a small container like a ring.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The ritual itself is performed at a difficulty of 4. On a win of the ritual roll, as long as the sorcerer has the wax on their person, the sorcerer may speak a word of power and make a Rouse Check (which counts as a level 3 power for the purposes of rerolling it) to make their Spell Test using Area Aspect + 2 as the difficulty.</p>\n<p>On a win, the sorcerer ignites or extinguishes flames according to the Area Aspect used as if this was a spell. This may also be used as countermagic against fire-based attacks of other sorcerers and each success on the caster’s roll removes a success from the fire-based attack. An example would be usage of this Path vs. another Lure of Flames user. Igniting flames is a full action while extinguishing or countermagic against fire attacks is a minor action of -2 dice. The sorcerer is unable to summon or extinguish fire after Duration expires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> A Botch when igniting flames has the sorcerer set themselves on fire, taking two poinst of Aggravated Health damage and the same amount on each turn after until they douse themselves with a Composure + Survival (Difficulty 3). Losing control when extinguishing flames simply fails the spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"286"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.cj8KjC83QGhJuRab"},"cledNEnAgwq3IvFT":{"name":"Mindless Corpse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer raises a dead body as a Mindless Corpse.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"341"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.cledNEnAgwq3IvFT"},"dFoqr4KqOOMcQopC":{"name":"Alter Temperature","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Special Effect (••). Aspects: Distance (treat the measurement as the radius of effect), Duration</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer can also alter the temperature by up to 10 degrees Fahrenheit or 5 degrees Celsius.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"357"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.dFoqr4KqOOMcQopC"},"dWyn0JwpwxV6Sysi":{"name":"Remember","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Divination Special Effect (•). Aspects: Divination uses only three Aspects. Time Period (past, present or future) and</em></p>\n<p>Divination works in symbolism and doesn’t give precise answers without high successes or the Storyteller needing a device to advance plot. Even when using technomagic and hypermath, the sorcerer generally gets probabilities rather than certain outcomes. It’s also easier to have vague predictions come to pass than highly specific ones.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"283"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.dWyn0JwpwxV6Sysi"},"dgef6a4dl55q0u7S":{"name":"Intelligent Servant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>If rigor mortis has set in already, the Spell fails. A human is raised as an undead with its old intellect and traits. It may speak and behaves in a manner closer to a living human than not. See Undead characteristics on p. XX. Intelligent Servant is revived with 1 dot less in each Social and Mental Attribute and Road rating, to a minimum of 1. Skills and Physical Attributes remain.</p>\n<p>An Intelligent Servant must be bought as a 1-dot retainer or might one day decide to go free after a Story. Intelligent Servants are not actually alive, merely their brains have been brought back to life with old memories.</p>\n<p>It cannot become a ghost, use numina or supernatural powers or even be targeted by Baleful Exorcism which suggestions a lack of a full soul. Still, Cappadocians speculate what this revival of a soul’s fragment (which they believe this spell to be) could mean for them and their founder who is said to have lost pieces of his soul.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.dgef6a4dl55q0u7S"},"dqvPVCB5ygqwz8xL":{"name":"School: Dibu e Debu","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-dibu-e-debu.png","system":{"description":"<p>Meaning, “to the gods and goddesses”, Dibu e Debu is an ancient form of sorcery predating Thaumurgy by more than a millenia. It is commonly practiced by the Mnemachian Nosferatu and is based primarily on Celtic druidism. Although it is primarily practiced by the Mnemachian Nosferatu (who have invented or reinvented many of its Paths), it is practiced by older vampires typically passing the ancient traditions down to their childer and their childer later.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<h3>Practices of the Druids</h3>\n<p>Adhering to the ancient ways, practitioners of Dibu e Debu venerate ancient gods and spirits. They tend to keep to their groves and Natural Sancta among nature and protect it from outside humans who would benefit at the forest’s detriment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Glyphs of Power. Natural Sanctum, Shrines (usually to Celtic gods)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Defense of all Nature: Druids see themselves as defenders of nature, its cycle of life and death and spirits they venerate. When acting in defence of another or of nature, Botch Difficulties are reduced by 1.</p>\n<p>• Grove’s Power: Natural magic is weak outside of nature, as the name suggestions. When outside of nature or when the user doesn’t benefit from a Sanctum, Difficulties for Spell and Ritual Tests are increased by 1.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Dibu e Debu</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy p. XX</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces (Wrath of Nature)</p>\n<p>• Enchantment P. XX.</p>\n<p>• Conveyance</p>\n<p>• Divination</p>\n<p>• Ephemera</p>\n<p>• Fear</p>\n<p>• Fortune</p>\n<p>• Healing</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting</p>\n<p>• The Green Path</p>\n<p>• Weather Control</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"231"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.dqvPVCB5ygqwz8xL"},"dsxnz7vyoJTNPzrg":{"name":"Balm of Bathory","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula requires one pint/half a liter of blood from a youthful victim (or victims) of clear complexion (the needed amount doubles per brewing, see below), chamomile flowers, and shea butter or sheep’s fat; optional essential oils for scent. This formula’s potency is always a week as long as it is applied regularly. It gives the one having the balm applied the Looks: Stunning Merit (or 4 dots of Looks to spend at their discretion in our brew).</p>\n<p>However, the use of the Balm of Bathory can be highly addictive and the amount of mortal blood required doubles per brew. (The eighth brew requires 128 pints, enough blood to fill a small bathtub, but the amount does not increase afterward.) The first batch of the balm has no side effects, but after the second batch, the balm turns toxic on the user’s flesh past the first week. They suffer 1 Superficial damage worth of necrotic flesh wounds nightly, affecting any part of their body exposed to the balm, until they either brew another batch or until they go without the balm for one month per batch brewed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.dsxnz7vyoJTNPzrg"},"e67J8g4HPxoAZbV2":{"name":"Sweet Sins","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>The target suffers a dice penalty equal to the user’s Path of Corruption rating until they commit a sin of their Road*break a Chronicle Tenet.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.e67J8g4HPxoAZbV2"},"eQAdM1o6i3l8Rk4W":{"name":"Master of the Weather","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The Carpathian Mountains and its basin see great changes in weather quickly. Naturally, the koldun have learned to petition the spirits to manipulate the weather in their favour, even being able to strike their enemies with lightning from the sky if the conditions are right.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None, but the spell uses the same as Weather Control</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the Rite (Difficulty 3), the koldun is granted command over the weather, giving them dots of Weather Control (p. XX) up to their rating in the Transylvanian Kraina for a single night. The koldun may use these Weather Control dots to evoke only the effects that may be associated with Transylvania.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"366"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.eQAdM1o6i3l8Rk4W"},"ehoqOqiuWa2S2hLx":{"name":"Animate Curios","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>As with Treasured Ornaments, the koldun rouses the spirits of objects around their haven. With Animate Curios, the ornaments gain animation and levitation – a sword may fight, a mirror break upon an intruder passing, armour stands may move and so on.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets (how many items are animated)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Once the rite is concluded, the combat and Intimidation Skill of anything animated is determined by Storyteller discretion. A mundane, traditionally non-violent tool or object bears an Intimidation pool no greater than 3, and a combat pool no greater than 4; a weapon or dangerous apparatus has an Intimidation pool no greater than 5, and a combat pool no greater than 7. Weapons usually float as if animated carried by the spirits when striking or moving. An item is disabled if it is ever struck and all attacks against it suffer a -2 success penaly due to a required Called Shot (even if the item is a sword carried by an animated armour set: it moves in unnatural ways). If struck, a weapon is disabled. The Rite concludes if the koldun’s haven is put to the torch. Items may never leave the boundaries of the property (use the character’s claimed Domain for the size of the property)</p>\n<p>Weapons must be bought as a Background with 1 dot enough for up to 5 weapons in order to keep the game fair.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"367"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ehoqOqiuWa2S2hLx"},"erpCsTPS7eDHfpF1":{"name":"Black Sea Kraina (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>When you sink to the bottom of the uninhabitable black depths of Marmora, you will see your own soul staring back at you.</p>\n<p>— Radislav, koldun of the Black Sea Considered to be one of the oldest of the Kraine, the Transylvanian Kraina channels the spirits of the Black Sea and all the bodies of water that flow into it and form a spiritual web of sorts.</p>\n<p>The kolduny of the Black Sea are masters of water, To them, water is an instrument of tricking with illusions, tracking through memory preserved by the ancient spirits of waters. However, when needed, they can turn just as deadly as other kolduny, able to sink entire fleets.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"363"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.erpCsTPS7eDHfpF1"},"f5J7Di6vaZ7XowbV":{"name":"Shroud Manipulation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster lowers or raises the Shroud rating by a single stage.</p>\n<p>Setting it to Impenetrable removes the ability for ghosts to perceive anything with clarity.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.f5J7Di6vaZ7XowbV"},"f5pEJOS8yM5enxQJ":{"name":"Herd Ward","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Binding, Summoning and Warding Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual protects the sorcerer’s herd from others who would feed on them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Damage, Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer mixes scorpion poison with their own vitae to draw the ward. The vampire can ward a single kine or inscribe the ward on a door so all who stay within the ward for that night are protected.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful Ritual Test, the ward is successful. When another vampire tries to feed on the warded kine, they must roll Stamina + Resolve against the Ritual Test. In a failure, damage equal to the margins or the Damage Aspect, whichever is lower. This is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, including the sorcerer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"272"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.f5pEJOS8yM5enxQJ"},"fBbFUSjtdhNTwJgk":{"name":"Scour Secrets","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The Blood Sorcerer unleashes rivulets of their Blood primed to search for information on a subject of their choosing. The Blood washes over the object of their search, allowing the Blood Sorcerer to investigate vast amounts of text and volumes in a matter of minutes. The object investigated bears a telltale bloodstain thereafter.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance and Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer bleeds out a little as their blood slowly navigates to sources of knowledge.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user makes their Ritual Test. The time spent depends on the size of the area to be searched: A single room might be scoured in minutes, while a library requires hours, if not the whole night. Distance Aspect dictates how far the blood will search and Duration how for how long. When blood passes over an object or through an entire room, the Storyteller decides whether or not something useful was learned. Going through a library can set your Academics, Investigation, Science, Technology or Occult at a certain rating if the information acquired matches the appropriate Skill for the roll. For example, going through an entire city’s library might set your Academics to 4, Science and Technology to 3 and Occult at 2 Additionally, the ruitual does not impart the ability to parse the information if it comes in an unknown language or in a cipher. If the knowledge sought is stored in non-written formats, such as painting, music, or on old floppy disks, the power works at the Storyteller’s discretion in what it brings.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, ritual goes on but provides false information.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"323"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fBbFUSjtdhNTwJgk"},"fFVCb1uyX6FnhWxR":{"name":"Protective Coating","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>A substance that makes it permanently impossible to chip or break the object (+2 difficulty to destroy). One dose covers around a meter radius and the effect is permanent.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fFVCb1uyX6FnhWxR"},"fTWGjEo9hDUT2edR":{"name":"My World","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Illusion Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Reaching the mastery of the Path, the sorcerer is able to craft illusions directly into the world independent of the observers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> Depends on the caster</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win (or once released as this ritual is often hanged) of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 6), the caster may cast any spell or a ritual of the Illusion Path without the Number of Targets system. Instead, the illusions exist independently of the senses of the targets, much like Chimerstry of vampires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As Path Spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"318"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fTWGjEo9hDUT2edR"},"fVdfhAxn4VGuBE9K":{"name":"Shape Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Learned mostly by the most ostentatious of Tremere, this Special Effect is used to manipulate blood or vitae into finely crafted images – whether entertaining your fellows at Elysium with gaudy magician’s tricks or diverting flowing rivulets into painstakingly precise Hermetic sigils. A few others have mastered this power to help clean up others mistakes.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration and Shape as the Aspect determining the complexity of the shape.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer either spills their own blood or uses blood present within the scene to manipulate it. This ritual does not have a casting time and instead the effect is instantaneous and present for the duration of the ritual. Maintaining the effect is a -2 dice minor action.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual takes 30 seconds to perform. The vampire makes their Ritual Test and by concentrating on the image to be formed, sends the blood or vitae flowing into the prescribed pattern or shape. To create shapes, more than one rank of Shape Bmust be used in a spell, varying with the pattern’s complexity: from a simple “SOS” (1 rank of Shape), to a portrait (3 ranks of Shape) or topologically complex sorcerous sigil (4 ranks of Shape Blood). The vampire can change its shape freely for the rest of the Duration if they can concentrate on it. This Ritual can be used to clean crime scenes, if needed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"322"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fVdfhAxn4VGuBE9K"},"fd4IkldC0lRrmJV4":{"name":"Fog over Sea","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Moving with the unnatural grace of a ghost, a koldun employing this power may stride across water as readily as land, leaving nary a ripple to mark their passing. Some vampires delight in using this power in conjunction with Pool of Lies to conjure panoplies of phantasms to attend them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the koldun is granted granted the ability to activate Fog over Sea Spell (Aspects: Duration) by making a Rouse Check and dropping some of their blood into a body of water.</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the koldun is able to walk on water. Unless the spell is dropped by the koldun, they are unable to submerge themselves or swim. The ability to be faster than most swimmers typically manifests itself through a 2-dice bonus on Athletics at storyteller’s discretion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"364"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fd4IkldC0lRrmJV4"},"fiCixgc7Avyb8BSC":{"name":"Vox Mortui","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>While some Cappadocians have learned Setite secrets of invoking a shade of a dead person to communicate with their memories as if they are a living, it is hotly debated who invented this Ritual first. This Ritual does not compel a target to tell the truth, and it does not actually communicate with the spirit of the person, but its vestiges. As such, the body can not speak of experiences past its death and may refuse to speak on some matters completely.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A cadaver with an unimpaired ability to speaking (having lungs, throat, tongue…). Normally, bodies older than three months are unable to be used in this Ritual unless they’ve been preserved in some manner.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The Necromancer ceremonially opens the mouth of the targeted human cadaver and bids it speak.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A win on the Ritual allows the body to speak as if it was living for the Duration. The body has self consciousness but lacks an actual soul as it is merely vestiges of the soul given voice. As such, it will not accept bargains as it knows it can not get anything nor is it phased by threats. The body speaks and doesn’t speak on whatever it wants according to what they would have in life, up to the moment of their death.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"344"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fiCixgc7Avyb8BSC"},"fyAhgYl1509juqVH":{"name":"Amulet of Protection","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An amulet of protection from physical harm. When worn, it subtracts three points of Health damage before halving like Toughness. However, each use subtracts five years from the user’s life, visible on their organs, but not on the outside. This does not affect the immortal.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.fyAhgYl1509juqVH"},"g3bNFYmyQqH7rLjO":{"name":"Impregnable Soul","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Necromancers deal with wraiths on a frequent basis and so do their, often mortal, assistants. This has Led to a few assistants here and there being possessed without the necromancer’s knowledge which can often cause a lot of problems, not to mention possibility of a wronged wraith willing to do harm to a necromancer. This Ritual was made to prevent such happenings by marking their assistants with Wards that would prevent the marked ones from being possessed. It also helps against getting their soul ripped out by another necromancer, however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration, Resistance (see below)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A holy symbol that has been damaged and is now misshapen (preferably by hand), incense</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The Necromancer lights the incense, gets on their knees, holds the misshapen holy symbol in their hands, and offers a mock (or a true, as some Cainites preserve their faith in unlife) prayer of protection from “horrid apparitions” for themselves or someone else.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The caster makes their Ritual roll and on a success the target of the Ritual gains Resistance as additional successes to possession, be it Puppetry of ghosts, the Auspex 5 power Possession, or some other avenue of possession. This extends to attempts to pull the Necromancer's soul out of their body. They gain no special protections from powers that affect the mind.</p>\n<p>The target may be someone other than the caster, but the caster must have a piece of them if they are not present. If the target wants to resist, they may roll Resolve + Composure vs. the casters Ritual Test.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"345"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.g3bNFYmyQqH7rLjO"},"g88zwfjpVTMBRIZy":{"name":"Pishacha’s Banquet","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>This grisly Ritual was developed by the Nagaraja as a method of feeding in desperate circumstances, Pisacha’s Banquet allows one feasting on the dead to acquire more than just blood. Iron Gullet is required to slake down Hunger from unfresh cadavers and to not puke as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Paths:</strong> Reanimation 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> This ritual is mostly practiced by the Nagaraja out of need. Scottish Giovanni family of Dunsirn developed their variant of it for fun.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Local herbs and ashes of living creatures</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer marks the remains of a sentient being with a mixture of ingredients, then physically consumes it over the course of a three turns, tearing apart flesh and bone with their teeth and forcing their undead body to swallow it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a Win, the necromancer can choose one of the following effects. On a critical win (or 3+ margins), they can choose two (or the same one twice):</p>\n<ul><li>Reduce their Hunger by two (counts as bagged or dead blood) regardless of Blood Potency restrictions.</li><li>Experience a brief Memoriam from the victim’s perspective.</li><li>Gain one Skill Specialty possessed by the victim as chosen by the caster, which lasts until the next feeding.</li></ul>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"336"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.g88zwfjpVTMBRIZy"},"g8QsLuSkC2q2HPGA":{"name":"Vertigo","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Mastery of the Mortal Shell Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following.</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer picks a body part to in which they will induce pain: head (Social and Mental), legs (Dexterity), arms (Strength). The victim cannot spend Willpower to reroll pools with Associated Attributes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"329"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.g8QsLuSkC2q2HPGA"},"g9QKU7ru2f7uIHH9":{"name":"Unshakable spirit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>Every target in range becomes immune to mundane intimidation and gains +2 dice to resist supernaturally aided attempts to do so or to influence their minds (Presence, Dominate...).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.g9QKU7ru2f7uIHH9"},"gDZx8St0GGjkPUn2":{"name":"Blood of Potency","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The vampire can concentrate their Blood, increasing its potency temporarily.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer chants an incantation to thicken their blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the sorcerer increases their Blood Potency by one for the Duration; a critical win increases it by two. The vampire can use this power to exceed their generation limit on Blood Potency. This power does not stack with itself, but it can be reactivated to try to settle for a stronger effect.</p>\n<p>Once a vampire enters torpor or daysleep, Duration ends. Non-vampires cannot use this ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Same as Path Spells.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"324"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.gDZx8St0GGjkPUn2"},"gEIPb9zu2aE1iSBR":{"name":"Engaging the Vessel of Transference","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>“Beware the gifts of the Children of Haqim”, elders of the Dark Ages used to warm their childers as those childers now issue similar warnings to their childers regarding the Tremere and their gifts. With this ritual, a sorcerer may create an object and infuse it with their own blood which will “swap” with the blood of the next person who touches it with their bare skin, building a blood bond and procuring blood for the sorcerer to experiment with and potentially cause other problems for the victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to act as a container which must be between the size of a small cup and a one-gallon/four-liter jug. It can be sealed, as long as it can contain blood. Once, a Tremere gave their regent a beautiful pendant which was in a small chest that was hollowed out and filled with blood, ready to be swapped.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer draws glyphs on the container and pours their own blood inside before sealing it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the container is ready. The next time somebody other than the sorcerer touches it with their bare skin (even thin cotton protects against this ritual), the rouse check of blood used for the ritual and a rouse check of blood of the person who touched the container swap places.</p>\n<p>Individuals with at least four dots in Occult, those who know this ritual or possess a power such as Sense the Unseen may roll Wits + Occult or the Discipline’s rating against the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge to perceive the glyphs and figure out their meaning.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"321"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.gEIPb9zu2aE1iSBR"},"gb2siWkzJjUlhOmd":{"name":"Lightning Strike","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>There must be clouds present. The sorcerer can make a Spell Test (Aspects:</p>\n<p>Distance, Lightning Strike, Number of Targets) vs. Wits + Athletics of targets within the line of sight to attack them with lightning, inflicting Health damage that is Aggravated even to supernaturals equal to the Damage Aspect or the margins on the contest, whichever is lower.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.gb2siWkzJjUlhOmd"},"gtg9FWCZt4j6o90k":{"name":"Lure of Flames","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (•). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the sorcerer conjures a flame in their palm which can provide light but is harmless until released. The sorcerer can release the flames at targets who can try to dodge the Spell Test. Those hit suffer fire damage equal to the margins or half the Damage rank, rounded up, whichever is lower.</p>\n<p>Vampires and other creatures with the Beast other than the sorcerer must also make a Fear Frenzy Test against the Difficulty equal to half the rank of Area, (rounded up), due to the flames. Those who are damaged with flames can never roll at a Difficulty lower than 2.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"284"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.gtg9FWCZt4j6o90k"},"hKdjS8gBvJ5y6MQU":{"name":"Vitae Preservative","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula preserves one Rouse Check or one Hunger’s Worth of vitae from the same vampire per dose in addition to one Rouse Check activation cost (blood in doses cannot exceed blood used as ingredients) and allows it to maintain ghouling properties indefinitely or until exposed to the sun.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"263"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.hKdjS8gBvJ5y6MQU"},"hQ8uLnCC7FY3JC6H":{"name":"Whip of Woe (Rosselini)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>After making a small wound, the caster draws their blood into a whip that may directly attack the ectoplasmic corpus of ghosts, dealing Aggravated Health damage even if they haven’t manifested.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.hQ8uLnCC7FY3JC6H"},"hZIgjVBDTZ6WIHJS":{"name":"Ephemera (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Some magicians — those focusing on the Path of Summoning, Binding, and Warding — command spirits. Those who follow the Path of Ephemera seek a relationship of greater familiarity with them. To magicians who use the Path of Ephemera, their relationship to spirits is more like family and friends than master and servant. When they call for a spirit, it’s an invitation, not a subpoena.</p>\n<p>Instead of binding and commanding spirits, the Path of Ephemera allows the magician to negotiate with them. This process, called chiminage, is a give-and-take. Each spirit wants something different, related to their nature. The spirit’s request depends on the power of the spirit and the size of the task involved. Asking an Incarna to undertake a difficult and long-term task requires a lot from the magician, perhaps something only possible with a difficult quest of their own. Asking a Gaffling to do something simple that ties directly into their nature (e.g., asking a fire spirit to light a candle) would, conversely, require almost nothing of the magician, perhaps only the opportunity to do it. This process, naturally, has a steep learning curve. Most magicians find themselves on the worse end of a deal or two before they get a sense of it.</p>\n<p>This Path almost always focuses on the Middle Umbra and, more rarely, on the Low Umbra. Rumors of versions that work with the High Umbra are persistent but remain nothing more than hearsay.</p>\n<p>To simplify, the Low Umbra is the Underworld, the Medium Umbra is the one that is beyond the Penumbra which intersects with the material plane is an ecosystem of spirits, a place where spirits of nature and objects dwell. The High Umbra, also known as the Astral Umbra, is where concepts and ideas dwell, a reflection of the collective human conscioussness.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Unless stated otherwise under the sorcerer’s Sorcery School, they have access to spirits of the Middle Umbra and the Metaphysical Weight (Naturae). Necromantic schools may possess Metaphysical Weight (Ghosts) for the Low Umbra while rare ones have easy access to the Metaphysical Weight (Astral) of the High Umbra or Metaphysical Weight (Infernal) of the deepest pits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> –2 to +2 Difficulty on certain Spells depending on the spirit’s opinion of the magician</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Demons, Ghosts, Spirits</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Metaphysical Weight and Spiritual Reach</p>\n<h3>Spiritual Reach</h3>\n<p>Most common Ephemera Rituals deal with invocation of the spirits as described under Spiritual Reach and require a certain Path rating depending on what is being called. Rituals to call spirits only use the Metaphysical Weight Aspect and the Ritual’s rating is equal to the Metaphysical Aspect’s rank that the ritual uses. As such, Metaphysical Aspect’s rank (and type: Naturae, Ghosts, Astral...) is static for each Ritual.</p>\n<p>Notably, Sympathetic Connection is absent as an Aspect in summoning rituals of the Ephemera Path; only the psychic impression of what kind of a spirit or which specific spirit the caster wishes to summon is required. The caster also may send a psychic impression consisting of their name, face, the location of the ritual, and a single-sentence message. The caster may choose to ommit one or more things in their psychic impression, but may never give false information as the call is the source of the information, not the caster.</p>\n<p>A ritual to travel to the Infernal parts of the Umbra is impossible to create.</p>\n<p>• The magician can sense spirits and get their attention but can do little else.</p>\n<p>•• The magician is restricted to calling upon a general type of spirit with rituals (rating of the level is equal to the Metaphysical Weight of the spirits called). For example, plant naturae, newly-made ghosts...</p>\n<p>••• Now specific spirits can be called through tailor-made rituals (rating of the level is equal to the Metaphysical Weight of the spirit), and almost always show up unless the magician has offended them in some way.</p>\n<p>•••• The magician can see into the Penumbra (where the spirit and the material world overlap), which increases the numbers and types of spirit they can interact with. The magician can also fight back against spirits if they need to, though most try to avoid it.</p>\n<p>••••• The magician can now go to the spirits instead of needing them to come to the physical world. Though physical travel into the Umbra is still impossible, the magician can astrally project there, using Wits for Dexterity, Manipulation for Strength, Intelligence as Stamina, and Willpower as Health Levels. They are connected to their body by a silver thread, and if they run out of Willpower, it snaps, severing their connection and leaving them stranded.</p>\n<p>A Low Umbra (Ghost) variant of this Path would venture into the Shadowlands, High Umbra (Astral) soars into the sky. It is impossible to go to Hell, however.</p>\n<p>Risk Dealings with spirits can be dangerous, especially at the early stages when all a magician can do is get the attention of whatever is nearby. Even at the higher levels, the most dangerous thing possible on a Botch is to call the wrong spirit, with banes being particularly common.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"303"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.hZIgjVBDTZ6WIHJS"},"hdoGKlJ2kkvgYrEV":{"name":"Blood of Ahriman","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The ritualist requires to have bound a Hungry Shade (see the Binding, Summoning and Warding Path, p. XX). On a win, the shade merges with the ritualist who may exchange Attributes or Skills with it if the Shade possess it at a higher rating. Additionally, the character counts their Obtenebration rating as one higher for the purposes of using powers (rolls, scaling, etc.). This lasts until the ritualist commands the Shade to leave. If the ritualist is still possessed and the Shade breaks out of its bonds, it may attempt to exert control over the mystic.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> For the duration, the character’s skin becomes covered in shadow regardless of the presence of light. If the character spends more days in this spectral state than their Composure at a time, their skin permanently becomes of shadow. Powers such as Sense the Unseen and Scry the Soul can detect the presence of the shade within the character.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"254"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.hdoGKlJ2kkvgYrEV"},"hmiaUvkqhRMnzsCh":{"name":"Pit of Contemplation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>The vampire personally murders an innocent mortal. While innocence is subjective, traditional sacrifices are children, virgins, and holy individuals. The vampire then takes at least three pints/six liters of the deceased’s blood into an unlit space and uses it to paint a doorway on a wall in the chamber (usually a closet later mailed to one’s rival). Finally, the vampire splashes a pot of ink onto the blood-painted portal. Focusing their will upon the gateway, the mystic opens a tear through to the lowest pit of Abyss.</p>\n<p>The hole draws objects, air, and people toward it and, if they fail a Dexterity + Athletics roll (Difficulty 4), sucks them into a pocket within the Abyss. The caster and those with Obtenebration 5 are immune to this pull.</p>\n<p>Anyone foolish or unfortunate enough to fall into the gap is immediately transported into a pocket of eternal black nothingness for as long as the mystic sees fit.</p>\n<p>While trapped, victims are suspended in an endless blackness. They cannot see or hear anyone or anything around themselves. Only the mystic who conjures this blasphemous gateway can free those held within, by pouring a vampire’s vitae over the painted door (sufficient to provoke a Rouse Check). Mortals sucked into the Abyss in this manner are instantly killed. If the mystic is destroyed without releasing their prisoners, any undead prisoners remain trapped in the void (unless and until another vampire reverses the ritual).</p>\n<p>The following Effects are rank 6 and as such out of hands of most playable characters (at Storyteller’s Discretion, as usual).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"257"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.hmiaUvkqhRMnzsCh"},"hyQM4Qp0CppxAgOB":{"name":"Drowning Tide","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Damage, Range, Area</em></p>\n<p>This requires an Olympic sized swimming pool or larger body of water like a river. Spell Test is opposed using Dexterity + Athletics or Strength + Athletics. Targets who fail are pulled underwater by a sudden wave or undertow, causing them to start drowning, taking damage equal to the Damage Aspect which cannot be made Aggravated if they require air to live.</p>\n<p>Victims are considered immobile targets. Each turn, the sorcerer must roll their Spell Test again (Minor Action -2 dice) vs. the entrapped victim’s Strength + Athletics, until either the sorcerer lets go or the victim succeeds.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"285"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.hyQM4Qp0CppxAgOB"},"iFyTbI5ysnWR7d8U":{"name":"Animate Beasts","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer resurrects an animal, keeping its old stats while reducing Social Attributes to 1 and Mental to 0. Buying them as Backgrounds to retain their function after a story (as, compelled by vestiges of insticts, they become disobedient and attack at random) depends on their mass. A dot may purchase up to 5 animals up to the mass of a dog, 3 for the mass of a dog up and 2 for anything larger than a human (like a bear). Mentally, these creatures function as Aggressive Corpses. Animals retain their natural weapons and special features like the ability to fly unless they're too damaged.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.iFyTbI5ysnWR7d8U"},"iJmYz2jXVSO4U6CJ":{"name":"Eyes of Babel","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This formula requires an eyeball and/or a tongue to work. Those who ingest the formula can read (eyeball) mand/or speak (tongue) the languages the person from whom the eye/tongue came from can, at the same level. The effect lasts for 24 hours.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"263"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.iJmYz2jXVSO4U6CJ"},"igl109KcHXpX5tGL":{"name":"Attrocity’s Release","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The ritual allows the vampire to temporarily make any trace of diablerie on their part undetectable.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Human reproductive organs thinly sliced, moss or a sea sponge, and scrapings of bezoar.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The performer must first create a number of poultices from the ingredients, something that takes at least one night to prepare, disregarding the time it takes to procure the components. Then, the sorcerer presses the ritually prepared poultices to a series of deep cuts on their target’s body, drawing out the stolen Blood. The Blood-soaked poultices are then burnt, their ashes left exposed to the sunrise.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> An unwilling target may resist with Resolve + Blood Potency, the Ritual’s Difficulty counting as the greater of the target’s successes or the standard difficulty. If successful, the caster picks which one of the two effects they wish to evoke:</p>\n<ul><li>The Ritual reverses the most recent instance of diablerie that the target has committed, including changes to Blood Potency, Disciplines, or personality, though not Road rating.</li><li>The target’s essence is destroyed instead, the victim becoming effectively possessed by their latest diablerie victim.</li></ul>\n<p>On a loss of a Botch, both the diablerist and their victim’s essences are destroyed, forcing the body into the wassail.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"328"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.igl109KcHXpX5tGL"},"izqQ1uAoBh9HVewY":{"name":"Sigil of Discord","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The vampire inscribes a sigil of confusion to play with the minds of their victims, making them dizzy and without a sense of space and direction.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (as a radius), Duration (ranks count as doubled) and</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist draws a sigil of discord and confusion in the middle of the area they wish to infect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, a miasma of confusion rises that may only be perceived by powers such as Sense the Unseen. All who enter an enclosed space with under the influence of this miasma and are not excluded from the effects must roll a Wits + Resolve contest against the initial successes on the sorcerer’s roll or find themselves hopelessly lost in the sorcerer’s haven, no matter how small it is. All who fail on the first roll automatically fail any Awareness roll while within the location.</p>\n<p>If an intruder attempts to follow or chase the vampire through the building, they will lose track of direction, make wrong turns, etc. Additionally, they are unable to remember any but the smallest details regarding the place. The miasma lasts for one full 24hour period, but the confused memories an intruder takes with them last until they visit the building when it is not under this protective ensorcellment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the Ward excludes no one, including the sorcerer.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"297"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.izqQ1uAoBh9HVewY"},"jG6nZ4dd4Krhs5sa":{"name":"Masterful shift","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Shapeshifting Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration, Scale and Subject described below. If the sorcerer is transforming others, then Range is also an Aspect.</em></p>\n<p>As full shift, but the user may become a swarm. See Swarm Animalism ••••• on p. 118 for more information.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"352"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.jG6nZ4dd4Krhs5sa"},"jgm90ymitN5asY5a":{"name":"Passage to Enoch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>By ritualistically killing themselves with a silver dagger plunged into heart (mortal variants of this Ritual do not tend to lethally damage the heart). After that, the user loses consciousness and physically awakens before the fields of the legendary city of Enoch in the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> This is only taught to the members of the True Black Hand, like some Harbingers of Skulls or Nagaraja</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A silver dagger</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster first chants a litany to Enoch, asking the powers of the Underworld to transport them to the ancestral city of all vampires</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the caster falls asleep and awakens physically in the Shadowlands near Enoch.</p>\n<p>If the 6th maestrom hasn’t yet happened or won’t happen at all in your game, Enoch will be whole and Tal Mahe Ra a proper sect that controls it. If not, you will see a bunch of ruins, at best.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the vampire is transported somewhere randomly in the Shadowlands.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"340"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.jgm90ymitN5asY5a"},"jtFl7kzBfRhR4lny":{"name":"Circle of Secrecy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>In Ceoris and Alamut, those who wield great secrets can do what those wielding great magical powers can not. In order to make sure others cannot eavesdrop or track them through supernatural sight and scrying methods, multiple traditions created variants of this Warding Circle that ensures no sound can be heard outside of the circle and no supernatural senses can penetrate it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (as a radius), Duration (ranks count as doubled)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist draws a circle in blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual creates a warding circle or a zone according to the Range. On a win on the ritual roll, no sound is heard outside of the circle, which includes eavesdropping from inside the circle while being in other worlds such as the Shadowlands. Those using supernatural senses such as Shadowsight or a scrying mirror cannot see any being inside this circle nor hear them. Those who wish to eavesdrop or see into the mirror must use their will to break through the barrier, rolling Resolve + Awareness against the total number of successes on the ritual roll. This ritual is a Warding Circle ritual. See systems for Warding Circles like increasing their size in Binding, Summoning and Warding Path.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, not even those within the circle will be able to hear anything.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"296"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.jtFl7kzBfRhR4lny"},"k0crAzCQ2mRinUBo":{"name":"Ghost Repellant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Same as Beast Repellent, but against ghosts. Uses cold iron dust.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.k0crAzCQ2mRinUBo"},"k1FeCGOwMYc5Ht6M":{"name":"Defender of the Haven","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The thaumaturge draws dozens of glyphs, runes or letters on a clay brick or a piece of stone and then draws those same symbols onto a clay brick or a stone within the building. After that, the brick/stone is given to the Gargoyle of at least Generation 10 to crush with their bare hands. Gargoyle is successfully attuned to the building and may sense the presence (but not the identities) of every sentient being within the building within a factor of 2 (for example, if 10 people are in the building, the Storyteller may rule that the Gargoyle feels the presence of a number of sentient entities from 5 to 20). The Gargoyle does not sense the exact location, but the place of the largest gathering. The ritual’s effect works even when the Gargoyle is asleep or in torpor. If the person is using powers to erase their auras such as False Resonance (Chimerstry 4) or to mask their presence entirely such as by Obfuscate, the Gargoyle may contest their rolls with Wits + Awareness, swapping Wits for Resolve if they are actively searching.</p>\n<p>The Gargoyle remains attuned to the building even if they are not inside it until they are reattuned to another building by this ritual. The power granted by this ritual does not work if the Gargoyle is not in physical contact with the building itself. Similarly, those not touching the building cannot be sensed such as those using Mist Form.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"302"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.k1FeCGOwMYc5Ht6M"},"k5LcqtGZQtj7FO0P":{"name":"Inspired bravery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (•). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>Every target in range gains a specialty in a combat skills (athletics, firearms, melee or brawl). The caster may select one skill per Rank of Inspired Bravery Aspect which may be raised like a common Aspect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.k5LcqtGZQtj7FO0P"},"kCRQTQaj4qOHP14s":{"name":"Homuncular Servant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer animates a bodypart to raise a homuncular servant.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"341"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.kCRQTQaj4qOHP14s"},"kGzpuDLVy7VXitqF":{"name":"Belle/Beau/Bright of the Ball","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>With knowledge of an upcoming social event and proper preparation, a sorcerer can make themselves quite the sensation. With an invitation in hand, whether intended for them or not, the magician infuses their presence into the upcoming gathering. For the duration of the specified party, they receive VIP treatment from all attendees and hired staff. The organisers give their blessing and offer a hearty “have a great night” without second-guessing their guest list.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Allure, Duration, Number of Targets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An invitation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist uses an Invitation as a conduit, chanting spells of fascination over it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual, the caster gains the Allure dice bonus over anybody within the event who has not succeeded on a Composure + Resolve Test (Storytellers should make one roll for separate generic “groups” of types of people there. Players always roll). Additionally, the caster is welcome there and is let in without anybody checking the guestlist for them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Same as the Path spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"307"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.kGzpuDLVy7VXitqF"},"kH6hsgpOxeQzZNgw":{"name":"Garb of Hades","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Often, necromancers wish to walk the streets without actually doing it themselves or negotiate with dangerous partners without placing themselves in harm’s way. Some Cappadocians have gotten the reputation of actually being more possessed corpses than vampires due to this ritual. By dressing like a dead person, including animated zombies, a necromancer may possess them. Strangely, zombies and other undead creations animated by other necromancers are immune to this possession.</p>\n<p>The Giovanni and others who have less qualm about the supposed sanctity of the dead (mostly adherents to the Principle of Judgment and a few Nagaraja) call this ritual the Throne of Hades, allowing them to possess entire retinues of cadavers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Paths:</strong> Necromancy 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (counted as higher by two), Number of Targets (the user may possess multiple cadavers at once like a hivemind)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Either clothes in which a person died in (this allows for possession of only a single cadaver). Alternatively, a throne fashioned out of bones and bodyparts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer first dresses in clothing which the cadaver wore at the time of their death. After that, the necromancer meditates, usually in a safe spot, and takes possession of the cadaver.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, the user sits on a throne of bones and bodyparts and invokes their authority over the dead, falling into a trance.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make the Ritual roll when the meditation begins and on a Win the necromancer falls to a torpor-like trance as their consciousness takes over the corpse. If the 2nd method was used and Number of Targets was adaquate, more than a single corpse may be possessed at once. Possession time is governed by the Duration Aspect.</p>\n<p>While possessing, the cadaver(s) effectively hasve the caster’s skills and Mental and Social Attributes while keeping the Physical Attributes of the cadaver. The body possessed may even be a zombie or a homunculus of the necromancer. The Ritual can be used in combination with Daemonic Possession to allow the necromancer to permanently transfer themselves into a brand-new body, turning the old one to ash. If done right immediately (or at least until the body turns to ash unless it is preserved by Ashen Relic), a vampire may transfer themselves into the body of another this way.</p>\n<p>Extending this possession without Daemonic Possession into the daylight hours requires the vampire to stay awake (Corebook, p. 219); seeing the sun requires a test for fear frenzy though the sunlight does not damage the animal being ridden. Failure to stay awake simply The user remains oblivious to their original body, but harm to it pulls them out of the trance and releases the cadaver. Destruction of the possessed cadaver also ends the trance, and the vampire takes a point of Aggravated Willpower damage from the shock.</p>\n<p>The user may only use mental Disciplines and may not perform Blood Surges while possessing a cadaver unless they transferred their entire soul into it through Daemonic possession.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"348"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.kH6hsgpOxeQzZNgw"},"kdOC0X7AEHpfMzKv":{"name":"Healing Slumber of the Spirit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Healing Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer puts their subject to sleep, calming their mind and allowing the healing process to accelerate.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Whatever the sorcerer uses to mend wounds and ease spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> By potion, spell, sound or some other instrument of healing sorcerer uses, the subject is lulled deep into sleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 2), the subject falls asleep for 9 hours; when they awake, Superficial Willpower damage equal to the Healing Aspect or margins if the target resists (Stamina + Resolve), whichever is lower. For each margin, subtract one hour from the required sleep time. This healing cannot heal Aggravated Willpower</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> See Path Spell Risk.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"315"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.kdOC0X7AEHpfMzKv"},"kfKhoauGutmUA1pV":{"name":"Certámen","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Inherited from the days when the Tremere were still a house of mortal magi, certámen stands as one of the oldest means of settling disputes between Hermetic wizards. Each combatant stands in a circle of blood two paces across, set at opposite ends of another circle ten paces wide. The challenger declares their grievance to all present, while the defender announces three strictures (mystical or ceremonial) that both parties must observe, such as “no usage of Fortitude”. The duel begins when both participants intone the actual ritual.</p>\n<p>Traditionally, each certámen combatant brings along a second who serves them much as a knight’s squire. However, instead of handing them fresh lances and weapons, the second stands behind and to the right with ritual components at the ready. In the event of frenzy — which results in automatic loss — the second must also aid any attending guards in subduing the challenger. A neutral arbiter stands outside the circles and judges the duel. They may end the battle at any time and declare either party the victor regardless of outcome, though overt favoritism attracts unwanted attention from superiors — and perhaps a second certámen challenge from the wronged party. Otherwise, a certámen is fought until one party perishes or submits and forfeits.</p>\n<p>During certámen, vitae quickens like the vis (also known as Quintessence) of mortal magic. Spells grow in grandeur, their forms expanded to the will and design of the caster. The very air crackles with power as bolts of flame change into burning dragons and summoned winds flicker with pale screaming phantasms. Barring interruption by the arbiter, certámen continues until one party yields or perishes. Although the ritual itself cannot compel the terms of challenge, the clan rigorously enforces the sanctity of the duel. Although such duels are all but unknown, certámen even functions when living Hermetic magi observe the rituals in a duel with Tremere thaumaturges. Magi of other Fellowships and blood sorcerers who do not practice Hermetic thaumaturgy cannot take part in certámen.</p>\n<p>A Tremere cannot use certámen to force a superior to give him rank or to show off his thaumaturgical might, but he could use the rite to legitimately dispose a superior with whom he had a personal grievance or to force a peer to stop interfering in his affairs. Similarly, a higher ranking Tremere may step in as an arbiter, and a dour pontifex may very well stop the whole process before it begins. And, of course if the battle results in death, why then someone more competent than the loser must step forward to take over the late vampire’s assets and duties. Very few members of the other Clans have even heard of certámen. Elder Tremere intend to keep it that way, lest princes demand Tremere to organise magical bloodsports.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Thaumaturgy (Hermetic or any other kind)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (usually 2)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something like chalk or even blood to mark boundaries of the exterior circle and blood to mark the interior circles for the two combatants</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The performer of the ritual, usually the presiding judge in order not to exhaust one of the combatants ahead of the certámen, performs the ritual with the combatants inside the marked area and finishes the ritual by exiting it and declaring the beginning of a certámen.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> For the duration of certámen after it has been successfully cast, a thaumaturge inside the circle who performs a Blood Surge to empower magic may add their Occult rating instead of the typical Blood Surge value if it is higher. Certámen confers no other Blood Surge bonus for other purposes. Additionally, Thaumaturgy effects assume whatever cosmetic changes their caster desires, though truly awesome evocations may require that the sorcerer Blood Surges at the discretion of the Storyteller. Everybody benefits from effects of successful Mystic Sight power against spells cast in the certámen, giving them a two-dice bonus to dodge or unweave effects. This also applies to spectators as magic is fully visualised by the combatants.</p>\n<p>During the certámen, time to cast rituals is narrowed down into a single turn like an ordinary spell.</p>\n<p>If a living Hermetic magus engages in certámen with a Tremere, they may spend as many points of Quintessence per turn as their Foundation Background (see Dark Ages: Mage, our 5th edition homebrew at least) indicates or their Occult rating (whichever is higher) and may similarly roll to identify magic as it forms. All benefits of Certámen end as soon as either party steps outside of their respective circle.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"325"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.kfKhoauGutmUA1pV"},"ksy8WpKz2qBBaLMH":{"name":"Lifeblood (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Life is the Blood. Lifeblood is one of the most common Paths practiced by both the Clan of Tremere and the Banu Haqim. Indeed, in the modern nights it will become the first one to be taught to new students as an introductory Path. Life-blood Path is perhaps the most well-researched and most-well developed out of any Paths due to its focus on the vampire vitae which is, as the hermetic theory claims, extremely dense with the prima materia. At its basest form, it is the manipulation of blood in and out of creatures. Masters of this Path can awaken the vitae of vampires to create simple life forms, increase the strength of vitae and boil the blood of others.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Spells of this Path are resisted using Composure + Occult or Fortitude if the victim possesses it. Usage of this Path requires physical contact or for the sorcerer to make a hand gesture. The victim and those nearby can discern that they are being afflicted them if they can see the user while making an Intelligence + Occult vs Wits + Subterfuge roll.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialty:</strong> Anatomy, Blood Manipulation,</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects:</p>\n<p>Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Special Effects, costing its rank in experience points</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Manipulation of blood requires focus and great precision. On a Botch, the sorcerer’s own blood is accidentally riled and they must make a Rouse Check for every 1 roll. They also suffer a point of Superficial Health damage from exertion, increased to a point of Aggravated Health damage for mortal practitioners of Blood Magic like ghouls and revenants.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ksy8WpKz2qBBaLMH"},"ktFVUEJW0cRcNJZh":{"name":"Deflection of Wooden Doom","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An object is enchanted and placed next to the heart to grant the user protection against heart-piercing wooden stakes by reducing the attacker’s successes by 2. After a single unsuccessful attempt (assuming the attacker outmaneuvered the sorcerer to strike), the object is shattered as it takes the hit</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ktFVUEJW0cRcNJZh"},"kzG2GhiPjZnMFD98":{"name":"Enchanted Mirror","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>The mirror allows the usage of Scry the Soul power through it, with those who know of its functions rolling with Wits or Resolve + Occult. The crafter may use Sympathetic Connection Aspect to bind it to certain people so that only they may use it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.kzG2GhiPjZnMFD98"},"l0L2zzaSRrUE0bAP":{"name":"Awaken the Forest Giants","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets (individual statblock as targets), Range and Green Power</em></p>\n<p>Fully animate a tree or any other plant, allowing them to move. Plants are now able to dodge at half rounded up of their attack dice pool value</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"353"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.l0L2zzaSRrUE0bAP"},"l286ODUucZ6z49LT":{"name":"Draining the Well of Life","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Ghosts feed on emotions. While many ghosts are perfectly capable of feeding themselves, some necromancers prefer to keep them locked away from the outside world and have to feed themselves. Draining the Well of Life allows ghostly servants of the necromancer (or ghosts with who they had struck a bargain) to partake in the vampire’s feedings. As the vampire feeds on blood, the ghosts feed on the emotions generated by the kiss which are usually pleasurable (but the agony created by the Hecata/Giovanni and the Nagaraja kiss still create strong enough emotions of agony which work for ghosts too).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write or carve letters into a person’s skin and a feeding victim.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer first writes the names of their ghostly servants onto the skin of the victim (it can be done with a simple quill and ink, but some sadistic Giovanni prefer to literally carve the names into skin) and drains them with the ghosts standing nearby to absorb the emotions generated by the kiss.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, ghost retainers around the victim whose name was written onto the victim lower Pathos Degeneration equal to the Hunger drained from the victim.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"335"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.l286ODUucZ6z49LT"},"l2XLLTL7OwsIHvfH":{"name":"School: Mechanical Thaumaturgy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-thaumaturgy.png","system":{"description":"<p>Magical clockwork, cannons and superscience. In the world where magic slides from a mage’s tongue, and especially in the middle ages when most believe in powers of the magi and witches, it might seem unusual that a group of magicians would dedicate themselves to supernatural science. Mechanical Thaumaturgy has multiple origins, with the most prominent lines coming from two directions: the Craftmasons (a splinter group of wizards from the Order of Hermes that will later join the Order of Reason), who wanted to use magic to better the everyday life of men, and the Krechevski, revenants who took on the name Ducheski after abandoning their Tzimisce masters to join their craft and knowledge with that of the Tremere.</p>\n<p>Mechanical Thaumaturgy as a Blood Sorceryis similar enough in paradigm that groups who never heard of each other may practice identical rites and call upon “magic” in similar fashion, despite never meeting one another. It encompasses all forms of supernatural science (or simply science, depending on who you ask) practiced through Blood Magic and may even be taught to mortals in a reworked way.</p>\n<p>As the Ducheski line waned, so too did the popularity of this unique School of Blood Sorcery as the Tremere Pyramid saw no use in cultivating it. The sudden and rebellious schism with Hacktivist Thaumaturgy practitioners had more than one Tremere elder ask whether or not Hacktivists came up with everything themselves or Mechanics had something to do with it.</p>\n<p>A group of Mechanists that has existed independently of the Tremere and the Ducheski will remain in the modern era and begin cooperating with technomancers, eventually adopting name sof modern sciences for their Paths. Little do they know whom they serve, however, as they’ve been dragged into the war of the magi for dominion over the Consensus.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Melancholic</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquerade Threat:</strong> Low-medium. By itself, Mechanical Thaumaturgy and its clockworks and machinery seem quite mundane to an untrained eye. No few Mechanics have went out into the world to teach the common man science and ended up attracting too much attention from the Church authorities over machinery powered by blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> In the Dark Ages, it was relatively common among scientists embraced as vampires and the revenant line of Krechevski/Ducheski was its supporter. In the modern era, however, cabals of superscientists exist but are often either so excluded from common magical practice that they do not interact with other sorcerers and do not develop much or are unknowingly working for the mortal group of magi known as Technocracy.</p>\n<h3>Sciences of the Mechanists</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Craft, Science or Technology, depending on the sorcerer’s style.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Natural Sanctum (a place where instruments are supercharged with energy), Laboratory (Give Sanctum benefits to Laboratory Domain Background)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Instruments, Not Words: All forms of working magic require some sort of an instrument that is modified with supernatural runes. While this creates a dependency on magical items as without them the sorcerer cannot access magic, it also makes working magic easier for the supernatural scientist. Always negate a single 1 for the purposes of Botches.</p>\n<p>• Laboratories of Superscience: Supernatural science is strongest where the Mechanist can rely on tools for additional energy. If benefiting from a Sanctum, Spell and Ritual difficulties are lowered by one, to a minimum of 1.</p>\n<p>• Supernatural Science: Mechanical Thaumaturgy is based on scientific principles, or so its practitioners claim. Mechanical Thaumaturgy may be taught to mortals in the form of hedge magic Supernatural Sciences. Note that every group of blood sorcerer and mortal “scientists” each have their own interpretation of sciences and their own discoveries that might make it harder to work with each other and benefit from one another, but not impossible.</p>\n<p>• Alchemy (Chemistry)</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding (Psychological Manipulation)</p>\n<p>• Biothaumaturgy (Surgery)</p>\n<p>• Conjuration (Space and Gravity Research)</p>\n<p>• Conveyance (Teleportation Research)</p>\n<p>• Divination (Probability)</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces</p>\n<p>• Enchantment (Engineering)</p>\n<p>• Fear (Terror Psychology)</p>\n<p>• Focus (Way of Self)</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood (Vampiric Chemistry)</p>\n<p>• The Green Path (Botany)</p>\n<p>• Weather Control (Meteorology)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"243"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"C6vUwFyS":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"Sa9D3mG9":{"path":"skills.craft"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.l2XLLTL7OwsIHvfH"},"ldV3ksDf88Cau1Qw":{"name":"School: Fjölkynngi","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-fjolkynngi.png","system":{"description":"<p>Said to be a gift of Odin (the Aesir Odin or the vampire priest of Odin named Odin, lines blur), Fjölkynngi (meaning Witchcraft) is the magic common to Norse vampires. While it is a school, it is further split into two distinct variations: Galdr, verbal and fast, and Runeworking, slow but powerful.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Blood Resonance:</strong> Choleric for Galdr, Melancolic for Runeworking</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Masquerade threat:</strong> Low-high. Galdr magicians tend to attract attention, but runeworkers are more subtle.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Mostly practiced by the Einherjar, vampires native to Scandinavia who follow Odin who is a vampire priest of Odin or Odin himself (nobody is really sure).</p>\n<h3>Pratices of the Wise</h3>\n<p>By chants or runes with authority of the world, the sorcerers of Fjölkynngi perceive and influence the fabric of reality. The most common magic among the Wisemen and Wisewomen is the art of Divination. Women practicing it are called völva, vala, eiðkonur and vísendakona and men are called seiðmenn (or seiðmaðr).</p>\n<p>Mechanically, Fjölkynngi is composed of two Schools of Sorcery that share same Paths and are both in-Clan to those who may practice Fjölkynngi as their in-Clan Discipline. A teacher of Fjölkynngi (Galdr) may not teach Fjölkynngi (Runeworking) if they do not possess any dots or Paths in Fjölkynngi (Runeworking), however.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Glyphs of Power, Natural Sanctum, Shrine</p>\n<h3>Galdr</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Ancient Chants: All applications of Galdr require singing or audible chants in one form or another. Without that, the sorcerer receives two additional 1s towards a Botch. Difficulties on Spell and Ritual Tests are lowered by one to a minimum of one when a sorcerer is singing or chanting.</p>\n<h3>Runeworking</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Runes: A sorcerer requires runes prepared beforehand to evoke magic. In game terms, they are either literal runes carved on stone or embroided sigils on cloth. Runeworking treats all Spells as Rituals for the purposes of casting time. Whenever a sorcerer gains a dot in a Path of Runeworking, they gain three free rituals equal to or lower than their new rating instead of one. Runes are far safer magic than common incantations and the runeworking magician always negates a single 1 for Botches.</p>\n<p>• Precise Carvings: It takes time to create runes, and even more time to make even better runes. If a sorcerer spends additional 5 minutes carving a rune, Spell and Ritual Difficulties are lowered by one.</p>\n<p>• Word of Odin: Runes were said to have been given to mankind by Odin. Whether or not that is true is up to debate, but Runecraft responds to the runic alphabet and norse language far better than to other alphabets and languages. If a sorcerer is not drawing or carving runes in Old Norse language, their magic loses on potency and the sorcerer counts ranks in their Aspects as lower by one.</p>\n<p>If the sorcerer is using the Old Norse language without being fluid in it (usually requiring a Linguistics specialty), they gain additional two 1s for Botches.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Fjölkynngi</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy p. XX</p>\n<p>• Battle</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Divination (Seidr)</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces</p>\n<p>• Enchantment P. XX.</p>\n<p>• Ephemera</p>\n<p>• Fear</p>\n<p>• Fortune</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood</p>\n<p>• Necromancy</p>\n<p>• Reanimation</p>\n<p>• Weather Control</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"234"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ldV3ksDf88Cau1Qw"},"lpOUzu3cncHo8eIe":{"name":"Flesh Golem","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer animates a flesh golem composed of at least 3 cadavers stiched together (Intelligence + Medicine Test Difficulty 4 required)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.lpOUzu3cncHo8eIe"},"lrGZQCGaXWDlNyKR":{"name":"Full Shift","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Shapeshifting Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration, Scale and Subject described below. If the sorcerer is transforming others, then Range is also an Aspect.</em></p>\n<p>Upon transformation, the subjects gain the Physical Attributes, senses, and native Skills and specialties associated with that creature and also gains that animal’s natural limits of communication, manipulation (most animals can carry one thing in their mouth), and so forth. If the user has Skills or Attributes at a higher rating and wishes so, they may carry those over but it may also affect appearance of the animal (a wolf with a greater muscle mass if the user has Strength 5, for example). In animal forms, the subjects do not suffer a Called Shot penalty to Bite Attacks but the bite becomes mundane like that of an animal. Subjects still calculate their Health as Stamina + 3 unless listed Health is higher.</p>\n<p>Replicating another human in this manner does not yield any Skills.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"351"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.lrGZQCGaXWDlNyKR"},"mC4xyQcGpjah8UID":{"name":"Binding, Summoning and Warding (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Almost without question the most dangerous Path, Summoning, Binding, and Warding is also one of the most commonly studied among all sorcerers. The ability to summon creatures, living or dead, physical or spiritual, to the sorcerer is a common feature of stories of magic… and of stories of hubris leading to those sorcerers falling. To succeed with this Path requires patience, research and skill, and many sorcerers are unwilling or unable to do all three.</p>\n<p>It is slightly incorrect to refer to Summoning, Binding, and Warding as a Path. It is actually roughly a dozen Paths bound together into four (or more) sets of three. When learned, the sorcerer must choose (or their teacher does) whether they are learning to summon and control Physical beings, Astral ones, Naturae, or Ghosts. The techniques for one may have similar appearance but require vastly different knowledge than the others, and these four specializations must all be studied independently.</p>\n<p>Another major difference between this Path and others is that it has Aspects that can truly be set to zero. A Ward does not require a Summoning Strength or Sympathetic Connection, for example, and an unwary and trusting sorcerer can Summon with neither Warding nor Binding, though the three tend to be learned and cast together (it is a good idea to ward one’s self while summoning an entity you’re also binding, all in a single ritual due to the necessary ritual casting time).</p>\n<p>Furthermore, there are no quick spells for this Path, all Summoning, Binding, and Warding magic is performed as rituals. Many sorcerers who focus on this Path have a place somewhere devoted to the rituals they perform, whether it is a library with a ritual circle (common for Astral), a quiet forest glade (for Naturae) or the location of a personally significant death (for ghosts).</p>\n<p>There is, in fact, a fifth specialisation (though some scholars consider it to be a further specialisation of Astral Summoning): Infernal. This Path is not strictly a Dark Path but knowledge of it in most Fellowships is forbidden. It is considered an effective warning sign for Infernalism, and allows the summoning of demons. Notably, books that teach the Binding aspect of this Path variant tend to be destroyed almost immediately after they come to the attention of a demon, though the rituals continue to find their way into the world, because demons are more than willing to screw each other over, and sell a sorcerer the binding rituals for their rivals, though the prices are high. A smart demon (and most of them are) usually make sure to teach the sorcerer rituals that exclude the ability to bind the demon.</p>\n<p>Recently, there have even been reports of variants of this Path focused on inanimate objects and virtual creations, allowing some magicians to ward their computers against viruses or summon their cars at need. At discussions of this Path, Abyss Mystics keep silent, hiding a secret specialisation that relates to Abyssal entities for themselves.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Whenever the sorcerer gains a dot in this Path, they may select a single type of a a ritual (summoning, binding or warding) and one specialisation (one type of Metaphysical Weight: Infernal, Abyssal, Physical, etc.) for that type of a ritual for free in stead of a free ritual. Otherwise, and a new type of a ritual and a specialisation each cost one experience point each. They are learned as lvl 1 rituals.</p>\n<p>Unless otherwise stated, targets resist this Path by rolling Composure + Resolve once the ritual is successfuly cast. Bound targets may roll Wits + Occult each sunset against initial successes of the sorcerer to break free without alerting the sorcerer, but many sorcerers regularly recast binding rituals.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> As a ritual</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled) Number of Targets, Metaphysical Weight, Sympathetic Connection (for Summoning and Binding only) and any combination of the following:</p>\n<h3>Binding Strength</h3>\n<p>These levels are cumulative, and it should be noted that the being can resist binding by spending a Willpower point, though the sorcerer may simply reroll to cast it again so long as the binding ritual performed has not been directly disturbed.</p>\n<h3>Exclusion</h3>\n<p>Exclusion allows the sorcerer to exclude certain subjects from their spells. This Aspect is identical to the Sympathetic Connection Aspect.</p>\n<h3>Warding</h3>\n<p>There are three types of wards: personal (The effected creature type loses one die per rank of Warding to all actions when trying to act against the sorcerer), Small Ward (up to a meter across) and a a Warding Circle (requires Warding rank 3, up to 3 meters radius). Small ward may be enlarged by up to one additional meter per additional Rouse Check and a Warding Circle by up to additional 3 meters of radius. These Rouse Checks cannot be rerolled. Some tattoo Small Wards onto themselves or stitch them into their clothing.</p>\n<p>All Wards use Metaphysical Weight to target who they effect (designated specific creatures or all possible creatures of a rank and below it). Sympathetic Connection is not relevant to wards so it may be placed at 0.</p>\n<p>If a creature against whom the Ward has been created touches a small ward or a warding circle, they roll Stamina + Resolve against the total number of successes. On a failure, they are repelled. A creature cannot be pushed by the sorcerer into touching the ward (such as by punching them with a warded sleeve or a ring).</p>\n<p>In addition to being repelled when touching a Small Ward or a Warding Circle, they take Superficial Health damage equal to the margins or twice the Warding aspect’s rank, whichever is lower. This this damage can be tinkered with by diverting Warding Aspect away from Damage: One to make damage unhalvable, two to make it Aggravated.</p>\n<p>Warding Circles also act as ways to contain supernatural phenomena associated with the creature designated at its creation. Each supernatural phenomena crossing into the Warding Circle compares its successes to that of the Ritual Test. If the phenomena was not created with as many successes as the Warding Circle, it is nullified and breaks against the Ward. Objects warded by the small Ward weaken the supernatural phenomena the same way if a power would directly affect them such as Conjuration.</p>\n<p>Warding Circles may be “inverted” so as to prevent creatures and their supernatural phenomena leaving the circle rather than entering, frequently used when one wishes to trap a demon after summoning it.</p>\n<p>While usually invisible to the naked eye, Wards may be detected by supernatural perceptions such as Sense the Unseen contesting against the successes of the ritual.</p>\n<p>If a targeted subject touches the ward, passes through the warding circle or wins a contest against one warded by a Personal Ward, the ward is destroyed. Wards may also be destroyed by destroying the object they are on which requires more than just damaging the floor a Warding Circle is on. For example, setting a wooden floor with a Warding Circle on fire and waiting for it all to burn to ash.</p>\n<h3>Summoning Strength</h3>\n<p>When summoning, the sorcerer designates a precise location (usually within a number of protective warding circles) or designates themselves as the intended target. When summoned, subjects must still arrive of their own power, they are not teleported to the summoner. But a vampire in hiding who is summoned must emerge, and spirits may have to find their way from an Umbral Realm or even the Deep Umbra in a few cases. For incorporeal beings, this doesn’t tend to take too long (as in, longer than a night) while demons summoned from Hell tend to quickly emerge from the designated location.</p>\n<p>Summoned targets can spend a point of Willpower to resist the summons for a turn or 20 minutes, whichever is longer, usually enough for them to settle their affairs in a hurry and journey towards the sorcerer. During this moment of “freedom” all of the subject’s non-tracker pools are halved and rounded up as their mind keeps forcing them to move.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The Path of Binding, Summoning, and Warding has no need for special Ritual Test failure or Botch costs. Instead, the consequences of failures and Botches are visited upon the magician by the being summoned, warded against or being bound. On a Botch, the being that would have been the subject of the summoning or binding ritual is instinctivelly alerted to the sorcerer’s location. The biggest source of these problems is that, on a Ritual Test failure or, worse, Botch, the magician often ends up under the mistaken impression they have succeeded.</p>\n<p>By specialising on certain Aspects of Binding, Summoning or Warding, the sorcerer is able to perform greater feats by narrowing down the ritual’s function.</p>\n<p>• The being cannot directly harm the sorcerer</p>\n<p>•• The being must answer a question truthfully</p>\n<p>••• The being must perform any one service within its power, though it can twist the intent</p>\n<p>•••• The being will follow the intention of the service</p>\n<p>••••• The being will perform a service or answer questions posed by someone else designated by the sorcerer</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"268"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.mC4xyQcGpjah8UID"},"mVi4NZcvCOHjz2hz":{"name":"Infusion of the Kalif","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>This ritual is important to all Sorcerers of Haqim, but its effects have been imperfectly duplicated by a number of blood magicians from a number of schools who abuse its ancient pedigree for a baser purpose: getting high. The ritual allows the blood magician to ritually infuse vitae into cannabis plants so that Cainites may imbibe of its processed leaves through a hookah or through feeding on those who’ve smoke the leaves and become intoxicated. Thought to be a proto-ritual to Viscera Garden, the Haqimites use these sacred plants to create a substance called kalif which they use to climb the Ladder of Heaven and commune with the very spirits which grant their power.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Dur-An-Ki</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist's blood, cannabis seeds, a pot or container filled with dirt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer first prepares the seeds by soaking them in one rouse check’s worth of blood for a night while in a Sanctum that benefits Dur- An-Ki. After this night, the ashipu then plants the seeds into the pot while watering them with another Rouse Check’s worth of vitae.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> n a successful ritual test (Difficulty 3), the plant will begin to rapidly grow into a kalif plant, taking a month to reach maturity. Once the plant has reached maturity, it requires one rouse check’s worth of vitae each additional month (including the month it reaches maturity) or it will rapidly wilt and die over the course of a single night. When fully grown, the plant will produce enough leaves monthly to be processed into one dose of kalif. A critical (or 3+ margins) on the ritual test will double the amount of kalif a plant can produce. As long as the plant is watered monthly with vitae, it will continue to produce kalif indefinitely, with some of the plants in Alamut said to be hundreds of years old.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"355"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.mVi4NZcvCOHjz2hz"},"maMpuiM803RA4360":{"name":"Speed the Seed’s Passing","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets (individual statblock as targets), Range and Green Power</em></p>\n<p>Cause a plant to quickly grow or decay, up to years equal to the successes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"353"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.maMpuiM803RA4360"},"mdTU57LmYglXDRH2":{"name":"Composite Abyssal Possession","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>(prerequisite:</p>\n<p>Medium Abyssal Possession) Identical to Flesh Golem from the Path of Bone (p. XX), requiring at least the same number of cadavers stitched together.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> Unless bought as a three-dot retainer, it becomes rogue.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"254"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.mdTU57LmYglXDRH2"},"mhjxiwCICGW69Usx":{"name":"Thaumaturgical Surgery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Biothaumaturgy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>By combining the arts of medicine and magic, the sorcerer is able to quicken regeneration and even heal what standard medicine cannot, including things like regrowing limbs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Healing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing except standard surgery tools or oils for those using more “natural” medicine.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The biothaumaturgist performs surgery, mending the subject’s wounds or reattaching limbs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the subject heals Health accoaccording to the Healing Aspect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch the victim suffers a number of Aggravated Health damage equal to the margin on the Botchinstead of being healed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"370"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.mhjxiwCICGW69Usx"},"n0qzlkPNJd6thS4n":{"name":"Blood Creature","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>With this spell, the vampire creates a small intelligent servant out of their own blood that they can use to spy.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Caster’s Blood</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster spills their blood and wills life to form out of it. Or at the very least a semblence of it that takes on the form of a small animal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The vampire cuts themselves and drips a bit of blood that forms into a shape of a small animal the size ranging from a shoe to a mobile phone (rat, spider, snake, pigeon). The animal obeys vampire’s orders and the two can telepathically communicate if they have a line of sight. Once the Duration Aspect expires, the animal dissolves.</p>\n<p>The blood-formed animal cannot fly, but can surprisingly jump high and squise through anything that isn’t hermetically sealed. It possessess two points of Health, Dexterity 4, Manipulation 2 Stealth 5, Athletics 5, Intimidation 4. Any other roll is made with two dice. Any attack dealt by this creature deals no damage as it is not really solid. The creature has intelligence of a human being, able to understand complex orders, solve puzles and report back to the vampire.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"324"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.n0qzlkPNJd6thS4n"},"nC535Ryg1Gk4lXpi":{"name":"Diviner’s Kit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A crystal ball, a special tarot deck, a mirror or something else that a practitioner of divination uses. Grants a two-dice bonus on all forms of predicting the future when used, like Premonition of Auspex, Eyes of Madness of Dementation and the Divination of Sorcery. If the owner regularly uses it, any form of divination against the owner suffers a penalty of two successes as their own subjective interpretations keep altering their future actions.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.nC535Ryg1Gk4lXpi"},"nQzXp8frOSoAL9kK":{"name":"Walk the Abyss","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyss Communion Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>While the Path of Abyssal Infusion is able to open a portal into the Abyss, Walk the Abyss is a much another for those willing to visit the Abyss and is frequently employed by the mystics to host their most secretive gatherings far from the prying eyes and ears of others. Some members of the Amici Noctis prefer this ritual to Into the Chasm of Abyssal Infusion when the abyssal sessions of the Amici are held as it allows them to attend even when it is daytime in their area, removing the need for logistics of planning when to begin the ritual and when to end it that Into the Chasm requires.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but a space devoid of light</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The mystic shuts themselves into a space devoid of light like a casket and drifts to torpor-like state, merging their consciousness with the infinite Abyss.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 6), the mystic’s spirit suffers a point of Willpower damage and leaves their body and emerges in the Abyss. While in the Abyss in this manner, the caster is made out of pure darkness like the entities that dwell in the Abyss but is otherwise fully corporeal. The character treats their Willpower tracker as Health and Health damage is given to their Willpower. If they ever run out of Willpower, the Abyss devours their being and their body remains in torpor for all time until destroyed.</p>\n<p>While in the Abyss, the user may concentrate and roll Wits + Occult (Difficulty 3). On a win, the user may project their senses through any shadow in the world that they can imagine as if using Shadow Perspective (which may be detected by supernatural senses as normal).</p>\n<p>To return to their body and awaken (unless it is daytime, at which point it is just normal daysleep), the mystic rolls Wits + Occult at a Difficulty of 3. A failure increases the Difficuly by 1 (maximum 7) with each roll beyond the first requiring a cooldown period of hour. Vampires who walk in the Abyss risk bringing a fragment of its alien malevolence back with them to the material world. After every sojourn, make a Willpower Test against a difficulty of trip’s duration in hours (maximum Difficulty 6). A failure means the vampire gains the effects of the Ingrained Flaw of Obtenebration Discipline whether or not they do or are able to possess it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the target suffers Aggravated Willpower damage equal to the margin and proceeds into the Abyss as normal. When returning, however, they return in torpor from which they must be awoken as normal.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"251"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.nQzXp8frOSoAL9kK"},"ndfLvr04qRY7VLgx":{"name":"Dancer’s Anklet","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A silver anklet adding two dice to rolls involving dance when worn and visible.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ndfLvr04qRY7VLgx"},"nfS7OnYqPkgh5VrJ":{"name":"Two Ducats","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Necromancers who wish to find out more about the Underworld without making the journey themselves often task their ghouls to make it for them. Using this Ritual, a necromancer “kills” a mortal before placing two coins on their eyes and sending the subject onward their journey into the Underworld. The subject can interact with the souls of the dead and travel elsewhere in the Underworld, while also retaining the power to speak to the vampire through their original body and describe what they are experiencing. The subject may not, however, physically interact with the Underworld and may peer across the Shroud normally per chart on (p. X).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (counted as higher by two), Number of Targets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A ceremonial dagger and two coins</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster first stabs their subject with a blade (Intelligence + Medicine DC 2 to stab them non-fatally, dealing 1 point of superficial damage). After that, the necromancer places their subject on the ground, on an altar on in a shallow grave and covers their eyes with 2 coins. The subject’s soul is expelled across the Shroud.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make the Necromancy Ritual roll after stabbing the subject. On a Win, the subject has been successfully expelled into the Shadowlands and may normally communicate with the caster from their own physical body. The subject sees everything like the wraiths in the Shadowlands and fall all intents and purposes counts as a ghost. The subject counts their Willpower rating as Health rating. If it falls to 0, their soul is consumed by the Oblivion.</p>\n<p>While in the Shadowlands, the subject may also try to peer over the Shroud normally. They are still vulnerable to necromantic attacks and other forms of non-physical attacks.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"347"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.nfS7OnYqPkgh5VrJ"},"nmejx6HJbJo4HD79":{"name":"The Eternal Band","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A small golden charm alerting the wearer when a single person it is attuned to is in danger. It must be reset each time by sprinkling it with wine.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.nmejx6HJbJo4HD79"},"nuX4diTboXmi1OGC":{"name":"Skin of the Chameoleon","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>After painting the skin of the Gargoyle with pigment, the Gargoyle gains the ability to change colour once per scene per Rouse Check activation, gaining a three-dice bonus to Stealth unless their colour pigments no longer allign with their surroundings, typically by moving away from a wall they blended in, etc.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"301"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.nuX4diTboXmi1OGC"},"oJRePtmffnFf5kM5":{"name":"The Green One (Blood Witchcraft)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The necromancer raises a dead body as a Green One.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"342"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.oJRePtmffnFf5kM5"},"oOmRqbMhW00iV6wr":{"name":"Rite of Introduction","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Chantries and other strongholds of the clan Tremere are always meant to be kept a secret, especially during the Dark Ages when the young clan was constantly under siege. This, however, created a problem that roaming Tremere would have a harder time finding where they are supposed to meet with the high-ranking members of the clan. To this end, the Rite of Introduction was created to allow any new Tremere to directly communicate with the local regent. This also enables the regents to hold conversations with other Tremere across the city, making this a very useful ritual for spying and news-bearing.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> This ritual is both useless and unknown outside of the Tremere Pyramid unless someone wants to spam call the regent.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The user brings the Blood to a boil and speaks a brief incantation while it boils.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The thaumaturge spills a bit of blood onto a surface and speaks to it directly, opening a channel of communication with the local regent. After the fall of the pyramid, this rituals establishes communication with who is considered the highest ranking Tremere in the area.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the ritualist sends an impression of their face, their name, and a short, one-sentence message to any master of a Foundation of the Chantry (see below) within the same city. The caster can choose to withhold any of these elements but not alter them. If no Foundation is within range, the caster is aware that their message was not received.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"322"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.oOmRqbMhW00iV6wr"},"oVpnpVMA2wgc7d0k":{"name":"Vanity Restored","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Requires one hair (eyelashes work) of a subject on whom this formula will be used. Once ingested, they begin to rapidly grow hair everywhere, even fixing balding. The hair can then be cut as normal. Vampires who undergo this process set their “default” hair to which they will return each daysleep to the one after the haircut.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.oVpnpVMA2wgc7d0k"},"obcyrQefvw7TAQKK":{"name":"Object Permanence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-conjuration.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conjuration Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Sorcerers naturally have an easier time summoning targets they have a strong connection to, such as a beloved pet or the ritual knife their mentor gifted them. With Object Permanence, the sorcerer forces a supernatural connection to objects for future summoning. This treats the target as “intimately-known to the sorcerer,” even if the sorcerer found it in a dumpster an hour ago.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (how long the familiarity stays)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer names the companions while they prepare the ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the ritual roll, the object is marked as familiar and intimate to the sorcerer for the purposes of Path of Conjuration. The sorcerer also does not need to exact location of the object to summon it, but a relative (like a neighbourhood).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The character attunes themselves too much to the object if they Botch. Their next Path of Conjuring roll will summon the item instead of whatever other item the sorcerer wishes to summon.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"275"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.obcyrQefvw7TAQKK"},"of3vr9smKGti1oi2":{"name":"Servitors of the Genius","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-koldunism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Genius Loci Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Known as Kupala’s Eyes and Ears among the Transylvanian kolduny, this ritual extends the koldun’s perceptions onto the more intelligent spirits of the land, the koldun may see as they do.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance (counted as higher by one), Duration, Number of Targets, Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Nothing but standard blood and will</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer pours the blood onto the ground, feeding the land and ending the ritual by pouring the activation Rouse Check’s blood onto the ground to seal their mastery over the spirits of the land</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> While in effect for the Duration (or until the koldun leaves the location of the Rite), the koldun is able to extend their senses spirits affected by this Rite, all at once like a spiritual hivemind. Animals are selected by Number of Targets and must be within the Distance upon activation. The koldun may select rough area of location or types of spirits where they wish to focus to narrow down the power.</p>\n<p>The koldun has no means of directly controlling the spirits or their actions through this. If the koldun has Binding, Summoning and Warding, they may perform the Binding Ritual without the Sympathetic Connection Aspect on the spirit whose senses they’re using and the spirit will hear the commands in a telepathic manner.</p>\n<p>The koldun may invoke any koldunic power they possess anywhere they can perceive, so long as the level of that power is equal to or less than dots in their Genius Loci rating.</p>\n<p>Those using supernatural perceptions (such as Sense the Unseen) may roll against the Rite successes to perceive that there is a supernatural will residing in the animal. Spirits may also attempt this roll, leading to awkward situations when the spirit is offended or hostile to the koldun. While spirits such such power are rare, it is possible for a spirit to deny the koldun access to their senses by rolling Composure + Resolve vs. Composure + Resolve of the koldun with the first to accumulate 3 margins winning.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"361"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.of3vr9smKGti1oi2"},"orXkKdpRaw9uCmWw":{"name":"Teddy Bear of Resting","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A stuffed animal guaranteeing restful sleep to anyone sleeping in the same bed as it. They recover an additional point of Superficial Willpower when the awake.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.orXkKdpRaw9uCmWw"},"owxzzTtGZsEzvDJZ":{"name":"Sword against the Restless Dead","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A sword for ghost-slaying. It has a core of liquid mercury and can harm ghosts on in the Shadowlands, damage to them as they were alive.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Translator’s Journal:</strong> A book that will</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.owxzzTtGZsEzvDJZ"},"p37ONO7o6sE5Pjj5":{"name":"Haunting (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-haunting.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>As the Maeghar fell farther away from the world and into darkness, their powers of illusion and trickery were warped by the shadows of Oblivion. Slowly they kindled within themselves the dark spark that would grow into the Path of Haunting.</p>\n<p>Other necromancers may also learn Path of Haunting and, indeed, the Cappadocian Bloodline of Giovanni has created it on its own, but it will slowly fall out of use once the Giovanni ascend into a Clan due ot its relative “soft”</p>\n<p>power.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>This Path only works in the Skinlands (the world of the living) and the Underworld as it works by manipulating the ephemeral nature of Entropy. On a win of the Spell Test, the illusion has been successfully created out of pure energy of the Underworld according to the Aspects. These Illusions are fully material to those in the Underworld, making weapons made by this Path a potent weapon against the restless dead.</p>\n<p>An illusion cannot be made where the sorcerer cannot sense unless using a Sympathetic Connection through a ritual to reach a target or a place far away. They cannot create a purely auditory illusion somewhere they can’t hear it, or the image of a crown if they are blinded. However, if they can sense the illusion in any fashion, they can create the other sensory components of it. That is to say, if they have certain ranks of Aspects, they can create an illusory bowl of stew and it will taste right, even if it were across the room from them. They could create that crown if it were to rest on their head, so that they could feel it.</p>\n<p>When appropriate, Realism may add its rank as a dice bonus to Intimidation of those who have not perceived the creations of the caster to be illusions. With Realism 4, the user may provoke a Frenzy in vampires and other creatures with the Beast, usually Fear Frenzy, at a -1 Difficulty. With Realism 5, the Difficulty of the Frenzy Tests is normal. This usually requires at least the sense of sight. Fire might demand smell in certain situations, or even sound.</p>\n<p>Those with supernatural senses such as Sense the Unseen or the ability to see past the Shroud may roll against the spells and rituals of this Path to reveal them to be but an illusion. Once revealed, they may tell it to others who may attempt to roll against the successes on the Spell/Ritual Test with Wits or Resolve + Awareness.</p>\n<p>Anyone who makes this roll can tell that what they are perceiving isn’t real, though they perceive both it and the truth. They also perceive that this is no mere illusion, but something made out of energies of death and the Underworld if they have ever gazed at the Lands of the Dead. An illusion cannot provoke a Frenzy against one who has disbelieved it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Range (as a radius of how big a single casting of an illusion may be; melee is something that fits in a plam), Senses and the following two:</p>\n<h3>Realism</h3>\n<p>All Rituals of Illusion may be learned as Haunting Rituals. In addition to mere trickery, Haunting Path is able to affect the spiritual by harnessing the Entropic energies and surrounding others with them.</p>\n<p>• Completely immobile and fixed; if visual, flat</p>\n<p>•• Changes with perspective so the viewer moving doesn’t disrupt it immediately</p>\n<p>••• Illusions can have moving parts, but only large ones</p>\n<p>•••• Smaller motions and variations are possible, but there are still subtle tells</p>\n<p>••••• Fully immersive, this level completely fools all targeted senses</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"373"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.p37ONO7o6sE5Pjj5"},"p53tPctyvYUxcPHD":{"name":"Quick Cure","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>A cure for the common cold or flu, at least for 24 hours. It can also halve the recovery time for the illness.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.p53tPctyvYUxcPHD"},"pBo7dSEMMHuElyy4":{"name":"Alter Temperature (Major)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>The local temperature can be changed by up to 20 degrees Fahrenheit or 10 degrees Celsius.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pBo7dSEMMHuElyy4"},"pDZnPa0ccolTLJr0":{"name":"Fire’s Wheal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-elemental-forces.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Elemental Forces Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Fire is one of the most dangerous hazards, capable of destroying even the almost-indestructible supernatural creatures With this ritual, the magician can give protection against fire of all sorts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets, Protection</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write or sew in glyphs of fire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer inscribes sigils of fire protection onto clothing or skin or their targets.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a successful ritual roll, the targets are protected from fire damage equal to double the protection Aspect (which cannot stack). Once Protection is spent, the subject burns as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the ritual appears to have gone well, until damage occurs.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"286"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pDZnPa0ccolTLJr0"},"pIa0ojD6OKPc06So":{"name":"Projectile Magnet","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A pocket flask that attracts bullets and arrows when carried in a breast pocket. It can only take one impact, but it subtracts three points of damage before halving before being destroyed. This enchantment is very popular among soldiers heading onto a battlefield and may have originated during the Crusades.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pIa0ojD6OKPc06So"},"pIg5kbzblpna89RQ":{"name":"School: Blood Witchcraft","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p>Practiced by the Bahari, worshippers of Lilith who revere her as the mother of monsters and vampires, Blood Witchcraft is relatively common for those who know where to look. It is practiced in the fields, in forests, mountains and parks claimed as Bahari gardens — places where covens of Bahari witches meet.</p>\n<p>Where Blood Witchcraft comes from is difficult to answer. Lilins claim it is the oldest form of magic, taught to Caine by Lilith herself and that Dur-An- Ki is but an appropriation of it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Resonance:</strong> Sanguine</p>\n<h3>Practices of the Priestesses</h3>\n<p>Witches tend to teach sorcery in a coven manner with the Mother (leader of the local Bahari garden) taking on the role of the teacher. This form of sorcery teaches appeasement of the spirits and respect for the natural balance of life and death.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Casting Roll:</strong> Intelligence + Occult</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sancta:</strong> Glyphs of Power, Natural Sanctum, Shrine, Spirit Presence, Weak Veil</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Motifs:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Coven Magic: Witches tend to work magic in covens. When performing a ritual or a spell with others, be they trained blood sorcerers or the Acolyte Background, negate a single 1 for calculating Botches.</p>\n<p>• Magic of the Garden: Bahari magics attune to the Garden of the witches. When practicing magic outside of a Sanctum or the Domain of the coven, add a single 1 to the result for Botches.</p>\n<p>• Pain of the Mother: The Bahari are taught that both physical and mental pain elevates a person by stripping off what’s weak. By self-inflicting a point of Health or Willpower damage, the user lowers their Spell and Ritual Difficulty Tests by one.</p>\n<h3>Paths of Blood Witchcraft</h3>\n<p>• Alchemy p. XX</p>\n<p>• Binding, Summoning, Warding</p>\n<p>• Elemental Forces (Wrath of Nature)</p>\n<p>• Enchantment P. XX.</p>\n<p>• Conjuration</p>\n<p>• Conveyance (Witch’s Broom)</p>\n<p>• Corruption (Revelations of Eden or the False Heart) p. XX</p>\n<p>• Divination</p>\n<p>• Ephemera Ephemera of Blood Witchcraft has access to both the Metaphysical Weight (Naturae) and Metaphysical Weight (Ghost)</p>\n<p>• Fear (Lilith’s Suffering)</p>\n<p>• Fortune</p>\n<p>• Healing</p>\n<p>• Lifeblood (Blood of Lilith)</p>\n<p>• Necromancy</p>\n<p>• Reanimation</p>\n<p>• Shapeshifting</p>\n<p>• The Green Path (Path of the Garden)</p>\n<p>• Mastery Over the Mortal Shell (Witch’s Curse)</p>\n<p>• Weather Control</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"230"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pIg5kbzblpna89RQ"},"pLeoFVGlNlipSIeg":{"name":"Mastery of the Undead Form","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corpse-in-the-monster.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corpse in the Monster Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Corpse State</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer learns complete control over their undead body. They are complely immune to dice penalties of Physical Impairments, Crippling Injuries short of physical mutilation and removal of limbs, staking and no mystical or mundane penalty may hinder their Physical dice pools. Cannot be used with Benedictio Mortis or Benedictio Vitae.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"373"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pLeoFVGlNlipSIeg"},"pUdaRvhZVgrE4aXN":{"name":"All-Cleaner","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A handkerchief which can clean any mundane spill or stain, including blood.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pUdaRvhZVgrE4aXN"},"paOEkMB5UEBAstSz":{"name":"Seizure","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Mastery of the Mortal Shell Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following.</em></p>\n<p>The victim experiences a seizure.</p>\n<p>They are unable to make any action and count as a stationary target in combat until the seizure ends. Additionally, they take a point of Superficial Health damage each turn increased to a point of Aggravated Health damage if the target requires air to live. Vampires and spirits inhabiting bodies are immune to seizure. However, vampires (and by extension ghouls and revenants) are unable to perform Rouse Checks as long as Seizure lasts. A victim may retake the resistance roll each turn until they succeed on the roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"330"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.paOEkMB5UEBAstSz"},"pejbtAO2NaRMVd6L":{"name":"Purgation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Special Effect (••). Aspects: Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects</em></p>\n<p>Victim throws up a number of Hunger’s worth of blood equal to the Hunger Aspect or the margins, whichever is lower. Hunger is vomited one point per turn. While vomiting, victims halve their perception pools.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pejbtAO2NaRMVd6L"},"pfpcW8yTDmIVXeej":{"name":"Path of Skulls (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Collect the skulls of thine enemies and friends. Death never need signal an end to their utility.</p>\n<p>— Gentilman, the Plague Doctor of Sarum Cappadocians are often dismissed as apolitical; fit only for work in dungeons and plague pits. They possess none of the bloodborne talents allowing for manipulation and control of mortals. The expectation that a Graverobber would be more at home elbow-deep in a cadaver isn’t fanciful, but there exists a strata of the clan who enjoy remaining hidden, allowing their childer to wade through corpses while they play kingmaker. These Cappadocians call themselves Harbingers. Their order has existed for millennia. It would form the backbone of the clan, were its numbers not depleted in recent centuries. Rumor holds that the founder of the clan confined a large number of Harbingers to Hell itself.</p>\n<p>The Harbingers use Necromancy in an atypical fashion. Before the Long Night degenerated into the War of Princes, they created the Path of Skulls — a necromantic Path dedicated to the theft of identity and knowledge from the dead. From throne rooms to merchant caravans, necroman cers who channel the Path of Skulls exert mastery over the diplomatic scene and Jyhad. Cappadocians invidiously guard the Path of Skulls, largely due to the secrecy of the Harbinger agenda. Cappadocians who know the Path will occasionally mentor those who show a subtle political acumen. Knowing whether to assassinate a monarch, his queen, or their heir; perceiving whether to deliver, amend, or destroy a message from one court to another — such behaviors are observed by the Cappadocians who call themselves Harbingers, and rewarded by tutelage on the Path of Skulls</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>Modifiers:</p>\n<p>-1 Casting Difficulty when using the Path of skulls on a fresh head from a body killed no less than a week back +1 Number of 1s rolled for the purposes of Botch when using the Path of Skulls on a cranium that has already been used for the Path previously. This is cummulative and resets after a full Moon’s cycle.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following Special Effects:</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Upon a failure of the casting or the Botchthe skulls withers into nothing and the energies stored within lash out at the caster, dealing 1 + the amount of times the skull has been used for the path of skulls superficial willpower damage.</p>\n<p>•• Calvaria Loquax; The caster draws upon the memories locked within a skull and forces the bones to speak. On a succcessful Spell Test, the skull is animated to talk. The skull has self consciousness but lacks an actual soul as it is merely vestiges of the soul given voice. As such, it will not accept bargains as it knows it can not get anything nor is it phased by threats. The body speaks and doesn’t speak on whatever it wants according to what they would have in life, up to the moment of their death.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"375"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pfpcW8yTDmIVXeej"},"ppWuUt3kNHEl7Vft":{"name":"Restless Medias","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>From time to time, the ground beneath the village of Medias will shake and roil, tearing buildings from their moorings and sometimes even killing the unwary. The locals blame the gods, but the koldun know it is Kupala, restlessly stirring within his earthen tomb. Those with the greatest skill know how to rouse his agitations.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None, but the Spell uses Damage, Distance, Duration (one rank is one turn of effect)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the koldun is granted the power to use Restless Medias by making a Rouse Check and a Spell Test using the Aspects of Damage, Distance, Duration and Restless Medias (Aspect 5).</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the ground trembles as earthquake rages according to the Aspects. Damage and Duration as Aspects determine the strength of the earthquake and how much damage it causes. Those caught within the distance of the quake roll Strength + Athletics Test each turn to contest the Ritual Test. If they do not score as many successes as the koldun, the victims take Superficial Health damage equal to the margins or the Damage Aspect, whichever is lower. This damage cannot be made Aggravated.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"369"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ppWuUt3kNHEl7Vft"},"pwJZtlqDw2inWZVV":{"name":"Calvaria Emissa","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (•). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>Project your vision through a previously prepared skull in any location you've placed them, benefitting from only your sight and whatever related bonuses/disciplines one might have. When being used to look the skull will glow a faint light that can be identified with a DC 5 wits + awareness test that would indicate the skull to be anything more than a simple cranium.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"375"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pwJZtlqDw2inWZVV"},"pyVTG3NG05M7l9sm":{"name":"Mutate Vegetation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets (individual statblock as targets), Range and Green Power</em></p>\n<p>Mutate plants into forms that can be unnatural, turning them into greater plants or something new. The caster gains dots equal to the successes on the spell to increase the plant’s Attributes. Maximum Physical Attribute for them is 15 while Social and Mental Attributes are capped to 5. Social and Mental Attributes translate into praeternatural capabilities of the plant which now may communicate (with “body” gestures if not given some sort of way to produce a sound) and think for itself. Plants like this may even be used as guardians.</p>\n<p>Plants with dots of Dexterity may walk now. The plant dies after Duration, unable to sustain itself, unless the sorcerer spends a point of Willpower to bestow them with magical essence, allowing them to propagate the plant species further. Plants prove less then obedient and will not risk themselves for the caster after a story unless bought as a two-dot Retainer.</p>\n<p>Plants may be ghouled to improve their regeneration (they heal like a mortal) and dice pools through surges, but they can only use Celerity, Fortitue and Potence and Discipline droughs (including Thanatosis, leading to potential pleague-carrying plants).</p>\n<p>SIMPLE PLANT STATBLOCKS Small plants (large flowers, usually counts as a “patch” of small plants rather than individual)</p>\n<p>Health 1, Strength 2 Medium plants (bushes)</p>\n<p>Health 3, Strength 4 Large Plants (small trees, large bushes):</p>\n<p>Health 5, Strength 6 Great Plants (trees): Health 9, Strength 8 Every time a character raises their Path rating, they may choose one Special Effect to acquire for free of an equal or lower rank than their Path’s rating instead of a free ritual. The Special Effect must be known to your mentor/source as some effects are tied to certain traditions and approaches. You may acquire more Effects with experience points equal to the rank of the Special Effect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"353"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.pyVTG3NG05M7l9sm"},"qTsb5he1KSJWYpyR":{"name":"Foxfire","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Folktales speak of sorcerer’s hut emitting an eerie bright-green light from a specially crafted candle to attract spirits. This Ritual creates such a candle to empower spirits of the dead and act as a beacon in the Shadowlands that can be seen from many kilometers away.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A source of a flame, usually a firepit or a portable candle and foxfire shrooms</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster chants to spirits or deities while throwing bioluminescent shrooms into the flame which is not extinguished, but rather turns pale green</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the green fire shines brightly and may be seen from Distance in the Shadowlands, possibly attracting curious ghosts.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"335"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qTsb5he1KSJWYpyR"},"qXkYGd7N4cXBPhlz":{"name":"Haunting","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster binds a ghost within the location, either within a room they’re in or in the building for the rest of Duration. The ghost may not leave until they succeed on the resistance roll again against the initial successes of the caster. The Test may be retaken each sunset.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qXkYGd7N4cXBPhlz"},"qYnCcNJJgzj1QOvm":{"name":"Conjuration (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-conjuration.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Conjuration (more frequently called Movement of the Mind after the consolidation of the Tremere in the Camarilla) is about mastering space: moving objects or people through space with one’s own mind, being able to conjure them from afar or telekinetically use weapons and lift objects and people with it. Those Tremere preferring flashy powers of often end up depend on the Movement of Mind for the most basic of tasks.</p>\n<p>Mechanically, this Path is split into two applications, each with its own set of Aspects and system.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Conjuration, Telekinesis, Teleportation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> As this Path is composed of two primary spells, there is a set and system for each of them. The main difference is between application of Range to move objects close to the sorcerer and Distance to move them from far away, referred to as as summoning or conjuration.</p>\n<h3>Telekinesis System</h3>\n<p>The sorcerer moves an object or a person that is within their line of sight with their mind.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full. Prolonged effects require full actions as long as the effect lasts.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Range (counted as higher by one), Number of Targets and Weight.</p>\n<p>The sorcerer may also choose to buy the following effects by increasing the Difficulty on the Spell Test:</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Losing control can have a myriad of consequences. A person grappled is simply released while a levitated person falls and suffers a point of Superficial Health damage. Often an object hits the sorcerer by mistake, injuring them with one point of Superficial Health damage per margin on the Contest roll.</p>\n<h3>Conjuration System</h3>\n<p>To conjure something, the sorcerer must know of the object or the person’s exact location or have them within their line of sight (mystical sight works as well). Unwilling targets may resist using Wits + Resolve. On a successful Spell Test, the object or a creature is successfully conjured almost instantly next to the sorcerer (wherever they want in a radius of 5 meters) or in the sorcerer’s hand. Objects and people can only be conjured where the sorcerer has sight. One cannot conjure a bomb inside another person</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Number of Targets, and Weight. Conjuration notably doesn’t use Speed. The summoned target appears instantaneously on the successful completion of a spell or ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Modifiers:</strong> </p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, a sorcerer might send an object to the wrong place, such as sending an item further out of reach or conjuring a prepared weapon directly into their own hand, taking its damage modifier as damage. Sometimes the sorcerer summons the wrong object entirely. Alternatively, a sorcerer might only conjure part of the intended item, and one only needs to imagine the horror of botching the Conjuring of a living being. In events like that, the creature suffers points of Aggravated Health damage equal to the 1s rolled on Botch and the creature must roll for a Crippling Injury.</p>\n<p>• 1 to be able to use telekinesis on one person or an object, including to clumsily attack without touching the weapon (–2 dice penalty to attack per weapon/attack). Using a weapon in this manner requires the roll of the user’s casting Attribute + Melee or Aim.</p>\n<p>• 2 to attack as above but without penalty, or to give fine motor control to any object conjured, such as using a set of lock picks from a distance.</p>\n<p>• 1 for each additional two turns the sorcerer wishes to maintain the effect such as telekinetically using a sword (the sorcerer still attacks using their Attribute + Melee or Aim, but there is no need for a new Spell Test or activation cost again). The sorcerer can’t drastically change the effect, such as telekinetically wielding a sword to strike someone after tossing it away, without rolling Spell Test again.</p>\n<p>Not all usages are described below as telekinesis is quite a versatile tool.</p>\n<p>• The targets can be thrown, resisting with Strength + Athletics against the spell test. They are pushed or pulled by one meter/yard for each margin on the roll +1, up to the maximum determined by the sorcerer in case they want to pull the target into melee range. If the sorcerer violently pushes or pulls them, the target takes a point of Superficial Health damage for each margin.</p>\n<p>• The targets can be grappled, resisting with Strength + Athletics each turn against the successes on the spell test.</p>\n<p>• Similar to above, targets (which can include the caster) can be lifted up and affectively fly moving at the running speed. Lifted targets cannot make any actions and the sorcerer must roll their casting roll as a dodge for each person under their grasp if they wish to keep them from harm. The sorcerer cannot lift themselves.</p>\n<p>• Objects can be used as weapons with a dice pool of Melee (for melee) or Aim (for throwing) + the sorcerer’s casting Attribute. Specialties apply. Roll only once, dealing damage according to the most common object used with its damage modifier + the damage modifier equal to the Number of Targets to represent the multitude of attacks.</p>\n<p>• The object(s) can be manipulated, usually at the speed of up to 50 km/h or 30 mph. Some tests like putting and turning a key in a keyhole might need a special roll.</p>\n<p>• -2 dice to use Conjuring on an object in the grasp of someone actively resisting.</p>\n<p>• -2 dice to attempt Conjuring to move a resisting target, reduced to -1 if they are within the line of sight.</p>\n<p>• 2 dice for an object well-known to the sorcerer (see also Object Permanence below).</p>\n<p>• -1 if the sorcerer is conjuring an item from memory, increased to -3 if they have barely ever interacted with it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"273"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qYnCcNJJgzj1QOvm"},"qZQL2hR96RrYelwD":{"name":"Taunt","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>Make the Spell Test against the opponent’s Wits + Resolve, on a win they take a -2 dice penalty to attacking anybody other than you. If there are multiple opponents it is a roll against the one with the highest pool to resist (players may always make their roll individually). Warcry and Taunt cannot be active at the same time.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qZQL2hR96RrYelwD"},"qfkakz6ciq7kArnx":{"name":"Blade of the Void","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>(prerequisite: Abyssal Blade) As Abyssal Blade, but the blade also avoids any protections from supernatural powers such as Fortitude. Natural resistances of vampires, demonic gifts and so on cannot be bypassed, however. Its Superficial damage is never halved. Those with at least 4 dots in Obtenebration cannot hold this weapon as their hands merely pass through it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> After a month and a night, the blade rapidly corrodes and becomes useless. The ritual may be repeated prior to the blade’s expiry, however.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"255"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qfkakz6ciq7kArnx"},"qk8Tvt4AtAmJkZmS":{"name":"Revealing the Crimson Trail","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The ritual allows a vampire to easily spot traces of spilled blood, no matter how insignificant or old.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A needle with an eye.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist ritually prepares the needle by stabbing themselves in one eye with it, and must then peer through its eye.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> If the ritual is successful, the vampire can clearly see any spilled blood, dried or not, as long as they (or somebody else) keep looking through the eye of the needle. Exceptionally old or minute traces can require a Resolve + Awareness or Investigation test to spot, as determined by the Storyteller. The needle reveals blood according to the Duration Aspect.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, ritual goes on but provides false information.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"321"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qk8Tvt4AtAmJkZmS"},"qlnaNgRbhEEo9DjW":{"name":"Better Bullets","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Bullets that increase the damage modifier by +1. Each success on the Enchantment roll creates one turn’s worth of bullets.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.qlnaNgRbhEEo9DjW"},"r0NYl4pbK1Q0xG3I":{"name":"Hawk Amulet","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A silver chain bearing a hawk’s eye medallion with a chrysoprase in that can, once per day, add 2 dice to a long-distance sight-based Awareness rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.r0NYl4pbK1Q0xG3I"},"r1sRPZPIfC8H5KQK":{"name":"Writ of Passage","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Binding, Summoning and Warding Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>A sorcerer probably does not want their guest to be shocked by just about every protective spell they set within their home, not to mention that the standard way of excluding people from effects of wards by using their biological material becomes useless pretty quickly to the Tremere as their chantries constantly welcome new apprentices. Writ of Passage is a ritual as old as this Path and it involves creation of a written “document” that allows a person to pass through wards of the sorcerer.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled), Number of Targets</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to write on and something to write with.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist writes down a permission and as many copies as there are Number of Targets. Once the ritual is done, they hand over copies of the permission while typically retaining the “original” copy for themselves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Win on the Ritual Test allows a target carrying the copy of the permission to pass unharmed through the wards the sorcerer designated at the creation of the permission (any and all of the currently active wards can be selected as well as the onse the sorcerer will make in the future).</p>\n<p>Should the original permission be destroyed, all copies of it will be mystically destroyed (clay tablets crack, paper sets on fire.) and permission reworked. A sorcerer cannot change permissions once the ritual is over except by revoking them through destroying the original permission.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> This ritual is far too simple to have unintended consequences.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"272"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.r1sRPZPIfC8H5KQK"},"rNubSzTL649nW7G8":{"name":"Unending Wealth","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A money pouch that never quite empties. So long as at least one quarter (or other medium denomination coin for other currencies) remains in it, up to four times a day it will contain about five dollars (or equivalent) in quarters. If ever used a fifth time in one day, the magic is lost forever. This translates into a dot of Resources that can never be lost, but is separate from standard Resources.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"293"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.rNubSzTL649nW7G8"},"rQrtIqyVmX7WPTrt":{"name":"Fetter Punishment (Giovanni)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>Any damage dealt to a Fetter is transferred to the ghost.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.rQrtIqyVmX7WPTrt"},"rYz6UYqXcAhl08PQ":{"name":"Vampire Repellant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Same as Beast Repellent, but against Cainites, ghouls and revenants. Uses garlic.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"266"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.rYz6UYqXcAhl08PQ"},"rpFFd1dC41YQA52x":{"name":"Blood Mastery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Having its roots in the days when the Tremere were a house of mortal magicians, Blood Mastery allows the thaumaturge to utilise sympathetic magic in order to gain advantage over their target by symbolically crushing their blood. This ritual alone is the reason why sires warn their childers not to go around handing over their blood to magicians they do not trust. The Banu Haqim scoff at this ritual, seeing it as a perversion of their intimate knowledge of blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A container and at least one Rouse Check of someone’s blood. Mortal equivalent of a Rouse Check would be blood gathered by dealing at least one point of Aggravated Health damage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The thaumaturge fills a container, often a small vial, with the target’s blood and then crushes it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win, the vial has been prepared. Once it is broken by the thaumaturge, the they instantly gain a die bonus to all rolls against the target for the remainder of the Duration. This bonus does not stack with similar sympathetic powers, but does with most other bonuses. Once the ritual is done, it must be discharged before it can be invoked again against the same subject.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"320"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.rpFFd1dC41YQA52x"},"rrCvI3Ec7l1WORit":{"name":"Subvert","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-corruption.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Corruption Special Effect (••). Aspects: Duration (count as higher by one, can be ended sooner by resistance or special conditions), Number of Targets, Range and the Special Effects described below. This form of casting requires either eye contact (Resolve + Intimidation vs the target’s Wits + Awareness) or for the sorcerer to make a certain hand gesture (detectable by Wits + Occult vs. the sorcerer’s Wits + Subterfuge).</em></p>\n<p>Target suffers a dice penalty equal to the rating of the sorcerer’s Path of Corruption to all actions (minimum pool of one die) not directly aimed at fulfilling their Desire or Ambition. Ends when Desire is completed or Ambition pursued.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"279"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.rrCvI3Ec7l1WORit"},"s4nE37Ck6UyantFA":{"name":"Future-Seeing Drugs","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>An enhanced form of LSD, weed or incense that lets the taker either see a vision of the future or discover connections between disparate elements within the story. These relationships always appear in symbolic form, making the visions difficult to interpret. Usage of this substance grants a two-dice bonus on appropriate Divination Path rolls (p. XX.). Note that this does not stack with other dice bonuses to Divination such as from Enchantment Path’s crystal balls and so on.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.s4nE37Ck6UyantFA"},"sKqejcEXp9lzBSCE":{"name":"The Green Path (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>The Green Path deals with the manipulation of plant matter of all sorts. Anything more complex than an algae bloom can theoretically be controlled through the appropriate application of this path. Ferns, roses, dandelions, and even ancient redwoods are all valid targets for this path’s powers, and living and dead plant matter are equally affected. While not as immediately impressive as some other more widely practiced paths, the Green Path (sometimes disparagingly referred to as “Botanical Mastery”) is as subtle and powerful as the natural world which it affects.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Herbalism, Plant Manipulation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets (individual statblock as targets), Range and Green Power Special Effects:</p>\n<h3>Green Power</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, plants decay instead of grow, grow instead of decay, attack the caster, refuse to obey, etc.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"353"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.sKqejcEXp9lzBSCE"},"sOp7jNgL2eN6CmZF":{"name":"Sobering Tonic","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>A tonic that instantly sobers the drinker up, no matter how drunk they are. The user does not suffer the negative effects of alcohol and drugs anymore.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.sOp7jNgL2eN6CmZF"},"sPUHm4eBgcpJK8Sg":{"name":"Strength Enhancer","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>A metabolic accelerator that raises Strength and Stamina by one each. Any mortal who takes it will be hungry during the time to fuel the change. Attributes can go above above 5.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.sPUHm4eBgcpJK8Sg"},"sVEuZHRq1xoYetmq":{"name":"Thaumaturgical Forensics","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Biothaumaturgy Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Much like modern forensics, the thaumaturge takes a sample of the target’s body and examines it carefully, yielding a wealth of information to the user that they may then exploit or use to diagnose and treat ailment.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Biological sample of the subject such as hair, blood, skin, nails…</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The biothaumaturgist observes the sample of a tissue under good lighting. Sometimes, testing blood to certain vampire bane-triggering agents aids the process.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The thaumaturge observes the sample, making their ritual roll. For every margin, the sorcerer can ask a single question and get a truthful answer from the storyteller. The information is limited to physical properties such as the subject’s Health (both in a mechanical and in a general sense), sex, clan, Blood Potency, traces of diablerie. The information gained is as old as the taking of the sample and it would not mystically reveal events that transpired after like diablerie.</p>\n<p>Note that when detecting a Clan or a Bloodline, the character must have already observed a sample of the said Clan or a Bloodline with the knowledge of the subject’s Clan in order to register the peculiarities.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the sorcerer acquires wrong or incomplete information.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"370"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.sVEuZHRq1xoYetmq"},"sXI6vAiuXHtHiY8x":{"name":"Werewolf Recovery","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The drinker heals two points of Superficial or one point of Aggravated Health damage per turn (at its start) for the rest of the story. This requires some werewolf fur and causes the user to sweat heavily and smell like an animal in a cage, giving +1 difficulty to all social rolls. Werewolves cannot benefit from this.</p>\n<p><strong>Malign Humours</strong></p>\n<p>Malign Humours are alchemical variants of Gargoyle Enchanting rituals (see p. XX) available only Thaumaturgical schools of Blood Sorcery. They create potent humours that can empower Gargoyles. Malign Humours have the same systems and powers they can replicate or create as Gargoyle Enchanting Rituals, but cannot be internalised. Like Gargoyle Enchanting Rituals, a formula may replicate any power as long as at least one Rouse Check’s worth of vitae from the one who knows the power was used with their Discipline rating being used for any comparisons and rolls.</p>\n<p>Each time they are ingested the Gargoyle rolls a d10 to determine a possible negative effect caused by the required equilibrium in sudden power surge:</p>\n<p>1 Hunger raised by one 2-3 One point of Superficial Health damage 4-5 One point of Superficial Willpower damage 6-7 One point of Aggravated Health damage 8-9: One point of Aggravated Willpower damage</p>\n<p>10: No effect</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"266"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.sXI6vAiuXHtHiY8x"},"saGc5q1YPZCbg2mV":{"name":"Treasured Ornaments","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Transylvania is the land of many tales, finding itself placed between the regions that have seen countless great deeds. Treasured Ornaments awakens the spirit of every ornament with a colorful past in the koldun’s haven. The spirits will not speak unless spoken to, and will be entirely fearful of the koldun. The purpose of this rite is primarily the creation of immobile sentries. They will – in simple terms – report what they’ve witnessed when questioned.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon completion of the rite, the koldun can converse with important ornaments in their haven. The effects are permanent, though leaving the spirits in situ tends to aggravate them over time. An ornament of importance is one with a history – perhaps a gifted weapon, or the cot of a deceased child. The rite is dismissed when an uninvited guest touches the ornaments.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"366"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.saGc5q1YPZCbg2mV"},"shO0O9MfKNwkqi4x":{"name":"Communal Vigour","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Based on sanguinary properties similar to the Vaulderie, this Ritual allows a Pack Priest to share their Blood Potency with their pack, while strengthening their own ability to keep those packmates in line.</p>\n<p>Rarity The knowledge of this ritual is held by the pack priests of the Sabbat and extracting it from the fanatical religious leaders of the Sabbat is nigh impossible, making this ritual unknown by other sects. In Dark Ages, some Tzimisce kolduny practiced it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> One fingernail of the Pack Priest</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster submerges the object in water and lets their Blood drip into the bowl, concentrating upon the last memory of their sire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The priest tears a nail from their finger and breaks it into pieces, one for each member of the Pack. They place their own portion of the torn nail in the Vaulderie chalice, while the other members place theirs under their tongue. If the Ritual is successful, the pieces of nail dissolve into the Vaulderie blood consumed.</p>\n<p>Note that duration expires regardless of the Duration Aspect once a vampire falls into daysleep or torpor.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As Path Spell.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"326"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.shO0O9MfKNwkqi4x"},"shcXALQ22jhWDysq":{"name":"Grave of the Marea Neagra","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The Scythians of old had their own name for the great sea around which they dwelt: axšaina, which translates to “unlit” to our ears. Their description is apt; sunlight fails to penetrate much beyond the surface and in the deepest portions, even air ceases to exist. It is, in many ways, the perfect sanctuary for a corpse, as attested to by the many ships and their crews preserved for eternity along its bottom, many of them put there by koldun who dwell beneath the waves.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None, but the Spell uses Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the koldun is granted the power to use Grave of the Marea Neagra by splashing their blood into a pool of body large enough to contain their volume, making a Rouse Check and performing a Spell Test using the Aspect of Duration and two-dot Grave of Marea Neagra Aspect. In the Black Sea, the spell has no requirement of a blood or Rouse Check.</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the koldun may submerge themselves into the water. They cannot be harmed by sunlight, their body is perfectly preserved for as long as they remain interred within, and they becomes extremely difficult to detect. To spot the koldun, a character must succeed on a Wits + Awareness Test at a Difficulty equal to the successes on the Spell Test.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"364"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.shcXALQ22jhWDysq"},"shvJG9N1RQJHfVn4":{"name":"Bloodwine","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>In addition to the Rouse Check activation cost, this formula mixes alcohol and one or more Hunger’s worth of blood which must be from the same creature. Bloodwine tastes similarly to normal alcohol even to vampires and slakes down a point of Hunger per dose as if normal blood. It has the original Resonance if the blood was fresh enough when the formula was made, the same Blood Potency as the one from whom vitae came, further affecting taste. A number of doses cannot exceed Hunger’s worth of blood used in the process. Note that slaking Hunger is still penalised by high Blood Potency as normal with bloodwine.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"262"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.shvJG9N1RQJHfVn4"},"t1PrXn2UIQK49CgI":{"name":"Evoke Hunger","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Special Effect (•). Aspects: Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects</em></p>\n<p>If the victim possesses Hunger tracker, they gain additional Hunger dice equal to the Hunger Aspect or the margins, whichever is lower. This does not affect actual Hunger nor can the victim go above the Hunger dice maximum of 5.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.t1PrXn2UIQK49CgI"},"tFJSBV0FlIqdWe23":{"name":"Rally","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range regain half rounded up path rating Superficial Willpower damage (only if said Willpower damage was sustained during the current scene). This may happen only once per scene and healing rate is point per turn.</p>\n<p>Hercules' strength: All of the targets in</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"268"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tFJSBV0FlIqdWe23"},"tJhkcpf6KdE6XrCJ":{"name":"Parting the Veil","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Said to have been first practiced at temples as sacrifices to the other side, Parting the Veil creates a bright blue flame in the Shadowlands that, when entered through the Skinlands, transports mortals to the other side. This Ritual is a must-have for those requiring food while traversing the Shadowlands and without it the Harbingers of Skulls that are trapped in the Underworld would have had to resort to diablerie and starvation.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Passengers</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Drums made from human skin, a personal item of the target or some other Sympathetic Connection</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer starts a fire in the Skinlands and douses it with their own blood. After that, the Shroud will be reduced to Absent and any mortal who passes through these flames in a location equivalent to it in the Skinlands will be taken into the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Before making the Ritual roll, the caster must make a fire frenzy check against a Difficulty of 2 as they douse the flames with their own blood. Once doused, the flames will reignite with bright blue light.</p>\n<p>On a win, the flames burn for the rest of the night and any humans (includes vampires) passing through that location from the Skinlands are transported to the Shadowlands. Note that this ritual does not cover the return.</p>\n<p>For the duration of the flame, the Shroud is reduced to Absent in the area and no roll has to be made to perceive the Skinlands as they are clearly visible from the Shadowlands.</p>\n<p>The blue flame goes away after Duration expires or the Passengers worth of persons have passed through, whichever comes sooner.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the ritual succeeds but attracts a number of angry specters equal to 1s rolled.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"338"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tJhkcpf6KdE6XrCJ"},"tKM7KZ6kfpbSsSs5":{"name":"Tretchery of Pontos","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>In order to deterr enemies from their homeland, the kolduny of the Voivodate would conjure up armies and ships on the coasts of the Black Sea by manipulating water and how it allowed light to pass. On a lower scale, it can be used to conjure up divine visitations or cunning seduction leading to drowning.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the Rite, the sorcerer gains a number of dots into the Illusion Path up to their rating of the Black Sea Kraina. The koldun may only cast illusions over water large enough to accomodate them as the illusion may not hover above land. If anybody ever perceives the illusions to be a lie, this rite is nullified and must be performed again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"364"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tKM7KZ6kfpbSsSs5"},"tVQuEsGwNFkTpGhb":{"name":"Know the Unnatural Beast","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>By drinking wolf blood and blood of one who knows Sense the Beast, the Gargoyle gains Sense the Beast (Animalism 1) power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"301"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tVQuEsGwNFkTpGhb"},"tYoczjtWO0ewIq6l":{"name":"Shallow Slumber","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>By temporarily dimming the vitality of their Blood, this ceremony can shorten a vampire’s time spent in torpor. Some even prepare themselves with this Ritual, in anticipation of future misfortune.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (counted as double)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Charcoal and one Rouse Check of the user’s vitae (in addition to the activating one)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The performer carefully writes a series of sigils on themselves or another vampire, while cursing the names of various gods or saints responsible for good health.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> So long as the sigils remain on the target’s body, for each success in the margin on the Ritual, their Blood Potency counts as one lower for the purpose of torpor duration and premature awakening. Likewise, their Road Rating counts as a similar number of dots higher but only for purposes of torpor duration.</p>\n<p>A vampire can use this Ritual on themselves or another vampire, even if the subject has not yet fallen into torpor. The sigils stay on the subject’s body until they are washed away, marred by injury, or intentionally defaced, though rarely longer than a week due to natural abrasion. The Ritual expires sooner if the sigils are removed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Control Test:</strong> A Botchfailure on the Ritual test extends the torpor instead, as if the subject had another dot more in Blood Potence and a dot less in Road Rating. Only one attempt per subject per torpor can be made.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"346"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tYoczjtWO0ewIq6l"},"tamG5kuMd6lkaEAT":{"name":"Extingish Vitae","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Special Effect (••). Aspects: Range, Number of Targets, Hunger (see below) and the following special effects</em></p>\n<p>Increases the targets Hunger equal to the Hunger Aspect or margins, whichever is lower.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"319"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tamG5kuMd6lkaEAT"},"tdUoxIwRrOH5aIv6":{"name":"Bind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>Any ghost may be bound to the caster’s will. The necromancer makes their Spell Test against the ghost’s Composure + Resolve while speaking its True Name or holding its fetter. On a win, they can demand the ghost perform one simple task or answer one simple question to the best of their ability. For tasks, the Duration Aspect covers how long the ghost must attempt to accomplish it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tdUoxIwRrOH5aIv6"},"tkEFGRJN7YHMpXjb":{"name":"Wrap the Shroud","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster places themselves on both sides the the Shroud, allowing them to interact with both the world of the living, but also to be harmed by both. With see the Kingdoms, the user could, for example, identify ladders or stairs that exist only in the Shadowlands and climb them which would probably spook more than one person unable to see them.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"333"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.tkEFGRJN7YHMpXjb"},"u72jFbhmRZtZEXSt":{"name":"Corpse Suit","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>The more successes caster has on the crafting Test, the nicer the Corpse Suit will look. At the end of the Ritual, the vampire must convince the now vaguely sentient Corpse Suit that it will be their new closest and most treasured companion, making a test of Manipulation + Persuasion or Subterfuge (depending on sincerity) at Difficulty 2. The Storyteller need not here reveal whether the test has been successful. If the vampire fails to convince the suit, it waits for an opportunity to betray them. When the vampire dons the suit, they gain a Folkloric Bane and Folkloric Constriction flaw chosen by the Storyteller.</p>\n<p>A correctly functioning Corpse Suit attunes to subtle signs of danger and senses all types of curses, wards, and the presence of ill-willed monsters. Noticing when its maker is about to walk into danger, the suit makes their skin prickle as if stuck by many small needles — assuming they’re wearing it. In game terms, they can translate by 2 additional successes on appropriate Awareness and Occult Tests. The wearer may roll these as if they possess Sense the Beast (to detect danger, not types of supernatural creatures) and Sense the Unseen.</p>\n<p>The wearer can even make appropriate Awareness or Occult Tests to anticipate danger. A discontented suit occasionally times these warnings to put the wearer at risk (-2 succesess on the mentioned rolls and no special power effects). The suit never contains any money except what others place in it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.u72jFbhmRZtZEXSt"},"uImeoP49y8Qb7ghQ":{"name":"Bind to Mortal Form","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>Necromancers who have mastered this Ritual can extend a human life past its natural expiration date without making a ghoul out of the mortal. This Ritual doesn’t keep a mortal young, just alive, and they continue to age both physically and mentally.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Number of Targets, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> The chosen mortal, an iron chain retrieved from the Shadowlands (note that some ghosts can smuggle items to and from the Shadowlands), a candle made with human tallow, dirt from a fresh grave, a thimble of blood or other body fluids from the mortal</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The Ritual begins by lighting the candle, which sputters and casts a circle of warm light around the two. The mortal must then swallow the grave dirt. Using a torch or a similar high-power heat source, the vampire proceeds to heat up the chain, using it to brand the mortal. This sucks all the light out of the room, extinguishing the candle. It also traps the spirit of the mortal within their decaying flesh. The vampire then has three nights to deliver the blood or fluids gathered from the mortal to the same grave where they got the dirt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The necromancer makes a Ritual Test (Difficulty 5). Each rolled success adds a decade to the mortal’s natural lifespan, but if the test is not an overall win, or if the sorcerer fails to take the mortal’s blood to the grave within the deadline, the mortal ages at twice the speed they would have otherwise aged, as the shadows binding their spirit eat away at their life force. Bind to Mortal Form can be performed several times on the same mortal; however, the chain loses its power with use. The shadows binding the mortal’s lifeforce also grow stronger with each Rituals. Thus, the Difficulty for the Ritual Test increases by 1 with each subsequent casting. A mortal sustained by shades of the Shadowlands eventually grows withdrawn, confused, and fearful of the light. Note that while these shades that assist in the ritual are from the Lands of the Dead, they are not ghosts. Merely creatures formed by memory and sorrow.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"346"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.uImeoP49y8Qb7ghQ"},"ueUsYhP9EY8bfK8v":{"name":"Bind the Accusing Tongue","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Binding, Summoning and Warding Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The Tremere developed this ritual early in their ascent to power, which may help explain that ascent. Bind the Accusing Tongue prevents the target from speaking ill of the caster, ensuring the sorcerer’s unspeakable acts remain that way.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> Invented by the Tremere and kept a secret from the outsiders.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled), Sympathetic Connection</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A picture, effigy, or image of the target, a something to fulfil the Sympathetic Connection with, a black woolen cord.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster winds the cord around the hair and image while intoning the ritual charm.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Win on the Ritual Test prevents the target from saying, writing, or otherwise directly communicating anything negative or harmful – even, or rather especially, if it’s true — about the caster without a Composure + Resolve test (Difficulty equals the caster’s Thaumaturgy rating +2). On a critical win, the Difficulty of the test equals the caster’s Thaumaturgy rating +3. The ritual lasts until the target succeeds on their test, or until the cord unwinds after the Duration, at which point the hair and image crumble into dust. This is a binding ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, the victim can’t speak of the sorcerer at all.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"271"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ueUsYhP9EY8bfK8v"},"vQrbEcI5Tsuud4cf":{"name":"Sing o Muse","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>An instrument anyone can play like a master once they give a prayer to the nine Muses (by name). Those playing it count their Performance as 6.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"293"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.vQrbEcI5Tsuud4cf"},"vbUPeTpr7AyQfEWk":{"name":"Taste of Blood","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>By tasting a drop of blood, the sorcerer can discern certain basic traits of the one to whom it belongs.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The user dabs the blood on their tongue and makes their Ritual test. With a win, the following happens if the blood came from a creature: the sorcerer can determine the Resonance and intensity of the blood if human.</p>\n<p>They can also identify whether the blood belongs to a mortal, ghoul (revenants are ghouls), vampire, or other supernatural creature. With enough experience of tasting, the sorcerer learns to differentiate specific supernatural creature after by taste (like a werewolf, specific Clan or Bloodline of vampires, mage, True Faith user…). For example, a Tremere who knowingly tastes the vitae of a Baali will be able to recognise future Baali by tasting their blood.</p>\n<p>Tasting vitae determines the relative Blood Potency (within one) and Generation (within one) of the vampire if blood came from one. The blood also reveals whether the subject has ever committed diablerie.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, ritual goes on but provides false information.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"322"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.vbUPeTpr7AyQfEWk"},"vo7PWaj9vqH7G8m3":{"name":"Abyssal Infusion (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Considered to be the foundation of Abyssal magics, Abyss Infusion is the gateway through which the Abyss seeps into material reality. Abyss Mystics infuse themselves, others and objects with Abyssal energies, granting unique properties.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Unless stated otherwise, effects of Abyss Infusion are permanent. This Path has no Botches, only Sequelae (the consequences) and is entirely composed of Rituals. Each time a character gains a dot in this Path, they may acquire one free Gift of the Abyss Special Effect equal to their rating of the Path or lower. See p. XX for buying more Special Effects.</p>\n<p>Ritual Tests of this Path have a Difficulty equal to 1 + the rating of Gift of the Abyss.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> As a ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Abyss</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Only the following:</p>\n<h3>Gift of the Abzss</h3>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"252"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.vo7PWaj9vqH7G8m3"},"voZc2dQrovCA2mwL":{"name":"Subsume the Darkness","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>After meditating on Abyss, the mystic makes their Ritual Test. On a win, they may immediately spend a number of Rouse Checks to heal equal number of Aggravated Health damage or twice the amount of Superficial Damage. This ritual can only be performed once per night.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> After use of this ritual, the Mystic’s blood becomes black and absorbs all light, creating a dark aura around itself. This effect clearly marks the Mystic as something terrifyingly alien.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"254"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.voZc2dQrovCA2mwL"},"vtwofKwm0NyPQfur":{"name":"Borrowed Power","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-lifeblood.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Lifeblood Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>Nicknamed “Stolen Power” by Tzimisce kolduny, Borrowed Power allows a sorcerer to use the strength of another’s blood in order to enhance their own powers. This ritual is very expensive and it is said that Tremere neonates used to capture ancient Tzimisce and Gangrels to “farm” their blood for this ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> While easily learnable by most sorcerers, Tremere are the most avid practitioners of this ritual</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (how long the created potion remains potent; Count ranks as doubled).)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> At least a Hunger’s worth of fresh vitae</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The the sorcerers pours the Blood into a small closed vial, condensing it</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the ritual roll, as the caster chants, the blood is condensed and may be placed in a small glass vial or a hallow object. As many doses are made with this ritual as Hunger was used. When blood is consumed, a -3 Minor Action in combat, Hunger is slaked as normal andthe drinker acquires the Blood Potency of the Hunger they drank from it if it is rated higher than theirs. This Blood Potency may not be added upon by additional powers like Blood of Potency.</p>\n<p>The condensed liquid may be placed into a Calix Secrets/Principal Focus of Vitae Infusion by simply performing the ritual with the blood.</p>\n<p>Once a vampire enters torpor or daysleep, the effect ends. Non-vampires cannot use this ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> On a Botch, the concoction makes the imbiber puke without any benefits, forcing a Rouse Check for every 1 rolled.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"324"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.vtwofKwm0NyPQfur"},"vu7kkAj5bHlGwIKW":{"name":"Burebista’s Throne","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>Legend has it that King Burebista, who terrorized the Greeks for centuries — first as the mortal king of Dacia, and later as a mighty koldun — built a great throne atop a mountain peak from which he could survey all his lands and track the movements of all his enemies. The legend is true, though in time Burebista no longer needed the throne to observe his kingdom. He passed on this secret to his childer, and today every koldun of the Transylvanian kraina can sit upon his throne.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Distance, Range</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On successful completion of the Rite, the koldun can send their senses soaring high into the air, or extend them beyond their body; how far the koldun may shift the point from which their perceive depends on the Aspect of Distance. Radius within which everything is perceived is governed by Range Aspect. This effect lasts until the koldun pulls back their senses or Duration expires, whichever comes first. If atop any mountain in Carpathia, the Rite has no activation cost.</p>\n<p>A person with supernatural senses (like Sense the Unseen) against the Rite Test might reveal supernatural presence in the air.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"366"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.vu7kkAj5bHlGwIKW"},"vzB96AGiNBacyZzy":{"name":"Clinging of the Insect","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>The one who ingests this formula is able to cling to walls and ceiling for the rest of the night. They must cling to the surface with both hands and feet; they move at approximately half their usual rate. Formula requires a spider as an ingredient.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"265"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.vzB96AGiNBacyZzy"},"w07ZYKdBrCBj7ZDV":{"name":"Scientia Surrepta","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Special Effect (•). Aspects: Number of targets (skulls targeted), duration (the duration aspect is always counted as one higher) as well as one of the following</em></p>\n<p>on a successful skill test the caster chooses one of the victim's specialties, obtaining it as their own for the duration of the spell. This specialty can be benefitted from even if the caster has no dots in the associated skill and it doesn't count towards the specialty limit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"375"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.w07ZYKdBrCBj7ZDV"},"w3wHJSG1RRp0iuYh":{"name":"Twitch","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Special Effect (•). Aspects: Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following</em></p>\n<p>The necromancer forces movement of a corpse, usually a roll or a twitch. If it still has vocal cords, the body may be forced to speak. A rather useless gesture, but it has spooked many a mortician and terrified families at a funeral.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"341"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.w3wHJSG1RRp0iuYh"},"wCqBASLnddhsmsRL":{"name":"Kupala’s Exhalation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>In the Berca basin, near the Eastern Orthodox monastery of Ratesti, vegetation ceases to grow and the strange gasses of the underworld bubble up into our world, forming tiny cones of mud that quickly harden while they exhale their frigid breaths. Some koldun have learned to summon these deadly gasses for themselves; they claim it’s the breath of Kupala himself. They may be right.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Upon successful completion of the rite, the sorcerer is able to activate Kupala’s Exhalation. by making a Rouse Check and performing a Spell Test with Aspects: Damage, Duration, Number of Targets, Range and Kupala’ Exhalation (Aspect 2).</p>\n<p>On a successful Spell Test, the koldun can cause the gasses beneath the ground to erupt upward, forming a tiny volcanic crater. These gasses are bitingly cold; anyone caught must roll Stamina + Athletics or suffer Superficial Health damage equal to the Damage Aspect or the margins, whichever is lower. Due to the cold, those who fail the test have their Dexterity pools lowered by two like with Pietrosu’s Hospitality.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"368"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.wCqBASLnddhsmsRL"},"wJEozRqFlvpbUCQ0":{"name":"Harvest Servant","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>The Green Path Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Through the use of a viscera garden's properties, the sorcerer is able to imbue false life into bodies made of roots and cellulose, creating little furtive helpers capable of handling small tasks and chores.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Dur-An-Ki</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood, cannabis seeds, a pot or container filled with dirt.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> A viscera garden or viscera forest, an animal heart and brain, seeds, spores or root cuttings of the desired vegetable.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The caster makes the ritual roll upon planting the clippings and viscera. On a failure the ground will rot and grow infertile, requiring the recasting of viscera garden in order to continue using it. On a success a replica of the vegetable chosen will rapidly grow above or underground depending on the specimen chosen. Upon the end of the week if the caster has taken good care of the plant, then it will sprot limbs and eyes before escaping the ground. It has the intelligence of a child and will be affectionate towards the first being it sees when it awakens, following any order it may be given to the letter. The servant is mute and cannot communicate beyond vague gestures and body language The resulting spawn will live according to the Duration rank, at the end of which it will seek the ground it spawned from and bury itself back into it before the end of the night. Should it be unsuccessful it will simply rot and die like a stale vegetable, if it succeeds the caster will be able to repeat the rouse check and ritual roll to regenerate the same creature a week later. After a story, the harvested servants develop too much personality to be obedient and should be purchased as Retainers. You may purchase the harvested servant as a Retainer: 1 dot should suffice for 5 of them.</p>\n<p>HARVESTED SERVANT Eating a harvest servant will give the same influence to mental discipline as a viscera garden produce. Eating them as a kindred slakes a single hunger and inflicts one stain.</p>\n<p>They are up to 1 foot/30 cm tall, but the sorcerer can clip the vegetable as it grows to control the size down to half of its size. It can carry something up to the weight of a small tool (a pickaxe, a small cauldron etc.)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Attributes:</strong> Physical 3, Social 4, Mental 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Attributes:</strong> Health 4, Willpower 1</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Skills:</strong> Stealth 3 (Specialty: Hiding), Survival 5 (Specialty: Foraging), Intimidation 5, Animal Ken 3, Awareness 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Perfect Facsimile:</strong> The harvest servant may retract its limbs and close its eyes, standing perfectly still and looking no different than a good example of the vegetable that it imitates. It is impossible to tell it apart from a normal piece of produce without powers that are able to sense creatures (Scry the Soul) which require a test at a Difficulty of 2.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Helpful Aide:</strong> The harvest servant is always there when the caster or assigned charge require it, providing surprisingly helpful aid with any kind of physical task. the harvest servant is able to provide teamwork on any Science, Survival and Craft rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"354"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.wJEozRqFlvpbUCQ0"},"wYOksiAqn35ZPlzH":{"name":"Obsidian Mirror","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A polished mirror that extends the user’s perceptions. All rolls of scrying and powers like those of Auspex (e.g. Sense the Unseen, Scry the Soul, Clairvoyance) or some of Dementation (e.g. Eyes of Madness) that can be performed using the mirror gain a two-dice bonus.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"291"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.wYOksiAqn35ZPlzH"},"wcK68I5YgGN95Pna":{"name":"Ritual of the Unearthed Fetter","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>A necromancer may track down the location of a specific fetter if they possess certain body parts of a wraith. For this reason, necromancers properly bury the bodies of their ghost servants and Erictho herself was famous for performing a funeral for a solder by which “no magus or necromancer may evoke them”.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Distance, Duration</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A fingerbone of a skeleton who turned into a ghost</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> By carefully crafting a compass using the wraith’s own fingerbone as a needle while chanting, they can prepare a compass of which the needle, that is the fingerbone, points at the closest fetter of the ghost whose fingerbone composes the compass.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Make a Necromancy Ritual roll after you have crafted the compass (Intelligence + Craft against a DC 2). On a Win, the needle of the compass instantly begins pointing at the closest fetter of the wraith. If the person whose bone was used has not become a wraith, the needle does not move. Compass lasts until destroyed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"335"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.wcK68I5YgGN95Pna"},"wp0HtFgf96UI1qR7":{"name":"Megafauna Transformation","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Shapeshifting Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>The Path of Shapeshifting allows the caster to assume animal forms, including partial transformations. But in the end, the caster can normally only fully transform into a normal animal or a human. This ritual allows them to become a gigantic version of whatever animal they want to transform into. In most cases, this is a full throwback to an extinct version of the animal, such as the megatherium (giant sloth) of the Americas. This Ritual also bypasses the ban on creatures that do not exist in the world at the present time, meaning that dragons are a possibility in the modern era.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> As the Path spell</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something of the creature: a horn of a unicorn, a bone of a megatherium or a normal sloth, a dragon’s tooth.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer makes a pendant with the material from the creature and casts the spell. The pendant will not be there when the user transforms, but will be there when they shapeshift back. If one has consumed something of a mythical or extinct animal, the pendant is not required. One who has drank the milk of a unicorn or blood of a dragon may always shapechange into them with this RItual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the transformation occurs as with the Path Spell, including the Control Test.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"352"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.wp0HtFgf96UI1qR7"},"x7l7Jjp8ByG4J5q9":{"name":"Alter Temperature (Minor)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Alter the local temperature by up to 10 degrees Fahrenheit or 5 degrees Celsius.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.x7l7Jjp8ByG4J5q9"},"xAivL1iRupWaulkF":{"name":"Orpheic Sujourn","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Usually, necromancers can not bring back items from the Underworld which is why various necromantic bloodlines spending time in the Shadowlands have developed a Ritual that would allow them to do just that. Rumours say that a member of the Giovanni family is looking for an ancient text describing a Ritual to godhood through this Ritual as the physical copy in the land of the living was destroyed in a great fire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Weight</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Any item, however one should note that the mass of the returned item may not be larger than that of an item used as an ingredient.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The caster who wishes to bring something back from the lands of the dead must bring something into them. They choose an item to destroy before entering the Shadowlands (by means such as Ex Nihilo) as a gift to the Underworld. The necromancer then shatters the item by an incantation, which there after permanently appears across the Shroud and may not be removed except by use of this Ritual. In exchange, the necromancer may bring back items up to the mass of the item sacrificed. Items must hold some emotional significance to somebody and can not be just a piece of wood or a rock you found outside your house. It could, however, be a monument, tombstone, ring, a chest…</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> At the end of the incantation, the caster makes their Necromancy Ritual roll and on a Win the item crumbles to dust and appears on the other side of the Shroud. The next time the necromancer enters the Underworld, they may bring out items up to or equal to the mass of the object they sacrificed. For bringing in or back items of larger masses, assume that each success of the Ritual destroys one kilogram of the original sacrificial item/thing.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"339"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xAivL1iRupWaulkF"},"xESQTE900DM21yuk":{"name":"Temportal","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>As a master of their own Haven, the sorcerer may reshape it so that the doorways lead to rooms other than those they are physically connected to. For example, the door to a bedroom might suddenly open into the study room or a door that leads nowhere and is literally fixed onto the wall might lead to the dungeons or the tower. Prudent Tremere often have doors leading nowhere in a chantry for which they swap the doors that intruders are about to enter to attack them.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Dedicate the Haven (••••) ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Permanent due to Dedicate the Haven.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer marks all the portals (windows, gates, doors, closet doors, trapdoors, etc.) with a sigil of their own using their own blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual may be performed only in a Haven upon which the sorcerer’s Dedicate the Haven (level 4 ritual) is in effect. On a win, the sorcerer may spend a point of Willpower and roll Intelligence + Thaumaturgy against a difficulty of 3 and touch a portal (like door or a window) in their Haven that is closed. On a win, opening it will lead into another room (or through another window) of the Haven. The portal upon which this power is used can be smaller or larger than the original one that leads into the room.</p>\n<p>The ability to manipulate space is lost when the Dedicate the Haven’s effect is no longer and the rooms revert to their present state. Those with powers such as Mystic Sight may roll to realise that a portal has been magically tinkered with. This is a Ward ritual.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"298"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xESQTE900DM21yuk"},"xTZiYtIeoOx0bioR":{"name":"Jolt","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Healing Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer magically awakens a person who is sleeping, unconscious, or comatose.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Whatever the sorcerer uses to mend wounds and ease spirits.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> By potion, spell, sound or some other instrument of healing sorcerer uses, the subject is lulled deep into sleep.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test (Difficulty 3), the subject awakens from unconsciousness. This power doesn’t heal the underlying illness, injury, or poisoning, and the patient falls unconscious again at the end of the scene if the healer doesn’t resolve the underlying issue. Vampires and other undead cannot be awakened with this.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> See Path Spell Risk.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"316"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xTZiYtIeoOx0bioR"},"xbca07okZ3rieFkW":{"name":"Cursed Eucharist (Rosselini)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The caster may perform Bite Attacks on ghosts and physically harm them in that manner, dealing damage as they normally would. The caster may also feast on the ectoplasm of ghosts. Draining “Hunger’s” worth of ectoplasm” increases the ghost’s Pathos Degeneration by the same amount, up to 5.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xbca07okZ3rieFkW"},"xdpe2qbgtRrm17zu":{"name":"Strike True","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range ignore Called Shot penalties.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"267"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xdpe2qbgtRrm17zu"},"xm71JcuORFhqSYRb":{"name":"The Sorcery of Life","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-reanimation.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Reanimation Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>In Setite apocrypha, it is said Set’s favored servants were the immortal entities known as Bane Mummies. Set and his childer attempted a version of Osiris’ Spell of Life in attempts to create their own eternal servants, but the Spell went horribly wrong. Where Osiris’ children were given eternal life, purity, and wisdom; Set’s immortal servants were possessed only of impurities, regurgitated by the demon Ammut, and reborn as Bane Mummies. Set’s frustration was destructive. He blamed his childer, and destroyed every Setite of the Fourth Generation unlucky enough to be in his presence. Eventually, Set devoted adoration to the Bane Mummies, but ever resented the failures of his childer in their creation. Bane Mummies still exist, but the secret of their conception is something guarded within the temple sanctums of the Hierophants. Few wish to anger Set in such an egregious way as to create new Bane Mummies. The Witches of Echidna — certain of an impending doom — are not so apprehensive. Their studies reveal a different route to empowering their servants, and in concordance with Lazarus’ Cappadocians bequeath this research to the Egyptian Setites. With the Sorcery of Life, a form of Proto-Bane Mummy is twisted into life. The practitioners risk their founder’s displeasure creating of these servants, but measure the gamble as one worth taking.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>School:</strong> Akhu</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A remembered deed harvested by Weigh the Virtues (Necromancy ••) and a reanimated cadaver.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer must have taken steps necessary to Imbue with the Grace of Anubis (Reanimation ••••), and hold in their thrall an animated corpse bearing the fire of emotion-fueled deeds. After this, they feed the reanimated cadaver some of their blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win (Difficulty 6), the mummy’s Social and Mental Attributes and Skills rise to 3 if they weren’t already. The proto bane mummy receives a dot of Torment and a dot of Divinity and 5 free Arcana dots to spend on whatever it/the player wishes. The “proto bane mummy” is then remade as a Gifted Individual by the Storyteller or the player. If higher, Physical Attributes of the animated cadaver remain after recreation. One proto bane mummy counts as a 5-dot retainer and its owner may increase its Divinity by spending experience points.</p>\n<p>See p. XX for Proto Bane Mummy systems. Proto bane mummy may acquire Taints and form Pacts as normal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> As Path spells.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"350"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xm71JcuORFhqSYRb"},"xxdr2cGboYfa0RRf":{"name":"Waterspout","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>Creates a waterspout with a Strength rating equal to the total number of successes on the casting roll to harm ships, drag or pull people. Attacks deal Aggravated Health damage to mortals, Superficial to others.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"359"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xxdr2cGboYfa0RRf"},"xydB8My4Uh75DSqt":{"name":"Torc of Hercules","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (••••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A silver torc granting superhuman Strength. The wearer gains two dots in Strength, up to 7, when using it. When not worn, it must be kept exposed to the moon whenever the moon is visible. The wearer suffers a die penalty to all Fury Frenzy rolls or to resist being angered.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"292"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.xydB8My4Uh75DSqt"},"yEw3PH2vnePtdJao":{"name":"Medium Abyssal Possession","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/school-abyss-mysticism.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Abyssal Infusion Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Only</em></p>\n<p>(prerequisite: Minor Abyssal Possession) identical to Minor Abyssal Possession except that the corpse raised is an Aggressive Corpse (p. XX)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Sequela:</strong> When a corpse is raised in this manner, it is automatically hostile to everybody, but the sorcerer binds it to their will as necromancers do zombies if they possess a dot of Binding, Summoning and Warding Path. If not bought as a Retainer (one dot should suffice for 3 cadavers), the zombie becomes aggressive and uncontrollable after a story as the bond breaks.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"254"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yEw3PH2vnePtdJao"},"yFk2uz2vBvK9CV98":{"name":"Bastone del Diavolo","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>One of the most feared (by ghosts, at least) weapons at the disposal of the Giovanni and their families is the infamous “stick of the Devil”. It is made by extreme cruelty and powered by the necromancer’s very will to strike at ghosts physically as if they were fully corporeal.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Rarity:</strong> This is one of the rare rituals Giovanni refuse to teach outside the family as it allows them to maintain the ability to be ghost hunters for hire.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> A human and something to sever the bone with without shattering it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer first must sever the leg of a living mortal, clean the femur bone from flesh and dip it in boiling lead. Once the lead has cooled off, the necromancer inscribes the femur with a litany of curses. After that, only thing that remains is to beat the mortal to death with their own femur (Difficulty 3 on Intelligence + Medicine to keep the mortal alive up until that moment).</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Do not make the Ritual roll until the target has been beaten to death by their own femur. On a Win, the curses inscribed on the femur grow noticably darker.</p>\n<p>To activate the Bastone for a scene, the necromancer must spend a point of Willpower. It may be used as a +2 heavy impact weapon, requiring Strength +, Melee to wield and dealing Aggravated damage to ghosts and ghost-like entities. It may strike a ghost regardless of if they have taken a physical form and the damage inflicted upon ghosts is never halved. If the target is under possession the target takes damage as normal while the ghost’s own Health/ Corpus tracker is attacked at the same time as if they were not possessing a host, ignoring any physical armour the ghost may have acquired. This is technically a very potent form of exorcism as a necromancer can club the ghost, dealing more damage to it than to the person being possessed by the ghost.</p>\n<p>Ghosts naturally sense the Bastone, making them suspicious and hateful towards the one having such a tool near a wraith. The caster gains a two-dice penalty on all rolls involving non-aggressive interactions like summoning ghosts or verbally interacting with ghosts except for Intimidation rolls, the Compel Ritual and the like.</p>\n<p>A Bastone only works in the hands of a necromancer that made it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"337"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yFk2uz2vBvK9CV98"},"yFplve6gcnGXCERy":{"name":"Memory of the Sea","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-black-sea-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Black Sea Kraina Ritual (•).</em></p>\n<p>The Black Sea has seen many civilisations rise and fall, carefully collecting its ships and dead on its bottoms. The kolduny of the Black Sea have learned to share in that memory, allowing them to view past events through the eyes of water.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Time Period</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> The koldun drips blood into the water before beginning the Rite. On successful completion of the rite, the koldun can see into the past through the perspective of water where they dripped blood, going as far back as Time Period Aspect allows it. Observing happens at a rate of 60 seconds of history observed for 30 seconds the koldun spends pondering. The koldun is able to narrow down the time period they wish to observe in order not to waste time.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"363"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yFplve6gcnGXCERy"},"yKmuaXUYIaZYc0Fs":{"name":"Borne by Wind","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-transylvanian-kraina.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Transylvanian Kraina Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Transylvanian Mountains are always surrounded by howling winds as the spirits of air sing their songs. Koldun who dwell high in the mountains have learned to sing those songs as well, allowing them to create gusts of wind and, with some mastery, use it as an extension of their own body.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Once the Rite is completed, the koldun is capable flight. With one turn’s focus, they begin traveling at the speed of the wind. For precise values, google or consult the Beaufort Scale on Wikipedia.</p>\n<p>This Rite’s effect ends if the koldun is taken down from the air while flying or if they utilise the boon of flying to launch an attack, and miss their first blow.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"367"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yKmuaXUYIaZYc0Fs"},"yZV9S3IbOjKHIFFD":{"name":"Conveyance (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Whether dreaming of teleportation or flying broomsticks like infamous witches, people have always wanted to travel quickly from place to place. No matter how quickly they can manage it, it’s never fast enough, and many turn to magic and strange sciences to get where they’re going that much sooner. To hedge magicians, these feats may not be routine, but few of them worry about being booked to a middle seat on an airline (or a carriage, in the middle ages).</p>\n<p>Until they reach the pinnacles of Path mastery (level 5 Travel Speed Aspect), a sorcerer using the the Conveyance Path requires some sort of vehicle, which is prepared for a single journey by the magician. Mystics tend to favor seven-league boots, flying carpets and brooms, and stranger things like chariots drawn by divine cats, while the more scientific magicians use jet packs, transforming cars, and the like.</p>\n<p>Powerful wielders of this Path, however, can cover distance without crossing the intervening space. Teleportation need not be instantaneous, but it tends not to need a vehicle; usually a ritual or device at the point of origin will suffice to send the magician wherever they want to go. It can also allow for small strike teams to infiltrate places.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Flight, Telekinesis</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> In addition to the Aspects of Distance, Travel Speed, and Passengers, the following each increase or decrease the Difficulty by:</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Transporting one’s friends and Botching leads to a disaster. If teleportation was attempted and Botched, each traveler suffers a point of Aggravated Health damage per 1 rolled and must test for a Crippling Injury. Other usages are up to the storyteller like the “magical broom” ceasing to function mid-flight.</p>\n<p>• -1 for well-known locations.</p>\n<p>• 1 to travel to a location the magician has never been to.</p>\n<p>• 1 for each unwilling target.</p>\n<p>• 2 to teleport, even if not instantaneous (Travel Speed determines how long teleportation lasts as if moving there physically) This is required to attempt to use Conveyance offensively.</p>\n<p>• Each barrier crossed increases the Difficulty by one, even if the passengers are teleporting. Barriers must not be hermetically sealed.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> Note that this Path can’t penetrate hermetically sealed chambers. Teleporting others requires knowledge of their exact location. On a successful spell roll, the [Passengers] begin their travel determined by how the spell was cast.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"276"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yZV9S3IbOjKHIFFD"},"ycABtHmKYyh6zCYR":{"name":"Strike true","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Battle Special Effect (••••). Aspects: Number of Targets, Range (as a radius)</em></p>\n<p>All targets in range add 2 successes to a melee or brawl attack roll done per turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"268"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ycABtHmKYyh6zCYR"},"yf4VLI3UEbJY3fyq":{"name":"Shitstorm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-conjuration.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Conjuration Ritual (••).</em></p>\n<p>Sometimes, accuracy of thrown objects is not as required as simply creating a safe zone for the sorcerer and their allies. Shitstorm provides exactly that by creating a swirling shell of many small inanimate objects</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Area, Duration.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Hundreds of inanimate objects of small weight.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The sorcerer prepares the ritual and chants. This ritual is frequently “hanged” as a defensive measure, but the items must be around when the ritual is activated.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win on the ritual roll, the sorcerer surrounds themself and companions within [Area] of them with a swirling shell of small inanimate objects that lasts for [Duration] or until the sorcerer lets the spell lapse. This adds +1 difficulty to hit anyone covered by the effect with a ranged attack for every 2 successes on the ritual roll.</p>\n<p>Additionally, anyone attempting within [Area] such as those attempting close combat against those affected by the ritual must attempt to avoid the debris with Dexterity + Athletics against [activation successes]. If they fail, they may not proceed forward and take damage equal to the margin, usually superficial, unless the sorcerer was in a room filled with broken glass, small knives, or other sharp objects, in which case the damage is Aggravated to most mortals. The sorcerer doesn’t have to target any enemy in particular for this to occur. Anyone, friend or foe, who gets too close to the flying debris must roll to dodge it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> The shitstorm becomes quite literal when the sorcerer Botches as the whirlwind of items is thrown everywhere and damages without discrimination. Thankfully, such a whirlwind dissipates after a single turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"275"},"level":2,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yf4VLI3UEbJY3fyq"},"ykLejDVndeoEdGB5":{"name":"Stitch Intelligence","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-path-of-skulls.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Path of Skulls Ritual (•••••).</em></p>\n<p>With this ritual, a necromancer literally sitches intelligence onto an animated cadaver.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Secondary Paths:</strong> Reanimation 2</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> None</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> An animated cadaver and a skull “awkened” with Calvaria Loquax</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The necromancer stitches the skull or a severed head animated with Calvaria Loquax onto an already-reanimated cadaver, which usually have its head severed first (but a zombie with two heads is nontheless a possible sight in tombs of the Clan of Death)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> On a win of the Ritual Test, the cadaver gains Social and Mental Skills and Social and Mental Attributes that of the person whose head was reanimated and stitched onto the body. If that person became a ghost, use traits from the moment of death.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"377"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.ykLejDVndeoEdGB5"},"yrt6OgjioR0bo6nl":{"name":"Mastery of the Mortal Shell (Path)","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-mastery-of-the-mortal-shell.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Roll your School of Sorcery’s Casting Pool. The Spell Test Difficulty is the sum of all Aspect ranks, halved and rounded up.</em></p>\n<p>Many legends tell of witches and warlocks taking control of bodies of those trespassing into their domain. It saw most use by the Tremere in a time of crisis to combat the Gangrel and the Tzimisce attacking their chantries in the Dark Ages. Mastery over the Mortal Shell allows its user to hinder physical functions of others and, at greater levels, take command of their bodies.</p>\n<h3>System</h3>\n<p>Body Control is resisted using Stamina + Occult or Fortitude if the victim has it. All spells of this Path require some form of hand gestures to manipulate bodies of others. Those in the know may roll Wits + Occult vs. the user’s Wits + Subterfuge to notice gestures.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Action:</strong> Full</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Specialties:</strong> Anatomy, Body Manipulation</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Number of Targets, Range and the following.</p>\n<h3>Body Control:</h3>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> Botching has the sorcerer afflict their own body and suffers a point of (unhalved) Superficial Health damage for each 1 rolled.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"329"},"level":0,"cost":0,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.yrt6OgjioR0bo6nl"},"z2QzVNfqiLL0ZO8B":{"name":"Damascus Steel/Unobtanium","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-alchemy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Alchemy Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>An extremely strong, light, and easy to work with metal alloy. It can retain an edge forever; melee edged weapons made from it deal an additional point of damage and ignore two points of armour. Requires iron or steel to work. When used in armour, it increases Armour Rating by one. The created work is permanent.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"264"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.z2QzVNfqiLL0ZO8B"},"z5rgmsOoixIAoCxC":{"name":"Warding Circle of Secrecy","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Binding, Summoning and Warding Ritual (•••).</em></p>\n<p>In Ceoris and Alamut, those who wield great secrets can do what those wielding great magical powers can not. In order to make sure others cannot eavesdrop or track them through supernatural sight and scrying methods, multiple traditions created variants of this Warding Circle that ensures no sound can be heard outside of the circle and no supernatural senses can penetrate it.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration (ranks count as doubled), Range (as a Radius)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Ritualist’s blood.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist’s blood, frankincense, and sage.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> This ritual creates a warding circle or a zone according to Range as a radius. On a win on the ritual roll, no sound is heard outside of the circle, which includes eavesdropping from inside the circle while being in other worlds such as the Shadowlands. Those using supernatural senses such as Shadowsight or a scrying mirror cannot see any being inside this circle nor hear them. Those who wish to eavesdrop or see into the mirror must use their will to break through the barrier, rolling Resolve + Awareness against the total number of successes on the ritual roll. This ritual is a Ward ritual.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Risk:</strong> If the Botchfails, not even those within the circle will be able to hear anything.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"273"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.z5rgmsOoixIAoCxC"},"z70dYJP8HPb07qUM":{"name":"Enthralling","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Special Effect (•••••). Aspects: In addition to the Aspects of Number of Targets, Range, and Sympathetic Connection, Fascination uses the Allure and Willbending Aspects.</em></p>\n<p>If you started throwing things, this place would become a full-scale riot; those present find themselves hard-pressed to resist your requests, doing almost anything you might request. Hell, they may even kill for you or put their life on the line for you if you ask the right way. You may make strong suggestions that wildly defy the target’s character and circumstances, such as suggesting that the spell’s victim burns down a bar for daring to announce last call.</p>\n<p>When used against players, be sure to communicate how Willbending is used. Persuasion Tests powered by Allure are required to force requests permitted by Willbending.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"307"},"level":5,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.z70dYJP8HPb07qUM"},"z8iWWwhDNg6Gq5K0":{"name":"Love Potion","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Fascination Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>With knowledge of an upcoming social event and proper preparation, a sorcerer can make themselves quite the sensation. With an invitation in hand, whether intended for them or not, the magician infuses their presence into the upcoming gathering. For the duration of the specified party, they receive VIP treatment from all attendees and hired staff. The organisers give their blessing and offer a hearty “have a great night” without second-guessing their guest list.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Aspects:</strong> Duration, Sympathetic Connection (optional)</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Ingredients:</strong> Something to fill the Sympathetic Connection Aspect</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Process:</strong> The ritualist creates a potion that triggers the effect one drunk or performs a normal ritual with Sympathetic Connection of the target.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>System:</strong> A lock of hair, a dram of blood, or a sentimental personal effect is required to create the right connection to the heart in question. When performed for an individual other than the sorcerer themselves, some part of the client is usually required as well. The ritual takes the form of the traditional potion in some practices; in others it is a sort of ritualised binding with a knotted cord. In all cases, the victim is tied to the anchor point of the sorcerer or their client.</p>\n<p>Once performed, the victim finds themselves uneasy and sick to their stomach (represented by Physical Impairment). The lingering nausea vanishes when the anchor is near, or when they are performing a task that they know would please the anchor.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"307"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.z8iWWwhDNg6Gq5K0"},"zWaOruX10eIJBFre":{"name":"Amulet against Vampires","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•••). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>Same as Amulet against Magic except that it impacts Cainite and Ghoul Disciplines (not Blood Magic)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"290"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.zWaOruX10eIJBFre"},"zbrYjcYK25TLtP9i":{"name":"Ward","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-necromancy.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Necromancy Special Effect (•••). Aspects: Duration and the Necromancy Special Effects.</em></p>\n<p>The sorcerer wards themselves or an object (up to a meter across) or creates a Warding Circe (up to three meters radius) against spirits of the dead. The dead must succeed on a Composure + Resolve roll or they may not pass a Warding Circle or touch the warded object. The warded caster or those who stand within a Warding Circle count Arcanoi successes of hostile ghosts as lower by three, to a minimum of 0. The Warding Circle may be inverted so as to trap ghosts and prevent them from leaving. The size may be expanded by making a Rouse Check, increasing a ward by up to a meter across or up to 3 meters radius for a warding circle per Rouse Check made.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"332"},"level":3,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.zbrYjcYK25TLtP9i"},"znRhQ6RIWWU36aUX":{"name":"Whirlpool/Undertow","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/path-weather-control.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Weather Control Ritual (••••).</em></p>\n<p>A whirlpool/undertow acts as a creature with a Strength rating equal to the total number of successes on the casting roll to harm ships, drag or pull people. Attacks normally inflict only Superficial damage which is not halved against inanimate beings such as ships. Grappling attack can be used to suffocate a victim, dealing one point of Aggravated Health damage for each turn a victim is grappled. Whirlpools and undertows can strike as many targets as logically possible, not abiding standard action rules.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"358"},"level":4,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"rituals","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.znRhQ6RIWWU36aUX"},"zpoaTlaxBL6g9VSw":{"name":"Reliable Firearm","type":"power","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/icons/blood-sorcery.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Enchantment Special Effect (•). Aspects: The</em></p>\n<p>A custom Aim Skill weapon subtracting one from the success penalty of all called shots.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"289"},"level":1,"cost":1,"dicepool":{"GvzBJfAE":{"path":"attributes.intelligence"},"B6C1xuXr":{"path":"skills.occult"}},"discipline":"sorcery","selected":false},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.blood-sorcery.Item.zpoaTlaxBL6g9VSw"},"1sdkxv81XzdfK2qf":{"name":"Banu Haqim","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Blood Sorcery, Celerity, Obfuscate</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts):</h5>\n<ul><li>Sorcerer Caste: Auspex, Dur-An-Ki, Quietus (Sanguinis)</li><li>Vizier Caste: Auspex, Celerity, Quietus (Animae)</li><li>Warrior Castes: Celerity, Obfuscate, Quietus (Cruciatus)</li></ul>\n<p><em>Known in V20 as the Assamite.</em></p>\n<p>The childer of Haqim, known as Assamites to the rest of the Kindred, are a silent knife in the dark, an order of bloodthirsty assassins who participate in the secret wars of the undead by operating as killers for hire. Outside the purview of the Sects, the Assamites are true independents and mercenaries, hiring out to whoever can pay their blood-price and ungoverned by the will of Prince or Priscus. By the time a mark realizes that he’s being hunted by an Assamite, it’s often far too late. Needless to say, this makes the Assamites both feared and reviled by many of the other Clans.</p>\n<p>In truth, the Assamites are more than simple thugs and killers. Theirs is a complex but insular Clan predicated upon the three principles of wisdom, sorcery, and diablerie. Most Assamites that other vampires encounter are members of the warrior caste, however, so Kindred society has painted them all with that brush. For their part, the Assassins have done nothing to stop this misunderstanding. If it helps them acquire contracts and it occludes the true nature of their Clan, the better for them.</p>\n<p>Long ago, the Assamites were brought to heel by a powerful curse to curb their bloodlust, levied by the Tremere at the behest of the Camarilla. They cannot taste the vitae of vampires without it causing them harm. In their ongoing quest to lower their Generation and bring themselves closer to their holy figure, Haqim (whom some outside scholars claim was of the Second Generation, while others insist he was a judge appointed by the other Antediluvians), the Assamites must refine the blood of Kindred into an alchemical solution. Were it not for this mystical yoke, the Assamites would surely be unchecked on a crusade of unholy diablerie.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Assassins</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> For the most part, the Assamites tend to be independent, letting the Sects hire them to operate on their own terms. That said, some Assamites believe an allegiance with the Camarilla or the Sabbat would allow the entire Clan to stand more strongly.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Older Assamites often come from Middle Eastern and North African cultures, though more and more young Assamites come from a wider demographic. In traditional environments, the Assamites prefer garb appropriate to religious or Clan custom. When in public, however, Assamites wear whatever the locals do, allowing them to fulfill their contracts without anyone noticing anything amiss. An</p>\n<p>Assamite’s skin grows darker with age (as opposed to other vampires, whose skin gets paler); particularly ancient Assamites are almost ebony in complexion.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Assamites often share communal havens with others of their local cell, remote structures that allow the Assassins to watch the larger domain from a distance. These havens are generally well appointed, but not so lavish that the whole place can’t be moved on short notice. Individual Assamites also tend to keep personal hideouts of a much more humble nature, for when they need a place to lay low.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Those Embraced into Clan Assamite tend to fall into two distinct types: The “provincial” members of the Clan fit whatever their locality is, and can blend seamlessly in with the people around them. The higher-profile “jet-setters” transcend cultures, bolstered by their ability to handle interpersonal and intellectual challenges.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Physical Attributes tend to be primary, with some Assamites favoring Social Attributes to help them get close to their prey. Talents and Skills are equally favored, but Knowledges may help the wise Assamite in a pinch. Few Assassins cultivate extensive Backgrounds, and instead specialize in an array of Disciplines that heighten their competence. The most accomplished Assamites follow the Clan’s unique Path of Enlightenment, and those who don’t often have to spend a great deal of effort maintaining their Virtues and Humanity.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> An insular, hierarchical organization shapes much of Assamite custom. “The Old Man on the Mountain” — the master assassin who makes his haven in the mountain fortress of Alamut — is the ultimate authority, and the Clan heeds the orders that trickle down to them with a mix of reverence and terror. Individual and local cells of Assamites known as <em>falaqi</em> frequently have license to act with autonomy, but “turncoats” against the higher cause are rare.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Your blood is anathema to me, but the act of spilling it brings me closer to Haqim.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>Where they are blatant, we are subtle. And that is why they are a broken Clan and we are ascendant.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Humility before God is itself divine, but certainly not humility before their unclean god.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>When the blade bites deeply, they die as readily as all other Kindred.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>They value their independence as we do, but they squander it in unholy debauchery.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>For all their posturing, they are quick to pay our fees and hire our knives.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>In the parable of the scorpion and the frog, they play the roles of both doomed creatures.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>But for their desperation to be included among the society that shuns them, they would make puissant</em> rafiq<em>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>Make yourself known to them and they will acknowledge their place.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>We kill to honor our God. They kill to avenge a wittier remark.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>Possessing the power of Solomon makes them none the wiser for it, and vengeance will be ours.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>They bear old grudges against us; meet them with a wary eye.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>Vultures picking at the corpse of a long-gone nobility.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>Every culture must have its Judas goat.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>A tower built in Babel by Icarus.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>They profane what is holy and pretend it is an eminent glory.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>The wisest of the tribes — because they know when to admit they don’t know.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 18)</small></h3>\n<p>Banu Haqim are drawn to feed from those deserving punishment. This is especially true for vampire Blood, the very essence of transgression. When one of the Judges tastes the Blood of another Cainite, they find it very hard to stop.</p>\n<p>Slaking at least one Hunger level with vampiric vitae provokes a hunger frenzy test (See Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 220) at a Difficulty 2 + Bane Severity. If the test is failed they attempt to gorge themselves on vampire Blood, sometimes until they perform diablerie upon their Kindred victim. This presents many problems for the Banu Haqim, as many Kindred see the Amaranth as anathema, and greatly at odds with the sort or orderly behavior the Judges presume to uphold. And of course, no few Banu Haqim excoriate themselves over the hypocrisy of their own failures.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Noxious Blood <small>(p. 56)</small></h3>\n<p>Death lurks in the Blood of the so-called Assassins. While its effect on other vampires is the same as any vitae, it is poison to mortals and risks the life of anyone the Banu Haqim would try to make their ghoul or Blood Bond. A mortal drinking the Blood of a Banu Haqim vampire suffers Aggravated Damage equal to the Bane Severity of the vampire for each Rouse Check’s worth of Blood ingested. In addition, Banu Haqim vitae cannot be used to heal mortal injuries (see p. 139). Banu Haqim vitae does not have any other toxic properties; in amounts below the quantity needed to create a Blood Bond, it inflicts no damage even if injected directly into the bloodstream, for example.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is useful when the chronicle is focused on mortal society and interaction over that of other vampires. Banu Haqim ghouls will be rare, with only the hardiest mortals able to survive the tainted power they provide.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 48)</small></h3>\n<p>Due to the Tremere bloodcurse, should an Assamite consume the blood of another Kindred, she suffers one automatic level of unsoakable lethal damage per blood point imbibed. Diablerie attempts result in automatic aggravated damage, one health level per point of permanent Willpower the victim possesses; the would-be diablerist gains no benefits (including Generation reduction) if he survives the process. In addition, Assamites must tithe some of the profits from their contracts to their sires or superiors (generally around 10 percent of all such earnings).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-banuhaqim","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"48"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.1sdkxv81XzdfK2qf"},"2qQkz74JIkAyIM3S":{"name":"Ventrue","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ventrue.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Dominate, Fortitude, Presence</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Dominate, Fortitude, Presence</h5>\n<p>Throughout history, while the other Clans have skulked about their petty intrigues, the Ventrue have curried favor with Caesar, whispered into the ear of Charlemagne, bankrolled the Age of Exploration, and even swayed policy in the Holy See. Theirs is a legacy of rulership, from Ventrue fledglings starting their climb to the top to the mightiest elders whose influence spans the world. Long have they played kingmaker in the shadows in the mortal world, and long have they been the Clan of Kings among the Kindred.</p>\n<p>Other Clans, of course, find all this insufferably pompous at best, punishably tedious at worst. Someone has to lead, surely, but why does it always have to be some long-winded, self-aggrandizing Ventrue? The Blue Bloods try to bear the criticism of their lessers with a sense of noblesse oblige — ever heavy hangs that head that wears the crown — but even the most munificent of Kindred leaders occasionally succumbs to tyranny and bloody rage.</p>\n<p>Tonight, the Ventrue are a synthesis of the modern and the ancient, often in stark contrast within the Clan and among one another. Theirs is money of old, from the vaults of Croesus, but their young manipulate stock markets and influence currencies. Elders may command armies or even whole governments, while neonates conjure their assets from a website or smartphone app. But for all their wealth, their distinguished history, and their status among the Damned, each and every Ventrue must still seek that one resource that makes Kindred society egalitarian: precious blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Blue Bloods</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> To hear the Ventrue tell it, the very idea of the Camarilla originated with them. Every other Clan realized what an invaluable proposition it was and flocked to their banner.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Ventrue favor conservative clothing and reserved presentation, unless they’re making a point about power or money. Ventrue Princes may well wear a circlet or carry a scepter as symbols of office, while young Blue Bloods display their own achievement via suits, ties, dresses, and accessories that are easily overlooked singularly but add up to a stunning total effect. If a Ventrue has so much as a hair out of place, it’s because he spent all night running down the Society of Leopold and demanding the Sabbat menace retreat.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> A Ventrue’s haven displays both her great power (read: wealth) and distinguished tastes. Opulent, grandiose, even baroque — these may all apply to Ventrue havens. They shun the gaudy displays of other Kindred, and their style tends less to the avant-garde than it does to the classical and traditional. To the Blue Bloods, a well-maintained haven is an extension of oneself, and for someone to see it in less than flawless state implies weakness, distraction, or even madness.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Anyone who has “made something of herself” may attract the attention of the Ventrue, who judge their childer based on their prominence and success even before they start to groom them for the Embrace. Socialites, moneyed family, corporate <em>wunderkinds</em>, military leaders, and even untested newcomers who show great promise are keenly valued among the Blue Bloods.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Ventrue usually have directorial or outgoing personality archetypes. Members of Clan Ventrue favor Social and Mental Attributes, but any Ability category can be primary, reflecting a personal avenue of expertise. Backgrounds go both wide and deep for the Ventrue, with almost every Blue Blood possessing some amount of Resources, Status, and Herd (particularly the latter, given the Clan weakness). Elders in particular cultivate enviable havens and sprawling Domains.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Clanwide organization of the Ventrue is localized and feudal, with various universally understood peerages, vassalages, oaths of fealty, and sworn boons taking the place of a rigid hierarchy. Many Ventrue style themselves as secret masters of their domains, consolidating power in longstanding networks and forming conspiracies. The Ventrue greatly value propriety and honor, and use many forms of address and respect — their Laws of Decorum are complex and rigid, and could fill several volumes. Almost every Ventrue worth his status can recite his lineage at least back to the elders, if not to the great Methuselahs.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>There is no shame to bending the knee to one such as myself, so pay your fucking tribute before I lose my temper.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Useful only so long as they don’t become a poison in our veins.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>Just admit you’re beaten and this will become much more pleasant.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>When you learn of them in your domain, do not hesitate to introduce them to their master, the sun.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Who would guess that Kindred so simple could be so groundlessly prideful?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Our stillborn siblings, who never developed a sense of right and wrong or what not to stick your cock into.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>Pretenders to our mantle of dignity and honor, though they are formidable.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>When you find yourself making excuses for them, it is time for the purge to begin.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>Surprisingly functional, should you be able to handle their odious personalities and overestimation of their own value.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>No kingdom survives by mingling with the conquered.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>For every king there is a queen, and there are a lot of queens among the ranks of the Rose Clan.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>They can prove dauntless allies or treacherous enemies, often both within the same skin, so let them know who commands.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>What more do you need than their cloying scent of corruption to know that theirs is an ill presence?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>They are mongrels, barely worthy of names. You might as well care about your furniture.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>It is our greatest triumph, but also our greatest responsibility.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>The inmates run that asylum.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>There is something admirable in what they do, yet how they do it is entirely puerile.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 102)</small></h3>\n<p>The Ventrue are in possession of rarefied palates. When a Ventrue drinks blood from any mortal outside their preference, a profound exertion of will is required or the blood taken surges back up as scarlet vomit. Preferences range greatly, from Ventrue who can only feed from genuine brunettes, individuals of Swiss descent, or homosexuals, to others who can only feed from soldiers, mortals who suffer from PTSD, or methamphetamine users. With a Resolve + Awareness test (Difficulty 4 or more) your character can sense if a mortal possesses the blood they require.</p>\n<p>If you want your character to feed from anything but their preferred victim, you must spend Willpower points equal to the character’s Bane Severity.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Hierarchy <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>While many Ventrue seek to lead, they are also swift to obey their superiors. In many cases chosen for a pragmatic mindset, Ventrue childer learn to heed their sire, and their Blood-borne instincts often make them yield in a similar way to any vampire of older Blood.</p>\n<p>A Ventrue vampire suffers a penalty to their Discipline dice pools equal to their Bane Severity when attempting to use their powers on a vampire of lower generation. They must also spend Willpower equal to their Bane Severity if they wish to directly attack a vampire of lower generation.</p>\n<p><em>If the chronicle does not focus on feeding scenes or the characters can procure vessels in more controlled ways, this variant helps define the Ventrue as everything from honoring anachronistic feudal social codes to being unabashed bootlickers.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-ventrue","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"72"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.2qQkz74JIkAyIM3S"},"45n2p06RHaDw8bb4":{"name":"Harbingers of Skulls","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/harbingersofskulls.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Auspex, Fortitude, Necromancy</h5>\n<p>The Harbingers of Skulls are a small bloodline that emerged from the mists of the past and threw their lot in with the Sabbat. The Harbingers are alleged to all be elders, and frighteningly powerful. While their sudden appearance suggests a long bout of torpor, they don’t behave like recently-awakened vampires. Indeed, they claim they have been <em>dead</em> these past five centuries. The Harbingers of Skulls resemble rotting corpses, much like the Samedi. Indeed, vampires that know of both bloodlines suggest a common origin, but since neither the Samedi nor the Harbingers discuss their origins publically, it’s just speculation. The Harbingers have only been members of the Sabbat for a short time, but during that time they have made themselves invaluable. The Sword of Caine, lacking Giovanni members, has difficulty dealing with ghosts. This can lead to problematic hauntings, since the Sabbat kills enough people that ghostly visitation can become an issue. The Harbingers can exorcises these vengeful shades, or, better yet, turn them into incorporeal spies and slaves. And for all of this, the Harbingers ask nothing except for favors to be repaid at a later date. The Sabbat, for its part, isn’t usually given to the favor-trading that one finds in the Camarilla, but what the Harbingers bring to the table is too valuable to miss. And besides, whether one believes the fairy tale about noble origins and “revenge on the traitorous moneylenders” that the Harbingers sometimes spin, their passion is palpable. That kind of murderous zeal is always welcome in the Sword of Caine.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Lazarenes</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Harbingers of Skulls belong entirely to the Sabbat. That said, given that the bloodline obviously has many secrets buried, it’s impossible to know if any Lazarenes claim membership in the Camarilla or remain independent.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Harbingers prefer to stay hidden in their havens, or at least remain among Kindred, and for good reason. They resemble emaciated, shrunken corpses. Their skin grows taut and white, and the gums recede, giving the impression of a bleached, grinning skull. Most Harbingers wearing flowing garments or burial shrouds, the better to highlight their affinity for death.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Unlike most Sabbat Kindred, the Harbingers of Skulls do not join packs, and therefore do not use communal havens. They usually choose lairs away from mortal interference, but close enough to the living that they don’t want for blood (or fresh corpses for their horrific arts). Crypts, disused laboratories, and slaughterhouses are common choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The background of a “typical” Harbinger is impossible to know. They are all elders, and who they were in life has been lost to time. They do seem to show evidence of a classical education, and one survivor of a battle between a Harbinger and a gang of Camarilla Brujah mentions that the Lazarene used a style of fighting that originated in Florence. Beyond those sparse clues, however, the Harbingers’ true identities are unknown.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> The Harbingers of Skulls tend toward morbid and somewhat violent concepts. They are obsessed with revenge, death, and the horrors beyond the grave. Social Attributes and Abilities aren’t a priority. Mental Attributes and Knowledges are generally highly rated, but their status as elders means that they are formidable across the board (and imply a significant amount spent on the Generation Background to boot).</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> No matter how much blood a Harbinger consumes, her skin remains deathly pale. Moreover, all Harbingers look like shriveled corpses. They have Appearance ratings of 0 and automatically fail Appearance rolls.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Harbingers have some kind of internal organization, and they do keep in contact and even meet with one another occasionally. They keep in touch using ghostly messengers, and they travel to meet each other using methods that other Kindred cannot match. Most of the Sabbat doesn’t quite know how many Harbingers exist or what they might be doing.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>It’s amazing what the dead can teach you about revenge. We have been listening to them for centuries.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>The more things change….</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>They can’t win. But we don’t want to</em> win.</p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Payback is coming very soon, and on that day all of your money will mean nothing.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 402)</small></h3>\n<p>No matter how much blood a Harbinger consumes, her skin remains deathly pale. Moreover, all Harbingers look like shriveled corpses. They have Appearance ratings of 0 and automatically fail Appearance rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-harbingersofskulls","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"402"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.45n2p06RHaDw8bb4"},"4jegGpArJzAor9vX":{"name":"Noiad","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/noiad.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Animalism, Auspex, Protean</h5>\n<p>Few Kindred have ever beheld the Northern Lights. The trek to the far reaches of the world, in a place inhospitable to man, beast, or vampire, with no reliable way to feed is enough to put even the most curious Cainite off the idea. And yet, a bloodline of vampires managed to dwell close enough to see these lights on a regular basis. This line — the Noiad — felt it was their sacred duty to witness the Northern Lights and interpret the will of the gods. Their mortal charges, a nomadic tribe called the Samí, lived in fear and awe of their godlike protectors. The Noiad defended the Samí from outside threats, both natural and supernatural, and acted as shamans and teachers for these wanderers. In return, the Samí gave of their blood and kept the Noiad safe during the long months with no darkness. Of course, the touch of the Kindred corrupts everything. The Noiad protected the Samí from monsters from the wastes, and their blood allowed the mortals to survive times of famine, but the Wanderers also refused to allow their mortal herds to change or improve. Interlopers were turned aside or killed, regardless of their intentions. When it became clear that the march of Christianity (and Lasombra working within the Church) could not be stopped, the Noiad pushed the Samí further north, away from the herds that would sustain them and away from the dawning of a new era. Whether the Noiad saw something in the Northern Lights that spurred them to this decision, or whether it was simply fear of change, is not important in the end. The Noiad drove the Samí almost to destruction.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Wanderers</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Noiad never belonged to a Sect, and were largely apart from the vampire politics of the day (at least until war pushed far enough north to inconvenience them). As a Gangrel offshoot, other Cainites would have considered them a Low Clan, on the rare occasion that other Cainites learned enough about them to have opinions at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Noiad dwelled openly among the Samí, and Embraced exclusively from this population. Most Noaid were dark-haired and strongly built from their demanding lifestyles pre-Embrace. Their skin tone, of course, tended to be lighter than their still-living comrades.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Samí constructed mobile “havens” that could be dragged behind them as they traveled by day. If terrain or numbers made this impossible, the Samí would watch in awe as their heavenly protectors sank into the earth, only to rejoin them the next night.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Samí selected for Embrace tended to be those with talent for divination, as well as dream and star interpretation. The Noiad also watched children descended from families that included at least one vampire, looking for the traits they favored. Physicality was not a primary concern, but only because Samí that were not physically capable of thriving in the harsh climates didn’t live long enough to be considered for the Embrace anyway.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Physical Attributes weren’t necessarily primary, but tended to be no worse than average. All Noiad had good ratings in Survival and Animal Ken, and at least a dot or two in Athletics, and Melee. Finally, all Noiad had at least a dot of the Herd Background.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Noiad were so intrinsically tied to the Samí and to their role as the divine protectors of the tribe that one of the legends about them came true. The Samí’s protectors, it was said, could not sup from animals, but could only take sustenance from the blood of the chosen (that is, the Samí). The Noiad, in fact, could not drink from animals, though they were capable of drinking from any mortal or Cainite, regardless of nationality or ethnicity.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Noiad usually saw one another when they came to witness the Northern Lights (this also gave them the opportunity to make sure the gods were all telling them the same things). The Noiad also used animals, either ghouls or those controlled by the Animalism Discipline, to keep in some kind of contact.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>We are the children of Midnight Sun. We have a family. We have what you never could. And you want to tear it all down, don’t you?</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>High Clans:</strong> <em>They can pretend to know of their god’s mind all they want. They wrote the book, and they want to claim their god did it. I know this trick.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Low Clans:</strong> <em>Too many have already been fooled. The ones that haven’t know to leave us alone.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 426)</small></h3>\n<p>The Noiad were so intrinsically tied to the Samí and to their role as the divine protectors of the tribe that one of the legends about them came true. The Samí’s protectors, it was said, could not sup from animals, but could only take sustenance from the blood of the chosen (that is, the Samí). The Noiad, in fact, could not drink from animals, though they were capable of drinking from any mortal or Cainite, regardless of nationality or ethnicity.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-noiad","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"426"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.4jegGpArJzAor9vX"},"5gRl8qKVIV0faDcd":{"name":"Nagaraja","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nagaraja.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dominate, Necromancy</h5>\n<p>A bizarre bloodline of flesh-eating witches, the Nagaraja are legends, feared by Kindred on all sides of the Jyhad. As a result of their hideous appetites, the Nagaraja have been hunted to near extinction in the modern nights by political enemies and the ghosts upon whom they prey. A few of these vampires still prowl the darkness, however, continuing their subtle plans.</p>\n<p>Although not particularly forthcoming with their origins, the Nagaraja supposedly hail from the Middle East. They are associated with a death cult in that region that ritually consumed human flesh and opened gateways into the lands of the dead. How these cultists became vampires is lost to history, but those few Kindred that have met the Nagaraja have theories: maybe a wandering Giovanni trader Embraced them, perhaps they tried to follow in Tremere’s footsteps, or they could be the last surviving Cappadocians. The Nagaraja refuse to confirm or deny such speculations, however, so the truth and rumor continue to entwine around these mysterious Kindred.</p>\n<p>Some of the Flesh- Eaters belong to a Sect of vampires called the Tal’Mahe’Ra or the “True Black Hand,” apparently distinct from the Black Hand of the Sabbat. What the Tal’Mahe’Ra is doing in the Deadlands isn’t clear, nor why they war with the Restless Dead, but their city in the Underworld is home to a number of the Nagaraja. Those in the land of the living are independent, now seeking to eke out whatever unlife they can.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Flesh-Eaters</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Nagaraja as a whole refuse membership in either the Camarilla or the Sabbat, but in Camarilla cities they follow the rules as well as they can (given their weakness).</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Nagaraja have pointed, irregular teeth, rather than the usual retractable fangs. This means that they seldom smile, and they tend to speak quietly around mortals — unless they plan to eat them.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Nagaraja take havens where they can. One thing that the Flesh-Eaters do try to incorporate into their havens, when they can, is a means for disposing of bodies. They do not have the luxury of merely sipping a bit of blood the way other Kindred do.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Most Nagaraja are well-educated, and many have some medical knowledge (either from their mortal lives or acquired post-Embrace). The bloodline’s small numbers make further assertions difficult to support. Some Nagaraja were priests or cultists before the Embrace, some antiquarians, and some exorcists or ghost-hunters.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Nagaraja usually have solitary or selfish concepts and Natures, though their Demeanors may be practically anything. Mental Attributes are usually primary, as are Knowledges. The most common Backgrounds for Nagaraja are Contacts, Resources, though a few twisted Mentors and Retainers, sometimes Embrace childer.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Nagaraja require raw flesh in addition to blood to survive. For every night a Nagaraja goes without consuming flesh, he loses one cumulative die from all his Physical dice pools. Eating one point worth of flesh restores one die to these pools until the vampire has “caught up.” A human body has 10 “flesh points,” which work just like blood points: A Nagaraja consuming one flesh point increases his blood pool by one. Unlike blood points, however, taking a “flesh point” from a vessel does one health level of unsoakable lethal damage to that vessel. The flesh the Nagaraja consume must be relatively fresh, though not necessarily “alive.” Indeed, some Nagaraja keep stores of ritually preserved corpses in their havens. This weakness does not allow them to eat food or consume other liquids.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> While some claim this bloodline belongs to the Tal’Mahe’Ra in its entirety, there are ample exceptions. A given Nagaraja might keep contact with another, but the bloodline as a whole has no organization.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Your fate is inevitable, but serve me, and I will protect you from it for as long as I can.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>An umbrella, until the drops grow heavy enough to break it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Perhaps we can kill enough of them? No. The contagion has taken root.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>You think you’re so powerful and decadent? How quaint.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 406)</small></h3>\n<p>The Nagaraja require raw flesh in addition to blood to survive. For every night a Nagaraja goes without consuming flesh, he loses one cumulative die from all his Physical dice pools. Eating one point worth of flesh restores one die to these pools until the vampire has “caught up.” A human body has 10 “flesh points,” which work just like blood points: A Nagaraja consuming one flesh point increases his blood pool by one. Unlike blood points, however, taking a “flesh point” from a vessel does one health level of unsoakable lethal damage to that vessel. The flesh the Nagaraja consume must be relatively fresh, though not necessarily “alive.” Indeed, some Nagaraja keep stores of ritually preserved corpses in their havens. This weakness does not allow them to eat food or consume other liquids.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-nagaraja","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"406"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.5gRl8qKVIV0faDcd"},"7OfkUdz3YIZBZQuf":{"name":"The Ministry","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Obfuscate, Presence, Protean</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts):</h5>\n<ul><li>Priest Caste: Obfuscate, Presence, Serpentis</li><li>Warrior Caste: Obfuscate, Potence, Serpentis</li><li>Witch Caste: Animalism, Presence, Akhu</li></ul>\n<p><em>Known in V20 as the Followers of Set.</em></p>\n<p>Addiction, debasement, corruption, and desperation strike fear into many Kindred who worry that theirs will become an unlife of ruin, but to the Followers of Set, these and more are the tools of the trade. Pimps, pushers, and priests, the Setites cater to the needs of the desperate, and convert them to a nihilistic cause in doing so. Whether one needs flesh, money, drugs, or dark secrets, the Followers of Set can provide it, and when they do so, they all but guarantee themselves a return visit from those who seek them.</p>\n<p>The Followers of Set are as much a chthonic religion as they are a Clan, though the faith includes the Clan. Its mythology is complex and convoluted, an impenetrable pantheon of god-monsters. At the apex of this worship stands a syncretism of the Egyptian Lord of the Underworld Set and the Greek hydra Typhon, as much spiritual guardians of secret places as they are the “liberators” of other’s souls. To outsiders, this is all blasphemous religious affectation, but to devout Setites, the cult and cause are real, and their dark lord works his will through them.</p>\n<p>Needless to say, the politics of desperation and the placation of evil gods place the Serpents on the outside of Kindred society. The Setites are fine with this. The Kindred can often find themselves in need of what the Followers of Set purvey, and the extra “service” of secrecy is one the Setites are happy to provide — at a premium. The Setites offer a devil’s deal, but on their own terms. That way, when they collect their due and feed blood and souls into the maw of the Typhonic beast, all of its sacrifices have been given willingly.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Setites, Serpents</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Externally, none of the Sects will have the Followers of Set. Internally, the Setites sometimes describe themselves in terms of both Sect and Clan. They have no real impetus to join either the Camarilla or Sabbat, and their goals are different from those of the Anarchs.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Many older Setites hail from the North African and Mediterranean ethnicities native to the Serpents’ historical territory, but they freely Embrace from among the mortals of their adopted homes. Some long-standing Setite temples are tied to locations where “Egyptian” Serpents might seem out of place, but where some aspect of serpent mythology is present, as in Mesoamerica or even far-flung Nordic locales, and thus draw their membership from local populations. Red hair is considered a mark of Set’s favor.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Where their hidden temples stand, the Setites make their havens, either individually or communally. These may be anything from “churches” with never-before-heard-of denominations or they may be outright cults that have to hide their existences. The secretive Serpents sometimes hide individual havens in places where other Kindred don’t often go, such as insular ethnic neighborhoods, abandoned domains, “the rough part of town,” and so forth. Some Setites also haven in secret mystical places that have value to the Clan, guarding them from outsiders.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Prospective childer for the Followers of Set often spend some time involved with a Setite cult, so they’re indoctrinated in the mysteries of the Clan before becoming one of its Kindred. They may come from any cultural origin, though many are outsiders, loners, or otherwise marginalized by society, which is often what led them to the forbidden fruits offered by the cult of Set in the first place.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Sires choose those who demonstrate acumen in Social and Mental Attributes, as the Clan needs its proselytizers and its priests to be charismatic and quick-witted. Knowledges are almost always primary, though Talents may be so instead, especially among those Serpents engaged in dealings with others. Setites focus on Backgrounds that give influence over others whether subtly or overtly, making Allies, Contacts, Influence, Resources, and sometimes Retainers popular.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Setite organization is mostly local, with a single temple or network of cults representing a city’s Serpent presence. Setites in the cities rarely scheme among each other, preferring to face outward threats in unity rather than the schismatic self-interest of the Sects. Whispers persist of a massive temple devoted to Set located somewhere in Africa, with a terrible Methuselah who claims to be the childe of Set himself at the head. If this is true, then the Clan’s higher agenda probably originates here, but the Setites themselves remain silent on the topic.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I know what you need.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>They aren’t so different from us, and it scares the hell out of them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>We could have saved their precious Carthage, but in their pride, they chose an eternity of ridicule and failure.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Don’t mistake their sullenness for noble savagery. They have vices, just like anyone else.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>They distract themselves with methods and neglect their goals.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>Behind many successful Lasombra is a debt to the temple.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>They make excellent allies because no one pays attention to them, and by the time they’re due their share, all they can do is gibber.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>Forge a relationship or you’ll find yourself dragged before a Prince or Archbishop with them as witnesses.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>Sorry, I don’t take credit. From you.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>Cha-ching.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>One in a hundred realizes what we might do together. The rest are up their own asses.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>The Dragon is not the only Serpent among the Damned.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>Let them hate us publicly, so long as they keep buying.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>How easily the trod-upon becomes a convert.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Hypocrisy is great for business.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Take one step forward, two steps back, set self on fire, kick own ass. Preen.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>An idealistic cult of personality that sometime succeeds in spite of itself.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 36)</small></h3>\n<p>The Blood of a Minister abhors the light. When exposed to direct illumination, whether natural or artificial, members of the clan recoil. Ministers receive a penalty equal to their Bane Severity to all dice pools when subjected to bright light directed straight at them. Also, add their Bane Severity to Aggravated damage taken from sunlight.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Cold-Blooded <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Often compared to that of serpents, the Blood of the Ministers runs cold in more ways than one. Without fresh, warm blood in their veins they find it impossible to mimic the breath and heartbeat of a mortal, and even then the act requires considerable effort.</p>\n<p>A vampire of the Ministry can only use Blush of Life if they have recently fed from a living vessel (in the same scene or up to roughly an hour ago, at the storyteller’s discretion), and when they do it requires a number of Rouse Checks equal to their Bane Severity rather than just one.</p>\n<p><em>Social and subtle chronicles where smooth interaction with mortals is at a premium can add tension to this variant.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-ministry","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"52"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.7OfkUdz3YIZBZQuf"},"9UJrjl7BF9Vg1iTm":{"name":"Lasombra Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Dominate, Obtenebration, Potence</h5>\n<p>Lasombra <em>antitribu</em> are simply members of Clan Lasombra who either predate the Sabbat and refused to join, or who disagree with the Sabbat’s philosophies. They are rare in either case, because the Sabbat goes out of its way to destroy them, but a few persist. Some join the Camarilla, while others remain independent. As a rule, they carry the Machiavellian attitude that typifies the Lasombra — they fully expect that they will win the Jyhad, and they are willing to do what is necessary to do so. They just feel that their Clan as a whole made the wrong choice. While some Camarilla vampires know that these Kindred exist, at least in theory, the Magisters themselves try to stay out of the spotlight. The Sabbat has been known to stage raids on Camarilla cities, raids that they cannot hope to bring to a victory, just to destroy a Lasombra <em>antitribu</em>. Some Kindred put this down to the tenacity and spite of the Sabbat, but others point out that it tends to be the Sabbat Lasombra — well known for being good tacticians — who initiate these raids. What are they so afraid that the Lasombra <em>antitribu</em> might reveal?</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Magisters (this was the Clan’s sobriquet for centuries before the Anarch Revolt)</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> In addition to the Clan’s usual problem with reflections (p. 59), Lasombra <em>antitribu</em> also have the “weakness” of being hunted relentlessly by the Sabbat, and targeted for destruction whenever their existence is made public.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I watched the fires of the Reconquista. I saw the trenches of the Great War and the fires that burned Dresden. The so-called “Sword of Caine” does not fill me with dread.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Lasombra V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 29)</small></h3>\n<p>Anyone seeing the reflection or recording (live and otherwise) of a Lasombra vampire can instantly recognize them for what they are, provided they know what they’re looking for. People with no prior knowledge will know something is wrong, but likely attribute the distortion to irregularities in the reflecting surface or recording errors. Note that this will not hide the identity of the vampire with any certainty: the Lasombra are no less likely to be caught on surveillance than any other vampire. In addition, use of modern communication technology, including making a simple phone call, requires a Technology test at Difficulty 2 + Bane Severity as microphones have similar problems with the voice of a Lasombra as cameras with their image. Avoiding electronic vampire detection systems is also done at a penalty equal to Bane Severity.</p>\n<h3>Lasombra V5 Players Guide Variant: Callousness <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Whether from their tendency to select particularly ruthless mortals for the Embrace or that <em>something</em> gnawing at their psyche, Lasombra vampires tend toward the inhuman, regardless of sect or unlifestyle. Something just seems off about them and Lasombra who care about their sanity must be extra careful, lest their Beast take another bite out of their Humanity.</p>\n<p>When making a Remorse roll, deduct a number of dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Lasombra vampire. This cannot reduce the dice pool below one die.</p>\n<p><em>Chronicles that focus on Humanity and the loss of it can benefit from this variant, as can chronicles in historical settings that are light on technology.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 436)</small></h3>\n<p>In addition to the Clan’s usual problem with reflections (p. 59), Lasombra <em>antitribu</em> also have the “weakness” of being hunted relentlessly by the Sabbat, and targeted for destruction whenever their existence is made public.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-lasombraantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"436"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.9UJrjl7BF9Vg1iTm"},"ClAnQ5K5IGAHnezA":{"name":"Ravnos","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Animalism, Obfuscate, Presence</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Chimerstry, Fortitude</h5>\n<p>The Ravnos move like the rumors that surround them. They are the thief in the night, the <em>raksha</em> chased by the wind, the nightmare-dream too fearful to be real. Whether associated with the Romani folk of Europe or the grave-robbing <em>ghûl</em> of Western Asia, Kindred society burdens the Ravnos with prejudices of foulness, uncleanliness, and wickedness.</p>\n<p>With reputations like these, the Ravnos are considered outsiders even among those Kindred who do not ally themselves with Sects. Many young Ravnos tend toward nomadic unlives, moving from one domain to the next or hiding on the fringes of established territories where they can escape if local Kindred sentiment turns against them. This exacerbates their reputations as transients, gypsies, and vagrant scourges, but Ravnos vampires adapt well, thriving in their marginalized role. In fact, many choose to make themselves into the ravening terrors that other Kindred believe them to be.</p>\n<p>The more enlightened among the Ravnos follow a Clan ideology inspired by the cycle of reincarnation espoused by many Indian faiths. Among some young and unprincipled members of the Clan, however, this philosophy becomes a penchant for capriciousness or an excuse for chaos. It is these latter Kindred who give so many Ravnos a bad name, but even the devout seem bizarre to many vampires.</p>\n<p>The Ravnos practice a unique Discipline known as Chimerstry that convinces their enemies that they see things that do not exist. Chimerstry does much to convince Kindred that the Ravnos trade in lies and misdirection, but it can also prove to be a Deceiver’s salvation and ease the vagaries of an outcast unlife.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Deceivers</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Clan Ravnos often has a difficult time heeding the rigorous order of many Camarilla cities, and has no inherent love for the violence of the Sabbat. Thus, the Deceivers find themselves independent for lack of a more suitable option.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Young Ravnos often come from Eastern European Romani stock, with a relative paucity of “non-gypsy” <em>gadje</em> in the ranks. What few elders of the Clan may remain are presumed to come from Indian or Middle Eastern origins. Given that the Clan is widely spread and holds no traditional central domain, no consistent look can be said to be predominant, and any mendicant Kindred might be of Ravnos origin.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Many Ravnos take to the road instead of establishing permanent havens, dwelling temporarily among itinerant communities, at roadside rest stops, or even in vehicles. When a Deceiver does put down roots in a domain, his permanent haven is often away from high-profile Kindred territories. Havens in ethnic ghettos, industrial outskirts, and isolated geography are safest and most easily cultivated for the Ravnos.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Ravnos are scattered and suspicious, and those childer who don’t have the tendency toward self-sufficiency don’t last long. In many cases, a Ravnos will either never sire, or sire for companionship or safety, with little concern for how well a childe will fare as a vampire. Ravnos rarely seek out childer actively, instead drawing from those whose paths they cross on any given night. As such, the hardluck drifter reputation tends to follow the Deceivers.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Outsider and selfish Natures are common among the Clan. Physical and Social Attributes predominate, as do Talents and Skills. Savvy Ravnos develop a breadth of Backgrounds that can give them an edge when they’re on the move or in a tight spot, such as stashed Resources, defensible Domain, and a few Allies or Contacts.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Ravnos are a far-flung Clan, with little to unite them and an open acknowledgement that each Deceiver looks after his own interests first. That said, Ravnos often like to make a great show of Clan camaraderie and cultural ritual, even though they know that promises made to one another are as fleeting as whispers on a night wind. Deceivers have been known to ally against common enemies such as tyrant Princes or Sabbat pogroms, but these alliances quickly fade once the threat no longer exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>What? Those aren’t bones. They’re wind chimes. Everything is going to be fine. Relax.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Half the time, I think we take a lot of shit because other vampires think, “All Arabs look alike” and confuse us with these assholes.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>You can get into a lot of trouble if one of these bloodsuckers imagines you did him some awful wrong when all you were doing was checking out his girlfriend.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>There’s no justice. People think</em> we’re <em>the demons.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>They know how rough it is out there, and sometimes you can even convince them to give you a break.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Gaudy, vain, and easy marks. Of course, they probably think that about us, too.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>Nobody cops that much attitude unless he’s hiding something to be ashamed of.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>Be careful. You can either fool them or you can’t, but they can’t decide which one it is until they either take the bait or go berserk.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>Rat them out and you’ll have a chance.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>They love being lied to but hate it when someone tips them off that you’ve been lying.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>Tread carefully or these pricks will drink your soul and blame you for it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>Nothing wrong with these guys that staking them in their coffins and setting them on fire wouldn’t solve.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>I think these cocksuckers actually enjoy being hated by everyone else. Who else would set themselves up in the line of fire all the time?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>Hard times make for desperate terms.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>All the fancy costumes in the world don’t mean you’re not stealing or killing every time you feed, you fucking hypocrites.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>I’ve seen this movie before, and the ending is shit.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>Not usually a problem unless they start asking you to vote or bend the knee. Once the revolution’s over and they’re declaring themselves emperors, time to hit the road.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 42)</small></h3>\n<p>The Ravnos are doomed. The sun’s fire that incinerated their founder rages through the Blood of the clan, erupting from their very flesh if they ever settle down for long. If they slumber in the same place more than once in seven nights, roll a number of dice equal to their Bane Severity. They receive Aggravated damage equal to the number of 10s (critical results) rolled as they scorch from within. This happens every time they spend the day in a location where they’ve already slumbered less than a week before, and applies to Ravnos in torpor as well. What constitutes a location in this regard depends on the scope of the chronicle, but unless otherwise stated, two resting places need to be at least a mile apart to avoid triggering the Bane. Furthermore, a mobile haven, such as a movers’ truck, is safe so long as the place where the truck is parked is at least a mile from the last location. Ravnos characters cannot take the No Haven Flaw at character creation.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Unbirth Name <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Ravnos vampires rely on a single inviolable truth to anchor them as they ply their trade on the edge of the real: their name. Whatever name they went by at the time of their Embrace, the Tricksters take great care in hiding it, for anyone who knows this “unbirth name” gains power over them.</p>\n<p>Anyone who speaks a Ravnos’ unbirth name to their face receives a bonus equal to the Ravnos Bane Severity to resist their Discipline powers, and the Ravnos suffers an equal penalty to resist supernatural powers used by the name-wielding opponent. <em>If the regular Bane would be too oppressive or insignificant (in very stationary or nomadic chronicles respectively) this variant can help define the Ravnos clan instead.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 64)</small></h3>\n<p>A turbulent history makes the Ravnos slaves to their vices. Each Ravnos has a penchant for some sort of vice — lying, cruelty, or theft, for example. When presented with the opportunity to engage in that vice, the Ravnos must indulge it unless her player succeeds on a Self-Control or Instincts roll (difficulty 6).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-ravnos","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"64"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.ClAnQ5K5IGAHnezA"},"DWlckNQ7EK8lC204":{"name":"Country Gangrel","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Fortitude, Protean</h5>\n<p>The Country Gangrel resemble the independent and Camarilla Gangrel — they are savage, vicious hunters, more comfortable in the wilds between cities than the concrete jungles. The Sabbat has seen a influx of Gangrel converts from the Camarilla, and these Hunters easily lost themselves in stalking and killing. The Country Gangrel serve the Sabbat as assassins and scouts, using their command of animals to gather intelligence on the comings and goings of other Kindred. Travel between cities was never exactly safe, as Lupines seem to prowl any patch of land large enough to grow a few trees (or so Camarilla elders have always told their childer), but with the Hunters prowling about, looking for wayward neonates, itinerant Kindred face even more dire threats. This might sound like Camarilla propaganda, but the Country Gangrel do all they can to make it true. Free from the constraints of having to look human, these creatures relish the chance to hunt as wolves hunt — savagely and in packs. If they have a regret, it’s that their prey can’t usually run fast enough to make the hunt last.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Hunters</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Hunters have the same weakness as their non-Sabbat counterparts (p. 55). Each time a Country Gangrel frenzies, he gains a temporary animalistic feature, which may replace an existing temporary one. The mechanical impact of such a feature is up to Storyteller discretion.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Lost? Bad place for lost. Wanna run now?</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Gangrel V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 73)</small></h3>\n<p>Gangrel relate to their Beast much as other Kindred relate to the Gangrel: suspicious partnership. In frenzy, Gangrel gain one or more animal features: a physical trait, a smell, or a behavioral tic. These features last for one more night afterward, lingering like a hangover following debauchery. Each feature reduces one Attribute by 1 point – the Storyteller may decide that a forked tongue or bearlike.</p>\n<p>musk reduces Charisma, while batlike ears reduce Resolve (“all those distracting sounds”). If nothing immediately occurs to you, the feature reduces Intelligence or Manipulation.The number of features a Gangrel manifests equals their Bane Severity. If your character Rides the Wave of their frenzy (see p. 219) you can choose only one feature to manifest, thus taking only one penalty to their Attributes.</p>\n<h3>Gangrel V5 Players Guide Variant: Survival Instincts <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Nothing matters more to the Animals than survival. Among the most tenacious of all vampire clans, the Gangrel can endure almost anything and thrive in the harshest conditions. Where the Beast can often put a vampire in unfavorable situations, a Gangrel’s instincts are calibrated foremost to make sure they see another night.</p>\n<p>Subtract dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Gangrel from any roll to resist fear Frenzy. This cannot take the pool below one die.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is good for danger-filled chronicles where fire and sunlight are constant threats but where mental and social conflicts are less common, or if the troupe just doesn’t want to bother with tracking temporary dice penalties.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-countrygangrel","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"430"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.DWlckNQ7EK8lC204"},"Ee0Ork6T6IsyjXYE":{"name":"Tremere Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dominate, Hermetic Thaumaturgy</h5>\n<p>Centuries ago, a small group of Tremere, fearful that the Sabbat would destroy the Camarilla and their Clan in the process, defected to the smaller Sect. They offered their skills as thaumaturgists to the Sword of Caine, in exchange for safety from their former Clan.</p>\n<p>The Sabbat, not able to turn down the benefits of blood magic (despite how much the Tzimisce objected), took them in. Reaction from Clan Tremere was swift, of course. The Warlocks conjured a curse that branded every Tremere <em>antitribu</em> with a mark, one that only “true” Tremere could see. Unable to hide what they were, the Tremere <em>antitribu</em> stayed in chantries, providing support and knowledge to their comrades. This state of affairs continued for centuries. The Tremere <em>antitribu</em> contributed much to the Sabbat. They worked with the Tzimisce to create the Blood Brothers, they prevented the Camarilla Tremere from destroying the Sect from afar, and, of course, they tutored other Sabbat Cainites in blood magic. And then one horrible night, they all died, burning to death in a mysterious conflagration that afflicted each and every Tremere subject to the traitor’s mark. Interestingly, those few Sabbat Tremere who had defected <em>from</em> the Sabbat to the Camarilla were not destroyed, which might shed some light on the massacre’s origins.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Spellbinders</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Tremere <em>antitribu</em> were all immediately recognizable as traitors to Camarilla Warlocks. The mark wasn’t a physical disfigurement, but a change to the character’s very presence. This wasn’t a weakness that carries an immediate mechanical disadvantage, but once identified, the Tremere <em>antitribu</em> could expect to be hunted down and burned as soon as the Tremere can possibly manage it. They were also still susceptible to the power of the blood, gaining a +1 to all Vinculum rolls.</p>\n<p>In addition, Tremere <em>antitribu</em> were barred from taking leadership roles in the Sabbat.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>It’s always been inevitable. That’s why we joined up in the first place.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Tremere V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 97)</small></h3>\n<p>Once the clan was defined by a rigid hierarchy of Blood Bonds reaching from the top to the bottom of the Pyramid. But after the fall of Vienna, their Blood has recoiled and aborted all such connections. Tremere vitae can no longer Blood Bond other Kindred, though they themselves can be Bound by Kindred from other clans. A Tremere can still bind mortals and ghouls, though the corrupted vitae must be drunk an additional number of times equal to the vampire’s Bane Severity for the bond to form. Some theorize this change is the revenge of the Antediluvian devoured by Tremere, others attribute it to a simple mutation. Regardless, the clan studies their vitae intently to discover if the process can be reversed, and, indeed, determine if they would want to do so.</p>\n<h3>Tremere V5 Players Guide Variant: Stolen Blood <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>Descending from vampires who appropriated the curse of Caine, Tremere vampires are unable to utilize their Blood as effectively as Kindred of other clans. While they are able to call on it for Disciplines and animation, the inherent ability to perform superhuman feats is far harder for Tremere.</p>\n<p>When a Tremere vampire performs a Blood Surge (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> p. 218) they need to make Rouse Checks equal to their Bane Severity. If these Rouse Checks raise the vampire’s Hunger to 5 or higher, they can choose whether to back off their Blood Surge or to perform it and then immediately hit Hunger 5 afterward.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is useful when the chronicle focuses on vampire society and interaction over that of mortals, or if Blood Bonding is rare, such as in some Anarch domains.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 432)</small></h3>\n<p>The Tremere <em>antitribu</em> were all immediately recognizable as traitors to Camarilla Warlocks. The mark wasn’t a physical disfigurement, but a change to the character’s very presence. This wasn’t a weakness that carries an immediate mechanical disadvantage, but once identified, the Tremere <em>antitribu</em> could expect to be hunted down and burned as soon as the Tremere can possibly manage it. They were also still susceptible to the power of the blood, gaining a +1 to all Vinculum rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-tremereantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"432"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.Ee0Ork6T6IsyjXYE"},"F63GBfJbAkfB1gwV":{"name":"Nosferatu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Animalism, Obfuscate, Potence</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Obfuscate, Potence</h5>\n<p>Those who doubt that the Embrace is a curse need look no further than the Nosferatu. Twisted by the mark of Caine, members of Clan Nosferatu are warped by the Embrace into hideous monsters. As such, they skulk and keep to the shadows, and they often rouse the ire and mockery of other Kindred for their nightmarish appearances. Still others are so terrified or revolted by the Nosferatu that these warped Kindred have little social interaction at all.</p>\n<p>To their credit, the Nosferatu come to possess many of the whispered secrets of their reluctant fellows. The Sewer Rats enjoy a grudging respect as the information-brokers of the Kindred, given their supernatural acumen at stealth and the fact that many Kindred would rather ignore them than acknowledge them. Savvy Nosferatu exploit this for all it’s worth, turning the hypocrisy of other vampires to their own profit.</p>\n<p>On the whole, the Nosferatu condition is lonely and alienating. How they react to the Curse of Caine varies with their outlook and mental stamina, but it’s hard to be an object of utter revulsion and not let it shape one’s disposition toward one’s “Kindred” in some way. Some of the Sewer Rats are cruel, as blighted on the inside as they are on the outside, while others are tragic, wretched creatures who have been cursed with eternal outsider status through no choice of their own. A few of the Sewer Rats even position themselves as prophets or avatars of the Damned, physically embodying the haunting riddle, “A Beast I am, lest a Beast I become.”</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Sewer Rats</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Clan Nosferatu belongs at least nominally to the Camarilla, though many of its members become Autarkis or support the Anarchs rather than navigating the Ivory Tower’s vicious social labyrinth. Some even contend that the Nosferatu need the Camarilla, because without they have no buyers in their economy of secrets.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Physical horror is the lot of the Nosferatu, and their unsettling deformations are countless. No two Nosferatu share the exact same malformation, and the Clan is a freakshow of snarled limbs, fanged protrusions, hellish countenances, serpentine spines, ruined faces, spasmodic appendages, and even features not usually seen on the mortal stock from which the Nosferatu are drawn. The Sewer Rats often hide these disfigurements under shapeless robes and rags, but some exult in the discomfort their presence causes, and don’t bother disguising them. They may even emphasize them.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Nosferatu Kindred often make their havens far from the scorn and spite of other vampires. Whether they construct warrens in the sewers suggested by their nickname or they sculpt a sprawling nightmare-nest in the spire of a condemned church, Sewer Rats value secrecy and distance from rivals in their havens. Nosferatu of humbler means may well squat in an abandoned tenement or a disused alley. So long as it’s away from other Kindred, it’s a good haven.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Sewer Rats mostly fit into one of two categories. Some Nosferatu Embrace the damaged, flawed, outcast, or vile, feeling some degree of kinship with them. Other Embrace spitefully, dragging the beautiful or privileged into an immortal hell of disfigurement and monstrosity.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Many Nosferatu come from loner and outsider concepts, Natures, and Demeanors. They favor Physical and Mental Attributes over Social, and they tend to be balanced in their regard for Talents, Skills, and Knowledges. Nosferatu prefer to specialize rather than generalize in Backgrounds, favoring those that carry favors and information like Allies, Contacts, Mentor, and even a bit of Influence.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Occasionally, a coterie of Nosferatu becomes a brood or cult, often based around a remote warren. These can sprawl into vast “kingdoms” of Sewer Rats, who often exist without the knowledge of a domain’s Prince or Archbishop. The Nosferatu are among the Kindred most likely to share a communal haven, if only for protection in numbers. As well, Nosferatu share information with each other via networks — whether digital, personal, occult, or something less definable — that defy the comprehension of other Kindred.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Nobody down here but us boogeymen. Hey, whose head is this?</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Secrets for amnesty seems like a fair trade.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>Emits showers of sparks. Point away from face. Point at some other asshole instead.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>They want our souls — usually until they understand the stench of a Nosferatu’s soul.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>We have some common ground in being outsiders, but that doesn’t mean you shouldn’t be careful around them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Down in the sewers we see a lot of shit, but never so much as when a Giovanni heads our way.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>I try to keep to the shadows, but I’ll hang out under the streetlights when I know one of these creeps has my number.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>See my face? That’s what these maniacs’ minds look like. Except they’re still bleeding in there.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>Peddlers of lies, they make great patsies, but they sure can bear a grudge.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>Sell them dirt on each other and they’ll scratch each other’s eyes out. It’s the only way to come out ahead with them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>They’re not even vampires, you know that? They did something way wrong back in the day and now it’s stuck to them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>I look like this ‘cause I</em> have <em>to. These fuckers</em> choose <em>to. That’s how nature says, “Stay away.”</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>High and mighty, until they need you. Charge them double when they come calling. They can afford it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> “I never even knew my father!” <em>Cry me a fucking river.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>I’ll be damned, they think their claims of domain extend into the sewers.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Either monumentally gullible or Bible-grade wicked; either way, you’re better off when they’re somewhere else.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>Sure, I’ll buy what you’re selling. For tonight, at least.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 85)</small></h3>\n<p>Hideous and vile, all Nosferatu count as having the Repulsive Flaw (-2) and can never increase their rating in the Looks Merit. In addition, any attempt to disguise themselves as non-deformed incur a penalty to your dice pool equal to your character’s Bane Severity (this includes the Obfuscate powers Mask of a Thousand Faces and Impostor’s Guise). Note that most Nosferatu do not breach the Masquerade by just being seen. They are perceived by mortals to be grotesque and often terrifying, but not supernaturally so.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Infestation <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>No matter where they go or try to hide, swarms of vermin follow the Nosferatu. Some are plagued by swarms of rats, others act as a beacon to roaches, while still others cannot get rid of the damn pigeons or their leavings. In many cases all of the above and more. No matter where they make their haven, they soon drive all other inhabitants away. Even a quick stop can provide unwelcome guests that stay long after the Sewer Rat leaves.</p>\n<p>A Nosferatu Haven is always infested with vermin<em>,</em> causing a penalty equal to two plus their Bane Severity to any attempt at an activity (by anyone) that requires concentration, as well as social tests at the Storyteller’s discretion. In addition, any time a Nosferatu vampire spends a scene at an enclosed location, the vermin infestation yields a similar effect, though the penalty is equal only to the vampire’s Bane Severity. Any attempt to (temporarily) control the vermin with Animalism is done at a penalty equal to Bane Severity. Note that with this Bane, Nosferatu are not necessarily deformed, though they can still be weird-looking, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>Try this variant Bane if you want your chronicle’s Nosferatu to be repulsive in ways other than how they look, and to provide them with a more visible presence. Be aware that Nosferatu in this chronicle are unlikely to be invited to Elysium, unless they bring their own pest control crew.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-nosferatu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"62"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.F63GBfJbAkfB1gwV"},"HVR54IRhr2oCunxU":{"name":"City Gangrel","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Fortitude, Obfuscate, Protean</h5>\n<p>The Sabbat boasts two Gangrel lines, the City and the Country Gangrel. The City Gangrel remind some Kindred of coyotes — they are creatures well-suited for wilderness life, but they adapt to an urban existence quite smoothly. The high preponderance of prey in the cities, coupled with the Gangrel’s inherent mutability of blood, has allowed the line to flourish. Their association with the Sabbat means that they Embrace prolifically, but also that their unlife expectancy is short.</p>\n<p>Unlike Country Gangrel, who can afford to let their animalistic ways take over and their appearances grow truly bestial, the City Gangrel must either adopt a more Nosferatu-like approach to unlife or blend in with their prey. To outward appearance, then, these latter Kindred seem human, dress as appropriate to their area and the social class that they mimic. But City Gangrel are largely Sabbat vampires, and that means they are predators. Any disguise they adopt is strictly that, camouflage to allow them to get close enough to bite.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Coyotes</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> City Gangrel suffer the same weakness as the main line of the Clan (p. 55). Whenever a City Gangrel frenzies, he gains a temporary animalistic feature of some kind (which may replace an existing temporary one). The mechanical impact of such a feature is up to Storyteller discretion. City Gangrel tend to develop features reminiscent of the animals commonly found in urban environments — rats, dogs, cats, pigeons, and even insects.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>The new community center is finally open. Call up the pack.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Gangrel V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 73)</small></h3>\n<p>Gangrel relate to their Beast much as other Kindred relate to the Gangrel: suspicious partnership. In frenzy, Gangrel gain one or more animal features: a physical trait, a smell, or a behavioral tic. These features last for one more night afterward, lingering like a hangover following debauchery. Each feature reduces one Attribute by 1 point – the Storyteller may decide that a forked tongue or bearlike.</p>\n<p>musk reduces Charisma, while batlike ears reduce Resolve (“all those distracting sounds”). If nothing immediately occurs to you, the feature reduces Intelligence or Manipulation.The number of features a Gangrel manifests equals their Bane Severity. If your character Rides the Wave of their frenzy (see p. 219) you can choose only one feature to manifest, thus taking only one penalty to their Attributes.</p>\n<h3>Gangrel V5 Players Guide Variant: Survival Instincts <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Nothing matters more to the Animals than survival. Among the most tenacious of all vampire clans, the Gangrel can endure almost anything and thrive in the harshest conditions. Where the Beast can often put a vampire in unfavorable situations, a Gangrel’s instincts are calibrated foremost to make sure they see another night.</p>\n<p>Subtract dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Gangrel from any roll to resist fear Frenzy. This cannot take the pool below one die.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is good for danger-filled chronicles where fire and sunlight are constant threats but where mental and social conflicts are less common, or if the troupe just doesn’t want to bother with tracking temporary dice penalties.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-citygangrel","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"429"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.HVR54IRhr2oCunxU"},"KZ3q8qYC2PvY4Fdj":{"name":"Blood Brothers","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/bloodbrothers.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Fortitude, Potence, Sanguinus</h5>\n<p>The Blood Brothers are Kindred only in a very loose sense. They are Sabbat shock troops, created by Tremere and Tzimisce sorcerers in order for the Sword of Caine to have loyal soldiers that can still pass for human. The process of creation (for the Blood Brothers do not undergo an Embrace) washes away any individuality and personality, leaving only strong, savage, and near-mindless quasi-vampires. Blood Brothers are usually created in small groups of three to 10. These groups — called “circles” — become family units connected by a kind of hive mind. The Sabbat also usually arranges for a Tzimisce to fleshcraft the circle’s faces so that they are identical. The Frankensteins are a secret weapon for the Sabbat for several reasons. The Sabbat finds that while the loyalty of the Blood Brothers is beyond reproach, they don’t possess much in the way of creativity. They aren’t capable of trickery, clever tactics, or improvisation, and unless another vampire can direct them in the field in real time (which isn’t impossible, given modern communication technology), they aren’t good for much beyond simple, direct missions. Second, they are difficult and risky to create. Creating a circle of Blood Brothers means killing half a dozen people. While the Sabbat doesn’t have a problem with that in itself, a pattern of disappearances can clue in mortal authorities and Camarilla watchers alike that the Sword of Caine is up to something. Finally, the Blood Brothers are more useful as a surprise attack, and survivors of those attacks run the risk of passing along knowledge of the Brothers’ weaknesses. Creating too many, even if it were possible or practical, would mean that eventually some Camarilla Tremere would figure out how to hurt them, or even how to reverse the magic that keeps them mindless, loyal, and useful. Some rumors say that this has already happened, but that the Tremere aren’t willing to share this knowledge with the Camarilla just yet.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Frankensteins</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Blood Brothers belong to the Sabbat exclusively. A Blood Brother that comes through the creation process with his individuality intact is destroyed.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> A Blood Brother often looks identical to the others in his circle, barring any burns or other marks that he might have picked up in battle. They usually dress alike. The members of the circle grow agitated when they see each other as “different.”</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Blood Brothers stay in whatever haven is provided for them by the Sabbat. As long as the circle is allowed to stay together, they don’t seem to care much about amenities.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> A Blood Brother’s mortal life is irrelevant. All that a person needs to do to become a Blood Brother is cross the wrong vampire’s path on the wrong night. The Sabbat kills the target, removes his body hair, smooths over his features, and places him with four or five others, and after that no trace of the manthat-was remains.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Blood Brothers prioritize Physical Attributes and Talents. Nature and Demeanor are usually the same; common choices are Conformist, Bravo, and Monster. Blood Brothers don’t usually have much in the way of Backgrounds apart from Generation and Mentor (their handler or creator).</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Blood Brothers cannot Embrace. If they attempt it, the mortal simply dies. In addition, the Frankensteins literally feel each other’s pain. When a Blood Brother suffers a wound penalty, all members of the circle suffer the same penalty for the next turn. If two Blood Brothers are wounded, only the greater wound penalty applies. Blood Brothers do not continually suffer these penalties unless one of them suffers a new wound.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Stranger. Kill? Watch. Watching. Kill? Watching. Kill?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The bloodline as a whole has no organization. It is loyal to the Sabbat, and the members are otherwise loyal to their circles. The Blood Brothers treat their handlers with complete loyalty, bordering on sycophancy. They treat other Sabbat Kindred with quiet deference.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Anyone Not Sabbat:</strong> <em>Kill. Do not obey.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Obey. Do not kill.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 396)</small></h3>\n<p>Blood Brothers cannot Embrace. If they attempt it, the mortal simply dies. In addition, the Frankensteins literally feel each other’s pain. When a Blood Brother suffers a wound penalty, all members of the circle suffer the same penalty for the next turn. If two Blood Brothers are wounded, only the greater wound penalty applies. Blood Brothers do not continually suffer these penalties unless one of them suffers a new wound.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-bloodbrothers","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"396"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.KZ3q8qYC2PvY4Fdj"},"Kbrp9OcbY80FkzV2":{"name":"Assamite Sorcerers","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/assamitesorcerers.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dur-An-Ki, Quietus (Sanguinis)</h5>\n<p>Outsiders to Clan Assamite don’t see the caste divisions clearly, but the Saracens have a history of blood magic that predates that of the Tremere. Assamite sorcerers began in the Second City, conscripted or lured into the Clan with the promise of immortality in which to perfect their magic. They were tasked with providing magical support, and for centuries, they were simply one caste of the Assamite Clan.</p>\n<p>And then came the Tremere. The Assamite sorcerers found themselves facing foes as magically tenacious as they were, foes that were quite happy to steal their knowledge. The sorcerers fought against this new menace as best they could, but then the Tremere levied their blood curse on the line. The sorcerers became ob- 433 sessed with finding a cure, an obsession which remains tonight.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Magi (singular, “Magus”)</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The line as a whole has practiced the art for so long that it permeates their blood, making each individual sorcerer stand out like a beacon to anyone with supernatural perception. Any use of a supernatural power on a sorcerer for purposes of perception enjoys a -2 difficulty. Additionally, attempts to penetrate a sorcerer’s supernatural concealment using an opposed power are considered to operate one level higher than normal (so a character with Auspex 2 trying to penetrate a sorcerer’s Obfuscate has an effective Auspex of 3).</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>We were calling down gentle rain and terrible storms when your people were still making crude charcoal marks on cave walls. Don’t presume to know us.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 433)</small></h3>\n<p>The line as a whole has practiced the art for so long that it permeates their blood, making each individual sorcerer stand out like a beacon to anyone with supernatural perception. Any use of a supernatural power on a sorcerer for purposes of perception enjoys a -2 difficulty. Additionally, attempts to penetrate a sorcerer’s supernatural concealment using an opposed power are considered to operate one level higher than normal (so a character with Auspex 2 trying to penetrate a sorcerer’s Obfuscate has an effective Auspex of 3).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-assamitesorcerers","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"433"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.Kbrp9OcbY80FkzV2"},"Klzom5Lkr6rKNegh":{"name":"Children of Osiris","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/childrenofosiris.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Bardo + two Disciplines from the original Clan or Bloodline</h5>\n<p>Egyptian mythology abounds with tales of the rivalry between Set and his brother, Osiris. Knowledgeable vampires are aware that the Followers of Set claim descent from an Antediluvian who took his name from that Egyptian serpent-god (though the Setites certainly seem to think Set actually <em>was</em> the god). If that’s the case, such Kindred wonder, who was Osiris? A mortal spellcaster that was Embraced? Set’s childe? Or perhaps another Antediluvian, lost to time and destroyed millennia ago? Whatever the truth about Set, there was once a powerful vampire called Osiris. He was a philosopher and a scholar in an Egyptian court (whose dynasty is unknown), Embraced by an unknown vampire who believed that his knowledge of the law would make him a useful ally in the battle to bring Egypt under Kindred influence. Osiris, however, was horrified by his new condition. Rather than meet the sun, though, he became determined to face and banish his personal demons, and especially his own Beast. His studies on sublimating the vampiric impulses may have helped form the basis for the search for Golconda. At the very least, they expanded it.</p>\n<p>The Children of Osiris might have become a Clan and continued into the modern nights, but Set slew Osiris. As mortal legendry has it, Set dismembered his “brother” and scattered his body throughout the land of Egypt. His sister, Isis (perhaps another vampire, but no one still extant seems to know for sure) found the pieces and reassembled them, and impossibly, Osiris was resurrected. Death, however, had taken its toll. His blood was inert, and he could no longer create more childer. This, however, did not stop him from spreading his path.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Since even the Kindred who know about the Children of Osiris assume them all to be dead, they do not have a common nickname.</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Strictly speaking, the Children of Osiris are a Sect, rather than a bloodline. They do not (and, by virtue of their own laws, cannot) join the Sabbat or the Camarilla.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Since the Children can hail from any Clan or bloodline, their appearance can vary widely. They all shave their heads every evening upon awakening, and when in their temples they wear white robes adorned with mystical symbols and hieroglyphs. When leaving their temples on business, they dress simply and stay inconspicuous.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The few remaining Children of Osiris in the world dwell in one of several temples. These temples are well-hidden and kept secret from other Kindred, meaning that they tend to be in areas that don’t boast much of a vampire population.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Children of Osiris renounce their Clans because they hate their condition, but do not wish to commit suicide. The search for Golconda leads some Kindred to the Children, while others encounter the Sect after running afoul of the Followers of Set.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Children of Osiris can be from any Clan (except the Followers of Set; while the Children might be willing to accept a truly repentant Setite, the situation has never come up). The character can have any combination of Attributes and Abilities, and they retain whatever knowledge of Disciplines they had before joining. Nature and Demeanor tend to be the same — the Children of Osiris are straightforward and honest.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> As appropriate for the character’s original Clan.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Each temple is under the supervision of an elder Child of Osiris (called the Undying King), and the entire Sect answers to a mysterious vampire called the Grand Undying King, rumored to be a direct childe of Osiris from before he lost the ability to Embrace. The Sect focuses on maintaining their Humanity, which is of course a difficult enough concern, but also thwarting the machinations of the Followers of Set without the Serpents knowing they still exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I could meet the sun, but I fear my heart would not balance the feather of Ma’at. Until I am sure, I will remain.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>The best option for most Kindred, and still not nearly good enough.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>If they are less evil than the Serpents, it is only because the Sword of Caine occasionally kills them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Osiris came back from death to avenge himself on Set. And so shall we.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 420)</small></h3>\n<p>As appropriate for the character’s original Clan.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-childrenofosiris","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"420"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.Klzom5Lkr6rKNegh"},"L0Fmk1Jj5rGZJPY4":{"name":"Lhiannan","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lhiannan.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Koldunism, Presence</h5>\n<p>The Lhiannan boasted a strange creation story. They claimed that their founder — a vampire simply known as the Crone — became bonded with a spirit of the forests. This spirit was a desperate, jealous being that could apparently see that people would destroy the forests and burn the trees that comprised them. Joining with an undying predator, then, was apparently the spirit’s attempt at dodging this fate. Whether or not any of that is true, the Lhiannan did have a connection to the natural world that no other vampire, not even the Gangrel, can equal. Whenever a Lhiannan Embraced, a shard of that original spirit broke off and empowered the new Cainite. This spirit-shard allowed Lhiannan to use their Ogham Discipline, which, in turn, enabled the style of unlife that the Lhiannan enjoyed. It also made the Lhiannan territorial above all else, and the Druids were quite limited in their mobility. The Lhiannan, much like the Telyavelic Tremere (see p. 438) found common cause with the pagan peoples of Europe. This, unfortunately, put them into conflict with Cainites and mortals who followed the Christian faith.</p>\n<p>The bloodline was never numerous (especially since creating a new Lhiannan meant that the sire grew weaker), but the fires of the Inquisition and the spread of the Christian religion took a harsh toll on the Druids. By the end of the 14th century, they had all been destroyed. In modern times, though, Gangrel trade rumors of powerful Kindred in European forests decorated with strange, bloody symbols, so perhaps some of them survived — or perhaps the forest spirit that initially created the bloodline has resurfaced.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Druids</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Lhiannan did not survive to see the dawning of the Sabbat or the Camarilla. In historical nomenclature, they were decidedly on the Low Clan side of the social divide, and most Cainites probably wouldn’t have known them from the Gangrel. Lhiannan rarely joined coteries, limited as they were in mobility.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Druids resembled the native people of the regions of Europe that they called home — the British Isles, for the most part.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Lhiannan made their havens in the forests of Europe, constructing simple dwellings or finding natural shelter. They would sometimes find a man-made haven and kill or enslave the inhabitants, or very rarely eke out an existence on the periphery of human civilization by taking the role of a shaman or wise woman.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Since performing the Embrace meant growing weaker, the Druids only created childer out of sheer loneliness or when they found someone who truly impressed them. A pagan spirit-speaker with real devotion to her craft, or a woodsman with impeccable respect for the forests might be enough to get a Lhiannan’s attention. The Lhiannan refused to Embrace Christians, Jews, or Muslims.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> All Lhiannan were capable of surviving in the wilderness, and so Physical Attributes, Skills, and Talents were important. A Lhiannan acting as a counsel to a mortal settlement was likely to have higher Social Attributes and appropriate Abilities. Herd and Retainers (animals) were common Backgrounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Lhiannan were part nature spirit, and the mark of their inhumanity ran strong within them. All difficulties to detect their nature via Auspex were reduced by two, and even normal humans felt vaguely uncomfortable in their presence. Additionally, any Lhiannan who left her territory became agitated — all dice pools were reduced by one die per week (to a minimum of the character’s Stamina) that she was gone. Once she re-entered her territory, her dice pools returned to normal within a few hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Once a Lhiannan Embraced a childe, she generally kept the new vampire around for a few years until that childe was ready to fend for himself (and the novelty of having someone else around wore off). The sire would then send the childe away to find his own haven and territory. The Lhiannan were generally aware of members of their bloodline whose territories were adjacent, but there was no system of communication in place. This, of course, may have contributed to their eventual destruction.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>When the trees are pulling you apart, you may feel free to call out for your god. If he saves you, I shall give up my glade.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>High Clans:</strong> <em>No one who wears such heavy, false robes or who shies away from blood is a leader to me.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Low Clans:</strong> <em>By accepting the title of “low,” they show it to be accurate.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 424)</small></h3>\n<p>The Lhiannan were part nature spirit, and the mark of their inhumanity ran strong within them. All difficulties to detect their nature via Auspex were reduced by two, and even normal humans felt vaguely uncomfortable in their presence. Additionally, any Lhiannan who left her territory became agitated — all dice pools were reduced by one die per week (to a minimum of the character’s Stamina) that she was gone. Once she re-entered her territory, her dice pools returned to normal within a few hours.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-lhiannan","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"424"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.L0Fmk1Jj5rGZJPY4"},"LUWWqgXa7F26mAn9":{"name":"Ravnos Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Chimerstry, Fortitude</h5>\n<p>Shortly after the formation of the Sabbat, a small group of Ravnos decided that following the orders of their elders and searching for the same “truths” the Clan always had wasn’t what they wanted out of unlife. They wanted the freedom that the Sabbat promised, and so they renounced their heritage and became part of the Sword of Caine. From there, they Embraced just as any Sabbat vampires did, and so the Ravnos <em>antitribu</em> are still an important part of the Sabbat. The Rogues lurk amongst the nomadic Sabbat packs, acting as scouts and con artists. The Ravnos <em>antitribu</em> may be thieves and liars, but if one gives his solemn word (accompanied by spitting blood into his hand), he will keep that vow even unto Final Death. They share their Clan’s propensity for vice, and for nomadic unlife. The freedom to pull up stakes and leave an area whenever trouble comes knocking — combined with the freedom to feed as they please — makes the Sabbat the only conceivable way of unlife for these Cainites.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Rogues</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Each Ravnos <em>antitribu</em>, just like their parent Clan (p. 65), has a penchant for some sort of vice — breaking taboos is hard-wired into the Rogues just as it is into the Ravnos proper. When presented with the opportunity to engage in that vice, the Rogue must indulge it unless her player succeeds on a Self-Control or Instincts roll (difficulty 6).</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Freedom means not giving a fuck about</em> marime<em>.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Ravnos V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 42)</small></h3>\n<p>The Ravnos are doomed. The sun’s fire that incinerated their founder rages through the Blood of the clan, erupting from their very flesh if they ever settle down for long. If they slumber in the same place more than once in seven nights, roll a number of dice equal to their Bane Severity. They receive Aggravated damage equal to the number of 10s (critical results) rolled as they scorch from within. This happens every time they spend the day in a location where they’ve already slumbered less than a week before, and applies to Ravnos in torpor as well. What constitutes a location in this regard depends on the scope of the chronicle, but unless otherwise stated, two resting places need to be at least a mile apart to avoid triggering the Bane. Furthermore, a mobile haven, such as a movers’ truck, is safe so long as the place where the truck is parked is at least a mile from the last location. Ravnos characters cannot take the No Haven Flaw at character creation.</p>\n<h3>Ravnos V5 Players Guide Variant: Unbirth Name <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Ravnos vampires rely on a single inviolable truth to anchor them as they ply their trade on the edge of the real: their name. Whatever name they went by at the time of their Embrace, the Tricksters take great care in hiding it, for anyone who knows this “unbirth name” gains power over them.</p>\n<p>Anyone who speaks a Ravnos’ unbirth name to their face receives a bonus equal to the Ravnos Bane Severity to resist their Discipline powers, and the Ravnos suffers an equal penalty to resist supernatural powers used by the name-wielding opponent. <em>If the regular Bane would be too oppressive or insignificant (in very stationary or nomadic chronicles respectively) this variant can help define the Ravnos clan instead.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 431)</small></h3>\n<p>Each Ravnos <em>antitribu</em>, just like their parent Clan (p. 65), has a penchant for some sort of vice — breaking taboos is hard-wired into the Rogues just as it is into the Ravnos proper. When presented with the opportunity to engage in that vice, the Rogue must indulge it unless her player succeeds on a Self-Control or Instincts roll (difficulty 6).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-ravnosantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"431"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.LUWWqgXa7F26mAn9"},"N8HOnnqxoaKAuRsT":{"name":"Nosferatu Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Obfuscate, Potence</h5>\n<p>The Nosferatu of the Sabbat aren’t too different from their Camarilla brethren. Both branches of the Clan tend toward subterranean havens, and both branches use their considerable skill in stealth and control of vermin to traffic in information. Just as the more beautiful Kindred of the Camarilla must occasionally get their feet dirty going to see a Nosferatu information broker, the Cainites call upon their deformed comrades for secrets and tactics. Interestingly, the Nosferatu <em>antitribu</em> are less brutal than many Sabbat Kindred. This may be because they know what it is to be a monster — it’s there, in their faces, from the first night of undeath. They don’t need to overcompensate, and so bloody excesses are unnecessary. This by no means makes the Creeps human, or even especially humane. It just means that if a Nosferatu <em>antitribu</em> does engage in wholesale slaughter or horrific violence, he’s doing it for a specific reason, rather than just for fun. But the Creeps may have another reason for avoiding overt depravity — they wish to stay as far under the radar as possible. While the Nosferatu <em>antitribu</em> are careful to avoid letting Clan business get out into the rumor mill, gossip occasionally surfaces that the Clan is terrified of its Antediluvian founder, more so than other Clans of the Sabbat.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Creeps</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Sabbat Nosferatu are just as hideous as their Camarilla counterparts, and suffer the same weakness (p. 63): they all have an Appearance score of zero, and they may never improve it</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>There’s always a scarier beastie. Look: You’re scarier than Dahmer or Gacy. I’m scarier than you. And… well. There are things scarier than me. Let’s leave it at that.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Nosferatu V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 85)</small></h3>\n<p>Hideous and vile, all Nosferatu count as having the Repulsive Flaw (-2) and can never increase their rating in the Looks Merit. In addition, any attempt to disguise themselves as non-deformed incur a penalty to your dice pool equal to your character’s Bane Severity (this includes the Obfuscate powers Mask of a Thousand Faces and Impostor’s Guise). Note that most Nosferatu do not breach the Masquerade by just being seen. They are perceived by mortals to be grotesque and often terrifying, but not supernaturally so.</p>\n<h3>Nosferatu V5 Players Guide Variant: Infestation <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>No matter where they go or try to hide, swarms of vermin follow the Nosferatu. Some are plagued by swarms of rats, others act as a beacon to roaches, while still others cannot get rid of the damn pigeons or their leavings. In many cases all of the above and more. No matter where they make their haven, they soon drive all other inhabitants away. Even a quick stop can provide unwelcome guests that stay long after the Sewer Rat leaves.</p>\n<p>A Nosferatu Haven is always infested with vermin<em>,</em> causing a penalty equal to two plus their Bane Severity to any attempt at an activity (by anyone) that requires concentration, as well as social tests at the Storyteller’s discretion. In addition, any time a Nosferatu vampire spends a scene at an enclosed location, the vermin infestation yields a similar effect, though the penalty is equal only to the vampire’s Bane Severity. Any attempt to (temporarily) control the vermin with Animalism is done at a penalty equal to Bane Severity. Note that with this Bane, Nosferatu are not necessarily deformed, though they can still be weird-looking, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>Try this variant Bane if you want your chronicle’s Nosferatu to be repulsive in ways other than how they look, and to provide them with a more visible presence. Be aware that Nosferatu in this chronicle are unlikely to be invited to Elysium, unless they bring their own pest control crew.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-nosferatuantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"430"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.N8HOnnqxoaKAuRsT"},"NUIlINIY1sjR3dZP":{"name":"Tremere","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Auspex, Blood Sorcery, Dominate</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dominate, Hermetic Thaumaturgy</h5>\n<p>In nights long lost to the passage of time, the Tremere existed, though they were <em>something else</em>. Those early Tremere then made a bargain — or wrought a spell, or any number of other harrowing methods attributed to the Clan — that changed them from what they had been into the vampires they are tonight. Some claim they stole the Curse of Caine from a torpid Antediluvian, or that they concocted the flawed immortality of the Kindred from the stolen vitae of other vampires. Such mysterious origins, which some describe as treacherous or even blasphemous, haunt the Tremere, as the other Clans look upon them with mistrust and suspicion. The history — and, indeed, the modern legacy — of the Tremere is one marked by Clan war, centuries-old grudges, and the stain of unwholesome mysteries long left unsolved. Tonight, Clan Tremere is a Clan shaped by its practice of blood sorcery. A flexible Discipline, Thaumaturgy is heavily entrenched within the Tremere, and they maintain cultic havens known as chantries to study its uses and share secrets among each other. To the Tremere, blood is both sustenance and the source of mystical power; they gather in their witch houses to further their understanding of the vitae that is such a focal point of their unlives.</p>\n<p>Beyond the practice of Thaumaturgy itself, the Warlocks are known for their close-knit hierarchy. They hail from the Old World, with an established power base in Vienna to which all members of the Clan answer to some degree. Although they may be one of the youngest Clans — in the terms with which deathless creatures like vampires measure time — they are as adept at the Jyhad as any Kindred. Beset by enemies who call them usurpers and backed by allies who may or may not defend the alliances they force, the Tremere have evolved to be self-sufficient. Indeed, many who eye them warily think they possess too great an edge, with their flexible Discipline and protective Clan and chantry structure, and move against them both secretly and overtly.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Warlocks</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Clan Tremere considers itself one of the pillars of the Camarilla. It is rumored that they once performed a ritual that all but eradicated those of the Clan not loyal to the Ivory Tower (and thus the Tremere’s pyramidal hierarchy).</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Tremere often have two distinct presentations: a traditional and severe public aspect and a much more eldritch mien better suited to wielding their blood sorceries. When out in public or at Kindred events, the Tremere favor conservative suits and dresses and muted tones. When in their chantries or convening with others of their Clan, they often prefer robes decorated with subtle occult symbols or garb with various folds and pockets for their bizarre ritual ingredients.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Many Tremere rely on a central chantry the Clan maintains in cities where it has a notable presence. More solitary Warlocks develop private havens, with all of the trappings one might expect from an occult scholar, from libraries to alchemical laboratories to moonlit balcony observatories and even more sinister oubliettes where vivisected “research subjects” bleed according to experimental Tremere-controlled stimuli.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Tremere draw from a fairly narrow pool of potential acolytes. Those who have an awareness of the supernatural, who are driven to succeed, who seek answers that elude less inquisitive individuals, yet who also have the discipline to heed the edicts of the hierarchy make good Tremere. This isn’t to say that individualists don’t have room in the Clan; rather, those who go their own way may well find themselves leading a chantry — or greeting the sun if their interests don’t align with those of the pyramid.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Mental Attributes and Knowledges are prominent among the Tremere. Many have high Courage and Willpower, but are somewhat lacking in Conscience or Conviction. They often favor Backgrounds that heighten their relationship to the Clan, like Mentor, Status, and Retainers (whom an accomplished Warlock may craft from otherwise inert components).</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The hallmark of Clan Tremere is “the pyramid,” the rigid hierarchy that governs the Clan and makes it the most organized of all the Kindred lineages. With many levels of membership, internal factions, and circles of mystery, the Tremere hierarchy presents a unified face to those outside the Clan, and is almost as unified behind the scenes. Still, the pyramid inculcates more than its share of paranoia, as both rogue Warlocks and a competitive environment of academic occultism pits each acolyte against her peers to the greater accomplishment of the Clan.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>When the world refuses to give, he who makes himself a master of the world may still take.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>They show signs of being able to subvert our rituals, so the time to tolerate them has expired.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>Hubris and a lack of self-discipline have laid low the Clan of warrior-poets.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>We pursue the secrets of the world and they pursue the secrets in its dark places. When too many know, secrets are no longer secrets.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Theirs is a simple but formidable power. Fortunately, their blood is as susceptible to mastery as that of all Kindred.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Their clumsy understanding of the secret ways will shackle them to empty ritual rather than set them free to work their wills.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>A once-accomplished lineage dragged into the muck by foul associations.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>Once we can synthesize their stochastic perception, the Kindred will no longer have to brook their disruptive caprice.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>Those involuntarily placed outside the system can prove fruitful allies or accomplices.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>Disorder sows its own punishments, yet I am happy to expedite those results.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>Form follows function, which makes the ongoing prominence of these self-worshipers such a dispiriting enigma.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>Peel away the rot and idolatry and all that’s left is the carcass of outmoded mummery.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>Caesar shall have his due, and to our benefit, his love of lucre blinds him to true power.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>As Vesalius gathered his cadavers, so do we solicit volunteers in the understanding of our sciences.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>A sundered house still offers shelter.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>They do not understand their own ends, and damn themselves thus.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>In the absence of structure, autarchy yields only the brutishness of lesser Kindred.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 97)</small></h3>\n<p>Once the clan was defined by a rigid hierarchy of Blood Bonds reaching from the top to the bottom of the Pyramid. But after the fall of Vienna, their Blood has recoiled and aborted all such connections. Tremere vitae can no longer Blood Bond other Kindred, though they themselves can be Bound by Kindred from other clans. A Tremere can still bind mortals and ghouls, though the corrupted vitae must be drunk an additional number of times equal to the vampire’s Bane Severity for the bond to form. Some theorize this change is the revenge of the Antediluvian devoured by Tremere, others attribute it to a simple mutation. Regardless, the clan studies their vitae intently to discover if the process can be reversed, and, indeed, determine if they would want to do so.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Stolen Blood <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>Descending from vampires who appropriated the curse of Caine, Tremere vampires are unable to utilize their Blood as effectively as Kindred of other clans. While they are able to call on it for Disciplines and animation, the inherent ability to perform superhuman feats is far harder for Tremere.</p>\n<p>When a Tremere vampire performs a Blood Surge (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> p. 218) they need to make Rouse Checks equal to their Bane Severity. If these Rouse Checks raise the vampire’s Hunger to 5 or higher, they can choose whether to back off their Blood Surge or to perform it and then immediately hit Hunger 5 afterward.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is useful when the chronicle focuses on vampire society and interaction over that of mortals, or if Blood Bonding is rare, such as in some Anarch domains.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 68)</small></h3>\n<p>Tremere dependency on blood is even more pronounced than that of other Kindred. It takes only two draughts of another vampire’s blood for a Tremere to become blood bound instead of the normal three — the first drink counts as if the Tremere had taken two drinks. (For more information on the blood bond, see p. 286). The elders of the Clan are well aware of this, and seek to impart loyalty to the Clan by forcing all neonate Warlocks to drink of the (transubstantiated) blood of the seven Tremere elders soon after their Embrace.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-tremere","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"68"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.NUIlINIY1sjR3dZP"},"NgF8imEDMgGDx9B3":{"name":"Hecata","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/hecata.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Auspex, Fortitude, Oblivion</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Fortitude, Mortis</h5>\n<p><strong>Nicknames:</strong> Necromancers, Devil-Kindred, Lazarenes, The Clan of Death</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>The Kindred are simply one expression of death, a still-vital consciousness clad in a flesh of fading lividity, or even vitality... and such expressions can be reinterpreted, as it were.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p>A motley collection of necromantic vampire bloodlines, the Hecata clan are united in the pursuit of a single subject: Death. They are students of the afterlife and resurrectionists of the dead — or worse. Selling their services to the highest bidder, or acting in their own interests, there are few who can hide from the surveillance of those who can summon and command the very spirits of the deceased.</p>\n<p>The Unclean. Flesh-eaters. Those who commune with the dead, and those who convince the dead to speak on their behalf.</p>\n<p>The Hecata are anomalous among the Kindred clans, in that they are practically a sect unto themselves, a clan consisting of strange bloodlines and even some genuine familial ties, all built upon the reverence of and occasional subjugation of death itself. Comprising everything from ancestorworshiping Old World crime families to effigywielding priests to degenerate flesh-eating monsters, the Hecata clan has an unwholesome fascination with the mortal coil in common among its member bloodlines.</p>\n<p>A morbid ritual recently bound together these disparate consanguinities into a “united” clan proper, and those disparate bloodlines make the clan seem more like a sect than a traditional lineage. Neither the clan’s impure predilections nor its penchant for playing all other sides against one other earn them much favor with Kindred society at large. And yet they have endured to bedevil the modern nights with their undeniable necromantic acumen.</p>\n<h3>Who Are the Hecata?</h3>\n<p>Being Hecata often means inheriting an almost superstitious catechism of proscriptions, ancestral guilt, and ritual that informs not only how clan members deal with each other, but also how they deal with the dead. To outsiders, it’s incomprehensible, but to the Lazarenes, it’s familial tradition. What the elders say, goes, because the very foundations of the clan reside with a patriarch who yielded up his own mortality so that his family <em>and every generation after him, mortal or Kindred</em>, would command the unhallowed gifts of the Damned.</p>\n<p>This monstrous “family” has grown prodigiously since then, inviting — or perhaps binding — into its ranks other vampires whose facility with the mysteries of death rivals their own. The clan has absorbed several fringe and funerary bloodlines and cults into its sect-like structure, spreading like necrosis throughout the periphery of vampire culture.</p>\n<p>Hecata subscribe to a cyclical philosophy in regards to the Embrace, which they symbolize by the three faces of Death, Rot, and Rebirth. Hecata share an aptitude in bridging the distinctions of life and death (and its nuances, like undeath). Hecata command spirits with the same skill that they “resurrect” corpses and the same passion that they divine the causes of death… and its culprits. This gives them not only an ability to learn secrets presumed safely gone and buried, but also to surveil ongoing activities through the servitude of ghosts.</p>\n<p>Needless to say, this is all the sort of stuff that less grotesque societies find taboo, so the Hecata have spent no little effort in cultivating a self-sufficiency that would be the envy of the most well-to-do. Indeed, their insight into death and command over it yields significant profit when wielded wisely: forever sealing the secrets of fortune-spawning crimes, investigating the location of long-forgotten hoards, and sending forth the restless dead to coax or compel secrets from the still living and those close enough to count.</p>\n<p>Hecata sires favor mortals either from their own mortal families or factions, or who provide links to outside groups from which the prospective sire would benefit. They prefer kine of a morbid, blackhumored, or pragmatic bent, and few sires see much need for the compassionate or empathetic, unless it serves to satisfy a particular need they seek to fill. To some Hecata, the clan — the <em>family</em> — is all, while others pay this lip service at best, though many sires lean on guilt, obligation, and a sense of familial duty to force their childer into line, regardless of their own true feelings on the matter.</p>\n<h3>Bloodlines Of The Hecata</h3><p>Clan Hecata includes numerous distinct bloodlines, from the ancient Cappadocian to the resurgent Nasyon san an. Some Hecata think these bloodlines were once full clans, but tonight nobody knows or cares when there’s profit to be made and macabre mysteries to plumb. See pp. 223-229 for Hecata Bloodline Loresheets.</p>\n<h3>Disciplines</h3>\n<p><strong>Auspex:</strong> Hecata often regard Auspex as perception of fate. Reading an aura becomes an exercise in discerning how the individual’s attitudes steer them toward their fate; taking possession of a body places the vampire at that tiller. It’s also a Discipline that requires a degree of finesse, from sensing unseen ghosts to reading the spiritual history of an object… and often neglected among the more brutish members of the clan.</p>\n<p><strong>Fortitude:</strong> Who better to exalt the physical durability of the undead form? Fortitude may manifest as the futility of hacking away at an already-dead corpse to a grand mastery over — and avoidance of — Death’s very laws.</p>\n<p><strong>Oblivion:</strong> Oblivion is among the most mysterious of Disciplines, as it can reach past the veil of the afterlife. Through its use, the Hecata might interrogate a ghost or set it to torment a rival. They might rot a mortal’s flesh with a touch or practice a debased ceremony to drive a meddling kine to a bleak reward. Oblivion risks the user’s connection to their humanity, but the Hecata see it as a necessary tool for understanding the relationship among life, death, and undeath.</p>\n<h3>Compulsion: Morbidity</h3>\n<p>The vampire experiences an immediate need to move something from life to death or vice versa. Any action not dedicated to ending or resurrecting something is taken at a two-dice penalty. This subject doesn’t have to be a person, or even a living thing, but can include objects or even more abstract things such as an idea or a conversation. The Compulsion lasts until the vampire manages to kill or return something to life (figuratively or literally).</p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 23)</small></h3>\n<p>The Hecata inflict a notoriously <em>painful Kiss</em>. The fangs of the Lazarenes bring not bliss, but agony. Victims caught unawares will violently resist unless restrained, and few people submit willingly to the torture that is the Hecata Kiss. When drinking directly from a victim, Hecata may only take harmful drinks, resulting in blood loss (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 212). Unwilling mortals not restrained will try to escape, and even those coerced or willing must succeed in a Stamina + Resolve test against Difficulty 2 + Bane Severity in order not to strain against the pain. Coerced or willing vampire victims of the Hecata bite must make a Frenzy test against Difficulty 3 to avoid falling into a terror Frenzy.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Decay <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>The Hecata connection to death and entropy cause them to practically radiate decay, slowly eating away at things both living and inert in their vicinity. Their havens turn decrepit in a matter of nights, plants wither around them, and even mortals grow haggard and sickly from prolonged exposure. Some Hecata come to terms with this, making do with dilapidated havens and few, if any, mortal servants. Those who don’t must spend considerable time and effort to keep their assets from decaying around them.</p>\n<p>Hecata vampires suffer additional dots in Flaws equal to their Bane Severity divided as they see fit among Retainer, Haven and Resources Flaws. These can either be taken at character creation or “bought off” by paying twice the amount of Background dots. In addition, purchasing dots in these Advantages costs an additional amount of experience points equal to Bane Severity.</p>\n<p><em>If the chronicle does not emphasize feeding scenes or the characters are able to procure vessels in more controlled ways, this variant can provide an interesting angle on the Hecata.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-hecata","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"20"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.NgF8imEDMgGDx9B3"},"OeoFcN7GVbQPSdhl":{"name":"Warrior Setites","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/warriorsetites.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Obfuscate, Potence, Serpentis</h5>\n<p>Not all Followers of Set are subtle, forked-tongued seducers. A number of them prefer to walk a more martial path, becoming holy warriors for their god. The warrior Setites eschew the Obfuscate Discipline for Potence — no slinking in the dark for these serpents. They are proud and brutal soldiers for Set, and are perfectly willing to go into battle to back up their subtler brethren. They act as enforcers, assassins, and even martyrs for the rest of the line, although thinking of them as mindless brutes is a mistake. A warrior Setite is still very much a Setite, and quite capable of leading others into all sorts of temptation. They just have the strength to make their points forcefully when necessary. The warrior Setite line is interesting in that it is not passed down by the Embrace. A mortal Embraced by a warrior Setite becomes a “normal” member of the line (that is, learning Obfuscate as a Clan Discipline) unless trained as a warrior from childe-hood. The line is therefore an example of how training and ideology can change the basic makeup of a vampire’s Clan. Warrior Setites generally embrace passionate ideologues. The ideology that they espouse isn’t important. A fanatical Christian’s fervor can be turned to Set as easily as the zeal of a pro-union rabble rouser, given the right amount of Kindred vitae and religious revelations from Set.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Horned Vipers</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The warriors suffer same weakness as the Followers of Set (p. 53): They suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subjected to bright light of any kind.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>The Serpent of Eden had his approach. Mine is more honest — that should be refreshing for you.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 435)</small></h3>\n<p>The warriors suffer same weakness as the Followers of Set (p. 53): They suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subjected to bright light of any kind.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-warriorsetites","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"435"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.OeoFcN7GVbQPSdhl"},"PCk9qSC2zxyV7rFP":{"name":"Tlacique","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tlacique.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Obfuscate, Presence, Protean</h5>\n<p>When the Spanish came to what is now Mexico, they carried Kindred with them in the holds of their ships. These undead stepped into the nights of the New World… and found that other vampires already claimed domain here. One bloodline of such Kindred was the Tlacique. Claiming descent from Tezcatlipoca, the Aztec god of night and black magic, the Tlacique might have forged alliances with the Kindred that arrived with the Spaniards, had those conquistadores not destroyed the civilizations on which the Tlacique preyed and infected their people with smallpox. As their society fell, the Tlacique briefly allied with the Sabbat, but when the two factions took the time to examine the others, they found their belief systems quite incompatible. The Sword of Caine eventually slew the weakened Tlacique, and the bloodline as a whole vanished from view. They still exist, however: a handful scattered across Central America and the southwestern United States. The Tlacique are unquestionably a Setite line, though only their vulnerability to sunlight marks them as such. They are mystics one and all, and status within the line depends in part by learning magic. In addition to their Disciplines, they practice a form of Thaumaturgy called <em>Nahuallotl</em> (in game terms, this Path is identical to the Path of Blood, found on p. 213, although they pay out-of-Clan costs to learn it).</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Jaguars</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Tlacique’s weakness is one of the only ways to trace the line’s lineage to the Setites. They, like the Followers of Set (p. 53), suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subjected to bright light of any kind.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>A heart to make the sun rise. The blood within the heart to sustain the sun through his nightly sleep. You are blessed — you get to provide both.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 435)</small></h3>\n<p>The Tlacique’s weakness is one of the only ways to trace the line’s lineage to the Setites. They, like the Followers of Set (p. 53), suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subjected to bright light of any kind.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-tlacique","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"435"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.PCk9qSC2zxyV7rFP"},"Prj63HfTo1keq0hq":{"name":"Panders","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/panders.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Panders do not have Clan Disciplines, meaning that the player may choose any Discipline at character creation (subject to Storyteller approval). In addition, Panders are subject to the same rules for raising Disciplines with experience points as Caitiff (see p. 124).</h5>\n<p>The Caitiff of the Sabbat, Panders are on the front lines when the Sword of Caine goes to war. That means, of course, that the vast majority of casualties in violent action come from their ranks. But as precarious as this position within the Sabbat may be, it <em>is</em> their position, and the rest of the Sect recognizes this. Recognition, as any Caitiff trying desperately to survive under a Camarilla Prince will tell you, goes a long way. Any Sabbat vampire that does not know his Clan, or any vampire Embraced by an established Pander, is considered a Mutt. This means that when the Sabbat goes looking for shock troops, it tends to be Panders that swell their ranks (since mass Embrace definitely qualifies as dangerous work). The Panders became an established part of the Sabbat in the 1950s, when a low-level Sabbat vampire named Joseph Pander united the Clanless Sabbat under his banner (and name). Ever since then, the term has been applied to these Kindred. They take on the dress and mannerisms of bikers, homeless, punks, and the dispossessed among humanity, the better to feed on and Embrace from these dregs. But rare is the Pander that bemoans his lot in unlife — these Cainites are true, loyal Sabbat.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Mutts</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Panders do not have a specific weakness. Pander characters cannot start lower than Ninth Generation, though they can subsequently lower their Generation via diablerie.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Call me a Mutt, yeah? That’s fine. Purebreds go crazy as they get older. And Cainites, well, we</em> live <em>“dog eat dog.”</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 431)</small></h3>\n<p>Panders do not have a specific weakness. Pander characters cannot start lower than Ninth Generation, though they can subsequently lower their Generation via diablerie.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-panders","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"431"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.Prj63HfTo1keq0hq"},"QJ0b9iQFTi7lZvz3":{"name":"Brahman","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brahman.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Animalism, Auspex, Chimerstry</h5>\n<p>The elders of Clan Ravnos long ago divided themselves up into castes, much like mortal Indian society. In theory, only a member of a given vampire caste could Embrace a member of the appropriate mortal caste. In 437 practice, though, the Clan’s elders couldn’t maintain such strict control over its members, even in India, and certainly not in the rest of the world. For the most part, these vampire castes (called <em>jati</em>) were identical to the main body of the Clan in terms of ability and blood. The Brahman Ravnos, though, have a gift of sight that the other <em>jati</em> do not, and often use it to divine the future or the locations of their enemies. They act as advisors and seers to their Clan in India. Another branch of the Clan, called the Phuri Dae, followed the Roma (Gypsies) along their travels in Europe. They are mechanically identical to the Brahman, and served much the same function.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Brahman suffer the same weakness as the main body of Clan Ravnos (p. 65). Each one has a preferred form of vice (though the Brahman have a special love for swindling through psychic readings and séances). Whenever the Brahman is given the chance to indulge in his favorite vice, the player must roll Self- Control or Instinct (difficulty 6) to resist the temptation.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Our traditions are not meaningless now, childe. If anything, they mean</em> more <em>now. We have so much more to preserve, and so few voices left to accomplish it.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 437)</small></h3>\n<p>The Brahman suffer the same weakness as the main body of Clan Ravnos (p. 65). Each one has a preferred form of vice (though the Brahman have a special love for swindling through psychic readings and séances). Whenever the Brahman is given the chance to indulge in his favorite vice, the player must roll Self- Control or Instinct (difficulty 6) to resist the temptation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-brahman","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"437"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.QJ0b9iQFTi7lZvz3"},"RJPESSGL6mAJaXcr":{"name":"Ahrimanes","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ahrimanes.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Potence, Spiritus</h5>\n<p>The strange tale of the Ahrimanes begins with a Sabbat Gangrel <em>antitribu</em> named Muricia. Caught between two warring factions of Sabbat Gangrel, she watched as her packmates were pulled into the conflict and ultimately killed. She renounced the Sabbat and the Gangrel Clan entirely, now calling herself “an Ahrimane,” and called upon shamanic magic to alter herself. How exactly this was accomplished is still a mystery. One theory is that Muricia was an anomaly even before she attempted her magical working, and had access to powers that other vampires didn’t. Another (possibly more plausible) theory is that Muricia had help from mortal sorcerers who were hoping the effect would spread throughout the Kindred of the world, sterilizing them and preventing any new vampires from being created. Regardless of the origin, Muricia found that she was not able to create childer or blood bonds. She did, however, retain the ability to turn other Kindred (at least other Gangrel) into whate v e r s h e had become. She kept the animalistic features that she can gained during her previous unlife, however, and it may have been her cat-like eyes that lent her bloodline its nickname. Muricia extended the offered of “becoming Ahrimane” to any female Gangrel who wished it. She made it quite clear she was not interested in performing the process on male Kindred, though the specific reasons for this remain unclear. A few Gangrel joined her, taking havens in the southern United States and avoid contacting with other Sabbat. The Ahrimanes were never numerous, and although they remained nominally allied with the Sabbat, Muricia made it quite clear that they were to be left alone. This isolation may have doomed them, however. In the late 1990s, all contact with the Cats ceased. Investigators found, sometime later, that their havens were empty, and no one has heard from them since. Most Sabbat believe that the Ahrimanes were destroyed, but concede that it is possible that Muricia might have led her line on a spiritual pilgrimage somewhere out of the Kindred’s reach.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Cats</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Ahrimanes were an independent Sect, or more accurately, a group of independent individuals. They identified more with the Sabbat than the Camarilla, if only because they did not wish to answer to any elder (expect perhaps Muricia).</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> All Ahrimanes were female, and most were either of African American or Native American descent (Muricia, apparently, was not partial to Caucasian vampires). Most Ahrimanes never went anywhere unarmed, and dressed to fight. Many Ahrimanes had animal features left over from their time as Gangrel.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Muricia was a vampire of some means, and she purchased houses on the outskirts of several cities in the American South (in Louisiana, Mississippi, and Georgia especially). New Ahrimanes could make their own arrangements or stay in one of these homes.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> All Ahrimanes were Gangrel before their magical transformation. As such, their Backgrounds varied just as much as the Beasts’. The sort of Gangrel who became an Ahrimane was female, usually non-white, and frustrated by the situation in her Sect or city.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> All Ahrimanes had at least three dots in Mentor, to represent Muricia’s guidance. Combat Abilities and Physical Attributes were common, but this was as much from the Ahrimanes’ Gangrel background as anything else. Likewise, a dot or two of Protean or Fortitude was common, since Ahrimanes began unlife as Gangrel.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> An Ahrimanes’ blood was inert. They were unable to create childer or blood bonds, and a person consuming Ahrimane blood did not become a ghoul.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>We are an evolution of your disease, an awakening from the nightmare of Caine. Come, sister. Awaken.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Since all Ahrimanes effectively had the same “sire,” they were all beholden to Muricia. She did not attempt to control or rule her creations, but did keep tabs on them and help them develop their powers of Spiritus. The bloodline was fairly tight-knit, as vampire “families” go, and threatening one Ahrimane generally meant battling several.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>I would suffocate under their rule.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Let them think they run us. They fear us, and they are not wrong to do so.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>The only vampires that understand true freedom.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 414)</small></h3>\n<p>An Ahrimanes’ blood was inert. They were unable to create childer or blood bonds, and a person consuming Ahrimane blood did not become a ghoul.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-ahrimanes","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"414"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.RJPESSGL6mAJaXcr"},"S61sHO6O7hwTZtOe":{"name":"Old Clan Tzimisce","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/oldclantzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Auspex, Protean</h5>\n<p>The Old Clan Tzimisce are a small group of Fiends who predate the use of fleshcrafting. They regard Vicissitude as a disease of the soul, and refuse to learn or employ it. In most other respects, though, they resemble the rest of the Clan. They share the Clan’s propensity for manners and respect for territory, and as most of the surviving members of this “bloodline” still reside in what is now Romania, they govern their territories using Old World methods and customs. In some remote places, they rule more or less openly, taking blood tithe from the mortals under their “protection” and keeping out other supernatural threats. As the world grows smaller through the use of information technology, though, those nights may be coming to an end. The Old Clan would prefer never to have to deal with the “defiled” members of Clan Tzimisce, but family is family and they can’t survive the modern nights with no support. They make treaties and trade favors with other Fiends, sometimes lending support to the Sabbat as a whole in exchange for help and support when they need. One thing they refuse to do, though, is share blood with a Sabbat vampire. One never knows what might be swimming in it.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> The Old Clan</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> As with the rest of the Clan (p. 71), the Old Clan Tzimisce must sleep in at least two handfuls of soil from their homeland. Failure to meet this requirement halves the Tzimisce’s dice pools every 24 hours, until all pools fall to one die. This penalty remains until they are able to rest for a full day in their earth.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Old ways do not die. Like old Cainites, they merely sleep, awaiting sufficient blood to reawaken them.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 439)</small></h3>\n<p>As with the rest of the Clan (p. 71), the Old Clan Tzimisce must sleep in at least two handfuls of soil from their homeland. Failure to meet this requirement halves the Tzimisce’s dice pools every 24 hours, until all pools fall to one die. This penalty remains until they are able to rest for a full day in their earth.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-oldclantzimisce","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"439"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.S61sHO6O7hwTZtOe"},"V8fpKozy4StvSzap":{"name":"Serpents of the Light","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/serpentsofthelight.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Obfuscate, Presence, Serpentis</h5>\n<p>The Serpents of the Light are the <em>antitribu</em> of the Followers of Set. They began as a splinter group, a Setite heresy that rejected the worship of the Clan’s founder as a god. This Sect made its way to Haiti, studying vodoun and the mysticism thereof, and during the 1970s came under the sway of the Sabbat. The Clan proper tried to bring the Serpents of the Light back in line with “orthodox” Setite belief, but to no avail. As with several other splinter groups of the Clans, the Serpents of the Light found the freedom of the Sword of Caine too alluring. The bloodline’s methods are familiar to those who know the Setites. The Cobras engage in seduction and sexual manipulation in order to achieve their ends (and, of course, to find the blood they need to survive). They dabble in drugs, prostitution, and any other method they can find to bring mortals into slavery to their own vices. In fact, apart from religious outlook, very little separates them from the Followers of Set. Some Kindred wonder, in fact, if the whole schism isn’t part of some much longer and intricate scheme, one that only the eldest of the line are fully privy to.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Cobras</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Serpents of the Light, despite the name, are just as vulnerable to bright illumination as their parent Clan (p. 53). They suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subject to bright light of any kind.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Let’s dance, you and I. No one watching but the</em> loa<em>. And hell, let’s invite them to join us. Roll up your sleeve.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Ministry V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 36)</small></h3>\n<p>The Blood of a Minister abhors the light. When exposed to direct illumination, whether natural or artificial, members of the clan recoil. Ministers receive a penalty equal to their Bane Severity to all dice pools when subjected to bright light directed straight at them. Also, add their Bane Severity to Aggravated damage taken from sunlight.</p>\n<h3>Ministry V5 Players Guide Variant: Cold-Blooded <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Often compared to that of serpents, the Blood of the Ministers runs cold in more ways than one. Without fresh, warm blood in their veins they find it impossible to mimic the breath and heartbeat of a mortal, and even then the act requires considerable effort.</p>\n<p>A vampire of the Ministry can only use Blush of Life if they have recently fed from a living vessel (in the same scene or up to roughly an hour ago, at the storyteller’s discretion), and when they do it requires a number of Rouse Checks equal to their Bane Severity rather than just one.</p>\n<p><em>Social and subtle chronicles where smooth interaction with mortals is at a premium can add tension to this variant.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-serpentsofthelight","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"432"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.V8fpKozy4StvSzap"},"W9Wv8wdM2VnsVZ29":{"name":"Baali","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/baali.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Daimonion, Obfuscate, Presence</h5>\n<p>Even monsters have their bogeymen, and Kindred are no exception. Sires often attempt to keep neonates from growing drunk on their own power as vampires, and one method for doing so is to explain to them what sorts of twisted, evil power awaits them in the dark. The Baali are one such power.</p>\n<p>History — or legend, perhaps — claims that the childe of an Antediluvian made a pact with forces beyond human understanding in order to make war on the thirteen Clans. He took the name Baal-the-Destroyer, and he led his apostles against the Second City. The battle was terrible, and none caught in its wake were spared — Demon and Kindred alike perished. But the Baali and their unholy allies were routed and fled, and over the millennia, they faded from memory, to legend, to cautionary tale. The Baali do still exist, though. The Inquisition weeded out the stupid and the careless, but left the clever and the patient. Some went into torpor, while others simply claimed membership in another Clan and begged for sanctuary. Now, in modern nights, the bloodline sees more concentrated activity than it has for centuries. Baali are explorers and seekers of secrets. They will do almost anything and sacrifice almost anyone to gain access to occult mysteries. They squander the wealth they have accumulated, they make deals they cannot possible hope to live up to, and they do so with no apology and no attempt at excuse. They seem to know that they are in decline, and that their bloodline is disappearing into the mists of time. The bloodline has enemies, after all. Infernalists — those who call up and worship demons — are one of the very few beings that can be called “common enemies” by most of the denizens of the World of Darkness, Kindred and otherwise.</p>\n<p>Despite their small numbers and host of enemies, the Baali are dangerous. They are not Faustian bargainers, looking for comfort or even knowledge. They do not wish to reign in Hell. They believe — they <em>know</em> — that the world is doomed. The End Times are here, and every sacrifice they make, every murder they incite, is one more chink in the dam holding back the deluge of blood called Gehenna. If they have to die to break that dam, so be it. They die knowing that what they do is what must be done.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Demons</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> While individual Baali might pretend to membership in the Camarilla or, less commonly, the Sabbat, the bloodline as a whole claims no Sect.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Some neonates adorn themselves with Satanic paraphernalia. Some Baali dress in expensive clothing, and some don’t bother. Some become so fixated that they neglect outer appearance. Older Baali surround themselves with insects, rats, or other vermin.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Baali tend to nest in places of antiquity. Libraries, universities, and stately homes attract the Demons. No matter where the Baali make their havens, though, the vampire’s very presence changes the place. Vermin creep in first, and no amount of effort can keep them out.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Baali value temperament above profession. Teachers, defrocked priests, laborers, artists — all have joined the ranks of the Baali. The bloodline even breeds their own recruits, drawing from ghoul families raised over the years to produce offspring suckled on the unholy truths that the Baali recite as gospel.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Mental Attributes, especially Perception, are usually primary. A Baali’s ability to tempt and entice depend on his insight into what a person truly wants. Social Attributes are also helpful, as the Baali need both to keep their identities a secret and convince others to open up to them.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Baali cannot bear to look upon or handle objects of any faith. Demons must avert their gazes from such objects, and touching them burns their flesh. In addition, should a Baali run afoul of True Faith, any hindering or damage effects are doubled.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>‘And behold, a pale horse, and his name that sat on him was Death, and Hell followed with him.’ Ever noticed it’s in past tense? That’s not a mistranslation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Baali gather in cabals numbering three to 13. Supposedly, these cabals answer to the bloodline’s founder. Any Demon is willing to die to protect his cabal-mates; not out of any loyalty or affection for them, but because they’re all doomed anyway.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Sooner or later, the masks have to come off. Take all the time you need. We’ll wait.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>A honeycomb in which to hide. Just learn how the little bees dance first.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>They have the right idea. They’re just playing for the wrong team.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 394)</small></h3>\n<p>Baali cannot bear to look upon or handle objects of any faith. Demons must avert their gazes from such objects, and touching them burns their flesh. In addition, should a Baali run afoul of True Faith, any hindering or damage effects are doubled.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-baali","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"394"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.W9Wv8wdM2VnsVZ29"},"WcPTBP0kiXVG9AqR":{"name":"Toreador","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Auspex, Celerity, Presence</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Celerity, Presence</h5>\n<p>From the Toreador perspective, when the sun fades, darkness gives rise to an eternal and wondrous world. Everything is fraught with wonder and terror, low politics and sensual glories, the profound and the profane, and an undeniable undercurrent of the sanguinary. These Kindred are the Toreador, and they spend unlives ensconced in pleasure.</p>\n<p>Of course, for vampires of this disposition, it’s easy to become jaded and bitter. More than the other Clans, Toreador often succumb to ennui, or fight the eventual boredom of unchanging immortality by playing at rivalries. An excess of stimulation turns them into slaves to the sensations they seek. The most debased Toreador can become true monsters, sinking to unimaginable levels of depravity in order to feel anything at all.</p>\n<p>The Kindred of Clan Toreador often involve themselves greatly in the world of mortals. They have any number of reasons, whether enjoying proximity to the blush of life, cultivating veritable cults of doting followers, or influencing and following the trends that their own kind simultaneously mock and venerate. To hear the Toreador tell it, they are the Muses of a desperate mortal world, inspiring through their beauty or patronage.</p>\n<p>Toreador culture is a mixture of sybarites, dilettantes, and visionaries. Some Toreador, with echoes of mortal passion, Embrace lovers or “project” progeny who seem to fly in the face of every Toreador custom. These either don’t last long or rise to great prominence as subversives and individualists. Ideas, trends, and</p>\n<p>“the next great thing” spread through the Clan, and other Kindred often look to the Toreador to guide them. The Degenerates know this, and many become Harpies, Princes, and other key figures in vampiric society.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Degenerates</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Camarilla would not have survived in the nights following the Anarch Revolt without substantial participation from the Toreador, and they remain among its most ardent supporters.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Almost to the last, they are attractive in some way, whether the traditional beauty of a runway model or the dangerous allure of something more predatory. The Degenerates augment their physical beauty with a sense of personal style, which may take the form of expensive couture, avant-garde street wear, or classical fashions designed to emphasize their appealing qualities. This isn’t to say that ugly Toreador don’t exist. Indeed, those gifted with less physical beauty often go that much further with their choice of accoutrements.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Degenerates spare no expense in appointing their havens in luxury, often with many original works of art. It is a point of pride among Toreador to have an unconventional (and thus memorable) haven with modern comforts; thus, many have striking lofts and penthouses, while the bolder among them renovate or repurpose everything from abandoned aquariums or deconsecrated churches to rooftop gardens or converted warehouse-galleries in fashion-forward neighborhoods. Share a communal haven? How <em>déclassé</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Many Toreador hail from high society or “bohemian” backgrounds. Indeed, many are themselves artists or influential among local art scenes or other subcultures. Actors, singers, musicians, sculptors, poets, playwrights, authors, and creative folk of any stripe may well find a home in the Clan, as do those who serve as patrons to (or travel in the entourages of) those artistic types.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Social attributes are almost always primary, with an even split among Talents, Skills, and Knowledges depending on how the Toreador distinguishes herself. Toreador also love to cultivate Backgrounds. Allies, Contacts, Resources, Domain, Haven, Mentors, Resources, Retainers — all of these have great value among Toreador. Wise Toreador may choose to develop their Virtues, Humanity, Path, or Willpower, because with an unlife of degeneracy, they must frequently attend to the ugly business of bringing the Beast to heel.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Clan Toreador is cliquish and parochial in its local domains, but very rarely on a level that affects Clan-wide custom. Certain Toreador (and a few outside the Clan) sometimes use the terms “artiste” and “poseur” when describing individual Toreador, often derisively, to describe whether the Degenerate in question is one who is seen as creative or simply a follower of established trends, but these are certainly informal distinctions.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Long after you die, I will still be young, beautiful, and adored by everyone whose life I touch.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Blood-soaked barbarians.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>A fire may be stoked, but if left unattended, it may destroy what it once warmed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Worms breed in their vile footprints.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Beauty and the Beast, only without the complication of Beauty. Pity them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Which is more unctuous: their smiles or their hair?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>If I looked like they do, I’d hide in the dark, too.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>The tedium of it all.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>They make a strong case for the Mark of Caine being a sickness.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>There are two types of these creatures: Awful, and absent.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>Aren’t they supposed to turn back into pumpkins at midnight?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>It is a poor artist who blames his tools, but that’s the only conceivable answer here.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>Why are older brothers always such corpulent bullies?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>The worst sort of fish is the one that feeds from the bottom.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>This house needs a good cleaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Such marvelous passion wasted on such craven dementia.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>Only a petty ruler acknowledges no greater purpose than himself.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 91)</small></h3>\n<p>Toreador exemplify the old saying that art in the blood takes strange forms. They desire beauty so intensely that they suffer in its absence. While your character finds itself in less than beautiful surroundings, lose the equivalent of their Bane Severity in dice from dice pools to use Disciplines. The Storyteller decides specifically how the beauty or ugliness of the Toreador’s environment (including clothing, blood dolls, etc.) penalizes them, based on the character’s aesthetics. That said, even devotees of the Ashcan School never find normal streets perfectly beautiful. This obsession with aesthetics also causes divas to lose themselves in moments of beauty and a bestial failure often results in a rapt trance, as detailed in the Compulsion rules (p. 208)</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Agonizing Empathy <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>The curse of the Toreador is that they feel too much — sometimes literally — of the sensations around them. Especially in intimate moments such as feeding, a Diva physically suffers the agony they inflict on a mortal, damage to their dead bodies mimicking the harm inflicted on the vessel.</p>\n<p>When a Toreador vampire feeding causes damage to a mortal, the vampire suffers similar (usually Aggravated) damage in return, though a single feeding cannot cause more damage than their Bane Severity. The damage takes the form of involuntary internal bleeding, the vampire exhibiting vivid bruising in whatever spot matches the bite location on the victim.</p>\n<p><em>With the regular Toreador Bane being subject to a lot of Storyteller adjudication, this variant can fit troupes that desire stricter systems.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 66)</small></h3>\n<p>When a Toreador experiences something truly remarkable — a person, an <em>objet d’art</em>, a lovely sunrise — the player must make a Self-Control or Instincts roll (difficulty 6). Failure means that the Kindred finds herself enthralled by the experience. The dazzled Toreador cannot act for the duration of the scene aside from commenting on or continuing their involvement with whatever has captured their attention. If the experience no longer affects her (whether by moving, being destroyed, or whatever is appropriate to the situation), the captivation ends. Enraptured Toreador may not even defend themselves if attacked, though being wounded allows them to make another Self-Control or Instincts roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-toreador","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"66"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.WcPTBP0kiXVG9AqR"},"XrZ0P5JHQHvHbMBy":{"name":"True Brujah","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/truebrujah.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Potence, Presence, Temporis</h5>\n<p>The Clan Brujah that most Kindred know in the modern nights is not, according to legend, the true lineage of the Antediluvian known as “Brujah.” The strange bloodline known as the True Brujah claim that 412 constant presence in Kindred society. Sometimes they wear their true nature openly, while other times they claim membership in other Clans (stating their Clan as “Brujah,” for example, and not bothering to correct Brujah’s childe, Troile, committed the misapprehension that ensues). Their primary focus diablerie upon him and stole conis the pursuit of knowledge, and to that end they have trol of the Clan. Brujah, it is made some strange alliances. The True Brujah occasaid, was not the hot-blooded sionally ally with the Followers of Set, and sometimes warrior/scholar that many even provide services as consultants to the Sabbat in vampires associate with the exchange for access to secrets and locations normally Rabble, but rather a staid, closed to them. passionless introvert. Why,</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Sages then, he chose to Embrace a</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Sages have become more or less indepenmaniac like Troile in the first dent. They follow the Camarilla’s rules when in their place is a mystery that will likely cities, and avoid the Sabbat lest they become pressed never be fully explained. into service or destroyed. While it isn’t impossible for a The True Brujah True Brujah to join the Sword of Caine, most of them were never subject to can’t summon up the necessary fervor. a massive purge as the</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> In nights past, the True Brujah were Salubri or the Cappapredominantly of Greek or North African descent. As docians were, but they the centuries wore on, though, the Sages Embraced don’t Embrace often progeny from across Europe and, eventually, the New and never recovered World. In modern nights, True Brujah tend to dress from the theft of their professionally, the better to fit in with the academic Clan status. Their settings they prefer. city — the Carthage</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Some True Brujah maintain multiple haof Kindred myth, in vens around the world, which they use as they travel which vampires openly pursuing whatever kind of knowledge they find most ruled over mortals — fascinating. Others stay closer to a given city, genersupposedly died out due ally with a centuries-old university or other center of to the treachery of Troile’s learning nearby. In either case, their havens tend to be childer (though other stories clean, well-maintained, and austere. suggest demonic influence). From there, the bloodline</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> It’s not entirely accurate to say that faded from history. A few the True Brujah only Embrace academics and scholnotable members of the ars, but it’s fairly rare for them to do otherwise. Sages bloodline appear through sometimes Embrace out of curiosity or even spite, but out history, but by the for the most part they choose people whose capacity of modern nights only Kinlearning and accumulated knowledge will benefit their dred historians or those sires. who chance to meet</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Mental Attributes and Knowlthem can speak of their edges are usually primary. True Brujah sires instruct existence. their childer in the basics of Occult and some form of Whatever the truth combat (it’s just not practical to be unable to fight). of their origins, the Nature and Demeanor are often similar, if not identi- True Brujah have recal, and never anything forceful or passionate. Commained a small but mon Backgrounds include Resources, Contacts, and Herd.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> True Brujah lose much of their emotional capacity when they are Embraced, and their ability for sympathy continues to deteriorate as they grow older. All Conscience and Conviction rolls are made at +2 difficulty (maximum 10), and ratings in Conscience, Conviction, Humanity, and Paths of Enlightenment cost double the normal experience costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> While their rarity precludes frequent meetings, the bloodline comes together twice a century to discuss matters that affect them all. The eldest members of the bloodline determine the location and specific time of the meeting. Outside of these larger meetings, many True Brujah stay in contact via letters, whether handwritten or online.</p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>After a certain point, a system becomes self-sustaining, and even if it no longer has any resemblance to its original purpose, it is too large to dismantle.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>I strongly suspect that the future of all Kindred can be found in this Sect. I also hope I am wrong.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>A misguided, angry thief is still a thief.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I spared you last night because we hadn’t finished our conversation. I might spare you tonight, if I think that tomorrow you might still interest me.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 412)</small></h3>\n<p>True Brujah lose much of their emotional capacity when they are Embraced, and their ability for sympathy continues to deteriorate as they grow older. All Conscience and Conviction rolls are made at +2 difficulty (maximum 10), and ratings in Conscience, Conviction, Humanity, and Paths of Enlightenment cost double the normal experience costs.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-truebrujah","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"412"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.XrZ0P5JHQHvHbMBy"},"YiKj35AXFG7AyZKo":{"name":"Kolduns","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kolduns.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): The kolduns aren’t truly a bloodline. Rather, koldunism is a practice within the Tzimisce where the vampire learns Koldunic Sorcery (at out-of- Clan costs). Koldun Clan Disciplines, therefore, are Auspex, Animalism, and either Dominate or Vicissitude (depending on whether the koldun is an Old Clan Tzimisce or not).</h5>\n<p>The koldun are the oldest sorcerers among the Kindred, or at least they claim to be. Their magic is ancient, to be sure. This magic does not depend on formulae and lifeless, categorized rotes. Rather, it draws power from the most unlikely source: the living earth. Koldunic Sorcery excels in manipulating the natural elements in subtle and grandiose ways, yet its paths follow no other directions. Many of its rituals build on spirituality and vitality thought lost to vampires. Kolduns, then, are Tzimisce wizards who are in tune with the land around them to a frightening degree.</p>\n<p>The kolduns aren’t protectors of the land, though, at least not in the way that the phrase usually implies. They <em>use</em> the land, drinking in its energy and reshaping it into weapons. They protect the land because it is theirs, and therefore they display the same territorialism for which the Tzimisce in general are so widely known. For many years, Koldunic Sorcery was the province of a few very powerful elder Tzimisce, almost all Old Clan (see below). In recent nights, though, the knowledge of the magic has filtered down through the Clan’s ranks, and even neonates may learn it. This is a huge departure from previous practices. It remains to be seen what effect Koldunic Sorcery will have on the world at large.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> None</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> As with the rest of the Clan (p. 71): The koldun must rest in the proximity of at least two handfuls of native soil. Failure to meet this requirement halves the koldun’s dice pools every 24 hours, until all pools fall to one die. This penalty remains until they are able to rest for a full day in their earth.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I</em> am <em>the land. You have not violated my home… you have violated</em> me<em>. And now you reap the consequences.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 439)</small></h3>\n<p>As with the rest of the Clan (p. 71): The koldun must rest in the proximity of at least two handfuls of native soil. Failure to meet this requirement halves the koldun’s dice pools every 24 hours, until all pools fall to one die. This penalty remains until they are able to rest for a full day in their earth.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-kolduns","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"439"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.YiKj35AXFG7AyZKo"},"ZZFEBUwXlmOsInaA":{"name":"Anda","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/anda.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Animalism, Fortitude, Protean</h5>\n<p>The Anda were a Gangrel offshoot, created by a Methuselah called Dobrul the Brave. Dobrul, a wanderer with great respect for the Mongol hordes, Embraced several of their warriors. These vampires became the spirit warriors of the steppe, following the nomadic Mongol tribes and protecting them from outward threats. The Anda had intricate rites governing the Embrace of new Kindred. Once a candidate was selected, any Anda in the area met for a discussion on that candidate’s qualities. The prospective Tartar had to be an impressive warrior, but tempered enough in nature to understand that only by protecting his tribe from outward supernatural threats could he keep it healthy enough to feed from. Once the decision was made, the prospective sire would wash the candidate in a river, a ritual funerary bathing in the Mongol tradition. He would then drain and Embrace the new vampire. This kind of ritual and observance governed much of the Anda’s existence. Long and lonely nights wandering the steppe, either with the Mongols or alone (and they did wander alone, sometimes even into Western Europe), the Anda’s Beast was the only company. Small wonder that these Kindred viewed themselves as spiritual warriors, their brutal ethos the only bulwark against madness and depredation. The Anda made war against a variety of foes. They would join their nomad tribes against whatever enemies they chose (as far as they could, since not all battles were fought at night). They also clashed with native Chinese shapeshifters and vampires, and it was this latter group that proved the undoing of the Anda in the 14th century. The bloodline’s heyday was the reign of Kublai Khan, and during this time they could be found openly celebrating their undead nature in China. When Kublai died, the Anda fell into decline, and in the modern nights none are known to exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Tartars</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Wandering Anda might join a particular vampire’s court, at least for a time, but their irresistible push to keep moving prevented them from becoming permanent fixtures anywhere in Europe. In their own lands, they watched over Mongol tribes called <em>yasun</em>, parasitically protecting and feeding from the mortals.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Anda Embraced exclusively from the Mongol tribes, so they were short, stocky, and bowlegged from a lifetime of riding. The skin of an Anda faded to an odd brown-yellow following the Embrace.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Anda dwelled on the steppe, far enough away from their <em>yasun</em> to avoid discovery, but close enough to catch up with them when night fell. The truly monstrous or depraved among them did not protect a tribe, but led a solitary unlife, feeding as opportunity permitted. These poor souls typically fell to torpor from hunger, and if any Anda still exist, it is likely one of these horrors, sleeping buried in somewhere in China.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Anda did not Embrace capriciously. They chose their candidates carefully, since this choice made a statement about who would protect their people. All Anda were skilled horsemen, trackers, warriors, and, to some degree, shamans.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Unlife on the steppe was harsh. Physical Attributes were generally primary, with Talents and Skills sharing priority (especially martial ones — all Anda had some facility in Melee, Brawl, and Survival). All of the Anda’s Disciplines were important, but neonate Anda generally found that learning Earth Meld was a matter of survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Like the Gangrel from which they were descended, Anda gained animal features from frenzying. Unlike the Gangrel, though, Anda gained such a feature once every <em>other</em> frenzy. However, the Anda suffered a further weakness — for each day after the third that an Anda slept within the same one-mile area, all dice pools were halved (to a minimum pool of one).</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I am not a monster. I am a shepherd. I protect the sheep from wolves like you, because</em> I <em>wish to eat them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> On the night of the new moon, all Anda within a given radius met to discuss the movements of their <em>yasun</em> and what kinds of threats might require their collective attention. The eldest Anda in such a gathering was called the <em>khan</em>, and all others were expected to submit to his authority.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>High Clans:</strong> <em>Strange tribes. Strange khans.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Low Clans:</strong> <em>Some truly do seem low — diseased, mad, weak. And others I would glad call brother.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 416)</small></h3>\n<p>Like the Gangrel from which they were descended, Anda gained animal features from frenzying. Unlike the Gangrel, though, Anda gained such a feature once every <em>other</em> frenzy. However, the Anda suffered a further weakness — for each day after the third that an Anda slept within the same one-mile area, all dice pools were halved (to a minimum pool of one).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-anda","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"416"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.ZZFEBUwXlmOsInaA"},"bwUUqjNfWXiLI4Sy":{"name":"Mariners","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/mariners.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Fortitude, Protean</h5>\n<p>The Mariners are Gangrel who have chosen to dwell full-time in the ocean. Gangrel <em>aquarii</em> (as they are sometimes known to scholarly Kindred) swim below the sunlit zone, their Fortitude Discipline keeping them safe from the pressure that would otherwise crush them. They ascend at night to feed, usually on sharks and whales, but they aren’t choosy — anything with blood is fair game. A shipwreck is a smorgasbord for the Mariners, and a large enough disaster is one of the few events that might draw more than one to the same region. Mariners sometimes act in defense of the oceans, sneaking aboard polluting vessels and killing everyone aboard. One some level this makes sense — if their prey dies off, they’ll have to return to the land (or quest ever further into the Deeps).</p>\n<p>The Camarilla and the Sabbat have, on the rare occasions they find them, offered Mariners membership. The Mariners decline. They have no sectarian interests, and escaping the Jyhad might have been why the first Mariner left the land to begin with. No one knows how long ago that was, but most of the Mariners are of low Generation, and some of them date to the Age of Exploration.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> None (the few Kindred that know of them simply call them “Mariners” or “aquatic Gangrel”)</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> As with most Gangrel (p. 55), the Mariners gain an animalistic feature each time they frenzy. These features, though, resemble fish, aquatic worms, cephalopods, and other sea creatures (the rare Gangrel develops a cetacean or seal-like feature). The alien nature of these features should be taken into consideration by Storytellers determining the mechanical impact of such features.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Why did I leave the land? Because in the sea, the sharks just swim up and bite you. They don’t smile first.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 435)</small></h3>\n<p>As with most Gangrel (p. 55), the Mariners gain an animalistic feature each time they frenzy. These features, though, resemble fish, aquatic worms, cephalopods, and other sea creatures (the rare Gangrel develops a cetacean or seal-like feature). The alien nature of these features should be taken into consideration by Storytellers determining the mechanical impact of such features.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-mariners","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"435"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.bwUUqjNfWXiLI4Sy"},"eqU0dvnnmO3NTuUz":{"name":"Toreador Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Celerity, Presence</h5>\n<p>The Toreador of the Sabbat are just as prone to creative expression and love of beauty as their Camarilla counterparts, but they enjoy a much higher regard within their Sect. This is because the Toreador <em>antitribu</em> codified much of the Sabbat’s present structure, forming its rites and expressing its philosophy in such as a way as to appeal to the young vampires of the time. They layered discourse with meaning, showing through satire, poetry, and (when necessary) straightforward explanation that the Sabbat held the keys to the future of all Cainites. These vampires haven’t lost their creative edge over the centuries. While Camarilla Toreador express themselves through fairly conventional means of art, the Perverts tend more toward extreme, bloody, and painful presentations. Torture is an art form, and body art — whether performed with tattoo needles or scalpels — is popular among the Perverts. Since such modifications are not permanent (unless the Pervert in question is able to learn Vicissitude, that is), the undead form is a constantly renewing canvas. The Toreador <em>antitribu</em> take advantage of this to send messages coded within the art of their own bodies, using a system of symbols that takes the vampires a lifetime to truly master.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Perverts or Pervs</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Over time, the propensity for the Toreador to become fascinated by beauty has twisted itself into a perverse need for cruelty. When faced with an opportunity to inflict emotional or physical pain — a captive that might be tortured, or a protégé whose ambitions might be squashed — the Toreador <em>antitribu</em>’s player must make a Self-Control or Instinct roll (difficulty 6), or spend a Willpower point. If the roll fails, the character must indulge in her savage desires.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Hold that pose. That’s good. Can you stop bleeding from the neck so much, please?</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Toreador V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 91)</small></h3>\n<p>Toreador exemplify the old saying that art in the blood takes strange forms. They desire beauty so intensely that they suffer in its absence. While your character finds itself in less than beautiful surroundings, lose the equivalent of their Bane Severity in dice from dice pools to use Disciplines. The Storyteller decides specifically how the beauty or ugliness of the Toreador’s environment (including clothing, blood dolls, etc.) penalizes them, based on the character’s aesthetics. That said, even devotees of the Ashcan School never find normal streets perfectly beautiful. This obsession with aesthetics also causes divas to lose themselves in moments of beauty and a bestial failure often results in a rapt trance, as detailed in the Compulsion rules (p. 208)</p>\n<h3>Toreador V5 Players Guide Variant: Agonizing Empathy <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>The curse of the Toreador is that they feel too much — sometimes literally — of the sensations around them. Especially in intimate moments such as feeding, a Diva physically suffers the agony they inflict on a mortal, damage to their dead bodies mimicking the harm inflicted on the vessel.</p>\n<p>When a Toreador vampire feeding causes damage to a mortal, the vampire suffers similar (usually Aggravated) damage in return, though a single feeding cannot cause more damage than their Bane Severity. The damage takes the form of involuntary internal bleeding, the vampire exhibiting vivid bruising in whatever spot matches the bite location on the victim.</p>\n<p><em>With the regular Toreador Bane being subject to a lot of Storyteller adjudication, this variant can fit troupes that desire stricter systems.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 432)</small></h3>\n<p>Over time, the propensity for the Toreador to become fascinated by beauty has twisted itself into a perverse need for cruelty. When faced with an opportunity to inflict emotional or physical pain — a captive that might be tortured, or a protégé whose ambitions might be squashed — the Toreador <em>antitribu</em>’s player must make a Self-Control or Instinct roll (difficulty 6), or spend a Willpower point. If the roll fails, the character must indulge in her savage desires.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-toreadorantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"432"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.eqU0dvnnmO3NTuUz"},"f2b1yWAvm5WXK1xH":{"name":"Malkavian","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Auspex, Dominate, Obfuscate</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dementation, Obfuscate</h5>\n<p>Clan Malkavian is twice damned: once by the curse of being Kindred, and again by the turmoil that disturbs their hearts and minds. Upon the Embrace, every Malkavian is afflicted with an insurmountable insanity that fractures her outlook for every night thereafter, making her unlife one of madness. Some consider this a form of oracular insight, while others simply consider them dangerous.</p>\n<p>Make no mistake: Malkavian insanity is a painful, alienating phenomenon, but it occasionally provides the Lunatics with bursts of insight or heretofore unknown perspective. Madness for the Malkavians may take the form of any clinical form of insanity, or it may be a hyperacuity of senses others don’t know they have; a supernatural puppeteer pulling the Malkavian’s strings, or a sense that the Malkavian is somehow ahead of evolutionary schedule. A Malkavian may believe herself to be an idea given physical form or an avatar of some concept the World of Darkness has yet to encounter. She may be a nonstop ravening psychopath, or may be a mostly lucid individual sometimes rendered catatonic by fear of an impending cosmic cataclysm.</p>\n<p>Their precarious stability makes it hard for other Kindred (or, indeed, any vessels with whom they may meet) to interact with Malkavians. The Clan sometimes indulges in elaborate, terrifying, and dangerous “pranks” which do little to endear them to other vampires. These incidents are nominally meant to educate the target, but the lesson can often be lost between the vampire’s scramble for safety and the inability to parse the Malkavian’s inner logic. Common pranks might be to replace a vain Toreador’s haven door with a guillotine or to redistribute a Brujah elder’s wealth while he’s at Elysium, or it might take the form of giving a hunter the location where the Nosferatu gather. Kindred both dread and resent the word “prank” almost as much as they do the Lunatics themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Lunatics</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> In their moments of lucidity, the Malkavians offer their unorthodox perspectives and Devil’s advocacy to the Camarilla, offering their visions and unique insights to cut through the webs of deceit. Princes and Primogen tolerate the Lunatics to varying degrees, but the Clan as a whole has always been a supporter of the Ivory Tower.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> While Malkavians can come from any culture, the madness following the Embrace tends to lead them to extremes of self-presentation. Malkavians may appear disheveled, injured, or simply dirty. They could still be wearing the same clothes from the night of their Embrace or they may have stolen clothes from a laundromat or a department store during a fit of confusion or fugue. Of course, Malkavians are just as likely to be meticulous and exacting in their appearance, trying obsessively to appear as normal as possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Consistency is rare among Malkavians. Quite simply, they establish havens where they think to, where they can, and where they can recall. A significant number of Malkavians have literally no home, spending each night where exhaustion or the sun’s rays leave them. Others may permanently have the presidential suite in a posh hotel, a squat in the Barrens, the dispensary at a county jail, or a broom closet in a historical landmark.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Malkavians Embrace with all the caprice one would assume from them. Lunatic childer come from all economic and cultural strata, though most have some sort of hard-luck story or black secret behind them that caused their sire to take note. Truly damaged Malkavians who are unaware of the meanings of their actions may not even be aware that they have sired childer, which makes for very difficult entry into Kindred society for these castoffs, many of whom end up among the Caitiff.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Loner, outsider, and deviant concepts and archetypes prevail among the Malkavians, as do Mental Attributes (with an occasional Social-primary madman or Physical-primary maniac hiding among the ranks). Talents and Knowledges are likely most popular among Malkavians. Backgrounds tend to be either broad and shallow or few and deep, representing the way the way the Malkavian caroms through unlife. Virtues, Humanity, and Paths often tumble quickly, usually in the wake of Willpower doing the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Rumor is more widespread than truth with regard to Malkavian organization, and any number of harrowing tales describe the function of the Clan. Some say the Lunatics all share a hive consciousness; others claim that this is in fact the lingering consciousness of the Clan’s progenitor Malkav. Still others claim that a network of madness puts all Malkavians in contact with one another and is the cause of their crippling insanity. If nothing else, the Malkavians prove inscrutable and uncanny. One night, each of them frenzies when they see one another, while the next night, they all converge at the same time at the Sheriff’s haven and accuse him of harboring Sabbat spies. Who can say how the “disease of information” or the customs of the Clan travel among them?</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I was trying to turn water into wine. Where the fuck did all this blood come from? Oh, well — waste not, want not.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>There’s something flattering in what they’re doing. Like I’m a little piece of their God.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>You can’t hide your horns, devil-man.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Walking westward, they should meet the sun before it sinks.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Pin the tail on the— OW, OW, OW, FUCK!</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Ashes, ashes, we all fall down, and get back up, and dance with ghosts.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>A hyena doesn’t ever choose to be a hyena, I think.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>I have dreamed what hunts them in their dark, humid warrens, and it is what each of us can become, if we’re not careful.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>It looks different depending on if I close my left or my right eye.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>Made up of the pieces of the people they’ve harmed. Each time they feed they become a little less.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>If you can rub those letters off their foreheads, they’ll stop in their tracks.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>I tried to sprinkle salt on one’s tail and he bit off my hand.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>I will make you a fine new waistcoat, my emperor.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>I didn’t do it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>When all you do is look behind you, you never realize that the door in front of you is closed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>They have the answer, but they don’t understand the riddle itself.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>All they from Sheba shall come: they shall bring gold and incense.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 79)</small></h3>\n<p>Afflicted by their lineage, all Malkavians are cursed with at least one type of mental derangement. Depending on their history and the state of their mind at death, they may experience delusions, visions of terrible clarity, or something entirely different.</p>\n<p>When the Malkavian suffers a Bestial Failure or a Compulsion, their curse comes to the fore. Suffer a penalty equal to your character’s Bane Severity to one category of dice pools (Physical, Social, or Mental) for the entire scene. This is in addition to any penalties incurred by Compulsions.</p>\n<p>You and the Storyteller decide the type of penalty and the exact nature of the character’s affliction during character creation.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Unnatural Manifestations <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Even the most mundane-seeming Oracle raises the hackles of those around them when they tap into the powers of their eerie Blood. Although they rarely recognize the source of these sensations, mortals respond with notable unease when a Malkavian uses their powers, and other vampires can readily recognize the presence of a fellow undead.</p>\n<p>Whenever a Malkavian uses a Discipline power, mortals in close proximity (roughly in the same room or equivalent) are spooked and any social interaction with them apart from intimidation suffers a dice penalty equal to the Malkavian’s Bane Severity. This sensation isn’t Masquerade-breaking, but the mortal is struck by a sudden fear or dislike of the vampire, lasting one scene. Other vampires experience a similar sensation, instantly recognizing the Malkavian as a vampire, though no penalties apply to interactions with them.</p>\n<p><em>If the troupe wants to emphasize the discomfiting nature of Malkavians and highlight their weirdness, this variant can fit the bill.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-malkavian","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"60"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.f2b1yWAvm5WXK1xH"},"iMqLuLgfFJrcv7Dk":{"name":"Daitya","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/daitya.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Presence, Obfuscate, Serpentis</h5>\n<p>The Daitya are an Indian bloodline of the Followers of Set. They revere Shiva the Destroyer as an incarnation of Set, and believe that by casting the world into greater depths of depravity, they can help it reach its nadir of true evil. When that happens, Shiva will open his third eye and gaze upon the world, destroying it, and setting the stage for the next cycle of existence. Like many Indian Kindred, the Daitya do not accept the Caine myth. They believe that they are demons, and that by killing, corrupting, and enslaving people, they are doing what it is in their nature as demons to do. They observe a caste system similar to that of mortals, and feel that being Embraced indicates a previous life of sin and profanity. By doing their duty in this incarnation (which can last many lifetimes), then the Daitya feel that reincarnation in a higher station is possible. Many Daitya run or are involved with a cult to Shiva, and recruit ghouls and childer from the cult. The bloodline is scholarly and devout, in its way. The Daitya are not, however, humble — they believe that the gods themselves listen to their prayers, and that they are the vehicles that will bring the world to judgment.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Blasphemers</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Daitya suffer the same weakness as the Followers of Set (p. 53): They suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subject to bright light of any kind.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Tonight we fast. Keep your hunger under control. Kill the mortal whelp if you wish, if it helps, but do not feed.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 434)</small></h3>\n<p>The Daitya suffer the same weakness as the Followers of Set (p. 53): They suffer two additional health levels of damage from sunlight, and a one-die penalty to all rolls when subject to bright light of any kind.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-daitya","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"434"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.iMqLuLgfFJrcv7Dk"},"jAQzdoohoCzxxhan":{"name":"Thin-Blooded","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Thin-Blood Alchemy</h5>\n<p>Sires tell their childer of the 13th and 14th generation never to attempt the Embrace as their Blood is too far removed from Caine. They say it will bring nothing but death and sorrow. But, be it by mistake or design, this edict is not always followed. A 14th generation Kindred, who only by miracle avoided the flaw of thin blood themselves, drain a mortal in a fit of frenzy and guiltily feed them a mouthful of vitae, hoping against hope that it might reanimate the broken body. A Ventrue from a long line of liars thinks herself closer to her progenitor than she really is, and believes she is creating a worthy member for her clan. A prince’s great-grandchilde attempts to force their diluted vitae into the mouth of their ancestor’s executed, treacherous ghoul just to get back at the old tyrant. A Tremere, often overlooked by his clan for his weakness of Blood and spirit, experiments with the Embrace to prove himself as good as them. In all these cases, a dusk-born may awaken, if the Blood wills it. Kindred who intend to sire thin-bloods often target individuals they already have a pre-existing relationship with, such as a trusted ghoul or a human family member they cannot forget, or they seek out vessels who demonstrate specific traits or rare talents that make them better equipped to survive as the weakest of predators. Many Kindred view the thin-bloods as a threat to the Masquerade and will destroy them, if given the chance. The thin-blooded can be of any mortal origin and are not affected by their parent clan’s curse nor by their preferences in the same degree as their sire. If they are allowed to rise, they will likely be turned away, gaining the luxury of freedom and the danger of solitude. In 2009 the Inner Circle of the Camarilla issued an edict that all thin-blooded must be branded or tattooed with the “mark of the crescent moon”. Inked by a Tremere tattoo artist or scorched with a beam of sunlight, the brand is irreversible. Even Anarchs and unbound think twice before associating with a marked Mercurian.</p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook <small>(p. 107)</small></h3>\n<p>Thin-bloods have no clan Bane. Instead they take one to three Thin-Blood Flaws (and an equal number of Thin-Blood Merits) at character creation (Corebook pp. 182–184).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-thinblooded","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"107"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.jAQzdoohoCzxxhan"},"jUiP0kFOpXM7HvOW":{"name":"Daughters of Cacophony","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/daughtersofcacophony.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Fortitude, Melpominee, Presence</h5>\n<p>The Daughters of Cacophony emerged as a distinct bloodline sometime during the 19th century. Before that, of course, there were always Kindred who saw music and song as a way to remain sane. Kindred scholars believe that the Daughters sprang from the blood of either Clan Toreador (for their passionate artistic pursuit) or Malkavian (given their propensity for causing madness), but their supernatural powers point more toward Clan Ventrue. It’s something of a moot point, 398 as the “first” Daughter of Cacophony is unknown. Regardless of their origins, the Sirens (as they are called in Kindred circles) are all singers, and the bloodline has always been predominantly female.</p>\n<p>Indeed, the Daughters of Cacophony base their unlives on song. They wander through eternity with music always in their ears, and that makes them seem unfocused or flighty to other Kindred. This is one of the reasons, along with their rarity, that neither major Sect of vampires considers them a threat or much more than a diversion. Individual Kindred, however, might have occasion to meet a Siren and wonder why it is that those who spend too much time listening to their songs always seemed changed by the experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Sirens</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Sirens might fall in with whichever Sect holds power locally. If asked, most Daughters either identify as independents or claim membership in the Camarilla.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> A Siren might be a street musician dressed in thriftstore chic, a pierced and tattooed punk rocker, or a high-class entertainer with money and prestige. Apart from the fact that the Daughters are largely female, their appearance varies widely.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Most Sirens have a quiet room with good acoustics where they can sing without anyone listening. As much as most Daughters love performing, they would prefer to be alone after the “show” ends, and so the outward appearance of their havens isn’t as important as maintaining its secrecy.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The majority of Sirens were singers before the Embrace as well. Very occasionally, a Daughter Embraces someone who never developed her skill at singing, but these discover their talents swiftly. The Daughters do not discriminate based on musical style. Jazz singer, nu-metal waif, opera diva, sultry lounge performer — any chanteuse might be a Siren.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Social Attributes tend to be primary. Expression and Performance are almost always high, and many have Fame. Sirens who die with a following reap the benefit of their fans (Herd). Those less lucky often still have Contacts in show business.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Daughters of Cacophony hear music constantly. This might be a form of synesthesia, or it might be a hallucination.</p>\n<p>This constant song distracts the Daughters as much as it guides them. The difficulties of all their Perception rolls increase by two. No Daughter of Cacophony may have Alertness above 3 dots.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Their numbers are too small for them to be truly organized, yet sometimes the Sirens act as one. Every so often, Sirens everywhere put on a performance on the same night. They don’t necessarily sing the same songs, but all of the waking Sirens sing at exactly the same time.</p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Would you like to hear another, my lord?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Very well. You don’t mind if I hum a bit, though?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>If only your art had real power, like mine.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I heard the song as I died. It led me out of darkness and cold, and it came to my lips with my mother-sister’s blood. And now I sing, because to end the song would be to end all.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 398)</small></h3>\n<p>The Daughters of Cacophony hear music constantly. This might be a form of synesthesia, or it might be a hallucination.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-daughtersofcacophony","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"398"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.jUiP0kFOpXM7HvOW"},"kEDZUlRBENHBu89n":{"name":"Lasombra","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Dominate, Oblivion, Potence</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Dominate, Obtenebration, Potence</h5>\n<p>To the mind of a Lasombra, it is better to reign in hell than to serve in heaven. Fear, frenzy, the power to determine whether another lives or dies: these are at the root of the power that the Lasombra hold dear. Whereas other vampires try vainly to hold the Beast at bay or give themselves wholly to it, the Lasombra beat 58 the Beast into submission, invoking it when it suits them but leaving it trapped inside when they wish to govern themselves.</p>\n<p>It is with these predilections the Lasombra style themselves a “dark nobility,” an aristocracy of the night that chose the supremacy of the Cainites over veneration of the Masquerade.</p>\n<p>Their regard for such ideas as redemption and salvation are cynical at best, and much of the pomp that shaped the Clan’s pre-Sabbat outlook remains only with a sense of blackest irony or open mockery. The grandeur and rituals of the Church and aristocracy remain dear to the Lasombra, however, and the Clan was instrumental not only in establishing many of the rituals of the Sabbat, but in the institutions that keep it from descending into chaos each night.</p>\n<p>Whether they see themselves as God’s instruments or as outcasts from His creation, the Lasombra believe they have a duty (whether to Sect, Clan, pack, or even just themselves), and obligation to their responsibilities that gives them a wicked sense of purpose. Even a Lasombra shovelhead may consider himself a cut above the rest; a surprising number of Lasombra mass Embraces survive, perhaps owing to the intervention of their sires.</p>\n<p>While the night belongs to all Kindred, the Lasombra are truly born to darkness, to the degree that their very Clan name reveals their tie to shadow. Theirs is the Discipline of Obtenebration, and it allows them to wield the stuff of shadow and darkness, and even reach beyond the darkness of the physical world into the abyss that lies beyond.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Keepers (as in “my brother’s…”)</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Sabbat would be very different without the influence of the Lasombra, and they remain its most prominent Clan. Young Lasombra often make auspicious starts as Sabbat Ducti or Priests, leading their fellows by charging into the fray or demonstrating mastery of the Beast.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Lasombra are frequently attractive. Whether through the Spanish, Italian, and Moorish stock associated with the Clan, or due to more cosmopolitan modern backgrounds, the Keepers cut a striking figure. Their dress is often conservative or religious, drawing on years of ceremony and faithful ritual. Rare is the Lasombra who cannot at least manipulate shadows to affect a dramatic entrance or enigmatic pose.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The obligation of their leadership leads many young Lasombra to maintain communal havens with other members of their pack. Wealthier Keepers and those who predate the Sect often maintain their own havens, whether sinister penthouse suites or sprawling Old World villas.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Prospective sires of Clan Lasombra seek both erudition and ambition in their potential childer. As such, many Lasombra come from professional backgrounds, and display outgoing and even aggressive personalities. Merit in their sires’ eyes takes fledgling Lasombra far, and the Keepers do not hesitate to cull their ranks of flawed, lazy, or boorish childer.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Lasombra often have disparate Natures and Demeanors. Mental or Social Attributes are equally likely to be primary. Attributes tend to be narrow and specialized, showing individual expertise. Keepers cultivate Backgrounds of all types similarly in (initially) low quantities, to better diversify themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> For the Lasombra, the nights of high aristocracy never faded, and the titles and offices a modern onlooker might associate with history still carry great weight among the Keepers. A complex system of patronage, mentorship, and lineage characterizes the Clan, similar to the courts and churches of centuries gone by. Childer benefit greatly from esteemed sires and vice versa, while acts that confer Sect or Clan status may also elevate a Lasombra’s peers, so long as she associates her success with her fellows.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Wielding the shadows is an extension of controlling the darkness into which we are all Embraced.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>They believe themselves lions but possess only the ways of jackals.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>Petulant whelps in a rage over their own loss of rule from a self-fulfilling prophecy.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Peer too deeply into the darkness, serpent, and you shall find that it has the means to smite.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Every society needs its beasts of burden.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Their inner foulness manifests in their every action.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>The fool does not dine at the table with the lords.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>They are martyrs, not monsters, and their faces are the stigmata they must bear.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>Filth, suitable only from which to make an example.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>A rose plucked from its garden remains lovely for only so long before it crumbles to dust.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>They revere only themselves, and they will reap a just reward for the sacrilege they practice.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>Although we may share a common goal, a murder of crows is always an ill omen.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>Such a misguided effort, to use the scepter of kings only to hide oneself from his lessers.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>Lords have ever sired bastards, and have ever hidden them as their marks of shame.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Who would wish to dwell in a castle that closes its gates for fear of unruly peasants?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Imperfect, and thus in need of ever more investment.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>They must never</em> be <em>equals — it is enough that they think they are.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 29)</small></h3>\n<p>Anyone seeing the reflection or recording (live and otherwise) of a Lasombra vampire can instantly recognize them for what they are, provided they know what they’re looking for. People with no prior knowledge will know something is wrong, but likely attribute the distortion to irregularities in the reflecting surface or recording errors. Note that this will not hide the identity of the vampire with any certainty: the Lasombra are no less likely to be caught on surveillance than any other vampire. In addition, use of modern communication technology, including making a simple phone call, requires a Technology test at Difficulty 2 + Bane Severity as microphones have similar problems with the voice of a Lasombra as cameras with their image. Avoiding electronic vampire detection systems is also done at a penalty equal to Bane Severity.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Callousness <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Whether from their tendency to select particularly ruthless mortals for the Embrace or that <em>something</em> gnawing at their psyche, Lasombra vampires tend toward the inhuman, regardless of sect or unlifestyle. Something just seems off about them and Lasombra who care about their sanity must be extra careful, lest their Beast take another bite out of their Humanity.</p>\n<p>When making a Remorse roll, deduct a number of dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Lasombra vampire. This cannot reduce the dice pool below one die.</p>\n<p><em>Chronicles that focus on Humanity and the loss of it can benefit from this variant, as can chronicles in historical settings that are light on technology.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Lasombra vampires cast no reflections. Whether in a mirror, in a body of water, on a polished surface, or in the rear-view of a taxicab, the image of the Keeper does not reflect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-lasombra","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"58"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.kEDZUlRBENHBu89n"},"lQ7xqZtbqwWVhGAi":{"name":"Lamia","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lamia.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Fortitude, Mortis, Potence</h5>\n<p>The Lamia were a bloodline of Cainites devoted to defending Clan Cappadocian. The founder of the bloodline, Lamia, claimed to be a daughter of Lilith, Adam’s first wife. According to legend, a Cappadocian elder named Lazarus found her performing sacred rites to Lilith, the Dark Mother. Impressed by her skill and perhaps taken with her fierce beauty, he Embraced her.</p>\n<p>While she died and the Change took her, Lilith gave her a vision, a deep understanding of her future and that of the Cappadocians. When she recovered, she whispered something to Lazarus, and the Cappadocian elder fled in terror.</p>\n<p>Lamia did find her way back to the Clan, though, and her bloodline became the guardians of the Cappadocians. They were never very numerous, but important Graverobbers often had at least one Gorgon standing at their side. The Lamia learned to feed sparingly, since their bite carried disease, and developed their own path of Necromancy based on the four humors. Over the centuries, they became an important resource for the Cappadocians. And yet, behind their loyalty was perhaps a greater dedication to the Dark Mother and to the vision of their founder. The Revelation of the Dark Mother seems to have led the line to tragedy, however. The Lamia died defending the Cappadocians when the Giovanni subsumed the Clan. Augustus Giovanni murdered Lamia herself following his diablerie of Cappadocius, and the last known member of the bloodline was destroyed in 1718 as part of a Camarilla blood hunt.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Gorgons</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Lamia were somewhere between the High and Low Clans. They were not a Clan, but were considered part of Clan Cappadocian in most Cainites’ understanding. Other Cainites, however, treated them respectfully as servants or soldiers, both out of deference to their skills and to their patrons.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Lamia did not share the Cappadocians’ pallor. Many of them were of Mediterranean or Semitic descent, though as the Cappadocians traveled across Europe predominantly female, but it wasn’t uncommon for Lamia to dress as men (usually armed and armored).</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Lamia shared havens with their Cappadocian patrons. When they made their own, they often chose crypts and sarcophagi, preferring to be with their Gorgon bodyguards, other nationalities came into the bloodline as well. The bloodline was alone among the dead (feeding on the recently dead also minimized their chance of starting an outbreak of plague).</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Since the Lamia Embraced women almost exclusively, they seldom had the chance to Embrace trained warriors. Instead, the Gorgons often chose women who had some experience with death. Women who had attempted or contemplated suicide, or had recently committed murder, were common choices. Likewise, women with a pronounced interest in black magic or scholarly pursuits in general were candidates for the Lamia Embrace. Training in combat could come after the gift of immortality.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> All Lamia had some combat training, usually from their sires. Physical or Mental Attributes might be primary, as were Skills. The Lamia also tended to have some familiarity with Occult, both from the rites taught to them by their own bloodline and simply by proximity to the Cappadocians.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Lamia carried the “Seed of Lilith,” a wasting disease spread by their bite. Anyone the Lamia fed upon was required to make a Stamina roll (difficulty 6 for women, 8 for men). If the roll failed, the victim contracted a Black Plague-like pox that was fatal within several days. Any vampire that consumed Lamia blood became a carrier of the disease until all of the Lamia vitae had been purged from his body.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Approach the Prince when I bid you, or lose your hands.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The sirechilde relationship in the Lamia was very strong, as most Lamia had to remain with their sires for at least a year to achieve the necessary combat skill to be of use. Lamia remained the leader of the bloodline until her destruction, but her first and most important order to her daughter was to serve the Cappadocians.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>High Clans:</strong> <em>Our masters’ fellows cannot be trusted. Bow, but with one hand on your blade.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Low Clans:</strong> <em>Their humors are purer. Sometimes far</em> too <em>pure.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 422)</small></h3>\n<p>The Lamia carried the “Seed of Lilith,” a wasting disease spread by their bite. Anyone the Lamia fed upon was required to make a Stamina roll (difficulty 6 for women, 8 for men). If the roll failed, the victim contracted a Black Plague-like pox that was fatal within several days. Any vampire that consumed Lamia blood became a carrier of the disease until all of the Lamia vitae had been purged from his body.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-lamia","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"422"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.lQ7xqZtbqwWVhGAi"},"lSRdN81oZGOALaHl":{"name":"Brujah","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brujah.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Celerity, Potence, Presence</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Celerity, Potence, Presence</h5>\n<p>The legacy of the Brujah is one of halcyon greatness marred by their own fiery natures. Theirs was the glory of ancient Carthage, but Ventrue treachery in ancient Rome brought the dream to an end. Since then, the Brujah have borne a grudge.</p>\n<p>Tonight, the Brujah are rebels and provocateurs, bat-swinging hooligans and agents of change in a society long crippled by stasis. As rebels, it’s in their nature to challenge the status quo — though sometimes, without adequate opposition, they embody the status quo themselves. It works out fine, because there’s always a hot-blooded Brujah waiting in the wings to bring down an uppity Clanmate grown too comfortable in the role of rebel-turned-dictator.</p>\n<p>More so than any other Clan, the Brujah still feel the flames of the passions that once inspired them as mortals. Clan Brujah loves a cause and is quick to act on a stirring speech, accusation of injustice, or a call to arms. This connection to passion can be a blessing, but inspiration can also yield to the madness and hunger of the Beast.</p>\n<p>No wise Prince turns his back on the Brujah, and rare is the Brujah who would allow herself to be manipulated or pandered to. A Brujah is her own master, first and foremost, and those who would bring her to heel face a terrible task. A Brujah who thinks she’s getting the short end of the stick will tear an enemy to ribbons first and maybe remember to ask questions later, no matter if he’s a Primogen or a mortal authority.</p>\n<p>Some Kindred historians claim that the Brujah have parlayed their ancient betrayal into a role as agitators against the very order that they help support. Others claim the Brujah have long since submitted to the will of that same order, being little more than loyal opposition rendered impotent. Most Brujah don’t care either way, as long as someone needs a good ass-kicking and they can be the ones to deliver it.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Rabble</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> For all its rebellion, Clan Brujah generally considers itself a member of the Camarilla. Young Rabble sometimes look to the wildness of the Sabbat, but elders assert that the Ivory Tower provides a fine structure against which to rage.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Many Brujah affect styles and mannerisms that reflect an attitude of rebellion. Multicolored hair, shaven heads, spikes, rivets, fetish gear, and t-shirts with bold slogans might appeal to a Brujah. While not every Brujah wears the “uniform,” the Rabble often enjoy adorning themselves in outfits intended to provoke. Some young Brujah prefer mobile devices as their tools of resistance, and can summon a riot at the touch of a screen.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Brujah may feel kinship to a city, but they rarely develop such ties to individual locations. Thus, at any given time, a Brujah probably has a half-dozen or more hideouts, safehouses, and flats available. These are often shabby and ill-kept until the Brujah needs them. Brujah havens might also have mortals who follow the Brujah’s ideology or his cult of personality. This works out fine: It never hurts to have a spare vessel in an emergency.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> As creatures of passion, Brujah often Embrace without really thinking much about it, and their childer tend to be a disparate lot. Sometimes, the Rabble Embrace those who share a similar outlook or enthusiasm for a cause as the prospective sire. Other times, they inflict the Embrace on those of opposite ideology, cursing a rival with vampirism as punishment.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Brujah are usually — though not exclusively — drawn from mortals with violent or ungovernable personalities. Their Natures and Demeanors are often similar, as Brujah have little use for guile. They lean toward physical Attributes, with Social and Mental about equal afterwards. Many favor Skills and Talents, but most respect the Knowledges that make them more than just thugs. Contacts, Allies, and Herd are common Backgrounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Certain causes rise and fall in Brujah fashion, but some of the more tenured are those who call themselves Idealists and Iconoclasts. Iconoclasts want to tear it all down, while Idealists enjoy solving problems through theory. This last harkens somewhat to the classical roots of the Brujah as philosopher-kings, and most Idealists are among the ranks of Brujah ancillae and elders.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I’ll give you a head start. You’re the tortoise and I’m the hare — a pissed-off, fuck-the-world hare with a score to settle and a thirst like the devil’s own.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Fast, invisible, fanatical, and they want to drink my fucking blood? Sounds like a good case for doing unto others.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>The apple doesn’t fall far from the tree, and the tree should have been chopped down long ago.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Much respect, so long as we agree it’s mutual.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>If you have to deal with one of these nasty dagos, you’ve already lost.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> No habla <em>Ventrue with a different hat on.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>Trust one? No. Find some common ground with one? Maybe.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>It’s better to have one telling you shit about someone else than the other way around, so be cool.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>I’ll bet I can throw one further than you can.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>If you can catch one, see if he’ll blow you. They might as well be good for something.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>If one of these witches starts looking at you weird, you’d better hope you can get close to him before your blood catches on fire.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>Nasty old bats with claws where their junk used to be. But at least they’re usually honest.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>Boot, meet face.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>Teach them something and maybe they’ll return the favor.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>It mostly works when we punch it into heading the right direction.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Giving your soul to the devil makes you a servant, you choads.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>Get on the bus and let’s tear this Prince a new asshole together.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 67)</small></h3>\n<p>The Blood of the Brujah simmers with barely contained rage, exploding at the slightest provocation. Subtract dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Brujah from any roll to resist fury frenzy. This cannot take the pool below one die.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Violence <small>(p. 56)</small></h3>\n<p>Unliving vectors of revolt, the Brujah cannot help but change things — often violently and not always for the better. While all vampires harbor a capacity for destruction, a hungry Brujah always does some kind of damage.</p>\n<p>On a messy critical result on any Skill test, a Brujah vampire causes damage (physical or mental, depending on the situation) to the subject of their interaction equal to their Bane Severity, in addition to any other result of the Hunger dice. The damage is Aggravated unless the player spends a point of Willpower, making the damage Superficial instead.</p>\n<p><em>This variant makes sense in a chronicle where Frenzy – including the regular Brujah Bane – rarely comes into play either because of the narrative or the troupe going for a more rules-light approach.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-brujah","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"50"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.lSRdN81oZGOALaHl"},"m7eWmp0SUkG2JTmZ":{"name":"Assamite Viziers","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/assamiteviziers.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Celerity, Quietus (Animae)</h5>\n<p>Assamite viziers are and always have been a vital part of the Clan. Magical knowledge and research remains the purview of the sorcerers, but the viziers are necessary for compiling and uncovering information on philosophy, government, the arts, and religion. The viziers have acted as leaders within the Clan — not where matters of war are concerned (this they wisely leave to the warrior Assamites), but when it comes to understanding and relating to mortals, the viziers make the best consultants. Unfortunately, while they can learn, understand, and interpret mortal customs and mores, they have difficulty actually relating to them. Assamite viziers are driven to accumulate knowledge with a zeal that borders on obsessive. They can understand why people act the way they do on a large scale, but understanding the motives of a particular person is much more difficult. For many years, Assamite viziers and sorcerers were regarded as one caste (this is why sorcerers are still referred to as “viziers” by the ignorant). In modern nights, the viziers have emerged from the shadows to conduct their scholarly research more visibly. Whether this will open the viziers to greater danger from the Assamites’ enemies or bring the caste more power remains to be seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Scholars or Artisans</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The viziers are mad. Every vizier finds himself caught up in the continuance of his chosen pursuits to the exclusion of trivial concerns such as daily shelter or nightly nourishment. Each vizier has an Obsessive-Compulsive derangement (p. 292) associated with his highest-rated intellectual or creative Ability. If the character’s focus of effort shifts, so does the focus of the derangement.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Seventy virgins. Seventy Kindred in the city. Virgin blood — have any of the Kindred in the city consumed the blood of their own childer? Well, find out!</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 434)</small></h3>\n<p>The viziers are mad. Every vizier finds himself caught up in the continuance of his chosen pursuits to the exclusion of trivial concerns such as daily shelter or nightly nourishment. Each vizier has an Obsessive-Compulsive derangement (p. 292) associated with his highest-rated intellectual or creative Ability. If the character’s focus of effort shifts, so does the focus of the derangement.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-assamiteviziers","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"434"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.m7eWmp0SUkG2JTmZ"},"mmppzydAbV9Apu35":{"name":"Caitiff","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/caitiff.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Any three of your choice</h5>\n<p>Those without a Clan are known as Caitiff. These individuals are undeniably vampires, but they display no inherited characteristics from their sires (if, indeed, they know who their sires are at all). Caitiff are frequently shunned in Kindred society, as much for their lack of pedigree as their ignorance of vampiric social structures. In most cases, Caitiff may be found among any of the Sects.</p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 107)</small></h3>\n<p>Untouched by the Antediluvians, the Caitiff share no common bane. Caitiff characters begin with the Suspect (•) Flaw and you may not purchase positive Status for them during character creation. The Storyteller may always impose a one or two dice penalty on Social tests against fellow Kindred who know they are Caitiff, regardless of their eventual Status. Further, to improve one of the Disciplines of a Caitiff costs six times the level purchased in experience points.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-caitiff","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"20"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.mmppzydAbV9Apu35"},"nQTySYn502Wi4drF":{"name":"Kiasyd","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Dominate (or Mortis), Mytherceria, Obtenebration</h5>\n<p>The Kiasyd are a bloodline of the Lasombra Clan; that much is obvious, given that these odd Kindred make use of the Obtenebration Discipline. But their aversion to iron, their odd power of Mytherceria, and their very appearance speak to even stranger ancestry.</p>\n<p>The Kiasyd are independent, focused more on their studies than on the causes of the Sword of Caine. The ones that do claim membership in the Sabbat aren’t passionately involved in the Sect’s activities. Some speculate that the Lasombra hold some power over them — did the Keepers create the Kiasyd, the way the Tzimisce created the Blood Brothers? Do the Kiasyd, whatever their origin, owe the Lasombra some debt? Whatever the nature of their relationship, it seems to benefit the Sabbat.</p>\n<p>The Kiasyd are, as a rule, calm and studious. They prefer to remain ensconced within their havens, poring over ancient texts, maps, artifacts, and other bits of antiquity. Other Kindred, it seems, can’t understand how a lineage of vampires that is so very <em>strange</em> could be nothing more than scholars. To hear the rumor-mill tell it, the Kiasyd can feed only on the blood of infants, or faeries, or unspoiled virgins. Their otherworldly heritage comes not from the Good Folk, but from demons and the forces of Hell. Their odd appearance isn’t because of mutation in their vitae, but a God-given warning: These are not Kindred. They are <em>other</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Weirdlings</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Most Kiasyd belong to the Sabbat, at least nominally. A rare few identify as Camarilla vampires, and others are independent. Since the Kiasyd don’t spend much time around each other if they can help it (see below), drawing a conclusion about their collective preferences is difficult.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Kiasyd aren’t called “Weirdlings” just because of their behavior. Most of them are well over six feet tall (two meters or more), willowy, and thin. Their skin isn’t just pale, as with most vampires, but glows faintly in moonlight. Their eyes are almond-shaped and have no visible pupils. They are often violet or jet black in color. Their ears are slightly pointed. The Kiasyd cover this with a hat or headband when going out amongst mortals, but even so, their height and skin tone make them stand out.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Most Kiasyd spend years building up their havens, improving security, library facilities, and other amenities. They are fiercely protective of their homes, and they do not allow guests without explicit invitations. When they do invite guests, however, they treat them well, offering vintage vitae, comfortable rooms and, of course, plenty of reading material.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Museum curators, talented students, librarians, rare book experts, and academics of all stripes are likely choices for Kiasyd Embraces. The Weirdlings want their progeny to be studious, polite, and educated, and they don’t want to take the time instructing them in the basics of erudite understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Mental Attributes and Knowledges are usually primary, though all Kiasyd have some degree of Etiquette. Occult is, of course, a common Ability. Resources, Generation, and Contacts (with other Kindred) are the only common Backgrounds. Kiasyd have difficulty mingling with mortals long enough to gain others.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Besides their somewhat freakish appearance, Kiasyd also have an allergy to iron. Touching iron requires an immediate roll to avoid frenzy, and any weapons made from cold iron inflict aggravated damage to Kiasyd.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Kiasyd don’t enjoy each other’s company, which is strange, considering how little they have in common with everyone else. After a short period of instruction (50 years at the most, and this period has grown much shorter in modern nights), a Kiasyd childe is released from her sire’s haven and is no longer the elder Kiasyd’s responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I didn’t invite you in. Leave. Touch nothing except the floor and the doorknob, or I swear on the moon and the stars I will have you on the other side of that mirror by morning.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>A grand experiment. I don’t think it has run its course quite yet, but it has so much ground to make up.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>What is the color of regret? What is the flavor of a bad debt? Like rust in my mouth.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>They have some sense of how reality really works. A shame that they squander it on parlor tricks.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 404)</small></h3>\n<p>Besides their somewhat freakish appearance, Kiasyd also have an allergy to iron. Touching iron requires an immediate roll to avoid frenzy, and any weapons made from cold iron inflict aggravated damage to Kiasyd.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-kiasyd","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"404"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.nQTySYn502Wi4drF"},"nb9vp4wg6Lo9w5MS":{"name":"Salubri","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Auspex, Dominate, Fortitude</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts):</h5>\n<ul><li>Healer Caste: Auspex, Presence, Valeren (Healer)</li><li>Warrior Caste: Auspex, Fortitude, Valeren (Warrior)</li><li>Watcher Caste: Auspex, Obfuscate, Valeren (Watcher)</li></ul>\n<p>The Salubri were once a true Clan. Their Clan founder, Saulot, was said to be a superlative warrior as well as a fair-minded judge. It was also said that he grew progressively more disgusted with Kindred affairs over time, and weary of the constant, bloody cycles of violence and betrayal. He left his home for lands far to the east. When he returned, he was changed. He was tranquil and contemplative, but also somewhat fatalistic. Most surprisingly, though, he had developed a third eye in his forehead. Saulot Embraced new childer, and his Clan slowly split into two distinct lines — the “warrior” and “healer” Salubri. For centuries, they acted as advisors, bodyguards, and sages to other Kindred and, to a lesser extent, mortals. And then came the diablerie of Saulot.</p>\n<p>The mages of the newly created Tremere line found Saulot’s torpid body, and drained his blood and soul. They usurped the power of the one of the Clans of Caine, and sent the Salubri into a downward spiral from which they never really recovered. Tonight, rumor has it that only seven members of the once-respected line exist at any given time.</p>\n<p>The rumors die hard, however. Modern Kindred know of the Salubri as diablerists who consume their own sires (and do it openly — patricide is hardly unknown among the other Clans, but they don’t <em>admit</em> to it) and remove the souls of other Kindred. The Soulsuckers are, supposedly, a Clan of defilers and liars from centuries past, possibly involved with demon worship, and demonstrably part of the Sabbat (see Salubri <em>antitribu</em>, p. 431). They ask, quite reasonably, how the Salubri are able to achieve Golconda with any regularity. They wonder how any line can sustain itself with so few members. And, finally, they pose this question: even if these first two notions are taken as given, why would the Salubri need to submit to diablerie? Why do their childer <em>need</em> to consume their sires’ souls, if not for some defect in the bloodline itself?</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Cyclops, or, less kindly, Soulsuckers</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Salubri are independent, though they do sometimes masquerade as other Clans and blend in with the Camarilla. The Salubri, however, are just trying to survive long enough to reach Golconda and pass along their blood. Sectarian politics mean nothing to them.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Each Salubri has a third eye in the center of their forehead. Otherwise, the Salubri are too few in number for any generalization about their appearances to be possible. Their ranks in modern nights have included children, the elderly, and adults of all races.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Salubri are unwelcome in most cities, and that means their havens are situated in isolated environments. They also usually have multiple escape routes, and only have what possessions the character can carry in a backpack. Quick departures are not uncommon for the members of this bloodline, so Salubri don’t get attached to their havens.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Salubri Embrace people whom they feel can find Golconda. Healers, teachers, empaths, and other people with high Humanity ratings are good choices, although occasionally Salubri Embrace evil people in hopes of seeing them redeemed (or, at least, setting them on that path).</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Social or Mental Attributes are often primary, as are Knowledges. Most Salubri also have decent ratings in Empathy. All Salubri must take five dots of Generation (to represent their diablerie of their sires), and most have at least a dot of Herd as well.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Salubri have difficulty feeding on unwilling vessels. If a Cyclops attempts it, she loses a point of Willpower.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Salubri are not, as a bloodline, organized. In fact, when Salubri do meet, it tends to be on the instruction of their sires — the dying wish of a given Cyclops to her childe might be to seek out another member of the bloodline and deliver a message or pay respects.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I say this and mean it truly — no burden I have ever carried is heavier than what this new eye has seen.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Judge a man by the company he keeps.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Fish gotta swim, birds gotta fly, warriors gotta make war.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>The enemy. For them, there can be no forgiveness.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 47)</small></h3>\n<p>The Salubri are hunted: Kindred of other clans are especially ... appreciative of Salubri vitae. When a non-Salubri partakes of the blood of a Cyclops, they often find it difficult to pull themselves away. Consuming enough to abate at least one Hunger level requires a hunger frenzy test (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 220) at Difficulty 2 + the Salubri’s Bane Severity (difficulty 3 + the Salubri’s Bane Severity for Banu Haqim). If they fail the test, they just keep consuming, to the point that they may have to be physically fought off.</p>\n<p>Additionally, the third eye that Saulot opened while on one of his many journeys passes down through the bloodline every time a Salubri Embraces. This third eye is not always recognizably human in origin, and rumors persist of vertical, serpentine pupils or even worm-like eyespots. While this third eye can be physically covered, such as with a headscarf or hood, it is always present, and no supernatural power can obscure it except those that hide the entire Salubri. Any time a Salubri activates a Discipline power, the third eye weeps vitae, its intensity correlating to the level of the Discipline being used, from welling up to a torrential flow. The blood flow from the third eye triggers a hunger frenzy test from nearby vampires with Hunger 4 or more.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Asceticism <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Something in the Salubri resists the vampiric condition, and the more they indulge their Hunger, the less inclined their Blood is to obey them. Only in abstinence are they free to employ the full extent of their powers, as a Salubri vampire needs the pangs of starvation to avoid stymieing their supernatural abilities.</p>\n<p>When their Hunger is below three, Salubri vampires suffer a penalty equal to their Bane Severity to any Discipline dice pools. This is in addition to the third eye, described on p. 47. <em>This variant provides characterization of the Salubri in chronicles where their hunted status isn’t practical or interesting to focus on, such as when the player coterie provides the majority of the vampires in the domain.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 408)</small></h3>\n<p>Salubri have difficulty feeding on unwilling vessels. If a Cyclops attempts it, she loses a point of Willpower.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-salubri","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"408"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.nb9vp4wg6Lo9w5MS"},"nqmT2xAFCpKfcDEa":{"name":"Salubri Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Fortitude, Valeren (Warrior)</h5>\n<p>The Salubri of the Sabbat are not the quiet, contemplative Kindred that seek Golconda in persecuted exile. No, these Kindred call themselves “Furies,” and they seek the blood of the Camarilla vampires — the Tremere especially — with a venomous passion. In nights past, as mentioned in the Salubri spread (p. 408), the Clan contained both healers and warriors. And now, in the modern nights, the warriors have reemerged — and stand to be counted with the Sabbat. The Salubri <em>antitribu</em> are devoted to the cause of killing Camarilla vampires, but not necessarily to other Sabbat agendas. They take part in the ritae of the Sect, and they share their blood, but what truly fires them up is the chance to invade Camarilla territory and burn some Tremere. If the ancient rumors of the Salubri 431 warriors being honorable knights are true, then times have indeed changed. These Kindred do not know “honorable combat” from “IED.” They just want to watch their enemies crumble to dust.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Furies</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Unlike their nonviolent brethren, Sabbat Salubri must consume blood taken by force, preferably in the heat of battle. Unless the vampire feeds on blood from a fallen foe or fights his target before feeding, the Vitae offers no nourishment. In addition, Salubri <em>antitribu</em> cannot start lower than Tenth Generation or higher than Twelfth, as their blood has yet to spread across a wider spectrum than that.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>I’d wear your skin, but I don’t want to smell like fear and gasoline.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Salubri V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 47)</small></h3>\n<p>The Salubri are hunted: Kindred of other clans are especially ... appreciative of Salubri vitae. When a non-Salubri partakes of the blood of a Cyclops, they often find it difficult to pull themselves away. Consuming enough to abate at least one Hunger level requires a hunger frenzy test (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 220) at Difficulty 2 + the Salubri’s Bane Severity (difficulty 3 + the Salubri’s Bane Severity for Banu Haqim). If they fail the test, they just keep consuming, to the point that they may have to be physically fought off.</p>\n<p>Additionally, the third eye that Saulot opened while on one of his many journeys passes down through the bloodline every time a Salubri Embraces. This third eye is not always recognizably human in origin, and rumors persist of vertical, serpentine pupils or even worm-like eyespots. While this third eye can be physically covered, such as with a headscarf or hood, it is always present, and no supernatural power can obscure it except those that hide the entire Salubri. Any time a Salubri activates a Discipline power, the third eye weeps vitae, its intensity correlating to the level of the Discipline being used, from welling up to a torrential flow. The blood flow from the third eye triggers a hunger frenzy test from nearby vampires with Hunger 4 or more.</p>\n<h3>Salubri V5 Players Guide Variant: Asceticism <small>(p. 58)</small></h3>\n<p>Something in the Salubri resists the vampiric condition, and the more they indulge their Hunger, the less inclined their Blood is to obey them. Only in abstinence are they free to employ the full extent of their powers, as a Salubri vampire needs the pangs of starvation to avoid stymieing their supernatural abilities.</p>\n<p>When their Hunger is below three, Salubri vampires suffer a penalty equal to their Bane Severity to any Discipline dice pools. This is in addition to the third eye, described on p. 47. <em>This variant provides characterization of the Salubri in chronicles where their hunted status isn’t practical or interesting to focus on, such as when the player coterie provides the majority of the vampires in the domain.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 431)</small></h3>\n<p>Unlike their nonviolent brethren, Sabbat Salubri must consume blood taken by force, preferably in the heat of battle. Unless the vampire feeds on blood from a fallen foe or fights his target before feeding, the Vitae offers no nourishment. In addition, Salubri <em>antitribu</em> cannot start lower than Tenth Generation or higher than Twelfth, as their blood has yet to spread across a wider spectrum than that.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-salubriantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"431"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.nqmT2xAFCpKfcDEa"},"q3XVZZYgkQ6lu2By":{"name":"Malkavian Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dementation, Obfuscate</h5>\n<p>The Malkavians are insane. It is their defining feature, and while not every Lunatic is dangerous, they are all unstable and unpredictable. And so the Malkavians of the Sabbat, the vampires of the Camarilla suspect, must be even crazier. They are quite correct. Malkavian <em>antitribu</em> are just as varied in dementia as their Camarilla brethren, but their madness carries with it a desperate, cloying edge. They are not satisfied with setting others on edge or forcing the “sane” vampires to challenge their own philosophies. They want — inasmuch as they can be said to collectively want anything — for the world to share their madness. Maybe they feel that their madness is overflowing, forcing its way from their minds into the minds of others through their Dementation Discipline. Whatever the case, the</p>\n<p>Clan at large — even outside the Sabbat — seems to have heard the message.</p>\n<p>Within the Sabbat, the Freaks take on roles appropriate to their madness. The truly vicious and uncontrollable ones are locked away in crypts and unleashed when the Sabbat needs shock and awe. The more functional Freaks lead war parties, perform ritae, or divine the future of the Sect from the entrails of their victims (often while those victims look on in horror). No matter what their role, though, the underlying goal of the Malkavian <em>antitribu</em> seems to be to bestow the gift of madness upon everyone around them.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Freaks</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Like all Malkavians (p. 61), the Freaks are completely insane. At character creation, the player must choose a derangement that the character can never overcome.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Consult the haruspex once again. Always learn the same lesson. Follow the tract, and it ends in the same place. We’re there, boys. We’re in the shit.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Malkavian V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 79)</small></h3>\n<p>Afflicted by their lineage, all Malkavians are cursed with at least one type of mental derangement. Depending on their history and the state of their mind at death, they may experience delusions, visions of terrible clarity, or something entirely different.</p>\n<p>When the Malkavian suffers a Bestial Failure or a Compulsion, their curse comes to the fore. Suffer a penalty equal to your character’s Bane Severity to one category of dice pools (Physical, Social, or Mental) for the entire scene. This is in addition to any penalties incurred by Compulsions.</p>\n<p>You and the Storyteller decide the type of penalty and the exact nature of the character’s affliction during character creation.</p>\n<h3>Malkavian V5 Players Guide Variant: Unnatural Manifestations <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Even the most mundane-seeming Oracle raises the hackles of those around them when they tap into the powers of their eerie Blood. Although they rarely recognize the source of these sensations, mortals respond with notable unease when a Malkavian uses their powers, and other vampires can readily recognize the presence of a fellow undead.</p>\n<p>Whenever a Malkavian uses a Discipline power, mortals in close proximity (roughly in the same room or equivalent) are spooked and any social interaction with them apart from intimidation suffers a dice penalty equal to the Malkavian’s Bane Severity. This sensation isn’t Masquerade-breaking, but the mortal is struck by a sudden fear or dislike of the vampire, lasting one scene. Other vampires experience a similar sensation, instantly recognizing the Malkavian as a vampire, though no penalties apply to interactions with them.</p>\n<p><em>If the troupe wants to emphasize the discomfiting nature of Malkavians and highlight their weirdness, this variant can fit the bill.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-malkavianantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"430"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.q3XVZZYgkQ6lu2By"},"q8psf4DRLyQOIA0M":{"name":"Cappadocians","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/cappadocians.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Fortitude, Mortis</h5>\n<p>The story of Clan Cappadocian is tragic, but illustrative of the parasitic cycle of Kindred existence. Nothing lasts forever in vampiric circles, but nothing ever truly dies.</p>\n<p>The Cappadocians were a Clan that focused on death and undeath. Their founder, an ancient known simply as “Cappadocius” (“of Cappadocia”), did not attempt to rule his Clan. He asked only that they continue the search for truth and the secrets of life and death. The Clan’s ancestral roots were rumored to be somewhere in Asia Minor, but by the end of the Dark Ages they had spread throughout Europe. Known as scholars, mystics, and necromancers, the Cappadocians were as firmly entrenched in the Cainite society of their time as any of the Clans of the Camarilla are tonight. The Graverobbers expanded their knowledge base by Embracing members of important families or promising scholars. By the 13th century they had inroads across Europe, in monasteries, universities, and in the courts of royalty, but also members and ghouls among the working classes. Everyone must eventually die, the Cappadocians realized, and every death was potentially a lesson. Their inclusive attitude may have spelled the Clan’s doom. They accepted a Venetian merchant family called the Giovanni (the family’s propensity for summoning up and controlling ghosts had as much to do with their Embrace as their skill with finance). Over the following centuries, the Giovanni worked their way into the hierarchy of the Clan, until Augustus Giovanni committed diablerie upon Cappadocius. Over the next few centuries, the Giovanni systematically destroyed the remaining Cappadocians. In modern nights, only small remnants survive, such as the Harbingers of Skulls. The lineage of these remnants is largely unknown.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Graverobbers</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Cappadocians did not survive long enough to take a side in the Camarilla/Sabbat divide.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> The Graverobbers were pale and corpselike, even for vampires. Their hair often darkened slightly after the Embrace, giving them even more of an ethereal appearance. The Cappadocians dressed appropriately for their station and role.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Cappadocians preferred a measure of solitude when choosing their havens. Although they had to stay close enough to mortals to feed, they often chose quiet locales to make their homes — monasteries, crypts, and even sewers or cisterns. The Cappadocians acting as scholars or advisors enjoyed a better standard of unlife, but even they often chose places that mortals had no interest in exploring.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> As a general rule, the Cappadocians were not concerned with ethnicity or nationality when Embracing, meaning that it wasn’t uncommon to find Moors, Jews, or even pagans brought into the Clan. The Graverobbers were more inclined to Embrace people with an interest in death. That didn’t necessarily mean <em>causing</em> death (though certainly they Embraced their share of soldiers and hunters), but scholars of the afterlife and religion, gravediggers, priests, and hermits all found their way to Clan Cappadocian.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Mental Attributes and Knowledges were often primary, and rare was the Cappadocian without at least a few dots of Occult. The Clan included warriors as well as scholars and priests, and a Cappadocian who chose to be a tyrant could be a frightening creature indeed. For the most part, though, the Clan tended more toward intellectual and spiritual pursuits, and choices of Abilities and Backgrounds reflected that.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> As mentioned, the Cappadocians exhibited a deathly pallor, and this only worsened as the vampire grew older. Young Cappadocians were able to pass for human (though mortals that knew what to look for could pick them out easily), but elder Cappadocians showed shriveled skin and a much more withered countenance.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Clan Cappadocian was, in its heyday, an important Clan historically. It claimed domain in several important cities across Europe, and it wasn’t uncommon for Graverobbers to act as spiritual or mystical advisors to other medieval Princes. Internally, the Clan had little strict hierarchy, though as the years wore on the Clan’s core became more localized around the founder (which, in turn, allowed the Giovanni to take it over).</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Everyone must ride with Charon, by and by. We merely wish to know what to ask him before we step onto his boat.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>High Clans:</strong> <em>We have seen the death of kings, and it is much like the death of peasants. But the final resting places are grand, and maybe that makes the difference.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Low Clans:</strong> <em>When towers have fallen and kings are thrown down, who will remember us? It will be the low-blooded, and I hope they are kind in their eulogies.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 418)</small></h3>\n<p>As mentioned, the Cappadocians exhibited a deathly pallor, and this only worsened as the vampire grew older. Young Cappadocians were able to pass for human (though mortals that knew what to look for could pick them out easily), but elder Cappadocians showed shriveled skin and a much more withered countenance.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-cappadocians","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"418"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.q8psf4DRLyQOIA0M"},"rSJ5pHh6WJiaXwpf":{"name":"Assamite Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/assamiteantitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Celerity, Obfuscate, Quietus (Cruciatus)</h5>\n<p>When the Tremere of the Camarilla subjected Clan Assamite to their curse, rendering Kindred blood a deadly poison to the Clan of Assassins, not every member of that Clan succumbed. Some broke away from the main body of the Clan, willing to face whatever doom awaited rather than submit to the infidel’s magic. The result was the Assamite <em>antitribu</em>. These vampires are Cainites in a more direct sense than many Sabbat. They regard Haqim as an abomination, just one of the hated Antediluvians. Instead, they revere Caine, and work to become closer to the Third Mortal. Their method, of course, is to lower their Generation through diablerie. Beyond that, though, no much differentiates the Angels of Caine from the “mainstream” Assamites. Both are masters of hunting and killing, and both observe strict codes of honor and fierce zeal where their ongoing crusade is concerned. Both grow darker of skin as they grown older and more powerful. And both forms of Assamite believe that in the Final Nights, the truly reverent and devout shall be rewarded, while the weak perish at their fangs.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Angels of Caine</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Assamite <em>antitribu</em> have the post-curse weakness mentioned in the sidebar on the previous page. In fact, their weakness never changed, while the rest of the Clan shackled with the blood curse of the Tremere. Whenever an Assamite <em>antitribu</em> drinks vampire blood, the player must roll Self-Control or Instincts (difficulty 3 + the number of blood points consumed). If this roll fails, the character is addicted. Once addicted, the character will indulge in drinking Kindred blood whenever possible. When she is given the opportunity to do so, the player must roll Self-Control or Instincts (difficulty 6). If this roll fails, the character frenzies, attacks the target and drinks as much blood as she can.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>What gives me the right to take your blood? Oh, I don’t have a “right.” I have a duty.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Banu Haqim V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 18)</small></h3>\n<p>Banu Haqim are drawn to feed from those deserving punishment. This is especially true for vampire Blood, the very essence of transgression. When one of the Judges tastes the Blood of another Cainite, they find it very hard to stop.</p>\n<p>Slaking at least one Hunger level with vampiric vitae provokes a hunger frenzy test (See Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 220) at a Difficulty 2 + Bane Severity. If the test is failed they attempt to gorge themselves on vampire Blood, sometimes until they perform diablerie upon their Kindred victim. This presents many problems for the Banu Haqim, as many Kindred see the Amaranth as anathema, and greatly at odds with the sort or orderly behavior the Judges presume to uphold. And of course, no few Banu Haqim excoriate themselves over the hypocrisy of their own failures.</p>\n<h3>Banu Haqim V5 Players Guide Variant: Noxious Blood <small>(p. 56)</small></h3>\n<p>Death lurks in the Blood of the so-called Assassins. While its effect on other vampires is the same as any vitae, it is poison to mortals and risks the life of anyone the Banu Haqim would try to make their ghoul or Blood Bond. A mortal drinking the Blood of a Banu Haqim vampire suffers Aggravated Damage equal to the Bane Severity of the vampire for each Rouse Check’s worth of Blood ingested. In addition, Banu Haqim vitae cannot be used to heal mortal injuries (see p. 139). Banu Haqim vitae does not have any other toxic properties; in amounts below the quantity needed to create a Blood Bond, it inflicts no damage even if injected directly into the bloodstream, for example.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is useful when the chronicle is focused on mortal society and interaction over that of other vampires. Banu Haqim ghouls will be rare, with only the hardiest mortals able to survive the tainted power they provide.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 428)</small></h3>\n<p>Assamite <em>antitribu</em> have the post-curse weakness mentioned in the sidebar on the previous page. In fact, their weakness never changed, while the rest of the Clan shackled with the blood curse of the Tremere. Whenever an Assamite <em>antitribu</em> drinks vampire blood, the player must roll Self-Control or Instincts (difficulty 3 + the number of blood points consumed). If this roll fails, the character is addicted. Once addicted, the character will indulge in drinking Kindred blood whenever possible. When she is given the opportunity to do so, the player must roll Self-Control or Instincts (difficulty 6). If this roll fails, the character frenzies, attacks the target and drinks as much blood as she can.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-assamiteantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"428"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.rSJ5pHh6WJiaXwpf"},"sxFueQ1EEYYjT8qx":{"name":"Brujah Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brujahantitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Celerity, Potence, Presence</h5>\n<p>The Brujah <em>antitribu</em> are, perhaps, the historical founders of the Sabbat. It was the young members of Clan Brujah, after all, who broke with their elders during the Anarch Revolt, and it was these same volatile Kindred that fought against the agents of those elders when other vampires might have considered compromise. Brujah <em>antitribu</em> are just as unstable and angry as their Camarilla counterparts, but unlike the Rabble, they don’t have to pretend to be in control. When a Brujah <em>antitribu</em> wants something — blood, money, property, or a new childe — he takes it. This attitude has led to the bloodline being one of the most numerous in the Sabbat, and one that supports the Sect with fervor. They turn their infamously violent passion toward every cause that the Sabbat puts forth. While they traditionally don’t assume leadership roles, when they do undertake their own missions, they execute them with brutal and ostentatious abandon. The Brutes take pains to make others uncomfortable. Whether that’s by their manner of dress, their barely concealed appetite for blood, or simply that they seldom retract their fangs even around mortals, the bloodline does what it can to stand out. That might mean a breach of the Masquerade, of course, but the Brujah <em>antitribu</em> reason that they are the Damned — so why shouldn’t they raise a little hell?</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Brutes</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The bloodline has the same weakness as the main body of the Clan (p. 51): All frenzy difficulties are increased by two, to a maximum of 10. Unlike Camarilla Brujah, though, the Brutes aren’t in the least bit sensitive about their tempers. Indeed, they revel in their rage.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Heads, we kill everybody here. Tails, we only kill the ones that stay after I tear that faggot’s ears off.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Brujah V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 67)</small></h3>\n<p>The Blood of the Brujah simmers with barely contained rage, exploding at the slightest provocation. Subtract dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Brujah from any roll to resist fury frenzy. This cannot take the pool below one die.</p>\n<h3>Brujah V5 Players Guide Variant: Violence <small>(p. 56)</small></h3>\n<p>Unliving vectors of revolt, the Brujah cannot help but change things — often violently and not always for the better. While all vampires harbor a capacity for destruction, a hungry Brujah always does some kind of damage.</p>\n<p>On a messy critical result on any Skill test, a Brujah vampire causes damage (physical or mental, depending on the situation) to the subject of their interaction equal to their Bane Severity, in addition to any other result of the Hunger dice. The damage is Aggravated unless the player spends a point of Willpower, making the damage Superficial instead.</p>\n<p><em>This variant makes sense in a chronicle where Frenzy – including the regular Brujah Bane – rarely comes into play either because of the narrative or the troupe going for a more rules-light approach.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-brujahantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"429"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.sxFueQ1EEYYjT8qx"},"tU1uTrJaUzT3s2FS":{"name":"Tzimisce","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Animalism, Dominate, Protean</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Auspex, Vicissitude</h5>\n<p>A blood moon casts a crimson light over the land beyond the forest and something fearsome howls its agony into the night. The Tzimisce call these lands their ancestral home. Since time out of mind the Fiends have been masters and lords of the domains of much of Eastern Europe. But theirs is a proud, selfish Clan for which tradition goes only so far despite their aristocratic origins. In fact, the Clan claims to have destroyed its Antediluvian, and in the wake of that momentous event, helped establish the foundations of the Sabbat.</p>\n<p>Tzimisce practice a strange Discipline known as Vicissitude that allows them to twist the skin and bone of their victims. In many cases, they refine their fleshly arts by practicing upon themselves, but they just as frequently use it upon their lackeys and retainers, turning their boyars and <em>szlachta</em> into monstrous thralls. Vicissitude itself is a much misunderstood Discipline, and debates over its origins occasionally plunge the Clan into vicious partisan rivalries.</p>\n<p>Clan Tzimisce is a Clan of extremes, and long, cold nights spent in remote castles have turned the Fiends’ perspectives both greatly inward and outward. Mystics of the Clan study a philosophy of metamorphosis, seeking to discover what lies beyond the state of vampirism. An alien attitude of spiritual secularism characterizes many Tzimisce. Young members of the Clan often find themselves detached from the historical role of the Fiends as terrifying landed lords, and throw themselves wholly into a cause of their own choice, whether as Sabbat zealots, fleshcrafted horrors, or transcendental <em>koldun</em> sorcerers.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Fiends</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Tzimisce are longtime but largely detached members of the Sabbat. They were instrumental in the Sect’s nascence, and remain one of its most numerous Clans.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Given their ability to manipulate their physical appearance with Vicissitude, Tzimisce look however they want, and they often want to provoke or frighten. Some prefer extreme modifications and experimentations with their bodies that leave them looking only vaguely humanoid. Others seek to redefine and even transcend the limits of their forms, rebuilding themselves in the images of angels, monsters, nightmares, and things even less recognizable.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Young Tzimisce are often Sabbat Priests or Ducti, and prefer to maintain communal havens with their packs. They encourage the pack to live in fearsome locations, such as beneath a hospital or morgue, or in the dank recesses of a mausoleum. Elders of the Clan sometimes have ancestral holdings in the Old World, and the image of the vampire on the craggy mountain in a crumbling castle owes much to Tzimisce lords. Rarely are these ancient holdings kept to any modern standards of comfort, but their lords are strangely hospitable to invited guests (and terribly intolerant of uninvited bores).</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Elder Tzimisce, particularly those of the still-landed nobility in hoary old domains, may have family lines from which they Embrace, or they may restrict their occasional siring to the terrified villages suffering in thrall beneath their estates. New World and younger Tzimisce aren’t as discriminating and are more practical. Indeed, many Tzimisce fledglings are little more than shock troops, Embraced and warped to the limits of their frames to cause revulsion and revel in bloodshed until put down.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Few Tzimisce know moderation, and thus often favor Physical or Mental Attributes, usually with one extraordinarily high Trait. Knowledges are usually favored, though Skills are valued as well. Mentor, Allies, Domain, and Retainers are all quite appropriate to Tzimisce, as are narrow — almost overdeveloped — Disciplines. Their alien mindsets often lead them to follow Paths of Enlightenment. The ghastliness to which many Tzimisce are often exposed during their fledgling years sometimes results in complicated derangements.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Fiends are, on the whole, mistrustful of other Kindred, especially others of their own Clan. As such, Tzimisce organization, such as it is, has a high regard for solitude. It is against their nature to be inclusive, and thus they have to work at being Sabbat (though this is less difficult for younger Tzimisce of at least somewhat modern outlooks). This is also a key reason so many of them undertake Paths of Enlightenment: to give their xenophobia purpose, but also to provide some common point of reference with others on the Path.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Why on earth would you think I wanted you to</em> stop <em>screaming? I find the results so much more melodious when you indulge your fear.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>The fox convinces the hens to let him watch over their precious eggs — delightful!</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>They plunged themselves into ruin millennia ago and continue to blame other for their own failures.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Why worship dead gods when one can become a god herself?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>They command fierce power, but wield it with little more than a primitive will.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Muck out the stables, you slope-browed lummoxes.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>Competent. Mostly.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>Suitable for making lampshades from their remains.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>Ah, what marvelous paradoxes, redolent of the slime yet possessed of the wisdom to adapt!</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>I’ll take thirty, staked for the sun and placed on the ramparts.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>Thou shalt not suffer a bitch to live.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>They are the gout of corruption that issues forth from a lanced boil, only they have none of its charisma.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>We have warred for so long that they are due a grudging respect before you snuff their heart’s fire.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>How droll! I always thought the term for them was “spare parts.”</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>When the serfs don’t believe their masters, the reich has lost the war.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>We play the game with the pawns— er, pieces we are given.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>There is a misguided nobility in what they do, though they are too childish to succeed fully.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 54)</small></h3>\n<p>The Tzimisce are grounded: Each Tzimisce must choose a specific charge — a physical domain, a group of people, an organization, or even something more esoteric — but clearly defined and limited. The Kindred must spend their daysleep surrounded by their chosen charge. Historically this has often meant slumbering in the soil of their land, but it can also mean being surrounded by that which they tonight rule: a certain kind of people, a building deeply tied to their obsession, a local counterculture faction, or other, more outlandish elements. If they do not, they sustain aggravated Willpower damage equal to their Bane Severity upon waking the following night.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Cursed Courtesy <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>Just as many Tzimisce are deadly serious about their own haven and property, so are they compelled to treat that of others. A Tzimisce vampire entering someone else’s home uninvited suffers severe distress and an almost physical desire to leave.</p>\n<p>A Tzimisce vampire wishing to enter a place of residence (homes and havens alike) uninvited must spend Willpower equal to their Bane Severity and also suffers a similar dice penalty to their Discipline pools during their stay. The invitation must be made by someone who lives (or unlives) there, and thus only actual inhabited homes or havens fulfill this condition. The Tzimisce can freely enter public or abandoned buildings; and flats or apartments count as separate homes for this purpose. Edge cases are left to the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>Tzimisce using this Bane cannot take the corresponding Folkloric Block as a Flaw.</p>\n<p><em>If the chronicle is set in an established domain with few, if any, changes of haven, this variant Bane shines another spotlight on Tzimisce characteristics.</em></p>\n<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 70)</small></h3>\n<p>The Tzimisce are inextricably tied to their domains of origin, and must rest in the proximity of at least two handfuls of “native soil” — earth from a place important to her as a mortal, such as the soil from her birthplace or the graveyard where she underwent her Embrace. Each night spent without this physical connection to her land limits all of the Tzimisce’s dice pools to one-half, cumulatively, until she has only a single die in her pool. The penalty remains until she rests for a full day amid her earth once more.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-tzimisce","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"70"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.tU1uTrJaUzT3s2FS"},"tm0xsfywx5UetsrD":{"name":"Giovanni","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Dominate, Fortitude, Mortis</h5>\n<p>Achieving prominence during the Venetian Renaissance, the Giovanni family built their fortune on the rise of the middle class and the ready profit of banking and Mediterranean trade (and the criminal enterprise that came with it). However, with the family’s rise came hubris, as its <em>paterfamilias</em> sought ever more power, and with that hubris came horror. With his earthly power at its apex, Augustus Giovanni turned to the arts of controlling the dead, and in doing so, gained the Embrace from a forgotten Antediluvian. With a conclave of conspirators, the Giovanni plunged a now-forgotten Clan into oblivion and built their own legacy on its corpse.</p>\n<p>Since those first nights, the Giovanni have accepted no limits on their ambitions, despite opposition from Kindred outside their Clan and a well-deserved reputation as “Devil Kindred.” They studied forbidden arts, becoming formidable in the <em>nigrimancy</em> that allowed them power over the spirits of the departed, and degeneracy followed in the wake of unclean ritual. To this night, the Giovanni are known for the insular nature of their Clan and the incestuous practices by which they populate it. A few outside families and factions fall under Giovanni auspices, but the vast majority of the Clan comes from the debased mortal family. The family remains successful despite all of their ghastly peccadilloes, and has amassed a vast wealth through crime, politics, and the secrets of the dead that keeps them in their position of degraded opulence.</p>\n<p>In public view, the Giovanni make a great show of humility and respect. Part of this gentility is a habit of centuries, still in place from when the other Clans hunted the usurping Necromancers (and to preserve their hard-won neutrality from the conflict between the Camarilla and the Sabbat). Another part of it is the velvet glove hiding the iron fist of their nature, remarkable for forcing ghosts, minds, and bodies alike to bow to their needs. To hear the Giovanni tell it, Princes and Archbishops alike owe them favors, and anyone with something to offer may earn their patronage.</p>\n<p>For the most part, the Giovanni participate little in the Jyhad, pursuing their own agenda of cultivating wealth and building a foundation of power in the lands beyond the veil of death. Outsiders rarely comprehend the goals of the Necromancers, but only the most trusted of the Giovanni know that the Clan wants to plunge the world into a state where the dead and the living commingle. And with their mastery of Necromancy, the Giovanni would be positioned to rule it all.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Necromancers</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Outwardly, Giovanni dress with subtlety and taste. Much of the Clan comes from the original mortal family, and have not only olive Italian complexions, but some amount of inherited family features. Those outside the immediate family often appear “of a type,” and in the traditional garb of their regional family branch.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The family wealth of the Giovanni is evident in their havens, which may take the form of villas or lavish estates. The Necromancers often have valuables invested in their havens as well, such as galleries of fine art or displays of jewelry. Many Giovanni also maintain secondary havens, where they may have elaborate necromantic crypts or just flats where they can lie low if necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Giovanni of the main family branch have usually spent some amount of time as a ghoul in a practice known as the Proxy Kiss. During this time, the Kindred-to-be learns about the treacherous and jealous reality of the vampire family. She learns ambition and an unhealthy dose of duplicity, in addition to the family history and customs. Giovanni rarely see much of the outside world on their own terms during the Proxy Kiss period, and often become insular and alienated, while at the same time eager to stand out among the other family ghouls.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Giovanni vampires often have outgoing, professional Demeanors that hide unpleasant Natures warped in their upbringing. Social or Mental Attributes are usually primary, though some of the “soldiers” of the family prefer Physical Attributes. Emphasis likewise usually falls on primary Knowledges or Talents, depending on proclivity. A split in the Clan sees those who favor the practicality of Backgrounds (particularly those tied to wealth and exerting influence) diverge from those who prefer the forbidden puissance of Disciplines. Few Giovanni could be described as well-rounded.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Like few other Clans, the Necromancers have a top-down organization where policy is made by a (presumed) still-active Clan progenitor, Augustus. The family maintains an enormous palazzo known as the Mausoleum in Venice, where elders and fledglings alike dance to the whims of their ancient puppetmasters. Clan structure is itself familial, with the added complications that degeneracy and vampirism offer. Incest, ancestor worship, necrophilia, cults of guilt, and bizarre relationships in which fathers and grandmothers are their own issue’s childer make a mire of the Clan and family, and fracture many Giovanni long before they become Kindred.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>You misunderstand the situation if you think you’re important to me alive.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Beware, as all the money in the world still cannot buy one passage back from the grave.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>Inexpensive allies or irascible enemies.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>Dangerous relics deluded by a primitive grasp of what lies beyond the Shroud.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Gangrel:</strong> <em>Let them have their mud and misery.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>Like us, they stand with one foot in the temporal world and one foot in the occult.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>Once they crumble to dust, they don’t have to suffer their madness anymore.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>Dead men do tell tales, and the Sewer Rats have so many tales to tell.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>The Kindred have forever to make of their fates what they will, and this is how they spend it?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>How tragic is the Kindred so desperately afraid of what she truly is.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>We don’t enshrine the history that made us what we are, so why do they?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>The worm that feasts on its own flesh must eventually starve.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>They often forget that Machiavelli worked in the wake of the Borgias and the Medicis.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>Without knowing their origins, what can they possibly amount to?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla and Sabbat:</strong> <em>Let them have their endless war, for we grow rich by selling each side what it needs to continue the effort.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>Until they build something of lasting import, they are the ugliest of the sisters.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Players Guide Bane <small>(p. 23)</small></h3>\n<p>The Giovanni inflict a notoriously <em>painful Kiss</em>. The fangs of the Giovanni bring not bliss, but agony. Victims caught unawares will violently resist unless restrained, and few people submit willingly to the torture that is the Giovanni Kiss. When drinking directly from a victim, Giovanni may only take harmful drinks, resulting in blood loss (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 212). Unwilling mortals not restrained will try to escape, and even those coerced or willing must succeed in a Stamina + Resolve test against Difficulty 2 + Bane Severity in order not to strain against the pain. Coerced or willing vampire victims of the Giovanni bite must make a Frenzy test against Difficulty 3 to avoid falling into a terror Frenzy.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Decay <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>The Giovanni connection to death and entropy cause them to practically radiate decay, slowly eating away at things both living and inert in their vicinity. Their havens turn decrepit in a matter of nights, plants wither around them, and even mortals grow haggard and sickly from prolonged exposure. Some Giovanni come to terms with this, making do with dilapidated havens and few, if any, mortal servants. Those who don’t must spend considerable time and effort to keep their assets from decaying around them.</p>\n<p>Giovanni vampires suffer additional dots in Flaws equal to their Bane Severity divided as they see fit among Retainer, Haven and Resources Flaws. These can either be taken at character creation or “bought off” by paying twice the amount of Background dots. In addition, purchasing dots in these Advantages costs an additional amount of experience points equal to Bane Severity.</p>\n<p><em>If the chronicle does not emphasize feeding scenes or the characters are able to procure vessels in more controlled ways, this variant can provide an interesting angle on the Giovanni.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-giovanni","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"56"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.tm0xsfywx5UetsrD"},"u4K8wCvfQfeaLNQ4":{"name":"Angellis Ater","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/angellisater.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Daimonion, Dominate, Obtenebration</h5>\n<p>The <em>Angellis Ater</em> are an odd bloodline, one that crosses boundaries between Baali and Lasombra. The so-called “black angels” believe that vampires are the instrument of Satan, and they worship sin and the darkness of the soul. Their Lasombra blood lets them reach into the blackness, pull it forth and manipulate it, and the black angels become dependent upon and addicted to use of the Obtenebration Discipline over time.</p>\n<p>The bloodline has been around since at least the Middle Ages, descended in part from a powerful Baali called Azaneal. This monstrous vampire acted as a leader within the Baali line for a time, and twisted the orthodoxy of some Kindred enough to create a kind of heresy, one that was all too attractive to the Lasombra. The Clan of Shadows’ power base traditionally included the Catholic Church, and over time the <em>Angellis Ater</em> made at least some inroad with these pious (or pious-seeming) Lasombra. How much influence in the Church they were able to wield is not known, but they did survive the Inquisition. <em>Angellis Ater</em> are rare in modern nights, but they do exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Black Angels</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The <em>Angellis Ater</em> have the same weakness as other Lasombra (p. 59) — they do not cast reflections.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>We reach into the shadows, reach into hell. We pull out what you find there, and paint the world with it.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 436)</small></h3>\n<p>The <em>Angellis Ater</em> have the same weakness as other Lasombra (p. 59) — they do not cast reflections.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-angellisater","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"436"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.u4K8wCvfQfeaLNQ4"},"vcOlFKKugDeVP5vU":{"name":"Ventrue Antitribu","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ventrue-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Dominate, Fortitude, Presence</h5>\n<p>The Ventrue pretend to nobility, but they are nothing more than the ruling class of the dying world — merchants and moneylenders. Clan Ventrue has a glorious past, a lineage of knights, paladins, and warrior-kings. The Blue Bloods of tonight relish backroom dealings, compromise and favor-trading. In other words, say the Ventrue <em>antitribu</em>, they are no better than the sniveling Tremere. The Ventrue <em>antitribu</em> might not be the moral backbone of the Sabbat, but they are definitely some of the most idealist Cainites therein (although there are rumors that there may be two distinct bloodlines both claiming to be the “true” Ventrue <em>antitribu</em>). They carry with them the notions of <em>noblesse oblige</em> and chivalry. They believe that their Camarilla counterparts have betrayed not only the true nature of the vampiric race, but the role of their Clan in particular and feel that only by taking up the mantle of Warriors of Caine can they atone. When Gehenna comes and the skies rain fire and blood, it will not be the Crusaders that hide in their havens and pray that doom passes them by. These devout Kindred will be at the forefront, swords — or machine guns — hoisted high and the ancient motto of their Clan proudly on their lips: <em>Regere sanguine, regere in veritatem est</em> (“To rule in blood is to rule in truth”).</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Crusaders</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> Sabbat Ventrue have the same weakness as their Camarilla brethren (see p. 73). The player decides with the Storyteller what specific type of blood suits the Crusader, and this choice is permanent. Blood of other types (even animals) simply offers the vampire no blood pool increase, no matter how much he consumes</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>You’re not worthy of the blood you’re carrying. Time to give it to a</em> real <em>child of Veddartha.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>Ventrue V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 102)</small></h3>\n<p>The Ventrue are in possession of rarefied palates. When a Ventrue drinks blood from any mortal outside their preference, a profound exertion of will is required or the blood taken surges back up as scarlet vomit. Preferences range greatly, from Ventrue who can only feed from genuine brunettes, individuals of Swiss descent, or homosexuals, to others who can only feed from soldiers, mortals who suffer from PTSD, or methamphetamine users. With a Resolve + Awareness test (Difficulty 4 or more) your character can sense if a mortal possesses the blood they require.</p>\n<p>If you want your character to feed from anything but their preferred victim, you must spend Willpower points equal to the character’s Bane Severity.</p>\n<h3>Ventrue V5 Players Guide Variant: Hierarchy <small>(p. 59)</small></h3>\n<p>While many Ventrue seek to lead, they are also swift to obey their superiors. In many cases chosen for a pragmatic mindset, Ventrue childer learn to heed their sire, and their Blood-borne instincts often make them yield in a similar way to any vampire of older Blood.</p>\n<p>A Ventrue vampire suffers a penalty to their Discipline dice pools equal to their Bane Severity when attempting to use their powers on a vampire of lower generation. They must also spend Willpower equal to their Bane Severity if they wish to directly attack a vampire of lower generation.</p>\n<p><em>If the chronicle does not focus on feeding scenes or the characters can procure vessels in more controlled ways, this variant helps define the Ventrue as everything from honoring anachronistic feudal social codes to being unabashed bootlickers.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-ventrueantitribu","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"433"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.vcOlFKKugDeVP5vU"},"vyAXsdPqoyox2TDf":{"name":"Gangrel","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V5): Animalism, Fortitude, Protean</h5>\n<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Animalism, Fortitude, Protean</h5>\n<p>A glint of red eyes in the darkness, the scent of a predator’s musk, a flash of fangs, the sound of flesh tearing: These mark the presence of the Gangrel. More than any other Clan, the Gangrel resemble the beasts associated with the legends of vampires: bats, wolves, and other creatures of darkness. Indeed, the Outlanders may develop the ability to transform themselves into these and other, more primal forms.</p>\n<p>The Gangrel have other characteristics in common with animals as well. Many shun the elaborate social constructs of both Kindred and kine. A number of them prefer to move alone or as a member of small packs or coteries. Most are tough and, when pressed, ferocious. And when Gangrel succumb to the depredations of the Beast, they are left with some feature redolent of the animal kingdom.</p>\n<p>As a Clan, the Gangrel are wary and aloof. Most would prefer to spend their nights stalking prey or wilding on the rooftops than minding Princely edicts or lobbying for recognition of domain. Theirs is a tense relationship with vampire society, and Outlanders are among the most frequent to turn Anarch or Autarkis. In some localities, the Gangrel have collectively abandoned membership in any Sect — insofar as the Gangrel truly ever do anything collectively.</p>\n<p>The Jyhad seems less pronounced among the Gangrel than it is among other Clans, and the Outlanders have little regard for the eternal conflict. Differences between Gangrel more often fall along the lines of domain and feeding rights than they do on generational mistrust, and the things that concern young Outlanders are matters that concern Gangrel ancillae and even elders. Still, one would be hard-pressed to consider the relationship among Gangrel of different generations amicable, except in isolated sire-childe situations. The Beast is ever suspicious of those who would take sustenance from it.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Outlanders</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> The Gangrel at least nominally belong to the Camarilla, but the Clan has always had a vocal faction that spurns membership in any Sect. They claim that political games and social structures do nothing for the primal Gangrel.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Personal presentation is often not high on the list of many Gangrel priorities, and a Gangrel’s appearance is often more a matter of circumstance than it is of active decision. The Clan’s weakness can contribute a great deal to their appearance, as does an extended unlife in the places where they make their havens, which are frequently short of modern conveniences.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Gangrel often lair where they can, taking refuge when the sun threatens to rise. Those who do maintain permanent havens often lean toward the utilitarian: Everything from a cave to a covered alley to an illegal squat may serve as a Gangrel haven, usually with little demarcating them as any sort of personal territory (until it’s too late for the unfortunate interloper).</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Gangrel sire childer like they seek prey: after long hunts during which the prospective childe doesn’t even know she’s being followed. Creating a fledgling means sharing limited resources, so each sirechilde relationship is unique and significant. Outlanders Embrace because they choose an individual, not out of whim or recklessness. Those who earn their attention are hardy, whether physically or emotionally.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Sufficiency is the Gangrel hallmark, and many have outsider or loner personality archetypes. Physical Attributes are far and away most frequent, as are Talents with a smattering of Skills and Knowledges. Many Gangrel focus on Disciplines rather than Backgrounds, preferring to rely on themselves more than others. Gangrel almost never have significant Resources, Influence, or Retainers.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>This is not a place for you, and it is too late to turn back.</em> Run<em>, little man.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Regional groups of Gangrel occasionally assemble in convocations that draw from ethnic or cultural influences. These are informal affairs, geared more toward sharing information and revelry than advancing any cogent agenda. Aside from these infrequent gatherings, almost all Gangrel organization is very local where it exists at all, from pairs of sire-andchilde through terrifying packs centered around one accomplished Outlander.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Assamite:</strong> <em>Pull off the fucking head and the body will die.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Brujah:</strong> <em>I wouldn’t call what we have “trust.” More of an understanding that we have a common enemy.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Followers of Set:</strong> <em>If it smells like carrion, I don’t want any part of it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>I try to make it a habit not to keep talking to things once I’ve killed them.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lasombra:</strong> <em>I’m not afraid of the dark, so you’d better have something else underneath that cape.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Malkavian:</strong> <em>Ten gallons of bullshit in a one-gallon jug.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nosferatu:</strong> <em>When they get nervous, get the fuck out of there.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ravnos:</strong> <em>If she starts calling you her brother, pop your claws and make your point. It’s the only way she’ll learn.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Toreador:</strong> <em>Assume that they want something from you when they lower themselves to your level.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>Fight or flight with the grayfaces. Choose carefully.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tzimisce:</strong> <em>When I see a carcass eaten through with worms, I’m smart enough to know it’s rotten.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ventrue:</strong> <em>If you like answering to a master, you can do worse… but not much.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Caitiff:</strong> <em>Unless they pull their weight, who needs another mouth to feed?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Any port in a storm, I guess.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>Self-righteous evangelical garbage.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Anarchs:</strong> <em>Almost. They almost get it.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V5 Corebook Bane <small>(p. 73)</small></h3>\n<p>Gangrel relate to their Beast much as other Kindred relate to the Gangrel: suspicious partnership. In frenzy, Gangrel gain one or more animal features: a physical trait, a smell, or a behavioral tic. These features last for one more night afterward, lingering like a hangover following debauchery. Each feature reduces one Attribute by 1 point – the Storyteller may decide that a forked tongue or bearlike.</p>\n<p>musk reduces Charisma, while batlike ears reduce Resolve (“all those distracting sounds”). If nothing immediately occurs to you, the feature reduces Intelligence or Manipulation.The number of features a Gangrel manifests equals their Bane Severity. If your character Rides the Wave of their frenzy (see p. 219) you can choose only one feature to manifest, thus taking only one penalty to their Attributes.</p>\n<h3>V5 Players Guide Variant: Survival Instincts <small>(p. 57)</small></h3>\n<p>Nothing matters more to the Animals than survival. Among the most tenacious of all vampire clans, the Gangrel can endure almost anything and thrive in the harshest conditions. Where the Beast can often put a vampire in unfavorable situations, a Gangrel’s instincts are calibrated foremost to make sure they see another night.</p>\n<p>Subtract dice equal to the Bane Severity of the Gangrel from any roll to resist fear Frenzy. This cannot take the pool below one die.</p>\n<p><em>This variant is good for danger-filled chronicles where fire and sunlight are constant threats but where mental and social conflicts are less common, or if the troupe just doesn’t want to bother with tracking temporary dice penalties.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"clan-gangrel","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"54"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.vyAXsdPqoyox2TDf"},"wLPCv33x7vYITTr5":{"name":"Gargoyles","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gargoyles.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Flight, Fortitude, Potence, Visceratika</h5>\n<p>The Tremere unveiled the Gargoyle bloodline in obviously strained. The Gargoyles’ revolt and induc- AD 1167, and the Slaves have been a steady, if infretion into the Camarilla has all but doomed the requent, part of Kindred society ever since. Gargoyles maining slave Gargoyles, as their masters now monitor might, in fact, be one reason that the Tremere weren’t exterminated entirely in their first few centuries of existence. These odd quasi-Kindred often look like their stone namesakes — rocky-skinned, ugly, winged creatures that exist only to serve their masters.</p>\n<p>Or such was the case for several hundred years. Recently, though, many of the Slaves have thrown off their chains and joined the Camarilla. Why the Gargoyles choose to ally themselves with the Sect that also protects their former masters is anyone’s guess. One of the going theories is that it’s a slap in the Warlocks’ collective face, but most Kindred don’t give the Gargoyles credit for being that sophisticated. More likely, the Rockheads simply realize that in the Camarilla, their talents will be recognized and no one will try and order them around (at least not directly).</p>\n<p>Gargoyles are created out of other Kindred, and this ugly fact makes their creation a seldom-discussed topic in polite vampiric society. The Tremere start with a Gangrel, Nosferatu, or Tzimisce vampire and perform obscene, bloody rituals using its blood, heart, and skin. Vitae from another vampire (from one of the other two Clans) is used to provoke a violent mutation, with the result that some Gargoyles look purely hideous (Nosferatu base), some look feral (Gangrel base), and some look fleshy and sport bony protrusions (Tzimisce base). No Gargoyle can pass for human.</p>\n<p>The Tremere tolerate the Gargoyles’ presence in the Camarilla as best they can — it’s not as though they can easily re-enslave them, after all. But relations are them ruthlessly for any sign of betrayal. The Tremere also aren’t above planting hypnotic suggestions in their Slaves’ minds, so that if they ever <em>do</em> rebel, they still serve a function for the Clan.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Traditionally Slaves, though free Gargoyles have been known to crack heads over it. Other common nicknames are Rockheads, Runaways, Freemen, and Grotesques.</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Gargoyles usually join the Camarilla. It’s not impossible for a Slave to join the Sabbat, but the bloodline as a whole frowns on this.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Not all Gargoyles look like the stone grotesques found spitting rainwater over old buildings, but all are nightmarish. Some have tufts of hair protruding randomly over their bodies, or odd, galloping walks. Most Gargoyles have gray, rock-like skin. All Gargoyles have wings. These grow as the character’s Flight (see p. 447) increases.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> Slave Gargoyles sleep wherever their masters allow them — closets, basements, crypts, cells. Free Gargoyles prefer sleeping in havens high above the ground. Bell towers and disused high-rises are favorites. Gargoyles are often muscle for other Kindred, sometimes in exchange for a bare-bones haven.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> Gargoyles creation rarely shows a discernible pattern. The Change tortures the new Gargoyle’s body, as bones elongate and split to form the wings. The face warps, leaving nothing to recognize the mortal or vampire by. They rarely remember their unlife or mortal existence.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> Created to be slaves, Gargoyles specialize in Physical Attributes, Talents, and a few select Skills (Melee, for instance). All Gargoyles start with a free dot of the Flight Discipline. Appearance is always zero. Gargoyles created by the Tremere are at the same Generation as the Kindred that act as their “base.”</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Master. Why did you burn her? She only wanted to fly free for one night… yes, master. I obey.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Slaves are hideous. That grotesquery takes different forms, but always results in an Appearance of zero. They are also highly susceptible to mind control from any source. A Gargoyle’s Willpower score (current or permanent) is considered two points lower when used to resist such powers.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> Gargoyles have a strong sense of community. They often keep communal havens. Sometimes Gargoyles petition the Prince for the release of slave Gargoyles, but more often they just tear slave owners to pieces. With no community, Gargoyles are likely to Embrace and build their own.</p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Our new home. Here, no one shouts or strikes us or burns us with mirrors and light. Here, they ask politely, and we are free to ask for payment in blood. Paradise.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>They are so brutal. And they love each other so. And yet, when I think of them, my eyes begin to burn. Why should this be?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Tremere:</strong> <em>I will never be a slave again.</em> Never.</p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 400)</small></h3>\n<p>The Slaves are hideous. That grotesquery takes different forms, but always results in an Appearance of zero. They are also highly susceptible to mind control from any source. A Gargoyle’s Willpower score (current or permanent) is considered two points lower when used to resist such powers.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-gargoyles","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"400"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.wLPCv33x7vYITTr5"},"xacQc5laQ1ilUU38":{"name":"Wu Zao","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/wuzao.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (V20): Auspex, Fortitude, Obeah or Valeren (player’s choice at character creation)</h5>\n<p>Saulot left Europe and traveled east, where he met native creatures that, though they fed on blood, were not truly Kindred. These “Wan Kuei” called him Zaolat, and regarded him as a trickster, a thief, and a barbarian. Unwelcome though he was, Zao-lat managed to leave a legacy behind — the Wu Zao. These Kindred are the descendants of two vampires Embraced by Zao-lat during his time in the Middle Kingdom (named Zao-zei and Zao-xue). These two vampires helped Zao-lat uncover the arcane secrets of the East, break into the lairs of demons, and learn their weaknesses.</p>\n<p>But the local vampires eventually drove Zao-lat from their lands, leaving behind his two childer to carry on his work. Since then, the Wu Zao have been intrigued or (more accurately) obsessed by whatever areas of arcane study pique their interests. Their scholarly ways have served them well — when news of Saulot’s destruction reached them, some few went into hiding and avoided purges in their own lands. The bloodline survives in modern nights — rare and quiet, but present.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Orphans</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Wu Zao are scholars by nature, and each of them has a special focus. At character creation, the player must select an area of arcane study — the Wan Kuei, the culture of a specific mortal group, a group of mystical tomes lost to the ages, etc. Whenever the opportunity arises to learn something about this subject, the player must make a Willpower roll (difficulty 6) to avoid pursuing that lead to the exclusion of all else.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>How much? Ha! For that old trinket! Never! Wait, wait. How much again?</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 438)</small></h3>\n<p>The Wu Zao are scholars by nature, and each of them has a special focus. At character creation, the player must select an area of arcane study — the Wan Kuei, the culture of a specific mortal group, a group of mystical tomes lost to the ages, etc. Whenever the opportunity arises to learn something about this subject, the player must make a Willpower roll (difficulty 6) to avoid pursuing that lead to the exclusion of all else.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-wuzao","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"438"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.xacQc5laQ1ilUU38"},"xm0ij6taligfG4O5":{"name":"Telyavelic Tremere","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/telyavelictremere.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Auspex, Dominate, Sielanic Thaumaturgy</h5>\n<p>In the early nights of Clan Tremere, before the diablerie of Saulot, a small band of Warlocks made its way into Lithuania. Clan records state that the original goal of this journey was to expand the Clan’s influence into these lands, or to find allies. Some elder Tremere, though, claim that these pilgrims took the journey to escape from the clutches of their Clan once and for all. Whatever the original motive behind the exodus, when the Tremere arrived, they fell in with the pagan peoples of Lithuania and learned of Telyavel, the protector of the dead. The local people saw the vampires — nocturnal, bloodthirsty, and obviously magical — as incarnations or avatars of their death-gods, and the Tremere grew to believe them. They cast off the name “Tremere,” and simply became the “Telyavs.” While they had some infrequent contact with chantries outside of Lithuania, for the most party they remained patrons and predators to the villagers. This was not to last, however. In the late 13th century, the Ventrue warlord Jürgen the Sword-Bearer destroyed one of the last remaining enclaves of the Telyavs. The bloodline managed to limp along for another few centuries, but by the 16th century, the main body of the Tremere reported that all of them had been destroyed.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Telyavs, Shepherds</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Telyavs bound their fates so tightly to their pagan herds that they took on some of the same fears and enemies. They are weak against Christian symbols and faith. Difficulties to resist frenzy are two higher than normal when confronted by an enemy using True Faith as a defense. They recoil from the sight of the cross or other symbols of the Christian faith.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>We reached too high. Power and enlightenment aren’t found in formulae. They are found in dirt, wood, and blood.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 438)</small></h3>\n<p>The Telyavs bound their fates so tightly to their pagan herds that they took on some of the same fears and enemies. They are weak against Christian symbols and faith. Difficulties to resist frenzy are two higher than normal when confronted by an enemy using True Faith as a defense. They recoil from the sight of the cross or other symbols of the Christian faith.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-telyavelictremere","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"438"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.xm0ij6taligfG4O5"},"y0pP8YyjeITEhDHQ":{"name":"Samedi","type":"clan","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/samedi.webp","system":{"description":"<h5>Disciplines (Dark Gifts): Fortitude, Obfuscate, Thanatosis</h5>\n<p>If the Nosferatu are repulsive and the Harbingers of Skulls are hideous, a word hasn’t yet been invented to describe the Samedi. Resembling nothing so much as walking, rotting corpses, these odd vampires take their name and their origin from the Caribbean and the religion of vodoun (popularly known as voodoo). Baron</p>\n<p>Samedi, in legend, was a <em>loa</em> of death and ancestor worship. The vampires that bear his name and visage (for Samedi is often portrayed as a corpse, albeit not one so disgusting as the Stiffs) also often share his rude, blunt demeanor. In fact, a vampire going by the name of “Baron Samedi” seems to be the founder, or at least the oldest extant member, of the bloodline.</p>\n<p>The Samedi might seem to be an offshoot of the Nosferatu, but their powers of Thanatosis — a Discipline dedicated to manipulating dead flesh — speak of a connection to the Giovanni or their doomed predecessors, the Cappadocians. If that is the case, though, the Samedi and the Giovanni went their separate ways long ago, for neither has anything good to say about the other. The reason for this enmity is not something that members of either line discuss in public, but the Giovanni apparently have a history with Baron Samedi himself. The Baron, still active in the bloodline’s affairs, refuses to discuss the “deal” that he had with Augustus Giovanni, but it must have been something of gravity for the mutual dislike to persist.</p>\n<p>Samedi take the roles of enforcers and assassins in Kindred society. They don’t do it on ideological grounds, like the Assamites, and thus they find work with Kindred who want to get what they paid for. The Samedi do not take contracts against Nosferatu or, strangely, Giovanni Kindred without a heavy fee, speaking to some regard or fear of these Clans. As with so many other facts about their line, the Samedi have nothing to say on the matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Nickname:</strong> Stiffs</p>\n<p><strong>Sect:</strong> Samedi occasionally join the Camarilla or the Sabbat, but for the most part they consider themselves independent.</p>\n<p><strong>Appearance:</strong> Saying that a Samedi resembles a rotting corpse is, sadly, in no way a euphemism. Their flesh is soft and tends to leak fluid if poked too hard. Their ears and noses rot away (but still function normally), and tiny bits of their skin tend to slough off and regrow. Skin retracts from the eyes and teeth, giving them the appearance of grinning skulls, but too much meat clings to the cranium for them to achieve the cleaner horror of the Harbingers of Skulls. Some Samedi are leathery and dry, like unwrapped mummies. These Stiffs still stink, but their stench is more reminiscent of rotted flowers and old decay than fresh putrescence.</p>\n<p><strong>Haven:</strong> The Samedi make havens in places where a moldering corpse and its stench aren’t out of place. Morgues, mortuaries, graveyards, and meat packing plants house the occasional Samedi. Some members of the bloodline choose to dwell below ground like the Nosferatu, but this does lead to conflict between the Stiffs and the Sewer Rats.</p>\n<p><strong>Background:</strong> The Samedi are a relatively young line. No Samedi older than 250 years is known to exist. Most of the older ones hail from the Caribbean islands or the southern United States. Neonate Samedi don’t always share this connection to vodoun, but frequently were close to death (one way or another) before the Embrace.</p>\n<p><strong>Character Creation:</strong> While Physical Attributes are a priority for those Samedi working as killers and enforcers, many Samedi lean toward Mental Traits. Samedi rarely have the Herd or Resources Backgrounds, and their standoffish and solitary nature means that Mentor and Allies are equally rare.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The Samedi, as mentioned, are putrid beyond words. Samedi characters have Appearance ratings of 0, and automatically fail Appearance rolls.</p>\n<p><strong>Organization:</strong> The Samedi are too rare to be organized. They might cross each other’s paths occasionally and trade stories, but no plan or schedules exists for these meetings, at least as far as anyone outside the bloodline can tell.</p>\n<p><strong>Quote:</strong> <em>Make fun of me one more time and I’ll hug you, you prissy bitch.</em></p>\n<h3>Stereotypes</h3>\n<p><strong>Camarilla:</strong> <em>Careful. These jokers have a built-in reason to set us on fire, and they aren’t afraid to use it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sabbat:</strong> <em>They’re more honest than the Camarilla, anyway. But I smell like a dead asshole already; I don’t need to actually</em> be <em>a dead asshole.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Giovanni:</strong> <em>Just because you’re older than me don’t mean I give a fuck what you think, dago.</em></p>","bane":"<h3>V20 Corebook Weakness <small>(p. 410)</small></h3>\n<p>The Samedi, as mentioned, are putrid beyond words. Samedi characters have Appearance ratings of 0, and automatically fail Appearance rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"bloodline-samedi","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"410"}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.clans.Item.y0pP8YyjeITEhDHQ"},"081vY7ZFyUom8GLp":{"name":"Pursuer","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some people will never be believed, while others will never be missed. You study your victim, learning their routine and whether they can disappear without raising a hue and cry. Then you stalk and pursue your victim throughout the night, only striking when your sensibility and your hunger reach their most delicious pitch.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Investigation (Profiling) or Stealth (Shadowing)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Animalism or Auspex</p><p>■ Gain the Merit: Bloodhound (•)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Contacts from among the morally flexible habitués of your hunting ground: club bouncers, homeless watchers, night vendors, street dealers, etc. Someone who answers when you ask “which way did she go?”</p><p>■ Lose one dot of Humanity</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Investigation to find a victim nobody misses, or Stamina + Stealth for long stalks of unknowing urban prey.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"108"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.081vY7ZFyUom8GLp"},"5daWimmGKBwqgmcr":{"name":"Tithe Collector","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p><em>Ancilla Predator type.</em></p><p>With age and status often come the comforts of convenience. The Tithe Collector holds enough power that the vampires around them pay tribute in the form of carefully selected vessels, delivered at regular intervals or upon request. As long as they maintain their position and return the vessels in reasonable condition, it’s someone else’s job to worry about the Masquerade.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Intimidation (Kindred) or Leadership (Kindred)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Dominate or Presence</p><p>■ Gain three dots of Domain or Status, representing a domain large enough to share or a specific position near the top of your community</p><p>■ Gain an Adversary (••): not everyone appreciates your influence</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"17"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.5daWimmGKBwqgmcr"},"6kYtSuJVfhqHBvlQ":{"name":"Siren","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You feed almost exclusively during or while feigning sex, and you rely on your Disciplines, seduction skills, or the unquenchable appetites of others to conceal your carnivorous nature. You have mastered the art of the one-night stand or move through the sex-club scene like a dark star. You think of yourself as a sexy beast, but in your darkest moments, you fear that you’re at best a problematic lover, at worst a habitual rapist. A former lover who escaped destruction might be your Touchstone or your stalker. (If so, add them to the Relationship Map.) Maybe in life you were a pick-up artist, movie producer, author, a glorious slutty kinkster – or a virgin who intends to make up for lost time post-mortem.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Persuasion (Seduction) or Subterfuge (Seduction)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Fortitude or Presence</p><p>■ Gain the Looks Merit: (••) Beautiful</p><p>■ Gain the Enemy Flaw: (•) A spurned lover or jealous partner</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Charisma + Subterfuge: you feed under the guise of sex. Pick up your victim, charm them, and take them somewhere alone to feed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"178"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.6kYtSuJVfhqHBvlQ"},"AnnwXcvUgGP5PTK0":{"name":"Bagger","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You steal, buy, or otherwise procure cold blood rather than hunt, relying on the black market or your skills as a burglar or ambulance chaser. Perhaps you still work the night shift at the hospital. Ventrue may not pick this Predator type.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Larceny (Lockpicking) or Streetwise (Black Market)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Blood Sorcery (Tremere only) or Obfuscate</p><p>■ Gain the Feeding Merit: Iron Gullet (•••)</p><p>■ Gain the Enemy Flaw: (••) Either someone believes you owe them, or there’s another reason you keep off the streets.</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Streetwise: you acquire preserved blood rather than hunt, or you feed from the dead or dying. Find your prize, gain access, and purchase or otherwise convince someone with the goods to give you access.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"176"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.AnnwXcvUgGP5PTK0"},"Chn8TNTCGEoNHMYa":{"name":"Roadside Killer","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You belong with the vagabonds, tourists, and truckers of the world — always moving, never at home. You know how to pick out the ones whose deaths are discounted as the risk of a woman hitchhiking alone, or who simply won’t be missed at all. You still have to fight to keep other vampires away from them lest the herd thins too much, though. In life, you either were one of them, or you met these vagrants as they rested at your roadside stop.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Survival (the road) or Investigation (vampire cant)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Fortitude or Protean</p><p>■ Gain two additional dots of migrating Herd</p><p>■ Gain the Flaw: Prey Exclusion (locals)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Dexterity or Charisma + Drive; you feed by picking up down and outs with nowhere else to go.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.Chn8TNTCGEoNHMYa"},"JAYGxh1b80eBtXLd":{"name":"Graverobber","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Graverobbers often feed from fresh corpses, but despite their name, they also feed from mourners in cemeteries and visitors and patients in hospitals. Melancholic Resonance in a victim’s blood appeals more than any other humor. This predator type often requires the vampire to hold a haven in or maintain connections to a church, hospital, or morgue.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Occult (Grave Rituals) or Medicine (Cadavers)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Fortitude or Oblivion</p><p>■ Gain the Feeding Merit: Iron Gullet (•••)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of the Haven Background</p><p>■ Gain the Herd Flaw: (••) Obvious Predator (your cold nature makes you act in a deeply unsettling matter when hunting)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Resolve + Medicine, sifting through the quiet dead for a body bearing rancid blood. Moving among miserable mortals for a vulnerable bite uses Manipulation + Insight. A cold corpse can slake up to 3 Hunger, but suffers the same slake penalties as bagged blood. A body fed on by another vampire, drained of blood afterwards, or missing parts slakes less.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"108"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.JAYGxh1b80eBtXLd"},"Lv7s7fBZNe7Y3Xyi":{"name":"Sandman","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You rely on your stealth or Disciplines to feed from sleeping victims. If they never wake during the feeding, they won’t know you exist. Perhaps you were very anti-social in life; you don’t feel cut out for the intense interpersonal nightlife or physical violence of more extroverted hunters.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Medicine (Anesthetics) or Stealth (Break-in)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Auspex or Obfuscate</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Resources</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Dexterity + Stealth: you feed from sleeping victims. Case a hotel or house, break in, feed silently and get out.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"177"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.Lv7s7fBZNe7Y3Xyi"},"Oh0f3p89h7cX5HoY":{"name":"Osiris","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are a celebrity among mortals or else you run a cult, a church, or something similar. You feed from your fans or worshippers, who treat you as a deity. You always have access to easy blood, but followers breed problems with the authorities, organized religion, and indeed the Camarilla. In life, you might have been a DJ, a writer, a cultist, a preacher, or a LARP organizer.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Occult (specific tradition) or Performance (specific entertainment field)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Blood Sorcery (Tremere only) or Presence</p><p>■ Spend three dots between the Fame and Herd Backgrounds</p><p>■ Spend two dots between the Enemies and Mythic Flaws</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge or Intimidation + Fame: you feed from your fans, church, or other adoring crowd. The Skill for which you’re famous may be Performance, Science, Craft, Academics, Politics, or something else. Display yourself, choose a victim, and flatter or bully them into feeding you.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"177"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.Oh0f3p89h7cX5HoY"},"RmzdB0JGnsjNCqi1":{"name":"Farmer","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You only feed from animals. Your Hunger constantly gnaws at you, but you have not killed a single human being so far (except perhaps that one time), and you intend to keep it that way. You could have been anyone in life, but your choice speaks to someone obsessed by morality. Perhaps you were an activist, priest, aid-worker, or vegan in life, but the choice never to risk a human life is one anyone could arrive at and struggle to maintain. Ventrue may not pick this Predator type. You cannot pick this Predator type if your Blood Potency is 3 or higher.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Animal Ken (Specific Animal) or Survival (Hunting)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Animalism or Protean</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Humanity</p><p>■ Gain the Feeding Flaw: (••) Farmer</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Composure + Animal Ken: you feed from animals. Find your quarry, catch your chosen animal, and feed from it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"177"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.RmzdB0JGnsjNCqi1"},"RttWYtBAk37pJw3t":{"name":"Grim Reaper","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Also known as a plague rat, you only feed on those about to die. You seek out hospice care facilities, assisted living homes and shelters. This means you are constantly on the move looking for new victims near the end of their lives and often have a difficult time settling down yourself.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Awareness (Death) or Larceny (Forgery)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Auspex or Oblivion</p><p>■ Gain one dot of the Allies or Influence Background in the medical community</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Humanity</p><p>■ Gain the Feeding Flaw: (•) Prey Exclusion: Healthy Mortals</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Intelligence + Awareness or Medicine.</p><p>You may also gain a taste for specific diseases, and have an easy time identifying them by taste.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"108"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.RttWYtBAk37pJw3t"},"aMowxHrYnKh01OLg":{"name":"Cleaver","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You feed covertly from your (or someone’s) mortal family and friends with whom you still maintain ties. The most extreme cleavers adopt children, marry a human, and try to maintain a family life for as long as they can. Add your family to the Relationship Map. Cleavers often go to great lengths to keep the truth of their condition from their family, but some also maintain unwholesome relationships with their own kin. The Camarilla forbids taking a human family in this fashion, and it frowns on cleavers as Masquerade breaches waiting to happen. Wiser Kindred may massacre your family for your own good if they find out your secret and care what happens to you.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Persuasion (Gaslighting) or Subterfuge (Coverups)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Dominate or Animalism</p><p>■ Gain the Dark Secret Flaw: (•) Cleaver</p><p>■ Gain the Herd Advantage (••)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Manipulation + Subterfuge: you take blood covertly from your mortal family or friends. Socialize with your victims, feed from them, and cover it up to groom them for next time.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"176"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.aMowxHrYnKh01OLg"},"ap2RGsY9vxgK3bLp":{"name":"Consensualist","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You never feed against your victim’s free will. You masquerade as a representative of a charity blood drive, as a blood-drinking kink-lord in the “real vampire community,” or by actually telling your victims what you are and getting their permission to feed. The Camarilla call that last method a Masquerade breach, but many Anarch philosophers consider it an acceptable risk. You could have been anything in life, but a sex-worker, a political organizer, or a lawyer could all be wary of feeding without consent.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Medicine (Phlebotomy) or Persuasion (Victims)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Auspex or Fortitude</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Humanity</p><p>■ Gain the Dark Secret Flaw: (•) Masquerade Breacher</p><p>■ Gain the Feeding Flaw: (•) Prey Exclusion (nonconsenting)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion: you take blood by consent, under cover of medical work or a shared kink. Cultivate your victims, feed from them, and validate their choice to feed you.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"177"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.ap2RGsY9vxgK3bLp"},"cxHfFpgCQmPARr82":{"name":"Alleycat","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>A combative assault-feeder, you stalk, overpower, and drink from whomever you can, when you can. You may or may not attempt to threaten or Dominate victims into silence or mask the feeding as a robbery. Think about how you arrived at this direct approach to feeding and what makes you comfortable with an unlife of stalking, attacking, feeding, and escaping. You could have been homeless, an SAS soldier, a cartel hit-man, or a big-game hunter.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Intimidation (Stickups) or Brawl (Grappling)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Celerity or Potence</p><p>■ Lose one dot of Humanity</p><p>■ Gain three dots of criminal Contacts</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Strength + Brawl: you take blood by force or threat, stalking, overpowering, and bleeding your victims. If you feed on criminals as a sort of dark knight of the streets, use Wits + Streetwise to find a victim.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"175"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.cxHfFpgCQmPARr82"},"eepVlDW58fMYr3PT":{"name":"Scene Queen","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You rely on your familiarity with a certain subculture and a well-crafted pose, feeding on an exclusive subculture that believes you to be one of them. Your victims adore you for your status in the scene, and the ones who understand what you are disbelieved. You may belong to the street or be literal upper-class, abusing the weak with false hope and promises of taking them to the next level. In life, you almost certainly belonged to a scene similar to the one you stalk now.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Etiquette (specific scene), Leadership (specific scene), or Streetwise (specific scene)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Dominate or Potence</p><p>■ Gain the Fame Advantage: (•)</p><p>■ Gain the Contact Advantage: (•)</p><p>■ Gain either the Influence Flaw: (•) Disliked (outside your subculture) or the Feeding Flaw: (•) Prey Exclusion (a different subculture from yours)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Manipulation + Persuasion: you feed from a high- or low-class subculture in which you enjoy high status. Make the scene, groom and isolate a victim from whom to feed, and gaslight or silence them to keep the scene cool. “I’ll let the others see us together if you keep it together.”</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"178"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.eepVlDW58fMYr3PT"},"mVblX3i2G2fW4TY8":{"name":"Blood Leech","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You drink from other vampires, either by hunting, coercion or by taking Blood as payment – the only truly moral way of feeding you can think of. Unfortunately, this practice is usually forbidden in Kindred society. It is either risky as all fuck or requires a position of enviable power.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Brawl (Kindred) or Stealth (against Kindred)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Celerity or Protean</p><p>■ Lose one dot of Humanity</p><p>■ Increase Blood Potency by one</p><p>■ Gain the Dark Secret Flaw: (••) Diablerist, or the Shunned Flaw: (••)</p><p>■ Gain the Feeding Flaw: (••) Prey Exclusion (mortals)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> none – you feed from other vampires; if you make a mistake, you die, either tonight or in a blood hunt. The Storyteller should not abstract something like this to a set of die rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"176"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.mVblX3i2G2fW4TY8"},"oPvnegSTF8DuB3Ex":{"name":"Trapdoor","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Like the trapdoor spider, you build your nest and lure your prey into it. You may lurk in an amusement park dark ride or a steamy Turkish bath, haunt a house or manage a fight club, but your victims come to your place of power. There you might toy with them in terror, imprison and drain them slowly, or just drink deep and send them on their way.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Persuasion (Marketing) or Stealth (Ambushes or Traps)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Protean or Obfuscate</p><p>■ Gain one dot of the Haven Background</p><p>■ Gain one dot of either Retainers (your roper, major-domo, etc.) or Herd (steady visitors for whatever reason), or a second Haven dot</p><p>■ Gain one Haven Flaw: Creepy (•) or Haunted (•)</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Charisma + Stealth if your victims enter your killing ground anticipating a fun night out, or Dexterity + Stealth if you feed on trespassers, urban explorers, or other passersby. Navigating the maze inside your den calls for Wits + Awareness (but you can add your Haven dots in dice to the pool since you know it so well).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"109"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.oPvnegSTF8DuB3Ex"},"rwtmdJCYxPIcQihf":{"name":"Extortionist","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The extortionist likes to force their victims to bleed for them. Ostensibly, the extortionist acquires blood in exchange for services such as security or surveillance, but as many times as the need for protection is real, it is just as often a fiction engineered to make the deal feel acceptable to the victim.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Intimidation (Coercion) or Larceny (Security)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Dominate or Potence</p><p>■ Distribute three dots between the Contacts and Resources Backgrounds</p><p>■ Gain the Enemy Flaw: (••) the police or a victim who escaped your extortion and now wants revenge</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> Strength or Manipulation + Intimidation; you feed through coercion both subtle and painfully obvious.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"107"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.rwtmdJCYxPIcQihf"},"ygHnChYmyb6zIwjK":{"name":"Montero","type":"predatorType","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Since the Middle Ages, aristocratic Spaniards have mounted the <em>montería</em>: a deer hunt in which teams of beaters drive game onto the spears (and later the guns) of the <em>montero</em>, the huntsman. You carry on that tradition, using retainers to herd your victims to you for the kill. Your modern <em>montería</em> may take the form of a long con, a flash mob, a protest kettle, an endless bureaucratic shuffle, or a seemingly senseless gang pursuit.</p><p>■ Add a specialty: Leadership (Hunting Pack) or Stealth (Stakeout)</p><p>■ Gain one dot of Dominate or Obfuscate</p><p>■ Gain two dots of Retainers</p><p>■ Lose one dot of Humanity</p><p><strong>Predator Pool:</strong> With an experienced team of retainers, the hunt comes down to planning: Intelligence + Stealth. With a well-practiced plan, the hunt comes down to patiently waiting for the game to arrive: Resolve + Stealth.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"108"},"dicepool":{}},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.predator-types.Item.ygHnChYmyb6zIwjK"},"2b70VAegIg10GcQJ":{"name":"••••• Blood Curse Immunity (Ghoul)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have discovered that you are resistant to the Clan or bloodline flaws present in the blood you drink. Your domitor might be a lower Generation Nosferatu, who lives in the sewers because their appearance is so hideous, and yet your appearance will not change. Should members of the Tremere or Tzimisce Clans learn of your Blood Flaw Immunity, you may be sought after for further study. After all, if a ghoul’s biology is such that you do not suffer as much as your domitor, surely there’s a “cure” for the flaws present in every Clan’s or bloodline’s blood, not to mention the potnetial for curing revenant Family Afflictions.</p><p>This Merit no longer applies if your ghoul or revenant is Embraced and is refunded.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2b70VAegIg10GcQJ"},"320ZxBZUGQeVnafL":{"name":"•• Nuit Mode","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Cainite’s body does not revert to it’s death-state each night, enabling them to keep new haircuts and body modifications. They can mend these changes anytime as if they were Aggravated damage. This does not work for characters with BP higher than 1</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.320ZxBZUGQeVnafL"},"3iEVgtfqiYC3DCCL":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Faded (Obfuscate)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire spends so much time hiding from others that they have begun to forget who they truly are. This lack of conviction weakens their moral fortitude and makes them second-guess decisions. When making Degeneration tests, the vampire rolls one fewer die (this cannot bring the pool to 0).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Faded (Obfuscate) (Degeneration (Remorse) tests)","value":-1,"paths":["humanity"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3iEVgtfqiYC3DCCL"},"3vN5uxKwK2fTHALN":{"name":"•• Eidetic Memory","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You remember what you see and hear with perfect detail. Documents, events and conversations, can be committed to memory with only minor concentration. Under stressful conditions involving numerous distractions, you must make a Resolve + Awareness roll (Difficulty 2) to summon enough concentration to absorb what your senses detect.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3vN5uxKwK2fTHALN"},"5ASFgUz04XAyjjMQ":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Permanent Wound","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You suffered injuries during your Embrace yor transformation somehow failed to repair or sustained heavy damage as a mortal. You have one point of Health less.</p><p>A five-dot version of this inflicts a dice penalty of -2 dice to all physical rolls adds +1 to all additional damage suffered.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5ASFgUz04XAyjjMQ"},"5LFhEFubrM2cdFVU":{"name":"Demon Master","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mawla (meaning protector, friend or a master in Arabic) is now a category of Backgrounds to best show the specific types of relationships that can happen. If it is too much of a bother, you do not have to build your Mawla’s sheet. Simply write their purpose such as teaching sorcery.</p><p>You may not directly control your Mawla or benefit from their Advantages. It should happen rarely in situations created by the storyteller, usually at a price. They may provide teamwork on projects not related to Backgrounds with a trivial boon required for others (one trivial boon should buy at least 4 project rolls’ worth of teamwork). Unusual Mawlae may require more unusual payments, however.</p><h3>Demon Master (Non-Ghost Only)</h3><p>You have sworn fealty to a demon, trading Faith and other services for whatever you have managed to bargain with them. You may be a thrall to only a single demon at a time. The Demon Master has a maximum number of 5 dots, spread across power and benevolence showing the balance between what they their services.</p><p>For Demon and Thrall mechanics around Arcana, pacts, Divinity and so on, see Appendix B, p. XX.</p><p>This Background does not represent enslaved demons as they will never be permanently enslaved and will come back for revenge if they wish so. A thrall may not use any powers to enslave their master, but may use powers to summon them as normal.</p><p>To demons, faith is a currency, fuel for their powers and even their existence. As a thrall, you will be required to do daily prayers or “religious” rituals or suffer a punishment from your master who can brutally sap faith from you at will, potentially killing you. A character may refuse to do this, but they will either be visited by a demon’s agent (or, worse, the demon themselves) or have their Faith ravaged from them (p. XX). A player character may be ravaged at the storyteller’s discretion for Bestial Failures, Messy Criticals or for story purposes unless the player invests in Benevolence.</p><p>A thrall with this Background is provided with a Celestial Name of their master. If a Celestial Name is spoken aloud or silently in one’s mind, a person may attract attention from a demon who will be able to sense where they are and perceive the world through the senses of the one who attracted their attention. For this reason, uttering names such as “Kupala” and “Belial” as a joke within the presence of demonologists is considered a grave faux pas.</p><p>This Background can only be obtained at character creation. Getting a demonic master during play should be free and fully tailored by the storyteller. Note that the chances of stumbling upon a “benevolent”, let alone “loving” demon master should be astronomically low. Demons of this Background are among the weaker ones. You will not get Belial or Asmodeus unless the Storyteller assigns him to you.</p><p>Demons are always controlled by the storyteller, but the control may be ceded to a player for a combat scene to reduce the storyteller’s burden. Players do not build their own demons, but the Storyteller does in secret (after discussing the limits of the power with the player and its capabilities) or simply uses the Storyteller fiat to represent the demon.</p><p>■ <strong>Power:</strong> </p><p>• Petty Demon: Build them as an Average Individual. Divinity 2.</p><p>•• Weak Demon: Build them as a Gifted Individual. Divinity 4</p><p>••• Average Demon: Build them as a Gifted Individual. Divinity 6</p><p>•••• Strong Demon: Build them as a Deadly Individual. Divinity 8.</p><p>■ <strong>Benevolence:</strong> </p><p>Without dots in Benevolence, the demon expects its full tax in Faith.</p><p>•• Caring: The demon is…caring enough (as much as that word can mean in this context). They have fully exempted you from the “faith tax” either because they see potential and some other form of value in them or because you have managed to negotiate it in the pact. You will never be ravaged.</p><p>••• Loving: Perhaps they have some genuine affection for your or are just pretending in order for their agenda to be advanced, they fully exempt you from the “faith tax” and may even aid you in life. Whenever you wish for them to show up, they will come in around 10 turns. The demon will speed up its journey if necessary to defend their investment, arriving in 3 turns if they sense their thrall is in danger and has been telepathically called for them.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"381"},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5LFhEFubrM2cdFVU"},"6cd4abzxgRwmUK5k":{"name":"•• Pale Aura","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Due to some quirk of fate or your reaction to the Blood, your aura is naturally pale. Any colour your character’s aura takes has a pale cast to it, making you indistinguishable from a vampire when viewed by powers such as Scry the Soul.</p><p>This Merit can be of great use if you wish to impersonate a vampire for a time, but can also cause a dangerous misunderstanding or lead to your capture for further scrutiny.</p><p>If a character viewing your aura scores 5 or more margins, they may notice your real aura behind the layer of pale.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6cd4abzxgRwmUK5k"},"6lZB6OUmW29N0vyU":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Low Pain Threshold","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are unable to deal with a lot of physical pain. When a character’s Health tracker is filled with damage equal to their Stamina, they become Impaired.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6lZB6OUmW29N0vyU"},"7VKxsdLg4eJKfCeZ":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Deaf","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot hear. While you may ignore some applications of Dominate, you may not hear warnings, and you have great difficulty in normal communication. Lose three dice on rolls that involve hearing, such as most perception rolls like spotting a surprise attack.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Deaf (involving hearing)","value":-3,"paths":["skills.awareness"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7VKxsdLg4eJKfCeZ"},"7iAHHyWMkdQ7mXir":{"name":"• Antitoxin Body","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Whether by drinking them in small doses to build immunity or by virtue of your blood, you are immune to mortal poisons, toxins, and drugs. This cannot be combined with other Substance Use Merits or Flaws.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7iAHHyWMkdQ7mXir"},"8sPHOOIFw2kwggRJ":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Untamed (Animalism)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Such a tight relationship with the Beast comes at a cost, as it refuses to be leashed. Failing to Ride the Wave during Frenzy inflicts two points of Aggravated Willpower damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8sPHOOIFw2kwggRJ"},"9Zj8X3X08g15L8I9":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Daymares (Auspex)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While rarely recalling more than an acute sense of terror, the vampire’s daylight rest becomes plagued by horrifying visions, causing them to sweat blood. When awakening for the night, make two Rouse Checks instead of the usual one.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9Zj8X3X08g15L8I9"},"9dJ0dqzMoRKs5XSi":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Deranged","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Either a pre-existing condition, a curse or the trauma of damnation has sown madness in you. For each dot in of this Flaw, a character has two triggers tied to a Derangement, to a maximum of 5 flaws or 10 triggers. A person may have multiple separate Deranged Flaws, each tied to a separate Derangement. See p. XX for Derangement system and triggers.</p><p>■ <strong>Faunt of Heart:</strong> You are easily sickened</p><p>•• by the sight of blood. Whenever you witness a gory scene or blood spilled, you must roll Willpower (Dfficulty 2). If the roll fails, you are overcome with nausea for the rest of the scene. While this doesn’t mean you spend the time vomiting (unless you roll a Total Failure; vomiting vampires raise their Hunger by one) it does mean that all non-tracker pools sufer a die penalty.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9dJ0dqzMoRKs5XSi"},"AlUuUXpHtGiON4Qm":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Monstrous Skin (Serpentis)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s usage of Serpentis brings them closer to their god…but not in a way one might expect. Each night, they awaken with a condition that can be described as their own skin that has taken on properties of scaly snakes, feathery monsters from folk tales or other mythical beasts. To shed this skin, the user must scrub it off, suffering two points of Superficial Health damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AlUuUXpHtGiON4Qm"},"ApJkJouBo1IZtMaC":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Blunt (Dominate)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The strain of enforcing their will upon others erodes something critical in the mind of the dominator, making mundane social interactions fraught. Vampires with this Flaw cannot spend Willpower to reroll a Social test.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ApJkJouBo1IZtMaC"},"BjKqVJOrq1iaup4y":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Phobia","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have an extreme amount of fear towards a specific stimulus, defined when you take this Flaw. When you encounter this stimulus, you must make a Difficulty 2 Fear Frenzy test. Must be something not-incredibly-rare.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BjKqVJOrq1iaup4y"},"CBRV7msvIDrSnmYx":{"name":"•• Medium","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You possess the natural affinity to sense and hear spirits, ghosts, and shades. Though you cannot see them, you can sense them, speak to them, hear them and, through pleading or cajoling, draw them to your presence. You may call upon them for aid or advice, but there will always be a price.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CBRV7msvIDrSnmYx"},"ChmEV0Ne0k62eIVW":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Dead Soul (Thrall Only)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>For whatever reason, you may not passively regenerate Faith like a common human and instead must find other ways to power your infernal gifts. Vampires have this by default.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ChmEV0Ne0k62eIVW"},"Ea2a1mprgkiXDnX0":{"name":"•• Devoted","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are devoted to service to or reverence of a higher power, be it Christ, Allah, Odin, or some other deity. Once per game session, you may pray for one full turn to your divine patron for aid and guidance. In game terms, your character receives one to two points of temporary Willpower (half the value of the Merit) for the scene. If these points are spent in a way that is frivolous or antithetical to the character’s Ethics, this Merit may be rescinded until they can make amends. These points of Willpower are not part of the tracker, it merely allows you to reroll up to two pools or spend them to activate a power...</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ea2a1mprgkiXDnX0"},"FUp4EMrcJjHtEBLp":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Unnerving Shadow (Obtenebration)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your shadow appears almost sentient, moving at its own accord. While it will never really try to endanger you by being a masquerade breach in of itself, it still does hinder your ability to interact with mortals in a normal manner as it unnerves others. Gain a three-dice penalty to interact with normal mortals in a non-threatening manner unless you are in an area where your shadow will not be visible.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Unnerving Shadow (Obtenebration) (non-threatening dealings with mortals where your shadow shows)","value":-3,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FUp4EMrcJjHtEBLp"},"FVlYGVvrpa7V1uZ5":{"name":"••• Supernatural Awareness","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can sense the hidden patterns of the world, seeing through illusions and magics where others would blindly believe. You may use your Awareness Skill (usually with Wits for reactive and Resolve for conscious usage) to perceive things hidden by magic like latent and warding spells, users of Obfuscate, users of Presence or see through illusions like Chimerstry or Path of Illusion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FVlYGVvrpa7V1uZ5"},"FYu1PWlpi3gS54dl":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Overconfident","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your confidence in yourself is unshakeable, but is so exaggerated that it makes working with others difficult. Benefitting or participating in Teamwork inflicts a point of Superficial Willpower damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FYu1PWlpi3gS54dl"},"G0EIf5oV49Dd8bf1":{"name":"• Concentration","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have the ability to focus your mind and shut out any distractions or annoyances. Characters with this Merit are unaffected by any penalties stemming from distracting circumstances (e.g., loud noises, hanging upside down) and distraction maneuver.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.G0EIf5oV49Dd8bf1"},"GABaCv8DI9qYKRLL":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Sanguinary Overload (Quietus)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have become so attuned to reading the vagaries of blood that during feeding, the flood of information and voices from the victim cannot be avoided, even persisting beyond the hunt. For any scene/up to one hour (whichever is shorter) following a feeding, the vampire loses two dice from all Physical (Quietus Cruciatus) Social (Quietus Animae) or Mental (Sanguinis) pools due to the distraction. This Ingrained Flaw may be taken 3 times, each time corresponding to one of the forms of Quietus. Spending a point of Willpower allows the vampire to ignore this flaw for a turn.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GABaCv8DI9qYKRLL"},"Hqgaotec0c4j0w2w":{"name":"•• Cainite Fangs","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Through strange thaumaturgical sorcery, unknown rituals, Vicissitude or even an odd quirk of your physiology, your blood has been imbued with the power of the supernatural. You’ve grown retractable fangs like a vampire and may perform Bite Attacks like them, dealing flat two points of Aggravated Health damage even to supernatural beings after a successful called shot attack. Potential for feeding on vampires in the middle of combat is an ironic one.</p><p>If the character acquires Distressing Fangs or Unretractable Fangs Flaw, the fangs may no longer be retractable. Cainite Fangs does not give the ability to give the Dark Kiss.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Hqgaotec0c4j0w2w"},"INiT7w8ydJ9H0lty":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Blind","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot see. Characters can compensate for the loss of vision by becoming more sensitive to other sensory input, but visual cues and images are lost to them. Actions involving hand-eye coordination are very difficult to perform, especially under stressful conditions. Make vision-related rolls (and most combat rolls) at a -3 dice penalty.</p><p>Vampires with Auspex and other creatures with supernatural senses are still able to use their powers (perhaps even to partially compensate for their loss of vision), though the information is interpreted via other senses.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Blind (vision and most combat)","value":-3,"paths":["skills.awareness","skills.firearms","skills.melee","skills.brawl"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.INiT7w8ydJ9H0lty"},"J629mvt4cFmu7UzH":{"name":"• Incantation (Mortal Only)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Incantations are the remnants of magic left in the world that the wise people or the old ladies in the woods know. Bits and pieces of world’s lore collected allow such powers to be passed from one generation to the next, usually verbally as many of its preactitioners are illiterate. Incantations are not nearly as flexible as Path-based or awakened magic known by sorcerers and witches or even the true magick known by will-working mages. Think of the old Egyptian amulets, the voodoo dolls sold to tourists and old curses levied by your babushas when you anger them.</p><p>Each time this Background is bought, a mortal learns a Ritual (or a spell performed as a Ritual like Conveyance Path’s Spell). The Background’s rating is equal to the level of the Ritual (which is equal to the highest rank of the highest Aspect that the Incantation may use). For example, picking Aggressive Corpse (a lvl 3 Special Effect of Necromancy Path) as a 5-dot Incantation allows a mortal to perform a ritual to raise Aggressive Corpses with the limit on the Aspects being 4 (so Aspects like Duration, Number of Targets may be set up to 4)</p><p>Incantations follow the rules of Hedge Magic (p. XX) for activation and rolls (default is Intelligence + Occult). A Special Effect or a Path spell like of Conveyance may be learned as a Ritual. Highest usable Aspect rank is equal to the The Merit may be picked normally at character creation to represent one’s heritage, but after that they must be learned as normal Rituals (same project as learning a normal Ritual, requiring two times as many total number of successes). Embrace fully refunds all Incantations as Incantations lack a linear structure to transfer knowledge into Blood Magic unlike Hedge Magic.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.J629mvt4cFmu7UzH"},"K6Iq4Z3Dxd8bL6JW":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Agoraphobia (Flight)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire has spendt so much time flying safely that they no longer feel safe on the ground and may even struggle with moving on it. The vampire’s Frenzy Difficulties are increased by one while in an enclosed space where they have no ability to soar into the sky. The benefit of this Ingrained Flaw is that the user counts their Flight’s rating as one higher, up to 5, as the Discipline only has a single thing to offer per level.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.K6Iq4Z3Dxd8bL6JW"},"MHU4xfTrqNInuIjy":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Mute","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot speak. You may communicate with the Storyteller and describe your actions, but you cannot talk to player or Storyteller characters unless everyone concerned uses Linguistics dots to purchase a commonly understood sign language or you write down what you wish to say.</p><p>Alternatively, this may be a Mental or a Social Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MHU4xfTrqNInuIjy"},"MaTRWOLrbLYb5lNL":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Elderly (Mortal only)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your body is frail and appears elderly whether as a result of aging or magic. Lose a die on all physical rolls. Ghouls and revenants may not take this Flaw. If a person with Elderly becomes a ghoul, they ignore the penalty.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Elderly (Mortal only)","value":-1,"paths":["physical"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MaTRWOLrbLYb5lNL"},"NbZEGz0xfYH40QQ9":{"name":"• Revenant Heritage (Vampire)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Despite your “ascension” over your revenant family members by acquiring the gift of vampirism, you have retained your old powers and many habits. Select one of the revenant families. You have access to its revenant loresheet and may even swap your in-Clan discipline spread for that of the revenant line. However, the Affliction that comes with your line is also present within you and you suffer the Family Affliction of the revenant line you have selected.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NbZEGz0xfYH40QQ9"},"Pa2IURl11noaWn5I":{"name":"•••• Huge Size","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are abnormally large in size, at least six-foot-ten an 300 pounds in weight. Aside from making you extremely noticeable in public, this extra mass bestows an additional point of Health. Characters with this Merit also count their Strength as higher by one for feats of strength and gain a die certain strength-realted t actions: push objects, open barred doors, avoid being knocked down and so on. This die bonus is never used on offensive dice pools in combat.</p><p>A person with Huge Size cannot take Everyman Looks Merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Huge Size (pushing, forcing doors, staying on your feet)","value":1,"paths":["attributes.strength"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Pa2IURl11noaWn5I"},"QKKVd9ON7TUmxxJl":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Lame","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your legs are damaged, which prevents you from running or walking easily. You are forced to walk with a cane or possibly leg braces, and you have a pronounced lim to your stride. Your walking speed is one-quarter that of a normal human, and running is impossible. You suffer a three-dice penalty to all rolls involving your legs.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Lame (involving your legs)","value":-3,"paths":["physical"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QKKVd9ON7TUmxxJl"},"RPw4kyeUrs25j9nc":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Killer Instinct (Potence)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When the blood’s up, the echo of the Beast is never far away, and the unholy strength of Potence only serves to feed it. If the character fails a Rouse Check when activating a Potence power, they must immediately test for Fury Frenzy as if physically provoked.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RPw4kyeUrs25j9nc"},"RQp4ve5WLYPn8rvo":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Leper","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Before you were Embraced, you suffered from leprosy or a skin disease that might be easily confused with leprosy. You cannot benefit from Looks Merits, and your flesh is marred by rashes, pale patches, festering sores, scars and so on. Your skin disease, whatever it actually is, is obvious to any who look upon you. Mortal authorities refuse you entry to any city, and you are treated with fear and revulsion. You may be compelled to dress in a specific manner and even carry castanets or other devices to announce your coming. In the three-point version, your condition is still contagious. Although it cannot have any further effects on your unliving form, you may transmit it to those upon whom you feed.</p><p>This Flaw is worth only one point to Nosferatu and the Troglodytes, and then only if their disease is contagious.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RQp4ve5WLYPn8rvo"},"SDOJrcO4vvBDqBmw":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Small","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are well below average height — four feet tall or less. You have difficulty reaching or manipulating objects designed for normal adult size, and your running speed is one-half that of a normally proportioned human.</p><p>A person with Huge Size must take Everyday man Looks Merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.SDOJrcO4vvBDqBmw"},"SY13wc2m0fCLdTld":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Stuff of Legends (Mytherceria)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire has used the fae-gift Discipline so much that they have begun to take on more and more characteristics of the fae. The vampire gains a number of Folkloric Constraints equal to their Mytherceria rating.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.SY13wc2m0fCLdTld"},"T86Oeq4LcJNUzWpd":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Ever-Shifting (Vicissitude)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Those who have altered their undead flesh often find that it has gains some of…“sentience” of its own. The vampire must spend a Rouse Check of blood to calm it down until they next awaken or risk being a walking masquerade breach. Uncalmed flesh also inflicts a penalty of two dice on the vampire’s Charisma and Seduction rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Ever-Shifting (Vicissitude) (Charisma and seduction while your flesh is uncalmed)","value":-2,"paths":["attributes.charisma"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.T86Oeq4LcJNUzWpd"},"TIAOoLdWT4RbaGJU":{"name":"Flaw: (•) All is Maya (Chimerstry)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Usage of Chimerstry stems from a tradition that the world is a “maya” – an illusion. The power itself lends credence to that idea as the users of Chimerstry can sometimes fail to differentiate between what is an illusion they have crafted. The vampire suffers a dice pool to all Awareness and perception rolls equal to half of their rating Chimerstry rating, rounded up.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TIAOoLdWT4RbaGJU"},"UDbEodrMVICYYDNe":{"name":"••• Detached","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have the rare ability to view your psychic abilities as separate from your physical existence and can maintain this distance even under adverse conditions. Mental Impairment and penalties to the mind do not impact your Psychic Phenomena rolls. If you’re Incapacitated, then you may spend a Willpower point to use a Psychic Numina power at half the normal dice pool (rounded up). This action may be performed only once per scene, after which the character falls unconscious, so choose last-ditch efforts carefully.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UDbEodrMVICYYDNe"},"Y0wtOxMoXsLmNA7o":{"name":"• Common Sense","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a significant amount of practical, everyday wisdom. Whenever the character is about to act in a way contrary to common sense, the Storyteller can make suggestions or warnings about the implications of said action. This is a very useful Merit to give to beginning players who are unfamiliar with the game.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Y0wtOxMoXsLmNA7o"},"YcRs6gud6ZY1w6PQ":{"name":"Unusual Retainers","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have one or more loyal and steadfast servants or assistants. Retainers may be ghouls who are blood bound to you, individuals you have so Dominated over the years that they are incapable of independent action, or individuals you have so overwhelmed with your Presence that they would do anything for you.</p><p>You must always control retainers in some fashion: a salary, vitae donation, or direct mesmerism. Though typically loyal, retainers may betray you if the reward outweighs the risk or if you have treated them badly. The Storyteller can always call for a scene between you and a retainer.</p><p>You may not directly benefit from Advantages of your Retainers without the permission of the Storyteller in rare cases. You may not feed on your Retainers for free as Herd exists to represent that. Retainers are characters, not puppets, even though players may play them to ease the Storyteller’s burden. At Storyteller’s discretion, players can improve their Retainers by paying for things like Specialties, Disciplines and Numina with their character’s own experience points cost, but they may not improve their Attributes or Skills directly.</p><p>Retainers should act as characters, not puppets. Even ghoul retainers aren’t supermen; the most skilled servants don’t always show the highest loyalty. Everything in Vampire is a tradeoff. Storytellers can use Retainers to add flavor to the chronicle; don’t let them or their misuse damage the story. If the Storyteller is fine with it, they may let a player directly control and roleplay the Retainer character.</p><p>Most retainers, including non-human ones, use human NPC traits (p. XX) to make things easier for both the players and the Storytellers.</p><h3>Human Retainer</h3><p>You either employ a human to work for you or, if you are a vampire, you probably ghoul them. While not as loyal as blood bound ones, paid mortals are loyal enough as long as the coin flows. The maximum number of Human Retainers is equal to one’s Resources + Business Establishment to represent the ability to upkeep them which is especially especially important for non-vampire players. Blood bound Retainers do not count towards this limit.</p><p>If you turn the human retainer into a ghoul, see ghoul mechanics on p. XX.</p><p>• A Weak Individual.</p><p>•• An Average Individual.</p><p>••• A Gifted Individual.</p><h3>Animal Companion</h3><p>You have managed to befriend and train an animal that follows you around and obeys your orders as much as it is able.</p><p>You need to possess at least Animal Ken 3 in order to purchase this Background. An animal may become a ghoul (see p. XX).</p><p>The maximum number of Animal Companion dots a character may possess in total is equal to one’s Animalism (+ Power Bonus)/Animal Ken + Kennel Haven Background. Ghouled animals do not count towards this limit. Famuli and familairs are not counted as Animal Companions since they are bought together with powers and rituals.</p><p>• Medium/Small: One medium (wolf, large dog, boar, large snake) animal or three small (bird, lizard, snake, cat) animals.</p><p>•• Large: One large animal (a bear, crocodile, lion, cow) or a swarm</p><p>••• Swarm: A swarm of small animals (bats, snakes, bees...)</p><h3>Bound Umbral Spirit</h3><p>Through deals, appeasements or magic dealing with spirits, you have a spirit of the umbra in your service. Usually, this Background must be justified through knowledge of a Path of magic or a Discipline that deals with such spirits or story encounters. Spirits represented here are from a weaker stock of spirits. Acquiring an Umbral Spirit Retainer requires either their consent, usually done through pacts, or knowing how to bind them (such as through the Path of Binding, Summoning and Warding). Advantages are not necessary when building your spirit retainers.</p><p>A retainer gains 15 times its dots in experience points to spend on Spirit Powers and traits for free.</p><p>•• Small Spirit: Build them as a Weak Individual.</p><p>••• Weak Spirit: Build them as an Average Individual.</p><p>•••• Average Spirit: Build them as an Average Individual.</p><p>••••• Powerful Spirit: Build them as a Gifted Individual. Powerful Spirits are rare and not available unless earned in-game.</p><h3>Ghost Retainer</h3><p>You have enlisted or enslaved a ghost which obeys your commands without much hesitation.</p><p>One may think that they do not need to buy a ghost servant as they could keep spamming Compel Soul or threatening their fetters for obedience. That one would, however, find that their haven was set on fire either by the vengeful ghost or their allies while they slept. Buying this Background ensures some safety and/or loyalty for the necromancer.</p><p>This Background can only be bought by ghost characters or by characters who have powers that would allow them to communicate with or enslave ghosts. After character creation, a character will require a ghost’s fetter or permission to purchase them. For portraying ghosts in a simple manner, see p. XX.</p><p>The Ghost Retainer gains experience points equal to 5 times the dot rating of the ghost to spend on Arcanoi powers and other traits (like Plasma Rating) of the purchased ghost. Ghost Retainers begin with Plasma rating 1.</p><p>•• Weak Ghost: Build them as a Weak Individual.</p><p>••• Average Ghost: Build them like an Average Individual.</p><p>•••• Seasoned Ghost: Stats of a Gifted Individual.</p><p>••••• Powerful Ghost: Build them as a Superlative Individual. These ghosts are rare and only given by the Storyteller.</p><h3>Necromantic Construct</h3><p>Necromancers frequently make zombies, various small homunculi and other undead creatures to keep them company, have them act as soldiers or fulfill some other menial tasks that they can’t be bothered with. Necromantic Construct Background serves to solidify your permanent hold on the construct created by the Discipline of Necromancy as otherwise, as stated within individual powers and Rituals, these constructs tend to go rogue or fall apart unless bought.</p><p>See Lesser Undead for Necromantic Construct costs.</p><h3>Revenant Retainer</h3><p>Revenants are the products of profane magics and centuries of eugenics programs that crossbred ghouls in order to create self-sustaining ghouls that could be more independent. These revenants were organised in family lines and most of them can trace back their origins to the Tzimisce, but not all. For how revenants work and their families, see p. XX.</p><p>Unless they are a revenant themselves, a character may possess one revenant retainer per point of Influence among their family line or relevant sect or clan status (for example, Tzimisce for Zantosa or True Black Hand for Enrathi). The exception to this are revenants acquired through loresheets.</p><p>•• Weak Revenant: Build them as a Weak Individual</p><p>••• Average Revenant: Build them as an Average Individual</p><p>•••• Gifted Revenant: Build them as a Gifted Individual</p><h3>Undead’s Familiar (Vampire only)</h3><p>While the Embrace destroyed your ability to function as an awakened mage, your bond with your familiar companion endures. The familiar is a spirit of high intelligence, able to telepathically commune with others or talk normally. It may take a form of a mouse, a cat or another animal the size of a dog or smaller that is often associated with practitioners of magic. It may not be a magical creature (dragons, imps, etc.), but creatures such as a homunculus, zombie or animated bodyparts might work well. The form the familiar takes must make sense for the former mage. For example, a character connected with nature and the living world would not keep a zombie or an animated skull as a familiar. All familiars have their own personalities and secret agendas which not even mind-reading or fortune-telling magicians can divine.</p><p>Familiars are immune to mind influencing and dominating powers such as Dominate, Dementation and Presence but are fully vulnerable to effects that would harm spirits such as Ward against Spirits.</p><p>Familiars may provide valuable advice as more often than not they know more than their master on the matters of magic. When learning Blood Magic Paths and Rituals, a Familiar may provide teamwork as well if it possesses at least two dots into Occult. Familiars will always sense the current emotions of their master, rushing to aid if they are willing to help. Characters who gain another familiar (such as by Blood Magic rituals) cut their connection with this one as the former-mage familiar will feel betrayed, replaced and highly insulted.</p><p>This Background is available only to characters who possessed a familiar before the Embrace and may be purchased only once. A destroyed familiar reconstitutes itself after a story and returns to its master, continuing its duties. Familiars also crave gifts, favors, affection, and so on. The nature of those offerings depends on the individual familiar and their relationship with their master and might be weird, arbitrary, or outright horrifying.</p><p>Familiars are built like Mortals stat-wise, but you swap their Physical Attributes for that of the animal. Mortal stats are that of the spirit, animal’s are the innate abilities and instincts of the host body. Familairs also possess two dots in a magical Path.</p><p>••• Flip a coin/roll one die. If the coin does not land on the called side/the die fails, the familiar will not carry out your request unless somehow forced (and they will not do a very good job). Build them as a Weak Individual.</p><p>•••• Your familiar will perform complex tasks tasks that will not endanger its own existence unless the master’s existence is at stake. Build them as an Average Individual.</p><p>••••• Your familiar is fully obedient to you and will do whatever you ask to it. Build them as a Gifted Individual.</p><h3>Retainer Flaws</h3><p>• Stalkers. You have a tendency to attract people who become a tad too smitten with you for your own good. A former retainer retains their memory of you and a desire to reconnect. They may be hungry, love-maddened, desperate, opportunistic, or any combination or variation. Should you get rid of them, another soon appears.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"383"},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.YcRs6gud6ZY1w6PQ"},"Z1JrqeKESr10TwUK":{"name":"•• Blood Empathy (Ghoul)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The ghoul can feel if their regnant is in danger or otherwise, needs them immediately, this does not allow for telepathic communication.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Z1JrqeKESr10TwUK"},"ZWjKLfcBsoKOxtap":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Distressing Fangs","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Having developed fangs like a vampire, the character suffers one die on Social pools with mundane mortals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Distressing Fangs (mundane mortals)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZWjKLfcBsoKOxtap"},"Zgsz2eeq52pVHzxw":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Breakdown (Celerity)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s physiology runs at the same pace as the vampire, burning blood and energy at equal volumes. Whenever the vampire fails a Rouse Check when activating a Celerity power, they sustain a point of Aggravated Health damage, and their body withers and ages before their very eyes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Zgsz2eeq52pVHzxw"},"ZnZREcVndE1eYJZY":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Song Unending (Melpominee)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The song heard by the Daughters of Cacophony and all the practitioners of this Discipline keeps being distracting and, unless you let it flow through you, you find it hard to express yourself. Lose three dice on all Social Skill tests if you aren’t utilising Performance, poetry or music of some kind.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Song Unending (Melpominee) (without performance, poetry or music)","value":-3,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"unless":["skills.performance"]}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZnZREcVndE1eYJZY"},"Zv7NR0STOKWG7qYD":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Crone’s Curse","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The character appears at least two decades older than they actually are which reduces their Health tracker by one. This is usually the result of a curse levied by a witch, but ghouling and embrace can also inflict it. Most notably, the embrace within Crone line of the Nosferatu.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Zv7NR0STOKWG7qYD"},"aVrW5niH9DKwTJb4":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Amnesia","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are unable to remember anything about your past, yourself or your family, though your past might well come back to haunt you. Your origins and the circumstances behind your amnesia are for the Storyteller to determine, and they encouraged to make it as interesting as possible.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aVrW5niH9DKwTJb4"},"bS0ekyJMMSkzljmX":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Realisation (Daimonion)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The suffering is everywhere in the world and no force can stop it, only master it. Those who feast themselves on Daimonion’s gifts see sin and suffering everywhere and they understand that their powers resemble the beasts already consigned to Hell at the day of judgement. While many lie to themselves, denying it or rationalising it, they are still reminded each time they gaze upon the world and what is has to offer. The vampire fulfilling their desire heals no Willpower as the user is in a perpetual existential crisis or in deep nihilism with or without their realisation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bS0ekyJMMSkzljmX"},"bsaWb3Y8N3YECsQC":{"name":"•• Misplaced Heart","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your heart is elsewhere in your body, no more than 2 feet from its original position, due to various factors such as dextrocardia or other means such as uses of fleshcrafting. An additional success is required to aim fort yourt heart.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bsaWb3Y8N3YECsQC"},"cYt018AF33itz1J0":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Creature of Habit (Vampire)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You always stake out the same places to hunt, night after night due to familiarity and comfortability. Due to this, feeding outside your usual haunts causes anxiety and discomfort. When feeding outside your Predator Type, increase the Hunting Difficulty by 2.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.cYt018AF33itz1J0"},"eRbY913Bd17sXl7y":{"name":"•••• Gifted Magician","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Perhaps you browsed countless texts, conversed with the most learned of scholars or just possess a massive library you frequently spend hours in. Learning magic has become second nature to you. You halve and round up all threshold Difficulties for learning or creating new magic. This does not stack with any similar reductions as they usually come from a different source such as better teaching materials. You are just better at it by yourself.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.eRbY913Bd17sXl7y"},"fPpCnWuj8Ocs6NGk":{"name":"• Celestial Attunement","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have an innate link to the passage of time and the movement of celestial bodies. You can estimate the time until sunrise or sunset within a minute or two, and you can follow the phases of the moon in your head. Those with some training in astrology and this Merit can even foretell certain astrological conjunctions without access to charts. You can accomplish any of these feats with only a minimum of concentration.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fPpCnWuj8Ocs6NGk"},"fVgu6c0IF0BHBOxe":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Stasis (Protean)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s static nature rebels against the forces of change. If the vampire fails a Rouse Check while activating a Protean power, when they revert to their normal form the change is incomplete — dulled and useless claws persist, a foot remains stuck in the soil, or the fleshcrafter finds their skin attached to their subject. Unfinished transformations never provide any benefit. The vampire must remove the affected area (sustaining one Aggravated damage) or wait for it to revert (un)naturally during day-sleep.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fVgu6c0IF0BHBOxe"},"flFHJ5yI9xtiMekp":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Egomaniac (Presence)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Having had others consistently fawn over you has stroked your ego to the point where you can’t tolerate a world without you at its center. Whenever a Presence power generates a messy critical or fails to sway your target, you must make a Fury Frenzy test at Difficulty 2.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.flFHJ5yI9xtiMekp"},"hEvodBxUslAfPhTv":{"name":"Sanctum","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<h3>Sanctum</h3><p>Your have access to a site that lies over a place of strong magical power that resonates with your sorcery or enchanted it (rule of thumb is 1 year per dot of Sanctum). While invisible to normal sight (though not perhaps to Auspex or various Rituals), it underlies a prominent area: the top of a staircase, a fountain in the garden, the center of a room. (Intelligence + Occult might allow someone to deduce its location, if they know what they’re looking for.)</p><p>While within a Sanctum, sorcerers who practice the type of sorcery that resonates with it acquire benefits to casting and do not trigger magical risks on a Botch casting magic within its boundaries.</p><p>When purchasing this Background, a character selects one type of a Sanctum. Each Sanctum has its own effects and types of magic it can benefit. Storytellers may experiment with their own types of Sancta and use the ones listed below as samples.</p><p>• A tiny sanctum the size of a pantry or a small clearing; +1 die to all rolls to use magic while in your sanctum.</p><p>•• A small room or copse of trees; +1 die to all rolls to use magic while in your sanctum.</p><p>••• A workshop and perhaps an adjoining bedroom or closet; +2 dice to all rolls to use magic while in your sanctum.</p><p>•••• A small house or cottage or a suite of rooms in a larger building; +2 dice to all rolls to use magic and -1 to casting Difficulty while in your sanctum.</p><p>••••• An entire mansion or a large wing of a castle, or perhaps a forest. You immediately know of any incursion onto your lands unless they’re hidden supernaturally (you may roll Wits + Awareness + Sanctum to notice them against their power); +2 dice to all rolls to use magic and -1 to casting Difficulty while in your sanctum.</p><p>Greater ratings and sizes are allowed at the Storyteller’s discretion. Sancta like Shrines may be portable. Detection through Sanctum is possible only if the magician attunes themselves to the place, usually by acting as its steward or owner.</p><p>Types of Sancta are:</p><p>• Glyphs of Power: One of the more common Sancta, Glyphs of Power is a location in which magical energy has been stored by those who can wield it, inscribing the very vis into the area through glyphs, art or some other auspicious elements.</p><p>When purchasing this Sanctum Background, select one sorcery school. The benefits of Sanctum will only affect it and potentially sycrethic sorceries. For example, sorcery of Celtic Blood Witchcraft might interact with that of druid hedge wizards or magi. Some say that Stonehendge is not a Natural Sanctum, but one created by ancient Celtic magi. Tremere are particularly inclined to this type of a Sanctum as it allows them to not rely on locations where others already dwell, even though it can require anywhere from several years to centuries to inscribe proper Glyphs of Power onto a location.</p><p>• Natural Sanctum: A naturally occuring Sanctum, rich in vis (later known as Quintessence). Some call them Caerns, others call the knots or forks in the veins of the Earth. It benefits all magic.</p><p>• Weak Veil: Either due to an accident, artefacts, frequency of dark rituals or due to a natural phenomenon, the Sanctum is a location where the Veil that separates the lands of the living and the lands of the dead is thin.</p><p>This Type of a Sanctum benefits necromantic schools such as Mortis and Nihilistics. Necromantic Paths from other Schools like Blood Witchcraft and Akhu may also benefit from this Sanctum. Shroud rating of this location is normally Frayed, and ghosts frequent it unless necromancers hostile to them hold the place.</p><p>• Shrine: Shrine is a constructed Sanctum powering religious magic such as Akhu, certain sects of Dur-An-Ki, Mortis, Blood Witchcraft, and even Shalimite Abyss Mysticism. When purchasing Shrine, select a deity. Those who believe in the power of that deity or a religion receive the Sanctum bonus for that magic. For example, a lector priest of Akhu might have Shrine (Set) or Shrine (Echidna), while a practitioner of Dur-An-Ki could could have a shrine dedicated to an ancient god or even a religion such as Shrine (Islam).</p><p>At greater sizes, Shrine is also often referred to as holy/unholy/sanctified grounds.</p><p>■ <strong>Spirit Presence:</strong> The area is busy with</p><p>• activity of spirits and is usually found in nature. Spirit Presence benefits magics based on spirits such as Koldunism and Dur- An-Ki. At Storyteller’s discretion, other practices and Paths could benefit as well from interacting with spirits in the area.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"379"},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hEvodBxUslAfPhTv"},"hyoVzOl8UQbn33yI":{"name":"• Coldly Logical","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While some might refer to you as a “cold fish,” you have a knack for separating factual reporting from emotional or hysterical coloration. You are probably not very emotional yourself, but you can see clearly when others are clouding the facts with their feelings. Gain a die bonus to all Insight rolls and rolls to perceive and anylse feelings, but lose a die to Charisma or Persuasion rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Coldly Logical (perceiving and analysing feelings)","value":1,"paths":["skills.insight"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}},{"source":"Coldly Logical","value":-1,"paths":["attributes.charisma","skills.persuasion"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hyoVzOl8UQbn33yI"},"iP9U3AEUhpklOye0":{"name":"••• Resistant to Mind-Bending","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your will is that of iron. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist an effect to sway or bend your will (Dominate, Presence, etc.) effect once per session. Should you acquire a dot in such Disciplines or acquire powers to sway minds of others, you lose the benefit of this Merit and it is refunded.</p><p>Whenever somebody is using a mind-swaing effect on you, you may roll Wits + Awareness vs. Wits + Subterfuge. On a win, you become aware of it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Resistant to Mind-Bending (resisting Dominate, Presence and the like, once per session)","value":2,"paths":["resistance","attributes.resolve","attributes.composure"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.iP9U3AEUhpklOye0"},"jRhXUJa0uU4rMRZ9":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Unretractable Fangs (Vampire only)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p></p><p>Not being able to retract their fangs, the character is quite scary whether they’re smiling with visible fangs or weirdly not smilling. The character suffers a die penalty to Social pools with mundane mortals. Nagaraja may not take this Flaw as it is a part of their Bane already. Ghoul variant of this is called Distressing Fangs.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Unretractable Fangs (Vampire only) (mundane mortals)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.jRhXUJa0uU4rMRZ9"},"kEUaipV8iq5eUvKo":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Magically Inept","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Known as Thaumaturgically Inept in Tremere lexicon, you simply do not click with magic. Difficulties on casting tests are raised by 1 for hedge sorcery, sphere magic, pillar magic and blood magic. Can only be taken by those who have at least one dot in sorcery, magick spheres/pillars or have Blood Sorcery as an-in Clan Discipline.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kEUaipV8iq5eUvKo"},"mHaZ7gNVFlWADT8r":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Decay (Thanatosis)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The powers of Thanatosis allow its user to debilitate and decay the flesh of their enemies, but its entropic energies reflect onto the casters when overused. The vampire counts their Strength as lower by two, to a minimum of 1. This may be mitigated for a scene by spending a Rouse Check to renew the flesh.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.mHaZ7gNVFlWADT8r"},"nPBsKJHsb4ldcVHe":{"name":"••• Resistant to Psychics","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Psychic Numina have little effect on you and you have little effect on it. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist any Psychic Phenomenon power or that of Auspex effect once per session. Should you acquire a dot in any Psychic Phenomenon or Auspex, you lose the benefit of this Merit and it is refunded.</p><p>Whenever somebody is using a Psychic Phenomenon or scrying effect on you, you may roll Wits + Awareness vs. Wits + Subterfuge. On a win, you become aware of it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Resistant to Psychics (resisting Psychic Phenomena or Auspex, once per session)","value":2,"paths":["resistance","attributes.resolve","attributes.composure"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.nPBsKJHsb4ldcVHe"},"o60I0jBsQyNSWDDr":{"name":"•• Pain Tolerance","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are gifted with a tough body, deadened nerves, or some ability to block out pain. When a character is Impaired through damage to the Health tracker, they suffer a -1 penalty instead of the normal -2.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.o60I0jBsQyNSWDDr"},"oHMFqWJ9jlu9gUDw":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Laham","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You belong to one of the bloodlines that literally descend from demons. Once called the Laham in ancient tongues, the term Nephilim is the more popular one recently. The Lahama tend to live to around 200 years unless slain prior as they age more slowly and more gracefully.</p><p>You gain a number of Arcana dots equal to your dot rating in this Merit to spend on your demonic powers. They are not a part of any limit like that of thralls as they are your inborn gifts developed during teenhood or early adulthood. Additionally, you the first two dots of Divinity at character creation and may purchase more as normal. Your maximum Faith Exhastion is equal to your Divinity rating. For your powers, see p. XX The Lahama have a natural presence that is detectable by, demons, infernalists and other evil spirits. When engaging with such creatures in their infernal courts and institutions, count your Status as two higher with them, to a maximum of 5. Many Lahama were also sired by demons possessing mortals, but not all of them are human. Folk stories tell of monsters coming out of wombs with no human words to describe them. Some were even raised up by the Church as weapons against evil, but they are a double-edged weapon as nobody can be sure were their true loyalties lie.</p><p>True Faith and aura-reading abilities like Scry the Soul may also detect Lahama.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oHMFqWJ9jlu9gUDw"},"qNU1P3u83s1JaOvD":{"name":"••• Resistant to Magic","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Magic has little effect on you and you have little effect on it. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist a magical (Blood Sorcery, psy, Pillars or Spheres of magi, Hedge Magic and so) effect once per session. Should you acquire a dot in any sorcery, you lose the benefit of this Merit and it is refunded.</p><p>Whenever somebody is using magic on you, you may roll Wits + Awareness vs. Wits + Subterfuge. On a win, you become aware of it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Resistant to Magic (resisting magic, once per session)","value":2,"paths":["resistance","attributes.resolve","attributes.composure"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qNU1P3u83s1JaOvD"},"qjNNHOxZ5fg0IhUE":{"name":"•••• Oracle","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can see and interpret signs and omens. You are able to draw advice from these omens, for they provide hints of the future and warnings of the present. When the Storyteller feels that you are in position to see an omen, you will be required to make a Resolve + Awareness roll, with the difficulty relative to how well the omen is concealed. If successful, you may then roll Intelligence + Occult to interpret what you have seen; the difficulty is again relative to the complexity of the omen.</p><p>Once per session, you may force an omen and roll Resolve + Awareness against a Difficulty of 3. On a win, roll Intelligence + Occult. Half the successes (rounded down) may be added as a dice bonus on non-tracker pools, but no more than three dice may be added per roll. Divination cannot aid pools to fortune-tell or where the user consciously forced some kind of a benefit (like from a Blood Surge, artefact usage or another dice bonus) as fate comes on its own and artefacts often influence change and fate by themselves.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qjNNHOxZ5fg0IhUE"},"r1lxBtoA5I4wlTAT":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Vengeful","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a score to settle, incurred either during your mortal days or after the Embrace. You are obsessed with taking your revenge on an individual or group, and must make a Fury Frenzy (Difficulty 3) in their presence.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.r1lxBtoA5I4wlTAT"},"sz3llTVzAfYc2gqB":{"name":"••••• Untainted (Thrall Only)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your enthrallment did not stain your soul with the poison of Torment. Although you do not need to check for degeneration, you may still gain Torment from using corrupt Arcana that impose taint. If you ever accumulate a dot of permanent Torment, you lose this Merit. Thralls with this Merit can only receive Arcana by taking Taints and Pacts.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sz3llTVzAfYc2gqB"},"t5jtTwArs4lLOHE1":{"name":"• Magically Attuned","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>All humans can use magic — if they are alive. Upon being Embraced, a wizard loses all of their magic and must instead rework their practice through Blood Magic which is not as easy to learn as it requires proper resonance like all vampiric Disciplines and even ghouls who have no need for Resonance know that something is off when it comes to learning blood magic in contrast to common mortal margic.</p><p>When you were embraced, you did not lose the ability to learn magic and the change onto Blood Magic was much smoother and you find little difference between the two when it comes to learning them. For one dot, you do not require Resonance to advance through Blood Sorcery Discipline. At two dots, you may learn Blood Sorcery Discipline at a cost of 6 x the new level, unless it costs less due to being in-clan Discipline or from other factors. For mortals, this makes the cost of Hedge Sorcery Schools 5 x new level.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.t5jtTwArs4lLOHE1"},"u0f7dWtv0vzteN9R":{"name":"• Ambidextrous","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and you can perform tasks with the “wrong” hand at no penalty. You must still use the splitting rules for taking multiple actions, but you do not suffer a difficulty penalty if, say, you use two weapons or are forced to use your off hand.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.u0f7dWtv0vzteN9R"},"ujXEhotbvpwDbjpF":{"name":"Flaw: (•) In Both Worlds (Spiritus)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Users of Spiritus often find themselves drifting in thoughts as their spirit is more unbound than that of most creatures inhabiting the material world. The user suffers a three-dice penalty on all rolls to perceive something that is in the material plane.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"In Both Worlds (Spiritus) (perceiving the material plane)","value":-3,"paths":["skills.awareness"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ujXEhotbvpwDbjpF"},"vgVTkXS3rxs75gI6":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Fractured Mind (Dementation)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vampire’s mind and sense of morality are fractured into many pieces as the picture expands. After all, is morality not a construct to enforce social insanity as sanity? If the vampire adheres to Conscience, they require 2 Touchstones per Ethic to maintain it. If they adhere to a Road with the Virtue of Conviction, they may not have more than a single Ethic.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vgVTkXS3rxs75gI6"},"vjB6yIVAw4n6uk1b":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Nightmares","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have extraordinarily vivid “nightmares” during your sleep, which hinder your mental recovery. Characters with this Flaw recover Superficial Willpower damage at the start of session equal to the lower of their Resolve or Composure. If the Attributes are equal, recover that amount -1 (example: Hiram’s Resolve and Composure are both 4, and so he only recovers 3 Superficial Willpower damage).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vjB6yIVAw4n6uk1b"},"wL9N8DwyyQAsbYCa":{"name":"• Hale","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are in good health and rarely if ever take ill. Gain a two-dice to resist disease whether natural or supernatural in nature</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Hale (resisting disease)","value":2,"paths":["attributes.stamina"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wL9N8DwyyQAsbYCa"},"wfj4QcDS9zo5vgkG":{"name":"•• Unbound Diabolist (Non-ghost, nondemon only)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You once made a pact with a demon and received investments of power, but your infernal master no longer controls your life. For two points, it may be banished back to Hell or simply far away and no longer concerned with you. Either way, you can live your life with a measure of autonomy, though it is always possible your master will return one day to reclaim you.</p><p>For four dots, your master has suffered final destruction at the hands of another demon (or some other powerful being) or released you from the bonds of thralldom (due to the pact struck with it or maybe it simply wanted a new thrall and got bored), leaving you truly free. Autonomy has its price, of course. Without a master, you have no way of gaining or losing Arcana and Taints. You can still improve Divinity and your Torment may rise or fall, but you can’t do anything with that potential. Thralls with the four-point version of this Merit can swear fealty to a new demon if they so desire, trading away their freedom for more power. Those with the two point version are not so lucky, since their absent masters still hold claims to their souls.</p><p>Selected during character creation, gains the first dot of Divinity for free and you may set the Torment rating at whatever. You may select Taints and buy higher Divinity ratings or Arcana with experience points.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wfj4QcDS9zo5vgkG"},"xVALW1mvQOOdDo5a":{"name":"• Inoffensive to Animals (Vampire)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p></p><p>Unlike most Cainites, you do not cause hostile or even unsettled reactions in animals you encounter. Instead, the animals simply treat you like an ordinary human. For one point, you can avoid upsetting a particular species and gain a die bonus to all Animal Ken rolls with them (including Animalism). For two points, you can be inoffensive to and gain a die bonus for all ordinary creatures (though those that are renowned for their affinity to the supernatural, such as black cats or owls, might still react unusually to your presence, at the Storyteller’s discretion).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Inoffensive to Animals (Vampire) (animals you are inoffensive to)","value":1,"paths":["skills.animalken","disciplines.animalism"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xVALW1mvQOOdDo5a"},"xgNrZdSC8YCEGPls":{"name":"Infernal Rituals","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While raiding a hole in which one of the suspected Baali was hiding in, we fell into a trap. The bastard left the writings on the wall and our ghoul men-at-arms, as mortals are always curious and dumb, paused to look at them, silently reading in this minds. Suddenly, the earth under our feet cracked wide-open and some “thing” arose from the circles and sigils drawn under us in blood.</p><p>Infernal Rites are a Background that represents the possession of knowledge of rituals to bind or summon demons as well as the knowing of their Celestial Names. Infernal Rites can only be bought during character creation. Learning them after character creation requires a special project. For mechanics of using or learning Infernal Ritual, see the systems on our homebrew server if the Demons and Thralls book is done finished or have your Storyteller simply weave it as they’re quite self-explanatory by name. You may have a maximum of 6 dots in this Background at character creation. Retainers may not possess this Background, but may use them.</p><p>These rituals may only be used by mortals (ghouls, revenants, changelings sorcerers and mages fall within that category). Vampires, demons and other such creatures must instead rely on their own powers to summon or bind demons. They may still purchase this Background and craft rituals as normal, but must resort to hiring or tricking mortals into performing the actual ritual. Those among the Baali who refuse to dabble in black magic frequently abuse mortal servants at their disposal to get them to perform Infernal Rites, with the easiest precaution being the blood bond to ensure that a mortal does not turn the demon on its master.</p><p>When selecting this Background, you learn either a summoning or a binding ritual that is specifically crafted to work on a single demon. In addition to it, you learn the demon’s Celestial Name as it is the minimum requirement for these rituals to even work. A non-idiot summoning a demon usually also prepares a binding ritual for it unless they simply seek information or to strike a bargain with the demon to become their thrall.</p><p>Demons that become available to summon or bind through the rituals learned by purchasing this Background at character creation must be discussed with the Storyteller who then builds them. While it may seem great to go for the strongest demon, know that they are more likely to tear your head off than try to reason with you should you attempt to enslave them.</p><p>•• One Ritual</p><p>•••• Two Rituals</p><p>•••••• Three Rituals All those purchasing this Background beware: you will be destroyed for dabbling in infernalism if uncovered or you may meet a gruesome end at the hands of a spirit you tried to compel to do once your control runs out.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"381"},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xgNrZdSC8YCEGPls"},"yWte0ruLLNxZrQyD":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Slowdown (Temporis)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While the users of Temporis toy with the laws of time, it creates many paradoxes that ripple through the fabric of reality which pushes back against the violator. Whenever the vampire fails a Rouse Check when activating a Temporis power, they sustain a point of Aggravated Health damage, and their body withers and ages before their very eyes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.yWte0ruLLNxZrQyD"},"ywywZbDiqkya88qA":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Baneful Residue (Ghoul)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While ghouls acquire Ghoul Bane according to the Clan of their domitor, sometimes these curses can stick around long after vitae is gone. The first Ghoul Bane ghoul had is permanent. If they ever acquire or have acquired a new domitor, they will possess two Ghoul Banes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ywywZbDiqkya88qA"},"zZK6nN3ZW75gFlLC":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Scar Tissue (Fortitude)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Flaw impairs the vampire’s ability to maintain the appearance of health following an injury. While rousing the Blood restores Health as normal, the physical appearance of any injuries sustained remains until they rest for the day. This can easily result in Masquerade breaches if not carefully managed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.zZK6nN3ZW75gFlLC"},"zk1ZR5L9NmDm3TQh":{"name":"• Vicissitude Alterations","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Having gone “under the knife” of a Tzimisce or some other practitioner of Vicissitude, you’ve been “gifted” certain bodily alterations. Each dot of this Merit represents one dot of the Vicissitude rating of the vampire who performed Vicissitude on you and corresponds to the number of Attribute dots you may pay for your modifications. Even vampires may start off with this Merit, and other creatures at Storyteller’s discretion.</p><p>Make sure you note the Generation of the vampire to determine whether or not you may heal it as a vampire. If your domitor is a character with Vicissitude, you do not have to buy this Merit to represent their modifications as they’ve already have their own Vicissitude rating.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.zk1ZR5L9NmDm3TQh"},"adv:v5-linguistics":{"name":"Linguistics","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Every player character has perfect fluency in their birth language and (except perhaps for a charming Hungarian accent) in the dominant language of the chronicle setting. For example, a Lebanese vampire in a Mexico City-set chronicle can speak and read Arabic (or Aramaic, if they are a very old Lebanese vampire) and Spanish. Kindly Storytellers might allow vampires whose birth tongue matches that of the setting to take a second language anyway.</p><p>Each dot in Linguistics allows the character to fluently speak, write, and read one additional language besides those two default languages.</p><p>Don’t get hung up on the changes in language over the centuries; assume that English remains English from Anglo-Saxon times to now, for instance, for the purposes of this rule, although reading</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HTuun3O4qHpF01bh"},"adv:v5-illiterate":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Illiterate","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot read or write. Your Academics and Science Skills are capped at 1, and you can have no specialty in them incorporating modern knowledge.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8Wko17G9v8FPh4DF"},"adv:v5-repulsive":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Repulsive","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You lose two dice from all relevant Social dice pools.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Repulsive (relevant Social pools)","value":-2,"paths":["attributes.charisma","attributes.manipulation"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RQpT0BdRYNTGK70o"},"adv:v5-ugly":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Ugly","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You lose one die from all relevant Social dice pools.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Ugly (relevant Social pools)","value":-1,"paths":["attributes.charisma","attributes.manipulation"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VmVqUZjBtYv1AYfF"},"adv:v5-beautiful":{"name":"•• Beautiful","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You add one extra die to all appropriate Social dice pools.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Beautiful (appropriate Social pools)","value":1,"paths":["attributes.charisma","attributes.manipulation"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IPPK6DBojolfDIfT"},"adv:v5-stunning":{"name":"•••• Stunning","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You add two extra dice to all appropriate Social dice pools, as above.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Stunning (appropriate Social pools)","value":2,"paths":["attributes.charisma","attributes.manipulation"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FE7qOE1vBOAZBpKe"},"adv:v5-hopeless-addiction":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Hopeless Addiction","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Lose two dice from all pools when the last person you fed from was not on your drug, except pools for actions that will immediately obtain your drug.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Hopeless Addiction (last vessel was not on your drug)","value":-2,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xCSxd2Rqj5PCxDqj"},"adv:v5-addiction":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Addiction","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Lose one dice from all pools when the last person you fed from was not on your drug, except pools for actions that will immediately obtain your drug.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Addiction (last vessel was not on your drug)","value":-1,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"180"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QJ3iQUAV8FKxY0jX"},"adv:v5-high-functioning-addict":{"name":"• High-Functioning Addict","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You add one die to any one category of pool (specify when you choose which substance you use) when the last person you fed from was on your drug.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"High-Functioning Addict (your chosen category of pool, last vessel was on your drug)","value":1,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"180"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9l4ptphfjp2fgYzL"},"adv:v5-archaic":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Archaic","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Ancillae or older only.</p><p>You haven’t been able to adapt to the present, or you have been long in torpor. You cannot use computers or cell phones, and your Technology rating is permanently 0. The Storyteller may penalize other dice pools involving very modern technology by one die.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Archaic (very modern technology)","value":-1,"paths":["skills.technology"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"180"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qL2bNqenNluloQEh"},"adv:v5-living-in-the-past":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Living in the Past","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Ancillae or older only.</p><p>You haven’t grasped the modern mindset, or you just don’t want to. You have one or more seriously outdated Convictions, e.g. “The Pope’s word is law,” “Women are delicate flowers,” “Lower classes exist only to serve,” or “Burn your enemies’ baggage.” These archaic moralities maintain your Humanity but are odious to many; you lose one die from Social test dice pools involving such archaic beliefs except with vampires your age and older, who may admire your steadfast virtue.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Living in the Past (involving your archaic beliefs)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"180"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HYtKMNF9uWl1WgGN"},"adv:v5-bondslave":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Bondslave","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You bond instantly with the taste of another’s vitae; only one drink of blood binds you, not three. Either you begin as your sire’s thrall, or consult with the Storyteller to come up with a reason that first bond has broken. Possibly your sire has been staked or in another city for more than six months?</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"180"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XrBcghIvEOopueXA"},"adv:v5-bond-junkie":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Bond Junkie","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Bond feels sweeter to you once it happens. Subtract one die from your dice pools to act against a Blood Bond.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Bond Junkie (acting against a Blood Bond)","value":-1,"paths":["willpower","resistance"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qGOAtvfCZ6P1fASf"},"adv:v5-long-bond":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Long Bond","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Blood Bonds on you lose their Bond strength more slowly than normal, decreasing by one for each three months without being reinforced.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MSe4kRluj5zcLvRp"},"adv:v5-bond-resistance":{"name":"Bond Resistance","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your Blood rebels against control. Add one die to your dice pools to resist a Blood Bond per level you take of this Merit (maximum three).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Bond Resistance (resisting a Blood Bond, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["willpower","resistance"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.JaEdNlgDwi6KzXlT"},"adv:v5-short-bond":{"name":"•• Short Bond","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Blood Bonds on you lose their Bond strength more rapidly than normal, decreasing at both the full moon and new moon (i.e., by two each month) if not reinforced.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.E0ewbq7BlBj8EkNv"},"adv:v5-unbondable":{"name":"••••• Unbondable","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot be Blood Bound. If you’re ever short of cash, you can probably sell your vitae to alchemists, too.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Q64rxZdPJcB5DYYv"},"adv:v5-farmer":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Farmer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ventrue may not take this Flaw.</p><p>You feed only on animal blood. You must spend two points of Willpower to drink human blood. Ventrue may not take this Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vWURFBO62ygcQCAz"},"adv:v5-organovore":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Organovore","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can slake Hunger only by eating human flesh and organs, especially those rich in blood such as the heart, liver, lungs, placenta, and spleen. (Most organovore Kindred these nights make smoothies from the organs first.) Only the heart provides Resonance, if any.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.W5kiIGeqFmWEXKX5"},"adv:v5-methuselahs-thirst":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Methuselah’s Thirst","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your Hunger can only be fully slaked by the blood of supernatural creatures. (Alchemists may be able to thicken the Blood of thin-bloods enough to sate you.) Otherwise, it constantly remains at a minimum of 1. (Or higher, depending on Blood Potency (see p. 215).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.k4Gq5fYGr8Cto11l"},"adv:v5-prey-exclusion":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Prey Exclusion","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You refuse to hunt a certain class of prey: drug users, women, children, policemen, innocents, a given minority or ethnic group, etc. If you feed on such prey, you gain Stains as though you had violated a chronicle Tenet. Witnessing other Kindred feeding on the object of your exclusion without interfering might also give Stains, at the Storyteller’s discretion. Ventrue with this Flaw gain an additional restriction, making their choice of vessels extremely narrow.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"181"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CFn5uzAx4BG8lDQY"},"adv:v5-bloodhound":{"name":"• Bloodhound","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can smell the Resonance of a human’s blood without tasting it. You still need to be within olfactory range of the person. Make a Resolve + Awareness test at Difficulty 3. Increase Difficulty for perfume or distance; lower it for more intimate contact.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MT2Nx3oPDu9LEU6q"},"adv:v5-iron-gullet":{"name":"••• Iron Gullet","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ventrue may not take this Merit.</p><p>You can feed from cold blood, rancid blood, and fractionated plasma. None of these provide Resonances. Ventrue may not take this Merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MgONaVbkaFELESSu"},"adv:v5-stake-bait":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Stake Bait","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You meet Final Death when staked through the heart, rather than entering torpor.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qqlJlL0Q3VPqqln2"},"adv:v5-folkloric-bane":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Folkloric Bane","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You take Aggravated damage from a folkloric bane. Folkloric banes include:</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Ultraviolet light (damage as direct sunlight)</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Silver or silver-plated weapons (damage as weapon damage: simply touching a silver coin or silverware does one point of Aggravated damage)</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Holy water (damage as fire)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6OfGJBCzj8pwn3jj"},"adv:v5-folkloric-block":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Folkloric Block","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When faced with a folkloric block, you must shrink away from it or spend a Willpower point to push through it.</p><p>Each folkloric block you take counts as a separate one-point Flaw. Folkloric blocks include:</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Holy symbols presented by any believer (even without True Faith)</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Crossing visible running water (not recommended in certain cities, such as Amsterdam, Stockholm, or Venice)</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Crossing a threshold to a home uninvited by the owner</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ White animal</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Garlic</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Wild roses</p><p class=\"sub\">▫ Spilled seeds you haven’t counted</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.n7t0xHksO6RIDZWU"},"adv:v5-stigmata":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Stigmata","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You begin to bleed from open wounds on your hands, feet, and forehead when you reach Hunger 4. This attracts attention, leaves traces, and may penalize some dice pools at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.paQOoxjoD9tLxfOu"},"adv:v5-eat-food":{"name":"•• Eat Food","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can consume food without effort and might even enjoy it, though it gives no nourishment and must be expelled before resting for the day.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5W3SFnMwYt62hrfh"},"adv:v5-baby-teeth":{"name":"Flaw: Baby Teeth","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You never developed fangs, or the ones you’ve got are useless for feeding. You either need to cut your victims open, or you extract their blood with a syringe.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Fb4UHsKwr28bDjs2"},"adv:v5-bestial-temper":{"name":"Flaw: Bestial Temper","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You are afflicted with the Beast equal to a full vampire. You need to test for frenzy as per the normal vampire rules.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.de9HPh2YmMeYZva7"},"adv:v5-branded-by-the-camarilla":{"name":"Flaw: Branded by the Camarilla","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>In some cities, the rulers simply hunt down and destroy thin-bloods. In other cities, the merciful Sheriff merely brands them, forcibly and painfully, to remind them of their place. (Many are then hunted down and destroyed, often on perceived slights or simple whims.) You have such an unhealing brand, and the Camarilla makes sure to keep its eyes peeled for the moment you step out of line. You can take Camarilla Contact with this Flaw; the Camarilla thrives on double-dealing of this sort.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.SqS7FgPeZcvLtb4t"},"adv:v5-clan-curse-thin-blood":{"name":"Flaw: Clan Curse (Thin-Blood)","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Remnants of your sire’s Blood still flows through your veins, carrying traces of their ancient curse. You must pick a Clan Bane to suffer from, counting your Bane Severity as 1 for this purpose. You can only pick the Brujah or Gangrel Bane if you possess the Bestial Temper Flaw, and the Tremere Bane only if you have the Catenating Blood Merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kbllXgFRH6uwOH1i"},"adv:v5-dead-flesh":{"name":"Flaw: Dead Flesh","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Your Blood is not strong enough to completely sustain you, and as an effect, your flesh is in a constant state of putrefaction, with a greenish tint and faint stench of rot. Any medical inspection will immediately identify you as deceased, and you receive a one-die penalty to any face-to-face Social test with a mortal. You cannot take Lifelike if you take this Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Dead Flesh (face to face with a mortal)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KgbU3NgQt7AEwlTQ"},"adv:v5-mortal-frailty":{"name":"Flaw: Mortal Frailty","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You cannot Rouse the Blood to mend, instead healing as a mortal (p. 126). You cannot take Vampiric Resilience if you take this Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VAmkXiSgXb4NTbCa"},"adv:v5-shunned-by-the-anarchs":{"name":"Flaw: Shunned by the Anarchs","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You broke an unwritten rule or thought yourself equal among equals, where some turned out more equal than others. In whichever case, the Anarchs of the regnum know of you and shun you. They’d rather throw you to the Camarilla than listen to your pleas. You cannot take Anarch Comrades if you take this Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.F2FBsyz024cSEKWY"},"adv:v5-vitae-dependency":{"name":"Flaw: Vitae Dependency","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Your Blood is unable to sustain vampiric powers by itself. Unless you drink enough vampire Blood to slake one Hunger each week you lose your ability to gain and use any Disciplines (including Thin-Blood Alchemy). You regain your powers as soon as you slake at least one Hunger with vampire Blood.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HWtka0skPn5ELXs9"},"adv:v5-anarch-comrades":{"name":"Anarch Comrades","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You have befriended the members of an Anarch coterie who tolerate your presence or even affectionately treat you as their pet. They cumulatively act as a one-dot Anarch Mawla, as long as you don’t deviate from the party line. Write them on the Relationship Map.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UGOlumy2iEnOuS2J"},"adv:v5-camarilla-contact":{"name":"Camarilla Contact","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You have caught the eye of a Camarilla recruiter who has promised you admittance and a chance at becoming a real vampire. All you need to do is keep your eyes and ears open, report on your and your coterie’s activities, and be prepared to perform any task asked of you.</p><p>You have the equivalent of a one-dot Camarilla Mawla who treats you badly. Write them on the Relationship Map.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.48T7Molng3izjCHl"},"adv:v5-catenating-blood":{"name":"Catenating Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You can create Blood Bonds and perform the Embrace as a regular vampire. Any vampire you create this way will be a thin-blood.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oXnIw9aMBuSCq2l8"},"adv:v5-day-drinker":{"name":"Day Drinker","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Sunlight halves your Health tracker (rounded up), but otherwise simply removes your vampiric abilities, including all Disciplines and Health benefits, and does no other damage. You still suffer from Hunger, however, and sooner or later you’ll need to sleep. If your health drops below your currently sustained damage levels as a result of this, you suffer the effects of Impairment or torpor (depending on type of damage) until you are clear of sunlight.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wVMCSZmIcd1wcCEv"},"adv:v5-discipline-affinity":{"name":"Discipline Affinity","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You have an affinity for a certain Discipline, picked at character creation. You gain a dot in this Discipline and can learn and retain additional dots in it through experience expenditures, as if you were a normal vampire. (The experience point cost is the same as an out-of-clan Discipline.) Drinking blood with matching Resonance does not reward you with any extra dots in this Discipline, temporary or not.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IOSO84MuzBDNnrmP"},"adv:v5-lifelike":{"name":"Lifelike","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You have a heartbeat, can eat food, and enjoy sexual activities like a mortal. All but the most advanced medical inspections reveal nothing out of the ordinary, assuming they happen at night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6OOpRBDDEjZFOzYH"},"adv:v5-thin-blood-alchemist":{"name":"Thin-Blood Alchemist","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>The Duskborn change as they feed. Thin-Blood Alchemy is the mastery of this process. Whether it is spontaneous or a skill learned by comparing notes with other bloodcookers, you have gained one dot and one formula (p. 282) in Thin-blood Alchemy. You can purchase additional dots and formulae through experience as usual.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Qu9roYRDATckuZOY"},"adv:v5-vampiric-resilience":{"name":"Vampiric Resilience","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You suffer damage as a regular vampire, treating regular puncture and slashing wounds as Superficial damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9VjGMPyaXCB0lKK3"},"adv:v5-allies":{"name":"Allies (Effectiveness)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Allies are mortals who support and help you: family, friends, or even a mortal organization that owes you some loyalty. Although Allies usually aid you willingly, without coaxing or coercion, they are not always available to offer assistance; they have their own concerns and can do only so much for the sake of your relationship. Usually, Allies appear about once per story.</p><p>Allies can be almost anyone in your home city, depending on what your Storyteller will allow. You may have friends in the precinct morgue, at a tabloid newspaper or gossip blog, among high society, or at the railroad yard. Allies are generally trustworthy (though they probably don’t know that you’re a vampire, or even that vampires exist). However, nothing comes for free. If you wind up drawing favors from your friend in the Russian Mafia, he’ll probably ask you to do him a favor in kind in the future.</p><p>Enemies are the opposite of Allies and are taken as Flaws.</p><p>You can use the Mortal Template rules to create Allies or Enemies when you buy them or first call on them, and you can write them down on the Relationship Map, though many groups leave this process up to the Storyteller.</p><p>Build Allies or Enemies from a budget of points based on their Effectiveness and on their Reliability. The maximum points in one Ally is six. Ally or Enemy groups appear in numbers equal to the number of player characters.</p><p>All Enemies are rated two fewer dots than their Effectiveness; a Gifted mortal Ally costs three dots as an Ally, but only provides one dot as a Flaw. Enemies all have the same Reliability: whenever the Storyteller thinks they should show up, but probably at least once per story.</p><p><strong>EFFECTIVENESS</strong></p><p>• Weak mortal, likely useless in a violent or potentially violent situation.</p><p>•• Average mortal or a tightknit group of Weak mortals (neighborhood kids who solve mysteries, church group, NGO chapter)</p><p>••• Gifted mortal or a dangerous group of Average mortals (a street gang, a celebrity entourage, a blue-collar union local)</p><p>•••• Deadly mortal, a Gifted mortal with magic or other supernatural powers, or a well-armed group of Gifted mortals (a private security squad , a lawyer contingent, a Russian Mafia bratva)</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Allies can be as complex as a think tank a vampire may fund under the table, and as simple as childhood friends or family members. Allies show up to aid you, within their power of course. How many dots you put into your Allies will decide how reliable they can be in a pinch. When building any number of dots in this Background, take time to think about other pieces of your character that would justify having access to an Ally. For a Kindred whose Predator type is Siren, his Ally may be his agent, who can help him make introductions to the newest Toreador in the Primogen at an art exhibit. Or a Ventrue whose Archetype is Godfather likely has both Allies and Enemies, coming from their crime family in life and a rival family moving in. Allies are never vampires, regardless of competency.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gCGpk5VyKPZd8Z6F"},"adv:v5-allies-rel":{"name":"Allies (Reliability)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p><strong>RELIABILITY</strong></p><p>• When you call on them, they appear half the time.</p><p>•• When you call on them, they appear within 1-10 hours (roll a die).</p><p>••• When you call on them, they appear as soon as possible.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.b6ppaA4ChxDMXDDF"},"adv:v5-contacts":{"name":"Contacts","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You know people – human people – from many different walks of life. Contacts primarily provide you with information in their areas of expertise, and they may want to exchange favors of various kinds. For different kinds of help, use your Influence (p. 187) in the mortal world, or call on your Allies (p. 184) or Mawla (p. 192).</p><p>A Contact is someone in an excellent position to get information. They might be a police dispatcher, rather than a homicide lieutenant, or a congressional staffer, rather than a senator. Information brokers, gossip columnists, underworld fixers, and reporters make excellent Contacts. You can define your Contacts when you buy this Background or as you need to introduce them in play. Whenever you create them, make sure to add them to the Relationship Map.</p><p>• One Contact who can do or get something cheap or common for you (Resources 1). Examples: a weed dealer, a car salesman.</p><p>•• One Contact who can do or get something useful for you (Resources 2). Examples: small-time gun dealer, veterinarian.</p><p>••• One Contact who can do or get something expensive or hard for you (Resources 4). Examples: security systems expert; police lieutenant in homicide, narcotics, or other useful field.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Where Allies help you make things happen, Contacts help you learn about things happening to others. How you have access to a particular Contact does not have to synergize with your background or Concept (a resourceful Kindred has eyes and ears everywhere after all) but a concrete reason for this specific Contact makes the story better and more plausible. Street-level coteries can leverage one and two-dot Contacts who provide street-level items and information as part of your shared milieu.</p><p>Go ahead and put a name and basic personality to a Contact, on the Relationship Map or in your notes. A character who worked as a radio personality may know the tech crew from their studio, or a mortician may still have a connection to their funeral home. How your death changed your relationship with them – or if it didn’t change it at all – provides juicy roleplaying potential in even a quick “drug buy” scene. As the vampire ages and meets new mortals to add to their arsenal, playing the game and being opportunistic about mortals you meet can create new Contacts and open up new possible story and resource opportunities.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"185"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.La1gyYfuNzVxhfnQ"},"adv:v5-fame":{"name":"Fame","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mortals know your name and eagerly seek out news of your activities. You might be a movie star, rock star, or other celebrity. Fame grants pull in mass and social media; you have more ways than most to manipulate the thoughts of the populace. You likely have ways to mask the fact that you’re never seen during the day, such as a body double.</p><p>In some circumstances, the Storyteller may allow you to use Fame in a Social test dice pool instead of another Trait, especially to get into an otherwise exclusive venue or event. “Do you know who I am?” doesn’t work everywhere, but it does work. Each level of Fame subtracts one from the Difficulty of Social tests against your fans and from many hunting tests, depending on your predation strategy – you have less trouble attracting prey. Fame has its downside, of course – it’s harder for you to tail someone unobtrusively, for example, and who wants groupies hanging around the door to their haven all day tagging its location on Instagram? You should look into taking Disguise as a specialty.</p><p>By default, Fame applies among mortals, but you can also buy Fame that applies among Kindred for the same cost. Some vampires, such as Dracula or Elizabeth Bathory, might have both! Vampires may admire, or at least be more interested in, a Kindred who was famous or infamous in life: Arthur Rimbaud, Crispus Attucks, Billy the Kid, and the like.</p><p>• A select subculture knows who you are and admires you.</p><p>•• You are a local celebrity, recognized by a plurality of the city.</p><p>••• Most people in the country know your name, at least.</p><p>•••• Everybody who even vaguely cares about social trends or your field knows something about you.</p><p>••••• Your Fame reaches mass national or even global audiences. You are a major movie star, stadium-filling rock act, or former president.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Taking Fame as a background generally means that the Storyteller characters in a chronicle want to have access to you. There are two ways that a vampire may have Fame in a modern setting, and each carries their own dangers. Mortal fame, and having to be in the public eye, puts these Kindred at risk of being sniffed out by the Inquisition, or of being blackmailed by unscrupulous fellow Kindred. Choosing to have Fame among Kindred reduces that chance, but means that members of other sects or factions may see you as a potential ally, or threat, or source of information or access to even more famous Cainites. The Storyteller may wish to craft a Loresheet around a character’s Fame, or offer the player the option of taking their Fame from an existing Loresheet group or legend.</p><p>Having Fame can make it difficult to get around places discreetly, what with humans tailing you wishing to post selfies with you, but it can protect you in some ways. Kindred risk breaching the Masquerade by overtly harming or attacking a Famous character, with human entourages and paparazzi always on the lookout in the area. You can also escape enemies by ducking into exclusive clubs or hotels with year-long waiting lists.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"186"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bie6RFkNTzo04B7A"},"adv:v5-infamy":{"name":"Flaw: Infamy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are famous for something horrible. At the very least, the Difficulty of most reaction tests increases by the amount of the Flaw; at worst, the authorities attempt to kill or capture you whenever you appear.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>The other side of the shiny Fame coin, Infamy comes with its own host of pros and cons. An infamous Kindred may have a hard time getting in good with the Prince, or humans may harass these individuals on their nightly goings-on. Other unsavory individuals however may see a vampire with Infamy as an asset, and more likely to help a fellow scoundrel out. While this certainly doesn’t help with your public image, other sketchy organizations or persons think you’re one of them, and you can sometimes use that sympathy to get things done. Just be careful to not make them angry as well.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"187"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IoplFz0RLG3WTfuf"},"adv:v5-dark-secret":{"name":"Flaw: Dark Secret","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can also take Dark Secret, a milder version of Infamy. The Dark Secret Flaw provides one fewer point than the equivalent Infamy, as your black deeds remain unknown to all but you and perhaps one or two very motivated enemies. The one-dot version of Infamy also provides one point as a Dark Secret, because it’s easier to uncover than a truly life-threatening secret.</p><p><strong>Flaw: (••) </strong> You are a Cleaver or serial breacher of the Masquerade, have been Blood Hunted out of another city, or have grievously offended this domain’s ruler.</p><p><strong>Flaw: (•) </strong> You owe a big debt to bad people or have made yourself generally odious. Alternately, your spouse, lover, or close family member has Infamy ••.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"187"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GXu8MTmQxNBVbrkC"},"adv:v5-influence":{"name":"Influence","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have pull in the mortal community, whether through wealth, prestige, political office, blackmail, or supernatural manipulation. Kindred with high Influence can sway, and in rare cases even control, the politics and society of their city, especially the police and city bureaucracy.</p><p>By default, Influence applies most within one group or region of your city. Groups can be large, even diffuse: organized crime, media, religion, the police, city government, etc. Regions should be larger than neighborhoods or all but the largest individual domains: Brooklyn, the Rive Gauche, the South Side, the Ginza, etc. Your Influence applies to the city as a whole at one dot less than it does within your group or region. Using local Influence in another city in the same area, state, or province might be possible at an additional one-dot penalty, and so on. So, a vampire might be Powerful (••••) in Hollywood, Entrenched (•••) everywhere in Los Angeles, merely Influential (••) in San Diego or San Francisco, and just Well-Connected (•) in Chicago or New York.</p><p>The Storyteller may require you to use Influence in place of a Trait in some dice pools, particularly Social tests attempting to sway minor bureaucrats or the equivalent in your group. This Background helps you have an “abandoned” building demolished (or preserved), not start global wars.</p><p>If the Storyteller wants to run a game of globe-spanning masterminds, they may recalibrate Influence to potentially apply nationally (••••) or even globally (•••••).</p><p>• <strong>Well-connected:</strong> You’re guaranteed a respectful hearing.</p><p>•• <strong>Influential: </strong>People want to do you favors.</p><p>••• Entrenched:</strong> Mortal power-brokers and factions hesitate to oppose you.</p><p>•••• Powerful:</strong> Without a good reason to do otherwise, functionaries and foot soldiers obey.</p><p>••••• Dominant: </strong>Lesser figures try to figure out what you want and do it first.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Based in political power,<strong> </strong> Influence adds intrigue and political warfare to a chronicle. Using Influence is more than just manipulating city politics or media in the abstract. Vampires of certain clans may have easier times adding and using Influence in various sectors than others. Brujah have an affinity for connecting with political activists or local militias. Malkavians more likely have Influence in weird collectives or mystical circles.</p><p>When creating Influences and deciding how they connect to a character, think about Predator styles as well: Who should you manipulate to help you hunt? Influence also provides backup protection for your coterie’s Domain. Within a coterie, having a wide spread of Influence (like a wide net of Contacts) gives your group flexibility in planning and reaction to enemy action. For example, having influence in a local political office would not be as effective at blocking off street traffic as a police district may, but the alderman’s office can put your Haven guards on the list of city-approved “youth action groups.”</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"187"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GTF5pP19UsP7GMDe"},"adv:v5-despised":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Despised","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>One group or region of the city lives only to thwart you and your faction. Subtract two dice from dice pools attempting to convince a neutral actor to support you politically or do you a favor. The Storyteller should take any opportunity to involve your haters in the story.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>These two Flaws allow for interesting gameplay if you wish to crank up your vampires’ self-reliance in their own Domain. Despised turns one group or region of the city actively against you, and even neutrals stay cold, while Disliked leaves everyone against you equally — either of which you can play up when rallying your own neighborhood. Of course, when you send your Allies and Retainers into other hoods, they may run into extra problems even if those Flaws don’t affect them (or your Contacts) directly.</p><p>You might also run into those consequences on a night out: a Toreador disliked<strong> </strong> for uploading a video slamming the local soccer team gets into endless sports arguments when they just want to feed in peace. You can even combine Despised or Disliked with regular Influence: the alderman only meets with you in a parking garage and insists on being paid in cash, but she still zones your nightclub for all-night operations and a loading dock.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Despised (winning a neutral party’s political support)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"187"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.WvAW4stYEu0ryUDQ"},"adv:v5-disliked":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Disliked","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Subtract one die from Social test dice pools involving any group in the city except your Contacts and Allies or other explicitly loyal supporters.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>These two Flaws allow for interesting gameplay if you wish to crank up your vampires’ self-reliance in their own Domain. Despised turns one group or region of the city actively against you, and even neutrals stay cold, while Disliked leaves everyone against you equally — either of which you can play up when rallying your own neighborhood. Of course, when you send your Allies and Retainers into other hoods, they may run into extra problems even if those Flaws don’t affect them (or your Contacts) directly.</p><p>You might also run into those consequences on a night out: a Toreador disliked<strong> </strong> for uploading a video slamming the local soccer team gets into endless sports arguments when they just want to feed in peace. You can even combine Despised or Disliked with regular Influence: the alderman only meets with you in a parking garage and insists on being paid in cash, but she still zones your nightclub for all-night operations and a loading dock.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Disliked (groups in the city other than your supporters)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"187"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.SsH3Hgr8WAavBq3h"},"adv:v5-haven":{"name":"Haven","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Like all Backgrounds, the Haven Background is entirely optional. A vampire with no dots in Haven has a suicide’s grave, a room in an abandoned motel, a rented office, or an apartment with windows blacked out with plastic bags. They can still remain safe and hidden by day in this relatively small and insecure haven by default. The Storyteller may allow a player character to default a somewhat better haven from another Background such as Resources, Status, or Influence. Of course, if those Backgrounds go away, so does the character’s nice haven.</p><p>A character with none of those other Backgrounds, however, can still have a perfectly reasonable haven as long as they have this Background. For instance, a character might not have enough money to afford a 20- room Victorian mansion in today’s economy, but if their great-grandmother left them one that had been fully paid for, there’s no reason they can’t remain in residence as it slowly dilapidates.</p><p>Base ratings in Haven abstract the haven’s size, security, and privacy. All of those factors affect the chance of spotting, penetrating, and surveilling the vampire’s actual resting place. Add +1 to the Difficulty of, or one die to dice pools resisting, such efforts for each dot of base Haven rating.</p><p>Kindred know their havens intimately. For each dot of base Haven rating, add one per dot to dice pools to notice danger (including awakening rolls, p. 219) while in your haven.</p><p><strong>BASE HAVEN</strong></p><p>• Small haven, but more secure and private than the default. Examples: basement apartment, crypt, locked storeroom in a warehouse.</p><p>•• Good size, security, or privacy. Examples: a singlefamily home or row house, wolf enclosure at the zoo, branch sewer tunnel.</p><p>••• Very large, secure, or private. Examples: a compound outside town, a bank building, a decommissioned subway station.</p><p><strong>HAVEN MERITS AND FLAWS</strong> You can add Merits and Flaws to your Haven if you wish. They stack with the base Haven rating to produce the total dots in this Background for projects and other uses.</p><p>Remember, you can make Haven a shared Background among your coterie – easily the best way to afford a Haven with lots of features.</p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p>• </p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>One of the most useful, and in some chronicles necessary, Backgrounds in the game. Having a reliable place to take shelter from the sunrise is vital to a Kindred’s extended survival, and it doesn’t hurt to make it nice either. Normally coteries build a communal Haven, but this is not mandatory, especially if it conflicts with other Backgrounds you have taken for yourself or the story you want to play.</p><p>Think creatively about your Haven, especially if you add Merits: a Laboratory has caustic chemicals and an anodized metal sink suitable for disintegrating a corpse or removing serial numbers from a gun. Your Storyteller may meet you halfway for custom havens like mortuaries: re-skin Machine Shop (p. 112) to build reanimated constructs in the back and to have a handy incinerator.</p><p>Remember, if you take no Haven dots and only a Haven Flaw (or Flaws), you still have access to a Haven. It’s probably not safe, and it’s definitely not comfortable, but it keeps the sun out. The only way to not have anywhere to sleep is to choose the No Haven Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Haven (noticing danger at your haven, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.awareness"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"188"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Dkj4CDWGvy7Y0OY0"},"adv:v5-no-haven":{"name":"Flaw: (•) No Haven","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You must go to some effort (at least a basic test) to find a new resting place every morning.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"188"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.iXUkpTr1F2FBD82U"},"adv:v5-compromised":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Compromised","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your haven has been raided once before, perhaps before it was yours. It probably appears on someone’s watchlist. Invaders or spies can add two dice to their pool to penetrate or surveil your haven. If you ever do get on the Inquisition’s radar, you should think about moving out.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"188"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KHZLMa4W9fr2Yki7"},"adv:v5-creepy":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Creepy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your haven looks like the den of a serial killer, which in fairness is probably exactly what it is. Unknowing neighbors might phone in a tip to the cops or just talk about the creepy place they saw. Your dice pools on Social tests to seduce or otherwise put human guests at ease are at a two-dice penalty.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Creepy (seducing or putting human guests at ease)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"188"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.WwD6HEEPmcQ6ylTB"},"adv:v5-haunted":{"name":"Flaw: Haunted","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your haven has a supernatural manifestation in it that you do not control or really even understand. It might just have a ghost, but a Haunted haven could hold a dimensional portal, a cursed meteorite, or anything else you can’t get rid of. Obviously, someone who does understand the manifestation could use it to breach your haven’s security. The Storyteller defines any other effect of the haunting, imposing at least a one-die penalty to affected pools used in the haven per dot of Haunted taken as a Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"188"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.keLoTWieH4StgAa7"},"adv:v5-hidden-armory":{"name":"Hidden Armory","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Each dot in this Merit adds a stand of arms to your haven’s supply: one pistol and one long firearm, with ammunition. They are as secure from discovery as your resting place.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6Ri9Eps9161LUH2L"},"adv:v5-cell":{"name":"Cell","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; Haven ••+.</p><p>Your haven has a dedicated, locked place to store two prisoners, with a base Difficulty to escape of 5. Each extra dot either allows you to store twice as many prisoners (up to a maximum of 32, only in very large havens) or adds +1 to the escape Difficulty. This Merit is not available in small havens.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BtAdNAAfZuZWJ8PB"},"adv:v5-watchmen":{"name":"Watchmen","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>You have either private security or criminal thugs guarding your haven. Each dot of this Merit supplies four Average guards and one Gifted boss (see Mortal Templates, p. 185). If guards would be conspicuous here, buy this Merit cautiously.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Tr30LT0FzvG23sFo"},"adv:v5-laboratory":{"name":"Laboratory","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; Haven ••+.</p><p>Your haven has an equipped laboratory with a dedicated industrial sink, gas jet, reinforced floor, etc. Each dot of this Merit adds one to the dice pool for rolls related to one Science or Technology specialty or to Alchemy dice pools for thin-bloods using the Fixatio method (p. 284). This Merit is not available in small havens.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Laboratory (one Science/Technology specialty or Fixatio Alchemy, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.science","skills.technology","disciplines.alchemy"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bPwyrn2AIvUyZRFv"},"adv:v5-library":{"name":"Library","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your Haven has a dedicated library on the occult, Cainite legends, city history, vampire lore, or the like. Each dot of this Merit adds one to the dice pool for research rolls for one Academics, Investigation, or Occult specialty. Small havens limit this Merit to a maximum of one dot.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Library (research for one specialty, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.academics","skills.investigation","skills.occult"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ajEOI0ExrJ1o6Gqq"},"adv:v5-location":{"name":"• Location","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your haven nestles in one of the most fashionable or otherwise exclusive areas of the city, in the Rack, on a small island, or otherwise in a prime spot. Add two dice (or +2 to foes’ Difficulty) to bonuses on the relevant die rolls from either Chasse (p. 195) or from your base Haven rating (pick one). If neither modifier precisely maps to what you have in mind, work out with your Storyteller when you can expect a two-dice bonus to occur. For example, a Haven close to Elysium might grant a two-dice bonus to Etiquette tests in Elysium and on tests to pick up on court gossip.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Pw7T4sCAa8hrMcGH"},"adv:v5-luxury":{"name":"• Luxury","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>High-definition flat screens, designer furniture, objets d’art, or other expensive details give you a two-dice bonus to Social tests dealing with mortal guests in your haven. If you don’t have at least three dots of Resources (•••), your décor was gained illegally.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Luxury (mortal guests in your haven)","value":2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ksfpeD8fVrxS43Ro"},"adv:v5-postern":{"name":"Postern","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your Haven has a rear exit, secret tunnel, grating in the cellar leading into the sewers, or other unobtrusive way out. For each dot of this Merit, add one die to your dice pools to evade or escape surveillance near your haven.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Postern (evading surveillance near your haven, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.stealth","skills.streetwise"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DvysjsQIKXsqaLMp"},"adv:v5-security-system":{"name":"Security System","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your haven has a better-than-average security system. For each dot of this Merit, add one die to your dice pool to resist (or alert you to) unauthorized entry into your haven.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Security System (resisting or noticing intrusion into your haven, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.awareness","attributes.wits"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QOC7pcwZCElq2qgQ"},"adv:v5-surgery":{"name":"• Surgery","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; Haven ••+.</p><p>Your haven has one room equipped as a field surgery or better. Add two dice to relevant dice pools, generally Medicine, for tests performed in your haven. This Merit is not available in small havens.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Surgery (tests in your haven)","value":2,"paths":["skills.medicine"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BbZDHoJVUjJyxx2f"},"adv:v5-warding":{"name":"Warding","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your haven possesses some kind of magical ward barring supernatural forces. You may not be able to deactivate it, but it allows you to pass. Discuss the limits of your ward with the Storyteller. Each dot of this Merit adds one to the dice pool to resist supernatural scrying, as well as whatever other entry the Storyteller allows it to prevent. The Storyteller may require you to possess Occult 3 or better, or Blood Sorcery, to buy this Merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Warding (resisting scrying of your haven, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["resistance"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.WvsA6sl8Ymzo0s4F"},"adv:v5-herd":{"name":"Herd","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have cultivated a group of vessels from whom you can feed without concern. You can use them to perform basic services, although they are neither as tightly controlled nor as loyal as Retainers. (If needed, build your Herd as a combination of Weak and Average mortals; see Mortal Templates, p. 185.) You can slake your Herd rating in Hunger each week without a roll. (This benefit can be shared among more than one vampire.) Overfeeding endangers your Herd, potentially dropping the rating as members die or flee.</p><p>• One to three vessels with random Resonance and no Dyscrasias: You can change one victim’s Resonance once a month with a successful Manipulation + Insight test (Difficulty 4). Examples: graduate students, musical pupils, cleaver family</p><p>•• Four to seven vessels, half of whom share one Resonance that you pick when you buy this Advantage. Examples: Aging devotees of the city’s fading goth or bhangra scene, fellow club scenesters, large cleaver family</p><p>••• Seven to 15 vessels, with two different Resonances that you pick each week when you feed on different Herd members. Examples: rugby team, swinger scene, dance or night school class, night shift employees</p><p>•••• 16 to 30 vessels: Pick two Resonances every week. Herd maintenance, cover ups, and recruitment take occasional roles in your chronicle. Examples: cult, large brothel, sweatshop</p><p>••••• 31 to 60 vessels: Pick three Resonances every week. Herd maintenance and recruitment begins to brush up against the Masquerade. Examples: Large cult, large sweatshop</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Some Predator types rely on this Background more heavily than others, such as Consensualist, while the Predator type Blood Leech won’t be able to make effective use of this Background at all. The chronicle may also help decide how Herds are used, if allowed at all, as contested territories, domains in war zones, or nomadic and fugitive coteries make it harder to access this Background. Herds may also blend into other Backgrounds, allowing you to get more story mileage out of mortals. Retainers and Allies may have tie-ins to a player’s Herd, and the Storyteller may allow players to use similar combinations with other Backgrounds as well.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.znmW5fcGDrplFCh5"},"adv:v5-obvious-predator":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Obvious Predator","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You exude a predatory demeanor, and humans instinctively fear and mistrust you. Lose two dice from any dice pool for hunting except purely Physical expressions of stalking, chasing, and killing. Lose one die from any dice pool for any Social test intended to put humans at ease. You cannot maintain a Herd.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Obvious Predator (hunting, except purely physical stalking)","value":-2,"paths":["feeding"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}},{"source":"Obvious Predator (putting humans at ease)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"189"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.j1HLTqWrVcHxkogC"},"adv:v5-mask":{"name":"Mask","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>As stealth predators, vampires have few weapons more potent than their pretense of humanity. The lies the Kindred live constantly shadow your stories, and no description of a lick should omit how they navigate mortal society – what they wear as a Mask. A good Mask explains the character’s nocturnal existence and offers plenty of opportunities to be alone with mortals.</p><p>Some vampires switch back and forth between Masks, risking deep identity confusion and slipups, while others forge single plausible identities and strictly adhere to them for a human lifetime before they switch, adding makeup as they “age,” and faking every aspect of life to look perfectly normal on paper. Others, almost always Nosferatu or unbound vampires, forswear safety for freedom off the grid or on the streets. By default (at no dots) a vampire either has no need of a Mask, such as those recently Embraced and still able to pass as the human they were, or has a single Mask and fake ID that can stand up to a traffic stop or similar surface scrutiny. A zero-dot mask does not pass a background check, much less a proper investigation by the authorities.</p><p>• You have a good fake identity, including a credit card, bank account, credit history, birth certificate, etc., all in your Mask’s name. You can pass a state or provincial-level background check.</p><p>•• Your Mask can pass a background check with the national police: FBI, Scotland Yard, or the equivalent. If you had a military or intelligence record in life, it has been classified.</p><p>If you have a two-dot Mask, you can also buy the following Advantages for one dot each: They asked St. Germain’s manservant if his master was truly a thousand years old, as it was rumored he had claimed. “How would I know?” the man replied. “I have only been in the master’s employ for three hundred years.”</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Political intrigue and political horror imply shifting or alternate identities, a key part of this Background. At its base, this Background lets you hold a job, drive a car, pay your phone bill, use a bank card, and otherwise remain somewhat tethered to mortal society. A chronicle that emphasizes shifted or alternate identities might also let a character with multiple Masks mitigate problems from Fame or hide out from Adversaries — for a while.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"190"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LUqATg6OCbPBnZWI"},"adv:v5-known-blankbody":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Known Blankbody","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your biometrics, name, history, known associates, and aliases appear in several intelligence agency databases, flagged as a potential terrorist. Any inquisitor can read between the lines and recognize you as a vampire.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p><strong>Corpse</strong></p><p>Like other Background Flaws, these don’t require the character to have any dots in the Mask Background. They act as storytelling vehicles, drawing the unwanted attention of enemies, or even loved ones. A character careful to avoid surveillance cameras or the attention of the security services can stay under the Coalition’s radar even with Known Blankbody, but Known Corpse is harder to shake without active disguise powers.</p><p>Your famous death leaves you vulnerable any time mortals notice you: perhaps Netflix made a documentary about your cold case murder, or activists plastered your face all over the city during your candlelight vigil. Did you run for local office? Did your grin adorn billboards advertising your ambulance-chasing law firm?</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"190"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.beZLH7NGtLmmYqGx"},"adv:v5-known-corpse":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Known Corpse","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>People know you died recently and react with shock and horror if you appear among them. This Flaw also applies to any database lookups on your identity.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p><strong>Corpse</strong></p><p>Like other Background Flaws, these don’t require the character to have any dots in the Mask Background. They act as storytelling vehicles, drawing the unwanted attention of enemies, or even loved ones. A character careful to avoid surveillance cameras or the attention of the security services can stay under the Coalition’s radar even with Known Blankbody, but Known Corpse is harder to shake without active disguise powers.</p><p>Your famous death leaves you vulnerable any time mortals notice you: perhaps Netflix made a documentary about your cold case murder, or activists plastered your face all over the city during your candlelight vigil. Did you run for local office? Did your grin adorn billboards advertising your ambulance-chasing law firm?</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"190"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RqQM7VBIcTZ35Fmw"},"adv:v5-zeroed":{"name":"• Zeroed","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Mask ••.</p><p>Someone in high places has purged your real records. You officially don’t exist.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Zeroed synergizes well with a character acting as a spy or agent for their sect, a ghost in the machine for their own ends or those of their patrons. Cobbler gives a player the ability to make masks for others, for a fee and small risk. This Background makes an excellent side hustle and story hook: characters can sell their craft for cash or boons, while also creating potential dangers from their clients’ hunters and enemies.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"192"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.0s3rAGqoRMsQ3RFI"},"adv:v5-cobbler":{"name":"Cobbler","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Mask ••.</p><p>You can make or source Masks. Making a Mask takes three days per dot and possibly exposes you online; sourcing Masks takes one day per dot, but costs something in return. How much depends on your leverage, margin on the Social test, or whatever else the Storyteller decides.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Zeroed synergizes well with a character acting as a spy or agent for their sect, a ghost in the machine for their own ends or those of their patrons. Cobbler gives a player the ability to make masks for others, for a fee and small risk. This Background makes an excellent side hustle and story hook: characters can sell their craft for cash or boons, while also creating potential dangers from their clients’ hunters and enemies.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"192"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.EtH0OBHc4NAL8SZJ"},"adv:v5-mawla":{"name":"Mawla","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Trait represents one or a group of Kindred who looks out for you, offering guidance, information, or aid once in a while. The word comes from medieval Arabic, meaning different things from “a trusted one” to “uncle” to “source of neighborly protection” to “non-Muslim whose oath still binds them.” Western vampires brought the term back from the Crusades, and as the Ashirra and Camarilla find themselves increasingly allied in the Gehenna War, the word enjoys a resurgence in vampire society.</p><p>A Mawla may act as your mentor. Such a role commonly falls to your sire, if they don’t treat you as property or ignore you.</p><p>Your Mawla might trade information to mutual benefit, or even come to your aid if you have respected the relationship. A Mawla may be powerful, but their power need not be direct. Depending on the number of dots in this Background, your Mawla might be nothing more than an ancilla with a remarkable information network, or they might be a centuries-old creature with tremendous influence and supernatural power. They may offer advice, speak to the Prince or Baron on your behalf, steer other elders clear of you, or give warning when you’re walking into situations you don’t understand. Your Mawla, of course, expects reciprocity.</p><p>A Mawla rating could even represent a group of like-minded vampires, such as the elders of the city’s Tremere chantry or a Trotskyite clique amongst the Anarchs in town. (The plural of Mawla is Mawali.) As a general rule, a Mawla group costs one dot more than a single Kindred of that level: a a coven of elder Mawali would be a four-dot Mawla group, for example.</p><p>Whoever they are, write them on your Relationship Map when you buy them.</p><p>As a general rule, Mawali provide a helpful word and occasional political pushes or cover, but they don’t fight your battles or call in valuable favors. If they must do so for their own sake to aid you, you probably lose a dot or more of this Background, after arousing their ire.</p><p>Characters can also acquire one-off Minor Boons (p. 315) from other Kindred, only during character generation. These boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla.</p><p>• Neonate</p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•••• Primogen or Anarch Revolutionary Council member</p><p>••••• Prince or Baron</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>In many cases, older or more experienced Kindred serve as your Mawla. A sire who seeks the advancement of their childer makes a standard Mawla, or some member of the faction or sect that “recruited” you is assigned as your zampolit or confessor to make sure you don’t squander the potential your makers saw in you. However, you might have a young Mawla: a loyal friend from your mortal days, also Embraced and remarkably still trustworthy.</p><p>More dots might indicate a Mawla in a more valuable position: a neonate worth one dot normally could be worth two or even three dots as your informant in the personal entourage of the local Baron or Prince. It’s up to the Storyteller to determine whether having a spy in the enemy camp is worth more or less than a spy in your own lord’s court in this chronicle.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"192"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VwTobQ9ysrySImqw"},"adv:v5-adversary":{"name":"Flaw: Adversary","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>A fellow Cainite who generally wishes you (or more likely your sire, lineage, or mentor) ill, an Adversary is the reverse Flaw of the Mawla Background. Adversaries range from one-dot elders to three-dot Princes or powerful cabals. The Storyteller uses either the Adversary’s Status or some specific other Trait when building dice pools with which to oppose the player characters, not the dots in Adversary.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Since an Adversary may drive the plot at any time, the Storyteller and player should discuss the specifics early and in some detail: the Adversary’s sect or faction inevitably winds up playing a major role in the chronicle’s narrative so it’s best to take an Adversary who already fits into it. More powerful Adversaries target you as leverage against their real enemy: your sire or Mawla or other nominal superior.</p><p>That said, a personal Adversary can be interesting: perhaps you and your Adversary were Embraced at the same time and have competed for advancement within your sect ever since. Or reverse the perspective: you’re actually your Adversary’s great enemy. Perhaps they betrayed you, framed you before the Prince, and got you ejected from the court or exiled. Now you’re back, and your greatest goal is to bring your Adversary down.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"193"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.I6VcbHElG7b80EJp"},"adv:v5-resources":{"name":"Resources","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Background describes your Resources. These benefits are not necessarily financial in nature and are rarely completely liquid, but you can often sell them to gain money. It could take weeks or even months to do so, depending on how much needs to be sold. Especially as the Second Inquisition closes down Kindred bank accounts, vampires increasingly return to holding their Resources in cash – or in gold, art, narcotics, guns, and slaves.</p><p>Dots of Resources provide an income for you to maintain your standard of unliving, but you must detail the source of your income and the form this Background takes. After all, it might dry up, get stolen, or otherwise vanish during the chronicle.</p><p>• <strong>Portfolio Proletariat:</strong> You live paycheck to paycheck: apartment, car, camping equipment.</p><p>•• <strong>Middle Class: </strong>Nice apartment or small house, several cars, high-end equipment</p><p>••• Rich:</strong> Great condo or nice house, luxury items, highend equipment for several people</p><p>•••• Wealthy:</strong> Mansion, helicopter or private jet, very specialized high-end equipment</p><p>••••• Ultra Rich: </strong>Many mansions, “anything money can buy”</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>In the WORLD OF DARKNESS and the world of the living, money always talks. Treat Resources<strong> </strong> as the last line of persuasion or interrogation when Minor Debt Major Debt Life Debt (•) (•)</p><p>(••) (•) (•)</p><p>(••) (•) (••) (••) (••) (••)</p><p>(•••) (••) (•••) (•••) charisma fails, or when your other Backgrounds can’t reach the problem. Resources can always reach someone. Younger vampires like yourself have a better understanding of what mortals can do for you than older Kindred who see them only as food or pets. And you have both the knowledge and the means to motivate them. Maybe you can’t stop an elder with money, but a thick stack of benjamins can convince plenty of mortal arsonists, reporters, or cops to try their best to do so.</p><p>Determine the source of your Resources, the more individual the better for the story. Do you sell Malkavian predictions (suitably redacted) to day traders, or live off the royalties of a decades-old hit song? Did you find a big bag of Mafia cash in the woods? Think of your Predator style as a possible Resources explanation: do you tithe your worshipers as an Osiris, or roll your victims for cash or drugs as an Alleycat?</p><p>Storytellers should also be mindful about allowing high (4+) Resource values. Gaining entrance to an exclusive club becomes far less interesting when a character can just buy the premises with a phone call.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"193"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.dtAhMRHbqZMQivxx"},"adv:v5-destitute":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Destitute","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have no money and no home.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"193"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ghL79nTsHu1F79V3"},"adv:v5-retainers":{"name":"Retainers","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have one or more loyal and steadfast servants or assistants. Retainers may be ghouls who are blood bound to you, individuals you have so Dominated over the years that they are incapable of independent action, or individuals you have so overwhelmed with your Presence that they would do anything for you.</p><p>You must always control retainers in some fashion: a salary, vitae donation, or direct mesmerism. Though typically loyal, retainers may betray you if the reward outweighs the risk or if you have treated them badly. The Storyteller can always call for a scene between you and a retainer.</p><p>Retainers should act as characters, not puppets. Even ghoul retainers aren’t supermen; the most skilled servants don’t always show the highest loyalty. Everything in Vampire is a tradeoff. Storytellers can use Retainers to add flavor to the chronicle; don’t let them or their misuse damage the story.</p><p>• Underwhelming retainer: A child, criminal lowlife, or horror nerd follows you around and does your bidding without a Blood Bond. Build them as a Weak mortal.</p><p>•• A ghoul, a family servant, a human lover, or a dominated thrall: give them a backstory. Build them as an Average mortal or as a ghoul with no Advantages. For more on Ghouls, see p. 234.</p><p>••• A retainer competent enough to act independently and make lesser problems go away. They are likely a ghoul with traits equivalent to Gifted mortals, on top of their supernatural abilities.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Retainers do things and go places that Allies and Contacts won’t, often even commit murder or other felonies. Mortals might even feel enough love for or loyalty to you to do such things without being ghouls or thralls. Since Retainers should be named individuals, it doesn’t hurt to look at their motives and their conditioning, and for the Storyteller to ask what might theoretically break the relationship.</p><p>The relationship itself varies. Some Kindred simply keep Retainers as reliable errand people who can go outside in daylight. Others use their Retainer as a confidant, a sympathetic ear to help shoulder the burden of eternal life and the sadness that comes with it. Whatever responsibility you feel for your Retainer, their responsibilities to you dwarf it. In troupe-style chronicles (p. 198), players often portray other characters’ Retainers. Especially in such chronicles, players need to recognize the power dynamics and roleplaying challenges involved (see <em>Power Differentials,</em> p.220).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"194"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AmorB7MfTvBDsGeu"},"adv:v5-stalkers":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Stalkers","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a tendency to attract people who become a tad too smitten with you for your own good. A former retainer retains their memory of you and a desire to reconnect. They may be hungry, love-maddened, desperate, opportunistic, or any combination or variation. Should you get rid of them, another soon appears.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>The temptation with this Flaw, which the Storyteller should absolutely encourage, is to use the Stalker as a “free” (if somewhat unreliable) Retainer – which of course blows up on you once you actually depend on it. The Storyteller should insist that you answer two main questions when you take this Flaw: what does the stalker want from you, and why haven’t you just killed them already? The tension between these two answers drives the stalker subplot.</p><p>Even more than most Flaws, build this Flaw collaboratively with the whole group to avoid unpleasant mirrors of real-life stresses and problems for another player. Absolutely discuss <em>Veils and Lines</em> (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade</strong>, p. 423) regarding when the stalker is played, who plays them, and their role (if any) in the game. It’s a bit harder to come up with a coterie Flaw version of Stalkers, except of course the classic “the coterie is a band” setup.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"194"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AVy7jGLCEKlTsVMO"},"adv:v5-status":{"name":"Status","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have something of a reputation and standing (earned or unearned) within a specified local community of Kindred, probably Camarilla or Anarch. Status among Camarilla society derives as often from your sire’s status and the respect due your particular bloodline as it does from personal achievement. High Status among Anarchs theoretically depends on the character’s contributions, but more often to their degree of connection (by Blood or coterie) to the current Baron or other ruler.</p><p>Status in one sect does not transfer to the other. By and large, Status held in another city effectively reduces by one dot; an Influential (•••) vampire in Cartagena is only Respected (••) in Buenos Aires.</p><p>The Storyteller may occasionally call on you to add your Status to a dice pool along with a Social Trait, especially for first meetings and court happenings. In other cases, the Storyteller may allow you to substitute your Status for a Social Trait – but other vampires may notice that you’re skating by on Status, instead of properly using the Etiquette Skill, for example.</p><p>Caitiff characters begin with the Suspect Flaw and may not purchase positive Status during character creation.</p><p>• <strong>Known: </strong>A properly introduced, welcomed neonate, seen as an up-and-comer.</p><p>•• <strong>Respected:</strong> You have responsibilities now, an average ancilla.</p><p>••• Influential:</strong> You hold authority over part of the group, an average elder.</p><p>•••• Powerful: </strong>You hold office in the group, such as Sheriff, Harpy, or Scourge.</p><p>••••• Luminary: </strong>You sit at the table of power in the group, a respected member of the Primogen</p><p>Players might build their characters individually, but they build their coterie together. Collaborative coterie creation creates shared buy-in to the chronicle, and it allows players to indicate which aspects of the World of the Darkness they have the most interest in exploring.</p><p>The coterie pool begins with one free dot per player character. (The Storyteller may allow player groups with three or fewer players to begin with a coterie pool of two free dots per character.) Players may also contribute their own characters’ Advantage dots to the coterie pool.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>Status generally doesn’t transfer between sects, as each sect values different accomplishments and virtues. Status might also apply more narrowly than a full sect, denoting rank in a Ministry temple or Tremere chantry, for instance. Status and Fame somewhat overlap, but Status generally confers respect. A vampire might have Fame for killing a rival in dramatic fashion, but a Banu Haqim warrior might also (or instead) have a dot of Status in their clan for their action. Status might also overlap with Loresheets, which could justify a somewhat murky or nebulous Status: a double agent of Theo Bell might have actual Status in the Anarch soviet, and a cover Status established within the Camarilla by some kind of shadowplay. Discuss local, limited, or murky Status with the Storyteller to make sure their chronicle focuses on challenges such Status meets or flavors.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"194"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.JaF8nquBJlsYNWQP"},"adv:v5-shunned":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Shunned","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re completely loathed by this sect. You betrayed them, crossed a local leader, or fought them in the past. Members of this group will actively work against you if they can.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"194"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VVIhsG8c1RUGdtA0"},"adv:v5-suspect":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Suspect","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re not good with this sect at all. You weaseled out of a boon, broke an oath, or did something similar. You can try to stay out of sight and out of mind, but unless you somehow make amends, you suffer a two-dice penalty to all Social tests involving the offended faction.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Suspect (the offended faction)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"194"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2rkRvLlDj6y0r6Dx"},"adv:v5-enemies":{"name":"Flaw: Enemies","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Allies are mortals who support and help you: family, friends, or even a mortal organization that owes you some loyalty. Although Allies usually aid you willingly, without coaxing or coercion, they are not always available to offer assistance; they have their own concerns and can do only so much for the sake of your relationship. Usually, Allies appear about once per story.</p><p>Allies can be almost anyone in your home city, depending on what your Storyteller will allow. You may have friends in the precinct morgue, at a tabloid newspaper or gossip blog, among high society, or at the railroad yard. Allies are generally trustworthy (though they probably don’t know that you’re a vampire, or even that vampires exist). However, nothing comes for free. If you wind up drawing favors from your friend in the Russian Mafia, he’ll probably ask you to do him a favor in kind in the future.</p><p>Enemies are the opposite of Allies and are taken as Flaws.</p><p>You can use the Mortal Template rules to create Allies or Enemies when you buy them or first call on them, and you can write them down on the Relationship Map, though many groups leave this process up to the Storyteller.</p><p>Build Allies or Enemies from a budget of points based on their Effectiveness and on their Reliability. The maximum points in one Ally is six. Ally or Enemy groups appear in numbers equal to the number of player characters.</p><p>All Enemies are rated two fewer dots than their Effectiveness; a Gifted mortal Ally costs three dots as an Ally, but only provides one dot as a Flaw. Enemies all have the same Reliability: whenever the Storyteller thinks they should show up, but probably at least once per story.</p><p><strong>EFFECTIVENESS</strong></p><p>• Weak mortal, likely useless in a violent or potentially violent situation.</p><p>•• Average mortal or a tightknit group of Weak mortals (neighborhood kids who solve mysteries, church group, NGO chapter)</p><p>••• Gifted mortal or a dangerous group of Average mortals (a street gang, a celebrity entourage, a blue-collar union local)</p><p>•••• Deadly mortal, a Gifted mortal with magic or other supernatural powers, or a well-armed group of Gifted mortals (a private security squad , a lawyer contingent, a Russian Mafia bratva)</p><p><strong>RELIABILITY</strong></p><p>• When you call on them, they appear half the time.</p><p>•• When you call on them, they appear within 1-10 hours (roll a die).</p><p>••• When you call on them, they appear as soon as possible.</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>On the other side of Allies are the background Flaw Enemies. If your Allies run a small private detective agency, their Enemies could be yours: a gang of smash-and-grab thieves who prey on their clients (and your Domain), or a police department using the bureaucracy to endlessly demand useless paperwork or seize evidence intended for you. You may also just not have Allies at all, only Enemies who try and throw as many impediments into your path as possible.</p><p>However, you can try using this Flaw to your advantage: manipulate the Enemies by using one of your Masks to deniably send them after your own foes, or frame them for such an attack on a rival. Of course, Enemies already have plenty of motivation to trace such mischievousness back to you or your coterie – and you run the risk of letting them meet your Kindred foes. Discuss such tactics with the Storyteller, and with any other players in the group, as this Flaw can affect them as well. Just like Allies, Enemies are always mortals, or in some cases, ghouls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"184"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8TGuxjDjzsJHQ7sq"},"adv:v5-minor-boon":{"name":"Minor Boon","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Trait represents one or a group of Kindred who looks out for you, offering guidance, information, or aid once in a while. The word comes from medieval Arabic, meaning different things from “a trusted one” to “uncle” to “source of neighborly protection” to “non-Muslim whose oath still binds them.” Western vampires brought the term back from the Crusades, and as the Ashirra and Camarilla find themselves increasingly allied in the Gehenna War, the word enjoys a resurgence in vampire society.</p><p>A Mawla may act as your mentor. Such a role commonly falls to your sire, if they don’t treat you as property or ignore you.</p><p>Your Mawla might trade information to mutual benefit, or even come to your aid if you have respected the relationship. A Mawla may be powerful, but their power need not be direct. Depending on the number of dots in this Background, your Mawla might be nothing more than an ancilla with a remarkable information network, or they might be a centuries-old creature with tremendous influence and supernatural power. They may offer advice, speak to the Prince or Baron on your behalf, steer other elders clear of you, or give warning when you’re walking into situations you don’t understand. Your Mawla, of course, expects reciprocity.</p><p>A Mawla rating could even represent a group of like-minded vampires, such as the elders of the city’s Tremere chantry or a Trotskyite clique amongst the Anarchs in town. (The plural of Mawla is Mawali.) As a general rule, a Mawla group costs one dot more than a single Kindred of that level: a a coven of elder Mawali would be a four-dot Mawla group, for example.</p><p>Whoever they are, write them on your Relationship Map when you buy them.</p><p>As a general rule, Mawali provide a helpful word and occasional political pushes or cover, but they don’t fight your battles or call in valuable favors. If they must do so for their own sake to aid you, you probably lose a dot or more of this Background, after arousing their ire.</p><p>Characters can also acquire one-off Minor Boons (p. 315) from other Kindred, only during character generation. These boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla.</p><p>• Neonate</p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•••• Primogen or Anarch Revolutionary Council member</p><p>••••• Prince or Baron</p><p><strong>Players Guide notes</strong></p><p>In most cases Status, Influence, Mawla, and other Backgrounds sufficiently represent a character’s social resources, but there may be times when the story calls for more granular use of debts and boons (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> pp. 314–315) owed to specific individuals: “My Sire saved your Haven during the Revolt, remember,” is more exciting and intriguing than a dot in Status. It’s also less generally useful, but provides more specific leverage on a specific vampire.</p><p>Individual characters can acquire one-off Boons from other Kindred during character generation. Minor Boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade, </strong> p. 193). Major Boons cost one dot more than Minor BOON AND DEBT COSTS Minor Boon Major Boon Creditor / Debtor</p><p>• </p><p>•• Neonate</p><p>• </p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Primogen or Anarch Council member</p><p>••• </p><p>•••• Prince or Baron</p><p>Boons. Characters may not purchase Life Boons at character creation without explicit Storyteller agreement. If the Storyteller agrees, a Life Boon costs three dots more than the equivalent Minor Boon.</p><p>Debts provide many fewer points when taken as Flaws than they cost as Merits. Debt sucks, what can we say? However, being in Major Debt (owing a Major Boon) to a higher-Status vampire is even worse than that. Once per story the character’s creditor or their minions can ask them for a favor they cannot refuse without risking being shunned, blood hunted, or worse. This favor can take on any form and often means exposing the character to mortal danger or reputational collapse.</p><p>If a character finds themselves in Life Debt to a high-Status vampire, they might as well just get the Blood Bond and enthrall themselves. Even non- Caitiff vampires may experience such indebtedness as Debt Peonage (p. 127).</p><p>In all other ways, Minor, Major, and Life Boons function according to the descriptions in <strong>Vampire: The </strong></p><p><strong>Masquerade</strong> (pp. 314–315). Track all debts and boons individually. Note that trivial boons, as simple, easily discharged promises, are not tracked using this system.</p><p>Boons and Debts cannot be purchased or bought off with experience points. After character creation, they can only be gained (or lost) through the character’s actions in play.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 5th Edition Corebook","page":"192"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.34NFGQqBPR5x70RR"},"adv:v5-business-establishment":{"name":"Business Establishment","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>You rent out your haven to a separate business: a 9–5 call center, coffee shop, laundromat, or salon. You might even operate the business yourselves (or with Retainers) as a Corporate coterie (p. 160) although the Storyteller should avoid turning this game of personal horror into a game of business management horror.</p><p>The kine mill around living their daily lives while you sleep in the repurposed storage basement, or one of the server rooms where all of the out of service equipment is stored. The business pays a monthly rent (or profit, if you own it), providing you with a steady stream of funding.</p><p>The downside: A lot of strangers walking around, and exposure to local officials, and business taxes that potentially put you very much on the grid. Renting to criminals takes the taxes and paperwork out of the picture, but adds even more exposure to cops and other less pliable criminals. Reduce your base Haven dots by one for pools involving your Haven’s privacy and for security against either financial or criminal intrusion (pick when you choose this Merit).</p><p>Dots in this Merit roughly equate to their Resources equivalent, and the Storyteller may let you use its dots instead of Resources if the substitution makes sense. It also adds two dice to pools to do things related to your business’ activities or core competency, to interact with other local businesses, and to tests of preparedness to already have something you need in the moment (<strong>Vampire: </strong></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"112"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LLQWIPMPXqDUsGVx"},"adv:v5-furcus":{"name":"Furcus","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your haven lies over a knot or fork in the veins of the Earth, or a frayed spot in the Veil. While invisible to normal sight (though not perhaps to Auspex or various Rituals), it underlies a prominent area: the top of a staircase, a fountain in the garden, the center of a room. (Architecture + Occult might allow someone to deduce its location, if they know what they’re looking for.) Kindred feel the pull of magical energies at the site, and kine often feel nervous or nauseated. Blood Sorcery Rituals or Oblivion Ceremonies (pick when you take this Advantage) tap into the furcus’ power.</p><p>Each dot in this Merit adds one die to Ritual (or Ceremonial) dice pools used at the furcus. (At the Storyteller’s discretion it may also subtract one die from Social pools used to calm or seduce mortals – or add a die when interacting with mortal hedge magicians or other occult groupies.) Bestial failures on such tests may also lead to especially horrific or eldritch results: energy from the furcus that harms or curses the sorcerer, accidental summoning of an entity, or a clear magical pulse from the haven’s location. Depending on the Ceremony, a total or bestial failure might automatically impose the Haunted Flaw on the haven.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Furcus (Ritual or Ceremony at the furcus, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["disciplines.sorcery","disciplines.rituals"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"112"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Nmuv5UD4fLz5xrAI"},"adv:v5-machine-shop":{"name":"Machine Shop","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your haven has state of the art tools, equipment, and machinery in a dedicated work space, plus supplies of oil, industrial chemicals, gasoline, and other household necessities. Each dot of this Merit adds one to the dice pool for Craft rolls, or to other tests related to building, repairing, or disassembling a piece of machinery or equipment: Intelligence + Firearms (Gunsmithing) to build a Sten submachine gun, Intelligence + Technology to fab a compound crossbow with a 3-D printer, Intelligence + Craft (Engineering) to soup up a car, etc. For particularly recondite machinery, you may have to explain how you sourced it if you don’t have Resources ••• or better.</p><p>Small havens with this Merit can only add to pools for one specialty of Craft: they don’t have enough room for a general machine shop or for automotive work.</p><p>A three-dot machine shop can include an incinerator; a three-dot machine shop in a large haven can include a car crusher or compactor.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Machine Shop (building or repairing machinery, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.craft","skills.technology"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"113"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.F8MPBwuQTBDrFkbb"},"adv:v5-shared":{"name":"Flaw: Shared","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your haven is not entirely your own. Other Kindred, besides you and your coterie, have access to it and can make decisions about it without consulting you. Decide the specific conditions and determine the severity of this Flaw based on those circumstances.</p><p>The one-dot version of this Flaw covers joint havens: e.g., you have one or more Storyteller character housemates, or you and a whole clutch of fledglings squat in an abandoned building. Any problems they bring back to the haven become your problems, too. A coterie that takes a haven together does not automatically invoke this Flaw; sharing with your coterie has only as much downside as you mutually create in play.</p><p>The one-dot Shared Flaw also covers short-term havens: you’re staying in an embassy or safe house or campground, with the expectation that you’ll be moving on and another Kindred may arrive to take your place at any time. Getting a new shared haven when that happens costs no more dots, but you may lose dots you put into improving the old one, in addition to moving all your belongings and re-training your Herd where to show up at night.</p><p>The two-dot version of this Flaw means you have a local landlord. Another Kindred has rights to the property (either an actual land title or a grant from the local Prince or Baron) and graciously (or not) allows you to roost there. After haven creation, you must discuss any changes you wish to make to your haven with your landlord. The landlord can enter your haven at will. Especially older Kindred landlords may also call on you for service or dirty jobs, treating you like a vassal or apparatchik.</p><p>Tremere chantries, Nosferatu warrens, and Ministry temples might be one or two-dot shared havens for characters of the appropriate clan, depending on the power balance between you and the other Kindred there.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"113"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BnH9LCVEbNDZXlJs"},"adv:v5-city-secrets":{"name":"City Secrets","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have somehow acquired a damning secret about the city’s Kindred power structure, perhaps specifically about someone in it. Decide the exact nature of the information with the Storyteller. If the secret refers only to mortal business (“the Mayor is secretly fucking a Russian Mafiya soldier”), it’s just a way to explain your Influence.</p><p>A few power players in the city know you have this information, or strongly suspect it. Hopefully, you’ve made arrangements for the secret to get out upon your mysterious disappearance. You could sell this information for a high price, but its truest value is protection: those who want it to stay secret have some interest in keeping you happy. Of course, if you push your luck, they may decide keeping you quiet forever takes higher priority.</p><p>This Background resembles Influence more than Status in some circumstances; the Storyteller should determine when or if it adds to a Social pool, or possibly a Research or Insight test.</p><p>You can take this Background a maximum of three times, with a different secret each time.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"118"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RCTaujA9WRGPzpNw"},"adv:v5-famous-face":{"name":"• Famous Face","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You bear a passing or very close resemblance to someone very well known. Sometimes this works in your favor. Other times this results in “Charlize Theron bit that cop on the neck!” You gain two dice to social tests in circumstances where you can leverage your lookalike status, but suffer a two-dice penalty whenever you try to hide in a crowd or in other areas where you want to avoid recognition.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Famous Face (leveraging your lookalike status)","value":2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}},{"source":"Famous Face (hiding in a crowd)","value":-2,"paths":["skills.stealth"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"118"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pgJEFcprBmvCDpoa"},"adv:v5-ing-nue":{"name":"• Ingénue","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You look completely innocent and blameless, making others believe in your good intentions much easier. Add two dice to any rolls related to avoiding suspicion or deflect blame, at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Ingénue (avoiding suspicion or deflecting blame)","value":2,"paths":["skills.subterfuge","skills.insight"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"118"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pWDxim1gM8ua9reI"},"adv:v5-remarkable-feature":{"name":"• Remarkable Feature","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You possess a rare, memorable feature such as a striking eye color, atypical pupils, or an unusual complexion, providing a two-dice bonus to social interactions with strangers. (The novelty quickly wears off.) Take a one-die penalty to disguise yourself.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Remarkable Feature (strangers)","value":2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}},{"source":"Remarkable Feature (disguising yourself)","value":-1,"paths":["skills.subterfuge"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"118"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XFk7dcubK9WNuIaK"},"adv:v5-vessel-recognition":{"name":"• Vessel Recognition","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>One easy way to piss off a Kindred is to feed off their kine. Kindred guard their herds jealously, and you’ve learned how to avoid them, being able to smell out which mortal belongs to a herd or is a particular Blood Doll.</p><p>A win on a Resolve + Awareness test at Difficulty 2 lets you smell whether a mortal has been fed on recently. A critical win lets you sense if the feeding is recurring, likely making them part of someone’s herd.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"118"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.JzZ2U5c80o5mO7Kh"},"adv:v5-luck-of-the-devil":{"name":"•••• Luck of the Devil","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Someone else will always take the fall for your missteps as long as someone’s available to take the blame. Whether this is because of a face everyone loves, a guardian angel, or something far darker, you manage to slip, skate, and dodge out of trouble, though you’re in constant need of new friends as you burn through them like a wildfire.</p><p>Once per session you can have a misfortune — an attack, an accusation, a blame, or anything in between — directed at you befall someone else close to you. This can be an ally, retainer, coterie mate, or even a Touchstone. The Storyteller is the final arbiter on what counts as a proper misfortune.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"119"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.0jUfWqaB1FhVI1eA"},"adv:v5-check-the-trunk":{"name":"• Check the Trunk","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mercenaries and gig workers have to keep their tools of the trade close at hand. They never know what their next job might require, but they do their best to keep a supply of useful tools close at hand. Caitiff, Ravnos, Anarchs, and even some mortals especially benefit from this Merit.</p><p>You have easy access to an armory or cache of tools and hardware. None of these items can exceed a value available to a character with Resources 2. Common items found in a cache could include a sawed-off shotgun, rusty bolt cutters, half a roll of duct tape, homemade stakes, half empty squeeze bottles of lighter fluid, and whatever else the Storyteller allows. Take an additional two dots in Preparedness pools (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade</strong>, p. 410) for items within this Resource band. Losing your car, haven, or other storage space may interrupt this Merit until you build up a new stash.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"119"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UqxlWi0Yg5gaYJ1X"},"adv:v5-side-hustler":{"name":"•• Side Hustler","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Folks on the fringes constantly have to grind to keep the little that they have. Juggling a million tasks, favors, and debts has become second nature to them. Once per session you can get your hands on an item, a piece of information, or access to an event as if you had two dots in the appropriate Resources, Contacts or Influence Background. (Items obtained with Resources likely have to be returned intact.) Often this requires you to spend part of the night calling in markers and pressing your informal network.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"119"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.emBFzG44LC2tIn0e"},"adv:v5-untouchable":{"name":"••••• Untouchable","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The powers that be are very reluctant to punish you for even the most egregious crimes. Perhaps you’re the secret power behind the throne, or you hold some damning blackmail over the local Baron, or you’re the secret childe of a Justicar or other political potentate. Once per story you can escape all official punishment for a crime that would otherwise see you destroyed or cast out of your sect, though you may be privately reprimanded or face consequences through unofficial channels. The Court or Council often ignores your lesser crimes as well, but lower-level Kindred may take it upon themselves to step to you for your perceived transgressions and flaunted impunity.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"120"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5pwTeLhz1n6FWf8G"},"adv:v5-transparent":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Transparent","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>For whatever reason, you aren’t a good liar, and it shows. You either have a terrible poker face or your parents instilled in you a strong urge to be truthful even when it hurts.</p><p>Lose one die from any pools requiring Subterfuge. You cannot gain dots in Subterfuge.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Transparent","value":-1,"paths":["skills.subterfuge"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"120"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.h2wytLhbD6dgFyl4"},"adv:v5-vein-tapper":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Vein Tapper","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You find the act of feeding extremely personal and cannot take blood from mortals while being observed. This means you often feed from the unaware and go out of your way to find (or create) drugged or unconscious victims.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"120"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lMv8EFLcMsyoC5rY"},"adv:v5-starving-decay":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Starving Decay","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your undead body is constantly on the verge of reverting to a corpse and only staving off Hunger keeps you from decaying.</p><p>At any point in which your Hunger is 3 or higher your body shrivels and decays, incurring a two-dice penalty to physical tests as well as social interactions with mortals, not to mention risking the Masquerade.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Starving Decay (at Hunger 3 or higher)","value":-2,"paths":["physical"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}},{"source":"Starving Decay (mortals, at Hunger 3 or higher)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"120"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.u4Vs44SdtDsMGyu2"},"adv:v5-twice-cursed":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Twice-Cursed","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are cursed with additional Bane, making you labor under additional weight borne out of your clan founder’s flawed nature.</p><p>Take your clan’s variant Bane (see p. 56) <em>in </em> <em>addition </em>to your regular Bane. The Storyteller can prohibit this Flaw if the second Bane would cause problems for, or lack impact in, the chronicle.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6vTNO0kUhnbcrSh6"},"adv:v5-knowledge-hungry":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Knowledge Hungry","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You always feared that you wouldn’t have the time to learn everything you wanted about what’s truly important. Well, that’s one problem solved. The time to learn is not always now, though — you regretfully put a bookmark in your studies and attend to more life-threatening matters.</p><p>At character creation, pick a topic that your character hungers to study. When your character comes across books, tutorial videos, college seminars, or other methods of learning about their chosen subject, make a Willpower roll at Difficulty 3 to resist chasing their obsession.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.jhtyimX9uXuRfDE4"},"adv:v5-prestation-debts":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Prestation Debts","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You owe another Kindred (or two others) two minor boons. The debt-holder wields more power and influence than you within vampire society, though not necessarily within your sect. They receive a one-die bonus in Social combat against you while they hold your marker. They may call in either of these debts at any time, but practically they do so when you really really don’t want to (or absolutely can’t) pay them off. However, if you do manage to successfully pay off both debts, you don’t need to take another Flaw to “balance” your character. The creditor still likes to lord it over you, though, and retains the one-die bonus.</p><p>You can absolutely take this Flaw alongside one or more Minor Boons (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade</strong>, pp. 193 and 315) at character creation. You cannot take this Flaw owing another player’s character the boons in question. For more a more detailed breakdown of Prestation Debts and Boons as Backgrounds, see <em>Boons and </em> <em>Debts,</em> p. 115.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.00PiBimlPlwo86A5"},"adv:v5-risk-taker":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Risk-Taker","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re one of those people who feel compelled to take every chance to experience life at its fullest.</p><p>When confronted with a chance to indulge a risky temptation you haven’t done before (such as snorting a new drug, imbibing vampire blood, or taking a Kindred lover), you suffer a two-dice penalty to all actions until you either partake of the new experience or the scene ends. This Flaw doesn’t compel you to do something suicidal ... but you may not always accurately predict the full consequences of your actions.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Risk-Taker (until you indulge the temptation)","value":-2,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KFARRhWebEyoQYeR"},"adv:v5-weak-willed":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Weak-Willed","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You struggle to assert your own personality when confronted with the will of another. You may not necessarily be submissive; perhaps you feel more confident when following another person’s lead. You might be part of a vampire’s entourage or Herd, but you aren’t necessarily loyal to that vampire.</p><p>Take a one-die penalty to Social pools against your leader or superior. Even when you’re aware of an attempt to sway you mentally or emotionally (such as with Dominate or Presence), you may not use the active resistance systems to avoid those effects. ■ Chapter Four:</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Weak-Willed (against your leader or superior)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2h7T1XJFY1IpOsrp"},"adv:v5-favored-blood":{"name":"•••• Favored Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>Vampiric power unfettered by the curse of the Ancients flows through you. Your intuitive and legendary command of Kindred prowess likely inspires both awe and fear in other vampires.</p><p>You can buy dots in any Discipline even if you’ve never tasted the blood of another vampire with that Discipline. Cannot be taken with the Muddled Blood Flaw (see p. 127).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"126"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3AqDKUY6zACEfhdX"},"adv:v5-mark-of-caine":{"name":"•• Mark of Caine","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>You bear a mark, either physical or spiritual, that gives other vampires pause. Camarilla and Ashirra vampires look on your mark with fear, while Anarchs claim to respect you for playing with elder superstition — but they don’t look at your mark too closely either. Even Sabbat Cainites hesitate to tangle with you, though particularly religious Noddists might target you for special attention.</p><p>You gain two bonus dice on any attempt to intimidate, bully, or cow other vampires who subscribe to the myth of Caine. Anyone attempting to commit diablerie on you cannot add their Blood Potency to the roll and any failure to their roll results in a bestial failure.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Mark of Caine (cowing vampires who believe in Caine)","value":2,"paths":["skills.intimidation"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"126"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.r26WeO3niykrIal0"},"adv:v5-mockingbird":{"name":"••• Mockingbird","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>Orphans are often unfairly labeled thieves and diablerists, but some actually do have the power to mimic the powers of other vampires after tasting their blood.</p><p>For one night after drinking one Hunger die worth of blood from a vampire, you can choose and employ one Discipline power possessed by the Kindred from whom you fed. The power cannot be of a level higher than your highest Discipline, though you do not need to possess the same Discipline. Roll Rouse Checks as normal to activate this power. Any other rolls required to use the power use the donor’s Discipline level and your Attributes. During this time, you mimic the donor’s clan bane with the same severity level as the donor. Only one power can be “borrowed” in this way per night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"126"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3UtQP1a4rcHfA5W9"},"adv:v5-sun-scarred":{"name":"••••• Sun-Scarred","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>While it’s a common myth among Kindred that Caitiff and thin-bloods can walk in the sunlight, only a rare few have anything resembling that ability. These few are sometimes called Daywalkers, and those Caitiff who have this power usually keep it a secret for fear of being hunted and cannibalized by others. Unsurprisingly, Outcasts who can walk in the day may find themselves sought out for their particular acumen and the advantage it grants them over those who must slumber by day.</p><p>The first turn you are exposed to Sunlight in a scene, take no Health damage, take 1 Aggravated Willpower damage, and automatically succeed on any rolls to resist terror frenzy. For the remainder of the scene all Aggravated Health damage you take from sunlight is instantly converted into Superficial damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"126"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wayUAr7V0zZaJpeg"},"adv:v5-uncle-fangs":{"name":"••• Uncle Fangs","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>The local thin-bloods know you as a mentor and guide. Whatever your true motivations, you can often find aid among the Duskborn without having to ask twice.</p><p>You have easy access to a local coterie of three to five thin-bloods. They often know what’s happening on the street before anyone else, and can sometimes provide you with Alchemical concoctions. Treat this group as an Ally group (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade</strong>, p. 192), though contrary to regular Allies, this group is undead, if only just. Cannot be taken with Liquidator (p. 127).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qvZpAydFSkdCnK6R"},"adv:v5-befouling-vitae":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Befouling Vitae","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>When you Embrace mortals or leave them for dead from feeding, the Blood twists them into maniacal corpses with an unquenchable hunger.</p><p>Any mortal you Embrace or kill by feeding returns as a desperate wight within a few nights. While these unfortunates are often too weak to be much of a threat (treat as a Ghoul; see <strong>Vampire: </strong></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BfEXsPIVtEOVz6Hb"},"adv:v5-clan-curse":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Clan Curse","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>A Caitiff by hair’s breadth, you still suffer the curse of your sire’s clan. While this can sometimes help you pass for a member of that clan, most often than not it just adds another pain to your tortured existence.</p><p>You suffer from a vampire clan bane of your choice, likely that of your sire (if you even know who they are). Its severity is halved (round down, minimum of 1).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.W41eWTscxC6iEPq9"},"adv:v5-debt-peon":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Debt Peon","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Orphans often have to sell their proverbial souls to Princes and Barons to find their place in the night. These debts add up and few Caitiff ever climb out of the pocket of those in power, because the prestation system breaks down when Kindred society refuses to recognize the political existence, much less the rights, of the debtor.</p><p>You owe several minor boons, or even a few major boons, to a high-Status vampire, possibly originally incurred by your Sire. Tracking these debts proves pointless, as the creditor extracts new boons whenever they have leverage over you and only considers any boon settled when forced to by their peers. They have a two-dice bonus to Social combat against you in front of other Kindred. Refusal to honor your debt adds the Shunned (••) Flaw, and may even result in a Blood Hunt against you.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RvSx2QtrNitpCQCk"},"adv:v5-liquidator":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Liquidator","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Thin-bloods call you a Pander and avoid you whenever possible. They even refuse to speak openly with your coterie mates or other associates. Perhaps you served as a scourge, or just displayed particular cruelty as a member of the Sheriff’s posse.</p><p>Take a two-dice penalty to all your Social skill dice pools against thin-bloods, other than Intimidation. Cannot be taken with Uncle Fangs (p. 127).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Liquidator (against thin-bloods)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"unless":["skills.intimidation"]}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bX4uQlfqnzoKy48K"},"adv:v5-muddled-blood":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Muddled Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your Blood cannot recall its nature and must be reminded of its inherent power every time you want to strengthen it.</p><p>Even if you already possess a Discipline you must drink the Blood of someone who possesses it in order to buy dots in it. Cannot be taken with the Favored Blood Merit (see p. 126).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.cIhnRqRGVVjIZgE4"},"adv:v5-walking-omen":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Walking Omen","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Many vampires rely on prescience or read omens on a nightly basis, and you happen to act as a magnet, skewing these supernatural compasses.</p><p>Any scrying, premonitions, or other forms of futureor fortune telling in your domain regularly points to you as a source of disaster and misfortune. While your close allies have learned to tune it out, other oracles single you out, screeching, when you are not being hunted by those who want to redirect a future that they believe will come to pass at your hands. The effects of this Flaw are ultimately up to the Storyteller, but it should at the very least cause one serious altercation per story.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.T2F37e0FIaHbdqGu"},"adv:v5-word-scarred":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Word-Scarred","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your body or a portion of it is covered in ancient texts of vampiric lore. Perhaps your sire inscribed them upon you, or they appeared on their own after the Embrace. Either way, shadowy forces conspire to use or destroy this lore.</p><p>The major occult foe or force in the chronicle often interferes in your life, seeking to imprison, flense, mind-wipe, Blood Bond, or otherwise neutralize you. (They would never lower themselves to recruit a Caitiff, don’t be silly. You’re just a lost, disobedient book to them.) Maybe two such forces fight over the right to edit you! Any other character who “reads” the full text on your skin learns a terrible secret regarding Gehenna, Noddist prophecy, Bahari mysteries, or the like. You and the Storyteller may decide this together, or it may be a mystery to you!</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"128"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UBxjkkL6ZU0LuodL"},"adv:v5-heliophobia":{"name":"Flaw: Heliophobia","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>An overly cautious Sire hammered the reminder to avoid sunlight into you a little too hard, or you saw someone catch fire in a sunrise early after your Embrace. Either way, you fear sunlight as though you were a full vampire. You are susceptible to terror frenzy from sunlight.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HauvHgnCUDF9Cu6z"},"adv:v5-night-terrors":{"name":"Flaw: Night Terrors","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You sleep as usual, with no memory of any of the dreams you may have had during the day. However, at night those dreams come flooding in at the most inopportune times. During times of stress, or when your Beast is strongest, nightmares pull through and feel excruciatingly real. They may be disjointed hallucinations, or they may be a way for the Storyteller to feed theme or foreshadow events. Once per session, your night terrors onset and you take a one-die penalty to all actions for the rest of the scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Night Terrors (rest of the scene once your night terrors set in)","value":-1,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.mglaKrAAmhVrRDgj"},"adv:v5-plague-bearer":{"name":"Flaw: Plague Bearer","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You are still susceptible to mortal sickness: colds, flu, and other more severe infections. Every time you feed you must roll a die for each Hunger slaked, and if any come up a “1,” you’ve caught something nasty. (Effect is up to the Storyteller, but could range from die penalties to slow Health degradation.) Mortal medicine doesn’t cure you; only drinking a functioning immune system by slaking your Hunger to 0 does so. And of course, you run the risk of infecting anyone you feed on, using the same die roll as above.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9IGLw2fnIGnln4Oh"},"adv:v5-sloppy-drinker":{"name":"Flaw: Sloppy Drinker","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Your bite is careless, or your fangs are ill suited to puncture skin cleanly. When feeding, make a Dexterity + Medicine test against a Difficulty equal to Hunger slaked to cover your feed marks before too much blood is spilled. On a failure, the wound is too ragged to close: the mortal may bleed out with Masquerade-threatening neck wounds.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IQhqd7goqwy2vv8n"},"adv:v5-sun-faded":{"name":"Flaw: Sun-Faded","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You cannot use Discipline powers – including Thin- Blood Alchemy – in sunlight, and active powers cease when you enter sunlight. You can use them indoors by day, with a two-dice penalty, as long as you avoid any hint of sunlight.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2psoUCPHFpH5PSi9"},"adv:v5-supernatural-tell":{"name":"Flaw: Supernatural Tell","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Whether it’s your smell, your aura, or something even subtler, other supernatural entities can sense your presence. They may be curious, hungry, or irritated, but they definitely clock you. Lose two dice from Stealth pools and similar against supernatural opponents including other vampires.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Supernatural Tell (against supernatural opponents)","value":-2,"paths":["skills.stealth"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FdhMgm1wibHmworr"},"adv:v5-twilight-presence":{"name":"Flaw: Twilight Presence","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>You make others uncomfortable around you. Most mortals don’t want to be around you at all, and other Kindred dislike you even more than they do regular thin-bloods. Only thin-bloods can adjust to your weird demeanor. Lose a die from Social pools involving anyone except thin-bloods.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Twilight Presence (anyone but thin-bloods)","value":-1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.WiudHczR8jy1q7QN"},"adv:v5-unending-hunger":{"name":"Flaw: Unending Hunger","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Your Beast always hungers for more, never finding sustenance with smaller sips. When feeding in a scene you sate one less Hunger than other thinbloods. This applies only once per scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.f43525YR45fcWLBX"},"adv:v5-abhorrent-blood":{"name":"Abhorrent Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Something in your Blood makes other vampires unable to stomach it. Whether it is the perverse mix of life and undeath or your history of questionable Thin-Blood Alchemy, other vampires gag and vomit when they try to drink from you. Other vampires abort any bite attempts after the initial damage and anyone who tries to keep drinking from you must spend two points of Willpower each turn that they do so. Mortals and Thin-Blood Alchemy are not affected by this anomaly.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"135"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HPSLwKvG3CmDJoYn"},"adv:v5-faith-proof":{"name":"Faith-Proof","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Whether you consider yourself an atheist or devoutly religious, you remain too close to mortality for True Faith to affect you.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oUmQCN6NTHwQIivK"},"adv:v5-low-appetite":{"name":"Low Appetite","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>When at Hunger 0 or 1, and when rising at sunset, roll two dice on your Rouse Check and take the highest of the two.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.tVzaPo5x3dVuyIJL"},"adv:v5-lucid-dreamer":{"name":"Lucid Dreamer","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Most vampires don’t dream because they don’t sleep. You dream; you can recall and even sometimes control your dreams. Once per session, if you’re asleep by day you can ask the Storyteller to provide a clue from the previous night’s memories or a hint about the story suitable for dream revelation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Q7qDRimtAqE0tS7M"},"adv:v5-mortalitys-mien":{"name":"Mortality’s Mien","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Your mortal nature still outshines your Beast. Your aura does not appear vampiric or supernatural; instead it looks mortal to anyone able to detect supernatural creatures. You can also add two dice to any attempt to make yourself appear mortal in other regards, such as makeup.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Mortality’s Mien (appearing mortal)","value":2,"paths":["skills.subterfuge","skills.performance"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.g6n3kHwcz34Qh0ZX"},"adv:v5-swift-feeder":{"name":"Swift Feeder","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Maybe you were a phlebotomist in life, or just didn’t like mess. You sip delicately and quickly, neatly slaking one Hunger in one turn, including licking the wound closed. This Merit can only be used once per scene.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HbhCC5Bc6DvG4dGi"},"adv:v5-baneful-blood":{"name":"Flaw: Baneful Blood","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Due to some mysterious property of the Blood, you experience the bane of your first domitor’s clan to a degree. Your first domitor, the Kindred whose blood you tasted in order to become a ghoul, must be of Clan Lasombra, Malkavian, Ministry, Nosferatu, Ravnos, Salubri, or Toreador. Even if your domitor changes, the bane you experience does not.</p><p>You experience the domitor’s bane at one level for each dot in this Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"141"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XU6zyvYXmFzjJc2V"},"adv:v5-death-proof":{"name":"• Death-Proof","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>You cannot learn Oblivion, but you are immune to it. <em>(The Players Guide lists this Merit in its Advantages Master List; its full text is missing from the book.)</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"126"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OKduWjZPtFPDiGGC"},"adv:v5-major-boon":{"name":"Major Boon","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In most cases Status, Influence, Mawla, and other Backgrounds sufficiently represent a character’s social resources, but there may be times when the story calls for more granular use of debts and boons (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> pp. 314–315) owed to specific individuals: “My Sire saved your Haven during the Revolt, remember,” is more exciting and intriguing than a dot in Status. It’s also less generally useful, but provides more specific leverage on a specific vampire.</p><p>Individual characters can acquire one-off Boons from other Kindred during character generation. Minor Boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade, </strong> p. 193). Major Boons cost one dot more than Minor BOON AND DEBT COSTS Minor Boon Major Boon Creditor / Debtor</p><p>• </p><p>•• Neonate</p><p>• </p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Primogen or Anarch Council member</p><p>••• </p><p>•••• Prince or Baron</p><p>Boons. Characters may not purchase Life Boons at character creation without explicit Storyteller agreement. If the Storyteller agrees, a Life Boon costs three dots more than the equivalent Minor Boon.</p><p>Debts provide many fewer points when taken as Flaws than they cost as Merits. Debt sucks, what can we say? However, being in Major Debt (owing a Major Boon) to a higher-Status vampire is even worse than that. Once per story the character’s creditor or their minions can ask them for a favor they cannot refuse without risking being shunned, blood hunted, or worse. This favor can take on any form and often means exposing the character to mortal danger or reputational collapse.</p><p>If a character finds themselves in Life Debt to a high-Status vampire, they might as well just get the Blood Bond and enthrall themselves. Even non- Caitiff vampires may experience such indebtedness as Debt Peonage (p. 127).</p><p>In all other ways, Minor, Major, and Life Boons function according to the descriptions in <strong>Vampire: The </strong></p><p><strong>Masquerade</strong> (pp. 314–315). Track all debts and boons individually. Note that trivial boons, as simple, easily discharged promises, are not tracked using this system.</p><p>Boons and Debts cannot be purchased or bought off with experience points. After character creation, they can only be gained (or lost) through the character’s actions in play.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"115"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"background"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AosxsliBtsgNP23x"},"adv:v5-minor-debt":{"name":"Flaw: Minor Debt","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In most cases Status, Influence, Mawla, and other Backgrounds sufficiently represent a character’s social resources, but there may be times when the story calls for more granular use of debts and boons (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> pp. 314–315) owed to specific individuals: “My Sire saved your Haven during the Revolt, remember,” is more exciting and intriguing than a dot in Status. It’s also less generally useful, but provides more specific leverage on a specific vampire.</p><p>Individual characters can acquire one-off Boons from other Kindred during character generation. Minor Boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade, </strong> p. 193). Major Boons cost one dot more than Minor BOON AND DEBT COSTS Minor Boon Major Boon Creditor / Debtor</p><p>• </p><p>•• Neonate</p><p>• </p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Primogen or Anarch Council member</p><p>••• </p><p>•••• Prince or Baron</p><p>Boons. Characters may not purchase Life Boons at character creation without explicit Storyteller agreement. If the Storyteller agrees, a Life Boon costs three dots more than the equivalent Minor Boon.</p><p>Debts provide many fewer points when taken as Flaws than they cost as Merits. Debt sucks, what can we say? However, being in Major Debt (owing a Major Boon) to a higher-Status vampire is even worse than that. Once per story the character’s creditor or their minions can ask them for a favor they cannot refuse without risking being shunned, blood hunted, or worse. This favor can take on any form and often means exposing the character to mortal danger or reputational collapse.</p><p>If a character finds themselves in Life Debt to a high-Status vampire, they might as well just get the Blood Bond and enthrall themselves. Even non- Caitiff vampires may experience such indebtedness as Debt Peonage (p. 127).</p><p>In all other ways, Minor, Major, and Life Boons function according to the descriptions in <strong>Vampire: The </strong></p><p><strong>Masquerade</strong> (pp. 314–315). Track all debts and boons individually. Note that trivial boons, as simple, easily discharged promises, are not tracked using this system.</p><p>Boons and Debts cannot be purchased or bought off with experience points. After character creation, they can only be gained (or lost) through the character’s actions in play.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"115"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RVzYnddF84cbK2bi"},"adv:v5-major-debt":{"name":"Flaw: Major Debt","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In most cases Status, Influence, Mawla, and other Backgrounds sufficiently represent a character’s social resources, but there may be times when the story calls for more granular use of debts and boons (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> pp. 314–315) owed to specific individuals: “My Sire saved your Haven during the Revolt, remember,” is more exciting and intriguing than a dot in Status. It’s also less generally useful, but provides more specific leverage on a specific vampire.</p><p>Individual characters can acquire one-off Boons from other Kindred during character generation. Minor Boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade, </strong> p. 193). Major Boons cost one dot more than Minor BOON AND DEBT COSTS Minor Boon Major Boon Creditor / Debtor</p><p>• </p><p>•• Neonate</p><p>• </p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Primogen or Anarch Council member</p><p>••• </p><p>•••• Prince or Baron</p><p>Boons. Characters may not purchase Life Boons at character creation without explicit Storyteller agreement. If the Storyteller agrees, a Life Boon costs three dots more than the equivalent Minor Boon.</p><p>Debts provide many fewer points when taken as Flaws than they cost as Merits. Debt sucks, what can we say? However, being in Major Debt (owing a Major Boon) to a higher-Status vampire is even worse than that. Once per story the character’s creditor or their minions can ask them for a favor they cannot refuse without risking being shunned, blood hunted, or worse. This favor can take on any form and often means exposing the character to mortal danger or reputational collapse.</p><p>If a character finds themselves in Life Debt to a high-Status vampire, they might as well just get the Blood Bond and enthrall themselves. Even non- Caitiff vampires may experience such indebtedness as Debt Peonage (p. 127).</p><p>In all other ways, Minor, Major, and Life Boons function according to the descriptions in <strong>Vampire: The </strong></p><p><strong>Masquerade</strong> (pp. 314–315). Track all debts and boons individually. Note that trivial boons, as simple, easily discharged promises, are not tracked using this system.</p><p>Boons and Debts cannot be purchased or bought off with experience points. After character creation, they can only be gained (or lost) through the character’s actions in play.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"115"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Y2FQUMcJGvyCwKfJ"},"adv:v5-life-debt":{"name":"Flaw: Life Debt","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In most cases Status, Influence, Mawla, and other Backgrounds sufficiently represent a character’s social resources, but there may be times when the story calls for more granular use of debts and boons (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade,</strong> pp. 314–315) owed to specific individuals: “My Sire saved your Haven during the Revolt, remember,” is more exciting and intriguing than a dot in Status. It’s also less generally useful, but provides more specific leverage on a specific vampire.</p><p>Individual characters can acquire one-off Boons from other Kindred during character generation. Minor Boons cost half the points (rounded up) of the equivalent Mawla (<strong>Vampire: The Masquerade, </strong> p. 193). Major Boons cost one dot more than Minor BOON AND DEBT COSTS Minor Boon Major Boon Creditor / Debtor</p><p>• </p><p>•• Neonate</p><p>• </p><p>•• Ancilla</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Elder</p><p>•• </p><p>••• Primogen or Anarch Council member</p><p>••• </p><p>•••• Prince or Baron</p><p>Boons. Characters may not purchase Life Boons at character creation without explicit Storyteller agreement. If the Storyteller agrees, a Life Boon costs three dots more than the equivalent Minor Boon.</p><p>Debts provide many fewer points when taken as Flaws than they cost as Merits. Debt sucks, what can we say? However, being in Major Debt (owing a Major Boon) to a higher-Status vampire is even worse than that. Once per story the character’s creditor or their minions can ask them for a favor they cannot refuse without risking being shunned, blood hunted, or worse. This favor can take on any form and often means exposing the character to mortal danger or reputational collapse.</p><p>If a character finds themselves in Life Debt to a high-Status vampire, they might as well just get the Blood Bond and enthrall themselves. Even non- Caitiff vampires may experience such indebtedness as Debt Peonage (p. 127).</p><p>In all other ways, Minor, Major, and Life Boons function according to the descriptions in <strong>Vampire: The </strong></p><p><strong>Masquerade</strong> (pp. 314–315). Track all debts and boons individually. Note that trivial boons, as simple, easily discharged promises, are not tracked using this system.</p><p>Boons and Debts cannot be purchased or bought off with experience points. After character creation, they can only be gained (or lost) through the character’s actions in play.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade Players Guide","page":"115"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UzN3jnB0Ea6FtHHn"},"adv:v20-acute-sense":{"name":"• Acute Sense","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>One of your senses is exceptionally sharp, be it sight, hearing, smell, touch, or taste. The difficulties for all tasks involving the use of this particular sense are reduced by two. This Merit can be combined with the Discipline of Auspex to produce superhuman sensory acuity.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"479"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.cjy6oTm2ForgjxBQ"},"adv:v20-bruiser":{"name":"• Bruiser","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your appearance is sufficiently thug-like to inspire fear (or at least disquiet) in those who see you. While you’re not necessarily ugly, you do radiate a quiet menace, to the point where people cross the street to avoid passing near you. All Intimidation rolls against those who have not demonstrated their physical superiority to you are at -1 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.J1QJSrGqJC4tSkP2"},"adv:v20-early-riser":{"name":"• Early Riser","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>No one can explain it, but you seem to have the ability to work on less rest than your fellow vampires. You always seem to be the first to rise and the last to go to bed even if you’re been out until dawn. Your Humanity or Path score is considered to be 10 for purposes of deciding when you rise each evening. Vampires with this Merit cannot take the Deep Sleeper Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.nn2y9QCdrf9jjrXz"},"adv:v20-friendly-face":{"name":"• Friendly Face","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a face that reminds everyone of someone, to the point where strangers are inclined to be wellinclined toward you because of it. The effect doesn’t fade even if you explain the “mistake,” leaving you at -1 difficulty on all appropriate Social-based rolls (yes for Seduction, no for Intimidation, for example) when a stranger is involved. This Merit only functions on a first meeting.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aR8BrZsy5DjdCqCW"},"adv:v20-enchanting-voice":{"name":"•• Enchanting Voice","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>There is something about your voice that others cannot ignore. When you command, they are cowed. When you seduce, they swoon. Whether thundering, soothing, persuading, or simply talking, your voice commands attention. The difficulties of all rolls involving the use of the voice to persuade, charm, or command are reduced by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AGk6is7abM5G3lOt"},"adv:v20-daredevil":{"name":"••• Daredevil","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are good at taking risks, and even better at surviving them. When attempting exceptionally risky non-combat actions (such as leaping from one moving car to another), characters with this Merit add an additional three dice to their rolls, and negate a single botch die that may result from such a roll. Generally, such actions must be at least difficulty 8 and have the potential to inflict at least three health levels of damage if failed.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FxV2LgxyWMsO9Mln"},"adv:v20-efficient-digestion":{"name":"••• Efficient Digestion","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are able to draw more than the usual amount of nourishment from blood. When feeding, you gain an additional point to your blood pool for every two points of blood you consume. This does not allow you to exceed your blood pool maximum.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wvEGYy0SlzoMSa0C"},"adv:v20-hard-of-hearing":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Hard of Hearing","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your hearing is defective. The difficulties of any rolls involving the use of hearing are increased by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"480"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oIEFAdrrBeGQw8wp"},"adv:v20-smell-of-the-grave":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Smell of the Grave","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You exude an odor of dampness and newly turned earth, which no amount of scents or perfumes will cover. Mortals in your immediate presence become uncomfortable, so the difficulties of all Social rolls to affect mortals increase by one.</p><p><strong>In V5</strong></p><p>Use the V5 Stench rules: lose one die from seduction and similar Social dice pools, and lose two dice from Stealth pools against opponents who can smell, unless you are upwind.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"481"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.z5igA1RpurZ4rxpC"},"adv:v20-tic-twitch":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Tic/Twitch","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have some sort of repetitive motion that you make in times of stress, and it’s a dead giveaway as to your identity. Examples include a nervous cough, constantly wringing your hands, cracking your knuckles, and so on. It costs one Willpower to refrain from engaging in your tic.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"481"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GyRYFrpu6JGw32mr"},"adv:v20-bad-sight":{"name":"Flaw: Bad Sight","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your sight is defective. The difficulties of any die rolls involving the use of your eyesight are increased by two. As a one-point Flaw, this condition can be corrected with glasses or contacts; as a three-point Flaw, the condition is too severe to be corrected.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"481"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.03cvqQ0w8VOffEzE"},"adv:v20-dulled-bite":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Dulled Bite","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>For some reason your fangs never developed fully, or they may not have manifested at all. When feeding, you need to find some other method of making the blood flow. Failing that, you must achieve double the normal number of successes in order to make your bite penetrate properly. A number of Caitiff and high- Generation vampires often manifest this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"481"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZGyYfRnzW7Ay7WPo"},"adv:v20-infectious-bite":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Infectious Bite","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You may not automatically lick the wounds of your feeding closed. In fact, your bites have a one in five chance of becoming infected and causing mortal victims to become seriously ill. The precise nature of the infection is determined by the Storyteller.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"481"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GIBOMHogHHPwKqxv"},"adv:v20-one-eye":{"name":"Flaw: (••) One Eye","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have only one eye — which eye is missing is up to you. The difficulties of all Perception rolls involving eyesight are increased by two, and the difficulties of all die rolls requiring depth perception are increased by one (this includes ranged combat).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"481"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Jqjn0rDZb9Li6BX2"},"adv:v20-vulnerability-to-silver":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Vulnerability to Silver","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>To you, silver is as painful and as deadly as the rays of the sun. You suffer aggravated wounds from any silver weapons (bullets, knives, etc.), and the mere touch of silver objects discomfits you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZMk44x7AjAHYk1Bt"},"adv:v20-open-wound":{"name":"Flaw: Open Wound","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have one or more wounds that refuse to heal, and which constantly drip blood. This slow leakage costs you an extra blood point per evening (marked off just before dawn), in addition to drawing attention to you. If the wound is visible, you are at + 1 difficulty for all Social-based rolls. For two points, the Flaw is simply unsightly and has the basic effect mentioned above; for four points the seeping wound is serious or disfiguring and includes the effects of the Flaw Permanent Wound (below).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1dk5Bz4BaPKSH06j"},"adv:v20-child":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Child","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You were a small child (between five and 10 years old) at the time of your Embrace, leaving your Physical Attributes underdeveloped and making it difficult to interact with some aspects of mortal society. You may not have more than two dots in Strength or Stamina at character creation, except when raising Physical Attributes with blood points, and the difficulties of all die rolls when attempting to direct or lead mortal adults are increased by two. Characters with this Flaw should also take the Short Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pitofUlf3aKnxpEe"},"adv:v20-deformity":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Deformity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have some kind of deformity — a misshapen limb, hunchback, or clubfoot, for example — which affects your physical abilities and interactions with others. A hunchback, for instance, would lower a character’s Dexterity by two dots and increase the difficulty of die rolls relating to social skills by one. It is the responsibility of the Storyteller to determine the specific effects of the deformity chosen.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FktfUSBJdl83yFD5"},"adv:v20-glowing-eyes":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Glowing Eyes","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have the stereotypical glowing eyes of vampire legend, which gives you a -1 difficulty on Intimidation rolls when you’re dealing with mortals. However, the tradeoffs are many; you must constantly disguise your condition (no, contacts don’t cut it); the glow impairs your vision and puts you at +1 difficulty on all sightbased rolls (including the use of ranged weapons); and the radiance emanating from your eye sockets makes it difficult to hide (+2 difficulty to Stealth rolls) in the dark.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.A8zgUrsA03dplGsg"},"adv:v20-lazy":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Lazy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are simply lazy, avoiding anything that requires effort on your part. Preferring to let others do the hard work, you lounge around. For any action that requires preparation, there’s a good chance you didn’t properly prepare. Difficulty rolls for spontaneous Physical actions (including combat, unless it’s part of a planned offensive) increase by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MufdIkxqkJnJ7XKF"},"adv:v20-monstrous":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Monstrous","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your physical form was twisted during the Embrace, and now reflects the Beast that rages inside you. Characters with this Flaw appear to be savage monsters and have Appearance ratings of zero. Nosferatu and other bloodlines whose weaknesses cause them to start off with Appearance zero cannot take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"482"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.mBQ2SrO40EWLqvRx"},"adv:v20-slow-healing":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Slow Healing","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have difficulty healing wounds. It requires two blood points to heal one health level of bashing or lethal damage, and you heal one health level of aggravated damage every five days (plus the usual five blood points and Willpower expenditure).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"483"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.mTe5YsQS7KARCdFy"},"adv:v20-disease-carrier":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Disease Carrier","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your blood carries a lethal and highly contagious disease. The disease can be anything from rabies to HIV, and Kindred who drink your blood have a 10% chance of becoming a carrier as well. You must spend an extra blood point each night on awakening, or you will begin manifesting symptoms of the disease (increased chance to frenzy for rabies, reduced soak rolls for HIV, etc.).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"483"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.he26YRDGW2LOPnCA"},"adv:v20-flesh-of-the-corpse":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Flesh of the Corpse","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your flesh does not fully regenerate itself once it is damaged. While you are able to heal yourself to the point of regaining full functionality, your skin still retains the cuts, tears, bullet holes, and other visible damage that you have incurred. Depending on the nature of the damage, this Flaw will make social dealings exceedingly difficult, and may decrease your Appearance dots over time (even to 0).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"483"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.tCJEQfshprOS80Aj"},"adv:v20-infertile-vitae":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Infertile Vitae","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>During your Embrace, something went horribly wrong, causing your blood to mutate under the stress of dying and rising again. All those you try to Embrace die. No matter what you do, you may not create any childer. However, your blood can still be used in blood rituals like Thaumaturgy and the Vaulderie, or for any other vampiric needs like making ghouls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"484"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LBqOI1iA9ZEvdEXq"},"adv:v20-introspection":{"name":"• Introspection","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have keen insight into the ulterior motives of all your actions. Through this nightly exercise, you also have incredible insight into the underlying motives of others’ actions. Add two dice to your Perception dice pool when you must take an action against someone with the same Nature or Demeanor as you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"484"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.dJF32lVPWK1d7g75"},"adv:v20-useful-knowledge":{"name":"• Useful Knowledge","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have expertise in a specific field that makes your conversation intriguing to an older Kindred. So long as your knowledge holds the other vampire’s attention, he has a vested interest in keeping you around. Then again, once he’s pumped you for every iota of information you possess, that patronage may suddenly vanish. (Note: This Merit should be played like a 1-dot Mentor with a specific interest. However, unlike a Mentor, Useful Knowledge does not imply a permanent relationship.)</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"484"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AJ7SlejZGc19xwCu"},"adv:v20-code-of-honor":{"name":"•• Code of Honor","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a personal code of ethics to which you adhere. The specifics of this code must be worked out with the Storyteller prior to play, and the character must follow it strictly. Characters with this Merit gain two additional dice to all Willpower or Virtue rolls when acting in accordance with their code (e.g., defending the helpless) or when attempting to avoid situations that might force them to violate their code.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"484"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KYe5Nm9nHoCoAf8a"},"adv:v20-computer-aptitude":{"name":"•• Computer Aptitude","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are familiar with and talented in the uses of computer equipment. Other Kindred may not understand computers, but to you they are intuitive. All rolls involving computers are at -2 difficulty for you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"484"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2zonPH1zqSa4o1af"},"adv:v20-light-sleeper":{"name":"•• Light Sleeper","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can awaken instantly at any sign of trouble or danger, and do so without any sleepiness or hesitation. You may ignore rules regarding how Humanity or your morality Path restricts the number of dice available during the day.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZRGVPDrUoil0uHbp"},"adv:v20-natural-linguist":{"name":"•• Natural Linguist","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a flair for languages. You may add three dice to any dice pool involving written or spoken languages, and each purchase of the Language Merit (previous page) gives you two languages instead of just one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.P0jbL7mC1QdSVneE"},"adv:v20-calm-heart":{"name":"••• Calm Heart","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are naturally calm and do not easily fly off the handle. You receive two extra dice when attempting to resist a frenzy. Brujah may not take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sTw5mkVaHddy9IzL"},"adv:v20-precocious":{"name":"••• Precocious","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You learn quickly. The time for you to pick up a particular Ability (or Abilities, at Storyteller discretion) is cut in half, as is the experience cost.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oBoBamqV6xKW6ixy"},"adv:v20-deep-sleeper":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Deep Sleeper","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When you sleep, it is very difficult for you to awaken. The difficulty of any roll to awaken during the day is increased by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HsAlaV8dhpAuxYz5"},"adv:v20-impatient":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Impatient","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have no patience for standing around and waiting. You want to do things <em>now</em> — fuck those slowpokes trying to hold you back. Every time you are forced to wait around instead of acting, a Self-Control roll is required to see if you go tearing off on your own instead.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ubAwCyjJQKisJAF8"},"adv:v20-shy":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Shy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are distinctly ill at ease when dealing with people and try to avoid social situations whenever possible. Difficulties for all rolls involving social interaction with strangers are increased by two. If the character becomes the center of attention in a large group, difficulties are increased by three.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fE62twldkSRPSxVK"},"adv:v20-soft-hearted":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Soft-Hearted","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot stand to watch others suffer. You avoid any situation that involves causing someone physical or emotional pain, unless you make a Willpower roll (difficulty 8). You must have a Humanity rating of 7 or above to take this Flaw — vampires on Paths of Enlightenment can only take this Flaw with Storyteller approval.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BD0GMJ6n2Nd5SyRe"},"adv:v20-speech-impediment":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Speech Impediment","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a stammer or other speech impediment that hampers verbal communication. The difficulties of all die rolls involving verbal communication are increased by two. This Flaw must be roleplayed whenever possible.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lSgvRTfEOmFLrK2X"},"adv:v20-unconvinced":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Unconvinced","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You fail to see the need for the core ideologies of your Sect or Clan, and have gone on record as saying so. Taking your stand has made you suspect in the eyes of your superiors, and may have attracted the attention of your enemies as well.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"485"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QLA4rZN3OAcThV9J"},"adv:v20-lunacy":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Lunacy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are affected by the phases of the moon, increasing your chances to frenzy. Under the crescent moon, difficulties to avoid frenzy increase by one. Under the half or gibbous moon, difficulties rise by two. When the moon is full, difficulties increase by three.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aGlEByF5t5XDcu4Z"},"adv:v20-short-fuse":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Short Fuse","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are easily angered. Difficulties to avoid frenzy are two greater. Brujah vampires cannot take this Flaw, as they already suffer from a similar weakness.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.I7tn1U81DNvCGJY4"},"adv:v20-stereotype":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Stereotype","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You buy heavily into all of the vampire stories you’ve read and heard. You wear a cape or body glitter, speak with an accent, and otherwise act in a cartoonish fashion. Such behavior is embarrassing in the extreme to other Kindred, who are likely to ostracize or mock you (+2 difficulty to Social rolls with other vampires who don’t share your habits). You also stand out to hunters.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.tGxO2H9jqjwYqBlu"},"adv:v20-territorial":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Territorial","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are extremely territorial, staking out a particular area as your hunting ground and reacting aggressively to trespassers. If another vampire enters your territory uninvited, you must make a frenzy roll. If you fail, you immediately attack the interloper and continue attacking until the intruder is dead or has left your hunting grounds. You are reluctant to leave your territory except in desperate circumstances.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LoEwGz4GEjY9SQjf"},"adv:v20-thirst-for-innocence":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Thirst for Innocence","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The sight of innocence — of any sort — arouses in you a terrible bloodlust. Roll Self-Control or Instincts, or else frenzy and attack the source of your hunger.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AnnDFNecQyAFu452"},"adv:v20-victim-of-the-masquerade":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Victim of the Masquerade","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Camarilla’s propaganda machine did too good a job on you. Even after your Embrace you refused to believe you were a vampire. You remain convinced that there is some logical explanation for your condition, and spend as much time as you can searching for it. You also have problems feeding, and may insist on trying to eat regular food. None of these habits makes you particularly pleasant company for other Kindred. This Flaw must be roleplayed at all times, and is generally taken by Camarilla vampires.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sw1qWzYbiQumoGJI"},"adv:v20-conspicuous-consumption":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Conspicuous Consumption","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>It is not enough for you to draw nourishment from the blood of mortals — you believe you must also consume your victim’s heart, liver, and other blood-rich tissue. Of course, this will necessitate the deaths of all of your victims (unless you are extremely creative), which might lead to numerous problems with maintaining Humanity (and a low profile). Characters with this Flaw should additionally purchase the Eat Food Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LeGzTgf59xDPpmbB"},"adv:v20-guilt-wracked":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Guilt-Wracked","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You simply cannot come to grips with the fact that you must drink blood to survive. You suffer horrible guilt over each time you feed (roll Conscience, difficulty 7, or else frenzy every time you feed — characters with the Conviction Virtue cannot take this Flaw) and try to avoid doing so as much as possible. This means that you rarely have much blood in your system, leaving you vulnerable to both attacks and hunger-based frenzies.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"486"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pxsQVsJUcFKVckrX"},"adv:v20-flashbacks":{"name":"Flaw: (••••••) Flashbacks","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You managed to make it through the Creation Rites or other similarly traumatic experience, but not wholly intact. The most insignificant thing can throw you into a different mood or state of mind, and as such your behavior is extremely unpredictable. Because of your precarious emotional state, your Willpower fluctuates. At the beginning of each story, make a Willpower roll (you may not spend Willpower for an automatic success). If you succeed, you may participate in the story as normal. If you fail, however, your Willpower score is considered to be 1 for the duration of that session, and you only have one Willpower point to spend. You may roll again at the beginning of the next session to see if you regain your Willpower.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"487"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DTsTtKTQVRjDYABu"},"adv:v20-elysium-regular":{"name":"• Elysium Regular","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You spend an unusual amount of time in Elysium. You see and are seen to such an extent that all of the movers and shakers of Elysium at least know who you are. Extended time spent in Elysium also gives you extended opportunities to interact with the Harpies and other Kindred of that stature — and they’ll know your name when you approach them. This Merit is generally taken by vampires that respect and attend Elysium on a regular basis.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"487"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.tm8qu4Y9ESgxZUTA"},"adv:v20-former-ghoul":{"name":"• Former Ghoul","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You were introduced to the Blood long before you were made Kindred. Your long experience as a ghoul gives you insight into and comfort with vampiric society. You are at -1 difficulty on all Social rolls when in the presence of other neonates (particularly those who haven’t been educated by their sires), and have a -1 difficulty on all rolls relating to vampiric knowledge.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"487"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3KhSMHkL7vlDlKYn"},"adv:v20-harmless":{"name":"• Harmless","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Everyone in the city knows you, and knows that you’re no threat to their plans. While that sort of estimation may seem insulting, it’s also what’s kept you from being killed. No one considers you worth their time to deal with, and that low opinion keeps you safe. If you start acting in a way that demonstrates that you are no longer harmless, others’ reactions to you will likely change as a result.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"487"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hlmEH4gqDdHXof9V"},"adv:v20-natural-leader":{"name":"• Natural Leader","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are gifted with a certain magnetism to which others naturally defer. You receive two extra dice when making Leadership rolls. You must have a Charisma rating of 3 or greater to purchase this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"487"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.q4gApgowdoHqU4Zc"},"adv:v20-prestigious-sire":{"name":"• Prestigious Sire","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your sire has or had great status in her Sect or Clan, and this has accorded you a certain amount of prestige. Though your sire may no longer have any dealings with you, the simple fact of your ancestry has marked you forever. This prestige might aid you greatly in dealings with other vampires, or it might engender jealousy or contempt.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OobZVSfABVUkmVZ2"},"adv:v20-prot-g":{"name":"• Protégé","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your sire watched you for some time before Embracing you, and spoke glowingly of you to acquaintances. These vampires may be inclined to look favorably on you by dint of your sire’s recommendation; you are at -1 difficulty on Social rolls with all those who’ve heard good things about you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2l4sCKDEW4na4fZJ"},"adv:v20-rep":{"name":"• Rep","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your fame has exceeded the bounds of your Sect. Everyone knows who you are, what you’ve done and what you’re supposed to have done (which might not be the same thing). The publicity can be good or bad; what matters is that everybody knows your name. Whether individuals outside of your immediate social circle know enough to match your face to your name is a different matter.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FzwTgVqR6pm7jsf3"},"adv:v20-sabbat-survivor":{"name":"• Sabbat Survivor","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve lived through at least one Sabbat attack or attempted recruitment. Your experience helps you anticipate situations where you might potentially be endangered by the Sabbat once again. You are at -1 difficulty on all Perception rolls when it comes to Sabbat-based matters. This Merit is generally taken by groups in conflict with the Sabbat, and comes into play most frequently as a means of avoiding ambushes.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kltmaAxehJjUDfmY"},"adv:v20-bullyboy":{"name":"•• Bullyboy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re part of the brute squad the local Sheriff or Bishop calls on when he needs some muscle. As a result, you get in on action that others miss entirely, score points with those in power, and occasionally get a chance to act outside of the law. How far outside the law you can go depends on circumstance and how much the vampire you report to likes you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3lqFBSo09aPZp49v"},"adv:v20-old-pal":{"name":"•• Old Pal","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>An acquaintance from your breathing days was Embraced at the same time you were. Fortunately, your friendship has endured even death and unlife, and you find a constant source of support and aid in your old friend. She expects the same of you, which isn’t always convenient, but at least you each have someone to hang onto who remembers the good old nights — and days. The Storyteller should play the Old Pal as a very loyal Ally.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hdzCWCcHzp46eMvw"},"adv:v20-lawmans-friend":{"name":"•• Lawman’s Friend","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>For whatever reason (maybe your winning smile or perhaps just your superb groveling technique), the local Sheriff or Bishop in charge of discipline likes you. He’s inclined to overlook your minor trespasses and let you in on things you’re not supposed to know about. He even gives you warnings about occasional crackdowns and times when the higher-ups aren’t feeling generous. Of course, abusing this connection might well turn a friendly vampire into an enemy — and the change might not be apparent until it’s too late.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6D7XEoAsHEGrh3fA"},"adv:v20-open-road":{"name":"•• Open Road","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Unlike many Kindred, you like to travel. You have a solid knowledge of safe travel routes and methodologies, not to mention haven space available in any number of destinations. Unless someone out there knows your exact route and is specifically looking for you, you can move between cities unimpeded by random encounters with Lupines, overzealous state troopers, and the like.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5hk2gKLeKid1oyWZ"},"adv:v20-sanctity":{"name":"•• Sanctity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Merit is sometimes called the halo effect; everyone considers you pure and innocent, though not necessarily naïve. You have a saint-like quality that is hard to pinpoint but cannot be denied. You are trusted, even if you are not trustworthy. At the Storyteller’s discretion, you tend to receive lesser punishments for wrongdoing, and you are liked by most.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"488"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AciGpoBixGK5NVTw"},"adv:v20-scholar-of-enemies":{"name":"•• Scholar of Enemies","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have taken the time to learn about and specialize in one particular enemy of your Sect. You are aware of at least some of the group’s customs, strategies, abilities, and long-term goals, and can put that knowledge to good use. This Merit is worth a -2 difficulty for all non-combat rolls pertaining specifically to the subject of your specialization. On the other hand, you are at a +1 difficulty when it comes to dealing with other enemies, simply because you’re so thoroughly focused on your field.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IyusSPIHKl7KTxc8"},"adv:v20-scholar-of-others":{"name":"•• Scholar of Others","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Merit functions identically to Scholar of Enemies, except that it applies to a group that is not necessarily inimical to your Sect.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BrEniw4fTCmyMm7i"},"adv:v20-friend-of-the-underground":{"name":"••• Friend of the Underground","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While you’re not a Nosferatu, you know your way around the sewers, tunnels, ducts, subway tubes, and other subterranean passages of your hometown. The local Nosferatu (and any other creatures dwelling down in the muck) may not actually like you, but they’re not inclined to kill you on sight when they see you in their territory. You are at -1 difficulty on any rolls involving the subterranean world (sneaking from place to place underground, finding routes into sub-basements, and so on). Nosferatu cannot purchase this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FPRPfBCurmyfK4AH"},"adv:v20-mole":{"name":"••• Mole","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have an informer buried in one of your Sect’s enemy organizations who funnels you all sorts of information as to what her peers are up to. What you do with the information is up to you, but abusing the knowledge might be a good way to get your informer killed. The other side has spies too….</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.01rv94u46AGyI1a7"},"adv:v20-rising-star":{"name":"••• Rising Star","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re one of the up-and-comers in the city, a rising star in your Sect. Everyone wants to know you and be your friend, even as those in power groom you for positions of greater responsibility. You are at -1 difficulty on all Social rolls against any vampires in your Sect who aren’t actively opposing your ascent.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fDTHw8JFJXKC3AwC"},"adv:v20-broken-bond":{"name":"•••• Broken Bond","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Sabbat vampires may not take this Merit.</p><p>You were once blood-bound but have secretly slipped the leash, and you are free to act as you will once more. Your regnant has no idea that you are not in fact bound, and continues to treat you as if you were. At Storyteller discretion, the experience of having been bound once may render you immune to ever being enthralled again. Sabbat vampires cannot take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DyPFBFyyLNSmpOkE"},"adv:v20-clan-friendship":{"name":"•••• Clan Friendship","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>One particular Clan (not your own) has a special liking for you. You might have done the Clan as a whole a favor at some point, or perhaps you’re just a loud voice in support of their aims. Whatever the case, you’re at -2 difficulty on all friendly Social rolls involving members of the Clan in question. Of course, the reaction your cozy relationship with another Clan is likely to draw from your own Clan leaders is an entirely different can of worms.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.U1xzwHs7u9PlEgEw"},"adv:v20-primogen-bishop-friendship":{"name":"•••• Primogen/Bishop Friendship","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The ruling vampires of the city value you and your opinions. You are called in to consult on decisions, and your recommendations carry great weight. Your position may not be an official one, but it’s powerful nonetheless.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xkqzNrUtSC6Dl58n"},"adv:v20-botched-presentation":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Botched Presentation","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When your sire presented you to the Prince of the city, you flubbed it. Now you’re convinced His Majesty hates you (whether he does or not). You need to succeed on a Willpower roll (difficulty 7) just to stand in front of the Prince or one of his duly authorized representatives without running, blubbering, or otherwise making a fool of yourself. This Flaw can only be taken by Camarilla vampires.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.iqBMw9Q7BDDgs3zS"},"adv:v20-expendable":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Expendable","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Someone in power doesn’t want you around. Maybe she wants territory you possess, or is jealous of the attention you’re getting from a prize mortal retainer — the details are irrelevant. What does matter is that she has the power to maneuver you into dangerous situations “for the good of the Sect,” and has no compunctions about doing so.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"489"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.zHYQPCtLifu8XvC3"},"adv:v20-incomplete-understanding":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Incomplete Understanding","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The whole matter has been explained to you, but you’re still not quite sure how things in your Sect work. Your imperfect understanding of the rules and regulations of your new existence means that sooner or later, you’re going to make a mistake. It’s only a matter of time….</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NpqG8a3wwsO4kZLG"},"adv:v20-infamous-sire":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Infamous Sire","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your sire was, and perhaps still is, distrusted and disliked by many of the city’s Kindred. As a result, you are distrusted and disliked as well.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.weGMfWTNFtOj91X3"},"adv:v20-mistaken-identity":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Mistaken Identity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You look similar to descriptions of another Kindred, which cause cases of mistaken identity. This can prompt numerous awkward or even dangerous situations, especially if your “twin” has a terrible reputation or is wanted for some crime.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Fa42wS9FcA8g41nA"},"adv:v20-new-arrival":{"name":"Flaw: (•) New Arrival","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve just arrived in your new city of residence, and don’t know anyone in the place. Existing factions may try to recruit or eliminate you, while vampires in positions of authority size you up and take your measure. Meanwhile, your ignorance of the city’s current events, history, and politics (not to mention the personality quirks of the vampires already in place) may cause you to make a serious blunder.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.PKj3tNS9zx20efYy"},"adv:v20-new-kid":{"name":"Flaw: (•) New Kid","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re the latest in the city to be Embraced or have yet to prove yourself to your Sect, and everyone knows it. That automatically puts you at the bottom of the social totem pole. Other neonates take every opportunity to demonstrate your inferiority, or rival packs constantly test your worthiness to the Sabbat. Even if someone else is added to the ranks of the unliving, you’re still regarded as something as a bit of a geek by your peers — a distinction that can have dangerous consequences if bullets start flying. All Social-related rolls are at +1 difficulty when you are dealing with other neonates. (Ancillae and elders lump you in with all the other neonates under their general disdain.)</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZAGkPCrFGcEHa4B1"},"adv:v20-recruitment-target":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Recruitment Target","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Someone in one of your Sect’s enemy organizations wants you, and they want you <em>bad</em>. Every effort is being made to recruit you, willing or no, and the press gangs usually show up at the worst possible time.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.PBWqMPx3BqaFmLcI"},"adv:v20-sires-resentment":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Sire’s Resentment","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your sire dislikes you and wishes you ill. Given the smallest opportunity, she will actively seek to do you harm. Your sire’s allies also work against you, and many elders may resent you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.dx3zbAvxAfSf9POB"},"adv:v20-special-responsibility":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Special Responsibility","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Shortly after your Embrace, you volunteered for some task in order to gain respect and approval from the vampires around you. Now, you wish you had never opened your damn mouth! While you are not given any special credit for performing this duty, you would lose a lot of respect if you were to stop. The nature and the details of your duty should be worked out with the Storyteller in advance. Ideas can range from lending money to other Kindred to acting as a messenger or possibly gathering victims for ritae (such as Blood Feasts).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.zTPOXZVmko09mmF1"},"adv:v20-sympathizer":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Sympathizer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have publicly expressed sympathy for some of the goals and policies of the enemies of your Sect. Your outspoken views on the subject have made you suspect in the eyes of the city’s hierarchy, and you may be suspected of (or arrested for) treason.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9H6cx8TFBRazGTFX"},"adv:v20-bound":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Bound","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Sabbat vampires may not take this Flaw.</p><p>You are blood bound to another vampire. Your regnant may not necessarily treat you badly, but the fact remains that your will is not entirely your own. The knowledge gnaws at you, even as you find yourself lost in devotion to your vampiric master. Sabbat vampires cannot take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.rGyOQ3bus3G7qFeL"},"adv:v20-catspaw":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Catspaw","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve done dirty work for someone high up in the city’s hierarchy in the past — the Sheriff, the Bishop, or even someone higher. However, instead of granting you favor, your deeds have made you an embarrassment or a liability. For the moment, your former employer’s concern is to keep you quiet. In the long term, it’s to get rid of you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"490"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.eFJ7YZLyOqVVkmwJ"},"adv:v20-escaped-target":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Escaped Target","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You had targeted a mortal for the Embrace, but someone else got there first. You cannot stand the humiliation of being cheated of your prize, and fly into a rage (+2 difficulty to avoid frenzy) whenever you see the one who got away. This hatred may lead you into other irrational behaviors, like Embracing enemies of the neonate, creating unauthorized childer, or even trying to kill your rival. Furthermore, your petty and irrational behavior is well-known and quite noticeable, and as a result you are at + 1 difficulty on all Charisma rolls until the situation is resolved.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"491"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.J0TcYkBpfYfYqpTC"},"adv:v20-failure":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Failure","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You once held a title in the city, but failed catastrophically in your duties. Now you are branded incompetent, excluded from circles of power and responsibility, and generally ostracized by those on their way up. Your exclusion may make you a target for recruitment by the Sect’s enemies (or so the whispers run, making you even more distrusted). Conversely, the consequences of your error might come back to haunt you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"491"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KSM5bnch134cscMz"},"adv:v20-masquerade-breaker":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Masquerade Breaker","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In your first nights as a vampire, you accidentally broke the Masquerade — and were spotted doing so. Someone else covered for your mistake, but holds the favor over you. Now you exist in fear that your error will be revealed. In the meantime, your “savior” takes pitiless advantage of you. This Flaw can only be taken by vampires in Sects or Clans that respect the Masquerade.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"491"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XWkG5ON1yxUUIM9Q"},"adv:v20-hunted":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Hunted","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are pursued by a fanatical witch-hunter who believes (perhaps correctly) that you are a danger to humanity. All those with whom you associate, be they mortal or Kindred, may be hunted as well.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"491"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.0PjpfzEml7Xh3kjS"},"adv:v20-old-flame":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Old Flame","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Someone you once cared deeply for is now with the enemy. He still attempts to play on your sympathies “for old times’ sake” while working against you. Unless you succeed on a contested Manipulation + Expression roll against your former friend, you cannot act against him unless the situation becomes life-threatening.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"491"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CZdbwijJySRuQ41X"},"adv:v20-rival-sires":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Rival Sires","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The flip side of Escaped Target, two vampires wanted to gift you with the Embrace. One succeeded, one failed — and she’s not happy about that failure. You, your actual sire, or both of you have become the target of the failed suitor’s ire. Regardless, your persecutor is at +2 difficulty to refrain from frenzy in your presence. In addition, she may well be working actively to discredit or destroy you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"491"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pyxb76xKUFfSRJwT"},"adv:v20-uppity":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Uppity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are proud of your new status in the Sect — so proud that you’ve shot your mouth off to other Kindred and made some enemies. Wiser vampires laugh at you and chalk your rudeness up to youth, but others find you arrogant and insulting. These enemies will take action to embarrass or harm you. Furthermore, you are at +2 difficulty on all Social rolls against any vampires you have alienated through your yammering — and you may not know who they are.</p><p>At Storyteller discretion, you may also be required to make a Willpower roll (difficulty 6) to keep your mouth shut any time the opportunity presents itself for you to brag about your pack, your Clan, or your status.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8BKcjcnPzgZyg9HE"},"adv:v20-disgrace-to-the-blood":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Disgrace to the Blood","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your sire regards the fact that he Embraced you to be a titanic mistake, and has let everyone know it. You are mocked at gatherings, taunted by your peers, and actively despised by the one who should be giving you guidance. Any request or petition you make is likely to be looked down upon by friends of your sire, and your achievements are likely to be discounted.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bFeyuWE9Sy4vWHVx"},"adv:v20-former-prince":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Former Prince","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Once, you held near-absolute power in a city, but those nights are gone now. Perhaps you stepped down, perhaps you were deposed, or perhaps your city fell to the Sabbat; it matters little in your reduced state. What does matter is that the Prince in the city where you now dwell is aware of your prior employment, and has concerns that you might be trying to make a comeback. The machinery of the Camarilla in the city where you now make your home is subtly stacked against you, and if the Prince sees an opportunity to get rid of you he just might take it. This Flaw can only be taken by Camarilla vampires.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.usCmKukRB8iIgi1r"},"adv:v20-hunted-like-a-dog":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Hunted Like a Dog","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Another Sect or group of vampires has decided that you’re a target for extermination, and pursues you relentlessly. On the bright side, the enemies of your enemy may well wish to help you out, potentially garnering you allies.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.neLeKLseQ1RCNYtZ"},"adv:v20-narc":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Narc","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are known to be a snitch, an informer firmly planted in the pocket of the vampires in charge. Those on whom you might yet inform loathe you as a result, feeding you misinformation when they can in an attempt to discredit you. Given the opportunity, they might do you mischief. Regardless, your reputation as a weasel precedes you, putting you at +1 difficulty on all Social rolls against those who don’t agree with your politics.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8TIVU2jgNQq4Yy5l"},"adv:v20-sleeping-with-the-enemy":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Sleeping With the Enemy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have some sort of intimate connection with a member of an opposing Sect or inimical Clan. You may have a lover, a childe, a friend, or a contact working the other side of the fence, but regardless of politics you retain a friendly (or more than friendly) relationship with your putative foe. Your close ties to someone on the other side would be regarded as treason by your superiors within the Sect, and if you are discovered, the penalty will surely be death.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vCeEO8nuGXR48Xfb"},"adv:v20-clan-enmity":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Clan Enmity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>One Clan in particular wants you dead. You have offended the entire Clan, from elders to neonates, and as a result every member of that bloodline wants your head on a plate. The effects of the Flaw may manifest as anything from very public snubs and insults to actual attempts on your unlife. You are also at +2 difficulty on all Social rolls relating to members of the Clan in question.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sBihC2tWJzBvp2q5"},"adv:v20-loathsome-regnant":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Loathsome Regnant","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Sabbat vampires may not take this Flaw.</p><p>Not only are you blood bound, but you are also in thrall to a vampire who mistreats you hideously. Perhaps you are publicly abused or humiliated; perhaps your master forces you to commit unspeakable acts for him. In any case, existence under the bond is a neverending nightmare, with your regnant serving to conduct the symphony of malice. Sabbat vampires cannot take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.nh41GC0JV6xxRs3e"},"adv:v20-overextended":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Overextended","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve got your fingers in too many pies, and people are starting to notice. You have too many ghouls, too many retainers, or too much influence, which means that a lot of people have a vested interest in trimming back your operations. These enemies take every opportunity to reduce your power and influence, and if that means lying, cheating, or killing, so be it. Furthermore, your enemies block every attempt you make to move into new areas of control. You’re boxed in, and the box is getting smaller.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"492"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.jWVHnEnNGydeuNTi"},"adv:v20-probationary-sect-member":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Probationary Sect Member","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are a defector. You turned traitor to the Camarilla, Sabbat, or other Sect, and you still have much to prove before you are accepted by the Kindred you have defected to. Other vampires treat you with distrust and even hostility, and your reputation might even sully those whom you regularly associate with.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hsbeLJAxy949jdnN"},"adv:v20-blood-hunted":{"name":"Flaw: Blood Hunted","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have been made the target of a blood hunt, and for you to return to your home city is death. For four points, this Flaw means that only your home city is offlimits to you. For six, it means that the entire Camarilla is howling for your vitae. This Flaw can only be taken by Camarilla vampires.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Wjix6AD2UfNzYCvZ"},"adv:v20-laughingstock":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Laughingstock","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Somehow you’ve drawn the scorn of the local Harpies, who make you their favorite target. You are at a +2 difficulty on all Social rolls in Elysium and a +1 anywhere else in the city. In addition, you are at +2 difficulty to use Intimidation or any Dominate powers on anyone who has heard the stories mocking you. This Flaw can only be taken by Camarilla vampires.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QStyqtc9R2xZvTHV"},"adv:v20-red-list":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••••) Red List","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are either being considered for or are already on the dreaded Red List, the registry of those vampires the Camarilla most wants extinguished. Any Camarilla vampire will either attack you on sight or, more likely, call in for a great deal of help.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vU4P0fyB93u8yw1t"},"adv:v20-deceptive-aura":{"name":"• Deceptive Aura","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your aura is unnaturally bright and colorful for a vampire. You register as a mortal on all attempts to read your aura.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.22K3GnvlWB6FFZak"},"adv:v20-healing-touch":{"name":"• Healing Touch","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Normally vampires can only seal the wounds they inflict from feeding by licking them. With but a touch, you can achieve the same effect, closing the puncture wounds left by drinking blood.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9TrK5hvdEhsAjow3"},"adv:v20-hidden-diablerie":{"name":"••• Hidden Diablerie","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The tell-tale black streaks of diablerie do not manifest in your aura.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wP8ieQxJ7QI1G5kw"},"adv:v20-lucky":{"name":"••• Lucky","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You were born lucky — or else the Devil looks after his own. Either way, you may repeat any three failed rolls per story, including botches, but you may try only once per failed roll.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Y02SGOfjICQWhaLk"},"adv:v20-spirit-mentor":{"name":"••• Spirit Mentor","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a ghostly companion and guide. The identity and exact powers of this spirit are up to the Storyteller, but it can be called upon in difficult situations for help and guidance.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Q3LwmxUSSAYGWJhb"},"adv:v20-true-love":{"name":"•••• True Love","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have discovered, perhaps too late, a true love. He or she is mortal, but is the center of your existence, and inspires you to keep going in a world of darkness and despair. Whenever you suffer, the thought of your true love gives you the strength to persevere. This Merit grants you one automatic success on all Willpower rolls, which can be negated only by a botch die. This can be a great gift but also a hindrance, for your true love may require protection and occasionally rescue.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"493"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.EML1g7amPuM24dUw"},"adv:v20-additional-discipline":{"name":"••••• Additional Discipline","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can take one additional Discipline (Storyteller discretion) as if it were a Clan Discipline. All costs to learn that Discipline are paid out as if it were native to your Clan. A character cannot take this Merit more than once, and Caitiff vampires cannot take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fsFMBvfPFr4g4U1x"},"adv:v20-nine-lives":{"name":"•••••• Nine Lives","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Fate has granted you the opportunity to come as close to Final Death as anyone can get and still survive. When a roll occurs that would result in your death, the roll is made again. If the next roll succeeds, then you live — and one of your nine lives is used up. If that subsequent roll fails, then another reroll is made, until either a successful roll occurs or your nine lives are used up. The Storyteller should keep careful count of how many lives the character has remaining.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5VJzH5Dg4sJHg3VH"},"adv:v20-true-faith":{"name":"••••••• True Faith","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a deep-seated faith in and love for God, or whatever name you choose to call the Almighty. You begin the game with one point of True Faith (see the sidebar on p. 372); this Trait adds one die per point to all Willpower and Virtue rolls. You must have a Humanity of 9 or higher to choose this Merit, and if you lose even a single point, all your Faith points are lost and may be regained only when the lost Humanity is recovered. Individuals with True Faith are capable of performing magical acts akin to miracles, but the exact nature of those acts are up to the Storyteller.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kct0zfjxSgsORSfR"},"adv:v20-cast-no-reflection":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Cast No Reflection","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You actually cast no reflection, just like the vampires of legend. This can have a detrimental effect when trying to pass as a human. Vampires of Clan Lasombra automatically have this Flaw (and you may be mistaken for one of them if you possess this).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VhzXpdbPt2DrkaA1"},"adv:v20-cold-breeze":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Cold Breeze","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>A chill wind follows you everywhere you go. While it may make for dramatic entrances, this effect also discomfits mortals (+1 difficulty on all appropriate Social rolls) and marks you as obviously supernatural. Cold winds sweeping through executive offices or crowded nightclubs can raise all sorts of questions.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9nQ12R81v15ZZ4pV"},"adv:v20-touch-of-frost":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Touch of Frost","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Plants wither as you approach and die at your touch. Your touch leeches heat from living beings, as though you were made of ice.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.dkj5cSzzvYMMseKG"},"adv:v20-cursed":{"name":"Flaw: Cursed","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are the recipient of a supernatural curse. The strength and pervasiveness of the curse depend upon how many points you wish to incur. Examples follow:</p><p>• If you pass on a secret you were entrusted with,</p><p>your betrayal will come back to harm you in some way. (1 pt.)</p><p>• You stutter uncontrollably when you try to describe</p><p>what you have seen or heard. (2 pts.)</p><p>• Tools break or malfunction when you try to use</p><p>them. (3 pts.)</p><p>• You are doomed to make enemies of those whom</p><p>you most love or admire. (4 pts.)</p><p>• Every one of your accomplishments or triumphs</p><p>will eventually become soiled or fail in some way. (5 pts.)</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9lL8yFtFGBIVugh2"},"adv:v20-deathsight":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Deathsight","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Everything appears rotted and decayed to you. The world appears to you as a corpse; mortals look diseased or skeletal, buildings seem decrepit, and your fellow Kindred seem to be walking, moldering cadavers. You are at -2 difficulty to resist all rolls based on Appearance, but by the same token you are at +2 difficulty on all Perception-based rolls. In addition, you find social interaction difficult and are at +1 difficulty on all Social-based rolls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"494"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fdBUYwarUanpOJ6w"},"adv:v20-eerie-presence":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Eerie Presence","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Mortals have an unconscious awareness of your undead nature, which makes then anxious and ill at ease in your presence. Because of this, difficulties of all rolls relating to social interaction with mortals are increased by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"495"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9Ecjeta1MK4hQchi"},"adv:v20-lord-of-the-flies":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Lord of the Flies","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Buzzing harbingers of decay swirl around you everywhere. Their constant presence makes it difficult for you to interact socially (+1 difficulty when appropriate) and nearly impossible to sneak up on someone or hide effectively. The buzzing of the flies inevitably gives you away — all Stealth rolls are at +2 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"495"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ppRRgo1evCRC1gQD"},"adv:v20-haunted":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Haunted (V20)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are haunted by an angry and tormented spirit, most likely one of your first victims. This spirit actively attempts to hinder you, especially when feeding, and does its utmost to vent its anguish upon you and anyone in your presence. The Storyteller determines the exact nature of the spirit, its powers, and whether or not it can eventually be laid to rest.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"495"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.p7E5cBl7eN3Hnm7a"},"adv:v20-grip-of-the-damned":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Grip of the Damned","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>There is no ecstasy in your Embrace — only terror and pain. Mortals upon whom you feed struggle and shriek while you attempt to feed, requiring you to grapple with them for as long as you wish to take their blood. For vampires with high Humanity, this experience may require a Humanity roll, at the discretion of the Storyteller. Giovanni cannot take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"495"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VCguRg8RQi8GgWwW"},"adv:v20-dark-fate":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Dark Fate","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are doomed to experience Final Death or, worse, suffer eternal agony. No matter what you do, you cannot avoid this terrible fate. At some point during the chronicle, your Dark Fate will come upon you. Even more ghastly is the fact that you occasionally have visions of this fate, and the malaise these images inspire requires an expenditure of a temporary Willpower point to avoid, or else you lose a die from all of your actions for the remainder of the night. It is up to the Storyteller to determine the exact nature of this fate, and when it will occur. This is a difficult Flaw to roleplay; ironically, though it may seem as though it removes all free will, the knowledge of one’s death can be quite liberating.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"495"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.mIH9OiFlBG4LdH9W"},"adv:v20-light-sensitive":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Light-Sensitive","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are even more sensitive to sunlight than other vampires are. Sunlight causes double normal damage, and the light of the moon can cause lethal damage in a manner similar to the sun, though it must shine directly upon you. Even bright lights hurt your eyes, requiring the use of sunglasses. Followers of Set and related bloodlines cannot take this Flaw, as they already have a worse version of it.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Vampire: The Masquerade 20th Anniversary Edition","page":"495"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Q5xDYIz9ANqC40RO"},"adv:v20-black-sheep":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Black Sheep","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You belong to a prestigious lineage that is considered to be paragon of another sect, or at least it did before you joined the Anarch Movement. You have embarrassed your sire, grandsire, and perhaps even farther back up the line; now the time has come to pay. Your erstwhile broodmates want to bring you back into the fold, if only to clean the stain from your reputation, and are unwilling to recognize your independence. Your sire and grandsire spare no effort to make things uncomfortable for you and any Anarchs who shelter you. This flaw combines the effects of Sire’s Resentment and Hunted Like a Dog, so those flaws may not be taken in conjunction with Black Sheep. (For more information on those Flaws, see p. 490 and 492 of V20, respectively.) In addition, many members of the Anarch Movement actively distrust you solely based on your lineage, believing that you are a double-agent. All social rolls involving your fellow Anarchs incur a +2 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Anarchs Unbound","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.mh4QCQhlbSMLmtFV"},"adv:v20-expiration-date":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Expiration Date","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your personal motto is simple: I do what I want. This has, as might be expected, caused you trouble more than once. You are wanted in several domains for various minor crimes, such as exsanguinating the Seneschal’s favorite ghoul. You haven’t been bloodhunted just yet, but the writing is on the wall. You’ve just about run out of time and no one wants you to take them with you when everything turns sideways. One more mistake and you sign your Final Death warrant. All social-based rolls to acquire help are at +2 difficulty, except those involving Intimidation.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Anarchs Unbound","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gVEeE5jimdY05NVS"},"adv:v20-peacemaker":{"name":"•• Peacemaker","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a reputation for having a good head on your shoulders and the honor to keep your word no matter what. As a result, your allies and sectmates ask you to mediate their disputes, even with other sects. This Merit allows your character to use her reputation as leverage to keep the peace during tense situations. Reduce the difficulty of all social rolls to keep the peace or to mediate honestly between factions (even other sects) by 2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Anarchs Unbound","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7cLLBNxlJLnztNgA"},"adv:v20-prized-patch":{"name":"•• Prized Patch","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You belong to an Anarch gang with a violent, effective, or otherwise impressive reputation. The history of the gang might extend a hundred years before you were born, but so long as you hold membership and wear the colors of your crew, other Anarchs naturally tend to respect you. Some may even occasionally perform minor tasks for you to try to curry favor with the others. When your membership is known, reduce the difficulty of all Manipulation rolls with other Anarchs by 2 unless a given Anarch has a historical animosity with your gang.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Anarchs Unbound","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ck3PYFQFJEug4vqF"},"adv:v20-soapbox":{"name":"••• Soapbox","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have some sort of special forum (a zine, a secure blog, a well-known podcast, or a social media account with a lot of followers) that allows your Anarch to express an opinion and have said opinion spread widely. The Soapbox Merit represents a social delivery mechanism that can influence Kindred outside of your character’s normal social circle or class. Reduce the difficulty of Expression and Subterfuge rolls by 2 difficulty when dealing with vampires who read the Soapbox.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Anarchs Unbound","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xrvyn6s0UY3m75Cc"},"adv:v20-sugar-daddy":{"name":"••• Sugar Daddy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a personal relationship with a high-ranking member of a different sect. You may invoke your Sugar Daddy’s name to lower the difficulty of Manipulation rolls by 2 against members of that sect when attempting to smooth out problems or acquire minor favors. Of course, the Sugar Daddy may expect similar treatment in kind, and is unlikely to look favorably on any behavior that impugns whatever status he may have.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Anarchs Unbound","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VPxSclY7KqEi4tQA"},"adv:v20-unbroken":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Unbroken","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Banu Haqim may take this Flaw.</p><p>For whatever reason, the Tremere curse was stronger in you than in most of your clanmates. This was no great liability while all of you were so afflicted. However, when ur-Shulgi cast the Breaking, the great ritual’s power did not fully cleanse you. You now share your caste’s thirst for Cainite blood — but for you, the very substance you crave is still a poison. The Tremere curse that once affected your clan has been weakened in you, but it is not completely gone. When you drink non-Assamite Cainite vitae, each blood point enters your blood pool normally. However, on the way down, it inflicts one automatic, unsoakable level of lethal damage in a combination of unnatural toxic shock and internal acid burns. On the plus side, all your difficulties to resist the warrior caste’s addiction are at –1 difficulty — think of it as aversion therapy. If you are a sorcerer or vizier, this Flaw is worth one additional point (for a total of 4), but you gain the warrior caste’s addiction in addition to your own caste’s weakness.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Assamite (Revised)","page":"77"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IwpSwjhGp1nETn73"},"adv:v20-compassionate":{"name":"•••• Compassionate","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Brujah may take this Merit.</p><p>You have the moral character of the Brujah of old, the proud warrior-poets who did what they did because their hearts called to them. If you ever fail a Conscience (but not Conviction) roll, you may spend a point of Willpower and attempt the roll again, at a difficulty of 1 higher than the last. If you succeed on this roll, it counts as if you succeeded on the first. You may do this only once per Conscience roll; you must accept the results of the re-roll. If you botch this second roll, you lose one permanent point of Willpower in addition to any consequences of the failed Conscience roll.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Brujah (Revised)","page":"68"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CAfawPyQuDUIKYTx"},"adv:v20-uncontrollable":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Uncontrollable","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Brujah may take this Flaw.</p><p>Rage and passion constantly war in the soul of a volatile Brujah. Perhaps you were ill tempered before the Embrace, or perhaps your Brujah lineage awakened some latent fury. In any case, even more so than your clanmates, you are prone to frenzy. Difficulties to resist frenzy are always 10 for this character. Prepare for a short, hellish ride.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Brujah (Revised)","page":"69"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.u8KbdCVF0osP2xMy"},"adv:v20-proxy-kissed":{"name":"•••• Proxy Kissed","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Merit.</p><p>Before the Embrace, you received the traditional Giovanni Proxy Kiss. This means you have one free dot of Potence, and the other members of your clan tend to be comfortable that you were Embraced in “the proper Giovanni fashion.” On the downside, you are partially blood bound to someone — probably someone other than your sire. Given the way the anziani like to balance things out, you may well have been Embraced by someone who dislikes or disagrees with your domitor. You may take this Merit a second time (for a total of 8 points) to gain a free dot of Fortitude, indicating a very long service as a ghoul. If you do this, it means that you’re: (a) partially bound to three Kindred, (b) onethird bound to one and two-thirds bound to another, or (c) fully bound to the Kindred who Embraced you, who will probably be in deep shit with the elders — if she isn’t an elder herself.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Giovanni (Revised)","page":"77"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xdLYrz19BIXC5mT5"},"adv:v20-weather-sense":{"name":"• Weather Sense","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>This acumen is very much prized among seafaring Lasombra. A few sires manage to pass on its intricacies to their childer. You subconsciously sense impending bad weather. The Storyteller makes a Perception + Survival roll (difficulty 7) on your behalf to give up to several hours' notice of storms and other weather problems before they manifest.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Lasombra (Revised)","page":"63"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XLTpAIJF5kThvAD3"},"adv:v20-pelagic-compulsion":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Pelagic Compulsion","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>You become increasingly agitated when on land. Raise the difficulty of all Willpower rolls made when you've been away from the sea for more than 24 hours by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Lasombra (Revised)","page":"64"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.rDIudlmpAzMktgwB"},"adv:v20-pelagic-harmony":{"name":"••• Pelagic Harmony","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>Being close to the sea calms you and reinforces your self-control. All Willpower rolls made while you're on or within sight of the sea have their difficulties lowered by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Lasombra (Revised)","page":"64"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1nUyGRrT65e35aHb"},"adv:v20-poseidons-call":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Poseidon’s Call","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your self-control varies with the weather. Make rolls to resist frenzy at -1 difficulty in completely calm weather, but +1 difficulty on rough seas, +2 in thunderstorms and +3 in hurricanes.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Lasombra (Revised)","page":"64"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NCG14OG7WEK1GLnV"},"adv:v20-deaths-reflection":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Death’s Reflection","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>You cast a reflection normally, which would cancel out the clan weakness — except that your reflection always shows the state you'd be in if you were dead. Any Lasombra more than a few decades old looks in reflection like an ambling skeleton. Younger vampires appear as rotting corpses.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Lasombra (Revised)","page":"65"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.s9bJOJUleqX8z5j7"},"adv:v20-benevolent-blood":{"name":"• Benevolent Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>Your blood still carries the Curse of Malkav, but its effects have been lessened just a little bit. Any ghouls you create suffer none of the usual deranging side effects of drinking Malkavian blood — they can be loaded to the gills with your blood and not come one step nearer to gaining a derangement. (They might still be driven insane by life with you, though, depending on how demanding your reality is.) Of course, any childer you Embrace will still gain a derangement after the Embrace as usual — although your vitae is easily diluted by mortal blood, the pure stuff carries the Curse as one would expect.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Malkavian (Revised)","page":"64"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5Y1wGuNJ1OSeRWnp"},"adv:v20-immaculate-aura":{"name":"• Immaculate Aura","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>Whether because of your iron control or some fluke of chance, your aura does not give away your insanity. The aura doesn’t shift or swirl at all, even when you’re confused, frenzied or in a psychotic fit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Malkavian (Revised)","page":"64"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.grTFMBYu9EHFEtZT"},"adv:v20-deadened-nerves":{"name":"•••• Deadened Nerves","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>Whether it was a condition you held in life or an odd side effect of the Embrace, your nervous system is missing a few connections. You have very little tactile sense, whether pleasure or pain. The downside of this is obvious: One of your senses is greatly impaired, which can keep you from noticing important warnings (a blade at your back, for instance — or in it). You suffer a +3 difficulty to all tactile-related Perception rolls, and the Storyteller may call for a roll to notice even the blatantly obvious; you might not even notice that you’ve been shot if the bullet doesn’t knock you down outright. However, your deadened nerves also protect you from pain, allowing you to ignore your wounds until your flesh is literally blasted from your bones. All penalties for wound levels are halved, rounding down; in other words, you suffer no penalties until you reach the Wounded level, where you deduct only one die from your dice pools, and even when Crippled you can still act at a mere two-die penalty. If the Storyteller is willing, it might be particularly rewarding for the Storyteller to keep track of the character’s health levels, and not let the player know exactly how badly his character has been wounded. Even if the Malkavian stops to give herself a quick look-over, the Storyteller puts things in the most general terms (i.e., “There’s a number of holes in your chest, but you have no idea whether the bullets are lodged inside or not,” “Your left arm refuses to move, although you’re not sure why,” and so on). This is a fair amount of extra work on the Storyteller’s behalf (particularly if in the interest of secrecy, the Storyteller makes all the Malkavian’s soak rolls in secret), but can add a lot of tension and verisimilitude to the game.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Malkavian (Revised)","page":"65"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5rBEKRXr6NerywmZ"},"adv:v20-disembodied-mentor":{"name":"••••• Disembodied Mentor","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>The voices in your head may tell you things, but by God, they’re useful things. You have a personal guide and advisor (bought as usual through the Background: Mentor) who exists largely in your own skull. He may have been a Malkavian who uploaded himself into the Network, or perhaps he’s an imaginary construct with access to the shared memories of the clan. Either way, it’s exceedingly hard for your enemies to cut you off from your mentor’s counsel, and it’s usually pretty easy to call on his advice when you need it. Unfortunately, this Merit also has its drawbacks; your mentor can find you whenever he chooses, and can be a real distraction when you’re trying to do something he finds irrelevant. You’re not freed from the obligations of your relationship, either; you find yourself running errands for your mentor just as often as any other pupil would, if not more so.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Malkavian (Revised)","page":"65"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7AX1gjta2q6Yvedk"},"adv:v20-sympathetic-bond":{"name":"••••• Sympathetic Bond","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>For whatever reason, you unconsciously cause a peculiar supernatural form of feedback through the links of the blood bond. Although you’re not immune to being blood bound (and cannot take the Merit: Unbondable), if you do become bound to someone, your regnant also becomes blood bound to you to an equal extent. Even if she was already blood bound to another, she now has the unenviable position of being regnant to two vampires at once. This can obviously lead to some unplanned and quite twisted codependent relationships.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Malkavian (Revised)","page":"66"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Akrm44DwMtuu1gJp"},"adv:v20-oversized-fangs":{"name":"• Oversized Fangs","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You have enormous fangs, snaggly tusks resembling those of an elephant or walrus. They cannot be retracted, but they do an additional die of damage and add one die to your Intimidation dice pool.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"70"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pssoprLOngC6ziYj"},"adv:v20-gaping-maw":{"name":"•• Gaping Maw","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You’ve got a nasty gash where your mouth should be. It can smile, it can frown, and most importantly, it can grin two or three inches wider than any human mouth can. An ordinary vampire can suck up to three blood points in a turn. You can drain up to four points a turn, provided you can latch your tooth-filled orifice around enough skin.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"71"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.A9vZpw030PqgvGGd"},"adv:v20-projectile-vomiting":{"name":"•• Projectile Vomiting","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>This talent is like the Eat Food Merit, but twice as versatile. Food comes in; food goes out very, very fast. A vampire with this ability can ingest, and possibly even taste, food and drink. He cannot gain any nutritional benefit from this ordinarily digestible matter, but he can store it for later use. When this need arises, the Nosferatu can not only disgorge his stored supply of food, but also aim it with a degree of precision. For the record, projectile vomiting in the Storyteller system usually requires a Stamina + Athletics roll; the difficulty is 8, and a victim can attempt to dodge this bolus of ejected victuals. Although this attack does not cause damage (save to one’s pride), the type of food ejected may temporarily obscure a victim’s vision, cause him to slip or merely force him to weep with shame at a Camarilla Toreador’s grand ball. Pity the poor vomitdrenched Toreador....</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"71"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TAWafTtUrGqYSRtC"},"adv:v20-reptile-buddy":{"name":"••• Reptile Buddy","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Yes, those legends about albino alligators in the sewers really are true. You’ve been nursing a few of them with your vitae in the local spawning pool for years. Your careful breeding and vigilant training has produced a reptilian slave of exceptional intelligence. It has a mind as sharp as that as a five-year-old child and teeth as keen as butcher knives. The beast understands your native language perfectly and can even follow complex directions. Faster and deadlier than any human ghoul, it is a highly efficient killing machine fully capable of patrolling your domain with ruthless efficiency. Reptile buddies also love to play “fetch” with human limbs (whether attached or severed). Ghouled Reptile Buddy — Physical: Strength 6, Dexterity 2, Stamina 6. Social: Charisma 0, Manipulation 0, Appearance 0. Mental: Perception 3, Intelligence 1, Wits 3. Disciplines: Fortitude 2, Potence 2. Blood Pool 5, Willpower 5. Attacks: Bite (7 dice), Tail Slap (6 dice).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"71"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.4tdrtxbiWI9Aog6O"},"adv:v20-blunt-fangs":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Blunt Fangs","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your teeth are huge and square, not sharp like those of other vampires. To do damage with a bite attack, you must score an additional success (thus, this extra success subtracts from the amount of damage you do). Once you have sunk you teeth into your prey, you inflict a level of damage for every two blood points you take. Once your fangs are locked in a victim’s flesh, you’ve got to chew and chew and chew....</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"72"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.T46cWefsbKt7uE2X"},"adv:v20-infamous-autarkis":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Infamous Autarkis","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>The Camarilla won’t accept you as one of its own under any circumstance. The Sabbat wouldn’t think of submitting you to Creation Rites — it’s just not worth it. Something in your past, your reputation or your sire’s background is so abhorrent that both sects reject you utterly. You aren’t just autarkis; your infamy spreads throughout both sects of vampiric society. Although you might find a coterie that’s willing to work with you, they would not dare take you to any gathering of Camarilla or Sabbat vampires for fear of damaging their own reputations. The Storyteller may allow you to buy off this Flaw but only after you’ve completed a story in which you’ve resolved and overcome this social stigma.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"72"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.YEEaynzmeM80bfiR"},"adv:v20-necrophile":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Necrophile","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>No, you don’t have sex with the dead, but you certainly enjoy their company. You are obsessed with dead bodies and “invite” them over to your domain. Your haven is distastefully decorated with severed and mutilated body parts of all kinds. You talk to your dead friends, dance with them, make art out of them and entertain frequently. Some vampires of particularly refined temperament may need to overcome a Courage roll (difficulty 4) to enter a room where you’ve left your guests and their accouterments. Toreador go apeshit; Toreador antitribu applaud. For some reason, this Flaw is very popular among Leatherfaces.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"73"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hJ8dvIKlK0D0mHEL"},"adv:v20-withered-leg":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Withered Leg","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>For whatever reason, one of your legs does not work as well as the other. You subtract three dice when attempting any action that involves movement, and you move at half normal speed.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"73"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.nt1vjEJ9kvOF5EXZ"},"adv:v20-traitor":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Traitor","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>Oh, you’re a bastard, all right, and if the other Nosferatu find out about this, they’ll kill you on sight. You’ve been leaving information (through a designated drop point) about your alleged allies. This might involve regular e-mails, messages hidden at the same spot or a package you drop off for a courier. You must betray secrets about your allies, usually the members of your own coterie, every game session. At the end of the session, you must tell the Storyteller what you’ve done; if you haven’t been enough of a bastard, one of your secrets winds up on the local information network.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Nosferatu (Revised)","page":"74"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZEjP6gPiI2rM3X5c"},"adv:v20-attuned-taste":{"name":"•• Attuned Taste","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>For whatever reason, your character finds blood magic instinctive — more so than for most Tremere. When your Tremere tastes blood, she naturally tastes the subtle currents and occult correspondences in the vitae. To her, it’s not even magic; it’s just a heightened state of taste stemming from the Embrace and the Tremere clan’s long experience with blood. When your character tastes blood, she may automatically glean one fact about the source, as per the Level One Path of Blood power, A Taste for Blood (Vampire: The Masquerade, pp. 178-179). No blood cost or roll is required; the vampire simply garners information as if with one success. You may still choose to use the Discipline itself in order to gain more specific information, in which case the normal systems apply and the results of the Discipline use supersede this affinity. This affinity is not always beneficial — your character may accidentally taste undercurrents of fear, extraordinary power or poison in vitae, and such tastes can cause nausea or incapacitation at the Storyteller’s discretion. This sensitivity cannot be turned off.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Tremere (Revised)","page":"66"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LnpO7A6CGkDalPsk"},"adv:v20-double-betrayer":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Double Betrayer","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>At some point in the past, your character undertook the Vaulderie. She may have legitimately tried to join the Sabbat, or perhaps she didn’t know any better or was compelled. Regardless, she now bears the mark of the Betrayer. She has since been redeemed and welcomed back into the clan’s ranks, but the mark will haunt her always. As usual with the mark, all Tremere can tell that your character betrayed the clan once. Those who know of your character’s past will treat her with contempt; add two to the social difficulties for interacting with other Tremere, so long as they’re loyal to the clan. Tremere who don’t know the circumstances will probably assume that your character is a Sabbat traitor, and might try to capture or destroy her, or at least attempt to inform the pyramid of her whereabouts. Should your character ever fail in her reports or relapse in bad behavior, you can expect that she will be hunted down just like the Tremere do with other threats.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Clanbook: Tremere (Revised)","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OTD4J6wLiCG4fNyI"},"adv:v20-bad-liar":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Bad Liar","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Regardless of how well you know the arts of subterfuge or how clever you think you are, some unconscious tell-tale sign gives you away when you are lying: a subtle quaver in your voice, a subtle facial tic, a darkly gleeful look in your eyes or something similar. All attempts to lie, regardless of the circumstances, audience or goal, are made at +2 to the difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Counsel of Primogen (Revised)","page":"105"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.w9zrMV2gxrRv3DY6"},"adv:v20-demonic-patron":{"name":"••••• Demonic Patron","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your actions drew the attention of a demonic entity. Maybe you deliberately sought it as a means to gain power, or perhaps it wheedled its way into your existence and now won’t let go. The entity became a patron of yours, offering guidance when sought and assistance against your foes. One night it will turn on you and demand something. You will be unable to refuse its request. Until that time, your patron counts as three dots in Mentor and two dots in Allies.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"108"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ygYsgEmEyNRfDwFC"},"adv:v20-rivalry":{"name":"Flaw: Rivalry","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Someone hates you so much that they cannot bear to leave you alone. The scale of this Flaw is similar to that of the Enemy Flaw (see above), but your Rival is not interested in seeing you dead. Rather, a Rival desires only to make your unlife as troublesome and hellish as possible. At lower point value, the Rival may also be a friend who arrives at precisely the worst moment to steal your thunder or make you embarrassed. At higher levels, your Rival loses sleep planning how to make your life miserable.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"424"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DyCvSn5StON1fREg"},"adv:v20-nameless":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Nameless","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You do not exist. Certainly, you still walk the earth and your friends and kin can still see and touch you, but all records of your existence have been destroyed. Not even your vampiric progeny can lay claim to your name. As far as anyone can prove, you never were. If you take this Flaw, you may not take any Social Merits that grant benefits based on reputation. At Storyteller’s discretion, you may also be barred from taking Backgrounds that require a social presence. Who or what have you made so angry that they have erased your existence from the face of this earth?</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"425"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.th6tTcEnX4MInjzI"},"adv:v20-outspoken-heretic":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Outspoken Heretic","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You hold a belief considered to be heretical by a major religious power, such as the Catholic Church. Unlike others who practice their heresies in secret, you are open about your beliefs. You must constantly face false accusations, harassment from others who fancy themselves true believers, and the denial of hospitality from steadfastly faithful stranger, or those who fear the religious authority. At the Storyteller’s discretion, this can impose a difficulty increase of up to 3 to applicable Social rolls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"425"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AJZ8s1uK8zvnA0Fz"},"adv:v20-initiate-of-the-road":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Initiate of the Road","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Dark Ages Roads.</p><p>Your life on the road to enlightenment has just begun. Though you fully grasp the tenets and precepts of your chosen Road, you have not yet put them to the test. This may be because you were recently Embraced or because you previously abandoned a former Road (in which case you may also consider taking the Apostate Flaw on page 425). With this comes a +1 difficulty in resisting frenzy and Rötschreck, and you lack an aura tied to your Road. However, you have an easier time changing Roads, if you wish to do so.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"426"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.H86ek1t1u7pC3D9r"},"adv:v20-cloaked-in-shadow":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Cloaked in Shadow","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Shadows cling to you constantly. These are not the ordinary shadows cast simply by existing; the darkness around you is thick and heavy, making it impossible for you to pass as anything other than something supernatural. In addition to this complication, the difficulty for Social rolls with other vampires (especially the Lasombra) increases by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"427"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HtdTV9PHtVRn2DPF"},"adv:v20-demon-hounded":{"name":"Flaw: Demon-Hounded","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>A demon has taken a shine to you. It comes at odd and inconvenient hours and offers you bribes of wealth and power. Some days, it asks for favors; on others, it offers them. What black schemes does hell have in mind for you that one of its servants would bother you so? What fell powers have you toyed with that you should not? At 1 point, the demon is little more than an imp, asking for minor favors or stealing small objects. At 3 points, the demon is your physical or magical equal, or is a smaller threat that is willing to also drag your close friends into this affair. At 5 points, the demon is significantly powerful, able to threaten your coterie, or a lesser demon willing to torment your friends, family, and acquaintances.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"427"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DQp7qPe09BiLR2wA"},"adv:v20-unlucky":{"name":"Flaw: Unlucky","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are spectacularly unfortunate. Disaster follows in your wake. You are magnetically attracted to misfortune. At 1 point, it rains whenever you forget your coat. At 3 points, it’s rare for you to recover from any failure gracefully. At 5 points, nearly everything you attempt ends catastrophically. A number of times per day equal to the point you have in this Flaw, the Storyteller may force you to reroll a successful roll and take the second result. Lucky and Unlucky are not mutually exclusive, but for simplicity’s sake, the same roll cannot be modified by both at once. That is, if a Lucky reroll is used, the Storyteller cannot force you to reroll the reroll.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"427"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gBb2GuSTDy6DOLLG"},"adv:v20-harbinger-of-the-abyss":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Harbinger of the Abyss","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Obtenebration (Oblivion) •+.</p><p>You have become the void: the endless black of night, an empty pool of darkness. Where you pass, shadows leap to join you and flames gutter and perish. When you are in the presence of mundane fire, you must make a reflexive Obtenebration roll against the soak difficulty of any nearby flame. A success extinguishes the fire instantly, casting the area into darkness. This Flaw also includes the effects of Touch of Frost (page 426) and Eerie Presence (page 427) and is therefore mutually exclusive with them. However, the clinging darkness around you provides one benefit (besides being an unusual way to extinguish unwanted fires): reduce the difficulty of all Intimidation rolls by two, to a minimum of 4. You must have at least one dot of Obtenebration to purchase this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"428"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gSN9SPYh37N5APYO"},"adv:v20-trophy-allegiance":{"name":"•••• Trophy Allegiance","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Alastors only.</p><p>Similar to the Clan Friendship Merit (pg. 489, V20) Trophy Allegiance opens doors and allows the character to be treated favorably by a specific Clan. The character has earned the Trophy from the Clan in question for taking down one of the Anathema. Word has spread throughout that Clan, that members should offer assistance and rewards to the character for their deed. While the Trophy itself has likely already been covered with specific rewards, this Social Merit reflects more of a general goodwill and offers the character -2 difficulty on all friendly Social rolls involving members of the Trophy Clan who are familiar with the Alastor’s deeds. Additionally, the character’s reputation may earn her the occasional small favor such as the use of a haven, a loaner car, access to information from Trophy Clan members.</p><p><em>Note:</em> This Merit is only available to Alastor characters who have killed an Anathema and earned a Trophy.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dread Names, Red List","page":"100"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HVwCjBHDIZ1aHnmG"},"adv:v20-trophy-arrogance":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Trophy Arrogance","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Alastors only.</p><p>The character has begun to wear out his welcome when it comes to making demands on the Trophy Clan. While the Clan has promised him any number of rewards, both monetary and supernatural, the new Alastor has been abusing the Trophy’s Clan hospitality and is taking advantage of them. Making demands of local Kindred resources, throwing the fallen Anathema back in the Clan’s face, or being overly arrogant about the fact that the Clan owes the character are just some of the ways an Alastor can demonstrate this Flaw.</p><p><em>Note:</em> This Flaw is only available to Alastor characters who have killed an Anathema. It is not available to those named Alastors by Justicars and Inner Circle.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dread Names, Red List","page":"100"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8JnxLwpBIvonSTIv"},"adv:v20-loyalty":{"name":"••••• Loyalty","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Though revenants are the tools of the Kindred, they aren’t known for their unshakable loyalty to a particular vampire or Clan. When a member of a revenant family does display such loyalty, it’s noteworthy. Word of that revenant’s actions will spread quickly among other members of the Clan or Sect the family serves. Most revenant families might welcome a patron vampire for one of their members. The family would likely be suspicious of placing their trust in a specific vampire or Clan, and would probably take steps to avoid becoming too dependent on a single domitor. The character can expect occasional favors from the vampire Clan that controls the revenant’s family. They may also be given considerable latitude to explore their own special interests, since the vampires “trust” that the character is furthering the Clan’s goals. Loyalty is also a good Merit to choose if the character is being groomed for the Embrace, especially if that revenant knows her future sire.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Ducheski, Rossellini, Kairouan Brotherhood, and Servants of Anushin-Rawan</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"126"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kkic5FENkOFS9os2"},"adv:v20-black-and-white":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Black and White","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The World of Darkness is complex, both morally and spiritually, but your character doesn’t see it that way. To her, every situation can be summed up as black or white. Your ghoul thinks simply, and believes her allies and enemies are either for or against her, hot or cold, good or evil, easy or impossible, stupid or genius. After being subjected to the blood bond, its influence can complicate your ghoul’s natural outlook on life. Under its influence, the blood bond may force your ghoul to view what your domitor says or does through a rosy, positive filter at all times. Alternatively, any character who does not agree or side with your master is, according to you, an enemy worthy of your contempt and deserves to be hurt. This Flaw is particularly prominent among the Giovanni, Assamite, Ravnos, and Ventrue Clans, along with the Followers of Set and many revenant families. A closed mindset can cost your character dearly as your perspective may result in your bad judgment, missed opportunities, and misunderstandings that might escalate. In social situations where your character’s perspective comes to bear, the difficulties of associated rolls increase by one.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VhMCDpB52o1AvZkX"},"adv:v20-fixation":{"name":"Flaw: Fixation","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Blood is exhilarating, but when the feeling wears off, you want another high, another “fix” to add to that heady rush. Your Fixation might involve substances designed to alter your brain’s chemistry and processing ability, or it could be all about your thrill-seeking, heart-thumping compulsion to live on the edge and get high off the adrenaline or dopamine naturally produced in your body. With this Flaw, you are forced to turn to outside activities or substances in your free time to get a fix that doesn’t come from the Blood. While the fulfillment of your Fixation may be temporary, you know that any satisfaction you feel is a pale imitation of the high you get from the Blood. The stronger your Fixation, the more likely you won’t be able to hide it from the other characters around you. Since this is a Flaw that cannot be “healed” by any physical, mental, or social means, your Fixation may cause unforeseen consequences for your character over time. To start, you’ll pick a type of Fixation your character is likely to have: chemical, psychological, or physical. Unlike an addiction to cocaine or drag racing, your overall Fixation is more generic than that. Essentially, you are deciding what category your fix falls in, and you’ll figure out how to satisfy it over the course of your chronicle. At your Storyteller’s discretion, you may make a stamina check during your downtime to avoid succumbing to your desires. The more dots you take in this Flaw, the more likely it is your Storyteller might require more checks or raise the difficulty. If your ghoul fails, you will feel a compulsion to get a fix, and will need to act on that. At two points of addiction, your character is still in control. Your ghoul may show up fog-headed, but she will attempt to carry out her domitor’s wishes to the best of her ability. At four points, your Fixation is so strong that you are more likely to forget a crucial errand, miss an appointment, or doze off spying on a rival. At six points, however, your ghoul is deep in the throes of terminal addiction. You may also decide, at your Storyteller’s discretion, that adding more points to this Flaw will cause greater and more violent consequences to the fulfillment of your desires. For example, a murderous revenant with a six-point Fixation might get a thrill from desecrating churches. As another, an independent ghoul who escaped from a brutal pack might get a rush from “curing” another ghoul of the blood bond. Failing to master your Fixation will likely be viewed unfavorably by most of your peers or your domitor, because your inability to control yourself is viewed as a weakness. Servants that run off and base jump off the top of a skyscraper at every opportunity are not as useful as the meek from a master’s point of view. Over time, a domitor might even make a troubled ghoul’s death look like an accident before replacing him. However, the stronger your Fixation is, the more chaotic and rebellious your ghoul will likely act as you take pleasure wherever you can find it. Thus, it is not uncommon to find independent ghouls or revenants with higher levels of Fixation, especially if they have a loose leash to satisfy their darker needs. You may attempt to deny your Fixation, but to do so requires you to spend a Willpower point to temporarily bypass it for a round. Then, you must roll to make a Frenzy check. Should you fail that roll, your Fixation will continue to build and may overwhelm you at the most inopportune time. In other words, a failure to master your Fixation doesn’t mean you’ll automatically fly off into a rage on your schedule. Your bitterness and resentment may boil beneath a thin veneer of control until you’re forced to wait too long at a store, you’re cut off at a traffic light, or another ghoul gets rewarded instead of you. With any dramatic failure on a Stamina check, your character’s Fixation will increase by two points. If your character has already attained the six-point version of this Flaw and rolls a dramatic failure on his Stamina check, the effects will be severe. You may overdose and die without immediate medical attention. Your car may have crashed and you’re now unconscious because you couldn’t maintain control of the wheel. To remove points in your Fixation Flaw, or to get rid of it entirely, your character must pledge and endure a program to remove that compulsion. Your Storyteller may allow the circumstances and requirements of your chronicle to shape your domitor’s reaction to your admitted weakness. Independent ghouls, Sabbat ghouls, and revenants may not have the ability to reduce the influence of this Flaw, nor may they be able to remove it without help due to the nature of their nightly activities. Please note that while Fixation is similar to the Addiction Flaw for vampires, the results of this Flaw are felt more acutely in a mortal body. A Fixation Flaw is likely to develop more often in ghouls than revenants, simply because revenants produce vitae naturally in their blood.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls, some revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"127"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7QdvqIggVKDNVwf6"},"adv:v20-duplicitous":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Duplicitous","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ghouls and revenants with this Flaw have an underhanded reputation based on their previous actions. In the past, you’ve probably lied, cheated, or played both sides against the middle during a deal that went south. The nature of your underhanded actions, as well as your intended target, has not been the same every time. A ghoul might, for example, go to her domitor and inform him of a transgression, real or imagined, and then suggest an appropriate punishment for her target. That same ghoul might turn around and fabricate stories that show how well-meaning she was to stand up for her fellow ghoul in front of her master. While you can be duplicitous as a character, when you take this Flaw, it impacts your relationships and dealings with other characters. You probably don’t seek to take the upper hand in every situation, but other characters expect you to. Your allies and enemies are on edge around you, because they’re not sure when and if you’ll ever treat them fairly. They may even backstab or try to undercut you, too, because they expect you to. The influence of the blood bond does impact most ghouls with this Flaw in subtle ways. While your domitor would know you are Duplicitous, you would not act out in a direct way against your master. Instead, you might backstab or double-cross your allies to curry favor and obtain rewards from him. Your domitor may also point you at a dangerous target, too, to use you as bait or to draw out his enemies, to taking advantage of your reputation.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"128"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CHw7bdrHOPKiLQSN"},"adv:v20-benevolent-domitor":{"name":"••• Benevolent Domitor","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Domitor Background.</p><p>For reasons unbeknownst to you, your domitor is fond of you. Perhaps your service has been exemplary, or you simply remind your master of a past love. Given the alien nature of a vampire’s mind, you may never know for sure, but you do not begrudge the benefits you’ve gained from your master’s feelings toward you. You feel safe in your master’s care, and do not dread spending night after night fulfilling your duties. Your domitor treats you with a measure of faux compassion, and does not casually endanger your life. Unlike the relationship between other masters and their ghouls, this Merit does not represent a temporary phase. You have already proven yourself to your master. Thus, your relationship is akin to a friendship between a great boss and his employee. Though this Merit shapes how your domitor treats you, it does not guarantee that you will never run afoul of your master. You may, at your Storyteller’s discretion, lose the right to keep this Merit should your actions necessitate such a decision over time.</p><p><em>Prerequisite:</em> Domitor Background</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Camarilla ghouls, some Sabbat ghouls</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"129"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.L9lNsZPR9KX1M7Jr"},"adv:v20-groomed-for-embrace":{"name":"••••• Groomed for Embrace","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Ghouls and revenants.</p><p>Your character has been deemed worthy of the Embrace, and is being prepared for the eventuality through careful, directed training by your future sire. While this Merit does not guarantee when you will be Embraced, your character is aware that you will become a vampire, and you know the identity of your future sire. The benefits of being Groomed for Embrace are related to your current role as a ghoul, and your future position as a vampire within a specific Clan or bloodline. As such, your role among the ghouls of your domitor’s house will be elevated to that of a valued apprentice as opposed to a disposable servant. Some Clans, like the Tremere or the Tzimisce, might track your progress through your academic study of lore, Disciplines, or thaumaturgical rituals. You might not be able to use Vicissitude, for example, but you may be permitted to assist your domitor at her laboratory table. Other vampires might be more careful, and may prepare you to be evaluated by the local prince before proceeding further. There may be a few drawbacks that your character might encounter over the course of your chronicle. Because you have achieved the dream of many ghouls, you may be subjected to fits of jealous rage or harm by other ghouls. Your domitor might even be aware of this fact, and may have encouraged these feelings to test you to see if you’re worthy of becoming a vampire. Additionally, should this fact become known, you may garner unwanted attention from your future sire’s enemies. Since it is most likely your future sire will also be your domitor, you may be kidnapped, spied upon, or pumped for information about your domitor’s haven, holdings, and schemes. However, many vampires do not reveal that their ghouls and revenants are being Groomed for Embrace, and keep their intentions secret. Should you decide to purchase the Groomed for Embrace Merit, you may purchase Benevolent Domitor at a cost of one point, provided you have taken the prerequisite Domitor Background. In this circumstance, you are signaling your Storyteller that your domitor has pledged to become your future sire. You may also, at your Storyteller’s discretion, learn one dot in a third Discipline known to your future sire or follow a Path after abandoning your Humanity.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Camarilla and Sabbat ghouls, as well as some revenants. Independent ghouls may be Embraced, but they would not be Groomed for Embrace due to their status as a pariah among ghouls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"129"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.unKANO4RS7idgVfy"},"adv:v20-isolated-upbringing":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Isolated Upbringing","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are a revenant who was raised by your family away from prying eyes. When you were growing up, your entire world was based on what your family taught you. For many years, your knowledge was limited to your family’s habits, customs, and politics. Consequently, you have a limited understanding of the outside world that impacts how you interact with other people. At your Storyteller’s discretion, whenever you are in an uncomfortable social situation outside of your family’s estates, subtract one die from your Social dice pool. This penalty does not apply evenly across all situations, because your interactions with the world will vary depending upon your particular family and your character concept. Revenants from the Obertus, Bratovich, and Ducheski families are good candidates for this Flaw, as are ghouls from the Giovanni Clan and the Kairouan Brotherhood. Independent ghouls, who have little to no memory of their life before they became a ghoul, may also choose this Flaw to reflect the fact that they were isolated from humanity and have forgotten how to interact with people.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"129"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aBn7uB8G3gl8WYXB"},"adv:v20-domitor-pariah":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Domitor Pariah","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your master is a vampire who, for whatever reason, is unwelcome among or even hated by other members of his Clan, bloodline, or Sect. Without allies, your domitor may struggle to retain her status and will likely rely on ghouls, like yourself, to fulfill her basic needs. You may be given additional responsibilities, or you might be manipulated into spying, attacking, defending, or stealing for your domitor. Should you take the Domitor Pariah as a Flaw, keep in mind that should other vampires discover whom you serve, your life might well be in danger. The events that led to your domitor’s downfall may be kept hidden from you, at your Storyteller’s discretion, or you might stumble upon your master’s secrets accidentally. Once a ghoul understands how challenging life may be with a Domitor Pariah, you might sow the seeds of rebellion despite the strength of your blood bond.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Camarilla ghouls, some Sabbat ghouls</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"130"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.u3HGkW5HQ2WmzR83"},"adv:v20-obsession":{"name":"Flaw: Obsession","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Even ghouls who lead relatively uneventful lives can become obsessed with a persistent idea, image, set of circumstances, or unshakable feeling. Many obsessions are so minor they would not be noticed by a casual observer, while others may become so powerful they’ll prevent a ghoul from performing the simplest of tasks. At one point, your ghoul’s Obsession is minor. For example, your character may prefer a particular brand of beverage, pair of jeans, etc., and may refuse to eat, wear, or use a replacement. Your minor Obsession will probably cause you to seek a replacement for an “inferior” item with your preferred one at the earliest opportunity. At two points, your Obsession threatens to consume you. If the focus of your insatiable desire is a person, you would likely have an image of that person in your possession at all times, or you will stop what you are doing to obtain one. If your Obsession involves an object or totem, like a lucky rabbit’s foot or a small statuette, you will need to have it within your reach. You might even grow more upset over time if it’s not nearby. At five points, your Obsession consumes you, and may bring others to harm. Your character might kidnap a local celebrity you’re obsessed with, and will likely go to unreasonable lengths to keep him under your direct control. Similarly, any object that you’re consumed with creates tremendous need at this level. If your lucky rabbit’s foot is misplaced or lost outright, you might not be able to function as you normally would, and may be prone to fits of destructive rage. Depending upon the severity of your Obsession, you could enter a near-catatonic state until the object is found or a suitable replacement is obtained. You would probably act in a similar fashion if the person you’re obsessed with is rescued or escapes. There may be times, however, when you cannot find a suitable replacement for an object or a person to satisfy your Obsession. In those instances, your character will need to succeed a Willpower check. The difficulty rating is your Willpower minus the number of dots in your Obsession Flaw to “snap out of it.” Should you fail, you may become so distressed that you will require medical attention.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"130"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.O8gKaXlwKP2rwumw"},"adv:v20-squeamish":{"name":"Flaw: Squeamish","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your ghoul or revenant might talk a good game, but you’ve never been around real violence before. When you’re confronted with the sight of blood, you probably won’t respond well, and may faint, panic, or run away. In situations that involve gunfire, bloody combat, or an aggressive use of Disciplines, roll Willpower (difficulty 7). If this roll fails, your character either freezes in place (if her Courage is higher than her Self-Control) or flees the area (if her Self-Control is higher than her Courage). At your Storyteller’s discretion, you may be forced to make a Willpower check once per relevant scene. Your character does not need to be directly involved in the fight; she may simply be a witness to a stabbing. When you accumulate five successes in a single session, no further rolls need be made for that night of game time. A gory show of violence might necessitate another roll, however, at your Storyteller’s discretion. Should you botch a Willpower check, your ghoul is so unnerved by the gruesomeness you’ve encountered you cannot act for the rest of that scene. Your ghoul is frozen from shock, and may soil herself or puke in response to your fear or revulsion.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"130"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DEJWBavcXeLxBIjJ"},"adv:v20-under-surveillance":{"name":"Flaw: Under Surveillance","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your ghoul’s activities are being monitored by members of law enforcement or government agencies. You might be Under Surveillance because you are suspect in a crime, or because you’ve been spotted associating with other criminals or suspects. The points you take in this Flaw represent the level of scrutiny your character is under. At two points, only local authorities are watching your ghoul’s activities. At four points, both city officials and state authorities are involved. At six points, local, state, and national law enforcement agents are coordinating efforts to analyze your movements, and they may share their concerns with foreign agencies should you travel outside the country. The effects of this Flaw vary, depending upon your circumstances, and you may not always be aware of them. For example, among Camarilla ghouls, any ghoul Under Surveillance will risk a Masquerade breach. Some vampires might decide that your character is too dangerous to live, and may take an opportunity to end your life at the hands of your associates or domitor. Other Kindred might take a different, more devious approach. They might use you as bait, create a body double, or plant false information. Generally speaking, Under Surveillance is less dangerous than the Fugitive Flaw, because your character is a person of interest as opposed to a wanted criminal. Thus, this Flaw, at your Storyteller’s discretion, may be reduced in points or regulated to a cold file with time and effort. Ghouls and revenants, however, might be given a limited number of opportunities to correct the situation before their domitors or families lose patience and turn on them. Independent ghouls might have a much harder time ditching nosy officers. Though their first instinct might be to run to the cops and confess, keep in mind that no agency is immune from the reach of a Clan, bloodline, or Sect. Thus, some independent ghouls who take this Flaw might suspect that their former domitor has engineered their situation, and they are now Under Surveillance as punishment for abandoning them.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"130"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.EwXEBrhTcD48CL7w"},"adv:v20-loose-lips":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Loose Lips","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ghouls are often privy to a great deal of sensitive information in the night-to-night dealings with their domitors, and some cannot resist the urge to divulge (or sell) these secrets. In general, ghouls and revenants who cannot keep secrets tend not to live very long. When you take the Loose Lips Flaw, you might be known as a gossip. In fact, this weakness may be the reason why any ghoul might become independent. It’s possible that the ghoul’s master felt that withholding feedings for the rest of his vassal’s pitiful life would be suitable punishment, and might still be keeping tabs on his former ghoul. Ghouls with this Flaw tend to not be trusted with jobs involving secrets, so their usefulness to higher-ranking vampires is limited. On the other hand, they make perfect tools for spreading false information, which can often be far more useful. Unfortunately, you may not know whether you’re spewing fact or fiction until it’s too late.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"131"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CmZc9b2ayCr1sdl8"},"adv:v20-blood-feud":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Blood Feud","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The hostility between revenant families such as the Grimaldi and Zantosa is well known throughout the Sabbat. Rivalries between the Brujah and the Ventrue occasionally bleed into the behavior of their ghouls throughout Camarilla-held cities. Fights between revenant families are all too common, and these feuds have been going on for so long that some family members may have forgotten how an argument first started. A Blood Feud might be a recent development, but more than likely a deadly rivalry has probably been smoldering for decades, if not centuries. Not all disagreements and rivalries between families or factions, however, are considered to be Blood Feuds. A Blood Feud is what occurs after a slight or injury has been escalated to impossible heights, and the initial cause of the argument can now only be rectified when both debts are settled, often violently. Other family members will even go so far as to swear a revenge-filled oath to harm the rival family at the first opportunity. Like the nature of the feud, the violence both families cause may range from slashing tires to slitting throats. While Blood Feud is particularly applicable to members of certain revenant families, this Flaw may also apply to rival Clans in a given city. Loyal ghouls might take offense at a misstep or take up vengeance for a slight against their Domitor, and their feelings could escalate into hatred and a desire to kill. A Gangrel ghoul might be involved in a bitter dispute over territory or domain with the servants of a Toreador Primogen. Enough betrayal and insults could be hurled over the course of the conflict that tense discussions erupt into bitter arguments. Usually, however, for a Blood Feud to exist there must be more than a simple rivalry between the two parties. In a Blood Feud, several members of each side should be willing to battle over the insult or slight. The hatred between the two sides has escalated over time, and calls for vengeance carry weight. When confronted with the possibility that your rival is nearby, your character must make a Frenzy check at a -2 difficulty. If successful, your character is able to control the blind fury caused by your thirst for revenge. If your character fails the check, you will feel compelled to act on your obsession for vengeance. You might seek out and confront your rival. You might attack someone who looks like her, or sabotage her car or attack her girlfriend. You might even rile up the other people around you to join the fight, too. How you act on your hatred is up to you, but a failure will guarantee that the Blood Feud is renewed. You may choose to spend a Willpower point to delay your moment of frenzy for one round. However, the longer you delay acting on your thirst for revenge, the greater the chances are that your rival will act upon hers.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Revenants who have a clear rivalry with another family, or by ghouls from warring Clans, bloodlines, or Sects. To take this Flaw, you must work with the Storyteller to name which family (or Clan) the Blood Feud is with, and how much the player character knows about its cause. You must also select a specific rival from the opposing side who is heavily involved with the feud or its cause.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"132"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MU03vXlOJgfToYhT"},"adv:v20-self-preservation":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Self-Preservation","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>For whatever reason, your character refuses to carry out orders that you feel will threaten your safety and put your life at risk. For ghouls, the Self-Preservation Flaw defeats the purpose of serving a vampire. If you cannot defend your domitor, then your worth decreases considerably. However, a Bound ghoul who can refuse to carry out an order bears close watching. This Flaw is also one of the most common reasons why a ghoul leaves his master’s service to operate as an independent. Revenants who have a sense of Self-Preservation may feel the effects of this Flaw in a different way. Your loyalty to your family may be questioned, for you might sacrifice another family member or ally when the time is right. Should you, too, be subjected to the blood bond, your inability to fully “commit” to a vampire’s orders may result in unforeseen consequences ranging from experimentation to further scrutiny. Mechanically-speaking, any time your domitor or master gives you an order that threatens your life, your character gets a check against Willpower at +2 difficulty. If successful, your ghoul or revenant may refuse to carry out the order. Should you refuse to carry out such a command in front of your master, you may expect to be punished severely. Clever ghouls and revenants will attempt to manipulate this Flaw to their advantage. They might attempt to carry out a life-threatening command while ensuring they do not come to harm, though this isn’t always possible.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"132"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.utnOE4AwF34EH5vY"},"adv:v20-turncoat":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Turncoat","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Turncoat is more than just a traitor. Revenants that take the Turncoat Flaw have betrayed more than the bonds of friendship, but also those of their familial connection and duty. Typically, a revenant that takes this Flaw has decided he is more interested in serving her family’s enemies, and has taken the requisite steps to do so. Most revenants with the Turncoat Flaw understand the ramifications of their actions. They understand that this Flaw requires them to protect themselves and hide from her former colleagues; they might even expect their families to hunt them down for their treachery. Depending on the nature and depth of the betrayal, allied vampires may decide to render their assistance to the hunt as well. A Turncoat revenant will have likely earned the trust of her new friends by killing a former ally, friend, or relative, or by turning over sensitive information, such as the haven of a (formerly) allied vampire or the passwords to access her family’s finances or accounts. Despite being protected by a powerful vampire, Clan, members of a local Sect, or revenant family, characters who take this Flaw know their problems are far from over. The Turncoat’s new allies will probably be skeptical of this character, and may not divulge anything other than minor details. After all, if the Turncoat betrayed her own family or a master while under the influence of the blood bond, she could do so again. In general, this Flaw is best used by characters who are embroiled in the politics of a larger organization. The ramifications of a revenant’s betrayal, such as a Grimaldi revenant divulging secrets pertaining to his family’s desire to leave the Sabbat, are often far-reaching and significant. Thus, independent ghouls may also select this Flaw when and if it makes sense for them to do so. However, an independent ghoul might struggle to find the allies required to help ensure their survival, since most vampires may seek a blood bond with the traitor.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Revenants, some independent ghouls</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"132"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DBos168bDj9YLY6f"},"adv:v20-fugitive":{"name":"Flaw: (••••••) Fugitive","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are wanted by the authorities. You may be a suspect in a case, or might have been charged and convicted with a serious crime. Your ghoul or revenant may have even gone so far as to escape a high security prison or mental health facility. You may have, on the other hand, run afoul of a Sect and are now being hunted by its members, whether they be Kindred, Cainite, Anarchs, etc. When you take the Fugitive Flaw, you are admitting that you have either committed (or have been framed for) a transgression that is so severe, agents who uphold the law, be they vampire or mortal, have ordered your capture. Any and all persons found in your company may immediately become suspect as well, and could be charged with additional crimes. A Fugitive is rarely hunted without good cause, and often those who seek to bring you in will have a personal reason for doing so. An officer might track you down because her negligence allowed you to escape. A rival ghoul, who managed to uncover how you were attempting to thwart your domitor, might want to prove his worth to your former domitor. A Sabbat pack may be hard on your heels, because they feel your escape is an embarrassment to them. These are all examples of characters who would probably pursue you at great personal cost in an attempt to recapture or even murder you. To the Camarilla, a ghoul or revenant Fugitive represents a serious threat to the Masquerade, regardless of who may be after you. While it is possible you may have been carrying out orders under the influence of the Blood Bond or Disciplines like Dominate, Kindred authorities cannot turn a blind eye to a desperate ghoul or revenant who’s on the run. To the Sabbat, on the other hand, a Fugitive may be hunted for other reasons, ranging from a sense of pride to a fear that you’ll run to the Camarilla and divulge secrets that must be kept buried. Though you are a Fugitive, you may have Allies, Contacts, or have clearly identified a place to hide for a time. Your domitor or revenant family, for example, might often you a place to stay for a few nights, but doing so puts them at risk. Safe houses may be arranged to ensure your survival, with the understanding that you are expected to curry favors or perform services that will help your benefactors. You may even be asked to follow a strict code of conduct to avoid exposing your accomplices.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and some revenants. This Flaw is particularly appropriate for independent ghouls who are on the run from their domitors.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"133"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.j6xQk52Q9alG6C91"},"adv:v20-problem-with-authority":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Problem with Authority","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Problem with Authority is unhealthy for ghouls or revenants who are expected to serve without question. It is especially problematic for ghouls who have violent or unpredictable masters, and may spur a ghoul to leave her domitor’s side. Many independent ghouls suffer from this Flaw, and continue to feel the effects of their disobedience. When taken to its extreme, your ghoul will become argumentative and unruly, and may take an issue with the simplest instructions. While you might argue with lesser authority figures in your ghoul’s life, like the police, your combative words are less likely to get you killed, but may result in being arrested or fired.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"133"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.epEhrApKbDqBxBuh"},"adv:v20-albino":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Albino","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Albinism occurs when a group of normally recessive genes become prominent, leading to a lack of pigmentation of the skin. While generally harmless, the appearance of an albino individual can be startling, particularly to others who are unfamiliar with the condition. The condition is marked by extremely pale skin and red irises. Albinism was viewed with great suspicion in most early cultures, such that even today people with albinism are shunned and ostracized, and considered to be an illomen in many parts of the world. While this condition is harmless from a physiological standpoint, it does cause afflicted individuals to stand out. This may reduce a ghoul’s capacity to blend into a crowd, without the help of a disguise. However, those who suffer from the Albino Flaw can also turn other people’s fear into a source of intimidation, particularly if your ghoul or revenant has a reputation that cannot be ignored. When you take the Albino Flaw, keep in mind that your skin is highly susceptible to sunburn, having little or no pigmentation to reflect the sun’s harmful UV rays.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"134"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aQosCwsr9Ainji4f"},"adv:v20-cannibal-ghoul":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Cannibal","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Though the Bratovich family of revenants is known for their depravity, the desire to consume human flesh is not limited to them alone. Historically, mortals have resorted to acts of cannibalism during times of war or starvation, but they have also eaten the flesh of the dead as part of a religious ceremony to honor the fallen, too. The vast majority of cultures, however, consider the practice barbaric and unnatural. To become a Cannibal, is to put your character at risk of ostracization and death. There are several physical disorders that can be transmitted through the consumption of infected flesh or organs. For the most part, such an infection is undetectable through normal, non-medical means. Should you eat human flesh as a ghoul, the vitae you’ve already ingested is likely strong enough to negate the effects in most cases. Should your ghoul be denied future feedings, however, the infection will take hold as your body returns to its natural state and age. A character with the Cannibal Flaw will generally only feed on the flesh of the living or the dead in privacy. Most cannibals are not rabid animals, and are cunning and highly intelligent. They are aware that modern society frowns on cannibalism, but they feel compelled to do it anyway. Thus, they are often careful to act on their Flaw in places and at times where they and their victims cannot be easily discovered. Missing corpses or well-loved people, for example, are likely to be searched for. If there are witnesses who spotted the cannibal and victim together, it might lead to further complications a Cannibal is not prepared to deal with. A Willpower roll may be called for to determine if the cannibal can exercise sufficient control and discretion to avoid leaving clues behind. The Cannibal Flaw may be selected by any ghoul, or may be bestowed upon a character by the Storyteller as a result of acquiring a Derangement. Though this Flaw is far more common among revenant families, it is not exclusively available to them. Because of the dramatic impact the Cannibal Flaw has on ghouls in modern society, Camarilla ghouls are discouraged from taking this Flaw.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Revenants, some Sabbat and independent ghouls</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.wo49eal1KP4fRMdk"},"adv:v20-hemophiliac":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Hemophiliac","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your character suffers from hemophilia. If your ghoul’s skin is cut, you will not stop bleeding without medical assistance. The Blood does not have any effect on a ghoul or revenant Hemophiliac. If your character suffers lethal or aggravated damage, he suffers an additional level of bashing damage every five minutes until his wound has been dressed to stop the bleeding. Any vampire who bites a Hemophiliac may “dress” such wounds. By licking the injury after biting the ghoul, the vampire will put a stop to any future bashing damage.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.WudgIrMkHWOeyi8z"},"adv:v20-seizures":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Seizures","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Seizures tend to occur during moments of duress, and few mortals have a more stressful or terrifying existence than ghouls. The affliction of the Seizures Flaw can also be more pronounced in revenant families whose members suffer from a limited genetic pool. Many types of seizures can be controlled or mitigated with medication following thorough medical tests or examinations. However, some ghouls and revenants aren’t willing to risk their lives by seeking the advice of a doctor, and these Seizures are often not related to a diagnosed illness. This Flaw is a side effect of the many horrors ghouls and revenants witness over the course of their lives. Seizures can potentially be disastrous, especially if they occur in the presence of a vampire or at an inopportune moment. Under any stressful circumstances related to combat, physical or verbal threats, or witnessing a terrifying, bloody sight the character with this Flaw will need to make a Frenzy roll. A success means a seizure was not triggered, and you’ve managed to regain control. Failure indicates your body was not able to cope with the stress and something has happened. If you roll a critical failure, your character suffers a full-blown grand mal seizure. You will have fallen to the ground and will thrash uncontrollably for a number of minutes equal to the number of 1s rolled on the Frenzy check. Your actions may wind up getting yourself, or others, hurt as your finger might accidentally pull the trigger, or you may slip and fall. A less than critical failure may likely result in your character blacking out until the next round. Depending on the needs of your story, your ghoul may pass out and drop to the ground, your vision might turn black for a few seconds, or your arm might tremble and you cannot regain control.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"136"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.52Slx0igB9JVHQsv"},"adv:v20-poisonous-blood":{"name":"••••• Poisonous Blood","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Due to mysterious circumstances, your character’s blood is poisonous to vampires. Should any vampire drink from you, every blood point imbibed causes one health level of bashing damage. Vampires naturally resent mortals known to possess this blessing and may well seek their destruction. Ghouls or revenants with this Merit may expect a similar reaction, since blood does not have any impact on Poisonous Blood. Other times, a scheming vampire might capture the character and use her as bait to physically harm a rival or enemy. This Merit is also recommended for independent ghouls or revenants who wish to remain free from vampiric influence. Storytellers are encouraged to have an explanation for Poisonous Blood in their chronicles, though the player (and likely character) may not be immediately privy to such knowledge. Some examples include: holy protection from a higher power, a rare ritual of protection, or a genetic abnormality.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"137"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.haB5af8awzktHHXP"},"adv:v20-artificially-aged":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Artificially Aged","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are an underage ghoul or revenant that has been fleshcrafted to look like an older, mature adult. Your true maturity is still far off in your future, but you appear to be a decade or two older than your actual age. As long as you remain a ghoul, your body will not age. Due to the terrors inflicted on your young body and mind, your character may never reconcile what has happened to you. Physically, you might pass for a much older person, but mentally and emotionally you are still a child. Thus, you will likely encounter challenges in most social situations, and your Storyteller may require you to subtract one or more dice from rolls involving your Social dice pools.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Like the Vicissitude Modification Merit, this Flaw may only be added at your Storyteller’s discretion.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"138"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BuSSQgnipEQfh0zu"},"adv:v20-finicky-palate":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Finicky Palate","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your ghoul must choose a Clan or bloodline, other than your domitor’s, whose blood causes you an allergic reaction when present in your bloodstream. Any time your ghoul ingests blood from a vampire of that selected Clan, either intentionally or forcibly, you must make a Stamina check to avoid vomiting it up. If you are successful, the blood remains in your system and all rolls involving Disciplines will be at +1 difficulty until either the offensive blood is burned off, or you consume new blood from a different Clan, whichever comes first. For most Camarilla ghouls, this Flaw represents little threat. In the unlikely event that a ghoul consumes the blood of a Kindred other than his domitor, it won’t take long for that ghoul to find the blood he does need. For an independent ghoul or a Clan-sponsored revenant family, on the other hand, this Flaw could represent a dire problem.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"138"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.A2nI7PFv2yLwXblO"},"adv:v20-offensive-to-animals":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Offensive to Animals","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Ghouls and revenants who take the Offensive to Animals Flaw will have difficulty in their encounters with animals. Dogs, wolves, bats, birds, and other creatures will act hostile toward the character whenever she’s around, and will likely attack the closer the character gets. Much like the vampiric Flaw of the same name, a ghoul or revenant who is Offensive to Animals may find it challenging to walk freely in certain areas. Animals will bark, howl, whine, and make noise when the character approaches them. They may not directly attack the ghoul unless provoked, but they will clearly be aggressive or, alternatively, might flee from the area. This Flaw is not recommended for ghouls or revenants who either plan on using the Animalism Discipline or come into contact with characters that do.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"138"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3lUS11lyMGVs3D2V"},"adv:v20-romantic-notions":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Romantic Notions","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You believe your entire existence as a ghoul is a marked improvement over your previous life, and you are convinced you owe it all to your domitor. You feel your domitor needs you, you’d die without her, and that every feeding is an act of pure love. When your domitor attempts to use either Dominate or Presence on your character, your Willpower is at -2. This Flaw is in effect regardless of how you react to the blood bond, and while under its influence you may feel an even stronger sense of artificial love toward your domitor. This Flaw does not affect revenants, who were born with their condition, in the same way as it does ghouls. At the Storyteller’s discretion, only a revenant who feels romantically inclined toward a specific vampire who has the use of Dominate and Presence may take this Flaw. Independent ghouls may not purchase this Flaw, as it does not make sense narratively for them to be romantically inclined toward a specific vampire.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls, except for independent ghouls, and revenants in rare cases.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"138"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xAizPCHT3CBMfSlF"},"adv:v20-virulent-strike":{"name":"••••••• Virulent Strike","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Through strange thaumaturgical sorcery, unknown rituals, or even an odd quirk of your physiology, your blood has been imbued with the power of the supernatural. You can cause Aggravated Damage to supernatural creatures by striking them, biting them, or raking them with bonecrafted spurs. This is considered standard Brawl Damage against mortals, and should normally apply to only one attack form (bite, bone claws, etc.). You should work with the Storyteller to find an explanation for this Merit, and the Storyteller may disallow this Merit entirely if she so chooses.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> All ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"138"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Dpj1n7HUvV11JcYD"},"adv:v20-vitae-sink":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Vitae Sink","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>For whatever reason, your ghoul or revenant metabolizes vitae more quickly than others do. You must be fed every two weeks, rather than once every month, or risk losing all supernatural Traits and will revert to your natural state. Few ghouls with this Flaw last beyond their natural life spans, unless their domitors are especially devoted to them. At this increased rate, however, it is likely that missed feedings might happen, which is why this Flaw is deadly to independent ghouls. Revenants who suffer from Vitae Sink replenish their natural vitae at half the rate they normally might.</p><p><em>Used by:</em> Most ghouls and revenants</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Ghouls & Revenants","page":"139"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gKoo7sgQGKdhsOJe"},"adv:v20-carrion-presence":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Carrion Presence","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/baali.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Baali may take this Flaw.</p><p>This affliction represents the Baali’s true connection to the decrepitude their bloodline commands. Pestilence is attracted to the Baali; flies leave their delicious carcasses to circle around the vampire, rats crawl along their limbs and squeak their praises, and vultures can be seen circling them at almost all times. The degree to which they are afflicted differs from character to character, but there is no getting rid of their swarm. Swatting their flies simply calls others to take their place. While quite disgusting to most, these vampires wear such a thing with pride. It does, however, make dealing in non-Baali circles quite difficult.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"16"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fDOVQPRg13rM7Jem"},"adv:v20-dark-aura":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Dark Aura","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/baali.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Baali may take this Flaw.</p><p>Even without the use of Auspex, the Baali seems somehow wrong to anyone they interact with, emitting a terrifying aura of sickness and power. The character is always at a slight disadvantage when dealing with other vampires or those touched by the divine, as they sense the depravity of the Baali’s soul. Baali with this Flaw suffer a +2 difficulty to all Social rolls, unless they are using Intimidation. To those who use Auspex to view the Baali’s aura, it appears as a thin, red mist surrounding the character with the occasional watchful eyes appearing within.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"16"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.cUeABACyZmNdLfNq"},"adv:v20-plague-of-demons":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Plague of Demons","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/baali.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Baali may take this Flaw.</p><p>No matter what, demons are constantly drawn to the Baali. Once in a while, this can be a benefit (Baali with this Flaw are at -1 difficult to summon a particular demon), but most of the time these nefarious spirits are distractions. They scream in the Baali’s ear, tug at her clothes, or screw with her possessions… and always at the worst possible moment. The character is at +1 difficulty for any rolls involving Perception. Further, once per session, the Storyteller can convert one of the Baali’s dice rolls into a botch.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"16"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MgERrDd4anIbvXW9"},"adv:v20-simply-waiting":{"name":"•••• Simply Waiting","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/baali.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Baali may take this Merit.</p><p>The Baali knows their place within the order of the universe, and where they stand against other vampires or those of their own bloodline. They also know the end of the world is coming, sooner rather than later. They appear apathetic to the world around them, but in truth, they are simply resigned to their fate and remain unmoved if others try to get them to stray from their path. All Social rolls against them are increased by +2 difficulty, as the Baali knows the true end of it all.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"16"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RbgZDBe9UUVFbxRl"},"adv:v20-the-high-price":{"name":"••• The High Price","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/baali.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Baali may take this Merit.</p><p>The burden of acquiring truth or power always comes at a high price. None know that more than these Baali, who sacrifice a part of their own soul in aspiration of greatness. Their physical body is wracked with weakness and pain, capping any Physical Attributes at four dots and costing 1 Willpower point every evening when they rise. In exchange for this tribute, the Baali receive +2 dice to any Discipline-related checks.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"16"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RpSqd52UFTP8QQ2m"},"adv:v20-banshee-in-waiting":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Banshee-in-Waiting","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/daughtersofcacophony.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Daughters of Cacophony may take this Flaw.</p><p>The Fugue is gradually driving you towards madness. If your temporary Willpower ever reaches 0, you automatically gain a derangement which can never be removed. You may remove other derangements you gain in other ways as usual. You may gain a maximum of three of these permanent derangements from losing your Willpower before being consumed by it. Should it happen a fourth time, you immediately lose yourself to the Fugue and become a character under the control of the Storyteller, walking in whatever direction the music takes you and killing anything you come across.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.z6rSY9Ez02bcSODy"},"adv:v20-chorus-trained":{"name":"Chorus Trained","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/daughtersofcacophony.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Daughters of Cacophony may take this Merit.</p><p>The character has learned how to use the Fugue to tune in to her sisters and work together with far more efficiency. For 3 points, this Merit grants the character 2 extra dice to any Melpominee dice pool when using the power with another character with this Merit. For 5 points, the bonus 2 dice can be added for any action being performed by a partner with this Merit, such as making art or even fighting together.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LqBv8kYIlAKv06zr"},"adv:v20-fugue-instinct":{"name":"••• Fugue Instinct","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/daughtersofcacophony.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Daughters of Cacophony may take this Merit.</p><p>You can hear warnings and gain insight by listening to the Fugue more deeply. You must forego one of your actions to listen properly to the Fugue within. During this time, you may defend yourself but take no active actions. If you do, then you gain an insight for your next action that grants you +2 dice to whatever dice pool you use.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KpV5JtKB0RIKiAcI"},"adv:v20-blood-weakness":{"name":"Flaw: Blood Weakness","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gargoyles.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gargoyles may take this Flaw.</p><p>The bloodline began as an experiment in manufacturing a Kindred from diverse bloodlines. They perfected the alchemical process, but somewhere along the way, a weakness in an ancestor caught hold. It manifested in your Embrace. In addition to the weaknesses you possess, you also possess either the Gangrel (V20, p. 55) or the Tzimisce weakness (V20, p. 71). For four points, choose one of the two weaknesses. For seven points, you possess both. Warrior Gargoyles with the Gangrel weakness version of Blood Weakness may gain both animal features and stone features from one frenzy. Additionally, choose one of the four Disciplines available to the Gargoyles. For you, this Discipline is considered out of Clan and costs the higher amount of experience points to advance.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"37"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.x5w5cwHLojuZ9zt8"},"adv:v20-heavy-hands":{"name":"••• Heavy Hands","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gargoyles.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gargoyles may take this Merit.</p><p>One of the effects of becoming a vampire is strange changes happening to your body. The obvious alterations to your hands have made them tougher, harder, and more impervious to pain. All difficulties for damage rolls using unarmed attacks go down by 1.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"37"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1XjVDlsVCHvJnRx6"},"adv:v20-stillness-of-death":{"name":"•• Stillness of Death","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gargoyles.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gargoyles may take this Merit.</p><p>Gargoyles often hide themselves as statuary. The difficulty for any searches to find you are increased by 2 when you stay perfectly still.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"37"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.EoiRGMpNejGjFIoD"},"adv:v20-stone-tongue":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Stone Tongue","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gargoyles.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gargoyles may take this Flaw.</p><p>The transformation of the Embrace didn’t just affect your appearance. It made it physically difficult for you to speak properly and clearly. It may be because of a clumsy tongue, some nasty looking tusks, or a raptor-type beak. No matter the cause, you suffer a +2 difficulty to any Social rolls requiring you to speak.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"37"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CtJdWkCs74wrkIrp"},"adv:v20-disciple-of-lazarus-japheth":{"name":"•• Disciple of Lazarus/Japheth","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/harbingersofskulls.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Harbingers of Skulls may take this Merit.</p><p>You speak for the powers-that-be within your bloodline, and bear the clean, white death mask of a respected Harbinger lost to one of the historic purges suffered by your people. Most Harbingers of Skulls will listen to what you say, and take your words at face value. This Merit adds two dice to any Social roll when you invoke the name of Lazarus or Japheth.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1FUzetKMuJXtzFNn"},"adv:v20-half-life":{"name":"•••••• Half-Life","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/harbingersofskulls.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Harbingers of Skulls may take this Merit.</p><p>You’re more than just a vampire; you’re a wraith in vampire form. Your awareness of the realities of death enables you to spend half the regular experience points cost for increasing Necromancy Paths after the first point, and permits you to move between Shadowlands and Skinlands through the expenditure of a Willpower point. You cannot be controlled as a normal spirit through use of Necromancy, but should other vampires discover what you are, you can expect to be hunted mercilessly. You suffer the same weaknesses and have the same strengths as a vampire, but if viewed with a power such as Aura Perception (V20, p. 135) the aura appears as a double-exposure, with a wavering, translucent humanoid shape merging in and out of yours.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qqvYCBd6CnY5UdIC"},"adv:v20-shadow-scarred":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Shadow Scarred","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/harbingersofskulls.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Harbingers of Skulls may take this Flaw.</p><p>Since escaping the Shadowlands, you’ve struggled to reacclimatize to the world of the living. You see ravenous Spectres in every shadow. You must roll your Courage (difficulty 7) or spend a point of Willpower whenever using Necromancy, as you fear the repercussions of touching the Underworld.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3DEPb1pohWyKywRA"},"adv:v20-styx-baptism":{"name":"••• Styx Baptism","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/harbingersofskulls.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Harbingers of Skulls may take this Merit.</p><p>You pledged fealty to the Harbingers of Skulls and swore to work against the machinations of Ashur, despite not being Embraced into the bloodline. You were escorted to the Shadowlands, where you were baptized head-first in the churning waters of the Styx. The flesh upon your head sloughed off with contact over the coming month, leaving just rotten patches or bare bone, reducing your Appearance Rating to 0 permanently. You’re now held in esteem by Lazarenes, gaining any one of the bloodline’s Disciplines as a Clan Discipline in place of one of your own.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hPpFIw0pGU22SDXO"},"adv:v20-unsanctioned-embrace":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Unsanctioned Embrace","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/harbingersofskulls.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Harbingers of Skulls may take this Flaw.</p><p>Lazarus decreed no Harbingers should Embrace, but your sire failed to listen. You’re hopelessly green and unworthy of the vitae in your veins. Until you perform acts befitting the nobility of your blood, your existence is shunned as a bad omen. Increase difficulties of all Social actions involving your bloodline by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DiH3wvqHT4xdrGzw"},"adv:v20-alien-perfection":{"name":"•• Alien Perfection","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Merit.</p><p>You possess beauty unsettling in its perfection. People stand in awe of your flawless form, while inexplicable nausea subconsciously creeps up. The difficulty of any Appearance roll is reduced by three. A Stamina roll is required by anyone in your presence for longer than one scene, if this is the first time they’ve encountered you. Failure drives them away from you with a sickness wracking their body.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"59"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ws5Grrf1RhgfFrIG"},"adv:v20-prized-collection":{"name":"Prized Collection","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Merit.</p><p>Kiasyd have appreciation for compilations of books, records, vintages of blood, and other collectable items among Cainites. You possess a collection that may not be worth much (1pt.) or could be worth a lot to the right buyer (2pt.). Importantly, it’s a point of interest for any visiting Kiasyd or expert in the field, and they’re likely to treat you well just to get access to it.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"59"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.b2g3NlKyrNGiF2ml"},"adv:v20-dreadful-mara":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Dreadful Mara","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Flaw.</p><p>You drank something that still lives within you and hates you, exerting alarming control over your thoughts and actions. Each night, the Storyteller may convert a single rolled success into a 1, potentially leading to a botch. At the Storyteller’s discretion, the entity may also whisper advice or insults, which can lead to increased difficulty during attempts to concentrate.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"60"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LTY4CEgCjZv1MRRA"},"adv:v20-lightweight":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Lightweight","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Flaw.</p><p>Shamefully, you cannot imbibe the blood of any creatures other than mortals, ghouls, and vampires. Whenever you attempt to drink from another entity, you’re forced to purge the contents immediately after and gain no sustenance from it.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"60"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.j7HuHnln3lpo04x5"},"adv:v20-paranormal-link":{"name":"•• Paranormal Link","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Merit.</p><p>You’re linked to another weird species of the world, and unconsciously find yourself able to understand their parlance, codes, and rituals. Difficulties to decipher the hidden rites and languages of one society (selected when this Merit is purchased) are reduced by three.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"60"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aCTVQrW9ZGyNLyG0"},"adv:v20-refined-palate":{"name":"Flaw: Refined Palate","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Flaw.</p><p>Many Kiasyd self-impose feeding restrictions: refusing to feed from the poor and diseased, or even those still wearing skin, or over a certain age. This manifests akin to the Ventrue Clan weakness (V20, p. 73), due to conditioning making any blood outside of the Kiasyd’s preference unpalatable. The severity of the Flaw depends on how narrow the restriction is.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"60"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.IhEIrdSKqA2q7b2K"},"adv:v20-skin-of-porcelain":{"name":"•••• Skin of Porcelain","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Merit.</p><p>Through concoctions imbibed or by a fluke of your Embrace, you possess a ceramic-like coating to your skin. In order for this to have any effect beyond looking strange, a Stamina roll is required (difficulty 8). Success allows you to convert up to three points of aggravated damage from a fire source to lethal damage before attempts to soak. The hardening dissipates for the remainder of the current story.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"60"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.JW4c2R4CMcn861cy"},"adv:v20-vitae-mutation":{"name":"••••• Vitae Mutation","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/kiasyd.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Kiasyd may take this Merit.</p><p>You’ve drank the liquid essence of entities both strange and powerful. Whether the vitae of Methuselahs, the boiling blood of lupines, or bottled dreams of fae, the volume and combination of the brews you’ve consumed have forever altered you. Each night a Willpower roll is required (difficulty 7) for you to manifest a single point of Auspex, Chimerstry, or Dementation. This point can be added to an existing rating in the Discipline, but disappears the following night.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"60"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.eeiXuEuFfjZEn78n"},"adv:v20-body-trail":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Body Trail","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nagaraja.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nagaraja may take this Flaw.</p><p>Whether the Nagaraja is just too sloppy and prone to overlooking details during her kills or she is especially fond of leaving a signature for every kill to send a message to others, others can use this to track her down. Each time there are items at a scene of a crime that could link directly back the Nagaraja, causing her to always look over her shoulder. The police are always one step away from busting her, or the Camarilla’s assassins have her in their sights. In any case, leaving a trail is a terrible thing. For every person they kill during a single story, the difficulty to Survival checks for tracking are reduced by 1. This penalty resets at the beginning of a new story… if they survive to the next story, that is.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"70"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LU5LDDEye7ForTwr"},"adv:v20-extra-sharp":{"name":"•• Extra Sharp","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nagaraja.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nagaraja may take this Merit.</p><p>All Nagaraja already have sharpened fangs for the purposes of cutting into flesh with ease. A character with this Merit, however, has additional rows of fangs, similar to a shark, each one poised to inflict the most pain possible. Instead of dealing 1 unsoakable lethal damage per bite, they deal 2 lethal instead. Some Nagaraja like this gift since it makes their victims stop squirming much quicker. Others fear it creates a connection to the painful bite of the Giovanni, and may see Nagaraja with this Merit almost as outsiders.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"70"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kpIbqwpnvPTnlm3b"},"adv:v20-the-largest-maw":{"name":"Flaw: (••) The Largest Maw","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nagaraja.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nagaraja may take this Flaw.</p><p>Due to the size of the Nagaraja’s shark-like rows of teeth, they find it hard to communicate verbally. Instead, she speaks through pools of spit and sometimes bites her own teeth in the process. This makes them quieter than other Nagaraja, but can also affect situations where clear speech is mandatory, such as when giving speeches or reciting delicate rituals. All difficulties for rolls where speech is a factor are raised by +2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"70"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.liG5BEvnCphFeBPT"},"adv:v20-wolverines-palate":{"name":"••• Wolverine’s Palate","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nagaraja.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nagaraja may take this Merit.</p><p>Though many do not know this, the dreaded wolverine is one of only a few creatures in the world that can eat their prey entirely, whether larger or smaller. The Nagaraja with this Merit has the ability to eat bone just as easily as flesh, sapping even more spiritual essence from her victim and converting it to blood points. With one bite, she can cut through flesh and bone and swallow it all at once with no ill effect. This also means she may gain up to 5 additional blood points from a body by consuming the bones.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"70"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.tdGrgaB0ln4iOb4H"},"adv:v20-permanent-third-eye":{"name":"Flaw: Permanent Third Eye","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/salubri-tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri or Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>While most sealed third eyes are visible as a slight scar, yours remains permanently open on your forehead. For 2 points, the eye is merely open, and may be closed for a scene on a successful Willpower roll (difficulty 6). For 4 points, the eye sheds light, increasing all Stealth difficulties by 2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"80"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UAYj6sm1iB6PRoLC"},"adv:v20-scent-of-the-other":{"name":"• Scent of the Other","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri may take this Merit.</p><p>“You don’t seem like a soul-sucker…” For whatever reason, your third eye shuts tightly when you choose to hide it, and your blood and aura don’t give away your Clan. You might be regarded as a Caitiff, or as a member of another Clan. In any case, Tremere or other Salubri hunters are thrown off your trail, unless you do something to reveal yourself again.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"80"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xaamkDdStoPbC1ZE"},"adv:v20-sight-beyond-sight":{"name":"••• Sight Beyond Sight","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri may take this Merit.</p><p>Your third eye occasionally experiences visions and pierces illusions. When your eye is open (via use of Auspex or Obeah), you occasionally pierce Obfuscate or Chimerstry. Some past Salubri have seen through faerie miens or glimpsed the dead lands of ghosts. You have no control over what your eye sees, though it’ll sometimes open involuntarily in response to the above phenomena.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"80"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XZeKxmRrgsZ7dQcb"},"adv:v20-the-eighth":{"name":"Flaw: (••••••) The Eighth","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri may take this Flaw.</p><p>Requirement: Ninth Generation or lower.</p><p>Your sire is one of the Seven, the diablerist Golcondaseekers, trying to ensure the spiritual wealth of salvation would never be lost with their Final Death. They sought you as a successor, and proudly prepared you for your sacred duty. You, on the other hand, smiled at their lies, listened to their bullshit, and ran for the hills the first chance you got. You’re constantly hunted, not only by the Tremere, but by your sire, the other Seven, and their various hangers-on. You must be at least Ninth Generation to take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"80"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.z101jaEZbBkCBdyP"},"adv:v20-warriors-heart":{"name":"••• Warrior’s Heart","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri may take this Merit.</p><p>Steel sits in the placid heart of the Salubri bloodline, and Adonai’s resurrection has caused your own heart to flutter ever so slightly. Like the Healers of old, you may learn the Valeren powers of the antitribu at a cost of current rating x6; if you know the first two dots of Obeah and Valeren, you gain a 2-die bonus to using the first two Discipline dots. However, your warrior’s heart suffers from the Precept of Samiel, the well-defined wanderlust that affects many Salubri — for every week you spend in the same location, you lose a point of Willpower, as your soul agitates without action. The antitribu might have a similar Merit allowing them to learn Obeah at a cost of always helping someone in need, at the Storyteller’s discretion.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"80"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.usu8k66L9WPVrXhJ"},"adv:v20-brittle-body":{"name":"Flaw: Brittle Body","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/samedi.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Samedi may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your already disfigured corpse doesn’t handle the physical demands of being a Kindred as well as it should. Your bones break at inopportune times, your skin tears if you move too quickly, and you have to be careful on a nightly basis. The 2-point version of this flaw gives you a +2 difficulty to all Physical rolls because of the delicate nature of your body. The 4-point version means that you also hang onto physical damage longer than other Kindred. You may still heal health levels as normal, but any dice penalties remain until the next evening when you awaken. This is in addition to the penalty to Physical rolls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"91"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8C6evZbRQMEw9k1F"},"adv:v20-death-grip":{"name":"••• Death Grip","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/samedi.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Samedi may take this Merit.</p><p>Thanks to your connection with death, you have a keen insight few other Kindred possess. When in contact with a dead body, you make a Wits + Alertness roll (difficulty 7). On a success, you intuitively know how the body died. On a failure, you suffer an illusory version of the wound that saps a point of Willpower. On a botch, your body mirrors the wound and hurts itself in the same manner, causing lethal damage equal to the final blow.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"91"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UhHoHocJyJlXFHZX"},"adv:v20-mortal-flashbacks":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Mortal Flashbacks","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/samedi.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Samedi may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your body remembers your death. Every night when you awaken, you vividly remember the pain of your death wound. The wound reopens during the night and closes itself shortly before you rise. This traumatic reminder of your mortality starts your night off in a bad way. You lose a point of Willpower each night when you awaken, because of the trauma of dying again.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"91"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UUe942hsEHrqfJNk"},"adv:v20-stitcher":{"name":"••• Stitcher","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/samedi.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Samedi may take this Merit.</p><p>Getting blown apart isn’t a big deal. When parts of your body are cut off or otherwise removed due to damage, you may make a Dexterity + Medicine (difficulty 7) to sew the part back in place properly. If successful, the cost of healing the wound is reduced by one blood point.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"91"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.97C27FFlyANAvQZm"},"adv:v20-advanced-tech":{"name":"Advanced Tech","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/truebrujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only True Brujah may take this Merit.</p><p>You have a device (or possibly a few) of an advanced design. While the True Brujah are not especially good at innovation, they are good at making improvements. So this device has no special functions unavailable to its contemporaries, but it does work a lot better. It reduces the chance of a mechanical fault (such as a jam) by half and all uses of the device are made with a -1 to the difficulty. The Storyteller should decide on the cost of the Merit, depending on how many items the character has, and how powerful they are.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.smAQv7I6XmEcSVBY"},"adv:v20-fatalist":{"name":"••• Fatalist","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/truebrujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only True Brujah may take this Merit.</p><p>The character’s study of time has led them to believe that what is done is simply done. Destiny is a fixed path and therefore we are all the slaves of our own futures. As such, nothing can truly be our fault, for nothing is entirely under our control. It therefore becomes a lot easier to excuse themselves from their worst excesses. The character subtracts 2 from the difficulty of all degeneration rolls against losses of Humanity or Path of Enlightenment.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.YTp28QNTGqro5Aed"},"adv:v20-out-of-phase":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Out of Phase","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/truebrujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only True Brujah may take this Flaw.</p><p>The character is a little too aware of time and the possibilities surrounding them. People often seem to be in slightly different places, and speech frequently echoes in their ears. They see layers of alternate timelines in too much detail. Any perception rolls they make are at +2 difficulty, as they try to see past the unreal.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.yDiSovc92aNyBB5m"},"adv:v20-true-celerity":{"name":"••••• True Celerity","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/truebrujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only True Brujah may take this Merit.</p><p>The True Brujah have an odd relationship with Celerity. It often comes easily to them, but a few refuse to learn it, as it has become a symbol of the traitor Brujah. However, many find it a useful skill for masquerading as Brujah within the Camarilla or Sabbat, and so a few True Brujah have developed a slightly different form of the Discipline. A character with this Merit has the ability to learn True Celerity. While it works exactly the same as Celerity, it feels very different. Instead of the character moving quickly, they slow time down all around them. This Merit confers no Discipline points for the character, but does allow it to be bought with experience as if Celerity was a Clan discipline. While this power functions the same as Celerity, it cannot be taught to anyone who does not have any levels of the Temporis Discipline.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Bloodlines","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7ly1pbFzMgzzP4RY"},"adv:v20-broken-antitribu":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Broken Antitribu","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Banu Haqim may take this Flaw.</p><p>Only Sabbat vampires may take this Flaw.</p><p>You are one of those most pitiful things — an Assamite antitribu who still labors under the Tremere Curse. You have pledged yourself to the Sabbat, but you cannot perform diablerie and can only partake of Kindred vitae already transubstantiated through the Vaulderie. As a result, the difficulty of all Social rolls against other Sabbat members is at +2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NJ6sCvRl9GHViVjF"},"adv:v20-multiple-curses":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Multiple Curses","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Banu Haqim may take this Flaw.</p><p>In addition to the normal curse imposed upon you by your caste or sect, you suffer one additional one associated with the Assamite Clan. Most likely, you are a rare vizier or sorcerer within the Sabbat who suffers the Baali Curse in addition to your normal caste-imposed curse, but at the Storyteller’s discretion, other combinations may be possible.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OgExJn90PSvUgBDS"},"adv:v20-outcast":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Outcast","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Banu Haqim may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your have rejected the ethos of the caste into which you were Embraced. Perhaps you are one of the few viziers or sorcerers to support the Web of Knives or pursue the Path of Blood. Perhaps you are a warrior who has little stomach for combat, preferring instead to be a diplomat or a student of the occult. Perhaps you made some kind of public spectacle that revealed your disdain for your fellow caste members. Your sire now rejects you, as do the other members of your caste. The difficulty of all Social rolls against members of your caste is at +2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.s7ZOAEkXfL7gyoTv"},"adv:v20-sectarian-ally":{"name":"• Sectarian Ally","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Banu Haqim may take this Merit.</p><p>You have a close friend in one of the Kindred sects. Perhaps you are a warrior who is in touch with one of the antitribu, a vizier who shares common business interests with some Camarilla Ventrue, or a sorcerer who corresponds with one of the rare blood magicians of the Anarch Movement. Your ally can help you navigate the currents of their sect, but they might want something in exchange from time to time.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8cXXvm6l4R4Ob5mU"},"adv:v20-thousand-meter-killer":{"name":"• Thousand Meter Killer","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/banuhaqim.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Banu Haqim may take this Merit.</p><p>You have proven yourself worthy to join the Thousand Meter Club through your remarkable skill with the sniper rifle. The difficulty of all rolls associated with sniping is reduced by -1. You also double the normal range when using a sniper rifle as a weapon.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.uCE2H56pQHsYpRmM"},"adv:v20-dynamic-personality":{"name":"••••• Dynamic Personality","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Brujah may take this Merit.</p><p>Your natural charisma draws mortals to you like groupies to a rock star. Consequently, it is easier for you to acquire certain Backgrounds related to mortals.</p><p><strong>System:</strong> In addition to any Backgrounds acquired at character creation or through roleplay, you can purchase new Backgrounds with experience points at the end of each story. The available Backgrounds are Allies, Contacts, Herd, Retainers, and each new dot costs the current rating in experience points.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Tn5S0tnRleOf1y3S"},"adv:v20-furys-focus":{"name":"••• Fury’s Focus","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/brujah.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Brujah may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Path of Entelechy (a V20 Path of Enlightenment).</p><p>Brujah who have devoted themselves to mastering their frenzies through the Path of Entelechy sometimes find tangible benefits resulting from their efforts. A Brujah with this Merit may briefly delay the full onset of frenzy.</p><p><strong>System:</strong> The player spends a Willpower point at the onset of frenzy and then rolls the Brujah’s Entelechy rating. The difficulty is one higher than the original roll to resist frenzy. The Brujah still frenzies, but the player controls her character’s actions for one turn per success. Furthermore, when the period of partial control ends and the Brujah loses control, the difficulty of any degeneration rolls triggered by sins committed during the frenzy are reduced by the number of successes rolled, to a minimum difficulty of 4.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"48"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DDTcBwENWpOe08OQ"},"adv:v20-addictive-blood":{"name":"••• Addictive Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Merit.</p><p>You don’t just peddle narcotics; through the blessings of the Dark God, you are the perfect drug. Your blood is especially delicious to others, Kindred or kine. Whoever tastes your blood must, during any subsequent scenes that they meet you, drink again or spend a Willpower point to avoid the pangs of craving. These cravings add +2 difficulty to any Mental or Social rolls. Setites with this Merit find it much easier to blood bond an opponent, as once they have tasted the tainted vitae, they will do almost anything to drink it again.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VyxAJ0eb0vLpTdIh"},"adv:v20-aura-of-the-typhon":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Aura of the Typhon","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Flaw.</p><p>Something slithers beneath your skin, coiled in your very essence — something that causes the Lupines to stalk you for some dread purpose you do not understand. Their howls follow in your wake. You catch glimpses of blurred motion and the gleam of eyes and teeth in the dark. They get closer every night. What do they want from you?</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.uBf4D9AdOtwrapdG"},"adv:v20-drug-resistance":{"name":"•• Drug Resistance","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Merit.</p><p>The Setite religion is one fraught with vices, both to compromise enemies and to enlighten initiates. Cultists tend to build up a tolerance to the substances they take directly (if human or ghoul) or through the blood of prey (if Kindred). You are unusually resistant to alcohol, narcotics, and similar addictive substances. You can pretend to be far more under the influence than you are in order to take advantage of an opponent. All rolls to resist the effects of such substances are at -2 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OlvrjqqMpuvCDjL4"},"adv:v20-forked-tongue":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Forked Tongue","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your tongue is forked, flickering, and inhumanly reptilian. Upholding the Masquerade becomes difficult for you. Note that this tongue does not inflict aggravated damage, nor draw blood.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.21Bf6wfnDPNqZXch"},"adv:v20-heartless":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Heartless","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have lost your heart. Either you removed it via The Heart of Darkness (see V20, p. 210), or an elder did it to you, but either way you no longer have easy access to it. The heart might be in the possession of a foe, or simply missing. If it turns out that a Cainite possesses the heart (say a Setite elder, or your sire) you must obey their every command. If it’s merely missing, the anxiety and obsession to find it interferes with your nightly existence, and may increase the difficulty of Willpower rolls by +1 at Storyteller discretion.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.iDnQ2WTfyPHh34KO"},"adv:v20-scales":{"name":"Flaw: Scales","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Flaw.</p><p>Set blessed you upon your Embrace, and you bear his mark. A portion of your skin is covered in scales. As a one-point Flaw, a small, easily hidden area of skin is covered. As a two-point Flaw, a whole limb is covered, while having a scaled, lipless face is a three-point Flaw. Kine are frightened and disturbed by the obviously unnatural scales, and Kindred have their own prejudices against the Setites. All social rolls receive a +2 difficulty when the scales are visible. This penalty does not apply to social interactions with other Setites.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1MtHAUs2fiGjW2Pi"},"adv:v20-setite-initiate":{"name":"••••• Setite Initiate","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Merit.</p><p>You were Embraced into a Clan other than the Followers of Set. However, you have accepted the Setite religion, undergone the vetting process and rites, and have been formally inducted into the cult. You have access to Serpentis and Setite Blood Sorcery (though you pay out-of-Clan costs to learn the,). You may even study one of their Paths of Enlightenment. It is important to note that “Setites” from other Clans or bloodlines are not treated as second-class citizens. You are no longer a dupe they can string along. Once you are in, you are a sibling of faith, which is a much more important distinction than blood. An outsider accepting the Dark God is a joyous event, even to the most conservative elder. There are even rumors of non-Kindred supernatural beings joining the cult.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.yMCBKkLX0CK6HG69"},"adv:v20-venomous-bite":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Venomous Bite","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/theministry.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only The Ministry may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have developed venom glands in the roof of your mouth. The venom is a virulent neurotoxin, fatal to mortals, although Kindred and other supernatural creatures are unaffected by it. You, of course, are immune. The problem is you have no control over your poison glands. When you bite, you always inject this venom, usually killing your human victims. You must learn to feed in other ways, perhaps drawing the blood you need with a syringe or razor, if you do not wish to kill every time you feed.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"67"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.uvU0uUPIs0z42mX6"},"adv:v20-hive-minded":{"name":"Hive-Minded","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gangrel may take this Merit.</p><p>Your Animalism works on insects and other creepy-crawlies in addition to mammalian animals. If you select the two-point version of this merit, your Protean forms may take the form of an insectoid swarm rather than a single creature (though the swarm must be of a size equivalent to a wolf or a bat, as appropriate).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ITwdiqyVsWoODnkE"},"adv:v20-lesser-mark-of-the-beast":{"name":"•••• Lesser Mark of the Beast","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gangrel may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Humanity only.</p><p>Common to the Gangrel known as the Knights of Avalon, you are able to control how your Beast manifests more than others do. Whenever you would gain an animalistic feature (V20, p. 55), roll your current Willpower (difficulty 12 - Humanity rating, maximum 9). If successful, you manage to channel your humanity to avoid gaining an animalistic feature. However, your Beast is further from you, making you at +2 difficulty to rolls involving Animalism or Protean (or combo Disciplines involving those powers) for the rest of the evening. Vampires on a Path of Enlightenment lose all access to this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Fbhgq875HFAxpTT3"},"adv:v20-member-of-the-pack":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Member of the Pack","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gangrel may take this Flaw.</p><p>You can only summon, speak to, and command a specific type of animal — ravens, rats, and so forth — with Animalism. Your Storyteller is the best adjudicator of what types of animal are appropriate for this Flaw, but the choice should be fairly limiting. Other animals do not respond to your uses of Animalism at all.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZEcfk333JXGvyPSa"},"adv:v20-rat-in-a-cage":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Rat in a Cage","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gangrel may take this Flaw.</p><p>Anytime you are penned in or physically restrained (such as by a cage, or with handcuffs), you suffer acute anxiety. The difficulties of any rolls made under such circumstances are increased by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fc5IcbSmrWmnz5tB"},"adv:v20-skald":{"name":"•• Skald","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gangrel may take this Merit.</p><p>Anytime you make an Occult roll to know a fact about vampiric history, you may add a die to your dice pool. Further, you have exceptional memory for oral histories, and you are a quick study when it comes to memorizing large amounts of rote information. This is not true eidetic memory, but constitutes the ability to memorize poetic eddas, codes, or complex messages with only a few hours of study.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HL13fxuPJW5RWmrg"},"adv:v20-totemic-change":{"name":"••••• Totemic Change","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/gangrel.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Gangrel may take this Merit.</p><p>Your Protean forms are flexible; you may choose a different animal form each time you change shape. The form you choose each time must follow all the conventions and rules of standard Protean animal shapes (see p. 91); you simply may choose to appear as a different animal each time you take Beast Form.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MfDsZbHqPSbJbLqt"},"adv:v20-cannibal-giovanni":{"name":"• Cannibal (V20)","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Merit.</p><p>Most vampires can’t eat food, and even those who can force it down, don’t gain sustenance from it. Like them, you still can’t stomach the crap most mortals eat. Human meat, on the other hand, brings you great joy. It can be baked, fried, or even raw, and you can tuck right in, and even gain sustenance. Even other vampires look askance at Kindred who devour their prey, though the Dunsirn applaud your respect for tradition. In addition to the blood points every human can provide, you can cannibalize a mortal and gain even more. An average human can provide up to seven helpings of meat (one per health level). Each helping provides you with one blood point.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"106"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TDDdHnnrJJ7NjI1G"},"adv:v20-consanguineous-resistance":{"name":"• Consanguineous Resistance","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Merit.</p><p>Your character cannot be blood bound by anyone who shares his mortal bloodline. That is, if you were born into the Giovanni family, you cannot be bound by anyone else who was born a Giovanni, though you can still be bound by, say, a Pisanob of the Giovanni Clan, or by Kindred of any other Clan. Similarly, a Dunsirn with Consanguineous Resistance could not be bound by others who were born into the mortal Dunsirn family, but could be bound by a Milliner of the Giovanni Clan. The Giovanni are extremely suspicious of anyone known to manifest this quirk. Although this blood-borne aberration hasn’t been documented, a few savvy Giovanni have a rough idea of what it is and does. It’s generally associated with being a rebellious young smartass who needs to be put down. This is not as unfair as it sounds; by the time a bond resistance is really obvious, it’s likely because a punishment isn’t working. A character who is discovered to have this trait probably earns her sire’s hostility at the very least.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"106"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.H8vhx1bwdfOEzaNb"},"adv:v20-mortuario":{"name":"Mortuario","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Not with Sanguine Incongruity.</p><p>You died. Perhaps you were murdered, or simply had a car accident. Whatever the cause, you were gone. But your sire found you too useful, or couldn’t let you go. You were Embraced using the Ferryman’s Recall ritual (see p. 109). The Embrace left you with the scars of your death, eternal tracework reminders of your trip to the other side. It also left you with the taut, pallid complexion of the dead. In addition to the traditional weakness of your Clan, you also suffer disfigurement from your time as a true corpse. While you are able to heal yourself just like any other vampire, the wounds do not heal cleanly. You retain the scars of every experience. Depending on the nature of the damage, this can make social dealings exceedingly difficult, and may decrease your Appearance dots over time (even to 0). However, your time across the Shroud also gave you a natural feel for necromantic blood magic. The difficulties of all Necromancy rolls are reduced by two. This trait costs 4 points for characters who already have an appearance of 0 (such as Samedi and the Harbingers of Skulls), or 2 points for any other Kindred. It is an incredibly rare condition even among the Giovanni, and essentially unknown outside the Clan. Giovanni with this Merit generally arouse the superstition of their Clan, and are treated with a definite wariness, particularly by Anziani. Characters with the Mortuario Merit may not also possess the Sanguine Incongruity Merit or similar flaws such as Monstrous.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"106"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6pLKGEBhEvYcngdH"},"adv:v20-sanguine-incongruity":{"name":"••••• Sanguine Incongruity","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Not with Mortuario.</p><p>Giovanni with this atavism are few and far between. Kindred possessing it do not bear the traditional Giovanni Clan weakness, the so-called Curse of Lamia; their Kiss causes no more damage than the blood loss itself. These vampires acquire a peculiar pallor upon their Embrace, however — they look like corpses, and no amount of blood ingestion can flush their features (as other vampires are able to do). Indeed, the bearer of this Merit more closely resembles the Clan’s Cappadocian ancestors, and they have a slightly unnerving air about them. As a result, all rolls involving a Social attribute (Charisma, Manipulation, or Appearance) are at +1 difficulty. Giovanni with this Merit are afforded wide berth, as the Giovanni tend to be quite superstitious about it. Characters with the Sanguine Incongruity Merit may not also possess the Mortuario Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"106"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CzweFzvOJC1HqHlS"},"adv:v20-inbred":{"name":"Flaw: Inbred","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Flaw.</p><p>Inbreeding, a common occurrence among the incestuous Giovanni Clan, can take many forms. The Inbred Flaw covers all manner of physical, mental, and emotional defects. A one-point Inbreeding is something simple and unobtrusive, such as eyes too close together or an underbite (+1 difficulty on Appearance rolls). A three-point Inbreeding is more severe: a congenital health condition (for mortals) or a crippling physical deformity (+2 difficulty on appropriate Strength, Dexterity, or Stamina rolls). Five-point Inbreedings are grossly disabling or emotionally crippling — everything from uselessly atrophied legs to a permanent Derangement — decided on mutually by the player and Storyteller. Inbred conditions may or may not be immediately discernible, though their point cost should be relative to their magnitude, as decided by the Storyteller.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"107"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.hAmLfK15xxcnvxGo"},"adv:v20-shadow-walker":{"name":"Flaw: (••••••) Shadow Walker","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/giovanni.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Giovanni may take this Flaw.</p><p>The Giovanni Clan is by its nature inexorably tied to the realm beyond the sudario. Giovanni suffering from this Flaw are so tied to the Shadowlands that even in the lands of the living they must interact with the world of the dead on a nightly basis. To shadow walkers, objects in the Underworld are as real as anything found in the physical world. Such vampires find that the ghosts of walls may impeded their flight, ghostly objects may strike them, and wraiths’ powers work as if the Kindred were on the far side of the Shroud. This Flaw is similar to the Ash Path power Dead Hand (V20, p. 163), except that Shadow Walker is always on and it in no way allows the character possessing it to perceive beyond the Shroud. The Storyteller may determine that certain Shadowlands topography interferes with you. Unless you have some ability to do so, you can’t see into the Shadowlands, so you have to be careful in feeling your way about — essentially, a blind man subject to the Underworld landscape. At the Storyteller’s discretion, immaterial walls or environmental effects may restrict you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"107"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sWeHBIn43d8uVFO6"},"adv:v20-bigger-boys-came":{"name":"•• Bigger Boys Came","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>When someone uses their contacts to their advantage, you can try to use yours to overrule them. You might get an editor to quash a reporter’s story, or get a gang boss to stop his thugs taking down a rival. To do this you must make a roll using Manipulation + Contacts (difficulty 8). If you can get more successes than the Contacts rating of your opponent, their contacts fail to come through for them.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8nO93kB8nQmesglJ"},"adv:v20-court-favorite":{"name":"Court Favorite","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>While the Courts of Blood are usually difficult to sway, you have become very good at shifting the balance. A mixture of experience and political empathy allow you to know just the right way to nudge the decision in your direction, or perhaps for that of your client. Any roll made that will affect the decision in a Court of Blood is granted a bonus or penalty (whichever is in your favor) in dice equal to the level you have of this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.cA4KfD4XC1Pcz5RI"},"adv:v20-eyes-of-shadow":{"name":"Eyes of Shadow","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>There is something about your eyes that makes you look dark and dangerous. Making eye contact with you is like staring into the Abyss. It may not be obvious why, but anyone you talk to gets a chill when they meet your gaze. The difficulty for any Intimidation roll is reduced by the number of points in this Merit (to a minimum of 2).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"121"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RFtI3NXAEQBp0Mbd"},"adv:v20-aura-of-command":{"name":"••• Aura of Command","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>Whether you are good at barking orders or simply have a commanding tone, people tend to do what you tell them to do. You are not so much a natural leader as a born commander. When using the Leadership skill to get others to obey you, the difficulty is reduced by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"122"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.us5q9eIrNx2IxMP9"},"adv:v20-call-of-the-sea":{"name":"•• Call of the Sea","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>There is something about the sea that makes you feel at home. You are in tune with the tides and rhythm of the ocean. When on a boat in the ocean or on a river, you may add a die to all your dice pools except for Disciplines.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"122"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9nrRdNJ9zJz7pyCA"},"adv:v20-controllable-night-sight":{"name":"•• Controllable Night Sight","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>Your night vision is extraordinarily good, even for a vampire. The deepest shadows are like looking into a well-lit room for you. However, in normal light or bright conditions, you have to switch back to less-sensitive vision, or the weakest light quickly blinds you. While your night sight is active, you suffer no penalties for the dark and can see perfectly well. Should you use it in well-lit conditions; you suffer a penalty inversely proportional to the usual penalties for darkness.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"122"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ekq9PA1ksdQemb5a"},"adv:v20-king-or-queen-of-shadow":{"name":"•••• King or Queen of Shadow","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>It is hard to hold onto your Humanity within the Sabbat, or even simply as a vampire. Your ability to empathize with the kine makes it more difficult. However, you have found a way to draw strength from that empathy. Any degeneration checks you make while on Path of Humanity reduce their difficulty by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"122"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ofUop4hva2zPZ54d"},"adv:v20-secret-stash":{"name":"Secret Stash","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>You have several resources, sleeper agents, or followers hidden away for a rainy day. For each level of this Merit you have one unassigned background point “sitting in storage” (2 gives you one point, 3 gives you two points, and so on). At any time, you may spend as many of them as you like to increase your level in Allies, Contacts, Herd, Influence, Mentor, Resources, or Retainers. Once spent, the points remain assigned permanently, but until then they cannot be affected by anything. This allows the vampire to create a new resource in an instant, without requiring the expenditure of experience points or awaiting a downtime. The vampire is not really gaining new levels, but revealing levels he has had all along.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"122"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fkZu7abK3d88TFPB"},"adv:v20-instrument-of-god":{"name":"••••• Instrument of God","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>Your self-confidence comes not from a belief in your own abilities, but due to a direct manifesto from the Lord. You have a divine purpose and He works through you, even though your goals may seem anything but holy. Whether it is because of you powerful will or an actual connection to the divine, you gain three additional dice to resist the powers of True Faith when they are used against you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"123"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KYWYnwAz9ghJwroj"},"adv:v20-insubordinate":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Insubordinate","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>You like to be in charge so much you have a hard time following anyone else’s orders. When given a plan or told to do something, you tend to do the opposite on principle. Whenever you are ordered to do something, you must make a Willpower roll with a difficulty depending on the importance of the superior and the danger of the task (difficulty 7 is typical for someone directly above the vampire assigning them a moderately dangerous task). If you fail, you will do anything except what you’ve been told to do.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"123"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2RBpRyxm0MB4nn4G"},"adv:v20-long-term-planning":{"name":"•••• Long-Term Planning","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Merit.</p><p>You never leave anything to chance; every action is a carefully considered stratagem. Once per session you may declare an action you are about to take is actually “all part of your plan” and reduce its difficulty by two points. The Storyteller may veto the use of this ability on particular rolls. The player and Storyteller should keep a note of each of these occasions and decide how they are linking together into a grand scheme.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"123"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KxECLma62bXoZ0h6"},"adv:v20-uncontrollable-night-sight":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Uncontrollable Night Sight","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your night vision is good, but you can’t turn it off. While you can see easily into the deepest shadows, any light is almost blinding to you. You suffer penalties the brighter the area you are in is lit, inversely proportional to the standard penalties for darkness. Even just standing in a well-lit room is uncomfortable to you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"123"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.uCUyXbgt5qpMHFWp"},"adv:v20-unproven":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Unproven","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>Somehow, you have failed to prove yourself worthy of the name Lasombra. Maybe your sire did not test you well enough, or an opportunity to prove yourself has not come up. Whatever the reason, you are not truly considered part of the Clan. All social dealings with other Lasombra suffer a -3 dice penalty. You may also not be chosen to sit in judgment in the Courts of Blood.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"123"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RrVfztPKDrxBqnb6"},"adv:v20-cold-read":{"name":"••• Cold Read","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>Whenever you meet someone for the first time, you may spend a number of points of Willpower equal to your Perception. For each point spent, you may ask the Storyteller one question about the character. The Storyteller must either answer truthfully or let you take the Willpower point back to you to avoid answering the question.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CZVQEnxsYV9fQEG8"},"adv:v20-dead-inside":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Dead Inside","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Flaw.</p><p>You feel nothing but pain and numbness. While others find ways to make their lives worth living, you sometimes don’t get that thrill. Once per session, the Storyteller may cancel any gain of Willpower you made by playing to your Nature or Demeanor.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BkPpLdmhJxsPwvdH"},"adv:v20-distracting-aura":{"name":"•• Distracting Aura","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>Reading an aura using Auspex requires the viewer to focus on recurring patterns and colors to detect a target’s emotional state. Because of the unique state of your psyche, your aura is even harder to read than most. All uses of Aura Perception are at a +2 difficulty against you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ztRihrHfsekJhl9H"},"adv:v20-infectious":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Infectious","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Flaw.</p><p>Madness flows within your blood, but your bite carries a taint as well. Mortals take a temporary derangement for every three points of blood you take from them. The derangements stay until the mortal restores the lost blood.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.68GVtgXPdbpXkFlV"},"adv:v20-overstimulated":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Overstimulated","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Flaw.</p><p>Malkavians notice things that many others do not. That means keeping their eyes and ears open far longer than anyone else does. That makes you easily distracted when trying to focus. Take a +2 penalty to all rolls involving Perception.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.l8wwrMoZnXQDagxc"},"adv:v20-paper-trail":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Paper Trail","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Flaw.</p><p>You’ve spent some time in state institutions like prisons or asylums, likely before your Embrace. Most people have some sort of information that relays date of birth and other bits of fact. This information is hard to eliminate, and may endanger the Masquerade. Enemies with the right influence may be able to track down the information and use it against you. It may lead to vulnerable targets or clue hunters to where your haven is located.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gShKNkCRWQFIL75u"},"adv:v20-prophetic-dreams":{"name":"•• Prophetic Dreams","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/malkavian.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Malkavian may take this Merit.</p><p>You have dreams during your daylight sleep. Dreams you remember. Sometimes, they even come true. Rather than regain a point of Willpower when you rest during the day, you may choose to have the Storyteller give your character a lucid dream featuring foreshadowing about upcoming events, characters, and situations.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"142"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2L1YW2YMlRNXJl05"},"adv:v20-foul-blood":{"name":"• Foul Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Your blood is vile; in fact, it tastes so disgusting it requires a Willpower roll (difficulty 6) just to avoid gagging and retching after tasting it. If someone is foolish enough to attempt diablerie on you, they need to succeed in three (difficulty 9) Willpower rolls to go through with it. The blood is so disgusting that no one can keep it down long enough to use it to become a ghoul, either.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"160"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.O7ubNRjC9dH1EX1u"},"adv:v20-lizard-limbs":{"name":"• Lizard Limbs","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Like a lizard, you are able to actually shed parts of your body. By spending a blood point and a little effort, you can detach a hand or foot, or even an arm or leg. This might be to escape bonds or a grapple. Unfortunately, the appendage will not reattach, and you will have to regrow that over time (usually a couple of days for a hand or foot, and a week for a limb). You also suffer a -3 dice pool penalty to actions that would require the use of more limbs.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"160"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9LPT9dOV64RR2Cf8"},"adv:v20-long-fingers":{"name":"• Long Fingers","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You have been blessed with unusually long and graceful fingers. This means you have an easier time with fine manipulation as well as grappling tasks, gaining an extra die when attempting such actions.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"160"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.dPGEglwSMNBJaCIM"},"adv:v20-monstrous-maw":{"name":"• Monstrous Maw","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You have either oversized tusks for fangs, or a huge mouth full of sharp teeth. Whatever form it takes, your mouth is that of a monster. When attacking with a bite you do an additional point of damage. You may also add a die to your Intimidation dice pool when you smile.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"160"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7RLyF6YtmMKAtYkE"},"adv:v20-dangerous-secret":{"name":"Flaw: Dangerous Secret","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have come to know something you really wish you hadn’t discovered. Worse yet, the people you have the dirt on know that you know. It might be that you have discovered the Prince’s haven, or that there are infernalists hiding in the diocese. Whatever it is, you are not sure whom you can tell, and if you do, you will only make the subjects of the secret more enthusiastic about getting rid of you. You may even be implicated in the secret and risk going down with them. The more potent the Flaw, the more powerful the people in question are, and the more they want it silenced.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.54zGkeOSm0MfDDyu"},"adv:v20-false-reflection":{"name":"••• False Reflection","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Even when using their Obfuscate abilities, Nosferatu still show up in their true form when noticed by machines, such as cameras or video surveillance. With this ability, the Nosferatu can extend their power to recorded images and media. However, as with Mask of the Thousand Faces, the original image isn’t actually changed; it is just that people see it the way the Nosferatu wants it seen. Unfortunately, computers are not so easily fooled: if the image is used for facial recognition software (for instance), the computer will see the Nosferatu’s real face and fail to find a match. The Nosferatu has best take care how this power is used, as it may wear off in time. Some archived photos have given librarians a nasty surprise, years after going into storage.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.P5iQc81kUMbkBnBW"},"adv:v20-patagia":{"name":"•••• Patagia","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Your arms and legs have leathery flaps of skin between them, similar to that of a flying squirrel. While they make finding clothes a nightmare, they also allow you to glide for short distances, given enough height and a decent wind. The Storyteller might require an Athletics roll when it comes to landing.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oJEm19bRzTBBviM1"},"adv:v20-piscine":{"name":"• Piscine","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Water is a far more comfortable environment for you, be it the sea or sewer effluent. Rolls involving swimming or underwater movement have their difficulty reduced by 1.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lZ0XtJTnoKEADiB8"},"adv:v20-rugged-bad-looks":{"name":"••••• Rugged Bad Looks","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>While you are still hideous, you are not quite as monstrous as most Nosferatu. You still have an appearance of 0, but you might pass for human in the right light. It is a still a good idea to cover up and stay in the shadows, but the sight of you (or even the smell of you) is not an instant breach of the Masquerade. Having said that, you are still ugly enough to unnerve the crap out of most people.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MMCCk1vYbU2qXBUX"},"adv:v20-sleep-unseen":{"name":"•• Sleep Unseen","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>The power of Obfuscate usually requires you to concentrate at least a little. However, you are able to lock the power on, allowing you to sleep while remaining hidden. To do so requires the expenditure of another blood point, but it will last throughout the day’s rest. Those with Auspex can still attempt to detect you, but mortals will be unaware of your sanctuary. This can be a useful trick for Kindred who like to travel.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BtDMxEkjbAgX3Nsh"},"adv:v20-slimy":{"name":"• Slimy","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You secrete an ooze, which is as disgusting as it sounds. The ooze covers your entire body and soaks into your clothes. It makes you slippery and difficult to hold, requiring opponents to gain two more successes to grapple you. The dampness also makes you a little fire resistant, reducing your difficulty to soak fire damage by 1.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.78jqAW1qGqqWGT3h"},"adv:v20-spawning-pool":{"name":"Spawning Pool","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You have a spawning pool of your own (or possibly donate heavily to the Clan’s main pool). Creating such a pool takes time, and requires regular infusions of blood, at least six blood points a week for a year. The Merit grants its level as a dice pool bonus to the Nosferatu’s use of Animalism in their home city, but only with animals considered vermin, such as rats and cockroaches. Essentially the bonus is only available for creatures that might conceivably drink regularly from the tainted blood. Once established, the spawning pool requires twice its level in blood points each week to it. Without proper maintenance, its level will drop by one. It can be rebuilt by maintaining it at the new level and adding four additional blood points each week for six months. The Merit cannot be improved above its initial level, as this also represents the convenience of its location and the variety of creatures that might find it.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LvWwGCezlyOR9zJN"},"adv:v20-tough-hide":{"name":"•• Tough Hide","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>Your skin is much tougher than usual, possibly like that of a rhino or a lizard. This hide protects you against most normal damage, granting you an extra die when making a soak roll. However, this bonus does not apply against fire and sunlight.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.YyFp81oro9Xa93JE"},"adv:v20-tunnel-rat":{"name":"Tunnel Rat","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Merit.</p><p>You are remarkably adept at moving through the underground tunnels that you call your home. When attempting to navigate, escape, or track through sewers and underground places you know, you gain an additional die for every point you have in this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"161"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Avhtm7toAracs77x"},"adv:v20-anosmia":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Anosmia","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your life in the sewer has removed your sense of smell and taste. This means you are unperturbed by even the worst stench or most disgusting flavor. However, it also means you cannot ever succeed at any Perception rolls that rely of taste or smell. It also does not make you immune in any way to gas attacks or poisons; you just won’t be able to tell they are there.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"162"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Cvew6Yz3dsI72mBE"},"adv:v20-parasitic-infestation":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Parasitic Infestation","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>Living in the dark has made you a home to all manner of creepy crawlies and bloodsuckers. Your skin is crawling with ticks, lice, and leeches of all descriptions. They constantly bite and burrow, and having fed on your vitae, they have become very hard to kill. Not only can you not command them, you have tried everything to get rid of them and still they persist. Whatever the reason, they find you so succulent they reduce your blood pool by the result of one die divided by 3 (round down) each time you rise. The constant itch also keeps you on edge, increasing the difficulty of any Self-Control or Instinct rolls by 1.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"162"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.t3AgTZAaW6fwS3dq"},"adv:v20-bestial":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Bestial","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>You are closer to animals than humans, and it shows. In addition to the Nosferatu Clan weakness, you have an additional weakness: whenever you frenzy, you gain an animal feature, similar to the Gangrel Clan weakness (V20, p. 55). With Storyteller permission, permanently acquired animal features may be justification to acquire certain Merits after character creation, such as Lizard Limbs (p. 160) or Monstrous Maw (pp. 160-161). If so, the Storyteller can simply award them, or require an experience point expenditure (such as two times the Merit point value). Similar Flaws can also be taken, but cannot grant additional freebie or experience points.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"163"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.G0jGzMYkCgllc4UB"},"adv:v20-contagious":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Contagious","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your body has died on the inside, filling you with noxious bacteria, spores, and even fungi. Mortals that touch you or on whom you feed must make a Stamina roll (difficulty 9) not to fall ill. The illness puts them in bed with fever and sickness, and each week they may attempt the Stamina roll again. The Storyteller might reduce the difficulty if the target is receiving proper medical attention. On a success they recover, but if they fail, they remain feverish. At the end of each month the victim remains ill, they lose a point of Stamina; if they are reduced to zero Stamina they die. If the Nosferatu knows of their contagion and infects someone maliciously, the Storyteller might call for a Humanity (or appropriate Path of Morality) degeneration roll if they die. Supernatural creatures generally have the ability to heal or cure such sickness in themselves, and vampires are immune to these germs of the dead.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"163"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FPzwkjjKdkUAiTtA"},"adv:v20-enemy-brood":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Enemy Brood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have made an enemy of another group of Nosferatu. Unlike your usual enemies, they know the places you like to go and aren’t too squeamish about following you there. They keep you on the move, hunting you from sanctuary to sanctuary. The sewers themselves might not even be safe for you anymore. If you move to a new city, they use their contacts to pass on the word to their allies to keep hounding you. Sooner or later, you are going to have to take them down to rid yourself of them.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"163"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.PLgrhDBJDo518JG6"},"adv:v20-incoherent":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Incoherent","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>Human speech is impossible for you. It might be that your mouth is too misshapen after the Embrace, or that years of living in the sewers have made you forget how to communicate. While you can understand what is being said to you, you cannot respond. Telepathy works on you as normal, and you have no problem communicating with animals, but human speech is barred to you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"163"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lHMJ25cj05SoRk2j"},"adv:v20-putrescent":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Putrescent","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/nosferatu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Nosferatu may take this Flaw.</p><p>The supernatural process that usually keeps a vampire’s form from rotting after death has failed to work on you. Your body has become putrescent and fragile as it gradually decays. All soak rolls you make have their dice pool reduced by 1. You may even lose body parts if you suffer a solid enough blow. Should this happen, make a Stamina roll (difficulty 6) and lose a part of your body (Storyteller’s choice) if you fail. Should you botch, you also receive a level of aggravated damage. These missing parts may regrow, but your body continues to rot.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"163"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gtGdLMXekkwzWzM4"},"adv:v20-brahmin":{"name":"• Brahmin","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>As a member of the Brahmin jati, you are a priest, artist, teacher, or other pillar of society. Perhaps you keep the lore of the Clan, or perhaps you act as an advisor to other Ravnos in need of wisdom. Once per session, if you fail an Academics or Expression roll, you may immediately reroll it. You do not have to purchase this Merit to be a member of the Brahmin jati, but only members of the Brahmin jati may have this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CDokUVMZoo8QO57V"},"adv:v20-chandala":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Chandala","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Flaw.</p><p>Being a member of the lowest jati, the Chandala, is a mixed bag. You are responsible for the disposal of corpses, as well as many other foul tasks, but at the same time, you are often ignored. Your Social rolls against other Ravnos are made at a +2 difficulty. You do not have to take this Flaw to be a member of the Chandala jati, but only members of the Chandala jati may have this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.K1PEiOc4N5JqU09f"},"adv:v20-critters":{"name":"•• Critters","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>You’re excellent with animals — so much so that they constantly seek to befriend you. Wherever you go, the animals are happy to see you, and more often than not, happy to help you when you ask for their aid. You receive a bonus die on Social rolls to affect small animals. Further, animal companions who have had continual interaction with you see you as something of a pet, and occasionally bring you small useful things. Once per game session, animals will bring you a useful piece of information or a small item relevant to events. This item might occasionally play into the individual Ravnos’ particular vice, as the animals quickly pick up on what pleases their friend.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Vc2T6sw3IE4a1YTG"},"adv:v20-flawed-reality":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Flawed Reality","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your illusions always contain a notable flaw, and as such, are easier to disbelieve. The difficulties of all rolls to disbelieve your illusions are reduced by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qwUCYCGV1o76OaVO"},"adv:v20-heart-of-needles":{"name":"••• Heart of Needles","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>Your natural abilities with illusions have rendered you particularly jaded and unimpressible. How can anything be as perfect as your own imagination? Because of this, your heart is harder than most, and you have a significant resistance to emotion control. All powers and Social challenges that attempt to manipulate you emotionally are made at a +2 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.uQ4iOjYah3jVUUbG"},"adv:v20-kshatriya":{"name":"• Kshatriya","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>You are a member of the Kshatriya jati; perhaps you are a warrior, a descendant of rulers, or a member of the military. Your role is to protect the Ravnos in war, and govern in peace. Once per session, you regain a Willpower when you successfully use a combat maneuver (see V20, pp. 274-278). You do not have to purchase this Merit to be a member of the Kshatriya jati, but only members of the Kshatriya jati may have this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.eKdhXtDfZnxwKIMC"},"adv:v20-legerdemain":{"name":"• Legerdemain","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>You’re extremely good at sleight of hand and other physical tricks. Difficulties when using Subterfuge for physical trickery, shell games, card tricks, and so forth, are decreased by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GlOSwvPxmyDUvCxB"},"adv:v20-mute-devotion":{"name":"• Mute Devotion","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>Your Animalism carries an unusual side effect: it lingers in the minds of the beasts you speak with or control, lending them a certain resistance to others. When someone else attempts to command a creature you have previously controlled with Animalism, their difficulty levels are at +2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.i7X6EYCXBhBtK6yT"},"adv:v20-oathbreaker-ravnos":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Oathbreaker","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Flaw.</p><p>Making an oath ties one person’s svadharma to another, linking the two spirits until the oath can be fulfilled. With this in mind, a Ravnos never breaks her word once given in good faith, and so long as it was done with proper ceremony. If the Ravnos spits into her palm and shakes on her word, then the oath cannot be broken without negatively impacting the vampire’s svadharma (or so Ravnos superstition dictates). The oathbreaker will lose her way, falling into vice and worthlessness, until the broken oath can be redeemed. Anyone who looks at your aura can see a sickly red slash indicating the broken oath. You may not spend Willpower to ignore your Ravnos vice, and you do not gain Willpower from fulfilling your Nature.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AHJUeang8LDGsa0g"},"adv:v20-vaishya":{"name":"• Vaishya","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>The Vaishya jati are tasked with utilizing influence, maintaining the human herds, and keeping finances. They are often seen as traders and merchants, but to the Ravnos, they are also a critical part of maintaining the Masquerade that hides vampires from mortal hunters. Once per session, you may call on of your Backgrounds as if you had an extra dot in that Background (up to the normal maximum of 5). You do not have to purchase this Merit to be a member of the Vaishya jati, but only members of the Vaishya jati may have this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"182"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xDepeW6wOLTnVfwv"},"adv:v20-lost-svadharma":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Lost Svadharma","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Flaw.</p><p>You once knew your svadharma, but when the time came to fulfill it, you failed. Now that destiny has passed you by, and there may never be another chance to make it right. Other Ravnos know the tale, and hold your failure against you. You are scorned, and your confidence has been thrown into doubt. Your total Willpower score is permanently reduced by one and you may not spend Willpower when performing actions where other Ravnos are directly involved.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"183"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Rv3ewdCJJTrKuldN"},"adv:v20-impressive-restraint":{"name":"•• Impressive Restraint","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Toreador may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Humanity only.</p><p>When you haven’t eaten, it can be torturous to be near mortals. The pounding thrum of blood through their veins does not leave you nearly as tempted as it might other Kindred. When opportunity presents itself, the difficulty of all Self-Control rolls to resist hunger are made against a -2 difficulty. Characters on Paths of Enlightenment that require Instinct cannot take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"200"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ML9AqRreLp3tn5tN"},"adv:v20-master-of-the-masquerade":{"name":"•• Master of the Masquerade","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Toreador may take this Merit.</p><p>There are many small tics, nervous habits, and autonomous bodily functions (like breathing) that Kindred simply forget to do. They can be unnervingly still or forget to breathe, particularly when they think they’re alone. You never let down your guard. The act of breathing remains unconscious habit to you, and you never lapse into that eerie statue-like stillness, even when transfixed or concentrating. Consequently, the difficulties of all Social rolls are lowered by one when interacting with mortals. This Merit does not allow you to eat food or benefit from the blush of health (V20, p. 480) — those Merits are still required to be a true master of the Masquerade.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"200"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FiYuBD24GqUajpTv"},"adv:v20-private-life":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Private Life","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Toreador may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have a completely separate life that no other Kindred know about. It may be your mortal family that you have turned into ghouls, or a YouTube channel that you use to talk in metaphor about your frustrations with Kindred society. If discovered, this could risk other vampires accusing you of breaching the Masquerade (if Camarilla), consorting with humanity instead of being a superior vampire (if Sabbat), or just overall paranoia and suspicion on why you’re keeping such deep secrets.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"200"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CehBhLPjz86HoOFI"},"adv:v20-slowed-degeneration":{"name":"••••• Slowed Degeneration","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Toreador may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Humanity only.</p><p>Your Humanity is strong and can more easily withstand the Beast’s assaults. You gain two additional dice on any Conscience roll. This degree of moral resilience allows a well-behaved vampire to lose Humanity at a much slower rate than would otherwise be possible. Only vampires following Humanity may take this Merit, and the Merit is lost forever in the event that the vampire takes up another Path of Enlightenment.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"200"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gjAHB3dlfFdINtw9"},"adv:v20-tortured-artist":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Tortured Artist","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/toreador.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Toreador may take this Flaw.</p><p>Nothing is ever good enough for your work. No matter how much praise is lavished upon you, you can only see the flaws and mistakes. This leads to long periods of ennui, which makes your artistic work irregular at best. Further, you throw yourself into business arrangements and social situations with intensity, which often leads to heartbreak, which leads to the pain and passion that fuels your next work. You are at +1 difficulty on Social rolls in which you are being praised, complimented, or treated with respect.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"200"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bKhNQ3j4zkfLhhjj"},"adv:v20-embraced-without-the-cup":{"name":"• Embraced without the Cup","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>For some reason you did not drink the blood of the elders when you were inducted into Clan Tremere. More dangerously, it may have had no effect on you. As a result, you are not bound to the Clan the way most Tremere are. Should it be discovered, it will usually be corrected, but one might almost believe there was some purpose to this lapse, as the Tremere don’t make mistakes. Perhaps the Clan has a special task in mind for you, one where you might be forced to act against the Clan to maintain a cover….</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"218"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CiPAhKZv3rffzQml"},"adv:v20-keys-to-the-library":{"name":"Keys to the Library","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Costs the chantry’s Library rating.</p><p>You have one of the most sought after positions in the chantry: a librarian. It is one of your duties to catalogue and maintain the magical lore kept in your chantry. This means you have complete access to it, and get to decide who can see it and who can’t. A vast array of rituals and Thaumaturgical knowledge resides here, making it simple to learn many of the secrets of magic. No matter how restrictive your chantry, you have complete access to the library for research. The cost of this merit is the same as the chantry’s Library rating (see pp. 221-222).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"218"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.H4mJqKPOXi0z1G8t"},"adv:v20-secret-society-member":{"name":"• Secret Society Member","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>You have found and joined one of the many secret societies in Clan Tremere. Your character must be suitable to join, such as having Necromancy to join the Covenant. You might pick one of the societies listed here or create one of your own. In most cases, your society membership should be kept secret, but your allegiance to it is not considered a crime. While your society expects you to uphold its tenets and agenda, they can also be counted upon to back you up and help you increase your power within the pyramid.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"218"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vIDkoPYnkYXNFBQq"},"adv:v20-arcane-curse":{"name":"Flaw: Arcane Curse","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>Because of either your studies or someone else’s, you suffer from a magical curse. It might be an aversion or allergy, or even a strange magical effect. The level of the flaw depends on how debilitating the curse is. Curing the affliction may be impossible, or require some sort of quest or advanced research.</p><p><strong>1 pt</strong> A minor oddity, such as an animal feature or strange eye color.</p><p><strong>2 pts</strong> A noticeable problem, such as your magic having a strange taint or pattern that makes it instantly recognizable, or that plants wither in your presence.</p><p><strong>3 pts</strong> Something problematic, such as people sickening in your presence or animals attacking you.</p><p><strong>4 pts</strong> Concerning handicap, such as developing another Clan’s weakness.</p><p><strong>5 pts</strong> Potentially deadly, such as moonlight being as dangerous to you as sunlight.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qTdLGhY1FvMdKXtU"},"adv:v20-betrayers-mark":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Betrayer’s Mark","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>Even though you are loyal to House and Clan Tremere, for some reason you are branded with the mark of the antitribu (see the sidebar on p. 216). It might be that you have returned to the Clan after leaving the Sabbat or that you unwittingly or as part of an undercover operation took part in the Vaulderie. Whatever the reason, the mark makes other Tremere wary of you. You will have to go that extra mile to prove yourself, and few among the Clan will trust you with positions of power and responsibility.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VJ4mJJN81M2ZZw6E"},"adv:v20-bound-to-the-clan":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Bound to the Clan","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have done more than just drink from the blood of the elders. Whether as punishment or by choice, you have become fully blood bound to Clan Tremere. You cannot act against the Clan, and find everything you do works in the service of the Tremere. You might not like it, but you can’t help yourself doing it. Of course, now you are bound to the Clan, you cannot be bound to another individual. Many older Tremere have this Flaw, as it has been common in the past to completely blood bind new Tremere.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.JvgYOeJbM9eFmnar"},"adv:v20-cloistered":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Cloistered","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>You have spent almost all of your undead existence in the halls of the chantry, making Kindred society rather new and confusing for you. You suffer a -2 dice penalty to any social interactions with those outside the Tremere.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Qhv9EVyNu3VGD5uB"},"adv:v20-mage-blood":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Mage Blood","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your blood is so tied to magic that you find you are unable to use any Discipline apart from Thaumaturgy (and for Kindred who are not Tremere, it is still paid for at out-of-Clan rates). While no path or ritual is barred to you, you may not gain any dots in any other Discipline.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ihDyjaqKydlIF9qF"},"adv:v20-outside-haven":{"name":"•• Outside Haven","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>You maintain your own private haven outside the chantry and Tremere control. Most Tremere are expected to rest in the chantry, where the Clan can keep an eye on them. However, you have been trusted with a little more privacy. This might be because you have already proved your loyalty, or perhaps because they are testing it.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ExDZibRdsDvK1jXM"},"adv:v20-quartermaster":{"name":"••• Quartermaster","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>You are one of the Kindred responsible for maintaining and organizing the chantry’s mundane supplies. You may take anything from the chantry’s stores as defined by the chantry’s Stores rating. While you will have to return or replace anything you borrow, you have access the whole range of equipment appropriate to the size of the chantry. This might range from assault weapons and explosives to advanced medical and scientific equipment.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xcyWgIJslx5akyYX"},"adv:v20-thaumaturgically-inept":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Thaumaturgically Inept","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Flaw.</p><p>Something about you refuses to respond to Thaumaturgy. Magic just doesn’t work for you. You cannot take any ability in the Thaumaturgy Discipline or any of its paths or rituals. For a Tremere this is doubly difficult, as skill in Thaumaturgy is often the key to position within the Clan. While some willingly choose to ignore their thaumatugical studies and serve in other ways, ineptitude is never excused. Only Tremere can take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kjm2A83IPenVudDV"},"adv:v20-unmarked-antitribu":{"name":"Unmarked Antitribu","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tremere.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tremere may take this Merit.</p><p>Only Sabbat vampires may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Tremere antitribu; 2 pts Telyavelic, 5 pts others.</p><p>While you are part of the Sabbat and a traitor to House and Clan Tremere, somehow you remain unmarked by the antitribu curse. You are not easily recognized as a renegade Tremere, and the magic that burned so many of your brethren cannot target you. Further, other Sabbat members cannot judge you at a glance. Those of the Telyavelic bloodline can purchase this Merit at 2 points, while other Tremere pay 5 points.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"219"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AGwyude2QtxyYqHv"},"adv:v20-bioluminescence":{"name":"• Bioluminescence","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Vicissitude •+.</p><p>Perhaps through biological expertise, or perhaps by unlocking something primordial within, you have accessed the secrets of bioluminescence. Using the Vicissitude, you may grant yourself (through Malleable Visage) or others (via Fleshcraft — both as per V20, p. 241) the ability to emit a soft glow. With muscle control and practice, you can control the color and pattern of the illumination. This can light a soft glow in the dark, create beautiful displays, or even act as a primal form of communication. Some Fiends grant their ghouls or childer bioluminescence, developing an eerily nuanced and wordless language with their thralls and broods. Only characters with at least one dot of Vicissitude may purchase this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"238"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.auI5voan53B11NFi"},"adv:v20-dracons-temperament":{"name":"••• Dracon’s Temperament","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Merit.</p><p>You emulate the ideal of Azi Dahaka within and without, to levels visceral and abstract. Your psyche flows with the permutable nature of change. Like the protean Dracon, you are a whirlwind of temperaments. This is not multiple personalities. You are one identity shown through the prism of ever-shifting Natures. No anchor fetters your sense of self. You can be any you. At the start of each story, you may choose one Personality Archetype to function as your Nature, spending the rest of the story perceiving the world through that perspective. You also regain Willpower according to your new Nature and may be affected by other effects or Discipline powers as per your new Nature as well.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"238"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.P6QOXhJ9e5kw7mfr"},"adv:v20-haven-affinity":{"name":"••• Haven Affinity","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Merit.</p><p>You are the land. The land is you. The home soil calls to you. You give to it, and it gives to you. Your connection to the earth of your prime haven grants you an extra die to all dice pools when operating there. It also acts as a mystic beacon, allowing you to home in on its location with a standard Perception + Survival roll (difficulty 6), +1 difficulty when a state or country separates you; +2 if you’re halfway across the globe. This applies only to your primary haven.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"238"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ONsV8aogLz9ZOiSg"},"adv:v20-promethean-clay":{"name":"••••• Promethean Clay","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Vicissitude •+.</p><p>Your flesh ripples and molds itself to your preternatural will, almost before you consciously invoke the change. The difficulty to use any Vicissitude power on yourself is two less than normal, and you may activate Vicissitude powers reflexively at your full dice pool while taking other actions. Powers that require multiple turns to activate still require the usual duration. The change simply occurs without conscious direction. As a final benefit, you need no physical sculpting to use the first three levels of Vicissitude on yourself, as your flesh undulates and extrudes to its desired shape. Only characters with at least one dot of Vicissitude may purchase this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"238"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vBRHDsrdKe6uFbTE"},"adv:v20-ancestral-soil-dependence":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Ancestral Soil Dependence","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your flesh yearns for a homeland you have never seen. The voice of Kupala punishes your day sleep if this yearning is not met. The soil from a place important to you as a mortal will not suffice; in addition, you require two handfuls of the tainted Eastern European soil of the ancestral Tzimisce homeland. This Trait mostly commonly manifests in the childer of koldun and the branch of the Clan thought to be descended from Yorak. It can even manifest in childer sired generations after their ancestors relocated. Characters Embraced in Eastern Europe can’t take this Flaw (they’re already dependent on the local soil).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"239"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HFkcBb4fw1KUFMB1"},"adv:v20-consumption":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Consumption","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Flaw.</p><p>There is something hungry inside of you. But what is it? Portions of the Antediluvian? Your Vicissitude gone horribly wrong? Whatever it is, it is active, acting like a cancer, devouring you from the inside out. Your very blood is wrought with corrosive, flesh-eating bacteria. At the beginning of each evening, you suffer one health level of lethal damage that cannot be soaked nor healed with blood. The only way to counteract the effect is by ingesting one-tenth of your body-weight in flesh to supplement your depleted carcass. Whether you kill and devour the skin from humans or raid the biohazard containers of liposuction clinics for siphoned fat, you need your ration of human flesh in order to survive. If you try and ingest this macabre meal before damage is done, you’ll simply vomit it out like any other food — this does not impart the benefits of the Eat Food Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"239"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aRTzWKsGb4EuNM32"},"adv:v20-faceless":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Faceless","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Flaw.</p><p>Requirement: Vicissitude •+.</p><p>You have escaped the tyranny of physical self-identity. Call no face your own. Every sunset, you awaken to a new visage, an amalgam pieced together from memory and dream. Your features, ethnicity, even gender become fluid things while you sleep. While this Flaw exemplifies the extreme ideals of Azi Dahaka (and should impress most Metamorphosists), it can cause complications for vampire concerned with maintaining recognition, reputation, or a mortal alias. Every sundown, the player rolls one die and is subject to the results on the following table:</p><p><strong>1</strong> No change.</p><p><strong>2–3</strong> Minor changes, somewhat recognizable.</p><p><strong>4–8</strong> Unrecognizable by even those closest to the character (such as her packmates or sire). Ethnicity and gender may change.</p><p><strong>9–10</strong> Total metamorphosis. Tentacles, bone spikes, or other inhuman features may appear.</p><p>This Flaw affects some Backgrounds (V20, p. 110). Without a stable face, Alternate Identity and Fame cannot be maintained. Backgrounds like Allies, Contacts, Influence, and Status might be complicated by the lack of a solid identity as well. The vampire can use Malleable Visage and a mirror to reconstruct her true face from memory, but this requires at least three successes at difficulty 8 to make her recognizable, and five successes for a flawless copy. The Mistaken Identity Flaw (V20, p. 450) cannot be taken. Other Merits/Flaws may be affected, at the Storyteller’s discretion. Characters must have at least one dot of Vicissitude to take this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"239"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ckGEsJxZZrWUYrIp"},"adv:v20-privacy-obsession":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Privacy Obsession","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Flaw.</p><p>Perhaps it is a trait carried in the blood. Perhaps your strict sire carved this lesson into your mind and flesh. Either way, you carry the Tzimisce respect for privacy to extremes. You must make a Willpower roll (difficulty 6) to enter another being’s dwelling without being invited, though you can go to fiendishly clever lengths to garner an unwitting invitation. When disturbed in your haven by an uninvited guest, you must make a Self-Control or Instincts roll (difficulty 7) to avoid frenzy.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"239"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5KPAdEGdWJPYQnRZ"},"adv:v20-revenant-weakness":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Revenant Weakness","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Flaw.</p><p>You were once part of a revenant family. Following the Embrace, you suffered both your Clan’s weakness and your revenant family’s limitation (V20, pp. 503-506). The Storyteller might let you manifest a weakness from a lost or destroyed revenant line. This could add mystery to your background and allow for a bit of genealogical detective work, certainly making you a curiosity to the Romanian Legacy Foundation.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"239"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.EYoIH9LAIHv1GWzb"},"adv:v20-unblinking":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Unblinking","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/tzimisce.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Tzimisce may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your eyes do not close. Ever. Perhaps you have left your humanity too far behind to upkeep such habits, or perhaps you fleshcrafted some form of transparent eye-scale or nictitating membrane. Your quirk probably makes astute observers uncomfortable, adding +1 to the difficulty of friendly social interactions with humans, Kindred on Humanity, and others with mortal sensibilities.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"239"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.W9o54SMHSZrvpCT9"},"adv:v20-blessed-by-st-gustav":{"name":"•••• Blessed by St. Gustav","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ventrue-antitribu.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ventrue Antitribu may take this Merit.</p><p>Only Sabbat vampires may take this Merit.</p><p>Many Ventrue antitribu replace their traditional affinity for Presence with an aptitude for Auspex by means of the ignobilis ritus known as the Prayer to St. Gustav. For your piety and devotion to the Sabbat cause, you have been especially blessed and have an affinity for both Disciplines.</p><p><strong>System:</strong> This Merit is identical to Additional Discipline Merit found on V20, p. 494, except that it can only be used to add Auspex as a fourth in-Clan discipline. Only Ventrue antitribu can take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"261"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.L1KvYcGF2kWJdmGs"},"adv:v20-connoisseur":{"name":"•• Connoisseur","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ventrue.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ventrue may take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Auspex ••+.</p><p>Your study of the Auspex Discipline, combined with your rarified tastes, allows you powerful insights into the character of any whose blood you taste.</p><p><strong>System:</strong> The character tastes another’s blood (potentially risking blood bond), and player rolls Perception + Empathy (difficulty 6 for mortals or 8 for Kindred). If the blood came from a mortal, each success allows him to learn one of the following: the mortal’s Nature, her Demeanor, any Derangements she may possess, whether she is blood bonded, and whether she carries any blood-borne diseases. If the vitae came from a vampire, he can learn all of the previous information, plus anything discoverable with the first level of the Path of Blood (see V20, p. 213). The Ventrue may taste the blood of a mortal who does not fit within his feeding restriction long enough to use this ability, but he must immediately spit it out afterwards. Vampires that do not have Auspex •• cannot take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"261"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.egPnFV5t6zEYqwjr"},"adv:v20-uncommon-vitae-preference":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Uncommon Vitae Preference","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ventrue.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ventrue may take this Flaw.</p><p>Your preferred source for vitae is rarified even by the standards of your Clan, such as “only Korean War vets,” “only Federal Court Judges,” or “only virgins over the age of 40.” The difficulty of all hunting rolls for your character is increased by +2, to a maximum of 9.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"261"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fAgC1i6mVMDdEJ7q"},"adv:v20-fangless":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Fangless","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Considered the mark of a true mongrel, you never developed your fangs, or you lacked teeth before the Embrace. Either way, you have to use a knife or otherwise drink from bleeding wounds. You have no natural way beyond Disciplines to inflict aggravated damage.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"269"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TBo367sDYcqHahFA"},"adv:v20-personal-masquerade":{"name":"••• Personal Masquerade","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Merit.</p><p>Thorough charm, manipulation, or just plain luck, you have managed to convince other vampires that you are a member of one of the Clans. Any social interactions with vampires ignorant of your true nature ignore your usual penalty for being Caitiff. However, you must constantly be vigilant of your ruse. Should anyone come to realize you have been playing them for fools, their vengeance will be swift. The higher you climb in Kindred politics, the more likely this becomes. The Clan Weakness Flaw can actually prove to be a boon in supporting your Masquerade. Others, however, must be that much more careful.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"269"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.azrvYG8U1hH0jek2"},"adv:v20-bulimia":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Bulimia","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>You hunger for blood, and like all vampires, you will eventually feed, but the thought of it still makes you sick. Maybe you’re just not cut out for an eternity of bloodsucking, or you had an eating disorder in life that has followed you to the other side of mortality. Whatever the reason, you can’t hold your blood. Whenever you feed, make a Stamina roll with a difficulty 8. If you fail, you vomit out the fresh blood before it can be absorbed into your body, spraying it everywhere (embarrassing at best, a breach of the Masquerade at worst). Note that blood taken in frenzy is absorbed normally, though this carries its own risks.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"270"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3Ugi8473jT8FGwup"},"adv:v20-ignorance":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Ignorance","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/clan-pairs/caitiff-panders.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Caitiff or Panders may take this Flaw.</p><p>Many sires abandon their Caitiff progeny without a word of instruction or warning about their new nature. For most Princes, ignorance is no excuse for a breach of the Masquerade, and Sabbat packs are quick to notice weakness. The character starts with no knowledge of the abilities, customs, or politics of the undead, and must learn from their mistakes or find a mentor. Of course, any vampire willing to take on a Caitiff will most likely blood bond the poor wretch, leading to a completely new set of problems. Ignorance is deadly for a vampire, and doubly so for the Clanless.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Lore of the Clans","page":"270"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.8fPIWpEDTKBI3sYC"},"adv:v20-alternate-sense":{"name":"••••••• Alternate Sense","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The character does not require vision because she possesses another sense with equal discrimination. Typical examples include a bat’s sonar or a bloodhound’s sense of smell. The character does not suffer the usual penalties from operating in total darkness, though specifically visual tasks (such as reading) remain impossible. The character can also identify other characters by non-visual means. Alternate Senses tend to “see through” Chimerstry illusions and Obfuscate concealment because few Cainites understand nonhuman or superhuman senses. When a character with Alternate Senses encounters a character who uses Chimerstry, Obfuscate or similar powers, use the system for “Seeing the Unseen” in Vampire: The Masquerade p. 152 — but treat the first character as having an Auspex rating two higher than it really is. For these purposes, a character who lacks Auspex entirely has an effective rating of two. Alternate Senses often show some connection to Auspex or to the body-altering Disciplines of Protean, Serpentis and Vicissitude. They also usually occur only in Cainites who suffer blindness or other extreme impairment of their normal senses.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Mexico City by Night (Revised)","page":"115"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.rbHPH5LyFTIUkZQa"},"adv:v20-kashaph":{"name":"• Kashaph","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Inconnu only.</p><p>The term kashaph is of ambiguous meaning, based on a Hebrew root word for “mutter,” and implying spoken sorcery and incantations. The term is used in the Old Testament to condemn the practitioners of witchcraft. True kashaph has long been extinct — save among the Inconnu. Kashaph is an enchanted language, and cannot be “decoded” by modern linguistics or cryptography. The Inconnu use kashaph to communicate secretly, and also as a method of identifying one another. New members of the Inconnu are not taught kashaph by normal means. It cannot be studied, nor learned like a conventional language. Instead, members are indoctrinated into the society through an elaborate ritual during which the language of kashaph is inscribed upon their soul. Once “learned” in this way, the language may be spoken, not written (there is no written form, and the sounds cannot be accurately captured by phonetic scribblings), and only another individual with this merit has the capacity to understand the words said in the language of kashaph.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Rites of the Blood","page":"100"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MTJs2V3iejF6K3Cv"},"adv:v20-blessed":{"name":"••••••• Blessed","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are favored by some higher power to the extent that the corrosive touch of Hell does not harm you. You are unaffected by the paths and rituals of Dark Thaumaturgy. Characters with this Merit may never learn Dark Thaumaturgy themselves, and they are affected by “normal” Thaumaturgy as any other character is. Storytellers, if a player wants to take this Merit without it fitting the character concept or having any rational reason, kick the stinking gumby out of your game.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Sins of the Blood (Revised)","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ehf3rORqQo0lDaWu"},"adv:v20-indomitable-soul":{"name":"••••••• Indomitable Soul","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In the horrific event of your diablerie, the Kindred who commits the act gains none of the benefits of diablerie (generation, Disciplines, etc.), but he does suffer the drawbacks (aura veins, loss of Humanity, etc.). Obviously, this won’t do you much good, but it is an excellent way to give one final “Screw you!” to your murderer. And the members of your former coterie can always avenge you…. A vampire with this Merit will never become a wraith. Instead, she goes into the unknowable beyond upon her diablerie.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Sins of the Blood (Revised)","page":"102"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.eZY63FuFnr9f881f"},"adv:v20-spoiled-beast":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Spoiled Beast","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>What the Beast has had before, it wants again, and it wants it now. Any time the player must make a Willpower roll in order for her character to resist a desire, she does so with only as many dice as she has in her Willpower pool, as opposed to her rating. That is, she rolls her current Willpower as opposed to her permanent Willpower. This Flaw can be particularly debilitating when it comes to staving off hunger, for example.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Sins of the Blood (Revised)","page":"103"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1Sfjdw9frOFShHai"},"adv:v20-unrepentant-beast":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Unrepentant Beast","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Beast leaves its mark on you long after it has run its course and had its way with you. If you ever botch a SelfControl (or Instinct) roll to resist (or ride the wave of) frenzy, you immediately acquire a compulsion derangement (see Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 222, listed under Obsessive/ Compulsive). The Storyteller should determine the nature of the particular compulsion, and you may indeed gain a wide and varied spectrum of compulsions.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Sins of the Blood (Revised)","page":"103"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ntM8YyfcH8IJGF9T"},"adv:v20-arcane":{"name":"Arcane","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Some vampires have a strange ability to slip from notice, which manifests as an aura of forgetfulness. Those trying to remember the vampire experience a sensation of jamais-vu. The ability may be deliberately developed, or the Kindred could simply be too ordinary to pay attention to. Though not similar to Obfuscate, Arcane doesn’t help in combat situations — the vampire can’t literally vanish. However, someone searching for them may well fail, as no one remembers the vampire or can give a reliable description. Each point taken subtracts one die from any dice pools used to actively locate the vampire or recall her from memory. Those with this Merit can choose to “turn off” Arcane if they wish. As a passive trait, it doesn’t help on Stealth rolls or other overt attempts to hide. If you have any dots in Status or Fame, Arcane ceases to function while you possess them.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"177"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.F2ygM8fgjAfrdpwa"},"adv:v20-berserker":{"name":"••• Berserker","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You possess the ability to willingly enter a berserker state for a scene. While berserking, you ignore wound penalties and reduce the difficulty of all combat rolls except for dodges by -3. You also can take no complex actions other than combat, dodging, or running.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"177"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CbqR3mSZqfk8EdGm"},"adv:v20-discerning-palate":{"name":"•••• Discerning Palate","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your naturally selective palate allows you to discern specific traits inherent to a sample of blood. With but a taste, you can determine potency, freshness, species, or whether a blood sample is contaminated, as well as attempt to identify the Generation, age, and even Clan of other vampires. If you sample even a single drop of blood, roll Perception + Awareness (difficulty 8).</p><p>• One success allows you to identify the relative potency and freshness of the blood, a species whose blood you have previously tasted and know to be of a specific kind, the Generation of another vampire within two steps of your own, if the blood is either under one hundred or over one hundred years old, and if it is of your same Clan.</p><p>• Two successes identifies whether the blood is contaminated by disease or poison, the Generations of vampires within four steps of your own, the approximate age within fifty years (if less than three hundred years old), and any Clan whose blood you’ve previously tasted.</p><p>• With three or more successes, you identify specific contaminates you’ve sampled before, Generations within six steps of your own, the vintage of blood within twenty-five years (if less than six hundred years old), and any Clan as well as family of vampires whose blood you’ve tasted previously.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"177"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.0Dj2EigkwOZ0OpYv"},"adv:v20-apostate":{"name":"•• Apostate","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Baali may not take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Not Baali.</p><p>Since their inception, Nergali Baali have subverted members of other Clans into their own ranks. Known as Apostates, initiates are first drained completely by a Baali nest-master, who then extracts the still-beating heart from a mortal victim, fills it with his own blood, and buries it in a pool of gore within a Well of Sacrifice. The initiate swims through the dismembered bodies and viscous remains to find and consume the heart. In this way, Nergali do not make a victim of those they Embrace; prospective members must claim it through strength of will. Apostates replace any one in-Clan Discipline with Daimonion, but in turn gain the Baali Clan weakness on top of their standard Clan weakness. Apostates can still pass with relative ease for members of their former Clans, but any childe they Embrace is indistinguishable from a standard Baali. Further, such a childe shares none of the Clan Disciplines or weaknesses of their sire’s former Clan, save those Disciplines that are innate to the Baali. Apostates do not lose levels in the Discipline they choose to exchange for Daimonion, but further levels in that Discipline are purchased at out-of-Clan costs. Baali may not take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"178"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ug1lvFCV2ThVucmu"},"adv:v20-entrepreneur":{"name":"•• Entrepreneur","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Making money comes naturally to you, and you know what it takes to succeed. All rolls involving acquiring money through business dealings have their difficulty reduced by 2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"178"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sFacSjLUzSQWxZEN"},"adv:v20-oathbreaker-talmahera":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Oathbreaker (V20)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Once you swore fealty to a lord or organization, or made a binding contract. You’ve broken that promise and everyone knows about it. Now it’s near impossible to earn the trust of others. The difficulty of Social rolls to convince someone of your trustworthiness is increased by three, and even members of your coterie may have reservations about you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"178"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.MiZD6tUVhUV2bDeE"},"adv:v20-paragon":{"name":"••••••• Paragon","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Others find you particularly compelling. Select one Background from the following group: Allies, Contacts, Domain, Fame, Herd, Influence, Mentor, Resources, Retainers, or Status. You receive one free dot in that Background, and your maximum Trait score in that Background may exceed normal Generational limits by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"178"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.4vyO8hhNSDnb6EZ0"},"adv:v20-vulgar":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Vulgar","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Tact is a foreign concept to you, and you’re famous for being abrasive and generally unpleasant. Rolls involving social graces or delicate situations suffer +1 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"178"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LTBUzQdMMzxt2Nd3"},"adv:v20-devils-mark":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Devil’s Mark","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Whether product of your Embrace or gained through exposure to infernal or other unholy power, you’ve been branded with the “Devil’s Mark,” an anatomical aberration that manifests the taint of the demonic. Possible deformities include, but are not limited to: bestial or inhuman eyes, hooves, horns, unnaturally colored or scaly skin, a birthmark in the form of a sigil, parasitic infestation, claws, misshapen teeth. or ineffectual (perhaps additional) limbs. You may never remove or “improve” these disfigurements, although magic or Disciplines can hide them from plain sight. A Devil’s Mark confers the following mechanical effects: Lower the maximum rating in one Attribute by one (if the maximum rating is 5, it becomes 4), while the cost to raise said Attribute increases by one (1 + the current rating x 4). If the mark becomes visible or is known to a mortal, it confers a -1 difficulty on Intimidation rolls when dealing with her, but in turn increases the difficulties for all other Social die rolls by one. Players should work with a Storyteller to determine an appropriate Devil’s Mark, and the Physical Attribute best suited to their mark.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vWN5cEZUNBoiAlGa"},"adv:v20-kiss-of-death":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Kiss of Death","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Should you drain a mortal vessel of all blood minutes after their heart stops beating, the corpse rises as a zombie (using the statistics for zombies on p. 164 of V20). These zombies are free-willed, hostile toward you, and cannot be directed without some sorcerous or necromantic means to command them.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.izUqAruF3SyuwGnj"},"adv:v20-lord-of-the-night":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Lord of the Night","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In your presence lights dim, tiny flames extinguish, and shadows cling to you or languidly move about with a surreal unlife, making it impossible to pass as anything other than inhuman. The particular shadow you cast is a malicious entity with a will of its own. It lashes out at friends and enemies alike, tearing at the scenery, and snarling at passers-by. These effects can be suppressed for a scene by spending a point of Willpower.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OHrCYQfHyWilPDjp"},"adv:v20-psychic-leech":{"name":"••••• Psychic Leech","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>At the cost of one Willpower point, you may feed on the Willpower of your victims from a distance. The consumed Willpower strengthens the potency of your own blood, effectively transforming it into temporary blood that dissipates at the end of the night. These temporary blood points may be spent just like regular blood points, but cannot be lost due to damage or blood drain, do not affect hunger rolls for frenzy, and may not be used to create or sustain blood bonds or ghouls. To activate this ability, the target must have at least one of your blood points in their system, and must be engaged in eye contact with you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aGJd7b79WnMJcakT"},"adv:v20-vassal-of-the-clan":{"name":"Flaw: (••••••) Vassal of the Clan","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Through natural predisposition or a curse, you bear a permanent one-point blood bond to those one Generation lower, a two-point blood bond with those two Generations lower, and a three-point blood bond to those three or more Generations lower than you within your own Clan. These blood bonds override any other blood bonds you may earn during play. This can be a great risk for Hand members, as the blood forces allegiances to potentially risky vampires, or worse, to heretical ideologies.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BEA6tSLz8mZ65G0u"},"adv:v20-without-a-trace":{"name":"•• Without a Trace","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When in the wilderness, the earth fills in your footprints. You leave no noticeable traces, not even a scent. Normal attempts at tracking automatically fail. Supernatural attempts are done with a +2 difficulty.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal’Mahe’Ra","page":"179"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.2ftjCEZnKh98e9Dr"},"adv:v20-bitter-blood":{"name":"• Bitter Blood","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Something in your genes or your diet makes your blood taste foul to vampires. Nothing is preventing a bloodsucker from bleeding you just for the fun of it, of course, but you’re the last vessel a vampire would willingly choose to drink from.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"27"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.dpik5vcYLN3BYBFg"},"adv:v20-night-vision":{"name":"•• Night Vision","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can see just fine at night and you’ve never understood why people have so much difficulty seeing just because it’s a little dark. You suffer no penalty to dice pools as long as you have at least light equivalent to moderate starlight. When going from brightly lit conditions to low-light conditions, it may take a turn or two for your eyes to adapt.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"27"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.WHUPTGRd5M3h8RPi"},"adv:v20-vampiric-recognition":{"name":"••• Vampiric Recognition","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can spot a vampire at 10 paces. You’ve learned to recognize some aspect of their undead nature — something that others seem oblivious to. Maybe it’s a slight metallic odor, or a learned tendency to look at a person’s neck to see if he has a pulse in his carotids. Whatever the case, you can tell a vampire from a mortal without difficulty. Certain complicated cases (such as vampires with the Blush of Health Merit) may require a Perception + Alertness roll (difficulty 6) for you to be sure.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"27"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OW2sqCHdlMRBySwp"},"adv:v20-vigorous":{"name":"••••• Vigorous","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You don’t need sleep. Your body may need to be still and rest for an hour or two every day, but you can’t “sleep” and don’t need to. The advantages to this are many, but primary among them is the ability to lead both a normal life during the day and a full and uncompromising nightlife.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"27"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.I27AcCNKnIVSrqzI"},"adv:v20-criminal-record":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Criminal Record","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re a convicted felon. You’ve done hard time in prison. You cannot legally buy firearms, and all social rolls with those who know your history are at +2 difficulty. Cops, in particular, are especially eager to screw you over, and you can totally kiss good-bye ever getting the benefit of any doubt from those who know you.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"28"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.E2coREQJPT0W6Njf"},"adv:v20-deluded":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Deluded","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re pretty savvy when it comes to the supernatural. Or so you think. Most of what you think you know is egregiously wrong. When you find out, for example, that silver bullets don’t in fact kill vampires (assuming you survive the experience), you’re likely to assume that it was something weird about that particular vampire, not that your “knowledge” was wrong.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"28"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Rsvz5gFEX50ttCoh"},"adv:v20-gullible":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Gullible","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re not necessarily stupid, but you are definitely far too trusting for the world you live in. Even obvious lies sound plausible to you. Increase by 2 the difficulty of all rolls to detect lies or chicanery.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"28"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.grrxWip0aYyQWQkA"},"adv:v20-insomniac":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Insomniac","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Getting to sleep is hard. Staying asleep is harder. You toss and turn and can’t sleep at night. Any amount of time you spend trying to sleep actually yields about half the normal amount of rest, leaving you bleary-eyed and mentally fuzzy. Increase by 2 the difficulty of all Perception rolls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"The Players Guide (Revised)","page":"28"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9JBxAewUz4PdeDWv"},"adv:v20-decrepitude":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Decrepitude","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>No thin-blooded vampire has felt the full force of this Flaw. None of them have existed long enough. In time, they will learn its full horror. The Curse of Caine holds most vampires in ageless stasis, repairing any damage they might suffer. In your case, the Curse doesn’t always succeed at fully repairing damage. When healing aggravated damage, the player must make a Stamina roll. The difficulty is 5 plus the number of levels of aggravated damage currently sustained (For instance, if you had taken 2 health levels of aggravated damage, the difficulty would be 7. After healing one level, the difficulty would drop to 6.) Failure means that, despite regaining the health level, your body still shows signs of damage. Perhaps burnt hair won’t regrow or a healed wound leaves a scar. Botching the roll means losing one dot of some Physical Attribute or Appearance, chosen by the Storyteller. You can repair this damage, if you know a skilled plastic surgeon — or mortician — who is privy to the Masquerade, but it’s not easy. After all, the surgeon has to do a fair bit of cutting too. On the one hand, you receive a difficulty of only 6 to heal the wounds caused by the surgeon. On the other hand, you might receive another botch and be worse off than before. As the decades pass and Attributes drop, decay sets in. Eventually you become a shambling, animate corpse. At such a point, Final Death may seem a mercy. See the 5-point physical Flaw “Flesh of the Corpse” in Vampire: The Masquerade for an even more severe version of this debility.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"78"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.pYxwoCjRSqp0Af27"},"adv:v20-hemetic":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Hemetic","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The very idea of drinking blood disgusts you and makes you physically ill… awkward for a vampire. In order to hold the blood within your system until you absorb it, you must make a Stamina roll at difficulty 8. Failure means the sudden, humiliating and extremely messy loss of all newly consumed blood points as you retch them out (the truly pathetic could carry a barf bag and try again). The only way to avoid nausea is to feed while frenzying: In that horrid state, with the Beast in full control, you can keep blood down. Aside from the social embarrassment frenzy can cause, however, it tends to endanger the Masquerade; you might as well phone up the local scourge and ask if he makes house calls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"78"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NmechhLAjbvr7rTQ"},"adv:v20-ravaging-years":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Ravaging Years","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>The curse of Caine did not grant you immortality. You still age, just very slowly — perhaps one mortal year for every 20 calendar years. Most of the time, neither you nor anyone else would notice, but eventually you will grow old and die. Sometimes your aging speeds up: Like a ghoul, your deferred aging can return in a rush. Whenever you heal aggravated wounds, you age a year in the course of a single day’s sleep. That may not sound like much, but over the course of a century it can really add up. It gives a great incentive to avoid sunlight, fire and the fangs of one’s fellow vampires.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"78"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qYdiUvhEl6e0zKL4"},"adv:v20-feeding-fetish":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Feeding Fetish","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You feel compelled to bite only a specific part of the body to feed. Attempting to bite a victim anywhere else forces a Willpower check (difficulty 6). The neck, of course, is most traditional, but the legendary Armenian vampire Dakhanavar bit only the soles of his victim’s feet.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"79"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.R3bsYPNeZ8U8LAOT"},"adv:v20-power-fetish":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Power Fetish","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You believe that much of your supernatural power depends on carrying a specific object (the Alp, a German vampire-spirit, needed its hat). Without that unique object, you must succeed at a Willpower check (difficulty 8) to activate any Discipline power.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"79"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TN4yG8MCJVsFmdS1"},"adv:v20-ward":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Ward","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>A mortal whom you want to protect depends on you in some way. They need a lot of protection, too: Wards have a way of getting sucked into awkward situations, forcing you to change your own plans or even put yourself in danger. No matter how many people depend on you, however, you can take this Flaw only once. A ward could be a relative, a lover, a friend or just about any mortal to whom one has a strong connection. Wards have no special influence or abilities they can offer in return (if they did, they would be Allies or Contacts). Does the Ward know that you are a vampire? Keeping a Ward from knowing requires great effort. You spend a lot more time with a Ward than with a casual friend, under more demanding circumstances. Sooner or later, you have to explain away strange events and behavior: Why she never sees you during the day, or why a mugger’s bullet knocked you down but didn’t make you bleed. Letting a Ward in on your biggest secret endangers the Masquerade, though.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"80"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.53ERDFWDCMcqkzx8"},"adv:v20-face-the-flames":{"name":"••• Face the Flames","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The weakness of your Beast renders you less susceptible to blind panic when faced with fire. You receive two extra dice on Rötschreck rolls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"81"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.aDeKCL16FMaDNbJI"},"adv:v20-supernatural-contact":{"name":"••• Supernatural Contact","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You know a werewolf, mage, fae, Kuei-jin, hedge magician or some other supernatural being. This does not mean that you are boon companions, only that the two of you don’t follow the usual kill-on-sight policy between supernaturals. If players want a more cordial relationship between characters of different races, they must develop it themselves in the course of play.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Time of Thin Blood (Revised)","page":"81"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.uvLELL5t8fmB13gl"},"adv:v20-blasphemous-pact":{"name":"•••••• Blasphemous Pact","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In your recent past or long ago, you knowingly made a pact with a being of unadulterated wickedness. Whether the being was a demon of immense power, a reaper wishing to claim your soul, or some greater monster seeking knowledge in exchange for its foul blessing, your pact resulted in your receipt of a gift. This gift allows you to stave off the Curse of Caine, to a degree. Perhaps your gift allows you the ability to walk in daytime while under cover. Maybe you can eat uncooked meat, still running with blood. The gift may allow you to breed. The dark nature of the pact means all this comes at a price. The entity with whom you consort will one night take everything you own, and bring it to ruin. It will destroy all relationships, any reputation, and any good you may achieve. It will claim your soul, and likely the souls of those close to you. Speak to the Storyteller about the nature of your gift. The Storyteller will decide on the price you must pay.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"108"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":6,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ccoj32jjxigJ4Ngt"},"adv:v20-profane-trappings":{"name":"•••• Profane Trappings","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You befoul everything you touch. Whether through intent or unfortunate happenstance, your Domain, Retainers, Herd, and trappings are all tainted with the unholy. While this pollutes your surroundings with a constant aura of nausea and unease, it does make any infernal acts easier to perform in their presence. By taking this Merit, any Disciplines you use are considered infernal; at the Storyteller’s discretion, using your Disciplines on land you hold, on servants in your employ, or with objects you possess, reduces the difficulty of the roll by –2.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"108"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ko71RPusB8zIHnRf"},"adv:v20-unholy-stain":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Unholy Stain","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your soul is stained with sacrilege. Some are born or Embraced this way; more earn the stain through wicked oaths and foul deeds. When interacting with any mortal, your FAITH Social difficulties increase by one, as people feel unnaturally ill at ease in your presence. Additionally, people of faith feel compelled to hostility when in your proximity. Their initial stance toward you is always aggressive, and it takes little to provoke them to violence. Mortals with True Faith will recognize you on sight, and often attack.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"108"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.qDQCx0u4S2Z0f3Wr"},"adv:v5-apocryphal-texts":{"name":"• Apocryphal Texts","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult member. Can also be taken as a 1-dot Flaw.</p><p>You possess the writings of one of your church’s founders or prominent figures. While they’re not official canon, the revelations within make you a sought-after expert. Gain two dice on related Intelligence rolls. Taken as a Flaw, the texts aren’t merely apocryphal, they’re considered dangerous by your cult’s leaders. On social combat rolls involving the writings, increase the Willpower damage modifier by 1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Apocryphal Texts (related to the texts)","value":2,"paths":["attributes.intelligence"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"95"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Rh4nFXRVRTF8deda"},"adv:v5-inspired-artist":{"name":"••• Inspired Artist","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult member.</p><p>You channel your divine fervor into art. You’re a painter, a singer, a poet, a sculptor, and your creations inspire all who experience it. Any artwork you craft consisting of your cult’s symbology or channeling its message has an entrancing effect similar to the Presence power of Awe (see Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 267), with onlookers being able to resist with a Composure + Intelligence roll (Difficulty 3).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"95"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Kr30lVwwvGLDepQD"},"adv:v5-travelling-preacher":{"name":"•• Travelling Preacher","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult member.</p><p>You spread your cult’s message wherever you go, despite the risks Kindred take when they travel. You’ve mapped out safe routes, established good places to go to ground along the way, and learned where surveillance systems falter. When traveling along established routes, reduce the Difficulty on rolls to avoid the Second Inquisition’s or Hunters’ notice by 1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"96"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.jsaHgBpbZUHOqJzE"},"adv:v5-excommunicated":{"name":"Flaw: Excommunicated","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Former cult member.</p><p>Once, you belonged. Something you did caused the leadership to cast you out and make you persona non grata among your peers. They’ve stripped your access to the cult’s support systems and resources. At two dots, subtract two dice from all rolls dealing with the cult or its members. At five dots, cultists actively try to destroy you: tanking your finances, undermining your reputation, and even calling a Blood Hunt if you dare enter their territory.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Excommunicated (dealing with the cult or its members (at two dots))","value":-2,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"96"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lk5qpXqSI3RoMgAX"},"adv:v5-faithless":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Faithless","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult member.</p><p>You don’t actually believe any of the cult’s doctrines. You’re only in it for the benefits, but true progress and power require a degree of commitment and you’re not willing to subject yourself to indoctrination. Keeping up appearances requires an exhausting degree of vigilance; it’s only a matter of time before someone asks you to prove your faith. Characters with this Flaw lose two dice on all Resolve rolls associated with acting for the cult and may learn no powers, Rituals, or purchase Loresheets associated with this cult higher than Level 2.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Faithless (acting for the cult)","value":-2,"paths":["attributes.resolve"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"96"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.M4WXiySKWPBdebMn"},"adv:v5-influencer-ashfinders":{"name":"•• Influencer (Ashfinders)","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Ashfinders.</p><p>You’re a social media sensation, with a follower count in the hundreds of thousands — minimum — hanging on your every post. They take your advice, purchase the products you hawk, and rally to any cause you point them towards. Once per story, you can call on them to perform a simple nonviolent action for you. It may be something like donating money to a crowdfunding campaign, flooding an elected official’s phone lines, or lighting a candle in their window on a certain night. Note that this only acts as a Merit for as long as your influencer status and actions are those of a mortal. If you start communicating Kindred secrets via the internet, you will earn a two dot Adversary Background (representing the vampire sent to silence you) and a two dot Enemy Background (representing mortal scrutiny, probably in the form of an Inquisitor).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"96"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.7O20NgbnBt7MTB15"},"adv:v5-memories-of-the-fallen":{"name":"•• Memories of the Fallen","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/thinblooded.webp","system":{"description":"<p>These Merits and Flaws only apply to thin-blood characters. They have no dot value; each Flaw balances a Merit, and vice versa. They thus do not count against the maximum dots in Advantages and Flaws allowed to be selected during character creation or purchased through experience.</p><p>Requirement: Ashfinders.</p><p>Partaking of Ashe grants the user memories of the Kindred who died to make the drug. You’re extremely receptive to the visions you have under its influence, keenly feeling the echoes of their power and experiences. On Blood Alchemy rolls relating to Ashe, 9s count as 10s.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.YVuobjUJSJOsxt3c"},"adv:v5-ashe-addiction":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Ashe Addiction","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Ashfinders.</p><p>The need to relive other Kindred’s memories and wield their power is a constant clamour in your mind. In addition to the Ashe Addiction rules in Cults of the Blood Gods (p. 46), after a failed Blood Alchemy roll, the character suffers a two dice penalty to all actions for the rest of the chapter.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Ashe Addiction (rest of the chapter after a failed Blood Alchemy roll)","value":-2,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.by7mfNQRrw7NgCqN"},"adv:v5-gardener":{"name":"Gardener","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Bahari.</p><p>As a faithful Ba’ham, you lead a Garden of your own. You guide new initiates, lead rituals, and direct those who walk in your Garden to carry out the Dark Mother’s will. This Merit acts as a selective form of the Herd and Influence Backgrounds, where the adherents (mortal and Kindred) feed you willingly, providing they receive the religious teachings and guidance of the Bahari, and you gain the region specified as territory. If ever you fall out of favor with the cult, this Merit goes with your reputation.</p><p>• Small Garden — 1-5 mortal adherents, located in a small, private space. The garden is no bigger than a few potted plants or containers.</p><p>•• Growing Garden — 5-10 mortal adherents, 1-2 other Kindred, located in a slightly bigger private space. This may be a rooftop garden in the city or a backyard greenhouse in a rural area.</p><p>••• Community Garden — 10-30 mortal adherents, 3-5 other Kindred. The garden is a larger plot of land, about an acre. This may be part of a city park or a town green.</p><p>•••• Sprawling Garden — 30-50 mortal adherents, up to a dozen Kindred. The garden covers a large swath of land: an entire city park, an orchard in the country.</p><p>••••• Major Garden — over 50 mortal adherents, more than a dozen Kindred, several of whom may branch out into Gardens of their own and oversee Rituals. The garden’s territory covers an entire city, or several towns in rural areas.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.P1DnCTHkLWCyZO3E"},"adv:v5-dark-mothers-song":{"name":"•• Dark Mother’s Song","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Bahari.</p><p>Your words make the listener’s pain and grief resonate like a struck harp. You drive them to deeper and deeper insights and plant the seeds of justice and vengeance in their hearts. Add three dice to Manipulation rolls when you’re convincing others to take up the worship of Lilith.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Dark Mother’s Song (converting others to the worship of Lilith)","value":3,"paths":["attributes.manipulation"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.p96an0yx3x11G5HE"},"adv:v5-fire-resistant":{"name":"• Fire Resistant","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Church of Caine.</p><p>You’ve participated in the Sacrament of Firewalking (Cults of the Blood Gods, p. 64) several times. As a result, your body is able to withstand exposure to flames outside of the ritual setting. You may take convert Aggravated Health damage to Superficial Health damage, up to your Blood Potency in Health boxes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.k9Qs8nsL076tgT50"},"adv:v5-schism":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Schism","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/lasombra.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Lasombra may take this Flaw.</p><p>Requirement: Church of Caine.</p><p>One of your great-grandsires was among the members of the Night Clan participating in purging the Church of Caine several centuries ago. Others within the Crimson Curia eye you warily, lest you share your predecessor’s views in modern nights. Suffer a two dice penalty on Social rolls with other members of your cult.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Schism (members of your cult)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QScAJHCnhW0r8WN4"},"adv:v5-fixer":{"name":"•• Fixer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Church of Set.</p><p>With little more than a brief conversation, you know what people want. Even when — especially when — it’s something they ought not have. You procure questionable goods for your clients. If word of such acquisitions got out, they’d be ruined. You keep their secrets because it’s good business, but sometimes that leverage comes in handy. Once per story, call in a favor from or threaten to expose a former client who has something you need. This may be using their political pull, granting access to restricted files, or even require the client to perform a criminal act. Once you’ve called in a favor from this client, they can’t be leveraged in this way again.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"97"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CdS2Svnxt4eeVit6"},"adv:v5-go-to-ground":{"name":"• Go to Ground","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Church of Set.</p><p>The Church of Set teaches its members to be mobile, ready to slip away into the shadows if persecutors come calling. You’re prepared to disappear with a moment’s notice: exit routes mapped out, go bag prepared. Add two dice to rolls to evade pursuit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Go to Ground (evading pursuit)","value":2,"paths":["skills.athletics","skills.stealth","skills.drive"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RfPRSqXfwSjuo73m"},"adv:v5-vigilant":{"name":"•• Vigilant","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Church of Set.</p><p>The Church of Set has an unfair reputation for paranoia, when in fact they instill in every initiate the need to keep eyes in the back of one’s head, having been subject to persecution for centuries. Staying alert keeps the cult safe. Once per chapter, if the Storyteller is having an SPC surveil you or about to ambush you, they will ask you to make a Wits + Awareness roll (Difficulty variable, dependent on whether the person or device monitoring you is hidden, and if a person, how many successes they roll on Dexterity or Wits + Stealth). Success allows you to detect the person or thing keeping you under watch. Failure means you don’t spot the spy, but you do still possess the feeling of being watched.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LFgvUKqArIsEfl3p"},"adv:v5-false-alarms":{"name":"Flaw: (••) False Alarms","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Church of Set.</p><p>The hairs on the back of your neck stand up. Your skin crawls. You feel the weight of unseen eyes upon you, even when you’re certain you’re alone. Your danger sense is not only always on, it’s in constant overdrive ... and it’s not always right. All failed Awareness rolls count as total failures (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 122). The Storyteller may name up to three people or pieces of equipment in the scene the character believes is watching them. Any or all may be utterly benign.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1qhvYbe0K2Hj3bsA"},"adv:v5-gematria":{"name":"• Gematria","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult of Shalim.</p><p>The Cult of Shalim uses a coded cipher called the gematria in their missives. You understand the system and can encrypt or decrypt messages using it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.LbkwCAf1SdUI2FxP"},"adv:v5-insidious-whispers":{"name":"•• Insidious Whispers","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult of Shalim.</p><p>The Embrace cracks a person’s very core, throwing into question everything she believed before her death. Many Kindred shore up their senses of self with ambitions, dreams, and ideals to see them through the long nights. As a Shalimite, you flow like water into those cracks, widening them until they split. When you make a Social roll to undermine a character’s Conviction (based on you guessing or interpreting it or something close to it), 10s count as immediate critical successes without needing to roll two.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.D9YitqTWXzu11jKH"},"adv:v5-empty":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Empty","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Cult of Shalim.</p><p>You’ve carved away all of your fleeting, temporal joys. No bonds of love or lofty ambitions remain. While within the cult, hollowness is good, that emptiness radiates from you in a way that disturbs the uninitiated. People try to extricate themselves from your presence quickly, making it hard to have more than superficial conversations with them. Subtract two dice from Social rolls.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Empty","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.rt81bmqiDNJ9vy6V"},"adv:v5-bargainer":{"name":"• Bargainer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Mithraic Mysteries.</p><p>Mithras was a god of merchants and traders, and as his devotee you see both the value and drawbacks to any deals you broker. Reduce the Difficulty on rolls to assess a transaction by 1.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fyu566ax4hjeGYDA"},"adv:v5-bull-slayer":{"name":"••• Bull-Slayer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Mithraic Mysteries.</p><p>Like Mithras, when you set your sights on a target, you pursue it until it falls. You may suffer setbacks, but you don’t give up — you adapt. During each component of an extended test, reroll up to two failures (providing your equivalent Attribute for the test — Stamina, Composure, or Resolve — isn’t rated at 1, where if it is, you can reroll one failure per component).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NZMZCHjNnjhnk25i"},"adv:v5-failed-initiate":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Failed Initiate","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Mithraic Mysteries.</p><p>You faltered while taking one (or several) of the Seven Steps, and your Pater has assigned a Mawla to ensure your future success. While it reflects the cult’s investment in you, this Mawla watches — and scrutinizes — your every move. She may interrupt your plans at whim, offering instruction and demand you prove yourself at inconvenient times.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.6ejYyV7w5V3jBUgU"},"adv:v5-archangels-grace":{"name":"•• Archangel’s Grace","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Nephilim.</p><p>You’ve invested considerable time in dance studios, gymnasiums, and dojos owned by fellow Nephilim. The training makes your movements lithe and assured, every gesture an art form in itself. You may use your Performance Skill in place of your Athletics Skill, or vice versa, when engaged in what for mortals would be a cardio-heavy physical exertion such as fighting, fleeing, or dancing.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"98"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ThAr92XCzufWH2iK"},"adv:v5-yearning":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Yearning","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Nephilim.</p><p>Your master is gone from your life. Perhaps she was Beckoned. Perhaps you broke off contact, or she did, or someone else came between you. No matter how much time has passed, you still wish you were at her side, pursuing her goals. When your plans come into conflict with those your master set down for you, spend two points of Willpower to work counter to her wishes. For extended actions, spend one point for each roll. If you have no Willpower points left to spend, your next action must be in pursuit of your master’s goals.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.C62qZYjizgMFivCn"},"adv:v5-holy-ground":{"name":"• Holy Ground","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; cult member.</p><p>Your haven exists in a place of significance to your cult. You may keep an apartment once owned by a respected Pater or claimed an alcove in the catacomb where the cult’s founder is said to have slept. Other cultists view you as the custodian of a holy place. Once per story, you may call on a small group of cultists to help defend your haven if it’s threatened.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.jerWl2U8Ts1fzzsy"},"adv:v5-shrine":{"name":"Shrine","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; cult member.</p><p>You keep a shrine in your haven.</p><p>• Tabletop Shrine — The space is no bigger than a desktop or small table, with items that easily fit in a drawer or a box you can stow beneath a loose floorboard: a few candles, a ritual bowl, a small vial of Vitae. You may perform Level One rituals, if they can be done solo.</p><p>•• Moderate Shrine — You’ve devoted a room solely to worship, complete with a small altar and several items relevant to your faith. You may perform rituals of Level Three and below and can accommodate one or two other worshipers in the space.</p><p>••• Large Shrine — You’ve spent considerable time and resources on making the shrine as close to your faith’s temples and basilicas as you can. You may perform rituals of Level Four and below and can accommodate up to 10 other worshipers in the space. If you also have the Security System Merit (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 189), add one additional die to rolls to resist unauthorized entry into the shrine.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Children of the Blood","page":"99"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.SJCydtFNs0miZ5R3"},"adv:v5-semblance-of-the-methuselah":{"name":"Semblance of the Methuselah","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Whether you’re aware of it or not, you have a striking resemblance to a methuselah of your line. Perhaps your own sire felt a strange attraction to your appearance that drove them to Embrace you for reasons they couldn’t quite explain, or it may be that the raw power of your ancestor’s blood physically shifted your looks to more closely match theirs upon your embrace. Upon taking this Merit, you may choose a specific methuselah of your clan from whom you are ultimately descended and whom you resemble. Alternatively, you may allow your Storyteller to choose and leave the nature of your similarity a mystery to you — for now, at least. Each dot in this Merit grants you one additional die on dice rolls to impress, intimidate, or attract the attention of those who recognize and fear or respect the progenitor whom you resemble. Each dot in this Merit may also add to your Status dots within your clan during official gatherings or ceremonies or within a cult that venerates the methuselah whom you resemble. If you are luckless enough to encounter the methuselah in question, each dot in this Merit also grants you one additional die to any social rolls when interacting with them, although depending on the ancient in question, it may anger them to see such similarity.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Semblance of the Methuselah (with those who know the methuselah, per dot)","value":1,"paths":["skills.intimidation","social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"},"perDot":true}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"85"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.A3eIICOWS2LbHSbj"},"adv:v5-penitence":{"name":"Penitence","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>In pain, you find absolution. By scourging yourself — lashing your skin with a whip, driving needles into your flesh, or some other self-abusive act — you reaffirm your faith. For each dot you possess in this Merit, you can scourge yourself once per session as part of a scene, suffering one point of Superficial Health damage and immediately healing one point of Superficial Willpower damage. Scourging usually takes an entire scene to complete.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9fjRPEmdwaaq7zwb"},"adv:v5-horrible-scars-of-penitence":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Horrible Scars of Penitence","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your devotion is writ large on your flesh in a mass of awful welts, the scarified marks of your faith, or the evidence of other tools of self-mortification emblazoned on your body. You are extremely distinctive in this respect, and anyone who looks at you can immediately recognize the signs of faith taken to an extreme — even if you cease to perform such physical acts of penitence. This Flaw is equivalent to the Repulsive Flaw (see Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 179), but only when you’re among vampires and mortals not from within your cult.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.clmt2wzJECOzh9EX"},"adv:v5-groveling-worm":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Groveling Worm","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Not with Penitence.</p><p>You don’t get affirmation from scourging your own flesh; rather, your utter self-loathing and abject terror of offending some higher power demands that you perform such acts regularly just to function at all. You must find the time in a session to scourge yourself once per session, suffering two points of Superficial Health damage, or else you will suffer a point of Aggravated Willpower damage at the beginning of the next session. Characters with the Penitence Merit cannot take this Flaw.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.4dPU1qnGtVFsc9em"},"adv:v5-zealotry":{"name":"Zealotry","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The fire of your faith is an unstoppable bonfire, driving you to intense heights of zeal. You won’t let anything come between you and the fulfilment of your beliefs, no matter the cost to yourself or others. For each dot in this Merit, once per session after rolling a normal success on an action that corresponds to or aligns with one of your Convictions, you may choose to turn that normal success into a messy critical instead. In this way, you gain the usual benefits for a critical win on the roll in question along with the appropriate drawbacks for the mess you create in the process. Note that this does not yield additional successes, but only allows you to count the win as critical in a case where it matters.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.fibSSHCgLZ0uQrr5"},"adv:v5-crisis-of-faith":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Crisis of Faith","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are terrified of losing control, especially to the Beast that threatens to make a mockery of your faith. Whenever your actions result in a bestial failure, you suffer one point of superficial Willpower damage in addition to any other effects from the roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.O4YnnbMEDvs3o3EL"},"adv:v5-unholy-will":{"name":"Unholy Will","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your will and soul are bulwarks against the power of True Faith (Vampire: The Masquerade, p. 222). You may have spent many nights upon holy ground or argued with clever, devout theologians until the intricacies of their faith are as familiar to you as the dead stillness in your chest. You might have even gone to great lengths to harden your mind against the burning ardor of true believers or cast yourself so deeply into the role of demonic foe through ritual personae that you are firm in your conviction that your purpose is to test such belief. One way or another, as angel or devil, the signs and forms of True Faith do not hold sway against you. At two dots, add one die to any dice pool when you resist or contest an individual with true Faith on matters of their faith, including their miraculous abilities. Furthermore, you suffer one fewer point of damage from holy sources that harness the power of True Faith, such as blessed weapons or sacred ceremonies. At four dots, add two dice and suffer two fewer points of damage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.i1lRt1SRccDJTk0b"},"adv:v5-beacon-of-profanity":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Beacon of Profanity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The dark power within you leaks out. Mortals with any amount of True Faith can sense your presence, not just those with True Faith 3 or higher.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.y9xLPZLDfHruNE8d"},"adv:v5-disease-vector":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Disease Vector","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The corrupt life energy of your vitae is fertile ground for the festering sicknesses of the kine. Whenever you feed from a sick mortal, you always contract that sickness and will pass it on to your next vessel.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"86"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.kxjqTvB3JA5le1cH"},"adv:v5-plaguebringer":{"name":"Flaw: Plaguebringer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have a disease in your vitae that you cannot expunge and that manifests its symptoms on your body despite your undead state; even totally flushing all the blood from your body and drinking afresh does not rid you of it. It seems to well up from your corrupt soul. At one dot, the disease is minor but expresses visible traces such as a rash, sores, coughing or sneezing, or oozing mucus. At two dots, the disease is potentially severe if you leave it untreated, although it is not necessarily fatal. Furthermore, it does not need to leave visible symptoms upon you. In either case, mortal victims of your bite who are susceptible to the disease will contract it.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GicdoZBLNppKslm0"},"adv:v5-sloppy-feeder":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Sloppy Feeder","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Wherever you go, a telltale pattern of attacks follows, as your feeding style leaves much to be desired. If caught feeding by the authorities, they’ll have sufficient evidence to connect you to a series of assaults (and probably murders), as well as leaking your location to Second Inquisition assets.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Fo54p0As265LPQ3Q"},"adv:v5-cold-dead-hunger":{"name":"••• Cold Dead Hunger","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When hungry, your Beast is a weak and quiescent thing; it still urges you towards self-indulgent atrocity but is easier to quell. Perhaps you starved it through meditation or self-denial, or girded yourself in rites of black sorcery and red transgression; perhaps it’s just the way you are, and you have no explanation for the cold, dead hunger that nestles in your bosom. Add two dice to your rolls to resist Hunger frenzy.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Cold Dead Hunger (resisting Hunger frenzy)","value":2,"paths":["frenzy"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.F88ICG63sFDHQEt7"},"adv:v5-pack-diablerie":{"name":"•• Pack Diablerie","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your thirst for the corrupt essence of other vampires is horrifically intense. If you attempt to consume a victim via diablerie alongside other partners, you are always the one who has the opportunity to consume the full soul unless you choose otherwise or one of your partners also has this Merit. Additionally, when you help another to consume the full soul and if they are successful, then you also gain 5 experience points to spend on raising Blood Potency or disciplines known to the victim, as if you yourself had committed diablerie and achieved one success on the Humanity + Blood Potency roll.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Forbidden Religions","page":"87"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OCwuM1mL0tYFD9lF"},"adv:v5-influencer":{"name":"• Influencer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Fame ••+.</p><p>People don’t just love you — they take your word as gospel. You write social media posts that make or break businesses, and even high-ranking officials who should know better listen to you. Once per story, with the Storyteller’s approval, you have the equivalent of an Influence rating equal to your Fame minus one towards a single fan or a field related to your stardom.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bZzsCS0HOPBzKNYu"},"adv:v5-enduring-fame":{"name":"• Enduring Fame","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Fame •••+.</p><p>As an immensely beloved figure before your Embrace, you’ve learned that the benefits — and drawbacks — of stardom remain after death. When you lose points of Fame during a story, they return to full value at the start of the next story with no effort necessary.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1NQ50akKyvviw7pb"},"adv:v5-mobile":{"name":"Mobile","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit.</p><p>Your haven is on wheels, wings, or the wide open sea. A one-dot mobile haven may be a car, van, or truck with a sunproof trunk or other compartment for daysleep, or pull a small trailer to carry gear. A two-dot mobile Haven could be an RV, motor coach, or bus. A massive, three-dot mobile haven could be an entire train, jet, or large ship. Coterie dots can be used for this Merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.yhkQMF5dKWpVb6dF"},"adv:v5-armored":{"name":"• Armored","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; requires Mobile.</p><p>You’re taking no chances with your haven, taking pains to reinforce panels, locks, and tires, and swapping out the glass for something more resistant to whatever gets thrown at it. The vehicle continues running perfectly until it sustains ten points of damage, while only Critical Hits penetrate sufficiently to affect passengers.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.cGsXtKjHi59rrARD"},"adv:v5-smugglers-stash":{"name":"Smugglers Stash","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; requires Mobile.</p><p>You’ve dedicated a small section of your Haven to security, ensuring that even if accessed and subjected to a thorough search, your cargo — or you — remains safely stashed away. For one dot, this space is equivalent in size to one person, while two dots expands the space to cover your entire coterie. Searches for the compartment are made at +2 Difficulty.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DFU0Ycz1Fl67gdgo"},"adv:v5-spare-plates":{"name":"•• Spare Plates","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven merit; requires Mobile.</p><p>Vehicles are easy to trace, and their size makes them harder to hide. To ensure your home’s reputation never trips you up, you keep a series of spare plates, stickers, and other identifiers that you can switch out with a turn’s worth of activity, giving you sufficient cover to avoid all but the most invasive searches.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.423ODhLK4hH1saCG"},"adv:v5-on-the-rails":{"name":"Flaw: (•) On the Rails","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven flaw; requires Mobile.</p><p>Your haven’s movement is not entirely under your control, such as on a ship or train. You might speed or delay a departure, or make an unscheduled stop, but the destinations are likely fixed. Work with the Storyteller to determine where your haven travels.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.84lyBiD0ruQhtzYR"},"adv:v5-temperamental":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Temperamental","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Haven flaw; requires Mobile.</p><p>Whether you love it despite its flaws, can’t afford the repairs, or are ignorant to your haven’s mechanical imperfections, your vehicle is unreliable and waiting for the worst moment to disappoint you. Any failed Drive test sees the vehicle grind to a halt, unable to be restarted without a successful repair test. Should you change your vehicle, this Flaw follows you unless bought off with Experience.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DuYykpFAKlKn83MZ"},"adv:v5-drive-thru":{"name":"• Drive-thru","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve mastered the art of feeding while on the move, grabbing what you need before getting the hell outta there. Once per session, by adding +1 to the Difficulty, you can safely complete a hunt within minutes.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RtMTrsLMr422SeUh"},"adv:v5-resonance-sensitivity":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Resonance Sensitivity","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’re sensitive to the secret flavors of blood, and one of them really messes with your head. Pick one Resonance, excepting Animal and Void. When you feed on a vessel with that Resonance at an Intense or higher Temperament, your Storyteller creates a unique Compulsion (Vampire, p. 208) based on that Resonance that must be carried out before the end of the story. This Compulsion persists even if you feed from a vessel with a different Resonance or the same Resonance with a lesser Temperament later in the story. If it isn’t resolved at the end of the story, these suppressed emotions are released in a messy, potentially violent way of the Storyteller’s choosing. You may select this Flaw again to be sensitive to an additional Resonance.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.czpErbhYAwKaZrVm"},"adv:v5-resonance-mimic":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Resonance Mimic","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The vessels you feed from have a habit of sticking around with you. You don’t just pick up the Resonances in their blood, you’re absorbing parts of their memories, personality, or maybe even their very soul. After you feed on a mortal of any Resonance at a Fleeting Temperament or higher, you experience vivid and distracting visions of your victim’s memories during the next scene, which inflicts a two-dice penalty to a test of the Storyteller’s choice. Killing someone through feeding has an additional effect: the victim’s personality and memories persist in your mind and might take over your body for a scene when your Willpower is Incapacitated. This effect lasts until your Willpower is Incapacitated or the end of the story.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Resonance Mimic (during the visions after feeding)","value":-2,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"25"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.bloSkTIYRwTuioIp"},"adv:v5-ley-line-leech":{"name":"• Ley Line Leech","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Whether through divination, archaic maps, or gut instinct, you follow the ancient paths of power while traveling, thus benefiting from their mystical energy. After spending more than a few hours travelling to a different city or locale, your Blood becomes positively charged, negating the need for a Rouse Check when rising the following night.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gfsIHR7THyjPfWGC"},"adv:v5-persistent-blush":{"name":"••• Persistent Blush","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your dead flesh is uniquely receptive to the Blood when it’s time to imitate life. Your lifelike demeanor and biological responses last much longer than your peers, and you can walk among mortals for nights at a time without extra effort. A single activation of Blush of Life persists for one week.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ilek5M36K5VFgOKM"},"adv:v5-land-locked":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Land Locked","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cannot leave the land, and any journey over air or sea enrages your Beast to the point of madness. You must make a Fear Frenzy test (Difficulty 3) to board a boat or plane.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.JCHcwi1WxqFitqqs"},"adv:v5-resistant-blush":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Resistant Blush","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your dead flesh accepts the Blood in every way save for the pretense of mortality. Blush of Life doesn’t come as naturally to you as your peers, and sometimes it won’t work without further effort. When you make a Rouse Check to activate the Blush of Life, roll twice and take the lowest result.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.g7KQD0DINXN1fCX5"},"adv:v5-corpse-flesh":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Corpse Flesh","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your body is truly and irrevocably dead, rendering you unable to use Blush of Life.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.vHAeOjWocazNPcEP"},"adv:v5-mortal-pretender":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Mortal Pretender","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>While all right-minded Kindred do their best to blend in with mankind, you go above and beyond to the point where you might have decided to lead a mortal life despite your new diet. It unsettles your undead peers. You suffer a two-dice penalty for all Social tests involving vampires specifically dedicated to upholding the Masquerade, such as Vehme coteries, Sheriffs and their Hounds, Archons and Justicars.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Mortal Pretender (vampires who uphold the Masquerade)","value":-2,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ZZSpQezrPCRUvza0"},"adv:v5-scene-kid":{"name":"• Scene Kid","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Every subculture has its trappings; you’re the unliving embodiment of its particular style. While it may not impress the masses, for those in the know, you look every inch the part. Add one die to all appropriate Social pools when dealing with that subculture.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Scene Kid (dealing with that subculture)","value":1,"paths":["social"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Live from the Succubus Club","page":"26"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.s6laBmd5nm14sdPh"},"adv:v5-soothed-beast":{"name":"• Soothed Beast","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Choose a storyteller character as your obsession. Once per session when in the presence of your obsession, you can choose to ignore one Bestial Failure or Messy Critical. If this character dies you immediately gain three Stains, but can choose a new obsession at the beginning of the next session.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood-Stained Love","page":"151"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AtSs4TGRIM9tGg2I"},"adv:v5-false-love":{"name":"• False Love","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Choose a storyteller character as your obsession. When in their presence, treat your Humanity as one dot higher (max 10) for purposes of the Blush of Life, eating or drinking, and sexual intercourse. If this character dies you immediately gain three Stains, but can choose a new obsession at the beginning of the next session. This merit doesn’t combine with others that treat your Humanity as higher: if you have multiple ways to treat your humanity as higher than it is, choose the most effective one in any situation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood-Stained Love","page":"151"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HR9P0hRpCGb0RoPS"},"adv:v5-up-all-night":{"name":"Up All Night","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Nosferatu may not take this Merit.</p><p>Requirement: Not Nosferatu; Hecata only ••.</p><p>Not all Kindred resemble walking corpses as they lose themselves to the Beast. For purposes of the Blush of Life, eating or drinking, and sexual intercourse, treat your Humanity score as one point higher than it is (maximum 10), or two points higher if this merit was purchased for 4 dots. Nosferatu cannot select this merit, and Hecata can only select it as a two-dot merit. This merit doesn’t combine with others that treat your Humanity as higher: if you have multiple ways to treat your humanity as higher than it is, choose the most effective one in any situation.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood-Stained Love","page":"151"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.X7GVERClkg6kOiaN"},"adv:v5-two-masters":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Two Masters","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You can be Blood Bound to two individuals at the same time. This may cause no end of trouble if your character is prone to drinking the Blood of other Kindred for recreational reasons. The Storyteller should disallow this Flaw if Blood Bonds are unlikely to be an issue for the player character.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Blood-Stained Love","page":"151"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.9juxWP5btXaJ41wo"},"adv:v5-bonds-of-fealty":{"name":"••• Bonds of Fealty","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Dominate.</p><p>Your powers of Dominate do not require a target’s eye contact so long as the target is blood bound to you. You still need to be able to communicate your instructions to them, though you do not need to be physically present.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"44"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Iyjmk6t9bcTOeqU7"},"adv:v5-enduring-bond":{"name":"• Enduring Bond","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your bonds last longer than those of other Kindred. Complete and partial blood bonds only weaken every other month, not every month.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"44"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HNVEW1VpoAQKA0cl"},"adv:v5-consanguineous-sense":{"name":"•• Consanguineous Sense","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Not Caitiff or thin-blood.</p><p>Your Blood calls out to your close relations, whether ancestors or descendants. You instinctively recognize those in your direct bloodline when you get within a few paces from them. A Resolve + Awareness test at Difficulty 3 lets you determine if a Kindred within about 3 meters of you shares your bloodline. You only learn that they share your bloodline, not the exact distance from your in terms of Generation. (Caitiff and Duskborn have ancestors and descendants, but their weakened bloodlines do not form a cohesive group for the purposes of these Advantages.)</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"44"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.t6V9UQBC7mWCfsji"},"adv:v5-consanguineous-influence":{"name":"•• Consanguineous Influence","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Not Caitiff or thin-blood.</p><p>Some find the powers of their Blood are more effective on those of the same bloodline. When you use a mental Discipline against a member of your own clan or a direct ancestor or descendant, you gain one bonus die to do so, or 2 bonus dice if the character is only one or two generations removed from you (e.g. your grandsire or grandchilde or closer).</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Consanguineous Influence (mental Discipline against your clan or lineage (2 if one or two generations apart))","value":1,"paths":["disciplines"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"44"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Mt8CcYZie4kNdb93"},"adv:v5-sins-of-the-fathers":{"name":"Sins of the Fathers","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Not Caitiff or thin-blood at •••.</p><p>After committing diablerie upon a direct descendant — or ancestor — you do not show any telltale signs of the crime. Powers such as Scry the Soul or A Taste for Blood do not detect your crime. As a three-dot Merit, this works when committing diablerie upon any member of your own clan. Caitiff and Duskborn cannot take this as a three-dot merit.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"44"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DJyz4TtsrM5qNH1E"},"adv:v5-unblinking-visage":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Unblinking Visage","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your dead body has forgotten the basics: blinking, breathing, and automatic reflexes. You count your Humanity as two levels lower (to a minimum of zero) for the purpose of Blush of Life and similar life-mimicking activities, such as mortal intercourse or food consumption.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"45"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.o4V9f1xK7W7iWQQF"},"adv:v5-secret-master":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Secret Master","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Mawla •+.</p><p>Your Mawla has a hold over you, and once per story requires you to do a minor task (equivalent to a minor boon) which cannot become public. If you fail to complete this task, next story it increases from the equivalent of a minor boon to a major boon. If the task is still left unresolved, this flaw is forfeited and should be replaced by 2 or more combined dots of other flaws, such as Shunned or an Adversary.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"45"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.DWA517Glzu0NpuQS"},"adv:v5-blatant-diablerist":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Blatant Diablerist","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: At creation only with a history of diablerie.</p><p>The telltale signs of diablerie are always noticeable to those who have the ability to sense it. Discipline Powers or Merits that reveal past evidence of diablerie, such as Scry the Soul (Auspex 3) or A Taste for Blood (Blood Sorcery 1) always reveal diablerie, even if the test would otherwise fail or fail to reveal other information. You can only take this Flaw at character creation if your character has a history of having committed diablerie at least once.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"45"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.BclmnsA4PMczfClf"},"adv:v5-inherited-bane":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Inherited Bane","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You gain another clan’s bane in addition to your own. Tremere may select this Flaw to gain the Salubri clan bane (Vampire Players Guide, p. 47) without having committed Diablerie.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Gehenna War","page":"45"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.I5rljJAAxEuRi5Vp"},"adv:v5-shameful-childe":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Shameful Childe","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Adversary Flaw.</p><p>You did the thing other Kindred have trauma from — you created a childe and left them to fend for themselves. Perhaps you didn’t know until it was too late, perhaps you lost control and were too ashamed to face the music. Either way, now they’re out there somewhere, and sooner or later, they’ll come back to confront you.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"20"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.5oVNRJDgUQsAFw6e"},"adv:v5-touchstone-embraced-by-your-enemies":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Touchstone Embraced by Your Enemies","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Adversary Flaw.</p><p>This former Touchstone of yours didn’t simply die or change as a person — they were Embraced. Now they run with your enemies. This Adversary is a single neonate, but they have allies, and the mere confrontation with what they’ve become carries a risk of Stains.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"20"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.zfezb3dIqD0qkiNL"},"adv:v5-custodian-of-history":{"name":"• Custodian of History","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>An elder has confided his version of a lost age to you before being called to the Gehenna War, destroyed in a FIRSTLIGHT paramilitary strike, or consumed by a Sabbat pack on the home front of the Eternal Struggle. You get tacit permission to refer to, use, read up on, and form your character’s opinion on one of the most important periods or characters in Vampire lore, granting +1 die to all relevant Skill tests pertaining to your subject. Possible subjects include: The Book of Nod and the First Cities (Noddist lore is still seen as heretical by many ancillae); The First Inquisition, including the Anarch Revolt and the Convention of Thorns; Constantinople and the Trinity; The Revelations of the Dark Mother (heretical to most); Antiquity and the War of Carthage.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Custodian of History (tests about your subject)","value":1,"paths":["skills.academics","skills.occult","skills.politics"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"20"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.UndL0KlHTgPtGasq"},"adv:v5-grief-phobia":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Grief Phobia","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The traumatic loss of a Touchstone saddled you with a permanent phobia for something that reminds you of them, the way you lost them, or the Conviction they used to embody to you. You lose one die in all tests made while in the presence of your phobic stimulus (determine what this is when you take the Flaw). Examples could include a genre of music, a certain smell, or the Touchstone’s family members. You can take this Flaw once for each lost Touchstone in your past.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[{"source":"Grief Phobia (near your phobic stimulus)","value":-1,"paths":["all"],"displayWhenInactive":true,"activeWhen":{"check":"never"}}],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"21"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Ajue0w7JhGBmpNmN"},"adv:v5-old-tricks":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Old Tricks","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve never mastered the modern age: all your specialties must be Archaic specialties.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"21"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TTyxSI04g1yZv5mG"},"adv:v5-banned-from":{"name":"Flaw: Banned From…","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>At some point in your illustrious past, you got yourself barred from a city ruled by Kindred who still won’t welcome you.</p><p>• A small city like Salzburg, Tromsø, Napier, or Portland.</p><p>•• A mid-sized city like Ljubljana, Malaga, Sacramento, or Venice.</p><p>••• A large city like Paris, Rio de Janeiro, Tokyo, Atlanta, or Cairo.</p><p>Getting in anyway is possible, of course, but there’ll be all kinds of trouble. Storytellers should note that this city should be relevant to the chronicle at some point.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"21"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.RZBLF0drqDkTZ41f"},"adv:v5-outdated-preference":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Outdated Preference","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This Flaw resembles the Ventrue Clan Bane, except your preferred mortal has become almost impossible to come by. Examples include castrato singers, quaalude addicts, footmen, phrenologists, milkmen, Redcoats, and wet nurses. You either have to capture and force mortals to fit with your preference, or you always spend 1 Willpower to feed.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"21"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.VijGnlXNs5ZTAAYb"},"adv:v5-object-of-power":{"name":"Object of Power","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Over the course of your travels, you’ve gotten your hands on a rare but powerful item.</p><p>• A set of uncommonly lucky dice. You may reroll a single die once per story, excluding Hunger dice.</p><p>•• Grimoire. It’s empty until you fill it, but it grants you a +1 die bonus to all Level 1 Ritual tests.</p><p>••• Golden eye amulet that protects you against threats. You get a free Premonition warning once per session when someone is about to cause you harm, even if you don’t have that power.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"21"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.k13CZwi678G5mvhc"},"adv:v5-cursed-object":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Cursed Object","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Something you own wants you dead. Once per session, at a time determined by the Storyteller, you must reroll a successful test, risking failure instead.</p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"In Memoriam","page":"21"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HIw1Y3QxUVCrqBsX"},"adv:v20-sturdy-da":{"name":"•••• Sturdy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Training, hardships, or natural ability have inured your body to pain and damage. While you are not necessarily a hulking brute, anyone who has seen you in action knows that you can take more damage than the average individual. You gain an additional Bruised health level and the Storyteller may award you with any appropriate bonuses when your innate toughness comes into play.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"420"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.SMfMBuwGKRLJLJ07"},"adv:v20-ragged-bite-da":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Ragged Bite","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Unlike your Cainite peers, you lack the ability to neatly seal up the wound caused by feeding. Instead, you leave torn, bleeding marks which have a chance of becoming infected. Your target must make a Stamina roll (difficulty the number of damage caused + 5) to resist infection. The impact of such a notable wound in conjunction with a diseased target is left to the discretion of the Storyteller. At the very least, this poses complications in hiding your identity as anything other than a monster.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"421"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.Em6L7TbLICWhifwu"},"adv:v20-unclean-da":{"name":"Flaw: Unclean","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Whether a lingering trait from your mortal life or an unfortunate side effect of your Embrace, your skin is marred in such a way that most casual observers assume you are a leper (or at least carrying some kind of plague). You may be barred entry to major cities, thrown out of most establishments, or subject to misguided attempts at healing or mercy. At 3 points, you carry leprosy (or some other contagious and visible disease). Nosferatu who take this Flaw receive only 1 point for it and must take its full effects (3 points).</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"421"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.A1gESTTxXYzjLT45"},"adv:v20-literacy-da":{"name":"• Literacy","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are a learned scholar, able to both read and write. The Merit assumes that you can read and write with fluency in your native tongue, as well as Latin and any languages you may have gained through the Multi-Lingual Merit. In some chronicles, literacy can be assumed. In these cases, Illiteracy becomes a 1-point Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"422"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.chYgSydP9laq6k4H"},"adv:v20-flesh-eater-da":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Flesh Eater","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You cling to the misguided belief that you must also consume the blood-rich flesh of your victim (such as the heart or liver). This has obvious impact on the size of your herd as well as the mortal population eventually linking mysterious, gruesome deaths to you. To keep down blood consumed without a murderous amount of flesh, you must succeed in a Willpower roll (difficulty 8) or vomit up the blood.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"422"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.04F8f0iTJMwJSlZN"},"adv:v20-the-company-of-saints-and-heroes-da":{"name":"• The Company of Saints and Heroes","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Life (and possibly unlife) experiences have brought you close to august religious figures, political entities, and legendary heroes. They do truly know you and think well of you, but you are not necessarily close. Use the Allies background to represent a tighter relationship. This Merit allows you to name-drop your famous connection and reduce the difficulty to applicable Social rolls by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"423"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.sCuaeBKTVfEJi16y"},"adv:v20-outsider-da":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Outsider","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>This is not your native land; this is not your mother tongue. You are not from around here. Everything is strange and different, from the customs to the food. You struggle with the area’s native language and the difficulty to applicable Social rolls increases by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"423"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.iivxSS0AHhdHvETi"},"adv:v20-marked-for-death-da":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Marked for Death","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Court of Blood has found fault with you and placed a price upon your head. You are earmarked for death, and one by one your peers will come to bring it to you. While you may be more than capable of taking your equals in a fight, you know not when or where they will strike. The festering paranoia claws at the edges of your thoughts; in addition to attackers of varying capabilities appearing at random, at Storyteller’s discretion you may also lose one die to Mental rolls due to distraction.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"425"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.gigk21YzKmhvKpy0"},"adv:v20-cannot-embrace-da":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Cannot Embrace","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Through some curse, weakness, or defect in your vampirism, you are unable to create progeny. You may not be aware of this until it is too late and a victim lays bleeding out in your arms.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"427"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ciCgB0aBT6nbPaxG"},"adv:v20-repulsive-to-animals-da":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Repulsive to Animals","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>When you are near, natural animals startle and panic. Unlike the normal disturbance caused by the presence of Cainites, animals near you either bolt in terror or become aggressive and violent, depending on their temperament. This makes riding a horse (or any other riding beast) nearly impossible, as you are unable to calm any creature down with a normal Animal Ken roll. You must use the abilities of the Animalism Discipline or feed the animal some of your blood, which transforms it into a ghoul.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"427"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.S9OBrB9hhg8GWfp0"},"adv:v20-weak-blood-da":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••) Weak Blood","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Something has diminished your supernatural vigor and lessened your abilities as a vampire. Where others effortlessly command the gifts of Lilith, you struggle. No Discipline you have knowledge of may be higher than 4. It costs you twice the normal amount of blood points to control any vampiric abilities. You must feed a person six drinks of your feeble blood before they can become your thrall, and you do not possess the potency to create a ghoul or sire a childe. This effect also covers the drawbacks of the Cannot Embrace Flaw, therefore the two are mutually exclusive.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"428"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xpGHG6aay4JWYUHg"},"adv:v20-weak-aura-da":{"name":"•• Weak Aura","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Merit at Road 4 or less; a Flaw at Road 5+.</p><p>The aura of your Road clings weakly to you, manifesting only when you perform acts of faith or when your fervor is at its most intense. This is a Merit if your Road rating is 4 or less, and a Flaw if it’s 5 or more. Consult the chart in the book for the effect on your aura modifier.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"428"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.OLMtUuF5KK73fLBz"},"adv:v20-potent-aura-da":{"name":"••• Potent Aura","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Merit at Road 5+; a Flaw at Road 4 or less.</p><p>Your aura is mighty, giving people a powerful impression of you. Your presence ripples through a room, giving you away instantly. This is a mixed blessing, as you must grow into your burgeoning power, finding it difficult to control at first. This is a Merit if your Road is 5+ and Flaw if it’s 4 or less. Consult the chart in the book for the effect on your aura modifier.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages: Vampire (20th Anniversary)","page":"429"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.ijhsM284cc4UH8D1"},"adv:v20-trackers-mark-da":{"name":"•• Tracker’s Mark","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri may take this Merit.</p><p>Well attuned to the soul of her quarry, the Salubri’s third eye sees the influence of her opponent in the world, rather than mere signs of his physical passage. When attempting a tracking roll, the Salubri’s player rolls at -2 difficulty. Ephemeral signs of a quarry are considered valid evidence to follow, even if a Salubri cannot normally detect them; for example, if an irate Brujah disturbed a market’s peace, the Warrior can discern his presence from the still-irritated traders hours later.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages Companion (V20)","page":"100"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.XIBhnSsfsNIxpbFE"},"adv:v20-blooded-by-the-code-da":{"name":"••••• Blooded by the Code","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/salubri.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Salubri may take this Merit.</p><p>Inducted by a true Salubri Warrior, rather than a mere member of the bloodline, the young Cyclops possesses supernatural might far in excess of what she might otherwise wield. She is allowed to purchase and use the Discipline power Song of the Blooded at normal cost (40 experience points), irrespective of Generation. No other Valeren Discipline power may be purchased unless the Salubri otherwise qualifies for it. Additionally, her Status is accounted as two higher when interacting with other Salubri.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages Companion (V20)","page":"100"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.FLjL8RFmLMHElDzk"},"adv:v20-ravnos-jati-da":{"name":"•• Ravnos Jati","type":"feature","img":"modules/tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder/assets/logos/ravnos.webp","system":{"description":"<p>Only Ravnos may take this Merit.</p><p>You belong to a widely-recognized Ravnos jati, affording you some measure of status with your Clan members. Whenever you interact with another Vagabond, treat your Status as one level higher; if they also belong to your jati, treat your Background as two levels higher and your Allies, Contacts, or Mentor as one higher where your jati is concerned. Once per session, if you’re in a city with a significant Ravnos population, you can find one member of your jati with no issue, who will likely help you out with no questions asked. Once per chapter, a member of your jati will seek you out for help with a troublesome and dangerous task.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Dark Ages Companion (V20)","page":"101"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.GQFbNWwL9QtkH4vP"},"adv:v20-heretical-sympathizer-da":{"name":"•• Heretical Sympathizer","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are the protector of a Witch of Echidna, or you possess a trove of their knowledge. Whether you do this with altruistic or selfish aims, you stand to benefit from the cult’s permanent support and earn the Orthodoxy’s enduring distrust. Depending on the situation leading to your taking this trait, you may be able to court favor with the opposing side by selling out your Gorgon ward and gaining the endorsement of the Egyptian Followers of Set.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"61"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.myHFFaufGCc9LUS6"},"adv:v20-hunted-by-the-hierophants-da":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Hunted by the Hierophants","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Setites claim their religion is one of freedom from indoctrination and conditioning. The Hierophants openly agree, and then crush those who seek to subvert Sutekh’s faith. Whether as a result of being a Witch of Echidna, one of their postulants, or simply a Setite who throws off the yoke of Sutekh — the Hierophants are displeased by your decision and seek you out, actively sending Setite warriors to bring you to heel. Depending on the depth of your sins, they will either aim to capture you and bring you back to the Courts of Egypt, or destroy you on the spot.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"61"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.QMeAv5A9M6tvaQcq"},"adv:v20-postulant-of-echidna-da":{"name":"••• Postulant of Echidna","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You receive the tutelage of one of the Witches of Echidna. This Merit allows you to take Setite Sorcery as a Clan Discipline in place of one of your others. If you’re outside the Setite Orthodoxy you may find yourself hunted for integration or destruction by others of your blood. Most Postulants of Echidna have several points in the Mentor Background to account for the Gorgon tutor. This Merit is predominantly held by Setites, but rumors hold of Witches of Echidna passing knowledge to vampires of other clans.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Tome of Secrets (Dark Ages 20th)","page":"61"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CfWaBQXW0nDJUPJL"},"adv:v20-malleable-visage-laibon":{"name":"•••• Malleable Visage","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Regardless of your Virtues, you can alter the supernatural features of your appearance to suit your mood or your plans. By spending a point of Willpower, you can change the severity of your appearance as based on either Orun or Aye by temporarily using one scale in place of the other. No actual values are altered, merely the appearance of the character to normal vision. The effect lasts for one scene or until canceled by the player.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.HJ56VexZRwXhvwW2"},"adv:v20-well-marked-laibon":{"name":"• Well-Marked","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have been ritually scarred as a warrior with a famous mark of protection and favor. This spot, no more than two adult handprints wide, is especially tough and resistant. Unless your enemy knows to avoid the mark, you enjoy one automatic success on soak rolls.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.i3b9DPh1niGeOMIN"},"adv:v20-earth-feeder-laibon":{"name":"Earth-Feeder","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Aye higher than Orun.</p><p>You are a rural Laibon raised to feed on the blood of certain animals. The spiritual bond you have with such beasts has altered your curse, so that you manage to draw extra nourishment from their blood. You can draw double the blood points, to a maximum of 10 points, from any mundane animals. For every point you place in this Merit, you may select another specific species of animal with which you share the bond. Humans and supernatural creatures do not qualify. You may select this Merit only if your Aye score exceeds your Orun score.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":0,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lJvkYcA9e0GJTlfw"},"adv:v20-milk-fed-laibon":{"name":"•• Milk-Fed","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have been weaned on an evil variant of the Masai blood-milk. Beyond being able to stomach fluids other than blood, you can also draw power from the Laibon warrior-drink, a concoction that raises Strength and Stamina by one dot each for every three blood points of the mixture consumed.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.lQQhhejHqE5gMZgx"},"adv:v20-excellent-thirst-laibon":{"name":"••• Excellent Thirst","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Disciplined fasting teases the thirst into more efficient burning of the blood, and you are practiced at doing this. By letting your blood pool drop to a rating less than your Stamina and not feeding for at least three nights, you gain two blood points worth of blood for every one point you drain from a living vessel the first time you feed thereafter. With regard to vampiric blood, this technique doubles only the first point you consume after fasting.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.AorzcxDSvvjaVubN"},"adv:v20-ill-marked-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (•) Ill-Marked","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have been ritually scarred against your will. Somewhere on your body is a large marking or collection of markings at least the size of two adult handprints. Besides being symbolic of something (but what?) the mark has left you especially vulnerable in one spot. Anyone who learns of the mark’s purpose gains an automatic success against you in combat.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.jcu1VfbK34lIyMN6"},"adv:v20-contrary-visage-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Contrary Visage","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Something about your appearance does not correspond to your Virtues. Worse, you might not even know why. Most often, moral Laibon with this Flaw seem like monsters, though it’s possible that this Flaw might make vile isolationists somehow attractive to unwanted parties. Whatever you want, your physical form portrays or invites the opposite.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.rVb0Fq9bH9fZ4GTx"},"adv:v20-corrupt-embrace-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Corrupt Embrace","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Not 14th Generation.</p><p>For some supernal reason, your Kiss is cursed. Most of your attempts to sire or make something your ghoul result in a monster more foul than anything spawned by Cagn’s curse. Your progeny become brutal, senseless killers hungry for blood and bone within an hour of receiving your Embrace. Any attempt to Embrace has a one-in-10 chance of being successful, with all other attempts resulting in aberrations. This Flaw may not be taken by Laibon of 14th generation or higher.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.tn4WscSBiDoXKVDa"},"adv:v20-fixed-generation-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Fixed Generation","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Twelfth Generation or thinner.</p><p>Your body is so thinly infused with the Curse of Cagn that its potency cannot be heightened. Your generation is permanent, regardless of diablerie. Whatever effects you would gain from drinking vampiric blood last only a single scene. Incredibly powerful vampiric blood is even rejected by your body once the scene is over, usually by vomiting ash. Only Laibon of the Twelfth Generation and thinner may have this Flaw.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"89"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.NTeAspGCVsaUBKut"},"adv:v20-wretched-embrace-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Wretched Embrace","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are the progeny of a weak-blooded neonate, arguably a being of the Fifteenth Generation, more likely some damned anomaly or the result of sloppy work. Your vampiric state is incomplete. You may never possess more than seven dots worth of Disciplines in total. Your Attributes may never be raised above 5 except through short-term applications of blood. You may not sire your own progeny or form blood bonds. You may not begin the game with any dots in Status.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"90"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TCD12hFg4WtKFnVp"},"adv:v20-foreign-taint-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Foreign Taint","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Bias, bigotry or overwhelming ignorance stands between you and any appreciation for your surroundings. Any action that requires you to demonstrate respect for distinctive local customs has its difficulty raised by two. Experience costs are doubled to learn Abilities or Disciplines that you would consider indigenous to Africa, including the local variations of the “Western” Disciplines.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"90"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.oQuyr2mQXUL7T6Cj"},"adv:v20-rural-fear-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Rural Fear","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You have no prior experience with the outdoors whatsoever. Worse, you have been brought up to believe that the wilderness is a vast catalog of possible catastrophes. For you, all outdoor-specific activities (survival, hunting, even running across certain terrain) have their difficulties raised by two.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"90"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.rk8CPbGKZO8BHXM7"},"adv:v20-plagued-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Plagued","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are followed everywhere by bad signs, ill omens and ugly portents. Flocks of inauspicious birds crowd the trees behind you or yellow-eyed hyenas stalk you everywhere. Whether or not there are meaningful supernatural effects, you are sure to be poorly regarded.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"90"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.80wPU6dGutJcIoMP"},"adv:v20-unfeeling-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (••) Unfeeling","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Your heart is dead and you act like it. It’s difficult for you to raise any concern for mortals or other undead, though you can fake it. You may never roll more dice for actions involving Charisma or Empathy than you have in Perception.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"90"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":2,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.xHwh4trR3ClkCd6H"},"adv:v20-strange-day-laibon":{"name":"••••• Strange Day","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: 13th Generation or thinner.</p><p>Some bond exists between your blood and the land, some trace of mortal life left in your flesh. One day a year, you may walk outdoors in the sunlight. This day usually falls on the anniversary of some personally or spiritually significant event, though not necessarily. By succeeding at an Aye roll and burning all your blood, you may force this year’s occurrence and survive the touch of sunlight for a number of hours equal to your Aye successes. Laibon lower than 13th generation may not take this Merit.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"91"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.3noTEQGAdjllkQzN"},"adv:v20-earth-bound-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (•••) Earth-Bound","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You are spiritually linked with some place on Earth. Travel far from this place results in nightmares, lethargy and madness. Every night you must make a Willpower roll with a difficulty equal to the number of nights you’ve spent away from your spiritual site. Every time you fail this roll, your Willpower drops by one dot until you return home. A botch indicates intermittent hysteria, depression or panic. Two consecutive botches result in frenzy.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"91"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.1YeAHcvolFvAgF88"},"adv:v20-empathic-sense-laibon":{"name":"• Empathic Sense","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Sangoma.</p><p>During their time as an Utwaza, the Sangoma learn to be sensitive to the spirits and the effects that they have on the mortal anatomy. As this sixth sense matures, an Utwaza suffers a sudden bout of phantom pains, which enables a Sangoma to properly prepare for her patient, who visits later that day. Mechanically, a character with Empathic Sense lowers the difficulties of any Medicine rolls for which she received advance notice by one.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"110"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":1,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.CShGj5txVDlkmWae"},"adv:v20-crippling-spirit-laibon":{"name":"Flaw: (••••) Crippling Spirit","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Requirement: Sangoma.</p><p>Although a Sangoma lives through her serious malady and survives to move beyond her time as an Utwaza, her body is not spared. A psychic scrutiny reveals the presence of an animal spirit that is almost unbearable to the Sangoma, crippling her body physically. She can walk only with the aid of a crutch, and even then at an infant’s pace — one-quarter that of an unhindered person, with running impossible. Additionally, the cause of the crippling spirit becomes obvious to anyone who scrutinizes the character’s aura.</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom (Revised)","page":"110"},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":4,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.K50l4WGtB0xyNpW5"},"adv:v20-crackshot":{"name":"••• Crackshot","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>You’ve trained extensively with firearms, to the point where the gun feels like an extension of your own hand. Spend a point of Willpower to make impressive trick shots. You still need to roll your Dexterity + Firearms if attacking someone. You can make bullets ricochet off a spittoon before hitting your target, shoot the lock off a door, or the cigarette out of someone’s mouth. (Source: Saligia Wiki. Not found in official materials; homebrew.)</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Saligia Wiki (homebrew)","page":""},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":3,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.EYfxW2AeWhXdBZBB"},"adv:v20-touch-of-the-wyld":{"name":"••••• Touch of the Wyld","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>Unlike nearly all other undead, you have no obvious Wyrmtaint. You are invisible to those who have the ability to naturally sense the Wyrm, smelling as a normal human might. Your true nature is only to be realized by displaying supernatural abilities or through some powerful extrasensory perception, such as reading one’s aura with Auspex, or a similarly functioning ability, such as the Gifts bestowed unto Garou by Luna. Even those with the Merit Scent for the Wyrm can’t smell your sin without the aid of Disciplines. (Source: Saligia Wiki. Not found in official materials; homebrew.)</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Saligia Wiki (homebrew)","page":""},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":5,"featuretype":"merit"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.KiNtOCXHwHffPODU"},"adv:v20-the-scourge":{"name":"Flaw: (•••••••) The Scourge","type":"feature","img":"systems/wod5e/assets/icons/items/discipline.png","system":{"description":"<p>The Scourge is a vampire-specific plague that infects the Blood. This disease is a mutated strain of leprosy crossed with the bubonic plague which directly attacks the vampire’s vitae. Once infected, the victim must spend more blood each time they expend vitae. The cost for any vitae expenditure increases by one for each night the victim suffers from The Scourge. In some cases, the victim also suffers from a disfiguring malady that impacts their appearance but does not threaten their life. While The Scourge is curable on a case-by-case basis, the disease itself cannot be exterminated. In extremely rare cases, an afflicted vampire becomes a carrier of the disease and immune to its effects. (Source: Saligia Wiki. Not found in official materials; homebrew.)</p><p><em>V20 entry: written for V20 rules, where certain systems worked a little differently. Agree with your Storyteller how it works in V5.</em></p>","macroid":"","bonuses":[],"dataItemId":"","source":{"book":"Saligia Wiki (homebrew)","page":""},"uses":{"max":0,"current":0,"enabled":false},"points":7,"featuretype":"flaw"},"effects":[],"flags":{},"_src":"Compendium.tokyostargazer-vtmbuilder.advantages.Item.TIbB0AvJCU4h0tHa"}};
